\par }{\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof {\shp{\*\shpinst\shpleft1440\shptop2070\shpright10800\shpbottom2070\shpfhdr1\shpbxpage\shpbxignore\shpbypage\shpbyignore\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt0\shpz2\shplockanchor\shplid2049{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn shapePath}{\sv 4}}{\sp{\sn fFillOK}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFilled}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fArrowheadsOK}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelh}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelv}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}}{\shprslt{\*\do\dobxpage\dobypage\dodhgt8194\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx9920\dppty0\dpx1190\dpy2070\dpxsize9920\dpysize0\dplinew15\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0}}}}{\f0\fs16  \par }}{\footerf \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }{\footer \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}} of {\field{\*\fldinst NUMPAGES}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President Trump Calls China Visit A "Success"; Interview With House Speaker Mike Johnson (R-LA); Sen. Cassidy Voted to Convict Trump in 2021 Impeachment Trial Following January 6; Louisiana Separates Democrat and Republican Ballots After Decades of Single Open Primary Ballot; The Politics of Abortion Policy; Trump Administration Zeroes in on Fraud; U.S. Targets ISIS In Nigeria; One-On-One With Amy Grant; Amy Grant Shares Stories of Her Life from the Past to the Present.\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Rachel Campos-Duffy, Alex Hogan, Jonathan Serrie \par \uc2 9,786 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2026\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2026 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2026 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 As the conflict in Iran nears a new milestone, here at home, a day of faith, music and prayer on the National Mall -- all part of the 250th birthday celebrations.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: God has blessed America for 250 years. But the faith of our founders didn't just shape the character of our civic institutions, it steeled the American spirit.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): We'll sit down with House Speaker Mike Johnson, who will lead the moment of rededication later today and discuss the President's trip to China.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This has been an incredible visit. I think a lot of -- a lot of good has come of it. We've made some fantastic trade deals, great for both countries.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: As well as the ticking clock for Congress to fund ICE and CBP, all before the Memorial Day Recess.\par \par \uc2 Then --\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , FORMER U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: What they have done with this decision by saying the politics of redistricting is okay is they are backdooring racism through politics.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY), MINORITY LEADER: We need dramatic electoral reform. We need dramatic campaign finance reform. We need dramatic judicial reform.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Those are institutional arsonists. It's a dangerous thing, a dangerous gambit. You don't just blow up the system when you lose.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll ask our Sunday panel about the Democratic Party's ideas for an expanded playbook.\par \par \uc2 Plus --\par \par \uc2 We travel to Tennessee to sit down with Amy Grant to discuss her early crossover success.\par \par \uc2 AMY GRANT, MUSICIAN: I'll meet people that say, "I was only allowed to listen to you growing up," and all I've said was, "I'm so sorry." There's so much great music because life is filled with everything.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And her 50 years in the music business and what led to her first new body of work in 13 years.\par \par \uc2 GRANT: It was like all these limitations were just creating a new path forward.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News today at Rededicate 250 on the National Mall, as folks start arriving for a day to give thanks, ahead of the nation's 250th birthday. They are expecting thousands here today.\par \par \uc2 More on that in just a minute, but we begin with a look at some of today's top stories.\par \par \uc2 An upset in Louisiana as incumbent Senator \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2  has been knocked out of his state's GOP primary. President Trump celebrated the news on his Truth Social account, citing disloyalty for Cassidy's impeachment vote back in 2021.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman Julia Letlow and State Treasurer John Fleming are set for a runoff election in June. We're going to have a live report from Baton Rouge later on in the show.\par \par \uc2 And Venezuela says it is extradited billionaire tycoon Alex Saab to the U.S. on Saturday. Saab, a close ally of deposed President Nicolas Maduro, faces federal charges for international money laundering and bribery.\par \par \uc2 And check it out, hundreds of family members and friends welcoming home more than 4,500 sailors from the aircraft carrier USS Gerald R. Ford in Norfolk Saturday after a record 326 days at sea. The carrier traveling from the Mediterranean Sea near Israel to the Caribbean for that operation that led to Maduro's capture in Venezuela, and finally, the Red Sea for Operation Epic Fury.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we're going to bring in House Speaker Mike Johnson to talk news of the day.\par \par \uc2 But first, let's go to FOX News correspondent Alex Hogan at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 With so much focus on Taiwan after President Trump's historic visit to China, Taiwan's president today reacting to that visit, saying that Taiwan will not escalate the conflict, although at the same time it's not going to give up its sovereignty or free way of life, nor will it be sacrificed or traded away.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, with this delicate ceasefire between the U.S. and Iran still underway, President Trump says that hostilities could resume if Iran fails to reach a deal.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice-over): U.S. Senate Republicans this week blocked the latest Democratic-led effort to end the war in Iran until it's approved by Congress. While three Republicans have now joined Democrats in efforts to rein in the president's war power, the attempt failed on a vote of 50 to 49.\par \par \uc2 The ceasefire in the Strait of Hormuz remains on shaky ground. Here at home, Americans say they're feeling the weight of the war at the pump.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We need to get a handle on this, and the U.S. needs to figure this out.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice-over): President Trump calling prices short-term pain. He made the comments during his historic two-day visit to China. The president touting the visit as a success, pointing to Chinese commitments to purchase American goods like soybeans and 200 Boeing aircraft.\par \par \uc2 China's Commerce Ministry Saturday announced the U.S. and China have agreed to expand agricultural trade. Still, critics argue the president failed to nail down enough concrete agreements.\par \par \uc2 RAHM EMANUEL, FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO JAPAN: I don't think the United States particularly got anything out of this.\par \par \uc2 China got the ability to show to the president lavish praise on him, but they're dogged in pursuing their strategic interests.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice-over): Taiwan-dominated conversations, although President Trump says America's stance remains the same.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I'm not looking to have somebody go independent, and, you know, we're supposed to travel 9,500 miles to fight a war. I'm not looking for that. I want them to cool down. I want China to cool down.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice-over): Meantime, shortly after returning home, President Trump announced that early Saturday morning, U.S. and Nigerian forces carried out a mission to kill members of ISIS, including ISIS commander Abu-Bilal al- Minuki.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. military's Africa Command calling him the most active terrorist in the world, saying he had a history of helping plan attacks and abductions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: The administration says that this strike took months of planning -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Alex Hogan at the White House -- Alex, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Speaker Mike Johnson.\par \par \uc2 Speaker, always good to have you back on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: Shannon, great to be with you. What a great day this is going to be out on the National Mall.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, and we have some sound issues, so we're going to use our fancy mics to make sure that we can hear each other.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Old school.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It is very old school.\par \par \uc2 Okay, so the overall assessment from the China trip seems to be, like, no harm done. The president talks about agriculture and other agreements that we have with China, but no joint statement. China doesn't always live up to the promises that they make.\par \par \uc2 What more would you like to see as fallout from this particular trip?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, the president was, I think, pleased with the outcome of the visit. He thinks it was a big success, and the people that were on that trip agree.\par \par \uc2 We have laid the groundwork, I think, for a new trade agreement, which I know Scott Bessent, our Treasury secretary, and the president have been working on in earnest. We're very hopeful about that.\par \par \uc2 I will say it makes a big difference who serves in the White House, right? You have a commander-in-chief who is not afraid of China as a threat. He's facing this head-on.\par \par \uc2 And he's made it very clear to everybody around the world, America cannot be taken advantage of any longer. It's an America First agenda, meaning American workers and American companies and manufacturers first.\par \par \uc2 And I think -- I think Xi respects that. And so we'll see what comes out of it. We're very hopeful.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What do you make of those critics who say, though, listen, the U.S. is in a tougher economic position, that China has an upper hand, that they feel strong, they felt strong coming into this conversation? And because of some of the tough economic issues and other things that we're having here, that the president wasn't at the highest level of leverage he could have been?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, I don't know. I think that was a central part of the conversation because much of this economic trouble that we have right now, of course, is related directly to the Strait of Hormuz.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: And the president and Xi had a good discussion about that situation, and we're hopeful that they'll help. They have a direct interest in commerce being open in the Strait of Hormuz, as much as we do, in fact, every country around the world. So, we've got to get that sorted out.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and the president said that President Xi actually offered to help, and he said, no, thank you. He told our Bret Baier, he said, if somebody helps you, then they always want something on the other side. So he doesn't want to take the help.\par \par \uc2 But there's also this issue of reports that China is backdoor-helping Iran. What more should we be doing to address that?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, that would be a concern if it's true, and I think we're trying to sort all that out.\par \par \uc2 Iran is the largest state sponsor of terrorism, okay? They have wreaked havoc around the world. They've killed hundreds of Americans over the last few decades.\par \par \uc2 We've got to make sure that we get that sorted out, and every freedom- loving person around the world and all of our NATO allies, and, of course, the Middle Eastern neighbors over there in that region have a direct interest in that. So we should all work together to get it done.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So let's talk about this issue of Taiwan. Obviously, China said it was front and center for them. That was what they wanted to talk about. They see it as kind of the biggest friction point between the U.S.\par \par \uc2 The president said everybody needs to cool it, nobody needs to start anything. We're hearing from Taiwan's president this morning saying a number of things, saying continued arms sales from the U.S. are necessary for them to maintain their position.\par \par \uc2 "Wall Street Journal" Editorial Board after the trip said this, "The test for Mr. Trump now will be whether he releases a long-awaited U.S. arms sale to Taiwan. Mr. Xi wants a veto over those sales, but Taiwan needs U.S. weapons as a deterrent as it works to reach its goal of spending five percent of its economy on defense by 2030."\par \par \uc2 So, couple that with the comments we heard from Taiwan's president this morning.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, look, we've been wary of China's objectives there for quite some time, of course. The U.S. military and every president has been watching this closely, and we continue to do so.\par \par \uc2 I saw the statement from Taiwan. I thought that was a reasonable thing for the leader there to say. They've got to flex their muscle a little bit and declare again that they are an independent country, and they must remain so. We have an interest in that, as, again, do all freedom-loving people around the world.\par \par \uc2 China cannot just go take over land, and we're going to stand strong and resolute by that. I know the Congress will.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay, well, speaking of Congress, I want to read something from one of your Democrat colleagues because they say Congress needs to be doing more with respect to this conflict.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Jimmy Panetta, he's a Democrat out of California, says, "Congress must reclaim its constitutional authority over the Iran war. Speaker Mike Johnson has abdicated Congress's role and failed to exercise any oversight of this administration," saying that's given President Trump free rein to conduct a military campaign in Iran. He says it's based on an incoherent and inconsistent strategy.\par \par \uc2 What should Congress's role be right now?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Jimmy's a friend and colleague, but he's clearly wrong on this, okay?\par \par \uc2 This is not the time for a War Powers Act. Operation Epic Fury has been declared concluded, and it is, and now we're into a new project, and that is to get the Strait of Hormuz open. This is about diplomacy and negotiation.\par \par \uc2 You don't have kinetic warfare right now. You don't have missiles flying around. And so we've got to allow the administration time to finish this and sort it out.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: How much time?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, we'll see. I mean, this is a day-by-day development. The president has his hands on this every single day.\par \par \uc2 The Pentagon, the Joint Chiefs of Staff, everybody is involved. They're pushing it as aggressively as they can.\par \par \uc2 The challenge has been in Iran, as I explain to everybody all the time, is we took out about four layers of leadership there. So they've needed some time to sort out who has the authority to get this straight.\par \par \uc2 But I'm very optimistic about it. I've talked to allied nations around the world. They want to assist in this as well, getting commerce open so gas prices come back down to planet Earth. We get back to the economic issues and the conditions that we've set in place, the Republican-led Congress, to allow all boats to rise, so to speak, in the economy. We're excited about that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What do you say to your Democratic counterparts who say by taking out those leaders -- levels of leadership, that we've ended up with a more radical group now who really doesn't seem to be interested in actually negotiating an end to this?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, I think it's premature to evaluate what we have now. That's the problem. We're not sure exactly who -- who is in charge. There is a new ayatollah, but he's not been seen since the conflict began. So we'll see how it's sorted out.\par \par \uc2 I know that there are negotiations going on in earnest. I know that the president is on this every single day. We're pushing it as aggressively as we can because we know that gas prices, we're acutely aware of the -- of what that means to American families, and we've got to get that settled out.\par \par \uc2 And I can tell you that the entire administration and all of us in Congress, Republicans, are working on that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay, I want to get to redistricting, because this case that came from the Supreme Court was out of Louisiana, of course. So it's now opened the door for a number of states, many of them in the South, to talk about redistricting.\par \par \uc2 Here is the reaction to all of that from some of your Democrat colleagues.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE COHEN (D-TN): This is a great turnaround and a turn back to Jim Crow era politics in the '50s.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): We're looking at losing possibly 19 members of the Congressional Black Caucus because of this, frankly, racist redistricting efforts targeted towards disenfranchising Black voters across the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So what is your message to Black Americans, Black voters, about this redistricting?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: The Supreme Court issued a long-awaited opinion, and I think it was long overdue, and they stated the obvious, that drawing congressional lines must be fair. You cannot draw lines on the basis of race, and that's what was done in Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 So they declared it to be an unconstitutional gerrymander, and it was. This is -- brings back fairness and certainty to the system, and I think a lot of states are looking to make sure that their maps are not unconstitutional like Louisiana's was.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay, we have another big legal issue of Louisiana we'll talk about with you right after the break.\par \par \uc2 And also this, when we return with the speaker, issues of faith, and also a preview of today's events on the Mall, and his reaction to Louisiana's Senate primary results, big ones, from last night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: President Trump proving overnight what the polling shows, that his leadership of the Republican party remains unchallenged, as primary voters in Louisianna rejected incumbent Senator Bill Cassidy's run for another term. The President actively campaigning against him after Cassidy's vote to impeach, well convict on the impeachment in the wake of January 6th. Fox News Senior Correspondent Jonathan Serrie joins us from Baton Rouge. Hello Johathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, Shannon. With President Trump's endorsement, Congresswoman Julia Letlow far outpaced her competitors with 45 percent of the vote, that was still short of the majority that she would have needed to avoid a runoff. She will face Louisianna state Treasurer, Don Flemming, on June 27 but the defeat of two term Senator Bill Cassidy sends a clear message, President Trump remains a dominant force amount Louisianna Republicans.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JULIA LETLOW (R), LOUISIANNA STATE PRIMARY CANDIDATE: For the longest time I've concurred you cannot turn your back on the people who you represent. Again, this is not my seat, it's the people's seat. And unfortunately believe he forgot that when he took that, that vote that he should not have and Louisianna did not forget.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: Cassidy was one of seven Republican Senators voting to convict President Trump during his 2021 Impeachment trial following the January 6th Capital Riot. On social media President Trump reacted to Senator Cassidy's defeat righting "his disloyalty to the man who got him elected is now a part of legend, and it's nice to see that his political career is OVER!"\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BILL CASSIDY (R), LOUISIANNA SENATOR: When you participate in democracy sometimes it doesn't turn out the way you want it to. But you don't pout, you don't wine, you don't claim that election was stolen, you don't find a reason why.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: New rules led to some confusion among voters in Saturdays primary, which moved to separate Democrat and Republican ballots after decades of a single open primary ballot. Now Shannon the Democratic primary for U.S. Senate is also heading to a runoff but, Republicans are favored to win the general election in the state President Trump carried with more than 60 percent of the vote in 2024. Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Alright, Jonathan Serrie in Batton Rouge, thank you very much for the update. We are back now with Speaker Mike Johnson, that, of course, being your home state. Care to comment there on this going to a runoff? I mean, clearly, the president has the bully pulpit, and he made his wishes known, and now we go to a runoff.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: We do. I was there to vote yesterday, and I can tell you all my friends in Louisiana had a difficult choice. We had great candidates on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 This morning, I congratulated my two close friends, Julia Letlow and John Fleming. And Julia is like a sister to me and a colleague here. And John was my predecessor in Congress and the first person to endorse me for this race.\par \par \uc2 So, great choice there. We'll see how it shakes out. But it's true that President Trump has a huge influence in our state, as he does still across the country. And you see that over and over in all these elections. It's the most powerful endorsement in the history of politics, and it continues to be shown.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: One of the headlines this morning said that this win and the ousting of Senator Cassidy shows that there's no room for dissent within the GOP. What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, I don't know. You see dissent here every day. I mean, I deal with it all day long with the smallest margin in U.S. history, working through people's philosophical differences and preferences. But, you know, this is a vibrant party. It's a strong party. We're in great position to win the midterms, defy history and win the midterms, and we're excited about that, working every day to make sure that happens so we can keep this agenda going to bring America back.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, CBS News had some polling out this morning showing 76 percent of people are concerned, and 67 percent say they are stressed about their own personal financial situation. You know that generally the incumbent is punished in the midterms, and people are feeling very uncertain about how they're doing personally. How do you campaign against that?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, this relates to the last segment. We were talking about the Strait of Hormuz. Really, all points lead back to that. Gas prices are too high because of that, and then that has an effect on how goods are transported to the grocery store and all the rest.\par \par \uc2 So, as soon as we get that straightened out, we will get back to the kitchen table issues, the economic issues that we put in place to make the economy grow. The working families tax cut, the big beautiful bill, all the legislation we passed put in pro-growth policies that will lead to bigger paychecks.\par \par \uc2 We already had the largest tax refunds we've had in a long, long time because of our policies. And so we're really excited, anxious for that to be resolved so that people will feel that, and I think they will before they go vote in the midterm.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, I want to ask you about this other legal big case that came out of Louisiana, which is you guys essentially down there have been fighting the use of the abortion pill or Mifepristone because Louisiana is basically outlawed nearly all abortions, but people can get this through the mail. It went to the Supreme Court about whether it would pause an order that meant you had to at least have an in-person visit. The court's not going to do that while the case plays out.\par \par \uc2 There were objections by Justices Thomas and Alito saying essentially by allowing these pills to flow into states where abortion is not widely available, it's going around the Dobbs decision. And there are calls for the administration to do something more.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Thomas and Alito are exactly right. It's an end run around state laws, and you have a number of states who have acted through our small democratic process to address that issue. And by mailing the drugs to women across state lines, you go around the state laws. And they're exactly right. It's a serious problem. Abortions have increased in numbers since Dobbs overturned Roe. And that's a great concern to us.\par \par \uc2 The mailing of this drug is a very dangerous thing. You're sending it to young women without physician oversight or assistance, and people are being harmed in more ways than one. And so, I think it's a big issue. I'm -- you know, I'm encouraging the administration to -- to assist with that, and I think there's a lot of evaluation on how they can handle it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, I know there's pressure that the FDA could be doing more potentially to oversee how that moves through the system.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, I want to ask you, we're obviously here at this faith event. Not everyone is happy about that, so let me give you a chance to respond to Americans United for Separation of Church and State. They say, "If the president really cared about this, he would be celebrating separation of church and state as a unique American invention that has allowed religious diversity to flourish, but they call this a Christian nationalist crusade that is just a very narrow view and version of Christianity that really doesn't serve Americans." And they say this is a public event on public land.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: This is a recognition of the deeply embedded history and religious and moral tradition of the country. This is an appropriate thing for us to do on the 250th anniversary, and the people who are upset about it oppose that. They -- they want to erase the history of America and pretend as if we're not a nation that was dedicated originally to God.\par \par \uc2 If you walk into the House chamber this afternoon with me, Shannon, you will see, "In God We Trust" inscribed in the marble right above the head of where I stand as the Speaker of the House. Congress put that there as a recognition of who we are. We are one nation under God, and to come here and gather for a happy, hopeful celebration to rededicate ourselves in that way as one nation under God is a -- is a healthy and appropriate thing.\par \par \uc2 The people who are the naysayers and who have created this new term of Christian nationalism as a pejorative, a derogatory term, are trying to silence the influence and the voices of Christians, and I think that's wildly inappropriate.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We have a little sneak peek at some of what you'll say here as the featured speaker tonight. You say that facing imminent battle and almost certain death, our founders held a day of humiliation, fasting, and prayer to ask for guidance at the dawn of their fight for freedom. So, you say that there is plenty of evidence that the framers and the founders were about faith being part of this American experiment.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Of course. Look, the father of our country, George Washington, in his famous farewell address, he said, "Of all the dispositions and habits which lead to political prosperity, religion and morality are indispensable supports." And John Adams, the second president, said, "Our Constitution is made only for a moral and religious people. It is wholly inadequate for the government of any other." Doing this today is a great celebration, an important thing for the country, and we're excited to be a part of it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we will see you there on the stage a little bit later tonight. Mr. Speaker, thank you for stopping in here first. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Up next, our "FOX News Sunday" panel on Vice President Vance's warning to blue state governors about fraud as we continue our special coverage here on the National Mall at Rededicate 250.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (R), U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: This needs to be fraudster against non-fraudster. This needs to be good government versus bad government.\par \par \uc2 So, I will extend the hand to anybody, whether they've got a D next to their name or an R next to their name, if you want to help the Trump administration fight fraud, you are going to find a willing partner.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Welcome back to this special edition of "FOX News Sunday" from Rededicate 250 here on the National Mall.\par \par \uc2 So, you just heard from the Vice President calling for a bipartisan fight against fraud. Let's talk about it with our Sunday group.\par \par \uc2 Director of the Ronald Reagan Institute, Roger Zakheim. Co-Host of "Fox & Friends" Weekend, Rachel Campos-Duffy, who is also the author of the brand- new book, it's Beautiful, "All-American Patriotism." And former pastor and congressman, now a diplomat with the State Department, Mark Walker.\par \par \uc2 Welcome. It's good to have all of you here.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL CAMPOS-DUFFY, FOX AND FRIENDS WEEKEND CO-HOST: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARK WALKER (R) FORMER NORTH CAROLINA CONGRESSMAN: Great to be here.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so you heard what the vice president said. He was in Maine and the governor there said this. Janet Mills, she says, "As District Attorney, Attorney General, and as Governor, I have always cracked down on fraud, often by working hand-in-hand with federal government. When the Federal government requested more information about how we address fraud we provided a comprehensive and thorough response."\par \par \uc2 So, Mark, you've worked here on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Is there enough bipartisan will to do something about this?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: I hope so. Well, I think the American people want every representative to come here and work on fraud. Three weeks ago, the Farm Bill was passed, $390 billion, $315 billion of that goes to social net programs.\par \par \uc2 We want to make sure, you know, Democrats have gotten where they're measuring their success on how many people they can put on the government programs as opposed to how many people should be transferred off to a better life.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and Republicans say they want these programs to be there for people who need them.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: America is charitable. We care about people. But the fact is, if there are fraudsters on there, it makes it tougher for the people who actually need these programs to benefit from that.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, we're going to put up a scroll. This is something from DOJ, places they say they've cracked down in just the last week. I mean, it's health care fraud. It's tax fraud. It's all kinds of things. But, Rachel, the goal is to make sure people who need it get it.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Of course. Every time somebody, there's some big Medicaid fraud and zillions of dollars get siphoned off somewhere else, that means somebody who really needs it isn't going to get that money that they need to survive. Who can deny what happened in Minneapolis?\par \par \uc2 I mean, we had a YouTuber who went down there and investigated, and instead of the local and state government saying, "Oh my gosh, this is happening." You know, the house is on fire. Let's fix this. Let's find all the people who've done bad stuff. They sort of closed ranks, and they said, "We don't want to hear this, and this is racist somehow.\par \par \uc2 And in fact, we learned that the people who were in the government and were seeing this were afraid to come out and say anything about this fraud because they were afraid of being called racist.\par \par \uc2 So, absolutely, every time you see this kind of fraud, you're taking away from a child who actually needs it.\par \par \uc2 I think it's very dangerous. You've seen the reports now from the left. They're -- they're sort of pushing back on this and saying, you're trying to take away social safety programs for the poor.\par \par \uc2 No. Taking -- taking the fraud out, some of it going to foreign countries and even terrorist groups, that's -- that's keeping it for the people who need it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and interestingly enough, we've seen after what was uncovered there in Minnesota --\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- that Governor Walz has now said he is partnering with the Feds and they're trying to work on some things to do together. There have been some skeptics, but he says. We're doing these raids. We are trying to help, make sure that the fraud is rooted out.\par \par \uc2 Now, truthout.org says, "It's campaign season. Republicans are eager to wave the flag of waste, fraud, and abuse. It's a way to distract the public from rising gas prices, tariffs, and an unpopular war that is definitely not over."\par \par \uc2 Gets to the point Rachel was making. They say, "For the GOP, going after programs that help everyday people meet basic needs is good politics."\par \par \uc2 Roger, they say this is just about campaign.\par \par \uc2 ROGER ZAKHEIM, THE RONALD REAGAN INSTITUTE DIRECTOR: Politics is at play everywhere. But the reality is two-thirds of our spending goes to entitlement programs, Medicare, Medicaid, and Social Security. It's an automatic vote.\par \par \uc2 In other words, the members of Congress, when the Congress and by left here, never get a chance to vote on these increases. They're an autopilot. And so what has to happen is that we need to have accountability. And here's an opportunity for the vice president. Not great being a vice president. No one's got the best job. The portfolio of vice president ain't like the secretary of state.\par \par \uc2 Here is an opportunity to distinguish himself, to have some accountability and make sure the safety net is actually getting to people who need it, as opposed to going to people who are exploiting the system.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, it is an election year, and has a heated one for midterms.\par \par \uc2 And so former Vice President Kamala Harris, who was the presidential nominee for Democrats last time around, has got some ideas. And she says, there are no bad ideas. Here's part of what she's proposing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS, FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: And let's invite, then, approaches and ideas that are about, for example, Supreme Court reform, including the notion of expanding the -- the court.\par \par \uc2 Let's invite a discussion about how do we do we, and if so, how do we push for statehood for Puerto Rico and D.C.?\par \par \uc2 How are we thinking about the Electoral College?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And just a couple of weeks ago, a long-time Democratic strategist, James Carville, had this to say about some of these ideas. He says, "Don't run on it. Don't talk about it. Just do it." Rachel.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Yes. I mean, listen, when the left talks about these kinds of structural changes, you better take them seriously.\par \par \uc2 By the way, with Puerto Rico, be careful. The governor is a Republican. So, this could like be like the Virginia redistricting situation, where be careful what you wish for. It might not work out in your favor. But -- but absolutely, we need to take that seriously.\par \par \uc2 And I think the answer, and -- and what we're seeing from what happened with -- with Virginia and -- and -- and other states with redistricting is the local politics really matters.\par \par \uc2 Republicans need to refocus on -- on winning prosecuting -- prosecutor races, school boards, city council. That's where all the -- the real power is. Often, it's not really in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 But take them very seriously, get the SAVE act of pass. Do what you need to do if you're a Republican, because they are not joking when they talk about these structural changes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Roger?\par \par \uc2 ZAKHEIM: Well, then the Democrats should focus on the power to persuade, and they're not persuading.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That's right. That's true.\par \par \uc2 ZAKHEIM: They're trying to make these institutional reforms. It didn't work for FDR, right? Court packing has been established as something that has failed. And here, if you can't win over the people, they try to change institutions. I think it's a failed strategy. And I'm looking for centrist Democrats, wherever they are, to come after this because the progressive agenda wants to change things radically.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Yes, quick.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Well, yes. The -- yes. Kamala Harris needs to reimagine her presidential campaign. And for Carville and crew, it's gone from the, it's the economy stupid to change the Constitution. We have a population base, it's called the U.S. House. That's why California has 53 reps. And Wyoming and Alaska and others have one.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. I want to make sure we get to faith, because that is what today is all about here on the National Mall.\par \par \uc2 Overnight, Friday into Saturday, we got news from the White House on an operation in Nigeria, where we have all been reporting on the oppression of Christians down there.\par \par \uc2 Pete Hegseth tweeting this. And I say that because it didn't come from the SECDEF account. So, just -- just to be clear, it comes from the Pete Hegseth account. He says this. "Since U.S. forces, in coordination with the Armed Forces of Nigeria killed Abu-Bilal al-Minuki and other ISIS leaders."\par \par \uc2 He says, "For months, we hunted this top ISIS leader in Nigeria, who was killing Christians, and we killed him and his entire posse."\par \par \uc2 Mark, I know this is the space you're working in now.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: The last year, we've been studying this, Shannon. Seventy-five percent of all Christians who are martyred in our world today, come from the continent of Africa.\par \par \uc2 Ninety percent of that number comes from the country of Nigeria. There are 100 million Christians in Nigeria that celebrate this decision.\par \par \uc2 And to think that Joe Biden took Nigeria off the CPC list, the Country of Particular Concern. Fortunately, President Trump put them back on there as move forward.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and President Trump said on Truth Social yesterday, "No games. Watch what's next on your favorite subject, President DJT."\par \par \uc2 He's warning the folks down there, he's not messing around.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: No, he shouldn't. I mean, if you've seen the -- the images, the videos of what's going on.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the other thing about this story, Mark, you'd appreciate it, it's like the faith of these people.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Oh.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: They know that just going to church could get them and their children killed, and they still go to -- they still go to church. They still go to mass. It's an incredible witness. But absolutely, we should not tolerate it.\par \par \uc2 But Christian persecution is around the world. We're seeing it in Lebanon. We're seeing it in China. We're seeing it across -- across the world, and even in our own country.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and, Roger, this administration has said they really want to spend a lot of effort and time and political capital on fighting anti- Semitism, too. We see this through the Department of Education. They've got a task force. They say they want to fight for religious freedom for everyone.\par \par \uc2 ZAKHEIM: I'm glad they're doing it because it's -- because it's needed. If you look at the statistics, they're mind-boggling. Jews make up about two percent of our population, and they're victims. Seventy percent of religious hate crimes are focused on Jews. Seventy percent.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you feel like this -- do you feel like this administration is making a difference with the efforts that got underway now?\par \par \uc2 ZAKHEIM: First and foremost, yes. And first and foremost, they have to get after it. And this proclamation the president made to make a national Sabbath, focusing what President George Washington said over 300 years ago, or 250-plus years ago, that our nation is a nation that has to protect all religious freedom. And he'd said that to those in the congregation in Newport, Rhode Island.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Panel, thank you very much. It's great to see you --\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- guys in our nice close quarters here. We'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Next up, a pioneer in contemporary Christian music who's weathered some serious controversies along the way.\par \par \uc2 I sit down with Amy Grant, rewinding the past 50 years.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AMY GRANT, AMERICAN SINGER-SONGWRITER AND MUSICIAN: Well, I -- I've -- I graduated from high school. I went to four years of college. And so I was always juggling a full-time school schedule with work.\par \par \uc2 And so what I really thought was, I have zero social life.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: There'd be no time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: She's often called the queen of Christian pop, a six-time Grammy winner with more than 20 Dove Awards, platinum records, and concerts around the globe. She may have even more eras than Taylor Swift.\par \par \uc2 From teenage breakouts to mainstream crossover to a new album in her 60s, we sat down with Amy Grant to talk about her decades in the spotlight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A teenage girl with a guitar stacking up number ones in the late '70's and becoming one of the biggest names in contemporary Christian music.\par \par \uc2 You are a teenager who was very successful and thrust into a very important limelight at a very young age. What was that experience like for you?\par \par \uc2 GRANT: My first record came out when I was 17. It was 1978. And there just wasn't a lot of fanfare, you know. If you -- if you did music you worked hard. There was a lot of travel. We'd sing, travel to the next place. And I juggled that along with college for four years and then did it full-time, but it was before social media.\par \par \uc2 And I think, you know, just a -- a grassroots following developed. And I look back now and go, well, that was like.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 GRANT: But you don't really know what's happening when you're doing it. You're just waking up every day and doing what you love and your job.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And it must have been such a different life than your peers who were teenagers. But was it just that that's the only life that you knew at that stage?\par \par \uc2 GRANT: Well, I -- I've -- I graduated from high school. I went to four years of college. And so I was always juggling a full-time school schedule with work.\par \par \uc2 And so what I really thought was, I have zero social life.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: There'd be no time.\par \par \uc2 GRANT: No. And sometimes, I would like fly into some place and they send the youth pastor to pick me up, and I was like, oh, gosh what I don't need to do is a one-time date.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right. A setup on the road.\par \par \uc2 GRANT: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You were really a pioneer of CCM. Did you realize it at the time? Or have you come to fully embrace that you were one of the people who put it on the map?\par \par \uc2 GRANT: I didn't realize that. It's funny because I grew up in Nashville, which is such a great, amazing music -- music community.\par \par \uc2 But there was a church down on Music Row that had a bookstore coffee shop connected to it. And all through my high school years, every Saturday night, they had live music there.\par \par \uc2 And so artists from California would come through. You didn't have to buy a ticket, you just show up. Everybody sitting across like it on the floor. And -- and it was right in the studio section of Nashville.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, in my mind, I wasn't the pioneer of anything. I loved the music that came before me. I was always there when there was ever live music. And then I was just adding to the repertoire.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Then came the crossover, from church pews to the top of the billboard charts.\par \par \uc2 What was that like to have different fans, in addition to the ones you already had, but a different audience, a different platform to be thrust into kind of that secular hit place?\par \par \uc2 GRANT: I never thought of my audience as being part of any one genre. Only because I knew as a music lover myself. I mean, I cut my teeth on The Beatles, Dan Fogelberg, Carole King, Joni Mitchell, Jethro Tull, Three Dog Night. I mean, I held my old -- my sister's records.\par \par \uc2 And then also then other music started coming in. My parents listened to Bill Gaither, Larnelle Harris, people that I'd heard at the coffee shop.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, I just assume everybody's listening to everything. I mean, clearly you were not. But --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I was not allowed to listen to all of the fun stuff until later.\par \par \uc2 GRANT: Well, can I tell you --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: (INAUDIBLE) listen to Amy Grant.\par \par \uc2 GRANT: Well, but I'll meet people that say I was only allowed to listen --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GRANT: -- to you growing up and I'll -- and all I've said was, I'm so sorry. So much great music, because life is filled with everything.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GRANT: And how great to have songs about -- about all of it. That's really music is just our way to communicate shared experience.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Some of that crossover happened with her friend and frequent collaborator, Michael W. Smith.\par \par \uc2 GRANT: He wrote a lot with the man that I was dating at the time and married Gary Chapman. We were married for 17 years.\par \par \uc2 Michael married a -- a schoolmate of mine. Debbie and I went to a fantastic girl school, Harpeth Hall.\par \par \uc2 And so we had a lot of overlap.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 And then we just kept writing together and then touring together, and we get along so easily. And he just as always felt like a brother to me.\par \par \uc2 I do remember touring an album in the late '80s called "Lead Me On," and we had -- and we toured that together. It was a very serious record. I'd been through a lot of personal challenges. Thank God there was no internet. But it -- sort of rebounding from that, I was starting to have a family, and I just wanted to have fun. And so, I have stepped into, you know, writing "Baby, Baby," "Every Heartbeat," a lot of those things because my life was just like lighten up.\par \par \uc2 GRANT (singing): And ever since the day you put my heart in motion, baby I realize that there's just no getting over you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): 1990s "Heart In Motion" went platinum five times, becoming her best-selling album and introducing her to an even larger audience.\par \par \uc2 GRANT (singing): Every heartbeat bears your name. Loud and clear they stake my claim.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): But with that success came scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: When news of your personal life would filter out and going through a divorce, I mean how difficult was that for you?\par \par \uc2 GRANT: What I didn't realize is, when you end a marriage it's just like, it was like a car wreck that through everybody from the car. And the last thing I cared about what anybody was discussing at their dining room table. I was -- I was trying to connect with my children, each of which were having their own hellish experience. My extended family. You know, all the years of investment with Gary's family.\par \par \uc2 And I remember sitting on his parents' back porch and -- and by then I was married to Vince. I had a daughter with Vince. And Mary and I are sitting there rocking in the chair. And she leaned over and patted my leg and she said, I'm not saying divorce is the answer, but sometimes it sure helps. And I -- what a gracious thing to hear from my former mother-in-law. And maybe all of our lives -- I know all of our lives would have been different had they not been so observed, just the unique pressures that come because of your life.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, much more with Amy Grant. She's not been immune from struggle. The life-and-death challenges she's faced in recent years brought new music. The inspiration behind her new sound, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: "FOX NEWS SUNDAY" coming to today from the National Mall.\par \par \uc2 We'll take you back now to our interview with multiplatinum singer Amy Grant.\par \par \uc2 In recent years she's faced a series of serious health challenges, from open heart surgery, to an accident that left her with a brain injury.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRANT: So, really turning 60 was, whoo.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Welcome.\par \par \uc2 GRANT: Yes. I know. Yes. Hey, nobody gets out alive.\par \par \uc2 But, yes, I found out through a -- by accident. I went with Vince to a doctor's appointment. His father passed away at 65. And he was getting winded going up the stairs. And he was like, what -- am I OK, you know? And so, I went with him to get the results of his treadmill test. And the doctor said, buddy, you're fat and out of shape. And Vince said, tell me something I don't know.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But is my heart OK?\par \par \uc2 GRANT: He said -- oh, he said, your arteries are clear. Your heart's great. And, yes. And then the doctor looked at me and he said, I want to see you. And I went -- and he put me through a battery of tests and called me first thing the next morning and he said, you need -- you need surgery. You have an undiagnosed birth defect that is a ticking time bomb.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Did he have some intuition that something was wrong, or had you discussed with him that he thought there was this need for this full battery of tests?\par \par \uc2 GRANT: This is so crazy. Dr. John Bright Cage (ph) is now retired. And when he retired, he wrote me a letter. And he said, two times in my life I have felt compelled to reach into someone's private life and say, come see me. And I was one of those. And I just would have dropped dead. And -- I know. And so -- I'm so grateful. And so, you know, Covid happened. Open heart surgery. Then two years later I had a bike accident with a head injury. So, that wasn't months of quiet. And then two surgeries recovering from that. And one was vocal surgery.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Did you feel a need to pour that into a song as a writer?\par \par \uc2 GRANT: Well, "The Me That Remains" was the first song that I wrote for this project, which I didn't know was going to be a project.\par \par \uc2 GRANT (singing): I recognize a light in my eyes that never did fade. And I'm going to find, yeah, find and revive the me that remains.\par \par \uc2 GRANT: And I started writing just as therapy because writing has always been therapy for me. So, I was really writing "The Me That Remains" to myself to go, well, girl, you going to lean in with something. Who'd you wake up looking at in the mirror today. Don't be so hard on her. That is what you've got.\par \par \uc2 And then I -- you know, one thing led to another. And then once I started writing, like, I felt like every week I was writing something. And reaching out to other writers, because my short-term memory was shot. You know, it's hard to write music when you can't remember what you thought of 30 seconds ago. But it was like all these -- the limitations were just creating a new path forward. And so, it was really a -- the whole thing was a therapeutic experience.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, let me ask you about this song as well, "How Do We Get There From Here." There's a lot of weight to that. How did that song come together?\par \par \uc2 GRANT: In March of 2023, there was a shooting at the Covenant School in Nashville. And in the months after that, you know, the whole community was reeling. And several of us who are artists, songwriters, entertainers, we decided -- we were asked to go to Capitol Hill to talk to Governor Lee, to talk with legislators from both sides of the aisle and just say, what can we do?\par \par \uc2 And I just kept -- I'm watching everybody, like, protesting, shouting, getting more puffed up about their opinion. And the songwriter in me just said, it's not going to work until we're working together. And that -- I was just sitting and looking in the fire, you know, later that fall, late fall, and I wrote the first verse and chorus and some version of that music. And one of the artists that day on Capitol Hill have been Ruby Amanfu. I didn't know her well, but I knew her little bit. And I just said, hey, any chance you'd help me finish this song? And for like a year and a half we would just, like, it was like firing one over the bow, how about this idea? I have -- I've hooked some music. What about this? And there was no timetable. I mean I wasn't writing for a record. This was 2023. And then she -- and in the spring of 2025 she reached out and said, I think I've finished this -- I think I've hooked it. And I went, I'm going in the studio tomorrow, are you available? And she said, I'm in town. And I said, come sing it with me.\par \par \uc2 GRANT AND RUBY AMANFU (singing): When the ink dries on our story what will history reveal? Will we have been part of the problem or a part of how we heal?\par \par \uc2 GRANT: And so, you know, just the timing. And when we did the video, I was actually on a trip with childhood friends and one was the grandmother of two kids that were at Covenant. And they have so struggled. The whole family has struggled. And they're the living. But her experience has made me see just, this is a lifelong shadow that this event cast. And I -- and I just said, I want you to hear this. I don't want you to feel like I'm capitalizing on that experience. And please hear -- you know.\par \par \uc2 So, I just -- I played her the song and the video that we had just done and it was released the day of the shooting. And so, you know, what you hope is that people feel hopeful. We have to talk to each other. We have to find a common goal. I mean about anything. Just a common goal.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What would you say is the role of faith in your life right now, and how is it expressed in this project?\par \par \uc2 GRANT: Oh, my goodness. I mean I -- I see all of life through a lens of faith. We are all part of creation made by God. And, you know, I mean, my prayer for myself and for everybody is that we fulfill a purpose for which we're made.\par \par \uc2 And so, if I do nothing else, I, every morning, speaking the Lord's Prayer and the 23rd Psalm, slowly, you know, just like -- like you're inside of a solar system and let the word's kind of spin around you and go, where do I fit in all this? And, yes. And then, it's interesting. I feel like the older I get, I use less faith language but that has nothing to do with how everything is oriented in my mind. For God so loved the world he sent his son. But I just think, how we live our lives speaks the loudest. And what everybody -- what people want is to be seen and -- and to be loved and be - - to be accepted where they are. And I just feel like -- I feel like actions speak louder than words.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Grant's new album "The Me That Remains" is out now.\par \par \uc2 Now to an update on a story we've been covering for months, the arrest and jailing of Pastor Ezra Jin in China. Last week we talked to his daughter again and son-in-law about their hope that President Trump would bring up the pastor's case to President Xi. On Friday, the president gave the world an update on his conversations with President Xi about releasing a number of prisoners.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I did feel optimistic about the second one that's now under consideration. The pastor. Very optimistic, actually.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: And I put him down. I made a -- I made a point out of it.\par \par \uc2 GRACE JIN DREXEL, DAUGHTER OF ZION CHURCH FOUNDER EZRA JIN: Dear President Trump, our family is beyond grateful for your leadership and pressing for the case of my father, Pastor Ezra Jin. Your historic visit in Beijing this week.\par \par \uc2 BILL DREXEL, SON-IN-LAW OF ZION CHURCH FOUNDER EZRA JIN: We have no words. It feels like a miracle.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, of course, we will keep you updated on whether China does indeed release Pastor Ezra Jin and the other religious leaders who were arrested in that same sweep last October.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us today here from Rededicate 250 in Washington. Keep it to Fox News Channel for continued coverage of this event.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a blessed week. We'll see you next "FOX NEWS SUNDAY."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2026 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020260517em5h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Issues New Threats Amid Strait Of Hormuz Mission; U.S. Destroys Six Iranian Boats; Judge Says Gala Shooting Suspect Not Treated Well; Brooklyn Leader Vows Continued Protests Against ICE; Sanders Campaigns For Progressive Candidates; Poll Finds Democratic Party "Too Liberal"; Remembering Dr. \uc2 Charles Krauthammer\uc2 ; The Late \uc2 Charles Krauthammer\uc2 's Views On \uc2 NASA\uc2  And Iran Discussed; Current Trends In Skin Rejuvenation Examined; Iran Reportedly Firing At Ships In Strait Of Hormuz; \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  Sends Warship Through Strait Of Hormuz In Attempt To Defy Iranian Blockade.\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Rich Edson, CB Cotton, Jennifer Griffin, Mike Emanuel,  Chad Pergram, Alexandria Hoff, Harris Faulkner, Jason Riley \par \uc2 8,014 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2026\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2026 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2026 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: That's it. That's it for us, have a great night.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: OK. I think that was a lightning round, that sounds good.\par \par \uc2 All right. Thank you, Emily.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I'm Bret Baier. We are coming to you live tonight from Fox News World Headquarters in New York. The suspect in the most recent Trump assassination effort gets an apology from the judge. We will tell you why.\par \par \uc2 And we meet the winners of this year's Dr \uc2 Charles Krauthammer\uc2  Memorial scholarships, we'll bring you that.\par \par \uc2 But first breaking tonight, President Trump says Iran will be, in his words, blown off the face of the earth if it attacks U.S. Naval vessels escorting ships through the Strait of Hormuz. President making the comment to Fox News' Trey Yingst today, the Pentagon saying its vessels have successfully escorted two American flag merchant ships through the Strait as part of -- part of President Trump's new initiative.\par \par \uc2 But Iran did fire at vessels, and that is leading military officials to say the U.S. is closer to the resumption of major combat operations right now than it was just 24 hours ago.\par \par \uc2 Senior National Correspondent Rich Edson starts us off tonight live from the North Lawn of the White House. Good evening, Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. President Trump promised this afternoon that rising oil prices will fall quickly after the conflict with Iran ends, though that timeline is unclear, after another day of competing claims between the United States and Iran over the situation in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We can't let Iran have a nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice-over): More threats and promises of Iranian capitulation as one of the world's most vital waterways languishes in an uneasy cease fire.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : It's working out very nicely. They have no Navy. They have no nothing.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice-over): Yet the United Arab Emirates says it intercepted an Iranian strike, including 15 missiles and four drones today. After President Trump announced this weekend that the U.S. would escort ships through the Strait of Hormuz, the U.S. military says it successfully guided two American flag merchant ships. The United States also denies claims Iran hit an American ship.\par \par \uc2 The president says the U.S. military sank seven small Iranian boats and again, prodded another ally to join the fight. Posting, "Iran has taken some shots at unrelated nations with respect to the ship movement Project Freedom, including a South Korean cargo ship. Perhaps it's time for South Korea to come and join the mission."\par \par \uc2 Both the United States and Iran claim to control portions of the bottleneck Strait.\par \par \uc2 ESMAIL BAGHAEI, IRANIAN FOREIGN MINISTRY (through translator): Ships, their owners and shipping companies know well that to ensure their security and safety, they need to coordinate with relevant bodies in the Islamic Republic of Iran.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice-over): Despite the revolutionary government in Tehran hanging on, the White House claims President Trump holds all the cards. The president tells Fox News's Trey Yingst the U.S. blockade is one of the greatest military maneuvers ever, and that if Iran attacks U.S. vessels, "They'll be blown off the face of the earth."\par \par \uc2 SCOTT BESSENT, U.S. TREASURY SECRETARY: I don't think they know what's going on, but what is going on is that they're reduced their Navy is not a Navy anymore. It's a band of pirates.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice-over): Democrats say the president's goals of disarming Iran's nuclear and ballistic missile programs and reopening the Strait of Hormuz seem distant.\par \par \uc2 REP. SUHAS SUBRAMANYAM (D-VA): Let's remember that this was not an issue before this war, and more reason why this war has been a disaster so far.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): Just ahead of President Trump's visit to Beijing, Secretary Bessent says that China should step up and press Iran to reopen the Strait of Hormuz, saying that Iran is selling 90 percent of its energy to Beijing, and that therefore China is funding the largest state sponsor of terrorism, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Rich Edson live in the North Lawn. Rich, thanks.\par \par \uc2 The head of U.S. Central Command calls the US effort to escort ships through the Strait of Hormuz essential to regional security and the global economy. Chief National Security Correspondent Jennifer Griffin has details tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ADM. BRAD COOPER, CENTCOM COMMANDER: The small boats were all going against commercial ships, and all were sunk by Apaches and Seahawk helicopters.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): CENTCOM commander Admiral Brad Cooper said the U.S. military defended two U.S. commercial vessels as they crossed through the Strait of Hormuz from incoming Iranian cruise missiles and drones Apache gunship helicopters blew up six Iranian fast boats threatening those ships.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: I've transited through the Strait of hormones probably 100 times in my career. You typically see between 20 and 40 small boats in a pack coming out today we saw six and eliminated them quickly. I think this is an example of the degradation of their capability. That doesn't mean it's all gone, but it's highly degraded.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice-over): The conflict entered a new phase today with the U.S. military beginning a new operation to break Iran's attempted blockade of the Strait, opening a safe passage Lane near Oman, while still enforcing a blockade of any tankers and ships leaving Iran's ports.\par \par \uc2 The clock is ticking on Iranian oil storage as crude piles up on tankers and at export hubs like Iran's Kharg Island, analysts estimate Iran could hit capacity in about a month.\par \par \uc2 MIAD MALEKI, FOUNDATION FOR DEFENSE OF DEMOCRACIES: So, Iran has about 13 days worth of storage. They have around somewhere between 15 to 18 tankers available in the Persian Gulf. You do the math, you know, they have a few days on onshore storage capacity left, and then another 30 to 45 days of tankers to fill up.\par \par \uc2 They have already started dropping the oil extraction significantly.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): U.S. Officials tell me we are closer to the resumption of major combat operations than we were 24 hours ago after Iran fired at U.S. vessels today no orders have been given to end the cease fire, but according to this official, we preserve the right to eliminate any threat to commercial shipping. We don't have to wait