half of Our Nation", in stores May 5th.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 Each year, we honor the legacy of Dr. Charles Krauthammer through a scholarship awarded in his name. Charles was a fixture on our show for many years, and we often miss his voice and perspective. In fact, every night.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we meet this year's scholarship winners and look back at some of our favorite moments with Charles.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHARLES KRAUTHAMMER, FORMER FOX NEWS CHANNEL POLITICAL COMMENTATOR: It's my job to call a folly a folly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): Charles Krauthammer was one of Fox News Channel's most consequential voices, a regular on the All-Star panel.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, a syndicated columnist Charles Krauthammer.\par \par \uc2 Who wrote a book you may have heard of things that matter.\par \par \uc2 KRAUTHAMMER: Three decades of passions, pastime, and politics.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. There we go.\par \par \uc2 Known for cutting through the noise.\par \par \uc2 KRAUTHAMMER: Allowing me to enlighten the viewers on what's really happening, which is that none of this is going to matter.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As well as his humor.\par \par \uc2 KRAUTHAMMER: I'm humbled and I'm speechless, which, as you know, is a rare experience, you know, for me.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): And colorful candidate casino predictions.\par \par \uc2 KRAUTHAMMER: 30 on Bush, 15 on Scott Walker, and I blow the rest on booze.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: On a significant bet on Jeb Bush.\par \par \uc2 KRAUTHAMMER: Right. But it's the booze that I'm looking forward to.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): Charles' passion for learning led him to study psychiatry at Harvard Medical School, finishing at the top of his class, even after a diving accident left him paralyzed.\par \par \uc2 KRAUTHAMMER: There is an element of that everybody's story. They are low point. Do you want it enough? And are you lucky enough? That's a part of it, too.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): Charles died in 2018, but his commentary remains poignant today.\par \par \uc2 KRAUTHAMMER: Everyone in the region sees it. Everybody understands who the new hegemon in the region will be. It's going to be Iran.\par \par \uc2 Putin is on the march. He could continue to march.\par \par \uc2 The overwhelming threat is China's rising.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): Fox News honors Charles each year, awarding scholarships in his name to the children of Fox News employees in partnership with the National Merit Scholarship.\par \par \uc2 TESS SONNE, RECIPIENT, KRAUTHAMMER SCHOLARSHIP: He was just very honest and blunt, and I think a lot of the people around him respected him because he was just honest, but in a respectful way.\par \par \uc2 It's just serves as a lesson to like, lead a life with integrity.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): Tess Sonne has an accomplished and diverse background. She is valedictorian of her class, a leader in Model U.N., plays varsity soccer, and is active with the Volunteer Ambulance Corps.\par \par \uc2 SONNE: I just didn't want to be siloed into like one field or interests. And I'm drawn to like different types of people doing different types of things. I love just trying new things. So, it keeps things exciting.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): Tess plans to attend the University of Virginia this fall, and like Charles, she hopes to earn a degree in medicine, having already achieved her EMT certification.\par \par \uc2 SONNE: I feel like just helping other people, it's just like leads to a fulfilling life.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): After leaving medicine, Charles came to Washington, where he worked with several presidential administrations and won the Pulitzer Prize for commentary.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA PARKER, RECIPIENT, KRAUTHAMMER SCHOLARSHIP: It's nice to be a 