n he talked about space and NASA. Here he is on the end of the space shuttle program.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLES KRAUTHAMMER, SYNDICATED COLUMNIST: The manned space program is dead. The administration has allowed it. The problem isn't the end of the shuttle. It was never a good idea. It was going to be ended, whoever was in the administration. The problem is that Obama has the canceled the follow- on. There was no way America is going to get into space, men into space. Right now we are going to have to hitch a ride, to beg a ride, to rent a ride on the Russian Soyuz. It's an incredible irony. And we left them in the dust in the moon race, and now we are a supplicant.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He didn't get to see Artemis II launch and now this continued program, but I imagine he would be happy about that.\par \par \uc2 KRAUTHAMMER: Yes. You know, Bret, I always try not pretend to know what my dad would say about current day events, but this is a big exception. I know he would be 100 percent over the moon, pun intended, over Artemis II and what NASA is up to. He really loved space exploration. And it moved his soul and moved his spirit. And he wrote about it time after time over the decades.\par \par \uc2 And if you look at what the new NASA administrator, Jared Isaacman, has laid out, it's doing exactly the kind of stuff my dad always advocated for, getting NASA back to pushing the envelope, getting us to the moon, to moon bases, eventually to Mars. He would be ecstatic about this and I think encouraging all Americans to be proud.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Why do you think he was so focused? I mean it was between baseball and space that got him really fired up.\par \par \uc2 KRAUTHAMMER: A little bit of chess, too.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, chess, too.\par \par \uc2 KRAUTHAMMER: I think space, it was almost spiritual for him. Actually, when the space shuttle was still going, he took me to launch when I was a kid. And 