 he was always emphatic that we couldn't delude ourselves. Appeasement with a regime like this wouldn't work. We couldn't hope for some supposed moderate ayatollah. If we were going to stop Iran, they had to be forced to stop.\par \par \uc2 And as you read, he was emphatic that Iran couldn't be allowed to have a bomb. But what's especially relevant today, he always brought up what many people shied away from, their conventional weapons, their ballistic missile program, the proxy network. None of this was in the Iran nuclear deal, and it was one of his primary targets of criticism.\par \par \uc2 And again, whatever the specifics of exactly what we're doing today, I think his overall message is just as clear now as then, that kicking the can down the road, it left us in a position we're in now, and that if we want a regime like this to set aside its malign intentions, it's not going to be from nice words. It's going to be from maximum pressure of all kinds on all fronts until they retreat.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We miss his words. We miss his voice every day. It's so great to see you every year. If people want to see or read more of it, they go to a website?\par \par \uc2 KRAUTHAMMER: Yes, absolutely. My father's official website CharlesKrauthammer.com has always of his old columns. You can read about his writings on Iran, on space, and his love of learning in general, all the different avenues. And it just makes me think so much that he would love what this scholarship is doing, encouraging the love of learning among these kids. And we're so happy to congratulate him.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Give your mom my best.\par \par \uc2 KRAUTHAMMER: I will, absolutely, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we start a new series looking at health and wellness trends. We'll tell you maybe what works, what doesn't. We'll take a look at it all.\par \par \uc2 And later, the panel with the latest developments in the Iran conflict and what could come next.\par \par \u