dy to naturally produce collagen.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: The biostimulator is having a big moment. One issue, how the body responds to it can be unpredictable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Botox remains far and away the most popular cosmetic injectable. And the price tag for all of this cannot be ignored. Even a moderate approach to trying to keep up with routine aesthetic treatments would cost at least $5,000 a year. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Alex, look forward to the next installment. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on President Trump's new initiative to escort ships through the Strait of Hormuz and what comes next with Iran.\par \par \uc2 First, some of our FOX affiliates around this country and what they're covering tonight. FOX 25 in Oklahoma City with the investigation into Sunday's shooting that left several people injured at a park. Police say two men wearing ski masks just opened fire. No suspects are in custody yet.\par \par \uc2 FOX 5 here in New York as a United Airlines passenger jet carrying 221 passengers from Venice, Italy, strikes a light pole on the New Jersey turnpike as it comes in for landing at Newark Liberty International Airport. The airline says none of the passengers or 10 crew members aboard that flight 169 were injured and it landed safely.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Chicago from FOX 32, our affiliate there. One of the big stories there tonight, some workers at the Brookfield Zoo walk off the job as contract negotiations between the zoo and its employees' union remain unresolved. The talks ended Friday without an agreement. The union says animal care specialists are continuing to work and that such care remains its top priority. That's good.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP, (R) U.S. PRESIDENT: Our country is booming now despite the fact that we're in a, I call it a mini-war, because that's all they are.\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SMITH, (D-WA) HOUSE ARMED SERVICES COMMITTEE: You have to ignore what Trump says from a factual standpoint.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN FETTERMAN, (D-PA): I love that he is strangling the Iranian economy right now and blocking the strait.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JACK REED, (D-RI) SENATE ARMED SERVICES COMMITTEE: This has been a tactical demonstration of prowess but has not achieved the strategic goal that the president announced.\par \par \uc2 COMMANDER KIRK LIPPOLD (RET), U.S. NAVY: We are not the ones that are the aggressor right now. We are the protectors. But at some point, if they continue to escalate, the United States is going to respond.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, we may be closer to responding as Iran has activated, fired some things at different ships. The blockade continues and the Iranians are saying they are not ready to accept what the U.S. is putting down. The foreign minister taking to X, Araghchi, saying "Events in Hormuz make clear there is no military solution to a political crisis. As talks are making progress with Pakistan's gracious effort, the U.S. should be wary of being dragged back into quagmire by ill-wishers. So should the UAE. Project Freedom is Project Deadlock." That's from the Iranian foreign minister. The UAE, mentioned there, came under fire, and they held off the missiles and projectiles fired there again today.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel. Harris Faulkner is anchor of "THE FAULKNER FOCUS," co-host of "OUTNUMBERED", "THE FIVE" co-host Jessica Tarlov, and Jason Riley, FOX News contributor.\par \par \uc2 Jason, what do you make? It seems like it's a very tenuous place. Clearly Iran is feeling a pinch economically, but at least rhetorically, they are pushing back.\par \par \uc2 JASON RILEY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: They are pushing back. I think they are trying to call the president's bluff here. And it's an indication that this war isn't over. And I don't think it's going to be over until we resume military operations, frankly. And I hate to be pessimistic, but the pattern here is Iran is they want to wait you out. They want to negotiate. They don't want to give an inch. And I think that's what it will take.\par \par \uc2 And I do hope that the president sees this through. Regardless of whether you think we should have gone in in the first place, it's very important that we see this through because Iran will be in a stronger position if we don't see it through. If they're left with the ability to control that strait, 20 percent of the world's energy, not only a propaganda victory, but if they can do that as well, they will be in a much stronger position. So I hope the president sees this through. And I think, unfortunately, again, it's going to take more military action to see it through.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Two U.S. flag vessels went through. Project Freedom, if we put up the bullet points, supports merchant vessels seeking to transit the Strait of Hormuz, enhances coordination among international partners, includes destroyers, over 100 aircraft, 15,000 service members, essentially an escort service through threw. Admiral Cooper of Cent Com talked about that today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ADM. BRAD COOPER, U.S. CENTRAL COMMAND: The IRGC has launched multiple cruise missiles, drones, and small boats at ships that we are protecting. We have defeated each and every one of those threats through the clinical application of defensive munitions. I have transited through the Strait of Hormuz probably 100 times in my career. You typically see between 20 and 40 small boats in a pack coming out. Today we saw six and eliminated them quickly. I think this is an example of the degradation of their capability.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Harris?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS FAULKNER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: So eliminated them quickly. I'm going to think that it got kinetic. And this is now what I would call a less ceasefirey ceasefire, right? But the thing to note here, and it's such an important point that you make, how much stronger Iran could get. I think how much weaker perhaps the relationships in that region would get for us if we don't finish the job at this point. So it's complicated.\par \par \uc2 With the Abraham Accords, there was more than hope there. There was, you know, an actual agreement. The UAE acting more almost like a NATO ally to us than our actual NATO allies at times, pulling out of OPEC and OPEC plus and doing things that could flood the market with oil and certainly benefit the United States and other countries around the world immediately when this ends.\par \par \uc2 So I'm looking at the end game. I agree with you. I don't think we get to the end game without becoming more kinetic. I think that you've got to push them back. We have got to figure out exactly how much enriched uranium they really have above 60 percent. Sixty percent still gives you a weapon, but not like 80 percent. So we really need some good answers at this point.\par \par \uc2 I'm prayerful that we don't have to put boots on the ground, troops on the ground. There are people in those boots. But I'm not actually thinking that we won't have to go back in with some firepower.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here is the treasury secretary on "AMERICA'S NEWSROOM" today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT BESSENT, TREASURY SECRETARY: Project Freedom is something that has been in the works. And we would encourage the Iranians to let these more than 300 vessels leave. They can fire on them. I think it's called the mosquito navy being swatted. Numerous ships, maybe in double digits, have been destroyed. And the U.S., we're just firing if fired upon. We are not the provocateurs here. But if the Iranians want to escalate here, we are willing to escalate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's the message all day today, really, Jessica.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, CO-HOST, "THE FIVE": It has been since the start of the war. We have heard every iteration of it's bridge and power plant day, to I'm going to wipe Iran off the face of the universe, to today the president out there, again, saying not only is it a mini war but this is going to be the he could of Iran. And it looks like Iran isn't taking us that seriously at this point. And maybe that is what ends up leading to a proper escalation, again. Maybe that is the way to finally finish that.\par \par \uc2 But we have gone away from talking about the original goals here. We don't talk about, you know, full eradication of their nuclear program anymore. We certainly don't talk about liberating the Iranian people from a regime that cares nothing for them. And the president has seemed over the past several weeks to not be as interested in this as he was certainly when they began. He's talking about Cuba now, focus other places. And I think he is fully aware of how little support he has with the American people for this war. It's now less popular than the Vietnam War, which we know was very unpopular.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He does every time he talks about it, though, Jason, talk about Iran will not be able to get a nuclear weapon and we will have eyes on that, as he calls it, nuclear dust that under rubble he says. How that all gets resolved and how the nuclear material, the enriched uranium gets out of there, is a big question yet to come.\par \par \uc2 I do want to put up this oil, U.S. oil crude export chart. It's truly amazing what the U.S. is pushing out of our country as the rest is not happening in the Strait of Hormuz. Now those tankers are coming to Texas.\par \par \uc2 RILEY: I think in one of your early lead-ins you mentioned -- you showed John Fetterman say we are squeezing Iranian economy. And that's an important point. Everyone talks about the U.S. calendar. The midterm elections are coming up. The markets are jittery. Voters don't like the war. It's unpopular.\par \par \uc2 But Iran is also on the calendar here. By estimates, the estimates I have seen, say they're losing hundreds of millions of dollars a day. They cannot sustain that, $100 million here, $100 million there. Soon it adds up to real money, Bret. And Iran, a country that size cannot sustain those losses.\par \par \uc2 That's why it's so important that we stay the course and finish the job there, and meet those objectives, getting rid of the nuclear program, getting rid of the missile program, getting rid of the enriched uranium that they have in stockpile over there, and ending the worldwide terrorism that they're sponsoring. Those have to remain the objectives, and I hope the president keeps his eye on the ball.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And how you get from point a to point b is really the question.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Well, yes, and I just want to say something to what Jessica said. We are hearing more about the Iranian people. There is a discussion that's going on about, you know, what it would take for them to stand up finally in the space that this is giving them. The problem is that they're in unarmed millions of people, a quarter of them are minors, which compared to the rest of the Middle East is not a huge number. It's still 25 to 26 million people underage, and you've got a population that's got decide when it can stand up.\par \par \uc2 I do think that the numbers matter here. And when the treasury secretary, Scott Bessent, says today that, you know, an unpaid military, that's going to be tough, he's right about that. I mean, we don't know what the defection rate is over there right now. We do know that the IRGC is drunk on its own like fantastic view of itself. But a lot of their leaders are gone. So we're not really sure what they have. The mining situation of the Strait of Hormuz turned out to be a little bit more their fantasy. Although we are really good at finding the mines, I haven't been convinced that we had that many to find. But we have to act like it because you don't know when they will go off.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And quickly, reading between the lines of the Iranian foreign minister is the line significant progress is being made with the Pakistanis.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: That was a surprise.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As he's saying, you know, whatever U.S., forget you. He's saying significant progress is being made.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: And who knows? He could be lying on Twitter. A lot of people are doing that these days. But it was the first time that we have heard anything about negotiation in at least the last four or five days, so that's a good thing after J.D. Vance's and Steve Witkoff and Jared Kushner's trip was canceled. I'm hopeful for a diplomatic solution.\par \par \uc2 The question will be for the nuclear program, can they get a deal better than the JCPOA, which Donald Trump took us out of in 2018? And right now with the posturing from their side and their willingness to drop out of these meetings, it doesn't indicate that they want to play ball at that level. Fighting about getting the Strait of Hormuz open was not how we intended to do this. And it has taken over the last four to six weeks of conversation. That's what concerns me.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And last word, Jason, in the middle of all of, in the economy is here, is still really cooking.\par \par \uc2 RILEY: It is. It is. And I hope that the public is patient with what's going on there, because in the long run, again, this is very, very important for the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, we will look at the congressional split over a law that shields social media.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, countdown is on. You can order my new book, "The Case for America, An Argument on Behalf of Our Nation." It actually hits stores in about five hours. You can order online at TheCaseforAmerica.com. I will be signing books at Raptis Rare Books in Palm Beach, Florida, this Friday, 7:30 p.m. You can get more information on my link on social channels. There you go. It's book time.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast. You can follow me on TikTok, X, and Instagram @BretBaier for exclusive behind-the-scenes content.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, live from New York, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2026 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020260505em5400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Chilling New Video From WHCA Shooting; The Left's Power Play Backfires; Dems' Dystopian Plan For CA; Democrats' dystopian plan for California; Democrats' grilling on the Hill; The clock is ticking on Tehran; Iran running out of oil storage space; Iran vows to protect nuclear capabilities; Biden versus believers, Biden DOJ created dossiers on Christians; SNAP scam exposed; Bentleys, Ferraris and food stamps; Crackdown on SNAP fraud\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Laura Ingraham, Kevin Corke, Paul Mauro \par \uc2 7,797 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 April 2026\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: Thanks all the folks here in Dallas, including Will Cain for letting me use his studio. And thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this Special Report. Fair, balanced and still unafraid. The Ingraham Angle starts in five seconds.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening, everyone. I'm Laura Ingraham. This is The Ingraham Angle from Washington tonight. Right off the bat, we have a big Fox News alert. Chilling new video shows Cole Allen stalking through the hallways at the Washington Hilton moments before he ran through a secret service barricade with plans to shoot everyone he could. Fox News Senior National Correspondent Kevin Corke has all the details. Kevin.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN CORKE, FOX NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Evening, Laura. This newest video released not long ago by the U.S. Attorney for the District, Jeanine Pirro, shown us what appears to be Cole at least getting off at least one shot as he sprints right past the bags, downstairs at the Hilton, Saturday night. Now, there's also a new host of images that show him to use the familiar vernacular, seemingly casing the joint, or maybe he was just wandering around the hotel.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, he actually was seen strolling through the hallways, checking out the gym at the hotel, smiling, even having a conversation with hotel staff with no outward appearance of what was to come just a day later. Then, of course, on Saturday night, decked out in all black, he ends up sprinting his way past agents, apparently firing that shotgun while avoiding being hit, then ultimately being placed into custody.\par \par \uc2 Now, this is all happening, Laura, on a day in which his lawyers argue that despite being armed to the teeth with multiple weapons during the alleged commission of a crime, no less, that Allen shouldn't remain in custody because he had no priors and apparently went to church and only had a pump action shotgun.\par \par \uc2 In an 11-page filing, his lawyers arguing that the government's rhetoric about a mass shooting is unsupported by its own proffered facts. Mr. Allen was not alleged to be holding an automatic or even semi-automatic weapon that are the hallmarks of the modern-day mass shooting.\par \par \uc2 Now, for the record, he was carrying a fully-loaded 12 gage Mossberg Maverick shotgun, not a 590 like mine. This one had an extended magazine. Prosecutors previously also released photos of him taking a selfie, apparently really armed, and talking about a 38 semi-automatic handgun, multiple knives and yes, shotguns have been used in multiple mass shootings, including a Columbine back in 1999, and of course, the Aurora theater shooting in 2012, there was also that mass shooting in 2018 over in Santa Fe, Texas.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the head of Secret Service, Sean Curran today, defended the security protocol Saturday night. He said the site was set up perfectly and that he would not change the site again. He also said the agent hit by gun fire, Saturday night, was not the victim of friendly fire.\par \par \uc2 As for Allen, he has yet to make a plea, enter a plea. His next hearing is set for the 11th of May. Very interesting to see those new images. As we get more information, I promise to pass it along. But for now, back to you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Kevin, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 CORKE: You bet.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The suspect goes to the gym. Here with reaction is Paul Mauro, Fox News Contributor, former \uc2 NYPD\uc2  Inspector. The thing that's chilling about this, Paul, is that he was, you know, just carrying about -- and carrying on about his business. I mean, I don't know if he's casing the joint in the gym, but nevertheless, he didn't appear to be a threat to anyone and yet we have these new images of him walking the hallways and looking around a little bit. Your thoughts as we begin to get these new videos in.\par \par \uc2 PAUL MAURO, ATTORNEY AND FORMER \uc2 NYPD\uc2  DETECTIVE: Well, my reflex, Laura, is that once again, we got pretty lucky. And I'm not even going to delve in necessarily to the secret service protocols here. We've been up and down. What they could have done, should have done, was it perfect, et cetera. But bottom-line is, had he been higher functioning, this could have been a heck of a lot worse.\par \par \uc2 And I'll give you the nightmare scenario from where I sit and what occurred to me right away, just 1993 World Trade Center bombing. In that instance, the suspects, the people who did it, the perpetrators had similarly done a lot of surveillance. They had figured out where they could place a van full of explosives in the basement garage of the Trade Center and they just misplaced it slightly. Otherwise we'd have had a very different story.\par \par \uc2 Well, this hotel has a similar underground garage. And when you consider the freedom of movement that you see here, and everybody I've spoken to, including a lot of Fox personnel who were down there and who parked in that garage, it does not seem like there was any pre-security before the event, day before the event, when people who were arriving in that garage, and if that garage had a van full of explosives right under the ballroom, you can imagine how this could have gone.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes. I mean, those of us have been going to this dinner for like 25 years, we've said this for years. You can check in with a bomb. You can walk around with a bomb. No one's going to stop you. Yes, you go through the magnetometer, it's going to be a problem. But and there is a perimeter, you know, it's on a different floor from the actual event, but nevertheless, you could do enormous amount of damage.\par \par \uc2 And what's interesting here, I think, when we see this additional video, we're going to show you, that shows him moments before the shooting, Paul. He goes into a door near the security checkpoint just a few moments before he ran through that magnetometer, and he was clearly looking to see how many agents were there. That's obvious.\par \par \uc2 MAURO: Yes. And he actually had the ability, according to all the reporting to assemble the gun, the long gun, the shotgun that we know he used, which is troubling as well. Look, hotels, we've talked about this, hotels are enormously difficult to secure. Every cop knows that you get crazy things happening in hotels. You get them in hospitals. Why? They all have these interior stairwells, they have these utility rooms, they have places you can hide. And that's very much what happened here. He came down an interior stairwell that secret service, I guess, didn't know was there, and that was how he was able to secrete himself close to the magnetometers, assemble the gun and then try to breach that.\par \par \uc2 Now look, in defense of the service, not only did they stop him, but had he gone down the stairs, had he gotten past the magnetometers, God forbid, my understanding is that in the ballroom itself, at the bottom of those stairs, a short stairwell. There was further security, which were --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, there was, yes.\par \par \uc2 MAURO: -- from what I understand, not secret service.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAURO: So there was another line of defense relative to the ballroom. We need to be fair here, but we also need to recognize that we're not going to keep getting lucky.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: No, the protocols, you know, President Trump references today and he said, you could -- you could -- you could just -- you have a great season, and you -- you have one bad play in a baseball game or a golf outing, everyone remembers a bad play. But this isn't a game. This is the president's life and the life of all of those in attendance, a lot of journalists, most of his cabinet, the line of succession, five out of six, I think were there. So, this can't be written off.\par \par \uc2 MAURO: Right.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And we love the secret service. And I think Sean Curran did a good job in answering questions today. But Paul, back to this video that we are showing where he's walking casually around the hotel. He goes into that gym and he -- he's -- he is in jeans and a sweater. Maybe he's -- I mean I guess trying to get some exercise before he dies or gets arrested. But what does this say to you given this proximity to the ballroom?\par \par \uc2 MAURO: Well, obviously, at no point does anybody say to him, hey, you know, what are you doing? What are you doing here? What -- are you looking for something? Like he doesn't get challenged. And look, that's -- that's part of the reason that a hotel is a bad idea, because he's a guest. The hotel is going to be operating as a hotel. People want to go to the gym. People want to get room service; they want to get their beds turned down. So consequently, it gives you a lot of freedom of movement.\par \par \uc2 And anybody does challenge him, he's just going to say, yes, well, I was thinking about coming to work out. I wanted to check out the gym. Now, why is he doing all of this? I mean, we can speculate. One of the things that occurs to me since -- seems to me like he had a plan and he knew how to execute it, to the extent that he did, was he might have been looking for a way to get out of there. Were he to survive this whole thing, he may have been looking for some way or other that he could get some egress from the event.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, when you look at the selfies he took, which have now come out, and the manifesto, my gut tells me that I don't think he expected to survive this. That's my own just instinct. I'm not basing on anything that's not public, and that tells me he was all in. Now, we'll see if in fact it was his weapon that fired at the secret service agent. I know they're saying that now. Here's the tell, Laura, and you know this is an attorney, if we see that charge added, attempted murder on a federal officer in addition to all the charges he faces now, if they don't add that charge, and that tells me there's still some gray area as to who fired, and I only say that because a shotgun blast should have left more of a signature on that vest. Thank God, it didn't, for that brave secret service officer, but I would have thought that a shotgun and it was buckshot, he put that right in the manifesto. The ballistics here should have been simpler.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, Paul, we appreciate it and we'll continue to dissect this. Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 All right, well, they're losing and they're mad. That's the focus of tonight's Angle. If you had to drink tequila, tequila shots for the number of times the word gut was used by the media over the past 24 hours, well, we'd all be admitted to the E.R. for alcohol poisoning. Reuters warned that the court's decision guts key provision in Voting Rights Act. The always dramatic Joan Biskupic have said John Roberts effort to gut the Voting Rights Act is complete, and take a bonus shot, because The Guardian used Kagan's language from her dissent claiming the court's latest move demolishes the Voting Rights Act. And it's as pathetic as it is predictable, is it not? They're recklessly and purposely misconstruing the court's ruling against racial gerrymandering in order to raise money and to stay in power. And by now, they've all tried the same old, tired talking points.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They effectively disenfranchised black voters. This is just the 21st version, 21st Century version. This is Jim Crow in new clothes.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CORY BOOKER (D-NJ): What the Supreme Court has now done is eviscerate that act, and what you see southern states openly talking about doing now is eliminating black representation, disenfranchising African American voters by drawing creative districts that completely take away any kind of representation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Is he kidding me? This was -- this was creative district line drawing. Just look at this insanity in Louisiana. Those districts and many o