for \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Great to see you, Kennedy, thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right. Make sure you set your DVRs and never miss an episode of THE WILL CAIN SHOW where we get to the heart of the matter from the heart of America. It is now time for THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 EMILY COMPAGNO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hello, everyone. I'm Emily Compagno, along with Harold Ford Jr., Jesse Watters, Dana Perino and Greg Gutfeld. It's 5:00 in New York City, and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 Well, you are looking there at shocking new photos of suspected shooter Cole Allen taken in his hotel room just moments before he attacked the \uc2 White House Correspondents\uc2 ' Dinner. The shooting marked the third attempted assassination plot targeting President Trump in just two years, and despite fresh calls to cool the rhetoric that puts a target on Trump's back, some on the left are digging in. Governor \uc2 JB Pritzker\uc2  when reacting to the DOJ indicting Comey, somehow finding a way to accuse Trump of inciting violence.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. JB PRITZKER (D-IL): This president is guilty of inciting violence, and now he's using the First Amendment. He's going after people for exercising their First Amendment rights and claiming that they're the ones who are attacking people. This whole administration seems full of people who are just following whatever this demented president tells them to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: Others on the left are deflecting, insisting that political violence is not a Democrat problem, even though Republicans have been the target of numerous attacks. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No one is legitimizing violence in any way. I have very little patience for this. Has the president faced three threats in the last year? Last couple of years? Absolutely. Is that abominable? Absolutely. The problem of political violence and extremism is evident on both sides.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , MS NOW HOST: The Democrats are blamed all for their rhetoric, when what I hear over and over again is Democrats saying, please tone it down. Please tone down the rhetoric. That's not what we stand for.\par \par \uc2 And we have seen, frankly, from the other side, from Trump and others, elevated rhetoric. Now you don't want to get it he said, she said place. But it's really infuriating.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: But Vice President J.D. Vance is pushing back, telling Will Cain that rage of fuel Democrats need to take a long look in the mirror.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE, VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: If you're engaged in that kind of rhetoric, you need to check yourself. If you're part of the left- wing in this country, you got to look yourself in the mirror and say, why is it that so many of the people who are firing guns at their political opponents agree with my perspective and listen to the same sources of information that I listen to. Maybe that suggests the left has got to tone down the rhetoric a little bit.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: Jesse, it's incredible how lopsided it is. I don't know the rose- colored glasses that they're using.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Well, the glasses are worn by two Democrat press flags for Democrat presidents. Where would they get this idea, Emily, that Trump is such a threat that he needs to be taken out? Where would they get the idea that he's such a unique danger a genocidal dictator like Hitler who at any day, could just kill everybody in sight? Where would they get the idea that he's mentally deranged and that the cabinet and perhaps the military might have to do something about that, that he's already putting immigrants, innocent ones, in concentration camps and killing innocent fishermen? Where would they get the idea that he was a Russian traitor, a sexist white supremacist who his slogan means really go back to when women and blacks couldn't vote?\par \par \uc2 This isn't coming from the dark underbelly of the internet. This is coming from the mainstream media in the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , on network T.V., on network late night television. You go online and this is what they say, and they can't stop saying it because they can't dial it back. It's who they are.\par \par \uc2 Everything they're saying defines everything about them. If you strip out the hate, they have nothing. There's no policy, there's no agenda. They can't have an executive say, you know, guys, maybe don't book the guy that calls Trump Hitler. Maybe don't have him on again. You know the rundown where you're calling him a genocidal maniac conducting illegal wars and raping and pillaging people. Maybe you don't use that tonight. No one is telling them that, because hate is profitable.\par \par \uc2 And until you break like the business model, you're never going to be able to tear this system apart. And that's what it is. It's about money, and that's why it's being driven so strongly, because the ratings are going down. And you can see, as the ratings go down, the hatred and the insanity goes up, and it's going to reach a breaking point pretty soon.\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: Well, some argue, Dana, the breaking point is three assassination attempts on the -- on the president, that, by the grace of God he survived them. But I mean, what the future it just -- it seems really terrifying.\par \par \uc2 And to your point, I was under the table with a lot of journalists that use words like Kristallnacht and likened his campaign rally here at MSG to Nazi Germany and Hitleresque. I mean, these are words that are in headlines in these mainstream media journalists.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Well, unity was over by the time President Trump finished the press conference on Saturday night and -- but I really think in some ways, obviously there are people on cable news hits, but I was mentioning yesterday, but since this is a new group, I will say it again, because I keep thinking about where everybody is in the muck and in the soup, and it's -- there's the cable news, yes, OK, and the media, yes, OK.\par \par \uc2 But where people are mostly getting information is online, and that is where it is really just disgusting, filled with conspiracy theories. We're going to probably talk about that in a little bit as well as we did yesterday, as we should.\par \par \uc2 The one thing I wonder about the Democrats is there's a lot of them who want to be president and they want to run in 2028, so they keep doing the same thing over and over again. There's been three assassination attempts. We have the same thing happen each time there's a call for unity or call for calming down the rhetoric. And then they'll say, OK, yes, we should do that, but he should do that. And then within 30 minutes, it's over, and everybody's going back.\par \par \uc2 And I just wonder, is there a Democrat out there who is smart enough to say, I'll do something different, Mr. President, could we meet, could we come in? There's three things we want to work on, one, two, three, and maybe we could figure out something to do, like, I'm willing to meet you halfway. Could you meet me halfway? Could we come see you?\par \par \uc2 Of course, the president is going to say yes. He'll meet with anybody that's willing to have a good faith conversation.\par \par \uc2 And so, is there a Democrat who has the courage to think about not just catering to the far left-wing base of the party, but to actually lead more in a way that says I'm willing to do something different, and to try to change the trajectory of these things that would maybe change the conversation, maybe get a few things done, like there are some big problems that the country is facing that we could all maybe use some unity on, and at least going forward and have somebody moderate the conversation. Maybe it's Harold.\par \par \uc2 Maybe Harold and I go together, and we moderate this conversation. I mean, there seems to me there's room for something new and different if somebody is willing to pivot.\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: I love that suggestion, Harold, and especially because it benefits the American people, whom the Democrats serve, as do Republicans.\par \par \uc2 But I note that it seems to me that any Democrat that tries to do that in any primaries or by their colleagues, they are slandered and slammed as allying with Donald Trump, that being bipartisan is actually a negative against their history, that somehow being defiant and engaging in this violent rhetoric is the only way for their stock to remain approved on the Democrat side.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I don't know about that. I think it probably goes both ways. It may -- you know, you see Tom Massie and others who differ with the president, they face some primary challenges.\par \par \uc2 It's good to be with everybody. I hope you guys had a great time at the dinner celebrating our country in the 250 years.\par \par \uc2 I'm reminded of King Charles as we talk about this, because I thought his speech yesterday was outstanding. I wasn't on the show yesterday, so I didn't get a chance to comment. The seriousness of the speech, the way he appealed to the best of us, and even when he talked about his differences in our country's differences, he talked about it, whether it be NATO or other things, he talked about it in such a -- with such calm and dignity and a sense that our belief in sharing with the American people, those who follow these things closely or those who don't, I think sometimes we get -- we're so invested in some of these conversations and some of these narratives and arguments that we can forget that a lot of Americans, if not the overwhelming majority of our audience, are working their tails off and watch us for a little news or for a little entertainment or for both.\par \par \uc2 And he, yesterday, a lot of people were being introduced to him. And I thought the way he talked about our differences, the U.S. and U.K. on NATO and other things, was so great.\par \par \uc2 Now, I've set my peace on this issue, and I think that, and I'll just repeat the highlights of it. I think that if we don't accept the fact that we're all drowning in all of this rhetoric, the negative rhetoric, I don't doubt, nor do I dispute, the Democrats are probably the biggest generators of it now, the negative things, the awful things.\par \par \uc2 But Emily and everyone around the table, I remind us, we are steeped, unfortunately, in a tradition of violence in our country, and even political violence at times that the data shows the last 10 years, maybe right-wing groups were more responsible for some of it in the last year. Now, this is just the data. The last year and a half, Democrats or the left-wing leaning groups have certainly been the biggest agitators, and according to the data, they -- more of it is coming from left-wing or Democrats and others.\par \par \uc2 Now, I take no solace over the last 10 years or contentment or inspiration because the violence may have been coming from right leaning groups. Nor should anyone take any solace because it may be coming from left leaning groups.\par \par \uc2 We're all going to be devoured by this rhetoric if our political body and political system is unable to function, because we all -- because as I said the other day, there's a rot in our country.\par \par \uc2 But I want to be clear, I'd go with Dana anywhere. Dana asked me to go to talk about politics and to talk about trying to solve things, because I think at the end of the day, if all we do, and I tried to say it yesterday or two days ago, if all we do is retreat to a crouch, saying it's them, they're the problem, then we're not going to solve the problem. There's nothing wrong with saying it's them to try to let everybody know that they made a mistake. But if it's them, because we're getting some contentment or satisfaction that it's not us, I think that's a dangerous thing.\par \par \uc2 So, Dana, let me signed up with you. If they let us in at the White House, I'm with you.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Where are we going?\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: And Greg, we're learning that the right-wing extremist violence was actually funded by the left-wing extremist. So, that it seems to me that, per your point recently, it is coming all from one source. And it would be nice if the leadership had any attempt at quelling it.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yes. And also the research that the media uses to kind of equate both sides is incredibly flawed, a joke even. I mean, I could just list the name -- the types of crimes, whether it is ambushes. We remember the ambushes. We remember the sustained rioting, the sustained looting, the attacks on government facilities, the attacks on law enforcement, businesses, individuals, religious institutions.\par \par \uc2 It's like something nearly 500 in the past. I don't know, seven years that could be linked to the left, one to the right, January 6th, and that has been exploited. We know that.\par \par \uc2 I am going to say let's just ignore the rhetoric. We're never going to get past that. The rhetoric thing is done. It's a tradition in America to say bad things about people. So, why don't we just ask who's guilty of the violence? You know, that's the avoidance that we're seeing in the media.\par \par \uc2 What they're trying to do is they're trying to get us into the battle over rhetoric. Well, you guys do it too. Well, I do it too. OK, then let's just find out what side's words lead to violence, because I agree with you, Trump says some really mean things, but we don't kill the Democrats afterwards.\par \par \uc2 Liberals won't advance to that next step. They want you to believe it's about the quantity of rhetoric, when it's really the quantity of violence from the rhetoric. The Dems simply compare the rhetoric divorce from the sustained violence. It is that simple, and I invested in that. I'm invested that a lot. I am very wary of people who say both sides, and I think because it betrays, it's a confession. When somebody says both sides are doing it, they're not denying that they're doing it, yes, you got me.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: But I want to be clear --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I'm not talking about you, Harold.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: But I'm guilty of what you said. I admit those sides do it. I mean --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, OK. Well, then let me -- when somebody says both sides do it, they aren't defending their side. They're just saying you do it too.\par \par \uc2 So, instead of arguing against the accusation, they expand the definition of what it is. So, we, I'm just talking about violence, OK, that was created by political instruction to do so by all means necessary, eliminate.\par \par \uc2 But they said, well, you call Democrats communists, you call them losers and creeps, not the point. I'm just talking about the sustained violence. So, when they say on both sides, they are basically just shifting, I hate to say goalposts, but they're changing it.\par \par \uc2 Last, my last point, then I'll shut up. We're all familiar with the broken windows theory, where visible signs of disorder create a lack of societal control, and that encourages for more and more serious crimes.\par \par \uc2 Where did this left-wing violence come from? Well, how many instances have we seen in the last 10 years where conservatives were targeted on campus? Pro-lifers get attacked. Riley Gaines get swarmed. Speakers are mobbed. Vandalism everywhere. No countless attacks. Nobody gets charged.\par \par \uc2 Tolerance towards that violence was the broken window theory that now makes it seem to younger people, young men, that violence is now more acceptable. It's the same kind of incubation you see on city streets where you know vandalism, public urination then leads to mugging, so.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That's a really good point. The broken window theory on political violence.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: But Greg, back when years ago, there were -- there were people who were pro-abortion -- people for abortion who were being attacked. You had abortion clinics being blown. My only point is I'm not denying that the violence is probably worse on one side to date, and it is before -- than it was before, but we can't ignore the fact that violence is -- has been a part because rhetoric has led them to it.\par \par \uc2 What I'm saying is, I don't -- I'm not comfortable, and I'm not saying you are either. I'm not saying it's OK if it's -- if it's Republicans doing and not me. And I think some people --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I'm not talking about you, Harold.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: I know and I know -- and I'm not talking about you. I think some people are OK with it saying it's them, when I'm telling you it's going to come for all of us if we don't find ways to deal with the (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: If it does come for all of us, it's not going to come from us.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: I disagree, because it has come from a side (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You can give me -- well, we've done this before.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Abortion and activist people, abortion clinics being blown up, Charleston, the guy going into a printer and saying, I'm killing you because you -- because you're black.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You want me to go through the 500 examples?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: No. But Greg, I think it goes to his word, you don't like the words, it's both sides. And to me, what both sides means is, America, we've got to solve this for America.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I think we're talking past each other.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: No, I think we're talking to each other.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You know what, I'm going to talk behind your back.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I'm going to talk about you.\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: If you just are talking about political violence, though, graduation matters. And when you talked about those words, when you said loser, when you said creep, when you said communist, those aren't words that put death targets on people's back. Name me one Republican president that has not been called a Nazi. You can't name one. That's the issue on the left, and that's what put started to that.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: What do they call Republicans before Hitler?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I don't know.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Mean-spirited.\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: Awesome. All right, coming up next, he's the anti-fraud fraud. Tim Walz trying to take credit for crackdowns the Feds actually ran.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Remember the quality leering center? Well, the Feds sure do, and they were just there busting it, along with more than 20 other businesses across Minnesota, all part of a massive series of raids aimed at social services fraud.\par \par \uc2 Governor Tim Walz attempting to take some credit for the operation on social media, and then he took a victory lap over the crackdown at his State of the State Address.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): We've created additional checks and balances. We brought on more investigators, more auditors, more law enforcement agencies as well as an outside firm, to take a look at high-risk programs. People who have ripped us off are getting caught and they are going to jail, just like today.\par \par \uc2 I've said the buck stops with me, and as I know some of you will take that as an open invitation to play politics with every incident of fraud that takes place here in Minnesota, even though I have to tell you, the statistics show it's happening in red states more than here, but so be it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: But FBI Director Kash Patel wasn't a fan of Walz taking credit, and he posted this. Come again? This FBI and DOJ, with our DHS partners, drafted and executed every search warrant today, but go ahead and take credit for our work while we smoke out the fraud plaguing Minnesota under your governorship.\par \par \uc2 Greg, I wonder why aren't the Dems more mad that the fraud was taking place and taking money away from the people they say they want to help?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Because Dana, didn't you hear it's happening on both sides?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Oh my God.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That is what he said.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's exactly what he just said. They're doing it, yes, and we hate it there too. For a guy who cares about fraud, he skipped the hearing on Tuesday, even while he was in the damn building. He's trying to appear above it all, when he's actually just swimming and drowning in corruption.\par \par \uc2 And while he's drowning in corruption, he's leading the fight against corruption. Who does that remind you of? The SPLC. They were leading the fight against bigotry while creating more of it.\par \par \uc2 This guy, he reminds me of, you ever see those surveillance videos of a burglary happening and the guard dog is sleeping? He's like the guard dog, except it's worse. He's licking his junk. Makes me ill.\par \par \uc2 He's an expert on Stolen Valor. We know that, right? He -- with his phony military service, now he claims that his only sin as governor is that he's too generous. That is also Stolen Valor, because you're giving away other people's money, to people who directly benefit you. And then he says there's something about Minnesota, like they're truly nice people.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Minnesota's nice?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. Well, then why are you exploiting them? You know, it's like Minnesota is like the kindhearted relative who can't say no to their deadbeat druggie son, you know, and everybody else in the family is you got to kick him out. You got to kick him out, but they can't cut him off. And Walz is the drug dealer keeping the kid on the junk.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Jesse, Governor Walz was a -- if they had won, he would have been a heartbeat away from the presidency.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You know, if I were as a Democrat, I'd want to know where the money went that they gave to Walz in Kamala. That's a fraud investigation that should be undertaken.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes, Beyonce might have --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes. I did a little walk around D.C., and Trump's redoing the reflecting pool. And so, it turns out, Joe Biden tried to spend $300 million repairing the reflecting pool in the Capitol. Trump is now doing it for $20 million, he's hiring a guy who does pools that he knows from one of his clubs, and they're just OK.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm beginning to think that Biden knows it's not $300 and they just throw money at all their union guys, their consultants, and that's the system that they operate in, that they're not trying to save money. They're just trying to throw as much out the door as they can to help all their buddies out, because that's what was happening here.\par \par \uc2 Even after Walz was flagged that people were stealing, he still shoveled the money out and that benefits all of his people, because they all vote Democrat.\par \par \uc2 Now, Vance is on this and so if Vance is crusading nationally against fraud, this really puts him up a level in the race, because it looks like he's doing something serious.\par \par \uc2 Gavin's selling knee pads. Kamala is on a book tour for eternity, and he's actually doing something for the taxpayer. And this is something Republicans, Democrats, independents all love. I mean, we feast on this stuff. We hear about a fraudster. We don't want to just kick him out of the country and throw him in prison. We want to spit on him. We want to break his ankles and lock him up and humiliate him.\par \par \uc2 And half of these people stealing are immigrants. That pisses us off even more. And then you think about the inflation that this stuff drives. It kind of encapsulates every single problem in this country. And you just right down the middle on it.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: All right, we only have about a minute left, so I'm going to give 30 and 30. Emily, they executed the search warrants. You have to imagine that a lot of people thought this was coming. Do you think do you think they tried to clean it up before they\par \par \uc2 got there?\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: Well, you know, who didn't think it was coming because they weren't told was the city of Minneapolis that specifically released a statement Mayor Frey saying we didn't have anything to do with it. It's like, yes, because you weren't told, because you would have tipped off these people.\par \par \uc2 Walz is the fraud. We know this. You cannot recast yourself halfway through the movie, from defendant and denier to hero and reformer. You guys remember when the fraud was brought to light years after it was going on. He specifically said Republicans are using the fraud scandal as an excuse to target immigrants. He specifically said this is what happens when they scapegoat, when they no longer hide the idea of white supremacy. He called the messenger, a 20-year-old guy that uncovered it all, Nick Shirley, a white supremacist and delusional conspiracy theorist. He called the federal agents modern-day Gestapo, and now he is trying to say he was on board the whole time after he lambasted them from second one, no.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, don't forget that these monies funded terrorism in Somaliland. So, this goes way beyond him patting himself on the back because he's about to be on the other end of an indictment.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Let the chips fall where they may. I said it at night that this story surfaced. I'm old-fashioned still. I think that everybody wants credit in law enforcement. Nobody wants to be held accountable.\par \par \uc2 Look, if Walz didn't help, if it was all Mr. Patel and the feds