ow, that was today in California. But it was not the only allegation of rape.\par \par \uc2 Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL ACCUSER: I went to the bathroom and I don't remember anything after that.\par \par \uc2 INTERVIEWER: You don't remember anything?\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL ACCUSER: I remember the next day, I can see flashes of that evening of him on top of me, me pushing him off, him grabbing me. It was a lot more aggressive. It was aggressive.\par \par \uc2 INTERVIEWER: Did you say no?\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL ACCUSER: Yes, I said no. I said -- I -- in my flash that I can recall, I was pushing him off of me saying no.\par \par \uc2 INTERVIEWER: And what did he do?\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL ACCUSER: He didn't stop. He didn't stop.\par \par \uc2 INTERVIEWER: And you woke up the next morning.\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL ACCUSER: I woke up the next morning naked alone in his hotel room.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Frankly, for everyone, obviously, that is hard to hear. But it does not end there. Two more victims spoke to CBS News about their horrific encounters with the former congressman, \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL ACCUSER: No, it's just the beginning. It's just the very beginning. And I know personally that we're not walking away from this fight.\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL ACCUSER: For me, justice won't be until he can't ever harm a woman ever again. And he has faced the consequences for the women that he has harmed.\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL ACCUSER: I think we just prevented another 30 to 40 years potentially of him harming people if he were to stay in Congress.\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL ACCUSER: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL ACCUSER: So in that sense I think we have served justice for his future victims.\par \par \uc2 INTERVIEWER: Wow.\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL ACCUSER: That won't exist anymore.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And yet another woman accused Swalwell of sending graphic, unsolicited pictures. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL ACCUSER: He would say things like, "What are you doing? What are you wearing? Sent me a photo of what you're doing right now." And then eventually one night, we were talking and he just sent me a photo of his genitals.\par \par \uc2 INTERVIEWER: Unsolicited.\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL ACCUSER: Unsolicited. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2  is responsible for \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2 . Not the media, and definitely not the women.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Now tonight, Mr. Fangfang Swalwell denies the serious allegations, but that might not matter. Not only is Swalwell's political career now officially over, but in all likelihood, he may soon be facing serious criminal trouble. I mean serious.\par \par \uc2 Now, the first accuser we showed you tonight is filing a police report with the Los Angeles County sheriff. Swalwell is also reportedly under investigation in New York City.\par \par \uc2 And meanwhile, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director Kash Patel, he is monitoring all of these allegations, writing, quote, "\uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2  has maintained that none of the allegations against him are true. And now that he has resigned, we would welcome him to sit down with the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and share any information that he has."\par \par \uc2 But tonight, I do have bad news for \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2 . Based on his own long- held beliefs, it is not looking good. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIC SWALWELL (D), FORMER CALIFORNIA CONGRESSMAN: My Republican colleagues do not trust women. That's what this is entirely about. Trust women, my friends. Trust women. They will get it right.\par \par \uc2 As a former prosecutor, as somebody who has spent my time in Congress working to stand up for the rights of victims, and now I'm calling on my Republican colleagues, this is not about red team versus blue team. This is about sexual assault victims who deserve justice.\par \par \uc2 But are the chances that three or four women independently who never met each other would have similar experiences with one person? So either, Ari, this person committed these horrific acts or he is the single unluckiest person in the world for these people to conspire and make up lies against him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Ultimately, this is all much bigger than a congressman from California. This is a story about the Democratic Party, the legacy media mob who wax poetic about women's rights and believing all victims if it helps them politically. Just like they don't really care about how many people were shot in Chicago this weekend, 28. I thought lives mattered. Does anyone even -- one Democrat know the name of one of the victims shot - - those shot and killed this weekend? I doubt it.\par \par \uc2 For years, it was an open secret that Swalwell was a giant creep. He even got caught purportedly sleeping with a Chinese spy. Hopefully that information will soon be released to the public. After all, he's all for transparency as well.\par \par \uc2 But the left-wing ecosystem in Washington, D.C. and the swamp said nothing. They did nothing. No interviews, no investigative reports, no calls for Swalwell to resign.\par \par \uc2 In fact, they stood by. They did nothing as he ran for governor of one of the most important states in America. Democrats only pretended to care when a crowded Democratic field in California split the vote, putting the governor's race in jeopardy. They needed Swalwell to drop out.\par \par \uc2 So, the Democratic machine, they got to work. Even Swalwell's best friend in Congress pretended to be totally, completely, utterly unaware of his best friend's behavior until just recently.\par \par \uc2 And that is Senator Ruben Gallego has totally abandoned his friend now as allegations against Swalwell mount.\par \par \uc2 Here with the full report tonight, Christina Coleman. She is with us.\par \par \uc2 Christina, very, very, very alarming.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA COLEMAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Alarming indeed, Sean. And you know, if all these allegations are true, he certainly is a giant creep. That's the best way to describe it. What you just said there.\par \par \uc2 Today, Swalwell's attorney called the sexual misconduct and assault allegations a calculated and transparent political hit job. But lawyers representing Lonna Drewes, who says he drugged, choked, and raped her, slammed that assessment.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAYWER FOR SWALWELL ACCUSER: This is about much more than politics, and belittling such serious allegations is offensive to Lonna and the victims everywhere. This is not about Democrat versus Republican. This is about accountability versus silence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Swalwell announced yesterday that he's resigning from his congressional seat, saying in part, quote, "I'm deeply sorry to my family, staff, and constituents for mistakes and judgment I've made in my past. I will fight the serious false allegations made against me."\par \par \uc2 But he didn't clarify what those mistakes even were. And either way, he got swift condemnation from both sides of the aisle, including from his friend, Democrat Arizona Senator Ruben Gallego, who claims Swalwell betrayed his trust.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RUBEN GALLEGO (D-AZ): This man led a double life. He lied to us. He lied to his family, lied to his constituents, some of the most powerful people in this country. You know, even before he run, he was trusted with some -- some, you know, positions that normally we would never think someone like this could do if they're leading that double life.\par \par \uc2 And again, I was manipulated. I was lied to, and the -- everyone else was, too.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: And tonight, Governor Gavin Newsom announced a special election will be held on August 18th to fill Swalwell's congressional seat -- Sean.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right, Christina. Thank you for that report tonight.\par \par \uc2 Now, tonight, I have more bad news for California Democrats. The rest of the Democratic field out there has big problems of their own.\par \par \uc2 First up, the front runner, far-left billionaire Tommy Steyer. Remember him? Spent over $250 million running in the Democratic primary, received zero delegates. And in case you forgot, here is one of the highlights of Steyer's campaign. Take a look at this.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO CLIP PLAYS)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: He's crushing it in that dance.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Steyer is now up against another Democrat in California who's even less likable. And that's former Congresswoman Katie Porter, accused of physically abusing her now ex-husband. And according to both video and multiple accounts, Porter has a long history of bullying her staff.\par \par \uc2 Here's a reminder.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KATIE PORTER (D), CALIFORNIA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: The state could lose four (EXPLETIVE DELETED). Get out of my (EXPLETIVE DELETED) shot.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I wanted to tell you that that's actually incorrect. It's not that it's electric vehicles. It's that if we don't meet the commitments under the Paris climate accord.\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Okay. It does. Okay. You also were in my shop before that.\par \par \uc2 We should have. Yes. Okay, everybody. I'm not that dark. It's too dark.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right, maybe, just maybe, there could be one person that could still save the day. Well, I can think of somebody, Steve Hilton.\par \par \uc2 But according to "The New York Post", Kamala Harris is apparently emerging as a possible contender through -- contender through a mail-in ballot campaign. Good luck with that.\par \par \uc2 Here with reaction, "The Larry Elder Show" host and former California gubernatorial candidate himself, Larry Elder, along with "The Big Weekend Show" co-host, Tomi Lahren.\par \par \uc2 Tomi, you know California politics really well, as does Larry. And this was -- this was an open secret in California apparently, at least among Democratic circles. Everybody seemed to know. Nobody ever said anything.\par \par \uc2 And it would appear that this takedown if it came from any political side, it would be the Democratic side. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 TOMI LAHREN, "THE BIG WEEKEND SHOW" CO-HOST: Yeah, certainly. I think the Democrats in California who run that state into the ground, I might add. I think they were awfully nervous that they might end up with two Republicans on that ballot because of their jungle primary system. And they certainly could not risk that. And I think that they knew that some of these allegations against Swalwell would likely come out if he were to be their candidate for governor.\par \par \uc2 So I think they wanted to get ahead of it. They learned their lesson not getting Joe Biden out of that race quicker. They didn't throw Joe under the bus fast enough.\par \par \uc2 So, Nancy Pelosi and the rest of them, they knew they had to get rid of Swalwell. I would venture to guess they probably came to him, said, "Hey, now's the time to drop out. He wouldn't do it because he's got an entitlement complex." And then I think they let it rain down on him.\par \par \uc2 We watched it happen in real time over the weekend. It was out of the governor's race and then resigning from Congress. But I would say this to all of his Democrat BFFs in congress and elsewhere, you better hope Swalwell doesn't have anything on you because I have a feeling he's backed into the corner and he might start singing like a canary.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: What a great observation and likely true.\par \par \uc2 Larry, you know California politics as well as anybody. I mean, you've been out there your entire life, and I find it fascinating now that it appears that there is a possibility that the number one and number two surviving candidates that could be on the ballot in November could both be Republicans. What are the odds of that happening?\par \par \uc2 LARRY ELDER, HOST OF "THE LARRY ELDER SHOW": We'll see. The fact is that the top two people in most polls are both Republicans. That's because of this cockamamie so-called jungle primary that the Democrats set up to keep them in power.\par \par \uc2 It may very well backfire on them because they're like kids in bumper cars. They're knocking off each other. And at some point though, Sean, trust me, the Democrats are going to coalesce behind one person. They're going to force everybody else out to make sure that at least one of the top two finishers are in fact Democrats.\par \par \uc2 I was surprised, shocked that Eric Swalwell ran because as Tomi pointed out, these rumors have been going on around for years and Nancy Pelosi claimed she never heard anything. Well, Willie brown, the potentate of California, former mayor and former speaker of the assembly for 15 years, said that the rumors had been around and he heard about the rumors from Adam Schiff and, wait for it, Nancy Pelosi.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Oh, okay. Who claimed -- who claimed total ignorance today.\par \par \uc2 You know, if you think back to --\par \par \uc2 ELDER: I knew nothing. I knew nothing.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I know nothing, right? You know, it's like Hogan's Heroes back in the day.\par \par \uc2 You know, Tomi, I want to look at this though more objectively. If you go back to the Kavanaugh case and as that story began to unfold, everybody's story began to fall apart. Well, I think they spiked the punch and I saw him serving punch to girls in line.\par \par \uc2 Well, he was near the punch bowl. That's what it evolved into. Well, there was a line of guys outside of a room and he was in line. Well, he wasn't in line, but he was near the line, and they destroyed this man's life.\par \par \uc2 When you make serious allegations, they have to be taken seriously to begin with. And I know the left always says they have to be believed. However, I paid very close attention to every one of the women that we played here tonight and one sounds more credible than the next. And yeah, especially that first woman claiming rape, being sober, completely sober, saying no, if that woman goes before any jury, I cannot see anything but a conviction on the horizon for Swalwell.\par \par \uc2 LAHREN: Yeah, her story was certainly very convincing as I watched it. I would note that a lot of other networks didn't feel that they needed to really cover this as much as FOX News has.\par \par \uc2 But I will say this -- I'm not one of those people that believes in just saying the woman's word is gospel. Believe all women. I don't think it's as black and white as this.\par \par \uc2 However, the allegations against Brett Kavanaugh, those were, you know, a decade or more ago when they came out. This has been recent stuff and it's a pattern over and over and over again. And I have a feeling more women are going to come out.\par \par \uc2 So for Eric Swalwell, by the way, to come off of the Fangfang incident and have just such an entitlement complex, so much narcissism, so high on his own supply that he just laughed that one off and then continued allegedly with certain behavior. It just goes to show the contempt that some of these Democrats have for the people that they supposedly serve. They think they can get away with everything, but Eric Swalwell found out because he threatened Democrat power in California, and they aren't going to have that.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You know, for a guy that wanted full transparency as it relates to Epstein, I would say we need full transparency now. And I'd like to see the release of the Fangfang files, Larry Elder.\par \par \uc2 ELDER: Yeah. And he's got more problems than that. He's also flat broke according to "The California Post". He owes money, student debt. He's borrowing from his credit card. He's raiding his retirement account.\par \par \uc2 And he ought to be working on his marriage. I would suspect that his marriage is in deep, deep trouble as well.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah. Well, I mean, how could it not be, right, with all these people making allegations?\par \par \uc2 All right. So, now we're looking at the legal side. I'll ask you both an exit question here with this going before Alvin Bragg and the L.A. sheriff at the same time simultaneously. The question is, I can imagine charges could be pending any day now -- Tomi.\par \par \uc2 Yeah, I'd imagine even though Alvin Bragg really doesn't want to put anybody in jail, I have a feeling in this case perhaps because now that the Democrats have turned on Eric Swalwell, there's a higher likelihood something's actually going to happen.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah. Larry?\par \par \uc2 ELDER: Same thing. He's in trouble legally and also very likely there'll be some civil lawsuits. Again, if he denies allegations and accuses the women of lying, I can imagine them filing a defamation suit. He's in serious, serious trouble.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah. And you're a good lawyer. I don't know if you can get them off. I don't know if anybody could at this point.\par \par \uc2 We appreciate you both being with us. Tomi Lahren, Larry Elder.\par \par \uc2 All right. And coming up, all eyes are on Iran tonight where a U.S. blockade is squeezing the terrorist regime. What does this mean for any potential negotiated peace? A full report from Tel Aviv is straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 Also, General Keith Kellogg will weigh in as we continue.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right, turning now to the Middle East where President Trump continues to enforce a blockade against any and all ships trying to enter or leave Iranian ports as Vice President Vance says that negotiations with Iran are continuing. Although from my vantage point, I'm not exactly sure what is left to negotiate.\par \par \uc2 The Iranians either have to accept the president's terms or they will watch Kharg Island and their entire economy ultimately get blown to smithereens.\par \par \uc2 But now though, the ceasefire is holding up as the president now is giving Iran a glimpse into a world where they will have no oil revenue, no economy. And I'll have more to say about this in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 But first, let's get a read on the latest developments from our very own Greg Palkot. He is live. He's in Tel Aviv tonight.\par \par \uc2 What is the feeling on the ground there? Because I imagine the Israelis also have other objectives that they want to accomplish probably separate and apart from the U.S.?\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Exactly. They're watching very closely everything going on, Sean, and it looks like that blockade that the Straight of Hormuz by the U.S. Navy is holding. Central Command saying in the last 24 hours, no ships leaving Iranian ports and going through the strait had made it further in the Gulf of Oman. Six ships have been intercepted, forced to return. There's been no interdictions or boarding of ships yet, though.\par \par \uc2 Over a dozen vessels, 100 fighter jets, drones, and 10,000 service members have been involved. All this aimed to striking back at Iran, which had applied a stranglehold to the Hormuz Strait, cutting off Iran's oil revenue, and working to get talks restarted.\par \par \uc2 President Trump expects, he says, negotiations to resume in the next few days. Vice President Vance said the administration is looking for a broader agreement, including an Iran with no nukes.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE, VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The president wants to make -- he doesn't want to make like a small deal. He wants to make the grand bargain. He said that if you're willing to act like a normal country, we are willing to treat you economically like a normal country. He doesn't want a small deal.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: To halt -- talks to halt the fighting on another front were also held in Washington. Secretary of State Rubio meeting with Israeli and Lebanese officials. Israel though is still pounding Hezbollah targets in Lebanon. The Iranian-backed terror group was not involved.\par \par \uc2 And the International Monetary Fund warns of the effect of an extended war, big trouble for Iran and an upended global economy a reason to get the fighting wrap sooner rather than later. Markets were up, the price of oil backing off recent highs.\par \par \uc2 And, Sean, as time on the ceasefire between the U.S. and Iran is ticking down. It's set to run out in a week. So far, only minor infractions so far.\par \par \uc2 Back to you.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. And, Greg, the price of oil, as I came on the air, $89 a barrel. Thank you for that report.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to Iran -- well, this is going to end one of two ways. Let's be very clear. You know, Donald Trump, if you read the tea leaves, if you actually take the time to listen to what he says, the regime will either deal and go along with the president's demands or with their economic destruction. That will be inevitable.\par \par \uc2 In other words, zero ability to make any money from oil for many years to come. Now, you actually have to give the president a lot of credit for the restraint that he is showing right now.\par \par \uc2 At this moment, he could easily wipe out the Iranian economy. He could destroy Kharg Island probably in less than two hours. And instead, he is now giving Iran a chance to see the light for themselves. And that he would be doing for the sake of the Iranian people, not for the third or fourth tier or fifth tier leadership that remains.\par \par \uc2 But one fact remains. The U.S. needs to get all of the enriched uranium out of that country. And Iran, as the president said, can never, ever, ever, ever have a nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 Never means never. Not now, not in 10 years, not in 20 years, not in 50 years. Never means never. Neither one of these conditions is negotiable.\par \par \uc2 Now, they're going to learn the hard way and they're going to either, you know, we'll see the blockade will be effective and they'll get a very good glimpse into what life without an oil economy. They have an oil-based economy looks like or they will push the president to the brink and at that point, they won't have any chance to recover their economy.\par \par \uc2 Here with reaction, he's the co-chair of American Security at AFPI, we have lieutenant -- retired Lieutenant General Keith Kellogg is with us.\par \par \uc2 General, great to have you. Thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 All right. So, from my perspective and maybe there's a different perspective on this and I'd love to get your thoughts on it.\par \par \uc2 Let's start with the enriched uranium. The Iranians have been told by the president. They can never be a nuclear power. They said they have an inalienable right to have a nuclear weapon. The president says no.\par \par \uc2 They said they have the right to enrich uranium. The president said no. We know they have 60 percent enriched uranium. We have to worry obviously about small amounts of that being turned into a dirty bomb, but more importantly that that 60 percent could be enriched to weapons grade 90 percent in less than 12 days.\par \par \uc2 So is it possible to entomb that uranium that they have and would that be secure enough so that they never can use that uranium in the future? Or is it something that we'll just have to take if they're not willing eventually to hand it over?\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. KEITH KELLOGG (RET), FORMER ASSISTANT TO THE PRESIDENT: Yeah, thanks, Sean. Thanks for having me tonight.\par \par \uc2 Look, Marco Rubio, Secretary Rubio said the old world order is gone. He's right. You know, when we had the negotiation in Pakistan with the vice president, you know, they're dealing with people who are really don't like us.\par \par \uc2 They're authoritarian. They're theocratic. They say "death to America". They kill thousands of their own people.\par \par \uc2 Remember, this is a war and president Trump is a wartime president. You're right about that.\par \par \uc2 And I think how -- what he needs to do taking the extreme position right now. We need to be ready to go and take Kharg Island and take the islands like Larak Island in the -- in the Gulf region in the Strait of Hormuz. You can do that with our great marines. You can deal with the 82nd Airborne. You can do it all and it can be -- it can be done.\par \par \uc2 You know, I'm reminded a little bit of our civil war. It wasn't until 1864 that Ulysses S. Grant was turned to by Lincoln and said, finish this war. He did it a year within a year. And he did it primarily by sending Sherman through the march to the sea when he broke the back of the Confederacy economically.\par \par \uc2 That's what we need to do. And the president as a wartime president should do that. You can't negotiate with these guys. I don't think you can.\par \par \uc2 There may be regime adjustment, but these guys have -- are this attuned to doing this for -- you see Pezeshkian right now, their president, finally getting involved. He's saying things. He's really not a Revolutionary Guard, but as long as you got people like Araghchi out there who keeps pushing the ball, he keeps nego -- trying to negotiate.\par \par \uc2 The president has laid out very clear terms. He's been right on this. You were right on it, too, just a minute ago when you talked about Kharg Island.\par \par \uc2 In 1988, President Trump said, take Kharg Island if something happens. He said that to "The Guardian" during an interview.\par \par \uc2 I think we need to go to that position. We