 think he must get back the highly enriched Uranium either through the IAEA or through some consortium.\par \par \uc2 What Juan mentions is true, but the reality is, we don't care what the Chinese think. We don't care what our NATO partners think. I very certain he doesn't care what the Pope thinks right now. Donald Trump cares about bringing Iran to heel and maybe getting freedom to 92 million Iranians. And that's what I think the Pope and the other people ought to care about too.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Iranians said to sit down, they wanted to make sure that there were also peace negotiations between Israel and Lebanon and the fighting with Hezbollah. "New York Times", "In rare direct talks, Israeli and Lebanese officials seek way forward. They're not expected to immediately produce a deal to end the war between Israel and Hezbollah, the Iran backed Lebanese militia that Israel says is attacking, it's attacked are targeting. Lebanon was hoping that a ceasefire would be reached. Netanyahu of Israel has ruled out a ceasefire. The aim of the discussions in Washington would be disarming Hezbollah." Here's the Israeli ambassador on that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YECHIEL LEITER, ISRAELI AMBASSADOR TO U.S.: We discovered today that we're on the same side of the equation. That's the most positive thing we could have come away with. The head of Hezbollah warned the government of Lebanon yesterday not to participate in these talks, and the government of Joseph Aoun bravely said no to Hezbollah. And this is the beginning of a very strong battle against Hezbollah.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, Annie, as we're talking about the fight with Iran and all that's happening, we haven't really been talking a lot about the battle between Israel and Hamas and Israel and Hezbollah.\par \par \uc2 LINSKEY: Yes. And I think this very well could be one of the unintended consequences and potentially a very positive one of this action in the Middle East. You know, these are two parties that have not spoken to each other in, what, 30 years, I believe. And now they are talking. And I think that point bears repeating that Hezbollah did not want them to go forward, and the Lebanese decided that they would do it anyway. And it does allow this possibility of boxing out another terrorist organization.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It would be something. Stand by.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a loaded to-do list for Congress. And then send your questions @BretBaier. I'll answer some of them. It is "TikTok Tuesday".\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ELISE STEFANIK, (R-NY): Congress goes back into session this week. And we are committed to making sure that our Homeland Security is fully funded.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHUCK SCHUMER, (D-NY) SENATE MINORITY LEADER: To be clear, Republicans could open DHS if they wanted to. We also passed twice a bill that would easily fund all of DHS aside from ICE and CPB.\par \par \uc2 REP. JODEY ARRINGTON, (R-TX) CHAIRMAN: The Democrats who are constantly obstructing, have no interest in governing. We've got to be the adults. We can use reconciliation to do it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A lot happening on Capitol Hill. Our own Chad Pergram very busy these days. We're back with our panel. As Congress focal points this week, you have got the reconciliation, which requires 50 votes, Republicans in the Senate amid the DHS shutdown, FISA reauthorization, Iran War Powers resolution, coming days, Save America Act, the Bondi deposition still pending, and member expulsion calls. A lot to do. The reconciliation, it seems like, Hugh, they're going to try to pack a lot in there.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: I hope they do, Bret. Senator Schumer seems intent on keeping the Republican competitive in the fall by standing in the way of the Save Act, standing in the way of DHS funding. And I think reconciliation, though difficult, will make it possible to reopen the government and get everything done on the agenda. And they need to get FISA done. That's the one thing that must be done. We can't go blind.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Speaking of that, the debate over FISA is real. It has always been. Here's the back and forth.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. PETER WELCH, (D-VT): This is the constant challenge of protecting our national security, and FISA has been helpful in that, in protecting our individual liberties. So it's always been a very tense balancing act.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL HAGERTY, (R-TN): Personally, I think we need to see it done. I've talked with John Ratcliffe, our CIA director. This is a tool that he certainly needs.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN KENNEDY, (R-LA): If you trust government, you failed history class. Color me skeptical. I don't know how I'm going to vote.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There's dissension, worry in the Republican ranks, too, Juan.\par \par \uc2 LINSKEY: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Oh, I'm sorry. Yes, absolutely. I would point out there's bipartisan call for some reform that includes warrants, so that warrants would be used as part of this process. The president is asking for a clean bill. He just wants them to pass something. He's pressing Republican members to be unified in getting this done.\par \par \uc2 But I think that what you're seeing here, and this, by the way, FISA would expire on the 20th of the month, so it's coming up fast. What I think you're seeing is that people -- and we just saw this from Senator Kennedy of Louisiana. There are Republicans that have concerns that if you allow the government to search American citizens' information, and you can do this inadvertently because you're going after a bad guy, but you come into a pocket of American information, and do you have access, can you exploit it. I think there are people who feel that that is in violation of Fourth Amendment privacy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You were going to say, I just want to add that without DHS funding, and then you have the possibility of this going by the wayside, there are a lot of security experts that say this is a perfect storm.\par \par \uc2 LINSKEY: Yes. And sort of many security experts saying this is a must-pass legislation. I feel like the Trump administration has been doing a really interesting job as they have been lobbying for it, and putting out some classified information, or declassifying information that's made it clear where this legislation, this authority has been useful, particularly in preventing a terrorist attack on Taylor Swift's concert in Austria and helping the U.S. government pick up a cartel leader in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Of course, Juan is right. This tension is real between protecting privacy of American citizens. But the authority is one that is powerful, and there are lawmakers on both sides of the aisle who want to get it passed.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hugh, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: I'd implore Senator Kennedy to go look at some of the warrants. I did FISA warrants for two attorney generals in the 80s. We have thousands of malicious, malevolent actors in the United States who are not American citizens. We have to keep track of them. You lose one of them, you lose lots of American lives.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Finally, you have these two congressmen, Juan, resigning, Eric Swalwell, Tony Gonzales. Swalwell, though, it was quick. The Democrats circled the wagons quickly. "New York Times", "Have these people learned nothing. I can't help but wonder how many Democrats in Congress and the California party had heard the whispers or otherwise had an inkling of this problem well before this scandal exploded. Wasn't there anyone in a position to at least stop him from strutting into a high profile race for governor?" They got him out quick.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: They got him out quick. But I've got to tell you, today in Washington among Democrats, there's a fever pitch that goes beyond the resignation to, what did you know and why didn't you say something? And is this now going to spread so that people who didn't would be held culpable and damaged? And it's a very interesting moment in Washington because we have lots of sex scandals going on around.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Year after year.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: But this one, for some reason, I think the Democrats sense could expose them and limit their liability, if you will, on some of the Epstein stuff, what's going on in the Labor Department and all that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: More accusers coming out, going public. We'll follow it all. Panel, thanks.\par \par \uc2 OK. It is time for "TikTok Tuesday". Pat writes, "I'd like to know if you think a deal will be reached with Iran." We're covering all sides of it, but I do hear in the administration both publicly and privately that they think they're a lot closer than they've let on as of yet. And so we'll see if these next talks get across the finish line.\par \par \uc2 O.J. wonders "What is your cheat meal?" Oh, man. You know what I gave up for Lent? I gave up cheese pizza, because I can look at a cheese pizza and gain weight. That's my cheese pizza love cheat meal.\par \par \uc2 Beth Yall asks, "How does Brit Hume really feel about Gutfeld impersonating him?" Here's Gutfeld.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS HOST: Roll it, please. This is at the Philly zoo, Dana. I'd like to go to Philly zoo with the lovely Mollie Hemmingway.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So I've talked to Brit about that. I wanted him to do a Greg Gutfeld impersonation. He didn't. He chuckles less these days.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2026 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020260415em4e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump Slack Lack Of Help From NATO On Iran; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Calls On GOP Senators To Pass SAVE Act; Talarico Stands By Comments: Dem: God Is Nonbinary, Six Genders, Trans Need Abortions; Obesity Is Not Health: Jillian Michaels Battles Body Positivity Activists; The Irish Goodbye: Experts: It May Not Be Bad Manners To Leave Abruptly\b0\par\par\uc2 Greg Gutfeld, Emily Compagno, Jessica Tarlov, Joey Jones, Dana  Perino \par \uc2 8,442 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 March 2026\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2026 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2026 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JACK BREWER, FORMER NFL PLAYER: I have seen no other way for us to overcome this social ill.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE JONES, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Jack, you're spot on. You need father in Christ, and you need fathers in the household as well. Thanks, brother. It's good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Thanks So, much for joining us. I'm Lawrence Jones, in for Will Cain. Catch me on "FOX & FRIENDS" tomorrow. 6:00 a.m. Eastern Time, here's THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hi, I'm Greg Gutfeld along with Emily Compagno, Jessica Tarlovski, Joey Jones, and she uses a four-leaf clover as an umbrella, Dana Perino. THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 Alert, President Trump going nuclear on \uc2 NATO\uc2 , metaphorically speaking, after they rebuffed his request to send some warships and secure the Strait of Hormuz. But he didn't need their stinking help anyways.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We don't need any help actually. In fact, we just put out a notice, I was watching over the last couple of weeks, and all of our \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies were very much in favor of what we did. They thought it was very important. We were just discussing it actually very important that we take out the nuclear threat from Iran, and we've done that very strongly, very powerfully.\par \par \uc2 So, everyone agrees with us, but they don't want to help. And we -- you know, we as the United States, have to remember that, because we think it's pretty shocking.\par \par \uc2 I think \uc2 NATO\uc2  is making a very foolish mistake.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: The IDF confirming that a top Iranian official who was openly taunting America just days ago got killed in an overnight strike. The president lamenting that everything is gone.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Everything is gone. Their leaders are gone. I guess one of the top person was they say, a lot of people say their actual top was killed yesterday, along with somebody else that who was responsible for the killing, the man that was responsible for the killing of 32,000 people over the last two weeks, he was in charge of the killing of protesters. It's an evil group.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Meanwhile, President Trump has Washington playing a guessing game after claiming that a former American president told him he wished he had taken action against Iran while he was in office. Was it \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: No.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Was it \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I don't want to say it. I don't want to say it because --\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : -- a member of a party -- a member of a party --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : -- they have Trump derangement syndrome and all but somebody that happens to like me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: All right, JJJ, \uc2 NATO\uc2  rebuffs Trump. Should we bomb NATO?\par \par \uc2 JOEY JONES, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: No, probably not. I'd like to hang on to our bonds for people who could actually hurt us, who have militaries and know how to use them.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Why do you think they did it?\par \par \uc2 JONES: So, I don't necessarily listen as an America First friendly person, I don't blame \uc2 NATO\uc2  for not wanting to get involved. That doesn't bother me. I also don't blame our president for telling them, hey, we've had your back for a long time. Look at all the money and resources we spent in Ukraine. We need a little bit of help right now.\par \par \uc2 But the problem is that little bit of help we need, we actually truly need. We don't have a way right now as it sits, and I'm not going to get into the specifics. We don't have a great capability to search for and defeat the mines, if the mines are there, and there's conflicting reporting on that. When you hear that anything passes through, then you know there's at least a navigable waterway, even if there are mines there.\par \par \uc2 We kind of, in January, we got rid of the boats that could do counter mine, and the ones that we're trying to bring over are unproven, and there's a lot of concerns about them.\par \par \uc2 So, the idea would be some of these countries that have this capability, this is the only thing we need from you, why don't you do it?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Excellent answer. You know, Dana, the -- obviously the Iranian military is in a shambles. The war is in what? How many days? 14 days, 19 days is actually going great. You know, it doesn't sound that way, because the media prefers to make it sound like it's a disaster, because that will help with the midterms.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Well, maybe, I don't even know if they think that far ahead.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I think they do, they're communist, Dana.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: OK, OK. So, a couple of things on that, militarily, looks like things going very well. Like when you wake up in the morning, you find out, oh, the next one's gone, and the next one looks like gone.\par \par \uc2 Larijani was a terrible person, and one of the people, as I understand it, who ordered the killing of over 30,000 people in January alone. Then you had the head of the Basij (ph) force also killed at the same time.\par \par \uc2 And there was a report that 300 of his people were also killed. So, Israel and the United States have exquisite intelligence inside the country, And so you can imagine that they feel rattled.\par \par \uc2 However, there is still support for the regime, and you see that when they come out into the square. I understand why the new Ayatollah V (ph) is actually alive, isn't coming out, because look what happened to him.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing is, when you look at the state run media of Al Jazeera, there was an op-ed that ran today, everybody was pointing to it because they -- comes out of Qatar. There's this piece that basically is like, militarily, this thing's going very, very well.\par \par \uc2 And so, if they are saying it, then you have to maybe take a pay attention to it, and also because it is state-run media, so, they're not doing without -- that without the blessing of the higher-ups who are saying you should do that.\par \par \uc2 On NATO, this is what I have. This is what I would say. Every country, including our own, has domestic political issues at home that they have to deal with. What are they dealing with in Europe? E are flat, and they have an immigration problem, and their far left is very anti-Israel.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Sounds like us, but we still help them.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, OK, but this is what I would do, President Trump's deal maker, I would say, no tariffs for us if we come in, no more tariffs, right? And he says, yes. And then they could -- they could take it back to their constituents and say, we're going to go, we're going to go help make sure that the oil prices stay down. We've got a better deal for our country going forward when it comes to tariffs. That's what I would do.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Well done. You know, Emily, it's -- the war is going so good that even Al Jazeera is calling it a triumph, and that's kind of like, kind of impressive. They can't even blame the Jews.\par \par \uc2 EMILY COMPAGNO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yes, I thought that that op-ed was phenomenal, because for someone like me who doesn't have an entire career and profession based around war and conflict, it made it really simple, right?\par \par \uc2 Mohammad Salun (ph) saying, look, when you look at this, this is a measured voice saying, you are watching the phases of a systematic degradation happening. And we see the ballistic missile arsenal, nuclear infrastructure, air defenses, Navy and the proxy command architecture. It's not one of U.S. failure, all of those things being degraded. It's one of systematic phased degradation of a threat that previous administrations allowed to grow for four decades.\par \par \uc2 And I think what we're seeing is a lot of fluff in that narrative, but when you just smooth away the feathers you see in these measured voices expectation that things are proceeding exactly as either hoped or planned for which both of which benefit the American people.\par \par \uc2 So, to that end, I think the inheritance that a lot of citizens accept, and clearly the Democrat Party of, well, it's quiet right now. Well, the volcano is latent. Well, they don't have the nuclear arms yet, so, it's nothing to see here.\par \par \uc2 We've seen -- and I think are have been subjected to a relay race of four and eight year increments, where at the end of the day, the threat was not the same. The entire time it was growing, we were looking the other way as an administration while the threat was allowed to foster and somehow that became acceptable.\par \par \uc2 And under whose watch then were they playing the Russian roulette of it actually coming to fruition, right? Sarah Adams has pointed out that in just the last few months, 3000 hardened ISIS fighters were released out of Syrian detention. Where are they all going? Exactly where they are supposed to. They are patient, they plan, and our homeland is still their target.\par \par \uc2 So, it's not about accepting a narrative of someone running for office right now, it's accepting the concept that this is our home, and our readiness and our ability to protect it is what's important.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Excellent point. Think I'm going to coin a phrase here, and said that the previous leaders kicked the can down the road.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That's interesting. That's an interesting saying.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: Brilliant.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Came up with it. So, Jessica, President Trump claims he talked to a former president who said that he wished he had done what Trump did. Do you have any insight into who he could have talked to?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Evidence would suggest since all of the living former presidents released statements or told news outlets that it wasn't them that 45 was talking to 47 about how well he was doing, nailed it.\par \par \uc2 So, I was -- I wasn't on yesterday, so, the weekend 