d Greg Gutfeld. It's 5:00 in New York City, and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 Fox News Alert day 12 in the desperate search for Nancy Guthrie. The Pima County Sheriff's Office begging the public to cough up any kind of video taken within a two mile radius of Nancy's house in the month leading up to her disappearance. TMZ has gotten their hands on a ring cam surveillance video showing a man five miles away from Nancy Guthrie's house the night she disappeared.\par \par \uc2 For more on that, let's go to Trace Gallagher in Tucson with the latest, Trace.\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: And Jesse, the reason the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is so interested in the video you just mentioned, obtained by TMZ, is because the person in that video was about the same timeline, right? He was also carrying a backpack that had reflective stripes on it, just like the backpack that the person had on on the doorstep of Nancy Guthrie's home.\par \par \uc2 But here's the problem with that. That video that we are looking at was taken at 1:52 in the morning, right? Now, remember, the Sheriff's Department says at 1:47 here at Nancy Guthrie's house, the Nest Cam was taken down. It was disabled.\par \par \uc2 So, if this five miles away video was taken at 1:52, that makes it impossible for this person to have literally come to Nancy's house at 1:47 and then made it back five miles away at 1:52.\par \par \uc2 But here's the other thing, TMZ is saying, that they have a law enforcement source that says the video we're watching of the guy who's trying to open the gate with the backpack -- wearing a backpack and holding one actually, the timeline is different. They believe that the video on the front porch of Nancy Guthrie's house was taken at 2:12.\par \par \uc2 If that's the case, then the person in the video that we're seeing today, the brand new video, would have had 18, 19 minutes to be able to get to Nancy Guthrie's house and then put the backpack on or give it to somebody else and that person to change clothes. Then it would be very relevant.\par \par \uc2 So, the bottom line here is, if this person is relevant to the case, either the sheriff's department is wrong, Jesse, or TMZ is wrong, and that's kind of how the timeline lays out.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Trace, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 And now out to the big news in Minnesota, Tom Homan announcing the end of Operation Metro Surge in the Twin Cities after rescuing missing kids and bagging thousands of bad hombres.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOM HOMAN, WHITE HOUSE BORDER CZAR: In addition to taking public safety threats off the street, ICE here in this state have located 3,364 missing unaccompanied alien children, children at the last administration lost and weren't even looking for them.\par \par \uc2 With that and success that has been made, arresting public safety threats and other priorities since this surge operation began, as well as the unprecedented levels of coordination we have obtained from state officials and local law enforcement. I have proposed and President Trump has concurred that this surge operation conclude, a significant drawdown has already been underway this week and will continue to the next week.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And America's sweetheart apparently has a sweetheart for some of those bad hombres. Katie Couric is saying no biggie to violent criminal migrants.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KATIE COURIC, JOURNALIST: Isn't all this talk about ridding the country of violent criminals a massive overstatement? It's less than 14 percent, again, of the 400,000 immigrants being arrested are -- had charges or convictions for violent criminal offenses.\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE) 14 percent, such a low percentage of 400,000 people.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If you're -- if your daughter gets raped by the guy that gets back out and he's one of the 14 percent, I don't think you're an equivalent 