ing we wanted -- total security, total access to everything.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: President Trump strikes a framework deal on Arctic security after weeks of uncertainty and saber-rattling, as the president's critics warn of long-term consequences for U.S.-European alliances.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D), CALIFORNIA: It takes decades and decades to build trust. It takes weeks, tweets, hours, minutes sometimes, to destroy it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: \uc2 U.N.\uc2  Ambassador Michael Waltz joins us with his insights, and reaction from Senator Jeanne Shaheen, the top Democrat on Senate Foreign Relations.\par \par \uc2 Plus -- snowmageddon. A massive storm impacting more than 200 million people from Kansas City to Boston. We're on the ground across the country.\par \par \uc2 All right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 We are following two major stories this Sunday.\par \par \uc2 The fallout after the deadly shooting of a Minneapolis protesters Saturday by federal agents, which has spurred more demonstrations and prompted Minnesota's governor to activate National Guard troops. We will go live on the streets there in just a minute.\par \par \uc2 And the massive winter storm that is pummeling half the country with heavy snow and ice now making its way up the East Coast.\par \par \uc2 FOX Weather's Katie Byrne is here outside our Washington bureau with very latest on the storm -- Katie.\par \par \uc2 KATIE BYRNE, FOX WEATHER CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Shannon. Well, we have at least three inches of snow on the ground in D.C., but that snow has changed over to sleet. So, we are being pelted with balls of ice.\par \par \uc2 Visibility is dropping at times, making it hard to even see the Capitol building behind me. And you can definitely see the streets are covered in snow. Plows have been trying to keep up with this, and as officials across the DMV region are urging people to stay off the roads through tomorrow morning. This is, of course, a massive system stretching across the country. It started out in the South, where it led to hundreds of thousands of power outages. In Texas, Louisiana, and Mississippi, many there waking up this morning still without power in bitter cold.\par \par \uc2 Then in Missouri, dangerous conditions triggering a major pileup on I-55, near the town of Marston. The Northeast is this storm's final stop with many here preparing to be stuck at home for days.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: People don't know how to handle like two inches of snow in the DMV area. And it's not that hard. It's really not. You just got to hold your wheels steady when you're driving if there's ice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BYRNE: After these conditions end, dangerous cold could freeze whatever is left over, weighing down tree limbs and power lines.\par \par \uc2 I'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, FOX Weather's Katie Bryne. Katie, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Okay, now to our other top story. We want to turn to FOX News correspondent Matt Finn in Minneapolis. He is following the aftermath of that deadly shooting yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Matt.\par \par \uc2 MATT FINN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 There's been weeks of unrest and chaos here in Minneapolis and now, the National Guard has been activated in certain neighborhoods. Minneapolis Mayor \uc2 Jacob Frey\uc2  says he requested the Guard from Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , saying his local law enforcement is stretched thin after federal agents shot 37- year-old Alex Pretti yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Here is some video from that tragic incident.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (WHISTLE SOUNDS)\par \par \uc2 (EXPLETIVES DELETED)\par \par \uc2 (GUNFIRE)\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What? (EXPLETIVE DELETED) What? What in the (EXPLETIVE DELETED) is wrong with you? What is wrong?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FINN: Alex Pretti's family writing, "We are heartbroken but also very angry. Alex wanted to make a difference in this world. Unfortunately, he will not be with us to see his impact.\par \par \uc2 The sickening lies told about our son by the administration are reprehensible and disgusting. Please get the truth out about our son. He was a good man. Thank you."\par \par \uc2 DHS says Pretti had a nine millimeter semi-automatic handgun on him and two full magazines when he approached agents yesterday. Officers tried to disarm Pretti, but they claim he violently resisted. An agent fearing for his life, ultimately shot Pretti.\par \par \uc2 President Trump writing, "This is the gunman's gun loaded with two additional full magazines and ready to go. What is that all about? Where are the local police? Why weren't they allowed to protect ICE officers? The mayor and the governor called them off?"\par \par \uc2 An outraged Governor Walz responding.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: I don't know how to say this in so many terms to watch the madness that we've witnessed over this last week coming out of the White House and what it's doing here. My confidence is and I say it to Minnesotans, as I've said, exercise your First Amendment rights, speak out, bear witness and document legally. But I understand the risk in that, and I understand the courage that it takes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FINN: The Trump administration has insisted for weeks that Governor Walz and Mayor Frey's anti-ICE rhetoric is boldening activists and protesters here. The DOJ even launching a probe into Walz and Frey for potentially impeding immigration enforcement operations.\par \par \uc2 Now, Democrats have warned they'll try to stop immigration enforcements by defunding DHS in the upcoming appropriations bill. Now, Senate Minority Leader Chuck Schumer says Dems will officially try to block funding to, quote, "rein in the abuses of ICE", potentially heading for another government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 Minnesota Democratic Senator Amy Klobuchar --\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. AMY KLOBUCHAR (D-MN): Right now, we are focused on getting ICE out of this state, and of course, we will use every lever that we have.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FINN: Now, the governor and police chief here say Alex Pretti had a legal license to carry. DHS says he did not have a readily accessible ID on him - - Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay, Matt Finn reporting from Minneapolis. We know you're going to stay there and follow this. Matt, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, former acting DHS Secretary Chad Wolf and FOX News contributor Jonathan Turley, because we have operational legal questions.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both for being here with us.\par \par \uc2 I want to start with you, Chad.\par \par \uc2 Would these officers have been wearing body cameras? Will we get access to that at some point?\par \par \uc2 CHAD WOLF, FORMER ACTING DHS SECRETARY: Well, the vast majority do not have body cameras. But as we know, there seems to be more and more video coming out.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: So, I don't think the lack of video will be the issue here. I think we need to let the investigation play out, right? Because the FBI, if they're leading the investigation, they'll dig into all the video that we've seen, but they'll also do a lot of eyewitness testimony. They'll also try to understand what happened before. What were the instructions the officers were giving to this gentleman?\par \par \uc2 And so, all of this provides context. What was the threat picture like? Did they understand?\par \par \uc2 Did he announce that he had a concealed weapon as he approached the officers? I assume he did not.\par \par \uc2 So, a lot of this is going to get taken into consideration as that investigation unfolds.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. And we were just talking literally just before I came to set, I saw a new video I hadn't seen before. I mean, they just continue to crop up which will be helpful for investigators.\par \par \uc2 I want to play something that Governor Walz said about this because there's been this talk that when state investigators showed up that they were blocked from gathering evidence and looking into this.\par \par \uc2 Here's what the governor said about the investigation moving forward.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Minnesota's justice system will have the last word on this. It must have the last word. As I told the White House in no uncertain terms this morning, the federal government cannot be trusted to lead this investigation. The state will handle it. Period.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, Jonathan, how does that showdown play out as we try to get answers?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, it's not true. I mean, this is unfortunately politics of the lowest kind and that the governor is saying something to the public that's simply not true. This is a shooting involving a federal officer. Federal government is allowed to conduct its own federal investigation. The state cannot dictate who or when that investigation occurs.\par \par \uc2 They can have a parallel investigation. That is certainly within their right and I can understand why they would want to do that.\par \par \uc2 But when he says that they and they alone will have the final word, it's not going to happen. And it's part of the complaint against the governor that he is out there fueling a lot of this rage and encouraging people to go out in the street to film these officers, which is fine if you do it from the sidewalk. In this case, you had someone who was impeding the officers, told to get out of the street, return to the street. And we have to drill down on what actually occurred in those final moments.\par \par \uc2 I -- they -- the legal standard here is clear. Did the officer act reasonably in these circumstances? The court does not second-guess split- second decisions.\par \par \uc2 But we need to know. We have to see his hands. We have to see what happened at that moment, right before an officer is heard saying "gun".\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Uh-huh. Yeah. And that's a critical point when that happens.\par \par \uc2 Staying with the legal beat on this. Local officials there also say they're going to go to federal court tomorrow to try to get this entire operation shut down, to try to get ICE blocked.\par \par \uc2 What do you think are the odds?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Zero. And you know, previously, you know, the governor and the attorney general went in to try to stop the feds from bringing in more personnel to investigate fraud. These have absolutely no constitutional foundation. They may have some political benefit, but they have no legal recourse here in limiting the federal government.\par \par \uc2 These are federal law enforcement officers. Despite what the governor said, where he told actual people to not treat them or refer to them as law enforcement, they are law enforcement officers, and they're allowed to enforce federal law.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, there's something that Secretary Noem said last night, if we could just work together. And she talked about a lot of jurisdictions where they are working -- fed, federal officers, ICE, Border Patrol with localities that do have Democratic leaders and that this is not the situation that we're seeing in Minneapolis, that they actually have, you know, deported tens of thousands of people through these targeted works.\par \par \uc2 Now, I want to play something that Bob Jacobson, he's the public safety commissioner there in Minnesota, what he said about working together with the feds. Here's what he said last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BOB JACOBSON, MINNESOTA DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC SAFETY COMMISSIONER: If there are violent offenders or violent criminals or violent crimes, we will investigate and we'll bring those to justice. If the federal government would like to work with us, to continue to work with us as they have in the past, to locate those violent offenders, we are always open to those discussions and always willing to assist.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. So, is something getting lost in translation here? Because the feds are saying the locals are not helping us going after these, and he says if it's a truly violent criminal versus just rounding people up because they're in the country illegally, they're willing to help.\par \par \uc2 Where has this broken down?\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Well, it'd be interesting to understand how -- what they are defining as violent, right?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: So, if you're a criminal illegal alien, that's who ICE is interested in. They lodge detainers. Many of those detainers are not honored by places like Minneapolis and others.\par \par \uc2 And so that's what ICE needs, which is the ability to go into those jail settings both there at the local level and throughout the state and to remove any criminal illegal alien that is in a jail setting. They can do that without going into the neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's very clear that both Minnesota or Minneapolis is not working with DHS and ICE in that way.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and how much of this leads up to this because it's something that the attorney general brought up yesterday is when they've operated as sanctuary cities and sanctuary states in the past, it means that there is going to be a concentration of people that ICE or CBP is going to be interested in removing.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Yeah, absolutely. Look, ICE goes through a targeting system to try to identify where are the criminal illegal aliens. And if you did a heat map, the vast majority are in these sanctuary cities, not all.\par \par \uc2 And I go back to your earlier point, like ICE does operations every day in places like Texas and Florida without any of this drama. And why is that? Because local law enforcement supports them, allows them to go into the jail, will go with them to provide that crowd control if they need it, if they have to go into a neighborhood to do a -- to do an operation.\par \par \uc2 None of that is at play in Minneapolis or a place like Portland or Chicago or anywhere else. And that's the issue.\par \par \uc2 On top of that, you had the mayor and the governor saying they're not law enforcement, saying that they're propaganda machines. They are incentivizing their residents to go out and do these actions against ICE. And that's where you have the issue.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and of course, Jonathan, it raises all these questions that we keep hearing about the Insurrection Act. People saying we haven't met those conditions in Minneapolis and Minnesota, but it's essentially, if state authorities and state courts are not able to happen -- to handle what is happening there, what's the legal path? Should the president pursue it or not?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Well, I'm hoping that the president will not have to use the Insurrection Act. If the question is whether he can, the answer I think most clearly is yes, he can. The act is generally worded. Courts generally don't second guess.\par \par \uc2 It was used during the civil rights period when southern governors and officials also oppose the enforcement of federal law, different federal laws. This situation could be analogized to that.\par \par \uc2 And what we're seeing in the city is this lack of local enforcement. You have ICE and customs having to do crowd control in addition to trying to carry out these missions. And when the locals did take over the crime scene, by the way, they quickly lost control and they had to pull out.\par \par \uc2 So, all of that certainly can support that type of invocation. The danger here is that it's going to escalate this even further in terms of the tensions.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, that is a very real danger at this point, and our men and women in uniform and people in these communities, it's very -- it feels very volatile for everybody involved. And the families and loved ones of Renee Good and Alex Pretti deserve answers. So hopefully, there will be transparency for exactly what happened just so people can have confidence in the folks who are there in uniform as well.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both very much.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Coming up, President Trump says he's got the framework for a deal on Greenland while making a move to find an end to the war in Ukraine this weekend. But up next, our Sunday panel reacts to this escalating problem. These tensions, the danger in Minneapolis.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): It's a campaign of organized brutality against the people of our state. And today that campaign claimed another life.\par \par \uc2 GREGORY BOVINO, BORDER PATROL CHIEF: The suspect also had two loaded magazines and no accessible I.D. This looks like a situation where an individual wanted to do maximum damage and massacre law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, very different takes on what went down yesterday. This is the third ICE-involved shooting sparking even more unrest in Minneapolis and questions about the political and legal impact of immigration operations in that city.\par \par \uc2 Time now to break it down with our Sunday group. Meaghan Mobbs, Director of the Independent Women's Center for American Safety and Security. Fox News Senior Political Analyst Juan Williams. Reince Priebus, former White House Chief of Staff and Fox News Political Analyst. And Hans Nichols, "Axios" Congressional Reporter.\par \par \uc2 It is good to see all of you safely here through Snowmageddon with us. We're grateful for that.\par \par \uc2 This yesterday has now reignited into yet another tragedy with another American citizen having lost their lives. Here's what the "Minneapolis Star Tribune" said about this. It said, instead of using technology to track down violent offenders, they appear to be door knocking random neighborhoods and swarming schools, healthcare facilities, Mexican restaurants, child care centers or gas station parking lots, all in hopes of finding someone, anyone to detain.\par \par \uc2 This columnist goes on to write, "This could have been an easy win for Secretary Noem, if it had been executed well."\par \par \uc2 But Reince, what do you make of their characterization there of what's happening?\par \par \uc2 REINCE PRIEBUS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I mean, part of the problem here is if you look at Minnesota, it's a pretty divided state, even though people think it's pretty democratic. You look at Texas, you look at Florida, pretty even states as well, obviously tilted a little bit toward Trump. And we don't have those problems.\par \par \uc2 And the problem is the politics of this rhetoric is becoming the politics of our streets. And you have a governor who refuses to do the most basic things, like give us the hundreds of people that are sitting in your jails that are -- that are felons, that are here illegally, offer that up. He didn't offer that up.\par \par \uc2 He's engaging in outrage, and it's -- it's going directly to our streets, and our politics are becoming our streets. And he needs to get -- go to the White House, talk to the president, talk to Susie Wiles, say, here's what I'm going to do for you. Here are the three things we're going to do to give you the win that you need so that we can de-escalate this situation. That's not happening.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and tons of reaction all across the political spectrum. One of these was something from Gavin Newsom on X, an account attributed to him. He says, "not the excuse to invoke the Insurrection Act the president wanted." He said it was a peaceful protest. He said, "So, today Trump made a shooting happen."\par \par \uc2 Does that help the conversation, Juan?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: I think that it's time to call out President Trump. I think you have to understand that people are scared in Minneapolis and nationally. We have mass protests in Minneapolis. They closed the schools. They closed the churches. They closed big businesses.\par \par \uc2 NBA games have been canceled. I think you have to understand that right now it's close to a super majority of Americans, not political partisanship, not political arguments. Sixty-one percent of Americans think ICE has gone too far, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 And I think a lot of this, you know, on a day like today, in the aftermath of the shooting that took place yesterday, you can get lost in the weeds about, well, was he doing this, was he doing that? So, much of this, it goes back to the shooting death of Renee Good.\par \par \uc2 When you see a single mom who's just dropped her child off at school, shot, and then the autopsy shows two of the shots came from the side. She was no threat to anyone. It seems to me, and there's no public investigation to this moment of Renee Good's death. I think you have to understand why people are so scared.\par \par \uc2 And then we had this week a visual of a five-year-old. He looked like Charlie Brown in Minneapolis there. He's got a little hat on, he's got bunny ears, and he just looks so scared.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, Juan, but to that point, there was a lot of disinformation and misinformation about that case. I mean --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No, no, you can go into the information --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- nobody wants to see a little boy scared like that.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: -- but I think you and I, human beings, understand a five-year- old.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: OK.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, absolutely. But the reporting was that his father ran from law enforcement and left him there. There's more to that story. Attempts to reconnect him with an adult inside the home who didn't want to do that was probably fearful of enforcement. But it's one of those things.\par \par \uc2 The "FoxNews.com" headline was "Media runs Wild With Egregious Lie That ICE Targeted Five-Year-Old in Minnesota. DHS Says Child Was Abandoned."\par \par \uc2 But again, this just adds more gasoline to the fire of this conversation about people being afraid and ICE being demonized.\par \par \uc2 MEAGHAN MOBBS, CENTER FOR AMERICAN SAFETY AND SECURITY: Yeah, I think that's exactly right, Shannon. I think what we're seeing, unfortunately, is that the rhetoric is too high on too many sides. Many of us correctly predicted that those who were saying interfere with ICE, get in the way, that this was only going to lead to more danger, more misfortune, and more tragedy.\par \par \uc2 And so, I really do think that there's a responsibility to tone down this rhetoric. I think there's a responsibility of media to accurately report what was happening because that disinformation fuels rage, it fuels anger, and then you have more people wanting to interfere with law enforcement and preventing them from doing their jobs.\par \par \uc2 I think what we're also missing here is ICE was there to do their job. Immigration enforcement is a federal responsibility. They were there purposefully to go after illegal aliens who are conducting criminal activity here in the U.S. And unfortunately, that narrative has spun out of control. And so, I think it's really incumbent upon all of our leaders right now to lower the temperature of this situation so that law enforcement can do their jobs and we can have appropriate investigations into what happened so Americans can have answers.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, they deserve answers. By the way, an investigation that's ongoing, continuing. A week ago, there were those folks who showed up in church. There was a protest there. There were all kinds of readouts on that as well.\par \par \uc2 But I want to play something from Harmeet Dhillon, heading up DOJ civil rights. There have been some arrests, but she says this is far from over, including with respect to Don Lemon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARMEET DHILLON, ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL FOR CIVIL RIGHTS: I intend to go after every single one of them. None of the people who invaded this house of God and harassed and frightened children and families is going to go unpunished.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Hans, there's a lot to unpack with this growing story.\par \par \uc2 HANS NICHOLS, AXIOS CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER: Yeah, look, I mean, we're talking about sort of responsibility to de-escalate. There may be or may not be. That's not my position to sort of adjudicate. What's clear is there's no incentive to de-escalate. And I think that's why where this story is going to go the next 24, 48 hours, maybe much longer, is that both sides are going to get inflamed.\par \par \uc2 On the Don Lemon question, you have the Department of Justice clearly indicating that they're going to at least push that a little bit further. On the more immediate question on the implications of what's happening in Minneapolis, behind us is the United States Capitol, there's very little incentive for lawmakers to compromise and to fund the government when the funding ends at the end of the month.\par \par \uc2 And it's so clear that this has inflamed the situation that we're going to be heading towards a shutdown. There may be some off-ramps, and it's just a partial shutdown. But it's just the nature of our politics, which I think all five of us at this table can agree, the incentive structure is to antagonize, it's to escalate, and that's not what the country needs.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Quickly.\par \par \uc2 PRIEBUS: Well, I mean, part of the problem is you can't de-escalate. It starts with a sanctuary state. It starts with a state that says we're not going to follow certain federal laws. It starts with a rebellious attitude toward the federal government and a rebellious governor who continues to fuel the fire.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I think that -- as I recall, our country was founded by people who rebelled against this kind of monarchical, you know, autocratic governance imposing themselves on us as American people.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Hang on a second. Let me just make this point to you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Make it quick.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: People have to understand, Minnesota's being targeted. This is not after -- by going after criminal immigrants. At this point, there's a surge --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, but --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: -- of people that are targeted by President Trump.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PRIEBUS: Why is there hundreds of felons being picked up?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Let's just say this, if you --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: They're not hundreds.\par \par \uc2 PRIEBUS: There's more than hundreds.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It is. It is.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: They are picking up people who are gardeners --\par \par \uc2 PRIEBUS: No.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: -- who are craftsmen.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, hold on, hold on.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: They are farmers. They are harassing people.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They -- if they're targeting the worst of the worst, we had -- you know, A.G. Pam Bondi talking about people sodomizing children and predators and those kinds of things.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Go get them.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Other people -- other people will be caught up in that. But that's the reality of what we're doing. If you don't like the laws, you don't like the power ICE has, you've got to change it because Congress passed those laws. They've 