th NATO Secretary General Mark Rutte in Davos. He also backed off his threat to take Greenland by force. Denmark's Prime Minister, Mette Frederiksen, says NATO has no authority to negotiate on her country's behalf or the autonomous territory within its kingdom. She refused to talk about Trump's proposed deal while on a visit here.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Madam Prime Minister, is the situation of President Trump been resolved?\par \par \uc2 Just 30 seconds, Madam Prime Minister.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Today, the U.S. Space Force operates a base in Greenland to detect a potential ballistic missile launch from Russia or other adversaries.\par \par \uc2 President Trump says he wants to expand America's footprint here and install the Golden Dome Missile Defense System to handle additional threats from drones, hypersonic and cruise missiles.\par \par \uc2 Would you welcome an increase of U.S. military forces here in Greenland and see the deployment of the Golden Dome?\par \par \uc2 MORTEN MADSEN, GREENLAND RESIDENT: I think -- I mean, we should -- everybody should hold on in the Western world to -- to increase security.\par \par \uc2 But I mean, it's up to the Greenlandic people to decide.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): In addition to Greenland, the president also announced his new Board of Peace to end conflicts around the world, including in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 While Trump invited a number of world leaders to join, he rescinded one to Canada after Prime Minister Mark Carney appeared to criticize Trump's approach to foreign policy, saying, rules and norms are increasingly ignored. Trump fired back.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Canada lives because of the United States. Remember that, Mark, the next time you make your statements.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: The President had similar words for the residents here, saying they also live free because of the protection of the U.S. military. Greenlanders say, their island is not for sale.\par \par \uc2 Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Lucas Tomlinson. Safe travels home, my friend. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, New Hampshire Democrat Jeanne Shaheen, ranking member on the Senate Foreign Relations Committee. Senator, welcome back to "Fox News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 SEN. JEANNE SHAHEEN (D-NH): Nice to be with you this morning.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, you were in Denmark. The president then said at this speech at Davos, we were not going to use any military incursion to take over any part of Greenland.\par \par \uc2 And he also said there's a framework that's come together. "The Hill's" headline says this, "Greenland deal triggers global sigh of relief." Are you breathing a sigh of relief?\par \par \uc2 SHAHEEN: Well, we had a bicameral bipartisan delegation in Denmark. We met with both Greenlandic and Danish officials, both prime ministers from Denmark and Greenland.\par \par \uc2 And what we heard very clearly from them is that whatever the United States is interested in doing on Greenland, they want to partner with us. We've had a treaty with Greenland since 1951. We used to have 17 bases there. The United States, after the Cold War, closed all but one of them. We are stronger if we work with our allies and partners.\par \par \uc2 And sadly, the president's rhetoric has raised real questions among our NATO partners about whether they can trust the United States. And that has long-term ramifications for our national security.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well --\par \par \uc2 SHAHEEN: What we heard in our briefing was that there is no immediate threat from China or Russia. But we need to work long-term to address the threat in the Arctic. And if we work with our allies and partners, we're going to be stronger, not weaker.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, it sounds like the President's argument is, you know, don't give that any time. Don't wait until Russia and China feel like a more immediate threat.\par \par \uc2 Here's what he says. We're going to have total access to Greenland. He says, we're going to be able to do whatever we want with the military. And they're going to start working on building that Golden Dome.\par \par \uc2 So, do you acknowledge there is, you know, short-term or long-term in need for that?\par \par \uc2 SHAHEEN: Well, certainly there's long-term need to work on security, particularly in the Arctic, and that's why Greenland's geographic location is so important.\par \par \uc2 But the reality is, if we undermine NATO, if we alienate our allies and partners in Europe and Canada, the only people who are celebrating that are Vladimir Putin in Russia and President Xi in China.\par \par \uc2 They want to separate the transatlantic alliance. They want to undermine NATO. And the president is playing right into those interests.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, to the point about Russia, I want to touch on that as well, because there have been these trilateral talks, the U.S., Russia, Ukraine, top officials, meeting this weekend.\par \par \uc2 Reuters quoted one U.S. officials saying, "We saw a lot of respect in the room between the parties because they were really looking to find solutions." We're told they're going to meet again next weekend.\par \par \uc2 Does this time feel any different to you? Do you think Putin has any intention of actually ending this war?\par \par \uc2 SHAHEEN: I don't. I think he continues to play for time. He continues to play the president like a fiddle.\par \par \uc2 If -- if we want to make Vladimir Putin really serious about coming to the negotiating table, then we need to be providing weapons to Ukraine, long- range weapons, and not just through the European partnership, but directly.\par \par \uc2 We need to provide support to Ukraine. And we need to put severe secondary sanctions on Russia so that we can stop their war machine.\par \par \uc2 And we have a bill in Congress that's been sitting there for a year now that has over 85 sponsors to put those secondary sanctions on, and the White House continues to oppose that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I mean, it felt like at one point the White House was moving in that direction. Do you think that stalled?\par \par \uc2 SHAHEEN: It sounds like it. Because that bill, if we could take that bill up this week and pass it with overwhelming bipartisan support, and we should do that.\par \par \uc2 We've had three bills come out of the Foreign Relations Committee with strong bipartisan support, one that would sanction China for its support for Russia's war machine, one that would go after Russia declaring Russia a terrorist state because of their kidnapping of Ukrainian children.\par \par \uc2 And the third, that would allow us to use those seized assets from Russia to support Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 You know, we never thought Ukraine would be able to continue this war in the way that they have, but they have fought courageously. They have stood up to Russia. And it's in our interest to support that effort.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, listen, if that legislation would -- if anything gets moved this week, I mean, we've got the weather situation, everybody trying to travel back in. The Senate has now got these --\par \par \uc2 SHAHEEN: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- funding bills that are sitting there.\par \par \uc2 After what played out in Minneapolis yesterday, you had a number of Democrats say no way that they're going to move to vote on any of that funding package if DHS, which is tucked into it, is part of that package.\par \par \uc2 Senate Minority Leader Chuck Schumer saying they will not use Senate Democrats provide the votes to proceed to the appropriations bill if the DHS funding bill is included, which it is at this point.\par \par \uc2 Now, you brokered the end, helped too, with the last shutdown. What do you think happens if you don't get those passed by Friday? We do go into a shutdown, but there are more and more of your Senate colleagues, Democrats, saying they will not vote to move it forward.\par \par \uc2 SHAHEEN: Well, people are outraged by what's happening in Minnesota and by the out of control officers, federal officers that they're seeing on the ground by this shooting yesterday. It is horrific.\par \par \uc2 What we need is an independent investigation. If this were a law enforcement officer in New Hampshire, that officer would be suspended and there would be an independent investigation that would be done of what happened. That needs to happen in Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 And I think there is reason why we need to raise concerns about what the Department of Homeland Security is doing and how their federal agents are operating.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, though we know the package of those House bills that sits there in the Senate, it's kind of an up or down thing. I mean, if you get to amendments, you're talking about kicking things back to the House, then back to the Senate, then to the president.\par \par \uc2 Doesn't seem likely any of that is going to happen. Certainly not all of it by Friday.\par \par \uc2 I want to read something from one of your GOP colleagues, Senator Graham, put on X this morning. He says, when you thought it couldn't get more chaotic surrounding the Democrats' obsession with maintaining sanctuary city policies, it just did. Democrats are now backing out of a bipartisan agreement to fund DHS, which makes sure our border is secure and our immigration laws are enforced as well.\par \par \uc2 Your response to him.\par \par \uc2 SHAHEEN: You know, Americans want the border to be secure, but they also, I think, at least my constituents, believe that we can have a secure border without having the kind of chaos that we're seeing in Minneapolis.\par \par \uc2 Look, we don't have any sanctuary cities in New Hampshire, and yet, we've seen ICE agents come into our state and arrest people.\par \par \uc2 I was at an event earlier this week where I heard from people who are concerned about taking their children to childcare because they're worried about what's going to happen. These are American citizens who are worried about this.\par \par \uc2 So, the situation needs to be deescalated and Senator Graham's rhetoric isn't helpful in doing that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Have you decided whether you're a yes or no vote to at least move this bill forward?\par \par \uc2 SHAHEEN: There is going to be a lot of discussion that needs to be made.\par \par \uc2 And -- and by the way, Speaker Johnson needs to bring the House back in. He needs to stop sending people home all the time when there's a tough issue that he has to deal with.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well --\par \par \uc2 SHAHEEN: Come back in. Let's sit down. Let's figure this out and let's get this done.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. The speaker will say the House has done its job. So, now we'll watch to see how this plays out.\par \par \uc2 SHAHEEN: Well --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- whether as a factor and all the other things that you discussed.\par \par \uc2 SHAHEEN: Yes. He -- he said that for a month back in September, too, when he just was AWOL and he's AWOL again.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, I'm sure he's going to wait to see what you guys do, and we'll follow it and track it as we inch closer to a potential another shutdown come next weekend.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thanks for being with us.\par \par \uc2 SHAHEEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Joining us now, the U.S. Ambassador to the U.N. Mike Waltz. Great to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 Let's start where we started with the senator, which is this issue of Greenland. I want to read you a couple of things.\par \par \uc2 "The Atlantic" says, "One senior European diplomat told us about a 'significant and probably irreversible rupture' between Europe and the U.S."\par \par \uc2 The headline from "The Hill", "Trump walks back Greenland threats, but Congress fears long-term NATO damage."\par \par \uc2 You heard what the senator said that she thinks there is long-term damage and that our NATO allies can't trust us anymore. Your response.\par \par \uc2 MIKE WALTZ, U.S. AMBASSADOR TO THE UNITED NATIONS: Well, thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 And just from a broader NATO standpoint, look at where NATO was the beginning of President Trump's first term with less than seven countries contributing to bare minimum.\par \par \uc2 Now, not only do you have everyone contributing to bare minimum, you have a number going to double that towards five percent of their GDP. NATO is far better for some of the tough love from -- from President Trump.\par \par \uc2 And look, I have to push back respectfully on this notion that the Arctic - - that there aren't imminent threats in the Arctic.\par \par \uc2 Russia is modernizing its nuclear program. It has nuclear powered cruise missiles now that they're testing to evade our radars. China is tripling the size of its nuclear arsenal.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, all of those assets, all of those missiles will fly over the Arctic if they were ever to attack the United States. President Trump isn't going to wait until they're right there on our doorstep.\par \par \uc2 So, what is he doing? He's putting in the Iron Dome. We have to have the latitude to do that.\par \par \uc2 Look at the shipping lanes. Russia, 60 icebreakers, China building its own fleet. What has President Trump done? He's now commissioned with Finland a plan to get a dozen of our own icebreakers up there.\par \par \uc2 How many heavy icebreakers from Denmark or from Canada, Shannon? Zero. Zero.\par \par \uc2 So, look, there is -- there is also Article Three of the NATO treaty that each member nation has a responsibility to defend its territory.\par \par \uc2 And the reality is, Denmark is a great ally in many ways, but they have ignored Greenland from a defense standpoint. And they have ignored the Greenlandic native, Inuit people.\par \par \uc2 Final point there. If you look in contrast to Alaska Natives, there's an Alaska sovereign wealth fund, Shannon