 life I've known a long time. He and his family escaped decades ago from Iran. He was so, you know, hopeful, optimistic, that something was going to change with regime change there. He's very disheartened about the thousands of people who've lost their lives.\par \par \uc2 "The Hill" has a piece written by an Iraq War veteran who says this, "We've spent billions to try to understand Iran so we could topple their regime. The opportunity was there and we might have just blown it because an empty threat resulted in a deadly reaction with no repercussions."\par \par \uc2 I know there's been talk of us moving military assets into that region. Is this the quiet before the storm? I mean what is our policy with Iran moving forward?\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Well, Shannon, I would never get ahead of the commander in chief in this regard. But I'll tell you what, this has turned out to be, and we're getting more information as the internet blackout and the jamming of Starlink -- what -- blackout has also occurred. This is a state-sponsored massacre. The U.N.'s own figures are saying now up to 18,000, although it looks like it may be far, far worse.\par \par \uc2 President Trump is going to take measured steps in the sense of making sure our bases are protected, making sure Israel is protected against any type of response. We have engaged with our allies and engaged with the region, and we're putting assets in the region for him to be able to make the absolute right decision. But at the end of the day, what is not going to change is the corruption, is the -- is the malpractice that has been the Iranian regime. And the second executive order that President Trump put in place was his maximum pressure. And we're -- as we see the Iranian economy continue to tank, I do not think the situation is going to get any better for the regime.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, let's hope it gets better for those people. As we know, we've watched many times in recent years for them to try to bring about change. Ambassador, always good to see you. Thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, coming up next, Vice President J.D. Vance looking to reassure pro-lifers at the March for Life in Washington this week. How the abortion debate is once again becoming a political flash point in this critical election year.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE, VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We're marching for life because we want our wonderful fellow Americans, even if they don't disagree with us, to know that life is worthy, it is worthy of protection.\par \par \uc2 Again, from the president of the United States, the vice president, you have an ally in the White House.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Vice President J.D. Vance addressing the annual March for Life here in Washington as some in the pro-life movement say the administration needs to do more on the issue.\par \par \uc2 We are back now with the panel.\par \par \uc2 OK, so these two things hit my inbox pretty quickly after that speech by the vice president. The first one was from the White House, saying, "President Trump is the most pro-life president in history," followed by something from SBA, the Susan B. Anthony List, Marjorie Dannenfelser, the president there of this pro-life group. She says, "it is because of the inaction of the Trump-Vance administration on abortion drugs that abortions are going up, not down." She notes that "Biden eliminated the in-person dispensing requirement for abortion drugs," that was during Covid, "flooding the market with mail-order abortions without any meaningful medical oversight. As a result, the Trump-Vance position of `back to the states' is being undermined every single day as abortion drugs flood illegally entered pro-life states."\par \par \uc2 Hans, my understanding is that there's real frustration with the administration, that they would be characterized as anything less than fully pro-life.\par \par \uc2 HANS NICHOLS, AXIOS CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER: It's a pretty hot quote that you have there. It's also, as a reporter, not a terribly difficult quote to get. When you call up someone in the anti-abortion movement and say, could you please give us an assessment on the Trump-Vance administration? They're always going to want more.\par \par \uc2 The problem and the real challenge, it's mostly a Vice President Vance challenge, is that he will be attacked or he will be forced from the right to be more pro-life. And that's going to come from someone called Josh Hawley, senator from Indiana, you're clearly -- excuse me, Missouri, you're clearly going to see an effort by Hawley to really seize this pro -- this anti-abortion movement, this pro-life movement and try to force this administration to be a little bit more active. He's making moves on it. This is a 2028 challenge for the vice president.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, well, Meaghan, they're pointing to things too, the pro-lifers, that the president has urged too much flexibility on the Hyde Amendment, which would keep federal taxpayer money from funding to any kind of abortions. They say, you know, there are all kinds of things that this administration could be doing and they feel like, you got our votes for these times we went and supported you and now you're not listening as much.\par \par \uc2 MOBBS: Well, but I think that what we're missing here is actually the administration is remarkably pro-family. And that's an inherently very, very positive thing. You do see the president and the vice president coming out being extraordinarily pro-life and championing that, but also understand the unique challenges that women face and championing things like in vitro fertilization. And so what I think we're missing when we have this debate is this administration is remarkably pro-family and pro-women for all of those reasons.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But, Juan, even some of the pro-life folks say they don't like the pro-IVF thing. That they -- you know, they worry about the destruction of embryos and they have a real moral and ethical issue with that.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Right. But I think President Trump is supportive of IVF. And he's equally supportive of his FDA in terms of approving a generic version of the anti-abortion pill.\par \par \uc2 So, to me --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: No, the abortion pill.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The abortion pill. I'm sorry, yes, the abortion pill.\par \par \uc2 So, I think from the pro-life, anti-abortion movement perspective, they've had tremendous wins under this president, but they don't see this as the finished issue. They would like a constitutional amendment to protect all forms of life beginning with conception. And they're just not going to get that. And I don't think the president is supportive of it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, that seems like a very heavy lift.\par \par \uc2 Reince.\par \par \uc2 PRIEBUS: Yes, I mean the idea that President Trump is not pro-life is outrageous. I mean he's pro-life. But -- without question. I mean Mexico City, fetal tissue, Supreme Court, my goodness.\par \par \uc2 I think the reality is, maybe this was something that in hindsight could have been done on day one on a -- on directing the FDA to look at it and take action. Now you're a few months from a midterm. Democrats are going to hammer you to death. What I would do is I'd say, Mr. President, why don't you call up SBA (ph) and pro-life and have a little private meeting and saying, after the midterms, I will direct the FDA to deal with this issue, but we're not going to talk about it right now. It is just too hot. I think the president is in favor of what they're asking for, but politically, at this point, you can't give that to the Democrats for a few months.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And history -- yes, history is not on the side of, we know, the party in power, the president in power. They've got a really heavy lift and a lot of challenges going into the midterms.\par \par \uc2 So, all right, panel, thank you very much. We will see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Well, this massive snowstorm pummeling much of the country and it is far from over. We've got a live report from one of the hardest hit areas and a look at the frigid days ahead that will complicate recovering from this. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Back to one of our top stories, the major winter storm affecting millions of Americans as it now begins to make its way up the East Coast after wreaking havoc in the heart of the country.\par \par \uc2 Let's go live to Greenville, South Carolina, and Fox News weather reporter Bob Van Dillen.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Bob.\par \par \uc2 BOB VAN DILLEN, FOX WEATHER CORRESPONDENT: Yes, good morning. Good Sunday morning to you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 I'll tell you, this storm, unfortunately, it came as predicted and it continues to gain some strength.\par \par \uc2 Now, we've already seen about three quarters of an inch of icing. That's freezing rain. In Nashville, Tennessee, power outages galore. And you hinted at it before. Once the power is out, the temperatures tonight are going to feel like the negative 20s in some of these spots. So you're talking about a windchill of about minus seven in Nashville itself.\par \par \uc2 Where is the snow going? Up into the mid-Atlantic right now. New York City, you're going to see about six to 10 inches of snow. Same with Philadelphia. But you're going to mix in with a little bit of sleet and freezing rain and it will buzz (ph) it down.\par \par \uc2 For you guys in Washington, about three to five inches of sleet and snow. It's really a winter mess. But Boston, the big winner in New England, Shannon. We're talking about over 20 inches worth of snowfall starting this afternoon, ending by tomorrow, tonight. This storm is stretching for 2,000 miles and, man, it has teeth.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. And like you said, it's the days to follow where, you know, a lot of folks, they're not going to get out of freezing.\par \par \uc2 VAN DILLEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And so everything that's falling now may be there for days and days to come.\par \par \uc2 VAN DILLEN: It's (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, Bob, we appreciate you being out there in the field for us. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 VAN DILLEN: Yes. You got it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, folks, bundle up. That is it for today. Thank you for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a safe week. We will see you next "FOX NEWS SUNDAY."\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2026 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020260125em1p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Major Winter Storm Could Affect More Than 240 Million; Crews Mobilize Ahead Of Massive Winter Storm; Officials Cannot Guarantee Help For Stranded Drivers; Trump Comment On Alliance Generates Backlash; Minnesota Protests Opposing ICE Operation; Housing Headaches: President Trump Takes Action To Help First-Time Buyers; California Charity Scam: Millions From Homeless Diverted In Alleged Fraud Scheme; \uc2 FBI\uc2  Arrests Wanted Olympic Athlete Turned Drug Dealer Ryan Wedding; Vice President J.D. Vance Speaks To Pro-Life Advocates In Washington D.C. Attending Annual March For Life; Details Of Framework For Giving U.S. More Control Over Parts Of Greenland Still Unclear; Democratic Lawmakers Criticize President Trump's Foreign Policy Approach To Greenland.\b