's a special treat for the storm.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: Frozen food is a great treat.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: See you guys.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Exclusive video of a major arrest in a multi-million-dollar taxpayer fraud scheme in California. We'll bring you there. A former Olympic athlete on the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is most wanted list is finally captured. And thousands of pro-life advocates gather in Washington for the annual March for Life.\par \par \uc2 But first, it's the biggest story anywhere. "FOX NEWS WEATHER ALERT", more than 240 million people are bracing for a major winter storm as it begins its trek across much of the U.S. People from Arizona to Maine are in the path of a potentially historic system that could extend over 2300 miles. Crippling snow, treacherous ice, expected to begin soon in many areas.\par \par \uc2 We have "FOX TEAM COVERAGE", David Spunt is at Reagan National Airport, and details on how government -- the government is coordinating responses, state, local, and federal.\par \par \uc2 But we begin with Fox weather meteorologist Mike Seidel in Tulsa, Oklahoma, with the latest. Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE SEIDEL, FOX WEATHER METEOROLOGIST: Good evening. Bret. A big change here in Tulsa. Yesterday, it was 57. Now, we dropped 35 degrees down into the low 20s, and the wind chill now in the single numbers, it's going to get much, much colder. Highs tomorrow, and the T is single numbers. 50 to 60 degrees below average, if you figure in the wind chill.\par \par \uc2 Let's start it off tonight with the watches and warnings. Not much has changed, except the warnings have been extended all the way up to Boston. A lot of real estate.\par \par \uc2 We have not seen a footprint for a storm like this in at least 15 years. It's either snow or ice. Hopefully, you get the snow. Ice is going to be destructive, and it's going to knock out power to hundreds of thousands of people.\par \par \uc2 Let's put that radar forecast in motion. Already, we've got snow north of us, across parts of the State of Kansas, around Wichita. Lubbock, Texas, has had sleet and freezing rain.\par \par \uc2 So, it's all getting underway. It's going to be heading in here in a few hours, and then, head east on Saturday and Saturday night, getting to New York by Sunday morning.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, as far as the precept goes, most of the ice is going to be across the deep south into the upstate of South Carolina and also the Carolinas.\par \par \uc2 Let's talk about the snow. A lot of snow. Here in Tulsa, we could break the all-time snow storm record of 14 inches. That's not out of the question. Big snows, a foot or more, extending from here across parts of the Ozarks, Cincinnati, Louisville, into west of D.C., New York City could get a foot of snow. How long has that been? What 2016? Even up to Boston.\par \par \uc2 So, a lot of snowfall, major impacts. Already. hundreds of flights have been canceled over the weekend. Tomorrow, Sunday, and many of those airports. So, check with your air carrier.\par \par \uc2 Now, more importantly, the ice. This will be a catastrophic ice storm. It's almost etched in stone. Some of these spots where we have the ice storm warnings could get as much as an inch of ice. That's going to knock out a lot of power. Trees coming down. Some of the hardest hit areas will be East Texas, the Piney Woods, Ole Miss, Oxford, Mississippi into Northern Alabama.\par \par \uc2 And then, I-85 from Atlanta, right up into Charlotte and the Carolinas. Atlanta, right on the edge, the northeast side of town may get a lot of ice. The airport may be in much, much better shape. We'll see how that plays out tomorrow night and Sunday.\par \par \uc2 And then, again, those warnings and watches continue. It's starting to precept now, Bret, back in Texas, and north of us in parts of Kansas. But the final flakes from the storm will not fall until Monday night over Cape Cod in Boston.\par \par \uc2 So, we have got a long way to go, a very tough weekend to get around anywhere in most of the eastern two-thirds of the U.S.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, countries watching. Mike Seidel with Fox Weather. Get the app, watch Fox Weather. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 Local, state, and federal officials are getting their personnel ready to deal with the effects of the storm. Many states have already issued emergency declarations. Officials are urging residents to stay home. For those who do not, delays on roads and airports could be long.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent, David Spunt has details from outside Reagan Washington National Airport. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you. Those delays and cancelations are piling up. Our latest number more than 3400 delays and more than 600 cancelations.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, officials from Maine, to Massachusetts, to Virginia, all the way down to Texas, are warning people on the roads to be careful.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I look for the fastest flight out and just try and get ahead of the storm.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): From the air --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Weather is -- can be unpredictable.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): -- to the ground. This weekend is shaping up to be a headache for 10s of millions in the storm's path.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JOHN STEIN (D-NC): That will lead to whatever slush that melted over the course of the previous day to turn into ice that night. And that's obviously incredibly problematic for traveling.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): The weather expected to jump around a foot of snow in the nation's capital.\par \par \uc2 MURIEL BOWSER, MAYOR OF WASHINGTON, DISTRICT OF COLUMBIA: Cold weather like this, mixed with precipitation puts people in a lot of danger.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): The federal government also coordinating closely with states.\par \par \uc2 KEN GRAHAM, DIRECTOR, NATIONAL WEATHER SERVICE: The big thing is, you know, stay home, have supplies and be ready.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Officials in Georgia, preparing for significant amounts of ice. But help may not be instant.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW KENT, COMMUNICATIONS MANAGER, GEORGIA POWER COMPANY: We want to make sure that everyone is being safe. If there are trees down along a road, if it's incredibly icy, we may not be able to go out.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): The storm, expected to affect millions all the way from Maine, south, to Texas.\par \par \uc2 KENNA MITCHELL, SPOKESPERSON, TEXAS DEPARTMENT OF TRANSPORTATION: With it being a sustained event, that doesn't mean that there might not be hazardous travel conditions throughout the duration of the storm.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Grocery store shelves in Tennessee emptying and quickly, and people are fueling up with propane in South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 ABIGAIL GOFORTH, EMPLOYEE, ACE HARDWARE: It's going to be the Black Friday of storm prep.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOFORTH: Over the course of the past two days, I think I have answered at least 350 phone calls about generators, propane, ice melts, gas cans.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Right now, 17 states plus the District of Columbia are under a state of emergency. That number could increase depending on the conditions. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 "BREAKING TONIGHT", President Trump's comment casting doubt on NATO's commitment to the U.S. if something happened, is prompting some pushback tonight overseas. This comes as the American leader and his Canadian counterpart spar over the U.S. effort to take control of at least part of Greenland.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House correspondent Peter Doocy has details tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 This is what people mean when they say the President Trump is creating a new world order if a U.S. ally, even a close ally, has a problem with a U.S. foreign policy proposal, President Trump says he doesn't care. Even when it's Canada.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We are going to build a Golden Dome.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): But the president says, "Canada is against the Golden Dome being built over Greenland, even though the Golden Dome would protect Canada. Instead, they voted in favor of doing business with China, who will eat them up within the first year."\par \par \uc2 That post follows a somewhat defiant response to recent Trump criticism of Canada from Prime Minister Mark Carney.\par \par \uc2 MARK CARNEY, PRIME MINISTER, CANADA: Canada doesn't live because of the United States. Canada thrives because we are Canadian.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): The commander in chief isn't just calling out Canada, the North Atlantic Treaty Organization NATO also on notice.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: That I know that we would have been there, or we would be there, you know, they will say they sent some troops to Afghanistan, or this or that. And they did. They stayed a little back, little off the front lines.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Democratic critics scoff at the president's claim.\par \par \uc2 REP. SARAH MCBRIDE (D-DE): He knows better. Denmark sacrificed more people per capita in Afghanistan after 9/11 than we did.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Now that the dust has settled after arresting dictator, Nicolas Maduro in Venezuela, the state department is talking about reports the White House next wants regime change in Cuba.\par \par \uc2 "It is our national security interests for Cuba, which is 90 miles away from our shores to be competently run by a democratic government, and refused to host our adversaries, military and intelligence services.\par \par \uc2 The most vexing foreign policy issue for this president in his second term, ending the war in Ukraine, which will require Russia to make concessions.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: And they will make concessions. Everybody is making concessions to get it done.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): A White House official says "Today's trilateral meeting in Abu Dhabi between the United States, Ukraine, and Russia was productive. Conversations will continue tomorrow."\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Anytime we meet is good. If you don't meet, nothing is going to happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): President Trump says there have been times when Putin wanted a deal, and there have been times when Zelenskyy wanted it -- didn't want a deal. Now, he thinks that both men want to make a deal, and he has got Steve Witkoff and Jared Kushner overseas trying to figure out what that's going to take. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy, live in the North Lawn. Peter, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Please join Peter for "THE SUNDAY BRIEFING". He will speak with Maryland Governor Wes Moore and other political leaders. It starts 11:00 a.m. Eastern Time.\par \par \uc2 We are learning new information tonight about the father, who federal authorities say abandoned his 5-year-old son while being pursued by immigration agents in Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 The picture of the boy has received international attention.\par \par \uc2 This comes as a major protest is underway right now in bitter weather conditions there. Correspondent Steve Harrigan is in Minneapolis again tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: ICE out! ICE out!\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Street demonstrations in 20 degrees below zero at a slower pace to avoid slips on the ice.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That will be what democracy looks like!\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: This is what democracy looks like!\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): The chance slightly distorted from frozen cheeks.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Immigrants are welcome here.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: Organizers here called for a day of truth. No shopping, no work, no school.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: ICE out! ICE out!\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: But the real battle for truth in Minneapolis is occurring online. Today's flash point, an image of a 5-year-old boy, returning from preschool in a bunny hat and a Spider Man backpack with his father, an illegal immigrant from Ecuador.\par \par \uc2 A school official not present at the scene, claimed the boy was used as bait by border agents to lure the father from the house. The claim was immediately amplified by the left. Kamala Harris posted in part, "Liam Ramos is just a baby. He should be at home with his family, not used as bait by ICE."\par \par \uc2 The family's attorney said they entered the U.S. lawfully using the Border Patrol's CBP One program.\par \par \uc2 MARC PROKOSCH, FAMILY ATTORNEY: And this family was not eluding ICE in any way. They were following all the established protocols, pursuing their claim for asylum.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): The Trump administration says the claims that the boy was used as bait are a vicious slander.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE, VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Think to myself, oh, my God, this is terrible. How did we arrest a 5-year-old? Well, I do a little bit more follow up research, and what I find is that the 5-year-old was not arrested. That his dad was an illegal alien.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (on camera): Border officials say the father and son are now together at a family detention center in Texas. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Steve Harrigan, live in Minneapolis. Steve, thanks, go get warm.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed today, following a week of terrorist -- tariff threats and cancelations. The Dow lost 285. The S&P 500 finished ahead two. The NASDAQ was up 65.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow was down a half a percentage point. The S&P 500 lost a third. The NASDAQ fell 0.06.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we will talk about the housing market and the overall U.S. economy with Charles Payne of Fox Business.\par \par \uc2 And later, a huge taxpayer fraud case in California and a major arrest has been made.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: America will not become a nation of renters. We're not going to do that. That's why I have signed an executive order banning large institutional investors from buying single family homes. I'm asking Congress to cap credit card interest rates at 10 percent for one year. And this will help millions of Americans save for a home.\par \par \uc2 Finally, I've instructed government-backed institutions to purchase up to $200 billion in mortgage bonds to bring down interest rates.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Trump taking steps to try to tackle a challenging housing market and make it easier for first-time buyers to purchase a home. Let's talk about that, plus the state of the U.S. economy and the trends we're seeing right now. Fox Business Anchor Charles Payne joins us tonight. Good evening, Charles.\par \par \uc2 You know, I want to start big picture.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK ANCHOR: Good evening.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: AP had this write up. Consumer spending pushes U.S. economy up 4.4 percent in the third quarter, fastest in two years. It's about 70 percent of the U.S. GDP grew at a healthy 3.5 percent pace. Surge in exports and a drop in imports also contributed to robust third quarter growth. Despite the strong growth numbers, many Americans are dissatisfied with the state of the economy, especially the high cost of living.\par \par \uc2 How do you see things as you see this growth and this push towards what -- this first quarter what it looks like?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, you know, we have the second and third quarter up more than four percent GDP. Right now, the fourth quarter is tracking five percent according to the Atlanta Fed. That's amazing momentum coming into a year where all the things from the Big Beautiful Bill kick in.\par \par \uc2 We know there'll be a massive tax refund. We know that businesses can start expensing major projects. Wages are anticipated to go much higher. So, you know, it's -- I wouldn't say Goldilocks because of the hole we're coming out of, but it's a lot better momentum than we've had for a very long time.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. What about these housing moves? You think they move the needle?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: they move the needle slightly. Here's the problem. This is a -- this worst affordability problem we've had ever. To put it in perspective, Bret, to get to 2009 affordability, three things could happen. Incomes can go up by 56 percent. Don't think that's going to happen anytime soon. Home prices can go down by 35 percent. Or mortgages can go to 2.65 percent.\par \par \uc2 So, you can see how daunting the task is. And the White House is trying their best to chip away at any aspect of it that they can. And the -- you know, listen, we need more homes have to be built. We hope mortgages start to come down. And the one thing that should help is basic economics. Home - - renting is so much cheaper in home ownership right now. Anytime this has happened in history, home prices have naturally come down.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. You know, the administration is trying to tackle the messaging of the economy, how to talk about it, talk about the affordability issue because they see the polls. Whether the president likes them or not, there are a lot of polls showing that people are dissatisfied about their current situation. Vice President Vance attempted that. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VANCE: The Democrats talk a lot about the affordability crisis in the United States of America. And yes, there is an affordability crisis, one created by Joe Biden's policies. You don't turn the Titanic around overnight. It takes time to fix what was broken.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Obviously, he's getting some grief because we know what happened to the Titanic there. But you look positively at what you see going forward.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: You know, and I think it's interesting because the timing might be perfect. If you look right now, the first and second quarters of this year are going to have so much frontloaded growth, so much wage increase. So today might have been a hint. We had a revision on the Michigan sentiment number. It came in much better than expected. Current conditions exploded. And by the way, all the subcategories politically, demographically, and age-wise, every single one went up. So, we hit some sort of a rock bottom situation, and we might start to really get a pretty good rebound as people start to feel this in their lives as the year goes on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Charles, we appreciate always you coming in. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: You got it. You got it. You, too. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, a major taxpayer fraud arrest in California. We'll bring you there. First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight.\par \par \uc2 Fox 59 in Indianapolis with details on the shooting of a county judge earlier this month. Court documents reveal Steven Meyer was attacked in his home before he was scheduled to preside over a trial involving a high- ranking gang member. Police say five people have been charged in connection with that shooting.\par \par \uc2 Fox 5 in New York with an evidentiary hearing in the case of the man charged with gunning down a health insurance executive in late 2024. Attorneys for Luigi Mangione want some evidence excluded. Mangione is accused of murdering United Healthcare CEO Brian Thompson in Manhattan. He has pleaded not guilty.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Tampa from Fox 13. One of the big stories there tonight. The Children's Gasparilla Parade takes place tomorrow downtown. It should be good weather. The event began in 1947. It features a stunning display of expertly designed pirate-themed parade floats, crews, marching bands, dance units, and community groups. That's tomorrow in Tampa. That's tonight's live look outside the beltway tonight for SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: South Carolina officials are reporting a surge to 700 measles cases. That includes 54 additional infections since Tuesday. Authorities are warning the widening outbreak could last weeks or months amid lagging vaccine uptake.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, exclusive video of a major arrest in what authorities say was a huge taxpayer fraud scheme in California. Prosecutors alleged the suspect diverted the funds to bankroll a lavish lifestyle, including a multi-million dollar home. Correspondent Matt Finn has details tonight from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MATT FINN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Exclusive video of the Fed's early morning bust of a California man prosecutors say brazenly misused up to $23 million state taxpayer dollars meant for the homeless to piggy bank a lavish lifestyle including a $7 million Los Angeles mansion, a second home in Greece, Hermes clothing, and an estimated $125,000 luxury SUV.\par \par \uc2 BEN ESSAYLI, ASSISTANT U.S. ATTORNEY: People are literally dying and this guy is out vacationing, buying homes, buying Range Rovers, and going shopping.\par \par \uc2 FINN (voice-over): Federal prosecutors charged 42-year-old Alexander Soofer with wire fraud, saying contracted with LA city and county to house and feed 600 homeless people, providing them with three nourishing meals a day. But during one inspection by local officials, they say Soofer had only canned beans and ramen noodles at one of his facilities. And he was unable to produce legitimate documents to show he was housing and feeding the 600 people he was contracted for.\par \par \uc2 LA County District Attorney Nathan Hochman's office has been running a parallel investigation and has also filed 11 state charges.\par \par \uc2 NATHAN HOCHMAN, DISTRICT ATTORNEY, LA COUNTY: My message again to the Mr. Soofers of the world. The people who've illegally gone ahead and defrauded the homeless programs out of either dollars, hundreds of thousands, or millions of dollars. Stop.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FINN (voice-over): First Assistant U.S. Attorney Bill Essayli says his office is investigating two dozen similar homeless fraud cases in California, where billions of homeless dollars have been spent.\par \par \uc2 ESSAYLI: California was pushing this money out quickly. A lot of money went out the door with frankly very little vetting, very little checks and balances. And he's one of the individuals that got it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FINN (on camera): And that fraud suspect faces up to 20 years in federal prison if convicted, 17 years if convicted on the state charges here in California. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Matt Finn in from Los Angeles. Matt, thanks.\par \par \uc2 A former Olympic athlete is in custody tonight after more than 10 years on the lam. Ryan Wedding is accused of running a violent narcotics organization. Senior correspondent Alicia Acuna has the latest tonight from Denver.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 KASH PATEL, FBI DIRECTOR: We are announcing the capture of another FBI's most wanted top 10 fugitive, Ryan Wedding. That makes six top 10 FBI captures in one year alone.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ACUNA, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Following what sources tell FOX was a negotiated surrender, former Canadian Olympian turned accused violent drug kingpin Ryan Wedding is now in U.S. custody. After more than a decade on the run and with a $15 million bounty on his head, FOX News is told Wedding was under so much pressure he could no longer go on. FBI director Kash Patel was in Mexico City yesterday for the arrest of the top 10 FBI fugitive.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: He went from an Olympic snowboarder to the largest narcotrafficker in modern times. He is a modern day El Chapo. He is a moder day Pablo Escobar.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: Wedding's fall from grace came after representing Canada in the 2002 Winter Olympics. Investigators say he ran a transnational drug trafficking operation with the Sinaloa cartel, moving hundreds of kilos of cocaine through Colombia, Mexico, Canada, and the U.S., allegedly using Los Angeles as his distribution point since 2011.\par \par \uc2 JIM MCDONNELL, LOS ANGELES POLICE CHIEF: Our collective efforts led to the seizure of more than 2,300 kilograms of cocaine, 44 kilograms of methamphetamine, 44 kilograms of fentanyl, eight firearms, and over $55 million in illicit assets.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: Known as "El Jefe" and "Public Enemy," the 44-year-old is also accused of ordering dozens of murders, including the killing of a federal witness.\par \par \uc2 AKIL DAVIS, FBI ASSISTANT DIRECTOR: Ryan Wedding tormented several people and several families that will never be the same. But today, they get the justice that they sought.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 ACUNA (on camera): And Wedding wasn't the only top 10 most wanted fugitive detained today. Director Patel flew from his news conference in California to North Carolina, to announce the capture of Alejandro Castillo, also in Mexico. Castillo was brought back to the United States to face murder charges from 2016. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alicia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, pro-life advocates gather in Washington for the annual March for Life. We'll tell you there.\par \par \uc2 And later, a live report from Greenland with the latest on President Trump's effort to gain some control of at least part of that island.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A live look at Washington there. Thousands of pro-life advocates in Washington today for the annual March for Life. Vice President J.D. Vance and House Speaker Mike Johnson were the headliners. Correspondent Alexandra Hoff shows us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CROWD: We are the pro-life generation!\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: The mood celebratory and yet complicated as bundled pro-life advocates from across the country return to the National Mall for the 53rd annual March for Life. Vice President Vance serving as featured speaker for the second consecutive year, fresh off the announcement that he and the second lady are expecting their fourth child, a baby boy, in July.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE, (R) VICE PRESIDENT: In my remarks last year, I told you all that one of the things I most wanted in the United States of America was more families and more babies. So let the record show, you have a vice president who practices what he preaches.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: In a pre-recorded message, President Trump highlighted how six years ago he became the first president to attend the march in person.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP, (R) U.S. PRESIDENT: Since then we have made unprecedented strides to protect innocent life and support the institution of the family.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: The one-year mark of President Trump's second term has brought much to celebrate for attendees. The administration's efforts to defund Planned Parenthood, the pardoning of pro-life protestors, and the reversal of several Biden era abortion policies. That's on top of the credit given to President Trump for reshaping the Supreme Court, leading to the overturning of Roe v. Wade in 2022. But the call to march to Capitol Hill remains, according to organizers, who are disappointed by the continued availability of abortion drugs like mifepristone. Concern was also sparked by a comment the president made two weeks ago regarding healthcare negotiations.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: You have to be a little flexible on Hyde.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: The Hyde Amendment bars the use of most federal funds for abortions. Language regarding Hyde has slowed progress in Senate talks and has offered for fuel for marchers to press on.\par \par \uc2 At the national mall, Alexandria Hoff, FOX News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A federal judge says the Trump administration must keep federal funds flowing to childcare subsidies and other social service programs in five Democratic-controlled states, at least for now. The ruling extends by two weeks a temporary one issued earlier this month that blocked the federal government from holding back the money from California, Colorado, Illinois, Minnesota, and New York. The administration says it wants the hold because the states are granting benefits to illegal immigrants.\par \par \uc2 Secretary of State Marco Rubio says Haiti's transitional presidential council must be dissolved by February 7th. Rubio is backing Haiti's current prime minister amid ongoing gang violence on the island. A member of the country's transitional council announced today that a majority of the panel has voted to fire the prime minister.\par \par \uc2 Greenland's prime minister says he still has no details on what President Trump calls a framework for giving the U.S. more control over at least some part of the Danish territory. President Trump saying he expects to close that deal within two weeks. Correspondent Lucas Tomlinson is again in Nuuk, Greenland, tonight.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Madam Prime Minister, has the situation of President Trump been resolved, just 30 seconds, Madam Prime Minister?\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Denmark's prime minister arrived here if Greenland today said in show of support to the large self-governing island under its realm. But she did not want to answer questions about President Trump's proposed deal. Ahead of her trip here, the Danish leader said NATO has no authority to negotiate on her country's behalf in an apparent rebuke of Trump's deal brokered with the help of NATO Secretary General Mark Rutte.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP, (R) U.S. PRESIDENT: We're all going to work together, and actually NATO is going to be involved with us. We're going to be doing it in conjunction, parts of it in conjunction with NATO, which is really the way it should be.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: American officials say negotiations continue between the U.S. and Denmark. The details remain unclear. Greenland's prime minister says the island is not for sale.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Sovereignty is a red line.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: The people here sound relieved that the U.S. will not use force to take the island but remain skeptical about any proposed deal.\par \par \uc2 You think the situation with President Trump has been resolved?\par \par \uc2 MORTEN MADSEN, GREENLAND RESIDENT: I haven't got a clue. I'm happy about what happened in the last days. There is a hope that the dialogue can start. And we don't need to have a discussion on social media.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: President Trump says he'll work out an agreement over the next two weeks to make a deal with Greenland. Do you feel confident that things will be resolved?\par \par \uc2 MIE LIND, GREENLAND RESIDENT: No, not really.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Why not?\par \par \uc2 LIND: Because he changes his mind all the time, so, you can't be sure of anything with him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): Other Greenlanders we tried to speak with didn't want to talk and simply wanted to be left alone. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Lucas Tomlinson live in Greenland tonight. Lucas, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on this week's foreign policy moves by President Trump, plus Winners and Losers.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. Syrian government forces take control of a prison housing members of the Islamic State group in the north of the country. This comes after hundreds of Kurdish fighters who were guarding the facility evacuated the area as part of a recent deal. The leader of the U.S. Central Command has taken a last-minute trip to the Middle East to discuss the crisis in Syria.\par \par \uc2 Global fashion celebrities attend the funeral for iconic designer Valentino in Rome. Top fashion names including designers Tom Ford, Donatella Versace, along with longtime Vogue magazine powerhouse Anna Wintour were at the service along with Hollywood stars such as Anne Hathaway.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Taiwan, courtesy of Earth Cam. One of the big stories there in the Indo-Pacific tonight, the Japanese Prime Minister dissolves the lower house of parliament, paving the way for an early election February 8th. The move is an attempt to capitalize on her popularity to help the governing party regain ground after major losses in recent years.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP, (R) U.S. PRESIDENT: The time limit is infinity, meaning there is no time limit. We can do anything we want. We can do military, we can do anything we want. And it's being negotiated, and let's see what happens. I think it will be good.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Is Denmark on board with this concept?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I think everyone likes it. I think. I'll let you know in about two weeks.\par \par \uc2 LARS LOKKE RASMUSSEN, DANISH FOREIGN MINISTER (through translator): What's needed now is to take the drama out of this. We need to get away from complaining, away from the breaking news flashes. We need to get away from the fact that every time someone says something, we have to comment on it and escalate the situation. Now we need to have a calm process.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A calm process. That's the Danish foreign minister. He's been on SPECIAL REPORT. As we're talking about Greenland, obviously it took over a lot of the conversation at Davos, everything the president said, and now this framework going forward. We'll start there, a host of foreign policy issues as well as domestic.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Trey Gowdy, former Congressman from South Carolina, host of "Sunday Night in America with Trey Gowdy", Leslie Marshall, FOX News contributor, and Matthew Continetti, scholar at the American Enterprise Institute and "Wall Street Journal" opinion columnist.\par \par \uc2 Matthew, what's your take on the Greenland situation? We think we know some details, or how the framework is looking. The president seems optimistic, as does the NATO secretary general.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI, AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE: That's right. And I think we've started on a two-track process, Bret. We have the NATO track, which is what President Trump and Secretary General Rutte came to an agreement over.\par \par \uc2 But then we also have the separate track with Denmark, which was started by Vice President Vance and Marco Rubio meeting with the Danish representatives last week. And I think it's important to keep those negotiations going on.\par \par \uc2 You know, President Trump drives change not through consensus. He drives it through conflict. And what that means is there are a lot of people with kind of -- who get rubbed the wrong way. They have bad emotions, but the change happens. That's what we saw with NATO spending in the first term. I think that's what we're going to see with an expanded U.S. and NATO presence in Greenland in the second term.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Trey, it wasn't just foreign ministers in Davos. There were Democratic lawmakers who were very critical of this whole effort about Greenland. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. SARAH MCBRIDE, (D-DE) HOUSE FOREIGN AFFAIRS COMMITTEE: In the context of Denmark and Greenland, it is trying to fundamentally remake the global order into one that is no longer rules-based, no longer respects sovereignty, but is essentially a might makes right in my neighborhood perspective. It's a philosophy that he shares with Vladimir Putin.\par \par \uc2 REP. MADELEINE DEAN, (D-PA) HOUSE FOREIGN AFFAIRS COMMITTEE: What he got in this framework of a deal is nothing. These are things that Denmark had asked all along.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What do you make of that, Trey?\par \par \uc2 TREY GOWDY, FORMER SOUTH CAROLINA REPRESENTATIVE: I don't think he's the first U.S. president who has kind of eyed longingly Greenland. It is strategically significant from a national security standpoint. Having said that, he has a slightly different communication style than you or I might have, Bret, but people knew that when he was elected. I'm going to bet that Leslie picked him as her winner of the week because he avoided war with Denmark and Greenland this week.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So we're going to save the winner, Leslie. You don't have to do that but what's your take on all of this and the fallout from Davos?\par \par \uc2 LESLIE MARSHALL, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Definitely Trey and I won't be dating or getting married any time soon, because he doesn't know me at all.\par \par \uc2 No. I'm going to add another word that the president uses to get people to the table, and that's chaos. And it's perceived by not just our allies but certainly in this case the people and the politicians and leaders of Greenland and also the kingdom of Denmark as bullying. And that's not the way to go about this.\par \par \uc2 Look, today the prime minister of Greenland was adamant. He said this doesn't look like a concrete deal, one. He also said that only, not NATO, but only Greenland and the Kingdom of Denmark will be able to make this decision as to what happens, if anything happens, going forward regarding a deal, whether it's, you know, mining or in the best interest of national security for the United States. Remember, Greenland and the Kingdom of Denmark are looking at what's in the best interest of their national security and what's going to benefit their citizens, not Americans.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, and Matthew, you've written about this, that Trump stirs the pot. He pushes international boundaries. He pushes institutional boundaries. He also did it when he was talking about NATO, and whether they are going to come to U.S. aid. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP, (R) U.S. PRESIDENT: But the problem is NATO is that we'll be there for them 100 percent, but I'm not sure that they would be there for us.\par \par \uc2 KEIR STARMER, BRITISH PRIME MINISTER: Let me start by paying tribute to 457 of our armed services who lost their lives in Afghanistan. I consider President Trump's remarks to be insulting and, frankly, appalling. And I'm not surprised that they've caused such hurt of loved ones who were killed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The U.K. prime minister, others piping up after that comment about NATO.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: Yes, I think it's important we recognize NATO is the most successful alliance in the history of the human species. It has prevented a great war break out on European conflict now for 80-some years, and that is a remarkable achievement. And, as the prime minister suggested, NATO came to our aid after 9/11, and NATO soldiers fought and died with American soldiers in Iraq and Afghanistan. NATO is worth saving. But that also means that we have to strengthen it. And you can either do that by trying to plead with the European powers to spend more on defense, which has not worked in the past, or you can kind of use the kind of broad-shouldered tactics, as Vice President Pence used to say, and that has proven to show results.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to turn to domestic. We talked about the economy, Trey, with Charles Payne earlier. Today is the March for Life in Washington D.C. This issue, obviously, comes up in every election that we deal with, and as we look at the video of folks who came out in brutal temperatures ahead of a snowstorm, they are still making a point. The Associated Press on its poll of states, should they allow abortion, overall, 2025, 52 percent. And that tracks from where it was, down from 24, but up from 21. And you can see Republicans and Democrats, and where those numbers are. What do you think about this issue, as we get ready for the midterm election and where it stands?\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: Most divisive issue of our lifetime, Bret. I don't know that that many minds have been changed. If they are going to be changed, it's going to be because of technology, or because of religion. Look, conservatives wanted this issue returned to the states. They got what they wanted in the Dobbs decision. But in most states, most states, I think efforts statewide to ban abortion have not been successful.\par \par \uc2 And I can tell you, in my own state, anyone who thinks a woman should get the death penalty, which we had lawmaker who thought that, you've got to find another political party. There is no public consensus for that at all. Look, the states wanted it. They got it. That's what Republicans want. Now 50 laboratories have to deal with it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here is the vice president, J.D. Vance, at the event.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE, (R) VICE PRESIDENT: Because with the Dobbs decision, what we did, what the president did, what the Supreme Court did, was put a definitive end to the tyranny of judicial rule on the question of human life.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 VANCE: He shattered a 50-year culture of disposability, one that treated human life as expendable the moment that it became inconvenient.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Leslie, is it possible that this midterm in 26 is not going to be focused on these big races about the issue of abortion? That it is going to be about affordability and about what people feel about the economy? Maybe it's going to be a little bit about foreign policy thought process, but that the issue of abortion is becoming less of an issue, do you think that's possible?\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: Yes. And, you know, sorry, women in sports, who is using what bathroom, defund the police, whether left or right, these were not winning policies, and this is not the present. The vice president is talking about the past, and the present is all about, the present is all about affordability. It's all about the economy. And it's all about that message that has Democrats in my party flipping seats state by state and winning. And they will continue to do so until that great tsunami comes in November when the House flips blue.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Wow, that was very forceful.\par \par \uc2 Let's do some Winners and Losers. Matthew, first, winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: My winner is easy. That's Indiana quarterback Fernando Mendoza. Undefeated season, Heisman Trophy, national championship with an incredible play there getting the touchdown, and now declaring for the NFL draft. And I'll call it here first -- the frontrunner for the Republican nomination in 2044.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: We'll play Candidate Casino.\par \par \uc2 And my loser is the Washington Nationals fans like myself. We traded our ace Mckenzie Gore for some prospects. When will we build a team that stars again?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, we've got to leave it there. We talked too much. I owe you Trey and Leslie. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right, that sound means it's Friday. Time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP, (R) U.S. PRESIDENT: these are the accomplishments. I could stand here and read it for week and we wouldn't be finished.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I wonder how we are being viewed.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are reaching a time of instability.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. Trump, -- off.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I don't have to use force. I don't want to use force. I won't use force.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Is it still under the Kingdom of Denmark in this framework deal?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That issue did not come up anymore in my conversations to the president.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: What we're asking for is to get Greenland, including right title and ownership. So we want a piece of ice for world protection. It's very expensive. It's a very big piece of ice. It's already cost us a lot of money.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A little piece of Greenland, a little piece of --\par \par \uc2 What we want is peace.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: We've an incredible year economically.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT BESSENT, TREASURY SECRETARY: Governor Newsom, who strikes me as Patrick Bateman meets Sparkle Beach Ken.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM, (D) CALIFORNIA: It was as if he was reading a diary and had just broken up with someone.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Restoring law and order.\par \par \uc2 End of Joe Biden's weaponization of our government.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm proud of the work my team did.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The only person who should be held in contempt right now is Pam Bondi.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Nobody on our side is defending Pam Bondi.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Pam Bondi is actually producing documents.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I'm glad my finger wasn't in that. But I would not have shown the pain.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's one week, that was a busy week of news.\par \par \uc2 Monday on SPECIAL REPORT, the Senate is on the clock, with just a few days left to pass six remaining government-funding bills and try to avoid another government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 Please join Shannon Bream on FOX NEWS SUNDAY. She'll talk to the U.S. ambassador to the United Nations, Mike Waltz, and Senator Jeanne Shaheen of the Foreign Relations Committee.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast. You can follow me on TikTok, X, and Instagram @BretBaier for exclusive behind the scenes content.\par \par \uc2 Be careful out there this weekend, that snowstorm, the winter weather. Just be careful.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. Make it a great weekend. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE starts in eight seconds.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2026 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020260124em1n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Liberal Press Fires Up The Pessimism Train; Trump Is Impatient For Solutions; The Left Can't Stand The White House Baby Boom; Massive Weather Storm Threatens Half Of The U.S.; At Least 17 States Declare State Of Emergency; The Left's Psychotic Break With Reality; Liberals Spew Open Threats Against ICE; Tennis Star Dismiss Reporter's Cynical Question; Jane Fonda Gets Hysterical On Late Night; Hotels Are Ditching Bathroom Doors; Ms. Rachel Cries Over Liking Anti-Semitic Comment; Mamdani Announces Free Childcare For Illegals\b0\par\par\uc2 Laura Ingraham, Ari Sarsalari, Raymond Arroyo, Kevin Corke \par \uc2 7,338 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 January 2026\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this Special Report. Fair, balanced and still unafraid. Make it a great weekend. The Ingraham Angle starts in eight seconds.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening, everyone. I'm Laura Ingraham. This is The Ingraham Angle. Thank you for joining us tonight. We have a Fox News alert. 200 million Americans are getting ready. They're bracing for what could be a historic winter storm, bringing heavy snow, lots of ice and frigid temperatures to half the country. We're going to be monitoring it and bringing you all the important updates as we get them in.\par \par \uc2 But first, operation optimism, that's the focus of tonight's Angle. Oh, the breathless headlines about the Trump administration at Davos. They're all so predictable. How dare he claim any strategic imperative in Greenland, Trump created a fraying world order, says NPR. He's sewing trade chaos claims \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  by ripping up the international order of course. Then Reuters warned of confusion caused by abrupt policy shifts and rapid reversals. Of course, if Trump didn't have a nimble approach to Greenland, they would have hammered him for that too.\par \par \uc2 But my personal favorite was accusing Trump of, quote, chaotic cruelty. How silly. Of course, what they call chaotic is actually dynamic. So contrary to the stale stereotype, Trump is the opposite of rigid and doctrinaire, and how he approaches negotiations and keeping our adversaries our allies, even guessing is often the best way to get the result that's the best one for the American people.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , if you know him, if you've watched him for a decade, and I've known him for almost 25 years, he never, ever takes options off the table, at least until it's absolutely necessary to do so. Now, why does he 