in it for the rest of -- the rest of them?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, we have a system of laws, and they exist for a reason. Jen, I understand your family wants to take legal action against that trucking company that hired this illegal who killed your brother, who is now out on bond, but you're running into some problems with that, tell us quickly.\par \par \uc2 JENSEN: So, regarding the trucking company, they do have a lawyer on it where they're running into problems is, is suing Washington, suing California, the company, yes, that issued the CDL. That's where they're running into problems of finding a lawyer that's willing to take the case and wanting to put up a fight, because there's so many layers to this and that it's not, unfortunately, this -- this clean case, that people that it seems like --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But Jen, hold on, Jen, Jen, I want to understand, I'm sorry to interrupt, but are you saying your family is unable to get legal representation in this case?\par \par \uc2 JENSEN: Correct. They've reached out to multiple law firms, multiple lawyers. They have friends of lawyers that have been trying to reach out to find someone willing to take the case, and they keep running into dead end. Sorry, we don't, we don't cover that in our -- in our firm or and there's no, there's no recommendations given. You know we see, we see the comments of people saying, they need to sue the administration, sue the company, sue the state and my family is willing to. They want to. They want everyone and everything that led up to this happening. They want there to be some sort of accountability, but, but no one's coming forward or willing to take the case.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, I'm going to see if I can help out there, Jen. This is ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 JENSEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This should be a national class action against these trucking companies. But I can't promise anything, but this just seems ridiculous. It should be a national