d States and the civilized world for 47 years. As I said, they've got the blood of hundreds of Americans on their hands, and unless there's a fundamental change in the behavior of the ayatollahs, the only way Iran will stop being this source of instability, disorder, chaos and violence, is for the Iranian people to take their future in their hands. Again, yearning for freedom for 47 years.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, it's up to them.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: We would certainly be -- it would certainly be better for us and all of our friends if Iran was not exporting revolutionary violence and terror every day.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Senator, the added benefit of cleaving China away from Iran, that can't be understated either. If indeed, there's new leadership there. Senator, we'll be watching this closely. Thank you so much, sir.\par \par \uc2 And it is happening. I think it's not a matter of whether we're going to take de facto control of Greenland, it's how and when. The leaders of both Greenland and Denmark traveling all the way to the White House to meet with Vice President Vance and Secretary Rubio today, and as expected, their leaders are digging in their heels.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: We had, what I would describe, as a frank but also constructive discussion. We also agreed to disagree. And therefore, we will, however, continue to talk. The big difference is whether that must lead to a situation where U.S. acquires Greenland and that is absolutely not necessary. I mean.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, that's fine. But who do you trust more to decide what's necessary for U.S. national security? Trump, Rubio and Vance or those two? All right. Having Greenland under U.S. authority, however that's structured, it's critical given both Russia and China's ambitions in the region.\par \par \uc2 So, except for wounding their pride, U.S. command of the second largest island in the world will mean both Denmark and Greenland are better off. Because after all, a strong and safe America means a stronger, safer Europe.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: You know, we're doing the Golden Dome. We're doing a lot of things and we really need it. If we don't go in, Russia is going to go in and China is going to go in. And there's not a thing that Denmark can do about it, but we can do everything about it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Here to discuss is Carla Sands, former U.S. Ambassador to Denmark and Chair for Foreign Policy at the America First Policy Institute. Madam Ambassador, great to see you tonight. You heard what the foreign officials were saying, but the Danish Foreign Minister says the U.S. has, quote, "wide military access already to Greenland," and we can ask to increase our presence there. So why wouldn't that be enough?\par \par \uc2 CARLA SANDS, FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO DENMARK: Why wouldn't American taxpayers pay more for Europe's defense or Greenland, which is a possession of Denmark, right? I don't think so. President Trump is looking at this with clear eyes. Greenlanders will go independent in the 21st century. Just like Iceland left the Kingdom of Denmark in the 20th century, Greenland will leave. President Trump is making sure they don't fall into the lap of China or Russia. That's a good thing.\par \par \uc2 Greenlanders really want the Greenlandic dream. They want prosperity, they want good paying jobs. They don't have that because they rely on welfare from Denmark. Denmark doesn't have the ability to develop Greenland. It doesn't have the ability to defend Greenland either.\par \par \uc2 And in fact, in 2019, when President Trump asked the current Prime Minister, Mette Frederiksen, to please put more assets into Greenlandic security because it was unsecure. She said yes, she would. She'd put two hundred million dollars into that security. And you know what she did? She put two million and it wasn't -- into security at all.\par \par \uc2 So, one percent of what she committed to, and that wasn't even real. Security. So, you can't trust what she says. She and Denmark will never be able to afford to defend or secure Greenland.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I agree it will be gone right now if we weren't going -- we weren't defending it. I mean, I'm sorry, but that's just a fact. I mean, they did over Canada. So, not to say we don't love our -- Canadian military counterparts. We do. We love them.\par \par \uc2 But without the United States might and all the billions and billions we put into our military, that already have fallen into China. But the NATO allies -- our ambassador is getting involved as well. According to reports, Germany, the Netherlands, Sweden, Canada are sending troops to Greenland amid continue threats. OK.\par \par \uc2 What is that going to do? I'm sorry I'm laughing. That's just the most ridiculous thing I've ever heard. They're not even willing to send troops to, you know, protect Ukraine. They're going to send troops to Greenland? OK.\par \par \uc2 SANDS: Right. And the prime minister, she never picked up the phone for a year to talk to President Trump. And instead, went to fill the heads of other European leaders with this idea of, you know, America bad. But they also did a sigh up on the Greenlandic people to make them terrified of the United States.\par \par \uc2 When I left in 2021, we were the best of friends with the Greenlanders. I had put a consulate there, gotten a Coast Guard bill for Arctic security, and we had given funds to the Greenlanders from our State Department to help them with their dreams. They told me what they wanted and the United States answered.\par \par \uc2 President Trump understands how important Greenland is to the Golden Dome. If you look at Alaska and Greenland, they are both part of the Golden Dome to protect us, but also our allies.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah, 850,000 square miles. It's unbelievable. Ambassador, thank you very much. Great to see you tonight. And coming up, Hillary is hiding. Why does the cat have her tongue?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: She's been celebrated at soirees, she's been honored by universities, she's done the media rounds, hasn't she? But Hillary Clinton is sure shy about appearing in one arena before a congressional committee to answer questions about Jeffrey Epstein.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: We're here today for the deposition of former Secretary Hillary Clinton and she did not appear. Just like her husband, Secretary Clinton is defying our duly authorized congressional subpoena.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Remember, to her, it's all just a vast right-wing conspiracy. Here with us, reaction, Kentucky Congressman James Comer, chairman of the House Oversight Committee. Congressman, I personally prefer to see all those leather chairs than to see Hillary, but I see your point. You wanted to talk to her. You said you're going to be holding both of the Clintons in contempt of Congress.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER (R-KY): Yes, that's --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Then what?\par \par \uc2 COMER: Ok, so we're going to vote Wednesday in the House Oversight Committee. They both defied legal subpoenas that were voted on in a bipartisan manner. This wasn't just my subpoena. This wasn't just the Republicans on the Oversight. Republicans and Democrats agreed to a list of eight people that were -- four Republicans and four Democrats, that were people of interest, high-ranking political officials to bring in and depose. Every single person came in except Bill and Hillary Clinton.\par \par \uc2 So, we subpoenaed them. They did not come in. They've been very combative. We're going to hold them in contempt in the House Oversight Committee on Wednesday, and then the following week when we're in session the House, the full House, will vote on 