t's a vast right-wing conspiracy. And she's just angry. Congressman, thank you for trying. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, Oprah pushes a new approach to weight loss. And a citizen takes on a city council in the spirit of 1776. Seen and Unseen, Raymond Arroyo, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's time for Seen and Unseen, where we reveal the stories behind the headlines. For that, we turn to Fox News contributor Raymond Arroyo. All right, Ray, I heard that Oprah returned to TV and that woman has a new mission.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: She does indeed. She has a new book, Laura, debunking that crazy theory that diet and exercise will help you lose weight. Instead, you get a shot, you get a shot, you get a shot.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Obesity is about biology. Obesity is not a choice, it is not a personal failure.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: So that means, you use the medications and to come out about using the medications and no longer feel shame about medications. Now I understand that if you carry the obesity gene, if that is what you have, that is what makes you overeat. As I always thought, like so many of you in the world are thinking, it's about willpower, it is not. You're still trying to do it through willpower, you're not going to make it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, I mean, this is rather incredible. You know, the side effects of the Ozempic and pills, shots like it, this was created really to treat type-two diabetes. And there was a JAMA medical report, it found after four years, only twelve percent of those on the shot had a healthy body weight. Twelve percent. Oprah selling it like it's the only thing.\par \par \uc2 Look, shoving Doritos in your mouth or little Debbies and chasing it with a liter of Coke, she's trying to claim that that's the obesity's fault. No, no, no. We have self-mastery over ourselves. And it's a sad day when Oprah kind of abandons that and goes this medical route.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, I think she's a can. She's always been a can-do person, but she struggled her whole life with weight. I know a bunch of people who are on those types of drugs, and most of them have had pretty good success, so I don't know much about them.\par \par \uc2 But I do think, like, if you're overweight and you can't lose weight, you've been trying for like ten years and you're on the verge of getting, you know, really, really sick -- now I don't know. I don't pretend to understand all of it.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: She's making it sound like it's, you know, the obesity is not an after effect of your actions, but something like cancer that you're carrying within you. That's a questionable reframing, particularly considering she was at Weight Watchers. She owned ten percent of the company for a number of years. They bought something that they turned into Weight Watchers Clinic, which turned to the shots. She sold her interest since then. But, hmm.\par \par \uc2 Well, Laura, talk about confused medicine on Capitol Hill. On Capitol Hill, there was a hearing about FDA-approved abortion drugs. And one OGBYN can't quite be sure if men can get pregnant.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Ms. Verma, can men get pregnant? Dr. Verma, can men get pregnant? As a doctor, give me your opinion as a doctor. Can men get pregnant?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I mean.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: OK. She was given another chance to answer the question, but Dr. Verma still wasn't sure.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Can men get pregnant?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Again, the reason I paused there is I'm not really sure what the goal of the question.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: The goal is just to establish a biological reality. You just said a moment ago that science and evidence should control, not politics. Can men get pregnant?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I take care people with many identities.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Good Lord. All these years, Laura, I thought only my wife could get pregnant. I guess I was wrong. She's witnessing about abortion drugs and its effect on women. She can't even be sure who's pregnant. This is like asking a proctologist about delivering a baby. This is really bizarre.\par \par \uc2 And the point of this was the Biden administration, Laura, who loosened all the regulations on these abortion pills and allowed them to be sent through the mail, that's what they're questioning here. And, you know, talk about fallout and side effects. Terrible side effects with these abortion drugs. I'm glad they're looking at this.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, Ketanji Brown Jackson still can't figure out what a woman is. And well-respected medical professionals struggle with these. I think they know the answer, right? But you can see in their minds they're doing like a calculation. If I say this, and these people are going to -- if I say this, you know, so everyone is in this vice grip of insanity.\par \par \uc2 All right, Raymond, great to see you as always. And coming up.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Can you hear me now? Good.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Can you hear me now?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: No, you couldn't hear anybody if you had Verizon. A lot of people hit by a cell phone outage. We have details coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, I thought my EP was ignoring me for the entire day, but she claims there was some big cell phone outage. I don't believe that. Joining us now, Fox News Senior National Correspondent Kevin Corke for a fact check. Kevin, I know Sam was just, you know, off taking dancing lessons.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN CORKE, SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Yeah, probably so. Can you hear me now? Verizon ad tagline in the commercials. You remember that one. Unfortunately, Laura, a lot of the company's customers today were asking that question because at its peak, the carrier suffered a major outage impacting more than 170,000 people, according to the outage tracking site, Down Detector. Who knew there was such a thing?\par \par \uc2 New York, D.C., Chicago, Atlanta, Houston, L.A., Portland, among the Metro's hardest hit. In a series of updates, by the way, Verizon kept saying every couple of hours, we know, we know, we're working on it.\par \par \uc2 Now you may recall they also had a big outage back in September of '24. More than 100,000 users that time were stuck without power and calls were dropping. And not to jump on the carrier when they're down. It's worth noting the company also had to fork over a million bucks to settle an investigation into 911 call outages by the SEC.\par \par \uc2 Now they say tonight things are better, but I have AT&T, so I guess I don't care.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK. Kevin, they always said they have the best coverage nationwide and we don't really know why this happened. And Sam claims she still can't receive any messages from me. So, she still walked out, my goodness. All right, Kev, thanks so much. And yes, very smart on the AT&T front.\par \par \uc2 All right, that's it for us tonight. Jesse Watters, next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2026 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020260115em1e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 U.S. Strikes ISIS Targets in Syria; Iran Protests Enter Third Week; U.S.- Iran Tensions on the Rise; Trump Weighs Options for Iran Strike; Trump Ramps up Greenland Rhetoric in Wake of Venezuela Operation; ACA Extension Passes House, Faces Uncertain Future in Senate; ICE Shooting Sparks Mass Protests Across U.S.; Trump's Foreign Policy Agenda; Impact Of Minnesota ICE Shooting On White House Immigration Enforcement Agenda; Interview With White House Border Czar Tom Homan; A one-on-one with Virginia Governor \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2  .\b0\par\par\uc2 Jacqui Heinrich, Trey Yingst, Juan Williams, Matt Finn \par \uc2 9,222 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 January 2026\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2026 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2026 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Jacqui Heinrich, in for Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 U.S. launches strikes on \uc2 ISIS\uc2  terrorists in Syria. And here at home, an ICE-involved shooting gets starkly different reactions and sparks mass protests across the country.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MAYOR JACOB FREY (D), MINNEAPOLIS: To ICE, get the (EXPLETIVE DELETED) out of Minneapolis.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE, VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Minneapolis officials are encouraging people to commit violence against ICE officials. It's ridiculous. It's got to stop.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice-over): Anti-ICE protests erupt after a confrontation with agents in Minnesota turns deadly and two others are shot and wounded by Border Patrol in Portland.\par \par \uc2 White House Border Czar Tom Homan on what it means for immigration enforcement moving forward.\par \par \uc2 Then --\par \par \uc2 AYATOLLAH ALI KHAMENEI\uc2  , SUPREME LEADER OF IRAN (through translator): A bunch of vandals showed up and destroyed buildings belonging to their own country just to please the U.S. president.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: If they start killing people like they have in the past, we will get involved.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice-over): President Trump issues a new warning to Iran as the Islamist regime cracks down on anti-government protesters just one week after the U.S. operation in Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2  : It's a whole different Venezuela. Venezuela is going to be very successful.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice-over): We'll talk to Senator Mark Warner, top Democrat on the Senate Intelligence Committee.\par \par \uc2 And --\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE LAWLER (R-NY): Seventeen Republicans joining with 213 Democrats sends a message that there is bipartisan support.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice-over): The House passes a bill extending healthcare subsidies as millions of Americans face steep hikes in their premiums.\par \par \uc2 We talked to Senator \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2  , a key player in the debate, as the measure heads to the upper chamber.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Virginia Governor \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2  on his historic term bridging the partisan divide in America and plans for the future.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Will you run for president?\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GLENN YOUNGKIN\uc2  (R), VIRGINIA: Well, as I've said, I'm finishing.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice-over): All right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 We begin with a look at some of today's top stories.\par \par \uc2 Widespread protests in Iran are entering a third week with thousands of people risking their lives to defy the Islamic regime's crackdown attempts. In a Truth Social post Saturday, President Trump said, "Iran is looking at freedom perhaps like never before. The USA stands ready to help."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in neighboring Syria, the U.S. launched a second round of air strikes against \uc2 ISIS\uc2  targets Saturday following last month's deadly attack against U.S. and Syrian forces in Palmyra. We'll get a live report from the Middle East later in the show. And thousands of anti-ICE protesters took to the streets in Minneapolis Saturday, calling for an end to the immigration enforcement there after a deadly confrontation on Wednesday. The \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  is preparing to send hundreds of more agents to the city.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we'll bring in White House Border Czar Tom Homan to ask him about that.\par \par \uc2 But first, Matt Finn is in Minneapolis with more -- Matt.\par \par \uc2 MATT FINN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Jacqui, that massive anti-ICE demonstration here in Minneapolis took place at a location called Powderhorn Park, which is the exact site where former Minneapolis City Council members announced their now failed nationwide campaign to defund the police.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FINN (voice-over): Rioters break into a Minneapolis hotel Friday night, where they believe immigration agents are staying, tossing furniture and spray-painting anti-ICE messages. Police declared an unlawful assembly.\par \par \uc2 CROWD (chanting): The people, united --\par \par \uc2 FINN (voice-over): At ICE's temporary headquarters outside of the city Saturday, a group of Democratic congresswomen, including Representative \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2  , alleged they were denied a full access tour of the detention facility.\par \par \uc2 REP. ILHAN OMAR (D-MN): What happened today is a blatant attempt to obstruct members of Congress from doing their oversight duties. When we appropriate funds as members of Congress, we are expected by the public to do oversight because the public requires their money be used with transparency and accountability.\par \par \uc2 FINN (voice-over): This week, ongoing tension in Minneapolis boiled over when an ICE agent shot to death 37-year-old Renee Good. DHS says Good blocked agents with her car and used it as a weapon.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You want to come at us? I said, go get yourself some lunch, big boy. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 ICE AGENT: Out of the car. Get out of the (EXPLETIVE DELETED) car. Get out of the car. Whoa!\par \par \uc2 (GUNFIRE)\par \par \uc2 FINN (voice-over): White House Press Secretary Karoline Leavitt posting, "This woman was not an innocent mother dropping off her child at school." She was a leftist insurrectionist who was purposely and illegally obstructing law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 Democratic Mayor Jacob Frey.\par \par \uc2 FREY: I can see a person that is trying to leave. I can see an ICE agent that was not run over by a car. That didn't happen.\par \par \uc2 FINN (voice-over): Despite this week's death involving a car, a FOX camera captured a person blocking in agents on Friday.\par \par \uc2 ICE AGENT: I'm giving her a warning to not interfere with what we're doing.\par \par \uc2 FINN (voice-over): And Border Patrol warning another woman to stop trailing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I hope you have a terrible day.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FINN (on camera): And Homeland Security has responded to Congresswoman Omar's claims of obstruction. DHS says that members of Congress must now give a seven-day advance notice if they want to tour an ICE facility -- Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Matt Finn reporting from Minneapolis -- Matt, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is White House Border Czar Tom Homan.\par \par \uc2 Tom, thank you for being here. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Immediately after the shooting, you said that "It would be unprofessional to comment on what I think happened in that situation. Let the investigation play out and hold people accountable based on that investigation."\par \par \uc2 Since that time, we've seen a lot more evidence come out, including cell phone video from the officer who shot Renee Good.\par \par \uc2 What is your assessment now?\par \par \uc2 TOM HOMAN, WHITE HOUSE BORDER CZAR: Look, you know, what I've -- when I did that interview, I went straight from an airplane tarmac straight to that interview. So when someone shows me one video, cherry-picked video, ask me to comment, I just think it was inappropriate for me to do so.\par \par \uc2 But look, I -- since then, I've looked at a bunch of videos. And I believe -- I truly believe that that officer thought his life was in danger to take that action.\par \par \uc2 Now, look, we will let the investigation play out. You know, there's a lot of things we all don't know. There's probably a lot of video we haven't seen, but the FBI has, even Ring video.\par \par \uc2 There -- what's the statement from the officer? What's the statement from the witnesses? What's ballistics? The ballistics, you know, with the -- I'm sure they're looking at ballistics and the forensics.\par \par \uc2 There's a lot of information needs to come out. So, I'm still going to say, let's stop the hateful rhetoric. You can't compare this to murder. Murder requires malice, and that is just dangerous to put that type of language out. Let the investigation play out and see what -- and see where -- see where it all lies.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The DHS secretary, Kristi Noem, concluded pretty quickly that this was a domestic terrorism incident. She said that while the scene was still being processed and said the dead woman was part of a left-wing disruption scheme.\par \par \uc2 You know, with those statements, why should federal authorities be the ones investigating it? It almost sounds like there's nothing left to investigate.\par \par \uc2 HOMAN: Look, it's a federal crime to impede and interfere with an ICE officer. And I think -- look, it's a coordinated attack. We can see what's happening throughout the country. I think it's a funded organized attack against ICE officers.\par \par \uc2 And what really irritates me is we -- we -- the left wants to play this game that ICE as -- you know, they're not law enforcement officers. They are federal law enforcement officers. Impeding them and interfering them is a -- is a crime.\par \par \uc2 No one will be arguing if -- you know, if we're arresting people that interfere with DEA before, you know, arresting a drug dealer, or U.S. Marshal in attempts to arrest a fugitive. If someone stood in your way, that's a crime. You would be arrested.\par \par \uc2 But it seems like they want to -- you know, they normalize, the media is normalizing interference with ICE. It is a crime to impede ICE officers.\par \par \uc2 Bill Melugin just put -- put out a list of 18 of the most -- worst of the worst in Minneapolis. Many of them child sex crimes --\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 HOMAN: -- child rape, child sodomy.\par \par \uc2 These are the people we're looking for. For anybody that's interfering, that is unconscionable. We need to enforce law and arrest these bad guys. We've already arrested over a thousand people in that state. And we're going to continue to do exactly what Congress has allowed us to do, what the law allows us to do.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Back to the investigation, you know, the state authorities reject the administration's claim that they don't have any jurisdiction to investigate. And some Republican senators, including Thom Tillis, Lisa Murkowski, took issue with them being excluded from this probe, saying that could create unnecessary suspicion.\par \par \uc2 And the Hennepin County Sheriff's Office said, "We've spoken with sheriffs throughout our county. We all agree that an investigative process that excludes local agencies has the potential to have harmful implications nationwide."\par \par \uc2 Why should people trust that this investigation will be fair?\par \par \uc2 HOMAN: It's a federal crime that needs to be investigated by the FBI. That's the way this whole system set up. I trust the FBI to do the right thing.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: We've also heard this justification that local authorities don't collaborate with federal officials on law enforcement. So, why should they be involved now that it's time to investigate a federal officer? But is this lack of collaboration a one-time decision that's specific to this case, or are we watching a new broader policy roll out here?\par \par \uc2 HOMAN: No, look, that state's a sanctuary state. It's a sanctuary city. They don't work with us and that's one of the problems here.\par \par \uc2 And it's one of the reasons we're in the position we're in in that city because law enforcement response has been sporadic at best. This is sanctuary city.\par \par \uc2 Let's remember why we -- why are we in that city right now? Because they're a sanctuary city that purposely releases public safety threats back into the public.\par \par \uc2 I just mentioned about all these cases that -- child sex crime, child sodomy, child rape -- that they released back into the community. These are people we have to go look for. And -- who set the stage for all this?\par \par \uc2 The last four years on open border, millions of people released in this country without vetting. A lot of criminals, a lot of national security threats. That's -- that's why we're there.\par \par \uc2 Let's not forget who set the stage on this. So, all these people on the Democratic side and all these liberal people want to scream and yell about our law enforcement activities. They didn't say a word when there's an open border. They didn't say a word when half million children were smuggled in this country, lost track of 300,000, and they weren't even looking for them.\par \par \uc2 President Trump and this administration found 129,000. They were silent then. They were complicit on millions of people being released in this country, many of them criminals. Didn't say a word.\par \par \uc2 Now, all of a sudden, because we're trying to fix four years of an open border, now they're all of a sudden, they got an opinion.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: There are already 2,000 or so federal agents in Minneapolis for this massive crackdown that's tied in part to this ongoing probe of alleged Somali fraud.\par \par \uc2 FOX is told that about a thousand more Border Patrol agents are going to be heading there to assist -- basically free up HSI officers.\par \par \uc2 There's also this growing network of local residents who are tracking ICE movements and warning people about them. And schools in the area are being given the option of remote learning for the next month because of how hot the environment is getting.\par \par \uc2 Yeah. Are you concerned about what we're heading toward?\par \par \uc2 And what do you say to those officials, like Mayor Frey, who's accused the administration of just looking for an excuse to invoke the Insurrection Act?\par \par \uc2 HOMAN: It's ridiculous. Bottom line is they don't have to shut schools down. They can't give me one example where ICE has walked into an elementary school or church or hospital. It's ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 And all this false narrative that they're pushing is just going to double down on hate against ICE. For instance, calling in a murder -- that the mayor calling it murder. The hateful rhetoric has caused a lot of this violence.\par \par \uc2 I said way back in March, if the hateful rhetoric doesn't decrease, there will be bloodshed. Unfortunately, I was right, and it's not over.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 HOMAN: There will be more bloodshed unless we decrease the hateful rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 To compare this officer to a murderer, are you kidding me? Threats against ICE officer -- actual attacks are up 1,300 percent. A lot of this is based on the hateful rhetoric from people who don't understand what's going on and don't want to face the facts.\par \par \uc2 If you want a safe city, if you want ICE out of your city, then stop being a sanctuary city. Give ICE the access to your jail to arrest a bad guy in the safety and security of a jail, which is safer for the officer, safer for the alien, and safer for the community. You force us in the community to find these people.\par \par \uc2 So, sanctuary cities are sanctuaries for criminals. More agents in your jail means less agents in your community.\par \par \uc2 But don't mistake anything. We're going to get this job done. If he forces in the community to arrest the bad guys, that's exactly what we're going to do.\par \par \uc2 And when we do that, we may find others who weren't a public safety threat, weren't the target of the arrest, but they're coming too because we're going to enforce immigration law.\par \par \uc2 Work with us. I'm begging you, stop the hateful rhetoric. I don't want to see anybody die. There've been too much death.\par \par \uc2 Work with us. Help us make your city safer.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Tom Homan, thank you so much for being here. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 HOMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Growing protests in Iran entering a third week even as the supreme leader, Ayatollah Khamenei, vows a crackdown. All of that despite U.S. warnings. We'll ask the top Democrat on Senate Intelligence, Mark Warner, whether the regime is at a breaking point.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Fox News alert now on new U.S. airstrikes against ISIS targets in Syria this weekend. Fox News Chief Foreign Correspondent Trey Yingst joining us from neighboring Israel. Trey, what's the latest?\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Jackie, good morning. Overnight the United States launched a series of strikes against ISIS targets in Syria. U.S. Central Command said these strikes were in response to the killing of two National Guardsmen last month.\par \par \uc2 According to reports, the U.S. used more than 90 precision-guided munitions, hitting over 35 locations. These were the second round of strikes against the Islamic State since that attack on U.S. forces. CENTCOM continues to monitor ISIS activity but officials tell Fox News they're also closely watching the ongoing protests in Iran.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, massive demonstrations swelled across Iran with fires set in major cities, government buildings torched, and mosques burned down. For the third day, Iranian officials have shut down the internet access there, but with Starlink satellite uplinks, some images and information are trickling out, including reports that security forces have opened fire on protesters in multiple cities. Crowds in northern Tehran chanted, "down with the dictator, long live the Shah, Pahlavi will return."\par \par \uc2 With widespread domestic unrest and a true social post from President Trump overnight, saying that the USA is ready to help, the Speaker of the Iranian Parliament is warning the United States against striking Iran.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHAMMAD-BAGHER GHALIBAF, SPEAKER OF THE PARLIAMENT OF IRAN (through translator): American military centers and bases and ships in the region will be our legitimate targets.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: This week, the Israeli Security Cabinet is holding consultations about the situation in Iran.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Trey Yingst, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, joining me now is Senate Intelligence Committee Vice Chair Senator Mark Warner. Senator, thank you so much for being with us this morning. Just to start off, thank you, your reaction to these strikes in Syria, anything you can say about the degree to which the U.S. has down -- degraded ISIS with this attack?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK WARNER (D-VA): Well, we continue to hit ISIS targets in Syria and they are bad folks, let's acknowledge that, and let's also acknowledge the fact that the Iranian people are very brave to stand up against their repressive regime, but if we step back for a moment, Jacqui, in less than a year, President Trump has used American military to attack Syria, Iraq, Iran, Yemen, Nigeria, Venezuela. We've got 20 percent of our fleet off the coast of Venezuela maintaining a blockade and our military did remarkable in Venezuela. He's now threatening Colombia, Greenland, and other nations.\par \par \uc2 You know, I'm against the bad guys around the world, but I'm not sure that America trying to be the policeman for the whole world is why Americans hired Donald Trump to be president.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: There's been no shortage of Republicans saying that there's a lot of precedent, presidential authority for those kinds of strikes that date back to Democratic administrations too, but on Iran specifically, the Times of Israel is reporting that Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu spoke with Secretary of State Marco Rubio that President Trump is, quote, "seriously" considering authorizing a strike on Iran amid this crackdown and the internet blackout there. In your opinion, is the regime at a breaking point and at what point should the U.S. get involved?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Well, the Iranian regime is awful and I stand with the Iranian people, but what I'm worried about is if we take a kinetic strike, does that actually unite the Iranian people in a way that the regime has not been able to? If, you know, I know this is history, but the last time we intervened in Iran in a major way was 1953 when the CIA overthrew the Iranian regime to protect Iranian oil for the West. Many historians would argue that led to the Ayatollah.\par \par \uc2 I think we can put outside pressure, I think, rallying the rest of the world, but taking a kinetic strike unless there is a plan on what next. And I remind your viewers, you know, we've got 20 percent of our fleet right now off the coast of Venezuela blockading that country. The USS Ford, which is one of our lead carriers, is supposed to be deployed to the Middle East. It's now deployed off the coast of Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 And the president said, we're going to take Venezuelan oil. It will take two years to rebuild the Venezuelan oil infrastructure. Is our fleet going to stay there for two years? Even as good as our military is, it's going to get stretched. And the president has said his only constraint in terms of how he uses American power is his own morality. And I'm just not sure that's what the American people or the world signed up for.\par \par \uc2 There is a reason why the Constitution says that no single individual can put our troops in harm's way by themselves without consultation with Congress and making a case to the American people.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Speaking of additional action on U.S. troops, I want to get your reaction to this. The president said that cartels are running Mexico. He suggested that land strikes on cartels in Mexico could be coming soon. The Mexican president, Claudia Sheinbaum, has called for increased communication between the U.S. and Mexico. Is there any justification for land strikes on cartels in Mexico?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: The drug cartels are awful. We need to use lots of tools. But the idea that we are going to take a military strike against Mexico -- remember, we may not like Mexican -- the president may not like President Sheinbaum, although he said nice things about her. But she was elected by the Mexican people.\par \par \uc2 Do we really -- are we going to continue to strike out against any leader around the world that Donald Trump doesn't like? I mean, if Donald Trump wants to make a case about striking Mexico, he should take it to the American people and to the Congress and ask for some kind of declaration.\par \par \uc2 But arbitrarily -- again, I point out the fact that he's used American military to strike six countries in less than a year. I think the people hired Donald Trump because he said he wanted to end these countless foreign wars. It seems like he's potentially starting more. And -- and even some of the countries you talk about -- Greenland now, which is part of Denmark, is a NATO ally. Is he going to literally blow up our NATO alliance to go after Greenland, where we already have a treaty that allows us to do virtually anything we want? I just don't understand what his policy is. And if the only check on the president -- and this is what he said to "The New York Times," his only check on his international power is his own morality. That's not the way our system is supposed to work, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Well, I want to ask you about Greenland because you are the top Democrat on the Intelligence Committee and there's a lot of reason to be strategically interested in Greenland. "The Hill" writes this, "melting ice has turned what was once a frozen buffer into a contested corridor. Shipping lanes are emerging, subsea cables snake across the ocean floor, missile paths shortened, surveillance gaps narrow. Russia knows this, China knows this. Both are investing heavily in Arctic presence, infrastructure and influence. Either the U.S. can treat Greenland as a distant curiosity or what it actually is, a forward position in a region that will define future power balances."\par \par \uc2 Is the president at all justified in pursuing Greenland?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: No. Well, Jacqui, Greenland is extraordinarily strategic. And we have a treaty with Denmark that gives us right to virtually do anything we want in Greenland.\par \par \uc2 But the idea that he's now threatening Denmark, which Greenland is part of, which is a NATO ally, if he were to take an action against Greenland, that would completely destroy NATO. And it's a little ironic the president is citing Russia since the president has basically been cutting back on our assistance to Ukraine, which is fighting Russia tooth and nail to try to push back Putin's aggressive war. How does he, on one hand, say he's concerned about Russia in Greenland, but not concerned about Russia in Ukraine, where the Ukrainians are fighting, in many ways, the NATO's fight?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: We're out of time, Senator. We'll have to leave it there. I've got to point out there's plenty of reporting to suggest he's pursuing a sale, a purchase of Greenland over any sort of an invasion. But this is obviously an evolving issue.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Let's see if we can -- let's see if we can get a settlement. But breaking against a NATO ally, I don't think the American public will stand for that.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Senator Mark Warner, thank you very much for your time.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Thank you, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Well, coming up, we'll get reaction from the other side of the aisle with Louisiana Senator Bill Cassidy and talk about his efforts to tackle the soaring cost of health care. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Welcome back to "FOX News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 We are now almost two weeks into the new year amid skyrocketing health care premiums for millions of Americans on Obamacare.\par \par \uc2 And joining me now is Louisiana Senator Bill Cassidy, the Chair of the Senate's Health, Education, Labor, and Pensions Committee.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you for joining me for being here. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL CASSIDY (R-LA): Thank you, Jackie.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: So, I want to start here. You've got some negotiations, bipartisan group in the Senate saying they're pretty close to a deal that would bring back the ACA tax credits. It would also extend the enrollment period. What Republican-backed reforms are in that deal as it's coming together? What are the chances that it passes?\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: Well, I can't yet tell you the chances that it passes because it has to conform first with what President Trump says he wants, which, by the way, I totally agree with. And that is don't give 100 percent of the money to the insurance companies who take 20 percent of it for profit and overhead. Give the money to the patient and she will use 100 percent of it for the -- for the health care that she knows that she needs.\par \par \uc2 This, of course, is an expansion of health savings accounts. Again, giving the patient the power, not the profit, to the insurance company. There's other things we need to do as well, but ultimately we've got to make health care and health insurance more affordable, and that's the goal. And this Republican and Donald Trump both want that to happen.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Right now you have pro-life groups that are pretty aggressively lobbying the White House and also lawmakers to prohibit ACA plans that include abortion. The Hyde Amendment, of course, already prohibits federal funds from being used on abortion services. But some states allow or even require ACA marketplace plans to cover abortions using state money or private money.\par \par \uc2 You posted on Thursday that SBA pro-life endorsed your campaign. So, would you support a plan that just continues the status quo?\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: The status quo is actually not acceptable. Under the Affordable Care Act, let's just take the original Obamacare law. Every state was supposed to offer a plan which did not cover abortion for those whose conscience did not want them to pay for abortion.\par \par \uc2 But there's like 12 or 13 states which mandate that every plan covers abortion. Now this actually goes against the law. So, if status quo is to continue to break the law, that is not a status quo that someone can accept.\par \par \uc2 If you really want to go and -- and kind of make it right for all Americans, if you're pro-choice, that's fine. If you're pro-life, yes, you also have a voice. Let's give them a voice.\par \par \uc2 The president, if the president weighs in, I think we can get something done. As always, the president on an issue like this, as Reggie Jackson, the baseball player, once said, is the straw that stirs the drink. And so the president coming to the table, helping us make a deal, I think we can go forward.\par \par \uc2 He did say that you might have to be a little flexible on Hyde to get a compromise bill, but there was also pushback from the White House that that was any sort of a change in the administration's policy. So, some members still wondering what exactly he meant by that.\par \par \uc2 But I want to also ask one other topic on healthcare. On Monday, we're on health more broadly.\par \par \uc2 The CDC on Monday abruptly changed the childhood vaccine schedule. No longer broadly recommends immunization for flu, rotavirus, hepatitis A, and meningitis.\par \par \uc2 You posted this at the time, "Changing the pediatric vaccine schedule based on no scientific input on safety risks and little transparency will cause unnecessary fear for patients and doctors and will make America sicker."\par \par \uc2 Looking back, this is the kind of concern that you had during the confirmation for HHS -- HHS Secretary RFK Jr. Do you regret voting to confirm him?\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: I'm -- I'm -- I'm often asked that, but I'd rather not this -- I'd rather not make this personal like, what are my feelings?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: OK.\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: It should be about the health of the patient. Let's talk about the meningococcal vaccine. Just Google, what happens with meningococcal vaccine -- meningococcemia? You'll see children who lose their fingers. You'll see people whose legs are amputated.\par \par \uc2 It's a rare disease, but when it happens, it is devastating. And anyone that thinks that our country is like Denmark, when it comes to the need for vaccines, has never actually lived with American people beyond those who are wealthy and well to do.\par \par \uc2 And I say that as a doctor who spent 18 years of my life or 20 years of my life in a hospital for the poorly insured and the uninsured. That's not our country.\par \par \uc2 If you meet our country where they are, not imagine that we're like Denmark, you -- you will find that you actually have to have this sort of vaccine schedule.\par \par \uc2 Lastly, I'll point out that the way this was done totally bypassed the normal mechanism. It didn't have a scientific review committee, didn't go through the advisory committee for immunization practices.\par \par \uc2 And unfortunately, when you Google and you see those pictures as a doctor, it grieves my soul that there'll be more pictures like that put on the internet. And I wish the people making this decision knew what I knew.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Are you going to do anything to change that?\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: So, there are -- the current law is that the health -- HHS secretary has the right to do this. But one thing is -- I met with a pediatrician yesterday, so frustrated. There are children dying in Baton Rouge, Louisiana, a small city of vaccine preventable diseases.\par \par \uc2 We had two children dying in West Texas, we -- of measles, a vaccine preventable disease. We had a woman in Canada lose her unborn child because of measles of vaccine preventable disease.\par \par \uc2 And -- and we've got a terrible flu season because flu immunization rates are an all-time low. That is tragic. And so let's just take care of people and move beyond your ideology.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: OK.\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: Google meningococcemia, and you'll see awful pictures.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: OK.\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: Oh, my gosh. I can't tell you as a doctor that grieves my soul.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Senator Cassidy, we're out of time. Thank you very much for joining us this morning. We will continue to follow it, especially of course, as members on the other side of the aisle tend to point to the appropriations process when they're talking about these kinds of grievances that upset them.\par \par \uc2 So, we will watch all of that, especially as the government funding deadline approaches.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you very much for joining us this morning.\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: It is time now for our Sunday group, Annie Linskey, "Wall Street Journal" White House reporter. John Ashbrook, Ruthless podcast co-host. Fox News senior political analyst, Juan Williams. And Ronald Reagan Institute director, Roger Zakheim. Thank you all for being here. Appreciate it very much.\par \par \uc2 Annie, I -- I want to start with you on the issue of Minnesota. This is a "Wall Street Journal" report that was out. It was the editorial board. Excuse me. They write. "The heated way Mr. Trump has gone about his mass deportation policy and the equally heated way the left has opposed it made a violent collision inevitable." If Democrats are wise, they'll calm the furies. As for Mr. Trump violent episodes like this aren't going to help him or his party in November."\par \par \uc2 Now, the administration is obviously not swayed by public polling on mass deportations. But what do you make of the claim from some of these local officials in Minnesota that the president is looking for an excuse to deploy troops?\par \par \uc2 ANNIE LINSKEY, WALL STREET JOURNAL WHITE HOUSE REPORTER: Yes, I mean, excuse me. You know, what we saw in Minnesota, the -- the -- the president's immigration policy has been, as you know, broadly popular. He came into office with this promise to close the borders. And that has -- and -- and -- and showed really that the Democratic Party had failed on that issue.\par \par \uc2 So, that has been a broadly popular part of his agenda. The mass deportation aspect of that, to your point, far less popular.\par \par \uc2 And when you have incidents like this, what we saw in Minnesota, and, you know, putting aside the particularities and an investigation will reveal that.\par \par \uc2 But when you have incidents like that, the president is giving an enormous amount of fodder to his critics. And you can see that with these mass protests that have ensued.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, calming the -- calming the temperature down politically probably could redound to his benefit.\par \par \uc2 So, it -- it is a -- and I will also just say that the -- the incident was incredibly tragic, whatever we find out from the investigation.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Well, I want to ask you, you know, ICE tells Fox News in a statement, and Tom Homan referenced this earlier on the show.\par \par \uc2 "Regardless of stage political theatrics, ICE is going to continue to arrest the worst of the worst criminal, illegal aliens in Minnesota and elsewhere. Some of these criminal aliens have had final orders of removal for over 30 years, but they've been free to terrorize Minnesotans."\par \par \uc2 They did give us a list of the people that they've been rounding up, and it's people who have been convicted of crimes like murder, rape, sodomy. But local officials, you hear them referring to these people as their neighbors. Is that fair to ICE?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: I think it is fair to ICE. I mean, the -- the reality is -- and this is picking up on what Annie was talking about. The American people are all in favor of deporting criminals, gangsters and bad guys.\par \par \uc2 It's when you come down to deporting neighbors, the gardener, you know, the construction worker, the restaurant cook. I mean, people are like, well, wait a second. Why are you -- what is this aggressive attack for?\par \par \uc2 The big giveaway in the, you know, tragic death of Renee Good is that we saw the vice president come out and demonize her. He made her look like she was a member of Antifa, or I think they called her a domestic terrorist. She clearly is not that.\par \par \uc2 And the administration, I think, therefore, knows they're in big trouble on this one. Because the independent voter, the MAGA base is going to support the president. I mean, look at the reaction to the video. It's all aligned politically, you know. Are you a Trump supporter or not?\par \par \uc2 But I think everybody, you know, feels that it was a tragic death and can see, especially independent voters, that this is really out of control when you have this mother just trying to reverse her truck and then say, oh, yes, that's justification for killing her? I don't think that's realistic.\par \par \uc2 And I think that you have to understand now that when people like the mayor and the governor are saying that this is a police state, when Jesse Ventura, the former wrestler, is saying this is out of control --\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: -- I think you have to understand that people are very upset.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: It is one of those issues that people are seeing two very different ways. We've got two more topics I want to get to. I want to start with you, John.\par \par \uc2 You know, the -- the president is taking an aggressive posture, reading the Western Hemisphere of bad actors. What's your reaction to that? Is MAGA sold on the idea of the Donroe Doctrine? Is it -- have they been convinced yet that running other countries is an America First policy?\par \par \uc2 JOHN ASHBROOK, CO-HOST RUTHLESS PODCAST: I don't know how anybody can look at the success of that operation in Venezuela and conclude anything other than the fact that America is back. America is strong.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of that, if not all of it, is thanks to this president and this president's effort to reassert dominance after four years of Joe Biden, where we saw terrible result in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 What he's done in Venezuela, I think, is tremendous. And if you think about it, Caracas is closer to us in the studio right now than we are to Seattle. It's very close, and it -- it hits home in a lot of ways.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Roger, "The Daily Mail" is reporting that some of these European officials are watching Trump's designs on Greenland and wondering if maybe it's just a -- a quick way to get out of NATO if it falls apart from within. What's your take on that?\par \par \uc2 ROGER ZAKHEIM, DIRECTOR, THE RONALD REAGAN INSTITUTE: I don't see that at all. I mean, Europeans are entirely unsettled by the rhetoric around Greenland. But if you look what President Trump has done specifically as it relates to NATO. He's motivated them through carrots and sticks.\par \par \uc2 And the sticks have actually yielded great results in terms of NATO investing in themselves. And that's been a key priority for President Trump, five percent commitment of GDP going to defense of NATO allies. I see the same thing happening here.\par \par \uc2 What President Trump is doing with respect to Greenland is quite consequential. He's making it clear to the American people, and most importantly, to NATO allies that this is the next frontier to American security in terms of Arctic security.\par \par \uc2 And that it's not just a matter of whether or not we have a defense treaty like Senator Warner was talking about, but the politics here in terms of the way China and Russia operates is consequential.\par \par \uc2 Fifty-six thousand people live in Greenland, right? It's a massive island. And we have to make sure as a country that we can secure it at a political level as well as a security level.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Well, if anyone can get a deal, it might be President Trump. So we will have to watch that, guys.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Thank you very much for joining us this morning. That was our quick round today.\par \par \uc2 In just days, Virginia Governor Glenn Youngkin will leave office after a historic first term. We sat down with him in Richmond earlier this week to reflect on his tenure as rumors swirl about his political future. That's coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Four years ago, Glenn Youngkin, then a political newcomer was sworn in as the 74th governor of Virginia, after what many saw as a surprise win over Terry McAuliffe, the Democrat who formerly held the office.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, we traveled to the governor's mansion in Richmond to sit down with Youngkin and discuss the lessons from his historic term.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Governor, thank you so much for joining "Fox News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 You had a historic victory in 2021. Before that, no Republican had won the governor's race since 2009. And at the time, Virginia was lagging behind the rest of the country in reopening schools, recovering from jobs lost during the pandemic. It was leading the country in fourth grade learning loss and the murder rate was at a 20-year high.\par \par \uc2 Fast forward a couple years, 2022, Virgina had returned to pre-pandemic job status and by 2024 more people were moving into the state than out of the state and there was a 30 percent drop in the murder rate compared to the previous year.\par \par \uc2 You did all of that with a split legislature. What can others learn about how you got this done?\par \par \uc2 GOV. GLENN YOUNKIN (R-VA): Well, first of all, thank you for having me and it's just a joy to be with you. And I -- as I told you earlier, I'm a little melancholy to be at the tail end of this administration because what we've demonstrated is that you can have an incredibly broad agenda, big aspirations, and you can get it all done.\par \par \uc2 And I think part of the magic is not acquiescing to the constant comment, what are the one or two things you're going to get done, but let's go after all of it. And so, yes, we set record economic development goals and we achieve them more than the last six governors combined at 156 billion, and record job growth. We made sure we deregulated. Gosh, we just hit 35 percent streamlining of all the regulations in the Commonwealth of Virginia. But we were relentless in our pursuit of those goals.\par \par \uc2 And even with a split legislature, and at times we got very red-faced with one another, and I vetoed 400 plus bills during this time, but we were able to execute against our objectives. And I have to say that it all starts with job growth and economic development because it fuels everything. We had record budget surplus as a result, $10 billion of record budget surplus. It lead the $9 billion of tax relief for Virginians. But also record funding in these critical areas that we were transforming, like education, like public safety and law enforcement, like behavioral health, to make sure that we were delivering for Virginians on all fronts. And I think that's why Virginians see this as not only an incredible successful four years, but we had more Virginians today, as you said, than we had when we started because people had decided, this is the place they want to build their future.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: So, leaving office, what is your biggest concern for this state?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: That's what we have demonstrated works, continues. And, you know, at the end of the day we have brought together a broad, broad transformation agenda, driven it all forward. And if the next generation of leaders in Virginia stay with pro-business and stay with regulation, then we'll continue to see Virginia grow. And that's the challenge. There's a lot of discussion around the first bills that have been dropped in this next administration that are going to undo some of that. And I sure hope they don't because at the front of it it's unleashing opportunity. And the way you unleash opportunity is to grow. And that's at the heart of having a pro-business agenda. And, boy, it works.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Now, despite all of that, Republicans did not do well in the 2025 elections. Abigail Spanberger be your lieutenant governor, Winsome Earl Sears by 15 points. Democrats picked up a solid majority in the House.\par \par \uc2 You have rejected the idea that the public is souring on President Trump's agenda and pointed to the government shutdown as a big cause for this outcome. But do you see warning signs for Republicans nationally?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Well, 2025 was disappointing, really disappointing, and I couldn't say anything else. But what was happening as we reached the last few weeks of that election period was the races were tightening considerably. Our attorney general was ahead. Our lieutenant governor, running for governor, had tightened that race big time. And the Senate Democrats, who engineered the government shutdown, knew what they were doing. They just brought to bear a blame game one who shut the government down. And I think Republicans didn't really prevail here. And it was clear what they were doing because as soon as the election was over, guess what, they opened the government back up, because it was awful what they were doing to the American public and to Virginians.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: But do you think that Democrats got ahead of Republicans on this affordability push? Because you have talked about the coalition that elected you. A coalition of forever Trumpers and never Trumpers. The same coalition that put President Trump in office for a second term. You know, are there issues that Republicans need to refocus on to put that coalition back in play for 2026 and 2028?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Well, clearly, affordability. The recognition that the inflation that the Biden administration unleased on America has had a huge toll on Americans and Virginians. I mean the runaway inflation caused grocery prices and fuel prices and electricity bills and just buying the everyday needs to become so stretched. And that's what President Trump committed himself to address, and that's what he's been doing.\par \par \uc2 I do believe one year in we're starting to really see the benefit of that. Listen, oil prices are down a $56 a barrel today. And we're seeing gas prices at the pump hit, I mean, literally years and years and years lows. And we're seeing prices in the grocery store come down, although there's few areas, of course, like beef and coffee, that it continues to be elevating, bananas. But at the end of the day, President Trump is going to work just like he promised everybody that he would.\par \par \uc2 But these are the issues that Americans care about. Not Democrats versus Republicans, but Americans and Virginians, because being able to afford the things that are most important to life is critical to enjoying the prosperity that America is starting to offer. And so, yes, Republicans need to make sure we stay focused on the accomplishments that we've seen in this past year, but also making sure we message well heading into these critical 2026 midterms.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: We're in a very polarized, political time. We're seeing that play out right now. You're seeing protesters clashing with federal officers after this deadly ICE shooting in Minnesota. You know, governor to governor, what would you tell Tim Walz?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Well, first, what happened in Minneapolis was a tragedy. The loss of life in any circumstance is just a horrific moment for anyone, not just a governor.\par \par \uc2 But what I would say to Tim Walz is, bring the rhetoric down. Don't inflame it. And what he did in his press conference was inflame it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): Kristi Noem was judged, jury and basically executioner yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: And that is a disservice to everybody. A disservice to, of course, our federal law enforcement officials who were there doing a very difficult job, but a job that needs to be done. A very, very challenging moment for those that are upset, and yet you have a governor inflaming it. And, in fact, he did it even beforehand. And what my experience has been, because as soon as the president came in, we established a Virginia homeland security task force and we worked constructively with the federal government. We've seen huge results. Thousands and thousands of violent criminals who were here illegally taken off the street. Done in a very constructive way.\par \par \uc2 And yet what you've seen from Governor Walz, and a lot of Democrat leaders, is a very vocal invitation for violence.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: And that is just wrong to begin with. And so, we've got to stop this invitation for violence against law enforcement. These men and women have the toughest job in America. They put on a bulletproof vest every day to go to work and they don't know if they're going to come home or not. And so, this is a moment for us to bring the rhetoric down, to not say things like you're going to call out the National Guard and that -- to suggest seemingly that that might be a confrontational moment, as opposed to one that would bring calm, and to make sure that we are allowing this investigation to happen, because it has to happen. But most importantly, bring the rhetoric down. Don't inflame it.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: At the same time, you know, what is your message to Americans who feel that the country is heading in the wrong direction? For example, you know, Virginia is a big military state. There are some families who are weary about the prospect of sending armed forces to Venezuela or these warning shots that we're firing to Colombia and Cuba or the, you know, increasingly aggressive pursuit of Greenland. What do you tell those people?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Well, first of all, thank you is what I tell them because the men and women who serve in America's military are the best in the world. And their families serve with them as well. We have a huge military and veteran population in Virginia. Nearly the largest in the nation. And, of course, we have the largest concentration of military establishments in Virginia. And so, first is, thank you. Thank you for your service. And thank you for the professionalism that you bring.\par \par \uc2 I also believe the what President Trump has demonstrated is that he understands peace through strength. He understands demonstrating that America has the best military in the world. And when we use it, like we did in order to take out the Iranian nuclear facilities, or when we use it in order to arrest a criminal, a criminal in Venezuela, that it is -- that it is precise, it's incredibly effective and that goes further to keep us out of conflict then you can possibly imagine because peace is actually achieved through strength.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: I want to end here. Your political future, you've ruled out a Senate bid. Your name was reportedly being tossed around within the Trump administration as a potential successor to the DHS secretary, Kristi Noem. Are you in talks with the administration to join their team?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Well, I have not had any direct conversations with the administration. And I appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 What I have talked about with the president and the administration is how committed I am to finishing. And that has been one of my most frequently uttered comments. You know, you get elected for four years in the Commonwealth of Virginia. That's it. You get one term and then you're done. And I wanted to make sure I was sprinting all the way up through the finish line. And I just appreciate the respect that they've had for that commitment for me.\par \par \uc2 I believe that we -- what we're seeing today is real leadership out of President Trump. And I think as people speculate on what's going to happen down the road in 2028, I agree with President Trump and Marco Rubio. I think J.D. Vance would make a great, great presidential nominee. But I also --\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Will you run for president?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Well, as I said, I'm finishing. And most importantly, I think the most important thing we can do, just like Vice President Vance said recently, is stay focused on the here and now. We have to deliver. We have to deliver for Americans and Virginians. We have huge elections in 2026 to keep and expand our majorities in the House and the Senate. And I just deeply respect the vice president's reminder that we have to stay focused on today. And if we stay focused on today and deliver results, then Americans will continue to extend our license to lead.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: I didn't hear a "yes" or a "no" on a presidential run in the future. Not ruling it out then?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Well, what I firmly believe, and I'll repeat it again, I agree with President Trump, I agree with Marco Rubio.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: OK.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: I think Vice President Vance would be a great nominee.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Had to try one more time. Governor Glenn Youngkin, thank you so much and congratulations on an historic tenure as the 74th governor of this great commonwealth.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Great. Thank you. God bless you.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Thank you. God bless you too.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Well, coming up, the countdown is on for a medical evacuation from the International Space Station this week.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: NASA is returning four astronauts back to earth early from the International Space Station due to a medical concern with one of the Crew- 11 astronauts. The astronauts will board a SpaceX Dragon aircraft on Wednesday and reach earth the following day, weather permitting. They will splashdown off the California coast.\par \par \uc2 Astronaut Chris Williams, who we interviewed last month from space, will remain aboard the station and is expected to come home this summer.\par \par \uc2 And that is it for today. Thank you for joining us. I'm Jacqui Heinrich. Have a great week and we'll see you next "FOX NEWS SUNDAY."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2026 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020260111em1b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Trump Takes Questions At White House; Fed Cuts Interest Rates by Quarter Point; Trump Hits Road to Tout Cost Cutting Agenda; Now, Trump Meets With Business Leaders at White House\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Jacqui Heinrich \par \uc2 11,173 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 December 2025\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [15:00:00]\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, thank you very much, John and Sandra. Good afternoon everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum.\par \par \uc2 And breaking right now, we are waiting for President Trump to speak live at the White House. We're just moments away from that. We're going to see what the president has to say about the decision moments ago from the Fed to cut interest rates for a third time this year, now down to about 3.6 percent. He has been aggressively pushing for cuts as we get closer to the time when a new Fed chair will be named.\par \par \uc2 We know the president has called him too late, as his nickname, \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 . And a lot of discussion in this Fed -- you know, amongst the Fed Governors about this decision, there was dissent over whether or not to cut, and we're going to get into that in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 White House Deputy Chief of Staff James Blair joins us, but first his Senior White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich. Hi, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha. The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  making its third cut in a row in its final meeting of the year, but they also signaled that there might be a higher bar for cuts in the future, which could be a problem for whoever replaces \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  as chairman when his term ends in May.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Is it a litmus test that the new chair lower interest rates immediately?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , U.S. PRESIDENT: Yes. Well, this guy too should too. But I think he's a combination of not a smart person and doesn't like Trump. But the reason he doesn't like Trump --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So, you've spoken with your potential --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, the reason he doesn't like Trump is because I hit him hard because he is doing a bad job. Interest rates --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So, you talked to your potential candidates?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We are fighting through interest rates.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: For months, the president's been aggressively lobbying for these rate cuts to bring down borrowing costs for mortgages, car loans, and credit cards, amid lagging voter sentiments about the economy and possibly a tough midterm ahead for Republicans. But it's unclear if the Fed cares more about inflation stuck above 2 percent, or a weakening labor market.\par \par \uc2 Pressure though from the White House, will not be ending any soon, and the president will hit the road more often at the urging of Chief of Staff Susie Wiles, he said, to tout his economic achievements. And we might also see him defending spending the last ten months with a heavy focus on foreign policy and touting a lot of the investments that he brought back with him on his trips abroad.\par \par \uc2 Officials here are also confident that tax time is going to bring around great news for folks with no tax on tips, overtime and Social Security. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, Jacqui, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 White House Deputy Chief of Staff James Blair joins me now. Great to have you with us today, thank you for joining me. As I said, we're waiting for the president in just a few moments. Your take on this interest rate cut decision today, James.\par \par \uc2 JAMES BLAIR, WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY CHIEF OF STAFF: Well, look, I think all of our economic advisers have made very clear that they believe that there is room for more. The president has made no secret of saying that \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  was too late to raise the rates originally under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and actually tame inflation when it was raising. Remember, he called inflation transitory. They said inflation wasn't a problem. Of course, it was a problem. They let it become the highest inflation we've had in this country ever, minimum in the last 40 years. And then obviously he's, again, too late to lower the rates.\par \par \uc2 So, the president's made very clear that he wants lower interest rates. He wants people to have access to capital so they can buy their cars, they can buy their homes, we can get the economy moving. This is really just a result of, you know, them moving too slowly around there. They've missed the forest through the trees the whole time. And, again, today's, I think, an example of that. But, hopefully, we'll get new leadership and we'll be able to move quickly.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, James Blair, thank you for joining us. You can stand by if you want to listen to the president who is speaking now. Let's go straight to that in the White House. Let's listen.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: (INAUDIBLE). Do you want to bring that camera over there? It's okay. Bring them over there. It's better. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 Bring them over into the center. Yes, it's okay.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Sir, we have the camera on.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : This is (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is the camera.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Nice to see you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Nice to see you.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, thank you very much. It's been an interesting day from the standpoint of news. As you probably know, we've just seized a tanker on the coast of Venezuela, large tanker, very large, largest one ever seized actually. And other things are happening.\par \par \uc2 [15:05:00]\par \par \uc2 So, you'll be seeing that later and you'll be talking about that later with some other vehicle.\par \par \uc2 Very excitingly for me and for the country, we've just launched the Trump Gold card. It was about 30 minutes from now, the site goes up and all funds go to the \uc2 United States government\uc2 . It could be a tremendous amount of money. It'll also be able to have people like this keep, as an example, just one example, there are a lot of examples, people just buying them.\par \par \uc2 It's somewhat like a green card, but with big advantages over a green card. And companies are going to be able to go to the Wharton School of Finance, the Stern Business School, Harvard, MIT, wherever you may get your students, any school, and you are able to buy a card and keep that person in the United States actually.\par \par \uc2 So you -- this certainty because a lot of, I've heard from \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  of \uc2 Apple\uc2  and I've heard from a lot of people, some of the people at this table, that, essentially, in the United States, you can't keep the student, you can't hire people from the best colleges because you don't know whether or not you can keep the person. They come and have the people, get out, they throw the person out of the country. You graduate number one from your college and there's no way of guarantee, I guess you could say, there's no way of guaranteeing that they're able to stay in the country.\par \par \uc2 And, Howard, maybe you'll give a little description of the Trump Gold card, please.\par \par \uc2 HOWARD LUTNICK\uc2 , COMMERCE SECRETARY: Sure. So, for an individual it's $1 million, and for a corporation, it's $2 million. And as the president said for a corporation, they spent $2 million, they can then have an employee, full vetting, the best vetting the government has ever done, $15,000 vetting to make sure these people absolutely qualified to be in America, absolutely qualify. And then the company can keep them here and they have a path to citizenship, right? Obviously, they have to be perfect people in America. And having passed the vetting after five years, they'll be available to become citizens and then the corporation can put someone else on the card.\par \par \uc2 So, for a company that can keep putting people on the card, one person per card. And for an individual, it's a million dollars and it's a gift to the United States of America to help America be great again under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And there's really two gifts. It's a gift of getting somebody great coming into our country, because we think these will be some tremendous people that wouldn't be allowed to stay. You know, they graduate from college, they have to go back to India, they have to go back to China, they have to go back to France, they have to go back to wherever they came from, very hard to stay. It's a shame. It's ridiculous things.\par \par \uc2 We're taking care of that. The companies are going to be very happy. I know \uc2 Apple\uc2 's going to be happy, but a lot of the companies. Nobody talked to me more about it than \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2 . He said, it's a real problem. And it's not going to be a problem anymore. As you know, they used to send people up to Canada and other places, other countries. So, we solved that.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing is it'll take in, we think, probably billions of dollars that will go to the Treasury of the United States, that will go to an account where we can do things positive for the country. So, it'll be a great thing. We'll take in, I think, you know, billions of dollars, many billions of dollars even. So, that's very exciting.\par \par \uc2 The Trump Accounts are doing really well. We have a man on my right, \uc2 Michael Dell\uc2 , who's an extraordinary person. His wife is even more extraordinary. I think I can say that. They have such a good relationship. You know, like she's an extraordinary person. She's a great wife. But they're two great people. As you know, last week Michael contributed $6,250 million and this is just something that's so special.\par \par \uc2 So many people are in love with that. He understood it right from the beginning. It was his idea, and he understood it right from the beginning. Could you give maybe just a little description of why you loved it for such a long period of time and we were able to get it through the great big, beautiful bill basically? That's how it went. What gave us the authorization to do it? Michael, please?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL DELL\uc2 , CEO, \uc2 DELL TECHNOLOGIES\uc2 : Yes, absolutely, sir. So, you know, when we first heard about this idea, the idea was that the government would give a thousand dollars to a newborn baby in an account that they would own, and it would go in essentially the S&P 500 and compound.\par \par \uc2 [15:10:00]\par \par \uc2 And when the child becomes 18, they could use that money to go to college, to buy a home, to start a business, to continue saving.\par \par \uc2 And I thought that was a great idea. I thought that'd be great if companies matched the government's contribution, which we're doing a while, the companies around the table who have committed to doing that. And also the thought about this is, wow, this is going to be like a platform for families and communities and philanthropists to contribute to those accounts.\par \par \uc2 And so we originally started with the idea of, well, what if we gave some money to all the kids in our home state of Texas, right? We thought that would be a nice thing to do.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : It's still a lot.\par \par \uc2 DELL\uc2 : Yes, it is. One out of ten new kids in America born in Texas.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Yes, it's pretty good.\par \par \uc2 DELL\uc2 : Happy to be a fan of State of Texas.\par \par \uc2 But, anyway, we thought about it some more and we thought, you know, this is going to be something really special. We have a foundation. It focuses on children. It's been around for 26 years. And we said, well, why don't we go bigger? And so we talked about it and, you know, now that the Investment of America Act is a law, which that in itself is a incredible thing that occurred, it does create a platform.\par \par \uc2 And we were super happy to announce that we would give, you know, $250. We arrived at $250 because next year will be the 250th birthday of America and 25 million children. We thought that was a nice thing to do because it would include most of the children that are ten and under that are not part of the government program, that live-in ZIP codes where the median income is less than $150,000. And that was also an important part of our contribution.\par \par \uc2 And I do believe there will be a number of other large gifts announced. I've already spoken with a few that will be announcing gifts at the state level.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Right.\par \par \uc2 DELL\uc2 : And more to come.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Yes, it's amazing and it's amazing the vision you have, but that's why you're so successful, frankly.\par \par \uc2 And to think that Michael started off with not a lot of money, I would say, right, not too much, like very little, and he can give a gift of $6,250 million and I know he said he's going to do a lot more, it's pretty amazing. It's really a beautiful thing to watch, so great for our country.\par \par \uc2 So, we have the Trump Accounts that are doing really well and it's become - - I have to tell you, just everyone's talking about it, you know, beyond what I would've even thought. And it's going to make some young people relatively very wealthy. And in some cases, I mean, they could really -- they could really be off to a great start. So, some people have had that advantage and some people haven't. But it's a wonderful thing. Thank you to you and your beautiful wife. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DELL\uc2 : Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : So, it's a pleasure to welcome so many brilliant minds and distinguished business leaders back to the White House. I love these brilliant minds, including \uc2 Michael Dell\uc2  of \uc2 Dell Technologies\uc2  who makes a great product. And I want to thank him for what he's done, what we just discussed, the accounts.\par \par \uc2 We also are grateful to be joined by Enrique Lores of H.P. So, thank you very much, Enrique. Great job you're doing. \uc2 Antonio Neri\uc2  of H.P. Enterprise, thank you very much. What's the difference between H.P. and H.P. Enterprise?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We focus on the cloud security network and large systems.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : The cloud's good. People have done very well with the cloud. That's great.\par \par \uc2 Arvind Krishna\uc2  of \uc2 IBM\uc2 , who's actually become, I would say, a legend, the job that he's done. He's taken the stock from a rather low price to a very nice price. I wouldn't say high because I'm sure you're going to say it's going to go up a lot more, right? So, I can't say a high price, but he took \uc2 IBM\uc2  into a new stratosphere that nobody, I think, saw if you go back to before when he took over the company. You would have -- nobody would have even imagined that you'd be able to do what you've done. So, congratulations. You become of a good friend.\par \par \uc2 Cristiano Amon\uc2  of \uc2 Qualcomm\uc2 , and thank you very much. Congratulations on your success.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I know a lot about your company. You've done amazing.\par \par \uc2 And Executive Director of the Technology CEO Council Bruce Mehlman, so thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 [15:15:02]\par \par \uc2 I think that these people know each other. We're going to be talking a little bit about technology today, and we'll take a few of your questions about technology. I'm sure you probably won't have too many of those questions. You'll be asking about some other things, right, Bruce?\par \par \uc2 Our administration is committed to total dominance in technology. Right now, we're leading artificial intelligence by a lot. And they -- you know, you look at what's going up and we're writing rules, regulations, we want to keep it at the federal government level. So, it's simple for the companies, very important, frankly. And I think we're pretty well- committed. I spoke to some of the senators today and I think we're committed to do that. We want to make it as simple as possible, as easy because you're competing with some very big sources and we want to stay number one by a lot.\par \par \uc2 We're leading the world and building new data centers and new semiconductor and chip manufacturing facilities and new capacity for A.I. And we're unleashing all of the forms, literally all forms of energy and natural gas and oil to clean beautiful coal and safe, reliable nuclear power. And I'm giving them approval to when they build these massive plants that cost $50 billion. You know, it used to be that for $50 million you could build a nice little shopping center, but for $500 million, you could build one of the biggest, most beautiful centers. And now they're talking about $50 billion. So, explain that one. These buildings are -- there's never been anything -- there's never been anything like that. So, you're all involved in that. It's pretty amazing.\par \par \uc2 One year ago, our country was considered dead by many leaders throughout the world, and now we have what's called the hottest country anywhere in the world. We're the hottest country in the world right now. Ten months it took us. I thought it was going to take longer, Michael, really? I didn't think we were going to have this success this soon. We have over $18 trillion being invested, much of it by, like you're in for a lot. What's the number that you're looking at?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: 150.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : $150 billion?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : That's good. I don't know, Mike. Am I allowed to ask you. You already gave $6,000,000,250. Have you stated a number that you're at for investment, future investment?\par \par \uc2 DELL\uc2 : Well, you know, if I think about all the, you know, investment that we make with the American companies that are adding capacity, particularly around semiconductors, it's several hundred billion dollars.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I think that's right. It's pretty good.\par \par \uc2 DELL\uc2 : Yes. Yes, it's pretty good.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : It makes the $6 billion sound very, very small. That's very good.\par \par \uc2 Antonio, what about you guys? What about you and Enrique? How's that going?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Well, we --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I mean, have you announced a number or anything?\par \par \uc2 ANTONIO NERI\uc2 , CEO, \uc2 HEWLETT PACKARD ENTERPRISE\uc2 : No, I'm actually happy to share it with you over the next few years, we're going to invest an initial $3.5 billion building C.Q. of manufacturing. We build the largest supercomputer for \uc2 United States government\uc2  that's supports both the DOD and DOE. So, in the next 18 months, we're going to build the largest five supercomputers that will keep United States ahead of the game, both in life science and A.I., but also to keep ahead owning an entire stack at the national security level.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Great. Are you doing it separately? Are you doing it together or separate?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No. We are totally separate company.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Totally separate, what?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From our side, we invest in the U.S. about $18 billion per year. So, you look at the last five next five.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : That's right.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It would be close to a hundred million billion dollars. And we are going to be investing in A.I. at the edge, not only on data centers, but how to make A.I. available to customers in P.C.s, in headsets, in devices that they will be using every day.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : So, it's over a hundred billion dollars.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And you're doing well. H.P. is doing really well, right?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : That's great.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We're competing currently right now but --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Are you direct competitors?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That's it. That's why they are at different sides of the table, sir.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : The old days would say, why don't you make a deal? I guess now it's not so easy to make a deal.\par \par \uc2 DELL\uc2 : We can have an arm wrestling right now --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And, Cristiano, you've been amazing, right, your company with the investments. How are you doing with investments?\par \par \uc2 CRISTIANO AMON\uc2 , CEO, \uc2 QUALCOMM\uc2 : We're doing great. I think, Mr. President, you have a great decision, I think, when you drove our company to continue to be innovating. And I think we're doing very good. We've been, in addition, doing phones. I think we're now -- our chips are going to every car company in the world, all of the car companies.\par \par \uc2 We're entering to the robotics. There's a lot of there's a lot of growth in robotics in the data center, new smart glasses can be the future mobile devices, industrial. I think a lot of the drive towards manufacturing the country is driving a lot of technology for smart manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 [15:20:03]\par \par \uc2 We're making chips for that. So, I think we're very excited.\par \par \uc2 And I think like Michael, I think the number of industries our chips are being enabling is driving hundreds of billions of dollars of investments. And most important for us, semiconductor manufacturing is very important. So, we are going to be customer of the fabs that are being built in the United States by \uc2 TSMC\uc2  and \uc2 Samsung\uc2 . So --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : That's good.\par \par \uc2 DELL\uc2 : Yes, those investments are very important and we are all customers of those valves.\par \par \uc2 AMON: Those two are my customers.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Memory stuff, memory, we don't -- we know the memory. We do all the -- who's the king of memory?\par \par \uc2 AMON: It's Micron and SKI (ph).\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And Micron's another one doing very well, right? That's great. Yes. I heard you're doing fantastically. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 AMON: Thank you. Thank you, Mr. President.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : The stock market has set a 51, think of this, 51 all time record eyes during the last ten months, 51. You guys are big beneficiaries. You said the records, right? It's amazing. 51 times we've hit a new high, and in the history of our country, if you think about it, 51 times during the last ten months.\par \par \uc2 And, Bruce, let me ask you, pull these people all together. How's it looking for technology?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A lot of global competition, but you'd rather be playing our hand than any other hand in the world.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I think so. We have a good hand right now, don't we?\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  secured just really much less than a trillion dollars of doing investments in four years. And in ten months we secured more than $18 trillion, so 18 times the amount in 10 months what they did in four years. And they would've gone negative. They were going to -- they were heading toward negative, in other words, not a trillion dollars, but it could have been $3 or $4 or $5 trillion less because people were leaving. So, it was a big turnaround in ten months.\par \par \uc2 The $18 trillion, it's a record. There's never been a country that's gotten anywhere near that in the Middle East. When I left the Middle East, we brought back $3 trillion and lots of \uc2 Boeing\uc2  airplanes, like 300. And \uc2 Boeing\uc2  gave me the award for the greatest salesman in the history of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , which is a nice little award. The greatest -- I think I've sold a thousand \uc2 Boeing\uc2  planes. Can you believe it? Now, all they have to do is make them, okay? They got to make them, but they will.\par \par \uc2 But we have made historic trade deals with the United Kingdom, China, Indonesia, Vietnam, Japan, the E.U., Malaysia, Cambodia, El Salvador, Argentina, Ecuador, Guatemala, Switzerland, Lichtenstein, Thailand, and South Korea and many others. And a lot of it's based on the November 5th election result, which was a massive victory. We won all seven swing states, the popular vote. We won the Electoral College by a landslide. We won counties. The counties, they do a -- I don't know, to me, it's the most accurate of all in many ways, where 2,700 counties against 525. That's pretty good, right, Cristiano?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Pretty good.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : 2,700 against 525.\par \par \uc2 That's why when you see the maps are all red except for a little, two little blue lines on the right and the left side of the map. They're probably changing too. Americans are great builders, and then the Trump administration, America is once again a country where innovators get a green light. You have the greatest innovators in the world at this table and representing a lot of their friends who are competitors and friends.\par \par \uc2 But we're really on a big move to get rid of red tape so they can run their businesses and not have to wait ten years for an approval. In the old days, getting some of these approvals that for plants that are already being built would have taken 10, 15, even 20 years prior to rejection. You get rejected. You'd work 20 years and then you'd lose five to four. And we're getting plants approved in a matter of weeks, the biggest plants ever built in a matter of weeks.\par \par \uc2 For every new regulation, we're slashing more than ten old regulations. So, we're not allowing regulations unless they slash ten old regulations. We did that in the first term and we cut more regulation than any administration ever. In our first term, we have the all-time record. I think we're exceeding it here. In fact, now, it's 1 in 24 is the number we're at. It's one in ten you have to do, otherwise we're not letting you do it. But we're actually up to 1 in 24. So, we get rid of 24 regulations for every one. That's not a bad deal, right? And it's been very exciting actually.\par \par \uc2 [15:25:01]\par \par \uc2 But including, we're allowing 100 -- in the great big, beautiful deal, we're allowing 100 percent expensing for capital expenditures, including investments in factories and equipment and structure, so including even structure. So, that if Michael goes out and builds a factory, you can take it against tax immediately, the whole thing. You don't have to wait 38 years and do it over 38 year period. And I think that's what made the first tax cut, which we, by the way, got extended in this tax cut. So -- and you ended up, it's the biggest tax cut in the history of our country.\par \par \uc2 But I think that the expensing factor, and it's good for ten years too, I think that's the -- maybe was the reason that so much growth was -- there were other reasons, but so much growth was gotten in the first term.\par \par \uc2 So, earlier this year, I said that the challenges of our time demand, a new spirit of patriotism and national loyalty and Silicon Valley smart people, and from the whole technology sector, and that patriotism is on huge display here in this room with some of the most brilliant people.\par \par \uc2 And I'd now like to ask Secretary \uc2 Howard Lutnick\uc2  to say a few words, and OSTP Director \uc2 Michael Kratsios\uc2  also say a few words, and I look forward to hearing from Michael a Little bit about this subject, Cristiano of \uc2 Qualcomm\uc2 , and Arvind, just say something and I appreciate it. And anybody else wants to say a couple of words, feel free. Good job.\par \par \uc2 So, why don't we start with Howard, please?\par \par \uc2 LUTNICK: So, the key to America's success is the amazing innovators that we have. I mean, we have invented everything, from the light bulb, transistor, GPUs. We are on the edge of everything. And we need to keep being on the edge of the people at this table, and this industry is why America's great. We need to build here.\par \par \uc2 You know, for too many years, we innovated and we let everyone else build our stuff. And we just can't do it anymore. So, with President Trump coming and winning the election on November 5th, we changed that and now we're going to build it here.\par \par \uc2 You've done everything you can and you've heard trillions and trillions of dollars of technology build coming home to America to protect and defend us and use our innovations here. You're driving interest rates lower and you're going to continue to drive lower. That makes it cheaper. It makes it more exciting to build here.\par \par \uc2 You've blown out the regulatory deep state so that people can build here and do it fast. And the last thing is because of our investment in energy that drive down the cost of energy for all Americans, including the people who are building here, drive down the cost of energy. It's great for America.\par \par \uc2 Put all of that together and you are going to see the greatest growth the United States of America's ever seen. You're going to see north of 4 percent next year, and then it's going to go up from there to fives and sixes. This president's going to drive the greatest GDP growth led by the tech sector coming home and driving growth.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, thank you. You know, one of the things I'd like to say just before going over to Michael, and this, Michael, we got a lot of this (INAUDIBLE), but one of the things I'd like to say is, in the old days, when you announced really good results in this country, the stock market went up, and that's the way it should be. And we're going back to that, because otherwise you can never really attain true greatness. In other words, GDP at four, oh, 4 is so high. Let's raise interest rates and kill it. And we're going to go back to the old days.\par \par \uc2 When we announced great results, it doesn't mean we're going to raise interest rates and try and kill it. It means that instead of 4 percent GDP or 3 percent, which I said it's so wonderful, 3 percent, it should be able to be 20 or 25 percent. I don't know why it can't be. You shouldn't kill it.\par \par \uc2 And so we hope that if this country announces great results, because you're going to see great results over the next couple of years, all these plants, and I'm not just talking about A.I., we have the biggest number of auto plants. \uc2 Toyota\uc2  just announced $10 billion in plants, built various couple of locations, few locations in the country just a little while ago, they're all coming here. And they're coming here because of tariffs, because they don't want to pay tariffs. But they're coming here because of the election and the mindset.\par \par \uc2 But when we announced good results, the market should go up. Now, it's when you announce good results, Michael, the market goes down because they say, oh, that means things are good, that means we're going to kill it by raising interest rates. I think it's really wrong. It should be the opposite of what it is now.\par \par \uc2 [15:30:00]\par \par \uc2 It should be the way it used to be. We now -- it's good results. The market should go up, and we should keep driving it up and have it keep going up and let it be much, much bigger, much, much better.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, growth doesn't mean inflation. They're always afraid that, oh, we have -- and if there is inflation, that's OK, then we can slow it down. But we should be able to do a lot better than '03 and '04. We're scheduled to be at 4 percent, which is pretty amazing because we have a, you know, deadhead -- Fed hair. I mean, this guy, the head of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is a stiff. And, you know, you fight that.\par \par \uc2 It's amazing. We fight that and yet interest rates are going down, except with him, not too much. He did a -- he did a -- I would say a rather small number that could have been doubled, at least doubled.\par \par \uc2 And that's the other thing. I think the rates for the United States are always the lowest in the world because without us, there is no world. Without this economy, there's no world. You know, you could go to some of these wonderful countries that you think are so great. Well, one of the reasons they're great is because we allowed them to get rich off of us. We didn't charge them anything.\par \par \uc2 You look at some of the deals. I don't want to say -- use Switzerland as an example, but Switzerland paid almost nothing in terms of a tariff or tax, or whatever you want to call it, license. They paid nothing. And, you know, we, they're paying now. They're paying a fair amount.\par \par \uc2 I had about 39 percent, but I decided to be nice. I was nice, and I think they like us a little bit better. But the money you're talking about is enormous. Our country is a very rich country. But if we do well, the market should go up and the value of our country should go up.\par \par \uc2 And if we do poorly, the market then should go down. It shouldn't be the opposite. And if we go up, we should encourage it to go up more. And those numbers, when you say, you know, 3 percent of GDP were 4 percent of GDP, they should be much higher.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, I wanted to say it. I do say it. I believe it. And we have to get a mindset that when a country is doing well, you don't want to kill the growth. That's what they're doing. They kill the growth.\par \par \uc2 I don't know if you guys even agree with that. They kill the growth because they're so afraid of inflation. But you can have tremendous growth without inflation. Everything goes up with the growth, but that's not inflation.\par \par \uc2 So I think we can do much better than traditional numbers. And you see that. You see that with what we're doing. We're leading in everything. And now, and remember this, as far as prices are concerned, and they talk about affordability, I inherited the worst inflation in the history of our country. They say it was 48 years, but I say it was the history of what difference.\par \par \uc2 It's 48 years they were telling me. They said, I was being inaccurate because I said the history. I think 48 years is a long time, but it's actually -- I disagree, I think it's the history.\par \par \uc2 So in the Biden administration, I inherited the worst inflation in the history of our country for 48 years. And prices were very, very high. I brought the prices down. We had yesterday three states where oil was at $1.99 a gallon, how about that? And you know these guys, it was $4, $4.5.\par \par \uc2 In California, it was like $7 because that's a badly run state. But the price for oil and gas and everything else, the price of gasoline $4.5-$5. And we just hit one dollar. We broke two dollars.\par \par \uc2 I was looking forward to the couple of states, three states. And I will say that when that happens, oil and gas and energy is so big. It's so big in Canada you don't even know what number two is. There is no number two.\par \par \uc2 And when you have the prices coming down for energy like that, you've killed inflation and you're lowering prices. So I inherited, very simply, the highest prices in history, and I'm bringing them down really fast led by energy. And when energy comes down, everything else comes down. But I've only been here a short while, and we gave them a beautiful thing and they destroyed it.\par \par \uc2 They would have destroyed our country had they been allowed to stay. If they -- if we didn't win this election, if we had Kamala, who's the same as Joe, low IQ people. But if we had Kamala as you would have a country that would be right now in ruins. I believe that 100 percent.\par \par \uc2 Things would be in a state like nobody's ever seen before, worse than anything you've ever imagined. Worse than 1929, in my opinion. Instead, we have the hottest country in the world and we're going to keep it that way.\par \par \uc2 But just to finish, we inherited high prices. We're bringing them down very rapidly. And we're bringing them down to very good levels. They'll soon be down at very good levels. Michael, please go ahead.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL KRATSIOS\uc2 , OFFICE OF SCIENCE AND TECH POLICY: Absolutely. As everyone in this room probably knows very well, leadership in science and technology has been foundational to our economic and national security for decades. And because of the policies that you have championed and the private sector partnerships that you have made, America currently is leading the world in S&T and it's benefiting all Americans every day.\par \par \uc2 The one thing that I want to remind this group of and many of you know, the President has been arguing for American leadership in AI well before ChatGPT was even invented. The President signed executive order on artificial intelligence leadership in 2019, which set the stage for the revolution that we're seeing today.\par \par \uc2 In the past year, we've built on that. We signed out the I Action Plan in July of this year, which the President launched here in Washington along with three EOs. And I know a lot of that effort is around creating a regulatory environment to allow for companies like yours to thrive and for the applications that you build to have the biggest impact on Americans every day.\par \par \uc2 So thank you for everything that you do. And I know we're going to keep working together to make sure America is the home for the next great technological discoveries.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Thank you, Michael. And Michael?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL DELL\uc2 , CEO OF \uc2 DELL TECHNOLOGIES\uc2 : Thank you, sir. Well, look, we greatly appreciate the focus the administration has on lowering energy costs. And energy is the substrate of manufacturing at the industrial level and really incredibly important input to the economy.\par \par \uc2 We can't be competitive without a super low cost energy industry. And had a great meeting earlier today with Secretary Wright and really appreciate all the work that he's been doing. This is, of course, incredibly important when it comes to AI because it does consume a great deal of power. All the semiconductor manufacturing consumes a great deal of power.\par \par \uc2 Another big area of focus for us is how we can take all this great AI technology, the AI stack of the innovative companies here in the United States, and sell that around the world. And, you know, I'd like to ask Arvind to speak a little bit more about this. Arvind?\par \par \uc2 ARVIND KRISHNA, \uc2 IBM\uc2  CEO: Thank you. So, Mr. President, first we really would like to thank you for everything you're doing for economic growth, as you said. I completely agree with you. Economic growth then drives a lot of other benefits to everything else. We also want to thank you for all the work you're doing on deregulation and making things easier for business to participate in that growth.\par \par \uc2 Michael spoke of the AI stack. It's incredibly important that under the action plans that you have laid out and \uc2 Michael Kratsios\uc2  just spoke of, we really help promote the AI stack which is not semiconductors only, those are incredibly important. But it's semiconductors, it's software, it's the systems which many of us here build and it is the software applications of doc.\par \par \uc2 The ability to take those to all of friends and countries, to all the friendly countries is going to be very, very important. And we wish to work with your government to make sure that we can do that. Because otherwise we are afraid that competing countries are going to be able to get that stack. So we would say that --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Anything we -- that you're looking at that we can help you with so that you create a bigger and better \uc2 IBM\uc2  and hire a lot more people, anything you need --\par \par \uc2 KRISHNA: Few countries, we'll speak to members of the administration to make sure that those can go and that barriers get lowered for being able to do it quickly. We agree guardrails are necessary, but lower barriers would be very, very helpful.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : You mean tariffs?\par \par \uc2 KRISHNA: No.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Because they -- are these countries -- are they tariffing you any, you know, making your life difficult? Are they doing anything else to make your life?\par \par \uc2 KRISHNA: It's the ability to take certain systems which have semiconductors inside them into these countries. So right now, because we have controls on where all the semiconductors can go, then the entire system is restricted. That's what we want to do. The same as --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : How many countries do that?\par \par \uc2 KRISHNA: Probably a half dozen.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : So I can tell you the countries I know.\par \par \uc2 KRISHNA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I know every country very well. We have ways of combating that quickly. You'll let me know.\par \par \uc2 KRISHNA: And the last part, I'd like to say is -- I'd also like to thank you for all that you're doing to help modernize government itself because we think that's incredibly important. You know, you made a number of announcements, but a couple that.\par \par \uc2 I'd just like to point out, we worked with Commissioner \uc2 Frank Bisignano\uc2  to help make Social Security better. So I would like to thank you for your help on that. And we work with him on making \uc2 IRS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : He's done a fantastic job.\par \par \uc2 KRISHNA: He is. Frank's great.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : He's done a great job. Frank, he's had a great career. Actually left a tremendous career where his salary, I think, was 100 times greater than it is now. And he was a \uc2 JP Morgan Chase\uc2  and he's doing fantastic. He did a great job at Social Security quickly.\par \par \uc2 KRISHNA: And we hope to help him replicate that now at the \uc2 IRS\uc2  also.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Very good. Thank you very much. Good job. Cristiano, please.\par \par \uc2 CRISTIANO AMON\uc2 , QUALCOM CEO: Just a few points. First of all, Mr. President, thank you for your vision. I thank you for understanding the importance of the American private sector industry I think in the growth of the economy.\par \par \uc2 I want to just build on some of the things that were said by my colleagues. I think we have built probably one of the most advanced technologies in the world. And part of it is because I think the commitment to innovation that we have in the country, but also the fact that it has been American technology for the world.\par \par \uc2 So I think we have an incredible opportunity. When I think about what. Michael said about exporting AI, I had conversation with Secretary Howard. Now that the trade deal has been made, it's an opportunity to really build on those trade deal and export, I think, American technology.\par \par \uc2 And also as we think about the technology, it's an important vector to drive the reindustrialization, I think, of the country with technology. So we're incredibly excited about that. I think it's a big opportunity. I think it's the next wave of growth that we can build on those trade agreements. And I think it resonates with also Arvind said about the importance of the American technology stack.\par \par \uc2 And then, I was just going to finish with two points. There are two exciting things happening in the world of technology coming right now. One is as AI become the new form of computing, AI is going not only to the data center, but to everything, to cars, to PCs. And we see now the ability to run models that you can only run AI in a data center.\par \par \uc2 You can run that on a microcomputer, on a computer, for Enrique, on a server, with small server on premise. So I think we're going to see the proliferation of AI.\par \par \uc2 The other one is 6G. We have made commitment working with your administration working with \uc2 FCC\uc2 . We're going to accelerate 6G. We're working to launch first in the world in LA Olympics and I think that's another opportunity for America to lead in the next world of evolution.\par \par \uc2 So thank you. I thank you for the opportunity. Thank you for the partnership. And we're going to be hard at work, I think, driving that agenda.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : So we're into 16 hours. I just -- I was a leader on 5G getting that done. And now they're up to 6.\par \par \uc2 AMON: Let's do it again.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : What does that do? Give you a little bit deeper view into somebody's skin? See how far -- I like the cameras the old days. So they just had a nice feature. Now they cover every little -- let's see.\par \par \uc2 Michael, you're in good shape. You're not going to. But I tell you, the 6 is coming, huh? Does it ever end and what happens? You'll be into 7, right? Before 6 gets old, you'll be into 7. Anybody have anything else to say? Anybody?\par \par \uc2 ENRIQUE LORES, \uc2 HP\uc2  CEO: I think the other thing is, we want to thank you as well for the openness of your organization and your team. We always find people that wants to hear what we need and is willing to design programs that will support us. So thank you for --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Oh, here it is. We want you to just dominate the whole world in terms of what you do. It's a very important world. And you're doing that. We're making it easy for you. And that means you're going to do it faster. It's more jobs. A lot of good things happen, right?\par \par \uc2 ANTONIO NERI, \uc2 HEWLETT PACKARD ENTERPRISE\uc2  CEO: Mr. President, you may not recall, but in your first term, you actually sponsored the Department of Energy to build the largest systems ever built. And thanks to your sponsorship, the Department of Energy owns the three top supercomputers in the world. And we build it with them. And these are systems that can do quintillion operations per second. And that means we can solve --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I know. I know. We made that possible. It's worked out very well.\par \par \uc2 NERI: So I know we're going to build the next generation again.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Good. Sounds good to me. Any questions, please?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you. Can you explain a little more about this Venezuelan oil tanker and share a little more information?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, I'll let the people do it. But you see the result, and I guess they probably have released the pictures by now, pretty soon. You'll discuss that with the appropriate people later on. But no, it was seized for very good reason.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Sir, there's a new proposal from DHS that would see tourists from a lot of countries have to hand over five years of social media history to get a tourist visa. Are you concerned at all that if that's implemented there would be a decline in tourism next year? And what sort of incentive do you think is --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: No, we're doing so well. We just want people to come over here and safe. We want safety, we want security. We want to make sure we're not letting the wrong people come into our country. We're getting rid of a lot of bad ones from jails, from mental institutions, drug dealers and we're getting a lot of bad people, Murderers.\par \par \uc2 11,888 murderers. Half of them, almost exactly half of them committed more than one murder. We're getting rid of them. In some cases we don't even want to get them out. We put them in jail because we're afraid they're going to come back into our country. We're not going to let them back in so we've taken a very tough stand.\par \par \uc2 And you see where we've been. Like Washington DC now people walk down the streets, they go into restaurants at night. It's beautiful. It's like this beautiful thing to see because until I got here, this was a very bad place. We had a terrible thing happen two weeks ago with terrorism with regard to two National Guards, people that are so phenomenal.\par \par \uc2 One is no longer with us and one amazingly, weren't looking with great hope on that, you know that Andrew. And today he got out of bed, actually got stood up, didn't say anything. It's a little early, but the chances of Andrew, other than his mother who said, nope, he's going to be OK. His mother was the most positive person and could be that she'll be right.\par \par \uc2 It's pretty amazing, made progress. It was -- both of them were so badly hurt. We never -- we never thought that one could survive. So looks like Andrew's done pretty well.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The winner of the Nobel Prize dedicated the price to you, the Nobel Peace Prize. And she at Venezuela at a (inaudible) with a ceremony, there is a reason she could be arrested, actually. What would happen if she's arrested? What message do you send or would you send to Nicolas Maduro?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, arrested by who? The Nobel Prize Committee? That's probably the ones I would arrest. I don't know anything about it. She was very gracious. She said I should have gotten the Nobel Prize.\par \par \uc2 And I don't, you know, I mean, I'm doing this to save lives, I'm not doing it for any other reason. But no, I don't like if she would be arrested, I wouldn't be happy with that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. President, Senate Republicans currently have a health care proposal that would take away, let those \uc2 ACA\uc2  subsidies expire and then put about $1,000 in people's health HSAs. Do you think that is enough money? And how do you ensure that people who will see those tax subsidies expire won't see their health care costs skyrocket?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, the Unaffordable Care Act, which is Obamacare, they call it now the Unaffordable because it's unaffordable. It used to be called the affordable, but it was never affordable. It was always bad. It was basically designed for the benefit of insurance companies.\par \par \uc2 And what we're doing is we want all of the billions and billions of dollars that went to insurance companies to go directly to the people and they can buy their own health care. And people love it. I don't know if the Democrats are going to approve it because they want the insurance companies to make money.\par \par \uc2 It was a scam. Obamacare was a scam. But we're going to see, I mean, we, the Republicans, I can tell you, want the money to go to the people, and we're winning that battle. The Democrats are having a hard time with it because they're in the pocket of the insurance companies. Yes, please.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You spoke to some European media today, I think, sir.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I did.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Could you tell us about that? And the revised peace plan in Ukraine has presented.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: The leaders of France we spoke to, and Germany and UK, all very good leaders, very good friends of mine, and we discussed Ukraine in pretty strong words. And we'll see what happens. I mean, we're waiting to hear answers before we progress.\par \par \uc2 You know, we're not spending any money in Ukraine. We're selling basically missiles and everything else to \uc2 NATO\uc2  and \uc2 NATO\uc2  is paying us, and then they distribute it to who they want. I guess they're mostly distributing to Ukraine, but we're not spending money.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, we're spending a lot of time and a lot of effort because last month 27,000 soldiers were killed. Both, think of it, 27,000, let's say if you take a football game and take half the stadium, that's 27,000. So 27,000 soldiers, Russian and Ukrainian soldiers were killed. And I want to see that stop. We'll find out.\par \par \uc2 Look, there are a lot of things happening right now. A lot of people say it's closer than it's ever been. It's a war that would have never -- if I were President, would have never started. It's a war that should never have started. But we'll have it figured out I think within a pretty short --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You say very strong terms, sir. Could you explain that?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Well, I think we had some little disputes about people and we're going to see how it turns out. And we said before we go to a meeting, we want to know some things. You know, they want to -- they would like us to go to a meeting over the weekend in Europe and we'll make a determination depending on what they come back with.\par \par \uc2 We don't want to be -- we don't want to be wasting time. Sometimes you have to let people fight it out and sometimes you don't. But the problem with letting people fight it out is you're losing thousands of people a week. It's ridiculous. The whole thing is ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. President, two questions. One, the oil tanker, who owns that oil tanker?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : You'll get that information later.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK. And have you spoken to Maduro since your last conversation?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: No, I haven't.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You only have one conversation.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And when you say that the European leaders want to have a meeting over the weekend, are you talking about with Zelenskyy or with them?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: With Zelensky and us.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And you said in an interview yesterday that you think it's time for Zelenskyy to start accepting things. What things are you talking about?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I think he has to be realistic. And I do wonder about, you know, how long is it going to be till they have an election. It's democracy. It's a long time. They haven't had an election in a long time.\par \par \uc2 It's losing a lot of people. And it's possible that the people, you know, if you look at the polls, I will say 82 percent. There was a poll came out, 82 percent of the people are demanding a settlement be made, Ukrainian people. They want to see a settlement be made.\par \par \uc2 I understand that they're losing thousands and thousands of people a week. They want to see it ended. And I do say, you know, at what point, when did they have an election in Ukraine? That's not casting dispersions on anybody but they do have a massive corruption situation going on there.\par \par \uc2 And they do say, you know, when are they -- people are asking this question, when do they have an election? Are they going to have an election or are they going to just keep it going like this? So I think it's time to get that war settled. And I think it's a war that can be settled, but it takes two to tango.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. President, Warner Brothers, the new owners have \uc2 Warner Brothers\uc2  discovery to make changes at CNN, big changes. And he's spoken to Jared about it. What are you doing with him or wall him off a little bit?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : So what do you want to know?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What changes do you want to see in CNN under new --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, I'm not involved in that. I will be probably involved, maybe involved in the decision, it depends. You have some good companies bidding on it. I think the people that have run CNN for the last long period of time are a disgrace.\par \par \uc2 I think it's imperative that CNN be sold because you certainly wouldn't want to put people just leave those people with some money, good money and CNN so that, you know, they can spend even more money spreading poison because it's lies. It's a disgrace.\par \par \uc2 So I wouldn't want to see the same company end up with CNN, the same company with money, because right now they don't have too much money, but they have value. I guess based on the bids, they've got value. But I think CNN should be sold because I think the people that are running CNN right now are either corrupt or incompetent.\par \par \uc2 That would be a factor in your decision making because, you know, we've got some --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, in my mind, but you know, I can be talked out of that by some very talented people that we have, antitrust people. But I just think that the people that have run CNN into the ground, by the way. Nobody watches, very few people watch. I don't think they should be entrusted with running CNN any longer.\par \par \uc2 So I think any deal should -- it should be guaranteed and certain that CNN is part of it or sold separately. But I don't think the people that are running that company right now and running cnn, which is a very dishonest group of people. I don't think that should be allowed to continue. I think CNN should be sold along with everything else.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. President, you spoke about affordability earlier in your remarks and yesterday in Pennsylvania. How much more can we expect to see you on the road kind of delivering that message? And are you worried at all about the Democrats winning a couple more things in Miami and Georgia?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I don't worry about it. You know, it's like statistically it's amazing when you win the presidency, even if you win by a landslide like we did, and even if you do a good job, people say we've done the best opening 10 months in history, you know, in the history of the presidency. I think we have, who's done better?\par \par \uc2 We ended eight wars. We have the strongest economy. We have $18 trillion coming in, which is more than any country had, times, I think, five or six. $18 trillion is a lot, even in your standards, right?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Even for that guy, $18 trillion is big numbers. But we have $18 trillion coming in. Pretty amazing, right? I think \uc2 Qualcomm\uc2  would be very impressed if they had even a little piece of that. But -- so we've done a great job. But for whatever reason, and nobody's been able to give me answer. When you win the presidency, you seem to lose the midterms. Even if you win the presidency by a lot and you do a great job as president.\par \par \uc2 You know, some presidents have done good and they've lost. I think it's like two times in the last many years it's been won. I don't know why. It doesn't make sense. Usually I can figure things out, but I don't know why but that seems to be. But other than that, we should win because we're doing great.\par \par \uc2 The economy is roaring. I think the economy is only going to go, you know, better with all this money pouring in. We have factories being built.\par \par \uc2 And forget about AI. We have AI more than anybody. We're leading AI by a lot. But we have many car companies coming in from Canada, from Mexico. They're closing up. They're coming in from Mexico, they're coming in from Canada, from Japan, from Europe, all over Europe, Germany, all over Europe.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we're doing great. But for whatever reason, it doesn't matter how well they're doing, you know, good and bad, the bad, you understand. But when -- even when a president is doing well, they seem to not do well in the midterms. So we're going to try and change it.\par \par \uc2 There should be no reason for it. If anybody at the table can figure out, Bruce, a nice psychological reason. But -- so I want to win, and winning the midterms is important. People want us to win the midterms, and I think we have great spirit. We should win the midterms. Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. President, under Biden's administration, during COVID specifically, Biden removed the safety standards on abortion drugs. Now, I'm hearing from a lot of pro lifers that they're very anxious because yesterday \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  reported that your \uc2 FDA\uc2  is slow walking a review of the abortion drugs that Secretary Kennedy promised.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Yes. I don't think you want to see that. I'll find out. I'll ask them.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you want to see --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I don't think they're slow walking anything, but I'll find out. Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Any update on the clashes between Thailand and Cambodia, sir?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Yes, I think I'll have to make a phone call. I was going to say that the Thailand and Cambodia, one of the eight that I settled, they've been fighting for a long time. They've been fighting for a very long time. Many, many decades.\par \par \uc2 But I got along great with both. I found they were two great leaders, two great people, and I settled at once. I think I can do it pretty quickly. I think we're scheduling a phone call tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. President, with Thailand on the venture --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : With Thailand, if you know -- just to answer. Steve, with Thailand and Cambodia, I think I can get them to stop fighting. Who else can do that? Think of it. It's a late wars. Every once in a while one will flame up again and I have to put out that little flame. I have to put it out, but we're going to put it out again.\par \par \uc2 So I think I'm scheduled to speak to them tomorrow and see if I can -- it was unfortunate what happened. I mean, I know exactly what happened. It got it started a little bit, but we'll see if we can put it out.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We're interested in the seizure of this tanker. What happens to the oil on that ship?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, we keep it, I guess.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Where does it go? What port does it go to?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, you have to follow the tanker. You know, you're a good newsman, just follow the tanker. Follow it. Get a helicopter and follow the tanker. But we're going to, I guess, we're going to keep the oil.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. President, on Fed Chair Kevin has said you were going to announce your decision within the next two weeks. Is that correct?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Maybe.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you doing your interviews with finalists today?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, I'm meeting with \uc2 Kevin Warsh\uc2  today, so I'm meeting with him and I sort of have a very good idea what I'm looking for. Again, I'm looking for somebody that will be honest with interest rates.\par \par \uc2 I just want honesty. Our rate should be much lower. Our rate should be the lowest rates in the world because all these countries that you see, you know, with low rates, many of them are low because of us, because they suck so much money out of us. But that's not happening anymore.\par \par \uc2 We should have the lowest rates in the world. And without us, none of them really exist as an economy. I mean, I can show you these really prime, really wonderful countries. They've had nothing but success. It's because they took so much out of the United States. I mean, it's one of those little problems.\par \par \uc2 I want to see that when we do well, we're rewarded for doing? Well, not the way it's been for the last pretty long period of time.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Are you asking these finalists to pledge to lower rates?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I'll be asking questions, and I'll be able to figure it out.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020251211elca00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump Rolls Back \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Era Fuel Regulations; Democrats Trying To Keep Boat Hoax Afloat; Halle Berry Nukes Newsom 2028; Sign Of Times: Bessent Says He Doesn't Read Fever Swamp NYT Anymore; Never Once: Travis Says He And Taylor Haven't Argued Since They Met\b0\par\par\uc2 Greg Gutfeld, Kennedy, Harold Ford Jr., Jesse Watters, Dana  Perino \par \uc2 8,412 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 December 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 WILL CAIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: And I know that you're very opposed to everything that's going on in this woke movement. And so, we wish the best of luck. I appreciate you coming in and hanging out with us.\par \par \uc2 VINCE OFFER, ACTOR: Thank you. And if you want to support, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.shamwow"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.shamwow}}}\uc2  guy.com.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: There you go. Or check out Woke Busters at any of your social media platforms.\par \par \uc2 Also now, right now, time for THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hi, I'm Greg Gutfeld along with Kennedy, Harold Ford Jr., Jesse Watters and she broke the glass ceiling at the North Pole as the first female elf, Dana Perino. THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 President Trump hitting the road again next week, back on tour to sell his economic agenda and hammer the Democrats who gave America the worst inflation in decades. Chuck and Hakeem leading the charge to pin their mess on Trump.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): He is in such an effing bubble that he doesn't even know what average people go through. He's a billionaire. He doesn't worry about paying for groceries. I don't know if he's ever been in a grocery store.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): Our country is far too expensive. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Republicans promise to lower costs on day one, costs haven't gone down. Costs have gone up across the board.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Thank you, straight Obama. So, President Trump is getting prices down by rolling back Biden era fuel efficiency standards for vehicles. Here's DJT and the Ford CEO.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think affordability is the greatest con job. They look at you and they say affordability. They don't say anything else. Everyone says, oh, their prices were so low. No, they had the worst inflation. We had the highest prices we ever had, and now they're all coming down.\par \par \uc2 JIM FARLEY\uc2 , CEO, FORD: Today is a victory of common sense and affordability. We believe that people should be able to make a choice, as you said, Mr. President, and we will invest more in affordable vehicles.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: So, Kennedy, this is pretty big news for the auto industry and for the purchaser of autos, because they're -- Trump is rescinding these cafe standards, which would end up finding hundreds of millions, even over a billion dollars to Ford and GM that goes away due to their draconian emission standards. What say you? How important is this?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, it's important if you're an auto maker and you want to make money, and if you want to make affordable cars that people can make and buy in the United States, I think that's priority number one for GM and Ford.\par \par \uc2 But unfortunately, they have been beholden to the standards, these ultra- leftist standards that are telling car manufacturers, you know, we have to have an arbitrary fuel economy number by the year 2031, 50 miles per gallon, it's a great pie in the sky dream. We're not there yet. We are not on track to do that.\par \par \uc2 So, they were forced to make electric vehicles that drivers didn't want to buy. They're expensive. The tax credits weren't enough to offset the cost. And you know, it's word of mouth from other people wasn't enough to boost that part of the industry.\par \par \uc2 And Ford stopped making a lot of those EVs, knowing that they were going to get penalized with these Biden error fees. But now what they can do is actually respond to the market, which is what they should be doing. People are a lot smarter and a lot more sophisticated than they were 10 years ago, and they know, people know, based on other people who bought electric vehicles and people who bought cars recently, hybrids right now are the way to go, where you can still put fuel. You can go to a gas station and still put fuel in your car. It's under $3.00 a gallon for the first time, March of 2021, and you can go home and charge it.\par \par \uc2 So, that's how you get better fuel economy, and that will put us on a more rational road to eventually having lower emissions, or give everyone a nuclear reactor for their own.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Oh, that would go great with my E.V. Bentley. So, Dana, let's talk about these dell accounts (ph). They're incredible. It seems like something like Trump -- like, why didn't anybody think about this before? Talk to the billionaires, make a deal with them, and all of a sudden you got 25 million accounts for kids, cash for kids that they invest. Explain it to me.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: OK, I don't -- I'm not saying I know all the details, but basically, the idea is, for any kid that is born between January 1st of 2025 and then it goes through 2028, they will automatically get $1,000 in an account. And then that money stays there, and parents can add to it, sort of like, you know, the education accounts. 529 account, you can add to it, tax free. Employers, your employee, like the Fox News, could say, OK, we'll add to it so that you start to get a little bit of a nest egg for a kid that I don't think they can get it till they're 18. They might use it for education. They might not. They might continue to invest.\par \par \uc2 We know that over time, the best way to compound wealth is to invest, starting early, and rather than starting at the age 26, it's going to make a huge difference.\par \par \uc2 The interesting thing on this one is you do have at least the first billionaires Michael and \uc2 Susan Dell\uc2  saying, OK, here's $6.5 billion to get us started.\par \par \uc2 And remember when \uc2 Warren Buffett\uc2  started the giving pledge, and this was to ask all the billionaires and pressure all of them to say you're going to give away all your wealth before you die. And so, that's what \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2  and all of them are not -- maybe not all, but many of these billionaires signed up for the giving pledge.\par \par \uc2 I think that this is very interesting, because maybe this could be part of the giving pledge where you can fulfill it, but you know that your legacy is moving forward, rather than you are giving it to, you know, the blah blah.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: The blah blah? What's that? You mean, cars for kids? No, it's interesting, because we don't -- like people just don't really look into charities. And this is so simple. My question is, and Jesse, you're probably wondering the same thing, is that just the kids' money? I mean, can we take it if we want it?\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I thought they were getting their own set of wheels. I thought that's what -- it didn't matter if the kid was seven or 17, he was going to get a hot rod.\par \par \uc2 I don't know. A lot of things are scams. I give my money to different types of charities.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Really, like what?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Jesse Watters Foundation. No, I actually am so generous. For some reason, that's not what generous people say. I know. But for some reason, over the years, I've gotten such a bad rep, mostly because of you about how I don't tip.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You said you don't tip.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That's not true. I'm such a generous tipper. I am. Like, that's like one of my best qualities, tipping.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You once door dashed for like, a $5.00 coffee. Do you remember that?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: No, no. What's the question?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I don't remember.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I don't care.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Talk about --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: What do you want me to talk about?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Gas prices are insane, but the media won't talk about it, because the media doesn't drive.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That's a good point. They \uc2 Uber\uc2 , or they're driven, but gas prices are down, rents down, and mortgage rates are coming down.\par \par \uc2 If I were Trump, I wouldn't say everything is great all at once, because it's not. It's getting great. And there are certain things that are amazing, but there are such thorny problems, because Biden drove up prices so high that it's difficult to get out of this.\par \par \uc2 So, if I were the president, I would just say, listen, hold my hand, I will take you to the Golden Age. This is what I've done. This is what I'm doing. This is what's going to happen. I would not say affordability hoax. Yes, it's a hoax that the Democrats are politicking on the issue of affordability, because they're the ones that made it unaffordable. But you can say the issue is a hoax, because there's things that are unaffordable and they're just going to run ads that say affordability hoax, and you're going to get killed.\par \par \uc2 I would just hammer these things, energy, stock market, jobs, jobs, jobs. This A.I. situation, the data centers, if you're a blue collar guy, you're making six figures doing business with these data centers. They can't even find enough blue-collar workers to do the job.\par \par \uc2 The amount of foreign investment that's pouring in, the mineral deals, it's an incredible amount of investment that's taking place. Is that going to be enough to change people's attitudes before the midterms? Maybe not.\par \par \uc2 But if you have some sort of solution for health care, I don't know what it is, cut out the middleman of Obamacare. If you have a solution for the insurance rates which are out of control, or the cost of tuition which is out of control, you talk about it and you talk about it over and over and over and over again.\par \par \uc2 Schumer talking about high prices is insane. He made the prices high. But you know what? It's Trump's economy now. That's politics. He owns it now, and for better or for worse, it's going to be the Trump economy. So, that's what I would do. I would hit those and I would hit him hard.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Wow. All right, you know Harold, speaking of Chuck, he said that Trump lives in an effing bubble. My theory is either you swear or you don't, right, what does effing stand for?\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Not sure, it's good to be back. I hadn't seen you guys in a while. I'm happy to be back around the table.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: We missed you, Harold.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: You asked a question about why did anyone come up with this, with the risk of sounding completely self-serving? 25 years ago, I did in Congress, introduced a baby account bill. We were inspired by something that happened in Australia. President Bush was in office, and there was an effort to try to be helpful to us. \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2 , who is in this building, also was a big proponent of what we're trying to do.\par \par \uc2 I cannot be more thrilled that Democrats, Republicans alike and President Trump is doing this. I hope they do it even more.\par \par \uc2 What I think it does, it gives kids who feel left out, who feel left behind, who feel divorced from the American dream. More Americans today feel that the American Dream is out of reach for their kids, and even their kids feel that way. We got to get keep people not only involved but invested in believing they can be owners, and this is one way to do it.\par \par \uc2 Two, I agree with you about 100 percent on what you just said. I think that Democrats are right to talk about affordability, and I'm concerned that President Trump doesn't understand how serious this issue is. Now maybe he's playing politics with it at the moment, this reminds me of when many around this table often and correctly talked last year, year and a half ago, about the importance the power of the issue of the border, when Democrats, including \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , were saying, we have it under control, when the Secretary of Homeland Security said, there's no problem here, we often around this table were critical of them.\par \par \uc2 I've said for a long time, politicians who ignore voters, who believe they know what's best for voters, or believe that the numbers show that voters are living a better life than they are, those politicians end up at home.\par \par \uc2 The last election, just a few weeks ago, showed us that people are concerned about this issue, and even the Tennessee congressional race, although the Democrat didn't win that race probably should not have been as close as it was, and I think it was because of affordability.\par \par \uc2 The only message I have for President Trump is what Jesse just said, you got to get out and give answers. Because if you believe you can say, just wait and wait and wait, people will wait, but your party will lose.\par \par \uc2 I think the winners in this whole thing, Greg, are the voters, the American public. Because if Republicans start focusing on affordability and Democrats, you got to hope that they're able to create a set of outcomes that improve the lives of every American.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Well, since you brought up immigration, I think there's a better analogy here, that like, if the Democrats suddenly came up and said, we're fighting illegal immigration, we would laugh at them. That's why we're laughing at them now, when they talk about affordability, because they created the spike in prices.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: You may be -- I don't disagree with you, my point is different. When Democrats said that the border was under control. For President Trump to say that this is a con job or hoax, that's what you were saying. This is not a con job or a hoax. This is real.\par \par \uc2 Democrats ignored the border at their peril. And you know, the partisan in me says that I should tell President Trump to ignore it so Democrats can win. I want more Americans to be able to afford their lives and to say it's not a problem, that it's a hoax. I think politically, that's a problem for the president, but more importantly, substantively, it's a problem for the country.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: All right, then, well, that was exciting, wasn't it?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Very good to be back around the table.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: You're very green.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: No, but you're very, very green, Kermit. All right, up next, the Pentagon, blowing the liberal media narrative on coke boats to smithereens.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Brand new information blowing a huge hole in the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reporting of a double-tap strike on a suspected Narco boat in the Caribbean. Navy Admiral Frank Mitch Bradley telling lawmakers that there was no kill them all order from Secretary of War \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  in the September 2nd attack that killed two suspected drug boat traffickers.\par \par \uc2 And ABC News now reporting those survivors were actually trying to salvage the drugs. And Senator Tom Cotton, who saw the video with his own eyes, backs up that account.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): I saw two survivors trying to flip a boat loaded with drugs down for the United States, back over so they could stay in the fight and potentially give them all the context we heard of other Narco terrorist boats in the area coming to their aid to recover their car road and recover those narco terrorists.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And Democratic Congressman \uc2 Jim Himes\uc2 , who was in the room for Admiral Bradley's testimony, even he and miss at the second strike was indeed on drug traffickers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM HIMES (D-CT): What I saw in that room was one of the most troubling things I've seen in my time in public service, the United States military attacking shipwrecked sailors, bad guys, bad guys, but attacking shipwrecked sailors.\par \par \uc2 Yes, they were carrying drugs. They were not in the position to continue their mission. The last thing I'm going to say is that the admiral confirmed that there had not been a kill them all order, and that there was not an order to grant no quarter.\par \par \uc2 That's all I've got today.\par \par \uc2 (END