acted by this. I think this is one of these stories where we're getting so distracted in the hair splitting that we're losing sight of the bigger picture. And the bigger picture should be, is this really the best way to combat narco terrorism?\par \par \uc2 And you know, I will continue to argue that the best way to do that is to reduce demand in this country. And we don't talk about that enough. We don't talk about it with our kids. We don't admit that people have a massive appetite for drugs in this country, and even if we're blowing the living crap out of boats in the Caribbean, they will find another way. It is incredibly lucrative.\par \par \uc2 These are multibillion-dollar agencies. These are not just cartel families that we saw in Narcos Mexico, which is one of my favorite series on \uc2 Netflix\uc2  ever. These are massive enterprises that are incredibly creative and adept at finding new ways to deliver to American noses exactly what they want. And that's what we have to deal with this in this country, because going drugs are bad, OK? It has not been enough of a deterrent.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's the -- that's the, I have to disagree. That is what makes this so different. It's not just saying drugs are bad. It's like, no, we realize the war on drugs was a failure. This is an actual, literal, we're taking it to them, and especially since.\par \par \uc2 We're not talking about drugs anymore, we're talking about really destruct one little fentanyl, like a kid going and getting coke or Xanax and dying. That is not what we -- that's not what we're talking, that's not recreational partying drugs. This is basically homicide.\par \par \uc2 You're killing hundreds of -- guy goes out, does -- buys a Xanax off a friend and he dies. That's not recreational drugs, that's murder. So, I think it's a different thing than the way you portrayed and how dare you.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: I agree with you there, and that's what's so scary, is kids have an appetite for doing things that are dangerous, they cross the line, make their parents really upset, and it's like, I have a child in college, and it scares the crap out of me.\par \par \uc2 Because this stuff is so incredibly pervasive, and it doesn't matter the lectures or how you raise them, it is a moment of curiosity, you're absolutely right, that takes someone's life. And I cannot tell you how many people I know who either lost a family member to fentanyl overdose. You know, a good friend of mine lost her stepson because he did cocaine for the first time that was laced with fentanyl, and those are the people who were dying.\par \par \uc2 But it's still an appetite and a curiosity and a form of rebellion that we have not gotten our heads around.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: If I could, just before we go to Harold and Jesse, I'm just going to play this. Because in 1989 somebody that we used to see on T.V. periodically when he was at the White House. He said this in 1989, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Let's go after the drug lords where they live with an international strike force. There must be no safe haven for these narco terrorists, and they must know it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Isn't that the same policy?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Sort of. I agree with Kennedy. One, I hate to disagree with my colleague in the table. I agree with you this. You said it well, the appetite and curiosity, it has to be both. I don't have an issue at the same time of going after these narco terrorists in these boats.\par \par \uc2 But it would seem to me, DP, what might be better to reconcile I think even what then Senator Biden was saying, maybe intercept these guys. Find out who the big guys are behind them. They're not the ones -- these guys in these little boats, I want to stop them, but they're not the guys that are behind these massive criminal enterprises that are flooding our country with these drugs.\par \par \uc2 And we are kidding ourselves if we don't address what Kennedy so eloquently laid out, my kids are only 11 and 10, but I don't -- you just don't know what they're going to touch or what's going to happen. You got to drill in them, that appetite, that curiosity, we got to figure out how we stop that.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You can do both.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: You can do both. Right.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You can do both.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: I know, but so -- but I don't think this is necessarily the best way to do it.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: So, you can't do both?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: No, the best way -- no, when I say, I don't think blowing up these boats, sure, some of them, but figure out who's behind all of this.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I think you could do that and you could do what you're talking about. You can do three things.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: You can do four, I agree with you. I agree with you.\par \par \uc2 Now, I will say this. I remember after Operation Midnight Hammer, there was a lot of media frenzy and speculation from the left and the right and about what happened, how successful the mission was, and Secretary Hegseth and Lieutenant General Caine, who I think at the time call them raising Caine might have been the Joint Chiefs already. They stood before the -- before the press corps and took a variety of questions.\par \par \uc2 As I listened to Secretary heck Seth and President Trump and Miss Leavitt and Admiral Bradley, they don't all seem to be on the same page over the last few days. DP would have a much better and smarter perspective on this than I do, but as I just listen to them, I think it would behoove the secretary and this Admiral Bradley, to stand before and answer some of these questions, the questions they can answer, so we can get some of the nonsense behind this.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: You don't think they did that today?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: They didn't -- they didn't do it publicly. 