ns. I don't have a problem with it if they do this for every single president regardless of gender, regardless of gender or age or ideology or party.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And Harold, every time anyone comes face to face with a liberal journalist, they should pull a Bessent because they really haven't still be been held to account.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: So, I don't know if they should pull a Bessent. I think the secretary was very clever there in answering that question. I think it's legitimate. I think -- I agree with Kennedy. It's appropriate, legitimate to ask questions of any president. And there's no doubt the media failed during the Biden era. It was not until President Biden -- it's not until the -- his poor performance against President Trump in that debate that you begin to see in mass a course of Democrats said he should not be the nominee.\par \par \uc2 If I were Secretary Bessent, what I was hoping to hear also from that are just some more idea -- some more of their ideas about reducing prices. That's what I'd be focused on. But look, he was clever to do that. And you know what? Not only is he clever, he was right. The media didn't ask those questions of President Biden, but that shouldn't preclude them from asking questions about President Trump.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Dana?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, the thing is that the Trump Administration, the entire cabinet is willing to go and talk to anybody. Like Tom Homan does the Daily Podcast, does the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  podcast. He does it regularly and it -- they ask him very tough questions and he's willing to do it. The Biden Administration doesn't do any of that. They would -- they would --- they would get so mad if you didn't ask them softball questions.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to Trump's health versus Biden's health, I think if I were Andrew, I would have just skipped that question. You know, you don't have to ask every question. I think one thing he could have asked him is about - - Andrew Sorkin just wrote a book. It's a bestseller. It's called 1929. And it looks at the year before the crash. I would -- I would have loved to ask Bessent where do you think we are? If we look -- take lessons from history, where are we right now when it comes to the stock market? And see what he thinks about the A.I. bubble. Like, that would have been very -- and maybe he didn't also -- not playing the whole clip. Maybe they did ask about that as well.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Greg Gutfeld.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You know, the secret to effective persuasion is when you go for the throat and you smile. I love -- I love Bessent because whenever he's making his point, he's enjoying it. You know, he's he eviscerates people, but he's just having a great time when he does it. There's -- I've really never seen him angry when he's doing this. And that's -- it's a testament to some -- the people that Trump has picked this time around. They're effective and they're smart.\par \par \uc2 How did Sorkin not see this coming? It was like \uc2 Jerry Nadler\uc2  walking down a desert. It's like you could see it a mile away. You know, are you that like, you know, out -- trapped in your close circle of friends? Every liberal should know by now that after the Biden coverup, anytime you mention Trump's cognitive ability, you will be humiliated. It's like OJ Simpson coming up to you and saying, hey, shouldn't you treat your wife better? It doesn't work. He's dead, by the way, so it's impossible.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Coming up next, do you buy this? Taylor and Travis never had a fight.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Welcome back. Now, do you buy this? Taylor Swift and Travis Kelce have never had a fight. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS KELCE, TIGHT END, \uc2 KANSAS CITY CHIEFS\uc2 : Well, it's only been two and a half years, and you're right, I haven't gotten an argument.\par \par \uc2 JASON KELCE, FORMER PROFESSIONAL FOOTBALL CENTER: All right.\par \par \uc2 T. KELCE: Never once.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Jesse, is he brilliant or is he not telling us the truth? Or both?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Well, I don't know. I'm trying to think of the last time Emma and I had a fight. It was -- we don't fight ever. But there was something. I wouldn't call it a fight. Come to me last. Let me think about it. Let me think. Let me think about it. I think there was a --\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Lisa, what's your thoughts on this.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: He's terrified of her. That's why he will never fight back because she's a billionaire and he is on the waning end of his career.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: D.P., you believe this?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I think that people who never have even tussles or disagreements, maybe he thinks of a fight as something else, however he defines it.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: That's a good point. Maybe --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: But like, if you don't ever have sort of with your spouse, then it makes it harder when like the big things come up.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Amen.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Mr. Gutfeld, your thoughts on this?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. I mean basically saying he's going to learn how to fight while he's fighting with her when they're married because you will. That's like -- that's like -- imagine a football player approaching the field and then looking at you. You know, I never played a lick of football. That's exactly what he just said. You know arguing to women is what sports is to men.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Now, Jesse, you have -- you have thoughts on this now?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: He -- Greg is -- Greg is absolutely right. You never argue with a woman. You turn into a woman. What you want to do with a woman --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Is that what I said?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes. No, it's true. You can't argue with them. You just have to not fight. You just have to be silent. You give her the silent treatment. Just breathe.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Say, yes, dear.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Maybe go into the other room. You don't say yes. You don't say yes, Dana.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes, dear.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: See, that's her advice. Guys, don't listen to her. Listen to me. And then eventually they calm down.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: You think Travis listened to that? We'll see. "ONE MORE THING." "ONE MORE THING" is up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Dana.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: OK. I saw this today, and I thought you might want to see. It's a tough -- it's not always easy being a reporter out in the field. Watch here what happened to this young woman? Oh!\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: What was that?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That's a bird.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Oh.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Ow.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: They call that a Fabio.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: You think Johnny has ever been through something like that? That's a real veteran right there. Glad she's OK.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Are you?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I don't think -- I don't believe that for a second. All right, it's me.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: OK.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Tonight, Michael Loftus, Tudor Dixon, Kat Timpf, Tyrus. That's tonight.\par \par \uc2 Let's do this. Greg's absolutely disgusting news with Brit Hume. Check out this baby kangaroo peeking out of its mom's pouch. See that little thing?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Whoa.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You think it's a belly button, but it's a newborn kangaroo.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I thought it was something else.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: At the Chester Zoo. No, it's not a penis, Jesse. Baby kangaroos are called joeys, much like my good friend from New Kids on the Block, Joey McIntyre. Kangaroos are born into their mother's pouches. Stay there six to nine months, like my good friend Juan Williams does when he's on vacation. Anyway, it's safe and it's warm and it's well-fed. Look at that little thing poking out of there, huh? Yes, I see what you mean, Jesse. It does look like a penis.\par \par \uc2 All right, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Do they stay in the pouch for nine months?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Oh, my gosh.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: What? Dana, are you all right? Oh, I think we know what's going on there. Tonight, "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME," Tony Robbins has a big announcement, 8:00 p.m., 8:00 p.m.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Harold.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Wow. I don't even know how to get into my thing. But \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 , we lost Ohio State over last weekend, but our basketball team is now ranked number one in the country. Had three big wins over the Thanksgiving weekend against San Diego State, Auburn, Gonzaga. Were the first team in AP poll era to ever win two consecutive games by 30 points more. And they were both ranked teams. Go Blue.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Just don't let Chris Webber call a timeout. Yes, look at this tiny Seal walked into a bar in New Zealand, said -- bartender says, we have a drink named after you. He said, really, you have a drink called chancy? He crawled under the dishwasher. They got him out with salmon. That really happened.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Oh, salmon.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's it for us. Have a great night, everyone.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020251205elc400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  Addresses Controversial Drug Boat Strike; Lawmakers Demand Answers...\b0\par\par\uc2 John Roberts, Bill Melugin, Edward Lawrence, Mary Katharine Ham,  Jonathan Serrie \par \uc2 8,007 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 December 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  Addresses Controversial Drug Boat Strike; Lawmakers Demand Answers From \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Administration; Suspect Of National Guard Shooting Facing Several Charges; Administration Pushes To End Mass Migration; \uc2 Steve Witkoff\uc2  And \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2  Meet With \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  In Moscow; Millions Hammered By First Significant Snowfall; Major Snowstorm Hits Northeast; Major Donation To Fund Trump Accounts For Kids; CEO Pledges $6.25 Billion To Trump Accounts; House Of Representatives Holds Hearing On Possible Legislation To Restrict Use Of Social Media By Minors; Tennessee Starts Voting In Special Election For U.S. Representative; U.S.\par \par \uc2  Supreme Court Hearing Case Involving State Investigation Into Faith-Based Pregnancy Crisis Center; Trump Administration's Second Strike On Alleged Drug Boat In Caribbeans Draws Accusations Of Possible War Crimes; President Trump States Pardons And Commutations Signed With Autopen During Biden Administration Null And Void; Official Charges Brought Against Afghan Accused Of Shooting Two U.S. National Guard Members\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST: You did a great job, Jessica.\par \par \uc2 KAYLEIGH MCENANY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: Good job, Jessica.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening. And welcome to Washington. I'm \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the Afghan National accused of killing a National Guard soldier and wounding another is facing formal charges tonight. Congress takes on the chat bots amid increasing concern over the dangers of social media for children.\par \par \uc2 And a huge investment in America's future as donors infuse cash into accounts for children.\par \par \uc2 But first, breaking tonight, President Trump says he will strike within Venezuela if necessary in order to prosecute his war on narco terrorists. The president hosted his ninth cabinet meeting of his second term today.\par \par \uc2 The controversy over the attacks on alleged drug votes continues to be a major focus. Senior White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich starts us off tonight. She's on the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, John. Secretary of War \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  denied seeing survivors after the initial strike, describing those moments as the fog of war, both he and the president praised the admiral who they say, made that call.\par \par \uc2 Incidentally, survivors of subsequent strikes have been rescued, but it's unclear if an order for that was made.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice-over): Sitting beside the president in another marathon cabinet meeting, war Secretary \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  explained he wasn't in the room September 2nd when Admiral Mitch Bradley ordered a second strike on an alleged drug boat, killing the initial survivors.\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, U.S. SECRETARY OF WAR: As you can imagine, the Department of War, we got a lot of things to do, so I moved on to my next meeting. Couple of hours later, I learned that that commander had made the -- which he had the complete authority to do, and by the way, Admiral Bradley made the correct decision to ultimately sink the boat and eliminate the threat.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice-over): That wasn't initially revealed September 3rd.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: I watched it live. We knew exactly who was in that boat. We knew exactly what they were doing, and we knew exactly who they represented.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: No, I wouldn't have wanted that.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice-over): President Trump said this weekend he wouldn't have wanted a second strike, today, adding this.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I didn't know about the second strike. I didn't know anything about people, I wasn't involved. I can say this, I want those boats taken out, and if we have to, we'll attack on land also, just like we attack on sea.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice-over): Of the 21 strikes since September 2nd, two have left survivors. October 16, two were repatriated to Ecuador and Colombia. October 27th, U.S. forces initiated search and rescue for one survivor of unknown origin, then handed off control to Mexican authorities. Hegseth says he's empowered commanders to make decisions after getting the campaign underway.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Now, the first couple of strikes, as you would, as any leader would want, you want to own that responsibility.\par \par \uc2 So, I said, I'm going to be the one to make the call after getting all the information and make sure it's the right strike. That was September 2nd.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice-over): But emphasized Bradley was responsible for the follow up hit and denied giving an initial order to kill everybody. President Trump says the anti-drug military campaign will continue wherever it leads.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Anybody that's doing that and selling it into our country is subject to attack.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Necessarily just Venezuela?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: No, not just Venezuela, no.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): New tonight, a U.S. official tells Fox, Bradley ordered the second strike to sink the boat because it could have posed hazard to navigation for other vessels, or survivors could have called back up to retrieve the goods on that boat. Those details are among the many that are likely to be the focus of Congress later on this week, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right, Jacqui, President Trump making some more news on Truth Social just a few minutes ago, going after the auto pen again and saying all pardons and commutations signed by the auto pen are now considered null and void?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Yes, that just happened a couple of moments ago. You'll recall the president, a few days ago, stated that Hunter's pardon would likely be valid because he believes that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did it, and also on Friday, he undid executive orders from the previous administration. But this seems to take that a step further. Of course, we'll have to get more details, because there were a lot of Pardons and commutations that came down in the final days and weeks of the Biden presidency, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right, get back to us if you've got some more on that. Thank you. Jacqui, for us on the North Lawn.\par \par \uc2 Bipartisan lawmakers are demanding answers about those military strikes against drug boats. Some of them believe the president should issue a declaration of war. Here's our Senior Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice- over): Bipartisan questions about U.S. strikes in the Caribbean.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It'd be nice to hear the explanation. It's not allowable to target two survivors on the water if that was the purpose.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice-over): The administration says Navy Admiral Frank Bradley ordered the strikes. He speaks with lawmakers Thursday.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He will tell us the truth. I want to hear his rendition of the story.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice-over): One Republican even suggested the U.S. is actually at war.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is wartime.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Why not have a declaration of war?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Maybe we'll get there. This won't be an endless war.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: But it would be a war.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It'll be -- well, your definition of a war, right. This will be -- this will be very -- whatever we do there. it will be very quick.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice-over): One Republican accused the White House of shifting justifications for the strikes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You can't really just say, well, we're at war. We're not declaring war. But because we're at war, we say these people are combatants. We can do whatever we want, including killing them the first time, or if they're still floating around after we tried to kill him once, killing them again.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice-over): Lawmakers believe the administration owes Congress answers.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JON HUSTED (R-OH): I think it's getting to the point that we have the responsibility to know what the plan is and whether or not it does meet that definition of a war.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice-over): Some of President Trump's backers duck questions about the legality of the strikes.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): I'm not going to prejudge any of that. I was pretty busy yesterday, I didn't follow the news.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: I want to ask you about these strikes and things.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No, another time.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice-over): However, the president has his supporters.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: President Trump is clearly within his Article 2 powers. I didn't see Mark Kelly or anybody talking about \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  doing this stuff.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): Bipartisan lawmakers will trigger a war powers vote if the U.S. carries out what they say is an unauthorized strike on Venezuela, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And Chad, tell us about this inter party spat between speaker Johnson and \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Well, the two are scrapping over a provision Stefanik wants in the annual defense bill, it forces the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to tell Congress when it investigates candidates for federal office. Now Stefanik threatened to kill the bill if it's not included. She claims that Johnson is siding with the Democrats and safeguarding, "The deep state." Johnson denies what Stefanik is saying. Stefanik called this, "More lies from the Speaker of the House." John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right, Chad, we'll keep watching that one for sure. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 The Afghan national accused of killing one National Guard soldier and wounding another is now formally facing serious charges. Correspondent David Spunt lays it all out for us tonight. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: John, good evening. He is facing serious charges. The suspect appeared from his hospital bed with a translator on hand he met virtually before a judge. He's recovering from a gunshot wound from a National Guard member who returned fire at the crime scene.\par \par \uc2 Authorities say Rahmanullah Lakanwal planned his attack. These are photos from around the time of the shooting, which took place the day before Thanksgiving, middle of the day, just blocks from the White House. He's now facing four charges relating to the shooting of two National Guard members. Charges include murder, possession of a firearm and assault with intent to kill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, U.S. ATTORNEY FOR WASHINGTON, D.C.: We are pursuing everything. There is nothing off the table right now. This is an individual about whom we don't know a lot, but we will, trust me, before it's over.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: John, we're told federal charges are possible, the Federal death penalty may also be on the table. Now, Andrew Wolfe remains in serious condition at a local hospital. The West Virginia governor says the National Guardsman is making slow progress.\par \par \uc2 Still, those who know Wolfe believe he's a fighter and will recover. He's just 24 years old, and of course, Sarah Beckstrom, the other National Guard member who was shot and ultimately died, is being remembered by those who knew and loved her. She was just 20 years old, leaves behind family and friends who are devastated, remembering her as a hero for answering the call to come to D.C. to protect the nation's capital.\par \par \uc2 Now, a judge denied bond and is keeping the suspect in federal custody. His next court appearance is in Washington, D.C. early next month, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Lots to follow that -- lot to follow with that at least in the days and weeks ahead. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: The State Department is watching a major program that restructures overall immigration from nonwestern countries. This comes on the heels of last week's National Guard shooting. Senior Foreign Policy Correspondent and anchor Gillian Turner reports tonight from the State Department.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR FOREIGN POLICY CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): The state and Homeland Security Departments have a new agenda called remigration. It's meant to put an end to mass migration from so- called third world countries to the west.\par \par \uc2 MARCO RUBIO, U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: Why has he focused on mass migration? It's very simple, because no country is safe if you can just walk in without us knowing who you are, into your country. We've seen the destruction that that's reaching, not just in our own country, but the impact that it's having in Europe and in many other parts of the world.\par \par \uc2 TURNER (voice-over): The stakes have never been higher and the goal has never been more clear. Remigration now.\par \par \uc2 According to a cable obtained by Fox News, Trump officials plan to pressure America's closest foreign allies to follow suit. The State Department ordering American diplomats in Europe, Canada, Australia and New Zealand to further the president's goal of ending mass migration from the third world. Diplomats must now raise concerns about violent crimes associated with people of a migration background and any related human rights abuses, calling them, "Widespread disruptors of social cohesion and public safety."\par \par \uc2 Diplomats must also report back to Washington about immigrant crime in their host country and evaluate their host government's response, especially policies that unduly favor migrants at the expense of local populations. The so-called Trump travel ban already includes 19 countries of concern from which immigration has completely stopped, a list that officials are now looking to expand.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We could go one way or the other, and we're going to go all the way if we keep taking in garbage into our country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TURNER (on camera): White House Press Secretary Karoline Leavitt acknowledged from the podium yesterday that the U.S. refugee program is essentially shuttered, with the exception of one small minority group of South Africans. That reality, combined with this new remigration agenda, really signals John a seismic, systemic shift for U.S. foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Indeed, it does. Gillian Turner for us at the State Department. Gillian, as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Leaders in the twin cities say an operation by the Trump administration to target reportedly undocumented, Somali immigrants appears to be well under way. A local immigration attorney in the Somali community tells our Fox affiliate that he is hearing from clients that an immigration enforcement operation started over the Thanksgiving holiday weekend. Minnesota Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  says he expects an increased presence of immigration officers in the area.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street stocks bounce back today as both bond yields and Bitcoin stabilized. The Dow gained 185, the S&P 500 finished ahead 17, and the NASDAQ jumped 138.\par \par \uc2 Russia says today's talks between \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and President Trump's top envoy were productive, but Moscow says much work needs to be done. Senior Foreign Affairs Correspondent Greg Palkot has got the late breaking details for us. Good evening, Greg.\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, John. Yet the talks are wrapped up. They ran nearly five hours. A Kremlin official saying they were, "Useful, constructive and meaningful, with a lot of caveats."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice-over): Russian troops pushing through a key eastern Ukrainian town, Moscow, claims it's gained control. Kyiv says it's still fighting as diplomacy to end the war in Ukraine steps up, Special Envoy \uc2 Steve Witkoff\uc2  and Trump's son-in-law, \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2 , in what looked like a friendly session at the Kremlin with Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and key aides presenting the Trump administration's plan for peace. The president sounding determined.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: People are over in Russia right now to see if we can get it settled. Not an easy situation. Let me tell you, what a mess.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice-over): What started out as a proposal, some analysts said was tilted towards Russia, has reportedly been modified to give Ukraine more of a fair shake through talks between the U.S. and Ukraine and more talks with Ukraine and European officials, Putin had fighting words for them.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2 , RUSSIAN PRESIDENT (through translator): We are not planning to fight Europe, but if Europe suddenly decides to wage war against us, we are ready to fight now.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice-over): Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  looked for more support today in Ireland. He had his own tough words for Russia.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, UKRAINIAN PRESIDENT: Russia's invasion of Ukraine is a criminal and unprovoked act of aggression which tries for justice.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice-over): Both leaders gave credit to the Trump administration for its peace efforts, amid reports, key issues remain unresolved, like Ukraine giving up territory Russia hasn't taken. Whether Ukraine can ever join \uc2 NATO\uc2  and what to do with billions of dollars in Frozen Russian assets as the battles go on.\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET.), FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: The fighting will continue, and likely some of the talking will continue, at least for the near term.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (on camera): What the Russian official added, while several peace options were shared in the talks, compromise on that crucial territory issue was not found. And perhaps most telling you stated, we are no closer to resolving the crisis in Ukraine. To be continued, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Indeed, to be continued. Greg Palkot for us in London, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, millions of folks are digging up from a powerful winter storm, and it's just the first week of December. We'll have a live report.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight, federal officials confirm that former Honduran President \uc2 Juan Orlando Hernandez\uc2  has been released from prison following a pardon from President Trump. Hernandez was sentenced last year to 45 years in prison for his role in a drug trafficking operation that moved hundreds of tons of cocaine to the United States. President Trump says, Hernandez was set up by the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 Israel says it has received remains handed over by Palestinian terrorists in 