onation to the Trump accounts that was created under the One Big, Beautiful Bill. Now, the CEO of \uc2 Dell Technologies\uc2  and his wife will give $250 to the Trump accounts of children 10 years and younger in underprivileged areas. That equals $6.25 billion, making it the largest ever donation to children according to them. We asked if there was any shock within the family at that amount.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No, I mean, was there reaction between the two of you? Someone came up with the idea?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL DELL\uc2 , \uc2 DELL TECHNOLOGIES\uc2  CEO: Oh, sure. Oh, well, so we kind of started with a smaller amount to be honest, and we started talking about Texas only at the beginning. And --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Adopting the state of Texas.\par \par \uc2 DELL\uc2 : Yes. And we went back and forth, as we do on these things. And we --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Got a little more excited.\par \par \uc2 DELL\uc2 : And as children begin to understand that they have these accounts, that creates a real need for our schools, our parents, our community organizations to really begin to grow financial literacy in the country, which can only be a good thing.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So the goal not just to give kids a chance at the American dream but also teach an entire generation how to appreciate capitalism and invest to make money. Now, President Trump says that he talked with other wealthy Americans about making a similar kind of donation and believes Trump accounts could be part of his greater legacy. Senator Ted Cruz believes that with continued funding of these accounts, they could be worth six figures by the time the child is 25 years old. John?\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right, a lot of money there. Ed Lawrence for us at the White House. Thank you, Ed.\par \par \uc2 A number of alarming lawsuits are claiming that chatbots are encouraging children to kill themselves. That has caught the eye of lawmakers in Congress. Today a House hearing examined a package of bills aimed at protecting kids online. Here is our congressional correspondent Bill Melugin.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Big tech is knowingly and willingly wreaking havoc on our kid's mental health.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Online safety for children, a bipartisan priority on the Hill today as the House Energy and Commerce Committee held a hearing on a package of 19 bills, all designed to protect kids online, including banning children under age 16 from having social media accounts, limiting children from having access to disappearing messages, and requiring app stores to verify user ages.\par \par \uc2 REP. GUS BILIRAKIS, (R-FL) HOUSE ENERGY AND COMMERCE COMMITTEE: These bills are not standalone solutions. They complement and reinforce one another to create the safest possible environment for our children.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: The most high profile bill, a revamped visit of KOSA, the Kids Online Safety Act, a bipartisan bill first introduced in the Senate 2022. A new House version is now before this house committee.\par \par \uc2 REP. DEBBIE DINGELL, (D-MI) HOUSE ENERGY AND COMMERCE COMMITTEE: In this room, there are parents who lost their children because big tech failed to protect them.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: One of those parents is Lori Schott. She holds a daughter of her daughter Annalee, forever age 18 after she died by suicide.\par \par \uc2 LORI SCHOTT, PARENT: She took her life at our farm in eastern Colorado.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Schott told FOX News Annalee suffered from depression caused by TikTok and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  algorithms.\par \par \uc2 SCHOTT: When we were able to gain access into her online platforms, it was horrific. The content being that was pushed to her by design was suicidal.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Today's hearing, the fifth in five years for the committee.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It is time for us to act, colleagues.\par \