ltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: It appears that the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  may allow a faith-based crisis pregnancy center to press its First Amendment claims in federal court. The facility is challenging a state investigation into whether it misled donors and the public about steering women away from having abortions. FOX News chief legal correspondent and anchor of FOX NEWS SUNDAY Shannon Bream is here with the details tonight. Good evening, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, John, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 This case started way back in 2023 when the New Jersey attorney general sent a subpoena to First Choice Women's Resource Centers, a group of five faith-based facilities, although the attorney general's legal team admitted to the justices today they'd never gotten a single complaint about the centers, which provide various prenatal services to women facing unplanned pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 The New Jersey's A.G.'s office says it's all part of an investigation into whether the centers engaged in deceptive or unlawful conduct when it comes to services that they do or do not provide. That subpoena requested the names of donors to the pregnancy centers. Instead of turning them over, First Choice went to federal court, arguing that the subpoena would have a chilling effect on its constitutional rights to free speech and freedom of association. New Jersey argues that any worries about those donors' information being released is speculative at this point. Several of the justices across the ideological spectrum expressed doubt.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHIEF JUSTICE JOHN ROBERTS, SUPREME COURT: So you don't think it might have an effect on future potential donors to the organization to know that their name, phone number, address, et cetera could be disclosed as a result of the subpoena?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Justice Kagan also seemed skeptical that anyone facing a subpoena wouldn't see it as a potential threat.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE ELENA KAGAN, SUPREME COURT: An ordinary person, one of the funders for this organization or for any similar organization, presented with this subpoena is not going to take that as very reassuring.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So this case has brought together some interesting alliances. Both the Trump administration and the \uc2 ACLU\uc2  are voicing support for the pregnancy centers in this case. The court will take an initial private vote behind closed doors on Friday, then the waiting, John, begins. We expect a decision in the spring.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Strange bed fellows. This could be a seven to two.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It could. Politics and controversial topics sometimes do that in Washington.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: We'll see. Shannon, great report, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Voters are participating in a special congressional election tonight in Tennessee. Republicans are desperately hoping to hang on to open congressional seat and keep a slim margin in the House from narrowing even further. Our senior correspondent Jonathan Serrie is in Nashville tonight and has this report.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: As voters determine the future of the open seat for Tennessee's seventh congressional district, Democrat Aftyn Behn hopes to move beyond controversial statements she made in the past.\par \par \uc2 SAM SCOTT, VOTER: She told everybody she is not even about Nashville, or she hates country music. And that's what Nashville is all about is music.\par \par \uc2 AINSLEY SMITH, VOTER: I have seen several statements about her being radical, and frankly, that's what I think we need.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you all so much.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: With voting underway, Republican Matt Van Epps made a final push to rally his base to the polls.\par \par \uc2 MATT VAN EPPS, (R-TN) U.S. CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: What we are doing today is we're rejecting a socialist.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: This follows a day of campaigning with House Speaker Mike Johnson and a virtual rally with President Trump, who called for another Republican victory in a district he carried by 22 points last year.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENT: So it was like two points less than that, they were like, oh, that's terrible. I guess Trump is losing his magic. I want Matt to do better than me, even.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: The outside attention has nationalized the district seven race as a referendum on the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 AL GORE, FORMER U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: The stage is set, and I do believe that Tennesseans are determined to claw back decency, self-respect, and common sense from the most corrupt and dangerous president in American history.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: Former Vice President \uc2 Al Gore\uc2  spoke during a virtual rally along with other national Democrats Behn embraces, despite Republicans labeling her the AOC of Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 AFTYN BEHN, (D-TN) U.S. CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: Representative \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2 , of which apparently I hold her name in Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: That's Jonathan Serrie with a report from Nashville where we're going to be monitoring the results all even long here on FOX and we'll bring it to you just as soon as we get the election results final.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the continuing controversy over blowing up alleged drug smuggling boats. And then, getting into the holiday spirit on giving Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. TED LIEU, (D-CA): I served on active duty as a JAG for four years, and let me be very clear -- killing shipwrecked survivors is a war crime.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JONI ERNST, (R-IA): I think it's really important that as members of Congress who do have that oversight authority get the correct information before making any judgment.\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, WAR SECRETARY: Admiral Bradley made the correct decision to ultimately sink the boat and eliminate the threat. It was the right call. We have his back.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: The takeout of drug smuggling boats a hot topic at today's cabinet meeting as we learn more about how a second strike against one boat unfolded on September the 2nd.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel now for more, Mary Katharine Ham, FOX News contributor, "USA Today" White House correspondent Francesca Chambers, and Tom Bevan, Real Clear Politics cofounder and president.\par \par \uc2 So, Mary Katharine, the argument would seem to be, was the second strike ordered to take out the boat or to take out the survivors?\par \par \uc2 MARY KATHARINE HAM, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Right. It's sort of hard to tell because between "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 's" credibility and "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 '" and then Hegseth's version, probably something in the middle is true, and we're getting to the bottom of that.\par \par \uc2 But look, I think today everybody was looking for whether there was trouble between Trump and Hegseth, and it seemed like they were quite happy to be sitting next to each other. I'm glad that Congress seems energetic about checking on this. These are serious allegations. This is life and death stuff. I do think there are legal questions here that need to be addressed seriously instead of by meme on X. And so I like having those questions asked, but it certainly seems like everything was fine at the table today, politically speaking.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: We're going to be hearing from Mitch Bradley himself, the special operations commander who was the joint special operations commander at the time when he appears before Congress. The question has been was \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  in the loop about the second strike, and did he tell Bradley take out survivors. Here is what Hegseth said about that today. Listen here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, DEFENSE SECRETARY: I did not personally see survivors, but I stand -- because the thing was on fire. It exploded and fire and smoke, you can't see anything. You have digital -- this is called the fog of war. This is what you and the press don't understand. You sit in air-conditioned offices, you're up on Capitol Hill and you nitpick and you plant fake stories in "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " about kill everybody, phrases on anonymous sources not based in anything, not based in any truth at all. And then you want to throw our really irresponsible terms about American heroes, about the judgment that they made.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: He said he did not see the second strike because he was in a meeting at the time. Is he off the hook, Tom?\par \par \uc2 TOM BEVAN, COFOUNDER, REAL CLEAR POLITICS: I don't know. I mean, it certainly seems from what "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reported, contradicting "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " with five sources instead of two, saying that Hegseth gave the initial order and didn't give anything after that. So that would seem to indicate that he is off the hook on that.\par \par \uc2 And then the question is, well, is Bradley on the hook? And by everything I have heard and read about him, he is a stellar officer has been doing this for quite some time. And within that context, there is a lot of gray area. He has a lot of authority within the chain of command to make these kinds of decisions.\par \par \uc2 And so I agree with Mary Katharine, though. I think these are important questions. They should be debated. The problem is, my problem is that Congress is so functional that we can't seem to have adult debates with these. You know, we have seen this movie a dozen times already. Next week they are going to have, the Democrats will get up there and grandstand and give five-minute lectures and then ask "yes" or "no" questions and then interrupt. We won't get anything, we probably won't get anything important out of that, unfortunately. This is where we are.\par \par \uc2 HAM: So many soundbites.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: So many soundbites.\par \par \uc2 Francesca, you have been doing extensive reporting about this. What do you think is going on?\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, "\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 ": This has certainly brought more scrutiny onto the Trump administration strikes on those boats, and you heard from Republican lawmakers over the weekend as well as Democratic lawmakers that they had questions about this. And defense secretary pushing back, as we saw, forcefully today. He said that he was aware of the second strike at the time, but he didn't hear about it until hours later was how he described that. Interestingly, the president really kicking it to Hegseth, though, during the meeting, saying that, you know, I'm getting my information from him. You know, he'll have to answer these questions.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right, I want to go to something that the president just posted on Truth Social. We know he is on the war path against the autopen. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's profile in the colonnade is that of an autopen as opposed to a photo. And this is what the president says now. He is taking on commutations and pardons. He said, "Any and all documents, proclamations, executive orders, memorandums, or contracts signed by order of the now infamous and unauthorized autopen within the administration of \uc2 Joseph R. Biden\uc2  are hereby null, void, and of no further force or effect. Anyone receiving pardons, commutations, or any other legal documents so signed, please be advised that said document has been fully and completely terminated and is of no legal effect. Thank you for your attention to this matter."\par \par \uc2 A bunch of people going to be going back to jail?\par \par \uc2 HAM: I'm not sure, because it was certainly legally questionable how the original pardons went down, and Biden had some unfortunate innovations in how he handed those out, some via blanket and not specific and via autopen.\par \par \uc2 However, the pardon power is pretty large. And I think it's also legally questionable whether you can claw a lot of this back. I think the way to focus on it might be to have Congress again, good luck to us, look at some reforms for how we deal with this power in the future.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: So is the president just twisting the knife here, or do you think this will have some effect?\par \par \uc2 BEVAN: I think he's twisting the knife a little bit. The autopen has been used previously. The question is, how much did \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  -- was it ever used without his expressed authority? And that is an interesting question that gets into whether those orders could be clawed back at some point. But just sort of, you know, unilaterally clawing everything back is probably not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: What have you found in your reporting, Francesca?\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: Biden has said that he did, you know, authorize those things. As far as whether or not Trump could do that or what is he up, he said this a couple days ago, too, on social media. The post tonight looking pretty similar to that in terms of whether or not he can. The White House has referred back to previous post on social media. The Office of Legal Counsel has previously said that presidents can use the autopen, so --\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Right. But, again, it's who was in the Oval Office at the time, and was Biden aware of this or was this just people around him who said we want to change this policy and so here is how we are going to do it.\par \par \uc2 The shooting of the National Guard members, which was just so tragic the day before Thanksgiving. Official charges have been filed against Rahmanullah Lakanwal. And here's the charges he is facing now. One count of murder -- which we expect will be in the first degree -- assault with intent to kill while armed, in possession of a firearm during a crime of violence. Kash Patel is talking to Laura Ingraham tonight on her program coming up in a few minutes. Here's what he said led to this. Listen here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KASH PATEL, FBI DIRECTOR: The problem with this case shows the emblematic failure of the Biden administration that anyone who came here from Afghanistan after the disastrous withdrawal, zero -- it's not like people are even partially vetted. They refused to do so. So it now falls to us in this administration to make sure we vet every single person that came in here legally or illegally or otherwise, and make sure there is not derogatory or criminal information or terrorist ties, and we are going to do that mission.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I remember, Tom, during the chaotic withdrawal Afghanistan, the Biden administration assured us over and over and over again that every single one of those people who came out was thoroughly vetted. This would appear not to be the case.\par \par \uc2 BEVAN: And you would think that because he worked for the CIA in Afghanistan, he was part of an operation there, a group, that he would have been vetted prior to coming in. But I think it points to the fact that the administration, the previously administration, let it so many people, not just across the border, but via refugee and asylum and temporary protected status, hundreds of thousands, millions of people. There's no way the government could have vetted all of those people appropriately. And so I think that's what Kash Patel is speaking to. We have to now go back and do the work and make sure that anyone in this country even legally has been properly vetted.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I said to Tricia McLaughlin at DHS this afternoon, Mary Katharine, who said he was working, to Tom's point, with the CIA. Would they have not known a lot about his background? And she posited maybe he was vetted enough to work with the CIA in Afghanistan but not vetted to the point where you would want him living here.\par \par \uc2 HAM: That is a question, right. Those distinctions were not made in the tarmac in Afghanistan, right, because that was a slapdash, horrible process that was thrown together. So there's no denying that part of it. Even if he were vetted in Afghanistan, whether in normal circumstances we would deem that appropriate for where he ended up, those are two different questions.\par \par \uc2 And it also opens the question, there were many, many more. And I have seen many Afghan families come here and dine with friends of mine and have Thanksgiving dinner and be perfectly sort of integrated into society. So it's not everyone, but we can't ignore the fact that there were oversights here for sure.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: We had Lakanwal. There was that guy in Fort Worth who was arrested for wanting to make a bomb to blow up a building in Fort Worth. How many other ticking timebombs are out there when 76,000 people came here in the course of 18 days?\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: Speaking of DHS, \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  has now suggested that they are going to expand the president's travel ban from the 19 countries that was the full ban or a partial restrictions on before. So I will certainly be looking to see what additional countries they might add to that list and when they might do that.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I should say, came out of Afghanistan in 17 or 18 days. Took them longer to get mere. But they told us they were all vetted, but clearly not well enough.\par \par \uc2 Thanks, folks, good see you tonight. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And finally tonight, a special day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We are really punching above our weight as far as experiences that we offer, and the donors and the supporters who give on Giving Tuesday help make those things happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Today is Giving Tuesday, an international generosity movement. The CEO of United Way rang the closing bell at the \uc2 New York Stock Exchange\uc2  to celebrate the event. It started in 2012 as a simple idea to encourage people to do good. The Giving Tuesday Organization estimates a record breaking $3.6 billion was given last year in the U.S. alone.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, on SPECIAL REPORT, we will have an exclusive report on this year's Reagan National Defense Survey.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. eastern, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for watching SPECIAL REPORT. I'm \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  in Washington. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE with Kash Patel is coming up next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020251203elc200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Special Congressional Election Set For Tomorrow In Tennessee\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Kevin Corke, CB Cotton, Lawrence Jones \par \uc2 7,654 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 December 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of 