vacuating some Afghans, but among them many extremists.\par \par \uc2 And then to top it all off, the Biden White House continued to import thousands upon thousands of more unvetted Afghan nationals on top of his wide-open borders. More than 5,000 of them, that's over 5,000 were flagged for national security issues, but they let them into our country anyway. Nearly 7,000 had links, you know, to potential derogatory information, including nearly a thousand who were flagged over public safety concerns. That means concerns about you.\par \par \uc2 And as of today, there are individuals with national security concerns. We don't know where they are. They're still at large inside the continental U.S. But according to Alejandro Mayorkas, the administration's chaotic failure, disaster, dangerous plan to import unvetted Afghan nationals and lie about it was a great source of pride for them. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, FORMER DHS SECRETARY: Operation Allies Welcome which we led in a coordinated effort across the government is a great source of pride for us to bring to safety in the United States. Individuals who stood alongside our armed forces in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): To those who got on the C17s and other planes, how many were vetted before they got on the plane by American officials?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: To the United States, it is our policy to vet and screen 100 percent of them.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: So, 100 percent of those people that got on the plane were vetted. That's your testimony under oath.\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: It is our policy to do so.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Are you telling this committee under oath that 99 percent of the people who got on these planes in Afghanistan were vetted by our government before they got on that plane?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: I want to be very clear because precision --\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Because you're not.\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: No