o deal with the porn industry. They want the minds and souls of our boys and our men, sons, husbands. It's insidious. It's another insidious force working against the family. That society just tells us, oh, it's another form of choice. It's another form of expression. But the truth is the porn industry makes billions off the social, emotional, and spiritual carnage it leaves in its wake.\par \par \uc2 So, our families, in many ways, need guardrails. We got to do our best to keep them safe. Our society needs to protect our children's innocence, not shatter it. But many in education, the media, big tech, the entertainment industry make it harder and harder for parents.\par \par \uc2 Now for many years, decades even, we've seen children sexualized in film and in fashion. We've seen it on \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , other social media platforms and streaming services. Traditional families, they're portrayed as backward, Bible-thumping, stupid. The pursuit of fame or luxury brands, clicks, well, that beats family dinners any day.\par \par \uc2 And the toll, this entire mentality is taken on generations of young people. The toll emotionally? It's incalculable. And the slope is indeed slippery. From tank tops on girls to mid-drift blouses. Now, everything looks like girls are wearing bustiers. And our daughters? They see celebrities on the red carpet leaving almost nothing to the imagination, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And what have we become? When the entire -- we have entire fields of academic study devoted to understanding sickos, people who fantasize about abusing, sexually abusing children. Normalizing this condition by referring to them as minor attracted persons or MAPs. This is depraved.\par \par \uc2 We need to protect our families, protect each other, build up our societal resistance to all of this. That means really embracing our faith. That means parents speaking up, schools being held accountable for what they teach and how they teach it. And there's good news.