es are trying their best to intrude upon and even rip apart our families, we, as Americans, need Thanksgiving more than ever. Because we need to remember the importance of each other, the almighty and tradition that holds us together.\par \par \uc2 You bet we live in a fallen world. Of course we do. But we're a blessed people. So set aside the politics, the family squabbles, and make good memories this Thanksgiving. And that's the Angle.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, parents fight back after a school is accused of forcing their young daughter to share a bed with a biological male. Speaking of ripping families apart and family values, they join us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This is wild. Two parents are really, really mad, so mad they're suing a Colorado school district after they say it tried to force their 11- year-old daughter to share a bed with a biological male during an overnight field trip two years ago.\par \par \uc2 Joe and Serena Wailes said that they were told that she'd be sharing a room with three female students, but one of them was actually a transgender. The daughter called her mom, who at the time was, at the same time, serving as chaperone at the event. The daughter eventually got to switch rooms, but was told not to discuss why.\par \par \uc2 So, her parents sued the school district, but the case was initially dismissed by a Biden-appointed judge. Now it's being appealed to the 10th Circuit. Here with me exclusively are those parents, Serena and Joe Wailes, and their attorney Matt Sharp, senior counsel of \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Serena, I remember when this first happened, I believe we covered it. So, why was your daughter told not to discuss why she was being moved?\par \par \uc2 SERENA WAILES, MOTHER SUING SCHOOL DISTRICT: Originally, they said that because they wanted to protect the privacy of the other student. And our thoughts are, what about the privacy of our daughter? But initially that's exactly why I said --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- didn't deserve any privacy.\par \par \uc2 S. WAILES: Right.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, and to your husband, as a father of a daughter, especially. I can't frankly imagine my reaction to this. It probably wouldn't have been as genteel as yours going to court. But did you try to make it known to the school early on before this lawsuit that this was completely wrong? And what was their initial response?\par \par \uc2 JOE WAILES, FATHER SUING SCHOOL DISTRICT: We didn't talk to school after this. We looked at the different options and we felt like the best course was taking legal action to make a difference. There's several issues going on within the Jeffco schools where we didn't feel like we'd be heard. We didn't feel like we'd get anywhere by talking directly to the school or even to the district.\par \par \uc2 We felt like we needed to do something to really take a bigger action. We felt like, you know, this work two years into this now, so it's definitely been a grind, but we made the right choice.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This is just insanity. I just did this whole Angle on how the family is under attack by the culture society education, woke educators. And Matt, you know, this case was brought on behalf four families. What sort of relief are you hoping for? Are looking for the school to change policies? Is there damages here? And if so, what are they?\par \par \uc2 MATT SHARP, ALLIANCE DEFENDING FREEDOM: We want parental rights protected. We want parents to be informed about bad policies like this. And we want their request to be honored. When they ask schools, don't force our daughter to share a room with a boy, the school needs to honor that. It needs to respect parents. It needs to protect girls' privacy. So, we want to see this policy enjoined and ultimately changed.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But are there damages that the parents here are claiming because of what the girl was told?\par \par \uc2 SHARP: Well, the real damage is the violation of the girl's privacy. And not just the Wailes' daughter, but in one of the other families, their boy was forced to share a cabin with a girl for an entire week where this girl was assigned to monitor the boys when they were showering, dressing, changing. So, there is damage to these kids, their privacy rights, and ultimately these parental rights as well.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This is completely insane. I can't believe we're doing this segment. Now, Serena, a Biden-appointed judge dismissed that original filing with prejudice back in August.\par \par \uc2 But Judge Regina Rodriguez wrote in that ruling, while parents may have the right to instill moral and religious values in their children, parents have no right to replace public education with their own personal views, nor a right to control each and every aspect of their children's education, et cetera, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 What does that make you feel like as a parent when your tax dollars are going to support that attitude?\par \par \uc2 S. WAILES: It makes me angry because this is a parental rights issue. Schools and parents should be joining each other in order to do what's best for their children. And when the school district hides information from us, it doesn't allow us parents to be part of that conversation. And then it puts our kids in risk situations that we could have resolved before all of this even happened.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: When it came to the curriculum issue the Supreme Court said earlier this year that parents must be given essentially a right to opt out of this, and you all should have been given, at the very least, a right to opt out of this before they ever gotten as far as it did.\par \par \uc2 Serena, Joe, Matt, we'll be watching this case very closely at the 10th circuit. Thank you very much. And coming up, Hollywood makes cigarettes cool again, really? What are the consequences?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Thanks to Hollywood, I guess, now smoking? That's making a comeback with vaping's bad. Go back to smoking. According to the Journal, about half of all movies that made their debut last year included appearances of, well, starring roles, cigarettes. And celebrities, they're embracing it.\par \par \uc2 Check out pop star Sabrina Carpenter wearing a corset made of marble gold packages for a photo shoot. Lovely. And singer Lily Allen wore a nun's outfit, also classy, while smoking to promote a new album.\par \par \uc2 Here with the details, Dr. Marc Siegel, Fox News senior medical analyst, author of the new book, The Miracles Among Us. Dr. Siegel, is this because vaping, we've learned, is even worse for you? And the way it's inhaled and the damage it does to your lungs, so now they're going back to smoking. What the heck is going on?\par \par \uc2 DR. MARC SIEGEL, FOX NEWS SENIOR MEDICAL ANALYST: That's part of it. And by the way, vaping is not worse for you, but we, doctors, have been pounding on the vaping thing for so long that teens, and I spoke to a lot of Gen Zer's about this, are saying, look, you told us vaping was bad. We got to do something edgy, something risky, and the celebs are doing it because it gives them ratings in a movie to show a cigarette, it looks trendy, it looks cool. And so, now they're going back to it.\par \par \uc2 They're going back to it for what you just said. They think vaping is more dangerous because we've been telling them that. Vaping is dangerous, nicotine is dangerous in any way shape or form, to the heart, to the lungs, to the brain, nicotine.\par \par \uc2 But tar and cigarettes is even more dangerous because it leads directly to cancer, to cancer in many different organs. Thirty percent of cancers are tied to cigarettes.\par \par \uc2 We got teens down, Laura, to about six percent of our teens smoking cigarettes. It used to be 33 percent smoke cigarettes back in the 70's. We got it down to 6 percent. I absolutely believe it's coming back. And I'm not sorry that I've been saying vaping is dangerous, but teens are looking for something edgy, so they're back now to cigarettes.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, it seems to me that, you know, we know so much about this now. You know, my mom died of lung cancer many years ago. She smoked many years previous to that, but she took in lot of secondhand smoke at the restaurant she waited tables at. And I always think of her, of course, everyone else I know who suffered from lung cancer over the years.\par \par \uc2 But again, kids are so impressionable. Social media is so prevalent. And if something's trendy, whether it's a new workout outfit or new shoes, they'll try it. And you get addicted to cigarettes really quickly, and it's really hard to break the habit.\par \par \uc2 SIEGEL: Laura, I checked on something your producers brought to my attention. Again, my Gen Z sources say it's true. It's called the drunken cig. People in bars say, I'm not really a smoker, but when I get drunk, I pull out a cig.\par \par \uc2 Guess what they're doing, according to my sources, they go from the drunken cig to vaping to Zin, which is the nicotine pouch, and then they do all three, and cigarettes come back. And, you know, again, I'm very worried about the risk of lung cancer here.\par \par \uc2 I'm very sorry you lost your mother to that. And we don't need more -- we definitely don't need more of it. We don't need it in our kids.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: No. Dr. Siegel, have a wonderful Thanksgiving, and it's great to see you as always. Now that's it for us tonight. Have a wonderful Thanksgiving. We'll see you back here, Monday.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020251126elbp00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Agitators Swarm Broadview, I.L. ICE Facility; Journalist Nick Sortor Arrested In Portland; Portland Agitators Torch American Flag; L.A. Mayor: Trump Is Taking Advantage Of Military; Senate Stuck In A Shutdown Standoff; Day Three Of Schumer's Shutdown; Mamdani Plans To Phase Out NY Gifted Program For Kindergarteners Once Elected; Pope Leo The Fourteenth Comments On Being Pro-Life; Judge Subramanian Hands 50 Months Sentence For \uc2 Sean Diddy Combs\uc2 ; Drag Queen LaWhore Vagistan Talks About Ethnography And "Rupaulitics" At Harvard.\b0\par\par\uc2 Gillian Turner, Joey Jones, Garrett Tenney \par \uc2 8,156 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 October 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT, UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: God bless you, Bobby. I hope I didn't catch COVID just there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS HOST: All right. Please join me this weekend for Fox News Sunday. I'll be filling in for Shannon Bream. Secretary of War \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  is going to be interviewed on the Sunday briefing at 11:00 a.m. Eastern. Thanks for watching Special Report. I'm Gillian Turner in Washington.\par \par \uc2 JOEY JONES, FOX NEWS HOST: I'm Joey Jones, in for Laura Ingraham. This is a special edition of The Ingraham Angle, and thanks for watching.\par \par \uc2 Chaos through the day, as anti-ICE agitators swarmed the ICE facility in Broadview, Illinois. Hundreds of protesters were met with police in riot gear and snipers on the roof.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD CHANGING "SHAME ON YOU" & "LET THEM GO")\par \par \uc2 JONES: We're going to go live to that scene in moments.\par \par \uc2 But first, another night of clashes in Portland, Oregon. Our own Bill Melugin was there for all of it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Federal agents bull rushing and taking down a protester who stepped onto federal property despite repeated warnings. One of the protesters on a bullhorn mocking the agent, saying, what are you going to do about it if we get on the property? Well, you see the results of that. Then there was this fist fight. We were watching these two guys jawing at each other all night long. Eventually, the fists started flying. The guy in the white shirt is an ICE supporter. The guy in the black shirt is an anti-ICE protester. He is the one who got laid out with a punch to the face, and a short time later, we saw him collapse motionless on the ground with some significant swelling to his face. There was no Portland police anywhere to be seen when that happened.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yeah. Ring the bell. He is out. And now Portland police are being investigated by the DoJ after they arrested conservative journalist Nick Sortor last night.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yeah. That was the same Nick Sortor who was on this show hours before his arrest. He was criticizing the police for what he called their laxed approach to these riots.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NICK SORTOR, INDEPENDENT JOURNALIST: They have cops here. They're told to stand down. They're told not to help innocent civilians. But at this point, they are on the side of criminals and Antifa out here. They are the ones that don't get prosecuted. You saw Katie Daviscourt from The Post Millennial get hit with a pole the other day, and Portland police walked away. They were told by top brass to stand down. So, they're openly siding with Antifa terrorists out here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yeah. Nick says he was getting a video of protesters crying after they were maced by federal agents, when some of my guests swarmed him and pushed him into what appears to be a flower bed. You can kind of see it there. He says they threw multiple punches. They hit him and they broke his camera. He got away. He says he walked over to police, and that's when he says he was arrested.\par \par \uc2 Nick joins us now with more on this. Nick, tell us what happened exactly.\par \par \uc2 SORTOR: And it's crazy. The entire day has been crazy. I mean, it's still the same day as yesterday for me, because I've been up all night because I was stuffed in a jail. And the jail here in Portland weirdly empty, because they don't actually arrest criminals around here, just people like me that report on what's actually going on, on the ground out here. I am next to the ICE facility. I'm on federal property right now, and Portland police won't dare step foot on federal property, as we know.\par \par \uc2 But Joey, we know that these Antifa thugs hate to be filmed in general. They hate it even more when you film them while they're screaming and crying because they were just maced, because they effed around and found out by poaching federal agents. They're not as tough as they want you to believe that they are. And so, the last thing they want is for you -- to for somebody like me to film them while they're crying with mace in their eyes. So, unfortunately for them, I have a First Amendment right as an American citizen. I can film them in public on the sidewalk, and there is nothing they can do about it.\par \par \uc2 So, that's why they broke down. I wouldn't go away. I wouldn't stop filming them. And so, they got violent and aggressive, and they started punching me. They started -- they broke my camera.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SORTOR: And somehow, that's criminal on my part. Haven't figured that part out yet.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Nick, a lot has been made about the fact that you were kind of reporting on the police not responding.\par \par \uc2 SORTOR: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JONES: They were burning American flags, getting, I don't know, antsy, I guess. You're just reporting on the police and kind of what you would say is not being laxed about it, then all of a sudden, you get arrested. Now I know there is a tussle there, and if we watch football, it's the guy that shoves last that gets the flag called on him. What is your perspective on this? Do you think that the police knew who you were, arrested you because of it, or do you think that you were just the last one kind of swinging there, and that's why they grabbed you?\par \par \uc2 SORTOR: Well, considering the fact that that they don't arrest anybody here ever, especially, you saw Katie Daviscourt that black eye that she got. The police followed the assailant and did nothing. They just let them go. Right? Now, all of a sudden they change it when I am here, when a conservative journalist is on the ground, exposing what's actually going on. Is that just a coincidence? How did that just happen all of a sudden?\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's -- I don't really think it is a coincidence, unfortunately, Joey, but -- because I was -- I didn't have very glowing reviews for them on Laura's show last night when I was here, and it took them over an hour to figure out what the charge was. They arrested me. They put me in the back of the police car. We parked on the side of the road for a while, and then after about an hour, they're like, OK, yeah, we'll charge them with disorderly conduct. So -- which is, if what I was doing is disorderly, what do you call people with megaphones at three o'clock in the morning --\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SORTOR: -- lighting fires in the middle of the street? What is that?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Did you notice when the police got there, were the protesters, were they pointing their ire at all police, at the local police as well, or did they keep it on ICE? Like, did you see any type of, I don't know if it's relationship, or did the protesters say, oh, they're on our side, let's keep it pushing towards ICE? Describe some of that to us.\par \par \uc2 SORTOR: Yeah. So, they really don't seem to have any ill will toward the Portland police. But why would they? The Portland police have been running cover for them for years now. It's like they're in the pocket of Antifa. When you've got these -- Bill Melugin, he was here for a few days as well, talking to these liaisons. They have these liaison officers that they station around here. They are supposed to only intervene in cases of imminent death, right? So, if you're getting beat in the street until you're unconscious and bleeding out, they're not going to step in. I mean, they'll tell you that if you ask them.\par \par \uc2 And so, the Antifa people have gotten used to it where there is no action from Portland police. So, yeah, they feel like they're on the same side at this point. Especially, you should have heard the cheers from the crowd when I was arrested. They loved that.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Are you out there alone and unafraid? Actually, I think we got a video, real quick, of you pulling the American flag. If we have that, I'd like to play it.\par \par \uc2 SORTOR: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JONES: And -- yeah. Let's play that real quick.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We got a Trump supporter. We got a Trump supporter right here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He is putting it on the ground, and he is making it worse.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hey give it here (BEEP).\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Now he is standing on it. You're not supposed to stand on it. Oh and now he thinks he is saving it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK. So, it looks like one of the flying monkeys from Wizard of Oz was trying to tell you that you made it worse. Were there any other counterprotesters out there? Are you the only person there with common sense that cares about the American flag, that understands that ICE has a job to do? I mean, were you there alone and unafraid in that?\par \par \uc2 SORTOR: So, I definitely wasn't -- I wasn't there alone. There were some counterprotesters. But in terms of -- maybe I'm built a little bit differently, right? I was willing to run into that and grab that flag, put that fire out, and I still -- I actually have that flag now. It's in the back of my car. I posted a photo of that. Actually, Bill Melugin took a photo of me with it earlier in front of the ICE facility, and I still have it. I'm going to bring it home with me, and I'll cherish it the way that it should be cherished. These people, they hate our country. They hate our flag. They hate everything that we're about.\par \par \uc2 I'm telling you right now, Joey, this place doesn't feel like the United States of America anymore. It feels like a third-world communist country, and that's the way that the government out here treats the citizens as well. I mean, people like me are not welcome here by the government. They literally threw me in jail. They tried -- they want to intimidate us into shutting up and going away and not coming here because, it's pretty obvious to the American people when you show them what's actually going on here on the ground. This is like a 90:10 issue.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Well --\par \par \uc2 SORTOR: 90 percent of the people would not support what's going on here.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I mean, I guess you got into a tussle there later on, but they didn't give much of a fight when you grabbed the flag. At least you're able to do that. I don't know what the proper protocol is. Maybe put it in a frame next to your arrest warrant and remind people what the price of taking and standing up for freedom is.\par \par \uc2 Either way, Nick, stay safe out there, but continue to do exactly what you're doing. We need to see firsthand what's happening and the fact you're arrested last night and you're back out there today, just keep yourself safe and keep showing the story.\par \par \uc2 All right. Meanwhile, just outside of Chicago, it was a wild scene outside the Broadview ICE facility.\par \par \uc2 Fox News National Correspondent Garrett Tenney is here, and he has s got the latest. Garrett, what have you got?\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Yeah. Joey, here we have had huge crowds of protesters coming out every Friday for the past month, and this is the first time we are not seeing chaos, and that's because today, for the first time, state and local officials allowed police to actually be involved in the crowd control with these protests, and that's what they did this morning, is they cleared out this crowd of hundreds of activists that included some paid agitators, according to federal agents. We saw at least a dozen people taken into custody, and at least five are now facing charges, including two accused of attacking officers.\par \par \uc2 Homeland Security Secretary \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  was here in Broadview at the ICE processing center this morning. You can see this video of her on the roof where a sniper's nest has been put up as the threats against ICE have increased after more than a month of often chaotic protest at this facility. Last night, though, despite not allowing local police to be involved in crowd control until today, \uc2 Broadview\uc2 's mayor argued ICE is completely to blame for the chaos.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KATRINA THOMPSON, MAYOR, BROADVIEW: When you see agents dispersing tear gas or chemical minutia or rubber pellets, what do you call it? It's just -- it's unacceptable. We don't need to be policed. We know how to maintain.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: The question from federal officials, though, is, if you know how to police, why haven't you been doing it? And after more than a month of these chaotic protests, federal agents have been left on their own to deal with these huge crowds of hundreds of activists trying to keep ICE officers from bringing in the illegal aliens that they've arrested as part of Operation Midway Blitz. And the change you can see here to now tonight, it is just night and day from what we've been seeing over the past month, Joey.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Thanks, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 All right. Leftists around the country are causing absolute chaos from Portland and beyond. But if you ask Democrats, everything is, well, it's just fine. It's business as usual. There is no need for help from, say, the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TINA KOTEK (D), GOVERNOR, OREGON: Portland, Oregon is a beautiful, vibrant, successful city. We are not a war zone.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JEFF MERKLEY\uc2  (D-OR): Portland, so, is pretty calm and peaceful and recovering, and business have been coming back downtown. We've been recovering from the riots that Trump incentivized and stimulated back in 2020, and we're like, stay away.\par \par \uc2 JB PRITZKER (D), ILLINOIS GOVERNOR: We are telling this unwarranted and unconstitutional occupation by ICE and potentially by military troops to get out of Chicago. You are not helping us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yeah, and as they ignore the real trouble happening in their own cities and states, the left is busy tackling, well, what they call real issues, like blocking bathroom access in a public building.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Can we used your restroom?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No, you cannot.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We can't?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Please don't come in the door. Please don't come in the door.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK. Alright. Thank you. Coming to go out and do some operations to pick up some guys with criminal convictions on them and the city police wouldn't even let us use the