day to paint the rapper as a changed man, playing this highly produced video montage showing Diddy running the New York City Marathon, praying with family, speaking to school kids, and sharing tender moments with his children. Diddy cried as the video played and placed his face in his hands when his children were pleading to the judge to give their father a second chance.\par \par \uc2 Multiple defense attorneys also pleaded for leniency, asking for a sentence that would have let Diddy walk soon on time served, pointing to Diddy's tough childhood, his addiction to pain killers after surgery, and his influence in music and culture. One attorney broke down in tears, calling him a visionary who inspired an entire generation to believe they could be the label, not just sign on.\par \par \uc2 But prosecutors doubled down and asked the judge for a stiff sentence of more than a decade. They pointed to trial evidence and witness testimony, calling this a case about a man who did horrible things for his own gratification. And they highlighted testimony from accusers, including Cassie Ventura, who was captured in this brutal 2016 hotel surveillance video getting beaten by the rapper. She wrote to the judge ahead of today, saying she still has nightmares.\par \par \uc2 Attorneys for neither side chose to speak with the media after today's proceedings. I also want to point out, Diddy does have the option to appeal. Gillian, back to you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, CB Cotton in New York City for us tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The current queen of pop music is dropping her new album today. Taylor Swift's "The Life of a Showgirl" is accompanied by a big screen event in theaters across the country this weekend. FOX Business correspondent Madison Alworth is following all the details from New York.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MADISON ALWORTH, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Taylor Swift fans are excited. The pop star releasing her 12th studio album today and