LEADER: It's all about President Trump and the Democrats needing to pick a fight to satisfy their far left political base.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES, (D-NY) HOUSE MINORITY LEADER: They continue to try to jam their extreme rightwing ideology down the throats of the American people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARCO RUBIO, U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: How the war ends is the following -- every single hostage is released, all 48, including those that are deceased, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  no longer exists as an armed group that's threatening Israel. That's how the war ends. So the question is, how do we achieve it? And ideally in a perfect world, you would achieve it through a diplomatic agreement.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, time to bring in our panel. We have FOX News contributor Ben Domenech, host of "The Big Ben Podcast", Real Clear Politics White House correspondent Philip Wegmann, and FOX News senior political analyst Juan Williams. Gentlemen, thanks so much for being here on this fabulous Friday.\par \par \uc2 Ben, what do you make of this claim from \uc2 Hamas\uc2  that they have agreed to release the hostages, but really it seems like all they are agreeing to is to now negotiate two years into the negotiations.\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Gillian, I think we both know and I think everyone on the set knows at this point you can't take \uc2 Hamas\uc2 's word on any of this at face value. We should view it in dubious terms until they actually live up to anything that they commit to.\par \par \uc2 I do think that the president sent about as strong a message as I have seen him send today. And he seems to be coming -- I think he is increasingly vociferous about his about his frustration with this situation, that he believes that there has been a clear path to ending this and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has not been living up to their various inclinations toward trying to find some solution. I think he is fed up with it. He is done with this situation. He wants to move on. He has enough on his plate as it is.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Juan, the best case scenario here, right, is \uc2 Hamas\uc2  means what it is saying, it is going to release. That is one point in this 20-point plan. So even if they do that, right, they are going to release, we think, 20 living people, about 25 or so corpses, sadly, at this moment. And in exchange now Israel is going to release hundreds of convicted \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists and withdraw from Gaza. Seems stacked.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Not if you consider, I think it's more than 60,000 Palestinians have died. And clearly, the destruction in Gaza has been horrific.\par \par \uc2 But I think what Ben said is right. You can't trust much of what \uc2 Hamas\uc2  says in these negotiations. But to me, the power dynamic here in this moment is President Trump emphatically stating that Israel has to stop bombing, stop its military action. And we have ongoing assault right now on Gaza City. So they have got to stop immediately, in President Trump's mind. And if they don't stop, then what course does he take in terms of sanctioning the Israeli prime minister? It's a very interesting power dynamic in several directions, not only the U.S. and Israel and, of course, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and Israel, but then also the Arab nations that have endorsed President Trump's deal.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: So, Phil, President Trump said to \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , you got to agree to all 20 points, them or leave them by Sunday at 6:00. It is now Friday after 6:00, so they have less than 48 hours. And they have come forth and said we agree to one point. It sounds like maybe it's just a ploy to get more time.\par \par \uc2 PHILIP WEGMANN, REAL CLEAR POLITICS: And certainly Israel doubts that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is ready for lasting peace, as President Trump said in that statement. I think that this is a White House that is willing to take what they can get when they can get it. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has said that they are ready to return those hostages and negotiate on the other points. Trump is ready to take them at their initial word here, begin that process.\par \par \uc2 I think that Juan is right, the significant headline here is that for the first time Trump is leaning on Netanyahu, saying stop the fighting. And to your point also about the power dynamic, now he has a lot of Arab nations, Egypt and Qatar, also leaning on \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , also coming to the negotiation table to finally bring this thing to an end.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: I want to switch gears here because we are now three days into this shutdown with no end in sight, especially after this afternoon's series of votes. Ben, take a look at this from NBC News, "Government shutdown delays key monthly jobs report at a pivotal moment for the U.S. economy. Like many other federal offices, the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  temporarily closed because of the ongoing shutdown. Now the Fed could be forced to make another decision on rates this month about the benefit of key federal data." This all sounds not great, but it has real world impact far beyond that for Americans' wallets.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: It does. But I also think that this is a situation that is going to find some pretty speedy resolution.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: The shutdown?\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: You have a situation where by the 15th, everyone knows the dynamic here, that once you start getting up to the point where paychecks are going out to people who are the most sympathetic, that there is going to be a need to come to the table. And I think that you already saw some movement on the part of some senators that is toward trying to find something here.\par \par \uc2 But the decision by the House, I think, and by Speaker Mike Johnson has been, you know, we are not coming back. And I don't think that they feel like they are going to be penalized in any way by their constituents for holding the line on this. In fact, I think that they believe that the Democrats have not been able to connect in the kind of messaging that they had hoped on the healthcare front, that they have an easy response for it when it comes to the illegal immigration issue, which they are comfortable fighting on.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I think this ultimately could end up being something where Democrats view this as unsatisfying in terms of the outcome of whatever we actually could see at the end.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: It is also conceivable, Juan, that the speaker of the House doesn't get blamed but the White House ends up taking a lot of heat for this.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: That's possible --\par \par \uc2 TURNER: In terms of healthcare benefits.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes. But the healthcare thing is very tricky because, clearly, most Americans want an extension of these Obamacare ACA subsidies, and the White House doesn't, and I think much of their base doesn't. So, the question is, you know, how much -- how far are you willing to go?\par \par \uc2 The counterargument coming from the Democrats is that you have the White House, the White House, the Senate, under Republican control and you can't keep the government open. And I think that is a pretty simple argument and has some attractiveness to it.\par \par \uc2 The difficulty, I think, is that, also, that if you think of it from the White House point of view, as you mentioned, Gillian, they don't want any political fallout. And they see now, I think if you look at the morning polls, not only "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " but "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " and elsewhere, that the blame is shifting towards them. And I think they don't like it.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Phil, a lot of Americans who are not deep in the political weeds here in Washington look at this and say, what is this all for? What are you guys doing? You can't each, you know, cut the checks.\par \par \uc2 WEGMANN: And I think the White House right now is confident in their approach to this, because they know that Democrats could make this a fight over Obamacare subsidies tomorrow. In fact, Vice President Vance told me this week that they were open to negotiation on this point. But, instead, things get muddled where have you Republicans point to the provision in the Democratic funding proposal which would fund healthcare for some of these individuals who were paroled during the previous administration that Republicans are in the process of deporting and call illegal immigrants.\par \par \uc2 But, right now, Democrats, I think, are trying to define this fight as it happens. It's incredibly difficult. And they may end up finding themselves in a situation not unlike Republicans were when the Tea Party shut down the government in 2013.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, stick with us, because coming up next, we have got the Friday Lightning Round, and then "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ZOHRAN MAMDANI, (D) NEW YORK CITY MAYORAL CANDIDATE: New York City deserves better than yet another mayor bought by billionaires.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW CUOMO\uc2 , (I) NEW YORK CITY MAYORAL CANDIDATE: You know, freeze the rent doesn't mean anything. It's a great slogan.\par \par \uc2 CURTIS SLIWA, (R) NEW YORK CITY MAYORAL CANDIDATE: He led to Zohran Mamdani. By him being so far to the left, closing prisons, no cash bail, turning criminals loose, it opened up a pathway for the democratic socialist. And now Cuomo doesn't know how to deal with it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: We are back now with the panel. Ben, the New York governor's race is the gift that keeps on giving to cable news. There's also a blame game going on in the governor's race in Virginia. This shutdown is filtering into the conversation there. Take a look at this from "Politico," "With early voting already underway and a