nk that this is a situation where we have seen these races, typically, historically, always be breaking against whichever party won the last White House. But I think in New Jersey, in Virginia, you could see, perhaps, a surprise in one of these.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Always room for a surprise.\par \par \uc2 All right, it's time for Winners and Losers. Phil, you're up first.\par \par \uc2 WEGMANN: My winner is area Philadelphia man Shane Gillis who stood up for free speech and said no to the Saudi Arabia Comedy Festival because he didn't want to censor his own set.\par \par \uc2 And then my loser, unfortunately, are the kids in Des Moines public schools after that school district hired a superintendent who lied about getting his doctorate, who had a gun charge, and who was arrested this week by ICE for being in the country illegally.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Juan?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: My winner, 800 military leaders got time off and a free trip to Washington, D.C. this week.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: You know, they had faced no crisis, no new orders, and there was no need to clap.\par \par \uc2 My loser, Gillian, the bar chair thrown from a Nashville bar roof in 2024. Police video shows country music star Morgan Wallen being arrested outside this Nashville bar. In the police car, the policeman started from his playlist play a song and Wallen was singing along to his own song.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Apparently, he pleaded guilty to reckless endangerment.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: That's pretty good. Ben?\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: My winners for the week are all Taylor Swift fans, not because this new album is particularly good, but because it's going to be particularly controversial, especially the point of whether a happy Taylor Swift is one that writes less interesting lyrics than an unhappy Taylor Swift.\par \par \uc2 My loser of the week is Dave Chapelle. Sorry, for a child of Washington. But the truth is you can take the money from Saudi Arabia to go and tell jokes, but if you sign on the dotted line and say you won't actually have any jokes about the powers that be, then you can't turn around and say that you're more free to tell jokes there than you are here. He essentially agreed to not do someone who can go through his schtick of keeping it real goes wrong.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Without the schtick, what do you got?\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, thank you, panel.\par \par \uc2 It's Friday, so it is time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENT: Yesterday could be the settlement in the Middle East that hasn't happened for 3,000 years.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: There's a lot of pieces that are moving here, right?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A new government is going to come in. And that will be in large part Palestinians.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER: If \uc2 Hamas\uc2  rejects your plan, Mr. President, then Israel will finish the job.\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: And it's the Democrats' fault. There is zero excuse for them to shut down our government right now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My constituents don't care about the finger pointing. They just want us to govern.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Leader Jeffries said in the Oval Office there was a frank and direct discussion. In Washington, that translates to heated at times. Was it heated?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Well, I mean I think it was --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Lively?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It was lively. It was spirited.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES, (D-NY) HOUSE MINORITY LEADER: The president has been engaging in irresponsible and unserious behavior.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And if you help us reopen the government, the sombrero memes we'll stop.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Are you watching \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : One thing with Obama, he would bop down those stairs. I've never seen it, dah-dah, dah-dah, dah-dah.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE, (R) VICE PRESIDENT: Karoline, I don't know how do you this. Is it just who is the best dressed?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 VANCE: OK, I really like your shirt. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, DEFENSE SECRETARY: No more identity months, dudes in dresses. We are done with that -\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We conclude another gigantic fat bear week. He remains bun of the biggest and fattest bears.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : They couldn't -- God bless you, Bobby. I hope I didn't just catch COVID just there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, please join me this weekend for FOX NEWS SUNDAY. I will be filling in for Shannon Bream. Secretary of War \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  is going to be interviewed on "The Sunday Briefing" at 11:00 a.m. eastern.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for watching SPECIAL REPORT. I'm Gillian Turner in Washington.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020251004ela300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Eyes Widening National Guard Deployment; Justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  Talks About Her New Book And Supreme Court Life; Barrett Talks Scalia Influence And Judicial Philosophy; Barrett Reflects On Feinstein "Dogma" Comment; Search For A Fourth Prime Minister In 12 Months; Defense Minister Message To \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ; Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  Criticizes Democratic Leaders for Not Supporting New York City Mayoral Candidate Zohran Mamdani; Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  Interviewed about Current Supreme Court Rulings On Presidential Powers; Immigration And Customs Enforcement Launches Operation Midway Blitz Targeting Criminal Illegal Aliens In Chicago.\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Greg Palkot, Chad Pergram, Mark Meredith, Brit Hume,  Mollie Hemingway \par \uc2 8,234 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 September 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Paul, Jessica, we'll catch you another time. That's it for us. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier. We are inside the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 , and in just a moment, we will talk to Justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  about her new book, listening to the law, about her time in the court and the big issues of the day.\par \par \uc2 But first, the big issue for the day for President Trump has been crime. Again, talking what he may or may not do around the country.\par \par \uc2 Also today, religious liberty. Joining us now, Jacqui Heinrich, Senior White House correspondent live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. Trump's Task Force to eradicate anti-Christian bias is accusing multiple agencies of discrimination under the Biden administration, citing denied religious exemptions to gender ideology in schools or COVID mandates, even limited humanitarian assistance to Christians compared to other groups. Officials here say a government wide course correction is underway, but they are still running into road blocks locally, where Democrats hold the reins.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: When faith gets weaker, our country seems to get weaker.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice-over): Announcing new plans to protect prayer in schools, speaking at the Museum of the Bible this morning, President Trump teed off on his predecessor for everything from alleged government wide, anti- Christian bias to January 6th prosecutions and the southern border.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : People don't realize about the Biden administration. It was a very mean administration. He's a mean guy, actually, not a smart guy.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice-over): Trump again, pressuring Democrat states to call in the National Guard to aid in a crime and illegal immigration crackdown, after telling reporters this weekend he'll decide within days whether to send in the troops uninvited.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : You try and reason with them, and it's like you're talking to a wall. It's just doesn't -- I assume it's just a political ideology. They're not stupid people. You can't do a damn thing about it.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice-over): It doesn't sound like Chicago will be calling for help anytime soon, despite pressure from local Democrats.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND LOPEZ, \uc2 CHICAGO CITY COUNCIL\uc2 : They think that standing up for law and order is only a Republican issue, and history will prove the Democratic Party wrong in this moment.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice-over): Maryland Democrats also resisting but ratcheting up local law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So, you're saying there's a total coincidence that Trump's threats to send troops in the city came same time.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON SCOTT (D), BALTIMORE MAYOR: This is about strategic deployment, right? This is what this is.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice-over): Trump's administration quick to highlight cases of lax enforcement dragging North Carolina's democratic officials for the murder of a Ukrainian refugee on a train by a suspect who had been arrested 14 times.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I just give my love and hope to the family of the young woman who was stabbed this morning or last night in Charlotte by a madman. A lunatic just got up and started, it's right on the tape.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice-over): Charlotte's Mayor urged the media not to share that video. Transportation Secretary \uc2 Sean Duffy\uc2  saying, why? Because she and other public officials in her city bear responsibility. This monster had a track record longer than a CVS receipt.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): And the president just posted about this, saying the blood of this innocent woman can literally be seen dripping from the killer's knife, and now her blood is on the hands of Democrats who refuse to put bad people in jail, including former disgraced governor and wannabe senator Roy Cooper. The message goes on. He tells people instead to vote for \uc2 Michael Whatley\uc2 , Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich live on the North Lawn. Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Here are some other top headlines tonight. Jury selection is underway in the trial of the man accused of trying to assassinate President Trump last year at his Florida golf course. Ryan Routh is representing himself. The judge ruled against his request today to question potential jurors about their views on the war in Gaza and the U.S. acquiring Greenland. Routh has pleaded not guilty.\par \par \uc2 A federal appeals court upholds a civil jury's decision requiring President Trump to pay $83 million to columnist E. Jean Carroll. The panel rejected the president's appeal, calling the damages as fair and reasonable. The rewards stem from President Trump's repeated social media attacks against Carroll after she accused him of sexual assault in the 1990s.\par \par \uc2 A separate jury also awarded Carroll $5 million in a 2023 sexual assault and defamation case. A spokesman for Trump's legal team says the American people support the president and, "Demand an immediate end to the political weaponization of our justice system."\par \par \uc2 Immigration enforcement operations are underway in Chicago, small and targeted missions, we're told, focus on undocumented individuals, illegal immigrants with criminal records. Fox cameras were rolling as ICE agents apprehended an illegal Mexican national convicted last year of forcibly sexually assaulting a child under the age of 13.\par \par \uc2 Officials say the targets are known to law enforcement but are out on the street because of sanctuary policies or cashless bail.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were ahead of several data reports this week, they were up as investors feel optimistic about a Federal Reserve rate cut, the Dow gaining 114 the S&P 500 finished ahead 14. The NASDAQ rose 98 to a new record close.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2 , thank you so much for the time.\par \par \uc2 AMY CONEY BARRETT\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE OF U.S. SUPREME COURT: Thank you for having me, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know the title of this book, Listening To The Law. Why did you write it?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: I wrote it because I really love the court and I love the Constitution, and I wanted to invite people to experience the court and constitution with me.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And was part of it demystifying the court or the process?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Yes, you know, I speak to a lot of school groups. I speak to young children. I speak to lawyers, non-lawyers, all kinds of people, and I get the same questions across groups. How does court get its cases? How does the court decide cases? Do the justices get along? And I can't answer all of those questions one on one, but I can write a book so people can find the answers if they're interested.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I've read it, and there's a lot of mystery and intrigue about what happens inside these walls. And the perception of that, like everything else in Washington, is that it comes from a partisan point of view, that there are somehow teams here, depending on which president nominated you for the position.\par \par \uc2 But you write about things like the assigned seating in the court, and what you wear when you go to the courtroom, and basically how the outside perception is not the inside reality.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: That's right. You know, we don't wear red and blue. We all wear black because judges are nonpartisan, and the idea is that we're all listening to the law. We're all trying to get it right. We're not playing for a team. We don't sit on specific sides of the bench, left and right. You know, we sit in order of seniority.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And when you listen to the perception about how it's done. What is your thought about that?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Well, there's a disconnect between what happens inside the court and what people think happens inside the court.\par \par \uc2 I often ask new law clerks what surprised you most when you started and one of the most common answers is the difference between what's happening on the inside and what people think is happening on the inside.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, for example, in fact, the focus is always on these divisive cases and decisions. But there are a number of unanimous decisions that the media doesn't cover every year.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we have 42 percent in this past term, 2024. 45 percent in 2023. 47 percent in 2022. You write one who follows the court casually might be under the impression that practically every case is decided, but the appointees of a Republican president on one side and the appointees of a Democratic president on the other, while that breakdown sometimes occurs, presenting it as the norm grossly distorts the court's work.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: That's absolutely right. You know, last year, I believe it was six cases. You know, roughly around 10 percent cases broke down by party of appointing president. That's a pretty small number given the overall number of cases that we're hearing.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That said, the current perception, like I said, is that the left is saying this court is protecting President Trump and his effort to consolidate power. You can see that really from the recent headlines. There's a lot of them about that, the overwhelming evidence that the Supreme Court is on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's team.\par \par \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , the Supreme Court is backing Trump's power grab. NBC, in rare interviews, federal judges criticized Supreme Court's handling of Trump cases, they say you're helping to certify this move towards authoritarianism. How do you respond to that?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: The court decides cases as they come. And one thing that I try to emphasize to students, and one thing I try to do in the book, is place the court in the context of history. We're not deciding cases just for today, and we're not deciding cases based on the President, as in the current occupant of the office. We're deciding cases about the presidency.\par \par \uc2 So, we're taking each case and we're looking at the question of presidential power as it comes and the cases that we decide today are going to matter four presidencies from now, six presidencies from now and so on. Each of these cases that we're getting, you know, well, I mean, some of them overlap, but many present different constitutional issues.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The right obviously looks at it completely differently, saying federal district judges around the country are overstepping. They're unelected, making sweeping decisions about a president's executive power. When you have to deal with those, it often comes in the emergency docket, which is something different that people don't fully get. Can you explain that?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Sure. So, the emergency docket, these are not cases that come up on our regular October term merit stocket. Those cases are the ones that I primarily focus on in the book. There are about 60 of them per year, and they proceed very slowly with briefs and oral argument and a lot of deliberation.\par \par \uc2 These emergency motions come up when a case has not fully been decided and settled in the courts below, and they ask for the court's emergency intervention to enter a stay, usually, or sometimes to lift a stay, to maintain a particular status quo while the issue is being litigated in the courts below.\par \par \uc2 So, it's not a definitive resolution. It's what Justice Kavanaugh calls the interim during the interim.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There are a lot of them, though, this administration is five, almost two dozen.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: There have been a lot.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And is that because you see more federal district judges stepping in and doing that?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: I don't know. You know, the phenomenon of cases being brought on our emergency docket started a few administrations ago. There were some in the Obama administration. There were some in the Biden administration. And now we do have, it seems like we're on a trajectory to have more in the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 I don't know. I think it's a combination of, you know, increasing use over time, not just this administration, of executive orders.\par \par \uc2 And I think that all the judges, district judges, too, are just doing the best we can with a lot of litigation that's been found.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Most times you're not saying again about the merits, you're saying about the process, and specifically what should or shouldn't happen at that time as the case is progressing through the system.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Exactly, it's just, what is the holding pattern going to be? What is -- what is -- what are we going to do for the interim, while the case is litigated? It does not mean that we have reached any definitive resolution about the constitutional or statutory question in a case.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In June, ruling on one of those cases, Trump V. CASA, on birthright citizenship, you wrote this, federal courts do not exercise general oversight of the executive branch. They resolve cases and controversies consistent with the authority Congress has given them. When a court concludes that the executive branch has acted unlawfully, the answer is not for the court to exceed its power too.\par \par \uc2 Some of your colleagues, including Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , said, that reasoning was a, "Existential threat to the rule of law." I mean, that obviously got a lot of attention. That whole back and forth and your response to her.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Yes, let's see. I do want to clarify that I was writing for the court. I always felt funny about saying, yes, I was -- I was representing the court, so it wasn't just my own opinion, but I was the drafter of the opinion.\par \par \uc2 And I think that Justice Jackson and I just had different views of the judicial power, mine narrower or the courts rather narrower than the one that Justice Jackson presented. I hasten to add that Justin Jackson and I get along just fine.\par \par \uc2 Justice Scalia used to say I attack ideas, not people, and that's my motto, too. So, we were sparring about ideas. It was not personal, but I do believe that. I think the judiciary needs to stay in its lane.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And she wrote a solo.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Correct.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And you responded in an interesting way. They got a lot of attention. You clerked for the late Justice \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2 , who was known also to have a pretty sharp pen at times. When it comes to your point of view and style, how do you see comparing or contrasting to Justice Scalia?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: It's hard for anybody to compare their writing style to his, because he was such a fantastic writer. So, I'm sure you know, my writing does not hold a candle to Justice Scalia is writing in terms of the heat, you know, he was Italian, so I think he had naturally brought a little bit more heat to the pen.\par \par \uc2 So, I calibrate mine to the situation. And in that particular opinion, I was writing, you know, in a little bit more of a forceful, or, shall we say, spicy way than maybe I sometimes do.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But as far as your point of view about the Constitution, a constitutional list, the text, explain that for folks. In that why you're close to Justice Scalia.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Oh yes, of course. In terms of philosophy, you know, I did, I learned from Justice Scalia. And in terms of philosophy, my philosophy tracks is, and that is that the Constitution's text controls and the way to understand what the text means is to ask how it was understood by those who ratified it when it became law.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And for the folks who say, well, the founders were dealing with completely different problems back then, they were not contemplating what a country could look like decades, decades later. How do you -- you know they want a living, breathing constitution that changes? How do you respond to that?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: You know, the genius of our Constitution, and I talk about this in the book, when I describe originalism and describe our written constitution, is that very levels of generality. So, sometimes it's very specific. The president has to be at least 35 years old. But sometimes it's very general. We are protected from unreasonable searches and seizures. We have freedom of speech. And to have freedom of speech doesn't mean that you have freedom only to pass out pamphlets in a Town Square. I mean, it means that you have freedom to be on broadcast news.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right. And the 22nd Amendment says you can only run for office for two terms.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: True.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And you think that that's cut and dry.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Well, that's, you know, that's what the amendment says, right? You know, after FDR had four terms, that that's what that amendment says.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Not asking how you think about an issue, but scheduling the tariff question. We think that would be in coming weeks?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: I don't think that has been scheduled yet, but we certainly take into account requests to move things along quickly when it's necessary. But that one has not been scheduled yet.\par \par \uc2 To my knowledge, you might have more current information than I do.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, I don't know about that. You live inside these walls here. You spend some time in the book discussing the Dobbs decision, and that that overturned Roe v. Wade and the precedent, ending the national -- nationwide constitutional right to abortion, as it's so called.\par \par \uc2 You write, "The Court's role is to respect the choices that the people have agreed upon, not to tell them what they should agree to."\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Yes. So, the Court's role, and essentially, what the Court said in Dobbs was not that we were taking away a right, but that as properly interpreted, the Constitution did not protect that right. That it's one left, like virtually every question to the democratic process. That's because the court in interpreting the Constitution, the Constitution is our fundamental document. It's the super law. It trumps every other law, and it represents our fundamental commitments as a people that we have agreed to.\par \par \uc2 And that's a product of the democratic process. That's not a product of what the court imposes on the democratic process.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think that decision is misunderstood broadly?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: I do. I -- the Dobbs did not say that abortion is illegal. Dobbs didn't weigh-in on that question. Dobbs said it belongs to the political process. And, you know, it's been thrown into the political process and the states have been working it out.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I mean, in fact, you write, "Getting ahead of the American people came at a cost.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Yes, Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , who was herself a supporter of abortion rights, pointed that out. I mean, when the Court decided Roe, the country was already moving in a direction of liberalization of abortion loss, but the Court then was in the business of making a lot of pretty fine-grained decisions about abortion regulations. That Dobbs held belonged to the political process.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you see the passion and how much -- how did that affect you? All of the fallout from that case in particular?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: I think the fallout from that case drove home a point that I make, and I think it's chapter two of the book, about the cost of the Commission and the oath. You know, you make decisions that are unpopular, and you have to face what comes. Right? So, we don't expect that we will be popular, and sometimes that can be difficult to be subject of public criticism, but that's the cost of the Commission.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In your nomination hearing in the Senate, Senator \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2  said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 DIANNE FEINSTEIN\uc2  (D-CA): When you read your speeches, the conclusion one draws is that the dogma lives loudly within you, and that's of concern when you come to big issues that large numbers of people have fought for, for years in this country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's about your faith. You write about your faith in this book. You say, "Some suggest that people of faith have a particularly difficult time following the law rather than their moral views. I face that criticism as a Catholic, most sharply when the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  conducted a hearing to consider my nomination to the Seventh Circuit. I'm not sure why." Are you still not sure why?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: When I say I'm not sure why, it means, what I meant to convey is that people who have faith and people who have no faith at all, each have deeply held moral commitments. I mean, I am fortunate to know many people who do not have faith who have deep, deep commitments to values.\par \par \uc2 And so, I say in the book, whether you are pro-choice because you think you are -- you are a secular humanist, but you think it is a fundamental moral right of a woman to terminate a pregnancy. Or whether you're a person of faith who is pro-choice because you believe the same, or you are a person of faith who is pro-life. Everyone, every single judge, every single person has moral commitments. And I just don't think it matters from where those commitments come. They all must be put aside if one is to judge.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Your book has great pictures from all different times in your life. And also, a picture that says a thousand words as you write. A snapshot of two justices riding an elephant together in India, captures the relationship between Justice Scalia and \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  really well. Are those relationships in this court?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Yes, I think that those relationships, I think what their relationship showed, and what people talked about so much after Justice Ginsburg died most recently, is that they could vehemently disagree -- vehemently disagree about ideas, but be close personally, have warm relations personally, and we work on that in the court, you know, to maintain collegiality, and friendship, and warmth, despite the fact that we have deep disagreements, deep jurisprudential ones.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Are you concerned about security and where the country is going, specifically, vis-a-vis the passion that you saw, the attempt we saw on Justice Kavanaugh, the security that you have to deal with day-to-day. Obviously, you have a large family, seven children. It's a thing that a lot of public people have to think about. What do you -- how do you think about it?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: You know, violence and the threat of violence should not be the cost of public service. And so, I know that the whole judiciary is alert to this. The chief justice has spoken about it. And yes, I'm very grateful for the security that we have, and it is something I'm worried about.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Public perception of the Court. How important is that inside the Court to all of you?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: One reason I wrote the book is I want people to be confident in the integrity of the court, and I want them to understand our decision- making process. The Court, however, can't take into account public opinion in making individual decisions, because the whole point of the Court, and the point I try to convey in the book, is that you have to follow the law where it leads, even if it leads in a place where the majority of people don't want you to go, and that's because the Constitution trumps regular statutes, regular executive orders, all those things that might be very popular in the current moment, the Court has to stand up for the law.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, what about that? Do you -- do you like doing this?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Oh, gosh, like every job, there are things that I like and things that I don't like, but the way that I try to think about the job is primarily public service. I am privileged to serve. And when people ask if I like it, typically, what I say is, it is an honor to serve the United States.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What's the biggest thing that surprised you now that you're into this time here?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: I think the biggest thing that surprised me was really the personal change of being a public figure. You asked about security before. You know, having security Intel was not something that I experienced when I was a law professor or a judge on the Seventh Circuit. That shift has been a pretty big adjustment.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, the book is listening to the law and thank you. We enjoyed listening to you.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I appreciate the time.\par \par \uc2 Thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: When we come back, everything we know about a deadly attack at a Jerusalem bus stop.\par \par \uc2 But as we go to break, just how do the Supreme Court justices get along behind the scenes?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Supreme Court justices, throughout the history, have been known to argue on the bench while having lunch as friends hours later. Chief Justice John Marshall, actually began this tradition at the time. Most justices did not live in Washington. Only met while Court was in session.\par \par \uc2 Marshall decided to have all the justices stay at the same place while they were in town to hear cases. The justices still have lunch together frequently, and Barrett says they have rules not to discuss cases at lunch. They also have a tradition of throwing new justices' welcome dinners.\par \par \uc2 Barrett says justice Kavanaugh dinner, planned by Justice Gorsuch featured the \uc2 Washington Nationals\uc2  president's race within the halls of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Justice Kavanaugh threw Justice Barrett, a New Orleans themed dinner, and Justice Barrett asked a Hamilton Broadway singer to perform at the dinner she planned for the new justice. Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here outside the Court. They are doing some renovations to the facade, adding some lighting. They should be done pretty soon, they say. It doesn't affect the lunches inside.\par \par \uc2 SPECIAL REPORT continues after this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to SPECIAL REPORT. French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  searches for the country's fourth prime minister in 12 months. French lawmakers ousted the minority government of Prime Minister \uc2 Francois Bayrou\uc2 . Today, Bayrou had gambled Parliament would support his view that France must slash public spending to repair its debts. But his confidence vote failed by a wide margin.\par \par \uc2 Russia launches its largest air attack of the war on Ukraine tonight. The main government building in Kyiv was set on fire.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian officials say at least four people were killed, including an infant. President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  says such killings now, when diplomacy could have begun long ago, are a deliberate crime and are prolonging the war.\par \par \uc2 President Trump saying Sunday, he is ready to move to a second phase of sanctions against Russia.\par \par \uc2 At least six people are dead tonight and a dozen others injured after an attack on a bus stop at a busy North Jerusalem intersection. Two shooters were killed by armed men nearby.\par \par \uc2 Senior foreign affairs correspondent Greg Palkot has the latest tonight from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Terror in Jerusalem. People run for their lives as two Palestinian gunmen shoot up a crowded bus in the northern part of the city.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (through translator): Someone suddenly shouted, terror attack, and people started running.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): Six people were killed, five men and a woman, including three rabbis. A dozen injured. The two suspects were killed by two armed men, including an off=duty soldier.\par \par \uc2 Authorities say the gunman came from the West Bank, where the \uc2 IDF\uc2  chief and his soldiers headed, sealing off neighborhoods, making an arrest. The prime minister also went to the scene.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , PRIME MINISTER, ISRAEL (through translator): We are at war, an intensified war against terror on multiple fronts.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): In Gaza City, more inroads made against the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorist stronghold. Multi-level buildings collapsed by the \uc2 IDF\uc2 , Threat Level Two.\par \par \uc2 Israeli Defense Minister Katz posting to \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , "Released the hostages and lay down your weapons, or Gaza will be destroyed, and you will be annihilated."\par \par \uc2 As both sides ponder the latest peace proposal from the Trump administration, calling for the release of all 48 hostages alive and dead, the exchange of 3,000 Palestinian prisoners and a cease-fire as next steps are taken. President Trump sounded optimistic.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENT: I think we're going to have a deal on Gaza very soon.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: The Israelis, too, seem to be considering it.\par \par \uc2 GIDEON SAAR, ISRAELI FOREIGN MINISTER: We are ready to accept a full deal that would end the war based on the cabinet decision.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: But while \uc2 Hamas\uc2  says it's willing, analysts say giving up their hostage bargaining chips and their arms might be a deal too far.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (on camera): \uc2 Hamas\uc2  didn't claim responsibility for the Jerusalem attack but did praise it. In a video message, Netanyahu poured scorn on the attack and also told the people of Gaza City to get out. Not much peace talk. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Greg Palkot in Tel Aviv. Greg, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, back in the U.S. how congressional Republicans are fighting back against Democrats trying to delay confirmation for President Trump's nominees.\par \par \uc2 And then as Democrats seek new voices, 84-year-old \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  continues to speak the loudest, louder than the rest. We'll bring you that.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to Washington. Congressional Republicans here on Capitol Hill frustrated with Democratic attempts to slow the confirmations of President Trump's nominees. Well, now they are trying to establish a new precedent tonight. Senior congressional correspondent Chad Pergram joins us tonight from inside Capitol Hill just in front of me. Good evening, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening. There's a Senate logjam of President Trump's nominees, so Senate Majority Leader \uc2 John Thune\uc2  is lighting the fuse to blow up the dam. He wants to quickly confirm lower tier and noncontroversial nominees. Thune accuses Democrats of wasting valuable floor time.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2 , (R-SD) SENATE MAJORITY LEADER: This is simply the world's longest, most drawn out temper tantrum over losing an election.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Thune wants to speed the confirmation of about 40 nominees for federal agencies and ambassador posts. This will not impact full cabinet secretaries or judges. The Democrats' message, buyer beware, especially when Democrats are back in the majority.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 , (D-NY) SENATE MINORITY LEADER: I say to my Republican colleagues, think carefully before taking this step. If you go nuclear it's going to be a decision you will come to regret.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Thune hopes to confirm batches of nominees all at once to save time. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chad, there are developments tonight in the \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  investigation. Tell us about that.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Well, the Oversight Committee received the first set of documents it subpoenaed from Epstein's estate today. It includes a birthday book that Ghislaine Maxwell assembled for Epstein's 50th birthday. Also in the documents, a 2007 non-prosecution agreement between the feds and Epstein. Now, the birthday book purports to include a letter from President Trump. Committee Democrats say that President Trump writes about, quote, "a wonderful secret" between he and Epstein. The White House says the president did not draw the picture nor sign it. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chad Pergram live on Capitol Hill. Chad, thanks.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Democrats want fresh faces to lead the party, but one of the party's loudest voices right now is democratic socialist, turning 84 today, Vermont independent \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , is shaping its future, throwing his support behind New York mayoral candidate Zohran Mamdani. Correspondent Mark Meredith shows us tonight. Good evening, Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you. Vermont Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  is a man in demand. The senator, who turns 84 today, he is crisscrossing the country, boosting progressives, including in New York City with the closely watched mayoral race. Over the weekend, Sanders campaigned with mayoral candidate Zohran Mamdani. Sanders urging New York's Democratic leaders, including the state's governor, to throw their support behind the self-described democratic socialist.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 BERNIE SANDERS\uc2 , (I-VT): I find it hard to understand how the major Democratic leaders in New York state are not supporting the Democratic candidate.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: This is our guy! So we've got another fight on our hands, and that the future of the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: A new survey out today from Gallup shows Americans feel less enthusiastic on capitalism, 39 percent of people now supporting socialism. Former New York Governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2 , who is running for mayor as an independent, is warning socialism will backfire for Democrats nationally.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREW CUOMO\uc2 , NEW YORK CITY MAYORAL CANDIDATE: If Mamdani wins, the Democrats will use him -- I'm sorry, the Republican will use him against the Democrats all across this country. The Republican will say these are the Democrats. They have been taken over by socialists.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Democrats insist they still have a winning message beyond just the Bernie machine, while some argue this is all still a work in progress.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ELISSA SLOTKIN, (D-MI): There is certainly debate going on, certainly among democratic leaders on how to respond to Trump. I don't think it's a secret that Democrats are on their heels since Trump was elected and haven't found their footing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Senator Sanders is expected to remain in the spotlight. He has got a new book coming out in October. And Bret, it will be around the same time that former vice president \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is on her book tour. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Mark, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel with reaction to the \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  interview, plus President Trump on religious liberty and fighting crime around the country. But first, a visit to the justices' Supreme Court office.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Inside the office of justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2 . Thanks so much for having under the circumstances.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE AMY CONEY BARRETT, SUPREME COURT: Yes, please come on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So this office before you came here has a long genealogy.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: It has a long genealogy. Justice Ginsburg, Justice Souter, way back in there is Justice Frankfurter somewhere, Justice Thurgood Marshall.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I heard this couch was retrieved.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: From storage. It belonged to Chief Justice Charles Evans Hughes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So there is a lot of history here.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: A lot of history here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So you spend time here, but you are interacting with your clerks?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Yes. My clerks and I will come in here and we talk about cases in this area. We debate oral arguments. They push me on things, tell me when I'm wrong.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The standing desk.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Standing desk. My husband talked me in to standing. So I have a standing desk, yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And speak about this portrait here. This is Abigail Adams.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: I out Abigail Adams up because the founding period surrounding the ratification of the original Constitution is pretty central to my work. It's the foundation of the court itself. But there were no women at the Constitutional Convention. So Abigail Adams is, I think, the closest that we have to a founding mother as opposed to finding father, because she advised --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You call her most influential during that time, as far as women?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Well, let's see. There were other people like Mercy Otis Warren. Or certainly Martha Washington invented the role of first lady. But Abigail Adams was so involved. Her letters to John reflect such deep involvement in affairs of state, interest in the Revolutionary War and the Constitution itself. I admire her. She also had a large family. And she managed that farm and all of their investments herself while John was away for many years at a time. So I admire Abigail Adams a lot.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Speak to somebody at home about how much time you spend going backwards, looking at what founding fathers wrote about what precedent of this court that goes back years and years and years and years.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Yes, the court has an amazing history. The Constitution, you know, is several centuries old, the original Constitution. And I do, I spend a lot of time going back to the Federalist Papers, to the notes, James Madison's notes from the convention, to see how they understood it at the time, what were they trying to accomplish?\par \par \uc2 But then this court's precedents stretch all the way back to the very beginning to Chief Justice John Jay, the first chief justice. Opinions that were written by chief justice John Marshall continue to be cited. I cited one myself just last term. So the court is a place of history. Not just the couch, but the law.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The law itself?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Yes, the law itself.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Are you worried that kids are losing a little of our history over time?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Gosh, I hope not. I know for my own children that's something we really encourage and it's something that the children who come to the court are certainly interested in. So hopefully the book will do something to spark some interest.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Excellent. We appreciate the time.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Thank you, Bret. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, SPECIAL REPORT continues after this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE AMY CONEY BARRETT, SUPREME COURT: We're not deciding cases just for today. And we're not deciding cases based on the president, as in the current occupant of the office. We're deciding cases about the presidency. So we're taking each case and we're looking at the question of presidential power as it comes. And the cases that we decide today are going to matter four presidencies from now, six presidencies from now, and so on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  in this interview today. This comes as the perception of the court has taken a hit. If you look at the latest \uc2 Pew Research\uc2  poll, Democrats are only at 26 percent favorability, Republicans at 71 percent, overall 48 percent. This interview kind of explaining her thought process about the various issues.\par \par \uc2 Now, let's bring in our panel. We'll start there with FOX News chief political analyst Brit Hume, FOX News contributor Mollie Hemingway, and "Politico" White House bureau chief Dasha Burns. Brit, first to you.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I thought she did a good job of establishing herself as a judicial conservative, which is not the same as a political conservative. Judicial conservatives look to the law, they look to laws they might not even like, but, nonetheless, feel compelled to respect. Political conservative does otherwise. They are not faithful so much to the law or to the Constitution as they are a political ideology to which they are devoted, much as liberals on the other side are devoted to their political ideology.\par \par \uc2 There's a big difference. She's a -- this is a judicial conservative, and a gracious, I think, woman, who did a pretty good job explaining the difference how the court is from the inside from how it looks from the outside.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Mollie?\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR IN CHIEF, "THE FEDERALIST": That poll is interesting, showing an overall 48 percent approval rating for the court. It is lower than we have seen in the past, but much higher than what we've seen for Congress or the media, other institutions where a lot of the public don't seem to trust them.\par \par \uc2 And, with the court, actually polling doesn't really capture what the job is. The founders intended the court to be removed from the political process. And I think that's one of the themes that really came out from your interview with Justice Barrett in pointing out that when they ruled in favor of Trump on some issues brought by Democrat lawfare, people said, oh, they are siding with Trump. One of the first beneficiaries of their rulings was, of course, President Biden, who is likely to be protected from some prosecutions that a lot of Republicans would like to see because of those rulings.\par \par \uc2 So I think the media could do a better job of explaining that when the court decides cases, it's not about particular winners or losers, but as Justice Barrett was noting, the current court seems to view its approach as being less about pushing their own ideology and more about deciding what the law says.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And there was a big separation here in the kinds of cases they take, Dasha, in other words, this emergency docket they have been dealing with nonstop because of all of the cases that have come before federal district court judges who have decided to make universal decisions. Just today, the Supreme Court upholds roving patrols for immigration arrests in Los Angeles, six-three vote. Justices granted this emergency appeal lift a Los Angeles judge's order that barred roving patrols from snatching people -- this the "L.A. Times" -- off of southern California streets based on how they look, what language they speak, where they work or where they happen to be, calling it a significant decision for President Trump, a victory. There have been a number of these, and this is one stop in the emergency docket. Dasha?\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, "POLITICO": Well, look, there is such a contrast, Bret, between the way that Justice Barrett talked about the role of the courts and the way that both Democrats and Republicans have tried to use them, really as a tool or as a weapon against the other side. You've seen Democrats really mostly try to fight President Trump via the courts, filing lawsuits, A.G.s in blue states fighting him there. You have also seen President Trump say, hey, we're going to win ultimately in the Supreme Court, and take some of his more unprecedented measures and wait for the Supreme Court that he believes is working in his favor, to rule and ultimately give him those kinds of victories, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This crime question, Dasha, I know you have been some reporting on it, about what the administration, what the president is going to do.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Look, I just spoke to a White House official, Bret, who told me that they are planning on some lightning rod, standalone pieces of legislation that they want to bring to the floor on the issue of crime where they want to put Democrats in a position to make some bad decisions, make some bad votes that then they can use. This is an issue that they really see as a defining one for the president, but also as a defining issue for the midterms.\par \par \uc2 They see this as one of those -- you have heard the president talk about it -- 80-20 issues. They believe that some of these images of Democrats refusing help from the president are going to be useful for campaign ads. They think that putting Democrats on the backfoot is going to be successful here. Even though you have seen the protest in Chicago and in D.C. and you've seen some polling that shows that people aren't thrilled with the idea of the National Guard, they believe they're ultimately on the right side here, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brit, now today in Chicago, this Operation Midway Blitz is underway. And to Dasha's point, the Illinois governor responded to that saying, "Once again, this isn't about fighting crime that requires support and coordination. Yet, we have experienced nothing like that over the past several weeks. Instead of taking steps to work with us on public safety, the Trump administration's focus on scaring Illinoians", that's from the governor. But politically the images majors are these targeted raids.\par \par \uc2 HUME: I think, Bret, that the Democrats have walked into a trap that the president's approach on crime has set for them, just as Dasha suggested. This crime issue is one where people want to see it stopped and suppressed. They want to feel safe, in our urban areas in particular but elsewhere as well. And to the extent what the president is doing is bringing that about is going to be popular