That's it for us, everyone. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Sorry to hear about Harold. Thanks, Dana.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 President Trump supports an effort to ban individual stock trading by elected officials in office. We'll bring in that story.\par \par \uc2 Los Angeles cracks down on so called street takeovers, which turn violent, sometimes deadly.\par \par \uc2 And the Midwest, Mamdani, we look at the Minneapolis mayoral candidate, a socialist endorsed by the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 But first, "BREAKING TONIGHT", President Trump says there will be very severe consequences if \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  does not agree to stop his country's invasion of Ukraine during Friday's summit in Alaska.\par \par \uc2 After a morning zoom call-French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  said President Trump now wants to achieve a cease fire during that meeting. Managing expectations and painting a picture about the possibilities two days before the Alaska Summit.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Alexandria Hoff starts us off tonight, live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Good evening to you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 President Trump said today that great things could be gained from Friday's summit. But as you mentioned, the White House and the president, they're both trying to manage expectations, and step one in this meeting is going in to find out if Putin is even interested in ending the bloodshed.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It's going to be a very important meeting. But it's setting the table for the second meeting.\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice over): President Trump, speaking from the Kennedy Center today, following a virtual meeting with European leaders and Ukrainian president, \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 , in preparation for Friday's U.S.-Russia summit in Alaska.\par \par \uc2 KEIR STARMER, PRIME MINISTER, UNITED KINGDOM: There could be no negotiations about Ukraine without Ukraine. That's been a long-standing principle of this group,\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice over): The White House and state department are not calling Friday's bilateral meeting a negotiation. With President Trump, reiterating that if anchorage goes well, real progress to end the 3-1/2-year war would come when President Zelenskyy is included in a follow up session.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Which will be more productive than the first. Because the first is I'm going to find out where we are and what we are doing.\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice over): But according to German Chancellor \uc2 Friedrich Merz\uc2  and French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 EMMANUEL MACRON\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF FRANCE (through translator): President Trump was very clear on the fact that what the United States wants is to obtain a cease fire during this meeting in Alaska.\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice over): President Trump was asked today if Russia will face consequences if \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  does not agree to stop the war after Friday's meeting.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Yes, they will. Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What will the consequences be?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Yes, will be consequence --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Sanctions? Tariffs?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : There will be -- I don't have to say there will be very severe consequences.\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice over): From Berlin, President Zelenskyy accused President Putin of bluffing by amplifying its ground offensive ahead of Friday's meeting to appear capable of occupying the whole of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, PRESIDENT OF UKRAINE (through translator): Putin is also bluffing, pretending that sanctions are not important to him and that they are not working. In the reality, the sanctions are very helpful and are hitting the Russian military economy hard.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOFF (on camera): Now, European leaders, they did commit to increasing economic pressure on Russia, if necessary, and said that they would reject any pathway to peace that included the illegal recognition of Russian occupied territory. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alexandria Hoff, live in the North Lawn. Alex, thanks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The top diplomat during part of the \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  administration says Russia's dismal economy may be a major factor in getting \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  to end his war with Ukraine. I spoke with former Secretary of State Condoleezza Rice, for my podcast today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: If you are advising President Trump, knowing what you know about \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and about Russia, what would you tell him ahead of this meeting?\par \par \uc2 CONDOLEEZZA RICE, FORMER UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF STATE: Well, first, he's got some pretty good people advising him. So, let me just start there. But I think recognizing that he, President Trump, has really set the conditions that Putin, in a sense, needs him more than he needs Putin.\par \par \uc2 Yes, he needs Putin to stop this war, but he holds the options, which he's articulated, of putting more pressure on a Russian economy that is really in pretty dire straits, when the Russians themselves, through their very fine central bankers, start to talk about the dark problems that the Russian economy has, I think maybe people are trying to say, even Russians are trying to say to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , we can't fight this war forever.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, I think that you have the possibilities with the Russians to say to them, we are not going to do this over the heads of the Ukrainians, because they have fought too hard.\par \par \uc2 And that means that there is nothing wrong with having a discussion with Putin, but nothing can be a proposal that looks like an American-Russian proposal on how this war stops.\par \par \uc2 The Europeans are going to be critical to whatever cease fire eventually obtained. The Europeans are going to have --are going to really be critical to making it work.\par \par \uc2 Now, I'd be the first to say, I think this war does need to stop, and it may have to stop with some inconclusive elements on how the territory will eventually look.\par \par \uc2 I think, Ukraine can be rebuilt. I think their defense industries are quite remarkable. They can continue to hold at risk, things that the Russians care about, as they showed what they did against the strategic bombing force.\par \par \uc2 So, let's not think of the Ukrainians as having no cards, they do have cards. And let's not think of the Russians as having all of the cards. They don't have all the cards.\par \par \uc2 But if you go in with that mentality, I think this will come out just fine.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Knowing Putin, why do you think he agrees to this sit down, from a place of concern or for a place of trying to get his way in that room, one on one?\par \par \uc2 RICE: Oh, I think it's probably a little bit of both. I do think it's concerned, and I think he just has a hard time accepting that he can't crush Ukraine, a country that he doesn't actually believe is a country. He told us it was made up country. But now, with the threats against the Russian economy, with the shot across the bow about secondary sanctions, maybe, it's starting to occur to him that he needs to stop this war.\par \par \uc2 But he's certainly trying to deflect any action that the president might take. And so, maybe he hopes that going into the room, he can, once again, use words to convince the president that he still wants peace. But I would just hope that the president remembers what he said very eloquently, that he got off the phone with Putin and they bombed Ukraine that night. That's the true \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: My thanks to Secretary Rice. You can listen to that interview tomorrow on the Bret Baier podcast at FOXNEWSPODCAST.COM, \uc2 Spotify\uc2 , or wherever you download podcasts.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we are learning about 400 D.C. National Guard troops that have reported for duty here in Washington, following deployment by President Trump. Also hearing of some initial successes of the federalization effort. Still, there are obviously critics.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we hear from one crime victim on how she feels about the intervention. Correspondent David Spunt has the story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ASIYAH TIMIMI, RESIDENT, DISTRICT OF COLUMBIA: I haven't heard from one detective, not from my husband, not from my son. Not one detective.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT (voice over): For Asiyah Timimi, crime in the nation's capital is personal. Her husband, killed in a stabbing four years ago. Her three sons have been the victims of gun violence and survived. She calls the federal takeover of the D.C. Police Department necessary.\par \par \uc2 TIMIMI: If I was out driving, I would feel a little bit more at ease, like I'm not going to be carjacked or something like that. Yes.\par \par \uc2 And I think a lot of the people feel safer.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Tonight, members of the D.C. National Guard will once again hit the streets, much like they did last night, but with even larger numbers. An army official tells Fox News, more than 400 members are in the city ready to deploy.\par \par \uc2 President Trump today, teasing an extension to the Justice Department's 30- day arrangement running the City Police Department.\par \par \uc2 The D.C. Home Rule Act calls on Congress to approve any extension past 30 days.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, if it's a national emergency, we can do it without Congress, but we expect to be to Congress before Congress very quickly.\par \par \uc2 PAM BONDI, ATTORNEY GENERAL OF THE UNITED STATES: Thank you for what you do.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Fox News was there exclusively last night, as Attorney General Pam Bondi met with federal agents before they patrolled the city.\par \par \uc2 BONDI: Thank God for all of you and for what you are doing for D.C., for our -- for our country and our world, by keeping us safe.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): D.C. police referred Fox News to the White House Press Office for updates on statistics. There, officials claim 43 arrests overnight, including seven illegal firearms seized. Critics insist the takeover has gone too far.\par \par \uc2 GOV. WES MOORE (D-MD): Our national guards are not trained for this.\par \par \uc2 And we know the missions that they are trained for, and municipal policing is not one of them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): D.C. Mayor Muriel Bowser, in a tough political position, offering mixed criticism and praise of federal officials now in charge. She also recently highlighted the drop in police staffing numbers in the department. In 2020, almost 3,800 officers made up the force. Bret, now it's less than 3,200, and we're told most of that is budget related. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Continue to follow it. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Also "BREAKING TONIGHT", we are awaiting a judge's decision in California's challenge of President Trump's authority to deploy the National Guard to combat violence in American streets.\par \par \uc2 Chief Correspondent \uc2 Jonathan Hunt\uc2  has details tonight from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: ICE out of L.A.!\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF CORRESPONDENT (voice over): As protests against the Trump administration's immigration policies turned violent in June --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That's it --\par \par \uc2 HUNT (voice over): The president sent on to the streets of Los Angeles 4,000 National Guard troops and 700 Marines, ostensibly to guard federal properties.\par \par \uc2 The California officials argue the troops were used as an arm of law enforcement, and that lawyers for the state say is illegal, telling the district court in San Francisco, "The Constitution and federal law do not permit anyone, not even the president, to use the military in civilian law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 Trump administration lawyers told the Court the troops were deployed for protection of federal assets only. Saying, "Federal law enforcement officers were injured. Federal property was vandalized and the local law enforcement response was not sufficient to prevent that from happening." That is a strong legal argument, according to one conservative legal scholar.\par \par \uc2 JOHN YOO\uc2 , FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL, DEPARTMENT OF JUSTICE: When they allow crime to get so far out of control, that federal authorities federal property is attacked, then, the president can trigger his authority under these statutes.\par \par \uc2 HUNT (voice over): We also got a glimpse during this trial of tensions between the military and federal agents. Major General Scott Sherman, who commanded the National Guard troops in L.A., confirmed to the Court that when he expressed reservations about joining federal agents on an immigration operation, the Customs and Border Patrol sector chief Gregory Bovino, questioned the general's loyalty to his country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HUNT (on camera): And just as the trial wrapped up today, L.A. Mayor \uc2 Karen Bass\uc2 , posted on X, "Stop the military abuse, end the overreach. The judge's decision, which could come at any time, will be closely watched in Chicago, Baltimore, Oakland, and other cities. The president has name checked for potential troop deployment. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Jonathan Hunt\uc2  in L.A. Jonathan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Waiting on that judge,