iams has won seven Grand Slam titles, singles titles. It's pretty cool.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: "BREAKING TONIGHT", Virginia's governor orders an investigation into allegations of school funded abortions in Fairfax County, near Washington.\par \par \uc2 Governor \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2  has directed the Virginia State Police Bureau of Criminal Investigation to launch a criminal probe. A published report details the claims that school officials may have facilitated and paid for abortions for multiple students without parental consent. The officials potentially used public funds to pay for them.\par \par \uc2 President Trump is said to be supporting a new effort to ban presidents, vice presidents, and members of Congress from selling stock while in office. Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent says a lot of people go to Congress to get rich.\par \par \uc2 Senior national correspondent Aishah Hasnie has details tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Tonight, renewed efforts to ban elected officials from trading stocks. Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent, saying President Trump supports the idea.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT BESSENT, UNITED STATES TREASURY SECRETARY: Well, the president is already backing it, and I think it's something there is bipartisan support.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): But so far, there is no presidential endorsement for a specific bill, and without one, any momentum could fall apart.\par \par \uc2 Right before August recess, a Senate committee passed a bipartisan bill that would ban members of Congress, the president, vice president, their spouses, and dependent children, from buying securities, commodities, futures, options and trusts immediately. And prevent them from selling these assets 90 days after enactment.\par \par \uc2 Trump