und by blaming Democrats for soaring electric bills. Critics cite the party's push to shut down coal and nuclear plants without suitable replacements. Correspondent CB Cotton reports tonight from Englewood, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It's $200 more on my electric bill. I was shocked.\par \par \uc2 CB COTTON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: In the garden state, it's more than heat making the summer months unbearable.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Just shocking. I mean, the out -- it's just outrageous.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: A rate increase in New Jersey's electric prices is sending bills through the roof. Starting in June, there was a 17 to 20 percent rate hike for most residents. The state's regulatory agency is chalking it up to decreasing supply and increasing demand. Critics say the state's Democratic leaders retired coal and nuclear facilities before securing reliable alternatives, leaving the grid strained. With voters set to pick a new governor this November when Democratic incumbent \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2 's term ends, people here are looking to find relief at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm really looking forward to a change come November.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think this year we're going to be red. I just think we have had enough.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Republican nominee \uc2 Jack Ciattarelli\uc2  and Democratic nominee Representative \uc2 Mikie Sherrill\uc2  are campaigning on making the state more affordable. Ciattarelli says Democrats' obsession with wind farms and bans on National Guard are to blame. Sherrill says the state's largest power grid operator is at fault, telling FOX, in part, that the company is, quote, "ripping us off and refusing to connect new projects."\par \par \uc2 But even before the rate increase, garden state residents were frustrated.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY KONING, EAGLETON INSTITUTE OF POLITICS: Over half of New Jersians tell us that they are having difficulty affording basic things like utilities and other bills. And it's these kinds of pocketbook, kitchen table issues that are really impactful and really salient for voters.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 COTTON (on camera): Today, one of the state's largest electric distribution companies sent a note to customers acknowledging the high bills and saying New Jersey needs to expand its electricity supply. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: CB, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Democrats in Minneapolis are supporting a socialist candidate for mayor instead of the incumbent member of their own party. State senator Omar Fatehis being compared to New York democratic socialist Zohran Mamdani Correspondent Garrett Tenney takes a look from our Midwest bureau.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 OMAR FATEH, (D) MINNESOTA STATE SENATOR: To make an affordable Minneapolis that works for everyone, we need a mayor that works as hard as you do.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Minnesota state senator and democratic socialist Omar Fateh is being called the Mamdani of Minneapolis in his race to unseat two term mayor and fellow Democrat \uc2 Jacob Frey\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 FATEH: For working people, it's getting harder and harder to build their lives here in Minneapolis, and our mayor and the status quo are failing them.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: Like Zohran Mamdani, the 35-year-old son of Somali immigrants is working to position himself as a fighter for the working class and an agent of change with policies that would take the city even further to the left. Fateh wants to make Minneapolis a sanctuary for abortion and gender transition, raise the minimum wage to $20, impose rent controls, and expand affordable housing by increasing taxes on the wealthy, including a new local progressive income tax. But he says his first priority, if elected, is defending the city's illegal immigrants against President Trump.\par \par \uc2 FATEH: Protect our city from a hostile White House.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: Fateh, who represents the south Minneapolis area where \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  was killed and who previously supported dismantling and defunding the city's police department, recently got a big boost to his campaign when the city's Democratic Party endorsed him over the current mayor.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN SCHIER, CARLETON COLLEGE: I don't think Fateh would be a competitive candidate for mayor of Minneapolis without the DFL party endorsement. This brings him resources that he didn't have before and makes this a toss-up race.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: While the local, more progressive Democratic Party is backing Fateh, top Democrats in the state like Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  and Senator Amy Klobuchar have endorsed \uc2 Jacob Frey\uc2 , who is now viewed as more of a centrist in deep blue Minneapolis. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Garrett Tenney in our Midwest bureau. Garrett, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a new possibility of peace talks between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists. And, later, the panel on President Trump's stark warning to Russia ahead of Friday's high stakes summit in Alaska.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The top diplomats of Britain, France, and Germany are threatening to reimpose sanctions on Iran. This comes at the end of the month deadline -- the end of the month deadline is nearing for the Islamic Republic to resume negotiations with the west over its nuclear program and cooperation with the United Nations nuclear agency. The three nations saying they are willing to trigger a process known as the snapback mechanism that allows them to reimpose \uc2 U.N.\uc2  sanctions if Tehran does not comply with its requirements. The Iranian government has not yet responded.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, a follow-up on last night's reporting that Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  is open to a ceasefire deal that would return all his country's hostages. The prime minister saying his country will allow Palestinians to leave Gaza during an upcoming military offensive in some of the territory's most populated areas. Chief foreign correspondent Trey Yingst reports again tonight from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: As the war in Gaza rages on, regional negotiators say there is still hope for a ceasefire agreement between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BADR ABDELATTY, EGYPTIAN FOREIGN MINISTER: And we still pushing for a deal based on proposal in order to move forward.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Israel's Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  indicated the Israelis remain open to a deal but would no longer accept a partial agreement. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has expressed similar sentiment in recent days to return to negotiations. The Israeli leader wants all of the remaining hostages released at once, he explained overnight, as his army prepares plan to surround and eventually occupy Gaza City. Netanyahu also explained he wants to let Palestinian civilians leave Gaza.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER (through translator): Give them the opportunity to leave first from combat zones and also from the strip, if they want. We will allow this. First of all, inside Gaza during the fighting, and we will also allow them to leave Gaza. We are not pushing them out but allowing them to get out.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: The idea of evacuating Palestinians has raised concern across the region after reports circulated last night indicating that discussions were underway to resettle Gazan civilians in South Sudan, something the country denied. Although with ongoing Israeli airstrikes, Palestinians feel there is no safe place to go inside the strip.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (through translator): Where do people go? Where do people go? Oh, Arab and Muslim? Now all of Gaza is targeted?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 YING