y of American military might. We had Trump's growing frustration with Putin. We had the threat of the secondary sanctions, and then we had the imposition of the tariff on India. All this got Putin's attention.\par \par \uc2 And Trump's comment today that there will be consequences if the ceasefire is not agreed to is another reminder to Putin that something needs to be done here or else he's going to face severe consequences for his economy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I talked to somebody today who knows a lot about Putin, and that is former secretary of state Condoleezza Rice, who kind of set the table for what that may be looking like.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CONDOLEEZZA RICE, FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE: I think he just has a hard time accepting that he can't crush Ukraine, a country that he doesn't actually believe is a country. He told us it was a made-up country. But now with the threats against the Russian economy, with a shot across the bow about secondary sanctions, maybe it's starting to occur to him that he needs to stop this war. But he is certainly trying to deflect any action that the president might take.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: They had that \uc2 Zoom\uc2  call with the European leaders and President Zelenskyy was on that. And he posted, "We coordinated our positions ahead of the meeting in Alaska, and today was very important to hear that Ukraine, all of Europe, and the United States are pursuing a common core. In particular, killing must stop immediately. Diplomacy on Ukraine and Europe must be conducted together with Ukraine and Europe, and security must, indeed, come with strong guarantees. Pressure on Russia works. Peace has no alternative." Olivia?\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA BEAVERS, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": I mean, I think walking into this meeting, President Trump made an agreement with European leaders, according to reporting my colleagues did, that he wouldn't be getting into negotiations with land. But I 