r \par \uc2 PIRRO: They do know that.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- in gangs. And the older kids send out the younger kids to do the real dirty work, because they know --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Accountability.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- they're fine.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: They absolutely understand. And youth violence is on the rise, not just in D.C., but across the country. And if you think that these kids need to be coddled and they need to be hugged, they need to have consequences. They need to understand that enough is enough, that we're going to put them in jail or some kind of youth rehabilitation detention facility and not allow the D.C. Council, one of whom I just recently indicted, to take cover for these kids. It's time to end it. That's what the president wants. That's what we're going to do.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Look at what worked in New York City when Giuliani was mayor. He went after the small crimes --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- which led to reducing --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Quality of life.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- the big crimes, so the quality of life, so people --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- defecating in the streets, blocks from the Capitol, which I saw the other day. That was lovely.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The tents on K Street, the tents under the tunnels over here.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: It's terrible.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And they cleared up some of those out in front of Union Station, but it's horrific. This is the nation's Capitol.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And all over the United States, we have a similar story told. St. Louis, we've still seen --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Baltimore, everywhere.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- blight -- this -- they want D.C. now to be Baltimore.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Well, here's the thing, D.C. is the nation's Capitol. People come here for pride and patriotism, not to get assaulted, carjacked or shot, get caught in a crossfire like a young kid did a couple last month.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Judge, I've been hearing from my sources at the DOJ that there is a very, very big problem with staffing. Assistant -- assistant attorney generals on down saying we have great plans. We're doing great things.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But we're down like two-thirds staff.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes. Down --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What's going on?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: -- 90 prosecutors --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Why is that happening?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: 60 investigators --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Why?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: -- because nobody cared. I'm telling you right now, nobody cared enough to make sure that office was running. I'm going to have that office running. If you want a job in the nation's Capitol, in the premier office, the largest U.S. attorney's office, contact me at jeanine.piro@usdoj.gov.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Are you talking to the --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: usdoj.gov.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- usdoj, OK, got it.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Are you talking to the D.C. Attorney General Brian Schwalb --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, I talk to him.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- who I -- OK, so --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: But I talk to the mayor more, I got to get the mayor --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes. The mayor --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Mayor Bowser is working with us.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes. Like and people say, well, she just has the Damocles sword of the federal takeover hanging overhead. That's -- but I disagree --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: No, I disagree.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- she has a lot of common sense.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I'm concerned about the attorney general, given what I heard about, well, we want prevention. Yes, well, I get it. Yes, we -- you do want to get to kids early. There's nothing wrong with having programs. That's fine. But you cannot replace real accountability with midnight