ccountability, responsibility. I work 12 hours at least every day. You're never not working. You have to pay for water. The government, they waste, let me tell you --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I know.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: -- they waste millions. I'm looking at contracts. I want to indict people for contracts that the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The worst --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: -- giving our voluntarily --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- the worst --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: -- the worst --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- waste of money. And then they get --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: They don't care.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- you little things.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: They don't care. And then they say to my prosecutors, the A.U. (ph) says you've got to -- you've got to join the water club for $7 a month to get -- to drink good water. I mean --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You can't get a --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: -- it's frustrating.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- a glass of water in your office?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: No, no. They have to pay for it. They have to join the water club. All right, it's ridiculous. My job. I have great prosecutors in my office. I'm very proud of that --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And they want to make a difference, do they not?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: They do.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I have national security prosecutors. I have civil prosecutors. We do everything from civil cases, terrorism to local crime. It's a huge office.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But judge, you need 90 more prosecutors, OK, this is --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And 60 investigators.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- OK, this is the story. I might have -- no -- they're in the show, but --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- I mean, it's -- but -- but -- but -- when people hear you talk with such passion --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- that gives them hope, because I think a lot of people feel like this city was abandoned, and it's not Bowser, but it's everything that came before, and it took a long time to get into this shape.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Look, the D.C. Council has passed laws that are absurd. They've got some kind of, I think it's a Violence Interruption Act, where they put $20 million, they take murderers, convicted murderers to go out into the community, pay them to interrupt violence. OK, does that make sense to you? It doesn't make sense to me. What the D.C. Council has to do is change the law to protect the people of the district.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: In the likelihood, one to 10, 10 being most likely. How likely is, we're going to see a federal takeover of D.C.?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: It's up to the president. I support him totally. If that's what we need to do to get it done, that's what he should do and I support the president. He's the only one coming here saying, I want to make it safe and beautiful. I haven't heard anyone say that, besides him and he's got the wherewithal, the determination, the perseverance and the power to do it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This is a beautiful city. Judge, come back --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Good to see you, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- soon. It is so great to see you. The judge.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: U.S. Attorney of the great District of Columbia, which will be beautiful again.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And ahead, President Trump delivers a big win for America First. Plus, a big announcement later in the show.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Another day, another top CEO visits the White House to announce plans to do what American business should have always been doing, making stuff here instead of outsourcing it all to China.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Companies like \uc2 Apple\uc2 , they -- they're coming home. They're all coming home. Today, \uc2 Apple\uc2  is announcing that it will invest $600 billion, this with a B, in the United States over the next four years. That's $100 billion more than they were originally going to invest.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Trump always squeezes him for a little bit more. I love it. Now, \uc2 Apple\uc2  CEO \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  joined Trump in the Oval and gave him another piece of gold to add to all the gold that's already in there. It was a gift of engraved glass on a gold stand, everything made in the U.S. See, it's possible. Even the box it came in. But look, we're in the trust but verify mode on the Angle, because remember the company that makes the iPhone, \uc2 Foxconn\uc2 , they promised a massive investment in Wisconsin in the first Trump term. They're going to bring in 13,000 jobs, but they never delivered. So, we hope \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  will make good on this pledge and that the people hired in these new ventures will be Americans, not H-1B workers.\par \par \uc2 Here to discuss is Charles Gasparino, Fox Business Senior Correspondent. Charlie, the \uc2 Apple\uc2  news is great, but I'm always, you know, a little -- I'm always a skeptic, a little bit, because we've heard this before, back in 2011 -- 2021. \uc2 Apple\uc2  told Biden, it was going to add 20,000 jobs over five years in the U.S., including 3000 in North Carolina, but that campus and those 3000 jobs have been put on pause. Charlie, are they serious this time?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES GASPARINO, FOX BUSINESS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Yes, you know, Biden -- they better be. I mean, listen, Biden is not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Expect the ask, you know what of the century if they don't come through with this. I mean, this is just the way this president has worked. I've been covering him for years. You know, if you -- if you lie to his face, which this would be? It would be the entire Trump administrative case -- state coming down on you. And one of his big hammers is tariffs. I mean, I'm not a big -- I'm not crazy about massive tariffs on everybody --\par \par \uc2