uc2 LAPD\uc2  on our side. I'm not done. I'm not done. Our fight is with ICE. You're putting these people in danger. You're putting us in danger. You're not doing your jobs and you're looking at your (BLEEP) phones.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Someone deported her brain.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yeah, well, they iced her right out of that L.A. council meeting. Now, I know you have a big announcement, so I'm going to be quiet and give you the ground. How's that?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: No, I'm not taking over ICE operations, but that would be fine.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Darn it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- other than joining the judge. All right, big announcement. Tomorrow night in an exclusive interview, I'll talk with Holly. She was the young woman savagely beaten by that mob in Cincinnati. And I can tell you after talking to her on the phone, she's been through a lot mentally and physically. And she wants to tell her story.\par \par \uc2 So make sure to tune in tomorrow night, or set your DVR, 7 P.M. eastern, to hear everything she has to say. I mean, you think about -- you just think about that moment. It could have gone a lot of different ways, but she's still hurting.\par \par \uc2 Don't forget to follow me on social media. Thank you for watching. Remember, it is America now and forever. We're going to bring these cities back, Raymond. Jesse Watters takes it from here.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020250807el8600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Senate Parliamentarian Rules Against Including Language on Medicaid Cuts in Senate Budget Bill; National Economic Council Director \uc2 Kevin Hassett\uc2  Interviewed on Congressional Budget Bill and State of U.S. Economy; U.S. Supreme Court Rules State of South Carolina has Legal Authority to Block Medicaid Funding for Planned Parenthood Clinics; Trump Administration Argues Strike on Iran Successful Despite Low Confidence Intelligence Assessment Stating Otherwise. Probe Into Iran Strike Intelligence Leak; Trump Promotes Budget Bill Amid Iran Airstrike Fallout; 50 Hostages Still In \uc2 Hamas\uc2  Captivity; Bernal Abruptly Cancels House Oversight Testimony; Middle East Impact Of U.S. Strikes On Iran\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Chad Pergram, Shannon Bream, Jennifer Griffin, Aishah  Hasnie, Trey Yingst, Rich Edson \par \uc2 7,777 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right. That is it for us. Have a great night, everyone. I'll see you in the morning with Bill Melugin. In the meantime, hey, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Wasn't sure where we were going with the hammering.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: No, me, neither.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Quite -- Dana, like the flow rider (PH). Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Would you please save us? Save the show.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES HURT\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. OK, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I'm Bret Baier. The Senate prepares to take action on President Trump's so called, Big, Beautiful Bill. We will talk with his National Economic Council director about where things stand.\par \par \uc2 Former Secretary of State Condoleezza Rice, on the impact of the U.S. strikes against Iranian nuclear facilities.\par \par \uc2 And the House Oversight Committee issues a subpoena to a former Biden aide, demanding testimony about the alleged cover up of his mental health.\par \par \uc2 But first, "BREAKING TONIGHT," there is a major effort underway to find and prosecute whoever leaked information about the preliminary low confidence damage assessment following the U.S. strike on Iran's nuclear facilities. This comes as leaders at the Pentagon stage an aggressive and defiant news briefing this morning against insisting those attacks were successful. Again, insisting.\par \par \uc2 President Trump maintains Iran nuclear fuel was not removed ahead of that attack, and his top military commander is praising a group of soldiers and for the pilots for their valiant efforts to eliminate the threat from Iran's counterattack.\par \par \uc2 Chief national security correspondent Jennifer Griffin reports from the Pentagon tonight on the timeline of these events. Good evening, Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 The defense secretary was upset that a leaked preliminary intelligence report produced by his own defense intelligence analyst suggested just hours after the strike, that Iran's nuclear program was merely set back, not destroyed.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): This video shows simulated testing using the massive bunker buster bombs against a mock-up of the Iranian facility at Fordow, a plan that had been conceived and honed over the past 15 years by Pentagon targeters who first saw the shaft in satellite photos in 2008.\par \par \uc2 The B-2 stealth bombers dropped six bunker buster bombs each into two ventilation shafts above Fordow. The air shafts provided a direct path, unimpeded by rock, into the main complex underground. The fuses were timed, so the 30,000-pound bombs blew up underground, five feet off the floor.\par \par \uc2 GEN. DAN CAINE, CHAIRMAN OF THE JOINT CHIEFS OF STAFF: The pilot stated, "This was the brightest explosion that I've ever seen. It literally looked like daylight."\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): The Iranians covered the air shafts with dirt and concrete in the days before the strike. The first bunker buster was dropped to remove that concrete. Images of a dozen trucks outside Fordow in the days ahead of the strike led some to speculate that some of the 440 kilograms of highly enriched uranium may have been moved to another location.\par \par \uc2 President Trump, after the briefing, "The cars and small trucks at the site were those of concrete workers trying to cover up the top of the shafts. Nothing was taken out of the facility. Would take too long, too dangerous, and very heavy and hard to move."\par \par \uc2 General Caine praised the 44 young soldiers who operated the two Patriot missile batteries that protected the U.S. Air Base at Al Udeid from 14 incoming Iranian ballistic missiles -- Iran's only response so far to the weekend strikes.\par \par \uc2 CAINE: Imagine you're that young first lieutenant, you're 25 or 26 years old. You know that you're going to have approximately two minutes, 120 seconds, to either succeed or fail.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): The key question the Secretary would not answer, where is the 440 kilograms of near weapons grade highly enriched uranium right now? Nuclear experts say the 60 percent highly enriched uranium, which is a gas can be moved using steel cylinders each the size of a water heater or scuba tank. The Iranians told the \uc2 IAEA\uc2  before the strike they plan to move it. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jennifer Griffin, live at the Pentagon. Jennifer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Trump is pushing hard for the Senate to finish work on his so- called Big, Beautiful Bill. He hosted an event for the measure a short time ago.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, many others in the administration are on a manhunt tonight for whoever leaked the early damage report on the Iranian nuclear site that classified piece of data.\par \par \uc2 The other bit of news today from today's White House gathering a new trade understanding with China.\par \par \uc2 Senior national correspondent Aishah Hasnie, reports tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 Bret, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 First, on Iran, President Trump is now blaming Democrats for that intelligence report leak as he now tries to focus on closing on some economic wins here at home.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Flanked by everyday workers, the president talking up his Big, Beautiful Bill today.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We will deliver a record tax cut, a record spending cut, a record investment in border security.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): And revealing a new trade deal with China, although with very few specifics.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We just signed with China yesterday. Right? Just signed with China. And the China deal, we are starting to open up China. Things that never really could have happened.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): But his administration is still talking about his strikes on Iran. Doing damage control today on an unfavorable but low confidence damage assessment.\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: There is broad consensus emerging already that Iran's nuclear capabilities were indeed destroyed.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): The \uc2 FBI\uc2  now searching for the leaker in the intelligence community and on Capitol Hill, as the White House plans to limit sharing classified information with Congress.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: What would you say to lawmakers who say they have oversight privileges? They need this information.\par \par \uc2 LEAVITT\uc2 : This administration wants to ensure that classified intelligence is not ending up in irresponsible hands, and that people who have the privilege of viewing this top secret classified information are being responsible with it.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Deputy Attorney General Todd Blanche, even threatening to prosecute congressional members if they are found to be leaking. Some Republicans casting doubt that it came from the Hill.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is a report that was low confidence. A lot of times we don't even see low confidence.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Senators keeping their lips tight after receiving their first classified briefing on the strikes. But both sides agreed. Anyone who spills the nation's secrets should be punished.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): I mean, one is in prison right now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I know --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Yes, the gold bar is in the mattress.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): The law applies equal to everyone.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And Bret, on the president's comments about that deal with China, we're getting a little bit more clarification now from some White House officials. The administration and China apparently agreed to a framework to implement the Geneva Agreement. So, this is not a comprehensive trade deal. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah Hasnie, live in the North Lawn. Aishah, thanks.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Iran's supreme leader is coming out with a very different interpretation of his country's war with Israel and the U.S. air strikes.\par \par \uc2 Ayatollah Ali Khamenei\uc2  is claiming victory in the former and what he calls a slap in the face in the latter.\par \par \uc2 Chief foreign correspondent Trey Yingst is in Tel Aviv again tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (voice over): From Tel Aviv to Tehran, cities are bustling again with life, after the 12-day war between Israel and Iran came to an end.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (through translator): This should have happened much earlier, because it's the people who are paying the price. Whether it's our people or theirs.\par \par \uc2 RONY HOTER-ISHAY MEYER, RESIDENT, ISRAEL: Hopefully, world peace will come to this area, and we can all become friends again.\par \par \uc2 YINGST (voice over): While civilians, express relief that an intense exchange of fire between the two countries is over. Officials on both sides are claiming victory while preparing for possible conflict ahead.\par \par \uc2 AYATOLLAH ALI KHAMENEI\uc2 , SUPREME LEADER OF IRAN (through translator): This is an event that could very well be repeated in the future. If aggression occurs, the cost for both the enemy and the protester will certainly be high.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , PRIME MINISTER, ISRAEL (through translator): We fought with strength against Iran and achieved a great victory.\par \par \uc2 YINGST (voice over): Focus inside Israel is now shifting to the Gaza Strip, where, after nearly 630 days of war, 50 hostages remain in \uc2 Hamas\uc2  captivity. 20 are believed to be alive.\par \par \uc2 The families of those held in Gaza are calling for an end to the war and the return of their loved ones.\par \par \uc2 UDI GOREN, COUSIN OF \uc2 HAMAS\uc2  HOSTAGE: We have proven that when the U.S. and Israel work together, there is nothing that can stand in our way, no matter how challenging the enemy. President Trump, please put your foot down. Bring all 50 hostages back home and make the Middle East great again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST (on camera): Israeli media reports tonight that a ceasefire could be reached to end the war in Gaza within two weeks. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Trey Yingst, live in Tel Aviv. Trey, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we follow up on a story we brought you last night. The House Oversight Committee has issued a subpoena for a former aide to then- President Biden and the First Lady Jill Biden. It's part of the panel's investigation into the alleged cover up of former President Biden's mental health.\par \par \uc2 Senior national correspondent Rich Edson has the latest tonight. Good evening. Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Yes, good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 And now, a disagreement over whether Biden aide Anthony Bernal is refusing to appear before the committee or just trying to reschedule. Regardless, the House Oversight Committee has just subpoenaed him. In a letter to Bernal, Chairman James Comer writes, "Given your close connection with both former President Biden and former First Lady Jill Biden, the Committee sought to understand if you contributed to an effort to hide former President Biden's fitness to serve from the American people."\par \par \uc2 Bernal was reportedly so close to the first lady that he's been referred to as her work husband. The book, "Original Sin," detailed how Bernal would enforce loyalty in the Biden White House. He was scheduled to appear this morning for a voluntary interview. A person familiar with his interactions with the committee says Bernal simply made a request for a different date.\par \par \uc2 Though Comer says Bernal's counsel, informed committee staff yesterday he would no longer voluntarily speak with them. The committee has already interviewed Biden's staff secretary and Domestic Policy Council director, \uc2 Neera Tanden\uc2 . A source familiar with her interview says she testified that as staff secretary, she was authorized to use the auto pen at Biden's direction, and in line with how she understood previous administrations, since Clinton's employed it.\par \par \uc2 Comer says Tanden said she had minimal interaction with Biden that she would transmit decision memos to the president's inner circle, and had no visibility of what happened between sending a memo and receiving it back for auto pen signature.\par \par \uc2 Tanden's attorney also disputes that characterization and says she was never in the dark about approvals, and that "The auto pen was used only after the president personally approved the decision. Aides, at no time, signed these documents on President Biden's behalf."\par \par \uc2 Biden aides Ashley Williams and Annie Tomasini are scheduled for interviews. The committee has subpoenaed Biden's physician, Dr. Kevin O'Connor. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll follow it all. Rich, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Stock Markets surged today, taking aim at all-time records. The Dow jumping. 404. The S&P 500 finished ahead 49. The NASDAQ rose 194.\par \par \uc2 Up next, former Secretary of State Condoleezza Rice, on the long-term effects of the U.S. strike on Iran's nuclear facilities, and maybe what comes next.\par \par \uc2 Then, the president's "Big, Beautiful Bill" becomes a big, giant headache for congressional Republicans. We'll talk to the president's National Economic Council director about what comes next there.\par \par \uc2 And later, the Supreme Court decision on Medicaid funding that could have a ripple effect. Shannon Bream has that story.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Do you definitively believe Iran wants to build an agreement?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE W. BUSH\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think so long until they suspend and or, you know, make it clear that the -- that their statements aren't real. Yeah, I believe they want to have the capacity, the knowledge in order to make a nuclear weapon, and I know it's in the world's interest to prevent them from doing so.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That was October 17th, 2007 the White House Briefing Room, then President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This morning, the Defense Secretary \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: This was an historically successful attack and we should celebrate as Americans, and it gives us a chance to have peace, chance to have a deal, and an opportunity to prevent a nuclear Iran, which is something President Trump talked about for 20 years, and no other presidents had the courage to actually do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So tonight, we're going to talk about how the war between Israel and Iran and the U.S. strikes on Iran's nuclear facilities may be an altering event for the region.\par \par \uc2 Joining us this evening, former Secretary of State under President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , Condoleezza Rice. Madam Secretary, thanks for the time. Your thoughts about the larger picture here, after these strikes and what you think this means, the cease fire and where it goes.\par \par \uc2 CONDOLEEZZA RICE, FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE: Well, I will say that this was an extraordinary effort by the American military, following on several extraordinary efforts by the Israelis. And we will eventually know precisely how much the Iranian program was damaged.\par \par \uc2 But I think all of the evidence is that it was substantially, significantly damaged, to the place that for a while, at least, it will be hard to build a nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 And I just want to say, Bret, to have a low confidence assessment come out 24 hours later was kind of irresponsible. And so, I hope people will put that aside and look at the overwhelming evidence that, indeed, this set Iran back significantly.\par \par \uc2 It also changes the nature of the region, not just this event, though this event is sort of the culmination of that.\par \par \uc2 But when you look at what the Israelis were able to do to \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , what they were able to do to \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , the significant efforts against the Iranian military establishment, against scientists. This really is a now crippled Iran, and a crippled Iran is good for the region, it's good for the Gulf States that Iran has threatened. It's good for countries like Iraq and Lebanon and Syria with the demise of Bashar Al Assad.\par \par \uc2 And so, this really is a moment of opportunity. My only concern is that we follow this up, perhaps even pushing the Russians, who claim to be friends of the Iranians, to say to the Iranians, time to let the \uc2 IAEA\uc2  in, so that we can really investigate on the ground what the Iranians are doing. But this is a momentous moment in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, I want to ask about that. The Wall Street Journal has this, and it writes, after the cease fire test for Iran, Mr. Trump could offer two tests of the regime's willingness to change. First, will it grant the \uc2 International Atomic Energy Agency\uc2 's inspectors immediate access to examine its nuclear sites, if not, Iran has no intention of dismantling what remains.\par \par \uc2 Second, will Iran publicly recognize Israel's right to exist and renounce its desire to wipe the Jewish state off the map? That's the Iranian regime's forever war. If its leaders won't renounce it, they aren't interested in peace.\par \par \uc2 Just wondering, knowing what you know, if you think the Iranians are willing to go down those roads at a negotiating table in coming days?\par \par \uc2 RICE: Well, Bret, I have to say I don't have any doubts that the Iranian regime is not interested in peace. I don't have to have tests of that. We've had 46 years of the Iranians destabilizing the region, killing Americans. We've had 46 years of their proxies holding terror against Israel, against Iraq, against the people of the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 So, no, they don't want peace, but do they want to recognize the weakness that they now are enduring? Do they want to step back from their disastrous policies in trying to build a nuclear weapon from their disastrous policies in what they called the Ring of Fire, these proxies that were terrorizing Israelis and others?\par \par \uc2 If they want to step back from that, then perhaps there is a way for the Iranians to begin to reenter the international system. But I have my doubts, given who they are, this is a regime that's entire legitimacy really is based on destroying the Israeli state, based on being able to export the theocratic Islamic revolution, and so it's hard for me to imagine that regime as a normal state.\par \par \uc2 We should certainly test the proposition. But right now, I'm just glad to see them weakened, because this is a good day for a lot of people in the Middle East, and hopefully even for the Iranian people if the regime begins to understand that it can't export its revolution.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, it's kind of a bad day for the Russians and others who have said that they were in an unbreakable relationship with the Iranians. It actually didn't take long to break.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, and also sends the message that we can get in unobstructed. There was not a single shot fired at our planes and bombers, no Iranian jets launched, got in and out in what is called a very successful mission. And that's obviously a message to China and Russia as well.\par \par \uc2 RICE: Well, I'm very glad that there was also, as a part of this, a message about American credibility. You know, there have been a lot of people asking about, are we turning isolationist, etcetera, etcetera.\par \par \uc2 But here, the way that the American military performed, the decision to actually help the Israelis after they cleared the way, I think it really is a shot in the arm for American credibility, which I frankly believe has been suffering some since our disastrous withdrawal from Afghanistan. And so, that's a very good thing as well.\par \par \uc2 And I have to say, coming just before a \uc2 NATO\uc2  summit that was also quite successful in showing \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  that quite to the contrary of weakening \uc2 NATO\uc2 , he's actually strengthened it with new commitments to defense spending with Finland and Sweden and we have to keep after it.\par \par \uc2 Credibility is not something that you establish one day and then you sort of dial it in and say it's done. We have to keep establishing that the United States is going to try and shape the international system, not just be a victim of it, but what's happened in the last couple of days is very, very good for American credibility.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And I ask you this every time I talk to you, but you're done with public service?\par \par \uc2 RICE: I am, Bret. I've done my part. I got fortunate to serve a president that I trusted and who trusted me at a time of consequence for the country. I'm serving in other ways these days, you know, I'm a professor at \uc2 Stanford University\uc2 . Maybe I get to shape young minds and perhaps get to convince them that public service is a very high calling and something that I hope everybody gets a chance to experience.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Madam Secretary, we always appreciate talking to you. Thanks for the time.\par \par \uc2 RICE: Thank you. Great being with you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, the president's national economic council director on whether he wants to be the next Federal Reserve Chairman. And, of course, the latest on the big, beautiful bill that's coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We have additional information tonight on the Senate's effort to try to pass President Trump's so called big, beautiful bill. Senior Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram tells us about some of the obstacles and what lawmakers are trying to do about them. Good evening, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening. It's a big, beautiful riddle. The Senate hoped to start debate on the bill tomorrow, but it's not done. The Senate referee nixed provisions to control Medicaid expenses that sent Republicans scrambling to fix the bill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Everything is challenging, but they're all speed bumps, and we will -- we will have contingency plans, Plan B and Plan C. So procedural vote still tomorrow. We're plowing forward.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Senate parliamentarian \uc2 Elizabeth MacDonough\uc2  rejected language to bar illegal immigrants from Medicare and Medicaid coverage. GOP Alabama Senator Tommy Tuberville demanded the Senate fire MacDonough. Some Republicans won't let debate start until they get answers.\par \par \uc2 Even if the bill is not done, will you vote to proceed?\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON JOHNSON, (R-WI) \uc2 SENATE BUDGET COMMITTEE\uc2 : No, no, because I haven't seen the bill. I have got to see the bill. I want us to go through every provision.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: House conservatives are fuming at the Senate. They refuse to let the Senate change the House bill and then expect the House to accept their version.\par \par \uc2 ERIC BURLISON, (R-MO): We have given them a lot of latitude. They can find spending reductions, they can find cuts. That's what Washington is good at is kind of jamming people last minute, giving you something you haven't had time to read.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Now the Senate likely works around the clock in hopes of passing the bill this weekend.\par \par \uc2 When you say this weekend, do you mean Saturday, Sunday? Does that mean Monday morning at 4:45?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MARKWAYNE MULLIN\uc2 , (R-OK): Before work starts Monday, I'm pretty confident we are going to have a bill.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): But the Senate is just half the equation. The House can only lose three votes and still pass the bill. That makes meeting the July 4th deadline tough. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Chad Pergram live on the Hill. Chad, thanks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The president is adamant about seeing this bill on his desk here at the White House by Independence Day.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MARKWAYNE MULLIN\uc2 , (R-OK): If we make big changes in the bill, then it can set us back.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAND PAUL, (R-KY): All they have to do is separate out the debt ceiling into a different vote. I'm not for raising the debt ceiling $5 trillion. If they take it off the bill, I can support the bill.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So let's discuss the prospects for the president's bill and take a look at what else is going on with the economy after the tenure of current Fed Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 . The president says is he ready to make another choice.\par \par \uc2 Joining us tonight, National Economic Council Director \uc2 Kevin Hassett\uc2 . Kevin, let me start with the bill. You heard Chad's reporting. It seems like a bumpy right here this weekend in the Senate and then maybe in the House.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT\uc2 , DIRECTOR, NATIONAL ECONOMIC COUNCIL: I think this kind of bill is always a bumpy ride at the end. What happens is you have got elected leaders of the Senate who are going through their normal process to dicker about last-minute things right up to the very end, and we respect that process. And we have high confidence that the majority leader is going to deliver a bill that can pass the Senate and pass the House, and that on the Fourth of July, or even before, President Trump is going to sign the big, beautiful bill.\par \par \uc2 And we're so confident in that because of the analysis that we have that shows both what happens if we pass the bill and what happens if we don't. If we don't pass the bill, it's the biggest tax hike ever. It gives us a four percent drop in GPD, the immediate recession. If we do pass the bill, then we get amazing growth, over three percent over the next 10 years. We put $10,000 into the pockets of American families. We secure the border, and so on.\par \par \uc2 And so the choices are clear. But, you know, people are going to be talking about the details right up to the last minute because that's what the Senate does. And we respect the process and we don't want to micromanage that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There are Senate conservatives, obviously \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  is one of them, \uc2 Ron Johnson\uc2  is another one, \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2 , that have real concerns about the deficit and debt, as you look at the numbers, $36.2 trillion on the national debt, $1.4 trillion in the deficit. And they are wondering, is there enough being done to turn the ship of state here away from massive deficits?\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Right. Well, there are not going to be massive deficits in the next four years because of this bill, and also because of the other things, the other steps that the president is taking to move towards the balanced budget.\par \par \uc2 But think about it this way, that if we get three percent growth, which we did last time, when we had a bill which wasn't even as good as this one, then over 10 years the Joint Tax committee tells us that we get $4 trillion more in revenue. The House bill costs a little bit more than $2 trillion, increases the deficit by a little bit more than $2 trillion. So it more than covers the cost of the House bill and reduces the deficit with just a simple growth assumption that was proven to be true last time.\par \par \uc2 And in addition, don't forget, that it's the mandatory spending that is part of the reconciliation bill, and so the discretionary spend something kind of off limits. It doesn't count for the spending cuts that people are talking about. And with DOGE and the Office of Management and Budget, we have been finding all sorts of things, discretionary things that we can send to Congress to rescind. And when those rescission packages go up, a couple of them are ready, then they can pass the rescissions, they can stop the wasteful spending with a simple vote of 51 people. Again, it doesn't have to go through the reconciliation process.\par \par \uc2 So there are so many spending cuts coming. There's so much revenue coming from growth. And I haven't even talked about the tariff revenue where the CBO tells us there's $2.8 trillion over 10 for that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There is concern about the tariffs. Obviously, FOX Business has the economy shrinking 0.5 percent in the first quarter 2025 amid uncertainty. Economists expect the GPD to contract at 0.2 percent rate, the 0.5 GDP contraction shown in the final first quarter GDP figures as the first quarterly contraction since the first quarter of 2022. So, you're saying the growth is going to overpass this with tariff deals. The China deal is not completed, right? You have a U.K. deal, but what other deals are pending here?\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Right, well, there are a heck of a lot of trade deals pending. The president mentioned today in this really wonderful event that we had in the White House just a couple of hours ago that we are very close to a deal with India.\par \par \uc2 But I just would take issue with your characterization of even the first quarter economy because, basically, what happened, right, was that there were a bunch of imports ahead of the tariffs, and so net exports gave us the sort of weird accounting trick of a negative number despite a booming economy. And right now GDP now, which is the best measure of what is happening in the second quarter is giving us a really almost four percent growth.\par \par \uc2 And all of that is happening, I think, because everybody is racing to get their production here in the U.S. to create jobs in the U.S. And they are doing that, by the way, while inflation numbers have gone down. Inflation by any measure is lower than it's been in over four years. And so the Trump economy is working. And the first quarter number, I don't think, is any kind of a metric of the trajectory of the economy right now.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The president is considering, according to "The Wall Street Journal", former Fed governor \uc2 Kevin Warsh\uc2  and you for Fed chair. Do you want that job? Do you know if you are being considered?\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: You know, the president has expressed frustration with the policy decisions of the Fed. And I think that that frustration that he has with the policy decisions is based on pretty sound analysis. The fact is that inflation is way down. Interest rates in the U.S. are amongst the highest anywhere on earth. And reducing interest rates would be sensible and would save the taxpayers lots of money right now. And --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think it's a good idea to have a shadow Fed chair even though \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  is still in the job?\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: I think everybody knows that in May, there will be somebody else at the Fed. A and I think that people will expect, if interest rates stay high, that after May they will probably go down if the situation warrants it, if the situation looks the way it does now. And so yes, I think that I everybody expects that there will be a change of leadership at the Fed. And, you know, if the Fed is data dependent, they should start lowering rates now. And if it has to take a little bit longer it will take a little bit longer. But that will affect longer term rates even right now.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, \uc2 Kevin Hassett\uc2 , thanks so much for the time.\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, a major victory for pro-life advocates from the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 . And then the panel with reaction to a very aggressive Pentagon briefing today about U.S. strikes in Iran and laying out what exactly they say happened on the ground.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tomorrow will be the final day of rulings during this summer's \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  session. Tonight, there is powerful reaction to a decision saying the state of South Carolina has the power to block Medicaid funding for \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinics. FOX News chief legal correspondent, anchor of FOX NEWS SUNDAY, Shannon Bream has the latest on that tonight. Good evening, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Hello, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Well, in a six-to-three decision today the court held that an individual patient out of South Carolina cannot sue over that state's decision to block Medicaid funding from going to \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinics. Back in 2018 GOP Governor \uc2 Henry McMaster\uc2  issued an executive order directing state officials to take any abortion providers out of the state's Medicaid program, citing a law prohibiting the use of public funds for abortion.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  patient sued, arguing she should be able to determine which provider she prefers since the group provides a range of other health services in addition to abortion.\par \par \uc2 Well, today, the conservative majority held the decision whether to let private plaintiffs enforce a new statutory right poses delicate questions of public policy which belong to the people's elected representatives, not unelected judges. Justice Jackson writing in her dissent, "Today's decision is likely to result in tangible harm to real people. It will strip those South Carolinians and countless other Medicaid recipients around the country of a deeply personal freedom, the ability to decide who treats us at our most vulnerable."\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  officials today expressed worry that other so-called red states will follow South Carolina's lead, calling the potential impact to their operations, quote, "devastating."\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow is the final day of the Supreme Court term with several key decisions still in the mix, including a decision on nationwide injunctions. That came to the court in the context of a case on birthright citizenship, but it's really about that issue of whether nearly one of 700 federal district judges nationwide has the power to shut down presidential actions, like executive orders for the entire country.\par \par \uc2 But that case did not follow the same procedural path as a normal case would, so not clear if the justices believe it has to be released tomorrow with the other decisions as the term ends or, Bret, whether we wait -- may have to wait a little bit longer to get an answer.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll be tuning in tomorrow morning. Shannon, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel with the latest from the White House and the Pentagon on the U.S. strikes in Iran.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 13 in Tampa as the National Weather Service surveys damage after a tornado touched down in Pinellas County amid severe storms yesterday. Not yet confirmed the strength of that tornado or how long it was on the ground. Officials estimate 40 to 50 homes were hit.\par \par \uc2 FOX 5 in New York, with closing argument unless the sex trafficking trial of \uc2 Sean "Diddy" Combs\uc2 , prosecutors saying Combs used power, violence, and fear to rule a criminal enterprise for two decades. The defense will have its turn to close tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Raleigh, North Carolina, from FOX 8, our affiliate there. One of the big stories there tonight, the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  is sending out the latest round of refunds to consumers, it says, were tricked into purchases they did not want from "Fortnite" maker \uc2 Epic Games\uc2 . Eligible players who have not yet been compensated still have time to apply. The \uc2 FTC\uc2  is distributing more than 969,000 refunds totaling more than $126 million. That follows the regulators' first round of payments amounting to more than $72 million which went out last December.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: There is broad consensus emerging already that Iran's nuclear capabilities were indeed destroyed.\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, DEFENSE SECRETARY: It's in your DNA and in your blood to cheer against Trump, because you want him not to be successful so bad you have to cheer against the efficacy of these strikes.\par \par \uc2 REP. JASON CROW, (D-CO): To tell a member of Congress, listen, you can't question anything, you just have to praise the troops -- we can, and we have to do both.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A lot of reaction, still, to the Iran -- the strikes by the U.S., the president coming out with a Truth Social post about concern of the uranium possibly being moved beforehand, saying "The cars and small trucks at the site where those of concrete workers trying to cover up the top of the shafts. Nothing was taken out of facility, would take too long, too dangerous, very heavy and hard to move."\par \par \uc2 But the original intelligence report still is raising eyebrows that it came out at all.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CONDOLEEZZA RICE, FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE: To have a low confidence assessment come out 24 hours later, it was kind of irresponsible. And so I hope people will put that aside and look at the overwhelming evidence that indeed this set Iran back significantly. It also changes the nature of the region.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: With that let's bring in our panel, Matt Bennett, former deputy assistant to the president for intergovernmental affairs under \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  and cofounder of Third Way, "\uc2 Axios\uc2 " political reporter Stef Kight, and Steve Clemons, editor-at-large for "The National Interest."\par \par \uc2 Steve, what's your take about the fallout 24, 48 hours after, and this Pentagon briefing today?\par \par \uc2 STEVE CLEMONS, "THE NATIONAL INTEREST": Well, look, I mean, the president dropped the f-bomb on Iran and Israel for trying to keep this war going. They're largely doing the same thing on journalists who have grabbed a low, you know, a low confidence report and are trying to highlight that report some example of the president not achieving what he is doing.\par \par \uc2 We are going to learn one way or another whether we succeeded fully. But in my book, what the president is trying to do is remarkable in the sense that we just had a major attack by the United States against a major adversary in a very big way, and now he is trying to calm the waters and keep others who want to keep this conflict going, and I might put Israel in that category, from continuing to fuel these flames. And I think that is the sense and the context that needs to be added to this question about the efficacy of the attacks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Matt?\par \par \uc2 MATT BENNETT, THIRD WAY CO-FOUNDER: Yes, I'm not sure that's what Trump is trying to do. I think what he is trying to do is make sure he gets credit, which is the thing that he always tries to do.\par \par \uc2 And, look, I agree with Secretary Rice that we shouldn't pay much mind to a preliminary report that was leaked 24 hours later, because I remember, in the run up to the Iraq war that, you know, Secretary Powell went before the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  with intelligence that turned out to be wrong about WMDs. And the problem in both cases is that we didn't have any eyes on the ground to confirm what we thought we might know about the -- you know, whether WMDs existed in Iraq at the time or in Iran now. So we just don't know, and I think it's wrong to suggest with any confidence that we do one way or the other.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, I mean, that briefing was pretty detailed today. And the chairman of the Joint Chiefs went into specific detail. Some lawmakers, Stef, were briefed up on Capitol Hill. Here is a couple of them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON, (R-AR) SENATE INTELLIGENCE COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: I think it's safe to say that we have struck a major blow alongside our friends in Israel against Iran's nuclear program.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK WARNER, (D-VA) SENATE INTELLIGENCE COMMITTEE: The only way we get certainty is with inspectors on the ground making sure not only about the enriched uranium, but the fact of what this regime, it's a bad regime, might be trying to do in secret.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Stef, we can go round and round about the damage assessment. What is important now is what comes next, what Iran says, what Iran does. Are they getting to the negotiating table? Are they letting the \uc2 IAEA\uc2  in? What does come next?\par \par \uc2 STEF KIGHT, POLITICAL REPORTER, "AXIOS": That is the question. And that's the question that senators are raising as well. And Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , I was listening to him talk to the press, you know, after the briefing, and he raised a lot of questions about whether Iran is really willing to come to the negotiable table. Graham, of course, has very high standards for accepting any kind of deal with Iran. And he's watching very closely what Iran does.\par \par \uc2 And what was interesting is both Republicans and Democrats coming out of that briefing said that, regardless of the impact of this particular strike, regardless of how much damage there actually was to these nuclear sites, the reality is if Iran wants to again build up their nuclear capabilities, they can. And that's what they're trying to avoid now moving forward.\par \par \uc2 And so, what they are watching for is whether negotiations continue in good faith, whether we see some kind of real outcome and movement in that direction. But, of course, both sides are kind of spinning their version of things, with Democrats saying it's too early to tell how much damage has been done, and Republicans insisting that the U.S. has set Iran back years.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right, Israel and even Iran is saying that as well.\par \par \uc2 Steve, the big, beautiful bill, we call that shorthand because the president has named it that, but it is this reconciliation effort, and it has to follow in the Senate the Byrd rule. The Senate parliamentarian, down in the weeds, but has a lot of power to say what qualifies and what doesn't under this budget reconciliation. She is saying some things don't, and it's changing the dynamic of what they are fighting for over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 CLEMONS: Look, this parliamentarian is well-known to all sides. I would even say well-respected by both sides. She frustrated the Biden administration significantly when they had their own version of a reconciliation bill, of course, around the time of the American Recovery Act, and she forced them to strip out things. She is doing that again because this reconciliation process is limited to an extremely limited window. It is not the kind of voting that you would get that's not subject to cloture votes. And so she creating consternation because she is not allowing things that would have to go through normal process and be litigated and legislated in the Senate in a way that can be forced to a 50- plus one vote.\par \par \uc2 And so, it's serious and we will see what happens. But, believe me, she did a favor for \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 , for a number of other senators who do not support the deep Medicaid cuts, and by stripping that out of them, while they are complaining, she gave them a favor.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, yes. It's Stef's piece, and Stef, you write "For Senate Majority Leader \uc2 John Thune\uc2  it removes a thorny political problem for Republicans with a handful of GOP members deeply opposed to the provisions, but it will also force the Senate to find additional spending cuts to pay for the tax cuts that are the centerpiece of Trump's one big beautiful bill." They have got to do this over the weekend. It seems like they are kind of pushing it back. And the House then has to vote on it again before it gets to the president's desk.\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: Yes, there's a lot that needs to be solved if they want to meet the July 4th deadline that Trump has set. And we know that Senate Republicans are working to try to get back into the bill some kind of similar provider tax measure that will solve the problems that the parliamentarian has raised.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, running out of time. Panel, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a SPECIAL REPORT salute to those who lead the way.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I want to thank each of the members of this Congress for giving me and every ranger this extraordinary award.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Amazing ceremony. The Congressional Gold Medal ceremony held this morning in honor of the Army Rangers veterans of World War II. Sergeant Joseph Drake and Private John Wardell accepted the award on behalf of all the rangers. Wardell said, quote, "We didn't do it for recognition. We did it out of duty to one another and to our country." And to all rangers and to those gentlemen, thanks for your service.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, we'll tell you how Iran is deepening its alliance with China following the U.S. strikes.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast. You can follow me on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and X @BretBaier.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight and every night. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020250627el6q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Gives \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  Oval Office Farewell; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Invades Reporter's Personal Space\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Katie Pavlich, Jessica Tarlov, Jesse Watters, Greg  Gutfeld \par \uc2 8,594 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 May 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHARLIE HURT\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Shockingly unsurprising.\par \par \uc2 JULIE BANDERAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: And I would go out the front of the hotel while she would sneak --\par \par \uc2 WILL CAIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I got 10 seconds. And that was a huge come-from-behind answer. The Kim Kardashian stand in, Julie Banderas.\par \par \uc2 Today, who wins Friday night spotlight, it will go to \uc2 Charlie Hurt\uc2 . All right, that's going to do for us today from the heart of America.\par \par \uc2 BANDERAS: Bias.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hello, everyone. I'm Shannon Bream, along with Katie Pavlich, Jessica Tarlov, Jesse Watters and Greg Gutfeld. It is 5:00 in New York City, and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 It was \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's last day at the White House. Well, not exactly. The world's richest man wearing a DOGE father t-shirt, joining President Trump for a farewell news conference in the Oval.\par \par \uc2 And despite the media trying to drive a wedge, it was a love fest. The president gifting Elon a golden key to the White House, making it clear Musk is invited back whenever he wants.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We are totally committed to making the DOGE cuts permanent and stopping much more of the waste in the months to come.\par \par \uc2 Many of the DOGE people, Elon, are staying behind too, so they're not leaving, and Elon is really not leaving. He's going to be back and forth. I think, I have a feeling. It's his baby, and I think he's going to be doing a lot of things.\par \par \uc2 But Elon's service to America has been without comparison in modern history.\par \par \uc2 ELON Musk\uc2 , CEO, TESLA: This not the end of DOGE, but really the beginning.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What do you think would be easier, colonizing Mars or making the government efficient?\par \par \uc2 MUSK: It's a tough call.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But that wasn't all from the Musk show, Elon getting one last parting shot at the media over a report on his alleged drug use and explaining that mysterious bruise you see on his face.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: There is a New York Times report today that accuses you of blurring the line between - -\par \par \uc2 MUSK: Wait. Is the New York -- is \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ? Is that the same publication that got a Pulitzer Prize for false reporting on the Russia gate? Is it the same organization? That \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ? Let's move on. OK, next question.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What happened to your -- to your eye?\par \par \uc2 MUSK: This just was around with little X. And I said, go ahead, punch me in the face. And he did. Turns out, even a 5-year-old punching you in the face actually does this.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Little X did that?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But Elon's critics won't let the billionaire ride off peacefully into the \uc2 Tesla\uc2  sunset, some Democratic lawmakers around criminal investigations if they gain back power.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We can't be distracted by this oval office dog and pony show. The battle against DOGE is squarely still here, and we're going to have to continue to make sure that we hold them accountable.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Some crimes may have been committed over the past three or four months, and they are going to come to light, one way or another, they're going to come to light, even if it takes us getting into power again, but we will subpoena people and find out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Oh, Jesse, they can't quit Elon. They're going to miss him. If they don't have him as a punching bag.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: They should beg for him to stay, because he's saving us a lot of money. Democrats don't care about that.\par \par \uc2 When they did save us money, when \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  knocked off about 400,000 workers, we all said that a boy, Bill, way to go.\par \par \uc2 If \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  had cut expenses, we would have said that a boy, Joe, because we're not going to fight just to fight like they do. We fight for what's right.\par \par \uc2 Now, they're accusing him of being a drug addict. There was drug addicts in the last White House, and there was cocaine all over the place. They didn't say anything about that when an actual Drug Act was running the country behind the scenes. I haven't seen any evidence of drugs, but I am suspicious of the black eye. More on that later.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: You're always suspicious about black guys?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Black guys? Yes, Greg.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats, when you ask them what their plan is to reduce spending and save us from debt crisis, they don't have a plan. Jessica, the other day said, well, we have to raise taxes. OK, well, that's funny, because you can't raise taxes out of a $36 trillion hole.\par \par \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  raised taxes and the debt went up. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  could have raised taxes, but he didn't. Why not?\par \par \uc2 So, can't we just join together? And I say this to Jessica and I say this to all the Democrats, why can't we be good partners. We're all Americans. Let's work together.\par \par \uc2 Let's not just kick the can down the road like you guys do and then explode when anybody tries to fix the problem you created. We are going to die as a country. I don't want to die together. I want to live together in harmony with the Democratic Party. And we can just save this country from financial ruin. We can do this. I know we can.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Jesse, maybe you could buy a Coke for Jessica.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Buy coke for Jessica?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Not cocaine, the \uc2 Coca-Cola\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We're old enough to remember the commercial.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It was a Pepsi.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I feel -- no, it was Coke. I feel like you're about to burst into I'd like to buy the world a drink. Can you work together?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Oh, wait. That's specific, I (INAUDIBLE). Forget it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Can you work together with Jesse?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: 100 percent but Jesse's got to give up his tax cut and he won't.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Then you're not going to work with Jesse?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No. He's rich and he needs that cut.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Not a tax cut, we're just keeping tax rates the same.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: That's literally untrue.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: But it's exactly what they're doing.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No, it's not, it's just not true.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: They are.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It's not true. Listen --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But if they don't pass this legislation, the tax cuts will go away, so there'll be a tax rates.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Oh, OK.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Shannon agrees with me, that means you're obviously wrong.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: That's his thing. Whoever sits in that seat, if you like half agree with him, then he says that he's right. Give up your tax cut, and we'll talk about some cost savings. We'll go -- I mean, \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  promised us an audit of the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It was never passed.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It's never passed and they're heading a bigger budget. Let's talk about cuts to national defense. What do you say?\par \par \uc2 All right, anyway, Elon is --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Is there waste within the Pentagon?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: What?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Is there waste within the Pentagon?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes, I'm sure -- I'm sure there's waste everywhere. But at the moment where you are supposed to be talking about waste, you don't need this massive transfer of wealth to the wealthy. And the 2017 Trump tax cuts actually did have more for the lower classes than this package does.\par \par \uc2 People earning over $50,000, their incomes are going to go down. People earning over what is it, four million for sure, and I think even over 2.5 million are going to get a lot more money because of their cut, because of the tax cuts.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Jessica, it's keeping the rates the same.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: If that was a democratic DOGE master that showed up with a shiner, and there was a report that he did so much ketamine that he couldn't pee straight, you would be leading your show with it 100 percent.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Can I bring in Greg here? Because he's filthy rich, so I want to know what he thinks.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes. What do you think of your tax cuts?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Filthy and rich I'm told.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I'm filthy and rich with blood. Well, no, again, Jessica proves Jesse's point that they say that they are for the idea, but not the process. It is always that off-ramp to actually doing something that they will find, whether it is crime, immigration or the economy.\par \par \uc2 I definitely want to get rid of waste, fraud and abuse, except wait, but, oh, I want safer streets. But what about no cash bail? We want an immigration process just like you, except we don't like the way you're doing it.\par \par \uc2 And then, you know, we realize they don't have a replacement. The idea of talking about these tax cuts is not even close to being a solution. The solution is cutting spending. We're spending ourselves off a cliff, and you're saying we need to raise taxes. Why? Because, you know you can't cut spending. You want to raise taxes because, you know you will never, ever cut spending.\par \par \uc2 We just want to go back to five years ago. That's all we want. Can't we just have a budget from five years ago? That's not too bad, pre-COVID. Why can't we do that? You guys.\par \par \uc2 All right, I want to go back to the front part of this segment, where these Democrats were pushing the bad things that happened because of DOGE, that there were crimes committed.\par \par \uc2 OK, let's compare their predictions to the other side's predictions. We sat here and predicted the border crisis. They didn't. They mocked us. We predicted the trans issue. We knew this was going to be a big deal. They didn't. They called us transphobic. We knew that there was going to be a Ferguson Effect after George Fl