d the exit, the video I saw, so that was some progress for him. Maybe his --\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, progress is good.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah. Raymond, thank you. And ahead, does smoking pot make you a better parent? Yeah. We have a debate with a mom who says yes. Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I know you've heard of wine moms. Well, there's something new called weed moms. Women are turning to marijuana to cope with the challenges of motherhood. What did they do two hundred years ago? Some claiming it makes them better parents.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: These are the Saint Louis Cannamoms, and this is game night.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Cana game night is a night out with the besties away from the kids. You're meeting new friends.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Straight.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: You're going to be gifted some joints. You're going to play some games, and you're just going to live your best kid free life for a couple of hours.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Mom said smoke weed should be just as acceptable as moms that drink wine. It should be, you know, something that is exactly the same.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I normally do it in the nighttime or right after I get my kids on their schedule to sleep, kind of like, replenish myself. I water myself like a plant.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I think it definitely helps me stay patient. I actually want to sit down and do stuff with my kids instead of sit in school on my phone all day.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Joining me now, one of the mothers you just saw, Kimberlee Kesterson, co-founder of the Saint Louis Cannamoms, and Rachel Bovard, Conservative Partnership Institute, vice president.\par \par \uc2 Kimberly, so I'm a single mom of three teenagers. So, yeah, is there stress? Yeah. Every minute of the day. Yeah. But are there other ways to relax or calm down that doesn't involve like exposing your kids to, you know, marijuana being the elixir of all elixirs?\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLEE KESTERSON, ST LOUIS CANNAMOMS CO-FOUNDER: Well, who says that I'm exposing my kids to marijuana? I think that if I -- what I do on my own time, when my kids are asleep, what's the harm in that? People drink at ball games and all types of things in front of their kids. You're exposing your children to alcohol, right?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, so your kids -- your kids don't know what you do in your private time. Is that your point?\par \par \uc2 KESTERSON: I mean, they do know what I do in my private time, but am I sitting there blowing it in their face? No.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, what's wrong with -- I mean, look, what's wrong with this? I mean, you have this situation, Rachel, where a mom's just trying to relax, and this is the way she relaxes. Big deal?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BOVARD, CONSERVATIVE PARTNERSHIP INSTITUTE: Well, I agree that motherhood is challenging. Right? A mom of two kids, two boys under three.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 BOVARD: Shout out to my husband who's putting them to bed by himself.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Good.\par \par \uc2 BOVARD: But, you know, I do think that coping with chemicals is not the best way to do it. Although, I will say, you know, Kimberly and I are going to disagree on substance, but creating a community for moms who are dealing with this stuff is very important.\par \par \uc2 But I do think that marijuana is fundamentally very different than wine. This isn't your grandmother's weed, this isn't wood stock weed, this is very high THC content, oftentimes synthetic. Schedule one drug illegal at the federal level.\par \par \uc2 We know there's a body of research that suggests it can lead to schizophrenia, brain damage, especially in developing kids. So, I'd love to take the conversation into ways we can support moms, because they are stressed out and modern parenting is difficult. So how can we address that? You know --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Kimberly, there's no doubt about it. I'm not trying to gang up on you. There's no doubt about it. Being a mom, especially today with social media, with all the pressures that kids are under, and they're under a lot of pressures, bullying at school, is everything that they're confronted with. It is hard, especially moms that are working, moms that are working without a lot of means, it's hard to cope.\par \par \uc2 But are you aware of the body of research that really has -- this is from Harvard, Yale, I mean the best of the best research institutes that have demonstrated that in the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2 , obviously, their 2021 study, that said that marijuana use among young adults is, you know, associated with higher risk of suicide.\par \par \uc2 Two hundred eighty thousand young adults, 18 to 35, you know, revealed increased risk of suicide, and the risks of were higher for women than among men, Kimberly. There's also the psychosis, schizophrenia, all the other disorders which are really serious, including bipolar disorder, which, you know, isn't really implicated with casual alcohol use. And everyone tries to equate the two.\par \par \uc2 But not trying to endorse any of them, but your reaction to those concerns, health concerns?\par \par \uc2 KESTERSON: I mean, there's -- just like there's research on cannabis that show the negative side effects, there's research on Xanax pills that they're prescribing moms, on pain medication that they're prescribing moms, Alcohol poisoning. That is a thing.\par \par \uc2 Like, when somebody has issues with alcohol and they're detoxing from that, it could literally kill them. And they compare it just as bad as to somebody detoxing off of a harder drug like heroin. So, for me, I think that the risks of consuming cannabis outweigh the risks of consuming pharmaceuticals.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, I'm not for the pharmaceuticals at all, so I would agree with you. Kimberly, what stresses you out the most about being a parent? And this is -- this is a mom conversation here, because believe me, I'm dealing with the teenage years, which is not easy. So, what stresses you out the most?\par \par \uc2 KESTERSON: You know, I was late diagnosed in my 30 -- at 30 that I had ADHD, and I was always labeled as the bad kid, the kid that couldn't sit still. And the more children that I started to have, I have four now, those symptoms started coming out a lot more and it was a lot harder for me to manage.\par \par \uc2 And one of the ways that I have cut myself off of pharmaceuticals like Adderall, and things of that nature is by using cannabis. It definitely kind of just brings me down from that overstimulation. Sometimes my thoughts can really be all over the place, and cannabis just kind of levels me out and gets me back grounded.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah. I mean, Rachel, there are moms, out there, are struggling. I think there are a lot of people struggling out there. And there's mental health challenges. There's just daily life challenges. That's not new to society, but there's something that's going on today that is much more, let's say, toxic in the environment. What is it?\par \par \uc2 BOVARD: So, I think you're absolutely right that there's the challenge of being a mother by itself. Right? It's a beautiful struggle. It's the most meaningful thing I will do in my life. But it's incredibly challenging. But added to it is just the structure of modern parenting.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned social media is a huge -- a huge issue. Lack of prenatal care, lack of childcare, or where the system itself, federal policy is making it difficult for moms to, you know, be moms and be working parent, all these things.\par \par \uc2 So, I do think we'll need to look, as a government, where federal policy is making it hard, where we can improve tax breaks for families, where we can incentivize marriage, where we don't, in the tax code, where we can give better prenatal care, all these things are areas we need to be looking at.\par \par \uc2 But it is, to be a parent in modern America, is tough, especially when you have schools pushing ideological agendas, doctor's offices, the places you're supposed to trust. Parents are in a defensive crouch.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Kimberly, is there a -- do you have a church group or any group of women beyond just the cannamoms, as they're called. Do you have a group of people, a faith filled people that can just, you know, give you the love and support that, obviously, I think you need and you want, we all want. Right?\par \par \uc2 But do you have that where you are? And if not, I'm going to connect you to someone great.\par \par \uc2 KESTERSON: My family actually goes to church on Sundays. So, we actually talked about doing Bible study with cannabis, and, you know, showing that. Just because you consume, doesn't mean that you don't get to read the Bible and go to church and praise God.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I didn't say that at all. I want to continue this conversation. We're not over with this, but we got to take a break for tonight. Jesse is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020250327el3q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 NTSB\uc2  Investigates Deadly Plane Crashes In DC, PA; Trump Imposes Tariffs On Canada, Mexico & China; Interview With Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy; Trump Offers Buyout to Federal Workers; Poll: Trump Approval Ratings 10 Points Higher Than in Beginning of First Term; Trump Cabinet Picks Grilled On Capitol Hill; Bipartisan Measure Calls For U.S. To Keep All Options On Table To Deter Nuclear Iran; Trump Signs Order To Impose tariffs On Canada, Mexico And China Starting Tuesday; Voters Have Unfavorable Opinion of the Democratic Party; Future of the Democratic Party; Robert George and Cornel West is Interviewed about their Book "Truth Matters."\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Juan Williams, Grady Trimble, Lucas Tomlinson, Josh  Kraushaar \par \uc2 10,014 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 February 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 Tragedy on the Potomac as we get new details about the deadliest air disaster in this country in more than two decades.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's indescribable in terms of like the loss that we have.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): What caused the collision still unknown, as evidence is still being pulled from the Potomac and families mourn. And the fallout from the crash becomes a big test for the new Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 We'll talk to Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy about the latest on the investigations and what steps are being taken to keep travelers safe now.\par \par \uc2 Then, where the president's cabinet picks stand in the confirmation process?\par \par \uc2 SEN. AMY KLOBUCHAR (D-MN): Did he say that the headquarters should be shut down?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. Chair --\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: I deserve an answer to that question.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I'll ask Senators Lindsay Graham and \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  how they are planning to vote on the president's most controversial nominees.\par \par \uc2 Plus, our Sunday panel on Americans' views of both parties now that the dust is settled on the 2024 election.\par \par \uc2 All right now on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 We begin with a look at some of your top headlines.\par \par \uc2 Three more Israeli hostages are free after more than a year in \uc2 Hamas\uc2  captivity in Gaza including one who has dual American citizenship. In exchange, Israel released 183 Palestinian prisoners. Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  is traveling to the U.S. today to meet with President Trump about the upcoming second phase of the ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 Investigators in Philadelphia are still sifting through burned car, damaged homes, and the charred debris of a medevac jet that crashed shortly after takeoff Friday, evening killing all six people on board and at least one person on the ground. Investigators have yet to find the cockpit voice recorder which could hold important clues about what happened.\par \par \uc2 Now that deadly crash happened two days after a commercial plane and military Black Hawk helicopter collided here in Washington over the Potomac on Wednesday, killing 67 people, and we learned last night that the federal aviation system's main online warning system for pilots went down. technicians have been working for hours to fix the problem a backup system is currently up and running.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we'll get the very latest from Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy. But, first, we turn to FOX News correspondent Grady Trimble near that Potomac crash scene.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Grady.\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 There are flight delays across the country right now as recovery efforts continue along the Potomac, crews have pulled 42 of the victims from the river, many of their families are here in the D.C. area after a horrific week for aviation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE (voice-over): \uc2 NTSB\uc2  investigators are on the scene of back-to-back transportation tragedies on the East Coast. On Wednesday night, the first deadly commercial plane crash in the U.S. in more than 15 years. An Army Black Hawk helicopter collided with an \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  flight from Wichita, Kansas, seconds from landing at Washington's Reagan National Airport, all 67 people involved died.\par \par \uc2 NTSB\uc2  member Todd Inman has been spending time with the family members who lost loved ones.\par \par \uc2 TODD INMAN, NTSB MEMBER: Someone that want to give us hugs, some that are just mad and angry. They're just all hurt. I don't want to have to meet with another of those parents like that again, not fair, could be your family.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: With questions swirling over whether the helicopter was flying too high and whether staffing levels at the air traffic control tower were too low, Inman says there were five air traffic controllers working at the time of the crash but only one communicating with planes and helicopters in the air. The black box from the plane, the most accurate measure of altitude shows it was at around 325 feet at the moment of impact. But preliminary data indicates the control tower saw the helicopter at the same time was at about 200 feet. \uc2 NTSB\uc2  can't yet explain that discrepancy.\par \par \uc2 President Trump claims DEI hiring practices for air traffic controllers during the previous administration also bear some of the blame.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Are you saying race or gender played a role in this tragedy?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It may have, I don't know. Incompetence might have played a role.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: Two days after the midair collision over the Potomac, a medical plane crash in Philadelphia killed all six Mexican nationals on board and one person on the ground, injuring 19 more.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE (on camera): Two of Reagan National's three runways remain closed, including the one that American flight was supposed to land on, so the airport doesn't interfere with those recovery efforts. Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy is restricting helicopters from flying over the Potomac near the airport, with some exceptions until the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  releases its preliminary report on the crash -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Grady Tremble, near the sight of that crash, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to Lucas Tomlinson. He's in Florida. He's live there covering the president.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, President Trump has slapped tariffs on the U.S.'s biggest trading partners, which some fear could spark a trade war. Canada, Mexico and China are already retaliating.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Tariffs don't cause inflation.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's new tariffs will go into effect Tuesday, 25 percent on Canada and Mexico, and 10 percent on China. Ten percent tariffs will be placed on Canadian energy. Trump acknowledged Friday that could raise domestic prices.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : There could be some temporary short-term disruption and people will understand that.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: TD Economics estimates that Trump's tariffs on Canada could lead to an immediate jump on U.S. gas prices by up to 70 cents a gallon. Canada supplies about 60 percent of the crude oil the U.S. imports.\par \par \uc2 Trump says he wants to produce more domestically.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : They've treated us very unfairly in trade, and we will be able to make that up very quickly, because we don't need the products that they have. We have all the oil you need.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Trump's secretary of state in his first term defended the move.\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE: I think some of the risk of inflation that people talk about is likely overstated. Some of this will end up being absorbed by the producers.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: On Capitol Hill, Trump's picked to lead the Health and Human Services, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and director of national intelligence received their confirmation hearings.\par \par \uc2 Key Republicans voicing uncertainty about some of them.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL CASSIDY (R-LA): I've been struggling with your nomination.\par \par \uc2 SEN. SUSAN COLLINS (R-ME): I have not made a final decision.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: The vice president taking to the airwaves with a warning to wavering GOP senators.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE, VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You don't get to make these decisions. President Trump gets to make these decisions and he already has.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: In one of his first acts as commander-in-chief, Trump says he ordered F-18 Super Hornets from the USS \uc2 Harry S. Truman\uc2  to strike \uc2 ISIS\uc2  targets in Somalia, killing a senior planner and other terrorists.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  is in route to Washington he's going to be the first foreign leader to meet President Trump since he returned to the White House -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Lucas, thank you very much -- Lucas Tomlinson in West Palm Beach.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Secretary, welcome to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY, SECRETARY OF DEPARTMENT OF TRANSPORTATION: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to start by asking you about the Notice to Air Mission system, or the warning system for pilots that was down overnight. Can you give us an update on where you stand with that?\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 You know, Shannon, before we do that, I just want to extend my deepest condolences to the families that lost loved ones this past week in these air disasters. And I want to say thank you to all the first responders who did such a heroic job on the scene. We are all grateful for them. I want to say that first.\par \par \uc2 But, yes, the notice to -- and, by the way, it used to be Notice to Air Men, now it's Notice to Air Mission. The last administration changed the name because of their gender ideology concerns.\par \par \uc2 But that system went down last night. The backup system is now up and running as we try to get the primary system online.\par \par \uc2 But this -- it's an old, antiquated system that has to be upgraded. That's in the works. It has been in the works for years. We have to rush this new system online because, again, this is -- this is the second time in two years it's gone down.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and it -- I'm glad that you mentioned the crews that are working around the clock. Living in this area, I drive by that site every morning and every evening.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And these people are working around the clock, dedicated, and doing everything they can to especially offer some amount of closure or help to these families of the victims there. So, hats off to them, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to talk about the situation with staffing and what's been reported about what was going on in the air traffic control tower here in Washington on Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " says that staffing at the air traffic control tower at Reagan National Airport was not normal for the time of day and volume of traffic. That's according to an internal preliminary \uc2 FAA\uc2  safety report.\par \par \uc2 Can you offer any more clarity about what was going on that night with staffing?\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: So, again, I'll take the \uc2 FAA\uc2  at their word, that it wasn't normal. And so, that's part of the review process that we have to do, Shannon. We have to look and there's a consolidation of air traffic controllers, an hour before it was supposed to happen during the time of this crash.\par \par \uc2 And so, what was the appropriateness of that? What are we doing to surge air traffic controllers in this country? That's a different story, but we've had -- we haven't had enough air traffic controllers in America for a very long time.\par \par \uc2 And so, we're working with the \uc2 FAA\uc2  right now to go, how do we surge the best and the brightest air traffic controllers to get into the academy, to get trained up and make sure we don't have these staffing issues?\par \par \uc2 Our air traffic controllers, they are stressed out, they are tapped out, they are overworked. That's no excuse. It's just a reality of what we have in the system.\par \par \uc2 So, we have a plan that's going to come out to fix the problem. But the issue is, you can't flip a switch, Shannon, and get air traffic controllers here tomorrow. It takes years to train them up. But that's one of the questions that we're going to ask is, what happened in the tower, what was going on with the staffing, what should have been done and what was done.\par \par \uc2 What was the language that was used by the air traffic control? Was it appropriate? Did they appropriately direct traffic consistent with procedures at the \uc2 FAA\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 But also we have questions about, what was the position of the Black Hawk? Why -- you know, the elevation of the Black Hawk? What -- were the Black Hawk pilots wearing night vision goggles? And, if so, why were they flying a mission at 9:00 at night, through really busy airspace around DCA, as opposed to flying that mission at 1:00 in the morning when there's very little traffic around DCA.\par \par \uc2 I want our military to be trained up and ready to go, but I also want air travelers to be safe as well. And there's a time and a place to do it, not at 9:00 at night when there's heavy traffic.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, you mentioned this more broadly, the issue of air traffic controllers. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reporting that more than 90 percent of the air traffic control facilities in this country are operating below the \uc2 FAA\uc2 's recommended staffing levels.\par \par \uc2 We know last year when the Congress passed a massive \uc2 FAA\uc2  reauthorization, they said at that point we were down about 3,000 air traffic controllers.\par \par \uc2 So, first question, are our airways and our airports safe?\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Yes, they are. We have the safest skies in the whole world. Traveling by air is the safest mode of transportation. And just to that point, if it wasn't safe, if there was an issue with air traffic controls or staffing that couldn't keep the system and the people safe in it, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  would shut it down. So, no.\par \par \uc2 And we have redundancies in place. It's not just air traffic controllers, but we do have technologies on airplanes to keep them separated.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, no, this is -- this is the safest system. Our system does need to be upgraded for sure, and I will have a conversation about that if you want, but it is safe to fly.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, what about, is it a funding issue for having more air traffic controllers? Is it, as you mentioned, the recruiting, the retention issue?\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Is it a mix of those things?\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: There's -- there's a number of issues. So, during COVID, there was an issue where they shut down the academies. And then when the academies reopened, the class sizes were smaller. And then even though, you know, our graduates from the academy got out, they couldn't get into a tower to train up. That happened for two years. And so we saw a plummet of air traffic controllers.\par \par \uc2 There's a mandatory retirement age for controllers. It's 55 years old. So, every year, we have people cycling out of the business. We need more people -- more young people coming in.\par \par \uc2 And again, you can't focus on diversity, equity and inclusion when you try to hire air traffic controllers. You fire -- you focus on the best and the brightest. You -- I mean, again, some people like -- may like to have this conversation around equity, but if it's your pilots or if it's your air traffic controllers, you want the best, you want the brightest protecting yourself and your family. That's what we're going to do at the department for every air traveler. Getting in the great students to make sure our skies are safe.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and, at this point, as you mentioned, we're very early on, so we don't know if any of that had an impact on either of these situations. We'll talk about Philadelphia, too.\par \par \uc2 But Congresswoman Jennifer McClellan, a Democrat out of Virginia, said talking about that right now, she said: It's irresponsible to blame anything or anyone right now. That does a disservice to the lives that were lost.\par \par \uc2 Has this conversation about DEI become a distraction in the middle of this whole thing?\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: No.\par \par \uc2 So, Shannon, the mission of DOT and \uc2 FAA\uc2  is safety. That has been the historic mission of this agency. And over the last four years, when they're having a conversation in the last administration about changing names from cockpit to flight deck, or Notice to Air Men to Notice to Air Mission because it's too gender specific, they're focused on social justice and the environment, racist roads, and EVs, they've lost sight of the mission of this department.\par \par \uc2 And so, we have no place for anything other than the best and the brightest. And so, when you look at what went wrong, they'll look at the people that you have in place at the DOT and the \uc2 FAA\uc2  and the leadership and the direction that leadership gives them. And that's been misplaced over the last four years. We're back on mission. We're focused on safety and having the best people delivering that safety.\par \par \uc2 And I don't care your race, your religion, your sex, your sexual preference, that doesn't matter. Just the best and the brightest.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to play something from the \uc2 NTSB\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tom Inman said this last night about the recommendations that come from that agency.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 J. TOM INMAN, MEMBER, \uc2 NATIONAL TRANSPORTATION SAFETY BOARD\uc2 : Unfortunately, at some point, all of you are going to be gone. We're still going to have the investigation open. We're going to make recommendations.\par \par \uc2 We go to East Palestine, a community that gets devastated, still nothing's happened.\par \par \uc2 We have several hundred recommendations open for aviation. You want to do something about it? Adopt the recommendations of the \uc2 NTSB\uc2 . You'll save lives.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: He sounded very frustrated.\par \par \uc2 So, when he says \uc2 NTSB\uc2  recommendations need to be adopted, who does that mean? Does that mean by Congress, other agencies? What does that mean?\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: So, it's partnership, right? It's part of the DOT, the \uc2 FAA\uc2 , and it's part of Congress. And so, there's a lot of recommendations there. I've talked to the \uc2 NTSB\uc2 . We want to put a list together of the top 10, 15 recommendations that they've made, focus on them, and start to implement them.\par \par \uc2 But, Shannon, our air traffic control system, a lot of that technology dates back to World War II. We developed the best technology in this country and other people use it, but we don't implement it here. So, we have to have a conversation with Congress, get their best minds, their best resources, to come up with a plan to upgrade the American system. It will make it more efficient. It will make it safer.\par \par \uc2 If I hear \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  talk about this once, he talked about it ten times, if the air traffic control system doesn't work in America as effectively as another place in the world, let's upgrade it. We should be the best. And so, again, I need Congress' help on that one.\par \par \uc2 But there's other recommendations that I'm going to work with the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  to categorize them as the most important to least important and start to implement.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, as a member of Congress, you know it can be a heavy lift to get things done over there, a former member.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Secretary, thank you very much. We appreciate your time and wish you all the best and the challenges of this new job.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Yes. Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So we've got senators from both sides of the aisle here to weigh in on the fate of President Trump's latest round of cabinet nominees with razor thin margins expected. Are they ready to announce their votes? I'll ask.\par \par \uc2 And up next, we're bringing our Sunday panel with their thoughts on the president's moves to slash the federal workforce. How that's going down?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : You know, people have been trying to reduce federal government now for 40 years, 45 years, and they haven't been able to do it. And this is a way of doing it, I think, very nicely. It's sort of a buyout, too. I think we're treating people very good. But if they don't show up, we've accomplished a very serious goal. We're trying to reduce government.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So President Trump talking about his buyout plan, offering millions of federal workers eight months' salary if they agree to leave their position. They've got to decide this week. It's all part of an aggressive push to shrink the federal workforce.\par \par \uc2 Time now for our group. Outkick Columnist Mary Katharine Ham, Josh Holmes, former Chief of Staff to \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , Fox News Senior Political Analyst Juan Williams and Josh Kraushaar, Editor-in-Chief of "Jewish Insider."\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. So you know the deal that is on the table. There are millions impacted. Here's the fork in the road. Designation that has come out in email but is also on the OPM government website, says the federal workforce is expected to undergo significant near-term changes. As a result, you may wish to depart the federal government on terms that provide you with sufficient time and economic security to plan for your future. And, Josh, they add, have a nice vacation.\par \par \uc2 JOSH HOLMES, FORMER CHIEF OF STAFF TO \uc2 MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 : Have a nice vacation. Very kind. Very kind.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think you're looking at a Trump administration that had four long years to think about what they would do differently. Clearly, they've had a mandate since the first Trump administration to try to reduce the federal footprint, and now they figured out how to do it. And I think, look, according to the Trump administration, 6%, 6% of the federal workforce shows up for work on a day-to-day basis. There is not an American taxpayer anywhere in this country that thinks that's acceptable. This is one way to remedy all of that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Senator Tim Kaine, Democrat out of Virginia, says not so fast. Here's his warning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM KAINE (D-VA): My message to federal employees who receive this is, yeah, the president has tried to terrorize you for about a week and then gives you a little sweetheart offer. If you resign in the next week, we're just going to pay you for doing nothing for the next seven months. Don't be fooled.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Josh, he's warning them, and we know a lot of people are considering it around here.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Well, look, the big question is, do you take on the federal workforce with a scalpel or do you do it with an ax? And this has got \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's fingerprints all over the changes that are taking place in the federal government.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think a lot of people support a lot of the wasteful initiatives, the DEI executive order. That's been a frustration even for people who are not particularly conservative. But I think there's going to be some challenges because Congress needs to appropriate the money for the buyouts, and it could cost billions of dollars if enough people take these buyouts, and it may prove to be legally dubious to be able to just cut as much of the workforce as \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  want to do.\par \par \uc2 So I think this is going to have some serious legal challenges to it. And it may prove that to actually take on the workforce and take on the federal government, you have to do it a little more carefully. And you also want to be careful that you don't lose some of the more essential people like air traffic control staffers that are essential to run the government.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and we do know that there are exceptions carved out, military, those who are involved with public safety. The \uc2 FAA\uc2  says they're still onboarding people, so we'll see.\par \par \uc2 But in the meantime, this is all coming as President Trump is getting good news, feeling emboldened about all these things, which are caught up in a lot of legal wrangling.\par \par \uc2 "Quinnipiac Poll" this week says, "President Trump is starting his second term in the White House with a job approval rating 10 points higher than at the start of his first presidential term."\par \par \uc2 Juan?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think it's a honeymoon right now. The question is, how long does the honeymoon last, Shannon? And what we've seen with recent presidents is that the honeymoon does not last very long. First 100 days, we saw, I think it was \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  went down 17, Obama went down -- and, of course, Trump went down, I think, 8 plus points in his first 100 days in his first term.\par \par \uc2 But I think that what you have to understand is that for this moment, when you look at Trump's behavior in the past week, we talk about these federal job cuts. Well, what happens if people don't like government? Everybody says, you know, don't 6% show up. OK, that's terrible.\par \par \uc2 But then what happens when you have \uc2 FBI\uc2  purging? What happens when you have questions about what's going on at the Secret Service, at air traffic controls? Oh, so we have fewer people. Does it mean less security for us as Americans and for our families? Does it mean that there are going to be consequences? Does it mean the stock market suddenly has a reaction to the tariffs? What happens if the stock market goes down?\par \par \uc2 So I think that you have to keep in mind two things. Honeymoons don't last in two. Trump's approval is higher right now than it ever was in his first term, but it's still not as high as most incoming presidents.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, he's done a lot of things, some of them controversial, some of them stuck in legal wrangling, like we said. The birthright citizenship order, you know, the federal hiring freeze, all kinds of things like that. But he's feeling very emboldened because the Quinnipiac poll shows a lot of support for other things that he does want to do or he's trying to do, things like troops to the border, that kind of thing.\par \par \uc2 But the headline for "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " is this, "Trump's 'Flood The Zone' Strategy Leaves Opponents Gasping in Outrage." "This time the flood is bigger, wider, and more brutally efficient."\par \par \uc2 M.K.?\par \par \uc2 MARY KATHARINE HAM, OUTKICK COLUMNIST: Right, I think that's part of the plan. And honestly, when you see things like the rescission of the one memo on the freeze of federal spending, it's out of character from the rest of it, which is going pretty smoothly. I think it's a different tack than last time.\par \par \uc2 Also, oh, to be terrorized like a federal worker with an eight-month severance package. Will someone please terrorize me? I have never been terrorized in such a way before. Federal workers, civil service workers have protections that the American people don't know about, have never been familiarized with. One of the goals of, I think, their communication process here in doing this is to advertise the way that federal workers are treated differently than normal Americans in their jobs. They're being asked to come to work.\par \par \uc2 To a lot of Americans, that does not seem unreasonable. Juan has a point, or you guys have a point, that if you don't do it with a scalpel, maybe you have some disruptions. But people -- the American people ask for change. He said he was going to change things. There is a sliding scale on the mandate for disruption, right, and how much you can withstand.\par \par \uc2 But the Leviathan is going to resist the harpoons being thrown at it. And I think that federal workers do risk a little bit overplaying their hands by thinking that they are the most important thing to democracy. Many people in America who are working differently than federal workers do, do not see them as the pillar of the democracy that they see themselves as. They see them as federal employees who work at taxpayer expense.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, they have to make a decision by February 6th, so it will be interesting to see how many numbers decide to pursue this route.\par \par \uc2 Panel, do not go far, because the president's most controversial nominees were grilled on the Hill as the president and his allies lean on GOP senators to swiftly confirm his nominees, or some say face the possibility of a primary. We're going to ask Senators \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  how they plan to vote. They're just ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The Senate has already confirmed eight of President Trump's exceptional cabinet nominees. We expect the Senate to continue quickly confirming the remainder of President Trump's well-qualified nominees in the days ahead.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: White House Press Secretary Karoline Leavitt, feeling optimistic about the rest of President Trump's cabinet nominees making it through the Senate. But the toughest are still to come, and our next guest may hold one of the key votes.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Senator \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . Welcome back to Fox News Sunday.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): Hi, how are you?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, I'm great. Let's start with RFK Jr. He's been through his hearing. This is what "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " says, "His confirmation hearings were even worse than expected. The \uc2 HHS\uc2  nominee's answers to Senators' questions were absurd and dangerous."\par \par \uc2 Now, I know that you've had multiple conversations with him. Where are you this morning?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I have. I've invested a lot of time to really understand his background and to learn more about the man. And I've sat down with him twice in my office to have a conversation. So whatever the vote does comes down on, that's going to come -- it's going to come an informed view.\par \par \uc2 And I approached with an open mind. And I watched the hearing and that's how the process works.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, have you made a decision?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I -- well, I've been following it. And I talked to my colleagues on both sides and I've said this on the record. It's like -- you know, it's been challenging for sure. Absolutely. It's certainly not a slam dunk for the nomination.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Will you reveal your vote before you get to the floor or is this going to be like a game-time decision for you?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: No. I mean, it's like -- you mean right before I stick my thumb in the door?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. Are we going to know before that?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: No. No. It's just -- it's just part of an ongoing -- it's part of the ongoing dialogue on where we are at this.\par \par \uc2 But I've made an investment to really understand and talk to all of the nominees. And I treated everyone with respect. And I took the time to listen.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: And that's been my commitment.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So you've also met with Kash Patel. He's been tapped to lead the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . There are reports that after your conversation, you said he assured you he would not go after his so-called enemies list. Have you made a decision on him?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Well, and again, that's part of their own process too. I think I believe I'm the only Democrat, or maybe one of the only few that have actually met with all of them.\par \par \uc2 And again, I've approached all of them with, again, that open mind to have a conversation. And I don't trust -- and certainly not, you know, many of the things that were written or said about me weren't true. So I assume the same might be them as well too.\par \par \uc2 So I've maintained an on dialogue to really understand and I've watched these hearings. And that's part of -- you know, if our job is to consent, to consent, and that is part of the process. And there are some along, out of all of them, I have voted for them. And there's some that I won't vote for them, but it's going to be about an open process that I've maintained throughout the entire time.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And I know that includes meeting with \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2  as well. I mean, she's got concerns from both sides of the aisle.\par \par \uc2 Can you tell us more about your conversations with her and if you've landed on a decision regarding her?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: No. It's -- again, I just really pretty much the exact same as the two that you just brought up.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Now you have, as you noted, voted for some of President Trump's nominees in this process and you were one of the only Democrats to vote for the Laken Riley Act. We saw you as part of the signing ceremony for that with her family, with President Trump this week.\par \par \uc2 Now, there's been some blowback from the left about voting for that act, about the ICE raids that are going on now across the country. I want to get your reaction to some local officials who say either they're not going to cooperate, they're going to actively block these, or in some cases, they're just going to give people advice on how to avoid the raids.\par \par \uc2 Here are some local officials reacting in the last few days.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACOB FREY\uc2 , MAYOR OF MINNEAPOLIS: Well, police officers will not be cooperating with federal law enforcement around federal immigration law.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JOHNSON, MAYOR OF CHICAGO: Being a welcoming city just simply states that our local police department will not behave as federal agents.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM SCOTT, SAN FRANCISCO POLICE CHIEF: We have a sanctuary ordinance, a state law SB 54, that generally prohibits our police department from participating in immigration enforcement.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So what obligations do or don't you think local officials have with respects to the Feds and these deportations?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I hope -- I hope every local official would agree that anyone here, any of the migrants that are here with criminal backgrounds or engaging in criminal behavior, I think they should support that.\par \par \uc2 Now, if ICE comes to round them up, I hope they would provide that kind of support. I certainly would never support terrorizing otherwise innocent migrants in those -- in those kinds of situations or kicking in a door at a school.\par \par \uc2 But I think we can all agree that every migrant here with a criminal background or engaging in criminal behavior should all need to go.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So you also were the only Democrat vote in trying to move together a measure pushing back against the \uc2 ICC\uc2 . Specifically, it seems for their decision to go after Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , among others. This Illegitimate Court Counteraction Act was supposed to impose sanctions with respect to the \uc2 ICC\uc2 , engaged in any effort to investigate, arrest, detain or prosecute any protected person of the United States and its allies.\par \par \uc2 Why do you think you were the only Democrat to vote on moving that forward procedurally?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Well, I said that on social media that I was incredibly disappointed. I absolutely disagree with that. I can't really understand why my party hasn't joined me in voting to sanction the \uc2 ICC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 When you've equivocated the Democratic leader of our special ally with the rapists and cowards of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , especially after what you've seen, after terrorizing that poor hostage, you had hundreds and hundreds of people, you know, the chaos. You know, it's like -- and they've equivocated that with the prime minister. It's vile.\par \par \uc2 I mean, my colleague that's going to be next up on your show, we've had a long-going conversation in order to sanction the \uc2 ICC\uc2 . And I was absolutely supportive of that. And I don't really can't defend why my party chose not to.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So the prime minister is going to -- he's on his way to Washington to have meetings here, including at the White House with President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that there will be a different relationship between the U.S. and Israel with the Trump administration versus a Biden administration?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Well, there's going to be some situations where I absolutely support some of the decisions with Israel that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  made. And I'm going to agree with some of the decisions with President Trump. Or I'm going to disagree with them as too. No. But that's why my vote and my voice is going to follow Israel through all of the situation.\par \par \uc2 I'm going to disagree with either of the presidents, but I'm going to stand with Israel, just like that vote against the \uc2 ICC\uc2 . And I absolutely was supporting blocking the ban on the 2,000-pound bombs as well.\par \par \uc2 I fully support bringing this back.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Senator, we will watch for your votes on all those critical nominees, but you can always let me know if you decide to before you press that button. It's always great to see you, sir. Thanks for coming on.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Oh. Well, thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So joining us now is your colleague senator -- from South Carolina, GOP Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 . Welcome back, Senator.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. I want to ask you about something, by the way, that you and Senator Fetterman have worked on a few things, but you also have both signed --\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- on to this resolution with regard to Iran's nuclear program.\par \par \uc2 Part of that resolution says that --\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- all options should be considered. You and I have had this conversation about whether you mean a U.S. military strike in Iran. What do you mean? What are you urging President Trump to keep open as an option?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: We're trying to define the world in which we live in accurately. Senator Fetterman has been a champion for the state of Israel. I went to him and asked him, would you work with me to do a census Senate resolution saying that over decades Iran has been trying to acquire a nuclear weapon, not a nuclear power plant, that they're the largest state sponsor of terrorism, that they would blow Israel off the map, that they had a nuclear weapon, they would come after us? And he said, I would be glad to.\par \par \uc2 So what this resolution does, it lays out the case against Iran's nuclear ambition. Bibi and the Israelis are going to have to make a decision relatively soon what to do about the Iran nuclear program. This is not an authorization to use force.\par \par \uc2 But I am here to tell you and the audience in the world that I think America should support an effort by Israel if they decide to decimate the Iranian nuclear program because I think it's a threat to mankind. Israel is strong. Iran is weak. \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , \uc2 Hamas\uc2  have been decimated. They're not finished off, but they've been weakened. And there's an opportunity to hit the Iran nuclear program in a fashion I haven't seen in decades.\par \par \uc2 And I think it would be in the world's interest for us to decimate the Iranian nuclear threat while we can. If we don't, we will regret it later. And Senator Fetterman has been a champion on this and every other issue regarding Israel.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So it sounds like you're saying, it's more about supporting Israel if they decide to strike versus us having --\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- a U.S. military strike? OK. Now --\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: They -- yes, they need some assistance, but it would be their actions.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Now I want to try to get some answers from you on some of these nominees. Let's start with RFK Jr. You've been one of the most pro-life folks --\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- in the Senate for a long time.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: "The National Review" has this warning.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Pro-lifers should be especially wary of a man who publicly advocated legislation to codify Roe v. Wade in 2023 and affirmed in 2024 that he supported legal abortion, quote, even if it's full term.\par \par \uc2 Given your dedication to that topic, is it something you're willing to compromise on?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Well, I am now OK supporting RFK Jr. because I think during the course of the hearing, he's committed to a Republican pro-life agenda, President Trump's pro-life agenda, Mexico City policy dealing with chemical abortions. So I will take him at his word. I'm comfortable with what he said on the pro-life issue. He has been radically pro-choice as a person.\par \par \uc2 But I do believe that as secretary, he will implement a pro-life agenda that will be pushed by President Trump. So I will be a yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: But I'll also watch every movie he makes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. I'm assuming you're a yes on RFK Jr. Assuming you're a yes on Kash Patel. What about \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yes. Yes, I'll be yes on her. I was waiting to see what she said about 702. That's the ability to track foreigners overseas who may be involved in plots against the country.\par \par \uc2 I actually served in a reserve unit with Tulsi. I've always liked her. I think a lot of her criticism against her has been really mean and off-base, but I'll be a yes now. She's shown her understanding of how vital 702 is.\par \par \uc2 So I'll be a yes on RFK Jr., \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And Kash Patel knocked it out of the park. He's the antidote to Crossfire Hurricane. He's going to go in and clean the \uc2 FBI\uc2  out to make sure it's never abused like it was in the past.\par \par \uc2 And Crossfire Hurricane was one of the saddest, dangerous -- most dangerous chapters in \uc2 FBI\uc2  history. He's the guy that helped expose it. And he'll be - - he'll be the solution not the problem --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: -- when it comes to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  in my view.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So you know -- you know that much of what he faced in questioning was about retribution, about what he's going to do within the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . He's not there yet --\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- but my colleagues at foxnews.com report this. Acting Deputy Attorney General, Emil Bove, sent a memo to the acting \uc2 FBI\uc2  director Friday evening directing him to terminate eight \uc2 FBI\uc2  employees and identify all current and former bureau personnel assigned to January 6th and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  cases for an internal review.\par \par \uc2 Now Senator Blumenthal, your colleague, says, I'm appalled by this vile retaliation against agents who sought to hold accountable people, who stabbed cops with flagpoles and crushed officers until they broke bones. The silence from my Republican colleagues is disgusting and deafening. Your response.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Well, number one, as to changing personnel at the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , I think that's a good place to start. How do you expect the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to change if you keep everybody in place that's been part of the problem?\par \par \uc2 I've been open about the January 6th pardons of people who committed violent acts against cops. I don't like it. But we're talking about the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . We're talking about people who look the other way. We're part of the problem. Allowed the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to get warrants against an American citizen called \uc2 Carter Page\uc2  based on Russian disinformation called the Steele dossier. They lied to the court. So I'm looking forward to some of these people finding a new job.\par \par \uc2 Now, if you're an agent working on a January 6th case or some other case, and you were assigned to do it, you know, you shouldn't be punished just for doing your job.\par \par \uc2 This idea of keeping everybody in place in the government after Trump won the election, is insane. I think he should have the ability to put people in management positions that share his vision of how to move forward on the American first agenda. There's nothing unusual about that to me.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. I want to ask you quickly about the tariffs. Mexico, Canada already saying they will issue full retaliatory tariffs in exchange. China is going to the \uc2 World Trade Organization\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Your colleague, Senator Coons, put out this statement when they became official yesterday. He says, you know, President Trump, he said he was going to drive down prices to make America more affordable. Then on day of 13 of his presidency, quote, he imposed tariffs that will send prices skyrocketing. Is it a good move?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Well, I think that 3,000 Americans die every two weeks from fentanyl poisoning coming across the Mexican border. They make the drugs, fentanyl, produce them in Mexico. Their precursors come from China. We've been overrun in terms of 11 million illegals coming across the Mexican border.\par \par \uc2 If you want tariffs to go away, Mexico and China, stop poisoning Americans. Stop sending fentanyl into America to kill Americans. If you want to have a better relationship, Mexico with the United States, do your part on the other side of the border.\par \par \uc2 To our friends in Canada, I really appreciate what you're doing on the northern border.\par \par \uc2 I think these tariffs are designed to get these countries to change their behavior. I am glad that we're pushing back against China and Mexico when it comes to fentanyl poisoning of America. I am glad we're putting everybody on notice that the old way of doing business is over.\par \par \uc2 There's been a 60 percent drop in illegal crossings in 10 days. So these tariffs, I think, are designed to bring about change. And if the change comes, I think the tariffs probably go away.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Senator Graham, always great to have you. Thanks for the time today.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Well, the DNC electing a new chairman as historically bad. New poll numbers show how Americans are feeling about Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Will the new boss be able to unite a divided party? Our panel tackles that, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There has never been a Native American in leadership at the DNC.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't think it's a good idea to do a Muslim caucus if we're not going to do a Jewish caucus.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Will you pledge to appoint more than one transgender person to an at large seat?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Raise your hand if you would commit to what she just asked.\par \par \uc2 OK.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE (singing): When your government is doing you wrong, you fight on. Oh, you fight on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, those are some of the candidates battling it out to be the next \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  chair. Ken Martin was selected for the position on Saturday. He's going to be tasked with helping the party to reset after big election losses.\par \par \uc2 We are back now with the panel.\par \par \uc2 And, Juan, this comes after the Quinnipiac headline was this. It says, on Wednesday, a "stunning finding, negative views of the Democratic Party outpaced positive ones by 26 points, 31 percent favorable, 57 percent unfavorable." And that's the highest ever in Quinnipiac's polling dating back to 2008.\par \par \uc2 So, Ken Martin's got a big job ahead.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No question. I mean the polls are a wakeup call for Democrats. Right now they don't have a clear message in response to Trump, other than that they don't like Trump. And secondly, they don't have a clear leader. And so the - the selection of Martin is very interesting to me in that he is no radical. I mean there were protests out there yesterday, sunrise group, some environmental group. They are pushing for leftist positions, especially on climate.\par \par \uc2 Ken Martin and the Democrats right now, the Democratic leadership, the people who voted, clearly they're moving toward the middle. They are still in the Biden mode in terms of what they want to present as a face to the American people.\par \par \uc2 So, what Martin had to say yesterday was, we're not \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 . We're not radical and crazy on the right. We're not trying to impose tariffs or do anything. We want to make things work. We want to make cities work. We want to make your social safety net, your Medicaid work. We're not going to take it away. That's the Democrat's best message at this moment. It's all they got.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, well, Third Way has written a memo to, quote, "every Democrat pondering a 2028 presidential run," about staying away from the more radical ideas, saying this, "This election conclusively proved that a Democrat cannot embrace the far left groups to please the activists in a primary, and then simply pivot to stances that voters actually like in the final months of a general election campaign. If you want to best position yourself for 2028, publicly distance yourself from far-left, losing ideas."\par \par \uc2 And, Mary Katharine, they talk about abolishing the police, wiping out every single student loan, declaring a climate emergency. I mean they were very clear on what they were telling future Democrat candidates to stay away from.\par \par \uc2 HAM: Yes, the Democrats, I think, were culturally dominant for a very long time, backed by a lot of cultural forces for many, many decades and got very hubristic about their positions. And now they have lost the pitch that we can make things work because they demonstrably did not.\par \par \uc2 A perfect example of this, and why I think the Democrats are so disoriented in this position at the moment is that they lost a generation-long double digit lead on trust in education from 2020 to now. It will persist because, as you saw, the national report card, the National Assessment of Educational Progress came out this week with devastating results showing that students are not coming back from the learning loss that they - some of them incurred starting in kindergarten, who's being tested right now. That kind of thing is a marker in many areas of governance that Democrats have lost ground on. And gaining trust back will take a long effort, I think.\par \par \uc2 Well, and they, as we talked about in the last panel, are sort of overwhelmed by everything that President Trump and Republicans are throwing at them right now. But Congressman \uc2 Gerry Connolly\uc2 , a Democrat out of Virginia, said this, "they're going to stumble. They're going to screw up, and we're going to pounce when they do. In their haste to remake the federal government, they're going to make big, big mistakes."\par \par \uc2 And, Josh, they're pointing to some of that happening this week with at least the backtrack on that OMB memo.\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR, EDITOR-IN-CHIEF, "JEWISH INSIDER" AND FOX NEWS RADIO POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes, like, cuts can be popular, but when you're cutting essential services, Head Start, Medicaid payments potentially, that can also lose you support. It's often very important to not spend your capital to fast because you want to, you know, a lot of the stuff that Trump has been doing, as we've been talking about, is quite popular. But when you generate a backlash, you give Democrats something to run on.\par \par \uc2 The one thing about the Democrat - the poll, the Quinnipiac poll that was so notable, the image of the Democratic Party is the lowest in the Quinnipiac poll's history. So, I'm not sure if it's automatically going to be a backlash against Trump. Democrats actually have to have a positive message, and they have to run away from some of this identitarianism that you saw in that forum.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. And I think Republicans are at a high in the Quinnipiac poll that - for, based on where they've been too.\par \par \uc2 So, as for that OMB memo, quickly, "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " says that, "President Trump was angered by the media coverage." They were caught off guard at the White House. They were furious they weren't read into the process over there.\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: Yes, I think Mary Katharine hit the nail on the head on that in that it stands out, right? I mean the first time around there was a whole lot of trying to figure out how these executive orders would actually be implemented. This time around, it is sort of in a - a category of one. Everything else has been relatively smooth. They've had to juggle this one a little bit.\par \par \uc2 I will say, I love my friend Juan in trying to sort of wish cast the Democrats have gotten to the middle. If you watch that DNC situation for like two minutes, the middle was not to be seen.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. We've got to go.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes, but I think if you look at DEI attacks this week from Trump, you understand how the Democrats might have a chance to come back.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we've got to go. More in pay-per-view during the commercial.\par \par \uc2 OK, panel, thank you very much. We'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, two colleagues with very different political viewpoints offer a road map for finding common ground in our Sunday special.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Princeton Professors Cornel West and Robert George have a unique friendship, ideologically opposed on most major issues, but determined to bridge divisions through honest conversations. In their new book, "Truth Matters," they explore how we can work to understand each other better and find truth in a deeply divided time.\par \par \uc2 Here's part of my chat with them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Doctor West, you say that Doctor George is a truth seeker. He's willing to engage. And right now people can't even agree on what the truth is. So, how does this work?\par \par \uc2 CORNEL WEST, PRINCETON PROFESSOR: Well, I think it has to do with a quality of character that my dear brother, who I have a deep love and respect for, he says what he means, he means what he says, he speaks from his heart, but he has a sense of humility. And that's the benchmark of spiritual maturity.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And he said some really nice things about you, too, in - in talking about that you're very strong in your opinions, but not so much that you aren't willing to engage in this conversation about trying to find truth.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GEORGE, PRINCETON PROFESSOR: In our discussions and our dialogs, even where we deeply disagree about some issues, we have in common a desire to get at the truth of the matter.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What do you think people can take from this book, from that - those principles and that conversation about, you know, if it's a family member or a friend or somebody you've become completely cut off from because you do have such ideological differences, them learning the value of this kind of friendship and conversation.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE: The lesson should be, a person, an honest, decent person who disagrees with you, has an honest disagreement with you, is not your enemy. He's to be engaged with in a truth-seeking spirit, understanding that although I may have something to teach him, I may also have something to learn from him.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you agree to disagree? I mean that sounds simplistic, but what do you do?\par \par \uc2 WEST: But I've already learned so much from him in our dialog anyway. But I think also, as Christians, we deeply believe in imago dei. Each person made in the image and likeness of a loving and almighty God, and therefore that's not reducible to politics. Love, never reducible to just politics.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE: It's always so easy to interpret what your critic or challenger is saying in its worst light. Don't do that. Interpret it in its very best light.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Have we lost some of those skills? I mean, how do we redevelop those as a society?\par \par \uc2 WEST: Oh, no, we've lost a lot of those skills. Are we concerned with integrity, honesty and decency and compassion and openness, or are we manipulating, transacting and dominating?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE: You know, Shannon, there is a beautiful part of the liturgy in the Jewish tradition for Yom Kippur, the day of atonement. And the congregation goes through the various sins from the Ten Commandments. And then one is added that I find so fascinating. We have been zealots for bad causes. Now, nobody is ever a zealot for a bad cause deliberately. They become zealots, we become zealots for bad causes because we don't recognize the cause is bad. We think the cause is good. We think we're warriors for justice, for right, for truth, but we need to be self-critical enough to realize that we are frail, fallible, fallen human beings can make mistakes not only about minor, superficial, trivial things, but even about the big, great issues. So, we always have to keep that self-critical spirit, maintain that intellectual humility.\par \par \uc2 WEST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, congratulations to you two on the book and to your continued conversation that are an example for the rest of us, and certainly for the country as well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Our thanks to them.\par \par \uc2 By the way, it is Groundhog Day in Gobbler's Knob in western Pennsylvania. Big news this morning, Punxsutawney Phil did, in fact, see his shadow, predicting six more weeks of winter. Huge crowd there, including our own Janice Dean in attendance, having a good time, but not real happy with the results. At least not me.\par \par \uc2 All right, a heads up, FOX NEWS SUNDAY is going on the road. We're going to be in New Orleans one week from today, live from the Superdome ahead of Super Bowl 59. We've got some really cool behind the scenes scoop we cannot wait to show you.\par \par \uc2 That is it for today, though. Thanks for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a wonderful week. We'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020250202el2200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Trump Talks Border At House GOP Retreat\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Tomi Lahren, Joe Concha \par \uc2 8,368 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 January 2025\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2025 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: And welcome to "Hannity" this Monday night.\par \par \uc2 We start with the FOX News alert. We are awaiting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's -- President Trump's arrival at Joint Base Andrews. We'll take you there live as it happens.\par \par \uc2 It is now week two of the Trump administration, but it would kind of take me the entire monologue and maybe the whole show to list everything that the 47th president has accomplished over the past seven days.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we're going to show you how Trump is cracking down on woke insanity in our \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2 . Plus, full highlights from Vice President J.D. Vance's schooling, you know the smug, legacy, state-run media mob, in this case, leftwing \uc2 CBS\uc2  host Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 But, first, we turn to an issue that most Americans actually agree on, that is immigration, that is law and order at our border and border security. According to a new "Axios" poll at whopping two-thirds of you, the American people want illegal immigrants deported. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made this a central part of his campaign and now as president, he is keeping his promise to you, the American people.\par \par \uc2 Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: So many people came across the border. Nobody has any idea. I used to say like 325, but we really don't know but we're going to know soon. We're going to know and I tell you, our -- our people are doing a -- Tom Homan and his staff are doing an incredible job getting the bad ones out.\par \par \uc2 And today in Tijuana, you probably saw they -- they I got a call from Tom Homan and some of the people they said, sir, it's unbelievable. There is nobody -- you have this huge area, it's a gathering area. It's always been packed with these people what they did to our country is so unbelievable. That they could do this to our country is so horrible, but he said there's nobody there.\par \par \uc2 You saw yesterday, we've made it clear to every country that they will be taking back our people that we're sending out the criminals, that the illegal aliens coming from their countries, we're taking them back and they're going to take them back fast and if they don't they'll pay a very high economic price and we're going to immediately install massive tariffs, it'll be placed on them and other sanctions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Known terrorists, known cartel members, known gang members, known murderers, rapists, other violent criminals, all allowed into the country.\par \par \uc2 Now, thankfully, America's back, common sense is back as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has now demonstrated over the past week, we can secure our borders, we can hunt down these criminals, we can send them packing back to their home countries, even if those countries don't want them.\par \par \uc2 As you know, over the weekend, Colombia turned away two repatriation flights full of their own citizens after initially agreeing to take them. President Trump, he responded immediately, he halted visa services at the U.S. embassy in Colombia, threatening a massive 25 percent tariff on Colombian imports, that would double within a week to percent, and then magically within minutes Colombia's far-left president backed down, agreeing to every one of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's repatriation terms, even offering his own jet to assist with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's operation.\par \par \uc2 This is not just a huge diplomatic win for the president, but a message to the rest of the world, new sheriff in town. The U.S. has a tremendous amount of leverage. This president is not afraid to use it or its power, especially in this case for the right reason.\par \par \uc2 Of course, many on the left, they are not happy. Congresswoman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  and others so deeply concerned about the price of coffee and flowers going up over sanctions that are actually needed.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, singer and actress Selena Gomez filmed herself weeping today. She was in a -- well, let's call it a performative fit of hysteria because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is deporting violent criminal, illegal immigrants -- people that didn't obey our laws our borders and our sovereignty. Watch this and then I have a question on the other side.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SELENA GOMEZ, SINGER/ACTRESS: I just want to say that I'm so sorry, all my people are getting attacked. The children, they don't understand. I'm so sorry. I wish I could do something but I can't. I don't know what to do.\par \par \uc2 I'll try everything, I promise.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Now maybe Selena Gomez can answer some simple questions, where were the tears and the posting for the little girl, 12-year-old girl Jocelyn Nungaray, you know, raped, murdered by -- allegedly by two illegal immigrants from Venezuela? Where were the tears and where were the -- when -- why weren't you crying for Laken Riley, a 22-year-old nursing student murdered on a jogging trail by another unvetted Harris-Biden illegal immigrant from Venezuela? Where were the tears for Rachel Morin, the young mother of five raped, murdered on a hiking trail in Maryland, allegedly, again by an unvetted Harris-Biden illegal immigrant from El Salvador now charged with her death?\par \par \uc2 And where were you, Selena, you know, where were your tears for the thousands of Americans killed, raped, including children or assaulted by people that entered our country illegally, unvetted under Harris and Biden, where were those tears? Why didn't you make social media postings then?\par \par \uc2 We have been scrolling the names of victims for years on this program but apparently in her ivory tower, you know, behind her mansion walls and private security, Selena Gomez I guess never noticed, but just so we are clear, here are just a few of the many criminals \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is deporting. For example, this is a fugitive from El Salvador with connections to terrorist organizations, and this is a Haitian gang member as you can see there on your screen who has been charged and convicted 17 times in the past years, another recent ICE arrest was a Dominican man who beat his pregnant wife to death.\par \par \uc2 In Colorado, 50 Tren de Aragua gang members were rounded up at a makeshift night - nightclub. Other ICE raids busting child rapists, suspected \uc2 ISIS\uc2  terrorists.\par \par \uc2 Only someone truly unhinge would have a problem with these deportations and if you're going to be angry at anybody or shed tears, shed it for the family and shed it for our country because there will be more victims, I hope I'm wrong, but I'm probably right, as a result of unvetted Harris- Biden illegals.\par \par \uc2 But anyway, right on cue, if you turn over to MSDNC, well, here's Nicolle Wallace this time. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NICOLLE WALLACE, MSNBC HOST: Families that are in hiding on this harrowing anniversary of -- Holocaust anniversary does have a really eerie echo to something that I think people thought how could that happen and that could never happen again? We are now on live TV talking about administration in live interviews with their faces showing that says proudly that schools are not off limits.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Right. I mean, Tom Homan is engaged in cosplay. He wants to be Bull Connor for our current days, is that right?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I have bad news for the Trump haters on MSDNC, fake news CNN, all the legacy state-run media mob, you are now screaming into a void. Nobody's watching. Nobody cares what you have to say.\par \par \uc2 You have lied. You have peddled your conspiracy theories for so long, nobody believes you except maybe, you know, a few crazy left-wing lunatics that might trust you.\par \par \uc2 The American people voted for border security, a return to sanity and common sense, that's what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his borders czar Tom Homan are going to deliver. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOST: How can you possibly afford to deport all of those people?\