ow here. So, we thank you guys for being here, gentlemen, for being here. And we're going to keep on you. We hope you'll come back soon and give us some progress updates.\par \par \uc2 BEAN: Amen.\par \par \uc2 [15:25:00]\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, gentlemen, \uc2 Pete Sessions\uc2  and Aaron Bean, Congressman, for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 So, rich and powerful, former foes lining up behind President-elect Trump in the wake of a meteoric MAGA win. Katie Pavlich, Clay Travis and Antoine Seawright join us next with their reaction.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAN JONES, CNN SENIOR POLITICAL COMMENTATOR: He has a massive media ecosystem bigger than the mainstream built around him and for him and a religiously -- religious fervor in a political movement around him. And his best buddy is the richest person in the history of the world. And the most relevant Kennedy is with him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Okay. We've got some breaking news here this afternoon in New York City.\par \par \uc2 [15:30:00]\par \par \uc2 The Manhattan D.A., \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 , and the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  commissioner, Jessica Tisch, are about to speak after announcing the indictment of Luigi Mangione in the murder of \uc2 UnitedHealth\uc2  CEO \uc2 Brian Thompson\uc2 . He was 50 years old, two children. We're going to take you there as soon as that gets underway in New York City.\par \par \uc2 We have retired \uc2 NYPD\uc2  Inspector Paul Mauro standing by with some added information on what this is going to look like. So he is standing by.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, President-elect Trump ushering in what he is calling a golden age of America, pointing out a tidal shift from four years ago as some of America's most powerful, most wealthy entrepreneurs signal an embrace of a second Trump term. Watch some highlights from this lengthy press conference that we saw at Mar-a-Lago yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , US PRESIDENT-ELECT: Two hundred million investments.\par \par \uc2 MASAYOSHI SON, SOFTBANK CEO: He is a great negotiator.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We have a lot of great executives coming in. The top executives, the top bankers, they're all calling. And honestly, in the first, I don't know what it was. It's like a complete opposite. In the first one -- and the press has covered that fairly, actually, for change.\par \par \uc2 The golden age of America, I call it. It's begun. So it's the golden age of America and that's what it's going to be.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Let's bring in our panel, Katie Pavlich, Townhall.com editor and Fox News contributor OutKick founder Clay Travis and Antjuan Seawright, Blueprint Strategy founder and CEO.\par \par \uc2 Antjuan, why is it so different this time? Why is the press more sticking to the subjects of actual issues that face the country when they ask questions to the president elect?\par \par \uc2 ANTJUAN SEAWRIGHT, FOUNDER/CEO, BLUEPRINT STRATEGY: Oh, because the president has both bodies of Congress and he has the White House. He made a lot of promises to the American people that the American people are expecting him to deliver. On some of them, we know for sure he cannot, like gas prices, like cutting car insurance, like landlords cutting rent, et cetera. But he's made a lot of promises.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think the press is going to give the president-elect a chance to deliver on the promises he made to the American people.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let's take a look at this from Van Jones, talking about the fact that he -- and he says this, Trump is smarter than everyone because as you say, Antjuan, he just won the White House, the Senate and the House and a popular vote. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAN JONES, CNN CONTRIBUTOR: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is smarter than me, you and