IE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes. Well, that was part of the reason why JD Vance was picked as the vice president, because they wanted a continuation of the MAGA movement, the MAGA agenda. Someone who could take the torch of the second Trump term and turn it into policy positions that could be implemented during the next administration after Trump is done in the White House for the second time.\par \par \uc2 But if you look at what happened at Mar-a-Lago this week, Martha, it's really interesting to see the difference in attitude towards business and towards prosperity in the private sector. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  came in and overregulated. As Democrats do business, Trump wants to do the opposite by unleashing Americans prosperity ingenuity by deregulating the federal government. You're seeing this through DOGE.\par \par \uc2 We're also going to see it through a number of federal agencies like \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  and others. And that is why you're seeing a number of CEOs going to Mar-a-Lago to work with the president, not to be punished by the federal government when you're trying to create jobs.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Quick thought, Clay, while we have your audio, and then I've got to skip over to \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 . Go ahead, Clay.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS: Look, I mean, I think what you asked earlier, Martha, was Trump recognized he needed to go after a new audience, and he went after that new audience. He swung men from minus 14 to plus 14, according to the Wall Street Journal. That's because he went after a new, untapped audience, unlike Kamala, who tried to get her base out.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Thank you all for being here. We've got some breaking news in New York. Great to have you.\par \par \uc2 So Manhattan DA \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  and \uc2 NYPD\uc2  Commissioner Jessica Tisch are about to -- they're speaking right now after announcing the indictment of Luigi Mangione for the murder of UnitedHealthcare CEO \uc2 Brian Thompson\uc2 . Let's hear what they have to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 ALVIN BRAGG\uc2  (D), MANHATTAN DISTRICT ATTORNEY: -- the life was lost. We are here to announce that Luigi Mangione, the defendant, is charged with one count of murder in the first degree and two counts of murder in the second degree, including one count of murder in the second degree as an act of terrorism, for the brazen, targeted and premeditated shooting of \uc2 Brian Thompson\uc2 , who, as you know, was the CEO of UnitedHealthcare.\par \par \uc2 This case was made possible by the collaborative efforts of the phenomenal public servants you see before you today, and so many others who are not here. I'm honored to lead the Manhattan District Attorney's Office and privilege to serve alongside Assistant District Attorneys Joel Seidemann, Kristen Bailey, and Zachary Kaplan, who were working from the outset, hand in hand with the greatest police department in the world.\par \par \uc2 The Chief of our Trial Division, Lisa DelPizzo, and the deputy chief, Chris Prevost, provided sage oversight and direction. And I want to acknowledge our High Technology Analysis Unit. There was a lot to be processed here. Steve Moran (ph) in particular, who heads that, and Marco Popek (ph).\par \par \uc2 We are privileged here at the DA's Office to work every day with the phenomenal public servants at the \uc2 NYPD\uc2 . It has been a distinct pleasure to work besides Commissioner Tisch, an exemplary partner, and Chief of Detectives Joseph Kenny. Almost every day we're in touch. And his leadership is second to none.\par \par \uc2 And then Deputy Commissioner Rebecca Weiner, who, as I tell her, there are a lot of challenging things happening in the world, and I sleep better at night knowing that she is the deputy commissioner in charge of terrorism. The detectives on this out doing the courageous frontline work. Detective Oscar Diaz (ph), Detective Curcio (ph). Exemplary, exemplary the finest.\par \par \uc2 It wasn't just those of us here in New York. I want to acknowledge the great work of our partners in Altoona, the Altoona Police Department, the Blair County District Attorney's Office, the governors of New York and Pennsylvania, the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and, of course, the brave members of the public who answered the call when our great commissioner put out the call for their assistance.\par \par \uc2 This was a frightening, well-planned, targeted murder that was intended to cause shock and attention, and intimidation. It incurred in one of the most bustling parts of our city, threatening the safety of local residents and tourists alike, commuters and business people just starting out on their day. Let me say a bit more about the charges.\par \par \uc2 They include one count of murder in the first degree as a killing and furtherest of terrorism, two counts of murder in the second degree, one charged of the killing was done as an act of terrorism, and the second pertaining to the fact that the killing was intentional. The maximum penalty possible for murder in the first degree and murder in the second degree as an act of terrorism is life without parole. The maximum penalty for murder in the second degree is 25 years to life.\par \par \uc2 There are additional counts as well, a number of criminal possession of weapon charges, two counts in the second degree, four counts in the third degree, one count in the fourth degree, and an account of criminal possession of a forged instrument in the second degree. We tell you a bit about the facts.\par \par \uc2 On November 24th, we allege that the defendant arrived in New York City to murder \uc2 Brian Thompson\uc2 . After the defendant arrived in New York City on a bus at \uc2 Port Authority\uc2 , he checked into the hostel on the Upper West Side using the name Mark Rosario (ph) with a fake New Jersey ID.\par \par \uc2 On the morning of December 4th, the defendant left the hospital -- hostel, excuse me, just after 5:30am and traveled to Midtown. For nearly an hour, we alleged, the defendant waited in the area near the \uc2 Hilton Hotel\uc2 , waiting for Mr. Thompson to appear. From 6:38am to 6:44am, the defendant stood across from the entrance of the hotel on West 54th Street.\par \par \uc2 When he saw Mr. Thompson, he crossed the street and approached him from behind. We allege he then took out a .9 millimeter 3D printed ghost gun, equipped with a 3D printed suppressor and shot him once in the back and once in the leg. The defendant then fled northeast on 54th Street and took an ebike uptown before getting into a taxi that took him to West 178th Street and Amsterdam Avenue.\par \par \uc2 On December 9, the defendant was spotted at \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  in Altoona, Pennsylvania. The local police were notified. He was arrested and he was found with a .9 millimeter handgun, a suppressor, two ammunition magazines, and the same fake New Jersey ID he used at the hostel.\par \par \uc2 I want to say a word about ghost guns. These weapons are increasingly proliferating throughout New York City and the entire country. Involving technology will only make this problem worse. Last year, over 80 ghost guns and ghost gun parts were recovered in Manhattan alone.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NYPD\uc2  and the Manhattan DA's office have been leading in the introduction of these ghost guns. And one thing I want to make clear, they're unserialized, they can be printed out. But as this case tragically makes clear, they are just as deadly as traditional firearms. This will remain a priority for us here in Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 Finally, I know there have been questions about the extradition process. The latest that I can offer you is that the defendant is currently scheduled to appear in court in Pennsylvania on Thursday morning, first for a preliminary hearing on his Pennsylvania charges. Immediately to follow that will be an extradition hearing.\par \par \uc2 We have indications that the defendant may waive that hearing, in which case he brought to New York forthwith. In any event, we have been preparing on parallel paths, and before the hearing, will send to the governor of the State of New York the paperwork that would be necessary if he does not waive during that hearing.\par \par \uc2 I now want to turn over to our great commissioner. And when I do so, I wanted to thank her for her partnership, not only on this matter, but on all we do every day together. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TISCH, \uc2 NYPD\uc2  COMMISSIONER: Good afternoon, everyone. The unsealing of today's indictment brings us one step closer to securing justice for \uc2 Brian Thompson\uc2  and his family, and affirming the primacy of the rule of law in the city of New York. \uc2 NYPD\uc2  detectives worked tirelessly to piece this case together. And along the way, the Manhattan DA's office made sure we had every legal tool available to us.\par \par \uc2 Whether quickly providing subpoenas to assist with our evidence collection, or traveling with us to Altoona, Pennsylvania last week after the apprehension, our partnership made this a successful investigation. So I'd like to thank District Attorney Bragg and his team for their work in bringing this case forward.\par \par \uc2 Part of that work included the relentless collection and analysis of surveillance video by our detectives, which ultimately produced a clear image of our suspect. That image was shared far and wide through the media, and thanks to an observant McDonald's customer and the employee who alerted the police, Mr. Mangione was quickly brought into custody.\par \par \uc2 This outcome is why we always emphasize the critical role of the public in our public safety mission. So again, I want to thank everyone who saw something, said something, and did something. That is why we have a suspect in custody today. As DA Bragg said, Luigi Mangione is now formally charged and indicted for murder.\par \par \uc2 And let me be perfectly clear. In the nearly two weeks since Mr. Thompson's killing, we have seen a shocking and appalling celebration of cold-blooded murder. Social media has erupted with praise for this cowardly attack. People ghoulishly plastered posters threatening the CEOs, other CEOs with an X over Mr. Thompson's picture as though he was some sort of a sick trophy.\par \par \uc2 And yesterday -- sorry. Yesterday, the New York Post reported that some extreme activists were circulating a deck of cards with other most wanted CEOs to be targeted for assassination. These are the threats of a lawless, violent mob who would trade in their own vigilantism for the rule of law that protects us all.\par \par \uc2 Let me say this plainly. There is no heroism in what Mangione did. This was a senseless act of violence. It was a cold and calculated crime that stole a life and put New Yorkers at risk.\par \par \uc2 We don't celebrate murders and we don't lionize the killing of anyone. And any attempt to rationalize this is vile, reckless and offensive to our deeply held principles of justice.\par \par \uc2 Now, as we await the extradition process in Pennsylvania to complete, we continue our public safety work here at home. That includes our focus on the growing threat of ghost guns, one of which was used in this attack. The \uc2 NYPD\uc2  remains committed to tracking down and removing these untraceable weapons from our streets.\par \par \uc2 And we will do that work hand in hand with District Attorney Bragg here in Manhattan and in every other borough to ensure that those who possess, distribute and use these weapons are held fully accountable.\par \par \uc2 Finally, I want to again thank every member of the \uc2 NYPD\uc2 , uniform and civilian working on this case, led of course by our great Chief Kenny. Whether in Midtown North Squad, Homicide, Crime Scene Unit, Intelligence Bureau and everywhere else, all of you contributed today's indictment. And you brought the Thompson family one step closer to justice in this case. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 BRAGG: Sure. So, you know, in its most basic terms, this was a killing that is intended to evoke terror. And we've seen that reaction. You know, the statutory elements we can go into, but its most basic term, that's what this is.\par \par \uc2 This was not an ordinary killing, not to suggest that any killing is ordinary, but this was extraordinary. And the New York State Legislature has, you know, set out, you know, both the paths that we talked about, both the murder one and murder two. And this, we allege, is certainly with squarely within those statutes which talk about intending to do exactly what we saw happen here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Anthony (ph)?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY (?): Thank you, Chief Kenny. For the defense, I'm wondering sort of the timeline between December 6, when the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was notified by the San Francisco police about this missing person that was called in from Luigi's mother, to the time that they contacted you about this.\par \par \uc2 (END LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, we are back. So obviously breaking news as we have been watching DA \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  describe the charges surrounding the that are against Luigi Mangione, the suspected killer of \uc2 Brian Thompson\uc2 , the 50- year-old CEO of UnitedHealthcare.\par \par \uc2 I'm joined by \uc2 NYPD\uc2  Inspector and Fox News contributor Paul Mauro, who is just an invaluable source when it comes to understanding these charges. So, Paul, were talking before this, and you were giving me a little bit of insight into this. There's murder one and then two counts of murder two. Explain the sort of unique nature of the murder one charge here.\par \par \uc2 PAUL MAURO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: So in New York State, you know, people saw when they originally put out what the charges they were going for. They had murder two, and everybody interpreted that as a down charge, which is not the case. Murder one is very specific in New York. Killing of a cop, a fireman in the performance of duty, an EMT, killing of a witness, killing of a murder for hire, for instance, OK?\par \par \uc2 There's a specific set of subsets that qualify for murder one. One of those subsets is further incident of terrorism. And they wouldn't have really had that charge buttoned up, in my estimation, until they got a hold of his manifesto.\par \par \uc2 He's probably made some statements in regard. We noted he yelled at the press at that point. But the point is this, between all of this stuff, they put together the idea enough to get it passed a grand jury for an indictment that he did what he did for an ideological purpose.\par \par \uc2 He killed in order to sway the opinion of the public, in order to bring them around to a particular ideological idea. In New York State, that's the definition of terrorism. That gets you to murder one charge.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, interestingly, \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  also said this. I think that supports what you're talking about in terms of murder one. He said that it was premeditated to bring shock and attention, and intimidation in an area where you are threatening tourists, commuters, all in the middle of the day. So it did invoke a feeling of fear and terror on the part of a large chunk of the city.\par \par \uc2 And then he was off, you know, on a bicycle through Central Park -- through Central park.\par \par \uc2 MAURO: Yes. In fact, if I recall, intimidation is right in the definition of terrorism in the statute. Murder one has a higher hit. As he said, we don't have the death penalty here, but it shows the seriousness with which they're taking this case.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So in terms of -- and, you know, we saw and well-deserved the kudos around the department. James Kenny, the chief of detectives, getting a lot of recognition here, as well as the McDonald's employee, and those in the McDonald's who were able to identify him based on all the images that have been put out there.\par \par \uc2 Now, Thursday, there will be a preliminary hearing in Pennsylvania, and then it will go on the Pennsylvania charges, which also exist, and then the extradition process. Explain a little bit of that for us.\par \par \uc2 MAURO: So he's going to face an extradition hearing after he gets -- after they do the piece relative to Pennsylvania, which realistically nobody cares about at this point. He's got no real basis to challenge the extradition, OK?\par \par \uc2 He doesn't face the death penalty. The charges are legit. Realistically interstate, there's very few ways that you can challenge it. And really generally comes down to -- to really boil it down, you got the wrong guy. You don't have enough facts. We don't have that here. There's so much in this.\par \par \uc2 He made statements. I did it alone. You got the manifesto, you got the gun. They're going to get the DNA. My understanding is the DNA is not back yet, but it's relatively imminent. He's got no real basis, which is likely why, as Bragg said, he's probably going to wave because you just -- you're delaying the inevitable.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. We also have the video which we just showed of him going into the courthouse in Pennsylvania, where he was also screaming out ideological language about what happened or related, broadly related to what happened. Is that further evidence to his. His motive and. And to the fact that he was Is this suspect in a pretty clear way?\par \par \uc2 MAURO: Doesn't help him. I mean, you know, when you put all of these things together, it's very clear that he did what he did for the purposes of intimidating or influencing civilian population relative to an ideological cause. When you hear terrorism, usually you think of the classic issues of terrorism that we had when I was working, which is we had al-Qaeda, we had \uc2 ISIS\uc2 , et cetera.\par \par \uc2 But that doesn't mean that if you do something in this way to intimidate people for an ideological purpose and you commit a crime like this, you're off the hook relative to terrorism. This is proof of concept. Now remember something, they got to pass the grand jury. So that means a big pool of New Yorkers already agreed, and that's why you have the new charges.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. So just give us an insight into that part of this -- part of what played out here. So they quickly took these charges to the grand jury --\par \par \uc2 MAURO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And that process was very fast in terms of what they put in front of them. And what they agreed was enough to indict.\par \par \uc2 MAURO: Sure. They would have hustled this right along. We had the initial charges. They put a warrant. I believe that they had it. Yes, because they had the charges so they had a warrant from a judge. They had -- that's when we saw the murder two charge.\par \par \uc2 And people were asking me, why isn't he going for a murder one? It looks like he's down charging. He's not. As we said, it's a specific set of subsets which they believed once they got all these new facts, the manifesto, et cetera. You know what, we have enough here to go for the terrorism charge.\par \par \uc2 Now you bucket that up, you present it to a grand jury. They looked at it. They, they did it quite quickly.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Not hard to get this pass a grand jury, right?\par \par \uc2 MAURO: They had to bring the Altoona cops here and all those witnesses. That woman from \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's probably got an all expense paid trip to New York because she's a very important piece of this.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: How important to get him here quickly?\par \par \uc2 MAURO: I think it's very important. Just for, you know, he's coming back no matter what. But just for the optic, you know, this was such a visible thing. It's in the Christmas season. It's midtown New York.\par \par \uc2 This is something of an infamous crime. Unfortunately, New York seems to specialize in them. You know, when I first saw this, I thought of the Sparks Steakhouse hit back in the day with Paul Castellano, something that you really don't want to see in the Christmas season with a city that's trying to come back. Get him back to show that we could solve this quickly, very important. Hats off to the new team.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Paul, thank you very much. Paul Mauro joining us on set this afternoon is that breaking news from \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  in New York City. The charges brought in the case where of \uc2 Brian Thompson\uc2 's murder.\par \par \uc2 So in the meantime, these are images that are quite familiar to people from this area and really everyone across the country now, because we've seen some of this activity in a number of states. Breaking right now, we have members of the House Intelligence Committee that have just received a classified briefing on these reports of these mysterious drones buzzing above America. The Biden administration insists that there is just no risk to the public from this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, WHITE HOUSE NATIONAL SECURITY COMMUNICATIONS ADVISOR: As you saw in the joint statement last night from four different federal agencies, our assessment to date, based on the information that we've seen, the people we've talked to, the tips we've corroborated. This is lawful legal aviation activity, manned and unmanned drones, civil aviation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Here is President-elect Trump on this issue. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : The government knows what is happening. Look, our military knows where they took off from. If it's a garage, they can go right into that garage. They know where it came from and where it went. And for some reason, they don't want to comment. And I think they'd better off saying what it is.\par \par \uc2 Our military knows and our President knows. And for some reason, they want to keep people in suspense.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Texas Congressman Dan Crenshaw joins me now. He was in that briefing moments ago as a member of the House Intel Committee. Congressman Crenshaw, great to have you with us, as always.\par \par \uc2 Give us a sense of what you can. This was a classified briefing. Give us a little parameter of what you just experienced in there.\par \par \uc2 REP. DAN CRENSHAW (R-TX): The truth, even in the classified settings, is always a lot less exciting than I think people imagine it to be. You're always going to be more surprised about what we don't know than what we do know. There's -- I do think -- I think it's inaccurate for a Biden administration to say that they're absolutely sure that there's no problems here.\par \par \uc2 They're not absolutely sure. There's about a. There's about a hundred cases of these sightings that are still under active investigation. Now, keep in mind, there was like 6,000 before. A lot of them have been assessed to just be planes, manned aircraft, things like that, satellites. And they're investigating every single one of them.\par \par \uc2 I think one of the biggest problems that we're coming across as members of Congress is that, there's not really a great legal framework to deal with this. Now, since 2017, 2018, we've been -- we've given the federal government authorities to detect and mitigate drone activity across the United States, but that time tends to be pretty limited.\par \par \uc2 So DOJ has authority, DOD has authority, DOE Department of Energy has authority, DHS has some authorities. But all of these authorities are pretty specific to, say, government facilities, and even some government facilities don't even fall under it. And so, you know, you might find some government facilities that if they shoot down a drone that's overhead, they're actually criminally liable.\par \par \uc2 So this is a real problem that we have to solve in Congress. There's actually a couple bills that have been floating around for a while that try to get at this problem, and I think they're going to get a lot more attention now. The biggest problem I see is, again, lack of that legal framework. I mean, you're not talking -- there's really like, no criminal penalties for these people who are maybe flying unlicensed drones, flying them in an airspace they're not supposed to be in. There's not a legal framework to deal with that.\par \par \uc2 And then, there's -- to me, from my understanding, there's just no lead agency. There's like -- there's no 911 button you call and say, OK, these are the people who come and deal with the drone. However it is you deal with the drone, whether you shoot it down, capture it, use a jamming signal, however it is, it's not clear to anybody who's actually in charge of that.\par \par \uc2 And state and local authorities don't really have the capabilities or the authorities to deal with it either. And that's why you're seeing this understandable freak out, I think, in the American public, because everyone's like, who do we call for this?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. You know what really strikes me, Congressman? I keep going back to this piece in the Wall Street Journal in October. This is the headline, Wall Street Journal -- in the Wall Street Journal, excuse me, Mystery drones swarmed a US military base for 17 days. The Pentagon is stumped.\par \par \uc2 And the piece goes on to describe members of the military personnel going up to the roof. They say, they, you know, watching this whole thing happened. They said, you know, 45 minutes after sunset, they start to come across. They said they were 20ft long, flew 100 miles an hour, three to four, at 3,000 feet to 4,000 feet, one by one, like a parade of lawnmowers over the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 That story didn't get much attention. But given what's going on now, does it surprise you that the Pentagon was alarmed and stumped about this a month ago, two months ago?\par \par \uc2 CRENSHAW: Yes. I'd have to look into that specific story and what they're - - what the investigation ended up being. I mean, because a lot of times, look it, what you just described to me sounds a lot like Reagan Airport. And that's often the case. There was another one where people thought there was a bunch of drones following a Coast Guard cutter, and it turns out that was just people going into LaGuardia, airplanes going to LaGuardia.\par \par \uc2 So it depends. But the final conclusion is still the same, which is we don't really have a good way to deal with this. And it's not obvious who's in charge of saying, hey, OK, we want to shoot this drone down, how exactly do we do it? Who do we even call? And that's something as a government we need to figure out we decide who the lead agency is --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I just have seconds. Did they give you assurance this is not a foreign entity?\par \par \uc2 CRENSHAW: That is -- that remains the case, yes. That they believe -- that they have no indications or evidence as a foreign entity. That doesn't mean it's not. It just means they don't have any evidence that there is.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Congressman Dan Crenshaw of Texas, always good to see you, sir. Thank you very much. Merry Christmas to you.\par \par \uc2 That is "The Story" of Tuesday, December 17th. "The Story" goes on. Tomorrow, my sit down with New York City Democratic Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2 , stay tuned for that.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020241218ekch00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Gunman Dead After Killing Two And Injuring Others; President-Elect Talks Economy In Wide-Ranging Update; \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  Says He Favors Polio Vaccine; Two States Receiving Federal Drone Detection Technology; Sen. Tom Cotton (R-AR), Is Being Interviewed Sending A Letter, Demanding Records On Border Wall Materials To Be Preserved For Accountability And Transparency; Drones Flying Above Several States; Calls For Action Escalate; Sen. Tom Cotton (R-AR) and Sen. Tim Kaine (D-VA) Interviewed on Bipartisan Bill to Monitor Prescription Drug Production in U.S.; Panel of Democrats Recommend Representative \uc2 Gerry Connolly\uc2  over Representative \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  for Oversight Committee; Biden-Harris Administration Trying to "Trump-Proof" Federal Environmental Policy; President-Elect Trump Holds Press Conference; Federal Government Still Not Providing Answers regarding Numerous Drones Spotted over Northeastern U.S.\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Garrett Tenney, Nate Foy, Griff Jenkins, Aishah  Hasnie, Chad Pergram, Rich Edson, Matt Bennett, Marc Thiessen, Stef Kight \par \uc2 8,041 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 December 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right, well, that's it for us everyone. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: For the rest of her life, really?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes, for the rest her life.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, thank you, Dana. Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 President-elect Trump outlines his second administration plans. Make some big promises. The incoming commander in chief also talking about the drone mystery, we will speak live with the White House national security communications adviser on this very issue.\par \par \uc2 And then, Democratic infighting centered on ideology and age.\par \par \uc2 But first, breaking tonight, just nine days before Christmas, and the ultimate tragedy for families in one Wisconsin community. One teenager, one teacher are dead tonight following an attack today at a religious school in Madison. Shooter is dead too. He was also a student. Two people are hospitalized with life threatening injuries, a motive not known.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Garrett Tenney is in Madison with the story tonight. Good evening, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, the alleged shooter in this case, is a 17-year-old girl who is a student at the Abundant Life Christian School, that's according to the Associated Press, citing a single law enforcement official. Police say, shortly before 11:00 a.m. this morning, the shooter, armed with a handgun opened fire, killing a teacher, a student, and injuring six other students, including two who remain in life threatening condition.\par \par \uc2 When officers arrived at this small, private Christian school, the shooter was already dead in an apparent suicide. Here is one of the many terrified parents who rushed to campus.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You don't think you're going to go through this. I bring my daughter to school knowing well that this happens in the world, that people are struggling, and I pray for my daughter's safety. I pray for the entire school safety. I pray for the hearts of the students, the teachers, the staff members. It's a very real thing.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (voice over): While police aren't releasing any other details on the shooter or victims, we are told that so far, the shooter's family is cooperating with the investigation and the detectives carried out a search warrant at the home this afternoon, as police try to piece together a timeline leading up to this tragedy and pinpoint a motive.\par \par \uc2 SHON BARNES, MADISON, WISCONSIN POLICE CHIEF: I think we can all agree that enough is enough, and we have to come together to do everything we can to support our students to prevent press conferences like these from happening again and again and again.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (voice over): Officials at the abundant life Christian School, which is K-12 are asking the country for prayers. Posting on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , prayers requested. Today, we had an active shooter incident at ALCS. We are in the midst of following up. We will share information as we are able. Please pray for our challenger family.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (on camera): And Bret, we are expecting to get another update from police in the next hour, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Garrett, thank you.\par \par \uc2 President-elect Trump takes office in 36 days, and he's setting his sights high among his promises and predictions today during a lengthy news conference in Florida, a richer, safer, stronger America than ever.\par \par \uc2 Up to $2 trillion in savings from his emphasis on trimming the federal government and the federal budget, the defeat of inflation and the creation of millions of jobs. He says, the golden age of America has begun.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Griff Jenkins is in West Palm Beach, Florida tonight with the latest. Good evening, Griff.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. It was the president-elect's first press conference since the election, wide ranging, tackling a number of issues for over an hour. He began with the announcement of $100 billion investment from Japan's \uc2 SoftBank\uc2  CEO, and he noted the number of business leaders now coming to visit him this time in Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Well, the biggest difference is that people want to get along with me this time.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (voice over): \uc2 SoftBank\uc2 's CEO is just the latest in a string of billionaires making the track.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We have a lot of great executives coming in, the top executives, the top bankers they're all calling.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (voice over): That has included \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Meta\uc2  CEOs with \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  on the dance card this week. Also joining Trump today, his Commerce Secretary nominee.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS\uc2 : Do you see another trade deal with China coming?\par \par \uc2 HOWARD LUTNICK, COMMERCE SECRETARY NOMINEE: I think reciprocity is something that is going to be a key topic for us. How you treat us is how you should expect to be treated.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Tariffs, properly used, which we will do, and being reciprocal with other nations, but it'll make our country rich.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (voice over): President-elect Trump also addressed the mysterious drones.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : The government knows what is happening.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (voice over): His Cabinet picks, the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Got to be a deal. Too many people being killed.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (voice over): Hostages in Gaza and border security.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We need borders. We need walls, but we need borders.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (voice over): Including the sale of border wall material and how to deport criminal migrants.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : They'll take them back.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (voice over): Back to countries like Venezuela that doesn't accept them.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : They're going to take them back. They're all taking them back. Yes. And if they don't, they'll be met very harshly economically.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (voice over): But there were no harsh words about TikTok, the social media giant today asked the Supreme Court to temporarily block a law that would force it to divest or face a nationwide ban.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : You know, I have a warm spot in my heart for TikTok, because I won youth by 34 points.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (on camera): And Bret, Fox has learned tonight, from a source familiar that Trump met today with TikTok CEO at Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 And finally, one more note about the wide ranging issues, Trump also expressed an openness to a possible pardon for New York City's Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2 , who is facing federal charges of bribery, wire fraud and conspiracy, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Griff Jenkins in West Palm. Griff, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2 , trying to defend -- trying to drum up support among senators who will decide whether to confirm him as Secretary of \uc2 Health and Human Services\uc2 . President-elect Trump is predicting Kennedy will be more moderate than many people expect.\par \par \uc2 Senior national correspondent Aishah Hasnie is on Capitol Hill with the latest tonight. Good evening, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you. Kennedy made news right out of the gate by saying that he is all for the polio vaccine, but his past and more recent comments could turn into land mines for him through this confirmation process.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Mr. Kennedy, do you want to revoke any vaccines?\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): RFK Jr. walking into the first of nearly two dozen meetings with Republican senators this week as he advised to become the next Health and Human Services Secretary.\par \par \uc2 But there are major concerns tonight about his views on vaccines. A recent report revealed in 2022 Kennedy's lawyer tried to revoke \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval for the polio vaccine. Kennedy told reporters today he's all for the polio vaccine.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR\uc2 ., HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES SECRETARY: I support it.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): President-elect Trump also adding from Mar-a-Lago that he does not want Kennedy to revoke any vaccines.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: No, I want him to come back with a report as to what he thinks, we're going to find out a lot. I think he's going to be much less radical than you would think. I think he's got a very open mind, or I wouldn't have put him there.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Kennedy's abortion stance is also likely to bring up questions amongst deeply prolife conservatives.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY JR.: I believe that we can reduce abortions in America by choice and not by force.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): The president of the \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  Pro-Life America group writing, we have concerns about \uc2 Robert F Kennedy Jr.\uc2 , but opting to give him a chance.\par \par \uc2 And while his criticism of the food industry is gaining traction, Kennedy's 2002 comments comparing hog farmers to \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2  and his plans to disrupt American farming stand to put him at odds with some senators like \uc2 Chuck Grassley\uc2  of Iowa, who told Semafor, he's made some statements about pigs and about GMO corn and soybeans. I can't believe that he's going to have a problem with that, but if he does, he has a problem with me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And Bret, Kennedy doesn't currently have any meetings scheduled with Senate Democrats at all this week in his blitz through Capitol Hill. But there are Democrats, at least Independents like \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , that do want to sit down and get a face to face with him quickly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah Hasnie live on the hill. Aishah, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed today. The Dow was down 111 to extend its losing streak to eight sessions. The S&P 500 gained 23, the NASDAQ surged 247 to another record close.\par \par \uc2 Well, you can add president-elect Trump to the list of people who think the government knows more than it's saying about the drone mystery.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the briefings and the demands for information are continuing. Correspondent Nate Foy has the latest tonight from Elizabeth, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Our military knows and our president knows, and for some reason, they want to keep people in suspense.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  declined to answer if he's received an intelligence briefing on mysterious drones over several states, but promised U.S. capabilities are strong.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Our military knows where they took off from, if it's a garage, they can go right into that garage. I can't imagine it's the enemy, because it was the enemy that blasted out. Even if they were late, they'd blast it. Something strange is going on.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): The White House still won't provide answers. New Jersey Governor \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2  received a briefing from \uc2 FBI\uc2  officials on Sunday as New Jersey and New York received federal drone detection technology, thermal imaging used in Far Hills, New Jersey shows what looks to be a drone is really an airplane, while federal officials confirm others are in fact, drones.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JOSH GOTTHEIMER\uc2  (D-NJ): I believe the people who I represent, the people in New Jersey, deserve clear answers.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): U.S. drone capabilities remain a concern. The Wright- Patterson Air Force Base outside Dayton, Ohio shut down its airspace for hours this weekend after drone sightings the Pentagon believes are unrelated to the other sightings.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in Germany, a spokesperson for the U.S. Air Forces in Europe confirms with Fox News, "In early December, there were sightings of small unmanned aerial systems, also known as drones, in the vicinity of and over Ramstein Air Base with no impacts to base residents, facilities or assets."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FOY (on camera): Tomorrow, at 2:00, the White House will brief the House Intelligence Committee on the drones. As for the Federal technology now deployed in New Jersey, Governor \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2  said that it has found, so far, little to no evidence of anything threatening, but added that technology is powerful enough to disable the drones if needed, although it's generally illegal on U.S. soil. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Nate Ford, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we will talk with White House National Security communications adviser, Admiral John Kirby, about this very issue, the drones.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 5 in New York, as the city Campaign Finance Board denies to grant public matching funds for Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2 ' 2025 re- election campaign. The panel cites bookkeeping irregularities in the federal criminal case involving straw donors.\par \par \uc2 Fox 2 in San Francisco has a tornado near a mall -- near a mall in central California, sweeps up cars, uproots trees, sends several people to the hospital. It happened Saturday afternoon in Scotts Valley. San Francisco authorities issued the city's first ever tornado warning there.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Boston from WFXT, our affiliate there, one of the big stories there tonight, the Boston Tea Party Ships And Museum offer free tours to mark the anniversary of the historic rebellion 251 years ago, in 1773. The Sons of Liberty, dressed as Native Americans, boarded three ships from the British East India Company. They smashed 342 chests of tea, dumping more than 92,000 pounds into the harbor to protest unfair taxes.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MASTRONARDY, SHERIFF, OCEAN COUNTY, NEW JERSEY: This is not -- definitely not a consumer. I don't -- we pay for our drones, and we can't afford what's up in the sky, in New Jersey right now.\par \par \uc2 These are large device that are in the sky. And, of course, one of the problems we have is to differentiate what's real and what's not real.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's the Ocean County sheriff in New Jersey, talking about the situation in the sky.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now to talk about these mysterious drones flying over parts of the U.S., White House National Security communications adviser, retired admiral, John Kirby.\par \par \uc2 John, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 REAR ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET.), WHITE HOUSE NATIONAL SECURITY COMMUNICATIONS ADVISOR: You bet.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You hear all of these officials. You've heard a lot of concern, a lot of coverage. Where are you tonight on this?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: As we look at the information we have got, Bret. And we've now examined some 5,000 different sightings. We've added detection capabilities, additional observers.\par \par \uc2 As you and I stand here today, to date, our assessment is that this is lawful, legal, commercial, hobbyist, and even law enforcement aircraft activity.\par \par \uc2 Some of it's manned, some of its, unmanned. We absolutely acknowledge that a lot of these are probably drones, but they are -- they are flying legally. And it is legal to fly drones in non-restricted airspace, as long as you register with the \uc2 FAA\uc2 . And there is thousands and thousands of these kinds of flights every single day. I think there is more than a million drones that are registered with the \uc2 FAA\uc2  just in the United States alone.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But this is different. It seems, these are bigger. They are six feet in diameter -- a lot of them. There was a reporter the other day that saw 50 coming from the coast at one like pack. I know when you first came out, you said that most of these are manned. Can you concede that maybe most of these are not manned?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Well, I said was at the time, and this was Thursday, when we were not as informed as we are today, based on the resources we have applied to the problem set, I said many of the reported sightings we believe to be manned aircraft. That was true when, when we said it on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 We still believe, Bret, that some of these are, in fact, manned aircraft. I couldn't give you a breakdown of what percentage. But we do believe that still, some of these sightings are, in fact, manned aircraft.\par \par \uc2 We also acknowledge that a lot of them are unmanned aircraft. But again, our assessment to date, given all the work that we've done, the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , state, and local officials that these are legal and lawful drones flying for the common good. Flying --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. The homeland security secretary said over the weekend that right now, they don't have any foreign involvement criminal activity. We don't see them as a national security concern. How can you get to that point? How can you get to that conclusion with what you're seeing?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Because we've done the -- we've done the analysis. We've done the detection and analysis. We've corroborated the sightings, and in every case that we have examined to date, we have seen nothing. Nothing that indicates a public safety risk.\par \par \uc2 There are drones flying over the skies of the United States every single night, every single day. And we've seen nothing in this -- in and around New Jersey that should give the people on the ground there, concerns for their safety for this activity. And we've seen nothing, working with the \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2  as well, that indicates a foreign adversary, actor involved, or any kind of pernicious national security threat.\par \par \uc2 Now, there have been some drone sightings, I think, as you know, over some military bases, we are -- we are obviously looking at that, that is a different category, and DOD has different authorities to deal with that. But again, we've also seen no disruption of operations at our bases in New Jersey, either from any of this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Here is the president-elect today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: They know where it came from and where it went. And for some reason they don't want to comment. And I think they would be better off saying what it is. Our military knows and our president knows. And for some reason, they want to keep people in suspense.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What's your answer to that?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Well, I think, it goes back to the first question you asked me, Bret. I mean, we have made an assessment based on the work we've done thus far, and our assessment is that this is lawful and legal activity, whether it's manned or unmanned.\par \par \uc2 Again, we see no indication of a national security or public safety. So, we do have an assessment.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. But there are so many of them, John. There are so many of them, John.\par \par \uc2 If this was happening over Delaware, Rehoboth Beach, would you be a little bit more concerned? I mean, I guess the people of New Jersey wonder why it took so long to get to the point you are now.\par \par \uc2 And if you see all of this, doesn't a local authority need the authority to be able to deal with it? Is that something Congress has to pass? Because they want to say, I don't want a six-foot-wide diameter refrigerator flying over my house and falling on my roof.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: That's a great question, and I'm glad you asked it, Bret. Because we absolutely do want to see congressional action.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration put proposed legislation for counter UAS authorities, additional authorities, to close some of the gaps, and seems that you are talking about. Not only between our own agencies here in the federal government, but the gaps that exist between us and state and local authorities. We absolutely want to see that legislation, and President Biden's calling for a bipartisan task force on Capitol Hill to do exactly that, to try to drive some legislative remedies.\par \par \uc2 I think everybody -- and I think we all understand this, Bret. I mean, we're in a new ecosystem in terms of unmanned aircraft in this country.\par \par \uc2 Again, all legal and lawful and doing the right thing for the public good, but it's a brand-new ecosystem. There are more systems flying, more purposes over more cities than ever before, and the authorities and the gaps and seams that those authorities don't give us have not kept up with that ecosystem, and so we want to see that driving forward.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. All right. Last thing. You don't see any linkage between the number of -- that this is all tied together in one thing, you think these are all different elements and different kinds of drones? Not with one company? Not with the military? Not with the DOE -- Department of Energy? You don't say that they're looking for some nuclear material. You say these are all different flying objects, some of them manned, some not.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Our assessment to date is that, Bret. That this is a collection of law enforcement hobbyists, commercial drones, and, of course, civil aviation aircraft that have caused all these sightings, and it's not attributable to an actor for a purpose.\par \par \uc2 And again, I want to stress to your viewers, we don't see anything that indicates a national security or public safety threat. The last thing I'll say, Bret, and I appreciate the time you give me is -- and we're still -- this is an ongoing investigation. We are still working our way through this.\par \par \uc2 Sightings continue to come in. We've taken them all seriously, and we'll continue to look at this. And if we learn more, if we know more, certainly, if we find something that's contradictory to what we believe, today, I'll be right up here on your show talking about it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. And we will have you on right away. John, we appreciate the time.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: You bet.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thanks. Up next, one Democrat, one Republican, we seek "COMMON GROUND", next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT CALIFF, COMMISSIONER, FOOD AND DRUG ADMINISTRATOR: When you're regulating 20 percent of the economy, you'd be amazed at the things that happen that just require an intervention. Someone comes into the \uc2 FDA\uc2  preaching about what it should or shouldn't do is going to sober up very quickly when there are a few supply chain shortages?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, that's the \uc2 FDA\uc2  commissioner talking about the ongoing shortage of drugs and some concerns about supply chain.\par \par \uc2 Tonight's "COMMON GROUND" segment deals with bipartisan legislation to help address that drug shortages, supply chain vulnerabilities.\par \par \uc2 Joining us tonight, Arkansas, Republican Senator Tom Cotton and Virginia Democratic Senator Tim Kaine.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both for being here. I want to talk about your bill and -- that you're working on, but a couple of things quickly first.\par \par \uc2 Senator Cotton, today, you sent a letter about demanding records on border wall materials being preserved, saying you want "accountability and transparency for the \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2  about its role in the disposal of excess border wall materials."\par \par \uc2 What's the issue there? And how concerned about it are you?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON, (R-AR): Well, Bret, the Biden administration has had many of these border wall materials going back to the Trump administration. We don't want to see them rush to sell those materials if, as we all anticipate, the Trump administration is going to be using them. We'd be selling them for just pennies on the dollar. We want them to actually explain what their next steps are, why they may be going forward with these sales. And if they are unreasonable sales, we want to stop that so the Trump administration can save the taxpayer dollar on what we anticipate will be finishing the border wall.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, Senator Kaine, about that. Obviously, we had an election, and immigration was one of the issues. And there are some people at home saying why get rid of this material if they are going to use it in the next administration to build more wall?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM KAINE, (D-VA): Yes. Bret, I haven't followed the issue of disposal of any of the material.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: We are expecting this to be a top priority next year, and we will deal with that as it comes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And let me ask you this, Senator Kaine. You obviously were a vice presidential nominee with Secretary Clinton, and we're in the aftermath of an election here. Is the Democratic Party having this kind of thought about the future right now? How do you describe it?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Yes, look, we are having a significant discussion about the next few years. I'm not so much a democrat. I'm a Virginia guy. So, I'm very focused on Virginia. But I think, if I could sum it up, the attitude now is advance everywhere we can together, and defend everything we must.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's talk about the common ground here, Protecting Our Essential Medicines Act. Create interagency task force, name drugs critical to health and safety, assess risk of drug supply shortages among other things. Senator Kaine, this is important?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: It's interesting. I'm on the help committee. Tom is not. But we're really tackling this as Armed Services Committee members, because we look at it as a critical national security issue.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And what is the national security aspect of it, Senator Cotton?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Well, if you look at the supply chains that we have for some basic drugs in America, a lot of it goes back to China or other adversarial nations. Our legislation would direct the government to take stock of where we are obtaining these prescription drugs, or in some cases over-the- counter drugs, or their precursor ingredients to make sure that we don't have adversarial nations like China that control them that could end up cutting them off in a moment of national cris. I think many people were worried during the pandemic when we discovered how dependent we are on China. They threatened to cut us off of those drugs. And then you take the fact that one of the biggest purchasers in drugs in our federal government is the \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2  for our troops who need all those basic drugs.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's also a price issue. Here's the president-elect today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENT-ELECT: The horrible middleman that makes more money, frankly, than the drug companies, and they don't do anything except they're a middleman. We are going to knock out the middleman. They are rich as hell.\par \par \uc2 We're going to knock out the middleman. We're going to get drug costs down at levels that nobody has ever seen before.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Does this all factor into that, Senator Kaine?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: The pharma companies at least do the research and create the lifesaving treatments and cures. The managers in the middle often are more profitable than the pharmaceutical companies, but we haven't paid attention to them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you both see the prospect of working together in the new Congress with the new president in the common ground way you are working now?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: I certainly hope so, Bret. Obviously, Tim and I have our disagreements on certain issues. Sometimes those issues are more high profile or prominent in the news. But when you are looking at things like the availability of lifesaving drugs for all Americans or the readiness of our troops, that is something where we have common ground, and we all hope to be able to work together for the common good of the American people.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator Kaine, last word?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: And Bret, I completely agree. One little piece of the bills that Tom and I are promoting is just simple communication. When pharmacies and pharmaceutical companies see an uptick in demand of a particular drug, we're asking them to share that with the \uc2 FDA\uc2  and others so we can see shortages before they develop. That will then enable us to use tools like the Defense Production Act and other tools to spur American production so that shortages never occur. This is good government stuff. There is no Dems and Rs in trying to stop drug charges.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Dr. \uc2 Marty Makary\uc2  nominated to be the next \uc2 FDA\uc2  commissioner. We know him well here. Senators, thanks so much for coming on "Common Ground."\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You can see all of the "Common Ground" segments on my podcast "Common Ground." Find that and the "All-Star Panel" podcast under the Bret Baier podcasts at FOXNewspodcast.com, \uc2 Spotify\uc2 , or wherever you download podcasts. We'll also put this discussion on the FOX News \uc2 YouTube\uc2  page.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, how the Biden-Harris administration is trying to Trump-proof its climate agenda before the president-elect takes office.\par \par \uc2 But, first, Democratic infighting centered on -- centered on ideology and age. Easy for you to say.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Ousted Syrian leader \uc2 Bashar al-Assad\uc2  says he wanted to stay in the country after rebels captured the capital. But Assad says the Russian military evacuated him from its base in western Syria after it came under attack. They were Assad's first public comments since he was thrown -- overthrown by insurgent groups just over a week ago.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, a fight to become the top Democrat on the high-profile House Oversight Committee is developing into a proxy battle over the future of the Democratic Party. It focuses on age and the direction of the party after this November's electoral losses. Senior congressional correspondent Chad Pergram explains tonight live from Capitol Hill. Good evening, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening. A blow to the youth movement in the Democratic Party. A panel of Democrats tonight recommended \uc2 Gerry Connolly\uc2  of Virginia over \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio- Cortez\uc2  to be the top Democrat on the Oversight Committee. Now, it was a generational choice for Democrats, youth versus experience. An icon of progressive movement, the 35-year-old AOC taking on decades of experience in the 74-year-old \uc2 Gerry Connolly\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the old guard won. Former presidential candidate \uc2 Dean Phillips\uc2  says the party blocks youth. The Minnesota Democrat challenged President Biden over his age.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 DEAN PHILLIPS\uc2 , (D-MN): It is time to elevate the best and brightest. We have literally kept them from ascending to leadership positions which means they go elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Democrats were concerned that an AOC win meant the party could veer left. Ohio's \uc2 Marcy Kaptur\uc2  is the 