 administration is trying to Trump-proof its climate agenda before the inauguration. That means spending a lot of your tax money on policies to solidify a green energy agenda before the Trump team tries to dismantle it. Senior national correspondent Rich Edson has details from the White House tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS STATE DEPARTMENT CORRESPONDENT: President Biden says his administration is working to prevent the Trump team from upending his legacy.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We got to make sure to protect -- protect the onslaught that's going to come.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: With Republicans taking control of the White House, Senate, and House of Representatives next year, the Biden administration has released a memo entitled "Making every day count." And with only 34 of them left in his term, officials are promising, quote, "The dividends of the Biden- Harris administration will continue to transform the country long after January 20th, 2025."\par \par \uc2 Before a Republican Congress can overturn Biden's signed laws on climate, foreign aid, and funding for microchip factories, officials are trying to spend that money. On Thursday the \uc2 EPA\uc2  announced $1.6 billion in grants for reducing pollution and increasing community climate resilience. The administration is also highlighting tens of billions of dollars to build microchip factories in the United States. Republican Senator Bill Haggerty has warned of any, quote, "lame duck attempt" to constrain the new Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 Officials are trying to finalize regulations on the environment and economy and push initiatives on student debt relief and artificial intelligence. The White House is promising more pardons and clemency to add to the nearly 1,500 President Biden has already granted, including for his son Hunter.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM COMER, (R-KY) HOUSE OVERSIGHT COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: It's unusual to