r stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENT-ELECT: Since the election I have been working every day to put the world at ease a little bit, to get rid of the wars. We're trying to get the wars stopped. That horrible, horrible war that's going on in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 We need a great media. We need a fair media. We need -- it's very important. And we need borders. We need walls, but we need borders, and we need fair elections.\par \par \uc2 We will take a look at TikTok. I have a warm spot in my heart for TikTok because I won youth by 34 points.\par \par \uc2 I think everything should be looked at. But I'm a big believer in the polio vaccine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, a big, extensive press conference today. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " writes it "covers drones, pardons, wars in the first post-election news conference. Trump spoke to reporters an answered questions for about an hour on Monday, giving wide ranging remarks. Topics included border security, drone sightings, the war in Ukraine. The appearance, which recall the lengthy exchanges he often had during first term, came after he appeared besides \uc2 Masayoshi Son\uc2  the chief executive of \uc2 SoftBank\uc2  at Mar-a- Lago to announce the Japanese conglomerate's plan to invest $100 billion in U.S. projects." Here it is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENT-ELECT: I'm thrilled to announce that \uc2 SoftBank\uc2  will be investing $100 billion in America, creating 100,000 American jobs at a minimum.\par \par \uc2 MASAYOSHI SON, \uc2 SOFTBANK\uc2  CEO: My confidence level to the economy of the United States has tremendously increased with his victory.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And 36 days from inauguration.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " columnist, \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2  senior fellow Marc Thiessen, Matt Bennett, Third Way co-founder, former deputy assistant to the president for Intergovernmental Affairs under \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , and "Axios" political reporter Stef Kight.\par \par \uc2 Stuff is happening, Marc. No matter where you are on the ideological spectrum, there's stuff happening even before this election.\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, COLUMNIST, "WASHINGTON POST": Very much so. And when is the last time you saw a freewheeling White House press conference like that. Ever since Biden's cognitive decline has been exposed it's like he's not really in charge anymore and Trump is president. He is straddling the world stage. He's meeting with business executives. He's going to France and meeting with Zelenskyy and negotiating peace. It's like he is already president.\par \par \uc2 And it's kind of unprecedented because Biden has left a vacuum. All these things have you seen Biden talking about the drones? Have you seen Biden talking about any of these things? They launched 75 missiles into Syria and the president didn't address the nation about it. It's like he is not there anymore.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Matt, obviously you raised your eyebrows in that list of soundbites.\par \par \uc2 MATT BENNETT, THIRD WAY CO-FOUNDER: Look, he makes a lot of claims, the president-elect does, and a lot of them are false. He said he was going to cut $2 trillion out of --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We don't know if it's going to happen or not.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: It would mean cutting the entire discretionary budget of the United States if he sticks to his word and doesn't cut Social Security and Medicare. So that would be a lot. All the parks would close, all the \uc2 FAA\uc2  would be fired. So, you are not going to do that. There is a lot of things saying is he going to do but he can't do. But he's made a lot of promises to people that he better try to keep, and one of them is to bring down prices. He is already walking that back, saying I don't know if I can do that. But he imposes tariffs that, is the opposite of bringing down prices, and if that's his first move economically, that could bring a problem for him down the road.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What about this invite to China about Xi attending the inauguration? \uc2 CBS\uc2  was reporting that they said no, or not expected to accept it. I don't think we have formal word yet from the Chinese. What about the move overall, Stef?\par \par \uc2 STEF KIGHT, POLITICAL REPORTER, "AXIOS": I mean, it's very Trump-like, right? He has long said that the way to work with our adversaries is to talk to them. It's kind of been his them, the way that he approaches foreign policy. So in some senses maybe it's not surprising coming from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but it has raised some eyebrows even on Capitol Hill among Republicans who felt like that's an odd move to invite someone like to attend inauguration, obviously unprecedented. That's not what most president-elects choose to do.\par \par \uc2 But again, this is the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  we have known for a very long time. He likes to make moves that are surprising to pp., to keep people on their toes. And it is notable that we are at least getting reporting that Xi would not consider attending.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: It's classic Trump escalation dominance. So you announce you're going to impose 10 percent tariffs on them. What Trump does is he goes up the escalation ladder to the very top and dares to you follow them but then gives you a way out through negotiations. So he announces 10 percent tariffs on China the day he comes into office but invites Xi to his inauguration. If you are a bad guy out there and Trump is being nice to you, that means there is a hammer coming.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And if you are Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , that's your buddy, Xi Jinping. And if there is suddenly a U.S.-China deal, it's a different dynamic.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Very true, very true.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As he may be considering sanctions to get Putin to the table.\par \par \uc2 Stef, what about this drone situation? "Wall Street Journal," "The Biden administration has squandered its credibility to the point that it's rational not to believe what it says. Remember the Chinese spy balloon that traveled across the continental U.S.? The feds need to explain to Congress and the public with more specificity what is really going on. But the larger need is for government, a government Americans can trust. Here's John Kirby on this show.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: That this is lawful, legal, commercial, hobbyist, and even law enforcement aircraft activity. Some of it is manned, some of it is unmanned. We absolutely acknowledge that a lot of these are probably drones, but they are flying legally.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I tried five different ways to go at it. I don't think there is people in New Jersey and other places that are buying that whole thing.\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: Yes, I think it's a very clear example of kind of the tension between social media, the ability for lots of people to see things with their own eyes through video footage, and space where we are having lots of people responding to this in different ways. We have Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  asking for more clarity while the Biden administration is saying that, oh, it's nothing to worry about, and no further questions are needed.\par \par \uc2 And I do think this does point to how little trust the American people have in the federal government, and want more answers. It's also kind of a tale as old as time. We have long been looking at the skies and asking questions about what is that up there, and the government has never really been able to give us clear answers. But it's certainly bothering many people, and we are hearing more and more on the Hill, lawmakers wanting to get more answers on what is going on here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, it's a little infuriating, Matt. And you can go at John Kirby. It was good have him on, but I really kind of wanted answers.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Look, he may not have answers. There is a ton of stuff in the air right now. There's drones all over the place. There's a lot of flights around northern New Jersey, there's three enormous commercial airports and a lot of smaller airports. There's just a lot of things out there.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, there are people like former Governor Hogan reported on seeing drones that turned out to be the constellation Orion. So people are misidentifying things in the air because there's a little bit of mania around this.\par \par \uc2 I will say on "The Wall Street Journal" editorial, all due respect, there have been problems with some of the things coming out of the Biden White House. But trust in government has been eroding for a really long time. And when Americans were told that COVID would go away and that they could treat it with bleach and all kinds of stuff, there have been things that go on both sides.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, but you can go down that road a bunch of different ways. It wasn't in the Wuhan lab, it was pangolin. All kinds of things.\par \par \uc2 Last word on the drones. I've got 15 seconds.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Just that it has exposed a big vulnerability for us, because if these drones were in Iraq, we could take them down. But if you try use that technology in the U.S., you could take down a commercial airliner. So we have a vulnerability here in this country that we can't use our counter drone technology that our enemies are watching.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, we'll continue following it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And finally tonight, a special day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You see the joy in these kids when they truly believe they are going to the North Pole to see Santa Claus. If we could live life through the eyes of a child, think like a child, forgive like a child, love like a child. That's what we get to see.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  taking more than 100 kids, some with serious health issues, on a fantasy flight to the North Pole. The flight is roughly 45 minutes long, circling the Denver airport, upon landing, being towed to a hangar completely transformed by employees to Santa and Mrs. Claus waiting for them. Pretty cool.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. Here's Laura.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020241217ekcg00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 3 Dead Including Suspected School Shooter; Awaiting New Info On Deadly Wisconsin School Shooting; Now: Trump \uc2 HHS\uc2  Pick RFK JR. In D.C. To Meet Senators; Trump HHS Pick RFK Jr. In DC To Meet Senators; New Pentagon Reaction To Reported Drone Sightings; Deadly Wisconsin School Shooting\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Paul Mauro, Trace Gallagher, Garrett Tenney, Ted  Williams, Aishah Hasnie, Jennifer Griffin \par \uc2 9,481 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 December 2024\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: OK, we are minutes away from an update on this deadly shooting at a Christian school in Madison, Wisconsin, as you have been following the breaking news on Fox this afternoon. Tragic as it is. We're waiting for some more information. Now, the latest is that police believe that three people are dead including the suspected shooter who, we're told, is a juvenile.\par \par \uc2 Police getting that call that no police station wants and no school wants, just a little before 11:00 a.m. today that there were multiple victims in that school with gunshot wounds. It was at the Abundant Life Christian School. They have about 400 students from kindergarten through high school. Now, the police chief understandably was emotional. A very long resident of this town speaking with reporters.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHIEF SHON BARNES, MADISON, WISCONSIN POLICE DEPARTMENT: To be honest with you, I'm feeling a little dismayed now. So close to Christmas. Every child, every person in that building is a victim and will be a victim forever. These types of trauma, don't just go away. And we need to figure out and try to piece together what exactly happened. And right now, my heart is heavy for my community, my heart is heavy for Madison, and we have to come together as a community and figure out what happened here and make sure that it doesn't happen in any other place that should be a refuge for students.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, that's all very true and that's what everybody feels watching this story yet again today. I'm joined by retired \uc2 NYPD\uc2  inspector, Paul Morrow, who is here, a Fox News Contributor. Paul, I was just watching your coverage moments ago on America Reports, and we're going to hear some more details. Take us through where you think they are in this process right now, because they come before the cameras because they have something to add.\par \par \uc2 PAUL MAURO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: So, the chief said, I think quite cognizantly, that there's no threat, and he means an imminent threat, right? There isn't somebody that they have in dishes running around at their second piece or some other associate with a gun and so on and so forth they did put out apparently that it was a handgun.\par \par \uc2 So, I think it would be, you know, he's obviously trying to tamp down the immediate concerns but there's certainly going to be a lot of investigation here now, and that's what's going on behind the scenes that we're not seeing. They are getting search warrants. You know who's involved in these things, and people don't realize this, is now you're going to have to lash up with the district attorney's office.\par \par \uc2 Because you don't just go walking in, yes, under exigent circumstances, emergency circumstances, you can. But to do it right, once you know you have the time, you need a prosecutor writing furiously to get into the phone, to execute all the search warrants you can, you got to dump the cloud, you got to go to the social media companies. Lot of investigation work here, and those things take time. You don't have time in a circumstance like this because you don't know who this shooter is hooked up with.\par \par \uc2 So, all of these things are churning behind the scene. And at the same time, the chief has to come out and tell us all, look, we got our arms around this. Everybody, you know, we're going to take a breath now. We don't have an immediate concern. But his real main concern, of course, is for the families and to make sure all those notifications go out.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely. And, you know, in terms of this shooter and what we've learned, we don't know yet, correct me if I'm wrong that this was a self-inflicted gun wound or not or whether or not somebody took him out in that school when they saw him carrying this out.\par \par \uc2 MAURO: Right now, we don't know that. I think the chief would have told us is the just getting, getting from the tenor of the press conference and the information he's been provided, been pretty forthright. If it had been the police, I don't know that. So, I think we're down to either a school resource officer if they had one who was armed or self-inflicted.\par \par \uc2 We do know as I said it was a handgun that argues maybe and you know we're talking about this with Trace earlier that maybe it was not as much of a mass shooting planned event. Hopefully, as, as maybe, initially you know we, we might have thought in light of some of the other ones that we've seen here, but of course a handgun means it's more concealable.\par \par \uc2 And so, one of the things you're going to look at here is, OK, if he had a concealed gun, and this was a very sort of targeted, you know, why did he choose who he chose? Was there a gripe? Or is it ideological?\par \par \uc2 And the latter, if you're sitting where the chief is, is a bigger concern, because if you start to see that, OK, he had some sort of ideological gripe, then you have to start to admit to the idea that there could be associates, people that he was with online, a group, and then you've got to start to worry about things like copycats.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And you can't take anything for granted. You can't look at the situation and say, OK, I think we know what this was about. He was targeting this adult, this child, we don't know yet, we're waiting for that information. How large, you know, for those who don't know, nine-millimeter pistol, how large is it? And perhaps it wasn't that he or she didn't want to do greater carnage, but maybe this is the gun that they were able to get their hands on.\par \par \uc2 MAURO: Absolutely, we don't know that and I'm not certainly downplaying the carnage that was committed. Nine-millimeter can be small. They have the, they call morph duties here in \uc2 NYPD\uc2 . They're, you know, not a whole lot bigger than your hands, and so they just don't hold as many rounds.\par \par \uc2 So, we don't know what he had, generally, it's in the 16 range or so with one of the chamber. And you're right, and you know, I start to default to we talked about it the last hour certainly a part of this investigation now is a new factor which is the precarious liability of how we got the gun, and I'm not putting any shade on his parents here, OK?\par \par \uc2 I want to be clear. But if you got the gun from somebody who was not supposed to give him a gun, whoever it was, well you have the liability of, well, if you knew this person maybe had certain intent, if you knew that they had stated it to you, et cetera. Are you now a co-conspirator or do you have this vicarious liability we saw in the Crumbley case? That's a new factor.\par \par \uc2 A lot of legal experts, you know, and I got to be honest, myself included, a little uncomfortable with that because how far does that extend? It's not really a basic part of American jurisprudence. It's a new development, but it's developed out of the fact that we've had such horrific events like this.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely. Well put. And this is just a horrific day for the families of those involved and all the children and their families at this school, kindergarten to 12th grade. So, we have no idea how the age of this shooter at this point, but as I said, we're just minutes away from another briefing. So, stay tuned. We've got breaking news on that.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Trace Gallagher, Chief Breaking News Correspondent and Anchor of "FOX NEWS @ NIGHT." Trace, you have been watching this throughout the last few hours on "AMERICA REPORTS." We're waiting for an update. And you hear Paul and I talking about this gun, about the parents, about really the questions that we hope will be answered soon.\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Yes, and a couple of things that really struck me as I was covering this on "AMERICA REPORTS" for a couple of hours. One, is the fact that on the very same day, Martha, Paul was talking about how deep this investigation goes and how time is of the essence because you don't know if this shooter was involved in any capacity with somebody else. On the same day, today, we had threats of school shooters in Green Bay.\par \par \uc2 There were two of them that came about, and it happened this morning. Green Bay is about 130 miles away from Madison, Wisconsin, so we're not saying in any way that those are connected, but it's interesting that it happened on the very same day when there have not been threats in Wisconsin for years, months, and so there. And when you look at the scene, we had pictures up. We have the reunification center, we have the school, but we didn't have any helicopter video.\par \par \uc2 And because of that, Martha, it was hard to kind of tell where the police, where the ambulances, where things were going. We didn't have the eyes in the sky. And I find it fascinating because we would know by now if police were at some house doing a deeper investigation, and we don't yet know that, maybe will learn that in a few minutes when that the news conference starts.\par \par \uc2 But, but a lot of the scenes that we have covered over the years, we have seen we have had that kind of information -- we know right away when police get a lead with police are following something because we're following them from the sky. In Madison, a smaller T.V. market didn't quite have that, and it is interesting that now everybody, you know, you talk about the guns, you talk about, you know how many victims those are the first and foremost.\par \par \uc2 And then you go right away to the social media footprint, who was this juvenile, who were they involved with what were they talking about? What did they, was there any kind of political ideology was there some other directive being given, some group something else that kind of led to this. So, a lot of fascinating things that you kind of look back on of years of, unfortunately, covering these things and the first things that come to mind are some of the things that you've seen historically, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Unfortunately, as you say, Trace -- I mean, unfortunately, this is more of an American story than it is in other places. It's just, you know, we have seen some of these kinds of shootings in other countries. And I was listening to the sheriff earlier who was saying that there were no magnetometers at this school, nor should there be, which I think I heard you point out earlier. And I'm wondering if this kind of gun would have been picked up on that.\par \par \uc2 We have seen an increase in school resource officers at schools. We've seen that with mixed success. In some cases, it's been helpful, in some cases it was tragic, as in Texas and in Uvalde when they did not respond appropriately. But your thoughts on all of that, on the measures that have been taken as we have covered so many of these stories over the last many years?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I mean, you go back to Columbine, right? You go to Columbine, which was the first big one, and we were to Columbine for the better part of two and a half, three weeks. That's how big of a story it was, because it was one of the first times we had ever seen something like this. And then, we got into the debate over the years of should they have a school resource officer, should they have metal detectors, should they have all of these things.\par \par \uc2 And you talk to these investigators, as we have done over the years, and they will always kind of point out that for all the metal detectors and for all the school resource officers, they might, they might slow it down. They might stop it from being worse, but the problem is that you can go back, Martha, and look almost to a shooter and find that there were red flags along the way. There were big red flags along the way. You throw out a name, and you can throw out five or six things that you could have brought this kid out and dealt with this kid.\par \par \uc2 So, the question becomes, is it about stopping it at the scene or is it about stopping it before they ever get to the scene, before it ever gets to that point? And I think that's now what a lot of schools are looking at, because as good as a metal detector might be, it might not pick up some. And I think if you look into the mindset of a lot of these kids, I think that tends to lead you down a more, I don't know, a more beneficial path to solving some of these problems.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, it may be that you have to get it from both angles. We've also heard stories of kids who were sent home because they drew a picture of a gun, right? On a piece of paper, it's so hard for officials and sometimes for parents as well to know when something is crossing the line and when there is a real concern.\par \par \uc2 But so many times we have seen that, you know, people have been confronted by law enforcement, that they are living in a situation at home that people are aware of that is not conducive to this person's mental health.\par \par \uc2 All these different examples come to mind at this point as we wait for some more information here. Stand by with me, Trace and Paul, we're going to wait for this latest news to come out from the podium and the microphones where they're getting set up right now. I can see them on the right, on the left-hand side here.\par \par \uc2 So, Fox is almost at the scene of this school shooting in Wisconsin and we're going to take you there live as we await this new update, which generally they -- it coincides with new information. So, we'll take you there as soon as that gets underway in Madison Wisconsin, as Trace pointed out.\par \par \uc2 A couple of other warnings at schools in Green Bay, know we do not know whether or not there's any connection between these two things, but we have not seen anything like this in a long time in Wisconsin. So, we stand by there live as we wait for that update from law enforcement next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we're expecting an update any minute from the deadly school shooting in Madison, Wisconsin. Let's go straight to our correspondent Garrett Tenney, who is now in Madison. Garrett, what have you learned so far?\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Martha. Right now, we are across the street from the Abundant Life Christian School.\par \par \uc2 And as we've been reporting, this is a K through 12 school, a wide range of ages for kids here. The campus is also shared with a preschool that includes kids from infants up to 4 years old. It really just pulls at your heartstrings.\par \par \uc2 Here behind us is a church that is on this campus, and this is where students have been held after this shooting, waiting to head to the reunification center, which is about half a mile down the road.\par \par \uc2 I want you to look at this bus that is outside the school. A city bus. We have had several of these pull up just in the last few minutes, and seen students walking from this church and loading onto the bus, where we presumed, they are headed half a mile down the road to that reunification center.\par \par \uc2 Martha, these kids, they looked like they were 7, 8, 9 years old, sitting there on a bus alone filled with police officers, escorting them to be with their parents.\par \par \uc2 The latest information we have in terms of the timeline for this is that just before 11:00 a.m., police got a call about an active shooter at the Abundant Life Christian School. This would have been almost three hours after school started.\par \par \uc2 When officers arrived, they found multiple victims inside who had been shot. Three were dead, including the shooter and six others were rushed to local hospitals with injuries ranging from minor to life threatening.\par \par \uc2 Police have not released more information about the victims or the shooter, their ages, gender, backgrounds, until the families have been informed. There is a huge age range for kids on this campus, Martha.\par \par \uc2 We talked about the school being K through 12, but sharing it with that preschool and a reporter for the journal central newspaper, so parents running into that reunification center to get their kids, some of whom were in infant car seats.\par \par \uc2 We have gotten a statement from the Abundant Life Christian School. Officials there are asking the country to pray for their community. Posting on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , "Prayers requested! Today, we had an active shooter incident at ALCS. We are in the midst of following up. We will share information as we are able. Please pray for our challenger family."\par \par \uc2 You'd also talked about some of what we are hoping to learn from police in this next update coming up in the next 10 to 20 minutes. Details about the shooter and the victims, what kind of security measures the school had in place, and what will likely take longer to find out which -- the everyone is wondering at this point is what led up to this horrific shooting at this private Christian school, nine days before Christmas.\par \par \uc2 And also in the last few minutes, Martha, we have seen a number of therapy dogs that have been coming up and going inside to this church where these young children are being held, waiting to be able to load onto the city busses, taken to the reunification center, where they will be brought back together again with their parents.\par \par \uc2 Can't imagine what they have all been going through this morning. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Garrett, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 That is true. And when you hear these little children on this bus by themselves as you report, it is just so terrifying. And you think about the families who are going to meet up with these children, and you know, just the unthinkable of people who might not be reunited today. It is 9:00 days before Christmas, as you point out, Garrett. We will come back to you, Garrett, as we get underway with this news conference.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, let's bring in Ted Williams, former D.C. homicide detective and a Fox News contributor watching all of this along with us, Ted. What goes through your mind as you see this latest school shooting today in Madison, Wisconsin?\par \par \uc2 TED WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: You know, Martha, I've been on with you before, over and over where we have, unfortunately, these kinds of incident, these kind of shootings at school. And all I can think about is - - I was listening to Garrett Tenney there a few minutes ago, are these young kids who just went to school to get an education. And they have to be put through this kind of trauma.\par \par \uc2 And those young kids who are now on that bus hoping to get to that reunification center to be reunified with their loved ones. Those were the initial thoughts.\par \par \uc2 But Martha, what we have here now is a crime scene, and the law enforcement officers are processing that crime scene in a methodical manner.\par \par \uc2 And if you listen to Chief Barnes, the chief of police there, you know that they have prepared, unfortunately, for these kinds of incidents at schools. Now, from what I understand, there were no magnetometers here at this school.\par \par \uc2 But you know, what you find, our communities are torn. They want their children to go to school and be educated. They don't want their children to have to feel that they are in a prison where they have to be magnetometers and they have to be security guards.\par \par \uc2 And so, it's a catch 22 trying to triangle those -- triangulate those kinds of scenarios and situation, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, you know, the flip side is, if these shooters think that they can't get into the building or they might get caught getting into the building, would that dissuade them from trying and knowing that there is nothing between them and the doorway has not led to an optimal situation in this country.\par \par \uc2 The other thing that really strikes me, Ted, when I'm thinking about Christmas right around the corner is Sandy Hook, because that happened on December the 14th in Connecticut. And I clearly remember, I mean, that -- this was another -- just, the magnitude of it was just unthinkable.\par \par \uc2 And there were Christmas trees all over town as those families were going through hell. And that's the hell that, at least, a few families and injured family of the -- families of the injured are thinking about today, Ted.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes. I also thought about Sandy Hook and those young kids that were killed there in -- at Sandy Hook. One of the things that I'm deeply concerned about, and we don't know, and I want to make sure I'm clear, we do not know that there is a nexus between the phone calls that has been received in other parts of Wisconsin and what is taking place here today, but we do know that Sandy Hook did occur right around this time. And it's a very sad situation of what we are looking at here in America.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, Martha, I -- is, I'm on with you right now, and the sad commentary, and I can guarantee you that at some stage in the very near future, we are going to be faced with the same scenario and situation. It's sort of like Groundhogs Day for us. We go over and over these situations, but we never find a remedy.\par \par \uc2 And I -- one of the things that I believe is, we're going to have to be more proactive with young people before they get to these schools, and hope that, that will also help to resolve some of these situations.\par \par \uc2 When you see something, you must say something. If you see a young person that's acting out, that person should be told, the authorities should know about it, and the parents should definitely be involved.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Yes, we need to harden our schools, I think, is one of the lessons here. And make people feel safer and dissuade people from thinking that they can get through those doors.\par \par \uc2 Ted, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Stand by everyone, because we're going to bring you that presser. I can see them setting up right now. We're a few minutes away.\par \par \uc2 And in the meantime, a live look on Capitol Hill, where we are awaiting Trump pick for \uc2 HHS\uc2  Secretary, RFK Jr., to arrive for a meeting with Republican Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 . The first of about a dozen that will take place. These are all the sit downs before the actual hearings that will happen in the Senate right after the holidays.\par \par \uc2 We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Robert Kennedy, he is on the Hill today. He is meeting with senators. What do you say to people who are worried that his views on vaccines will translate into policies that will make their kids less safe?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: No, I think, he is going to be much less radical than you would think. I think he's got a very open mind, or I wouldn't have put him there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: RFK Jr., about to meet with more than two dozen senators this week. He is going to be pretty busy this week with all of these sit downs. He is nominated, of course, for Health and Human Services secretary. He has this cause to make America healthy again, which President-elect Trump has embraced.\par \par \uc2 Senior national correspondent Aishah Hasnie reporting live on Capitol Hill on the very latest. She'd been watching all of these folks come through. Exciting time and we'll see what happens today.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha.\par \par \uc2 25 senators is a lot, so are 25. Reporters and producers who are currently waiting for him to walk through 