hings are on the to-do list for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 So what are the offsets to those cuts and what about this issue of the national debt which frankly a lot of folks think did not get nearly enough discussion if any at all in the campaign trail?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Well, two quick things -- when anybody talks about tax policy from these foundations, what they never say to the American people is that when they talk about $7 trillion is the cost, that's using static modeling. I don't want to get too technical, but that is static modeling. It doesn't take into a -- into effect the impact of lower tax rates and economic growth as a result.\par \par \uc2 So, no, it doesn't cost $ trillion and the reason why we know this is because under his first tax policy which is current tax law today, the Democrats and all these outside groups said it would cost $ trillion the truth is the federal government raised more tax revenue that was that was ever modeled or contemplated by any of these organizations. So, President Trump's tax policy is actually going to be a net benefit to the United States because it's going to breed more economic growth.\par \par \uc2 Number two, there are massive areas in the federal government that do need to be scaled back, that need to be cut.\par \par \uc2 One of them that we have to take a long hard look at are the Green New Deal tax credits in the, quote, in the Inflation Reduction Act. We need to look at those because they told us in D.C. it would cost $276 billion. Well, if you go talk to \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  and anybody on the street, the value is actually $1.4 trillion.\par \par \uc2 So if you want to find money to pay for tax policy so we can grow our economy, so working families can get ahead with no tax on tips and no tax on overtime, how about we get rid of the Democrats spending boondoggles that came in under the last administration?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to make sure I ask you about something reported by "The Daily Wire".\par \par \uc2 They broke a story a couple of days ago about FEMA. My home state is as well as where you serve, Florida, and your district has been hit very hard by numerous big storms the last couple of years. "Daily Wire" reporting that people were told when they're out canvassing in central Florida looking for people who may need help, that FEMA workers were told to avoid homes that had Trump symbols or Trump flags.\par \par \uc2 FEMA responded to "Daily Wire" saying this: We are horrified that this took place and therefore have taken extreme actions to correct the situation.\par \par \uc2 Governor DeSantis says he's launching an investigation there.\par \par \uc2 What extreme actions do you think should be taken? Is that something Congress gets involved with?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Anybody involved with that should be fired immediately, not asked to resign, not brought in front of a congressional hearing, he should be fired immediately.\par \par \uc2 We are not going to have a country where we're going to decide who gets help and who gets -- who doesn't based upon who you support politically or what you look like or anything else.\par \par \uc2 Secondarily, when it comes to FEMA, my recommendation for the Trump transition team is that FEMA becomes a standalone agency that directly reports to the White House. You have to get it out of Homeland Security. That agency is a massive labyrinth with a bunch of different departments.\par \par \uc2 FEMA should -- FEMA should be a direct report agency to the White House because when disaster recovery needs to take place. We need quick answers out of FEMA, not bureaucratic ones that go through the labyrinth of federal departments.\par \par \uc2 My district dealt with this firsthand a couple years ago during Hurricane Ian. It was very hard to get answers out of FEMA, still somewhat hard to get answers out of FEMA. I think if you talk to any governor, they would agree with this.\par \par \uc2 FEMA needs to be a direct report 