un again. One of the postmortems on this whole election this week comes from the "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ." They write this. "Democrats began the campaign season by peddling the fiction that Biden was physically and mentally fit to serve another four years in one of the world's most demanding jobs."\par \par \uc2 Do you feel there's some blame that should be assigned to those close to the president who maybe did not give a full -- they didn't get full access, and people didn't have a full picture? I don't know about from members of Congress, but we've heard complaints from many people that they couldn't get to President Biden. They couldn't spend time with him. And that there was this shield that propped him up as being maybe more mentally and physically fit than he was for the campaign ahead.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIPS: The answer is yes. You know, encapsulation is a very dangerous strategy in American politics that thrives on transparency and truth. President Biden is a good man. Even my Republican colleagues will say the same thing. But he's an old man, and his physical decline, his communication decline are real. And an overwhelming majority of Americans saw that as evidence of an inability to serve another term.\par \par \uc2 And it's not what might be real. It's what perception is, and that's what's reality in politics. And yes, there are many people that I believe are accountable for making a terrible mistake in judgment. He was not the only one that could have beaten \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He was one of the few that couldn't. And sadly, we ended up elevating yet another candidate who was not positioned well to win.\par \par \uc2 And Shannon, the answer is so simple in America. It's competition. We are a country predicated on it. And when any party suppresses it, when state party leaders in Florida and North Carolina, Wisconsin and others remove candidates from ballots, that's antithetical to democracy. And both parties have to do a little bit of soul-searching right now. My friends on the Republican side, my friend Byron Donalds, you know, they've got a big responsibility, my Republican colleagues, coming up in the next four years to hold this president accountable and ensure that the Constitution comes first.\par \par \uc2 And I hope they're up to the task. And I just want both parties to do some soul-searching, leadership on both sides to prioritize problem-solving and demonstrate that democracy still works, because there are a lot of people around the world, leaders in Russia, China, North Korea, Iran, that are looking to us to fail. And the answer lies with all of us, not our leaders in Congress or the White House.\par \par \uc2 And I'll call to action once more. All of us have got to stop the nonsense and prioritize competency and decency. And I'm going to do my very best to inspire it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So in the more immediate future, Democrats are doing sort of an autopsy on what happened in that shellacking on Tuesday night. You've said in recent days the party has, quote, "good product and terrible packaging and distribution."\par \par \uc2 But here's what Maureen Dowd writes about Democrats. She says, "The party embraced a worldview of hyper-political correctness, condescension and cancellation. They have to begin avoiding extreme policies that alienate many Americans who would otherwise be drawn to the party." You've acknowledged that there are some positions the left flank of your party have taken that have alienated millions and millions of Americans. So is it more about the policy, the content, or is it about the messaging?\par \par \uc2 PHILLIPS: Well, I think it's fair to say, Shannon, \uc2 Ritchie Torres\uc2  has been saying this, I think, quite eloquently recently. The elements of the far left of the Democratic Party, far right of the Republican Party should be disenfranchised because they do not represent America. But those elements cast a shadow on both sides of the aisle. I think that's true.\par \par \uc2 But if you look at some of the ballot measures just in this last election, Florida voting overwhelmingly to protect women's reproductive rights, Missouri voting to --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Wait a minute, but Florida did not, just to be clear, Florida did not approve that -- the measure that would have expanded abortion access there potentially.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIPS: Shannon, I said an overwhelming majority of Floridian voters did.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Because they didn't get to 60% there.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIPS: No, but they didn't get to 60. But a majority of Floridians voted to protect women's reproductive rights, yet they voted for a candidate who was opposed to them, Missouri, raising the minimum wage, paid family leave, Alaska. You look around the country, a lot of red states were favoring Democratic policy.\par \par \uc2 So I would argue, yes, our product is not the real problem. Our packaging, our messengers and our distribution is a real problem. And I think it's fair to say, if you ask people what the Democratic brand stands for right now, it's real complicated. And that's a real important problem right now, that we have to articulate what we stand for.\par \par \uc2 And, again, solving problems, that is the issue that no one, no party, no leader right now is really addressing. And we have a political system, Shannon, that really has incentives that actually are working against us. And the corruption has to be addressed, because we've legalized it in this country. Both parties are subject to it. And I'm going to start telling a lot of truths here in the coming months and years.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, well, you can always tell them here on Fox News Sunday, if you've got anything that you'd like to share, like to say.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIPS: I look forward to it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to play something from David Axelrod, who said this early Wednesday as it became clear that President Trump was going to be reelected, about partially at least what he thinks happened.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID AXELROD, CNN SENIOR POLITICAL COMMENTATOR: There were appeals to racism in this campaign. And there is racial bias in this country. And there is sexism in this country. And anybody who thinks that -- that did not in any way impact on the outcome of this race is wrong.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But you know the numbers. President Trump improved his numbers with women, with black voters, with Latino voters. The radio host Charlamagne is out there saying that people voted for him because they wanted to feel safer. They wanted to feel better about their economics. And he's one of those who's warning against Democrats simply saying sexism, racism, and the country got it wrong. And that's why they picked this man to be their president again.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIPS: Well, if that becomes our primary focus, we're going to lose perpetually because that's exactly I think what we've done wrong. We've used condemnation of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  versus invitation to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  supporters. I've never known any industry, political or professional, in which a strategy of condemnation works better than invitation. So there you have it.\par \par \uc2 It's really true. And by the way, listening, you know, traveling the country and simply listening to people is the easiest recipe for success. And I encourage my colleagues, Democrats particularly, to get out into rural America and sit with people, listen to them, have a cup of coffee and understand they're not racist, they're not bigots, they're not anti- Semites. They simply want what all of us want, security and safety and opportunity and a sense of some American unity as well. And get rid of imposition. I think both parties have a lot to learn on that subject right now.\par \par \uc2 And we are desperate for leadership that will do so. I'm not certain it's going to come in the next four years for obvious reasons. But hopefully, we'll see our better angels rise in the next -- in the next election.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, if for you that means venturing into a third party. There were certainly strong conversations about that this time around that didn't quite take hold. But whatever the future is, Congressman, keep us abreast. We'd like to know.\par \par \uc2 PHILLIPS: Thank you. Keep the faith and appreciate the invitation. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Up next, the battle for control of the House. I'm going to break down some of the remaining undecided races that could determine whether Republicans score a clean sweep of both ends of Pennsylvania Avenue.\par \par \uc2 And our Sunday panelists here with a closer look at what happens to all those legal cases now pending against President-elect Trump.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Welcome back. Days after the election, we're still waiting to see whether Republicans will hold on to the House or Democrats will flip enough seats to assume control come January.\par \par \uc2 Right now, 20 seats across the country still remain undecided. Democrats have won 202 so far, so they would need 16 more to flip the House. Republicans have locked in 213. And that means they need five more if they want to hold on to the majority.\par \par \uc2 So let's take a look at some of the races that have yet to be called here in Arizona. Let's look at their sixth district. Incumbent Juan Ciscomani is in a tight race with Kirsten Engel. Ciscomani and Engel went head to head in 2022. Ciscomani won there by fewer than 6,000 votes.\par \par \uc2 Now in California, which has gotten a lot of attention as well, the 27th district there, Mike Garcia, is facing George Whitesides. Garcia has been a member of Congress since 2020 and has defeated a Democratic challenger in three previous elections, but is in danger of losing his seat this time around.\par \par \uc2 Let's take you to another really close one that is in Iowa. And there it is still yet to be called in this district. \uc2 Christina Bohannan\uc2  in the first district is challenging incumbent Mariannette Miller-Meeks who has served two terms in Congress. Abortion has been a very hot button topic in that race.\par \par \uc2 Now if Republicans retain the House, they will hold the majority of both chambers of Congress giving the party a clean sweep of Washington.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So let's talk about with our Sunday group, Howard Kurtz, media critic and host of "MEDIA BUZZ" on Fox News Channel. Editor-in-chief of "The Federalist," Mollie Hemingway. Former State Department spokesperson, Marie Harf. And Jeff Mason, Reuters White House correspondent. Welcome everyone.\par \par \uc2 So 20 of those races still undecided in the House. We'll see. But if Republicans end up in charge, the "Associated Press" notes this about the speaker's plans if he is speaker again. "Tax cuts, securing the southern border and taking a blowtorch to federal regulations are at the top of the agenda if the GOP sweeps the White House and Congress."\par \par \uc2 Jeff, if they do, there's going to be a lot of pressure on them to actually deliver on a lot of big promises.\par \par \uc2 JEFF MASON, REUTERS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: There absolutely is. And that's been one of the problems or the challenges that the Republican majority in the House has faced this year, is not being able to get a lot done despite having a majority.\par \par \uc2 That majority was very slim. And if they get it again, it may be slim, although 20 seats up, that's, you know, they could certainly make that a bigger majority than they had.\par \par \uc2 But there will be pressure. And, you know, I think if you watch the market reaction over the last few days, there's also an expectation among financial markets that those tax cuts are going to come and that those regulations are going to go away.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So while we wait to see how the House settles, President Trump is working on his transition back into government. And there are a number of reports that there are people in these agencies planning to Trump proof the situation before he gets there.\par \par \uc2 "POLITICO" says this about immigration advocates. "Some are already preparing to push current leadership at the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  to take steps to stymie the incoming Trump team."\par \par \uc2 "POLITICO" also in another report says it's about State Department, "Despondent Biden administration officials are mulling how to protect their national security priorities. Discussions about what to do next come as Trump's defeat of Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  on Tuesday sent waves of fear through federal workers."\par \par \uc2 And, Howie, "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " has similar reporting about the \uc2 EPA\uc2 . It's a lot of doom and gloom.\par \par \uc2 HOWARD KURTZ, FOX NEWS HOST: Yes. Well, good luck with that, because outgoing administration tries to put tax in front of the incoming tank. And it rarely works because the new president has sweeping powers to come in and reverse. We saw this when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  came in. We saw this in Trump's first term.\par \par \uc2 But I do want to emphasize, because I think the House, by whatever margin, will go Republican, they will do basically what President Trump wants because of his sweeping mandate and it is a mandate.\par \par \uc2 But it's more fun being in a minority. What happens is, anything that happens that screws up is your fault. You can't just kick Kamala around anymore. And so you're left with competing factions within your own party. So I think the tax cuts are too expensive, that kind of thing. So it is -- it's nice to win, but then you've got to deliver.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and Mollie, in the interim, we've got this lame duck session where there's a lot of stuff they want to get done. We're going to run out of government funding again just to warn everybody before Christmas.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Republicans are going to have a lot to deal with once they are in control. And presuming that they do take the House. This will be the first time that's happened since the first Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 The first Trump administration saw resistance, not just from unelected, unaccountable bureaucrats in these federal agencies, they also saw resistance from fellow Republicans. And even you saw \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  earlier this year claim he was going to fight Republicans more than he was Democrats.\par \par \uc2 I think with this election, what we've seen is there's zero appetite for this type of resistance from Republican establishment figures or those unelected, unaccountable bureaucrats. And a lot is expected of Republicans. And there's a lot to do to save the country. The people voted overwhelmingly for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and so and other Republicans and so a lot will be expected of.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and there's a lot of hand-wringing about how that voting went. Marie, we were together on election night for eight hours of this.\par \par \uc2 MARIE HARF, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: We were.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: "Axios" says this about the finger-pointing. One person involved with Harris' team told "Axios" the 107-day Harris campaign was nearly flawless. The Biden campaign that preceded it was the opposite. Well, former Biden staffer dismissed the Harris team's criticism as making excuses for the vice president's failures. Quote, how did you spend a billion dollars and not win? What the bleep?\par \par \uc2 HARF: Yes. The Democratic circular firing squad is churning out on all cylinders today.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  never should have run. And his administration should have told a more compelling story about the things they did to lift up the middle class, to bring back jobs, including manufacturing jobs.\par \par \uc2 I think \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  ran a very good campaign, given the limited amount of time, and given that there is globally this post-pandemic, post-inflation sort of anti-incumbent sentiment that is sweeping around, around the country, around the world today.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are also wrestling with the fact that in many states that went red, voters voted for an increase in the minimum wage to protect abortion rights. That just happened in a state like Missouri that is reliably red. So they're voting for some of the core tenets of the Democratic message, but not for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Democrats also held onto Senate seats in Michigan and Wisconsin and Arizona and Nevada. So we're also trying to figure out, is Trump singular, right? Is he just a cultural phenomenon that tapped into this incredible anger at the current administration where \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  could not run as a popular incumbent because the administration was not popular? And she couldn't run as an outsider because she works at 1600 Pennsylvania Avenue.\par \par \uc2 So the Obama coalition is over, it's done. And Democrats have to figure out how to rebuild the next new coalition in a way that we did after 2004 when \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  beat \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  and then we got \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 . That's our work we have to do now.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and there are questions about who the leader is going to be --\par \par \uc2 HARF: That's right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- for the party moving forward.\par \par \uc2 HARF: That's right.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: There's a lot of job vacancies right now. No, I just wanted to briefly add that for all of the journalists, and I've never seen such tilted coverage towards one candidate, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who told us that it was a great campaign and she -- and look, maybe she -- nothing she could have done, given the magnitude of Trump's appeal to more black Latino candidates as one example.\par \par \uc2 Suddenly, as soon as it was over, they said, oh, it was such a muddled campaign by Kamala. She makes some mistakes. And that suggest that they knew this all along and we're covering for her because it turned on a dime.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and it makes you think about the fact that they weren't being fully honest with us potentially about President Biden either and how he was doing --\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Exactly. Parallel them.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- and that led to him running again.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: The media were wrong about basically everything this entire campaign season and they have really lost their ability to drag a candidate over the finish line.\par \par \uc2 But there are these really big issues with the Republican Party and the Democrat Party. The Republican Party, we were told, that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was damaging to that brand, that he was going to destroy the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 Instead, he has delivered the biggest Electoral College victory since the '80s, since the Reagan era. And he has also done something that the Republicans had really wanted, which was to create a multi-racial, multiethnic, really broadly appealing party.\par \par \uc2 So that's all happened, but also the Democrat Party is in a really bad situation. They -- if they want to moderate, they might be able to have a way going forward, but voters clearly thought their views, whether it was on the radical trans agenda, their lack of concern for the economic situation, it was just way too radical. So if they need to improve going forward, they're going to have to moderate quite a bit.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Jeff, I want to bring you in on the -- part of this about the legal cases with President Trump, because part of the blame game that is going on are those who say, he should be convicted and already in jail. And if you guys had done a good job on that, then he wouldn't have been on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 "POLITICO" Magazine has a piece there, blaming the attorney general saying, "Trump got away with it because of the Biden administration's massive missteps."\par \par \uc2 It is now clearer than ever that Garland was a highly questionable choice to serve as attorney general from the start. A lot of folks did not feel that way at the time. From the outset of the Biden presidency, it was readily apparent that Garland had little desire to investigate and potentially prosecute Trump.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, the signals this week that the federal cases against him are going away.\par \par \uc2 MASON: I think you'll be hard-pressed to find any Democrats in town who will be praising \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  in the coming weeks and months.\par \par \uc2 And I think that applies to people at the White House as well. So certainly, there's some consternation with him. There's consternation in that article, at least with people who believe this is how it should have gone with \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and Republicans for not having voted to impeach or to convict former President Trump, then former President Trump, in his second impeachment, because that would have prevented him from being able to run again.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: But this --\par \par \uc2 MASON: Of course, the alternate view, which I'm sure Mollie's about to jump in on, is saying is all those charges weren't fair in the first place.\par \par \uc2 It doesn't matter. He won, and those cases are going to go away with the perhaps exception of the case in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: There's some states things. Right.\par \par \uc2 MASON: Right. But in general, Democrats, I think it's fair to say, are going to be frustrated that a democratic administration didn't move faster.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: The abuse of law was one of these underrated issues that motivated a lot of people to vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Having Democrat prosecutors up and down the Eastern seaboard trying to imprison their political opponent, bankrupt their political opponent, remove him from the ballot.\par \par \uc2 HARF: And he committed a bunch of crimes, Mollie.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: This was viewed as an assault on democracy. At the same time, they were trying to push that message.\par \par \uc2 HARF: This is the problem though.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: And people are hoping that they will have rule of law going forward and no more of these political prosecutions designed to go after, not just \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but people who were challenging school boards, pro- life activists. This was the underrated story because our media are so in favor of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and the Democrat Party, in general. They have failed to tell that story accurately.\par \par \uc2 HARF: The reality is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was credibly accused of dozens of felony charges, was convicted of many of them, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has stood on the campaign trail, this campaign cycle, and said he wants to go after his political opponents and put them in jail for --\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: In fact, he was talking about --\par \par \uc2 HARF: Mollie, let me finish.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: -- the threat posed by the -- this abuse of rule of law.\par \par \uc2 HARF: Mollie, let me finish. I let you -- I let you talk a lot here.\par \par \uc2 My point is that for Democrats and Americans who look at the litany of charges \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been charged with, some of which we saw him do with our own eyes, the fact that you can be elected president and all of that criminality and possible convictions just goes away and you are held above the law, to many Americans today, that feels deeply unfair about our system.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: The idea was that the way the Democrat Party treated it was as if he and other Republicans were below the law. And that period is over and a lot of people are happy about it.\par \par \uc2 HARF: (INAUDIBLE) so congrats.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I agree that I think it was an issue for a lot of people that connected and that maybe was, you know, it's not showing up on our list where economy, abortion --\par \par \uc2 HARF: Right. That's right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- immigration, but I do think for a lot of people that was a big issue.\par \par \uc2 OK. Stay with us because we're going to talk about a lot more of this.\par \par \uc2 The media praising Vice President Harris' quote, flawless campaign, as Democrats do their post-election autopsy. Is the media due for an autopsy too? We'll discuss that straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  ran basically a flawless campaign.\par \par \uc2 FORMER REP. DAVID JOLLY (R-FL), MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: I think because she ran an incredible campaign, it's been very telling about who we are, because her campaign did focus on the threat to democracy.\par \par \uc2 SUNNY HOSTIN, CO-HOST, "THE VIEW": She did - she ran a flawless campaign. And I maintain that.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 , "WASHINGTON POST" COLUMNIST: I cannot use her failed run in 2019 and hold her - against her after watching this incredible camping she's run now.\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: This really was an historic, flawlessly run campaign. She had - Queen Latifa never endorses anyone.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: She did this time.\par \par \uc2 OK, many in the media praising Vice President Harris', quote, "flawless campaign," despite the fact that it ended in an electoral landslide for her opponent in an election year when it seems podcasts sometimes leapfrogged over traditional media outlets.\par \par \uc2 We are back now. Luckily, we've got our own expert.\par \par \uc2 So, Howie, I mean, how much of this was clearly a lot of mainstream media outlets, Harris was their favorite candidate. We saw that in a number of different ways. Is it time for the media to do a little bit of self- reflection as Democrats are doing that now too.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Yes, but don't hold your breath. Soul-searching is not the media's strong suit and they were already going on to, oh, like, who's going to be in the cabinet.\par \par \uc2 And I guessed Kamala's campaign was a little - a smidgen less than flawless now that we look at it. But look, this is the most massive media failure of the century. Just a humiliating debacle on so many levels. Not blowing the forecast of the campaign, but misjudging the country, misjudging the people - that there would be a majority of people, including minorities, including even people in urban areas who would support now President-elect Trump.\par \par \uc2 And I think if you look at it in that sense, there's really very little good to say about the fact that they were largely in a bubble. Look, there are always exceptions. Didn't understand why people would support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Just viewed him as, you know, a danger to democracy and all of that.\par \par \uc2 And then finally Democrats, at least those who were not in their therapist's office right now, are mouthing the right words, well, maybe we moved too far left, why did we alienate our working class base, but actually carrying that out is very difficult when you're leaderless and when you're kind of shell shocked and when you're still tied in my view, to identity politics.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, something "The New Yorker" wrote about this. This is how they view this vote. It says, "America has now twice elected him," President Trump, "as its president. It is a disastrous revelation about what the United States really is."\par \par \uc2 So, Mollie, they're pointing the picture - the finger back at Americans.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Well, it's a good example of the kind of content that a lot of people in corporate media have toward average Americans. They clearly do not understand the country that they proport to represent. You're seeing actually less self-refection this time than we saw in 2016. And you might remember that in 2016 they went on to create and participate in the Russian collusion hoax and to have - have these elaborate conspiracy theories about how Republicans were able to take control of the presidency that year.\par \par \uc2 So, there is much that needs to be happening with the media. But the most important thing probably this cycle is how unimportant they became relative to podcasters. You had \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  not even appeared on "60 Minutes" because of their corruption and how biased they are. And they also had that situation where they didn't release the unattended transcript and video of their 45-minute interview with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . We still don't know what they're hiding. Instead, the Trump campaign effectively used podcasts to reach out to a different audience than the one served by corporate media.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Three hours with Joe Rogan. That counts for a lot.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I mean - it does. Especially when one candidate does it and the other, for whatever reason, they can't reach an agreement and that doesn't happen.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: And the other - right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , early in the hours of November 6th, when it looked like President Trump had locked this thing in, he puts on X, you are the media now.\par \par \uc2 Jeff, as a member of the media, how do you take that?\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: You've been drafted.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I mean he wants to talk about democratizing free speech. He has a lot of critics who say what is happening on X includes all kinds of things that are not so palatable to people.\par \par \uc2 MASON: Well, I mean, I think that's a shift we've been seen for years now is that everyone is a journalist. Anyone with an iPhone can take video. Anyone with a - with a social media account can put out their own views, and for that matter their own reporting. I don't think, obviously, as a journalist, that that means we are moving into irrelevance. I think we've got a really big job. We had a - we've had a big job for a long time. But we're competing with a lot more different people than we used to. I mean when I first joined Reuters more than two decades ago -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Youngster.\par \par \uc2 MASON: The wire services were the first ones to get everything out. I mean we weren't - we didn't even feel like we were competing with newspapers then. And, good grief, that's changed entirely.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, he's not wrong that everyone out there with an X account, former \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account, and other social media has a way to get stuff out the way reporters do.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: But the world's richest man has turned X into a pro-Trump platform.\par \par \uc2 HARF: Absolutely. No doubt.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Obviously that makes a lot of people unhappy (ph).\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: It's actually more important that it's a free-speech platform.\par \par \uc2 HARF: But it's not. It's not.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: And it's the only social media platform that is for free speech, where people can debate and enter and they've got community notes (ph) where you can fact-check people.\par \par \uc2 HARF: That is factually inaccurate, my friend, Mollie Hemingway. He has turned it into a pro-Trump platform where people on the other side are being taken - their account - their accounts are being suspended, their posters are being taken down. He is now using it in the way that Republicans used to accuse Democrats of using social media.\par \par \uc2 And, look, as someone who's worked on campaigns and who's thinking about how campaigns should deal with the media going forward, to sort of what we were talking about, about podcasts, right, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  actually was talking about things that help the middle class. She was talking about housing. She was talking about childcare tax credits. But she wasn't doing it in the - as Mollie rightly, I agree with this, the bro podcasts, the - reaching people where they are, trying to get to voters, not just by traditional door knocking. They knocked on so many doors in Pennsylvania the weekend before the election that by traditional -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But something didn't connect.\par \par \uc2 HARF: Exactly, by traditional campaign standards, it didn't work, right? The things that when I worked for \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 's campaign, the things that worked aren't working anymore. And that's organizing, that's how much money you raise, that's - that's what media you engage with.\par \par \uc2 And so, for Democrats, look, after 2004 we never - we were so - we felt the same way we do today. It's a better comparison than `16. \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  won a second term when we knew what he was like as president. And so -\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: (INAUDIBLE) -\par \par \uc2 HARF: And we never - let me finish. And when we - and when we got to 2008, nobody would have predicted four years earlier that \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  would win states like India.\par \par \uc2 So, a lot can change in four years.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Oh, yes, it can.\par \par \uc2 HARF: Democrats have a lot of work to do. We have lots of young leaders in our party, Wes Moore, Josh Shapiro, \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2 . We're going to be fine. But we have to address this question of how we engage with voters and how we reach them and through what media platforms.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: OK. So, while that is not true that free speech is necessarily pro-Trump, although Republicans do seem to be better on speech than Democrats right now.\par \par \uc2 HARF: Oh, that's funny. It's amazing.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: It is a big situation that social media continues to engage in censorship, sometimes with the help of the government through this censorship industrial complex. We're seeing it - we're seeing it through -\par \par \uc2 HARF: \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  was doing it to liberals, Mollie. Does that bother you?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: And \uc2 Google\uc2  -\par \par \uc2 HARF: Does that bother you when \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  does that to liberals?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Yes, go ahead and answer.\par \par \uc2 HARF: Does it bother you?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: We're seeing \uc2 Google\uc2 , in particular, doing a lot to suppress news -\par \par \uc2 HARF: Interesting non-answer.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Oh, my goodness. To suppress news and information as it relates to all the issues that people vote on. And so censorship is going to be a major issue going forward. And hopefully other social media platforms will also engage in more free speech and debate.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And there's litigation involving a lot of this too -\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: There is.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That is going to flush out a lot of these conversations.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: That's right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I thought this piece in "The Federalist" was very interesting, saying, "Culture did a way better job of signaling Trump's wins than broke polls." It talked about the success of "Maverick: Top Gun" and this rise of anti-woke comedians and podcasts. And the - and the author of this piece, Mollie, for you guys, said, you know, they could have sensed it through the culture. Did we miss that?\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: To a large extent, although when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was clever enough to go to \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 , and those images, I mean he sort of won the day in that politics is often about images.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Totally.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Got the apron on. Scooping out the fries.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Yes. But, you know, I'm not as optimistic as Jeff Mason. I think the media have lost an enormous amount of credibility. Ratings are in the toilet. Distrusted by both sides. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  didn't love the media. He didn't like giving interviews either, and that was part of his problem with his presidency.\par \par \uc2 MASON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: But I saw - I think there was a bubble there. And I - the soul- searching you mentioned, I'd like to see more of it because podcasts have taken over. They're more fun. They're, obviously, often partisan. And we're seen as the dull dinosaurs.\par \par \uc2 MASON: I think they will -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Who's got a podcast at this table?\par \par \uc2 MASON: I was just going to say, I think there will be some soul searching amongst Democrats as well.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Everybody's got a podcast.\par \par \uc2 MASON: "Reuters' broke the story - I broke this story with a couple colleagues that - that they were talking to Joe Rogan but then it didn't happen. And I spoke to at least one aide in her camp afterwards who said, we should have done that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, there will be a lot of should haves as people look at the - everything in the wake of this. And as we still wait on the House and see where that dust settles as well.\par \par \uc2 Panel, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 By the way, a very special way to salute our veterans as the country prepares to honor our service members, that's up next. How you can get involved.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, we here at Fox believe all of our veterans deserve to live with dignity and independence. So, we want you to join us and Make Camo Your Cause, in support of U.S. Vets campaign which aims to eradicate veteran homelessness. You can visit usvets.org/foxforward. You can donate, find some cool camo gear. You can also post your camo pics on social media with the hashtag honorusvets. We look forward to seeing you're pictures. You can share your thanks there as well. You can also pick up some great swag modeled by all of us. It includes ties, mugs, shirts and hats, and it's all for our veterans as we thank them every day, but tomorrow especially.\par \par \uc2 Just a quick note, my podcast "Livin' the Bream" drops today. This week I talked with Pastor Darren Whitehead about all the heated emotions surrounding the election, how you can make peace now with the people who didn't vote the way that you did. "Livin' the Bream" wherever you like to get your podcasts.\par \par \uc2 That's it for today. Thanks for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a wonderful week and we'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020241110ekba00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Trump Completes Historic Political Comeback\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Charlie Hurt, Tammy Bruce, Jeanine Pirro, Jimmy  Failla \par \uc2 7,772 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 November 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: And welcome to "Hannity" this Friday night.\par \par \uc2 President-elect \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  will be sworn in on January the 20th, 2025, but tonight, just days after winning a landslide victory, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is already seeing big policy wins both at home and abroad. We're going to explain that in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 Plus, another big win for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in court. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's weaponized DOJ has vacated all remaining deadlines in the bogus case against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Now, for the first time, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will take office without a federal witch hunt hopefully hovering over the administration.\par \par \uc2 Let's see how long that lasts before Democrats and Jamie Raskin and the state-run media mob want him impeached or imprisoned again. But the president-elect now has a clear mandate to govern this country. He won the Electoral College in a landslide. He won the popular vote by millions and millions of votes to spare.\par \par \uc2 And you might have noticed, the Democrats -- they are not taking it very well. We've gone from the denial stage, the shock stage into the anger stage.\par \par \uc2 On TikTok, self-proclaimed feminists, well, they are shaving their heads. I don't know why, I assume out of anger. They're vowing to punish Trump's supporters by boycotting sex and procreation and relationships and marriage.\par \par \uc2 This is something they're calling the 4B movement. I can't make this up. Viewer warning, what you're about to see is pretty disturbing. I didn't make it up. I promise. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLKIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Stop talking to men, divorce your husbands, leave your (EXPLETIVE DELETED) boyfriends, leave them. They don't give (EXPLETIVE DELETED) about you and I promise you come over to this side, I will bake cookies, I will shave your head.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There's no way I am letting any man near me for the next few years. I've been happily single for the past four years. I can wait another four years.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: But I also have decided that for the next four years I am going to abstain from schmecks with men.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It doesn't matter if you -- if you take away abortion like, we'll want to have your babies even less, right? Even if you take away contraception like women are really good at celibacy because you guys are actually a threat to us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You honestly can't make it up. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not going to have a national abortion ban. He's not going to stop IVF, and he's not going to limit access to contraception. As it turns out, many Democrats have been drinking that, you know, that Kool-Aid and actually believing all the lies, all the hysteria coming from the state-run media mob and Democrats. They believe the lies.\par \par \uc2 Now, some are even reportedly stockpiling abortion pills and hormones, which will still be available after Trump has sworn in. There is no shortage of them.\par \par \uc2 And meanwhile prestigious Ivy League schools, they are postponing exams. They are cancelling classes. They're providing students with crayons and Legos and cookies and milky you know to cope with Trump's election win.\par \par \uc2 This is madness. What happened to the land of the free, the home of the brave? We're now, what, we're now the home of -- you know, comfort pets and the home of woke, you know, lunatics that need comfort? I -- it's bizarre.\par \par \uc2 New Jersey's Governor \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2  has dramatically pledged to fight to the death against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's policies. The far-left "\uc2 Guardian\uc2 " newspaper is offering therapy to devastated staff members.\par \par \uc2 And last night over at "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ", a columnist, MSDNC contributor named Jennifer Rubin posting on X, quote: It is 1933. Hitler is a power no time for a bleeping seminar on Democrats messaging errors. Talk about snowflakes.\par \par \uc2 If anyone still wonders why "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " and all of these legacy media people have lost all credibility, nobody trust them, take a look at Jennifer Rubin.\par \par \uc2 But tonight, it is official. The Democrats are definitely in disarray. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has frankly, he has won and he has broken them, maybe beyond repair.\par \par \uc2 Now, turn on any left leading outlet, the chaos, the infighting, the hysteria through the roof. You decide. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETE DOMINICK, COMEDIAN: I'm sorry but \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  hates veterans. He called them suckers and losers. He did he absolutely despised them, he has his whole life he has his entire life.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I know you're super emotional. I just --\par \par \uc2 DOMINICK: Of course, I am. I'm terrified.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm telling --\par \par \uc2 JOE SCARBOROUGH, MSNBC HOST: Democrats never got it, that while we were looking at all of the crazy things \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was saying on the campaign, all of the frightening things \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was saying on the campaign trail, they were looking at their wallet. They were looking what groceries cost, what gas costs, what rent costs.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are being branded as the most extreme of us. It is not only politically problematic as we just saw because none of this stuff helped the other day, without a doubt, it's a problem. But we need to take stock of why we are being held hostage to the far left.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: In my view, there was an over listening to and an over lifting up of people who left Trump, not people who left the Democratic Party. The people who left the Democratic Party are the people who are going to, you know, win in the future. The people who left Trump, the never Trumpers who have important voices and have, that is not the winning coalition.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Liberal Joe, can you go back and maybe watch your own show and listen to the things you were saying. You're as guilty of the very thing you're saying the liberals and Democrats are guilty of.\par \par \uc2 Now, the co-host of that hard-hitting news show "The View", they're having a very hard time processing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's win as they try to figure out just how many racists are lurking in the shadows. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The bigger question should be, yes, Sunny, why did they vote for him?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: In sweeping --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We need to be introspective.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No, we need to be introspective. If we voted for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , we need to say, what didn't resonate with the voters? You know what didn't resonate with the voters when they were saying we don't feel safe and the left focus on defund the police and bail reform.\par \par \uc2 They also denied the border was a crisis and kept saying no, no, no, it's fine. This is not a --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There was a border bill though. There was a border bill.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: But, Joy, my point is they screamed and screamed and screamed they didn't vote for him because he's a racist or a misogynist. They voted because they needed help in their everyday lives. The majority of this country because every stat coming out on the people willing to self-reflect are showing these --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Oh, wait, they're going to say, I'm a racist and a misogynist in the exit poll, I don't think so.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You really think 74 million people are racist.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Hey, guys, no excuses this time. They lost everything.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There's still half of the country who doesn't agree with him.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The environment to be ravaged, it's children to be shot, it's wealth to be hoarded, it's workers to be exploited, it's poor to starve, it's cops to murder.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So, you think America is the problem?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The country in prob -- in trouble.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: If we played any more of that, I'd have to give free extra strength Excedrin to every one of you out there watching. Believe it or not, it actually gets worse.\par \par \uc2 According to reports, Biden's staff and Kamala's staff, they've all turned on on each other, blaming each other, making for a very awkward last few months.\par \par \uc2 And meanwhile, former New York Mayor \uc2 Michael Bloomberg\uc2  just wrote an op-ed criticizing Democrats for hiding \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's mental decline.\par \par \uc2 I have a question for the former mayor. Mr. Bloomberg, we on this program were reporting on this in 2020 before Joe went to Washington. Don't you think you're a little late, just a little?\par \par \uc2 And get this apparently, Democrats are now, you know, sparring over whether you know the leftwing Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  should be forced out of office off the Supreme Court before Trump is sworn in so Biden can pick a replacement and some are even floating \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  for that position. Can't make this up either. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I hope that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  makes the next weeks as consequential as he can um I don't care about drawing outside the lines what Republicans may think about it this is within your purview. You can actually do it and you should do it.\par \par \uc2 And you know, one more thing, John, is you have a hell of a vice president right there who has a legal pedigree to sit on the Supreme Court and let Republicans go crazy and -- I'm even mentioning that option.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you floating -- are you floating this? You know, 7:39 a.m. on the East Coast, did Bakari Sellers just float Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  as a potential Supreme Court nominee.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Not only am I floating it, but I want to stir up everything. I want people's heads to explode this morning so we go into the weekend just knowing that the chaos has not ended just yet.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I have a sad prediction. This is not only not going to stop, it's going to get worse. The panic for them is real. It is not a good time to be on the left, and whether they realize it or not, the Democratic Party, the deep state, Washington bureaucracy, the state run media mob are all facing a reckoning, as President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  can sit back, watch them implode without any need for retribution which they claim he's going to be doing.\par \par \uc2 He's not. The American people no longer care what legacy media, what Hollywood, what Obama, what celebrities have to say. Why? Because they have zero influence.\par \par \uc2 Legacy media, celebrity influence is completely dead otherwise this election would have turned out very differently because they spoke in one voice, they told the same lies, they had the same hysteria, the same propaganda, misinformation, the same smearing, lying, besmirchment.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's ultimate revenge will be success for every American, slashing regulations, decentralizing the federal government, cutting out waste, fraud and abuse, flushing out corrupt actors. They have to go people that are weaponized and destroyed the greatest law enforcement agency the world has ever known, the greatest intel community the world has ever known. They got to be cleaned up.\par \par \uc2 Ending partisanship, ending a weaponized DOJ, ending lawfare ending woke DEI policies, fixing the crisis created by the Biden administration, securing our border, bringing down the cost of living for every American, and driving up energy production, stop forcing us to get a certain stove and refrigerator and putting a car in our driveway that maybe people want or don't want. If you want, go buy a \uc2 Tesla\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Peace through strength will be brought back. That will help bring stability to the world and in just a little over two months, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can finally hit the ground running.\par \par \uc2 Here with reaction FOX News contributors Charlie Hurt and Tammy Bruce.\par \par \uc2 Tammy, let's get your overall take.\par \par \uc2 TAMMY BRUCE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Look, you've got a great head of hair. We don't want you shaving your head. Thank God that never happened. The good news is, I -- you know, at least with what the women are doing it's going to save men and some women a lot of time because now you can see them coming, right? They're going to have the shaved heads, they're going to wear their t-shirts and everything will be purple. You just don't know what's going on.\par \par \uc2 But this is about them making it about themselves. They -- what they -- it's really an existential identity crisis because -- and there's a realization with these number, Sean, with this popular vote that no, not everyone in the country is an evil monster, and they had pretended that that was the case, and now there's nowhere to go, they don't know what to think and God forbid should they think that they were wrong.\par \par \uc2 And you know, I'm a little troubled because these are people who continued with the awful rhetoric, despite the fact that President Trump had already been shot in the face. The nature of what they were willing to do to the country we cannot forget, but the way to rectify it is in fact to change what's happened in Washington, deal with the agencies, deal with yes, the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and the -- you know, the intelligence community, and make it impossible to go forward again and do all of this because it's attracted people who believe that they are allowed to do this.\par \par \uc2 So I think the American people in watching what it's the leftist elite, right? The response was that the celebrities were as disconnected and out of touch with our lives the average person's life as everyone else in Washington and we understood at that point that we really did not matter to them in any way, shape or form. So this is now -- it was a statement that we've really never seen before that Trump was blunt, he described everything he wanted to do and the country screamed, yes, and that now is undeniable.\par \par \uc2 We must maintain that enthusiasm. I know President Trump and his team understand that they've got only four years that must be a bullet train on all the issues. Congress must it -- we not back to the same games, we already have -- you know, the international scene to some degree, \uc2 NATO\uc2 , yes, you know, New York, people are already adjusting themselves. One group of migrants coming up to the border has broken up.\par \par \uc2 So, it's -- you know, what it is, Sean, daddy's home. Daddy is home and everybody else is Bueller thinking they've got to get their act together and they do.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Wait a minute, I thought America was going to vote for a mamala. Was Drew Barrymore wrong?\par \par \uc2 BRUCE: No, no, yeah, look, you know who voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? Moms, that's where the mamalas were. It was the average woman in this country, all ethnicities, all, you know, sexual orientations, everybody who's got a family to worry about, to care for. There were millions of mamalas who said, daddy's coming back, and that's what they've wanted, and that's what they're going to get, and it's going to be glorious.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I think it's kind of simple. I think people want our border secure. They want the cartels gone, the murderers, the rapists, the other violent criminals, the gang members gone. I think they want lower prices when they fill up their tank, lower prices at the grocery store. I think they would like law and order and safety and security, and I think they'd like the world to be a safer place, Charlie Hurt. I really don't think it's that complicated.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE HURT, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yeah, and I think that -- I think the people that want exactly what you just said are more than just the people who voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  because the entire Democrat campaign was -- based on hatred of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  but the real problem and I don't hear any Democrats grappling with the real problem they face, and it's not just that they just lost this election and they lost the popular vote. It's that if you took a like a blind taste test and gave it to all the Democrats who voted for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  because they hated \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And just put those issues you just listed in front of them, again, a blind taste test, you remove \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's name from it and you offered them the agenda you just laid out, and the agenda that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ran on, the same agenda he ran on in 2016, you would probably pick up about 40 percent of Democrat voters because you're exactly right what they want is very basic simple stuff.\par \par \uc2 And this agenda, it's not a conservative agenda. It's a common sense agenda and that's why when -- you know, the real troubling thing for Democrats is not just that there was this wave of red states on the map right now, and the fact that the entire blue wall turned red, the real problem for them is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  swung places like California and New York 12 points in his direction despite their campaign of hatred calling him Hitler and all this stuff, and across the entire country.\par \par \uc2 You can't find -- I think there might be one or two states that didn't move dramatically towards Trump and it's all based on those issues. They have -- they lost the election but they lost on the issues 10 times worse than they did on the election, and I don't hear anyone with the exception of what \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  said in that montage. By the way, you owe me and you owe Americans like a free drink for having to listen to that entire montage, but the only person in it who sounded like she was sort of realizing what's going on was \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BRUCE: Too late.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: "The View" was particularly rough. I'm not going to lie.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Tammy, Charlie, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 All right. We now turn and take a closer look at how so many of America's major polling organizations got it wrong so often.\par \par \uc2 Remember, Des Moines, Iowa, state -- "The Des Moines Register" state poll, yeah, they were off by points in Kamala's favor. Oh, it was being touted as, oh, she's one of the most trusted pollsters. NPR, Marist, had Kamala winning the popular vote by four points, a seven-point failure. Marist showing Kamala winning by two in Pennsylvania, a four-point spread, Kamala's favor.\par \par \uc2 Frankly, most polling companies should be ashamed of themselves, that they got it wrong again. Two guys that got it right in `16 and `20 and `2024 were our pollsters Matt Towery and Robert Cahaly. Take a look at your screen.\par \par \uc2 Matt Towery, Insider Advantage, deadly accurate. A polling difference of around one point or less in five key battleground states. Meanwhile, Robert Cahaly, Trafalgar, was also spot on four battleground states off by a single point or less. Well done.\par \par \uc2 Robert Cahaly, Matt Towery join us now with more.\par \par \uc2 Matt, you guys got it right three times in a row, the presidential race. Everybody was kind of off in 2022, but it was post-COVID, post-Roe v. Wade, it was kind of a hard year to poll. But you got the last three presidential elections dead on accurate. And my -- and I remember Nate Silver and we put it up the last time you were on actually appraised you guys but he doesn't mention you guys anymore. Why is that?\par \par \uc2 MATT TOWERY, POLLSTER: Well -- well, first of all, Hannity, I've known you since 1992 and I want to thank you for sticking with me and with Robert. Not only you're a dear friend, but we been a great colleague through all this stuff.\par \par \uc2 But we've been treated like the garbage men of pollsters. When they write about the polling from these various, you know, expert groups they say, the quality "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " poll, the quality Ann Selzer poll, we're never considered the quality. Decision desks won't use our polling because it's not up to their standards.\par \par \uc2 Well, guess what? Three times in a row, we've gotten this thing closer than anybody else and Robert has done a fantastic job. But the one thing I want to mention is, we caught the turnaround in this race after the second assassination attempt of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and that was never noted by anyone. We talked about it on your show over and over again, that's when the turnaround started.\par \par \uc2 And so, people I read today -- well, nobody got the trend right, that Trump was going to win. We started talking about that trend a month and a half ago. And so, it wasn't hard to see.\par \par \uc2 Now, it's hard to poll and I think Nate said we were herders because we were all sticking to like one or two points. Well, Pennsylvania was basically one, one and a half points. So if you're right, you're right. But we knew who was going to win and we said -- we told it to your own viewers just the other night. We told them where we thought this race was headed, and Robert did a fantastic job.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Robert, what's your take on it?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT CAHALY, POLLSTER: Well, I mean, the first thing, I'm still trying to find these hidden Kamala voters. I don't -- I don't know where they are, maybe they're confused about where the polls are, maybe they next week --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: But Matt's going to be looking for a golf ball at a very famous golf course soon and he's not going to be able to find it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TOWERY: Oh, no, no.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I'm sorry, Robert. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 CAHALY: But the fact is, no, the fact is -- is this -- this -- you know, they have all this polling orthodoxy and their -- their little clubs and all the little groups and there's no accountability. And the fact is nothing is going to change, they're going to still call these the quality pollsters. They're going to still do everything different because I've always said you there a poll has one of two purposes, it is designed to reflect the electorate or it is designed to affect the electorate.\par \par \uc2 And you look at what that Ann Selzer poll did at the last minute. It gave the whole country, all the Trump people heartburn. It gave all the other side they got so excited, and I mean, even the prognosticators did a tick up for Harris in the last day. It was all nonsense based on something else.\par \par \uc2 That was not reflecting the electorate. That was designed to affect it and until they're outed for this, their error rates prove their wrong and prove their purpose. A rate is how we're judged and they will fail.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I want to thank you both, congratulate you both. That is three presidential elections in a low -- in a row. Maybe Nate Silver should do an article about Matt Towery, Insider Advantage, Trafalgar, Robert Cahaly were right again. Don't hold your breath though guys.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Matt, I wish you the best of luck. Robert, I wish you the best of luck finding the hidden Kamala voters and, Matt, good luck finding that golf ball, or maybe the third or fourth one.\par \par \uc2 TOWERY: Thanks, Hannity, you really helped me out on that.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You might have to go swimming for it.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, the left's lawfare against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  crumbles before their very eyes. The great Judge Jeanine Pirro is next.\par \par \uc2 And Joe Rogan urges President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  not to attack his critics in this his second term. I'll tell you my take and much more. Thanks for being with us this Friday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (NEWSBREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. The left wing, their lawfare against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is crumbling. The judge in the politically motivated D.C. election interference case paused all proceedings as the special counsel \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2  weighs the future of his case and \uc2 House Judiciary Committee\uc2  Chairman Jim Jordan, he's demanding that \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2  preserve all records related to his Trump prosecutions.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Joe Rogan is urging \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  not to attack his critics in his second term and let them talk, you know, all their bleep. He's not wrong. While urging Trump to unite the country after his landslide victory on Tuesday which is what he did on Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 According to a new report, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  brass are, quote, stunned and shell- shocked over \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's win. And unable to stop the fear-mongering, all former intelligence officials are now claiming they are worried that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will try to politicize and weaponize the CIA. That's rich.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, here's my take: throughout the campaign, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  repeatedly said, my revenge will be all of our success. Let's be successful.\par \par \uc2 And sticking to that I think will be great for the country. He is currently position for great success. He will secure the border. He intends to wipe out the deep state, kick out political operatives in these agencies, lessen the bureaucracy, the size, the scope, the influence of government, have more freedom for American -- the American people. But he also should resist any call temptation to get back at people that abuse their power to go after him.\par \par \uc2 Would you want him to do what they did to him? \uc2 Eric Trump\uc2  actually told "The Daily Mail" that his father, quote, would want nothing to do with prosecuting \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  or \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . He doesn't give a damn. He wants these games to end.\par \par \uc2 During one of my interviews with President Trump, I asked him about this very question. He gave me an answer, I'll only be a dictator for one day, typical \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and that was used by Democrats, taken completely out of context.\par \par \uc2 So let's play the full question and answer and you decide.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: The media has been focused on this and attacking you under no circumstances. You are promising America tonight you would never abuse power as retribution against anybody.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT-ELECT OF THE UNITED STATES: Except for day one.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Except --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : He's going crazy. Except for day one.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Meaning?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I want to close the border and I want to drill, drill, drill.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That's not -- that's not retribution.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I'm going to be -- I'm going to be -- you know, he keeps -- we love this guy. He says you're not going to be a dictator, are you? I said, no, no, no, other than day one. We're closing the border and we're drilling, drilling, drilling. After that, I'm not a dictator.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That sounds to me like you're going back to the policies when you were president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: By the way, you could tell he was laughing.\par \par \uc2 Here with reaction, co-host of the hit show "The Five," our friend Judge Jeanine Pirro.\par \par \uc2 You know what's amazing, you know him well, we have both known him for 30, whatever many years, and you know, he was joking but he's saying, yes, day one, for one day, I'm going to fix the border, be energy independent.\par \par \uc2 Every Democrat, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , Obama, he says he's going to be a dictator. They were flat out lying, taking it out of context, but it didn't work, Judge, did it?\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, CO-HOST OF "THE FIVE": No, it didn't work because the American people are smarter than that.\par \par \uc2 Look, the left had nothing to run on other than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is this horrible human being. He's -- he's Hitler and he's -- he's -- you know, he's a fascist. But then when they started coming after the rest of us and said, well, we're MAGA extremists, and we're semi-fascists and we're garbage, and you know, we're deplorable, and we cling to god and guns. I do religiously and I'm thrilled.\par \par \uc2 Once the American people saw through that, they knew the president was right when he said, they're really coming for you. I'm just in the way and what the president has an opportunity to do is exactly what he did in 2016. Sean, I went back and looked at the president's acceptance speech in 2016, do you know how much time he spent congratulating \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  for her years of work for the American people that we owe a major debt of gratitude, and he went on and on. He never indicted her, he never went after her. That's not who he is, and when the left now is standing up and they're saying, we're going to resist President Trump, will you go right ahead and do that you fools?\par \par \uc2 Because this president won the popular vote, he's got a mandate from the electoral college, and this president has an obligation, Sean, to take down those institutions that have been politicized whether, it is the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , the intelligence agencies, the CIA, he's got an obligation to return them to the non-political nature that they are supposed to be. And that's what America wants. They don't want retribution.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn't do that kind of thing. He wants success. That's why we have \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 . He -- RFK is going to make us healthy again, and \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 , all of these people are about working for Americans.\par \par \uc2 And I am so sick of the left and all their nonsense and they -- the fact that they're not as self- introspective to realize they lost because they lied, they try to take him down politically, financially, emotionally, and they tried to kill him twice. I don't know if it was -- I don't know who it was.\par \par \uc2 But you know what? Enough. This president has earned the right to run this country the way the American people expect him to run it.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I can't say it any better but you're right about one thing, I would like to see the \uc2 FBI\uc2  return to its former greatness. I would like American intelligence agencies to be the envy of the world, not weaponized, not politicized, not -- no fake FISA warrants, no 51 intel officials.\par \par \uc2 I want the Department of Justice -- I would never want what has happened to him, abusing power the way they did, and weaponizing that department to ever be done to anybody ever again. That will mean that you have to clean out those agencies.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: He has to.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: These operatives that are political when they have a job to do which is to uphold the Constitution, the rule of law and that's all he -- he's -- that's all I want you want and we know he wants.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And my understanding is everyone in the \uc2 FBI\uc2  who is above a GS-14 level is scared to death, as well they should be. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  should not be going after parents and calling them domestic terrorists. They should not be going after Catholics and pro-lifers. Their job is not to indict a president of the United States through this Crossfire Hurricane and lied to courts the way they did with a \uc2 Jim Comey\uc2  unverified affidavits.\par \par \uc2 And then you've got the so-called intelligence community that lies and calls what is ultimately exhibit one in a United States case against \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . They call it Russian disinformation. They lied. We know they lied and it's time for this president to return America back to the institutions that our Founding Fathers believed in.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Judge, we'll see you down in Florida often. We appreciate you being on the show.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, Democrats are suddenly walking back their liberal policies ahead of a second Trump term. Huh, wonder why. We'll get reaction, \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2  is next, straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was elected president only a couple of days ago but his massive victory is already starting to improve things both here at home and abroad. Now New York city Mayor Eric Adams announced this week that the city will no longer be giving -- giving out these free preloaded debit cards to unvetted Harris-Biden illegal immigrants. Now that's after this the city spent more than $50 million on that program alone. They spent about $7 billion total on illegals.\par \par \uc2 Now, multiple Democrats have already publicly admitted the party should no longer support allowing biological men to compete in women's sports. The CEO of the fashion company Steve Madden announced plans to severely cut down on sourcing products from China, instead moving production to friendlier countries closer to the U.S., countries like Mexico and Brazil hopefully also home.\par \par \uc2 All of this happened in less than four days after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was projected the winner of the election and became the president-elect.\par \par \uc2 Now, imagine where we will be, you know, after four years of him back in office.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, she is the co-chair of President Trump's transition team, \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Will you think about going into the administration? I think you are a phenomenal asset in so many ways that people that I know, that people may not know, and I've got to imagine that there is a position in this administration for you. Are you interested?\par \par \uc2 TULSI GAB