 it.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: No, what's amazing about Kimmel getting offended so easily is he's a guy who wore black face on the man show. You'd think this guy -- I mean, really --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: He put a cucumber inside his pants and asked strangers to touch it, too.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Well, listen, I'm not going to judge --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That video --\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: I'm not going to judge him for that when I was -- I'm kidding, I'm kidding. But let me explain this to you, okay? The reason I don't --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: What am I going to do with you?\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Yeah, we're going to be fine. The reason I don't beg on Kimmel and Colbert is because if they didn't suck so bad, me and Gutfeld wouldn't be so successful, okay? That is the reality.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That's true. That's true actually.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: They had a 4--year head start in late night and then Greg came along and became the king of late night on the -- yes, on the weekends, I'm like the Rodney King of late night, you know? I get beat up a little bit but it's a good gig, everybody knows what's going on over here, so I'm not complaining.\par \par \uc2 So I want to thank them for sucking but they're failing their audience because comedy is supposed to make you feel better about life, not feel worse because half of the country doesn't agree with you. That's where Colbert's an embarrassment.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: It -- they're not funny and the official countdown clock to their cancellation has begun not because of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  it's because nobody watches them anymore and why? Because they're not funny. You on this segment were funnier than they have been combined in the last four years.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Oh, man, well, if you keep talking --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I mean, it's pathetic. They're not --\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: I know, but if you keep talking --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You and Greg are funny.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Yeah, we're doing great but if you keep -- if you keep complimenting me on the air, I'm going to have to give my drug dealer a raise. So let's rein it in a little bit. I can't.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: What -- what -- whatever. I don't know what I'm going to do. What I'm going to with you.\par \par \uc2 All right. Jimmy Failla, thank you.\par \par \uc2 More "Hannity" straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. On a personal note, I want to say thank you. Thank you to all of you that took the time to be informed voters, to go out, some of you didn't love voting early but you did it anyway. It made a big, big difference. Those of you that waited online for hours on election day, I really believe you made the difference, and I'm grateful to every one of you. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 All right. That's all the time we have left this evening. As always, thank you for being with us. Thank you for making this show possible.\par \par \uc2 Please set your DVR so you never, ever, ever, ever, ever miss an episode of "Hannity". News anytime, every time, all the time, foxnews.com, hannity.com.\par \par \uc2 And in the meantime, let not your heart be troubled. Greg Gutfeld standing by to put a big, big election smile win on your face. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document SHAN000020241109ekb800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Names Susie Wiles As Chief Of Staff; \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2  Unseats Bob Casey, Grows GOP Senate Majority; Democrats worry Trump will abandon Ukraine; The left puts Ukraine first, America last; Trump names campaign manager as White House Chief of Staff; Migrant caravan shrinks after Trump win; Southern border still at risk; Left still hoping Trump will face prison time; Voters rejected lawfare; Angry women swearing off men; Colleges offer services to distraught students; Kamala fumbled the Gen Z vote block\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Laura Ingraham \par \uc2 8,051 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 November 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: -- mass illegal immigrant deportation. What that looks like. Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 06:00 p.m. in the East, 03:00 p.m. on the West Coast. You can follow me on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  @bretbaier, exclusive behind the scenes, photos, videos. Had a good time up in New York for all the election stuff. Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this Special Report. Fair, balanced and still unafraid. The Ingraham Angle starts now.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening, everyone. I'm Laura Ingraham. This is The Ingraham Angle reporting from Washington tonight. Thank you, as always, for spending some time with us. Now this is a Fox News Alert. I guess \uc2 Mark Cuban\uc2  was on to something, because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is so threatened by strong, intelligent women that he just announced his first major staff pick, and it's his campaign chief, Susie Wiles.\par \par \uc2 Now, Susie Wiles will be the Chief of Staff of the White House. She's a political veteran, tough as nails, and this is something that you have to know about Susie. She's a rarity in politics. Why? She never wants credit. She just wants to get things done.\par \par \uc2 Now, one of her top conditions for taking the job, apparently, was that she wanted to be a real gatekeeper for people who can walk into the Oval Office, people who can't. And the quote is the clown car can't come into the White House at will, a source telling CNN. All right, that means gone is going to be the stream of informal advisors, friends, relatives. You know, we all have them, but in the White House, it gets very chaotic who had access to President Trump during his first term, and sometimes complicated the job for his chiefs of staff. So that will be a change. Now, Wiles kept the campaign really disciplined and focused on the road and to great success. So congrats to Susie.\par \par \uc2 And in more news tonight, a huge Senate pickup in Pennsylvania, where Republican \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2  has unseated incumbent Democrat Senator Bob Casey. Now, of course, Trump won the key battleground state by 2 percent, but many thought that Casey, with his last name, of course, iconic in the state, his father was governor, would be able to hold on. But no, it was not the case.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Pennsylvania Senator-elect, I love saying that, \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2 . Congrats, Dave, Senator-elect, sorry. So what made the difference here? I know you're giddy tonight.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MCCORMICK (R-PA), U.S. SENATOR-ELECT: Yes. Well, good evening, Laura. Thanks for having me. You know, I think what made the difference is people want change. They -- I think they represented that desire for change by electing President Trump. I think the same with me. They're deeply distressed by the skyrocketing prices, the wide open border, the crime in our cities, the war on fossil fuels. And they want change and common sense leadership and that's why I think they elected President Trump, and I think that's why they have elected me.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I've got to tell you, I'm honored to do it. I'm so excited. It's critical time for our country and honored to represent the great people of Pennsylvania. Regardless of party, by the way, represent, you know, Democrats, Republicans, independents.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You bet.\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: My job represent every single one of them.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Senator, Casey has not yet conceded, which is a bit surprising. His campaign, in a statement said, as the Pennsylvania Secretary of State said this afternoon, there are tens of thousands of ballots across the Commonwealth still to count, the races within a half a point and cannot be called while the votes of thousands of Pennsylvanians are still being counted, we'll make sure every Pennsylvanians voice is heard. Senator-elect, any concerns there?\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: Well, you know, listen, the reason I think the AP called the race is they saw the same thing we saw, which is, I have 30,000 or more than 30,000 votes lead in the race right now. And the outstanding counties, Cambria County, which I'm winning 75 percent of the vote, and there's, you know, 20,000, 30,000 more votes to be counted. So there's no mathematical path for Senator Casey winning. And I think that's -- I think that's why the AP called, that's why we, we've had so much confidence.\par \par \uc2 But, but listen, I lost an election by 900 votes. So I know, I know, I know you got to process it. I know Senator Casey has got to process this, and he's been in elected office for 30 years. His family's had a great history of public service, and so, you know he's going to have to find his way to the right answer. But I think the math is indisputable, and I think that's why the AP called it, and why we're confident moving forward to start preparing to be the next senator from Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And Senator, Republicans have for race after race, year after year, they've kind of written off Philly. And the joke is, you can always find the votes in Philly, it's always a running joke, late at night, you can find the votes where you need them, if you're Democrat, but you did find some fertile ground here for Republican growth. Again, why is that? And what can Republicans learn going forward into the midterms?\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: Well, listen, I am -- I tried to campaign across our great Commonwealth and those suburbs in the cities, I didn't -- I didn't think those weren't fertile ground for us. Even though they're majority Democrats. These problems, whether it's skyrocketing prices, or the crime, or obviously the fentanyl that comes from the wide open border, those are affecting all of Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 And so, I started my race, I started to talk about issues and leadership and the kinds of common sense solutions we need to the problems. I think that appealed to people and listen, I also think they don't want career politicians. They want change. And I represented change. I, you know, I was someone who was saying, we don't want to make incremental changes. We need to shake things up. That's my campaign.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And you actually showed up in Philly and in some of these tough neighborhoods where again Republicans don't like to go there, but you actually campaigned there. That also sends a message to people that they're not forgotten. It doesn't matter if you didn't vote for them last time around, you are not forgotten. And I think, and I'm going to talk about this in a moment, this has to be the strategy going forward in every state across the country. Do you agree with that? And then you can answer about how soon fracking is going to resume.\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: Well, listen, I absolutely agree with that. I think, I think party affiliation is up for grabs. I don't think people identify themselves as clearly as Republican or Democrat. They want problem solvers. And so whether it's the Jewish community or the trade unions, or the African American community, or the Hispanic community, I looked at all of those as a place where I could bring leadership and common sense solutions, and so, I spent a lot of time campaigning there and fortunately, it paid off. And I'm honored about that.\par \par \uc2 In terms of fracking, I want to have the conversation stop being about banning fracking which, which, by the way, you can ban fracking without actually banning it. You can ban it with red tape --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, regulations.\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: -- regulations. I want to talk about doubling, tripling, quadrupling fracking in Pennsylvania, because we need the pipelines. We need the energy source to create great paying jobs. We need it to be energy dominant from a national security perspective, and that natural, beautiful, clean natural gas is the key to reducing carbon emissions around the world. So I think it's a great opportunity to offer something to Pennsylvania, but also the rest of the country and the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And Senator-elect, do you believe that you can work with your colleague, soon to be colleague, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , who has some populist tendencies. You hear them come out every now and then, as well as people like \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , but specifically your colleague from your state.\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: Well, he's not always -- he's not always been nice to me on social media, but I absolutely think that I'll be able to find ways to work with him. Listen, we probably disagree on 90 percent of things. There's no doubt he's got a very different view of economic policies. He's much more of an avowed socialist, and I'm certainly think free enterprise is the path to the future.\par \par \uc2 But he's been incredibly strong on fighting back on antisemitism. He's been incredibly strong support of Israel. He's been one of the rare Democrats who has actually called out the crisis on the border. So I think he's a man that speaks his mind, and I admire that. And when he speaks his mind about something where I think he and I can work together, I'll be the first to try to do that.\par \par \uc2 And listen, these problems, as I said, don't lend themselves to one party. They're problems that affect everybody. So, you know, I'm going to be one who fights for on principle, for what I believe is right. But I'm also somebody who knows that you got to find common ground with others to be able to make things happen.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, and especially on those jobs in Pennsylvania that you all need desperately, good paying jobs, and Fetterman should be in favor of all that. Senator-elect, thank you very much. And congratulations. You worked your butt off. So thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You're welcome. And now California, here we come. That's the focus of tonight's Angle.\par \par \uc2 All right, it's not even been, what, 48 hours since we learned that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  blew the doors off the presidential race. And our so-called experts, they kind of remain in a suspended state of shock, shock by President Trump's strength across pretty much all demographic groups.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE SCARBOROUGH, MSNBC ANCHOR: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has in 2024 put together a working class coalition of white working class voters and working class voters of people of color.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The Democrats are in danger of becoming a regional coastal party.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The curious visit by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to the South Bronx to have a rally back in May. And a lot of people who didn't get it said, what's he doing there? Well, it's clear what he was doing. He was sending the message to people there, to Latino Americans, to black Americans, that I see you, I hear you, I care about you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, not only did Trump grow his support among Hispanics, among young people, African American men, he grew his support in deep blue states that he lost. In New York, where Trump lost by almost 23 points in 2020. On Tuesday, he cut that margin in half. And then in New Jersey in 2020 and we saw a little bit of this with that governor's race that was so close, Trump lost by a whopping 16 points to Biden, but this time, he came within five points of Harris. This is incredibly encouraging for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Now, basically the same story in Illinois, where Trump also cut his deficit there by about a half. So, as these numbers settle in, you have to wonder, do Democrats or their informal advisors, and they recognize some of the problems, you could hear that in those clips that we played, but do they really show signs of understanding what substantively went wrong?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Trump was very good at sending cultural signals. Virtually every Democrat I've talked to has said we cannot win if our people are not on these podcasts, these bro podcasts.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They're on TikTok and whether it's teenagers or college kids or people in their 20s, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a superstar.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We got to watch this.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And it's not about politics, it's not about policy, it's about a pop cultural movement.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Again, I don't want to beat up on people who lost this race, but that's a total misreading of the situation. The only reason Trump could have those moments, those viral moments, is because he, in fact, offered the right policies, and he had a fantastic record on inflation, on wages and the border, and as for sending cultural signals, well, that wasn't it either. He spoke to the real concerns of people. He didn't just say, I care about you. He spoke to their concerns. And then he took it a step further, because he advanced common sense solutions.\par \par \uc2 Boys shouldn't be in girls' sports. DEI is unfair and unworkable. Your taxes are too high. The jobs you're getting aren't paying you enough. I'll change all that. That was clear. But of course, messaging is important, but what the Democrats had was not a comms problem. Remember, that's what the Biden-Harris people kept thinking over the last two years, if we only have a better comm strategy. That was never it. It was always the policies that were terrible.\par \par \uc2 The Biden and Harris wrecked the economy. They flung open the border. They did it on purpose. Hispanics understood what was going on. Young people saw what was going on, and more women recognized all of this than the Democrats ever predicted they would. Yet there is little to no recognition still expressed over the past two days of how calamitous all of this was to working people. By the way, the working people of all backgrounds who began to flee their party, their Democrat party in Wisconsin and Michigan, Pennsylvania and beyond. By the way, their messaging on abortion. How much did we hear about abortion over the past five months? It was totally off. And frankly, in the end, I think a lot of women were insulted by it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH, CNN ANCHOR: The question about whether or not women were going to come out for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , whether or not men were going to come out for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in a post-Roe world on that issue. And if you just look at, for example, suburban women, they -- according to the national polls, they went for Trump.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes. Well, guess what? Women actually, you know, see the grocery bills. Women understand the heating oil costs in the winter. They get all that. They see the problem with migrant crime. They're not one issue voters. They never have been. We tried to warn the Democrats, they didn't listen to us.\par \par \uc2 Now the stark reality for them is a huge opportunity for Republicans. So after three and a half years of high prices the open border, enthusiasm for the Democrats, it's just -- it's astounding. It took a major hit across the whole country. And meanwhile, Trump grew his support for the GOP everywhere from Brooklyn to Philadelphia to Cook County in Illinois. So here is an idea.\par \par \uc2 When Trump, you know, realized, finally, OK, I've won this thing, and now we know he won. Here is the next tour. I know they are tired of tours and rallies maybe, but why not go to the major blue cities that he never really had the chance to visit during the campaign? Go to Boston, go to San Francisco, go to LA, go to Chicago, spend some time in Seattle, maybe even Portland, Oregon. Go to places like that. Go there with all this momentum and the wind he has at his back and make it very clear that he loves those cities.\par \par \uc2 He loves the people of those cities, even if they didn't support him, and he's proud to be their president, and that he's going to do everything possible to serve them and their interests during his time in office, those cities have to be rebuilt. They have to be rethought, and he's the perfect person to do that. And he should, yes, and everyone always says, I'm crazy when I say this, but he should make a big play for California, where, by the way, we heard today that \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  is setting the stage for massive resistance against the Trump agenda. Well, we know that's all about Gavin in 2028.\par \par \uc2 But the truth is, well, Gav's party lost support in 2024 even in California, because in 2020 Trump got 34.3 percent of the vote there, and as of now, 56 percent of the vote there counted, he has 40 percent of the vote. So I think the time has come to really go after the vote in California, in a common sense populist, attractive way, bring it home, bring California back to the GOP. That's where it belongs.\par \par \uc2 I'd send \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  out there. I'd said Vance out there. Spend lots of time, lots of energy talking to Californians, especially to the growing Hispanic voters in Southern California, and the 2028 Olympics are going to be out there, so it gives you a great chance. And while we're at it, why not have the 2028 convention out there? I know we just finished this race. I know I'm thinking of 2028, because the more money the Democrats spend to defend California, the less they're going to have to create havoc for good candidates in the rest of the country.\par \par \uc2 So, Republicans have nothing to lose and everything to gain by making an all-out effort to flip California. And my friends, it's time to end the division between the red states and the blue states. Bring the country together, and if anyone can do this, Trump can. And that's the Angle.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, \uc2 Lara Trump\uc2 , RNC Co-Chair. Lara, I know you're thinking, I just got finished with this race, and Ingraham is already pushing me on 2028, but it's a compliment to you, because of the great success the party had in growing its base and beyond to all these demographic groups that sometimes had been written off by Republicans, to be perfectly candid. So how does the party plan on taking this success and blowing it out to really, really go after some of these blue or blue states?\par \par \uc2 LARA TRUMP, CO-CHAIR, \uc2 RNC\uc2 : Yes, give us an hour, Laura, my gosh, yes, we just finished this one, but I agree with you. Listen, we've always had to be forward thinking if we're going to win, and that's why \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won this election. He did go, you know, you talk about the South Bronx. That rally in the Bronx was really epic, and it really caught the Democrats off- guard. And he did go to other places.\par \par \uc2 Now you're right. He didn't go to every blue city in America, but he went a lot of places. He showed up in Atlanta, he showed up in Detroit. He went to these places because he believed that he had to ask for people's votes, and he wanted them to know, yes, I'm here. I want to hear from you. I want to be your president. And it obviously paid off. But I think the amazing thing with this election right now that we just had you saw how the numbers of people coming out for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  truly were historic.\par \par \uc2 In almost every voting bloc, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  gained voters. This party gained voters, and we want to expand the base of the party. This is a party for every person in America. It doesn't matter what you look like, it doesn't matter your religion, it doesn't matter -- we don't care who you love. We want you in this party, and we want you as a voter. And I think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , he's always said that success will unify us. I think that's going to happen during his second term in office. And I think a lot of people are taking another look at the Republican Party, because we are a party for everybody.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, so that, that moment for him in Detroit with the pastors, the South Bronx. You know, he spent a little bit of time in California, but you see my point that you all put together a strategy based on the 50 state strategy. It was a national campaign. Republicans kept making the mistake of going regional. You went national, and I think that completely took the Democrats off-guard. I don't think they were ready for it. And I think you, you rocked their world because they had more money, three times the money you all had.\par \par \uc2 So you had less money, you had fewer volunteers at one point, and yet you had the policies and committed people on the ground to make a difference that that stunned me. I got to say just, just the organization of it, Lara, you've never done anything like this before. What was the one thing you learned from these last two years?\par \par \uc2 L. TRUMP: Oh my gosh. I mean, I've learned a lot in the last nine years. Certainly all of us in the Trump family have. I probably know more about election integrity than I ever anticipated, but I think that was -- that had to happen in this election. Look, we had to restore people's faith in our electoral process, and I really feel like we did it this election. We wanted to send a message out there to people, don't even try it. But we also had to reassure our voters that, yes, when you come out and vote, it is going to be worth it. Your vote will matter, and your vote will count. And I think that that was very powerful for a lot of people, so I probably had a quick lesson on a lot of things, but those of us in the Trump family are very quick studies, we've had to learn and pack it all in the past nine years. And it's really been an honor.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I was honored to be a part of this team. I think it's absolutely incredible. It's historic. And I'm incredibly proud, of course, of my father-in-law, no one deserves it more than he does.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Lara, your reaction to the Susie Wiles pick for Chief of Staff, just like with you and Kellyanne, all these people over the years. I guess he just doesn't like strong, intelligent women.\par \par \uc2 L. TRUMP: Yes, yes, sorry to tell \uc2 Mark Cuban\uc2  about that. This is a great pick, obviously. Susie was a reason, one of the big parts of our campaign, and one of the big reasons that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to be the 47th President. She's going to do an incredible job. And honestly, I was sort of shocked to know that there had never been a woman as Chief of Staff before. So congratulations to Susie Wiles. She deserves it, and leave it to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , of all people, Laura, to appoint the first woman. Glass ceiling broken. You're welcome, Democrats.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, that's history. That's history, but he won't get credit for that. Laura, congratulations and thank you very much. All right. \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2  has a lot to say about this and more coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We've accepted a narrative that despite an actually great economic recovery, the vibes don't feel good, so we want to reject it and get something else. What will the future hold now that America has just decided that we're going to F around and find out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I don't even know what that means, but the Left is blaming everyone everything, trying to find something to blame for Kamala's lost but and they still don't realize that they're just totally out of touch with the concerns of everyday Americans.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCARBOROUGH: Somebody who is going to be voting for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  came up to me and said, oh, my God, Trump's going to win. I go, why is that? Because I just, I went to the grocery store, butter is over $3. I kind of laughed.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: 7.\par \par \uc2 SCARBOROUGH: What's that?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: $7.\par \par \uc2 SCARBOROUGH: Butter is $7.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCARBOROUGH: What? Is it framed in gold?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Right. OK, did he not realize that gas had gone up? I realize the gas goes up like several times a week. Joining me now, \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 , former Democrat, co-chair of Trump's transition team. OK, Tulsi, maybe that was like a joke, I can't really tell, but it does kind of bring to bear what's really at stake in politics in general. Do you really get what bothers the American people and have a solution to fix it, but they still think this is largely a communications problem with the American people. Your response to the head scratching on the other side?\par \par \uc2 TULSI GABBARD, FORMER U.S. CONGRESSWOMAN: Yes, Laura, you know that that clip you played from MSNBC first really kind of said it all is, is they continue to think that they are awesome. They did a great job. Look at this huge economic recovery. Why don't the American people know this or appreciate this? They are so out of touch with the hardships and the reality that the American people have been living through over these last four years.\par \par \uc2 They sit in their ivory tower eating their cake and look down on the people who are struggling and suffering, saying, oh, gosh, I don't know what's wrong. The reality is that President Trump went out and traveled across the country. He listens, he sees people and he recognizes the challenges they face and tells them that he will work for them. He's got a four year track record of doing that during his first administration, so they believe him when he says, I'm going to bring down the cost of living. They believe him when he says, I am committed to preventing World War III. He's the -- \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , I don't think she ever once mentioned nuclear war, not during her four years as Vice President, not during this campaign.\par \par \uc2 President Trump, almost everywhere he went, talked about the seriousness of the threat of nuclear war, and that we are closer to the brink than ever before because of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and that he'll do all that he can to prevent nuclear war. These are the kinds of things that drove people from across party lines, from across the demographics, to say, yes, my vote is with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You know, I think a lot of folks in the Democrat Party weren't as much concerned about making America economically strong again, or certainly not making America great again, but they were very worried about making Ukraine great and safe again. And this is some of the reaction.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: One of our closest Democratic allies Ukraine now depends on a man who has criticized Ukraine, has attacked the support we have given Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm heartbroken for the people of Ukraine, because I am terrified about what is going to happen in a Trump administration.,\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A Ukrainian soldier in the trenches as their ally just abandoned them, is wondering what I'm going to do next.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Tulsi has \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  abandoned the people of Ukraine? Or will he?\par \par \uc2 GABBARD: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is standing with the American people, Laura, I was with people in Western North Carolina in and around the communities surrounding Ashville the day before Election Day. And you know what I heard from them? They are coming home from funerals of their loved ones.\par \par \uc2 They are telling me, hey, we still have over 500 people who have not been found in the wake of Hurricane Helene, and it's been over a month. Why aren't those talking heads talking about the American people who need a president who has their back?\par \par \uc2 President Trump has their back and this is why he's earned their trust and their confidence.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Tulsi, and I know you're not going to answer this, but I'm going to ask anyway, can we see you or will we see you perhaps in the administration?\par \par \uc2 GABBARD: That's a question only President Trump can answer.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Will you take, you know, like the lower level job, like special assistant to the undersecretary at \uc2 HHS\uc2 ? I mean, like something like that? I mean, I'm just teasing. Because he doesn't like strong, intelligent women.\par \par \uc2 GABBARD: Right, yeah, exactly, clearly. And congratulations to Susie Wiles. She is fantastic. I've so enjoyed working with her. She is a straight shooter and I'm looking forward to seeing her as Chief of Staff.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Her source was quoted as saying, the clown show is basically not going to have access to the Oval Office, essentially, I'm paraphrasing, but the word clown show was used. Is that a good thing?\par \par \uc2 GABBARD: Yeah, you know, she works very well with President Trump. I think she did -- she is of great credit to winning this campaign and being able to make sure that the train didn't go off the rails. And so, you know, I think she's going to do a fantastic job as White House Chief of Staff.\par \par \uc2 President Trump seeks counsel from those that he wants to, but Susie Wiles will provide a great leadership on his behalf in running that place.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Tulsi, always great to see you. Congratulations. All the hard work you put in. And coming up, Trump is elected again, just not even 48 hours ago. He's already, already securing the border. How can that be? We'll explain it next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: A massive caravan of migrants, 3,000 strong was making its way through Mexico to our southern border, but I don't know, President Trump's victory already seems to be having a pretty positive impact for America. Reuters is reporting that today the caravan shrunk down to about half its original size. And obviously the Biden administration is not going to do anything to stop the ones who do show up.\par \par \uc2 NBC News already says DHS is working on what they're calling contingency plans for this surge of migrants, questioning whether the numbers will overwhelm the system and just force agents to release them into the U.S. with court dates set years into the future, which of course they'll never show up for.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Texas Lieutenant Governor \uc2 Dan Patrick\uc2 . Dan, I call him Alejandro Mayor -- you're welcome to come right in. OK, because basically if you're making your way to the border, they'll figure out a way to allow you to stay here.\par \par \uc2 But they tried to lead people to believe before the vote that they had really gotten tough on the border. But what do Americans need to understand tonight in these 74 days before Trump is inaugurated?\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 DAN PATRICK\uc2  (R-TX): Well, the American people, and thanks for having me on, Laura, of course. The American people need to understand that over the last four years, somewhere between 16 and 18 million people have come here illegally. We don't know where hundreds of thousands of children are. They're probably being sex trafficked, sadly, tragically.\par \par \uc2 We know we have apprehended a terrorist crossing the Texas border. We know we have criminals committing horrendous crimes all over the country. And tonight, I'm so relaxed. I'm so at ease because I know the cavalry just arrive on Tuesday night, and America has gotten a reprieve. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to come in here and this is going to be get shut down.\par \par \uc2 Trust me, we spent four billion dollars in our last budget and we've done that now three sessions, I think, in a row to secure our border, that's money we could use for our taxpayers. I mean, four billion dollars for to cut taxes, to put in our infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm going to guarantee the American public tonight, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Greg Abbott, and \uc2 Dan Patrick\uc2  are going to close this sucker down. And so, you know, a word, if anyone wants to translate to them, to the criminals who have come across the border, you might want to try to illegally go back into Mexico, because we're going to throw you out. Serious business -\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: How is that going to work, Dan? Dan, so deportation of the numbers of people that we're talking about, which is millions, practically speaking, how will you assist the DHS in removing people, including and mostly, I guess mostly single men who've come here, but families have come here as well. Small children are in school. How that look?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK: So, most of them have been single men, and we're going to focus on those criminals. We know most of the time where they are, we're going to find them and we'll be able to deport them under federal law, number one. I wrote and passed a bill last session, the Senate passed it, the governor signed it, that now allows our law enforcement to apprehend people. It's the Supreme Court now, we won one court battle, we think it will be upheld constitutionally.\par \par \uc2 So, we're going to put our law enforcement into the mix too, so that we can grab them and we can either arrest them if they've committed a crime, turn them over, and they'll be sent back, unlike the Biden administration has done.\par \par \uc2 And Laura, understand this, that when we put our National Guard down on Shelby Park, there were 5,000 people a day coming across. When we went in, we cut it down to 10 people a day, 5,000 of them people. Greg Abbott, \uc2 Dan Patrick\uc2 , President Trump, we know how to get this done and it's going to be shut down when he gets in here.\par \par \uc2 And Laura, he has changed the whole border of politics. Star County is, if not the most Hispanic county in the country, is one of the most. He won it 57 percent Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I saw that. Unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK: Yeah, and I'm with you two weeks ago, by the way, and I said he's going to sweep every state and he's going to get 312 electoral votes, and I think it worked out that way. I had faith in the campaign.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You did.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK: \uc2 Lara Trump\uc2 , they did a great job. Michael Watley, the whole team. But I had faith in the American people that they would stand up and take their country back, and they did.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah, Ted Cruz, great victory for him as well. Dan, you were a big part of this. You and Governor Abbott. Great to see you and congratulations. And just ahead, the people voted against lawfare, but what happens to the cases against Trump, and what are Democrats planning next? Coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I think there's, again, a 50-50-ish chance that Judge Merchan will impose a prison sentence as opposed to a probation sentence. But even if the judge does hold this sentencing on November 26th, and even if the judge says I hereby sentence you to X in prison, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not going to serve that time while he's in transition as he is now, or certainly while he's president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, certainly while he's president, leaving open the possibility that, you know, if they're wanting this, they're salivating over this, that somehow the statute of limitations for these absurd, in these absurd cases is paused and then Trump goes off in an orange jumpsuit after he has a successful second term. This is insanity.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Chris Landau, former Supreme Court clerk, former ambassador to Mexico. Chris, we've been covering this, it seems like forever, together. Respond to that, this quixotic dream of still putting Trump in prison.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LANDAU, FORMER AMBASSADOR TO MEXICO: Listen, the hardcore, that's the way I guess they put themselves to sleep with those dreams of him in an orange jumpsuit. But Laura, I think the good news and the big headline is that lawfare backfired massively on the Democrats in this election season.\par \par \uc2 This was going to be their magic wand to try to get rid of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for once and for all. And if anything, it just made him stronger. And I think it's so healthy for our political system to have lawfare decisively repudiated in this way.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  got reelected. I didn't even know she was up for re-election, Chris, but apparently, she got re-elected handily. But the American people, I think, in general, they want to determine things at the ballot box. And all of this looked like a giant pile-on from the beginning.\par \par \uc2 Yet, there is talk among some of these so-called legal experts on the other networks of pausing the statute of limitations. Is that even possible?\par \par \uc2 LANDAU: Well, this is all garbage. I mean, look, again, like these legal experts can talk to each other. I mean, these cases were on life support before Trump won the election. One of the two federal cases had already been thrown out by the judge because \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2 's appointment was unconstitutional. The other one, the \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2  one in Washington, got gutted by the Supreme Court's immunity ruling.\par \par \uc2 Fani Smith's thing in Georgia got gutted by her own improper conduct, not to mention the Supreme Court's immunity ruling. And then you have the \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  one in New York where -- that sucker is like a target rich environment for appeal, putting aside the fact that that's also impacted by the Supreme Court's immunity ruling insofar as some of the evidence that was used there.\par \par \uc2 So, putting it aside the election, Laura, these things were going down for the garbage cases that they were, as any serious lawyer would tell you, anybody other than the most fevered partisan. So, you know, again, I suspect now that the Democrats saw it didn't work, that it's going to be like that Homer Simpson meme where Homer's kind of like backing into the bushes and kind of saying, never mind, nothing to see here, folks. I mean, the Emmett, yeah.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Chris, as always, it's great to have you on. Thank you very much for the analysis. And liberal women, they have a plan to make men pay for electing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . We'll tell you about it next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now some women are so wound up about the results of Tuesday's election that they say they're swearing off men for good.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Stop dating men. Stop having sex with men. Stop talking to men. Divorce your husbands. Leave your (BLEEP) boyfriends.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I really want to get behind this 4B movement. No dating men. No getting married. No having kids. And no having S-word.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Wait, that woman cutting her hair off? Oh, bold. Now, \uc2 Google\uc2  searches for the 4B movement, which originated in South Korea, have spiked since Trump won the election. It advocates no sex, dating or marriage with men, and no children. In other words, it just changed the dating pool for the Trump bros.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Brianna Lyman, correspondent for The Federalist, and Aldo Buttazoni, PragerU personality. Aldo, are you heartbroken now that you don't have a chance with all these angry, vituperative liberal women?\par \par \uc2 ALDO BUTTAZZONI, PRAGERU PERSONALITY: Their terms are acceptable. Where do I sign? Where's the pen and paper? You know, the ironic thing about this is they think they're punishing Trump supporters and conservatives in this, but in reality, we're the ones that have been suggesting this and saying that if you don't want an unwanted pregnancy, simply don't have sex with men you're unwilling to have a child with.\par \par \uc2 The men this will impact are the low T, beta, soy boy, liberal men who only want to use women for consequence-free sex. And so, I think these women are doing a great job. I salute them. I want them to stand up for themselves. And they have my complete endorsement.\par \par \uc2 As for me, I'm going to stick to dating conservative women because they already have these values. They seem a lot happier than these liberal women. And quite frankly, they're a lot more attractive.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, I may or may not have worn a Trump baseball cap in the grocery store just to see the reaction. And I've got to say, I was surprised, Brianna, that I didn't have a lot of negative comments. And I had a couple really positive comments from minority workers at the grocery store, which kind of tells the story about this election.\par \par \uc2 But we were told that angry women were going to save us from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That was the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  column on Monday by Michelle Goldberg. What happened to that?\par \par \uc2 BRIANNA LYMAN, THE FEDERALIST CORRESPONDENT: Well, Goldberg is the expert. Look, all these women who you just mentioned, part of this 4B movement, if they want to end their gene pool, that's natural selection and that's fine. But I think the bigger issue here is that these women were exploited by Democrats for being low information voters.\par \par \uc2 They were sold the lie that abortion, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would sign a national abortion ban. They would be denied care if they had a miscarriage. All of this was trying to scare voters into voting for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . And she tried to quell those fears yesterday by saying everything will be OK.\par \par \uc2 A lot of voters bought those lies, hook line and sinker. They should be disgusted with the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  campaign for lying to them and misleading them and leaving them scared in the dust.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Aldo, apparently a lot of schools, colleges, trying to kind of give some comfort and aid to distraught students at the prestigious \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2 , they were offered a Lego station, coloring books and milk and cookies.\par \par \uc2 At the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 , one of the top schools in America, they can get art therapy and post-election processing. \uc2 University of Oregon\uc2  had an actual therapy duck on campus, although young people need help, apparently, or just liberals.\par \par \uc2 BUTTAZZONI: Well, my home state is Michigan. I spent a lot of time in Ann Arbor around \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 . And so, that doesn't surprise me too much from the stock of students that I've seen walking around the campus.\par \par \uc2 But the bar has been lowered in so many ways for these university students that should be challenging themselves, that should be engaging in hard ideas, and really, should have the fortitude to deal with election results that they lost. It's really sad to see this.\par \par \uc2 But hopefully in the next four years they'll gain a little bit of grit and they'll be able to get through this without sucking on their baby bottles.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Brianna, I want you to take a look at Trump's performance with voters ages 18 to 29. Harris beat him with young voters by only 6 percent, which is shocking compared to Biden beating Trump by 25 percent just four years ago.\par \par \uc2 So, she thought she was going to win Gen Z huge. What happened here?\par \par \uc2 LYMAN: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  redefined what it means to campaign. He was going on podcasts, which young men, especially 18 to 29, tend to listen to more. He was redefining that you don't have to go on the propaganda press to set the narrative, right? You can go on these podcasts and expand your base. And that's what he did.\par \par \uc2 And he comes across as authentic and likable. And most importantly, he comes across by saying, you want to graduate college, find a job, and be able to pay bills and not live at home with mom and dad? My economy is going to make that happen for you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But, Aldo, it wasn't just the forum of the podcast, which I think was important, Brianna's right, but it was the policies that he talked about. He didn't talk down to women, and he didn't talk down to young people.\par \par \uc2 Even when he said I want to be your protector whether you like it or not, that was like I'm going to keep you safe. I'm going to make sure you have more money in your pocket. I'm going to enforce the border. You're going to be safer, and eventually you're going to like it even if you don't like me. Ten seconds, can he grow this voter block further, Aldo?\par \par \uc2 BUTTAZZONI: Absolutely. The policies of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  help all Americans, man, woman, of all creeds, of all races. He is going to be the president for all Americans. He's made that clear in his last administration, and he'll do it again, and I'm so excited to see it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, it's so great to have you both on, both future bright lights in this country. Brianna, Aldo, thank you.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us tonight. Thanks for watching. Remember, it's America now and forever. Check me out on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and X. Jesse Watters, he takes it from here.\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020241108ekb700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Republican Election Spoils In Congress; \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Concedes Election To \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ; Economy Ranks As Top Issue Driving Voter Decisions; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Expands Margins In Historic White House Win; Republicans Win Senate Majority, House Undecided; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Elected 47th President Of United States; Pollsters Examine Accuracy Of Presidential Polls; Republicans Gain Control Of \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 ; Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  Criticizes Democratic Party For Not Supporting Working Calls Enough.\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jacqui Heinrich, Aishah Hasnie, Mark Meredith, Howard  Kurtz, Brit Hume, Martha MacCallum, Kayleigh McEnany, Jessica Tarlov, Chad  Pergram \par \uc2 7,727 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 November 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: That's it for us. Have a great night, everyone.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: OK, all right.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I'm Bret Baier. We are coming to you live tonight from Fox election headquarters in New York.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are celebrating what appears to be a political Trifecta tonight. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  fought off a host of allegations and slurs, a criminal conviction on dozens of felony counts and two impeachments, two assassination attempts as well to reclaim the White House. The now president-elect ran away late with the electoral college tally, and as of now, is winning the national popular vote. Republicans have captured the majority in the Senate, and the House GOP appears poised to maintain control of that chamber.\par \par \uc2 It's a stunning loss for Democrats. It's being characterized by many as a rejection of the Biden-Harris administration's economic, social and immigration policies, and frankly, a rejection, specifically, of the nominee Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight with one of the most significant political comebacks in history. Senior National Correspondent Aishah Hasnie is with the Trump campaign in West Palm Beach, Florida. Good evening, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you. The president-elect received a phone call today from Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who congratulated him and talked about the peaceful transition of power. Other than that, it's been a pretty quiet day here in Mar-a-Lago, two sources are telling me that the president will -- president-elect will not be making any announcements about his cabinet positions or even his chief of staff today as he takes in what has been a long fought victory.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): A new image tonight shows president-elect Trump taking in a quiet moment to fix his tie before taking the stage in West Palm Beach to declare victory.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), U.S. PRESIDENT-ELECT: This will truly be the golden age of America.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): The 47th president is celebrating his landslide win, powered by his strength on the economy and immigration, the top two issues for voters.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : It was a historic realignment, uniting citizens of all backgrounds around a common core of common sense.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): This time, Trump built a bigger coalition, in part by registering new voters who don't normally turn out in key battleground states. He also targeted traditional Democratic voting blocks disillusioned by the left.\par \par \uc2 According to Fox News voter analysis, Trump made large gains amongst Hispanic, Black and young voters, all up from 2020 and he doubled his support from men, while fewer women went for Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  than President Biden in 2020.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL WHATLEY, RNC CHAIRMAN: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  reached out to those voters. He went on alternative platforms. He went to the \uc2 National Association of Black Journalists\uc2 . He did events in downtown Philadelphia, downtown New York. He went to the Bronx. He went to Chicago. He went to Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Trump swept the blue wall states, and with bold stops like his \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  rally, he even made double digit gains in solid blue states like New York, where he took 45 percent of Jewish voters, compared to 37 percent in 2020.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : They came from all quarters, union, non-union, African-American, Hispanic-American, Asian-American, Arab-American, Muslim-American. We had everybody. And it was beautiful.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And Bret, tonight, more good news for the president- elect. Special Counsel \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2  is now winding down his federal prosecutions against Trump, and that's because the DOJ has a long standing policy of not prosecuting sitting presidents.\par \par \uc2 Most recently, Trump actually came out and said that he would fire Smith within two seconds after taking office, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah Hasnie live in West Palm Beach, Florida. Aishah, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  says she is proud of the race she ran and the way she ran it, and she says Democrats must accept the result of the election. The defeated Democratic nominee declined to speak with supporters on election night and waited until late this afternoon to make her first public comments since the votes were counted.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich is at \uc2 Howard University\uc2  in Washington night where Harris spoke a short time ago. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. The vice president thanked her supporters, campaign staff and President Biden, but in the day it took for her to put together that message. Democrats began the post mortem on what went wrong, from her choice of running mate to her policy positions, even how Democrats treated President Biden.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: The outcome of this election is not what we wanted. The light of America's promise will always burn bright, as long as we never give up.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The defeated vice president briefly choking up as some Democrats are owning up to the outcome.\par \par \uc2 Congressman \uc2 Ritchie Torres\uc2  saying, the far left, "Has managed to alienate historic numbers of Latinos, Blacks, Asians and Jews, from the Democratic Party with absurdities like defund the police or from the river to the sea or Latinx."\par \par \uc2 REP. DEBBIE DINGELL (D-MI): I'm going to be very blunt. As Democrats together do some soul searching, we're getting a message from a lot of people that we're not hearing them.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We do have a problem connecting with working class voters.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): But others aren't looking in the mirror.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2  (D-CA): He won on some fundamental issues of the economy and inflation, and incumbent parties have been losing all over the world.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Campaign officials admitting the party platform is too liberal on social issues for Black and Latino men in states like Georgia and Arizona. Harris also underperformed Biden among moderates, independents and union households.\par \par \uc2 SYMONE SANDERS-TOWNSEND, FORMER HARRIS SENIOR ADVISER: Democrats orchestrated a very public stab fest, a proverbial stabbing in the front of the sitting President of the United States of America, and then didn't use him in his hometown of Scranton, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): As the campaign reeled, surrogates were left with no guidance all day on what to tell disappointed supporters or even donors.\par \par \uc2 LINDI LI, HARRIS FINANCE COMMITTEE: If anyone has seen "Game of Thrones", it was a red wedding. It was a bloodbath. So, everyone is in a state of shock.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Without any official line, one spokesperson tweeted to friends, do not ask what happened, it's not the day, neither is Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 Harris closing out with this:\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Sometimes the fight takes a while. That doesn't mean we won't win.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): Now, the highest levels of the party, the reflecting has turned to mudslinging. \uc2 Obama\uc2  was among the forces that pushed Biden out of the race and now his former campaign manager who joined Harris' team is blaming Biden for leaving his vice president too deep a hole to dig herself out of.\par \par \uc2 A Biden loyalist scoffed in response, telling Fox that Biden beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , something they couldn't manage to do.\par \par \uc2 Today, the President Biden spoke to president-elect Trump, congratulated him and invited him to meet at the White House, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich at \uc2 Howard University\uc2  in D.C. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I am here to say, while I concede this election, I do not concede the fight that fueled this campaign. America, we will never give up the fight for our democracy, for the rule of law, for equal justice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, the political post mortem has already started. Politico says why \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  lost the election. She never sufficiently buried Biden's ghost, severely hamstringing her ability to sell voters on the idea that hers was the turn the page candidacy. It happened simply because Harris refused to make a clean break from the last four years when voters indicated that's what they wanted. Worse, she hesitated to draw any daylight between herself and her boss on Biden's biggest vulnerability, his stewardship over the economy.\par \par \uc2 With that, let's bring in our panel, Fox News Chief, political analyst, Brit Hume, \uc2 Kayleigh McEnany\uc2 , Fox News analyst, co-host of "OUTNUMBERED" Martha MacCallum, anchor and executive editor of "THE STORY WITH MARTHA MACCALLUM" and "THE FIVE's" co-host, Jessica Tarlov.\par \par \uc2 OK, you agree with that, Brit?\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: No,\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You don't?\par \par \uc2 HUME: No, I don't. I mean, I think it --I think that contributed to it, but there was no way that the sitting vice president could easily detach herself from the record of an administration of which she was a part, and to hear her tell it and to hear Mr. Biden tell it an integral part.\par \par \uc2 So, I think, you know, an effort -- an effort to toss aside Biden and his record would never have worked.\par \par \uc2 I think that what we have here Bret is a case of the Democratic Party under President Biden, storing up for himself so much trouble from the economy to Afghanistan to the border to men in women's sports and locker rooms that it was like a time bomb. It did not go off in 2022 during the midterms, last night it did.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Martha, the Fox News voter analysis really is interesting, because the comparison, the contrast, really between \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  number -- Harris's numbers and Biden's numbers with women, suburban women and college graduate women, they all went up, you know, for Trump with all of those groups, and it was the driving force of what the Harris campaign was saying was going to change.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: You know, I think everyone thought that if there was a younger candidate, and in this case, a female candidate, she would naturally do better than older President Biden, with all of these groups, with college women, with younger voters, but she fell short of where Biden was, and that is the perhaps insurmountable obstacle that \uc2 Jen O'Malley Dillon\uc2  talks about that they couldn't surmount. They couldn't, you know, overcome, which is, I think, very striking.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, I think that what they've learned is that she didn't -- she never had a real message, right? If you ask people on the street what was her message, I think they would say anti-\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and abortion freedom, and that was her other biggest thing.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here's Senator \uc2 Chris Coons\uc2  on he's kind of getting grips with it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHRIS COONS\uc2  (D-DE): A big focus on the economy and inflation, and frankly, a lot of folks not recognizing the real progress that was made by President Biden and Vice President Harris that we've seen landmark bipartisan legislation signed into law in the last couple of years that does important things that average Americans say they want, reducing prescription drug prices, for example, capping the price of insulin, infrastructure, but they didn't feel it, and they didn't give us credit for it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And that's the problem that Americans didn't get it about the economic success, Jessica?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, it's your fault.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's what I mean.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes, this has been a problem, though, throughout the entire administration, I think, from the podium to the surrogates, that the message of the accomplishments of this administration are not being communicated in a way that is resonant with the American public, and this was a very much -- very much an election about what you can see, the realities that we see around us, whether that's your necessities that you're buying on a grocery shelf, and how you have felt over the last four years about them being more expensive, what your perception is about the migrant crisis, which is now in all of our cities, here in New York, Chicago, Baltimore, all over the place.\par \par \uc2 And the fact that you are hearing a message from your leaders that is in Congress, with your lived experience.\par \par \uc2 And this jockeying about why we lost, and saying we should have done this one, go to the Al Smith dinner, or go on Joe Rogan. If she had lost by a small margin or with a specific group, I would say, OK, let's talk about making mistakes like that. This was with everybody. She underperformed every single category from where \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did this, that chart, the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  chart with the red arrows, they're all -- they're everywhere, and they're going in one direction. You can't -- you can't politic it that way.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, here's somebody with the DNC trying another version.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: One of the things that are top of mind is the choice of \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  as the vice presidential candidate. A lot of people are saying tonight that it should have been Josh Shapiro. Frankly, people have been saying that for months.\par \par \uc2 I know a lot of people are probably wondering tonight, what would have happened had Shapiro been on the ticket. I hate to say this, but I'm not sure how much \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  contributed to the ticket.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, listen, I think they're trying to throw something against the wall, and it felt like on the campaign that that may be how it was run. You know, let's try this. If it doesn't work, let's try something else. And now I think the explanation of it, there's four different answers we have.\par \par \uc2 KAYLEIGH MCENANY\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: 24 hours ago, I would have said, yes, the fact that you did not pick Josh Shapiro is a huge blunder, and could cost you in a close race. But that wasn't your problem.\par \par \uc2 The problem, you know, in all due respect, Jessica, I think it wasn't a messaging problem. I think it was a facts problem. I mean, this administration was handed two percent inflation, 2.4 percent and it went to 9.1 percent and Republicans would say that's not by accident, the American Rescue Plan. Kamala cast the tie breaking vote. The vice president cast the tie breaking vote for the Inflation Reduction Act, setting off catastrophic inflation. That's before we get to the 84 executive --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: COVID set that up though, it started under Trump.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: OK, spending contributed, there's no doubt about it. I think that's the sole responsibility. Not to mention the executive actions lifted the \uc2 Obama\uc2  or, excuse me, the Trump era immigration policies. There's a facts problem. It's not a messaging problem. It's a structural one, and it runs deep.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, the lot of talk about the concession and you know, what was said and what not, we do know that President Biden called former president. The Trump campaign just put out a statement. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  called President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  and to congratulate him on his victory and extend an invitation to the White House to ensure a smooth transition between the current administration and the incoming administration. President Trump looks forward to the meeting, which will take place shortly, and very much appreciated the call. So, there is that.\par \par \uc2 HUME: That's a good sound. That's a good tone to have from both camps at this moment, and it's something I think the American people usually expect. I don't think \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's statement was bad, but it didn't strike the same -- the same tone. It did miss that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, more with the panel in a bit. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Here's some other headlines, Lebanese rescuers have pulled 30 bodies out of the rubble after a late night Israeli strike on an apartment building. It remained unclear if there were any survivors or bodies still trapped under the rubble following Tuesday night's air strike.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's defense minister says his country's troops are, for the first time, engaging with North Korean units that were recently deployed to help Russia in the war. Senior Pentagon official telling Fox it began Monday in Kursk, a region in western Russia, the official did not say if there were any North Korean or Ukrainian casualties.\par \par \uc2 Hurricane Rafael made landfall in Cuba as a powerful Category 3 storm. Forecasters say it could bring life threatening storm surges, wind and flash floods to Western swaths of the island after it knocked out power and dumped rain on the Cayman Islands and Jamaica yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, stocks soared to record highs today. The Dow, liking the whole election thing, surging 1,508 points. The S&P 500 jumped 149 -- 146 rather, NASDAQ gained 544.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we will analyze how president-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  managed to gain with Hispanic voters and take down the Democratic blue wall.\par \par \uc2 And an update on the balance of power. Who will control the House remains an open question, but even with races still open, Republicans did take control of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to SPECIAL REPORT, president-elect Trump leads Vice President Harris right now nationally by more than four million votes. If that popular vote holds up, it will be the first time in 20 years a Republican wins the popular vote, and that margin of victory came from gains in the battleground states and beyond, and it really gives the now president-elect and his team the basis for a pretty convincing argument that he has a big mandate.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we take a closer look at the broad coalition behind Trump's historic White House come back and let's start in the state of New York. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  beat Trump by a little more than 11 points, although he did not win the state, he did close the gap from 2020 when he lost to President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  by more than 23 points. We'll put up the two side by side here.\par \par \uc2 If we zoom in to Nassau County, for example. Nassau County, you see that Trump actually beat Harris by almost five points in Nassau County, he lost Biden there by almost 10 points.\par \par \uc2 And if we go over to the Bronx, this little one right here, you can see that that's where Trump held that camp -- those campaign events, two of them, the visit with the barber shop with Fox. He outperformed his 2020 results there by 11 points.\par \par \uc2 OK, so let's go to another state where we see some action on this front going back to the comparison to 2020 and that is Pennsylvania, the biggest battleground of them all, part of the Democrats blue wall. The Trump team flipped the state from 2020 as you see here, if you look at Philadelphia County down here by Philadelphia, Harris underperformed where Biden was in 2020. The turnout the campaign was hoping for really never materialized in those suburban counties.\par \par \uc2 Now, if we look at Bucks County, just to the north here, you can see that that's where I interviewed Vice President Harris last month after her appearance with Republican \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , Trump appears to have flipped Bucks County red. That's the first time that will have happened since 1988.\par \par \uc2 OK, let's go south now to Florida. That also tells a different story when it comes to the popular vote and different places here, president-elect Trump outperforming his 2020 results by almost five percent. Harris saw five percent less support than President Biden. The margin of victory and the early call really was an indication that Trump was going to have a good night.\par \par \uc2 Latino voters really played a role in the widening edge in Florida. If we look at Miami, Dade County, for example, where about 68 percent of the residents are Hispanic, Biden received more votes here in 2020 than Trump, but Trump outperformed Harris by 11 points. He is the first Republican presidential candidate to win Miami Dade County since 1988, another historic win.\par \par \uc2 OK, let's go out to Texas, the biggest prize electorally for the Republicans. And there you see Trump as the first Republican presidential nominee. If we go down to this border county right down here, star county, he is the first Republican to win since 1896, take that, and that's a lot of years.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the wide coalition of voter support, president-elect Trump will return to Washington with a Republican majority in the Senate, which is also a big deal.\par \par \uc2 Senior Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram is on Capitol Hill tonight with a preview of this new balance of power. Good evening, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening. Republicans say there were major coattails for president-elect Trump. That's why Republican \uc2 Bernie Moreno\uc2  handily defeated Ohio Democrat \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 . Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  says the power of Trump helped the GOP.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): I think it was a referendum on the current administration, in part, people were just not happy with this administration, and the Democratic nominee, obviously was a part of it. It was a hell of a good day.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): But there were limits to the Trump juggernaut. While Trump carried Wisconsin and Michigan, Democrats won Senate races in both states.\par \par \uc2 The House is on a razor's edge. Right now, there are 200 GOP members and 181 Democrats. 54 seats are outstanding. One race is in northern Maine.\par \par \uc2 Democrat Jared Golden is trying to fend off GOP challenger Austin Theriault.\par \par \uc2 REP. JARED GOLDEN (D-MN): Any viable path to a win for my opponent has closed, so I'm here to declare victory. I certainly prefer to be in the majority. But we don't know the outcome.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: But GOP leaders say president-elect Trump could help them hold the House.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ELISE STEFANIK\uc2  (R-NY): If you look at President Trump's performance in carrying Senate candidates over the line, there is no modern day candidate of either party who turns out voters more effectively than President Trump.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): Now, the performance of Vice President Harris likely cost Democrats the seats of Susan Wild and \uc2 Matt Cartwright\uc2  in Pennsylvania. House Minority Leader \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  says the House, "Is very much in play." He touts potential seats to flip in Oregon and California, it may be days before the House is called, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chad Pergram live on Capitol Hill. Cha, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, our panel of pollsters and consultants breaks down what really happened on Election Day.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: America has given us an unprecedented and powerful mandate.\par \par \uc2 Winning the popular vote was very nice. North Carolina, I love these places.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Georgia, Pennsylvania, and Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 We have a great feeling of love in this very large room.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Now President-elect Trump reacting, and the headlines are reacting to all of this. "Wall Street Journal", "Stocks soar, dollar jumps as Trump's comeback reverberates through markets." "Trump's win is his greatest resurrection in a career of comebacks." "Opinion, Trump is president again and Democrats can blame Biden's ego." All kinds of thoughts about why.\par \par \uc2 But let's get some analysis on the whole thing from our guests, Democratic pollster Carly Cooperman, CEO of Schoen-Cooperman Research, and political consultant and co-founder and chairman of Purple Strategies, Alex Castellanos.\par \par \uc2 OK, Carly, as we tick through these states, this is the Real Clear Politics of average and then the actual vote, it looks like pretty much across the board Trump was undercounted again by two or three points.\par \par \uc2 CARLY COOPERMAN, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: That's right. Trump was undercounted again. But I would actually argue that the polls were a lot closer this time --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Than they were.\par \par \uc2 COOPERMAN: -- than we have seen in previous cycles. The averages in all of those battleground states were virtually tied, these candidates going into the election. And it looks like Trump is going to win them by about one to two points, at least in the five states that have already been called. And this is well within the margin of error. So I would actually argue that for the first time in three cycles, polls in these states were actually pretty close to where things --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right, and we had the caveat that if one candidate got one or two points that they could tip the whole thing, which is exactly what happened.\par \par \uc2 COOPERMAN: That's exactly right. I've been asked today, did you see a big red wave coming? And I'm like, this is what can happen when you have several states, all of these states that can go by one or two points. But if they are going to go that way with one single candidate, then the impact is just a really tremendous, a much bigger win than --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alex, I will give you credit. You on this set said a wavelet was coming. I didn't you went full wave, but you went wavelet. And you saw it because of the issues and what you were seeing and hearing from your folks.\par \par \uc2 ALEX CASTELLANOS, POLITICAL CONSULTANT: Yes, I think so. I wasn't courageous enough to say wave. If I had, I should have gone to Las Vegas and put some money on it.\par \par \uc2 But no, when you look at the numbers, Trump really did something remarkable. It wasn't just he won an election. He has initiated a realignment, working class realignment of working class whites, Hispanics, and black Americans. That's a very different Republican Party than we have seen. And you could see that in the numbers with independents in states like Pennsylvania were tilting to Trump, increasing Republican registration. And I think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  delivered on that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 ," Carly, the quote, "\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  told Americans exactly what he planned to do. And when asked to give him power to do all of that, the voters said yes. This was a conquering of the nation, not by force, but with a permission slip. Now America stands on the precipice of an authoritarian style of governance never before seen in its 248-year history." Kind of overwritten there, but there's a lot of people saying, trying to interpret who the Trump voter was and what they cared about. Can't we say that they cared about the economy and immigration, and they are just not into the woke stuff?\par \par \uc2 COOPERMAN: I think it's absolutely we're hearing from voters that they're unhappy with the cost of living. They care about the economy. Inflation is problematic, and it really comes down to how expensive their day-to-day life is.\par \par \uc2 I also think one of the things that came out from the exit polls is that what voters said they wanted in a presidential candidate -- or a president was a strong leader and someone that was going to bring about change. And Trump won among voters who said those things by 66 percent, 75 percent of the voters supported him on those marks. And so, clearly, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was not able to embrace and convince voters that she was the agent of change. They're unhappy with how things are right now and believe Trump's message that things were better when he was president.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alex, his numbers went up with suburban women versus Harris instead of Biden, up with blacks, Hispanics, to your point, and different wages. And it sounded, in the exit polls and our FOX News voter analysis, that they were saying listen to us. We're telling you what we want.\par \par \uc2 CASTELLANOS: And that's why "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " story, blaming the voters is exactly the wrong analysis of this thing. And what shocked me today is that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in her alleged concession speech did the same thing. She said -- this was the most anti-concession concession speech I have ever heard, because it was anti-unity. What she did, she got up there and said, listen, we are going to keep fighting for the same things we fought for in this election. The campaign is going to go on. It was trying to cement the division in the country. And what that is saying is, voters, you just didn't just get it. Our wonderful ideas are superior to yours, and we're going to keep this country divided.\par \par \uc2 That's terrible politics for the Democratic Party. After a big loss like this, sometimes you see parties reduce themselves to their core and you get in primary fights where the candidates try to see who is the purest. It just shrinks them and makes them negative. And I think that is what is happening to the Democratic Party now. It's a big mistake, but not only that. It's terrible for the country.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  put out a scathing letter, statement. Meantime, very quickly, Carly, \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 , a tweet, "Big congratulations to our 45th and now 47th president on an extraordinary political comeback and decisive victory. No nation has bigger opportunities. Wishing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  all success in leading and uniting the America we all love." He's a businessman, one of the biggest. And the markets loved what they saw last night today.\par \par \uc2 COOPERMAN: Right. I'm not surprised to see Bezos said that after notoriously "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " withdrew its endorsement. So obviously people are making decisions based on business sense. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made it very clear that he is going to take revenge on the people that did not support him, so I understand why Bezos is doing this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. We'll see what happens. Alex, Carly, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 COOPERMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, we look at what happened with the issue of abortion, which was on almost a dozen state ballots. And, later, our panel recaps election night and where we go from here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Abortion was a big issue in many locations around the country. It was on the ballot in nearly a dozen states. Correspondent Mark Meredith has a round up tonight of this issue. Good evening, Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you. Voters in 10 states chose whether abortion should be limited or protected in their communities. The results were mixed. Even though Democrats made abortion one of if not their biggest issue this entire cycle, in seven states last night, voters approved efforts to either constitutionally protect abortion or reject previously imposed limits on the procedure. Planned Parenthood Political Action Group wrote, Banning abortion and restricting access to essential care is a losing issue. Voters chose freedom."\par \par \uc2 But in three states, Florida, Nebraska, and South Dakota, voters chose do leave abortion restrictions in place. Pro-life leaders telling FOX tonight they plan to spend more time and energy getting voters to enact similar restrictions.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARJORIE DANNENFELSER, SUSAN B ANTHONY PRO-LIFE AMERICA: So I definitely see new ballot initiatives ahead. We now have got our footing. We know what to do. We know that governors have to be in charge and the legislatures in their state have to own that. And we will need to go back to some states.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: According to our FOX News voter analysis, abortion was among the top three issues on voters' minds this cycle. But it ranked far below the economy and immigration.\par \par \uc2 Now, during the campaign, President-elect Trump vowed not to sign a national abortion ban even if Congress were to pass one. But with conservatives seeing gains in Congress, it's possible some lawmakers may try to bring up the issue again for debate. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Mark, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's take a look at how election night was covered in the media. FOX News media analyst and host of FOX's MEDIA BUZZ, Howard Kurtz has our story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOWARD KURTZ, FOX NEWS HOST: It's no secret that most of the media rooted for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and relentlessly criticized \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are there enough checks and balances in place to protect democracy if Trump gets from and behaves like a fascist dictator?\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: So when the president-elect not only cruised to victory but appears to have won the popular vote, much of the coverage was grudging or worse. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " "America Hires a Strongman." "Huff Post, "The Nightmare Returns."\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What concerns me is I don't think he cares about really solving problems except for the most expedient one of not going to jail.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That message obviously was not enough to get enough white women to vote for Vice President Harris, a fellow woman.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I can't help but wonder if the American people have given up on democracy.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Others tried to grapple with the strong message sent by voters.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS SCHOEN, FORMER CLINTON ADVISER: Fundamentally, this is a rejection of the Democratic Party, my party, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , and a leftwing ideology.\par \par \uc2 JON STEWART, COMEDIAN: We're all going to have to wake up tomorrow morning and work like hell to move the world to the place we prefer it to be.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: And some blame the media for exaggerating Harris's chances.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I also feel like this election, as we sit here and poor over this tonight, is something of an indictment of the political information complex. We have been sitting around here for the last couple of weeks, and the story that was portrayed was not true.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: If the press once rationalized Trump's 2016 wins as a fluke, the same can't be said for yesterday's triumph when he made gains with black and Latino voters. Trump often gave his detractors fodder, from insulting Harris' intelligence to talk of retribution to ripping the news business, which he called the enemy of the people. But the obsessive coverage of his four indictments and New York conviction and quickly moving on from two assassination attempts reminded many voters of why they distrust the press.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 KURTZ (on camera): It's hardly unusual for journalists relying on flawed polls to botch campaign predictions. The larger problem here is that the media misjudged the country and need to grapple with how that happened. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Definitely. Howie, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, so what now? Our panel comes back, has post-election breakdown.\par \par \uc2 And then with politicians fighting like cats and dogs, some voters, they just had a welcome surprise.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: This will forever be remembered as the day the American people regained control of their country.\par \par \uc2 REP. DEBBIE DINGELL, (D-MI): I'm going to be very blunt. As Democrats, we've got to do some soul searching. We are getting the message from a lot of people that we're not hearing them.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The thing that people should have looked at, he was always carrying 41 percent of the Hispanics, and 25 percent of the black males.\par \par \uc2 HARRY ENTEN, CNN SENIOR POLITICS WRITER AND ANALYST: It's across the map. It's across different parts of the Latino electorate. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , simply put, doing better than he did four years ago.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A lot of analysis today. Two covers two covers, "New York Post", "He's Don It Again." And "Newsweek", "Trump, The Comeback." Meantime, Cook Political Report, "Trump's sweep brings a likely GOP trifecta to Washington. At this point the most likely outcome is a GOP trifecta including a narrow Republican House majority. Yet given the breadth of Trump's triumph, it's remarkable many down ballot Democrats are performing as well as they are, outrunning Harris. For example, there are five Democratic Senate candidates who still have paths to victory in states Trump is on track to win. Democrats won multiple row offices in North Carolina."\par \par \uc2 As you look at the screen, we have these races that are popping up. And you see the Senate majority. You can see some of the individual races below. And that will stay up.\par \par \uc2 We'll bring back our panel, Brit, Martha, Kayleigh, and Jessica. Brit, a couple of those races, the Senate races, went to the Democrats. In Wisconsin, \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2 , in Michigan, \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2  over \uc2 Mike Rogers\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HUME: Close.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Close, very close. Still a couple yet to go. Nevada, Arizona. It looks like Arizona is going to go to the Democrats, and Nevada may actually go to the Republicans. Which would be one more. Pretty significant.\par \par \uc2 HUME: It is pretty significant it wasn't necessarily forecast, although the map did favor the Republicans in this cycle. And the House outcome, of course, is a chance here for the Republicans. They could hold it, but if they do hold it, it's going to be a margin at least virtually as close as it's been maybe a little closer. So, it will be rowdy over there.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I should point out that these, the counts in some of these states are continuing, and it's not -- the secretaries of state are saying there is nothing fishy. It's about doing absentee ballots, sometimes it takes some time. Some of these states like California, Arizona, Jessica, take a long time.\par \par \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , big statement today. "It should come as no great surprise that a Democratic Party which has abandoned working class people would find that the working class has abandoned them." I think Senator Sanders is going to go on a tear again.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Always. You can always depend on Bernie for one of these postmortems after something like this happens. I would say that while I think he definitely has a point and you see that borne out in the results, President Biden walked the picket line, right? He was out there with the auto workers. He negotiated for new pensions for the teamsters. This is man who has stood by the working class.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But he wasn't on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Well, either she ended up losing because she was part of the Biden administration, or she didn't. She was part of that as well. I'm sure that President Biden would have performed better with that cohort. Not enough to overcome Trump's numbers with this. But I think it's a little bit unfair with how supportive this administration has been of those groups. But Bernie has been making this pointed since 2016 where he feels that he was shoehorned out of the primary process because the DNC wanted Hillary to be the nominee.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's not a bad argument. He kind of was.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It is not. I have it with my husband basically every time he wants to set me off. He's like, you know what happened to \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 . I'm like, don't do it!\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Don't do it. Kayleigh?\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: I think \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  diagnosed a problem but provided little solution. I think with the second Trump administration you are going to see progress for working class families. You've heard him say drill baby drill. I think on day one opposite of what Biden did, rolling back the Keystone XL pipeline. He's going to unleash American energy.\par \par \uc2 I think the number one legislative priority is going to be economics, some of those tax promises that were made. And when it comes to immigration, I think you're going to see him leverage tariffs, potentially at least the threat of them against the president of Mexico and make big progress there just on the executive.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: With the House and Senate you could do reconciliation which requires only 50 votes. You don't need the 60, and you could do a lot of stuff economically right away.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You could. And I think that you are going to see a big push on the border. You're probably going to hear a lot of discussion about the wall. And I think that has to be their first priority.\par \par \uc2 Just one other thing on \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 . The issue is that, you know, he has always fought for organizing, right, and unions and leadership. But we saw in this race that President Trump stood up there with members of the union behind him who were in disagreement with their leadership.\par \par \uc2 HUME: And let's be clear about \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 . His complaints about the administration not being strong enough with the working people, it is about not being liberal enough to suit him.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right.\par \par \uc2 HUME: And those kinds of policies are among the things that union workers and other working class people are turning away from. So his point stands if you don't look at it too closely.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That too, and then early in the Biden administration, the influence that \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  and Elizabeth Warren had in policy affected how Biden was perceived.\par \par \uc2 HUME: You could say that it was \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's administration but \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 's agendas.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. How long does it take the Democratic Party, Jessica, to come to this postmortem and figure out what to do next? A long time? Like grief, are we going to go through the five, the stages of --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes, but I think it's happening pretty fast because it was so widespread. Again, I think if this had been something small, that you could say if I would have tweaked. We should know that the margins are actually pretty slim in this. It's 1.1 percent. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is underperformed his vote total from 2020. She underperformed Biden by --\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: He won the popular vote for the first time in 20 years.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I'm not dismissing what he accomplished. I'm saying he has 72 million votes now and he got 74 million in 2020. So it will take a little bit of time. The question is, will we be ready for the midterms if they do have the trifecta. That usually then favors the people who lost. So we should do really well there. And how long are these voters going to stay with the Republican Party? Is the bro vote gone forever? What about the Latino coalition that is usually part of the Democratic base.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And how do you get it back?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: "The Federalist," "2024's biggest losers, the corporate media industrial complex. If the past eight years and the first few hours of cope streaming from T.V. Wednesday morning are any indication, the legacy press isn't planning on repentance. They don't feel obliged or obligated to represent Americans. They feel entitled to control them. The loss of that control is making them apoplectic." The bullhorn is gone, Martha. They're not listening.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I saw it was shock. Hunker down and fight back. They're continuing the language of basically fascism and how do we fight back against Trump. One thing I thought was interesting, though, is that this election, no one is disputing the outcome, which I think is very healthy for the country. One commentator this morning on MSNBC said we lost this election, and it had nothing to do with Russia.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Just to be clear.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Clean break.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And I thought, well, that's refreshing.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's refreshing.\par \par \uc2 HUME: One thing about the legacy media, Bret, and that is I think it made it easy for Democrats not to face up to what the voters were saying to them in 2016 and in certain elections since then, including last night. And if the legacy media would start facing up to these things and reported it, we would -- the Democrats would have easier time waking up to it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Thanks, panel. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a special day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Voting can be a stressful time for a lot of people. We also want to celebrate everyone doing their civic duty and coming out to vote. So we thought what better way than bringing some puppies out to the polls.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, we just decided we needed puppies at the end of the show. The Arizona Humane Society went out and they helped people, surprised voters. They were feeling stressed about the election, and they had all kinds of terrier mixes and all kinds of puppies. We decided, you know what, we are going to end this election coverage with puppies, because we all need puppies.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, thanks for tuning in. You watched us the most. We really appreciate it. Fair, balanced, and unafraid. Here's Laura.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020241107ekb600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Fox News Democracy, Coverage Of The 2024 U.S. election\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Martha MacCallum, Bill Hemmer, Madison Alworth, Sandra  Smith, Laura Ingraham, Karl Rove, Dana Perino, Brit Hume, Jonathan Serrie,  Nate Foy, Carl Rove, Garrett Tenney, Harris Faulkner, Steve Harrigan,  Kellyanne Conway \par \uc2 10,469 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 November 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Live Event\par \uc2 FXLV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS HOST: And it is 7 o'clock on the East Coast, and polls have just closed in half a dozen states. The Fox News decision desk is making its first calls. The most closely watched state at this hour is Georgia, one of seven battleground states that could decide the presidency. That one obviously is too early to call.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: And we'll dig into those numbers soon. It's also too early to call Virginia, though Vice President Harris has a lead in our Fox News voter analysis, waiting for the raw vote total to dump there. Fox News can project that former President Trump will win the state of Indiana.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Trump will also carry nearby Kentucky for a third time, and it is too early to call the state of South Carolina, but former President Trump has the lead there, according to our Fox News voter analysis.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Meanwhile, Vice President Harris will sail to victory in liberal Vermont, and as we look at the race for 270, electoral votes, that's what it takes to win throughout the night, we will be adding up as each state is called right now, you see 19 electoral votes for the former President, three for the Vice President, and we are going to be following all of this throughout the night, and also on the bottom of the screen, you can see these races as we get the raw vote total in.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, and that's going to start happening very soon. But let's turn to the Senate for a moment, because this is another big story. Control of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , Democrats have almost no room for error. The Fox News decision desk can project that Indiana Republican Congressman Jim Banks will win his first Senate race.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In Virginia, it is too early to call the race between incumbent Democratic Senator Tim Kaine and his Republican challenger, Hung Cao. We're going to be looking into Virginia.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Long time independent Senator Bernie Sanders will win a fourth term in the Senate in Vermont, and he caucuses, of course, with the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As we look at the balance of power in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , you can see we'll be ticking up. You need 51 for the majority. The Republicans need to pick up two to take the majority, and they're looking at states like West Virginia and Montana, but there could be others on this board. Right now, as you see, 39 Republicans to 29 Democrats.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Lot of toss-ups sit in that group as well in the Senate races across the country. Let's go to Bill Hemmer and get an update from the board.\par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, FOX NEWS HOST: Hey, guys. We've just seen a little of this vote come in from Florida. So why don't we go ahead and start there, and I'm just going to punch around see a little bit of what we're getting here. We know the turnout was big. We know Republicans based on some of the early voting, and the voting today was pretty decent here, but 8 percent of the statewide vote.\par \par \uc2 This is Duval County, Jacksonville. I haven't seen this yet, just go ahead and -- so you got about two-thirds of the vote coming in, in Florida right now. Florida counts fast, by the way, by the early votes, they rack it up. And so right here in Duval County, you got \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  leading by -- I'll tell you what we do, why don't we do this? It's very interesting, because for one of the first times in a long time in anybody's memory, I guess, going back to FDR the late 1800s we've got a candidate on the ballot where you can compare the number from four years ago and compare the number from eight years ago.\par \par \uc2 So, we're going to do this a lot throughout the night, we'll get a measure about how Trump is performing versus Hillary Clinton and versus \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . So let's go back eight years now. Just watch his number here with two- thirds of the vote reporting in Florida. There's a one county in Jacksonville, so all right, here we go. Trump was just shy of 50 against \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  in that county, and then four years ago, he was a little less than that, about two and a half shy of the midway mark and overall Duval, so he's doing it right about the same number he did with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  four years ago. So let's look at Volusia County.\par \par \uc2 OK, you got a little more of the vote in here. Trump, easily nearly 60 percent of the vote, 77 percent reporting, Flagler 86 percent reporting as well. So we expect them to do quite well in Florida. Let me move away from this, because what I wanted to show you in a moment here is, we're just getting a flavor right now of perhaps what the suburban vote is doing for Trump versus Harris, and then what the rural votes doing.\par \par \uc2 And what I did was, I want to pick out two places here, because there's a county just north of Indianapolis. This is Hamilton County. Population about 350,000, 360,000 about two-thirds of the vote coming in right now. What I want to show you here is Trump's at 48.8 now, there's more vote out there. Indiana counts the mail in ballots first. So, let's do the same exercise. OK?\par \par \uc2 This time, watch the slide come out here and now it gives us a direct comparison for eight years ago against \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . This is the weakness in the Trump campaign, right? Eight years ago, he was just shy of 57 percent in the same county, and then four years ago, got slightly weaker. OK? Down about two points, and then current day today. OK, so there's more vote out there, so we'll see how it goes. But right now, a little weaker there in that suburb just north of Indianapolis, conversely, right? Trump's been working the rules, right?\par \par \uc2 Well, this is Kentucky. You got a lot of the votes still out there, even though we called the race. OK? This is Taylor County. It is rural. It is small. There's about 10,000 to 12,000 raw votes there. Trump's percentage of the vote is big, at 77 percent, so let's do the same exercise again. Go back to 2016, the percentage of the vote, OK, it was lower. Four years ago in 2020, same county now, right around 75 percent and as of tonight, OK, by the way, raw vote, 9376 hold on to that number. OK. It's important because he's doing better on a percentage basis, but the raw vote doesn't help him a ton. That may suggest, as we pop around the map throughout the night, that the turnout for this vote could be lower than 2020, that's a COVID year, a pandemic year.\par \par \uc2 Lot of people vote by mail, et cetera, and we'll see whether or not we're able to top that number from 2020, but it's questionable and debatable at the moment. If I've got time, I'll just show you Florida. Just one more time here. 30 percent in. Miami is missing. You know, we're waiting for the Hispanic vote down there in Miami, the Cuban vote as well. Broward County, 70 percent in, heavily Democratic there, but about 60 percent of the vote right now going for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Trump team is saying that they're getting on the radio right now. They're cautiously optimistic. They're doing a radio in Pennsylvania, Wisconsin, and Michigan, all the way at the top. That means the former President telling people there's still time and finish strong. That's the message from Mar-a-Lago right around down here in Palm Beach. Back to you, Bret and Martha.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You have time, Bill. We're going to have a lot of time.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Roger that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We have a lot of waiting to this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, Georgia is really crucial.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As we look at this raw vote total, and they're also getting a lot that we expect to come in very fast, Madison Alworth is actually in Norcross, Georgia with the view from the ground. Hey Madison.\par \par \uc2 MADISON ALWORTH, FOX CORRESPONDENT: Hey Bret. Hey Martha. So yes, polls have closed at a majority of locations here in Georgia, including location that I'm at. I can actually see from my vantage point that at least one person has tried to come in past 07:00 p.m. and they are turning them away because this poll location, like most closed at 7, but there are a few because of issues that will be open past 07:00 p.m., so five locations in Fulton County, one here in Gwinnett County, those six had issues with unsubstantiated threats, so they were closed for a certain amount of time during the day. They've been allowed to be open for that amount of time past 7.\par \par \uc2 We also have seven precincts in DeKalb. That's another area in the Atlanta metro area that had bomb threats. We're still waiting to see if those locations are going to be allowed to be extended past that 07:00 p.m. window. So majority results are coming in. We've talked to the Georgia Secretary of State, \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2 , a number of times. He has reiterated he's really confident that majority results will be reported by 08:00 p.m. And now, when it comes to the -- how close it is here, recounts are not necessary, but if it's within point 5 percent, a candidate can request it. We're going to start to get those results really any minute now. Guys, I'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Madison, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, let's go to Sandra Smith, who is looking at these Fox News voter analysis surveys and picking up some information about different voter groups out there. What are you learning? Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS HOST: Which could be telling a big story right now, and this is the first time you will see this, and the first time since the polls have now closed, begun to close there, that we can break this down by candidate. OK, first up, timing of your vote. We know so many went out for early voting. Let's see how those votes are breaking down in Georgia, those who cast their vote early, look at this. They did not prefer a candidate if they early voted. It is split, 49 for Harris, 49 for Trump.\par \par \uc2 All right, let's look at some of these other key data points. We know race was huge. The black vote was huge for Biden when he won by just a bit in Georgia there in 2020, so let's look at how they are turning out in this election. All right. This is the first time we can break this down among black voters and their support for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , while strong, she is below where Biden was in 2020, four years ago. This number for Biden was more like 91, so she's running about eight points behind where Biden was with the black vote in Georgia in 2020.\par \par \uc2 All right, I have another data point where I'm going to combine this with gender. OK. So now you look at the black male voter in Georgia. We know both candidates have spent a lot of time courting them there. Democrats have been trying to convince them that they could be better for the economy. Trump has been trying to woo those voters. Remember, inflation is more than 20 percent in that state with black men, Harris is at 73 percent, Trump at 25 percent.\par \par \uc2 Let me give you perspective and context on that. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , in 2020 had this number at about 87 percent, OK, so right now she's running about 13 points with black men behind where \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was in 2020. All right, another data point there, Bret, because I know you asked. Black women as well in Georgia, we had been watching this, here it is, strong showing 89 percent, but let me give you the perspective on that. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  with black women in Georgia four years ago was at 95 percent of their support. She is also running behind with them by about six points.\par \par \uc2 I'll leave you off with one other data point here. Again, this is the first time we are able to show you this. Education and race, whites with no college degree in Georgia, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , a strong showing there, he's at 77 getting 77 percent of their support. So we have a lot more data coming in, and we will continue to bring that to you. Bret, Martha.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Those are really interesting numbers --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Sandra, thank you. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: -- to tell the story, or story they start to.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, so let's bring in our panel and see what they think about these numbers. Brit Hume, Dana Perino, Harold Ford Jr., Karl Rove, Laura Ingraham and Robert Wolf. Laura, welcome. Great to have you here. She's underperforming but is the headline there.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Yes, it looks like right now, Harris isn't getting enough from women, at least in Georgia that she would need to overcome what Trump is getting from men. Both increase in support from black men and men overall. This is -- this is going to be the question throughout the night, because the Trump people and all their surrogate groups have tried to really mobilize men. We talked about this, this afternoon, Martha. I mean, from Joe Rogan to \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and all the other people down the line, they are trying to turn out that mail vote.\par \par \uc2 I would keep an eye on Virginia, and Karl knows this better than everyone else. But in 2020 in Virginia, Trump lost by 9.2 and in 2016, he lost by five, 4.9. Watch what happens in Virginia. Youngkin has been phenomenal on the ground for Trump, and if -- even if, that lead is cut by half, let's say it's four points that could be a bellwether of something to come. Also in New Hampshire, where I spent four long winters.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: See what happens in New Hampshire was very tight in 2016.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Robert, welcome to you as well. You know the state of Georgia. Biden flipped Georgia for the first time since 1992 largely because of the black electorate. Looking at those numbers right now, it doesn't seem like she's delivering that same kind of power.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT WOLF, FORMER CHAIRMAN AND CEO, \uc2 UBS AMERICAS\uc2 : Yes, Those numbers seem soft, although I would tell you I was predicting North Carolina over Georgia for the Vice President, because I think Stein will be like plus 15 versus Robinson --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The governors.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: -- the governors. And I think that may help the Vice President, but these are soft numbers, and I thought with Warnock and Ossoff, two senators that are popular, we would have maybe seen a better turnout. So I'm hoping this is just the early voting.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, not all the data, obviously is in yet. Karl, what's your read on it?\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, first of all, we're looking at half of the problem. The problem is that we're looking at is what's the division of the black vote by men and women, Trump versus Harris. The other thing we need to look at is turnout. A 1 percent decline in black turnout in Georgia over the last election would wipe out the 10,000 vote margin that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  had. So, we're seeing part of the picture. Let's see if it's compensated or offset a little bit by how much the black turnout is, is it bigger than last time or smaller than last time?\par \par \uc2 I'd make one other point, the other part of the Georgia equation that we're going to have to watch. We have a little bit of evidence from, and I mean a little bit of evidence from Indiana that we ought to be worried about if the Trump campaign ought to be worried about, and that is the suburban support. Hamilton County went Republican four years ago. Trump won 52 to 45 tonight with about two-thirds of the vote in, he's losing it to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , 50 to 44, excuse me 50 to 48. So, he's running behind his performance. If that happens in -- excuse me, in Georgia, with these suburban counties like Forsyth and Cherokee, Gwinnett, which was won by Biden, but it has a huge number of, biggest number of Republican voters in the state Gwinnett, if you remember.\par \par \uc2 Hamilton County, 34 percent of the Republican primary voters in May voted for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  when she had no chance of winning. We're seeing that effect tonight, with the county being blue, at least at this point.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Harold, it's just worth pointing out that the Fox News voter analysis is this rolling poll over several days, 120,000 voters asked a series of questions. When we look at that data that Sandra talked about with the raw vote total coming in, we have a sense of how accurate or not accurate it is. So far, it's lining up. Your thoughts where you are.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR. FORMER U.S. CONGRESSMAN: So, I think Karl's point is right. I think one of the things I would love to see in Georgia is not just racial breakdown, but just breakdown of women versus men. Because if indeed he underperforming the suburban areas in Indiana, you could extrapolate perhaps that happens in Georgia, but I would agree, if these numbers hold up, if he's polling, he being President Trump, 25 percent amongst black men, that's a challenge.\par \par \uc2 If that -- if you see that in the other big cities, in the blue states, Detroit and Philadelphia, that could be an issue as you think about the tightness of this race. But this campaign, the Harris campaign has always said that it's women. They're going to be the key here. I agree with Robert, I think the North Carolina piece that the Stein, Robinson race, that gubernatorial race will have an advantage for Harris. But this is early, she's still ahead in Georgia. And I think once we get some more of these numbers in, once you get to 10 percent, 15 percent, 20 percent, I think we'll have a little bit better sense of whether or not this 25 percent African American male thing is, is as serious as it seems.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We have on the screen. You know, you have the numbers, and then the you look at the percent of precincts in right now, it's at 3 percent, so as you look at the screen, that's what you'll be seeing throughout the night.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, one of the things that the Harris campaign, which Harold just touched on Dana, is that they're hoping that they're going to make it up with moderate, some moderate GOP voters that will be persuaded over to the Harris camp. What's the feel for that at this stage of the game?\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS HOST: I would be skeptical that there is a lot of that kind of vote out there. So, Governor Kemp had some problems with President Trump for a while, but they patch those things up. And I believe that as Youngkin has been very good for President Trump and Virginia, that Kemp has an amazing operation there in Georgia, and he has been tireless out there for him. And I also think that we should thank Georgia for doing what it needed to do in order to help get us these results in as quickly as possible, to have integrity in the vote for people to have confidence that the system worked for them, that they could get through those lines quickly, and that the rules are the rules, and that, I think, will help people feel confident about the results.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brit, the economy is the number one issue in Georgia, by far.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS CHIEF POLITICAL ANALYST: It's evident.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's evident, but pretty much leads the way in our Fox News voter analysis. They give -- they break Trump's way significantly on the issue of the economy.\par \par \uc2 HUME: That's important. The other thing we have to keep an eye on throughout the night, it seems to me, or what to use, a piece of newly minted jargon we have, is just the low propensity voters.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You hate that word.\par \par \uc2 HUME: I think it's awful jargon, but I don't -- can't think of a quickly better way to say it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: People who don't vote very often.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Trump campaign has really set a great deal of store by the fact that they believe that they have reached these people and will turn them out in significant numbers.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But explain who they are. There are people who didn't vote in 2020 or maybe even didn't vote in 2016 and then 2020.\par \par \uc2 HUME: And they're the kind of people they think are lousy for Trump, if you ask them, right? And that if they can turn them out, they think they could be the decisive factor in this election. We don't know enough now, I don't think to assess how that's going, but that's something to keep an eye on.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: We might have an indication of it. If you look at some of these Georgia rural counties have been watching Bill work on his wall. If you take a look at some of these rural counties, small counties, there are 150, 180, 159 counties in Georgia, you'll see that Trump is getting a bigger number of votes in the smaller counties and a bigger percentage. This is, again, goes back to the episodic, or low propensity voter.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, we're going to dig into these numbers, and we'll head back to Bill momentarily. Election Night, just getting started.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Some of the first polls are about to close in hotly contested state of North Carolina, so we're going to have some data there to show you, coming up right after this break as our special coverage continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Watching all the branches tonight, so control of the Senate could be decided as well in the state, potentially of Ohio. Democrat incumbent \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  fighting to hold on to his long held seat against GOP newcomer \uc2 Bernie Moreno\uc2  in one of the years closely watched races and a very expensive race. Let's check in with Nate Foy, who is live in Strongsville, Ohio, where the polls are about to close. Hey, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey guys, so yes, more than $400 million in ad spending in this Senate race, and a few people still coming in here to vote just minutes before the polls closed at 07:30 and voter turnout has been strong. More than 2.5 million Ohioans voted early this morning at 05:00 a.m. there was a line stretching around this entire building with people ready to vote, and it really reflects what's at stake.\par \par \uc2 17 electoral votes. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is expected to carry the state for a third time. But as you mentioned, the race that everybody is watching is between the incumbent Democratic Senator \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  and GOP challenger \uc2 Bernie Moreno\uc2 . Now Senator Brown has campaigned seeking a fourth term, trying to make this about reproductive rights, which are enshrined in Ohio's constitution, and for that reason, Moreno told us last night, it should be a non-issue in this race, and he says that Senator Brown is turning abortion into a political weapon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BERNIE MORENO\uc2  (R), OHIO SENATE CANDIDATE: There is no ability federally to be able to handle abortion at the federal level, one way or the other. So what I'm going to focus on as United States Senator is to bring people together on this issue.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: And we will see the impact that former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has down the ballot. Keep in mind, no Democratic senator has won an election in a state that Trump carried while Trump was on the ballot. We'll see if that changes tonight. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Nate Foy with the golf voice inside the polling area, that was nice.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He said there weren't that many people out there.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Whisper.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I wish he felt like he had to be quiet.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, I think so. Battleground North Carolina also among the next batch of poll closings. A tight presidential race in a state Trump won twice. Many voters there still recovering, obviously from Hurricane Helene. Senior Correspondent Jonathan Serrie is live in Raleigh. Hi. Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Good evening, Bret and Martha. Most of the polls throughout the state are going to close just minutes from now, with the exception of two precincts that had technical problems that delayed voting this morning, the issue in Wilson County involved a printer cable, and in Burke County, it was a laptop that wouldn't boot up. In both instances, it involved equipment used for checking in voters, not the actual voting machines.\par \par \uc2 Other than that, elections officials say things have been moving pretty smoothly, including in the western part of the state, which was heavily damaged in Hurricane Helene, 710s with electric generators and voting machines are in place to accommodate Election Day voting in areas where normal voting sites were destroyed or inaccessible. Election officials say the state's voter turnout would already vote early voting records is on track to meet or exceed the record set in 2020 for total ballots cast. Back to you guys.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Jonathan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So let's take it around our panel. You know, we just heard a little bit about the abortion issue, Laura, and it's interesting just looking at our Fox News voter analysis, because we saw in 2022, in th