ed them one way or the other. And the other person who's with us tonight is a friend I've invited, polling expert \uc2 Lee Carter\uc2 , president of Maslansky and Partners. Warm welcome to you, Lee. You've got a big night.\par \par \uc2 LEE CARTER\uc2 , POLLSTER: Thank you. Big night. So excited to be here.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Yeah, we've been talking to voters and so on and so forth. And let's just start off with a show of hands real-quickly, because the numbers for early vote, 84 million, just now, and still counting all those mail-in ballots. So, it's huge, incredibly large, setting records this year. How many of you voted early?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Wow.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: That's a lot, right?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: It's a lot.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Yeah. And how many of you voted today? Yeah. Well, we would expect not as many, right? So how does that compare with what you find in your analysis?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Well, what we're looking at right now, they're saying about half people have early voted. And I think that's a -- you know, so we could be looking at record turnout. Now, there's some people who say it's going to be more than half have already voted early, but I think we're going to be looking at -- the numbers are showing that we're looking for a big night.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Yeah, that would be huge if that were the case, because 2020 really was the burst on that. OK, a quick rundown of what we'll be showing you tonight before we hear from the audience. Our team compiled key moments from the Trump and Harris campaigns. And Lee is showing some of those comments to American voters along the way.\par \par \uc2 And they use their computers to respond to those in real time. The lines on the screen that will show you their reaction, red representing Republicans, blue for Democrats, and gray for independents. And our first topic, we heard a lot of this fiery rhetoric, this from Vice President Harris.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: So, listen, so much is on the line in this election. And this is not 2016 or 2020. We can all see that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is even more unstable and more unhinged. And now he wants unchecked power.\par \par \uc2 And this time, and this time, there will be no one there to stop him. Not even the Supreme Court, which months ago, told the former president that he is essentially immune, no matter what he would do in the White House.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: I'm mostly watching the gray line because we've been told so much about independents and how important they are. What did you learn with this?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: So, you could really see Democrats responded favorably. But independents gave this a D, while Republicans gave it an F. And one of the things that we heard from independent voters is that Kamala was supposed to be this joyful candidate. She positioned herself right off the bat as somebody who was going to be different, bring us together. She said we have more in common than divides us.\par \par \uc2 And the more negative she got, more negative independent voters got towards her. And I think we saw that happen in the polls. She had a surge in the beginning and things sort of flatlined once she sort of turned very negative.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: All right. Here's one from former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT, 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: He's running on a campaign of demoralization. It's a campaign of hate. Their lives and her slanders are very shameful and really inexcusable. Today, we're going to talk about the real character of Kamala and a person who has no remorse for the anguish she's inflicted upon families all across America.\par \par \uc2 Kamala intends to conflict and keep this misery going. And she's going to keep it going as long as she can, because that's the only way she can get elected.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Now, you can see a very different reaction here than we had with the other voters. Democrats, not surprisingly, gave this an F. Independents kind of flatlined it, gave it a C. Republicans gave it an A. I think this kind of rhetoric is priced into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . They want to see him be tough. They want to see him be a fighter.\par \par \uc2 A lot of the things that he talked about were things that people agreed with. So, I think people give him more of a pass on the tough rhetoric than they do on Kamala.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: All right. Let's talk to some voters. I can't wait for this part. OK. So, Steve, you are a Democrat, and I understand you voted for Trump. And you live in the all-important battleground of Pennsylvania. So, tell me about your journey and what it's been like and why you made that decision as a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Well, Harris, for me, it was very simple. The issues for me are fighting anti-Semitism, being strong on our greatest ally, Israel, economic strength, fighting the border, and peace through strength. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , we have a four-year history of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as president. And he demonstrated all of that. And it was a no-brainer. It was been a very interesting reaction for people to see me demonstrably promote my rapport for Donald.\par \par \uc2 But I also have supported \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2 . So, that was sort of a double- edged whammy for me. But it was a no-brainer. And supporting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is an easy decision.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Yeah. As a Democrat, you said that people were reacting to that. I appreciate your answer. I appreciate everybody's time. We will hear more from our Voters' Voices throughout the night. Let's go back to Bret and Martha.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Harris. Thank you, Harris.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the clock is ticking down to one of the biggest poll closings of the night. Pennsylvania is about to finish the voting process. Our special coverage continues with Jesse Watters and \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back. We do have a call to make. The Fox News Decision Desk can now project that former President Trump will win the state of South Carolina. So, we are counting down the minutes to the next poll closing. And there you see the race to 270 ticking up on the former president's side at 32, 3 for Vice President Harris, as the states are called. We will continue to see that. And obviously, 270 is the magic number.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We got a long way to go. A lot of filling in of this board and these states. But some places are getting very interesting as we get closer to our next round of poll closings, which are coming up in just a little while. And that will include Florida, where the Trump campaign is watching the results tonight.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Steve Harrigan. He is at Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 's campaign headquarters in Bonita Springs, Florida, which has tightened up a little bit. That race has gotten interesting. Steve?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Martha, they're starting to cheer here. It's a pretty relaxed, confident mood. \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  told us earlier this was the first time he's ever run for state office, where he's actually been predicted to win. He said that's largely because of a strong performance in early voting, more than 8 million Floridians did vote early. That's about 50 percent of registered voters.\par \par \uc2 And we were out there today at one ballot station, and there were people lined up, hundreds of people, who stood two hours in the rain to cast their ballots. Some people expect counties could go as high as 90 percent voter turnout.\par \par \uc2 A couple of items could complicate things throughout the night. One is an abortion initiative on the ballot that would extend Florida's strict abortion law. The other would be to legalize recreational marijuana. Both of those items will need 60 percent to win. Martha, back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, Steve Harrigan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The delay on the TV, they just got it. They just saw it. We're back with our panel, and we add Kellyanne Conway, Jesse Waters, and Jessica Tarlov. Kellyanne, you see some other things that you're watching?\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Looking at some suburban bellwethers coming in. Hamilton, Indiana, 70 percent of the voters flipped back to President Trump. Loudoun County, Virginia, we know has really been trending toward the left. It's been a swing to the right by eight points compared to 2020. So, you look at something like that, you understand why Carl Rove today said it could be tight in Virginia. Georgia, Trump plus two, that's a place that I had said early on, Harris will have to do well there to compete in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Also, as Pennsylvania closes its polls, Bret, I think it's worth noting, Martha, that this is like political malpractice that she didn't pick \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  between the fracking, the school choice, the Jewish-Americans. But I think that we'll be talking about that tonight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We will look a lot at Pennsylvania. Election night in America just getting started.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FXLV000020241106ekb500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Polls Close In More Than A Dozen States\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Bret Baier, Jesse Watters, Jessica Tarlov,  Harold Ford, Jr., Bill Hemmer, Brit Hume, Aishah Hasnie, Sandra Smith,  Maria Bartiromo, Larry Kudlow, Charles Payne, Kellyanne Conway, Harris  Faulkner, Sean Hannity, Jacqui Heinrich, Alexandria Hoff, Dana Perino,  Kayleigh McEnany \par \uc2 11,016 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 November 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Live Event\par \uc2 FXLV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 VOICE OVER: Illinois, Tennessee, Florida, plus the critical battleground.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: If we win Pennsylvania, we win the whole thing. It's very simple.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Pennsylvania, it all comes down to this.\par \par \uc2 VOICE OVER: With 177 electoral votes on the line, we have the results as they come in. The moment is here.\par \par \uc2 America is watching,\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: And it is now eight o'clock in New York. Polls have just closed in over a dozen states. All eyes, of course, are on Pennsylvania, which could be the deciding state in this race, 19 electoral votes at stake.\par \par \uc2 Fox News Decision Desk says that this is obviously too early to call in Pennsylvania, as we will be watching that one really, until the bitter end.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF POLITICAL ANCHOR: So Fox News is projecting that former President Trump will win the state of Florida, putting him 30 electoral votes closer to the White House.\par \par \uc2 Too early to call a winner in New Hampshire, where Vice President Harris has an edge.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: In Maine, the Fox News Decision Desk says that it's too early to call the statewide race. Maine gives two electoral votes to the winner of the state and one vote to the presidential winner in each of their congressional districts.\par \par \uc2 Harris will win the first district, the rural second district, too early to call in the state of Maine.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In the Southeast, Fox News can project that Trump will win Tennessee, a long-time Republican stronghold, not a really surprise there; further south, the former president will again win Alabama. Big numbers there.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And the former president will win neighboring Mississippi. Trump will cruise to victory in Oklahoma as well, where Democrats haven't won a single county since 2000.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And Missouri will also deliver a win for the former president. Vice President Harris will also take some big victories this hour. The Fox News Decision Desk can project that the Vice President will win Illinois, adding 19 electoral votes to her tally.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: She will also win New Jersey, another reliably Democratic state. Some people have thought New Jersey might be a little bit tight, but we're already ready to call --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's your home.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- New Jersey, in my home state, exactly.\par \par \uc2 Voters in Massachusetts will deliver a clear victory to Harris as well. So no mystery in those states.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Staying in the northeast, Fox News can project that the vice president will win Connecticut. She will also win Maryland, that state worth 10 electoral votes. There is an interesting Senate race there.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Now, Harris will also take Rhode Island, another Democratic stronghold, and she has a lead in Delaware. President Biden's home state, of course, but it is too early to call in Delaware at this point.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The vice president will win the District of Columbia, which has never voted for a Republican and continues not.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the Electoral College and the race to 270, it obviously changes with all of these numbers. It is in the corner of your screen, 105 to Harris, 72 and there you see it full screen as we start to fill in this map, little by little, we're going to get closer to 270.\par \par \uc2 Remember, there's some big states, including California, all the way out in the West, that will adjust this number for the vice president.\par \par \uc2 We're back with our panel, Jessica, Jesse, and Kellyanne. We added you, Jesse, thoughts.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST, "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME": Are we sure about New Jersey?\par \par \uc2 I voted next to a guy this morning wearing a garbage bag, so I thought the state was a lock. I guess I was wrong about that. I also just heard from a person who was not going to vote, and then his girlfriend called him and said, if you don't vote for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , we're breaking up. So he just drove two hours to the polling station and waited two hours in line, so I still don't understand people.\par \par \uc2 And I'm not going to give you the county by county, because I don't have that in my wheelhouse, but I will say I was just watching something interesting in Georgia, where she's not performing as well with Black voters as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has flipped the script with Indies. He's up 11 with Independents, whereas Biden had beaten him by nine with Independents. So those are just interesting things to look at.\par \par \uc2 Other than that, if you look at these exit polling or the Fox News Voter Analysis, you think this would be a runaway \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  victory. I don't understand if only 13 percent of Americans feel they're getting ahead financially, that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is even competitive, and bravo to the Democrat machine to make this race competitive, because she came out of nowhere. No one knows who she is. She didn't run a very specific campaign, and she is making it, they are making it a very competitive race. We haven't called anything surprising, except for New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You were surprised.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I was.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jessi?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Jessica Tarlov, welcome. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CO-HOST, "THE FIVE": Yes. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Your thought so far on the evening.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Not surprised by New Jersey, and I'm sorry for you. You had been bragging about that one for months to me.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I was wrong once.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: One time. It's been interesting to see, obviously, what's going in the suburbs if this is a harbinger of a good night for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , that's where it's going to happen. Obviously, you need the urban turnout to be huge. And I think Philadelphia was a big story this day already, in the first hour of Election Day polling that they were smashing records. And we'll see where that all turns up.\par \par \uc2 But what I'm really focused on is seeing once all the exits are out, you know what ended up being the one, two, and three most important issues, because in Ann Selzer's final poll of Iowa, she had preserving democracy as number one, that also showed up in the Marist polling.\par \par \uc2 And if it is preserving democracy, then that bodes very well for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' evening. And if it goes preserving democracy, the economy and then abortion, that's definitely a good night, especially to your point, Bret, from a few minutes ago, where you said that she had closed the gap with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in terms of who's best to handle the economy and is leading on protecting the middle class and cares about people like me.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Harold, can we talk a second about Florida. You know, it wasn't close. We just called it for the former president. It is a big electoral vote haul. But if you think about it, you know, in elections past, Florida was a swing state.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD\uc2 , JR. FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yup.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In 2022, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  changes that and makes a red state. And even in Miami-Dade County, the Republican is taking home a vote, including Latino vote, which could bode something else beyond Florida.\par \par \uc2 FORD: DeSantis will probably be a big part of that story. It's important to note that they also had an abortion initiative on the ballot there in Florida, it looks like it's going to win, the right to an abortion issue, 57 to 43 almost like the same numbers that Trump won with, maybe a little bit better.\par \par \uc2 So people split the ticket there, those that were going out in the deep, perhaps some Republicans voted for that. But you've got a strong governor, like you have in Florida, you have one in North Carolina. She's going to need that governor, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , to help her get over the hump there. I mean, they've got a Democratic governor there and a Democratic that's now been elected.\par \par \uc2 I'm curious about these suburban areas. I listened to Karl and now Jessica, now Kellyanne. It may be that even Virginia and you were talking Brit, you look at the issue around LGBQT+, and how those issues have played in the Virginia School System there, and that Loudon County numbers, interesting number. Yet you see in some of the other suburban areas in Indiana, she has not performed quite as well.\par \par \uc2 So it's interesting to me to see, maybe these states are a little different now than voters are in the suburban areas.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill, you have Loudon County --\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Fairfax County, that's an ex-urban county.\par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: What was 2020?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Let's look at 2020 versus now.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Okay, I want to show you guys the Fox Insights that we have, and we can go into this county here. You see this green outline, so we can show you what the vote was at this point four years ago, okay. Let's go and click on this and find out together, okay, Loudoun County, with 96 percent of the vote reporting in that county, Trump's got a 3.3 jump higher than he was performing four years ago.\par \par \uc2 Now we'll clear this off here, how it computes here on the computer four years ago, that's a slip about four-and-a-half points for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in that county.\par \par \uc2 And Harold, maybe it is the issues in the schools, maybe it is the transgender issue that the Trump team pushed so much during this campaign, and maybe that's having an impact on that. But let's move away from Virginia. There's a couple interesting stories that you want to bring our viewers up to date on.\par \par \uc2 Just punching around this map here, you can find a little good news for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . You can find good news for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and I'm going to show you why here. Down in Florida, just look at Duval County and Jacksonville, okay. This is advantage Trump from where he was four years ago in Duval County. You see that? You see that swap? Flipped it by five points there.\par \par \uc2 And I want to go up to Georgia here and in the rules, east of Atlanta into the south. Now, the Fox Insight here, right. Okay, that's Baldwin County. The county seat in Baldwin County is Milledgeville. It was the Confederate capital of Georgia during the Civil War.\par \par \uc2 Now, our Fox Insight tell us right now, look at this, okay, it has two colleges in this county. The African American population is about 44 percent countywide. Trump right now, at this moment, with 84 percent of the vote reported in Baldwin County, he is up seven-and-a-half points from where he was in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Now the Trump team is going to take that; likewise, okay, the metro area of Atlanta, let me pop out here. You've got 26 counties to concentrate on. The Harris folks are telling us in Douglas County is that she's performing better than Biden did four years ago. These are suburban voters all over the Atlanta Metro area. So she has a net gain of 18,267 right now. You've got about a quarter of the vote still out there.\par \par \uc2 Biden finished in Douglas County with a net gain of 17,360 that would be good news for the Harris campaign, if this number holds there in Douglas County.\par \par \uc2 Okay, let me pop out here a moment and show you another thing here that we were looking at.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Can you stay in Georgia for one second?\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: I can.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Just before we leave there. The Fox News Voter Analysis and the network's exit polls, we're being told, show the former president doubling his percentage of Black men in Georgia. He was at 11 percent in 2020 and now at 25 percent according to these two surveys.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: So that may sync up with what --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So it is starting to match the raw vote.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: That may sync up with what we're seeing now in Fulton County, and you've got almost half the vote still out there, okay.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Fifty-two percent is the estimated number of votes that are in at the moment. Okay, so roughly 48 percent out there.\par \par \uc2 This is Fulton County. This is \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . She's going to win the county, okay, but look at the raw vote total here. It's impressive, 129,000. But look what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did in the same county, okay, 242,000. You'd have to go into the precincts to go to the precincts to know where the rest of the remaining vote comes from and maybe we'll take a look at that in a moment here.\par \par \uc2 But that would be, you know, you're one of 159 in terms of population. So maybe what you're saying, Bret, about the African American male vote there is true in places like Fulton County, Georgia. I just want to caution you, there's still a lot of vote out there, but we're going to keep a very close eye on Fulton County, starting right now into the night here in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And Bill, do you have any indication that turnout is a bit lower than 2020 because that would affect some of what you're doing in these comparisons as well.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: I can tell you this, Martha, before Election Day, \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2 , the Secretary of State, set up an awesome website. And I mean just county by county, with the early vote, I was on there every hour of every day, just trying to analyze it.\par \par \uc2 One thing that was evident from the early vote, I don't know about today just yet, but based on the early vote, the African American population in Georgia is about 33 percent statewide. What the political analysts will argue is that you need in order to win statewide as a Democrat, you need 30 percent of that vote, and the early vote indicated that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was just a tick above 26 percent, so that's another indicator, maybe another data point that we need to keep an eye on throughout the night as to what Brett was talking about a moment ago.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brit, what are you looking at?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, I've been keeping an eye on this issue that was raised in our voter analysis that said that fully half of all voters say the future of democracy is the most important factor in their vote. That's a big number that's a lot of people are worried about that.\par \par \uc2 Now, Trump gets some of those voters, but 60 percent of them are going for Harris. I think that issue, democracy is about one thing, about Trump and fear of Trump, it's the same. It's the same factor we keep coming back to time and again, is, you know, the Trump factor in this race is, is he able to overcome the resistance to him, which has been so dominant in the three previous cycles? And that's what that vote is about.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Because if you were just running on the issues that include the most important economy, immigration, you wouldn't see --\par \par \uc2 HUME: Right. You wouldn't see that, but, but yet, half the people are thinking that's important. That's a Trump issue.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It was a huge issue for 2020 and it also resurfaced a bit with candidate selection in 2022.\par \par \uc2 HUME: That's what he did.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That he got blamed for. So the question is, are we going to see the increase in his approval numbers translate into something positive.\par \par \uc2 HUME: And if you work your way back to what that's about, that's about January 6th.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Can I clarify something --\par \par \uc2 HUME: The price he paid politically for January 6th is still being paid.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Because --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It also was hammered over and over again by former president, by current President Biden, the former candidate. You know, the threat to democracy was really on his lips pretty much every minute.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And Vice President Harris didn't take that bowl for a lot, but she did come back to it towards the end of the campaign.\par \par \uc2 HUME: The other thing about it that I think is striking is it shows you the power of the media, because in many ways, a BS issue, when you think about it -- if you go back to January 6, which was supposed to be the moment when we all thought we should fear this, that our democracy was fragile. It was the premise of the January 6 special committee that that we -- as the co- chairman of the committee put it, we came critically close to losing our democracy.\par \par \uc2 It's ridiculous. I mean, our democracy is pretty sturdy. Our checks and balances worked. The thing was over in a matter of hours, and yet here we are, it is still a factor.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And here we are with a political rise from January 7, 2021 to a candidate who is at least tied and in a good position and has a path to 270 to win the White House.\par \par \uc2 Stand by panel, if you would.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Speaking of that, let's go check in with Aishah Hasnie at the Trump campaign headquarters. She is in West Palm Beach, Florida tonight. What's the mood over there? A long way to go tonight. But how are things looking -- Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Things are looking good here. The crowd behind me still hasn't really filled up the convention center, and that's because, I'm told that there's a long security line. Apparently, a lot of folks are at the Hilton party right next door and still haven't made their way over and that's because there's a really long security line. They've got to go through the mags before they get in here.\par \par \uc2 So that is what's holding up the people, the spaces that you see behind me, and why that hasn't filled up just yet.\par \par \uc2 I want to just talk about -- you guys were on the big screen when you called Florida in here, and everybody erupted into a cheer. I was just talking to Congressman Mike Waltz of Florida, the great state of the great state of Florida, and he was telling me how it was a record early vote turnout, and that's why, and he thinks that's going to resonate across the country today. People are going to come out and vote for Trump.\par \par \uc2 Back to you -- guys.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah, thanks. Let's check in with Jacqui Heinrich. She is at the Harris campaign headquarters in Washington, DC.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, guys.\par \par \uc2 It's quite a different scene out here compared to where Aishah is. I should note that at Harris headquarters, we don't actually have Harris campaign officials, because they're all in Wilmington tonight. So we're trying to get a sense from campaign officials about who was invited to this event, how many thousands will be here, and who's in the crowd.\par \par \uc2 But I am hearing from people close to the vice president sort of watching these reports come in. They started tonight, very anxious about Georgia, and the first batch of data they're getting is feeling -- leaving them feeling pretty buoyed about it.\par \par \uc2 A source close to the VP says that they're seeing really high turnout in the Metro Atlanta area, and as of a short while ago, they had a 10-plus point lead among women showing up, and also they noted that Black turnout was slow to start, but it is finishing strong, and they attribute that to their Get Out The Vote effort.\par \par \uc2 One thing they've been wrestling with is whether or not they underestimated Trump's Get Out The Vote effort. So far, they're not seeing signs of that, but they're definitely feeling happy about that first report coming out of Georgia -- guys.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Jacqui, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Before we make some race calls, Harold, you want to clarify some you said.\par \par \uc2 FORD: The 58 percent 42, the Florida abortion initiative, it didn't pass though, because you had to have 60 percent, so I misspoke, but it got 58 percent, but fell by two.\par \par \uc2 A matter of fact, they had a marijuana initiative, which I would have voted against, I might add, but it didn't reach the number either to get to 60.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORD: So both failed.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Governor DeSantis is touting both of those things down there in Florida.\par \par \uc2 Okay, we have some calls.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We begin in Pennsylvania, home to a razor thin Senate race between incumbent Democrat Bob Casey and Republican businessman