number is closer than it's been the last two times around.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You want to talk to your co-anchor, Dana?\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Well, one of the things I think is pretty interesting is especially when you're looking at or considering the political realignment that we're living in, and also the population changes, right? So post-COVID people leaving the North and the Midwest and going South. That's one of the things that, as somebody from Colorado will say, all of these people moved in from California and Colorado was reliably red, when I was a kid, it is now reliably blue, and so you get South Park making fun of California all the time and being very frustrated that they moved there. That's not happening in the South.\par \par \uc2 So if people are leaving the Northeast and moving to Florida, they're not all of a sudden, bringing a bunch of Democratic politics with them. So there might be a little bit of a change here to watch in the numbers there.\par \par \uc2 The other thing I wanted to say is that one of the things I think we're seeing, especially with some of these lower propensity voters, or whatever we're calling them, unlikely voters, is that the policy of the Biden-Harris administration pushed them into politics and into voting because their situation is dire and they want a better life.\par \par \uc2 So they went to the rallies and they went to the podcast and they had fun and they got involved. What will be interesting to see is as Kellyanne might be able to correct me, but as I understand it, if you don't vote often, or if you're a first time voter, whichever party you vote for the first time, you're likely to vote for that party the second time, and then you might have that voter for life.\par \par \uc2 So the political realignment that is happening and the coalitions that Harris and Trump are putting together could have longer legs.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, it goes to what Kellyanne was saying earlier about the working element of the Republican Party shifting and I think that when you look at Ohio, you are seeing a concentration, likely coming from the suburbs, which is leaning more to Harris.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: There is something else I see very quickly, and I see it in Virginia. Remember, this is the first presidential election since \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2  was elected over \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  on the issues of wokeness and education, just really post-COVID policies, the school board elections.\par \par \uc2 I think we're having a parents renaissance in this country that continues. And I think two pillars that we don't cover enough in all the issues, the themes here are wokeness and weakness.\par \par \uc2 In our Fox News Voter Analysis, Trump is seen as the strong leader by more than 10 points over \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Strength over weakness, but it's also fairness over wokeness. People feel like life is increasingly unfair. They don't want men in women's sports. They don't want to think their kids are stuck in schools based on their zip code. They don't want the pipefitters and plumbers to pay for student loans of doctors and lawyers, and they don't want ten million people to come here, including in all of these states we're seeing, and get cash and cell phones and clothing and hotel rooms. They just say it's unfair.\par \par \uc2 But wokeness is on the ballot, whether we feel it or not. I'll never understand how \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  chose someone in \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  who was to the left of her on the woke agenda.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Harold quickly.\par \par \uc2 Ford: So you look at -- we talk about early surprises. Jesse and I love sports, so do you, Bret, when you get a quick touchdown in the game against somebody you're not supposed to beat, if he wins, Virginia, this is a big thing. This is one of those early things that could, could go your way.\par \par \uc2 Similarly, her numbers in Ohio are interesting to me. I did not think -- you talked about the Selzer poll and Jessica and I talked about this before we got on, that number suggests that's what she was suggesting, it could close there. We'll see as the night goes on.\par \par \uc2 But Georgia and North Carolina, still, the state said she's got to perform well and maybe win one. And if he can win Virginia, we thought we were going to have a long night. We might have a longer night as we think about these numbers here over the next few hours or so.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Or shorter night.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And the night would be a shorter night.\par \par \uc2 HUME: A shorter night.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Or shorter if he wins Virginia.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So we really have to look at the gender breakdown in these states and where we are at half the vote in in some place like Ohio and Texas, where that vote is coming from. We're going to get.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Coming up.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: In minutes, minutes away, Bret, that is what we're dealing with.\par \par \uc2 We're going to get some major poll closings that are coming up, Arizona, Michigan, and Wisconsin, all about to close, and \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  will enter our chat as our special coverage continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We are getting a lot of new numbers in here, and we are about to see it close in Arizona, Michigan, and Wisconsin, all obviously battleground states, all very hotly contested, and those closes are just minutes away.\par \par \uc2 So meanwhile, let's check back in with Harris Faulkner who is speaking with voters and hearing their voices.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Harris.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS FAULKNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: And watching everything you guys are doing, Martha. Thank you. This is Voters Voices, and I'm Harris Faulkner here, with me, polling expert, \uc2 Lee Carter\uc2 , and we have a fabulous audience of voters talking with us about the top issues in the race for the White House.\par \par \uc2 Most polls show the economy is the voters top issue. Now a clip from Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . The lines on the screen show how voters reacted to the comment in real time. That's what you've gathered. And the red lines represent Republicans, the blue Democrats, the gray are Independents. Here it is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: While inflation is down and wages are up, prices are still too high, you know it, and I know it. And unlike \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who had $400 million served to him on a silver platter, I come from the middle class, and I will never forget where I come from, which is why my common sense plan will lower the prices you pay on everything from prescription drugs to groceries to housing; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will raise costs on you and your families.\par \par \uc2 In fact, independent economists have analyzed our plans and found that mine will cut your costs and strengthen our economy. His will increase inflation and lead to a recession by the middle of next year.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Independents, Republicans didn't find what they wanted. Again, we're watching for Independents.\par \par \uc2 LEE CARTER\uc2 , POLLSTER: That's right. So you can see there, Independents gave this a D. Democrats gave it an A, Republicans gave it an F. Now, I think what's so interesting about the Independent voter there is they really were turned off when they heard her talk about the middle class.\par \par \uc2 And I also heard from voters there, they wanted more specifics on the economy, and they said that you can find experts to reinforce whatever you want to believe. I'd rather hear what you're going to do for me.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Yes, all right, I want to go to Alexandria now. Republican registered voter, still not sure all the way up until the time you voted. What did you do and why?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA, VOTER: Absolutely. Well, thank you so much for having me tonight, Harris, and I think that for me, both candidates really had to prove themselves. They had to win my vote.\par \par \uc2 And they can talk about everything relative to the economy, but what really matters is what actually happens and we have to ask ourselves, was it easier to buy groceries under the Biden administration or under the Trump administration? And for me, I can say personally that I am concerned with the direction that the country is headed in.\par \par \uc2 And I would say for a vast population of the electorate that the same can be -- the same sentiment is shared. So, I think that we all have to really contextualize all this information and ask ourselves the actual questions of how we feel in the circumstances.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Who did you vote for?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA: I voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: All right, this is the second time, you're 22, that you voted in a presidential election. Was your vote different this time than it was in 2020. Did you vote for him last time?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA: I did not vote for him last time.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: It sounds like the economy made a huge difference for you.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: This is fun, getting to know all of you as we watch in real time. We've got some races that are coming into and we don't want to miss a minute with Bret and Martha, so we will hear more from the Voters Voices throughout this evening.\par \par \uc2 So glad to be here with all of you.\par \par \uc2 Bret and Martha, back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thanks very much, Harris.\par \par \uc2 So we welcome \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  to the panel.\par \par \uc2 Sean, you're watching all of this along with us tonight, and you say that you have some significant takeaways already.\par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST, "HANNITY": I have a lot of takeaways from tonight. Obviously, you guys have gone over the issues and what is important to people, and what they've been telling all the pollsters, exit polls, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 I'm looking at this from a little bit different perspective tonight. To me, it's a numbers game. I was on with Dana and Bill Hemmer earlier today and I made the point going into today that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and her team had a numbers problem. And the numbers problem was they didn't have anywhere near the votes or the percentages of votes that they had in 2020 when it was Biden and Harris.\par \par \uc2 Now that's a math problem that you have to overcome with unprecedented day of voting, which Democrats are not used to doing.\par \par \uc2 I checked in with a lot of people that I trust, a lot of people that I think have a lot of knowledge in individual states. For example, Reince Priebus, long conversations with him. He feels that the turnout was so massive for Republicans in the lead up to today, and that the Democrats did not show up in the numbers that would be needed to overtake that early lead that was built up in the early voting.\par \par \uc2 And that's also backed up by Mark Halpern, if you took a look, he's been doing a really good job analyzing numbers, I think.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, he has.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Now, in Philadelphia, I'm trying to get an answer to an important question to, I did notice other networks, they kept showing \uc2 Temple University\uc2  and a massive line of students. Okay. The only problem was, if you go back to early voting in 2020 the Democrats had a 1.1 million vote lead heading into Election Day. This year, it was about 400,000 so that's a 700,000 vote decline in a year.\par \par \uc2 That would mean today, they would have needed massive turnout. They would need massive turnout. Yes, at the universities, it seems like they got it, but there's about 25,000 students that go to Temple. There's about 10,000 that go to \uc2 Penn State\uc2 , there is about 6,000 that go to \uc2 Lehigh University\uc2 . Okay, that's not making up 700,000 votes.\par \par \uc2 So then we look at traditionally, who else is voting in Philly? African Americans, lot of union workers. Did they show up in the numbers? There are people reporting that it was a massive turnout in Philly today. I just got off the phone with the Trump War Room in Philadelphia, and they said they don't see it.\par \par \uc2 Now it's either one way or the other. The fact that they don't know the answer to that is a little surprising to me. If those numbers aren't there, that means Pennsylvania is a state that is in the state of play big time tonight. It may not show up early, but it will show up as the night evolves, just like Wisconsin will.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I would just mention one other thing about the college students is that there's been such a focus on young men and these podcasts are the podcast that college boys are watching and listening to, Theo Von, Joe Rogan, Jake Paul, all of these guys.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, just because they're in college doesn't mean this time around, that they're necessarily voting the way that you think college students would.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We welcome \uc2 Kayleigh McEnany\uc2  and \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Kayleigh, the interesting thing I mentioned earlier was Florida. You know, we've already called that state for the former president, but inside those numbers, there's a massive Puerto Rican turnout in Osceola County and other places.\par \par \uc2 KAYLEIGH MCENANY\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL CO-HOST "OUTNUMBERED": Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, in other places, the media would tell you that that MSG rally really upset the Puerto Rican vote outpouring, but Trump won that overwhelmingly with a massive Puerto Rican turnout. I'm not sure it affects things one to one, but it is interesting to look at.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: It is, so Osceola County, as you point out, a big Puerto Rican population. Listen to this, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won Osceola County by 14 percent. With 95 percent reporting currently, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is leading by one. Now, what other national lessons can we take from Florida? Miami-Dade County, a huge Latino population, no presidential candidate has won that since 1988.\par \par \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , of course, won Miami-Dade as a governor, but currently \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in Miami-Dade County with 95 percent reporting, is plus 11.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 The electoral vote count about to go up. Stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FXLV000020241106ekb50008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Now, Voting Underway, Hours To First Polls Closing; Harris To Watch Results At \uc2 Howard University\uc2  In D.C.; Sources Say, Musk To Spend Election Night With Trump; Final Hours Of Voting Across The US\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum; Brit Hume, Robert Wolf, Marc Thiessen, Sandra  Smith, Laura Ingraham, Kellyanne Conway, Shannon Bream, Bill Melugin \par \uc2 8,458 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 November 2024\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS HOST: Hi there you guys. Happy Election Day to everybody. Good afternoon. I'm Martha MacCallum and this is The Story on Election Day 2024. The first polls will close just a few hours from now in an election that is guaranteed to make history one way or the other. Will America elect its first female president in Vice President Harris, which would also be the first South Asian American to hold office in the United States, or will former President Trump complete one of the greatest political comebacks in history and become just the second president in our history to serve non-consecutive presidential terms? Last one was quite some time ago, 1893, Grover Cleveland was the last to do that.\par \par \uc2 So, a live look now at what we all love to see on Election Day, people lining up and getting ready to vote across this country, Scottsdale, Arizona. On the left, Chicago, we've been seeing some long lines. Some places don't have lines at all when we look around at the different rems (ph) that we have. Both campaigns will join us to give us their take on turnout so far today, how their candidates are feeling as they head into these final hours.\par \par \uc2 But let's kick things off with our great panel in studio for us on Election Day, Brit Hume, Fox News chief political analyst, Marc Thiessen, Fox News contributor, and Robert Wolf, former economic adviser to President Obama, 32 Advisers founder and CEO, also a Fox News contributor. Gentlemen, great to have all of you.\par \par \uc2 Brit, let me start with you. You have covered a lot of elections, and you're always good at cutting through the haze, and there's a lot of that out there right now. How do you see this as we get ready for polls?\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS CHIEF POLITICAL ANALYST: I'm pretty surrounded by the haze. I'm just glad it's here. I love seeing the lines at the polls and people turning out. I mean, I realize it's the law, that's how we do it, but it's also a great tradition. It really is democracy in action. Politicians are responsive to the public. Now, the public gets its say, and I'm glad to see it, and I'm also glad this campaign, which in many ways, particularly in recent weeks, has been pretty dispiriting, I'm glad it's over.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We always talk about late breakers -- but you are not alone. I mean, everyone in us, oh my gosh, let's get this thing -- let's light this candle. So, late breakers deciding, people who decide late, they always are the thing that we watch when we start to get some of these tabulations and which way they're going. What's your feel for this right now?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, you know, on, you can make a pretty good case on paper with the issues set the way it is and the top issues being ones, three or four, there are about four of them. Trump seems to be -- it seems three of them seem to favor Trump. But the key factor in our elections since 2016, our national elections, both the midterms and then the presidential election in 2020, has been public resistance to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And the question in this election is, has he somehow begun to overcome that? Because, you know, they had a, they had a disappointing result in the midterm in '18, they had a bad result in 2020 for the Republicans and a very disappointing result in 2022, although Republicans got control of the House. So, what Trump needed to do was to overcome that in some way, and the question of this election, has he done that?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And we've seen evidence. Robert, I'll skip around to you on that. We have seen evidence that Trump -- you know, we hear a lot about Trump derangement syndrome. His approval numbers have gone up a little bit. In the Fox poll, they were at 53, which is much higher than they were when he was president of the United States. Do you think that is real? And does that concern you?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT WOLF, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: It doesn't concern me because former presidents always seem to do better post presidency. It's like even George Bush would say, hey, you miss me now. So, we've seen that before.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We haven't seen them running again.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: That's true. I mean, here's what I would say. I would say, you know, I've gone from nauseously optimistic to cautiously optimistic to optimistic. I wouldn't say I'm confident, but I spoke with the campaign today. There seems to be an air of confidence around the campaign, on the vice president's side. They're seeing, you know, incredible number of turnouts in Philadelphia in-person, to Brit Hume, about people voting in person. They're seeing great things at the universities at Michigan.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Van Jones said he was very nervous when he looked at Philadelphia County.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Yes, well, I think that's a way of Van to say, get up.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He talked in particular, Robert, about the Jewish vote and he said that Biden was up -- had 70 percent of the Jewish vote and that she's at about 50-50. He said that that number could kill you in Philadelphia County alone. Do you agree or not?\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Well, I agree it could kill you, but I think Kamala will be about 73 percent of the Jewish vote, so I think it's going to be where Democrats normally are. And I also think she's going to outperform on the black and Hispanic vote. I think a lot of this rhetoric has changed over the last ten days, because I actually think President Trump has kind of gone off talking about the economy and immigration and talking about all these other things that really don't -- you know, aren't what the people are concerned about.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Marc, as you look at this, and we're all seeing different numbers about turning out these low propensity voters in different places, and one of the things that has fascinated me throughout this cycle is that when you get a set of numbers, it says, you know, if we miss the polls, like we did in 2022, or we missed the polls like we did in 2020, and it's a very different situation, how do you see this playing out?\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: So, one of the problems with the polls is that there're so many people who say they might change their minds. I mean, this really is a very -- it is both a very tight election.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What's that? You said that number's 26 percent?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes, 20, and in The Washington Post/Schar School poll. So, it's both very tight and very fluid. There's 26 percent of the electorate that says that either they are undecided or they're leaning towards Harris or leaning towards Trump, but are not firmly decided, could change their mind. That's a huge persuadable electorate.\par \par \uc2 And I did a deep dive into them in my \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  column. There's seven in ten are moderate to conservative 79 percent say -- 74 percent disapprove of the job Biden is doing, which is more than the general public. 68 percent disapprove of Harris, eight in ten say they disapprove of the current economy and think it's getting worse, but they haven't committed to vote for Trump.\par \par \uc2 So, these are what I'd call reluctant Trump voters and he hasn't really gone after them in a clear way. And that's why they could either go -- Brit doesn't agree.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I can tell you --\par \par \uc2 HUME: No, I don't disagree at all. But I would say about it is it's an interesting phenomenon. Is there really anybody in America who might vote who doesn't already have and has and have had for a long time a clear view and opinion of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I mean, he has been with us now these many years. He was four years as president. I think people -- I'm not saying people have all made up their mind whether to vote for him or not, but they've all made a kind of made up their mind what they think about it.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Well, that's true.\par \par \uc2 HUME: and I think that the campaigning in recent weeks by him and, you know, you detect these shifts in the momentum and it looks like Harris is doing a little better and Trump isn't emphasizing this or that, I don't know that at this stage of the game, in this race, with this candidate, Trump, that it makes any difference, because I think people -- you know, they may not have come to terms with whether to vote for him or not, but I don't think that you're going to change their people's minds about him. They're going to be voting for him --\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: If I can just say that they're not -- that they don't have an opinion on Trump. In fact, 44 percent of those voters say the reason that's holding them back is character and temperament.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Right.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Right. So, realize --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, they're deciding at this point, I might actually decide to vote for him even though I really know what I think about him.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: They think he'll do a better job, but I'm just having a hard time committing to doing it, and so the question is, do they do it or not.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Super quick, because I want to get your thoughts on the other side of this next panel, sir.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Yes, I would just respectfully disagree, because I think they have now a different opinion since Dobbs, okay?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Of Trump?\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Of the outcome. Since Dobbs, Democrats have outperformed the polls by four to six percents. I have no idea if this Des Moines Register is anywhere near close. But if it's halfway accurate, the gender vote is going to be much higher.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, we'll see. That may be the big story. But I'm going to get thoughts from you guys on the other side of our discussions with the two campaigns. So, stick around for us. Great to have you all here. Both camps are going to tell us how they feel in the final hours.\par \par \uc2 Democrat Senator \uc2 Mark Kelly\uc2  is a Harris surrogate and Trump 2024 Senior Adviser Alina Habba joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Get Trump re elected. That's -- the man needs to be back in office.\par \par \uc2 Money's not going as far as it used to.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is a long awaited moment, a woman of color being at the top of the ticket is very important to take our place in history. I pulled my daughter from school.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: When I got -- become a citizen, not long time ago, I said, I want to vote for this guy because I think he's a man for America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. So, here's one thing we've established today. There is a long line to vote in Chicago. I can also tell you that in some other places there is not a long line. So, let's just keep all of that in mind as we look at what's going on out there.\par \par \uc2 And how about some trivia to think about as we head into Election Day? So, if Harris were to win, she would be just the third vice president in close to 200 years to be elected to the presidency right out of the V.P. job. So, who are the others? George H. W. Bush succeeded \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  in 1988. Martin Van Buren followed Andrew Jackson in 1836.\par \par \uc2 Our panel, of course, knew all of those things, but they are standing by. Trump campaign senior adviser is also here, Alina Habba. But, first, we're going to Arizona Senator \uc2 Mark Kelly\uc2 , a surrogate for the Harris campaign. Senator Kelly, good to have you with us. Thank you very much for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 Give us the feel for what you're seeing. We're seeing these long lines in Chicago. What are you hearing about the actual turnout on the ground on Election Day in Arizona?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MARK KELLY\uc2  (D-AZ): Martha, I'm going to be heading over to \uc2 ASU\uc2  after this interview, and the lines there are probably longer than what we saw in 2022. When I saw long lines in 2022 on the Arizona campus, at that point, I knew there was a really good chance I was going to win, same thing at \uc2 Arizona State University\uc2  in Tucson.\par \par \uc2 So, at the universities, which are largely the young people are very voting for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , those lines are really, really long. And it makes sense because all the work we did over the past few weeks, but especially over this last weekend.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, let me ask you this, because some of those young voters, we've seen this huge push by the Trump campaign, digging in deep to these podcasters, Theo Vaughn, Joe Rogan, they've really been trying to get out low propensity male voters, highly possible that some of those are at these large universities around the campus as well, right?\par \par \uc2 You know, let me just play this. This is -- because this is brand new. It's \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's discussion with Joe Rogan last night. Quick clip and then I'd love to hear your thought on the other side.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK, CEO AND OWNER OF X, TESLA AND SPACEX: You and I used to be Democrats.\par \par \uc2 JOE ROGAN, HOST, THE JOE ROGAN EXPERIENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MUSK: So --\par \par \uc2 ROGAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MUSK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROGAN: It's nuts.\par \par \uc2 MUSK: It's nuts, man. If the Dems win this election, they will legalize enough illegals to turn the swing states and everywhere will be like California. There will be no escape.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, so there's two things in there. One is this question about the bro vote, I'd love you to address that among young people in Arizona, and then also this issue of illegal -- about immigrants coming into this country and how that might change the electorate.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Well, let me start with the first part. So, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  took away women's reproductive rights across the country, and what that meant in Arizona is women have bounced --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, that comes down to Arizona. They vote on that in Arizona, depending on how Arizonans feel on it, and it's on the ballot. So, that's not a Trump factor.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: And these -- yes, but these kids standing in line are Arizona voters. And women so far, especially here in Arizona, across the country, are voting at a higher rate. We'll see if that keeps up throughout the day. But my expectation because of the work we did, we knocked on over 200,000 doors over the weekend, 600,000 phone calls, and that's going to really, you know, make a difference.\par \par \uc2 And I feel the energy is here and bouncing back between one abortion ban and another over the last couple of years.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But abortion is not banned in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: There's a 15 week ban with no exceptions for rape and incest. But the issue is if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is back in the White House, he has previously said that, given the opportunity, he will put in a nationwide abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He didn't say that.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: He's also said that --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Senator, I'm sorry, he has not said that.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Yes, he has previously said -- he has previously said he would sign a nationwide -- I thought we were talking about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Because she was against fracking. She has a lot of things she previously said. But on the campaign trail, if we're going to work on what people said now --\par \par \uc2 KELLY: So, now we're talking about fracking? I thought we were talking about the women vote in Arizona. So, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has said that. He's also said that he will punish --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But he's made it clear that he will not ban abortion as president. Do you accept that as true?\par \par \uc2 KELLY: He has said he will punish women and he will -- and doctors should go to jail, and women know that here in Arizona. They've heard that and they've seen themselves bounce back and forth between one really bad ban and another, and they get that. And I think that's why they're turning out at high rates.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, you may be right that that's why they're turning out. I just want to make sure that we're talking -- speaking, you know, the facts here. And the facts are that Arizonans get to decide what their abortion policy is because it's on the ballot. So, it doesn't have anything to do with Trump. It all went back to the states after Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: But let's talk about the difference between the two candidates. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  said --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But it's not a federal issue anymore, Senator. It's a state issue, not a federal issue.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: It's going to become a federal issue with the next president, where, you know, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has said, made it very clear, that given the opportunity, she's going to bring back the rights and the protections that people had under Roe v. Wade. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has said previously that he would sign a nationwide abortion ban. Those are two very contrasting views of what the country --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Just to be clear, he said he won't sign an abortion ban. She has not made it clear. She said she wanted to reinstate Roe v. Wade, which has a policy for the first two trimesters. She won't say what she'll do after that, though. The final trimester --\par \par \uc2 KELLY: She has made it clear that she stands with women and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does not.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's a very -- all right, that's a broad statement that I'm not sure how it applies or what it actually means, honestly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: I think the women in Arizona understand that and that's why they're showing up at pretty high rates. They're voting at a high rate, especially young women because they know that this right was taken away from them by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He appointed the justices and he has said -- Martha, he has said, he broke --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The state has