ifferent things when it comes to Roe v. Wade and women's reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But he's been clear during the course of this campaign that he would veto a national ban. So, when you put these words -- that's why I think he didn't understand why I brought up fracking. But he said, well, you know what he said before counts. So, I was just making the point but she said before --\par \par \uc2 WOLF: There has been interviews before, if you look at the old Chris Matthews interviews, where he has talked about promising --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Chris Matthews? That's a long time ago.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Yes. Well, people --\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: What about fracking then?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What about fracking?\par \par \uc2 WOLF: I'm happy to have any debate you want. I'm only answering that he has made comments in the past about Roe v. Wade. Let me just respond though, how I would respond to \uc2 Mark Kelly\uc2  a little differently. Here's what I will say about Arizona. \uc2 Ruben Gallego\uc2  is going to beat Kari Lake by seven to ten points. And I think they will do very well on the women's reproductive referendum. And therefore, I think it will carry \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to win Arizona.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Okay. Marc, it doesn't change the fact that abortion could end up being a pivotal issue.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: It is. Though it's only 3 percent of those swing -- those undecided or late deciding voters care about abortion. That's not what's driving the -- stopping that vote. And what he said was just flat untrue, which is why they're trying to gin this up. They're moving in the wrong direction because those voters who could be persuaded in the final days, they don't care about what he was just talking about. They care about other things.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, I don't know how much surrogates and what they say matter, I'm imagining that Brit's about to say they don't matter at all.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: But what 