2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: All right. This is a real phone thing. Get back to making calls. Please, please, please, we got voters to still get up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. We see a lot of that, obviously, on Election Day. She's making the rounds to thank people at the DNC this afternoon. She will be watching tonight at \uc2 Howard University\uc2 . That's where they will be based her alma mater. And so, there she is in DC.\par \par \uc2 So live look now at the polling place in Milwaukee, which is obviously a huge battleground and a huge population center. Very important part of that Wisconsin vote. Ten electoral votes at stake there. One thing obviously, as we've already discussed this hour to watch closely is how women are voting and how they are turning out.\par \par \uc2 President Biden won women by 12 points in 2020 when they made up 53 percent of the voting population. They tend to vote in larger numbers than men in most elections. \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  won women by 15 points in 2016, but of course, she did not go on to win the race. And in that year they were 55 percent.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in "America Reports" co-anchor Sandra Smith with a preview of how to look at our amazing bank of data and knowledge that comes in just a couple of hours with the Fox News voter analysis, which we're so glad Sandra will be helming for us. Hi, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thanks, Martha. I'm shuffling papers around because we're getting ready for the big night, Fox NEWS voter analysis. This is our improved method for collecting data from the voters, in this case over 120,000 voters in 50 states. And this is really in an effort to more accurately reflect the way people vote today, the way the American voter votes today.\par \par \uc2 You've got a lot of early voting. So this is not like the exit polls. It's different. This is not waiting for Election Day when they cast their ballot and ask them questions. We ask these questions over the phone, the landline, the cell phone, online, Martha, leading up to and on Election Day.\par \par \uc2 And I'm going to give you a little sample, because I can't show you any of the data yet that we're going to have tonight. As that comes in, it's going to start to tell the story of this election night and this election. Here's a sample of some of those questions that we put to voters in all 50 states, Martha.\par \par \uc2 How much change do you want to see in how the country is run, right? This is arguably an election about change. So those respondents would say they want complete and total upheaval, substantial change, small change or no change at all. Martha, we're going to be able to break that down by candidate after the polls close tonight.\par \par \uc2 Just to give you another idea of another question in our Fox News voter analysis, when it comes to the economy and how it affects you, how concerned are you about the cost of food and groceries? Why will this tell a story in all these states that we're watching, especially those battlegrounds that are seeing about 20 percent higher prices, Martha, those that are very concerned, we'll answer that.\par \par \uc2 Somewhat concerned, not too concerned or not concerned at all. This is going to help us tell the story of this election, Martha. And I can't wait to provide you on the election desk and all of our viewers, our Fox News voter analysis, because you will not get this anywhere else.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. This is way better than exit polls, much more detailed, a much larger sampling that we have here at Fox. So, Sandra, thank you so much. We'll see you throughout the evening.\par \par \uc2 With that, let's bring in Alex Castellanos, Co-Founder and Chairman of Purple Strategies. Alex, great to have you with us. We always like hearing your analysis. So I'm just going to open it up to you and hear what's on your mind as you look at some of these turnout numbers on Election Day and what we've seen in a huge early vote.\par \par \uc2 ALEX CASTELLANOS, CO-FOUNDER, PURPLE STRATEGIES: Oh, I think two things are on my mind. One is the, what a miraculous day. The strength of America, the heart of America, we are going out to vote. And, you know, for -- we've been talked so much about the end of democracy. It seems to be doing all right.\par \par \uc2 What I'm looking at in this election is, right now there seems to be a Republican surge, the culmination of four years of growth under the Biden administration, of Republican registration, Republican success in early voting and mail voting.\par \par \uc2 I was looking at some numbers in Pennsylvania among just Independent voters. And among just Independents, the swing voters in Pennsylvania, Trump was winning 45 to 39. So add all that up and look at the underperformance we're seeing of the core urban vote in cities like Philadelphia and Milwaukee, and Detroit, I'm beginning to see a picture here that says this could be a good night for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So that question about whether or not Republicans have just redone the way they vote, and turned out early and cannibalized the day of vote. It sounds like you don't believe that to be the case, at least based on what you see now.\par \par \uc2 CASTELLANOS: Well, there's some of that. There has to be. But what you get overall is a sense of more urgency among Republicans, which is good for early vote and Election Day voting. So no, I'm not too worried that we've cannibalized that vote.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: In terms of women turnout, you know, we're hearing this is Jim Messina earlier today speaking about women voters. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JIM MESSINA, FORMER OBAMA CAMPAIGN MANAGER: Women are angry in this election and they're voting early in historic numbers. And it's why were hanging out earlier and he said how you feel, and I said I put my faith in women voters and I think tonight they're going to deliver a very clear message not to screw with them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So your reaction to that, Alex?\par \par \uc2 CASTELLANOS: Well, I think he's made some good points, but more so earlier in the week when he was concerned about lack of Democratic energy. I do think that we're seeing more women voting as a larger share of the vote now, but strangely enough it's because of black male underperformance in urban areas. So I don't think it's actually that good a sign.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Alex Castellanos, always good to see you. Thank you very much, Alex.\par \par \uc2 So power panel extraordinaire here, Laura Ingraham, Shannon Bream, Kellyanne Conway, final thoughts on how tonight could play out and what you should be watching in the coming hours.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So here's a first look inside former President Trump's election night watch party in Florida. Just got some cameras in there, thousands will be on hand as those results come in. The former president revealing today that he does not have a speech prepared. If he wins, he says, he pretty much always knows what to say. That's what he said this morning when he came out after voting.\par \par \uc2 Our panel is standing by but first, quickly, to national correspondent Bill Melugin at Trump campaign headquarters where he will be stationed tonight in West Palm Beach. Hi, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha. Good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 That's right, the doors just open up. We just got in here at the official watch party headquarters for the Trump campaign. I can tell you off camera behind us, they have rows and rows of alcohol which they hope they're going to be using tonight for a celebration.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, we caught up with the former president and I asked them, when the first polls close, is Georgia what your eyes going to be on? Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Will Georgia kind of be your first indication of whether it's going to be a good night?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER US PRESIDENT AND PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: So, I mean, I hear we're doing very well there in Georgia. I hear we're doing very well. I think -- I hear we're doing very well everywhere. Now, I may be -- I may regret that statement, but I'm hearing that we're doing very well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And earlier this morning, Trump also stopped by his campaign headquarters here in West Palm Beach. While he was there, he thanked staffers, supporters. He said he hopes to have the results tonight and expressed some frustration that some states take days to count their ballots.\par \par \uc2 Also earlier this morning, take a look at this. Trump's running mate, JD Vance, voted in Ohio this morning. He's been crisscrossing the country to different battleground states. He says he's feeling confident. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH), VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: So I hope people will get President Trump over the finish line. I hope people here in Ohio will get \uc2 Bernie Moreno\uc2  over the finish line. Look, I feel good. You never know until you know, but I feel good about this race.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And, Martha, sources with knowledge confirmed to Fox News that \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is going to be joining former President Trump at Mar-a-Lago tonight. They're going to watch election returns live together as those returns start coming in. Things just getting kicked off here in West Palm, we'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Big night for the country all around. Thank you so much, Bill Melugin.\par \par \uc2 Here now, Laura Ingraham, host of the "Ingraham Angle," Shannon Bream, Fox News Sunday anchor and chief legal correspondent, Kellyanne Conway, former senior counselor to President Trump, host of "Here's The Deal" on Fox Nation. What a great crew. I'm just going to not say anything. I'm going down the line.\par \par \uc2 Laura, what are you thinking this afternoon?\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Well, there's some interesting tidbits that have broken, including just now that \uc2 Google\uc2  is admitting that there's been a search discrepancy among people who search for where can I go vote for Trump versus where can I go vote for Harris? But they said it's going to be fixed. So they --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Looks like they adjusting.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, they adjusted and acknowledged an issue that users have been flagging and where they try to search for where do I go vote, where are my precincts. And they say they are working on fixing that. So that's one thing.\par \par \uc2 But, look, I think I spoke to former President Trump in the last half hour. He got back to West Palm Beach at, I believe, 5:00 in the morning. So the last rally was, you know, long. It was like an hour-and-a-half long. So the man has been the Energizer Bunny of camp of candidates.\par \par \uc2 I have never seen anything like it, and Kellyanne knows this better than anyone. But even compared to 2016, this has just been a sprint of no break and go-go-go for the American people. He believes that they're well positioned, turnout has been very strong. And yet he understands they're trying to swap the vote with, you know, disgruntled female voters who their main focus, I guess is abortion.\par \par \uc2 But they seem very confident tonight, but we'll be watching. I think Georgia is key, as is North Carolina. They're very confident in Pennsylvania, even with some of the glitches in the system.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, Kellyanne, you were the campaign manager in 2016, and you watch polls very closely. What is your straight take on whether or not your former boss should be concerned about anything? Is there anything you see out there that concerns you?\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FORMER TRUMP SENIOR COUNSELOR: That one thing that would have concerned me over time, but less so today is \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  making the trade of a century. Not even trying to chase those rural voters, some of the urban voters, blacks, Hispanics, unions, Jewish Americans, younger people, all of whom were trending a little bit more toward Trump than your typical Republican.\par \par \uc2 But the trade they made was just go for broken with white college educated women. And particularly in the counties in these three blue wall states that with the exception of 2016, have gone Democrat regardless of who won the election from 1992 to 2020. So they voted as a bloc. The only time, the only time you see red is 2016.\par \par \uc2 So the irony for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is, somebody told her, you're not doing well. These more racially diverse states, Arizona, North Carolina, Georgia, Nevada. You got to go back to the white states where Biden was competing. So that would have concerned me.\par \par \uc2 But look, I don't think we've cannibalized the vote. The places I can speak confidently about that are North Carolina, Arizona and Georgia. Well, Nevada too. Nevada where you've seen, I'll say Nevada, Arizona, Georgia, rather. Nevada, we've got the best early vote in the history of the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 And two short years ago, Kari Lake lost for governor in Arizona, \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  lost for Senate in Nevada, in part because people were told take your paper ballot to the elect -- to the voter polls on Election Day. You're taking the chance grandpa can get out of the bathroom, let alone the house. I want his vote in October. So that has all changed this time.\par \par \uc2 And now and overlay that with pretty strong returns in places like a Bucks County, Pennsylvania. Trump lost that to Hillary by about 4 points. Biden won it even more narrowly. Trump can win that tonight because Republicans are outpacing Democrats in voter registration. So I think the investments in the non sexy parts of politics, the ground game, the voter registration, the get out the vote program, the stuff that we can't see that matters in a trench warfare, technical tact campaign like this.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So I guess one of the questions, Shannon, is has \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  been able to pull away some of those moderate Republican women voters? Is that a group that you're watching?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Yes, definitely, because we think about suburban moms and you talk about the college educated women and what really matters to them. We know that women aren't monolithic just like any other voting group. They do care about things like paying for their groceries and feeling safe in their neighborhoods and worried about the border. So we've had so many of those cases that have gotten a lot of attention that have involved women as victims.\par \par \uc2 And so that raises a lot of that conversation. I will say it feels much more organized this time around on both sides when it comes to the legal fights. We're tracking more than 200 cases at this point. Several of them have been issued today. Emergency orders in places like Pennsylvania, which are so critically important. Very quickly, judges are resolving those.\par \par \uc2 And some of these, we've seen that they're bipartisan filings where Democrat and Republican elections officials are coming together to say there's a problem at this particular campaign outlet. Let's get it fixed, a judge ruling in it. So we know this is going to be the most litigated election in US history. These teams have already been at it. They will continue to be at it. It just feels more organized this time around in the wake of 2020.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Well, I think the, you know, Georgia moniker of it should be easy to vote and hard to cheat is something that both sides should be able to get behind. I just want to play this from Andrea Mitchell because this is the second time we've had a woman run for the presidency. Here's what Andrea Mitchell had to say. And, Laura, we'll get your thoughts on the other side.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: There are a lot of men who just simply black and white, men who just simply don't trust a woman to be, you know, chief executive, commander in chief. They just don't want to vote for a woman.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you see that?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I have never seen more cynical people in the way they view marriage, especially heterosexual marriage, and the way they view relationships. I mean, so this is what -- this is the close of the campaign. You're a Nazi, you're Hitler, and men are either, you know, undercover abusers of their wives or significant others, or they're somehow threatening them into voting their way.\par \par \uc2 I got to tell you, that is so sad for the country, and I think it's completely inaccurate. People vote in their own interests. If you think late term abortion in Mississippi is more important than maintaining the US Border and having a vibrant economy, then I don't really know what I can do for you. But if you want a strong economy and an enforced border, and a culture that makes some sense, then Trump is your only answer.\par \par \uc2 I don't understand what her point is, but it's very, very negative.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I remember \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  after the election basically saying that, you know, white -- she was quoting \uc2 Sheryl Sandberg\uc2 , I think, that white women were afraid to vote for Trump because, you know, their husbands and their uncles, and their brothers would all give them a hard time, which is just absolutely ridiculous to me.\par \par \uc2 I want to play this final ad from Bob Casey, Kellyanne, and get your thought on it because it's been really interesting to watch some of these candidates how they've kind of tried to do the weave, for lack of a better phrase. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Four candidates for Senate, who's standing up to China? It's Bob Casey, who stood with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to put tariffs on China, to protect American jobs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Wow. The steelworkers don't believe it, the frackers don't believe it, the Jewish Americans around Pittsburgh and Philadelphia think that this party has very little credibility on Israel. And let me just say a word about women. We've got a double gender gap this time.\par \par \uc2 I've been working on the gender gap since I made $8 an hour for Dick Wirthlin, \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 's pollster. And you know what, now men are -- they don't like the Democrats and the Kamala message. And I think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  losing by 13 points among women to Hillary is how he won. You just have to mitigate those usual deficits.\par \par \uc2 Kamala has been so bad as a candidate. Nobody can say sexism, racism. They're going to say eyesight and hearing. We paid attention and she's awful.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Thank you, both, all three, that would be three of you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's an early night.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well see you all tonight. Thank you so much for being here. So that is "The Story" of Tuesday, November 5th, which is Election Day. Get out and vote, everybody. "The Story" goes on.\par \par \uc2 I will see you back here at 6:00 for Election Day special coverage alongside Bret Baier and our great team, all of whom you see here on the set with me. It's going to be a very big night. Stay with us throughout.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020241106ekb500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Final Days Of Presidential Campaigning; Harris And Surrogates Campaign Across Michigan; Whitmer "Grateful" For Republican Support; Harris And Trump Locked In Tight Race; Key Races Could Flip Senate Majority; Tester Loss Could Flip Senate Control; Sheehy Centers Campaign On Economy And Border\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Bill Melugin, Jacqui Heinrich, Bryan Llenas, Rich  Edson, Rich Edson, Bill Hemmer, Madison Alworth, Karl Rove, Dan Springer,  Brit Hume, Jessica Tarlov \par \uc2 7,991 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 November 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: Always one of my favorites. Thanks, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST: Yep.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening. I'm Bret Baier. Less than 48 hours to Election Day. Both presidential candidates enter the final stretch of the 2024 election campaign.\par \par \uc2 Here live, coming to you from Fox News Election Headquarters in New York, the final Sunday before the election. As the presidential nominees, their running mates, and their surrogates crisscross the country, the focus is on several swing states that will decide this election.\par \par \uc2 We have Fox Team coverage. Bryan Llenas in Philadelphia with a look at how both parties are trying to shore up support in the crucial battleground. Rich Edson is in East Lansing, Michigan, where Vice President Harris will speak at a rally at \uc2 Michigan State University\uc2  in a few minutes. Jacqui Heinrich in Detroit, where Harris spoke earlier at a church congregation. But we begin with Bill Melugin in Macon, Georgia, a Trump stop where Trump supporters are standing by.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you as well.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump is scheduled to take the stage here in Georgia in about 30 minutes or so. He's got a huge crowd waiting for him, but he's running over an hour behind schedule right now.\par \par \uc2 This is going to be his third campaign rally of the day in three different battleground states, and we are now just about 49 hours away from the first polls closing on Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: That's a nice scoop.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice-over): Former President Trump, on his final weekend of campaigning, visiting Kinston, North Carolina this afternoon. Earlier this morning, Trump raised eyebrows when he made this comment at a rally in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : -- to get me, somebody would have to shoot through the fake news. And I don't mind that so much.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  was quick to respond.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 , (D-CA) FORMER HOUSE SPEAKER: I think that what the ex- president just said is a further indication of his cognitive degeneration.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: The Trump campaign says President Trump was stating that the media was in danger and should have had a glass protective shield. Also, there can be no other interpretation about what was said. But the Harris campaign is also facing criticism after a surprise appearance on Saturday Night Live last night.\par \par \uc2 The Trump campaign says Trump was not invited to be on the show, and SNL had previously said they wouldn't have either candidate on. One Republican \uc2 FCC\uc2  commissioner tells Fox he believes it is an \uc2 FCC\uc2  violation.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN CARR\uc2 , \uc2 FEDERAL COMMUNICATIONS COMMISSION\uc2 : This is all the appearances of at least some leadership at NBC, at SNL, making clear that they wanted to weigh in in favor of one candidate before the election.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: This as the highly respected Des Moines Register poll came out, showing VP \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  with a three-point lead on Trump in reliably red Iowa. But in an internal memo, the Trump campaign pushed back, hard, writing, "Des Moines Register is a clear outlier poll. Emerson College, released today, far more closely reflects the state of the actual Iowa electorate." That Emerson poll has Trump up 10 points over Harris in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : They tell me I'm down in Iowa. I don't think so.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And Bret, RFK Jr. announced yesterday that if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wins this election, his administration is going to remove fluoride from all public water systems in the country. RFK Jr. is saying he believes fluoride is connected to arthritis, bone fractures, bone cancer, thyroid disease, and other health issues. When asked about it, Trump said, quote, "That policy sounds good to me," end quote.\par \par \uc2 As for Trump, we are told he is in the air right now from North Carolina. We don't know when he's going to land here in Georgia, but it's safe to say his schedule is running significantly behind right now. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill Melugin in Macon. Bill, thanks. Meantime, Vice President Harris emphasizing unity on this, the final Sunday before the election. While she spoke to churchgoers in Detroit, the rest of her campaign team was spread out to other key states. Senior White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has the wrap up from Detroit.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. So far today, the Vice President hasn't mentioned Trump by name even once. A hard turn from her fiery speech on the Ellipse where she attacked Trump two dozen times on Tuesday. We'll see if she changes her tone tonight. But the strategy in the end appears right now to be as much about not losing votes as it is about winning them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , U.S. VICE PRESIDENT & DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: This moment in our nation has to be about so much more than partisan politics.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice-over): Vice President Harris closing out the weekend with a unity pitch, barnstorming Michigan. Other Democratic heavyweights blanketing the Blue Wall and Sun Belt. Doug Emhoff in Pennsylvania and Georgia, where Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  started his day before heading to North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Former President Obama also campaigned there as former President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  hit Wisconsin. But the sitting president watched the closing hours of his former campaign from the bench, seen by his VP's team as a liability.\par \par \uc2 Today, lunch with an old friend at a Wilmington golf course, a day after yet another crass comment about Trump and Republicans.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) U.S. PRESIDENT: These are the kind of guys you like to smack in the ass.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Harris can't afford to alienate anybody with her target coalition split, adds courting Jewish voters in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I will always stand up for Israel's right to defend itself.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Talk about the same war differently to Arab Americans in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: What has happened in Gaza over the past nine months is devastating. We cannot allow ourselves to become numb to the suffering and I will not be silent.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The former prosecutor wouldn't even say how she filled out her own California ballot on a tough on crime measure.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Honestly, it's the Sunday before the election and I don't intend to create an endorsement one way or another around it.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Harris now just trying to grab eyeballs wherever she can, including on late night TV.\par \par \uc2 MAYA RUDOLPH, ACTOR: The American people want to stop the chaos.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And end the drama-la.\par \par \uc2 RUDOLPH & HARRIS (in unison): Keep calmala and carry on-a-la.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The V.P.'s surprise appearance on SNL may have earned NBC an \uc2 FCC\uc2  violation because Trump was not offered the same opportunity, according to his campaign. But it turns out it wasn't just Harris who may have run afoul of the \uc2 FCC\uc2 's equal time rule. Senator Tim Kaine, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 's former running mate, also appeared in a skit drawing protests from his challenger, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich, live in Detroit. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania is one of the key states, really the key state, a lot of people think, in the presidential contest. Former President Trump was about an hour and a half from Philadelphia this morning. His running mate and his eldest son spoke at a rally in Aston a short time ago. First Lady Jill Biden made a pair of stops in the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Top Democrats have expressed concerns about the Harris campaign there, while Republicans say they might get thousands more votes this time than four years ago. Correspondent Bryan Llenas is in Philadelphia tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Even on Saturday Night Live, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  had Pennsylvania on her mind.\par \par \uc2 RUDOLPH: I'm going to vote for us.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Great. Any chance you are registered in Pennsylvania?\par \par \uc2 RUDOLPH: Nope, I am not.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Well, it was worth a shot.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Polls show it's a tied race in the Commonwealth. Both campaigns know it comes down to voter turnout.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH BRICELAND-BETTS, HARRIS CAMPAIGN CANVASSER: This is the most doors I've done.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Deborah Briceland-Betts traveled from Maryland to Philly and knocked on a thousand doors for Harris.\par \par \uc2 BRICELAND-BETTS: The enthusiasm level is much higher right now. We had a lot more negative reaction at the door in '16 and '20, and a lot more undecided.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Harris needs to run up the vote tally in deep blue Philadelphia, where Trump had his best result yet in 2020, making his biggest gains in Philly's poorest working-class neighborhoods.\par \par \uc2 VINCE FENERTY JR. PHILADELPHIA REPUBLICAN WARD LEADER: If we can get 20,000 maybe 30,000 more votes, we're going to win the state of Pennsylvania. People in Philadelphia, we're not rich here. The Bidenomics are killing us at the grocery store. And that's what I think it's all about.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Voter turnout is especially important for Democrats, considering their mail-in ballot return advantage over registered Republicans has dwindled from over a million in 2020 to just 370,000 today. Still, Democrats say Harris will win.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): Trump definitely has a connection with voters here in Pennsylvania, and that's why it's going to be close. But there's a lot of tremendous amount of energy for Harris as well, too.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Tomorrow on the eve of the election, Trump will hold two rallies in Pennsylvania. Harris will hold three, including a final concert right here in front of the \uc2 Philadelphia Art Museum\uc2 , an iconic venue featuring Lady Gaga and Ricky Martin, in a last effort to drive up voter turnout.\par \par \uc2 Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bryan Llenas in Philadelphia. Bryan, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Harris is addressing a crowd in East Lansing. Earlier today, Senior National Correspondent Rich Edson talked to one of the Harris surrogates, Michigan Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 . Rich is in East Lansing right now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS STATE DEPARTMENT CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. Well, the Vice President, as you hear behind me, is making her closing argument right now to voters at \uc2 Michigan State University\uc2  in East Lansing. She's been across the state today, as have her surrogates.\par \par \uc2 We were out in Novi, where Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  was. She's been campaigning for Harris. We asked Whitmer about the significant error vote in this state, and those who say they're abandoning Harris. Thirteen percent of voters chose uncommitted in February's Democratic primary, largely as a protest over the administration's support of Israel. Still uncommitted. Michigan Congresswoman \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2 , who's refused to endorse Harris.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (D-MI): That's I think what unites everyone in all these communities. We want peace in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: But in a race that close, isn't that malpractice from a Democratic member of Congress?\par \par \uc2 WHITMER: You know, this election's not going to come down to one person or another. One community or another. It's going to be a close race, and we want people to, you know, endorse and support \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 . But we're doing the work all across the state, and I'm not going to criticize anyone.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Harris is spending all day today in Michigan before spending all day tomorrow in Pennsylvania. Two swing states Trump has narrowly won and lost the last two elections. Harris has abandoned more progressive positions on immigration, energy, and crime. We asked Whitmer if that's enough to convince swing state voters to support her.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): What do you say to the swing voter in Michigan who thinks that they're going to get a more progressive 2019 \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  if she's inaugurated next year?\par \par \uc2 WHITMER: You know what, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is pragmatic. She wants to get things done. I'm really grateful that there's so many Republicans who've endorsed her here in Michigan. We don't have to agree on 100% of the agenda.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: As she left that interview, we asked Whitmer if Harris was going to win Michigan. She said yes.\par \par \uc2 Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Rich Edson in East Lansing. Rich, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Here's some of the other headlines today. The Israeli military says it has carried out a ground raid into Syria, seizing a Syrian citizen involved in Iranian networks.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  visiting the Israel-Lebanon border today, saying his focus on trying to keep \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  from rearming itself with Iranian weapons transferred to Lebanon via Syria.\par \par \uc2 Storm survivors hurl mud at the Spanish royal couple during their first visit to the epicenter of their nation's deadliest natural disaster in living memory. Heavy rains and flooding killed more than 200 people in Spain, while thousands had their homes destroyed.\par \par \uc2 Tens of millions of people in the central U.S., from Texas to the Midwest, are on alert for severe weather after tornadoes left widespread damage and injuries across the region. At least five people were injured in Oklahoma City, with numerous homes and businesses severely damaged there.\par \par \uc2 And finally, two first-time winners of the New York City Marathon. Abdi Nageeye became the first person from the Netherlands to win the race, and Sheila Chepkirui won the women's contest. It was her first-ever run of the New York City Marathon. Congratulations to all of the marathon runners, saw a lot of them going by today.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the most important number Tuesday, 270. Bill Hemmer with a new way to look at the path to the Electoral College victory for presidential nominees.\par \par \uc2 Plus, legal challenges by both sides in the battleground states, and almost certain, they're almost certain to happen. We go to Georgia, where they're already underway.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tonight, we look at the path to 270. The number of electoral votes necessary to win the Electoral College and the presidency. America's Newsroom co-anchor, Bill Hemmer, who will be on the billboard on election night, charts the path with the help of our brand-new augmented reality feature.\par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, "AMERICA'S NEWSROOM" CO-ANCHOR: Bret, thanks and good evening. And this is what we believe the state of play is. Everything you see in yellow behind me, we believe is a toss-up. That Iowa poll from Saturday night, notwithstanding, we still believe Nevada and Arizona and the Southwest, Georgia and North Carolina and the Southeast, and then all these blue wall states, Wisconsin, Michigan and Pennsylvania are up for grabs.\par \par \uc2 For the sake of this exercise, we believe in Nebraska. All right, that one district, that one electoral vote, right now is still leaning toward the blue teams. We're going to go ahead and give \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  226 electoral votes to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's 219. You know where you need to get to, 270.\par \par \uc2 So let's look at the path to 270. And under this scenario, what you're going to see here, it's the same numbers over there, right? You got Harris at 226 and Trump at 219. Now you can slice this a hundred different ways, but just for the purposes of this moment here, based on some of the polling and the early turnout in places like Georgia and the Peach State, we believe the 16 electoral votes in all likelihood will go to Trump come Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 We also believe based on some of the polling and the early votes and really the history in the Tar Heel State, we believe North Carolina will also go for the Trump team.\par \par \uc2 What happens in that upper Midwest? What happens in that blue wall? Well, they voted together just about every time going back 40 years. So maybe Michigan's 15 electoral votes isn't just right for the picking right now, and it stays with the Democrats and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . And likewise, if they're going to vote the same way, maybe Wisconsin does as well.\par \par \uc2 So right now we're at 251 Harris, 251 Trump. So Pennsylvania has got a lot of mail-in ballots. We believe it's going to be a late-night Pennsylvania regardless, maybe into Wednesday even.\par \par \uc2 So in the Southwest, we've been watching the polling in Arizona, watching the early vote as well. And we see some Republican strength out there. So I'll go ahead and give Arizona to Trump.\par \par \uc2 And that brings us to Nevada. Similar story, early votes have been very strong for the Republicans. Democrats say they've got time to catch up. But for now, let's go ahead and give Nevada's six electoral votes to team Trump. And that leaves this one right here.\par \par \uc2 This is the keystone state of Pennsylvania, 19 electoral votes. And under this scenario, however it comes down, will determine the next president of the United States. For \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , she would effectively run the table perfectly, right?\par \par \uc2 Upper Midwest, Blue Wall, Nebraska Congressional District 2. And maybe it doesn't go that way. Maybe it went for a lot of surprises on Tuesday night. Maybe there are states out there that are not even on this graphic here, OK? They could come into play, and we're not saying they won't. But right now, this is what we're dealing with based on the battleground states behind me that you saw on the map.\par \par \uc2 Maybe Pennsylvania in 2016 was ripe for Trump, and maybe it's ripe for him again. And under this scenario, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would go back to the Oval Office.\par \par \uc2 Now let's go ahead and clear this. And come back over to the map and show you yet again. This is where we believe, and I'll go ahead and take Nebraska 2 off for the moment, but this is what we believe we'll be looking at with a high concentration come 48 hours from now on election night. We shall see.\par \par \uc2 Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We shall see. A lot of what-ifs there, Bill. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Attorneys are gearing up for legal action following Tuesday's voting. No matter which side is ahead when it all stops, there has been a lot of activity so far in Georgia legally with more anticipated.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business Correspondent Madison Alworth is in Atlanta tonight with that. Good evening, Madison.\par \par \uc2 MADISON ALWORTH, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. We are two days away from Election Day, and we are already seeing legal challenges here in Georgia. The Georgia GOP, along with the \uc2 RNC\uc2 , has filed a federal lawsuit against several Georgia counties over their decision to open election offices this weekend and accept absentee ballots. The suit covers seven counties. Five of the counties are in the Atlanta metro area. All seven typically vote Democrat.\par \par \uc2 The GOP says the move to open the election offices is illegal because under state law, drop boxes are not permitted after early voting ends. Early voting ended on Friday. Critics of the lawsuit say the move is legal and that handing ballots into an election official is different than a drop box.\par \par \uc2 The Georgia GOP and the \uc2 RNC\uc2 's original lawsuit was thrown out by a state judge. They plan to appeal the state decision as well. While the matter is being handled, the GOP is asking counties to sequester the weekend ballots that were submitted without proper oversight by election monitors.\par \par \uc2 More lawsuits are expected after Election Day, which is why the nonprofit Election Defense Fund is aiming to raise $5 million for legal expenses for election officials in case they are personally sued. They have already collected $2 million.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RYAN GERMANY, ELECTION DEFENSE FUND FOUNDER: We really wanted a group that is going to defend the election officials and the process and the results no matter what it is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALWORTH: One of the election offices targeted in that GOP suit is behind me. Fulton County officials tell us that over the course of Saturday, they collected 105 ballots. We are still waiting to hear if they are sequestering those ballots.\par \par \uc2 Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right Madison, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, what the poll numbers are telling us about what may happen, Tuesday. Karl Rove, Alex Castellanos, and \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2  are standing by.\par \par \uc2 And later, what could be the most important Senate race in the country. We will go live to Montana.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm still weighing it. I know my mind wants to do one thing. My heart wants to do another thing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm a Trump girl. So I feel like there's a lot of enthusiasm for Trump. And we'll just drive around and see how this all ends.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm voting for Kamala. And I -- personally, I thought under Trump's presidency, the country was a little bit too chaotic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All those women in Pennsylvania. You hear their thoughts about the race come Tuesday. And that brings up the issue of the gender gap. And we saw it in an "NBC" poll just out. And how big that split is, is pretty significant. If you look at women at 57 for Harris, men at 40, and then 58- 41 for Trump.\par \par \uc2 The "RealClearPolitics" average nationally now stands at Trump at 48.5% to Harris at 48.3.\par \par \uc2 What about this? Let's break down where these races stand on the final weekend of the campaign. Joining us tonight, Karl Rove, former Deputy Chief of Staff of President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 . Political Consultant, Co-Founder and Chairman of Purple Strategies, Alex Castellanos. And \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , former Advisor and Pollster for President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 . He's CEO of \uc2 Stagwell\uc2 , Chairman of the \uc2 Harris Poll\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I feel like I have the brain trust here. The big names.\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: CPR, right here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: CPR.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Castellanos, Penn, and Rove.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, there we go. Where are you right now?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Coin toss. This thing is too close. It could be affected by the last- minute decisions of voters and by the quality of each side's ground game. If you look at all three of these sort of major aggregators, RCP, 538, and Silver Bulletin, today four of them had -- four of the 21 numbers they gave us. Three aggregators times seven battleground states. Four of them are two points. Seven of them are 1 to 1.9, and 10 of them are less than one point, separating the two in these states.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , who do you want to be right now? Who do you want, which side do you want to be this evening looking at what you're looking at?\par \par \uc2 MARK PENN\uc2 , FORMER BILL CLINTON ADVISOR: Well, I'd rather be Trump this evening for the simple reason that there are lots and lots of polls that show a dead even.\par \par \uc2 The only fact we know is that the Republicans have done a lot better in the mail-in and early voting than they ever have. And I believe that they were losing one to two points in voters who never showed on Election Day who supported them.\par \par \uc2 And so the fact is they're better off. The jobs report was 12,000 jobs, which was pitiful. I would have taken Trump's hand, although I know my Democratic friends are, in fact, cheering tonight. They feel a lot better since that Iowa poll came out, and they feel good momentum on the ground.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're talking about the Iowa poll. This is Des Moines Register, and this poll had Harris up, Alex, three points, and there you see \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  at three points. An Emerson poll came out with Trump up 10 just about a half an hour before that.\par \par \uc2 Polling in the last few days before an election, what's your thoughts on that?\par \par \uc2 ALEX CASTELLANOS, POLITICAL CONSULTANT: I'm ready to say, let's kill all the pollsters instead of the lawyers, except my friend \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 . I like \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 . I think the pollsters are getting this wrong. We're all missing something because they're giving us the same poll over and over again. There isn't even statistical variation. You know, 95% confidence level means that 5% of the time you're going to get a crappy poll.\par \par \uc2 We hardly ever see that. In fact, they're telling us we're watching a basketball game where everybody plays a jump ball. Somebody is missing something. What I think they're missing is a massive shift in voter registration underneath all of this. Thirty-one states have voter registration by party. Thirty of them in the past four years have seen movement toward Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Miami-Dade County is now voting more Republican. Iowa was plus one Republican. It's now plus 10. And yet we're getting these surveys that I think are off base. Somewhere, somebody is missing something. I think there's -- I'm not going to call it a wave, but I think there's a wavelet out there of Republican enthusiasm and registration. If I register to vote Republican, whether I'm switching or new, what am I going to do?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We just don't know where the independents are that have already voted. There's a big significant chunk of those, and if they break in a significant way towards Harris or towards Trump, it obviously determines the election.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well, and look, Alex is right about the Republican registration gains like in Pennsylvania and North Carolina. And even Nevada used to be the old rule of thumb was you had to have 70-to-100,000-person gap between Republicans and Democrats for the Republicans to have a chance. It's 19,000 now.\par \par \uc2 But you put your finger on it. At the same time that the Republican registration has been rising in many of these states, like North Carolina, it's been outpaced by, in that case, the unaffiliated. In Nevada, it's been outpaced by what they call the nonpartisans. But it basically declined a state, not a Democrat, not a Republican, an independent. And we don't know how they're voting.\par \par \uc2 Look, the Iowa poll, Ann Selzer is a great pollster.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And has a great track record.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: And has a great track record. However, she's suggesting that the state in this poll is that basically has moved 11 points away from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . One out of every five people who supported \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  four years ago no longer support him. I think we'd feel that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mark, I'm going to put up New York Times/ Siena battleground state polls here. And they also caused waves this morning. And you saw the betting markets, you know, all kind of moves going on. And people texting me, like, is this a major shift? In the bottom of that poll, it says across these final polls, white Democrats were 16% likelier to respond than white Republicans. That's a larger disparity than our earlier polls this year. And it's not much better than our final polls in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Even with the pandemic over, it raises the possibility that the polls could underestimate Mr. Trump yet again. This is at the bottom of the poll in the little fine print. And it kind of encapsulates the whole thing that we're looking at here, right?\par \par \uc2 PENN: Well, and I think that's the big question that you really have going into this. Is there Trump enthusiasm and is there Trump shyness when it comes to polls? That nonresponse number by Republicans is serious that they don't want to give their views in polls.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think there's no question that there have been some registration changes. But no matter how close the polls are, the underlying change is the Republican Party is now a working-class party. The Democratic Party is now a professional upper-class party. And that is what we're seeing play itself out. And we don't know the result because we don't know if the professional upper class is really going to win combined with the minority vote versus the working-class vote with a lot of senior men, particularly, you know, downscale men who have shifted to Trump. There's a battle here. This is not a homogeneous country or a homogeneous situation by any means. That's why it's so difficult for these polls to call it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I can, you know, tell that I'm around smart people when they all nod together. When you say something, you know, they're all nodding.\par \par \uc2 PENN: We worked that out beforehand.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yeah, right. Alex, what about closing with celebrity? It didn't really work in 2016 for \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . You're going to have a number of concerts and a number of things. You obviously had Saturday Night Live last night. Thought about that and how it affects things?\par \par \uc2 CASTELLANOS: People have too much information these days about who these candidates are and what they're going to do to be influenced by something like a celebrity. It does say class, though, to Mark's point. Are you with the working people or are you with the Hollywood and entertainment elite? May not be the best thing for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to