uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  has Republicans poised to flip the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, let's take a look at the path to the majority of how to get there. All of these yellow states are races that we are watching on Tuesday night. They're tracking very closely. We begin in West Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Republican Governor \uc2 Jim Justice\uc2  expected to flip that seat held by the retiring Democrat turned independent \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 . So we are going to move West Virginia for the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Our latest power rankings have Michigan, Pennsylvania, Wisconsin all moved from Democrat to toss up. Republicans have been seeing gains in these states, but polling shows these races within a margin of error.\par \par \uc2 A GOP win in any of these states really would be on top of the projected majority flip. But a good night for Democrats means they hold on to all of these. So let's just say for the sake of argument, they do. All three of those.\par \par \uc2 In Nebraska, Republicans have been slow to respond at the first part to a competitive challenge from independent candidate Dan Osborne. Incumbent GOP Senator \uc2 Deb Fischer\uc2  is she's won twice before, but Osborne, a Navy veteran, local union leader, is really making this tight. That said, it still leans Republican for this scenario. We're going to give Nebraska's Senate seat to the Republicans. That's another one there.\par \par \uc2 In Maryland, former Republican Governor \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  running against Democrat County Executive Angela Alsobrooks. Hogan has publicly stated he does not support former President Trump. He's rejected the former president's endorsement. He's been trailing in the polls, though. Alsobrooks is ahead in the polls. So we are going to turn Maryland blue.\par \par \uc2 All right. So then we go to our power rankings out West, Arizona, Nevada, lean Democrat. We're going to give both of those for this hypothetical to the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Ohio is another toss up Senate race right here with incumbent Democrat \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  outspending Republican challenger \uc2 Bernie Moreno\uc2  by nearly $65 million.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump is ahead in Ohio, leading Vice President Harrison polls there. Let's say the former president is able to pull Moreno to victory. So we will give in this scenario Ohio to the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 And that leaves Montana. Republicans at that point with Ohio would gain control of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . But if Senator Brown's spending overwhelms, you would all go down to Montana for control.\par \par \uc2 Right now, that's tonight's 50 races in 50 days. The Republicans lead in Montana. And if they managed to do that, it would be 52-48.\par \par \uc2 But for a closer look at that race, we head out to Correspondent Dan Springer in Bozeman with the latest on this Senate race in Montana.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Dan.\par \par \uc2 DAN SPRINGER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yeah. Yeah, good evening, Bret. You set it up. Well, how big is this election in Montana for the Senate? Well, it's already the most expensive race per capita in U.S. history of any kind. And why? Because this really could change the balance of power in the Senate. And it's already being three hundred dollars being spent per capita for every registered voter in the state.\par \par \uc2 Democratic incumbent \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2  launching ads attacking Republican challenger Tim Sheehy over a bullet wound to the arm that he says he suffered in combat. But he also told the Montana Park Ranger it was an accidental shooting in the parking lot.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: But I know two things. Sheehy is a liar and that bullet was from glacier.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2  never served and his false attacks on Tim are pathetic.\par \par \uc2 SPRINGER: According to Fox News power rankings, \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2  is the most vulnerable Democrat up for reelection. A loss by him will almost certainly flip the Senate to Republican control. And for the first time in his political career, he will be on the same ballot as former President Trump, who won this state by 20 points and 16 points respectively.\par \par \uc2 Senator Tester has made abortion a centerpiece of his campaign this time.\par \par \uc2 TESTER: A decision on health care needs to be made by the woman, their doctor, their family, OK. Not by a U.S. senator, not by a judge, and not by a bureaucrat.\par \par \uc2 TIM SHEEHY (R) MONTANA SENATE NOMINEE: I've heard pretty clear from Montanans they want common sense back. What does common sense mean to Montanans? They want a secure border, safe streets, cheap gas, cops are good, criminals are bad, boys are boys, girls are girls.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPRINGER: Republican Tim Sheehy is a first-time candidate, a successful businessman and retired Navy SEAL officer who received a Purple Heart and Bronze Star for valor in Afghanistan. He says he made up the story about an accidental discharge to protect his SEAL teammates in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 Now, Sheehy has held a sizable lead in all the polls the last several weeks, but you know, Tester, with his deep roots here in Montana, has defied the odds before, as you know.\par \par \uc2 Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Dan Springer, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, as the candidates and their surrogates fan out across the country, the panel on what we've seen in this, the final weekend before the election, and what to expect in the next two days. We'll dig in.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) U.S. PRESIDENT: A speaker at his rally called Puerto Rico a floating island of garbage. The only garbage I see floating out there is his supporters.\par \par \uc2 Now, I know some of you guys are tempted to think he's a macho guy. I'll tell you what, man. These are the kind of guys you like to smack in the ass.\par \par \uc2 JON FAVREAU\uc2 , HOST "POD SAVE AMERICA": I would probably look at the numbers. I would look at the race and say, I'm going to focus on being president for the next couple of days. And that's probably the best, that's the best thing you can do for not just the Harris campaign, but himself and his legacy too.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yeah, President Biden on the trail this weekend, got a little bit of coverage.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel. Fox News Chief Political Analyst Brit Hume, The Five co-host Jessica Tarlov, Guy Benson, Political Editor at Townhall.com, hosts The Guy Benson Show on Fox News Radio, and \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  columnist, \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2  Senior Fellow Marc Thiessen. All those intros.\par \par \uc2 Brit, you know --\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS CHIEF POLITICAL ANALYST: The panel's over, right?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yeah, yeah. President Biden, no longer, I don't think, welcome on the Harris train.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Yeah, and it's, you know, he's Biden being Biden. He's always been gaffe-prone. Now he's gaffe-prone and senile. And the Harris people are going to be saying, would you please just take a rest, watch the games? There's lots to do, Mr. President, please stay off the trail. And, you know, he's proud, and I guess he's disappointed, and he's being Biden. Biden being Biden.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Deployed in Philadelphia, Jessica. I mean, obviously, Pennsylvania is still the center of the universe.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, "THE FIVE" CO-HOST: Totally the center of the universe, and their schedules reflect that on both sides of the campaign, though. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's having to spend more time in North Carolina than I think they would hope that he would have to this late in the game.\par \par \uc2 President Biden, though, has a constituency that still likes him a lot, especially with black voters and senior voters who are big fans. And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has the chance to win seniors. I mean, if you look at the internals and a lot of these polls, she is performing beyond expectation. A lot of that due to the fact --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Especially with women.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Well, yes, women across the board, and female seniors as well. But she is doing well with male seniors, too, because she talks about prescription drug prices. She talks about lowering the cost of things, things that seniors -- and protecting Medicare and Social Security.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Rhetoric in closing days, Marc. Here is the former president, raised some eyebrows with his commentary, and then the New York governor.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We had the safest border in the history of our country the day that I left. I shouldn't have left. I mean, honestly, I have this piece of glass here. But all we have really over here is the fake news, right? To get me, somebody would have to shoot through the fake news. And I don't mind that so much.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2 , (D-NY): You're voting for these Republicans in New York. You're voting for someone who supports \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And you're anti-woman. You're anti-abortion. And basically, you're anti-American, because you have just trashed American values and what our country is all about over and over.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, so shoot through the news media, and then you're anti-American if you vote for the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, WASHINGTON POST COLUMNIST: Yeah, so garbage, Nazis, fascists, and now you're anti-American, you know -- there was a poll earlier this election that something said six -- they asked -- or has \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ever said anything to offend you? And 6 in 10 said either no or not recently. So his goal should be between now and Tuesday not to do anything, say anything to offend anybody.\par \par \uc2 Look, there's about a quarter of the electorate, this Post-Schar School Poll, a quarter of the electorate is not firmly committed going into this Election Day.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: How is that possible?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: It's amazing, because --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Is it possible that Peggy Noonan had it right that they're undeclared? They're not -- they're not undecided. They're really just undeclared.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: It's a combination of people who say they're undecided or they're leaning Harris or leaning Trump, but they say they could change their minds. So that's the universe of those people, where they're not firmly committed to one candidate. Those people are -- I would say they're not swing voters, they're reluctant Trump voters.\par \par \uc2 Seven in ten are moderate to conservative, 74% disapprove of Biden, 68% disapprove of Harris, 79% say the economy is doing poorly, and 52% said Trump would do a better job of handling the economy. Only 18% say that Harris would, but they have not committed to Trump.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Guy, the close for Harris seems like she is taking a softer pitch. She is trying to do the unity thing, common ground. I'm trying -- and I like common ground. I'm a big fan of common ground. But it does sound like her pitch has changed from fascist to, you know, whatever to now --\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, "THE GUY BENSON SHOW": That's a tough heel turn. Fascist-Hitler unity doesn't really go together, but she's trying. And I understand that because she's trying to make a play for these final decider independents, basically. That's what she's attempting to do. Big ads she ran today, I think a two-minute ad, basically making that pitch.\par \par \uc2 You know, if I'm the Trump campaign, I'm not an expert, but I would suggest maybe spending a lot of time talking about the worst jobs report in four years would be a better idea than anything else. Or maybe the terrorist attack and anti-Semitic hate crime committed by an illegal immigrant who entered the country under \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in Chicago --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Which, by the way, hasn't gotten a ton of airtime besides here.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: -- which is why I brought it up. I know. I feel like these are two closing events that play right into the strengths of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and leaning into that just strikes me as a good plan for the Republicans, as opposed to all the rhetoric conversations that we're having.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, we talk so much about the numbers, Brit, but the bottom line is, you think there'll be a surprise on Tuesday?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, there'll be some, because we'll get them in down-ballot races.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 HUME: There's always a bunch of those, or several at least. The surprise would be if somebody won big, right? I'm not saying massive landslide, but if we had an early night and a convincing win for one or the other, that would surprise us all, because we've been looking at polls for several weeks now that have all been basically the same, which makes me a little suspicious of them all.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 HUME: That they're all coming out.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Which those guys said earlier, the pollsters.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Yeah, well, even they were suspicious of the pollsters as suspicious of the polls. So, you know, you do have to be, but yeah, I think there's likely to be some.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I think one thing is clear. It's either going to be really, really close. Harris is going to run away with it, or it's a Trump landslide.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes, thanks for narrowing that down for me, Bret. Was there a question in there? One thing that you have to consider in all of this, and I've been wondering where the press releases from the Trump campaign have been about their ground game. I mean, the Harris campaign was knocking on 2,000 doors a minute yesterday in Pennsylvania. We're talking hundreds of thousands of phone calls being banked.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yeah, may also make some people mad, but anyway.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: But that's not -- then you put that little sign outside.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But you're right. No, they're very accomplished. They're very accomplished.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: And ground game matters, and those late deciders in all of the surveys are breaking towards the Harris campaign, and the \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  rally has been a pivotal moment in that. Whether you took it, the Puerto Rican garbage moment, carving watermelons, which was the centerpiece of LeBron James' endorsement. I mean, that was overlooked, the racism of that comment.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yeah. The question is whether the celebrities moved the needle, and I asked that question earlier, but you're right on ground game, and that's a big question for Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Panel, thanks.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tomorrow's Special Report, final full day of campaigning, our special election night coverage begins Tuesday, 6 p.m. Eastern time. If you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6 p.m. in the East, 3 p.m. in the West Coast. It's Sunday, isn't it Sunday? Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, we'll be here Monday, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. Here's Laura.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020241104ekb300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Economy Remains Top Issue for Voters; Trump Campaign Not Utilizing \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  to Help Close Gender Gap; Abortion on the Ballot in 10 States; Poll: Many Americans Worry About Revisions to the Affordable Care Act; Fight for Control of the House; New York Has Several Tight House Races; Harris, Trump make Final Pitch To Voters; Interview with Sen. \uc2 Mark Kelly\uc2  (D-AZ); Interview with Rep. \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  (R-NY)\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Karl Rove, Brit Hume, Juan Williams, Jessica  Tarlov, Jacqui Heinrich, Aishah Hasnie, Bryan Llenas, Mike Tobin, Garrett  Tenney, Madison Alworth \par \uc2 10,289 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 November 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 With just two days until the final votes of 2024 are cast, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  crisscross key battlegrounds as they head for Tuesday's historic showdown.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT & 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Now the fate of our nation is in your hands. I'm asking you, remember this, to dream big again.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES & 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Your vote is your voice and your voice is your power. Are you ready to make your voices heard?\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): America decides between two competing visions for the future and two very different plans to address the issues impacting Americans nationwide.\par \par \uc2 We'll bring you the very latest from the battleground states that will determine our country's next president, as millions of voters cast early ballots in what could be the closest election in modern history.\par \par \uc2 Plus --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I want to protect the women of our country.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: He does not believe women should have authority or agency over their own bodies.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Trump and Harris fight to shore up perhaps the most critical voting bloc of 2024, women.\par \par \uc2 House Republican Conference Chair \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  on the gender gap. Plus, the fight for the House majority which runs right through her home state.\par \par \uc2 And Arizona Senator \uc2 Mark Kelly\uc2 , once a top contender to be Harris's running mate, joins us on the vice president's final pitch to swing states like his, as Democrats face an uphill battle to hang on to their Senate majority.\par \par \uc2 Plus, our Sunday panel breaks down the flurry of pre-election legal battles and previews the mountain of litigation almost certain to come after Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 All straight ahead on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Let's get a check of your headlines.\par \par \uc2 By the way, in case, you forgot, today is the first day of Standard Time. Most Americans turning back the clock and getting an extra hour of sleep. Daylight Savings Time returns in early March.\par \par \uc2 And as of this weekend, more than 75 million Americans have already cast ballots in our historic 2024 election. That's at least 44 percent of the entire turnout in 2020, with a majority of early voters opting for in- person voting, mail in voting has seen a drop post-pandemic.\par \par \uc2 All four presidential and VP candidates have a heavy schedule of campaign appearances today, focusing on battleground states that will likely decide this election from Pennsylvania and Georgia and North Carolina, to Michigan and Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we will talk with Democratic Senator \uc2 Mark Kelly\uc2  from battleground Arizona. And later, Trump surrogate Congresswoman \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But, first, we start with team coverage on this last weekend before Election Day with Aishah Hasnie covering the Trump-Vance campaign in North Carolina, and Jacqui Heinrich with the Harris-Walz campaign in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui, we begin with you in Detroit.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, good morning. Vice President Harris is trying to get creative in the final moments of the campaign, diverting Air Force Two overnight for a surprised appearance on "SNL".\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAYA RUDOLPH, COMEDIAN: The American people want to stop the chaos --\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And end the drama-la.\par \par \uc2 RUDOLPH & HARRIS: Keep Kamala and carry on-mala.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Well, the \uc2 FCC\uc2  commissioner says this is a clear and blatant effort to evade the \uc2 FCC\uc2 's equal time rule. The purpose of the rule is to avoid exactly this type of biased and partisan conduct a licensed broadcaster using the public airwaves to exert its influence for one candidate on the eve of an election, unless the broadcaster offered equal time to other qualifying campaigns.\par \par \uc2 Well, President Trump did not receive an invite according to his campaign, and it's unclear why the show's executive producer changed plans to bring neither candidate on, citing that very law because, quote, you can't have the main candidates without having all the candidates, and there are lots of minor candidates that are only on the ballot in like three states and that becomes really complicated.\par \par \uc2 Team Harris is seizing all opportunities to break through her events in Michigan today compete with football so the answer for her was a two-minute ad buy during swing state games, and her ads running in Michigan and Pennsylvania show she's trying to court a broad audience with competing views vastly different messages for Jewish and Arab American voters.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I will always stand up for Israel's right to defend itself and I will always ensure Israel has the ability to defend itself.\par \par \uc2 What has happened in Gaza over the past months is devastating. We cannot allow ourselves to become numb to the suffering and I will not be silent.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2  who is Palestinian declined to endorse Harris at a Michigan rally on Friday, and President Biden this weekend made Harris's unity pitch a bit harder with another crass remark about Republicans.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: There's one more thing Trump and his Republican friends want to do, they want another giant tax cut for the wealthy. Now I know some of you guys are tempted to think it's macho guy, but I'm serious. These are the kind of guys you like to smack in their ass.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The Harris campaign is publicly projecting confidence, pointing this morning to a new "New York Times" poll that they say affirms their internal data that shows late deciders are breaking for Harris by a double- digit margin, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Jacqui. We'll break down those latest results with the panel coming up. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Aishah Hasnie in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 The Trump campaign feeling mighty good about the groundwork that they have laid here in the battleground state of North Carolina, and it is a very critical state for them in order to get to the White House. In the meantime, the former president seems to be shifting his final pitch his final message really primarily towards those suburban female voters. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : The suburbs are under attack right now when you're home in your house alone and you have this monster that got out of prison. He's got, you know, six charges of murdering six different people, I think you'd rather have Trump.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And that message comes as a new "Des Moines Register" poll has Harris now beating Trump by three points in Iowa. It's a shock poll. It reveals that female voters are carrying her in that state.\par \par \uc2 I've been talking with the Trump campaign about this poll. They're calling it an outlier. They say, well, it might be close in those battleground states, it is not close at all in Iowa and they're questioning, in fact, the timing of the release of this right before all of these Sunday shows today.\par \par \uc2 The campaign also pointing to an Emerson poll instead that has Trump up by 10 points in Iowa, saying this, Emerson College far more closely reflects the state of the actual Iowa electorate and does so with far more transparency in their methodology.\par \par \uc2 Now, Trump is non-stop for the next 48 hours. He'll make seven stops across four battleground states. And perhaps a little bit of superstition, Shannon Trump is ending his 2024 campaign with a primetime rally in Grand Rapids, Michigan, just like he did in 2016 and 2020.\par \par \uc2 The ticket isn't just hitting swing states though. They're also going after those blue-leaning states like Virginia last night. Senator J.D. Vance is also in New Hampshire today with Don Jr.\par \par \uc2 And the campaign tells me that they are feeling very pleased with how they have reached to those low-propensity voters, voters who don't normally turn out for elections. Their internal numbers are showing them that they are succeeding, exceeding Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in that voting bloc in all of the battleground states -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Joining me now, Arizona Democratic Senator \uc2 Mark Kelly\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MARK KELLY\uc2  (D-AZ): Good morning, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. So all of the polling shows us the number one issue for people across the country and there in Arizona, too, is the economy. Majority of Americans now say that they feel like they're worse off economically than they were under President Trump. He's got an edge on that in every state where we've tested it.\par \par \uc2 Here's what "Reuters" says: voters in the seven battleground states, including yours, that will determine the winner of the November 5th election have a negative view of the economy. Sixty-one percent say it's on the wrong track, 68 percent say the cost of living is on the wrong track.\par \par \uc2 But when the vice president was asked what she would have done differently than President Biden, she said she couldn't think of anything.\par \par \uc2 So why should voters who their economic reality is very tough for them when it comes to gas and groceries and everything else put their trust in the vice president?\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Well, Shannon, I think the vice president has made it perfectly clear that she is not going to be the same president as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was. She has plans. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has talked about having a concept of a plan.\par \par \uc2 And when you consider the future of our economy, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is committed to bringing down the cost of things like groceries and health care and housing and education.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's concept of a plan includes 20 percent tariffs on everything that comes into this country, which is going to mean an increase in cost of about $4,000 a year.\par \par \uc2 And if you live here in Arizona and this is really important, Shannon, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  last week said he was going to kill the CHIPS Act which is bringing all these semiconductor manufacturing jobs. And just this week, the Speaker of the House Mike Johnson confirmed that if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is president, they're going to end this program.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, just to clarify though --\par \par \uc2 KELLY: We're talking about tens of good paying jobs -- go ahead.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The speaker has made some clarification on that and said he misspoke or misheard a question when he was asked about the CHIPS Act and there is no plan in the House or the speaker to get rid of that.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Yeah, Shannon, I went back and -- I went back and listened multiple times to what he said and the question. It's very clear to me that he didn't misunderstand what was said there.\par \par \uc2 He actually repeated part of the question in the answer, and, you know, said that if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is president, they're going to end this program, which is probably the biggest foreign investment in our country, we're talking about from \uc2 TSMC\uc2 , $63 billion in the state of Arizona alone. Overall, the investment in Arizona's hundred billion dollars from private companies and these are jobs that you can --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay, his clarification was that they're not going to kill CHIPS. I think that his follow-up was that they would like to better the program, that they would like to embolden and bolster it from his explanation, but again, that's coming from him. You heard it one way. He says he didn't hear the question.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Well, when you consider -- yeah, but when you consider that and you -- what he said, and you combined it with what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made it perfectly clear that program is dead if he is president again.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So --\par \par \uc2 KELLY: I was with some ironworkers just the other day, and these folks are going to lose their jobs if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is president. And that's why I think we're seeing really high turnout here in Arizona. You know, it's that (ph) and what the --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So let's talk more broadly about the economy because there are people now who say that they're having a tough time struggling to put things together two or three jobs to keep themselves going.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: I understand that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Horrible jobs report on Friday and people want to point to a lot of different things, a couple of big storms, but 12,000 jobs created and those were government jobs. The private sector actually lost jobs in the last jobs report, and both August and September were revised downward for more than 112,000 jobs that were reported to happen, that didn't happen.\par \par \uc2 That follows earlier in the summer when we were told that the previous March to March (ph), 100 -- 818,000 jobs that were reported as created weren't created.\par \par \uc2 So people are saying my personal situation, the way that I vote is going to be impacted by what I'm experiencing and it's not good. And they don't feel like they've heard enough, even some Democrats will say, from the vice president, an articulation of why she didn't do things differently under this administration and how she's going to make concrete changes. So voters are left with that.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Well, I think it simply comes down to -- simply comes down to the fact that she is a plan and he after 10 years of either running for president, being president for four years, then running for president again for four years, he has nothing to offer the American people accept grievance and tariffs, and tariffs turns into taxes for families, $4,000 a year.\par \par \uc2 It's in -- you know, the people around him put together Project 2025, that is really a bad deal for the American people and these tariffs are going to be devastating for them. Where \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is going to give a tax cut for 100 million middle class Americans, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is offering to double down on his tax cut for billionaires.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What do you say though when you talk about tariffs -- the fact that this administration, the Biden-Harris administration not only kept most of the tariffs that the Trump administration had instituted, but they added their own as well?\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Well, I think tariffs can be used for specific problems with countries in industries where there are unfair trade practices, and they've been used by multiple presidents over the history of our country. So in certain places, they makes sense but a tariff on everything coming into this country is going to raise the cost for American families by economists say $4,000 a year. It's also going to be devastating to our economy.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  -- I mean, he talks about how he went to \uc2 Wharton Business School\uc2 . But he -- this very basic thing about how a tariff works, he just doesn't seem to understand it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, people do have four years of President Trump. They know what the economy was like then. They know what their personal situation was like.\par \par \uc2 They do in assessing that against the last four years feel better about how they were doing under President Trump, unemployment hit new lows. We had to get through a pandemic clearly, but poverty was down, home ownership was up, and prior to the pandemic --\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Shannon, unemployment --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- hitting, people felt good about where they were.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Shannon, unemployment is at a historically low level right now. I understand that people -- they still and they are, they're struggling with the -- with costs because of inflation, but inflation is also now down to 2-1/2 percent.\par \par \uc2 And I'll come back to, you know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's economy. He botched the handling of this pandemic. A lot of Americans lost their lives unnecessarily --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But would you say -- his team will say, the numbers will show that more than half the jobs lost by the pandemic by the time he left a year into it, they were back. The economy was running again to the assessment of most Americans that they felt hopeful.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KELLY: And, by the way, Shannon, all of those jobs were back under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . We're back down to historic unemployment.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost 200,000 manufacturing jobs. This wasn't because of the pandemic. This was because of his policies to overseas, to foreign countries, 200,000 good-paying jobs.\par \par \uc2 When \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  got into the White House, they worked with the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  and the House in a bipartisan way to come up with programs like the CHIPS Act, bringing these manufacturing jobs back to the country. This was bipartisan legislation, by the way. This is the way that the government is supposed to work to benefit the American people.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's entire presidency and now his campaign has been one about grievance. He talks about the enemies from within. Those enemies are people in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , in Congress, that he wants to specifically go after.\par \par \uc2 He's not a guy that's going to bring us together and solve these problems for the American people in a bipartisan way. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is committed to being a president for all of America, not just for the people who vote for her.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so let's talk about that a little bit, because there's been a lot of language in the closing argument by the Vice President that has referred to him as fascist, has talked about threat to democracy, existential threat, bad for the country, divisive, all of those things. So with that in mind, should he win the presidency? You're a member of Congress that will have something to say about that as the electoral votes come in. Would you as a member of Congress certify the votes for him should he win on Tuesday or whenever this election is decided?\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Yeah, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you think the Vice President would certify as well?\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Yes, of course she would. I mean, there -- and Republicans, some of them in the Senate, you know, we understand the importance of the functioning of our government and the peaceful transfer of power. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does it. He seems to already be trying to set up the conditions where he could do what he did in 2020, do it all over again.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But he did leave in 2020.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: But you're not going to -- after he sent a mob to Capitol Hill, I was in the Senate chamber that day. There are people that died that day because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  refused to accept the election. If it turns out that he wins this election, you're not going to see the same thing from myself and my colleagues, including the Vice President.\par \par \uc2 I mean, she understands that the importance of having in a democracy that you have to peacefully turn over power. And I will do my best if he was elected. I don't believe he's going to be elected, by the way. The momentum is on the side of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . The American people understand that she wants to be a president for everybody, not just the people who vote for her.\par \par \uc2 And here in Arizona, by the way, we have just yesterday, we knocked on 112,000 doors, made 600,000 phone calls. The energy in this state for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and Democrats, by the way, up and down the ballot, it's tremendous. And I think she's going to win here. And I think she's going to be the next president.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, the country is definitely going to be watching Arizona as one of the big ones come Tuesday night and beyond. Senator, we thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, the other side of the closing argument. We're going to talk with Trump campaign surrogate Congresswoman \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2 . As Democrats say, they're bullish on flipping the House away from GOP control.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You're looking live at Lititz, Pennsylvania, where former President Trump is gearing up to take the stage any minute in that critical battleground state. We're just two days from Election Day, and so far, women appear to be outpacing men significantly in early voting. The Harris campaign taking that is a good sign. There's a gender gap.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now to discuss the impact is House GOP Conference Chair \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ELISE STEFANIK\uc2  (R-NY): Good to be with you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let's talk about the gender gap, because clearly it's there. There are also people who haven't made up their minds yet. So Marc Thiessen writing about this over at the "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " says this, about using \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 . "The former \uc2 U.N.\uc2  ambassador is the very model of a reluctant Trump voter who has put aside her reservations and decided to pull the lever for the former president. So, why is Trump not deploying her on the campaign trail?" Mark goes on to say, he thinks it's political malpractice and that these two should just bury the hatchet, get her out there. Why hasn't she been used?\par \par \uc2 STEFANIK: So a couple of points here, Shannon. I think it's important for viewers to know that President Trump is doing better among women than \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is doing among men. There is a significant gender gap, but President Trump is overperforming. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is significantly underperforming.\par \par \uc2 President Trump's campaign has strong, intelligent women deployed across the country. I've been on the campaign trail. \uc2 Lara Trump\uc2  is on the campaign trail. \uc2 Sarah Huckabee Sanders\uc2 . All of the women are working together to turn out the vote. And we are going to earn this support because if you look at President Trump's record when it comes to women, it was the highest number of women ever in the workforce. The largest wage and salary increase for working women ever.\par \par \uc2 Childcare was affordable. And we had safety and security, whether it was a secure border or a president that supported our law enforcement. Look what's happening today across this country impacting women.\par \par \uc2 Inflation. I'm still the grocery shopper in my family. Women go and they see the impact of Kamala's failed economic policies with increased prices at the grocery store, increased utility costs, energy costs.\par \par \uc2 And then of course you have the wide-open border, which safety and security is a top issue for women. So President Trump is going to continue to talk about his record as well as many strong and intelligent women surrogates who are out there.\par \par \uc2 And what I think is very telling is \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' campaign. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  did not condemn \uc2 Mark Cuban\uc2 's just disgraceful comments saying that President Trump does not surround himself with strong, intelligent women, whether you compare it to the first lady, his daughter, \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2 , or so many senior level women that are working in support of his campaign.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to ask you about something else that they've been a lot of inroads with women in the conversation from the Vice President has been that he doesn't respect women's autonomy and their agency. Here's a little bit more of what she said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's not done. He would ban abortion nationwide. He would restrict access to birth control, put IVF treatments at risk and force states to get this to monitor women's pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Now we've all seen the ads. They're run endlessly. And this is the message I hear over and over again. And it seems to be resonating with a lot of women. How worried are you about the states that do have issues like abortion on the ballot and, and how that has been working for the left?\par \par \uc2 STEFANIK: President Trump has been very clear. He believes that this issue should be decided at the states. He has been specific throughout his time in office. He supports three exceptions, rape, and incest, and life of the mother. That is very important for voters to know.\par \par \uc2 And when it comes to IVF, that is a false smear. President Trump wants to expand access to IVF, make birth control available. And the reason why Democrats are only talking about this issue is these are scare tactics because they are losing on every other issue, whether it's the economy, the border safety and security around the world, we're going to run and win. And I think that women, as they look at the key top issues, they are increasingly looking that their lives were much better under President Trump versus the crises that we've seen that are \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  today.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So I want to talk about a couple of things that Senator Kelly brought up. One of them is the CHIPS Act. And he says the president's going to get rid of it. It's going to cost jobs in his state and a lot of other places. I heard the speaker's explanation in trying to say he doesn't believe in getting rid of the CHIPS Act, but there could be modifications or things he thinks would actually strengthen the program. But Senator Kelly said, President Trump says it's gone.\par \par \uc2 STEFANIK: No, the Speaker was very clear. He said, we need to strengthen the CHIPS Act to make sure that the jobs actually come in America. One of the critiques of the CHIPS Act is that they're too much for other countries where jobs are created there and not here right at home. It is important in upstate New York. It is important in the state of Arizona and other states across the country. President Trump's economic policy is America first, not these giveaways to other countries.\par \par \uc2 The other part of the CHIPS Act that is problematic that we want to get rid of a small part is the Green New Deal subsidies. We want to promote manufacturing in the United States. And that's what President Trump believes. That's what Republicans believe. And that's what the speaker was saying. Get rid of these far-left progressive radical Green New Deal subsidies and actually focus on manufacturing chips in America. That's what we're focused on.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. The Affordable Care Act, because that's the -- the Speaker had to do some explaining on this too, as well. There were those who said, he's talking about getting rid of the Affordable Care Act or Obamacare, as some people call it.\par \par \uc2 The top Democrat in the House, Congressman \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  says they are determined to end the Affordable Care Act as we know it.\par \par \uc2 Now, maybe that qualifier language is true. There has been talk about modifications for the act or to the law as it currently stands. 60% of Americans have a positive view of Obamacare. So what about this concern? Should they have concern that if you run the table and House Democrats or House Republicans have the means to that they would somehow modify or get rid of Obamacare, which leaves them without options.\par \par \uc2 STEFANIK: We want to improve healthcare. We want to lower costs. We want to make sure that people have access, whether you're in rural, suburban or urban communities. That's what President Trump is focused on. If you talk to anyone, Shannon, they continue to see their price of healthcare, healthcare go up and up under \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's failed administration with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . So we want to lower those costs, make healthcare accessible for all and improve it in America. That's why you're seeing this slogan, make America healthy. Again, that is resonating with the American people who feel that we can do so much better. President Trump is leading that movement with a big tent Republican party that is focused on improving healthcare outcomes and access for every American.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, obviously, the House, a slight GOP majority, which has been tough, I think to manage on some issues. It's one of the tightest in history and now we go into all the seats, of course, up every two years.\par \par \uc2 "Cook Political Report" on Friday shifted six of these close house races in Democrats' favor, including one of them at here in New York. You have several critical House races here in New York. Democrats have had a significant fundraising advantage. They're feeling bullish. They think that they are going to flip the House regardless of whatever happens elsewhere. Democrats feel that that when the dust settles, and I know it took it to December last time that they're going to be in control of the house.\par \par \uc2 STEFANIK: Well, certainly President Trump on the ballot is a net gain to every Republican candidate for Senate and House. And as we've seen in 2016 and 2020 house Republicans consistently overperform when President Trump is on the ballot. And in both of those cycles, the prognosticators predicted that we would lose seats. We picked up seats in both of those cycles.\par \par \uc2 You know, New York, I'm from New York. Obviously, New York is the epicenter for the race for the House Republican majority Republicans when it comes to early vote, our voting increased by 50% compared to where we were in 2022. And that was the cycle when of course we flipped those five house seats. So we are working hard to turn out our votes. The most important part of this election is getting those votes.\par \par \uc2 So I want to encourage all the viewers to turn out to vote, but we feel very strongly we're going to win on the issues and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is underperforming \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in every single swing district across the country. If you look at \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's performance in 2020, so districts in New York that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  carried by 15 points, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is up by one or two points. That's a 16-point significant shift. And that's one of the districts that the prognosticators moved in the wrong direction towards Democrats. I believe you're going to see a sweeping Republican victory along with the presidential on election day. We just need to get out our voters out to vote. They are voting early election day is going to be very important.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I don't think I know anybody on the GOP side of things that has a better really in-depth knowledge of like all of these districts. You've been a lot of places, you know, the data. Would you care to make a prediction about where you think this lands of the 435?\par \par \uc2 STEFANIK: Again, I've been on the ballot with President Trump in 2016 and 2020 and Republicans surge and turn out to vote on election day, not just Republicans, but unaffiliated voters as well, who President Trump is able to speak to and get them out to the polls.\par \par \uc2 I think on a good night, we can win up to 10 seat, Shannon, which would be a sizable Republican majority. We're not taking anything for granted, but the issues are on our side. People want a secure border. They want a strong economy. They want peace through strength and a stronger national security around the world.\par \par \uc2 On every top issue, Republicans are winning. President Trump is winning. And I think we could be in for some surprises on election day that House Republicans will overperform the predictions.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We're all just trying to figure out how long it's going to take for the dust to settle on all of these races. You brought up \uc2 Mark Cuban\uc2  earlier. You wrote an op-ed responding to his comment that President Trump doesn't surround himself with strong, intelligent women.\par \par \uc2 He since issued an apology on X. Part of it, he says, I didn't get out exactly the way I thought I did. So I apologize to anyone who felt slighted or upset by my response. As I said, it wasn't about Trump voters, supporters or employees. Do you accept his apology?\par \par \uc2 STEFANIK: You know, certainly, I think it's a trend that Democrats are saying out loud what they really believe. First, they called us deplorables. Then they called us Nazis. Then \uc2 Mark Cuban\uc2  said there's no strong or intelligent women around President Trump. And just yesterday, you had \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  smearing voters who support Republicans as anti-American.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are saying out loud -- and they're attacking the American people and voters with that rhetoric. Look, we could have political disagreements. We're running to win on the issues. President Trump is running to unify this country on goals of securing the border, strengthening the economy. So \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  should have condemned that. And they didn't. Their campaign failed to do that.\par \par \uc2 And that's offensive to so many women across this country, not just elected official women, but Gold Star moms, Gold Star wives, women veterans who are proudly supporting President Trump.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we'll see where those numbers land on Tuesday or beyond. Congressman, thank you for coming in.\par \par \uc2 STEFANIK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 All right. We are going to head to the board and look at each of the candidates' best possible paths to 270 electoral votes.\par \par \uc2 And our panel is here with me in the Big \uc2 Apple\uc2 . We're going to talk about the election litigation already pending in states all across the country and the tsunami of lawsuits you may be expecting to follow the big day on Tuesday. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Welcome back to FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 So the path to 270 runs through the tossup states you see here in yellow. They are up for grabs, according to our latest Fox News Power Rankings.\par \par \uc2 Now, we've already filled in the red and the blue states you see here where the two candidates, right now, have clear lead. So let's walk through the two easiest scenarios for both Harris and Trump to get to 270 electoral votes.\par \par \uc2 Let's start with Vice President Harris. Her best chance of victory runs through the Midwest blue wall states, Wisconsin, Michigan, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Now, she and her running mate are spending a significant amount of time in those states. Of course makes sense this past week.\par \par \uc2 But remember this too that Nebraska too, that one district there, that single electoral vote in Omaha, that would put her -- oh, I have missed one here, Pennsylvania. That would put her at 270 and headed over the top to the White House.\par \par \uc2 Now let's talk about President Trump and his path forward. His easiest path runs through the battleground states of Georgia and North Carolina and picking up one of those traditionally blue wall states we were just talking about. The former president won them in 2016, but lost them in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Let's say he scores the really big prize in Pennsylvania. Everybody wants that one. The former president there is neck and neck with the vice president in all of the polling. That would put him at 270 exactly.\par \par \uc2 Now, if you were adding in Nevada and Arizona where they are feeling optimistic in the Trump campaign, Arizona, by the way, is where he has the best polling against Vice President Harris in all of the battleground states. That would make his path to 270 even easier, but come election night, we'll actually find out. It's anyone's guess for now.\par \par \uc2 It is time for our Sunday group. They'll break it down for us. Fox News chief political analyst, Brit Hume. Karl Rove, former Bush White House advisor. Co-host of "THE FIVE," Jessica Tarlov. Juan Williams, Fox News senior political analyst.\par \par \uc2 All right, guys. I'm going to join you here at the desk. And, Karl, I'll start with you. You don't have the whiteboard with you today, but it's fired up for the week ahead. What's the best path forward for these two?\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, you outlined it. I would say I'd have one minor thing. You're right. These sort of blue belt states of Wisconsin, Michigan, and Pennsylvania are her best chance.\par \par \uc2 His best state today in the polling is Arizona. And there's an attempt, big attempt by the Harris campaign to take away either Georgia or North Carolina. North Carolina has sixteen electoral votes, so does Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Georgia, a third of the vote. Thirty percent of the voters are black. Another significant percentage are Asian-American, Latino, so there should -- the Harris campaigns making a bid for both of those states.\par \par \uc2 But you've got it, it's the Sun Belt strategy for Trump, plus winning Pennsylvania or the Blue Belt strategy of the Upper Midwest for her.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and it's interesting that we've got these New York Times/Siena Polls out this morning that are the kind of the last group look from them anyway at these battleground states with some interesting things.\par \par \uc2 As you pointed out, Karl, they do have Trump there at a plus four, but they've got Harris up in Georgia, in Nevada, in Wisconsin, in North Carolina, Juan. That's going to be good news for the Harris campaign this morning.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes. I think they're feeling, you know -- you know that phrase nauseously optimistic?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. We keep hearing that. I think, you know, Democrats are given to anxiety. And I must say, a week or so ago, that anxiety was palpable. But given these recent polls and so forth, things look to me to be, Karl and I -- I heard -- overheard Karl say, it just looks like a tie. He's never seen anything like it. I'm quoting you, Karl. But --\par \par \uc2 ROVE: You could be my spokesman.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Credit to Karl. Trademark.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: But the point -- but the point is that things could break either way. And a lot of what it comes down to now is not people who are persuadable per se, but people who are unlikely voters. And I think that's where the ground game comes into play.\par \par \uc2 Can you get those people to actually go out and vote? "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " poll this morning has late -- has people who've already voted, and you pointed out, you know, millions have already voted.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Seventy million.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Breaking for Harris by --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The late --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The late deciders.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: But the rest of them, the people who are sitting there in their house who haven't voted, the ground game, if it gets them out, that's the key.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: I have a minor disagreement with my spokesman this morning.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Only minor, but yes.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: I don't -- I don't think it's just simply the people who are deciding whether or not to vote. I think there are literally people who are on the knife's edge saying, there's a reason for me to vote for him and there's a reason for me to vote for her and I've got to make a decision.\par \par \uc2 It may be two or three percentage of the electorate --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Right.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: But this race is so close --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, that's significant.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: -- in so many of these states that that could make the determinant.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and I thought this piece by Peggy Noonan over at "The Wall Street Journal" was interesting. She said, "Is anyone undecided at this point? I think it more likely they're just undeclared. They know where they're going and for whatever reason they don't want to announce it to themselves or to others."\par \par \uc2 Brit?\par \par \uc2 HUME: That raises anew the question we've had for several cycles, which is, is there such a thing as a shy Trump voter? Are there people who are prepared to vote for Trump, will vote for Trump, who either won't talk to pollsters, or who will tell pollsters they're voting for a Democrat?\par \par \uc2 And we don't know whether that's still going on. I know it's bedeviled pollsters and has led to errors in the -- in polling for the past two cycles or three, and so here we are.\par \par \uc2 Is there still such a thing as a -- as a shy Trump voter and, you know, not undecided but undeclared as she said? We don't know.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. And there are questions too, Jessica, about whether there are shy Kamala voters.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That's part of the conversation too. I mean she was laid into the game. Her unfavorables are up, the longer she's been in the race. And so maybe there are people who say, like, of these two that's still my choice, but they're not being loud and proud about it.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Well, her favorability though is still in positive territory as is \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  is. He ended up being the most popular character in all of this. And J.D. Vance and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  are hanging out around negative 10. So --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But their favorables are up.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Right. They went from abysmal to just bad, I guess, on there. And they --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They're in the same neighborhood. All of them are sort of in the same neighborhood now.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Either way, Kamala has this shortened campaign. She had to introduce herself to America. She had to make her case, get her platform out there in 100-plus days instead of having years. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's been running for president for nine years at this point.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Let's not forget, however, that when she first got into the race, that's when she was at the highest. She came up just blaze of excitement and --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Fundraising and polling was excellent.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Oh, my God, you know, it was an optimistic campaign and a delirium about somebody who wasn't a -- wasn't Biden and thanking her.\par \par \uc2 If you think about it \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is where she is for two reasons. One, she's not Biden. The other, she's not Trump. And that --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And for some voters, that's all that matters.\par \par \uc2 HUME: That's exactly right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And that's it.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It just seem -- can I just say on the undeclared voter or the shy Kamala voter there? It does seem like that that's really what's happening with a lot of people, especially with female voters. There's the whisper campaign, people leaving post-its in women's restrooms, for instance, that your vote is your business.\par \par \uc2 "Axios" had an article out this week about a survey that showed that 23 percent of people have admitted that they have lied about who they're voting for. If there was a shy Trump voter, there definitely wasn't 2016, less so in 2020 but still existed.\par \par \uc2 That number, it feels, would be lower than it has been in the past and that the shy Kamala voter, if that's even two, three percentage points, that that could make or break this race.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I feel sad for people who have to lie to their loved ones.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Me too.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You know, you just feel like you should have a relationship where you could just be truthful about who you're voting for.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: But, you know, the gender gap is the story, I think, of this election.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's definitely significant.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: And so that your loved one may be voting in the other direction.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Which is fine, but if you have to lie about it, then I would suggest a little counseling.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Maybe so you could communicate about it.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: But, you know, I mean, you mentioned this earlier in your interview with the congresswoman. That's why I can't understand. I thought it was shocking this week that there was no sign of \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  campaigning for Trump.\par \par \uc2 And I think it was kind of shocking when you, you know, you stop and think about some of the comments he's made about protecting women. It sounded very controlling. It's like he's not -- he's not interested in reaching out, especially to Republican women.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Think what would the reaction have been if he'd talked about not protecting women more.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I don't know why he got into it.\par \par \uc2 HUME: He can't win on that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: He could have just not said any of it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, whatever. When we get to election day, here's what we know. From a Harris campaign memo, a couple senior staffers, they say the 2024 presidential election is already the most litigated in American history, but we are the most prepared campaign in history for what we face.\par \par \uc2 Karl, we know the Trump team is lawyered up, too. They're ready to go head to head.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Yes. And look, we've already seen this play out. And, frankly, it was in some -- to some degree, helpful to get these things out in advance.\par \par \uc2 But full disclosure, I helped organize a group called Restoring Integrity and Trust In Elections, which is involved in a lot of this litigation defending state voter I.D. laws or defending the rules as they are on the books of the states.\par \par \uc2 Like Wisconsin attempted to say, you know what, the law requires that if you've sent in a mail-in ballot and it's incorrect, it's up to the voter to cure it, that local election authorities have to tell you to come in and cure it, but you have to cure it.\par \par \uc2 And the local election authority said, you know what, we're going to -- we're going to ignore the law and will cure the problem. And we had to go into court three times in order to enforce the law as it was written on the books.\par \par \uc2 Same thing happened recently in Bucks County where Bucks County said, the law in Pennsylvania is clear. If you're standing in line to cast an early vote, and you get to the closing hour, they have to still handle, let you vote.\par \par \uc2 And the Bucks County people said nope, you go and come back later. And the judge stepped in and said, that was wrong. That's in violation of state law, so you have to add extra hours so people can come back in and vote.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: There are more than 200. As you saw there on the map there are. More than 200 cases all across the country and I'm sure more to come.\par \par \uc2 All right. Panel, stick around.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we're going to take a closer look at whether it really is, quote, all about the economy stupid. This time around, we'll debate what's actually motivating voters in 2024.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I will give a middle-class tax cut to over 100 million Americans. I will enact the first ever federal ban on corporate price gouging on groceries.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I stand before you today as the only candidate who can rescue our economy from total obliteration.\par \par \uc2 As the just-released numbers plainly indicate, the economy is now a disaster.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Both candidates making their cases to the American people about how they would handle the economy.\par \par \uc2 And you heard former President Trump there referencing the jobs report, which was not good on Friday.\par \par \uc2 "The Washington Examiner" says this, "it was the worst since 2020 when the economy was clawing its way out of the pandemic. For the first time since the pandemic, the private sector shed jobs, losing 28,000."\par \par \uc2 Jessica, I don't know how much this data filters down to people in their real life. They still feel like they're paying too much for groceries and gas.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Definitely. I think that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has done a good job of acknowledging that. I was watching her closing at. It's two minutes long. It's the first thing that she says, right? And she talks about going after corporate price gouging, which message test says the most popular policy. And everything she talked about was an economic policy.\par \par \uc2 And I found it astounding to learn that the number one issue that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is advertising on is trans issues. Number five was the economy. So, he can stand up there at rallies and he can say, I'm going to bring down inflation. We know tariffs and deporting everyone isn't going to do that. But when the advertising that people are seeing, especially during football games is all about trans issues. That's not connecting. And the Mariss polls, for instance, had her completely cutting into his lead on the economy down to one, two points in those surveys across battleground state. That means that she is going to have a decent shot at being able to keep her blue wall together.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We actually have some new Fox polling this week that hits on some of the key battleground states. Who is better on the economy? In Pennsylvania, there's a four-point edge for Trump. North Carolina, a six point edge for Trump. In Michigan, two points an edge for him, for Trump.\par \par \uc2 But, Brit, he's - she's really closed the gap on those numbers.\par \par \uc2 HUME: She has.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They used to be much bigger.\par \par \uc2 HUME: She has. And I think if he loses, when we look back on this, a lot of people will think that he - he got off message and failed to take advantage on an issue where he was strong.\par \par \uc2 And, in fact, you know, what we know about this from the polling is that he stands pretty strong on the economy because people do not hold him responsible for the downturn that was (INAUDIBLE) by the Covid outbreak. And they were - they think of him and the economy, they think of pre-Covid. So, he's been fortunate in that. And the question is whether he's taken full advantage of it. And he may not have.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, ads during football games are expensive, but they are also effective.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You're meeting - you're, you know, reaching a huge audience. So, in that same polling, in those same states, people were asked, who does a better job - or who do you think will do a better job of helping the middle class? And this is where the vice president has done really well. Pennsylvania, she's got a six-point lead on that question. North Carolina, a three-point lead in it. And in Michigan, ten points.\par \par \uc2 Karl, those are numbers that make a difference.\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: They are. And look, the economy is important because it's the number one issue, followed by inflation, which is, they're knit together, followed by the border, and then it drops off dramatically to abortion, health care and democracy. And she has wisely gone out there with focus group tested, poll-tested ideas that will have absolutely no impact whatsoever. Like, you know, one of the thinnest margin businesses in America is the grocery business, and she's picking out your neighborhood grocery as the reason why we're paying more. That - inflation came from too much money chasing too few goods. In other words, too much government spending.\par \par \uc2 But we miss one thing by focusing so much on the economy. The other thing that is at play here, she's wisely narrowed the margin there. The other thing is characteristics. At - when this was back, Biden versus Trump, Trump led on - had the physical capacity and mental acuity to be effective as president, represented change, strong leader, cares about people like me. When she got in, he lost all those advantages except strong leader. And some of those disadvantages, like cares about people like me, have grown.\par \par \uc2 So, even if you feel great about the economy, or you feel bad about the economy, sometimes what matters is, how do you feel about the person who's saying, let me be at the control. Let me be in the Oval Office for the next four years.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Juan feels good if you're going to put it in those terms. I see him over here.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes, I was just thinking as you were speaking. I mean I don't know, you know, you know, just to be blunt about it, so many of the characteristics demonstrated by former President Trump are not things you'd want your children emulate. And, you know, I just - I - it's hard to - it's hard to understand -\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: How people put up with it. But people make excuses. They say - he says awful thanks and they say, well, he didn't really mean that. Did you understand what he said? I heard it, you know. He had a comedian talk about a whole country - part of the United States as garbage. Yes, I heard that, but he didn't mean it. It's a little much.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well, he - if he -\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: To me, though -\par \par \uc2 ROVE: If he had said, I - that was bad, that would have - that would have solved the issue.\par \par \uc2 But remember, they're - they're looking at her saying, is she up to the job?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I can tell you -\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Does she have the wisdom and the experience? Does she have the inner strength? Is she going to be a strong leader? Will she be effective?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, that's where misogyny and race come into the picture.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well -\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: And I think, you know, because, listen, she - she was not highly thought of for most of her time as vice president, but she has run an excellent -\par \par \uc2 ROVE: For a good reason.\par \par \uc2 WILLAIMS: But she has run an excellent campaign as evidenced by the fact that she's in a tie today. And -\par \par \uc2 ROVE: No. No, no, no, no. No. Really? She couldn't come up with one thing she would have done differently.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Excuse me, that's - that's -\par \par \uc2 ROVE: This whole campaign has been colored by that.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: That's long gone.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: No, it isn't. It's right here (ph).\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That was a couple weeks ago. That was a couple weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM: That's long gone. She just said that she is not going to be - not going to be Biden.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to -\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The big issue here is abortion, by the way.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: That's proven to be a winner for Democrats in 2020, 2022 and all the referendum. I think it comes (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 HUME: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And it's on 10 state - it's on 10 state ballots this time.\par \par \uc2 HUME: It's just worth remembering that while, you know, she has some things she ticks off as examples of how she'd be different, they don't know how to do very much. And people can see that. And when she said she couldn't think of a thing that she would do differently, I don't think anybody's forgotten that. I really don't.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Jessica, a final, quick thought to you.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I think people's problem with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was too old and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is not too old to do this job. And people are taking stock of that. Abortion, definitely. I know that the Selzer poll from Iowa was an outlier, but look at how women are flocking to the Democrats on this, especially older women who remember a time when they were free and that their daughters and granddaughters will not have the same privileges that they did.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I can't wait to see what happens in Iowa, to see what this polling was all about.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, panel, thank you very much. We'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we're going to take you live to several key battleground states. Our reporters are spread across the country. They'll give us the latest scoop from the trail, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: With just two days until the final votes are cast, our teams are on the ground across all the critical battleground states that we think will ultimately determine the next president.\par \par \uc2 Let's kick things off with Bryan Llenas in that critical state, it's a commonwealth, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump won't win Philadelphia, but if he's able to improve his 2020 performance by even just a few thousand votes, it could win him Pennsylvania and the whole election. Trump has gained the most ground in Philadelphia's poorest neighborhoods amid concerns about black and Latino voter turnout for Democrats. Former Philadelphia Mayor Michael Nutter says Harris will need historical turnout, winning by some 600,000 votes here, surpassing even former President \uc2 Obama\uc2 's performance. Harris' last campaign event is here Monday night, a concert in Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: And this is Mike Tobin in Green Way, Wisconsin, where former President Trump and Vice President Harris are locked in a dead heat. Both of the presidential candidates have made their final appearances here, but the vice presidential candidates are expected one more time, and a parade of surrogates is working the ground, as they understand that 10 electoral vote prize form Wisconsin could be enough to tip the scales. The last two elections were decided here by less than a percent.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: I'm Garrett Tenney in Omaha, Nebraska, which is also known as the blue dot in this deep Republican state. Democrats are spending millions of dollars here to win a single electoral vote that could potentially get \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to the 270 she needs to win the White House. And as a bonus, all of the money is helping boost Democrat Vargas in his bid to unseat Republican \uc2 Don Bacon\uc2  in one of the most competitive House raises this cycle.\par \par \uc2 MADISON ALWORTH, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: I'm Madison Alworth in Atlanta, Georgia. Election Day is two days away, but legal challenges are coming fast and furious here in the Peach State.\par \par \uc2 Republicans suing six counties over their decision to keep electoral offices open over the weekend in order to accept absentee ballots in person. The GOP says this is against state law, which states that drop boxes are to be closed after early voting ends. A Georgia judge dismissed the case, but Georgia Republicans tell us that a federal lawsuit is imminent and that they plan to appeal that decision. In Georgia during early voting over 55 percent of active voters cast a ballot.\par \par \uc2 Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Madison, thank you very much. We'll track all those legal challenges.\par \par \uc2 And Fox News has you covered for election night. Fox News Channel's Bret Baier and Martha MacCallum will lead election coverage on Tuesday from New York City beginning at 6:00 p.m. Eastern. And John Roberts and I will lead election coverage on your local Fox broadcast stations, kicking off at 7:00 p.m. here on the East Coast. Check your local listings for that.\par \par \uc2 All right, that's it for today. Thanks for joining us. If you're passionate, get out and vote. Have a great week. Stay safe. And we'll see you next Fox News Sunday. It will be back in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020241103ekb300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Trump Defends Remarks About \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 ; Businessman Attacks Female Trump Supporters; Harris Denounces Trump Comments; Campaign: Winning Undecided Voters By Large Margin; Court Fights And Probes Over Ballots And Registrations United States Economy Added Just 12,000 Jobs In October; Undecided Voters Talks Women's Health And Immigration; \uc2 Mike Rogers\uc2  And Elissa Slotkin Locked In Toss-Up Senate Seat Battle; Rep. \uc2 Debbie Dingell\uc2  (D-MI) Interviewed On Her Comments About \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Putting His Political Opponents In Detention Camps If Elected President; Michigan Journalists Discuss Democratic And Republican Statewide Get Out The Vote Efforts.\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Rich Edson, Jacqui Heinrich, David Spunt, Taylor  Riggs \par \uc2 8,154 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 November 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Austin, hilariously still offer the officer his license, while his wife was inside. She declined, and baby Henry was born a couple of days later.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Can you tell us more about this?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: You just say about --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: What did you -- three "ONE MORE THINGS".\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: No, that was two.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No, I didn't. I said another day "\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- time for mine, which was a really good minorburg (PH), but you can look it out.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Oh.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Oh.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Congratulations to your cute family. Very cute family, friends.\par \par \uc2 That's it for this (INAUDIBLE) But we're here all weekend, folks. We'll see you on Sunday. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hey, Martha, I'll wait for the minorburg (PH), if you want. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'll send it to you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: See you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'll sent you the minorburg (PH).\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. OK, good.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I'm Bret Baier. Welcome to SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 Spotlight, the battleground states. We are coming to you live tonight from Bloomfield Hills, Michigan, about half hour northwest of Detroit.\par \par \uc2 We're at Franklin cider mill. This is a national historic site started in 1837, actually, the same year that Michigan gained its statehood.\par \par \uc2 Back here in 2024, Michigan figures to be one of the most important states in Tuesday's election. It has a hotly contested Senate contest that will go a long way toward determining the balance of power. We'll dig into that in this show.\par \par \uc2 And it's 15 Electoral College votes could decide the presidential race. We'll hear from Michigan voters a bit later and talk with Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Mike Rogers\uc2 . We'll also visit with Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Debbie Dingell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The presidential nominees focusing on the Midwest today. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was in Janesville, Wisconsin earlier this afternoon. She will be in Milwaukee tonight.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is here in Michigan, about a half hour away in Warren. He'll also be headed to Milwaukee a bit later.\par \par \uc2 Senior national correspondent Rich Edson is in Milwaukee right now with the latest on the campaign.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. Both candidates are in Wisconsin for the second time in 48 hours. This time they are in the same city.\par \par \uc2 The Trump campaign says the former president is going to focus on the economy here tonight, even as other events overshadow this race.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): Former President Trump campaigning in Dearborn, Michigan, before a speech in Warren, just north of Detroit.\par \par \uc2 There, he defended his remarks about former representative \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: If she had to do it herself, and she had to face the consequences of battle. She wouldn't be doing it.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): Last night in Arizona, Trump called Cheney a warmonger. Asked how she would feel about standing on the front lines with nine barrels shooting at her. Cheney reacted, This is how dictators destroy free nations. They threaten those who speak against them with death.\par \par \uc2 Trump's campaign says the former president was clearly talking about Cheney, sending other Americans to fight.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Even in my administration, she was pushing that we go to war with everybody.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): Trump is also traveling today with surrogate \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  He said Trump has promised him control of public health agencies if he wins.\par \par \uc2 The campaign says it's made no formal Cabinet decisions, while Trump tries to stress issues like the economy, other developments have dominated his rallies. Like this Cheney spat, garbage truck rides, and safety vests.\par \par \uc2 Trump supporters are also attacking \uc2 Mark Cuban\uc2 's comments that you never see Trump around strong, intelligent women, because they are intimidating to him.\par \par \uc2 Cuban then wrote, I apologize to anyone who felt slighted or upset by my response. As I said, it wasn't about Trump voters, supporters, or employees.\par \par \uc2 Cuban says he stands by his opinion that Trump does not like being challenged publicly.\par \par \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's mother used that to knock Vice President Harris's jumbled sentences.\par \par \uc2 MAYE MUSK, MOTHER OF ELON MUSK: It's certainly not us and it's certainly not my friends, because they can put sentences together, and you know, who can't. The absurdity of the absurd and the absurdity is absurd.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: A new memo from Trump's chief pollster maintains that the Real Clear Politics average of polling shows that Trump is in a significantly better position than he was four years ago in the swing states.\par \par \uc2 Tony Fabrizio\uc2  says that's not to stoke over confidence, but he says, victory is in our reach. Bret.\par \par \uc2 Rich Edson, live in Milwaukee. Rich, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Harris will be speaking in Appleton, Wisconsin shortly. After that, she goes to Milwaukee for a rally.\par \par \uc2 Today, she continued her attacks on former President Trump's campaign trail rhetoric, in her words. Most recently, that comment about former Republican congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Senior White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich is in Milwaukee, tonight. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 The Harris campaign is barnstorming the blue wall, and their strategy in the final stretch is to zero in on Trump's character, whether or not the details match the broad strokes of their messaging.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The vice president taking a swipe at Trump under the wing of Air Force Two. The campaign even rolling out a podium.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: He has increased his violent rhetoric, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has, about political opponents and in great detail. In great detail, suggested rifles should be trained on former representative, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 . This must be disqualifying.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The Trump campaign says, his point, was Cheney's hawkish views on sending soldiers into war would be tempered if she was in the line of fire, and forcefully denies he suggested Cheney should go before a firing squad at an Arizona round table last night.\par \par \uc2 But Arizona's democratic attorney general is giving Harris's interpretation a boost, saying the office is Looking into whether \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's comments about \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  violated Arizona law.\par \par \uc2 The White House also chiming in.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: It is unacceptable. It is dangerous to speak to political violence.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Whether the women like it or not "\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): It's the second time in as many days, Team Trump says Harris has taken his words out of context to fit a narrative. In Arizona yesterday, Harris framed Trump's comments about protecting women from migrant crime, whether they like it or not, as if he was talking about abortion.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: His latest comment is just the most recent in a series of examples that we have seen from him. How he devalues the ability of women to have the choice and the freedom to make decisions about their own body.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The Harris campaign says its strategy in the final stretch is working, claiming a double-digit lead among swing state voters who made up their mind in the last week.\par \par \uc2 IAN SAMS, SPOKESPERSON, WHITE HOUSE COUNSEL: We are seeing women voting in record numbers in the early vote. We are seeing the Trump campaign already starting to worry about some of their coalition not turning out in the early vote. And we're seeing late breakers coming our way.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): While Harris campaigns in Wisconsin, President Biden was hundreds of miles away, talking to union workers in Philadelphia at an official event.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Fox News has learned multiple campaign calls that were publicly advised on the president's schedule for yesterday afternoon were canceled as he faces continuing fallout over his garbage comment on a zoom earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 None of the calls that had been scheduled were officially sanctioned by or affiliated with the Harris campaign, and Biden has no official campaign events on his schedule ahead of the election. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich, live in Milwaukee. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 BREAKING TONIGHT, we are keeping a close eye on the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 . The justices are considering a case about provisional ballots in Pennsylvania. We told you about that last night. There are also dozens of other matters pending before various courts about the election, and there are new concerns about foreign interference in the election.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt is keeping track of all of it from Washington.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, hello and good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the top levels of the intelligence community are out with a fresh warning about fake election-related videos at the hands of Russia.\par \par \uc2 The director of national intelligence, the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and cyber security officials say Russia is behind a new video showing people who claim to be from Haiti and are voting illegally in multiple counties in Georgia. It is fake, according to these Intel agencies.\par \par \uc2 Russian propaganda aimed to sow doubt in our election system, to the Supreme Court, where the justices could rule at any moment on a request from the Pennsylvania GOP to sequester thousands of provisional ballots from being counted after they were flagged for having protocol errors and issues, like not arriving in secure envelopes.\par \par \uc2 The State Supreme Court previously allowed the votes to be counted. A district attorney in Monroe County, Pennsylvania says his office is investigating about 30 fraudulent registration forms, not ballots.\par \par \uc2 He is blaming a political organizing company out of Arizona with ties to Democrats. We asked the state's governor about this case. Just a few hours ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 JOSH SHAPIRO\uc2  (D-PA): Someone raised their hand and said, I'm not sure this looks right here. I want us to look into it. That's exactly how the system is supposed to work. So that only legal eligible voters were able to cast their ballots at the end of the day.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: And Bret, DOJ officials announced today they will monitor polls in 86 jurisdictions in 27 states to make sure there is compliance with federal voting rights laws. This is not a new practice. It's been happening for years at the justice department.\par \par \uc2 Late today, a federal judge in Pennsylvania ruled that \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's case must go back to state court after his attorneys fought to move it to federal court. Musk is being sued by the Philadelphia District Attorney \uc2 Larry Krasner\uc2 , who says that Musk is operating an illegal lottery with his $1 million giveaway to people who sign a pledge for free speech and gun rights. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David Spunt in Washington. David, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  led U.S. stock indices higher today. The Dow gaining 289. The S&P 500 was up 23. The NASDAQ rose 145.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow lost 1500s of a percentage point. The S&P was off 1- 1/3. The NASDAQ dropped 1-1/2.\par \par \uc2 We'll talk about some bad timing. Tonight's October jobs report is the worst since before the Harris-Biden administration took over. And once again, we are finding out some of the previously touted numbers were not true and revised down.\par \par \uc2 Taylor Riggs, co-host of Fox Business' THE BIG MONEY SHOW joins us from New York with that. Good evening, Taylor.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR RIGGS, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK HOST: Hey, Bret. Yes. So, of course, the October jobs report out. And the economy adding just 12,000 jobs is the fewest since 2020, and of course, a year ago, the economy adding 150,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 So, a big slowdown. More concerning, as you mentioned, are the revisions. Adding 112,000 fewer jobs than originally reported.\par \par \uc2 Still, as you mentioned, Wall Street rallied today investors looking past the week jobs report, highlighting that, of course, there were a lot of distortions between two hurricanes and a major strike at \uc2 Boeing\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Talking about that strike at \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , it actually may come to a close soon. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  workers are set to vote Monday on a contract that the union leaders have endorsed. It does include a 38 percent pay rise in the next four years and a larger signing on bonus.\par \par \uc2 But of course, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  faces a huge financial issue, and planning to lay off about 10 percent of their workforce.\par \par \uc2 Back to today's data, at least, Bret, investors happy that, at least, the unemployment rate remaining at 4.1 percent. That is a number that the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  saying that it was not distorted by hurricanes and strikes.\par \par \uc2 Now, all of this means that when the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  meets next week, they still are likely to cut by about 25 basis points. Remember, this comes after the big jumbo 50 basis point rate cut that they already did last September.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next from Michigan, we talked to voters in this key battleground state about what will really determine their vote on election day.\par \par \uc2 But first, a bit of history. We're here at the World War I polar bear memorial in Troy, Michigan. It was dedicated in 1930. This monument commemorates members of Michigan's own polar bear division, who fought in the frozen conditions of northern Russia against the Bolshevik Red Army in World War I. 56 members buried here.\par \par \uc2 You know, veterans make up six percent of the U.S. population, according to the census. And according to a recent \uc2 Pew Research\uc2  poll, six in 10 veterans plan to vote for President Trump in this election.\par \par \uc2 We'll see if those numbers make a difference on Tuesday here in Michigan. SPECIAL REPORT continues after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to Bloomfield Hills, Michigan, just outside Detroit.\par \par \uc2 Tonight we hear from voters in this crucial battleground state about what issues are important to them, and what they're thinking about the vote.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: The fate of our nation is truly in your hands, Michigan.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Are we ready to win?\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): In the 47 presidential elections since becoming a state, Michigan has voted for the winning candidate 34 times. The Rust Belt state has more recently been adopted into the blue wall, voting for the Democrat in every presidential election since 1992.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We're hours away from a once in a lifetime change.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): With the exception of 2016 when former President Trump flipped the state.\par \par \uc2 As it stands, Michigan is a coin flip, according to The Real Clear Politics average of polls and the latest Fox News poll. With such a tight race heading into the final weekend, both campaigns are fighting for every last vote.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I'm asking you to dream big again for you and your family, your children.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Michigan, it all comes down to this. We know what is at stake.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): With Election Day right around the corner. Oakland County resident Andy Paulson is undecided.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last night you have a conversation that kind of turns your thinking.\par \par \uc2 ANDY PAULSON, OAKLAND COUNTY, MICHIGAN VOTER: Turns my thinking again, because the primary thing I'm concerned about, I guess, right, that's on my mind as women's health. My wife and my daughter are very strong in women's health and choice, and I support them a hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): In a razor thin election, Andy is part of a dwindling group of people whose vote is still up for grabs.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Are there issues that you don't think are being addressed enough? You know, things that you haven't really heard that much about?\par \par \uc2 PAULSON: The borders, the one that I haven't heard the most about for plans. I'm not against immigration at all. I think immigration is good for the country. Illegal immigration is not. I don't think anyone has talked about specifics.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): Andy, he says at times he feels like his down ballot decisions will impact his choice for president.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Why is that?\par \par \uc2 PAULSON: If we have a split government, they kind of keep each other under control. So, I kind of -- if I vote one way down ballot, I may end up voting a different way at the top of the ballot.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): His final decision in this race might come down to Election Day.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Andy, the clock is ticking.\par \par \uc2 PAULSON: I know, I know.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): In the Fox News Power Rankings, the economy is consistently the most important issue in deciding voter's ballots.\par \par \uc2 As home of America's automobile industry, Michigan relies heavily on manufacturing to drive its economy, an issue front and center for Jeff Simek.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, the economy and manufacturing, how it affects manufacturing?\par \par \uc2 JEFF SIMEK, GENERAL MANAGER, \uc2 RCO ENGINEERING\uc2 : Yes, no, absolutely. We have 600,000 manufacturing workers here in the state of Michigan, and so it's a big piece of our economy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): Jeff is general manager of \uc2 RCO Engineering\uc2  in Roseville, a leading supplier to the automotive, aerospace and defense industries.\par \par \uc2 SIMEK: After this interview, we're going to turn the machines back on and keep moving. But you know, manufacturing is our history. I mean, there is deep, rooted history in this state, and we take significant pride in manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): Top of mind for him, tax policy and regulation on the manufacturing industry.\par \par \uc2 SIMEK: The 2017 tax reform essentially created jobs and capital investment, and Bret, you're in a building right now that was the fruits of that tax policy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): Jeff is also concerned with the current administration's aggressive push for electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: The president and I have invested now trillions of dollars in America's infrastructure, in clean energy and a clean energy economy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: How big a deal is E.V. issue and electric vehicles and kind of where that mandate came down?\par \par \uc2 SIMEK: The E.V. mandate has been a big disruption to the auto industry. We're used to looking at long term forecasting models. And right now, it's been disrupted.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): Jeff voted early for former President Trump, one of the state's nearly 2.5 million voters who have cast ballots so far.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: If there's a national abortion ban, nobody is safe.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Let the states make the decision. After 50 years, we were able to do that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): Abortion is a primary concern for another Oakland County resident, Kelly Jones.\par \par \uc2 KELLY JONES, OAKLAND COUNTY, MICHIGAN VOTER: It's really important to me that we remember who we are and that we protect each other as people.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): But she feels both candidates are kicking the can down the road on other key issues.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What other things are you concerned about this election?\par \par \uc2 JONES: I'm concerned about the vitriol. I'm concerned about the violence that we're seeing. I'm concerned about the anger. This is a hard time for us. We have a lot of growing pains. We are going through a lot of really difficult times, but we shouldn't be divided.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): Kelly is no stranger to supporting a Republican. She voted for Presidents George H. W. Bush and \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 . She's hoping this election will send a lasting message of unity.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think that this state is going to be really close just talking to folks?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes. But it's going to be OK. No matter what happens, we're going to figure it out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): We talked to a lot of other voters here too, and this is a tight race.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a pivotal Senate battle here in Michigan. We will talk with Republican nominee \uc2 Mike Rogers\uc2  about what's at stake.\par \par \uc2 First, we take you to a place where vote counting mixes with remembering a boxing legend.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is Huntington Place in downtown Detroit. This day, it is home to Youmacon, a convention celebrating anime, gaming and pop culture. It's also the place where they count ballots. And in 2020, there was a major protest here because of the controversies.\par \par \uc2 Officials don't expect problems this time. Huntington Place is also the home to the statue of Joe Louis, legendary boxer who got a start here in Detroit. There was actually a Joe Louis Arena, a couple blocks away. It played host to the 1980 GOP convention where \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  got the nomination.\par \par \uc2 RONALD REAGAN\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: For those who've abandoned hope, we'll restore hope, and we'll welcome them into a great national crusade to Make America Great Again.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: To this date, it's the only major party convention ever in Detroit. SPECIAL REPORT continues after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to Bloomfield Hills, Michigan. This state is not only a major player in the presidential race, the contest for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  here could be the difference in the battle for control of that chamber.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, I talked with Republican nominee \uc2 Mike Rogers\uc2 , who's up against Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2  in a very tight race.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome to Michigan. How do you feel?\par \par \uc2 MIKE ROGERS\uc2  (R), SENATORIAL NOMINEE OF MICHIGAN: I feel great. The momentum has over the last about six weeks, really picked up, really started with our Farm Bureau endorsement. Had an endorse a Republican since 2006 and boy, that just set off a whole series of events around the state where people started getting excited, realizing, hey, these guys can win.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, if you were to say, what's the most important issue in this election, what do you think it is?\par \par \uc2 ROGERS: Well, certainly the economy. It's jobs. We just lost 2,500 jobs from \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , which was the old Chrysler, a thousand jobs from \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , Ford, just idled, it's Ford F-150 Lightning, which is their electric vehicle.\par \par \uc2 Which means, you know, Merry Christmas and Happy Thanksgiving, you're out of work for the next three or four months.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's been bad. And so, the economy and the cost of goods and services, followed by the border, people are very, very concerned about the lack of security on our border.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The E.V. issue is a big one?\par \par \uc2 ROGERS: Huge, huge, because the E.V. mandates have really kind of gotten the big three out of whack, right? So they invested all this money. Ford just announced they lost $5 billion in their E.v. unit, which means there's no bonuses coming up this year because the government told them they had to produce these things.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congresswoman Slotkin comes after you on a couple of issues. In the debate she went after you on abortion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ELISSA SLOTKIN, (D-MI): Mr. Rogers has voted for every bill, every ban, every restriction on abortion that came in front of him for 20 straight years. He had 100 percent voting record with his party. He never broke once. He has shown us who he is. Do not trust him on this issue.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: How do you respond to that?\par \par \uc2 ROGERS: I trust Michigan voters, and Michigan voters decided in 2022 that abortion would be legal and made it a part of our state constitution. And listen, that's the most heart-wrenching decision a woman would ever have to make, making it here where her doctor is, her partner is, her faith is, her family is, which to me is really, really important. They've made that decision. They went to the polls. And I respect the voters of Michigan. So we have said often I will not go to Washington, D.C. to change what the voters have put in to the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: How much does President Trump factor in here for you?\par \par \uc2 ROGERS: I mean, I think he is a once in a lifetime kind of a political figure. So he is important here. And he talks to the people who are -- you know, I worked in an assembly line. He can speak that language in a way I have never seen somebody do before in political life. He's connecting with people who are worried about their futures and their jobs.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So obviously, the former president says some things to raise some eyebrows, and his critics jump on them, and his supporters say this is just him. Last night, he said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Very dumb.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : She is a radical war hawk. Let's put her with a rifle standing there with nine barrels shooting at her, OK? Let's see how she feels about it, you know, when the guns are trained on her face. You know, they are all war hawks when they are sitting in Washington in a nice building saying oh, gee, let's send 10,000 troops right into the mouth of the enemy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Obviously, a lot of people took that and said you are talking about putting \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  in front of a firing squad, at least the analysts said that today. How do you respond to that?\par \par \uc2 ROGERS: He was basically saying, as somebody who has served in the military, by the way, fourth generation. My son is here with us here even today who is in the military, just getting out of the military. What he is saying is it's easy to say how tough and we ought to be fighting everybody on all occasions. It's harder to be standing on the frontline when you're the result of that decision. I took it that way.\par \par \uc2 Listen, sometimes it's not the most artful way to say it, but why people love \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is because he says it when he is thinking about it in a way that you might say it on the factory floor. I thought he was basically saying easy to be a hawk, not so easy to be a soldier as all result of your decision to be a hawk.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You worked with \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 . You know her up on the Hill. She said, "This is how dictators destroy free nations. They threaten those who speak against them with death. We cannot entrust our country and freedom to a petty, vindictive, cruel, unstable man who wants to be a tyrant."\par \par \uc2 ROGERS: Yes. OK, check, she doesn't like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Got that. Let me tell what you people here are talking about. I talked to a mother the other day, she works part time. Her husband works full time. They have four kids, with tears in her eyes described how the last two days of the month she has to go to the food pantry. All right, so all of that are the big wealthy folks fighting about with big wealthy folks. People here are worried about their jobs. They are worried about putting food on the table.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congresswoman Slotkin represents the district that you did, right?\par \par \uc2 ROGERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It was redrawn, but she portrays herself with moderate Democrat who can work across the aisle. Is she not that?\par \par \uc2 ROGERS: I think it's a little deceiving when she says that. She voted for the banning of gas engines. That's not moderate. She voted to undo Title IX and let biological males compete against our daughters. She has run away with from all of that, every little bit of it. And then the worst one to me is the fact that she voted over and over and over again for these E.V. mandates knowing its it would disrupt and kill the U.S. automobile market here, which it has.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: She has an ad about the E.V.s.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ELISSA SLOTKIN, (D-MI): No one should tell us what to buy, and no one is going to mandate anything. But here's the thing, if there is going to be a new generation of vehicles, I want that new generation built right here in Michigan, not China.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROGERS: Listen, I would love for our car companies to build the cars that people want to buy. That's how you survive. They are already building cars that the government told them to build that people don't want to buy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So are you going to sprint across the finish line?\par \par \uc2 ROGERS: We are going to sprint across the finish line. We have got our door-to-door crew here and we are doing today, tomorrow, Sunday, even Monday we will be out on the road ginning folks up. And again, what we are seeing in early voting is the trend is moving our way. I always said by October we are going to the prettiest girls at the dance here in Michigan. We are going to get the job done and we're going to get the attention we need to get this race over the finish line.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thanks for the time.\par \par \uc2 ROGERS: Thank you. Thanks for being in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We asked Democratic Senate nominee Congresswoman \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2  to join us for an interview, equal time on the show. But like Senator Casey in Pennsylvania yesterday, she, too, declined. We welcome her on SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the Democrats' point of view, and, later, our local journalist panel on what Michigan means to the national picture in the final weekend before Election Day.\par \par \uc2 Right now, an iconic spot downtown with some political history.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is the Gem Theater in downtown Detroit. It was built in 1927. It's on the national registry of historic places. Actually, it's also in the Guinness Book of World Records for the furthest relocation of a sizeable building. It was at one point five blocks away, came here in 1997. It was also the theater where I hosted two FOX town halls in March of 2016 with Hillary Clinton and Bernie Sanders.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I want debt free tuition. You will never have to borrow money to attend a public college or university.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS, (I-VT): That middle class family in the middle of the economy would save about $5,000 a year on their healthcare costs. That's part of our Medicare for all program.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Clinton went on to lose the Michigan primary to Sanders and then won the nomination but lost Michigan in the general election. There was one person who was warning not only about the primary but about the general election for the Clinton camp, and that was Congresswoman \uc2 Debbie Dingell\uc2 . She will join us here after the break. More SPECIAL REPORT after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Nice crowd out here at Franklin Cider Mill. It is time now to get the Democratic perspective on what is happening in Michigan and around the country. I was able to talk earlier with Michigan Congresswoman \uc2 Debbie Dingell\uc2  who represents the sixth district west of metropolitan Detroit.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: How do you feel about this election? You always have pretty good feelings about things.\par \par \uc2 REP. DEBBIE DINGELL, (D-MI): Bret, I don't know who is going to win on Tuesday. I would rather be us than them, but we are a battleground state. It's really been a battle on the ground. There are a lot of intense feelings. I have never seen it more vitriolic. There has been a lot of focus on union halls, and Trump is going to get some union workers, but there are a lot more union workers that are studying the issues. I think we are doing better with African American young men than we --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think they are coming home?\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: African American young men? Yes. That of all three groups are the ones that I think are coming home the most right now in the last couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I tell people that before that march 2016 town hall that we had with \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and Bernie Sanders, you kind of sent up the red flare that she could lose Michigan. She did to Bernie Sanders. And you did it again in the general election. So you kind of have a sense of things. Right now, you don't have a sense?\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: It's tight. I think this weekend is going to be a very important weekend. I think voter contacts, getting people to understand that we have got to go out and vote, and we can't make any mistakes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What do you think the most important issue is here?\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: Honestly, the economy. People are worried about it. They are worried about their jobs. They are worried about the grocery prices. You can't be in Michigan and not know that the Mideast is a very serious intense issue. People are hurting on all sides, all sides.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: How do you talk about the turn the page thing with the sitting vice president, who is the vice president of the president who has low approval ratings when it comes to the economy?\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: Look, I know that she understands the issues. And it what's very loyalty, I'm one of those old-fashioned people that thinks loyalty matters. So she has got to be respectful, talk more about what her economic plans are. I said to them, I said a month ago unions halls don't know who she is. They have got to know she is going to go in and fight for them. They know who \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The last few days there has been a lot of words back and forth. There's been a comedian at \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  saying "garbage." There is the president of the United States saying "garbage" and walking it back, all of that. There is fascism talk. There is what the former president said last night at that rally that got a lot of attention today about \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 . You got a lot of attention recently by something you said about interment camps.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: The Arab American community needs to be reminded and cannot forget he wants to ban Muslims, he wants to deport Muslims, and he wants to start internment camps. And that's what we are busy talking to every voter. He is telling you what he is going to do, believe him.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Internment camps?\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: Yes, he has talked internment camps. You know what, Jake, you may have to visit me in one, I get worried enough when he talks about what he is going to do to political enemies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: Here is why I said it. He has, by the way. I can give you the references to detention camps.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: About illegal immigrants.\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: And it's not a joke. He has been very clear in his language that he will go after his political enemies. What he said about \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  isn't OK last night. We have to -- all of us, all of us, all of us have got to dial down the language.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Trump campaign says "President Trump was 100 percent correct that warmongers like \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 ," these are their words, "are very quick to start wars and send other Americans to fight them rather than go into contact. This is a continuation of the latest fake media outrage days before an election in a blatant attempt to interfere on behalf of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 I have known you for years. You don't think you are going to internment camp. Do you really believe the four years of President Trump is going to change so dramatically that something like that would ever happen?\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: So, Bret, I want to answer it this way. I have had very serious people tell me you need to worry about yourself. But I do get scared about what we see. And what also scares me isn't only the words that he uses, and he names people by names. It's how it instigates other people to potential violence.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: If you say that, can you turn to your party and say --\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: -- I do.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: -- tone down the fascism, tone down the Hitler, tone down the nazi, tone down the MSG was a nazi rally. Come on.\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: So I did tell everybody we need to tone it down. You know me.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let me ask you a couple quick specifics about this place. Electric vehicles, is that a big issue in this election?\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: I think it's a bigger issue in the Senate race, quite frankly. We are not banning the production of internal combustion engines. People can drive them. The global marketplace wants electric vehicles as well. They are part of a production mix, part of a product mix. I'm going to keep this country, this state competitive so they are part of the vehicle mix.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But you hear the reality, Ford pausing the F-150 Lightning?\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: But do you also know that they have shut down, several of the companies have internal combustion vehicle shutdowns in those plants, too?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: The industry, I happen to know the industry, is an industry of ups and downs and cycles.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I just hear some people say the consumer should be the buyer, and if they don't like electric vehicles --\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: OK, but can I also remind you that the companies didn't like catalytic converters. They didn't like seat belts. They didn't -- we need - -\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So you think the emission standards as they are set, which some people call a mandate.\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: There is an offramp on it. They have got to look. They've got to see what the consumer wants. But we also need to make sure that we are ready for the vehicles of the future. And global climate change is real.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You're going to sprint to the finish here. You have your own race where obviously you are running. But do you think Michigan, the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  team can put it over the edge?\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: I think they can. But I don't take it for granted. I will not because I have a record of predicting accurately. Ask me on Monday what I'm predicting. I got a weekend of a lot of hard work of being in union halls and schools and veterans halls, and farmers' markets and football games and tailgates. Maybe a little beer pong.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: But I want to -- I'm going to be out this weekend. I'm going to be talking to people, and then I will tell you where we are.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We always appreciate you talking to us.\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Breaking tonight, just in the past few minutes, the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  has declined to block the counting of some provisional ballots from voters in Pennsylvania whose mail-in ballots are rejected. This concerns ballots rejected for various technical errors including those that arrived without mandatory secrecy envelopes. This is a defeat for Republicans in Pennsylvania who were fighting a lower court ruling.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we talk to a pair of journalists who cover Michigan politics full time. We'll get their take on what to expect come Election Day.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I will end Kamala's insane electric vehicle mandates, saving the Michigan auto industry from extinction.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is even more unstable, and more unhinged.\par \par \uc2 So, Michigan, it all comes down to this. We are here together because we know what is at stake.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's bring in our panel, local reporters Zach Gorchow, president of Gongwer News Service here in Michigan, and Jordyn Hermani, capital reporter for "Bridge Michigan." Thank you both for being here. I want to kind of tee it up by saying, what are you not seeing? What do you see on the ground? First to you, Jordyn about this race. It's obviously super tight.\par \par \uc2 JORDYN HERMANI, "BRIDGE MICHIGAN": Absolutely. No, what we have been covering a lot, and I've seen the national outlets cover as well, the Arab American vote. But parsing into it more definitively, the fact that Arab American voters are not a monolith and getting more towards the core of this concept that there are Arab Americans who do support President Trump, who do want to see him elected President Trump. And it goes beyond, frankly, just the Israel-Palestine conflict.\par \par \uc2 ZACH GORCHOW, GONGWER MICHIGAN STATE AFFAIRS: I think that goes right to the thing that I'm looking at, which is what is former President Trump sealing in Michigan. He got 47.8 percent of the vote four years ago, 47.5 eight years ago. There's a lot of debts whether he can get above 48. And so I think the goal for his campaign probably is get Harris, Vice President Harris, below 48 like Hillary Clinton in 16.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. So a lot of people have questions about the process in Pennsylvania. They pass something that they are going to count no matter what. They are not going to stop overnight. Does Governor Whitmer pledge that here, or do we know how this process is going to work?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GORCHOW: I think that the general sense is that Michigan's system is pretty stable, but everybody is loading for bear. Both parties are going to have a ton of attorneys at the ready. The Republicans have been filing case after case after case in the Michigan courts. They have won a couple. They have lost more. But the Democrats I don't think will be ambushed in the way they were four years ago with legal challenges.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It seems like the get out the vote thing is really important here too, and usually here and in Pennsylvania Democrats seem to have an edge on that front. Fair?\par \par \uc2 HERMANI: I would say so. So just speaking from my own perspective, we have seen tons and tons of door-knocking, frankly, on both sides. But Democrats have made a heck of an effort. They are back here, frankly, every weekend with some other surrogate for the Harris campaign. Trump, for what it's worth, has done a heck of a job in crying to meet that demand. But by and large Democrats do seem to be leading on that front.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, they visit this place a ton. It's always -- there were three visits today.\par \par \uc2 GORCHOW: It's been unceasing. It's kind of incredible. We've had both presidential candidates here on the same day, I think, multiple times. Trump is planning his kind of what's now his ritual, midnight speech in the Grand Rapids area the night before the election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He did it in 2016 and 2020.\par \par \uc2 GORCHOW: Exactly. So it seems like he's trying to get the lucky rabbit's foot out. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is going to be on the \uc2 Michigan State University\uc2  campus on Sunday, essential turnout there for any Democratic statewide candidate.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: If we are looking at Michigan and we're looking at our big graphic and Bill Hemmer is at the board and I'm at the anchor desk, what part of Michigan should why focus on?\par \par \uc2 HERMANI: Historically Saginaw County has gone on to pick winning presidential candidates. I believe of the last several decades has only missed about three times. So the turnout there is going to be crucial and should give you some indication of the headwind for the rest of the state.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And are you excited to stop commercials eventually, and text messages and everything else?\par \par \uc2 HERMANI: I was told when I went out to early vote that it would stop, but it hasn't yet, so no guarantee it will after November 5.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're almost there, almost there. Thank you so much for coming in.\par \par \uc2 GORCHOW: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HERMANI: Of course, thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, it is Friday, and you know what that means. It's time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CROWD: USA! USA! USA!\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: It was like a love fest, an absolute love fest.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This is someone who is unstable, obsessed with revenge, consumed with grievance. I'll give them a seat at the table.\par \par \uc2 HULK HOGAN: You know something Trump-maniacs? I don't see no stinkin' nazis in here.\par \par \uc2 TONY HINCHCLIFFE, COMEDIAN: There is literally a floating island in garbage in the middle of the ocean right now. I think it's called Puerto Rico.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I have breaking news for you, Mr. President. You may not have heard this. Just moments ago \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  stated --\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The only garbage I see floating out there is his supporters.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: He was not calling Trump supporters garbage.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The White House changed the transcript. This is literally changing the truth.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's very obvious to me there is an apostrophe at the end of supporters there.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: For he not knoweth what he said.\par \par \uc2 How do you like my garbage truck?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think folks are fired up to not only vote against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR\uc2 ., FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I don't want your vote. I want you to vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: When was the last time I talked to the former president?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2 , (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Last time I talked to him was back in June.\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN\uc2 , BUSINESSMAN: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you never see him around strong, intelligent women.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I see a lot of really smart, independent women here.\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE, ACTOR: Pennsylvania, keep punching.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Can you believe that was one week? One week.\par \par \uc2 And we will have a special election edition of SPECIAL REPORT Sunday, 6:00 p.m. eastern from New York. In the meantime, can you also join Shannon Bream for FOX NEWS SUNDAY. Her guests are Arizona Senator \uc2 Mark Kelly\uc2 , Democrat, and House GOP Conference Chair \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And of course, Tuesday, our FOX News election coverage begins at 6:00 p.m. eastern. We have cast of thousands ready to weigh in.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast. You can follow me on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  @BretBaier for exclusive behind-the-scenes photos and videos and more.\par \par \uc2 Our thanks to the Franklin Cider Mill here.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE starts after these folks wave. Say hi to Laura.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020241102ekb100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Mark Cuban\uc2  Insults \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's Female Staff; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Protect, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Patronizes. Is Biden sabotaging Kamala's campaign; Biden's revenge tour; Illegal immigrant arrested for raping children; Chinese national allegedly votes illegally in Michigan; \uc2 Mike Rogers\uc2  battles Slotkin in Michigan Senate seat; Ted Cruz makes final push to keep Allred out of Senate; Down with democracy, Avengers actors assemble to endorse Harris and Walz.\b0\par\par\uc2 Laura Ingraham, Jeanine Pirro, Raymond Arroyo \par \uc2 7,481 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening, everyone. I'm Laura Ingraham. This is The Ingraham Angle from Washington tonight. As always, thank you very much for spending time with us. Kamala's women problem. That's the focus of tonight's Angle.\par \par \uc2 The richest surrogate for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , billionaire business owner \uc2 Mark Cuban\uc2  thinks conservative and independent women working for Trump are stupid and unaccomplished.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN\uc2 , BUSINESSMAN: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you never see him around strong, intelligent women, ever. It's just that simple, they're intimidating to him. He doesn't like to be challenged by them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes. Well, speaking of someone who has challenged him on many occasions, and I think he still respects my opinion, that's a really dumb comment. So just proving billionaires can be bozos as well, I guess, Mark? Well, here's a fact check for Mark who doesn't have a clueban. Here's a small list of those supposedly unintelligent women associated with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 His wife, Melania, I don't know, does \uc2 Mark Cuban\uc2  speak five languages? His campaign manager, Susie Wiles, a season political pro. America First Policy institute Chief, \uc2 Brooke Rollins\uc2 , brilliant. \uc2 Linda McMahon\uc2 , also with \uc2 AFPI\uc2 , former \uc2 WWE\uc2  exec came up from nothing. Kellyanne Conway, of course, we all know Kellyanne. Governor \uc2 Sarah Sanders\uc2 , \uc2 Kayleigh McEnany\uc2 , and the list goes on and on.\par \par \uc2 Now, of course, Cuban was responding to comments made last night by former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: People told me about four weeks ago, I would say, no, I want to protect the people. I want to protect the women of our country. I want to protect the women. Sir, please don't say that. Why? They said, we think it's -- we think it's very inappropriate for you to say so. And I said, well, I'm going to do it, whether the women like it or not. I'm going to protect them. I'm going to protect them from migrants coming in. I'm going to protect them from foreign countries that want to hit us with missiles and lots of other things.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, of course, it's clear what he means, that he would be a strong president. Yes, a protector. The president is supposed to protect us, right? Protector of our borders, our economy, and for all people, even for liberal female voters who don't support him now. And all the women I know and yes, including Democrats, they do love men who are protectors. What do they think we want men who break and can't fix things, not attractive.\par \par \uc2 Now, it's not sexist for men to want to protect women, and it's not weak for women wanting men to protect them. Sorry, it's natural. But when Harris is on the trail, she rarely seems natural, and she always seems like she's just reciting rehearsed lines, in this case, pretending to be offended by Trump, the protector.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE U.S., (D) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Did everyone hear what he just said yesterday? That he will do what he wants, quote, and here's where I'm going to quote, whether the women like it or not. We must vote, because this is the thing. You know, there's a saying that you got to listen to people when they tell you who you are and who they are. And this is not the first time he has told us who he is. He does not believe women should have the agency and authority to make decisions about their own bodies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Who their agency. She sounds like a big lawyer lady there. Actually, she's just thoroughly unconvincing. It doesn't matter how much she laughs her way through her remarks. It doesn't matter that she claims to be a uniter who will work with both sides. None of that changes the fact that at her core, she is a divisive San Francisco radical, and with her and her, you know, call you a Nazi or Hitler, because what, she's going to do that because she can't defend her own agenda.\par \par \uc2 Everything she did as VP hurt women. Her open borders, which let migrants in, who abused, trafficked, raped and even killed women and girls. Her inflation that rob young women of the chance to buy a home, that forced senior women back into the workplace to afford daily life, that forced women to raid their 401(k)s, and yes, her demonic trans agenda that forced women to compete against biological men in sports.\par \par \uc2 And Kamala claims to be pro-choice, but when it comes to vehicles, her climate change mandates will give women no choice in automobiles. If Kamala wins, most will be forced to ditch their gas-powered cars. I like mine. Thank you very much for EVs and or the Greeniacs have their way, there'll be no cars at all. That's the ultimate dream.\par \par \uc2 So, how is any of this pro-women. Now, female voters, I guess, are supposed to agree to a lower standard of living in unsafe streets and throw in perpetual war just because the Supreme Court returned the abortion decision to the states. OK, someone has to explain that to me. How is that logical? The women who people like \uc2 Mark Cuban\uc2  look down on aren't one issue voters.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What's driving your vote?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I just think securing the border and hopefully boosting our economy. I voted for Trump and Trump 2024. I think he truly made America great in his last run, and I hope he can do it again.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Farmers matter, automotive matters, Trump matters, and he's going to take care of the farmers and the automotive industry.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Trump supporting women. He's for women's rights and even fertility rights, and I like that he's against abortion and keeping our country safe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But Democrats and their media boosters are so flummoxed by Trump's staying power that they keep falling back on the F word. Even his wanting to protect women now is a threat.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: When you look at him as the protector, I'm not sure what he's talking about. It's kind of some sick distorted fascist way.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Of course, most of these people don't believe that Trump is a threat to women, just like they don't believe he's a fascist or Hitler. They're just out of ideas and actually they're just out of gas, and they -- all they can do is hope to scare enough women to push Kamala and her terrible record over 270 electoral votes. And look, they have a lot of money. They may succeed.\par \par \uc2 But you're supposed to take solace in stories like this. Right now, more women Democrats say, and it looks like this is the case, than men are voting early 55 to 45, but that also includes GOP women voters as well. So, we'll see how things end up on Tuesday. But the trends now are obviously positive in favor of Trump. The most accurate poll the 2020 cycle Atlas Intel, has Trump up in six of the seven swing states right now, although the margins in some of them are very, very tight.\par \par \uc2 Now, remember, Kamala signed off on a strategy to go light on policy and heavy on fear and smear during this campaign. And in the end, they went all in on January 6 and fascism with a touch of you're garbage, courtesy of Biden. She repeatedly lies about the abortion issue to frighten women, and of course, the press isn't going to fact check her.\par \par \uc2 But Trump, he answered her by smiling at the \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's drive-thru, by having fun taking reporters' questions in a garbage truck, and by forging on undaunted. So, confident that he's keeping states like New Mexico and Virginia in play. But remember, Kamala and her surrogates don't think women voters need to hear solutions on inflation or the border, only abortion fear-mongering. Now, which candidate insults and patronizes women again? And that's the Angle.\par \par \uc2 Now, we should note that \uc2 Mark Cuban\uc2  tweeted out a clarification earlier today, noting that there are strong, intelligent women who support Trump, but his comment is in line with the personal nastiness going all the way back to Hillary's deplorables comment. And joining me now, Judge Jeanine Pirro, co-host of The Five. Judge, now Cuban clarified, but we all remember the Kavanaugh confirmation battle. They think Trump supporters are kind of something out of The Handmaids Tale, right?\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, CO-HOST, THE FIVE: You know, it all started with Obama, we cling to God and guns and our religion, and then we were with Clinton, the basket of deplorables. And then with Biden, we're fascist, and now we're garbage. Look, the Dems are divisive, and \uc2 Mark Cuban\uc2 's comment, Laura, is a continuation of that divisive rhetoric. We're stupid too. Anyone who supports \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , there aren't any intelligent women, except five hours later, there was so much pushback given the number of women that you yourself identified in that terrific open, you know, who were supporting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But what about all of the millions of women who were entrepreneurs and business owners and teachers and nurses and doctors? I mean, this is what the Democrats are doing. They are trashing women. They are trashing Trump supporters. And Kamala can stand back and say, oh, I would never trash a supporter of any candidate, but if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is Hitler, or if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a fascist, what does that make the people who support him? We're Hitlerian, we're fascists.\par \par \uc2 And so, the comment that Trump always makes, which is, they're after you, but I'm just in the way, is so telling, because they are criticizing us. This isn't just criticizing a politician, they're criticizing half of America, and it's not working. And honestly, Laura, it's beginning to tee off a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , Judge, wrote this about white female voters. The sting of Mrs. Clinton's loss and the familiar notes of sexism and racism that Ms. Harris has faced this election season may have mobilized many Left-leaning white women to try to prevent history from repeating itself. So Judge, they're already setting up the narrative that if Kamala loses its racism and sexism to blame, clearly, they're not going to change their policies, and they're not going to blame their policies --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Right.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- again if, in fact, she doesn't get to 270 electoral votes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Well, you know what, they're right about one thing. It is about sexism. It is about the fact that they're allowing men in women's sports to destroy the dreams of young girls to knock out women, to literally put them in locker rooms with young girls. Yes, they are. That is sexism. They are destroying the dreams of young girls. And how about the women and the mothers that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and her team is allowing into this country who are killing women and young girls?\par \par \uc2 I mean the damage that's occurring to women in this country, and the comment that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made about protecting women. He made it very clear in the sentence that he was talking about illegals, which even \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  came out and said, had the Biden Harris administration vetted these people properly, Laken Riley might not be dead, along with people like Jocelyn Nungaray and Kayla Hamilton and on and on.\par \par \uc2 They are hurting women. They are destroying women. And if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants to look at protecting us from foreign wars and protecting us from sexism that kills us and this, this, this, this, in the -- the -- what's going on at the border from killing us, then God bless him, let him protect us.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes. Well, Judge, Judge, I just think it's hilarious and sad, and also telling, all three, that this is the -- this is it. This is where the Democrats end up after four years of Biden, really, this is now going to be 12 years of the Obama policies, 12 years, and all they can say for themselves at the end is abortion and you're Hitler, that's