for us, we're all Latinos.\par \par \uc2 So, it's not even that we can't take a joke, it's just like, dude, the joke sucked. It was trash, it was bad, it was horrible. It was horrible, it was complete trash. But what I do get worried about, because the ones vote for Trump, we're voting for Trump, because we know better.\par \par \uc2 But the ones that might be on the fence, that might, you know, out of rage, do something. You got to just think about it like, man, how we've been living the last four years, how your peers around you have been living the last four years, how they treat us right now, how they've done nothing for Puerto Rico after these hurricanes, have done nothing for Puerto Rico, that she's locked up more Latin and black men than any other person that's run for presidency.\par \par \uc2 Go look up her record, how many people she's locked up for smoking marijuana, Latin people, black people. People who look just like me. And now we're going to go vote for her because some weak ass comedian said a poorly timed joke that wasn't even funny. They told me he was a comedian. I was like this guy gets paid to tell jokes? That's crazy man.\par \par \uc2 You know and I'm thinking, yes, the consensus are right. His mom should have swallowed him, man, because this guy stunk. They just like F the bet, you know. So.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Jorge, that's never been said on the Angle before. But Jorge, do you think they're playing --\par \par \uc2 MASVIDAL: The truth?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah, no, no.\par \par \uc2 MASVIDAL: They always say the truth here. I'm joking.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Jorge, the idea of AOC and Walz playing a video game, a football video game on the platform Twitch, that they think that's going to get men voters, or ads on porn sites are going to get male voters? I don't understand this strategy on their part.\par \par \uc2 MASVIDAL: It's a desperate strategy. They're down by a lot. They're tucking their head down and they're just hoping for a Hail Mary, hoping that whatever they throw at the wall will stick. You know, but we know better. We're already seeing these tactics and we're not going to get shaken into no way, no how, another four years of this misery. Like, no way.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What was your line? Jorge, what's your description of what happened at the Garden last night? Yeah, you know, big arenas. What did it look like to you?\par \par \uc2 MASVIDAL: It looked amazing. The power in there, it felt great. Especially in a state like New York, you know. So, I loved it. I wish I could have been a part of that one, because it looked like a great environment. The American spirit was strong there. God bless everybody.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It was fun. Jorge, you should have been there. You should have been there, man. Next time.\par \par \uc2 MASVIDAL: You don't invite me. You never invite me for the -- I'm always in New York. You don't invite me for in-studio. You don't take me for coffee. Nothing. You don't get me here running around.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We'll get it done. All right. We're a little busy. We'll get it done, but I promise you. You're in. Jorge, thank you. Muchas gracias. Por todo.\par \par \uc2 All right. Raymond went inside the Garden to talk with the Trump rally folks, and Kamala goes out for a drink. Seen and Unseen, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's time for Seen and Unseen, where we reveal the stories behind the headlines. For that, we turn to Fox News contributor Raymond Arroyo. All right, Raymond, having been there, the media depictions of this Trump rally, unbelievably unhinged.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Laura, MSNBC aired a piece echoing \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 's libel. She started to remember that Trump's Madison Square Garden rally was a recreation of a 1939 Nazi rally.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: That jamboree happening right now, you see it there on your screen, in that place is particularly chilling, because in 1939, different fascist leader Adolf Hitler packed the Garden for a so-called pro-America rally. Against that backdrop of history, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is once again turning \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  into a staging ground for extremism.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: So, we asked participants, Laura, at the \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  event, their reaction to Clinton's depiction of their rally.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Absolutely despicable that she said that.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: She's a liar.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: It's the most diverse rally I've ever seen, the most diverse movement I've ever seen, because we are all united for the love of our country.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON, FORMER U.S. FIST LADY: Trump actually reenacting the \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  rally in 1939. Neo-Nazis, fascists in America.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Didn't \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  accept the nomination here?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: More rhetoric from Hillary Clinton about the deplorables. Now it's Hitler, Nazis. You never get away from the law.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: She's crazy, you know? It's not a Nazi thing, you know? They just say the most craziest stuff.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: There's nothing but love and support over here. It's incredible what this movement that we have, and it's historical.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: This is probably the most diverse crowd I've ever seen in my life. The only thing I don't see here are Nazis.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I can't imagine Cole and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Hitler, because he's nothing like that. He's a father. He's a husband and he's a man that loves our country.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: This is a rally full of people who support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and support making America great again, and seeing change in our nation that we need to see. And we welcome everybody.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: It's the most offensive thing and it's dangerous to the people that live in New York City, the people that work hard every day and love our country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, I would point out, having been at the Garden this Sunday from noon to 9 P.M., I saw zero Nazi flags, but there were a ton of Israeli flags behind the president all around the arena, which I don't think historically is a common feature at Nazi rallies.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah, I think after tonight, we're even not going to even talk about this anymore because it's so ridiculous, and it's obviously just out of ideas.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yeah, but they keep talking about it, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, we have to ignore them.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: The campaign that was supposed to unite the country, Laura, continues to divide by calling half the country fascist synopsis.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah, they hate you. They hate you and they hate America, other than that.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: And if you're tilting against Trump's everyman appeal, Laura, where else would Kamala go to reach the common man? Why -- hitting the bars with \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Oh, we have microphones in here just listening to everything. I didn't realize that.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: OK. Well, now you tell us. We just told all the family secrets.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Can you believe this? So, they stage a photo op at a bar, supposedly on Sunday, the two girls are watching TV, watching football again. And you see the awkwardness. And then she's cursing, I'm spilling the family secrets. Oh, and you know, it's an embarrassing display. And it shows again her inauthentic self and how she just doesn't fit in, no matter where you put common sense.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: No one looking at this, no one looking at that and think that's a future president of the United States. No one. Trump can make an apron in McDonald's work. She can't make that work.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, she's 53 to 37 down, according to the recent Suffolk poll, with men, OK? Trump's at 53. That's why she's attempting these sad reenactments of what normal people do in suburbia on a Sunday. It's not working for her. No one is convinced that she's like them. And men don't care if you're sitting in a bar or not, frankly.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, so men are either drinking or they're watching porn because they have those porn ads too, right? During porn, those are popping up.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Don't give them any ideas, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: No, they have ads apparently. That was written about yesterday. So, they have that, they have -- and then it's like fake loading of gun. They can't load the guns. These people are a gang that can't shoot straight. Raymond, it was fun to spend some time at MSG with you. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 All right, that is up for us tonight. Make sure to follow me on social media. I have some A&M photos up there from the big game. That was fun. We beat \uc2 LSU\uc2 . Jesse Watters, next.\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020241029ekas00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Trump Packs Iconic Madison Square Garden In NYC; 2024 Campaign Enters Final Stretch With Eight Days To Go; Vice President Harris: First Priority Is Restoring Roe V. Wade; \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  Urges Men To Vote For Vice President Harris; Iran Warns Of "Consequences" After Israeli Strikes; VP Harris Gains Ground On The Economy In New Polls; New Economic Data To Drop Days Before The Election; Former President Trump Speaks In Battleground Georgia\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Gillian Turner, Marc Thiessen, Trey Yingst, Mark  Meredith \par \uc2 8,482 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All righty, John and Sandra, good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum and this is THE STORY. Former President Trump rolling out some novel campaign moves eight days left in the race for the White House. There was the Friday Marathon with Joe Rogan on his podcast. Then, as you all saw, the enormous \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  rally, which was an extraordinary campaign move, to be sure, definitely out of the norm. He was in deep blue New York City, getting reaction like this from some in the media, and here's reaction from some of those political watcher.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This really was something different. \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  was packed. Trump has created a movement, there is no doubt. I cannot think of another Republican figure of my lifetime who could have come into a Democratic city like New York and put together anything like that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think we got to acknowledge that this guy is beating the pants off of us with these so-called publicity stunts.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, right now former President Trump is back to his core battleground strategy. He's going to a faith event in Cobb County, Georgia. He lost Cobb to Biden by 14 points in 2020, trying to erase that. He has a rally tonight at \uc2 Georgia Tech\uc2  in the heart of Atlanta. Meanwhile, Vice President Harris is drilling down on Michigan, stopping in Saginaw, where both Biden and Trump won by narrow margins in 16 and 20. She is about to head to the Trump stronghold of Maycomb County, which is outside of Detroit before a final event tonight in solid blue Ann Arbor, as polls show a race that likely will come down to some very fine margins.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't want to overstate it, but at the same time, it's hard to ignore these four. The CNBC poll came out the other day has Trump ahead by two. The Wall Street Journal poll, Trump ahead by three. These are national numbers. CNN and the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , a tied race. CNN, a tied race. The significance here is broader movement. Again, we're seeing in Trump's direction.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let's bring in \uc2 Hogan Gidley\uc2 , a former Principal Deputy Press Secretary for President Trump, now Vice Chair of the Center for Election Integrity. He was at the \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  rally yesterday. And Philippe Reines, former Deputy Assistant Secretary of State for Strategic Communications for \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . Gentlemen, thank you both very much for being here. Philippe, you hear Steve Kornacki talking about that movement that he sees, and you hear in our setup some of the well-known political observers talking about this strategy. What do you make of it with eight days to go?\par \par \uc2 PHILIPPE REINES, FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT SECRETARY OF STATE FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: Look, it's tight as a tick statistically. I don't think anyone is comfortable with that, whether they are for Team Trump or Team Harris. There's no reason for either team to be overly confident, but I know that the Harris campaign is doing everything it can do to win.\par \par \uc2 No, they're not using ideas as novel as President Trump's Madison Square Garden, but that's probably for the best given the coverage of it. I read somewhere earlier that there are more people Googling the event and the people who've spoken that made some pretty bad remarks than Taylor Swift.\par \par \uc2 So, if anyone left to me or if, you know, Hogan has a say in the matter, please do that novel stuff every day for the next eight days because I know Hogan and I are not friends, we don't know each other well, but I know he winced when he was sitting there. If he can go back 24 hours in time, he would change the program.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, what do you want to say to that, Hogan?\par \par \uc2 REINES: And if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  loses --\par \par \uc2 HOGAN GIDLEY\uc2 , VICE CHAIR FOR CENTER OF ELECTION INTEGRITY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 GIDLEY: It's hilarious to hear things like this coming from the left, of course, because after the McDonald's event, for example, after \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 REINES: \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's was great.\par \par \uc2 GIDLEY: The reason they're apoplectic isn't because they think the whole thing was a failure. The reason they're angry is because they know it was a success. People are turning out for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  like they've never done in the past and these events continue to showcase his ability not only to bring all kinds of people together regardless of race, religion, color, or creed, but he's expanding the tent from the Republican Party, pulling over Democrats, giving a message of hope and optimism and solutions to the problems that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  herself had put in place that really have gone to hurt all Americans.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I think what, what Philippe just hinted at, at the end there is, you know, once this is over, hopefully somewhere around November 5th or in the days that follow right after that, we're going to be talking about whether or not this was a fantastic strategy or not, right, on both sides of the equation. And you know, it makes, but he is clearly, Philippe, pushing outside of the places where Republicans have gone before.\par \par \uc2 He's going to go to New Mexico. He's going to Virginia in the coming days, which I think is very interesting. And going on these podcasts, definitely digging for the young male, black, Hispanic voters in these groups. Digging into that group, trying to, very much the way he dug into rural forgotten men and women in 2016. Do you think that that is a strategy that, you know, just as a political observer makes you say, maybe that'll work?\par \par \uc2 REINES: Well, I'll say as a New Yorker that his going there yesterday I don't think is going to do him any good in terms of New York's electoral votes. The vice president is making points of going places like Texas where it's also tied as a tick with Senator Cruz and I'm not predicting any kind of win there. In terms of going on shows, I mean she is on the same. She has a, I think she was on, I'm going to forget his name, show name, Shay Shay, but it's Shannon Sharp. She's done Charlemagne tha God.\par \par \uc2 I'm dating myself with age by not being able to do this, Howard Stern. And look, it's not just going on these shows. Yes, he went on Joe Rogan for three hours, but it's also what you say. It's not just having an event where you're packing a 20,000-person arena. It's what you say. And what he is saying, for some reason, has him tied as a tick with the Vice President.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I think the polls are very, they're clear in one thing that is not clear at all. This is \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  in Pennsylvania, because the more we all pour over these numbers, it could very well be all about Pennsylvania. Watch what \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  said over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You said that Trump has a special connection with the people of Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): A hundred percent. Anybody spends time driving around and you can see the intensity, it's astonishing. You have, Musk is now joining him. I mean, to a lot of people, I mean, that's like, hey, that's Tony Stark, and that's the world's richest guy. And he's saying, hey, that's my guy for president, that's going to really matter.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Interesting coalition there, to be sure, with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 , RFK Jr., kind of breaking apart some of these lines, Hogan, that we have seen in the past.\par \par \uc2 GIDLEY: No question, union bosses coming out for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as well. I've been on the campaign trail, and I don't work for the campaign, but I'm just going out there on those bus tours talking to real Americans, finding out what they care about. Pennsylvania, I'm telling you, you ask people, has anyone voted yet? Everyone raises their hand.\par \par \uc2 The tenacity, the excitement, and the enthusiasm is something that I've never seen before in my 25 years in politics, and I don't know that it's ever going to be duplicated again. And when Trump goes on these shows, you're talking tens of millions of listeners and viewers to Andrew Schultz, to Patrick Bette David, to Joe Rogan, Aiden Ross -- these are folks that never hear from politicians.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is taking a completely different and unique tact to try and get into their ears and let them understand how important this election is. If it pays off, it's going to be a massive success.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: She went on "Call her Daddy." You know it's definitely going to be the first election where podcasts may have some sort of ability to move the needle, and I think that's also something we're going to look at very closely the impact of that in the coming days. Philippe, thank you. Hogan, thank you. Great to have you both.\par \par \uc2 GIDLEY: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, right now, Senator J.D. Vance in Wausau, Wisconsin. That is in Marathon County. Trump won by 18 points in 2020, but JD Vance obviously trying to dig deeper there to get out more vote in Wausau, Wisconsin. Let's listen in for a minute here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE (R-OH): So, I didn't even realize this actually until I looked at what I was going to talk about today. I didn't realize the margin was so small in the state of Wisconsin. We work -- this state was decided by 0.63 percent, that's 20,000 votes, and that could very well be the difference. Frankly, it could be even smaller than that, the difference between President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, here's the second thing I want to ask you to do. We're never going to get honesty from \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in the corporate media. They're never going to tell the truth about this campaign or about the condition of the country. But you know who can tell the truth? We can. So, take out your phone, take a photo of this event, send it to your buddies, post on social media, do whatever you do and get the truth about it.\par \par \uc2 Why are you voting for \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 ? Because I believe that the honest words of a thousand American patriots is worth far more than the dishonesty of the corporate media and the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  campaign. And I'll make the final request here and that is that I want every single person in this hangar to get out there and vote ten times. A few of you are like, what?\par \par \uc2 We're Republicans, we only vote once, we do things the legal way. And I'm sure, we'll hear and see it in the New York Times tomorrow that J.D. Vance came to Wisconsin to encourage mass voter fraud in an airport hangar. But here's the legal way, the legal way to vote ten times, and that's to take yourself to the polls and take nine of your friends and family with you. That's how we're going to vote ten times. Please get out there and do it.\par \par \uc2 And the reason we have to do this my friends is because yes, we can we can laugh and have a good time and I will say that having watched way too many \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  events over the last few weeks and I've of course participated in a whole lot of Trump-Vance events. We've got all the joy and we're having all the fun on the \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  team, and that's a thing to be proud of. But we're also talking about some very serious business for the United States of America. I for one would like to raise my children in a country that is not --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, J. Vance, we will keep an eye on that. We also know the Vice President is on her way to Macomb County, Michigan this afternoon. Watching where these candidates are spending their precious time in these final days tells us a lot about where they think they can drive out vote as this continues to be a very tight race.\par \par \uc2 So, former Clinton and Obama Economist, \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2 , is going to join me in just a few moments. North Dakota Governor \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  is here on THE STORY as well today. And Mark Thiessen, always a favorite guest, joins us in just a few minutes as \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  scold men for not supporting Vice President Harris. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER FIRST LADY: If we don't get this election right, your wife, your daughter, your mother, we as women will become collateral damage to your rage.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, a hot mic moment at a campaign stop in Michigan, appears to have caught Vice President Harris, admitting to Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  that her campaign has an issue that they are working on with male voters. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: So, my thing is we need to move ground among men.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (D-MI): Yes.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Oh, we have microphones in here just listening to everything.\par \par \uc2 WHITMER: Oh, my.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I didn't realize that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You always got to be watching right on the campaign trail. We asked the Harris campaign if they wanted to clarify the comment, but we didn't hear anything back.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Gillian Turner, live outside the White House with more on Harris's push to gain some late support with male voters. Hi, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. It is both candidates literal last chance now to try and close some of that very stubborn gender gap that essentially has been widening ever since Harris herself ascended to the top of the Democratic ticket.\par \par \uc2 We are seeing a big push this week, over the coming days in prime-time ads as well as from both Harris and Trump surrogates. Take a look at the latest polling. It comes from \uc2 New York Times\uc2  and Siena College. It shows Harris leading Trump among women by 54 to 42 percent, and then Trump ahead of Harris by 14 points with men.\par \par \uc2 Former First Lady \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  with Harris in Michigan this weekend, dinged Trump on abortion, and essentially all the men who support him. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER FIRST LADY OF THE UNITED STATES: The only people who have standing to make these decisions are women with the advice of their doctors. We are the ones with the knowledge and experience to know what we need. So, please, please, do not hand our fates over to the likes of Trump.\par \par \uc2 To think that the men that we love could be either unaware or indifferent to our plight is simply heartbreaking.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: President Biden, himself, is echoing the very same theme. Take a listen to him.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. President, what do you say to the men that supported you but are not supporting Harris?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We are making a mistake, in my humble opinion.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Despite the polling and her own campaign's messaging this week, though, Harris herself says she is just not seeing a gender gap. She said it over the weekend. She said her only internal gage when it comes to polling is what she thinks and believes internally based on what she sees.\par \par \uc2 She cited in a radio interview a short while ago that she is still drawing crowds at her rallies of up to 30,000 people, so, she takes that as an indication there is no gender gap.\par \par \uc2 You're going to hear from Harris herself. Tomorrow afternoon, she'll be here at the White House making what her campaign says is essentially the last pitch, the closing argument to voters. So, we'll see if she mentions it there. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Gillian, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Fox News contributor Mark Thiessen, with a look at this. Very interesting. We are still going to be talking, I think, for a while, about this gender gap, and both efforts on each side to try to close it a bit.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: There are usually more women voters than men voters, so that is something to keep an eye on. This is from "CBS Sunday Morning", because obviously, abortion rights, is something that Harris has made very central to her argument. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: My first priority is put back in place those protections and to stop this pain and to stop this injustice that is happening around our country.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O'DONNELL, CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, \uc2 CBS\uc2  NEWS: So, then, why not say what restrictions you would support as part of that.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I have told you. Let's put back in place Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, shouldn't like Norah O'Donnell pressing on the issue of exactly what restrictions she would be in favor of, Marc. Your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Because there are none. That's her position is no restrictions on abortion. Period. That's the democratic line is abortion on the demand opens on --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But Roe v. Wade, just to drill down on this, Roe v. Wade did have restrictions after the second trimester, and then, it was up to the states.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: It allowed this.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It was up to the states after that, right?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes. And so, what you're seeing is -- and everyone talks about the extremism of the Republicans and the red states on abortion. What you're seeing is in the blue states, them getting rid of all the restrictions that existed before.\par \par \uc2 You're having -- you're having states going the complete opposite way and led the -- Democratic orthodoxy on abortion used to be, abortion should, in the \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 's words, safe, legal, and rare.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Today, it's abortion on demand, taxpayer funded up until the moment of birth. And in some play -- in some cases, even after. That -- that's just a radical position. But I guess that that resonates with some women voters, some segments of the female electorate. It's not -- it doesn't resonate with men.\par \par \uc2 And so, when you get \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  going out there and talking, using abortion as an issue to pummel men and say, how dare you not support \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . You know, I put her out there every day.\par \par \uc2 For \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , should want \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  to be out on the campaign trail delivering that message every day between now and Election Day, because that's not going to be very effective in winning over male voters.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I thought it was -- \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 's statement was interesting. She said, this is paraphrasing, but she said, you know, don't sacrifice the women in your life, essentially. Their rights for your rage.\par \par \uc2 I don't know -- I'm not sure what that meant, actually.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: It's a stunning, stunning, you know, the aloofness is throughout the entire Obama family, it seems. You know, everyone says we should be very afraid of \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  running for office. If that's how she campaigns in the final week, I have no fear of \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  as a candidate one day.\par \par \uc2 The aloofness, the worst border crisis in American history. I mean, you know, one of -- one of the things that they've lost ground on is Hispanic males.\par \par \uc2 So, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has completely wiped out the democratic advantage of it in Hispanic males. In 2020, yes, at this time, he was losing to Biden with Hispanic males by 19 points. Today, he is losing by two points. So, that's a 17-point gain.\par \par \uc2 What -- and a majority of Hispanic Americans, driven by Hispanic males, support mass deportation of illegal migrants out of this country.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, that's right.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: You know, they are paying $125 for the same $100 basket of grocery as everybody else, and she is saying, well, don't -- your rage about the border crisis and inflation and how much harder you're having to work in order to carry your family, don't worry about that, because you should be voting on abortion.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: That's just not an effective message to wipe out all the disasters that the Biden administration has inflicted on the country.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Interesting. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  had a front-page story about the fact that the data is showing that there has actually been, sadly, more abortion since Roe v. Wade was overturned in this country, not less.\par \par \uc2 Marc, thank you very much. Good to see as always.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, still ahead, former Clinton and Obama economist, \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2 , shares which presidential candidate he believes would be the best for the U.S. economy, after sending some warnings in the past about Bidenomics.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE SUMMERS\uc2 , FORMER UNITED STATES TREASURY SECRETARY: I think one should distinguish what the administration has mostly done, a large part of which I agree with, from the administration's doctrine, which I think is increasingly dangerous.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Vice President Harris, with a warning for Iran, after Israel launched what it called precise strikes on Iranian military targets, retaliation for Iran's missile attack on Israel this month.\par \par \uc2 Here is the vice president's message to Iran.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Do not respond, it would be a mistake. And we are prepared to defend Israel, as we have before we will again.\par \par \uc2 But the critical point is that there must be a de-escalation in the region, and we are working through diplomatic channels and other channels to ensure that there is a de-escalation in the region.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Chief foreign correspondent Trey Yingst, reporting live from Tel Aviv. Hi, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Martha, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, the Biden administration has a more forceful message this time around. Back in April, they told Iran, don't before the country rain down missiles on Israel.\par \par \uc2 There is concern though they could do it again, and that's why Israeli strikes targeted in and around the Iranian capital on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 Today, the chief of Iran's Revolutionary Guard Corps issued an ominous comment, warning a bitter consequences that will be unimaginable.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for \uc2 Iran's foreign ministry\uc2  issued a more direct threat, saying the Islamic Republic will use all available tools to respond. So, what could that look like?\par \par \uc2 Well, Sources tell Fox News that Israel remains on high alert, fearing that Iran may launch hundreds of more ballistic missiles toward the country. Israel could use its Arrow-2 and 3 systems to shoot down the incoming fire, along with the THAAD antiballistic missile system that was recently delivered by the United States.\par \par \uc2 Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , spoke out today about the ballistic missile threat from Iran and why, he says the strikes targeted production-related facilities in Iran.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , PRIME MINISTER, ISRAEL (through translator): I would like to emphasize, these were not workshops that we attacked in Iran. These are not small explosive laboratories, as you know from Judea and Samaria or the Gaza Strip. These are industrial death factories on an industrial scale, and we dealt them a real blow. I thank our allies, in particular, the United States, for their continued cooperation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Now, given that Iran has sold ballistic missiles to Russia for their war in Ukraine, there are questions about how these Israeli strikes will affect that supply chain. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Trey Yingst, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So, the battle over the economy on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will weaken America and our economy.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: If \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  gets four more years, our economy can never recover. (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Former Clinton and Obama economist, \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2 , who was called Biden's biggest inflation critic, with the money that poured into stimulus programs and the like, weighs in next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Vice President Harris closing the gap a little bit with former President Trump on the number one issue of the economy. He now has a seven point lead in the latest New York Times Siena College polling, down from a 13 point lead last month.\par \par \uc2 And new numbers we're going to get this week, we'll see how they actually come in. They could potentially be helpful to Harris' causes from Axios. Wednesday we're going to get the third quarter GDP expected to show 3 percent growth. And then Thursday, we're getting a key inflation report that is likely to show the lowest rate since early 2021. And the jobs report comes in on Friday and we will see what that number looks like.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in \uc2 Lawrence Summers\uc2 , former Clinton Treasury Secretary, former Obama National Economic Council Director and former Harvard President. Last year, though, he called Bidenomics" increasingly dangerous." Larry, great to have you with us. Thank you very much for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE SUMMERS\uc2 , FORMER CLINTON TREASURY SECRETARY: Good to be with you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So one of the things that really stands out to me when I listen to both of these economic programs is that, on the Harris side there is really a lot of talk about a lot more spending. And when I look at the spending and you look at what the, you know, how much we are in the red, we spend $1.13 trillion just servicing the debt. That's 23 percent of all the revenue that the government takes in. So, what do you think about that problem in terms of the economic plans?\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: Gee, Martha, I've studied the numbers pretty carefully and I don't think any incremental spending associated with Harris is even a quarter as big as the incremental tax cutting associated with President Trump. So if you're worried about too much borrowing, if you're worried about the kind of mistakes we made in 2021, the Trump program, as it's been put forward, is just a vastly larger inflation shock --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, let me just ask you about that, Larry, because --\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: -- and we've seen since OPEC.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. So I want to ask you about that because we looked up the fiscal years 2017 to 2022, and the receipts for the government went up after the tax cuts dramatically up to nearly 4.9 in boosted revenue increasing over the course of those years after the Trump tax cuts, which he's promising to extend as you know.\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: Respectfully, Martha, four point -- that's not a serious analysis, 4.9 what? The economy normally grows, so revenues normally go up.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And they go up all the time., absolutely\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: The question is you got the revenues went up. The question is, what they went up relative to the counterfactual if those tax cuts had not been put in place, and I think almost all analysts, whether they're at the more Democratic Brookings Institution or at the more Conservative American Enterprise Institute would agree that there was a very substantial increase in the deficit associated with the Trump tax cuts.\par \par \uc2 And what's particularly troubling is that, President Trump is proposing not to allow the corporate tax cut to expire, not even to keep the corporate tax cut constant, but to blow up the size of the corporate tax cut after he already gave corporations a larger tax cut --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No, that's a difference. It's a clear difference. He wants to cut the corporate tax rate down to 15 percent for domestic manufacturers.\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: That's right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But just to be clear, if I wasn't clear before, I apologize.\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: That's very expensive.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So the tax receipts from the 2017 tax code change increased 47.8 percent. That's a huge jump in the five years following the Trump tax code. So a 48 percent increase in tax revenue over the course of those years. So cutting taxes actually --\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: That's a reflection --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- because companies had more money to spend, they hired more people. That's average hourly wages go up during that period.\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: This isn't serious analysis. This fails to recognize --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, these numbers are also on the Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: -- that inflation pick up through -- of course the numbers are, but the question is what's the counterfactual relative to what would happen if those tax cuts had not taken place? Where was the deficit five years after the Trump tax cut? I promise you it was vastly larger than where it was --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, you're right about that.\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: -- when the Trump tax cuts started.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Nobody has been good at cutting spending, that's for sure. So I do want to -- I want to ask you about that. This is from the \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  last night, and this is \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  on stage. You know that he wants to lead a waste, fraud and abuse effort. I want to ask you what why you think about that.\par \par \uc2 Here's a quick soundbite from Howard Lutnick and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK, TESLA CEO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HOWARD LUTNICK, CEO, CANTOR FITZGERALD: How much do you think we can rip out of this wasted $6.5 trillion Harris Biden budget?\par \par \uc2 MUSK: Well, I think we can do at least $2 trillion.\par \par \uc2 LUTNICK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MUSK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LUTNICK: Two trillion.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you think about that, Larry?\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: Respectfully, I think it's idiotic. It's not a serious statement. Where are they going to where cut it out if they think these people think it's like some business, but here's the problem. Only 15 percent of the federal budget is for payroll. So even if you took all the employees, every single person who works for the federal government out, you couldn't save anything like $2 trillion.\par \par \uc2 Maybe what they're saying is that they plan to cut Social Security benefits. Maybe what they're saying is that they plan to scale back our Medicare --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, we know --\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: -- very simple question.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The former president said he doesn't want to do one of those things. And I agree with you, you know --\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: I was saying that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- there's not a lot of specifics here.\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: I understand that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But would agree with broad sense that --\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: Very simple questions.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: --- that there is waste, fraud and abuse in the government -- in government spending or not, would you agree with that or not?\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: Of course there is waste in the government and, of course, it's the job of responsible officials to cut it out by, for example, using more computerization. That's something that the Biden administration, Biden- Harris administration pushed with respect to the \uc2 IRS\uc2 . They thought we shouldn't be using 1960 version computers with vacuum tubes, that they should be using modern technology instead. Candidate Trump has opposed --\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: (Inaudible) 80,000 --\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: -- all that kind of thing. So I -- of course we should be scaling back waste, but there's a need to be at least a little bit serious here. And so, I'm just asking a simple question. If people were to take seriously this $2 trillion number, even if people are yelling and shouting about it, tell us where it's going to come from. Tell us where there's $2 trillion outside of national defense, outside of Social Security, outside of Medicare. That's there to be a cut. Otherwise, you're just talking nonsense.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But don't you think it's unserious to assume -- now, I agree with you. I'm not the president of Harvard, OK, so maybe I'm not, you know, going to be talking, you know, in the same way about this as you do. I'm just a regular American citizen.\par \par \uc2 I think it's abhorrent to see the way that this budget has blown up over the course of my lifetime. And it is inconceivable that there isn't a way to stop that from happening, because what happens is it costs Americans across the country more out of their own pocket every single month and every single year. And they don't feel like they're getting back for it what they're investing.\par \par \uc2 And they want to keep that money themselves. They want to make their own decisions about how to spend that money. They trust themselves, in many cases, more than the government. So, you know, is that a sentiment that makes any sense to you?\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: So here's the problem. Here's the -- it doesn't make a lot to be honest.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK.\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: Here's the problem. Here's the problem, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK.\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: Just listen. The problem is that a share of the population that's over 65 is almost twice as big as it used to be. So if we just want to do Social Security the way we've always done it, the amount of government spending has to go up. The cost of education or the cost of medical care, they've gone up. So if we have to do the -- do things, it's going to be more expensive.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, let me tell you something --\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: I think what's actually important is that people are able to buy insulin at a much lower cost than they used to be --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, sure that's -- I mean, that's the way --\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: -- because of what's been done.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, they probably end up paying for it somewhere else. But, you know, the examples that you gave, people do not feel healthier and like they're getting better health care than they used to. And we certainly know that the money that we're spending on education is not working. We are like 14th and 38th in Math and English in this country.\par \par \uc2 So the investment, we spend more and more money on students per year and we have lower results. So again, I know that analysis might be a little too big for you, but that's a problem the way I see it.\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: You're right. You are absolutely right that no child should be left behind. You are absolutely right that we don't have the right kind of standards in education at the high school level --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No one talks about it, yes.\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: -- or at the college level. You are absolutely right that we place too much emphasis on self-esteem and not enough emphasis on excellence. You are absolutely right in expressing those concerns. But I will tell you that if you look at the record of the Trump administration when it was in office, they didn't do anything about any of those problems.\par \par \uc2 And it doesn't solve all the problems. There's a lot of things that need to be done. But what the Biden-Harris administration did was a set of things that made community college more accessible to a large number of Americans. And what's actually probably a more important issue, they did more for the two thirds of Americans who don't have college degrees in terms of their ability to get jobs.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, we just need to think through these issues and talk about numbers a little bit.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know what --\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: Here's what I don't understand. I don't understand -- here's what I can't understand. Maybe you can explain it to me because, you know, you're Fox News and you've been supportive of President Trump. How can it be that putting a tax on all the goods we import of 10 percent, in some cases of 100 percent, how can that help fight inflation?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, first of all --\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: Just tell me how it's going to be possible for parents who we're coming up on the holiday season. Most of our toys are imported from China. They're going to double in price with that 100 percent tariff. How's that going to help families?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Here's what I will tell you what we've seen in the past with the tariff efforts, and I don't -- I'm not going to acknowledge -- I don't support one candidate over another on this show. And if you watched it, you would see that I am quite fair in that regard, so.\par \par \uc2 But with regard to the tariff ideas, here's what I saw. With Mexico, when he was president, Trump basically said to Mexico, we're going to do an incremental tax tariff increase on everything that's coming in from Mexico or you can give us more law enforcement at the border. So it was supposed to kick in over the course of June into July. By the time they got to the end of June, I believe there was now 30,000 Mexican law enforcement at the Guatemala border and then also at the southern border. So the tariff threat went away.\par \par \uc2 I think in a lot of cases, he's looking to leverage that tariff threat in order to get better negotiating leverage on other things. Now, I don't know, I could be wrong, but that's what I saw in that case. And going forward --\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: How could you -- that could be a hope. On the other hand, I would ask you, which tariffs that he put in place did he ever take away?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, that -- Mexico.\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: -- because he leveraged them to achieve something. I don't know. He didn't ever put those in place.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, the threat of tariffs that were taken --\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: I don't think there's been -- I don't think there's been much -- if you want to say that the threat of 10 percent across the board tariffs, of 100 percent tariffs on China, if you want to say that's a rhetorical bargaining chip, I understand that argument, but, gosh, I can't really relate to it because candidate Trump says he's going to replace the income tax with the tariff revenue. And so, how's he going to do that if he's really planning to wipe out tariffs?\par \par \uc2 So sounds like if he wants to abolish the income tax, he has to really meet it about the tariffs and that's going to contribute a lot to inflation.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Well, I will -- we will see. And, you know, all of these things about what people feel works better for them. They've had an opportunity, actually, and a unique opportunity to see the past four years and the four years before that. So, you know, it's good to have a conversation with you. I'm glad you came on today, Larry, but it's all going to come down to on November 5th, what people's actual life has -- experience has been and what they feel works best for them and their families. So we will see what the American people decide on November.\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: I just want to say one thing. I think it's also going to come down to their degree of personal confidence in the two candidates. And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  isn't perfect, there's things she said that I wish she hadn't --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Like what?\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: -- shed. But I've spent a lot of time in the business world and I can tell you that there is no board of directors in America that if they had a CEO who spoke in the unstable, unhinged way that candidate Trump has, would not take that CEO out. So whether you like a Republican ideology or - -\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I hear you. I hear you.\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: -- you don't, I think that it's something that's very powerful.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know what, and I heard people say the same thing about Biden's ability to run the country over the last several months. They had the same question about whether or not he could have been a CEO and he's still in office so we'll see where all this goes. But the great thing about it is that the American people get to decide and we'll see what they do on November the 5th. \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2 , I thank you for an lively conversation.\par \par \uc2 SUMMERS: Pleasure to be with you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Hope we can do it again. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 So any moment now, former President Trump's first event since his massive rally here in New York City. We're going to bring you to battleground Georgia as well, as soon as he takes the stage there coming up next. Stay with us on "The Story."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Former President Trump about to speak in battleground Georgia. He is in Powder Springs outside of Atlanta in Cobb County. President Biden carried that county by 14 points in 2020, winning Georgia's 16 electoral votes. The former president is trying to win it back. Correspondent Mark Meredith joining me now from Powder Springs. Hi, Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Martha, good to see you. Former President Trump will be on this stage right here behind me in the next 10, 15 minutes. He's going to be taking questions from voters of faith, looking about a few issues they think will make a big difference in this campaign, whether it be the Supreme Court access to abortion or school choice.\par \par \uc2 And we spoke to a number of people this room who they believe Trump has already locked up the voters of faith in this election.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think honestly God is on Trump's side. And so I think that's going to seal the deal for Trump. God, not necessarily people, but the Lord is using Trump greatly in this hour and he's the man for the job.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think he's been talking about issues of faith all the time. This is not too little, too late. On the contrary, I think is right on time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Now, when it comes to the election eight days away, Georgia officials believe they're going to get results fast. Remember, four years ago, 95 percent of the vote was counted by election night. But you remember the race was still very close. President Biden won this state by less than 12,000 votes. But Republicans think they've got a better message now and more solid footing this cycle.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KELLY LOEFFLER (R), FORMER GEORGIA SENATOR: Because here in Georgia, we're a microcosm of what has happened in this country. Open borders with the tragic death of Laken Riley, the \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  lawfare effort, and then election integrity\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Minnesota Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , he'll be in Georgia tomorrow with stops planned in two other major cities, Savannah and Columbus. The vice president, she had a huge rally in Georgia just last week, and there are questions whether or not she's going to be able to turn out black male voters, which will be a big voting block here in the state of Georgia, given some of the recent polling we've seen showing Trump gaining ground among that community. But the Harris team has insisted that they are going to be able to.\par \par \uc2 Martha, we also expect the former first lady \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  here in Georgia tomorrow. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Thank you very much, Mark Meredith. Let's bring in North Dakota Governor \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2 , who ran against former President Trump in the Republican primary but now supports him and was at the \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  rally yesterday. Governor Burgum, good to have you here today. Thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 I'm curious what your overall reaction was to that in New York City. It's not something you probably see every day in North Dakota. How did you like it?\par \par \uc2 DOUG BURGUM\uc2  (R), NORTH DAKOTA GOVERNOR: Well, I would say it was a historic show of strength by President Trump to be able to fill an arena and have huge lines outside. I think oversubscribed. I mean, six times as much ticket demand as there were seats in the arena.\par \par \uc2 And doing this, you know, in a city in Manhattan itself where 9 out of 10 people are registered Democrats, I think it just shows the incredible strength and he's a historic figure in politics to be able to pull together such a diverse set of people that are also enthusiastically supporting him. The energy in the place was entirely electric.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So I want to play this because there's all this discussion back and forth about fascism. We hear it on both sides. It seems to be the tenor of the conversation, unfortunately, at this point. This is a voter speaking about this with MSNBC on Sunday. Let's watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALLEN TOMKOWIAK, MICHIGAN VOTER: I've heard so much about Trump being angry and he's a fascist and a dictator. The problem is when I listen to him, I don't hear any of that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So that's one man's opinion in Michigan. We've heard this kind of language coming from both sides. Do you support it? Do you condone it?\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Well, I think the guest you just had on the clip you played captures it all, which is this is a tired trope of the Democrats. The first time in history was 1948. That's when \uc2 Harry Truman\uc2 , as a Democrat running for president, called the Republican candidate a fascist. So they've been doing this thing for decades and decades, and decades.\par \par \uc2 And here they are doing it now. And as you said, it doesn't match the reality. You can't -- just because you say something doesn't make it true. And if you look at the history of fascism, as anybody but historians would, what fascists do if they're trying to take over a country is they control the media, they start censoring stuff, they talk about -- if they can't get it through legally through the legislature, then they pack the courts to make their actions illegal, and then they want to take away everybody's guns, and then they want to jail their opponents.\par \par \uc2 And if you take that list of five core principles, that's the game plan the Democrats have had against President Trump for both his first term when they were trying to impeach him, and then when he was out office trying to get back in with all the lawfare. And all the censorship that we know happened. So it's not only false, it's actually 180 degrees backwards.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, you've heard both Walz and Harris say that they don't want to take away anyone's guns. We'll see where that goes.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you about \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2 , who I just spoke with. I'm not sure whether or not you were able to hear our conversation.\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But he basically talked about how, you know, the Trump tax cuts were so destructive to the United States economy and how the tariffs that former President Trump is proposing would be destructive to the US economy as well. And I'm curious what your thoughts are on that.\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Well, it's a, you know, \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2  is a credentialed academic and he's a, you know, former Democrat economic advisor. But this is the deceit and the arrogance of that approach, which is they're trying to, you know, lecture you, lecture the public versus asking people about their actual lived experience. People -- their lived experience on the economy was much better under President Trump. Their real income went up, inflation was low, interest rates were down. The American dream was there. And it's the exact opposite than the Harris --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But why do you think that the gap is closing right now between Trump and Harris on the economy, governor? Then we got to go.\par \par \uc2 BURGUM: Well, they say it is but, you know, her approach is we're going to tax more, borrow more and do more giveaways to special interest groups. People aren't buying that. People trust President Trump on national security, on the border, on the economy. That's why he's going to win.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, we will see. Governor Burgum, thank you very much. Good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 That is "The Story" for this Monday. As always, "The Story" goes on and we will see you back here tomorrow at 3:00. Stay tuned, though, "Your World" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020241029ekas00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Harris Lays Out Her Closing Message To Voters; Today: Trump Holds Rally At \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2 ; Interview With Republican Florida Sen. \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 ; Reports: U.S. Leak of Intel Documents Delayed Israel Response to Iran Attack; Gov. Polis on Dealing with Venezuelan Gang Tren de Aragua in His State; Gov. Polis on \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' Abortion Stance; Fight For Control Of The House; Reports: Some Democrats Concerned About Battleground Polling; Some Unions, News Outlets Choose Not To Endorse A Candidate\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Juan Williams, Trey Yingst, Peter Doocy, Alexis  McAdams \par \uc2 10,449 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  gearing up for their final full week on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT & 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Now, the fate of our nation is truly in your hands, Michigan.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): From battleground --\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES & 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Hard work is joyful work. And make no mistake: we will win.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- to battleground.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's all because of policies. It's not personalities. It's because of policies.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: To the tens of millions of votes already cast, the presidential candidates hone their closing arguments.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : If Kamala gets four more years, she will obliterate our economy, and kill millions of jobs and destroy your family finances. That's what's going to happen.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Let us be clear: if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wins again, he will ban abortion nationwide.\par \par \uc2 BREMA: Abortion on the ballot in 10 states. We'll talk with leaders from two of them.\par \par \uc2 Plus, ask Florida Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  about the Trump campaign's efforts to sway Hispanic voters.\par \par \uc2 And Colorado Governor \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2  about the Harris campaign's focus on Trump as a threat in the final days.\par \par \uc2 Then, Trump prepares to take Manhattan and the Garden as the battle for control of Congress runs right through key blue states.\par \par \uc2 Our Sunday Panel on House Republicans' fight to save their razor thin majority -- right now on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 We begin with a look at your headlines. Israeli police are investigating what is suspected to be a deliberate ramming attack by a truck at a bus stop north of Tel Aviv, injuring several civilians, as the Israeli military continues strikes against militants in Gaza and Lebanon and after its strikes on Iran early Saturday.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 L.A. Dodgers\uc2 " continued their winning streak against the \uc2 New York Yankees\uc2  in game two of the World Series last night with a 4-2 win in their hometown. Game three is that for Monday at Yankee stadium in the Bronx.\par \par \uc2 And the U.S. is not the only country with a nail-biter election. Japanese voters go to the polls today for parliamentary elections that could throw out the prime minister's ruling party in the lower chamber which has held power for all but four of the years since 1955.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we will talk with Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  who's been campaigning with former President Trump this week and later, we'll be joined by Harris- Walz surrogate, Colorado Governor \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But, first, let's get team coverage from the trail with Alexis McAdams at \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  in New York, where former President Trump is set to rally today and senior White House correspondent Peter Doocy covering Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  set to rally supporters in Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 Peter, we started off with you.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, there was a very specific group targeted by the Democrats' top surrogate, men. \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 's message to them in her campaign trail debut was that she thinks reproductive rights could affect them as much as they affect anybody else.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER U.S. FIRST LADY: I got to ask myself, why on Earth is this race even close? Your girlfriend could be the one in legal jeopardy if she needs a pill from out of state or overseas. Your wife or mother could be the ones at higher risk of dying from undiagnosed cervical cancer because they have no access to regular gynecological care. It will affect you and your sons.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: So the closing argument for the Harris walls ticket is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a threat to abortion access and that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a threat to democracy and sensitive to criticisms that people don't know her well enough yet, the VP is once again going back to emphasizing her experience as a prosecutor to make her latest anti-Trump appeal.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I took on perpetrators of all kinds, predators, fraudsters and repeat offenders. I took them on and I won. Well, Michigan, in 10 days, it's \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's turn.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The vice president and her top advisers are betting that undecided voters want to hear more about January 6th. So her big event this week is going to be remarks -- will be remarks on the ellipse in Washington, D.C. where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  spoke almost four years ago -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Peter Doocy in Philadelphia, thank you, Peter.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to Alexis McAdams at \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  in New York. That is where President Trump's going to make a big closing message to voters who've been lined up it sounds like for hours, Alexis.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Shannon. Yeah, people slept here in front of \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  if you can believe it overnight in New York City is the former president is going to be in the Big \uc2 Apple\uc2 . This time, he's not here for a court hearing. This time, he's going to be on stage at \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2 . I want to show you right now what's going on behind me.\par \par \uc2 This is the look in front of MSG. You can see the crowd is growing here as people get excited for this. The campaign's expecting \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  to be full. \uc2 Tesla\uc2  CEO \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  will be here. Senator J.D. Vance and House Speaker Mike Johnson up on stage, just some of the guests. It's nearly 20,000 people are expected to pack the world's most iconic arena in deep blue New York City.\par \par \uc2 So this is going to be Trump's third event in New York state, okay? Back in May, he was out in the Bronx, we remember that, and just last month, 16,000 people packed into a rally out on Long Island.\par \par \uc2 The former president says he is going to make a play to win New York but he has lost the state in the last two elections by more than 20 points.\par \par \uc2 A Republican presidential candidate has not won New York since \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  in '84, but new polls show Trump is rating better here than he did last election.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We've had a lot of rallies, and we're going to have our biggest of all at \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2 . We're going to make a play for New York, hasn't been one in many, many decades. But with what's going on in New York between the illegal migrants and the crime they're causing and hurting people so badly, and all of the problems in New York, we're going to give it a hell of a shot.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: On Saturday, former President Trump spending the day in the swing state of Pennsylvania. He was at \uc2 Penn State\uc2  last night, and two rallies in Michigan, touting his plan for the economy, promising to revive large cities that he says are on the decline like Philly and Detroit.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We will bring back our manufacturing jobs, our energy jobs, our coal jobs, our steel jobs, and we're going to bring back our dreams. Starting in January, we will give our companies the lowest taxes, the lowest energy cost, the lowest regulatory burdens, and free access to the best and biggest market on the planet.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Okay. So, nine days if you can believe it, Shannon, until the presidential election. People here are excited and plan to hit the polls. We know that immigration and the economy is a huge issue, not just here for New Yorkers but across the country. The former president also has a major campaign schedule packed where he's going to then leave New York after Madison Square Garden and head to areas like Pennsylvania, Wisconsin and Michigan -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Those are the places.\par \par \uc2 All right. Alexis McAdams from New York, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Florida Republican Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to the show, Senator. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): Good morning. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. So let's start here. The vice president is making her closing argument and she's talking about President Trump as a threat to the country.\par \par \uc2 Here's a little bit of what she said in recent days.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER, CNN MODERATOR: Let me ask you tonight, do you think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a fascist?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Yes, I do. Yes, I do.\par \par \uc2 He does not want a military that is loyal to the United States Constitution. He wants a military that is loyal to him.\par \par \uc2 I believe \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a danger to the well-being and security of America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: She says those aren't just her words. They're also coming from people who worked very closely with the president -- Senator.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Well, first of all, anytime a candidate starts talk -- she sounds just like \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I mean, literally, she's right back to being \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . She's using the exact same rhetoric that didn't work for him, the same attacks they've been using against Trump now for like eight years. No one believes these things.\par \par \uc2 You talk about people that work for the president. No, look, these are people that worked in the administration or around the administration, and then they figured out pretty quickly, if we want jobs after we leave this administration, we have to become anti-Trumpers.\par \par \uc2 You think \uc2 Mark Milley\uc2  winds up on the board -- teaching at Princeton, teaching at Georgetown, on the speakers bureau, advising \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 , you don't get hired for those jobs unless you pronounce yourself anti-Trump and say things against Trump, you know?\par \par \uc2 So, you have to go out and say these sorts of things and order to be able to be employable because corporate America and all these other interests won't hire you if you used to work for or around Trump. I look at the stuff about General Kelly that they -- they, you know, the Democrats, like I remember Elizabeth Warren, \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2  called him corrupt. Pelosi launched an investigation into him. They said all kinds of horrible things about him when he worked for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So how do you cleanse yourself of that and allow yourself to become employable and viable in the business world after you leave government service? You have to become anti-Trump. So there's a financial incentive here for these people when they come out and say these things.\par \par \uc2 And my last point that I want to make about this is how interesting that the these claims that were made this week pop up with a week and a half to go before the election. If you in fact believed as some of these people claim that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is all these horrible things, Nazi, fascist, all these crazy terms they throw around, why didn't you stand up and walk out of that White House or that administration the moment you heard those terms? Why didn't you say that at the time and not wait until after the fact?\par \par \uc2 Those are questions are never going to get asked because this is entirely a political hit job where these people are trying to elevate themselves to become more employable and be able to make more money, and it's also timed perfectly so that they can ask her about it on CNN and she can make the answer that she made.\par \par \uc2 So she's not focused on her record or her plans because they're just like \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's and they're full of failure.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you think people like General Milley, like \uc2 John Kelly\uc2 , are lying?\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Yes, I do, and not more -- but even if they aren't lying, or even - - let's say you don't want to call them a liar, I would say it's very dubious to see these accusations coming at the very last minute, right before an election.\par \par \uc2 These are the kinds of things -- you know, somebody says that in front of me, I say it right away, I walk out, I resign, I leave. They didn't do any of those things.\par \par \uc2 And I think it's hypocritical to see a bunch of Democrats who spent months, weeks, trashing these people, particularly in the case of \uc2 John Kelly\uc2 , that trashing him, saying that he was corrupt. That they accused him of potentially being a Nazi at one point, I remember. They attacked him over some Civil War comments that he made. They were horrible to him.\par \par \uc2 So, all of a sudden, now, these people -- this guy who they hated and said horrible things about, now, he is the standard of proof here. Here he is, the absolute final word on these topics.\par \par \uc2 I just think anything like this that's coming out at the last minute being strategically planted is nothing but an effort to have an impact on this election because what \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is not going to talk about is the fact that for four years, she's been the vice president of, potentially, if not the single worst administration for this country and for Americans in my lifetime.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. I want to talk about the fact that part of their argument against him is they say he's too friendly with autocrats like \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . There's reporting out of "The Wall Street Journal" this week exclusively that \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  who's now fully supportive of President Trump and out campaigning with him has been having conversations with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  regularly over the last couple of years. There have been some questions raised about given the government contracts, the access to security information, that classified information that Musk has, that there is a problem there, potentially a national security issue.\par \par \uc2 Bill Nelson\uc2 , now the \uc2 NASA\uc2  administrator, used to be Democrat senator from Florida, they're a colleague of yours, he says there should be investigation into this regarding Musk.\par \par \uc2 Do you think there should be?\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Well, first of all, anybody that has contracts with the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  undergoes a constant review for security background and clearances for that matter. I will tell you that without \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , I don't know how we're going to rescue our astronauts that are stuck in space. I don't know how we're going to do some of the things that need to be done.\par \par \uc2 All that said, I can't opine on whether Musk called Putin or not because I don't know, and he's a private citizen and if it somehow that imperils his clearance, there's a process for all of that. And it's not through the media and it's not in the middle of a political campaign.\par \par \uc2 Let me say something about being friendly to autocrats and so forth, I am not a fan of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . I think \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is an enemy of the United States.\par \par \uc2 By the same token, I assure you, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and any future American president as well is going to have to interact with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  because he has the largest nuclear stockpile in the world, because he has one of the world's largest militaries because he's in -- because they're a power in the world that has to be dealt with.\par \par \uc2 That doesn't mean you have to be friendly to them. That doesn't mean you have to endanger the United States in order to interact with them.\par \par \uc2 But, you know the Biden administration has had interactions with the Putin -- with the Putin regime over there and so forth. It's an -- it's the reality of governing in the world, so it's a stupid accusation.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, while we're on the subject of Russia, the White House confirms this week it believes that thousands of North Korean troops have gone over to aid Russia in its war on Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of that development? What should the U.S. response be?\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Well, I think I make of it that the world is in total chaos and you can link it all the way back to the day that we were run out of Afghanistan by the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , humiliated in the way that we were, and that was a signal to the world, not just that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  are weak, but that America is weak and now is the time to act.\par \par \uc2 And it's not a coincidence that since that moment, we had the invasion in Ukraine. We had October 7th. We had all the things happening now in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 We have the Houthis endangering global shipping in the Red Sea. We have the Chinese harassing the Philippines and making plans to invade Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 None of these things are coincidental. They were all triggered by that symbol -- by that that very graphic example of American weakness under Biden and Harris.\par \par \uc2 Now that's what I make of it. The world is spinning into chaos because the Biden-Harris administration's been feckless.\par \par \uc2 And let me argue this: it's going to get worse if \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is president of the United States. This was a person that as vice president prioritized not the national security of our country but whether our intelligence agencies were being fair and nice and appropriate and -- to women, and then whether gender was being appropriately accounted for.\par \par \uc2 They -- you saw the reporting overnight. They sent back analysts to take a special class to ensure that in our intelligence analysis, they -- when they were talking about global leaders that were women, that they were somehow not discriminated against or that we didn't use tough language in describing them.\par \par \uc2 That -- we should be focused on the security of our country, the threats to America, not all this woke ideology that she will inject -- has already tried to inject into our national security apparatus in our country.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You talk about the intel community. You also -- you mentioned the Middle East there because I want to ask about this, too. You're the ranking member on Senate intel.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2  is now investigating a leak of documents or material related to Israel, our ally, may have delayed their response, their attacks on Iran. How worried are you that that happened because of somebody who is working within the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Well, it has to be someone who was working in the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  that had access to that information, that either gave it to someone who put it out there or put it out there directly. We know, because we've seen it in all these different dissents, there are a bunch of cowards, by the way, all these people that are anti-Israel. And they say, we -- we think with the -- you know, this is wrong and we shouldn't be supporting Israel. They never generally openly signed the names to any of these things.\par \par \uc2 But let me tell you that there are elements within our \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  that hate Israel, that sympathize with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . And this looks to me, I can't prove it, we're going to see what the investigation shows, but this proves to me that this -- this indicates to me that this was a treasonous act by someone in the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  to undermine Israel.\par \par \uc2 And by extension, the national security of the United States, I hope they find who did this or the people who did this, because it was done deliberately to harm Israel and ultimately endangers Americans as well. And it's treason. And it should be prosecuted as such.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Senator, real quickly before you go, the Vice President has suggested, should she win and Democrats hold the Senate, that she would get rid of the filibuster, at least on the issue of abortion. Do you think your Democrat colleagues, if that's the scenario, would go along with getting rid of something that could come back to bite them when they're not in power?\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Yes, because I think that they believe -- I think that they will do it. If the Democrats keep the Senate, they will get rid of the filibuster, especially now that the two Democrats that were against doing that are going to be gone. They're going to do that. And it's not just going to be on abortion. They're going to do it to remake the Supreme Court and pack it. They're going to do it to pass the Green New Deal. They're going to do it to pass every idea that isn't popular or couldn't pass, that she's not campaigning on now, by the way, as many of these issues, because she knows they're unpopular. They're going to use that to ram it through the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . That's why they want to get rid of the filibuster at this point.\par \par \uc2 I'm convinced if they win, they will get rid of the filibuster and they will do that to ram through all these radical left-wing ideas that she supports but isn't campaigning on because knows she can't win if she does.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Senator Rubio, thank you very much for your time. Good to see you, sir.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, you're going to hear from Harris-Walz's surrogate, Governor \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2  of Colorado. We'll get his reaction to the continuing fallout over migrant gangs in \uc2 Aurora\uc2  that are making a lot of headlines.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: In Aurora, Colorado, you have the worst, probably the worst gang. MS-13 might even be, you know, those two are the worst gangs. These are Venezuela gangs. They have taken over apartment complexes, and they're going to want to take over the whole thing. And you have a weak governor, a pathetic governor, who's a radical left Democrat. He doesn't know what the hell to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That is former President Trump criticizing Colorado's governor over reports of violent gangs from Venezuela operating in his state. Well, to hear his side of the story, joining me now, Colorado Democratic Governor \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Governor, welcome back to the show.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JARED POLIS (D-CO), HARRIS-WALZ SURROGATE: Always a pleasure, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so let's start there. You've got the management company for two apartment complexes in \uc2 Aurora\uc2 . They say they're now totally under control of gangs. They say they've reached out multiple times to all kinds of different authorities. They say they sent a letter for help to you. And they're pointing to things like one of their managers being severely beaten.\par \par \uc2 The pictures are out there. He filed a police report last fall. They say that what they're getting instead is a denial of the reality of the situation that innocent people are left to fear for their lives and that they can't regain control of these complexes. What's the plan?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Well, I -- look, the Aurora police, the Republican mayor of Aurora, have a very different version that they've told me. What I've told the city of \uc2 Aurora\uc2  is the state is ready. You named it. We have access to any of our state troopers. We're ready to back you up in any operation. But the police, the mayor, continue to reassure us. And, of course, they've had a number of events. They've been to the complexes. They've said that they've never been under gang control.\par \par \uc2 So it seems like another one of those things that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is either exaggerating or making up. But the city of \uc2 Aurora\uc2  is great. I was there with my kids last week. I'm there all the time. It's our third biggest city. Crime is down two years in a row.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so this is coming from, you know, the apartment complex owners, and there is an outside group that went in to investigate. They're connected to one of the lenders, so they have an interest in knowing what's going on there. They also, their assessment was that they're under the control of these gangs.\par \par \uc2 There's reporting, though, this week as well, that authorities in \uc2 Aurora\uc2  had concerns and some awareness potentially about this particular gang. As far back as the fall of last year, DHS now says there are hundreds of people in this country linked to this particular gang and that Venezuela is refusing repatriation of flights, regardless of how people got here. If they want to send them back, they won't take them back.\par \par \uc2 We know there aren't full screenings for everybody who is crossing our border. These are only the ones that we know about. Why is there not more concern about Colorado, which is often seen as a magnet? We've talked about this before. You know, Denver is a sanctuary state. More worry about your constituents there in Colorado.\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Well, first of all, \uc2 Aurora\uc2  is not a sanctuary city in any way, shape or form.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Denver is.\par \par \uc2 POLIS: And we, yeah, that's the next, the other city next door that's not having these issues. Yeah, \uc2 Aurora\uc2  is in a fight with the owners of this apartment complex. They said, why haven't you cleaned up the garbage? They've cited them. I think they're engaged in operations against them. Again, the state is ready to back it up.\par \par \uc2 I think what we really need here, Shannon, is border security and somebody in charge who's a former prosecutor and law enforcement official like \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  who's going to get it done.\par \par \uc2 When people commit a crime against our country, they've got to be out. No ifs, ands, or buts. That's what \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will do.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you think, though, coming across the border illegally is a crime against our country or no?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Well, again, these are mostly Venezuelans who came here as refugees or with status. They have a -- so again, they were fleeing a socialist dictator in Venezuela. Most of them were able to work legally. These Venezuelan gangs are committing crimes against other Venezuelans. There's been nine arrests in Colorado, and we hope that we got them all. And if not, we're going to get them all.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so Texas Governor Greg Abbott, who, like you, deals with this issue on a daily basis, has said that Tren de Aragua, this particular group, should be designated by Vice President Harris, the administration right now, as a terrorist group. Do you agree with that?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Well, I don't -- whatever the status of other gangs are, yeah, they should be treated the same as Bloods & Crips and M13, so I don't know the terminology, but they are very much in that same category as other kinds of crime.\par \par \uc2 You know, you've got to take on all crime. I mean, that's why it doesn't matter where the gang is or who it is or if it's just a group of criminals, right? You've got to take it on, prosecute it. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has put criminals behind bars, and she'll be tough as President of the United States to make sure to improve our safety.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But you know, a lot of people will note she is part of the current administration, and this has been happening for years. So how does she then pivot to make the argument that she's going to be completely different on the border if she's elected?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Well, I think they've made some progress on the border in the last few months, of course. But look, this is somebody who's been Vice President. There's a number of areas she's outlined that she's entirely different, a new face for our country, a new path forward rather than returning to a president we've already had who didn't secure the border during his time in office.\par \par \uc2 She has a number of ideas, not just to secure the border, but also to provide tax credits for families, more deductions for small businesses with a tax cut for every small business, and increase housing supply to help make sure that people can afford to live in our country.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, and I know that's something that you have been working on there in Colorado as well.\par \par \uc2 Now, listen, the other issue that the Vice President talks quite a bit about and has campaigned on is the issue of abortion, where she holds an edge when people are asked who they'd rather be handling that issue.\par \par \uc2 Colorado, as we've talked about, has got no limit on abortion through nine months, but you do have a measure on the ballot this time to enshrine that in your state constitution and also to allow for the use of state taxpayers' dollars for some abortions.\par \par \uc2 You've become a bit of a sanctuary state when it comes to the issue of abortion because so many other states around you are so restrictive. Could state taxpayer dollars be used for people traveling into Colorado from out of state for abortions?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Well, all this constitutional amendment does is it puts the protection of the right to choose, which we have already in law, into our state constitution. But it's not just about, and of course, it is important to make sure that women can control when and if they have kids, but it's also about making sure they can raise those kids in a loving home. That's why \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has a tax credit for family affordability, is making progress on home costs, a big issue here in Colorado and other places, and will improve and invest in our public schools rather than eliminate the Department of Education, which her opponent has talked about.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So this week the Vice President was asked about the idea of whether people who have objections, religious or otherwise, to performing abortions should be forced to go ahead and perform them. Here is a bit of that conversation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: What concessions would be on the table? Religious exemptions, for example? Is that something that you would consider?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I don't think we should be making concessions when we're talking about a fundamental freedom to make decisions about your own body.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So federal law currently, as you know, has a lot of protections for people with regard to the issue of conscious and abortion. To a lot of people that sounded like the Vice President would want to get rid of those. Do you think that would be policy under Harris' administration?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Well, I don't think that's what she was saying. I mean, in Colorado we absolutely have Catholic hospitals that don't perform abortions, and there's no stigma at all associated with that. Of course, we want to make sure that people are aware of what they can and can't get at different hospitals and different providers, but this is a matter of conscience. It's between a person, their doctor, and their faith. And I think that's what \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  believes as well.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But when she says no on religious concessions or exemptions, that sounds like in conflict with what you're saying or what the federal law is currently.\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Well, the government doesn't control the hospitals. States don't control hospitals. Again, you don't control what a doctor does or doesn't do.\par \par \uc2 So I don't know exactly what that quote was or what the context was, but I can certainly tell you in Colorado, a totally pro-choice state, that no hospital or doctor is ever forced to perform a procedure that they don't want to or don't morally agree with. I mean, that just doesn't happen, and it's -- it's not legal to force them to do that. Or should it be?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, you were on a hit show, and the audience loved you the other night with Bill Maher. You were there, and you said, listen, the vice president should get out and do more nontraditional things. People need to see more of her. Here's a bit of your appearance.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 POLIS: I think the more people see of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , the more they like her. You can put her out there. She's terrific. She's likable. She's joyous. She's fun. She's clever. Get her out there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They certainly felt that way about you and that audience. But here's the thing. When we have our recent polling on favorable and unfavorables, over the last month, unfavorables have gone up for Vice President Harris, for Governor Walz, while favorables have gone up for J.D. Vance and for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So why is it that you think as people are getting to know these candidates more, they're shifting away from the vice president, they have a less favorable impression?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Well, we don't see that on the ground. I think when \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  first became the nominee, there were a number of folks with sort of the hangover from the \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  era where they didn't want to get him out as much or he didn't have the energy to get out as much. She does, and she's great. She should get out more. She should do more media appearances. She is. She's talking. She's doing a lot of interviews in different venues.\par \par \uc2 Fundamentally, and I've gotten to know her as vice president. I was in Congress when she was a senator as well. She's likable and smart and clever and on the ball. And the more people see of her, the more people say, that's who I want for our future. That's who we need to move us forward and break from this broken past of divisiveness and bring us forward in the spirit of unity that this nation deserves.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, as we all know, the ultimate poll is on Election Day, although voting has been going on for weeks, if not months in some areas.\par \par \uc2 Governor, we always appreciate your time. Thanks for coming on.\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, Republicans battling to hang on to their very slim majority in the House. We're going to go to the board, take a look at some of the critical races as both parties plot a path to taking over the balance of power.\par \par \uc2 Plus, our Sunday panel on Democrats growing fears over the top of their ticket as analysts warn the Trump campaign's overconfidence could backfire. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Welcome back. As we inch closer to November 5th, Democrats are sounding more and more confident about retaking the House. Republicans hold a razor thin majority here, 222, 212, with three vacancies.\par \par \uc2 Now, assuming a full Congress, Democrats will need a net gain of four seats to retake the House. Of course, all 435 seats are on the ballot this year.\par \par \uc2 As of now, Fox News Power Rankings list 22 of these as toss-ups. So let's take a look at some of those critical races that will ultimately decide the balance of power here.\par \par \uc2 OK. Interestingly enough, the road to the majority runs through two coastal blue states. New York, where the 17th district is a tight one. Republican Mike Lawler facing off against \uc2 Mondaire Jones\uc2 , former chair of the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee who lost his seat in 2022. Now, Fox News Power Rankings just moved this one to liens Republican.\par \par \uc2 And in California, the 27th district features one of the key races. Mike Garcia, former Navy fighter pilot is looking to hold on to his seat versus Democrat, \uc2 George Whitesides\uc2 , former CEO of Virgin Galactic.\par \par \uc2 There is also of course battleground, Pennsylvania. In the 10th district, six-term Republican Congressman, Scott Perry, former chair of the House Freedom Caucus, is locked in a battle against former T.V. news anchor, Janelle Stelson. This race just moved to a toss-up.\par \par \uc2 It would be a significant flip in a district that has been reliably red for decades.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, let's talk about with our Sunday group, "The Hill" national political reporter, Julia Manchester. Josh Holmes, former chief of staff to \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 . Fox News senior political analyst, Juan Williams. And Mario Parker, \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  managing editor for U.S. economy and government. Good to see all of you today.\par \par \uc2 All right. Julia, how worried are the two parties about where the House is going? Because like it's been not fun, I think, for the various speakers who have been trying to control the GOP with such a slim margin, but Democrats seem pretty bullish on flipping the House.\par \par \uc2 JULIA MANCHESTER, NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER, THE HILL: They do seem bullish. And I think when you see -- look at the, you know, in PA-10 with Janelle Stelson and Scott Perry.\par \par \uc2 I mean, for a while, you know, talking to House Republicans, they were feeling quite confident about that race, but you're seeing Democrats really start to make inroads. Obviously, now it is a toss-up, so they're feeling confident about that.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, in general, I think there is some worry on the Republican side because, you know if Republicans were to win the House, I think there's a question as to what happens next, you know, by how much margin, what does -- happens with leadership. So I think there's anxiety on both sides maybe for two different reasons, but Democrats I talked to very confident right now.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and, Mario, we know a lot of times voters like to split things up, they don't like to concentrate power. And it looks like Republicans have a much better Senate map this time around, obviously.\par \par \uc2 MARIO PARKER, BLOOMBERG MANAGING EDITOR FOR U.S. GOVERNMENT: Oh, absolutely. I was just thinking that's the only place of optimism for Democrats right now. Tester looks like he's in trouble right now. \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 's looking a little bit shaky in Ohio as well. We'll see whether or not he can survive.\par \par \uc2 But I think Democrats, as much optimism as they have for maybe pulling something out of the House, they've all but kind of resigned themselves to a tough road ahead with the Senate.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and you talk about Democrat worries. "The Hill" has this report this week. Democrats fear the race may be slipping away from Harris. They're worried about the top of the ballot, too.\par \par \uc2 It says, "Democrats are privately expressing worry that battleground polling appears to be moving in Trump's direction over the last two weeks." They quote one strategist saying, if this is a vibe election, Juan, the current vibes ain't great.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Right. So, are Democrats anxious? Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 And I think, you know, it's -- for the Democrats, their sense is there's so much at stake. And you think about this past week with Trump talking about enemies within. And, of course, you have the comments that you referred to earlier in your interviews about, you know, \uc2 John Kelly\uc2 , Matt -- they're talking about him as a fascist.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I heard James Carville say -- you know, James Carville famous for having said way back, it's the economy's stupid. This time he's saying, it's the Constitution's stupid.\par \par \uc2 And so despite the nervousness that's very real among the Democrats, when I look at the numbers, I don't see any evidence of a big shift right now. The big polls have it basically tied on a national level.\par \par \uc2 And in the battleground states, when you look at it, you know, it's about even. It's within one point at most in five of the seven.\par \par \uc2 So I don't know. I just think it's -- in the swing states, especially. with the early voting and there was a lot of talk this week about early voting and some people saying, you're seeing more Republicans come out than ever before. Trump has shifted now since, he's an early voter.\par \par \uc2 But a lot of that also has been very heavily female. And if that's the case, then, oh, my gosh, women in this gender division are more favorable to Democrats. It's hard to say.\par \par \uc2 But I don't know why, but it's true. What you said was absolutely true, Shannon. Democrats are nervous.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They are. But the vice president really seems to like to run from that position. Like she likes to be the underdog. It motivates people to get out. There's danger as, you know, Juan's language that you would use. Although I see Josh, you know. I always say rolling his eyes, but maybe a little skeptical over there.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, "The Washington Examiner," the letter -- a letter from the editor is warning Trump about constantly saying at his rallies. We've got this in the bag. We're way ahead. Our polls show that.\par \par \uc2 He says, "This is a message not well calculated to get his low-propensity voters to the polls in sufficient numbers to turn poll indications into the reality of victory and the realization of power."\par \par \uc2 Should he be more careful about couching that so that folks turn out for him?\par \par \uc2 JOSH HOLMES, FORMER CHIEF OF STAFF TO \uc2 MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 : Well, I think there's a distinction between what he's doing, putting on a show, for people at his rallies and what the campaign itself, what the party and all of the apparatus on the outside are actually doing.\par \par \uc2 I actually don't think the Republicans know how to be overconfident in an election over the last four years.\par \par \uc2 And I think there's two things. There's the analytical side, which you look at all of these polls and you would say, yes, everything looks like it is on the tip of one way or another. All the swing state polls are extremely tight.\par \par \uc2 But you also look at that in contrast to where it was in 2020 or 2016. You're certainly polling a lot better today than he ever was even when he won back in 2016.\par \par \uc2 The other thing is the psychological piece to this, which I think is worth looking at because you can always tell an awful lot about a campaign, based on how they're acting in the closing days.\par \par \uc2 And you've got a Trump campaign that looks like it's having a lot of fun. They're going out there. They're trying to execute. He's doing things like Joe Rogan speaking to different audiences.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: French fries.\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: Serving up French fries at \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The French fries on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: And you've turned on the -- on the Harris side to this very dark rhetoric where we're talking about Nazis. We're talking about fascism. We're talking about, you know, things that are frankly sort of hard for the average American voter to believe, especially after they've had an eight- year experience now with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And so I do think, if it's a vibes, what you said, if it's a vibes election, that feels pretty good for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and what doesn't feel good for Democrats, too, are all of these non-endorsements. You've got the Teamster staying out, the International Firefighters staying out. You also have the "L.A. Times" for its own reasons, and now "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " this week, which explains it this way in a letter from the publisher and CEO.\par \par \uc2 They say the non-endorsement is a statement in support of our readers' ability to make up their own minds on this, the most consequential of American decisions, whom to vote for as the next president.\par \par \uc2 But, Mario, it lands with Democrats as a non-endorsement is taken by the Trump team as a victory.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: No, absolutely. You're going to see Trump try to get an edge any way he possibly can. The fact that he's been at open war, I should add, with "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " over the last eight years, also for him is somewhat of a victory that they aren't endorsing in this race.\par \par \uc2 For him, he feels as though that he's applied appropriate amount of pressure. And given that this has come out about less than a little -- less than two weeks before the election may one what does wonder just about the timing.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I must say as someone who worked at "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " for 20 plus years, what a disappointment.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I can just -- you know, I came to "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " just after Watergate. And Kay Graham, who was then the publisher, stood up to \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  when he was threatening her broadcast licenses.\par \par \uc2 Now, it looks like, not only at "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," but the "L.A. Times," the very wealthy owners who have saved these print operations are afraid of the possibility of Trump winning and the consequences for him.\par \par \uc2 The publisher of "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " recently said, he sent people overseas to study autocratic governments and the repression of the press. And I'm thinking, wow, why aren't they saying that this is the threat?\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: This is kind of a commentary though on legacy media in and of itself. That everybody who works there is somehow totally disappointed that their employer didn't heavily weigh down for the Democrat in this race.\par \par \uc2 I feel like this is one of the obvious reasons why so many people are going to alternative sources of media. Why the Joe Rogans of the world wake up and they have these millions of people that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is trying to work with who are low propensity voters because you've got this stable of "Washington Posts" and "L.A. Times" around the world that everybody just assumes they're obviously going to come down on one side or another. And the fact that there is such disappointment, I think just confirms that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and "POLITICO" said this. Why take a stand on principle now, 11 days before the election, rather than months ago? Why is the paper continuing to endorse in other races like recent nods for Eugene Vindman and Angela Alsobrooks, the congressional races in this area?\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: Yes. Look, it's a good question. My thing to add here is, I would hope that readers don't conflate the editorial side with the journalistic side and don't punish the hardworking journalists at the "L.A. Times, "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," and other publications for a decision made by their editorial board.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Panel, stay with us.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a federal judge blocks Virginia's efforts to purge non-citizens from voter rolls this week, as a defiant Governor Youngkin vows to take this case all the way to the Supreme Court. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GLENN YOUNGKIN\uc2  (R-VA): We have been instructed by a federal judge to - to put 1,600 non-citizens back on the voter rolls when they declared that they were non-citizens. And it's just beyond any measure of common sense. But it's also inconsistent with what we all know the Constitution reads and, of course, what state law reads.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That's Virginia Governor \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2  reacting to a federal judge's ruling requiring the commonwealth to add potential non-citizens back onto Virginia's voter rolls.\par \par \uc2 We are back now with the panel.\par \par \uc2 And listen, what the judge says, Judge Giles, who handed down this decision said, "This process has resulted in eligible voters having their voting registration flagged." Josh, he said it's just not worth the risk. And that's why there's this 90-day period of not doing this before an election.\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: Well, you know, Governor Youngkin actually move beyond the 90-day window. It's just now that - that the judicial side of things is weighing in, that we're - we're closer than that.\par \par \uc2 Look, every once in a while in American politics, to paraphrase the late, great Danny Green, they are who we thought they were. And you have a Justice Department in the Biden administration that seems hell-bent on trying to make sure that you have non-citizens eligible to vote, in this case in Virginia. And I think Governor Youngkin makes an incredibly articulate point. How are we even talk about this. It's a relatively small number of people. But the idea that at this day and age that we're still debating whether - and, by the way, it's illegal. It is illegal for a non- citizen to vote in the Commonwealth of Virginia as it is anywhere in the United States. So, why is it that we're having this debate? It's confounding but it - it underscores all of the concerns the Trump campaign has made, Republicans have made writ large. It's a perfect dovetail to the closing message that they're making.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well -\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: This is exactly why they're making this case. This is about 12 to - he said - Governor Youngkin said 1,600.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But - but, remember -\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: It's small. This is a state of 7 plus million people. And what the judge is saying is all - all he's saying is, you are now eliminating people who previously were put on the voting rolls as U.S. citizens and they have the right to prove they are U.S. citizens before you take them off.\par \par \uc2 But this plays into all the conspiracy theories that Democrats want illegal immigrants voting for Democrat. It's nonsense.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But, Juan, let me just say a couple of things.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: It's scare tactics.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: He says that they removed more than 6,000 people off the roles as non-citizens the last couple years in Virginia. But he says this - these people are triggered when they somewhere, on government document, indicate themselves, or self-identify as non-citizens. So, that's what triggers this process. And - but they can remediate this by coming back to say, no, there was a mistake in the paperwork, or whatever it was, I'm a citizen.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes, that's all it is. That's a - it's illegal, as Josh said, it's illegal. They know it's illegal. The state knows it's illegal. They'd be prosecuted. But, again, it's not really about - it's no reality to this. This is - these are scare tactics being used by Republicans to suggest that, oh, those nasty Democrats, they're getting illegal immigrants to vote for them.\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: Today's conspiracy is tomorrow's headline, as we've found out.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Well, let's get to an issue that affects a lot more voters, which is this issue of abortion. We've got a map here because there are ten states that have got abortion measures on their fall ballots. That can drive people out. And what you see, a couple of those are critical swing states, Nevada and Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Julia, how much could that factor in to the - the up and down ballot situation in those states?\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: I think it very well could. I'm from Florida. I cover Florida quite a bit, so not exactly a swing state anymore. But what I can tell you, and I caution this for people saying that in Nevada and Arizona could make a difference.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think voters oftentimes separate this idea of a ballot measures from who they're voting for at the top of the ticket. So, for example, in Florida there's been a number of recent ballot measures over the past few years that are left-leaning, that voters in Florida have voted for and approved, but they've turned around and voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  and other Republicans.\par \par \uc2 So, Arizona and Nevada, obviously two very different states from Florida. I think abortion is a good issue for Democrats. It's proven successful for them in the past, in 2022 and 2023. I don't know if two years after the overturning of Roe v. Wade, that issue has the same potency, but we'll have to see.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and it's just one big part of the Vice President's closing message.\par \par \uc2 "USA Today" says this, also about her speaking at The Ellipse, wanting to draw attention back to January 6th and President Trump, says, "The VP is using the symbolic venue to make a call for voters to end Trump's, quote, era of chaos and division, and talk about a new path" forward, Mario.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: Yes, when you talk to the Harris campaign, what they say that they're doing differently than the Biden campaign is putting a finer point on some of the issues that Biden sought to attack Trump on, right? Biden held his big democracy speech in Philadelphia for obvious reasons. Constitution, et cetera. For Harris, what she wants to do is something that has alluded Democrats for the most part of this raise, which is to stir that - that visceral - like that angst that they had about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  about four years ago when they - when he lost the last election. So, they're thinking that this image with her there at The Ellipse where, four years ago, on January 6th, his supporters used it as a launch pad to go to Capitol Hill, that this was stirred - further stir that image for voters and some - and draw that contrast.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, in talking about that, and using the word "fascist" and other things that we've heard in the last few days, the Vice President also added this on Friday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And I think, again, the president of the United States should be someone who elevates discourse and talks about the best of who we are and invests in the best of who we are, not someone like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who is constantly demeaning and belittling who the American people are. America deserves better.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, when she was pressed on using the tougher language with regard to Trump, and then saying, well, we need to elevate the conversation, she said, well, the American people need to know the truth about Trump, and that's why I'm going to say those things.\par \par \uc2 Josh.\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: Well, they tried the optimism. They tried the hope. And I think that they know and understand that this is a change election. And that's a tough spot for somebody who's a sitting vice president. So, they thought they could get through what is a protracted schedule here to try to make the case of high mindedness and what we would do differently, and it didn't work. And now they find themselves, in the last couple of weeks of the election, sounding an awful lot like the guy in Philadelphia who was making this case back in 2022, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The rhetoric is indistinguishable. This is basically what the Democratic case against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been for four long years now. They're trying to hold on to those very, very sort of small amount of swing voters, largely in suburban areas, that identify with Republicans on almost all of the issues, but haven't been comfortable in '22 and '20 ultimately supporting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or his candidates.\par \par \uc2 We'll see. It seems pretty late in the game to go back to what didn't work even before she was a candidate.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Wait, wait, wait, I think it did work. I think that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did that speech at Independence Hall in September of '22, before the midterms, and there was no red wave. The Democrats did very well.\par \par \uc2 Right now I think what you're going to hear on The Ellipse when Harris is there this week, she's going to say, look, this is what she said on the campaign trail this week, so I'm just repeating it. He's got an enemies list. I've got a to-do list. And I want you to understand, this is about the Constitutions of the United States being upheld. And that's what I think you're going to hear. It's on America's a garbage can and American carnage.\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: But, Juan, none of that changes your grocery bill, which is what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is talking about, and I think might be decisive here.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: With tariffs? With tariffs?\par \par \uc2 PARKER: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, we're going to go into overtime, but you've got to pay extra for that. It's (INAUDIBLE) review during the commercial.\par \par \uc2 Panel, thank you very much. We'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Israel strikes Iran as the prime minister now says its goals have been accomplished. What happens next in that volatile region? Trey Yingst is there live on the ground with the latest, we'll take you there, as the U.S. urges restraint.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: New fears of a wider regional war in the Middle East after Israel launched its latest wave of strikes on Iran to date in retaliation for Tehran's missile attack on the Jewish state earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 Trey Yingst is live on the ground tracking it all in Tel Aviv, Israel.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Early on Saturday, Israel launched waves of strikes against Iran in response to a ballistic missile attack earlier this month. We know the first target hit was a series of air defense systems, not only in Iran, but also in Iraq and Syria. Once the path was cleared, more than 100 fighter jets participated in the strikes the target military positions inside Iran. Explosions rocked missile production facilities near the Iranian capital, setting the country's program back at least a year according to the reports. Israeli officials tell Fox News that the country remains on high alert over the possibility that Iran may respond to the trikes to avoid looking weak on the world stage.\par \par \uc2 With the tensions still high, Israeli soldiers are still fighting on the ground in Lebanon and Gaza. Thirteen soldiers were killed over a 48 hour period late last week, ten while battling \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and three while fighting \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Inside Gaza, Palestinian civilians desperately wait for a diplomatic solution that would end the conflict. Israeli strikes have killed dozens in recent days.\par \par \uc2 Now, ceasefire talks are set to resuming this week in Doha, Qatar. There are hopes following the death of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leader \uc2 Yahya Sinwar\uc2  that these conversations will be more productive.\par \par \uc2 Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Trey, our thanks to you always.\par \par \uc2 So, the World Series is underway. And you can catch all the action here on Fox. The Dodgers currently have a two-game lead after defeating the \uc2 Yankees\uc2  4-2 last night in Los Angeles. But Dodgers' star Shohei Ohtani partially dislocated his shoulder while attempting to steal a base. He's now being evaluated, we're told. The series heads back to New York City on Monday night.\par \par \uc2 Before the series kicked off this week, For President Trump, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  both discussed their picks during media appearances.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KILMEADE, FOX NEWS HOST: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , your prediction on the World Series seen on Fox.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I'm going to say the \uc2 Yankees\uc2 . The Dodgers have a great team. You know, they have a great team. But if the \uc2 Yankees\uc2  are hot with their bats, I would say they have maybe a little bit of an advantage.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: \uc2 Yankees\uc2  or Dodgers?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Dodgers every day.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The color says it all.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: But, actually, that's my husband's team.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And so, I'm there with him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, whichever team you're cheering for, you can watch game three of the World Series on Fox. Pregame coverage starts at 7:00 p.m. Eastern Monday night.\par \par \uc2 That is it for us today. Thanks for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a great week. We'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY from New York City as we gear up for around-the-clock election coverage. See you then.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020241027ekar00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Report: Chinese Hackers Targeted Trump And Vance Phones; Harris Highlights Impact Of Abortion Bans; Democratic Defectors For Reagan; Trump To Address Rally In Battleground Michigan; Mother Waves Off Harris Apology; Republicans Focus On Threatening Rhetoric; Pennsylvania County Researches Potential Fraud; Cease Fire Talks To Resume As Fighting Persists In Middle East; Climate Concerns Cited In January LNG Export Pause; Former Texas Governor Rick Perry And Former Louisiana Senate \uc2 Mary Landrieu\uc2  Interviewed On Federal Government Energy Policy; "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " And "The \uc2 Los Angeles Times\uc2 " Newspapers Refrain From Endorsing Presidential Candidate; Journalists In Nevada, Pennsylvania, And Ohio Interviewed On Presidential Campaigns In Those States; Harris Campaign Characterizes \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  As Threat To Democracy In Finals Days Of Presidential Race.\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, David Spunt, Jacqui Heinrich, Mark Meredith, Griff  Jenkins, Greg Palkot, Howard Kurtz, Josh Kraushaar \par \uc2 7,677 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right. That's it for us. Have a great night and a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hey, Judge, have a great weekend too.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Texas becomes a battleground for the presidential nominees, with Vice President Harris in Houston and former President Trump in Austin.\par \par \uc2 Another case of apparent attempted voter fraud heading into the election and a major decision about putting non-citizens back on the voter rolls now getting appealed.\par \par \uc2 And the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  becomes the latest major newspaper to refuse to make an endorsement in the presidential race.\par \par \uc2 But "BREAKING TONIGHT", new information about another alleged foreign attempt to disrupt the U.S. election now just 11 days away. \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reporting government officials are pointing the finger at China and say the Trump campaign may have been targeted by Chinese hackers. Different hackers, but again --\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt has our top story tonight. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening. The hacking operation aimed at stealing information from the phones of former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Senator J.D. Vance. What's not clear, did the hackers accomplish their goal? The FBI and Justice Department officially declining to comment on the report from The Times. Members of the Harris and Walz campaign and figures in both political parties on Capitol Hill also reportedly targeted.\par \par \uc2 Sources briefed on the matter say it goes beyond a few phones and hackers reportedly used a sophisticated penetration of several carriers, including Verizon, who told Fox News in a statement, in the last few hours, "We are aware that a highly sophisticated nation-state actor has reportedly targeted several U.S. telecommunications providers to gather intelligence. Along with federal law enforcement, industry peers and third-party cyber experts, we are working to confirm, assess and remediate any potential impact."\par \par \uc2 Hackers from China and Iran have both been targeted in DOJ investigations. Within the past few weeks, we reported on Iranian hackers who went after e- mails from former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's campaign.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the Trump campaign said of this recent reported hack from China, "This is the continuation of election interference by \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and Democrats who will stop at nothing, including emboldening China and Iran attacking critical American infrastructure to prevent President Trump from returning to the White House."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2 , in a statement late this afternoon, confirmed the overall Chinese hacking effort, but did not specifically mention any political campaign. But Bret, this appears to be affecting both sides of the aisle, and we still don't know the full scope.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, David. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Former President Trump and Vice President Harris are locked in a bitter battle that most experts say is really a coin toss heading into Election Day. What is known as a reliable Red State has become ground zero, at least for a day.\par \par \uc2 The Republican nominee was in Austin, Texas earlier today. He has a campaign rally tonight in Traverse City, Michigan. The democratic hopeful is hosting an event in Houston tonight, a state with an unusually close Senate race that could actually determine the balance of power in that chamber.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich is in Houston tonight. Good evening. Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Harris -- good evening, Bret. Vice President Harris has virtually no chance of winning Texas. Trump is leading her by seven points in the latest polling, and no Democrat has won a statewide race here since 1994. But the campaign hopes that putting the spotlight on the state's abortion restrictions will reverberate where it counts.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Vice President Harris, rallying in deep-red Texas to grab headlines in states that will decide the election, like Arizona and Nevada, where abortion is on the ballot. Harris, framing November's choice as a matter of life and death, because of what she calls Trump abortion bans.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: This is not just some theoretical concept. Real harm has occurred in our country. A real suffering has occurred. People have died.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Harris, appearing to reference the case of Amber Nicole Thurman, a Georgia mother who died of complications from abortion pills she got out of state because of Georgia's six-week limit.\par \par \uc2 Thurman's mother, who will appear alongside Harris in Houston tonight, claims doctors delayed treatment fearing legal backlash. But whether Thurman died because of Georgia law is disputed.\par \par \uc2 The statute allows doctors to intervene in medical emergencies or if the fetus has no heartbeat, both of which applied in Thurman's case.\par \par \uc2 Team Harris says just talking about abortion draws voters, traditionally in Trump's camp to Harris. The strategy in the final stretch, more of an effort to mobilize the base than appeal to undecided, something Bernie Sanders called for, with progressives unhappy, Harris spent last week courting Republicans with \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Sanders saying, "There are a hell of a lot more working-class people who could vote for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  than there are conservative Republicans. She has to start talking more to the needs of working-class people."\par \par \uc2 The campaign answered with plans for a Bernie rally in Pennsylvania tomorrow without Harris, who will continue to focus on Trump as an existential threat. \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , likening his rally this weekend to a Nazi reenactment.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER FIRST LADY OF THE UNITED STATES: Trump actually reenacting the \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  rally in 1939.\par \par \uc2 Please open your eyes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): The Trump campaign hit back at Clinton, saying she is still so messed up from her 2016 loss that she forgot, that she did an event at \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  as a senator, and her husband accepted the Democratic nomination there, and "putting aside" her hypocrisy, her rhetoric about half the country is disgusting. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich in Houston. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 In tonight's "FIFTY RACES IN 50 DAYS", we dive into the presidential contest in Michigan, home to a unique barometer in the political landscape. Located just northeast of Wayne County, and that's home to Detroit, is Macomb County, right here on the corner.\par \par \uc2 And you go in there, it is known as the birthplace of the Reagan Democrat. Voters who traditionally supported the Democrat for president, but cast their ballots in support of \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  and his economic populist message.\par \par \uc2 Reagan actually received 53 percent of the vote in Macomb County in 1980, and then 66 percent in 1984 for his re-election, both outperforming his margin of victory statewide.\par \par \uc2 Macomb County is also home to the infamous Michael Dukakis photo op, 1988. You heard Brit Hume talk about that earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 The blue-collar county voted twice for \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , but former President Trump and businessman \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  flipped it in 2016, winning by more than 11 points. Then, in 2020, he did it again. He won Macomb County again by eight points.\par \par \uc2 Now, our Fox News voter analysis says the former president got 43 percent of the support with traditionally blue union members in Michigan. The county once famous for Reagan Democrats may now actually be home to a new Trump coalition.\par \par \uc2 The former president will need strong support in Macomb County to win the key battleground state of Michigan.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Mark Meredith is traveling with the Trump campaign. They are on the other side of Michigan for a rally in Traverse City. Good evening, Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump, he will be here in Michigan about an hour from now. The pre-program just getting underway. You can hear the national anthem just getting started. This is Trump's latest stop in the blue wall state, states that Democrats are counting on for their electoral victory. But Trump is appearing more confident than ever that victory is within his reach.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Former President Trump stopped in deep-red Texas today to give Senator Ted Cruz a boost amid a close re-election contest.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: He is tough as hell. He fights. He is always fighting and, you know, and he is fighting for the state.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Trump hammered Vice President Harris on immigration, highlighting the case of 12-year-old Jocelyn Nungaray, who was murdered this summer in Houston, allegedly by two illegal immigrants from Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 Bret asked about the case during his sit down with Harris last week.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, do you owe them an apology is what I'm saying.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I will tell you that I am so sorry for her loss. I am so sorry for her loss. Sincerely.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Today, Jocelyn's mother waved off the apology.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS NUNGARAY, MOTHER OF JOCELYN NUNGARAY: I think it was very empty. It had no emotion or nor any empathy or sympathy behind it. It felt like it was just forced because she was put on the spot.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): While in Texas, Trump also sat down with podcast host Joe Rogan for an in-depth conversation, which could help Trump increase voter turnout among the show's heavily male audience.\par \par \uc2 With election day fast approaching, Trump plans to barnstorm battleground states every day. Stops already planned in Pennsylvania, Georgia, and Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 ERIC TRUMP, EXECUTIVE VICE PRESIDENT, TRUMP ORGANIZATION: I'm not sure if I've ever seen him more locked in, having more fun. I mean, a lot of things really seem to be going his way right now.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): And as Democrats continue to label Trump a fascist, major concerns remain about Trump's safety. Today, top Republicans issuing a rare joint statement. House Speaker Johnson and Senate Minority Leader McConnell, writing, "We call on the vice president to take these threats seriously, stop escalating the threat environment.\par \par \uc2 On the trail, Trump himself continues to attack Democrats.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : She is a radical left Marxist, rated even worse than crazy Bernie Sanders or Pocahontas,\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Which critics say creates a double standard.\par \par \uc2 MARK CUBAN\uc2 , OWNER, NATIONAL BASKETBALL ASSOCIATION'S \uc2 DALLAS MAVERICKS\uc2 : The man is the king of name calling. And if you're going to call everybody a name, you better be able to take it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): Trump will be making another stop at Michigan tomorrow, a rally that's going to be happening outside of Detroit. The vice president, she too, will be in Michigan tomorrow. She'll be in Kalamazoo. It shows you the significance that Michigan is playing for both of these presidential campaigns.\par \par \uc2 And Bret, already we're seeing record turnout in terms of absentee votes being cast, with more than a million already being returned to the state of Michigan. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Mark Meredith, traveling with the Trump campaign in Michigan. Mark, thanks.\par \par \uc2 The Dow lost 260 today to end a six-week winning streak. The S&P 500 was off two. The NASDAQ gained 103.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow was down 2-2/3 percentage points. The S&P 500 fell one. The NASDAQ was up a fraction.\par \par \uc2 Up next, why are people who are not citizens of this country being returned to the voter rolls? We'll tell you why that's happening in one place and what could come next.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. W -- KRQE in Albuquerque, as a New Mexico judge upholds her decision to dismiss an involuntary manslaughter charge against actor and producer Alec Baldwin over the fatal shooting of a cinematographer on a movie set.\par \par \uc2 The judge says prosecutors did not raise any factual or legal arguments that would justify reversing her decision.\par \par \uc2 FOX32 in Chicago with the expansion of a deadly outbreak of E. coli poisoning linked to \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's quarter pounders. Federal health officials say, now, at least, 75 people have become ill in 13 states. Two people developed a dangerous kidney disease complication. One person has died.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look inside Dodger Stadium in Los Angeles. The big story there tonight from FOX11. Game One of the World Series that as Los Angeles hosts the \uc2 New York Yankees\uc2 . You can see it on the big Fox. Big Fox broadcast network pregame coverage begins at 7:00 p.m. Eastern Time. It should be a good one.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Breaking tonight, there are several states experiencing controversy over voting methods ahead of the election, less than two weeks until Election Day. Suspected voter registration fraud in a key swing state, a major ruling involving non-citizens voting in the November election.\par \par \uc2 And moments ago, a federal court striking down a grace period law for mail- in ballots could have bigger implications. Correspondent Griff Jenkins is in Washington tonight with the latest. Good evening, Griff.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, good evening, Bret. And you're right, a conservative U.S. appeals court has just struck down a Mississippi law that allowed election officials to count mail ballots postmarked by election day but arrive after it.\par \par \uc2 Now, the ruling sends the issue back to a lower court for further consideration, and could be headed for the Supreme Court, as Republicans are applauding the decision because it stemmed from an \uc2 RNC\uc2  lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 Now, meanwhile, in the battleground state that is neck to neck right now, Pennsylvania. Remember, Biden nearly won it by just over one percent in 2020, the Lancaster County Vice Chairman made this announcement today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RAY D'AGOSTINO, VICE-CHAIRMAN, LANCASTER COUNTY, PENNSYLVANIA: To the staff's normal review process. As many as 2,500 completed voter registration forms are being researched for potential fraud stemming from two separate drop batches by individuals.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (voice over): The official said the fraud included false names, duplicate handwriting matching, and inaccurate Social Security or license information and that it does not seem to target one party in particular, believing it may have been linked to canvassing.\par \par \uc2 Now, all of this as Virginia Republican Governor \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2  appeals a ruling that reinstated 1600 voters who some Virginia officials suspected to be non-citizens.\par \par \uc2 Now, Youngkin initially issued an executive order on August 7th that removed them. He says he's prepared to take this to the Supreme Court if necessary.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GLENN YOUNGKIN\uc2  (R-VA): Let's just be crystal clear, what this federal judge did today was order Virginia to reinstate over 1500 people who have self-identified as non-citizens back