TER (through translator): This is a war crime based on a collective responsibility to protect.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  rockets continue to rain down on Haifa and northern Israel. Most intercepted, but one got through, killing two, injuring seven.\par \par \uc2 The Secretary of State Blinken wraps up his trip in London with meetings with Arab leaders, readying talks for Sunday to try again to reach some kind of cease fire with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and at least the partial release of the 101 hostages still held.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: We are intensely engaged, and we are working hard to move this forward and get to that diplomatic resolution.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): As all await the threat and response of Israel to this month's Iranian missile barrage. CENTCOM announcing the deployment of another squadron of F-16 fighter jets to the region.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, WHITE HOUSE NATIONAL SECURITY COMMUNICATIONS ADVISOR: Our commitment to Israel's security remains ironclad, and that means, as appropriate, making force posture changes that we think need to be made.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (on camera): A top military analyst said here tonight that he expects Israel to attack Iran in the next few days. And that there will be a further response from Iran soon after as the temperature rises. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Greg Palkot live in Haifa. Greg, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, our Common Ground segment on energy and how the Biden-Harris policies on LNG may affect the election.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. CLAY HIGGINS (R-LA): I ask you a simple question, has the Department of Energy determined whether exporting LNG to our allies around the world is in a public interest?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRANHOLM\uc2 , U.S. ENERGY SECRETARY: And I'm answering in a simple way, we are in the middle of updating ours.\par \par \uc2 HIGGINS: That would be a no, is what we're dealing with, Mr. Chairman, we can't get a straight answer out of this administration.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, emotions there tonight. In our Common Ground segment, we discuss a bipartisan approach to liquefied natural gas, LNG exports, and why the Biden-Harris administration's pause announced in January is getting some pushback from both sides.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Republican, former Texas Governor, former Energy Secretary Rick Perry and Democrat, former Louisiana Senator \uc2 Mary Landrieu\uc2 , thank you for both being here.\par \par \uc2 This LNG pause, Senator Landrieu, has caused some angst, really, with Republicans and Democrats. How do you see it, and what do you think the administration should be doing?\par \par \uc2 MARY LANDRIEU\uc2 , FORMER LOUISIANA SENATOR: Well, Bret, it has caused some pause from both Democrats and Republicans for many months now, so this is not necessarily a new story, but what is a new story is how important natural gas is to the future of America and for our allies. And I am believing and thinking and saying that Vice President Harris has gotten quite an earful in Pennsylvania, Ohio and Michigan.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I will not ban fracking.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LANDRIEU: She's listened to voters, and what voters are telling her is, hello, natural gas is very important to our domestic agenda and our national security.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, I guess, Governor, there is still a question about, you know, how that moves forward? How do you get across the aisle to get the pause lifted and to kind of start moving forward, whether it's this administration or the next?\par \par \uc2 RICK PERRY, FORMER TEXAS GOVERNOR: Well, and I'm kind of simplistic about it, the best way to get that changes to have a new president in January of 2025.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We will frack, frack, frack and drill, baby drill.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERRY: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his administration obviously believe that American liquefied natural gas not only will create millions of jobs and literally billions of dollars of GDP for this country, but it will also send a message to our allies if you count on us.\par \par \uc2 We understand that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is interested in trying to get elected, and she'll say whatever she needs to say to the Pennsylvania folks. She doesn't want them to hear what her climate advisor has said about the people in the fossil fuel industry, which is to call them eco-terrorist.\par \par \uc2 An eco-terrorist, if you will, in Pennsylvania, if you work in the oil and gas industry, that's what \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's advisor is calling you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What the governor was pointing to is this Camila Thorndike who currently serves as the Harris campaign climate engagement director. And she sent out on X, "A world in which my kids wouldn't die young is the reason I became a climate activist 16 years ago. I always wanted children and don't live particularly lonely/alienated life. But the fossil fuel industry's ecoterrorism is still great birth control." And those statements by her climate adviser kind of sent, as you can hear, Governor Perry's sentiments --\par \par \uc2 LANDRIEU: Well, I understand that. And I understand it's very unfortunate. But, you know, my brother also was her adviser, and my niece is also her adviser, Grace Landrieu.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, that's right.\par \par \uc2 LANDRIEU: And they most certainly understand the benefit of a balanced, all of the above, energy policy. So, you know, I can pick out one or two of Trump's advisers, you know, who are crazy as loons, you know, and point to that. But I think she has been pretty balanced in the campaign. But most importantly, what I wanted to say to you Bret and to the audience is that Democrats -- more and more Democrats are really understanding the value of natural gas. We need more pipelines. We need permitting reform. We are building a great collision of the users of natural, the producers of natural gas. And that's going to be a big part of our energy moving forward along with nuclear.\par \par \uc2 So I think that President Trump has been pretty knowledgeable about these things. He most certainly has been pro-fossil fuel. But I hope he is also interested in reducing emissions. And that's what natural gas does. When we replace coal in India and China, and send them, you know, and send Japan and South Korea our natural gas, we reduced emissions.\par \par \uc2 I believe she has been informed, and hopefully this ban will get lifted whether she wins or President Trump.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: I can assure you one thing. The ban will be lifted on day one when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is in office. I wouldn't hold my breath that the ban is going to go away on day one with the Harris administration.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So I tried to get to common ground here, and you guys also have an LSU-Texas A&M game coming up.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PERRY: There's very little common ground there.\par \par \uc2 LANDRIEU: If you start that, we'll get into a big argument, Bret. So we should leave it at that. But I say that the climate need to move forward.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: The Tigers are going to be in for a real show. Welcome to Kyle Field.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last word, Senator Landrieu.\par \par \uc2 LANDRIEU: Welcome to Death Valley.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: I love you, Mary, but we are going to treat you rough on Saturday. We love you, but we are going to treat you rough.\par \par \uc2 LANDRIEU: All right, take care, you guys.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This just one went off the rails. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 You can see all of the "Common Ground" segments on my podcast "Common Ground." Find that and the "All-Star Panel" podcast under the Bret Baier podcasts at FOXNewspodcast.com, \uc2 Spotify\uc2 , or wherever you download podcasts. You can also see the discussion on the FOX News \uc2 YouTube\uc2  page.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I shouldn't have brought up the football.\par \par \uc2 There is dissension in a couple of the country's biggest newsrooms tonight. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " has announced it will not endorse a presidential candidate this year. "The \uc2 Los Angeles Times\uc2 " made a similar decision earlier this week. Resignations have followed. FOX News media analyst, host of FOX's MEDIA BUZZ, Howard Kurtz has the story tonight. Good evening, Howie.\par \par \uc2 HOWARD KURTZ, FOX NEWS HOST: Bret, good evening. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " in a surprise move, as you noted, is making no endorsement in the presidential race for the first time in decades and will refrain in the future. The editors had turned in a \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  endorsement. But under owner \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  that has been killed. "Post" publisher \uc2 Will Lewis\uc2  called this a statement in support of our readers' ability to make up their own minds.\par \par \uc2 But for at least two years the paper's left leaning editorial page has been slamming \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and more recently, praising the vice president. Former editor Marty Baron accused "The Post" of spinelessness. While owners are entitled to set editorial policy, Bezos does a lot of business with the federal government through his \uc2 Blue Origin\uc2  rocket company and \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . He may not want to jeopardize his already testy relationship with Trump. "Post" board member Robert Kagan, a security expert at the \uc2 Brookings Institution\uc2 , confirms to FOX that he has resigned.\par \par \uc2 A similar scenario like "The \uc2 Los Angeles Times\uc2 ," a liberal newspaper where billionaire owner Patrick Soon-Shiong killed an endorsement backing Harris. Editorial chief Mariel Garza resigned in protest, saying she could not remain silent and was followed by two other top editors, including a Pulitzer Prize winner.\par \par \uc2 The owner says he wants the paper to be less divisive in a polarize atmosphere, though that hasn't stopped years of anti-Trump pieces in that same atmosphere. More than 1,700 people have canceled their subscriptions.\par \par \uc2 Newspaper endorsements don't matter much these days in national elections. But if they are going to punt on the most important decision of the year, why have editorial pages at all? Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good question, Howie. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is formally apologizing to Native Americans for what he calls the sin of a government run boarding school system. The president calling the program that forcibly separated Indian children from their parents a blot on American history and a sin on the country's soul. The Interior Department says at least 973 Native American children died in the system over 150-year period that ended in 1969.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we talk to a journalist panel about the presidential election in their battleground states that they cover on the ground. And later, the FOX all stars on the final 11 days of the campaign.\par \par \uc2 As we go to break, "Grateful Dead" bass guitarist Phil Lesh has died. Lesh also co-wrote several songs, including "Box of Rain," a tribute to his father. He sang harmonies and was renowned by fans for his thundering bass lines. Lesh was a founding member of the legendary band that formed in San Francisco in the 1960s. Phil Lesh was 84.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Hello Las Vegas, we love you, Las Vegas. And hello to Nevada.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Oh, it is so wonderful to be back in Nevada.\par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE, (R) VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We are going to in Pennsylvania, we are going to make \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  the next president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ, (D) MINNESOTA: We do the work. Pennsylvania goes blue. We win the White House.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I'm thrilled to be back with the proud, hardworking patriots of the great state of Ohio.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A lot of moving around there by the candidates. If you look at the Real Clear Politics average of polls and as we head down to Election Day, you can see we're about the same place, very close. But the former president has tight leads in a number of those states.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our journalism panel, journalists on the ground in some of these swing states and places of consequence, Jessica Hill, politics reporter for "The Las Vegas Review-Journal," Marc Levy, Pennsylvania state house and politics reporter for "The Associated Press," and Karen Kasler, state house bureau chief for Ohio Public Media.\par \par \uc2 Jessica, let me start with you. In Las Vegas, we have seen this early vote in Nevada, and Democrats, Republicans, others, you know how it breaks down and what the vote looks like. "The Nevada Independent," Jon Ralston saying "GOP has a 24,000 ballot lead statewide or five percent. It's having a trickle-down effect on down ballot races. Whether this is about a frontloaded GOP vote and Dems will begin to change the mix is a question we can't answer yet." Early on Ralston has said that he was surprised by all that early vote. I'm just curious what you all hear on the ground and what it looks like so far.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA HILL, "LAS VEGAS REVIEW-JOURNAL": Yes, this is definitely a new trend. Usually, Republicans come out more so on Election Day and not so much on early voting. But like Ralston had said, it remains to be seen what significance this will have. We have basically a two week period of early voting, so one more week left. We'll see how it goes.\par \par \uc2 But this really goes back to the change in messaging from the Republican party and the Trump campaign who previously really were all about one day election voting. And this time they really embraced early voting. I think you are seeing that in these numbers.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, and you may see that across the board really across the country.\par \par \uc2 Marc, let me talk to you. The registrations we have talked about before in Pennsylvania, Republicans have cut into Democrats overwhelming lead in registrations. Democrats still lead in that, in fact, but Republicans have really closed the gap.\par \par \uc2 MARC LEVY, "ASSOCIATED PRESS": Right, yes. Look, when \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  was elected in Pennsylvania twice, the gap was over a million. Now we're looking at under 300,000 more Democrats registered than Republicans. And it could be a blend of different things. It could be more Democrats dropping off the rolls. It could be Democrats switching their registration to Republican to reflect how they have been voting for decades. But it's also almost certainly Republicans doing a better job signing up new voters. And the question is what it will mean. Democrats have almost dominated statewide elections in the last few years, in the last decade.\par \par \uc2 But we also see Republicans doing a better job mail-in voting. The gap in past years has been three to one Democrat. Right now it looks like it's more like two to one.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Marc, is there a sense on the ground that, one, it is as close as these polls say? And two, that whatever happens, it's going to reflect that that Senate race with McCormick and Casey?\par \par \uc2 LEVY: Without a doubt. Everyone feels that it's close. I have talked to partisans on both sides who feel confident that their side will win. But everyone acknowledges that it is very close. It's intense. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , their vice presidential running mates are either here every day or one of them is here every day if not two of them.\par \par \uc2 And in the second question you asked? It slipped my mind.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Does it translate to the McCormick-Casey Senate race?\par \par \uc2 LEVY: So, without a doubt. Everyone says that McCormick is running a little bit behind Trump. The better Trump does the better McCormick will do. The sense is that there are some people who will split their tickets. They will vote for Trump and they'll vote for Casey. To the extent that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does better and better, he will pull \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2  across the finish with him.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And really, that's the dynamic, Karen, in Ohio. In years past, races past, Ohio has been one of the swing states that we talk about all the time. But this time it's about that Senate race, \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  and \uc2 Bernie Moreno\uc2 . Very close according to the average of polls. How does it look on the ground?\par \par \uc2 KAREN KASLER, OHIO PUBLIC MEDIA: Well, we have had about 1.4 million Ohioans who have cast ballots since we started early voting here in October, on October 8th. We have two weekends of early voting coming up. Republicans are returning early ballots in higher numbers than Democrats right now. But that could be just a matter of Republicans are so banking their votes, essentially, rather than waiting until Election Day. This race is the most expensive \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  race in Ohio history, about $270 million being spent, and almost all of the ads are negative, negative \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  or negative \uc2 Bernie Moreno\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's amazing, those numbers. And if you go around the country, it's massive. We're going to check in with all of you as we get closer to the election. We really appreciate all you have tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, our all-star panel with the latest on the presidential race around the country. Then it's Friday, "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We are doing well in all seven states, every one of those swing states.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Do you want a president who, on one hand, in the former, President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , tried to undo the will of the people? Or do you want a president who respects the will of the people, free and fair elections?\par \par \uc2 HARRY ENTEN, CNN SENIOR POLITICS WRITER AND ANALYST: The fact that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has a legitimate shot of winning the popular vote is something that I think a lot of folks, including in my line of vote, really didn't think could possibly happen when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was running last time around. He could make history.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, whatever happening, it's going to be history. And it is tight. Do you know how tight it is? The two new national polls out today, two of them, take a look, CNN, 47-47. "New York Times,"-Siena, 48-48. There you go.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring our panel, Josh Kraushaar is a FOX News Radio political analyst, Morgan Ortagus, the host of "The Morgan Ortagus Show" on \uc2 Sirius XM\uc2 , founder of Polaris National Security, and Ross Douthat, he's a columnist for "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ".\par \par \uc2 You see the moving around, you see where the closing argument happens. Morgan, let me start with you. Clearly Vice President Harris has come to that point where the message, the closing message, along with a speech on the Ellipse, we are told, on Tuesday, is going to be about threat to democracy. We didn't think it was going to work, that message in 2022. It did in the closing arguments for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Is it working now?\par \par \uc2 MORGAN ORTAGUS, FORMER STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESPERSON: Well, we will certainly see in a week-and-a-half on Election Day. To me, it reeks of desperation, Bret. When you look at this, I think they were really, because of the two assassination attempts against former President Trump in the last few months, especially the one over the summer where he was almost shot in the face, he was shot in the ear, almost killed, they were short of shamed out of using this rhetoric. They stopped saying "threat to democracy." They stopped saying "fascist" in all this rhetoric because so many people around the country said, listen, this rhetoric is radicalizing leftists who are getting guns and trying to assassinate the president.\par \par \uc2 In the final week, they have turned back through the rhetoric that may have been responsible for motivating people to try to assassinate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and it just reeks of desperation. And to me, I think it's totally irresponsible. They were shamed out of doing it earlier, and they should be shamed from doing it now.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Ross, in the meantime, the vice president asked about the closing argument. She has defended what she's talking about. In the meantime, one of her surrogates, former Democratic nominee \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , went on TV last night and said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: You'll see next week Trump actually reenacting the \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  rally in 1939. Neo-Nazis, fascists, in America were lining up to essentially pledge their support for the kind of government that they were seeing in Germany. So I don't think we can ignore it.\par \par \uc2 Now, it may be a leap for some people, but, please, open your eyes to the danger that this man poses to our country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Obviously, Ross, her husband accepted the Democratic nomination in \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2 . The Trump campaign statement came out quickly, "\uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  is so messed up from her raging eight yearlong case of anti-Trump derangement syndrome she forgot she did an event in Madison Square Garden when she was a senator, and her husband Bill accepted the Democratic nomination there. Putting aside her hypocrisy, Hillary's rhetoric about half the country is disgusting." What about that and how it factors in in these final days.\par \par \uc2 ROSS DOUTHAT, "NEW YORK TIMES": I think it's clearly not where the Harris campaign wanted to be, right? I think clearly their whole theory of the case from the start was they were going to do something different than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , that democracy, sort of panic about democracy was a Biden issue and they were going to campaign on joy and uplift. And somewhere between joy and uplift and doom and gloom they didn't find a clear agenda.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I disagree with Morgan. I think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did try to overturn an election. I don't think there is anything invalid about trying to make that point as part of your closing argument. I think hyping it up into references to Nazis in the 1930s is not really going to persuade anyone, especially when the counter image is, you know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  serving fries in a \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's drive-in. So, it's not it's not where the Harris campaign wanted to be, even it is, you know, reasonable to have as part of the debate.\par \par \uc2 ORTAGUS: Well, if I may just answer that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, go ahead, really quickly.\par \par \uc2 ORTAGUS: Yes, that's not what I said. I'm talking about the rhetoric of saying that the presidential nominee is a threat to democracy, that he is a fascist, using rhetoric that is inciting people to violence is irresponsible. You can have policy differences. You can criticize \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  every day of the week. There is a big difference between that and calling him a fascist and using words. Maduro calls his opponents fascists. Putin calls his opponents Hitler and Nazis. And that's what the Democrats are doing. There is a big difference between what I said and what you are alleging.\par \par \uc2 DOUTHAT: Just for the record, though, it's true that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has called \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  a communist and fascist at various points, right?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's true.\par \par \uc2 DOUTHAT: This is a freewheeling political debate.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, the Harris campaign points to the remarks of general \uc2 John Kelly\uc2 , and as quoted in the Woodward book, General Milley, the chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff.\par \par \uc2 Josh, the closing argument for the former president seems a lot more joyful. He's going on Joe Rogan, or has, and that will drop, we're told, tomorrow on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 . And that should be interesting.\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR, FOX NEWS RADIO POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes. Look, it's part of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's outreach to working class voters, male voters. He's making inroads with some unusual constituencies.\par \par \uc2 And getting back to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , also, this election feels a lot like 2016 even with that \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  cameo there in that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has pitted her message from the economy to saving democracy. She is focusing on elements of the base, like some of those working class voters, Gen Z, younger Americans, African American men. They are slipping from her reach. That's very familiar to anyone who followed \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 's campaign when she was losing parts of the base in 2016. Even Bruce Springsteen is doing a last minute cameo in Philadelphia, which was what happened for the Clinton campaign in 16 as well.\par \par \uc2 So there is a lot of similarities to what Democrats are feeling like now and what they felt like with Hillary Clinton's campaign in 2016.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, Morgan, the Republicans seem to feel good when they're talking about they feel like they are winning, and that's what motivates them. Democrats seem to be exactly opposite, that they motivate people by saying, this is not happening, and getting fretful and sweating it out. And that could be some of the messaging as well.\par \par \uc2 ORTAGUS: Listen, we talked a lot about the Clintons tonight. \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  said in the 90s it's all about the economy, stupid. It was true then. It's true now. People are still paying way too much for milk, eggs, bread, and bacon, and their gasoline, and that's why I think they are going to be voting differently next week.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We will see. Panel, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 DOUTHAT: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know that sound. It's Friday. Time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Hello, everybody. My first day at \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 . I'm looking for a job.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: If \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  can vote early, you can, too.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : The main thing is you've got to get out. You've got to vote. And I'll be voting early.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Our job is to make sure everyone has a free, fair, and fast election. That's what we are doing this year.\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: -- vomit on my sweater already, mom's spaghetti.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I'm here with a message of hope for all Americans.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We got to lock him up.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Politically lock him up.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER, CNN ANCHOR: Do you think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a fascist?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Yes, I do.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2 , (R-SC): To every Republican supporting her, what the hell are you doing?\par \par \uc2 LIZ CHENEY\uc2 , (R) FORMER U.S. REPRESENTATIVE: Don't listen to \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , number one. It's good life advice.\par \par \uc2 TULSI GABBARD, FORMER HAWAII CONGRESSWOMAN: I'm proud to stand here with you today, President Trump, and announce that I'm joining the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: I have never voted for anyone that I didn't believe in.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: North Korean soldiers are traveling to Russia to fight against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And if the parole concurs with my assessment, and it will be their decision, they will be released accordingly.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Everybody got what they wanted. You have two powerhouse teams.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Some breaking news that Israel, according to some sites overseas, may have started a strike against Iran. We're monitoring that. We will see if we get some more information.\par \par \uc2 Monday on SPECIAL REPORT, we'll take a close look at the vote, how long it may take.\par \par \uc2 This weekend, Shannon Bream FOX NEWS SUNDAY, her guests are Florida Republican Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  and Colorado Democratic Governor \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast. You can follow me on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  @BretBaier, exclusive behind the scenes look, especially as we get closer to Election Day.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. Make it a great weekend. Laura is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020241026ekap00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Media Trashed \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' Town Hall As Total Disaster; L.A. District Attorney Recommends Menendez Brothers Resentenced; \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's Final Pitch To Voters: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Is A Fascist; Border Wall Flip-Flop: VP Confronted On Policy Shift After Calling It Stupid; They're Not Your Bros: Keaton Tries To Talk Men Out Of Attending Trump Rallies; Yard Sign Warfare! People Setting Up Booby Traps, Trip Wires\b0\par\par\uc2 Jesse Watters, Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Dana Perino, Greg  Gutfeld, William La Jeunesse \par \uc2 8,653 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: -- this is a very controversial case. It doesn't push or pull in either elective favor, depending on where you stand on this, there's a whole new generation that wasn't even around when these murders occurred in 1989 that knew nothing about this, but it has galvanized crowds on TikTok and elsewhere to say, justice must be done and those men should be free. We'll continue following this, but history is being made regardless by revisiting this. Here's THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hello, everybody. I'm Jesse Watters along with Judge Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Dana Perino and Greg Gutfeld. It's 5:00 in New York City, and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 Fox News alert, at any moment, L.A. District Attorney George Gascon set to announce that he's asking the court to re-sentence Erik and Lyle Menendez, who are currently serving life sentences without parole for killing their parents in 1989, we'll bring that to you live once it happens.\par \par \uc2 But in the meantime, what the heck is she talking about?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: There was a lot that was done, but there's more to do, Anderson. And I'm pointing out things that need to be done that haven't been done but need to be done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: With the polls razor tight, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  face-planting in one of her last chances to win over voters, Harris malfunctioning when trying to answer basic questions at a CNN town hall. The scripted sound bite machine failing miserably to articulate clear policies on pressing pocketbook issues.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: One of my aspects of doing what we need to do to bring down the cost of living for working people and the middle class in America is to address the issue of grocery prices. We need to take a look at the filibuster to be honest with you.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER, CNN HOST: But if you're earning $500,000, $600,000, $700,000, under your plan, there's a good chance your taxes go up?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: It's -- we can't have this conversation without knowing what -- it's a very complicated situation, right?\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Do you believe you would be more pro-Israel than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I believe that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is dangerous.\par \par \uc2 JOE DONAHUE, CUSTOMER SERVICE WORKER & LOCAL ELECTION OFFICIAL: What weaknesses do you bring to the table?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: That's a great question, Joe. Well, I am certainly not perfect, so let's start there. And I think that I -- perhaps a weakness, some would say, but I actually think it's a strength is I really do value having a team of very smart people around me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Within minutes of that catastrophe, CNN had to admit she blew it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID AXELROD, CNN SENIOR POLITICAL COMMENTATOR: The things that would concern me is, when she doesn't want to answer a question, her habit is to kind of go to word salad city.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER, CNN ANCHOR: She focused a lot more on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I think it's fair to say, than she did on many specific.\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH, CNN CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: If her goal was to close the deal. They're not sure she did that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Dana, typical interview. They tell you that when someone asks you what your greatest weakness is, you turn it into a strength. Was that the moment to do that?\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Well, of course, and I was thinking today about she's really never had to have a tough campaign, right? So, when you run statewide as a Democrat in California, I guess the primary is difficult, but you don't really have to differentiate yourself from an opposing viewpoint. And that's the same for the Senate races.\par \par \uc2 And then, she was in the Senate, she did -- I think she skipped 60 percent of the votes when she was in the Senate, so she wasn't even there for that debate.\par \par \uc2 And then, she gets this. It gets handed to her. Well, because she's on the ticket with Biden, you could maybe say arguably that she helped, but that was Biden, and she's just on the ticket like \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  is on her ticket.\par \par \uc2 I think last night, they finally put her out there on a high wire without a net, and she fell over and over again on her face. And that's -- don't take it from me, that's David Axelrod from CNN. He said it much more politely.\par \par \uc2 But even for me, I've looked for ways to compliment her. But I sit there and I think, how in the world, when you are at the White House, you can call anybody, anybody will pick up your phone call. If you're -- the vice president would like a briefing on this or that. The vice president when -- the vice president will want something that's -- they are at your beck and call, you could get up to speed.\par \par \uc2 So, they spend the entire day the other day getting ready for interviews and a town hall that basically she just steps on a rake the entire time.\par \par \uc2 I think it was the most damaging campaign event that she's had to date. This morning Jim Messina, also an Obama guy. He was on "AMERICA'S NEWSROOM". He also said, not great, but he said she has room to grow. And I said, OK, but does she have time to grow? And he said, that's a great question, and I think we all know the answer to that.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: 13 days, Greg, does she have time to grow?\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I think she doesn't have to grow. I like it the way she is. Thought she was amazing.\par \par \uc2 You know, when CNN is slamming you, that's like parents at a little league game heckling their own kid, they realize they couldn't find a silver lining on this big sack of poo. It's like -- it's like, it's physically impossible to spin wet garbage because it ends up all over.\par \par \uc2 I think we need to retire the phrase word salad, because at least salad could be nutritious. You know, you can have actually authentic ingredients, in this case, her manner of speaking, it's an affront to lettuce and kale growers everywhere.\par \par \uc2 You know, after every disaster, you know, relief pours in. It's kind of like that with Kamala, it's like there's this, like Hurricane Kamala comes in. There's a rush to raise money for the victim. There's benefit concerts. They're asking for small donations, because every time she lands, there's devastation, mainly to herself, but also to her party.\par \par \uc2 I think what you're seeing, though, the realization here is the downfall of the inauthentic. You know, whatever you say about Trump, good or bad, you know, it's real. He's authentic. So authentic, he doesn't need to employ a fake accent wherever he goes, or create fake personal stories or professional histories.\par \par \uc2 With Harris, whatever you say about her, good or bad, you know, it's inauthentic, right? Nothing rings true, nothing. Her upbringing, her professional experience, her very words that come out of her mouth like toxic glitter stick all over you. Every time she's talking, your brain cells want to commit suicide.\par \par \uc2 So, I think -- I think what you're seeing is an American public coming to the realization that they may not like Trump, but they don't know what the hell this is.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: What was that last night, Judge Jeanine?\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Well, that's what a billion dollars gets you, a candidate that is so horrible she can't even articulate what she's going to do to make your life better.\par \par \uc2 And the amazing part of this is she is so out of her league. She's like a high school J.V. playing in the pros. She could not articulate other than to say she's not \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . She can't say what she's going to do better. She doesn't have any answers to solutions, and she has had the audacity to sit in a position of power in the White House, the highest rank that a woman has ever attained in the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 And she hasn't made any difference for the American people who are suffering from inflation, the economy, crime, immigration, and she just sits there like a queen on the pedestal and says, when I get in, if you vote for me, then I'll deal with the issues. That's not how it works in America. She couldn't even connect with the people who were asking her questions. It was literally no -- here we go.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: We have the Fox News alert, L.A. District Attorney George Gascon now speaking about the resentencing of the Menendez brothers. Let's listen.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE GASCON, L.A. DISTRICT ATTORNEY: Jose Menendez. Jose Menendez and niece Karen VanderMolen and Kitty's niece. They're all here with us.\par \par \uc2 Also with us here is Barack Brock Lunsford (ph), he's the assistant Head Deputy for the full post-litigation conviction unit, and Nancy Surveyor (ph), who is the Deputy District Attorney in charge of the resentencing unit.\par \par \uc2 Let me begin by telling you that this is a case where we've had many people in this office spend a great deal of time review on the case. I have to tell you unequivocally that we don't have a universal agreement. There are people in the office that strongly believe that the Menendez brothers should stay in prison the rest of their life, and they do not believe that they were molested. And there are people in the office that strongly believe that they should be released immediately, and that they were, in fact, molested.\par \par \uc2 I have to tell you that after a very careful review of all the arguments that were made for people on both sides of this equation, I came to a place where I believe that under the law re-sentencing is appropriate, and I am going to recommend that to a court tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 What that means in this particular case is that we're going to recommend to the court that the life without the possibility of parole be removed and that they will be sentenced for murder, which, because there are two murders involved, that will be 50 years to life.\par \par \uc2 However, because of their age under the law, since they were under 26 years of age at the time that these crimes occurred, they will be eligible for parole immediately.\par \par \uc2 The teams that have worked in this on the resentencing side of it have spent literally, probably hundreds of hours right now looking at this case. These cases were originally presented on behavior (ph) side of it, which I'll explain in a moment what that is.\par \par \uc2 Last year, and then, the request for prosecutor initiator resentencing occur earlier this year, and there have been people in this office working in this cases from the very beginning, as well as many other cases that we have.\par \par \uc2 The reason why I'm here today and why I came in front of all of you about 10 days ago is because there was a more recent documentary about the case that, again, brought a tremendous amount of public attention, and we know there have been other documentaries. So this is not the first. This is a more recent one, and frankly, our office got flooded with requests for information.\par \par \uc2 And even though this case was already scheduled to be heard in late November, I decided to move this forward because, quite frankly, we did not have enough resources to handle all the requests. And one of the things that I thrive to do in this office is to be very transparent in everything that we do.\par \par \uc2 In this case, we review the prison files, and you have to understand that the way the process works, when you're talking about a sentence and re- sentencing under the law, it really focuses not necessarily on what the original crimes were, but it focuses on, has the person been rehabilitated, number one, and number two, can they be released safely into their community?\par \par \uc2 Under that rubric, since I've been in office, we have resentenced over 300 people, including 28 for murder, only four have reoffended. If that was a regular recipient rate in this country, we will be the safest nation in the world. But we know that it's not, in fact, somewhere between 40 and 50 percent of the people that go to prison reoffend and get re-arrested, and that's why we have so many problems.\par \par \uc2 However, when you look at the case of the Menendez brothers, you see two very young people, one was 19 and the other was 21 when they committed this horrible acts. And I want to underline there were horrible acts.\par \par \uc2 There is no excuse for murder, and I will never imply that what we're doing here is to excuse that behavior, because even if you get abuse, the right path is to call the police seek help. But I understand also how sometimes people --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: So, that was George Gascon saying he's recommending resentencing of the Menendez brothers who were convicted for the 1989 murder of their parents. Let's go right to William La Jeunesse to break it all down. William.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, Jesse, doesn't mean they'll be released immediately, but they could in a matter of weeks, or maybe a month or so.\par \par \uc2 So, this is going to go, this recommendation for resentencing will now go before a judge. That judge will hear any mitigating circumstances of the crime, which, of course, has now come up with this new letter and this new victim coming forward.\par \par \uc2 But also there are some objections, some from family members that will oppose their release. But what Gascon said here is, listen, they were originally sentenced to life without parole. Now he wants that charge, in a sense, be released to murder, which has a lesser amount period of time, and then, given they've already spent 30 years in prison, he said they would be eligible for an immediate release if the judge does agree to that.\par \par \uc2 So, the two new pieces of evidence really quickly. One is a letter that came up that basically says that, confirming that before the murder, that their father physically, sexually abused them, that came out.\par \par \uc2 And the second thing that came out is an individual who came forward, who is a member of a boy band, a young man who also claims that he was raped by the boy's father, Jose Menendez, given that that is why the D.A. in this case has recommended re-sentencing that will ob