on, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020241024ekan00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Polls Show Razor-Thin Race With 13 Days To Go; V.P. Harris Scrambles To Shore Up Black Male Support; New Charges In Alleged Iran Plot To Kill Activists; Now: Activist At Heart Of Alleged Iran Murder Plot; Sen. \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  (R-SC) Is Interviewed About North Korea Troops In Russia, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ; Report About Trump, Slain Soldier Under Scrutiny; Biden Backtracks After Saying "Lock Up" Trump; House Control A Toss-Up With Election 13 Days Out; Rep. Mike Lawler (R-NY) Is Interviewed About House Control\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Robert Wolf, Katie Pavlich, Lawrence Jones \par \uc2 8,935 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Yeah, deep breathing over here, too. Thank you guys. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum, and this is The Story.\par \par \uc2 A deeper dive right now into some of the blue wall warning lights that are flashing with just 13 days to go in the United States presidential contest, NBC reporting that a senior Harris campaign official fears that Wisconsin and Michigan may, quote, fall off.\par \par \uc2 One issue in Michigan is the stubbornness of the uncommitted Democrat. We saw this during the primaries. They voted uncommitted when they voted for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in the state of Michigan. Many of them are angry with the handling of the war in Gaza by the White House. They number about a hundred thousand. Trump lost Michigan in 2020 by about 150,000. So, this is a significant factor in this vote.\par \par \uc2 Let's take a look at Pennsylvania. Politico reporting that, quote, Dems see warning signs for Harris in that state with Latino men. Cook Political Report just shifted a key Senate race there from lean Democrat. That race for the Senate is now a tossup in Pennsylvania, steady climb there for McCormick on his side of the fence.\par \par \uc2 But the race is so close that prominent election forecaster Nate Silver, who has been pushing out all of his data and numbers and working very dedicatedly on all of this says he's just going with his gut at this point. He says his gut tells him at this moment, 13 days out, that Trump will win. Veteran Democratic Strategist James Carville writes that he is certain that the election will go to Harris in the end. So, here we are.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Katie Pavlich, Townhall.com editor, and Robert Wolf, former economic advisor to President \uc2 Obama\uc2  and 32 Advisers, founder and CEO, both are Fox News Contributors. Great to have both of you here, welcome.\par \par \uc2 Robert, do you share these concerns about the blue wall?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT WOLF, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I obviously share the concerns, but Martha, you and I have been speaking since the night of the debate with Biden. And I think since that day, I have said Trump will win, including after the Democratic Convention and including after the debate. I am now switching my view. I think Kamala is in a better situation. She has more pathways to 270.\par \par \uc2 I just spent the weekend in Philadelphia. I'm a data guy. I've been in finance for 40 plus years, and it just seems to me that based on what I'm seeing a way from just the blue wall, whether it's in North Carolina and Nevada, I actually think if it was today, she would win.\par \par \uc2 And I also think the most important part --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, are you saying that you believe she will hold Pennsylvania, Michigan -- excuse me for interrupting -- and -- Pennsylvania, Michigan, and Wisconsin, she holds all three, in your opinion?\par \par \uc2 WOLF: I think she definitely holds Michigan and Wisconsin. I think today she would win Pennsylvania. But if she lost Pennsylvania, I think she could also win North Carolina and Nevada. That makes up for Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 And the major part is that she's doing better on the economy. Even though the president's up, he was up 15 points on the economy. He's up single digits now on the economy. And if you look at the jobs market, you look at wages, the stock market, actually the win's been pretty much at the vice president's back the last month. So, I just have a change of view of where I think this election is going to end up.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Okay. You know, Katie, you go by the gut and the feel of how things are right now. Here's Harry Enten talking about Harris' polling among independents. We'll get your thoughts on the other side.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRY ENTEN, CNN SENIOR DATA REPORTER: Michigan, Pennsylvania, Wisconsin, the Great Lakes, that blue wall, right? \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  last time around won them by five points over \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Look at where we are today. This is the type of movement \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  likes to see in the center of the electorate, up by a point.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Katie, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, the wind has not been at the back of the vice president. She's only gone down in her polling since she's been more out in front of the American people trying to make her case, which tends to be different depending on the audience she's talking to.\par \par \uc2 But when we're looking at the blue wall, Martha, I went back to 2016 because the last poll person who did not secure the blue wall as a Democrat was \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , and she is out there today trying to make the case for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to be the next president.\par \par \uc2 But if you look at the reasons why \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  was not able to hold on to Michigan, Wisconsin, the blue wall is because she lost blue collar workers, white workers, and she was unable to keep her base together, specifically among young voters and African American voters. You're seeing the same thing playing out right now with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in those states.\par \par \uc2 And she -- her message today combined with what the White House is seemingly coming to the rescue on is that Trump is a fascist, that he's too dangerous to be the president. She's not talking about the economy. She's trying to make this about character and a choice between two people with different personalities rather than on a record that she still has not defined.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, you know, here's another headline from today, Robert, and I want to get your thoughts on what Katie just said. But the headline of Politico, don't let Trump's buzzy sideshows distract you from his traditional swing state strategy. And it breaks down what they see as a very big concentration of time in Pennsylvania by both Trump and also J.D. Vance. So, they're saying when he's going to other places, like California and this weekend he'll be at \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2 , he is looking to generate enthusiasm. Certainly the \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's stop was a big win for him this week. And that is, I think, why you see some of the momentum and gut feeling heading in his direction.\par \par \uc2 So, how does Harris, you know, bring back that joy that we saw coming out of the convention?\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Yes. So, a few things, I'm not here discounting President Trump. I mean, this is a flip of a coin race. I just think that today there are more pathways for Vice President Harris, so just for clarity. Secondly, with the blue wall, you can't even compare the two plans. The plan of former President Trump is one of isolationist nationalism. He lost manufacturing jobs. We could debate that all day who has a better plan for that? That's why Wall Street Journal went after he gave his plan, he talked about this idea of deer and headlights. No disrespect, Kate, I've been in finance and business for 40 years. You've been in politics.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, hold on. Hold on. Hold on. On the economy, Trump is still ahead in terms of what viewers feel, I mean, voters say they experienced during the Trump.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: I'm the one that said that he's still ahead, but it's eroded from 15 percent down to 9 percent. And when they both gave a plan on manufacturing, The Wall Street Journal hammered President Trump's plan, hammered it. They called it like a deer in headlights. And they actually said Vice President Kamala's plan was friendlier to business.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, Katie, you know --\par \par \uc2 WOLF: That's your Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'll let you explain. I understand that's The Wall Street Journal. I just see what regular folks seem to be thinking and feeling and saying across the country. And I'm not sure they care too much, you know, what The Wall Street Journal's opinion is. But, you know, we're going to see that on Election Day. Katie?\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Well, with all due respect to Robert, I'm someone who grew up in a blue collar small business, so I understand how business works as well from the level of people working very difficult and difficult jobs that take a lot of work. And you're seeing a number of people in Michigan pushing back against \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' plans to destroy the auto industry, through her mandates of these electric vehicles, which she says she was for, now she's against it. She's for it again They say they will destroy their jobs there. And the tax plans that she's tried to implement will also make business harder for small businesses like the one that I grew up in.\par \par \uc2 So, with all due respect, I think we both have our experiences that we can speak to when it comes to this race.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: That's just not accurate. We've had a factory boom. We have, I mean, the Foxcom plant that everyone talks about in Wisconsin, that wasn't even built.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Why is it that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  didn't get the \uc2 UAW\uc2  endorsement, Robert? What about the union endorsement that didn't come through for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Listen.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Why isn't she getting that endorsement?\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Listen, there are going to be unions that support her.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: They've been endorsing Democrats for years after years.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know what, what we are seeing --\par \par \uc2 WOLF: There will be unions that support her, and there will be unions that don't support her.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right, but there are a couple of very big unions that do not support her, that did not support anyone in this race. But I find what's more interesting, and we got to go, is what the workers at these plants think and feel. You know, it's not so much about what the bosses are saying and who they're endorsing as it is about the everyday folks in terms of what, who they're actually going to vote for.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Their wages are 22 percent. I'm with you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: From a very low point post-COVID. And many of them remember what they had in the last administration and are telling us when we ask them and go out and poll and listen to people that they feel like they were better off than. I don't know. None of us know how this is going to go. And I think it's so interesting that both campaigns are expressing so much caution. Former President Trump is very superstitious. He's like, I'm not talking about anything post-election. I want to wait and see what happens.\par \par \uc2 So, we thank you both, as always, for tuning in.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: He threw that salt right over his shoulder at \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. We'll go to that. Robert Wolf, Katie Pavlich, thank you. See you guys soon.\par \par \uc2 Let's actually pop in, Vice President Harris right now is making a stop at the 4th Street Deli in Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 Let's dip in here and hear what she has to say.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , U.S. VICE PRESIDENT, DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: And that we are going to, as we all say, turn the page on an era that was about trying to have people point fingers at each other, trying to suggest that there are these issues that divide us when the most fundamental important issues we care about unify us.\par \par \uc2 And so you all are putting that into action every moment of each day. You've been doing this work. We have 13 days to go and I am so deeply thankful to you. You are having an impact on, for the most part, people that you may never end up meeting because your work here will impact people around the country. Understand that.\par \par \uc2 You are having an impact on people you may never meet, people who may never know your name. But because of the time and the heart and the effort you are putting into this, you are going to forever impact people in a very important and positive way. And this is about the fact that we love our country, and we are prepared to fight for the best of who we are.\par \par \uc2 And so I thank you. I thank you. And in front of all the friends, I thank your mayor because this force, this force --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Okay. Vice President Harris is in Philadelphia. Obviously, there are a lot of votes in that Philadelphia area. That's why you have seen President Trump in Pennsylvania, I think about 17 or 19 times, I think, is the count. So, this is where it all may come down to in the state of Pennsylvania. And we're waiting to hear from President Trump this afternoon as well. He is in Georgia today, also a very important state. So, we'll take you there when that gets underway, giving time to both of these candidates as they bounce around the country.\par \par \uc2 And Clay Travis and Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  are still ahead this. Our so stick around for that and Lawrence Jones is coming up to talk to us a little bit about the research on the ground that he's been doing around the country, talking to particularly black male voters in different barbershops around the country and see how they feel about the media, what the media tells them to think, what the Democrats tell them to think, what they're hearing from Republicans when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Black voters and black men specifically are turning the corner saying, wait a minute, you know what? I was with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the 80s. I was with him in the 90s. I let these people influence me? These people trying to tell me what to do. One thing you don't tell a black man is what to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Vice President Harris and her surrogates making a push to shore up support among black voters. The latest Fox News poll shows Harris with a 38-point advantage over former President Trump. But that is down from the 83-point margin that President Biden won by in 2020. This drop around 24 points, depending on, you know, how you look at this, 24 points with black voters is an enormous, enormous move, the likes of which we do not see in politics from one race to the next.\par \par \uc2 Lawrence Jones has been talking to lots of voters across the country. And in this series, he has been speaking with black male voters in barbershops across the country. He went to Atlanta to hear why some are considering a vote for former President Trump watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So, what happened is the media got a hold to black men. That's exactly what happened, and they started to brainwash them. They started to believe the things that the media said, although they knew that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a businessman. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can bring this economy back, but they let the media influence them.\par \par \uc2 These people trying to tell me what to do? One thing you don't tell a black man is what to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Fox and Friends co-host Lawrence Jones joins me now. Do you sense, Lawrence -- I know we've talked about this the last couple days, that President \uc2 Obama\uc2  kind of scolding the brothers, as he put it, didn't go over that well?\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE JONES, FOX NEWS HOST: Well, you heard it from that brother right there. Yes. One thing you don't do is tell a black man on what to do, how to vote, how to live their life. You know, I've talked --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Most men, I find.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Most men. It was just disrespectful. But I think there was a certain condescension right there from the former president and it does have some weight because he was the first black president. And so to lecture us publicly that way just really -- it was kind of -- it was a little snarky, elitist and the voters have rejected that.\par \par \uc2 So, you see the franchise series when we go to the diners across America, all right, I've been talking about them. I've been going there, but then the barbershop is different, because you're not going to see the brothers going into the diners to have those type of conversations, but they do have them in the barbershop. Martha, they're saying the same things in different ways.\par \par \uc2 And I think when I was saying this two years ago, it should have been alarm bells for the Democratic Party. That's not a typical Republican constituency, a black barbershop with all the brothers. But what is happening right now is the economy continues to be the main issue.\par \par \uc2 Immigration is number two because it's disrespectful to see your community struggling and then the government not providing any assistance, not taking care of the schools there, but you see illegals overrunning those schools, you see them getting debit cards.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'm amazed when you look at the numbers of kids who can read and do math, I'm really -- it bothers me that education hasn't been a bigger focus in this election because I know that so many communities are struggling with where their kids are, are stacking up. They're never going to get anywhere if they can't read and write effectively.\par \par \uc2 But I want to play this from Spike Lee because he's upset that black men, in some numbers, are supporting former President Trump. Watch what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's sad, but true. Some of my brothers have been drinking that Kool-Aid.\par \par \uc2 And I just hope that they get their minds straight. I got to give credit to President \uc2 Obama\uc2  because he's been going really hard on the brothers. Some might think too hard, but I don't think so.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES: We ain't been drinking Kool-Aid because we can't afford Kool-Aid, okay? So, are you going to make up the difference? Do you plan on going to Black America and going to all the single parents and then the brothers that are taking care of their family and making up the deficit because they can't provide for their families? Are you going to go and put all the brothers' children in private schools, in charter schools, so they can get better education?\par \par \uc2 I mean, all the elites that are on media, and all the pundits that are talking about these issues in a way, when their families aren't affected, it's ringing on hollow ears right now because we gave this past administration a shot.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you say to those who say that it is sexist and racist that black men are not supporting this black woman? What's your answer to that?\par \par \uc2 JONES: My answer to that is that we're choosing our families. And it would be one thing if the numbers were different with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . They were losing support amongst black males under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as well.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And that points straight at the economy.\par \par \uc2 JONES: So, it's not a matter of the gender of the candidate. The candidate has still yet to address basic issues that matter to black men.\par \par \uc2 The plan that she proposed, imagine us saying that immigration, prices of food are main priorities for us, and then you put on weed on your policy proposal. We can't even afford weed. It's a luxury at this point.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And it's one that's not great for the country or for anybody's economic benefit. And I thought that was a very interesting move on her part to talk about legalizing marijuana in more states. So, that's a whole another story, which we are digging into actually about the impact on the country of the legalization. Lawrence, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Thank you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Can you go to all the pot shops after this?\par \par \uc2 JONES: That'll be another series after the election.\par \par \uc2 That's another series coming. Thank you, L.J. Good to see you in town. He's almost never in town. He's always all over the place. So, we thank him for that.\par \par \uc2 So, Iranian-American activist \uc2 Masih Alinejad\uc2  reacts to an alleged murder plot against her that would have been carried out on U.S. soil. The man behind this in the Iranian National Guard has now been charged with this crime.\par \par \uc2 They have identified who was behind this plot and we will talk to Masih in just a moment. That is the breaking news on that.\par \par \uc2 Also, Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  on the major escalation of Putin's war in Ukraine as North Korea is now sending and training 3,000 troops from North Korea to train in Russia. This has huge implications.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, WHITE HOUSE NATIONAL SECURITY COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR: Let's be clear. If North Korean soldiers do enter into combat, this development would demonstrate Russia's growing desperation in its war against Ukraine. Russia is suffering extraordinary casualties on the battlefield every single day, but President Putin appears intent on continuing this war.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: A top Iranian general and three other operatives facing murder for hire charges for an alleged plot to kill Iranian opposition activist \uc2 Masih Alinejad\uc2 . who lives here on U.S. soil. In their indictment, the feds point to this doorbell cam video from 2022 where a man showed up on her front porch with an AK-47 and they say that he was there, quote, intending to kill. Masih has been moved to 21 different safe houses, but has refused to remain silent. She is extraordinarily brave and she joins me now. Masih, good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 What is your reaction to the news that this general is now facing murder charges in the attempt against you?\par \par \uc2 MASIH ALINEJAD, IRANIAN-AMERICAN JOURNALIST: To be honest, I heard it from the beginning of I mean, from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  without going public. They told me that this time they charged the senior member of Revolutionary Guard. I screamed out of joy, Martha, because it is beautiful to see that they are being humiliated by the law enforcement of the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 They were sitting there in Iran, ordering a man with loaded gun in front of my house to kill me, the members of Revolutionary Guards, which is in charge of killing innocent women inside Iran, raping women for the crime of just showing their hair, sending money and weapons to \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  to kill and rape women on October 7 as well. So, that's the Revolutionary Guards we are talking. So, of course, I am celebrating their humiliation, but at the same time, this is just the beginning, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, I wonder what you think about the you know, the hit list that Iran has besides you going after President Trump and others, other generals, including \uc2 John Bolton\uc2 , \uc2 Mark Milley\uc2 , others on that list? Secretary Pompeo has had security for several years because of this. And this headline in Politico, Iran has a hit list of former Trump aides. The U.S. is scrambling to protect them. Do you think they can be successful with all of those targets?\par \par \uc2 ALINEJAD: First of all, I have to say that's like a scary movie, a foreign government trying to kill the U.S. officials. That should not be acceptable in the first place. The reason that the regime being emboldened to target the U.S. officials and U.S. citizens on U.S. soil is because of the appeasement, because of the lack of actions, I have to actually now talk directly to the future president of America, because I heard from \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and Biden's administration trying to get a deal with this government, to negotiate with them. These are the negotiators of terror. They don't understand language of diplomacy. Their language is only hostage taking, killing, torturing, assassinating.\par \par \uc2 And President Trump actually said that he doesn't look for regime change in Iran. But I have to say, we look for regime change. We want to end this regime. That's how we will face stability in the region and across the globe. So, whether they get successful or not depends to the reaction of the democratic countries.\par \par \uc2 So far, let me tell you something, Martha, I am the only woman in America being the target of Islamic Republic. But eight people were charged by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALINEJAD: Eight people, four of them are in prison, four members of Revolutionary Guards being charged yesterday. No single female politicians from my adopted country, America, condemn this act of terrorism.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That is incredible. No, it's incredible that it doesn't get more attention because these are opportunities to speak out against this regime, which is attacking our own men and women abroad on military bases, and we've seen what they have been doing to Israel. Do you think that -- would you like to see the White House more forceful in terms of encouraging Israel to take out those nuclear sites in Iran?\par \par \uc2 ALINEJAD: You know, whether we like it or not, millions of people in Iran who do not want war, but they believe that this is the war of the Islamic Republic and they celebrate when every single high ranking members of Revolutionary Guards, when the leader of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , when the leader of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  were eliminated. This is the fact, you cannot hide it.\par \par \uc2 And I want to talk here directly to Americans, when \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2  was killed, what was the reaction of Americans? Celebrating, because they believe that getting rid of a terrorist is a sign of justice, because 3,000 innocent Americans were killed in September 11. But what is different between Iranian people than the people of America, when we see an elimination of terrorists like a sign of victory, then people here say that I am \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , I am \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and supporting, unfortunately, \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and the Islamic Republic on university and college campuses, that is not going to save the lives of Palestinians.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALINEJAD: That is not going to save the life of Iranians. If we want to free Palestine, we have to free Palestine and Iran from Islamic Republic --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right.\par \par \uc2 ALINEJAD: -- \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: In these terrorist groups and regimes that are running it and have taken it over from the people of these countries.\par \par \uc2 Masih, thank you very much. I know you have been trying to find a place to live. It's hard to find a place to live when you have --\par \par \uc2 ALINEJAD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- around the clock security and a threat against you. It's not always something that the neighbors want to have near them. And I really pray for you and your family, and I thank you for being here. Masih, thank you.\par \par \uc2 ALINEJAD: Appreciate that, Martha, thank you so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So breaking today, the Biden administration says that at least 3,000 North Korean troops are now in eastern Russia to train for potential combat in Ukraine. Earlier today, Jackie Heinrich pressed John Kirby on whether the administration is doing enough to deter Putin's war.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACKIE HEINRICH, FOX NEWS SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Why not green light the long range missiles for Ukraine's use, which is Zelenskyy's number one ask, as you're sounding the alarm about what could have far reaching implications if North Korean soldiers go into Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, W.H. NATIONAL SECURITY COMMS ADVISOR: Well, for one thing, Jackie, we don't exactly know what these guys are going to do.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: What else could they be there for?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: We don't know what they're going to do. We don't know if they're going to deploy into combat or not. We don't know if they do and what strength.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Let's bring in Republican South Carolina Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , who traveled to Ukraine in August.\par \par \uc2 Senator Graham, good to have you here. This is a disturbing development that North Korea is sending 3,000 soldiers to train in Russia for the Ukraine war. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): Yes. Well, I think they're not tourists. I think this is just another sign of how dangerous the world is under Biden- Harris. There's no deterrence. Deterrence has been lost everywhere. So North Carolina -- North Korea feels comfortable joining Russia trying to destroy Ukraine, says all you need to know about lack of deterrence. When Trump wins, this is going to come to end pretty quickly.\par \par \uc2 I'm in Hamtramck, Michigan, I think I got it right, with Amer, the mayor, is a Muslim majority town in Michigan. I've been with Arab Americans, Christian Chaldeans from Iraq, they're on fire to see their homelands liberated. The biggest Satan in the planet right now is not Russia, it's Iran. Iran, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , \uc2 Hamas\uc2  the Houthis in Yemen. I told everybody here, if you want your homelands to live in peace and prosperity, we need to elect Trump to stand down Iran. And the day Trump wins, Russia is going to be less likely to recruit North Koreans to fight in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 So, if you want to get the world back in order, is a pretty easy decision. Harris is going to continue what you see, and Trump is going to stop it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let me ask you about this story that made headlines today about generals Kelly, Mattis and Milley suggesting --\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- that President Trump, that the behavior they saw was fascist and that he is a danger to the country.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you say about it? And what do you say about their timing?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Number one, I appreciate their service to our country. I know General Kelly lost his son. I couldn't disagree more. I'm tired of the Hitler crap when it comes to Trump. The one and only Jewish state on the planet is Israel.\par \par \uc2 Nobody is more popular in Israel than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He recognized Jerusalem as the legitimate capital of the state of Israel. He recognized the Golan Heights as part of Israel. There has been no better friend to the Jewish state. Hitler wanted to kill all the Jews, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the best friend of the Jewish state.\par \par \uc2 When President Trump was in office, we didn't invade other countries. We didn't try to destroy people. We didn't create concentration camps. We had the strongest military, and we had no wars.\par \par \uc2 To these generals, you're entitled to your opinion, but I think you're dead wrong. I think you're completely wrong. If you vote for Harris, you're voting for continued chaos. So at the end of the day, these generals have every right to speak their mind. But here's what I would say, how about a little self-reflection? For 20 years, we gave hundreds of billions of dollars for you and others to train the Iraqi and Afghan army to stand up to the bad guys, and they folded like a cheap suit.\par \par \uc2 So I've known President Trump up close and personal. I reject the characterizations made by these generals as to who he is and what he believes. And I yearn for peace and stability. During his presidency, Israel had no better friend. The world was at peace.\par \par \uc2 The Arabs were making --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: -- peace with Israelis.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: All of that's been lost. To these generals, you don't know what you're talking about, in my view, when it comes to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his record.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, Senator Graham. Good to have you with us in Michigan today. And this story is getting a lot of attention. Good to have your viewpoint on it. I believe you're in the hometown of Kat Timpf, by the way.\par \par \uc2 So, we will see you on the campaign trail. Thank you very much. Thanks, Senator.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So Clay Travis and \uc2 Tim Murtaugh\uc2  from the Trump campaign are still ahead, but we also are waiting to hear from former President Trump. He will be in Georgia in just a few moments. We'll take you there as well, coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Basically, the four states that are most likely to determine this outcome are all within a point, and when we span this out to the Electoral College, what do we get? We get exactly this, Harris with that 276 electoral votes, because she carries these great lake battleground states, right? But let's just say we flip Pennsylvania and the polls don't match the result exactly right, what happens? We flip Pennsylvania to red, and then we get Trump to 281 electoral votes, which is not really much of a long shot.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. So that is the puzzle board game that's going on everywhere right now. One state, it could be that close with 13 days left. So let's take the temperature of the Trump campaign this hour. We want to note that we invited both campaigns for a discussion back and forth between the two sides on the story. We hope that that will happen sometime before the election. And we're glad to have with us today Trump campaign Senior Advisor \uc2 Tim Murtaugh\uc2  joining me now.\par \par \uc2 Tim, good to have you with us. We got a couple new polls. I know you guys are, you know, grinding through these polls like we are. You got your internals and what you're seeing out there. This is just -- these came out at 2:30 this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 So this shows Michigan, Harris ahead by four. And Wisconsin, this shows her -- shows them tied in Wisconsin. And we showed yesterday the Georgia new polling that showed plus four for Trump in Georgia. Your reaction to these polls, and what are you seeing?\par \par \uc2 TIM MURTAUGH\uc2 , TRUMP CAMPAIGN SENIOR ADVISER: Well, first, Martha, thank you very much for having me on, and I'm sorry that my counterpart, Ian Sams, couldn't be here. There must have been a sale on soy products at \uc2 Whole Foods\uc2  today, or something.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. OK.\par \par \uc2 MURTAUGH: Talking about the polling, you know, if you look at the Real Clear Politics polling average, it shows \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  with the lead in all seven of those battleground states that everyone is talking about. And the only polls that we really trust and rely on are the ones that we look for ourselves, the ones that we pay for ourselves, because we don't pay for polls to get lied to. We want to know what's really happening, and we show ourselves in our own internal numbers that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is leading all seven battleground states. And we believe that the Harris campaign sees that too. If you can tell by the way they're acting, they're flailing around.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is closing on a positive message that he's going to fix the country's problems, and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is closing on a message that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is bad. So I think you can see the two different directions that the campaigns are going. We feel very good about where we are.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. It's interesting to watch where everybody is spending time too, as well. What are your -- what are your internal polls show in Michigan?\par \par \uc2 MURTAUGH: Michigan is one of the seven battleground states in our internal polls. We released them not too long ago on a Friday. And Michigan is among those seven battleground states where we show internally that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has a lead. And it's because this election is --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: By how much is my question.\par \par \uc2 MURTAUGH: -- always set up.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. How much in your poll?\par \par \uc2 MURTAUGH: Oh, by a couple of points.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: By a couple of points.\par \par \uc2 MURTAUGH: By a couple of points.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK.\par \par \uc2 MURTAUGH: And this elect -- this election has always set up very well, because people have lived through both administrations. They know what the economy was like under President Trump. It was great. The border was secure. The world was at peace.\par \par \uc2 Under \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , the economy is in shambles. Inflation is through the roof. The border has been effectively erased, and the world is in chaos. We just saw the previous report about North Korea sending soldiers to help train in Russia to enter the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, that's (inaudible), isn't it?\par \par \uc2 MURTAUGH: It really is.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. So, let me ask you about this, this is David Plouffe, who is Harris's campaign senior adviser, talking about the undecided vote. I want to get your reaction to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID PLOUFFE, HARRIS CAMPAIGN SR. ADVISER: This race has basically been 48-48 percent, 48-47 percent in those states for some times. When you say, OK, how are those undecided voters likely to break? Who's got an advantage on turnout, in terms of, you know, various turnout scenarios?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Right.\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: We like our position more than Trump.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, it's hard to understand who is still undecided out there, Tim, but what would you say to Trump -- to Plouffe on up?\par \par \uc2 MURTAUGH: Well, I would say that that's just talk, and anybody can say that from any campaign. But I would look at a tangible evidence of what's happening in what they call the blue wall states, Pennsylvania, Michigan and Wisconsin. In those states, three Democrat Senate candidates, two of them incumbents, got Bob Casey in Pennsylvania, \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2  in Michigan, who is running for election for the first time in the Senate, and then \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2  in Wisconsin. All three of them are running television ads right now in which they speak positively about how they work with President Trump --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We've show those ads.\par \par \uc2 MURTAUGH: -- in the past.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That is quite remarkable.\par \par \uc2 MURTAUGH: That is evidence.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It tells you quite a bit. I only got a couple seconds here, but you're concerned about a few counties in North Carolina given the hurricane. Real quick, if you can, Tim.\par \par \uc2 MURTAUGH: Yes. No, look, North Carolina set some records in early voting the other day when it opened up. And that's very encouraging for President Trump. It's going well. But the three counties, Buncombe County, especially in the rural parts, this is where Asheville is, but in the rural part, Buncombe County, Henderson County and Maxwell County, they need to do more to set up early voting sites. And if you're in North Carolina affected by the hurricane, hurricane relief.gop is where you should go to find out how to vote if you've been affected by the hurricane.\par \par \uc2 Buncombe, Henderson and Mitchell counties need to do more to help people be able to vote. It's very important.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, we appreciate you coming on, and those people have been through a lot, and we hope that they have access to voting, as every American should across this country. \uc2 Tim Murtaugh\uc2 , thank you. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MURTAUGH: Martha, good to see you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So this might not go over so well at the Harris campaign. Listen to President Biden talking about former President Trump yesterday in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I know this sounds bizarre, it sounds like I said this five years ago, you locked me up. We got to lock him up. Politically lock him up. Lock him out. That's what we have to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. That's kind of like the moment he put on the Trump hat.\par \par \uc2 One former Biden official told an Axios reporter, we need to lock Joe up. And a current Biden official said, no one is 