ajority, very slight majority, right now in the House of Representatives, so any moves on this could really make a big difference. One of the closest races that we will be watching very closely on election night is New York 17. This is where Republican incumbent Mike Lawler is up against former Congressman \uc2 Mondaire Jones\uc2 . The race shifting from a toss-up now to lean Republican in our latest power rankings.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is that New York Congressman Mike Lawler.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, always good to have you with us. There -- you were just telling me there's 80,000 more Democrats in your district --\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE LAWLER (R-NY): Yes. And it's home --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- than Republicans.\par \par \uc2 LAWLER: Yes, and it's home to the Clintons, \uc2 George Soros\uc2 , so by no means a Republican bastion.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: How are you ahead?\par \par \uc2 LAWLER: We do the job. I've been rated the fourth most bipartisan member of Congress. Mondaire Jones rated 381st. I've passed 10 bills through the House, five of which got signed into law by President Biden. He passed one to rename a post office.\par \par \uc2 He supported defunding the police, cashless bail. Called cops racist and white supremacist. Fifty percent of households in my district have a cop, a firefighter or a veteran living in the household. They don't take too kindly to those claims.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, he says that you voted to cut funding for police three times.\par \par \uc2 LAWLER: It's false. The -- even "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " said it's false. He's talking about an earmark from former Senator \uc2 Richard Shelby\uc2  for $600 million in Alabama that was removed from an appropriations bill. I have fought to support law enforcement. I fought to repeal New York's disastrous cashless bail law.\par \par \uc2 He supports it. I have fought to ensure that cop killers remain in prison without the possibility of parole, partner clemency