OF THE U.S., (D) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is an extremely accomplished, experienced and capable in every way that anyone would want if they're president.\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: You never saw anything like what happened at the debate night behind closed doors with him?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: It was a bad debate. People have bad debates. He is absolutely --\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: But that's the reason why you're here and he's not running for the top of the ticket.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Well, you'd have to ask him if that's the only reason why.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: What do you think?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I'm running for President of the United States. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is not.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: It's a judgment question, that's why I asked, can the American people trust you in these moments, even when it's maybe uncomfortable for Americans to have to level with Americans in that way.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I speak with not only sincerity, but with a real first-hand account of watching him do this work. I have no reluctance in saying that. No, of course, I don't.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: She's so indignant. Every time she's asked a logical question, you're questioning \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's credibility. You're claiming he's exhausted, and then you're making that ridiculous point about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . None of it adds up, and that took her all day to prepare for. No wonder she's losing support, even among traditionally Democrat voters, I may add, which brings us to, He Se Puede. That's the focus of tonight's Angle.\par \par \uc2 Doesn't it seem like yesterday, Chicago, the DNC, all that joy, all that excitement.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EVA LONGORIA, ACTRESS: In our community, we have a saying, Si Se Puede, which means yes. It means yes, we can. But tonight I'm here to tell you yes, she can. So, we're going to say She Se Puede. When somebody says, is she qualified for the job? We're going to say, She Se Puede. When somebody asks, is she going to be the first female President of the United States? We're going to say, She Se Puede. She Se Puede, She Se Puede.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But there is growing evidence tonight that for Kamala and the Hispanic vote, it's No Se Puede. A new USA Today Suffolk poll shows that Latino voters now prefer Trump over Harris by on se puntos, 11 points. But how can this be? Because the D.C. establishment, for years, had warned conservatives that their tough stance on border enforcement, ending birthright citizenship, that that would destroy its chances with America's fastest growing demographic group, Hispanics.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O'DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: No one has come up with a math, mathematical route to the presidency that allows you to have that kind of negative number with Latino voters.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And somebody is doing is actively repelling those people, actively making those people not want to vote for him.\par \par \uc2 REP. VERONICA ESCOBAR (D-TX): I have been talking to communities across the country for the campaign, especially Latino communities. I have reminded them about what we experienced for four years under Trump. We know that that Trump and the GOP has abandoned Latino voters.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That could be my favorite montage of the week, and we've had a lot of good montages. All right, but it turns out, all those years ago, Trump and a few of us were 100 percent correct, and the D.C. swamp, the Chamber of Commerce crowd, all the media types, they were wrong. Hispanic voters, we insisted, wanted a strong economy. Hispanic voters wanted good schools. They wanted safe neighborhoods. They believed in family values, and we said that that mass amnesty argument and open borders would hurt legal immigrants and poor people the most.\par \par \uc2 Well, fast