RUMP: Nice and full.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, this was pure genius. I loved it, and as such, it's just driving the Left and the media totally bonkers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There's no logic to it. It's a stunt. He's engaged in some really bizarre types of activities during this campaign. So this is just another one of those stunts.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Staging stunts at \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  in a desperate attempt to get people to pay attention to his lie that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  never worked there.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER RUBIN, MSNBC COMMENTATOR: He is so inept at pretending to be a real person that he really, literally cannot operate the fry machine as normal worker. This just shows what an ultimate fraud he is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Jennifer Rubin would rather have the McDonald's be in Ukraine and then should be really happy. Well, they all wear bitter well, don't they? Newsweek questioned if Trump's McDonald's event was staged, and then they whined that he didn't work a full shift. Are they kidding me? Do they think we thought he was going to like, sign up and work their full? First, nothing is stopping Kamala from doing something just like this.\par \par \uc2 After all, she worked at \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 , right? So, no trainings necessary. She already knows the fry machine. I know the fry machine. I worked at \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 , and I can prove it. But then again, when her campaign tries to capture her in these unofficial settings, the word genuine is not how what I'd use to describe how she comes across.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE U.S., (D) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Dougie, there they are. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I know you want those. What do you get?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: These are -- this is my go-to, the original nacho cheese.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: How was debate (inaudible)?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Well, haven't had it today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, the truth is, as the election draws to a close, more Americans are seeing who the real champion of the working people is here. And in fact, this isn't the first time Trump has shown appreciation for the working people.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, I'm going to try doing your job. It's not going to be easy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Don't do it too well.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I'll help you with those bags. Look at this, right? You didn't expect this, right?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I did not expect this.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Take your dog, you go outside, it's freezing, and you walk around for a while, and it's not that easy. OK, be careful. Room service, look at this. This is serious room service. I hope you give me a nice, big fat tip. I like this job, Mia.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You should come help me off the side.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, can you picture Kamala doing that. Of course, she's too busy raising a billion dollars from Wall Street and Silicon Valley. According to \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , her campaign and party committees raised a staggering $378 million in September alone, more than double what Trump raised. And while Democrats like to obsess about \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  support for Trump, he's given away a million dollars at events, the sheer number of billionaires and the mega rich supporting Harris is very significant.\par \par \uc2 Most donating to the pro-Harris Super PAC Future Forward are secret. Most of the donors, you don't know who they are. It's called Future Forward. But the ones we do know, according to the Times, include \uc2 Facebook\uc2  co-founder \uc2 Dustin Moskovitz\uc2 , crypto billionaire Chris Larsen and Illinois Governor \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2 , and then this was interesting. There is an undisclosed donor, the biggest donor of all, from another dark money group.\par \par \uc2 This all flies in the face of Harris's smears that Trump only cares about the powerful, she is with the little guy, it's a total lie. She is the candidate of the status quo in the establishment, they do not believe that Trump is the candidate of the billionaires, because they have most of the billionaires and all of their smears, all of the other ones. I really don't believe they believe any of it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There's a presidential nominee running a bizarre, haphazard, R Rated campaign these days, vulgar comments, crude insults.\par \par \uc2 LIZ CHENEY\uc2 , FORMER U.S. REPRESENTATIVE: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  represents is, is, in many ways, just cruel and not the kind of dignity and the kind of person that that we all want to be able to look up to.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Dignity? Did \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  say dignity? Now the party that she's now aligned with is the party of pornography, the party of pot, the party of sex workers and trans athletes and women's sports. Now they're all afraid that Trump's language is too coarse or too crude. Of course not. It's another lie. I don't remember any of them being too upset a few weeks ago when we learned that Biden had a very vulgar nickname that he likes to use for President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Now, as you'll see in a moment, there are promising signs that Americans, they're just not buying the Democrats game of smear and fear. None of it really adds up. It's kind of like the entire Harris-Walz campaign. It's fitting that Trump did his deal at \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's over the weekend, because they used to have this jingle, you deserve a break today. Well, the only way working people will get one is if he wins two weeks from tomorrow, and that's the Angle.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Victor Davis Hanson, Senior Fellow at the \uc2 Hoover Institution\uc2 . Victor, what about Trump resonates with the working class voters that was encapsulated really with that time that he spent at \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's?\par \par \uc2 VICTOR DAVIS HANSON, SENIOR FELLOW, HOOVER INSTITUTION: Well, it's authenticity, it's his issues, his agenda. They say consistent. He didn't change them to the same thing, secure border, predictable, deterrence abroad, fight crime, promote fossil fuels. It's consistent, and everybody knows it's consistent. It's aimed at a middle class. And then there's the person Trump. He wears the same type of clothes, whether he's in the inner city or whether he's out on the campaign trail or he's up in Trump Tower. He has the same Bronx accent. He doesn't have a Jamaican accent, a black accent, Hispanic accent, depending on the crowd he's addressing, like \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He didn't switch over and modify his positions in an antithetical way just to get elected, and that's in contrast with her. The reason we know that he's authentic and she's not is because the last 90 days, she's refuted, Laura, all of her lifetime agendas and has embraced things that are eerily similar to Trump. And the same thing is true of these weird, purple state Democratic candidates for the Senate, these incumbents, they are now pigging back on the Trump agenda, but he's been consistent. They're the ones that are changing, and she's the one that's changing.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Victor, it's also -- it's also -- sorry to interrupt, but it's also sort of a -- there is a sensibility, though, is there not that Trump has because he's been on factory floors, he's been on work sites his whole life. This has been who he's been really for since he was a kid. So, he's very comfortable in that milieu where \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  always seems, I'm sorry, but she comes across as a total phony in those situations. We just played that Wawa video where he -- she was with her husband picking up Doritos. I mean, it just all seems so -- it just seems stupid. Forget anything else.\par \par \uc2 HANSON: Yes, well, that's because she's been in government and among elite, the elites her entire life. She's a child of two PhDs, and she just went straight into the political world, and has never come out of it. He's a builder, so he has to know what cement workers do, what electricians do, what plumbers do. And he likes the people, because they build his things, and he feels one with them, and they understand that, and they like him. And he doesn't, he doesn't, you know from his entire career, whether it was boxing or wrestling or kind of dubious people that other people would shun. He didn't. He always judged things on the merits, and he wasn't ashamed to say, you know what, I know this guy, I like him, or I'm a friend of this guy.\par \par \uc2 But other people were, you know, they would locate people by their class, their credentials or their degrees. He was never part of that elite. He was wealthy and he was privileged, but he was not an elite in the traditional sense that they see it out.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, Victor, I want to change to foreign policy for a moment, because \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  today decided to make this point about, well, the engagement and foreign affairs, and she was sitting next to \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  for most of the day talking about this. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: This concept of isolation. We were once there as a nation, and then Pearl Harbor happened. We have to remember history that isolationism, which is exactly what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is pushing, pull out a \uc2 NATO\uc2 . Abandon our friends. Isolationism is not insulation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Victor, so Ukraine is like Pearl Harbor. That's kind of what we were getting from, from that.\par \par \uc2 HANSON: Yes, well, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  left his borders and invaded in three of the last four administrations. He didn't do it in \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's administration, and as far as \uc2 NATO\uc2 , \uc2 NATO\uc2  finally was forced to pony up $100 billion in defense agendas and expenditures, and that came from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He didn't get out of \uc2 NATO\uc2 . He wanted to support it. He's a Jacksonian. No better enemy, no better friend, no worse enemy. And he did more to harm \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , whether it was get out of an asymmetrical missile deal or kill the \uc2 Wagner group\uc2  people, than any prior administration.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, the Pearl Harbor -- when you have to go to Pearl Harbor, when you're trying -- when you're sitting next to \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , I don't see how that's going to get all those voters that you need to come on your side. Doesn't make any sense. Victor, always great to have you on. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 All right, pot and abortion on the ballot. So, how can Republicans counter this? State initiatives are being left out of the debate and the conversation, but not on the Angle. Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is here next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: If you're interested in smoking all that high THC weed that's causing psychosis and schizophrenia, bipolar disorder, and if you lose sleep at the thought that third trimester abortions aren't happening, then leftists in Florida have a few ballot initiatives just for you. Amendments 3and 4 are on the ballot November 5, and Amendment 3 legalizes recreational marijuana because that's worked out so well in other areas of the country, and Amendment 4 makes abortion a constitutional right, effectively making it impossible to add any regulations of the procedure at all.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . Governor, wonderful as always, to see you tonight. Now, just as we're learning about the severe mental and physical effects of marijuana use, big money poured into your state for this Amendment 3. What can you tell us tonight?\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): Well, Amendment 3 is being bank rolled by one big weed company that's traded on the Canadian stock exchange. They have poured over $100 million into passing this Amendment 3. They're not doing that because they care about the best interests of Florida. They're doing it because they want to make profits. And people say it's the legalization of recreational weed. That's partially true. They're saying you have a right to possess and smoke it, but only if you buy it from them, because they're actually setting up a cartel in the constitution of Florida. So, if you want to grow your own marijuana, that is not in the amendment.\par \par \uc2 They also have nothing in here that would prevent, that would allow any type of limitation on public use. So, you look at places like Colorado, people see weed everywhere. Colorado actually has prohibitions against using it in public. Here, there is nothing in there. They're saying you have a right to use it with no limitations. So whatever negative has happened in Colorado, I think this particular amendment, the way it's written, I think the problems would be much more significant in Florida as a result of amendment three.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So everyone has to vote no on that. I've been on this from day one, and \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , of all places, just published a piece in the last few weeks about the dangers of marijuana use. As America's marijuana use grows, so do the harms. The accumulating harm is broader and more severe than previously reported again and again, \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  found dangerous misconceptions. Many users believe, for instance, that people cannot become addicted to cannabis, but millions do. It went on to talk about those over the age from 18 to 25, the most damage done to them Governor in terms of mental effects of marijuana use because of the high THC levels. I mean, this is just a disaster. It's the last thing our young people need, or frankly, any Americans need, given the experience in other states.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Well, exactly. Laura, I mean, when Colorado did it, it was kind of an issue of first impression. They claimed it would be no black market, big tax revenue, safe and effective, and none of that happened. So now Florida is looking at those results, and it's not just the 18 to 21, there's actually a great commercial running now against Amendment 3 with an ER doctor from California. What she says is she sees very young kids coming in because they package it like candy. So you think it's sour patch kids, but it's really marijuana.\par \par \uc2 So, the experiment has failed. This is not something that's appropriate to put into a constitution. And I think you're right that the no vote on Amendment 3 is what's in the best interest of the state of Florida. We don't want to become California through the back door --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, my God. Or New York.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: -- right some, some, some very, you know, deceptive amendment summary. You don't even put everything that's going to be in it when it's on the ballot. Voters don't get the full picture, and then you sink $100 million in and wallah, you get to ensconce yourself in the constitution of a state as this big weed cartel. That is bad government, even if you like marijuana. This is not the appropriate vehicle for it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And Governor, the issue of abortion, obviously, it's pretty much all \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has to run on with no record or disastrous record, and so the Left wants to constitutionalize the right to abortion, try to nationalize the issue. How do you think this is going to play out?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Well, as people find out about how this Amendment 4 is written, the support goes down dramatically. You're seeing that. It is basically a proposal of abortion all the way up until birth for virtually any reason, it eliminates parental consent for minors. So in Florida, if you want to prescribe a minor Tylenol, you got to get a parent consent, but somehow they're going to take abortion out of that, and a parent will not be able to have the decision about their own child.\par \par \uc2 But then, something that's probably more radical than even what California has done, they replace the requirement that a physician be the one performing an abortion with the term health care provider. Well, Laura, as you and I know, a health care provider is not the same as a licensed physician, so you will actually have very dangerous situations where non licensed physicians are greenlighting and performing late term abortions. This is an easy no. It should not be in the state constitution, and it would have really negative impacts in the state of Florida.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Florida has got to do the right thing. No on Amendments 3 and 4. Governor, thank you for your leadership on this and during the hurricane as well. We appreciate you. All right, another slap in the face to Christians, courtesy of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, is this becoming Kamala joy out of luck club, as we've seen with how they treat conservative African Americans from \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  to Ben Carson, the most frightening thing to Democrat Party officials are minorities who think for themselves.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  still leads among black voters, but it's the trend that is so worrisome, right? You go back to Obama in 2012, he won him by 90. Then \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , 85. Biden, 81. Look at what \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is today. She's down 15 points from where, from where Biden was just four years ago, and she's down, get this, 24 points from where Obama was 12 years ago.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  knows she struggling with black men, because black men are struggling to make ends meet in her lousy, wretched economy. Of course, rather than address their concerns, she ignores them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: There is this narrative about what kind of support we are receiving from black men that is just not panning out in reality, in terms of when I go to last night, Atlanta and had, I think 10,000 people at a rally.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, denial a river in Egypt, but it's not a campaign strategy. But apparently, they're already laying the groundwork to explain a Harris loss on election night. Blame white people.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: What I think is a mistake is to let white folks escape the accountability that they must face for not showing up to save democracy themselves. It's time for white folks to turn their ire and their attention to each other to ensure they also do their part.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Joining me now, Florida Congressman Byron Donalds. Congressman, now first, Obama blames sexism, now media types are blaming white folks. So, I guess they think, what, they can shame people into voting for her?\par \par \uc2 REP. BYRON DONALDS (R-FL): They're going to try to shame people, but they should be ashamed of themselves. The \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  record has been a disaster. Everybody knows that. Immigration, our economy, foreign policy. I'll give you another one that is resonating when you talk to voters around the country. This radical transgender movement that's taking puberty from children and hiding it from parents. That radical stuff, putting men in women's sports. These radical ideas from \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and the radical left. That's what they should be ashamed of.\par \par \uc2 But unfortunately for them, the American people look like they're going to elect \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  because he has a recipe for our country to be successful. So, they can blame whoever they want, but they have to blame themselves first.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Congressman, this is what John King found during a trip. This is what he claims he found during a trip to a Detroit auto factory. Check this out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: On the factory floor, they say there's more racism and sexism now. Before it was just Biden's too old. Now the workers who support Harris say they're hearing more racism and more sexism.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, that's the narrative they're using to explain the lack of support for Harris among working class people, racism and sexism.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: No, it's just a joke. And the media, they're the number one culprits in all of this. Because for the first 40 to 50 days of her campaign, instead of holding her accountable like you would any other presidential candidate, they treated her like she was a Hollywood actress. We all remember when she wasn't saying anything to anybody and they thought it was the greatest thing ever.\par \par \uc2 Now that the American people's skepticism has turned to rejection, this is where the media goes. But the media, like the Democrats, are the reason why nobody trusts them. Because instead of looking at the reality on the ground with wide open borders and massive increases in prices for every good and service, what do they do? They want to blame shift, they want to deflect. But hey, that's fine by me. That means \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is losing.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah, how are they going to explain the Latino shift in the new USA Today Suffolk poll? It's astounding. Congressman, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 All right, on the same day Kamala skipped that Al Smith dinner, she held a rally where she sent a clear message to Christians. You're not welcome here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: That they would undo the protections of Roe v. Wade, and they did as he intended.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Christ is King. Jesus is Lord.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Oh, you guys are at the wrong rally.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's not the first time Democrats have insulted Christians, though.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: A lot of small towns in the Midwest, the jobs have been gone now for 25 years and nothing replaced them. It's not surprising that they get bitter, they cling to guns or religion or antipathy to people who aren't like them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: There's outrage over Speaker Mike Johnson's faith too.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: In his first speech as speaker, he said, God ordained us to be here. So, separation of church and state out the window with this guy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now don't forget Biden's declaration that Easter Sunday this year would be known as Transgender Day of Visibility. That was cute. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  mocking the Eucharist.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Love you. I do need you. No matter what I do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Christian problem? Joining me now, Grant Beth and Luke Polaske, who both juniors at the \uc2 University of Wisconsin\uc2  at La Crosse, who say they were thrown out of the rally after that exchange with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, Grant, what was your -- what did you go through your mind when you heard the vice president address the two of you as she did?\par \par \uc2 GRANT BETH, UNIVERSITY OF WISCONSIN-LA CROSSE STUDENT: Honestly, my message was heard in that moment. So, I wasn't really thinking about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Obviously, she told us to leave a rally just for talking about our faith and saying Christ is King, Jesus is Lord. We were mocked not only by \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , but by her patrons as well.\par \par \uc2 I think it's important to understand that, you know, we were cursed at. I was assaulted at the rally. And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  doesn't care about Christians in this country and that is why we have this platform here today.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Wait, you were assaulted? Tell me about that.\par \par \uc2 BETH: I was assaulted. I was assaulted by a woman. You can see in that video on your right there, I was assaulted by a woman probably in her 70's or 80's. She pushed me in the back. I continued to walk and preach that Jesus is Lord and Christ is King. And that's about it. I continued to speak for my lord and --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, she pushed you. I mean, Luke, you know, you look like a pretty fit guy like a seven-year-old. I mean, I -- she put -- I mean, she assaulted you. She pushed you. It sounded like she was like typical, you know, typical, you know older feminist response to these things.\par \par \uc2 But Luke, until recently, I know you were an -- is that true you were an undecided voter, is that right?\par \par \uc2 LUKE POLASKE, UNIVERSITY OF WISCONSIN-LA CROSSE STUDENT: That's correct.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, what changed your mind and what are you doing now?\par \par \uc2 POLASKE: So, for a little backstory, I personally went to the Trump Town Hall for undecided voters here about six weeks ago. I had some really good conversations with Trump supporters on why he deserves my vote. That was pretty much our purpose of going to this rally too, for me personally. We had some good conversations with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  supporters on why we should vote for her and why we should not vote for Trump.\par \par \uc2 But there was a lot of good conversation, but there was some bad as well. I remember a man getting in our face, spitting out, like damn near spitting on us. But when she talked about abortion, something went through my mind that, as a Christian, it's just not right. And that's when I decided to speak up and say, Jesus is Lord.\par \par \uc2 And after her response, it's clear she doesn't deserve my vote and she doesn't care about Christianity.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, to both of you, do you think this is, Grant, a trend among Democrats that just kind of disdain for Christians, certainly vocal Christians? I mean, if you're a quiet Christian and you never speak up, then I guess you're OK. But if you're a vocal Christian and you evangelize wherever you are, do you feel like you're looked down upon?\par \par \uc2 BETH: You know, I don't feel like I'm looked down upon at all, because I only answer to one person and that's my Lord and Savior. But I do feel like this is a trend not only in politics but around this country. It's been a trend over the last probably, you know, my whole entire life. And I do feel like it will continue with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Christians make up a third of this world, and probably 60 -- 50 to 60 percent of this country. And it's a very dangerous thing when a presidential candidate says public things against a religion that it makes no sense, and it's very disheartening as, you know, a young American and someone who believes in God.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, and free speech. Your freedom to express --\par \par \uc2 BETH: Absolutely\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- what you believe. And if you had come out and screamed out that you were, you know, transitioning, you would have gotten a standing ovation by the crowd. But you know, you said what you said, and that was not acceptable. It's not the first time Jesus has heard something like that though. Not the first time someone rejected him.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both for sharing your stories tonight. We appreciate it. All right, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  seems to be surging two weeks before this election. Is this momentum real? Is it firmly on his side? What do we believe at this point? Coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Can you believe two weeks from tomorrow, election night. But MAGA right now has the momentum. Trump is now the favorite to win with five major election forecasters. And the RCP Average has Trump leading in all seven of the top battleground states.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Doug Schoen, Democratic pollster and strategist. \uc2 Ari Fleischer\uc2 , former White House press secretary, Fox News contributor. Doug, let's start with you.\par \par \uc2 So, the Economist, the Hill, 538, Nate Silver, could rattle up a few others, they seem to be shifting. I mean, both in terms of numbers, and then you can feel the kind of -- you can feel the change, the intangibles out there on the trail.\par \par \uc2 But the accuracy of this polling, the prevalence of Republican leaning polls in recent weeks is something that's being raised. What are your thoughts on that?\par \par \uc2 DOUG SCHOEN, DEMOCRATIC POLLSTER AND STRATEGIST: Well, as I see it, Laura, I've looked at every poll and every prediction. Every indicator is moving in the same direction. So even if you took out the so-called Republican- leading polls, which were not that accurate in 2020, but in the past have been more accurate than a lot of the nonpartisan or Democrat-leading polls, but even if you take out the Republican polls, all the momentum in the national polling, and as you say, the swing state polling is to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The issues work for him, immigration and the economy, and there's a sense of failure in the Biden-Harris administration, particularly among swing voters, that's clear and unambiguous. Elections tend to be referenda on the incumbent and that spells ill for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now Ari, 538's Nate Silver said that the data continues to be pretty negative for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . There are now three recent, I guess high quality national polls showing that Trump is leading. Trump has a 53 percent chance of winning the electoral college. The Economist has Trump with a 54 percent chance as well, Ari.\par \par \uc2 ARI FLEISCHER\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: And I think she really hurt herself in this past week when, in spontaneous moments, when unscripted, you really get to see who is this person. When she made that remark about the rally when somebody said Jesus is his king and she said you're at the wrong rally, boy, does that send a terrible signal to Christians. And then she sent a terrible signal to Jews and to people who support Israel.\par \par \uc2 When she said about someone who accused Israel, a protester who accused Israel of engaging in genocide, she said that this is real. This is real. Damaging, damaging, live, spontaneous moments for her. And that helps explain why the polls are shifting in the direction of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 You can't make mistakes like that, especially when everybody's watching, because they know just how scripted you are. Real moments cost you even more.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Doug, I see it this way. I see, certainly, what Ari said is true, very recent. But I see that Butler rally, the J.D. Vance debate. And this weekend, Trump in that apron at the window. That spoke to a lot of people. There's people coming up to me about it on the street, which was, you know, when that happens, it's, you know, it's something different from the usual, you know, hullabaloo on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 But what changed the momentum here?\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: I think a couple of things. First, the absence of any messaging from \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  about specifics, Laura. I think as the campaign has worn on, she has worn less and less well. Trump has always been a good closer in both 2016 and 2020. He's closed strong. And I think we're seeing that now as he becomes more focused, as he's more in touch with voters, and as Harris, frankly, as seen as really offering nothing beyond as she says, a continuation of the failed policies of the Biden administration as the electorate perceives.\par \par \uc2 So, I expect it will be a close election. It's not decided. But all the momentum, as I think you suggest and Ari suggests, is with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And there's really very little countervailing evidence at this point.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And Ari, I mean, whatever you think about Bush-Cheney era and, you know, people have different views. But just as a matter of political strategy, when you have to get your base out to vote. How does trailing around Wisconsin with \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  going to bring those voters home? I'm not following that at all.\par \par \uc2 I don't think anyone on Earth believes Trump is a threat to democracy. And I think they keep saying it. And it's not landing. And it didn't land when \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  tried it today. It didn't land.\par \par \uc2 FLEISCHER: Yeah, that's not landing. But there is an unease of college- educated suburban women with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . This is the group that as much as Trump is gaining with Hispanic-Americans, African-Americans, Jewish- Americans, you've got to keep your eye on this. If he loses even more in '24 than he did in 2020 or 2016 with college-educated suburban women, that could tip the election toward Harris.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that's what the \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  play is all about.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Do you think \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  -- \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  -- Ari, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  said today she was trying to put a pro-life spin. It was the most incomprehensible approach to campaigning. But she has spent her whole life as a pro-life Republican, now suddenly that's all out the window because of January 6th or Ukraine? Really? That doesn't help the women.\par \par \uc2 FLEISCHER: I'm not here to defend \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  at all. \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  can explain herself. There's no explaining \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  these days. But my point is \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  is the vessel that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  wants to use to appeal to \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  voters, to appeal to the women who have some sense of unease with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That's the only explanation I can see for bringing her out.\par \par \uc2 They're doing it a lot, so that tells me they have some data that says it makes sense for them to do it with the voters they need.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah, well, I mean, her father was called a war criminal for how many years by the Democrats, but somehow that's their closing argument. I don't understand it. But maybe you're right, Ari. Maybe it's just a play for those few female voters that might not otherwise go to the polls. Doug and Ari, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And the stars come out for Kamala, but is there sparkle fading? Raymond Arroyo has it all. Seen and Unseen is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's time for Seen and Unseen, where we reveal the stories behind the headlines. For that, we turn to Fox News contributor Raymond Arroyo. All right, Raymond, Trump is visiting hurricane victims, connecting with the common man. But what's Kamala's campaign?\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, remember many months ago, Laura, I told you that I thought this was going to be a pain election. How much pain are the voters willing to endure? With that in mind, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is closing this campaign not with solutions, but with stars. Lizzo hit Detroit for her, and I mean, she hit it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: This is how a bad (BLEEP) saves democracy. You (BLEEP) couldn't even spell democracy.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: You know, they say if Kamala wins, then the whole country will be like Detroit. OK. Proud like Detroit.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Nothing says, I'm coming to help you, America, like Lizzo promising that every town will be Detroit if Kamala's elected. The tone deafness, Laura, continued all through the weekend. Stevie Wonder serenaded Kamala. Usher campaigned in Atlanta. And Cher tried to turn back time to the Biden era from her house.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I followed \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  since she was my U.S. senator. She fought for me then and she is fighting for all of us now. That's why I'm proudly voting for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Happy birthday. Happy birthday to you. Happy birthday to you.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I love you more. But I love \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  even more.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, it never ends. And look, for Cher, this is very on brand. Remember, she has always championed gypsies, tramps, and thieves. So, you know, this is part of her shtick. But again, and at the celebrity avatar standing in for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , whether it's Molly Shannon or Usher, none of this is connected with the American people.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But, Raymond, the most hysterically funny thing was Lizzo doing the head turn with the hair flip on the way to the private jet. She's like, I got my PJ. I'm going to the PJ and we're all good, because we're all for Harris. That cracks me up.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, remember --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Totally out of touch.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Detroit is the second most dangerous city in the United States, according to a recent study. So, look, Harris is having trouble with black male voters as well.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: As we talked about.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: But this ad that they are posting on \uc2 Snapchat\uc2  and dating apps targeting black men takes the cake. The voters, I'm not so sure.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Hello ladies, I'm Trey. It's good to be here.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: So, what do you do and how much do you make?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I work in finance, making six figures.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: How tall are you?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Six-five.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Do you have a plan to vote?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I didn't plan on it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: That is a Harris ad, Laura. So, the idea is we're going to hold men's dating life hostage unless you vote for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: They've tried that before.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: That's going to backfire.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Hillary tried that, no sex until you register to vote. What was that with the voter? Remember that? We did that on the radio. That was hilarious.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: The celibacy play doesn't work, I think, with American men.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Promises, promises from those people. All right, Raymond, I know \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  really is the secret weapon to reach male voters. It's the epitome of the masculine ethos. And if you're trying to reach men, you'd naturally go on The View.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): Vice President Harris and I grew up middle class. We understand that. She actually worked in a McDonald's.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: We wanted to help you out. We know that your flannel had been through some stuff. So, we've got The View. Dad in plaid.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: That's the single greatest thing I've ever gotten.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: More like something rotten and cotton, Laura. Look, this guy, it's everything scripted. How many times are we going to say we were born middle class? Tonight, she was with Maria Shriver, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was. Some of the people said, can we ask a question? Maria said, no, I'm quoting. No, you're not going to ask questions. Unfortunately, we have some predetermined questions. Everything here is predetermined and you can see it, so can the voters.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, do you think \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  gets them a single vote? Like, literally, who is -- who, when you see \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  up there, you're like, oh, definitely. Never ending wars and someone who totally turned her back on all of her views, but now it's for Harris. I'm definitely voting for Harris. Does that make any sense to you?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: No, I don't understand who she brings in. I worry about that. These are people who lost tragically. Now you're trotting them out as if they have a following.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh my God, it's desperation. Raymond, the Lizzo PJ move is still my favorite. That is my favorite of the night. That's it for us tonight. Thanks for watching. Make sure to stay tuned because Jesse's next.\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020241022ekal00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Continues To Dominate Retail Politics Battle; \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Relying On Star Power To Attract Voters; That The Game Plan: Cringe Walz Video Tris To Link Trump To Project 2025; Not Deranged To Fear This: Lib Hosts Defending Having Trump Derangement Syndrome; Bird Blunder: AOC: Knock On Eagles Fans' Door During Eagles Game\b0\par\par\uc2 Dana Perino, Jeanine Pirro, Richard Fowler, Jesse Watters, Greg  Gutfeld \par \uc2 8,273 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: -- and my colleague, John Roberts, weekdays, 1:00 p.m. Eastern Time for "AMERICA REPORTS".\par \par \uc2 real quick, Dow check, as we are an hour out now from the close, it was a down day on Wall Street, a drop of 344 points for the Dow as there are continued growing concerns over where this market goes next, still sitting at record highs though.\par \par \uc2 All right, that does it for us. Thanks again for joining us. THE FIVE starts right now.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hello, everyone. I'm Dana Perino along with Judge Jeanine Pirro, Richard Fowler, Jesse Watters and Greg Gutfeld. It's 5:00 in New York City, and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 Well, the king of retail politics, he's at it again while \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  accuses \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  of being exhausted, the former president, he was busy schmoozing voters and charging through three unique campaign pit stops in the past couple of days. There was a barber shop in the Bronx with "FOX & FRIENDS", a Steelers football game and a McDonald's in the battleground of Pennsylvania. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  putting on an apron and getting to work on the fries while disputing Kamala's claims who have worked at the Golden Arches back in the '80s.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I'm looking for a job, and I've always wanted to work at \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 , but I never did.\par \par \uc2 Never touched by a human hand, nice and clean.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you, Mr. President. You made it possible for people like us to meet you. I mean, thank you so much for all you are doing.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Worked for 15 minutes more than Kamala.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you going to put this in your resume?\par \par \u