're not on this team, buddy.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know, Richard, doesn't he understand that, you know, supporting tampons -- supporting tampons in boys' bathrooms is really not the essence of a a masculine man. And by the way his stats make it clear that Walz is seen as definitely masculine by 27 percent and Vance 38 percent. He's a really you know masculine guy. Why doesn't he visit like professional football games and go out there and be a real man and enjoy that stuff?\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Visiting a professional -- oh, I like this color on you, by the way.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know, I got a -- I got a line but I'm not going there. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It's the cover of the season.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Visiting a professional football game makes you a man. I didn't know that. But listen, I'm glad you brought up his record because I think his record is actually important in this moment. \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  has passed the largest tax cut -- largest child tax cut in in the nation in his state. He also enacted free meals for all kids K through 12. He eliminated state income tax for Social Security beneficiaries and he's doing that while the state has seen a five percent growth in their GDP. And he's doing that while Minnesota is the sixth best state -- best state to do business in and you see companies expanding in Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 The Mayo Clinton is spending $5 billion in expansion. He's also -- there's also a semiconductor plan happening there, the Polar Semiconductor plant. They're spending almost a half a billion dollars to open a plant in Minnesota. So, what's happening in Minnesota is actually working. He's passing many of the policies both Trump and Vice President Harris agree with and it's working. So, that I call manly because the businesses are going there and that's the manliest thing I've ever seen.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Well, you want to talk about his business. What about the fact that Walz gave $126,000 contract to a busines