par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right. If you're watching tonight and you're thinking, OK, we must have hit peak Trump, well, think again.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: If you believe that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has somehow become less popular over time, let me change your mind about that. In fact, he is more popular at this point in the campaign than he was at this point in the 2020 campaign or the 2016 campaign.\par \par \uc2 The momentum of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has stalled and I think they feel like they have to change something that's going on in their campaign because they were seeing a rise and now it has stalled out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I don't know. Maybe her campaign believes the change she needed was the most-friendly interview she's done this month. But when she was actually faced with real questions, the types of questions that journalists used to ask and that Bret Baier did ask this week, well, of course, we saw the real Kamala.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Matt Towery, political analyst and pollster. And Florida Congresswoman, Anna Paulina Luna. Matt, is that an accurate description of what we're seeing? There's lots of different polls out there. There's an Activision, kind of a new poll, 1,000 likely voters, that also says that Trump is up 2.2 percent nationally, active vote, excuse me. So, is this where we are right now, stalled momentum for Harris?\par \par \uc2 MATT TOWERY, POLLSTER: Yeah, that's where we are. You know, if you recall maybe three or four weeks ago on your show, we talked about the fact that we could see the momentum changing. And it really started right after the second assassination attempt of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And then it went into the V.P. debate, which normally doesn't mean anything, but it was so hyped up, that moved the polls even more.\par \par \uc2 And then the campaign, as you alluded to, decided to put her on these very friendly shows and the vapid answers, that got spread everywhere. And her numbers just started to plummet and his numbers started to accelerate. So, this is a real acceleration for Trump.\par \par \uc2 I always warn people as a pollster, anything can happen in three weeks. And I wonder what could happen that's external that could change things, because certainly the pandemic, although much further out, that changed 2020. So, anything can happen. But right now, she is definitely installed, and he is definitely gaining momentum in almost every battleground state.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And congresswoman, Politico is reporting that she's struggling with Hispanics in Pennsylvania and elsewhere. So, she's trying to bring in some help. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: This is Mark Anthony. Even though some have forgotten, I remember what it was like when Trump was president. This election goes way beyond political parties. Let's remember what the United States represents and stands for.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Why is he wearing sunglasses? I'm not sure why he's wearing sunglasses in that ad. But Congresswoman, do you think that Hispanic voters really care about another rich celebrity's endorsement?\par \par \uc2 REP. ANNA PAULINA LUNA (R-FL): No, not at all. In fact, I think a lot of Hispanic-Americans right now are focused on, number one, the economy, and also border security and immigration. But you know, it's interesting that you bring this up, Laura, because, again, Hispanic-Americans are now the largest voting minority in the country, and they are shifting in favor of President Trump.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, we also hear, Matt, from some of the old \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  campaign folks like Mark McKinnon, you know, he despises Trump. But he said, according to what he's seeing out there, that there's a lot going on, he said, under the radar for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . And his spidey-sense tells him that that's going to be enough for her.\par \par \uc2 And I guess they're talking about turnout machine, getting people to the polls, door knocking, which is another thing they were talking about today, including David Plouffe, by the way.\par \par \uc2 TOWERY: Well, they're better at it than the Republicans. We know that. That's always been the case. And we've talked about that for over the last year and a half, Laura. But let me just give you a little example of how things aren't like they were in 2020.\par \par \uc2 In Georgia, early voting is what in-person voting is higher than it was in 2020. And that's a big turnout in North Georgia and South Georgia that are big Trump areas. But guess what's not big? Absentee ballots. They're way down because Georgia changed their election laws and they're not flooded with absentee ballots.\par \par \uc2 Now, you have to really prove you need one and they're just not out there to be harvested if one might call it that. They can't be collected. So that's way off. That's going to change Georgia.\par \par \uc2 So, some states they can do their turnout magic. Some states like Georgia, it may be a little more difficult than everyone expected.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And of course, we've got \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  spending an enormous amount of time in Pennsylvania, Congresswoman. He believes that's the key to this entire election. He's pretty smart. He has lots of algorithms always going on in his head. And so, that's what he's focused on.\par \par \uc2 And for him, it's all about turnout. He did a town hall this week, and he's going to do other events next week, apparently. How important is that presence, and obviously, the money he's giving, since Republicans are being outspent like four to one?\par \par \uc2 LUNA: I think it's incredibly important. You know, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is not your average Republican, not your average conservative. He, I think, was formerly a Democrat, obviously a very independent thinker, and not to mention he appeals to a very young audience. And so, I think that it's going to be incredibly powerful.\par \par \uc2 I know Scott Pressler has been putting in a lot of work in Pennsylvania, and I think that, really, this election is going to be won in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm excited to have his support. I know the president is. And frankly, having met \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and Trump, I can tell you Trump is definitely the one that we need in office.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, Matt and Congresswoman, thank you both. All right, Trump is revising what was considered in some circles a controversial IVF mandate, and Kamala lays an egg at the premier Catholic political event of the year. Raymond Arroyo has it all. Friday Follies, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's Friday and that means it's time for Friday Follies, and for that we turn to Fox News contributor Raymond Arroyo. All right, Raymond, I know you were skulking about at the Al Smith dinner last night, and as we said earlier, this is just an amazing event and it's a political fundraiser, really a charitable fundraiser. But \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  decided, you know, she had other things to do.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, but as you mentioned earlier also, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was there. Boy, did he bring the heat and the yucks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT, 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I must say, I was shocked when I heard that Kamala was skipping the Al Smith dinner. Instead of attending tonight, she's in Michigan receiving communion from \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There's a group called White Dudes for Harris. Have you seen this? But I'm not worried about them at all because they're wives and their wives' lovers are all voting for me.\par \par \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is here looking very glum. But look on the bright side, Chuck, considering how woke your party has become, if Kamala loses, you still have a chance to become the first woman president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I think that was my favorite joke of all, that and the Whitmer joke.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yeah, well, and the Whitmer joke was so smart because he tied this outrage that is still so fresh in the Catholic community to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . And the fact that she wasn't there, after. And this guy, he's running all over the country. He had three back-to-back interviews, including one with me, before he hit the stage here, Laura.\par \par \uc2 I don't know how he was standing, but this was a very different crowd at the Al Smith dinner from 2016 when he appeared. There was some hostility there. This was a very Trumpy crowd, I have to say.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, and I loved how Letitia James was sitting a couple rows back. And, you know.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, and \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 . You texted me something during the Al Smith dinner, because Schumer and Trump, they're chatting throughout the dinner. What did that tell you?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It told me that none of them believe this thing that Trump's a fascist, Trump's a dictator in waiting, Trump's going to suspend the Constitution. None of that is real. It's all a lie. They know it, we know it. They've all been friends with him for a long time, and now he came in to, you know, upset their apple cart in the swamp, and drain the swamp, and they don't like that.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Speaking of upsetting apple carts, the flop of the evening was V.P. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , whomever advise her to skip this event to reach Catholics across the country should be given their walking papers. Instead, she sent a tone deaf pre-taped skit with SNL alum Molly Shannon. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I'm Catholic, and tonight is one of the biggest dinners next to the Last Supper.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: I'm giving a speech. Do you have some thoughts about what I might say tonight?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: My feelings about what you should say tonight would be best expressed in a monologue from one of my favorite made-for-TV series.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: OK, let's hear it.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Don't you see, man? We need a woman to represent us. A woman brings more heart. It's time for a woman, bro.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, my god.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It was the worst thing I have ever seen in politics that tries to be humorous.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: But why didn't she just give her own monologue? Why do you need another actress? And by the way, this character she's playing is best known for smelling her body odor. Why you would want that character identified with you? Why didn't she bring Father Guido Sarducci or Ed Grimley out?\par \par \uc2 I mean, other characters from the 90's that people have very little frame of reference for. My kids watched this and said, dad, who's that lady in the school uniform? They had no reference at all. This was a bad -- and there were a lot of people offended in the room, I have to tell you by the video.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, it was disrespectful to Catholics. Like, making fun of the Catholic schoolgirl. Because, you know, they hate Catholic education. They hate Catholic education.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: It ended with booze, Laura, all across the room. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I don't even know what that noise you're making is. So, ah.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: You know what it was?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Some applause from the anti-Catholics in the room.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yeah, but that was on the stage. The crowd, a lot of boos. I was in that house. It was not warmly received, I can tell you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Raymond, I know you did an exclusive interview with President Trump. Before the dinner, you made some news about his IVF mandate.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: The Catholic Church believes when you implement this technology you're killing embryos. Will you have a religious exemption to your IVF mandate for religious organizations in businesses that feel this violates my religious principle.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT, 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Well, you know, I haven't been asked that, but it sounds to me like a pretty good idea, frankly. Certainly, if there's a religious problem, I think people should go with that. I should be -- I really think they should be able to do that.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: The pope was asked about your candidacy and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , and he instructed Catholic voters to vote for the lesser of two evils. Who do you think he means and why?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, I think he wants them to vote for me, and I stand for really everything that you stand for and that the church stands for, and she doesn't. She's a Marxist. Her father was a Marxist and still is a Marxist. And they are not big into religion, I will tell you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, that's called reclaiming the narrative from the Pope. Pretty good move there. But the idea -- look, this IVF exemption for religious sensibilities, and he told me he'd reinstitute the Mexico City policy, which forbids spending money on foreign abortions. That's big news, particularly from some folks in the faith community who had misgivings about the new Trump, who sounds like the old Trump.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: When I was watching, great job in the interview by the way, when I was watching the dinner on, it was on Fox, it almost looked like one of those old Dean Martin --\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, I always say he's like Don Rickles, and he was last night.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: A little bit. Combo Reagan. He looked like Reagan a little bit. Raymond, thank you. That is it for us tonight. Make sure to follow me all weekend long on social media. Thanks for watching. Jesse Watters takes it next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020241019ekai00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Trump Sits Down For All-Women Town Hall On Fox; Now, Sen. J.D. Vance (R-OH) Campaigns In Battleground Pennsylvania; Report Says, Willis Tries To Reinstate Charges Vs. Trump; New Info On Vetting Of Afghan Terror Suspect; New Concern About Migrant Gang Members In Aurora; Vance Campaigns In Battleground Pennsylvania; Harris Appeals To Black Men As Trump Makes Gains; Memorial Service For RFK's Widow Ethel Kennedy; Harris Campaigns In Battleground Pennsylvania\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum; Harris Faulkner; Tyrus, Griff Jenkins, Alicia  Acuna, Juan Williams, Peter Doocy \par \uc2 8,532 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, John and Sandra, thank you very much. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum, and this is The Story.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump just wrapped up a wide ranging town hall here on Fox just hours ahead of Vice President Harris' sit down tonight with Bret Baier, and that will happen on special report at 6:00 P.M. Eastern. The former president took questions from many of the women who were gathered in that audience.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, there were 6 percent more women voting than men. So, you have to take that into account, he is right now down by 14 with women and up 16 with men in the latest NBC polls. We have new polling coming in pretty much every day from a lot of places, but that is one of the more recent ones.\par \par \uc2 Now, abortion is nearly tied with the economy, 21 to 23 there in a recent poll of women voters. Earlier, the former president spoke about the Supreme Court decision to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT, 2024 PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: They wanted it back in the states for a vote of the people, and that's where we have it. And the states are now voting for it. And, honestly, some of them are going much more liberal, like in Ohio, I would have thought it might be a bit different.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS FAULKNER, FOX NEWS HOST: Some of them are not.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And some of them are not, but it's going to be redone. It's going to be redone. They're going to -- you end up with a vote of the people. And some of them, I agree, they're too too tough, too tough. And those are going to be redone.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, that was just a short time ago. Harris Faulkner joins us live from battleground Georgia, where she hosted that Trump town hall.\par \par \uc2 Harris, great job, really interesting to watch these women and have a full audience of women expressing their questions for the former president. What really stood out to you in terms of what got the biggest response from the group there?\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: That would be the economy. Yes, I mean, and the some of the some of the questions included very personal details of the living conditions for some of the women in the audience. You know, they're struggling. They've got daycare $1,400 a month. I mean, that's like quadruple what they had been paying, you know, 3.5 years ago. So, there are some tough situations out there.\par \par \uc2 Some of them left their jobs to be at that town hall for a day. I mean, you know, the economy impacts everything we do. Those women didn't let it stop them from showing up and asking questions. They're looking for answers. And, you know, there are 6 percent undecided voters in the state of Georgia. If you look at the last few elections, that has made a difference in the past.\par \par \uc2 Now, the early vote total that was confirmed last night was 307,000 people. Now, early vote is in-person here. Martha, those lines were long all over the state. Now, we don't know county by county yet. That's going to make a difference. But these women were from all over the state.\par \par \uc2 So, we got to see the issues that not only matter to women in Georgia, but, I mean, issues are issues. Yes, they can be female focused, but we know that we care about the economy. We sometimes run more households. We married two great guys, you and me, but I don't know how it is in your household. I'm running the pocketbook. Many women are living like that. God bless them.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'm not. I got out of that job. And it's my husband's birthday today since you brought him up. So, happy birthday to him.\par \par \uc2 I want to play this because I thought this was interesting. And I want to ask you what their -- you know, overall, what your feel in the room was for how much immigration to these voters. And here's what former President Trump said on cracking down on Mexico, what he did and what he felt worked. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Okay.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We built 571 miles of wall and that wall worked. Then I got Mexico to give us soldiers, a lot of soldiers, 28,000 soldiers, to protect us. I said, you have to protect us because you're letting all of these criminals come into our country, you have to protect us. They said, we're not going to do it. I said, good, then I'm just going to tariff all your goods until you do it. They said, like I said, we're going to do it. And they gave us - -\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, you can hear the laughter in the room. And this is what I find so interesting about the tariff discussion, because it's clear to me, and I'm sure, as we have both watched him over the years, the tariff thing is an opening salvo. It's like, if you don't do this, then I'm going to slap these on you to get people to do what he wants them to do. And it has worked in the past. What was the reaction to that in the crowd?\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Well, they liked it because it's movement forward. And, you know, we talk about reflexively going back to the policies that worked. He's also moving them forward. He wants to incentivize international partners because they're now going to be competing. If he lowers the taxes for those who do business, that's going to be tougher for everybody.\par \par \uc2 So, those relationships across borders and around the world with the next president who would do such a thing with the strength of a threat of higher tariffs on goods that they're producing is probably a one-two punch.\par \par \uc2 Do business with us, do business with our better-leveraged American corporations because I'm lowering the taxes on them to let them hire more workers and produce more goods here. And also, by the way, if you don't help us secure our border, you're going to get penalties with higher tariffs.\par \par \uc2 So, it's an interesting strategy. He drilled down and, Martha, I think that's the point.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: I think people are ready for a closing argument and specificity and he offered it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, it was a great town hall Harris. And thanks for taking some time to talk with us after. I'm sure you're getting ready to head back here to New York. Thank you very much. Good to see you, Harris. And we look forward tonight.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: You too. And happy birthday to your beloved.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you. Thank you so much. We'll see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 So let's bring in Fox News Contributor Tyrus, who is about to sit down with former President Trump for an interview that he is doing on OutKick this Friday. Tyrus, great to have you with us. Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 TYRUS, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Always a pleasure.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I'm curious what you think about this idea of this gender gap when we showed those numbers earlier. What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: I don't make anything of it. And I think this is an election of people. These are people that are coming together from -- I tour the country. I do my standup shows all across the country. I'm always traveling and it's a consensus. I was actually in California this weekend when he had his rally in Coachella. The entire city stopped. Stores are sold out. People are -- but they're all -- it's the same thing and it's all walks, men, women. Sexualities didn't matter, colors didn't matter. Everybody's, according to the pocketbook, I believe, as Harris so eloquently put out, everyone's pocketbook is hurting. Everyone does not feel good.\par \par \uc2 This is this election is -- I keep saying this. This election is not close. This is not -- they're trying to sparse and men against women. Whether they like it or not, we all live in the same communities and all the communities feel the same way. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  just adjusted the crime report to see that Americans are not crazy for what we're talking about. We're being -- our hotel, our apartments are being taken over by other Venezuelan gangs. We're seeing these things happen.\par \par \uc2 And when people are complaining and talking about they're afraid, they're being shut down, and now it's coming together. People on the street, if you just go walk around, there is a feeling that was just like during the pandemic, there needed to be a change. And it's the same type of momentum.\par \par \uc2 This election, I told -- I made jokes. I said, I'll be done 10:05 Eastern. I don't see any path for her, in my opinion, based on what I'm seeing around the country and hearing from folks --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, are you calling for -- you know, because people ask me this question all the time and I ask other people I'm with, you know, because I'm trying to get a feel for what -- how people feel, what their gut check is at this point. So, are you calling -- are you thinking it's going to be a landslide for Trump?\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: I think it's going to be one of the biggest electoral victories in the history of the presidency. Matter of fact, I'm so confident I'll put my beard on it. I will shave my beard off if President Trump does not.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And will you come back and say, I was wrong about what I said on October 16th, Martha?\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: I will say, I will apologize to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and everyone I was wrong and I obviously need to go back to school and figure some things out. So, you heard it here first. I'll put my beard on it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, I find it fascinating that he was in Coachella, California. He's going next week or a few days after that to \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2 . So, I watched the places that people are going. I see him still trying to expand the map, which I kind of see, and also it's a more congressional place.\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: They're in play. New York is in play. New Jersey is in play. Believe it or not, California is in play. I was in California for five days. People are fed up. There's no more like, hey, yes, Tyrus, they're screaming on the street.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, if you're right, we're going to kick all the pundits off the set and off the show. And we're just going to be like, Tyrus, how did you figure this whole thing out?\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: Or I will just take a nice -- maybe a nice 