mala Harris\uc2 , every time they ask her a question, she can't answer basic details about how she wants to govern this country, then how can we ever trust her in a room with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  or Xi Jinping? The answer is, we can't. So, let's send somebody we can trust. Let's send \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  back to the White House.\par \par \uc2 Now, I want to talk about, you know, one of the biggest issues I hear from, not just in Pennsylvania, but all across the country is we hear a lot about people not being able to afford a home. And, you know, I'm the first person of the millennial generation to ever appear on a national ticket and I know that a lot of folks in my generation, they're, frankly, very angry at something that they would like to be able to do that their parents generation could do, but they can't, which is to afford a nice home on a normal job in their own community.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and I want to rebuild that American dream. We want to make it affordable for our young people to buy a home in the nation that their parents and grandparents built. That's a simple thing that we can get back to if we've got better leadership.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, the media does this thing where they say, well, you know, there's no connection between the border policies and the housing crisis that we have in our country. And I think any of us with a lick of common sense, we're saying, whoa, whoa, wait here, explain to me this, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . When you let in 25 million illegal aliens into this country, people who shouldn't be here, we got to house them somewhere. And if you're giving 25 million illegal aliens homes that ought by right go to American citizens, then you are making the American dream of home ownership unaffordable for our people.\par \par \uc2 American homes ought to go to American citizens, and that simple principle is something that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and I are going to fight for.\par \par \uc2 But we know, of course, it's not just, you know, that's the price of housing that has been made worse by this terrible border crisis. This is an unbelievable and unspeakable human tragedy that has affected thousands and thousands of residents of this state.\par \par \uc2 And let me just tell you a little story. I was in I was in Georgia campaigning a few weeks ago and they were showing me all the things -- I was with the sheriffs and they were showing me all the things that they had picked up from the Mexican drug cartels who were trafficking drugs and, frankly, also a lot of illegal guns into their communities. One of the biggest drivers of illegal gun crime in this country is the Mexican drug cartels. I didn't know that until recently, but it's true.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm talking to these sheriffs, and you know, you've got everything behind me and in front of me and in front of the table. You've got, you know, a library shelves of marijuana. You've got cocaine. You've got meth. You've got pills. You've got, you know, sort of more refined THC. And then you've got fentanyl, of course. And there's one thing that looks really out of place, and I say, well, what is that, sheriff? And he says, I say that that's candy. That looks like a big box of candy is what it looked like, Nerds candy. And I think there was like a box of sour patch candy. And I said, Sheriff, what's going on there? And he said that's not candy. That's drugs.\par \par \uc2 Because the Mexican drug cartels have figured out that if they disguise this candy as -- or disguise these drugs as candy, it's going to make it easier to get into our country and easier to disperse it all over our communities. And every single one of us knows what's going to happen that a little kid is going to open up a packet of candy on an American playground, and he's going to pay for it with his life.\par \par \uc2 And when we talk about securing the southern border, the Democrats talk a whole big game about compassion. Well, compassion