tholic voters in particular, which make up a very important part of the electorate. And Harris has chosen not to be at that dinner. The last person who wasn't at the dinner was \uc2 Walter Mondale\uc2  back in 1984 from the Democrat side.\par \par \uc2 Corey, thank you very much, always good to check in with you. We'll see you soon.\par \par \uc2 LEWANDOWSKI: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You bet. So, new details about the Afghan national, quote, radicalized inside the United States, still some question about how all of this happened and in what sequence. He entered through a DHS parole program that was created during that chaotic and deadly exit as people were scrambling to get out of Afghanistan. That's when he got into this country. That, of course, happened during the Biden-Harris administration.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So new details about the Afghan man accused of plotting an Election Day terror attack here in the United States. The Feds say that they arrested 27-year-old Nasir Tawhedi last week in Oklahoma. He had picked up two AK-47 rifles, 10 magazines and 500 rounds of ammo that he had ordered.\par \par \uc2 They say this is the suspect reading to two children, and they say that close up of the book shows that it is about rewards that a martyr gets in the afterlife. US officials are now saying that they believe he was radicalized after he came to this country under a program to help our allies after that very memorable chaotic exit from Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 Griff Jenkins joins us, national correspondent, reporting live from Washington with more on what we know about this man. Hi, Griff.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEW NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. And officials have struggled honestly to explain how Tawhedi entered the US after the withdrawal, cleared multiple vettings and was planning to kill as many Americans as possible on Election Day. Now some of the confusion may lie in the \uc2 FBI\uc2 's initial statement that he was approved for a Special Immigrant Visa, because that involves, Martha, the most stringent vetting that.\par \par \uc2 Never happened because Tawhedi didn't follow through on his SIV application. Today, officials clarified that Tawhedi was vetted three times, first to work for the CIA as a security guard, second for humanitarian parole to enter the US, and a third time in 2023 to extend his humanitarian parole. DHS Secretary Mayorkas is standing by the recurring vetting process.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, HOMELAND SECURITY SECRETARY: When we vet an individual, it's a point in time screening and vetting process. If we obtain information subsequently that suggests the individual could be of danger, we take appropriate law enforcement action. That is exactly what we did in this case.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS\uc2 : And officials claim the system worked in this case. They believe Tawhedi was radicalized two years after coming to the US and apprehended after new information came to light, although it's unclear what identified him as a threat. This comes after DHS' own inspector general found in 2022 some evacuees were admitted into the US who were not fully vetted due to incomplete info.\par \par \uc2 And now there are new questions, Martha, of whether he was part of a larger global \uc2 ISIS\uc2  terror plot. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  share with authorities in France a relative of his was plotting a similar attack there.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. That -- I mean, that's the question that comes to mind throughout this whole report, is there -- are there others who were planning this as well. Griff, thank you very much. Griff Jenkins in Washington,\par \par \uc2 Graphic new video from Aurora, Colorado a place that has been very much on the map. Take a look at this beating horrific. A management company claims that this is a staffer getting beaten up at one of his apartment buildings, and