or's Office also says if \uc2 Aurora\uc2  finds evidence of a crime, the state will work with law enforcement. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, the story just keeps getting more twisty and interesting. Thank you very much, Alicia, Alicia Acuna. We'll keep digging on that one.\par \par \uc2 Hours away from a must see election interview here on Fox. Vice President Harris gearing up to sit down with our own Fox Chief political anchor Bret Baier for the interview that will be airing tonight at 6:00 PM. Our political panel on the potential impact of Harris reaching a new audience next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Let's chip in, just taking some questions here. This is JD Vance, the vice presidential candidate, campaigning in Williamsport, Pennsylvania. Let's hear a little of this Q&A for a couple minutes.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH), VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: -- the late term baby for no reason. You know, sometimes it's necessary because the health of the mother, the life of the mother, but aborting a late term baby is not something that we should take lightly in this country. And I don't like that. I really do not like that. I think it's important to say.\par \par \uc2 Now, (inaudible) of course, come at this from a different perspective from \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and a lot of Democrats, I want us to protect every vulnerable life in this country. And I think that's really important. The reason -- the reason why, I mean, think about this, the way that we do that is to -- we ensure that each state is able to make its policy the way that it thinks it should.\par \par \uc2 So I'm not going to tell Pennsylvanians what they should do in the same way I'm not going to tell Californians and I'm not going to tell Alabamians exactly what they should do. I think it's important to realize the real contrast between this is that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  wants to renationalize this issue. She wants taxpayer-funded abortion at the ninth month, which is disgusting.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants to preserve the right of the states to make these decisions. He, in other words, wants to preserve the right of voters to make these decisions, and that's a big difference between him and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Next question.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you. The question is about health care.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Trump paired never got hit the ground running, and we'd like to hear what you're --\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Yes, sir. You know our --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: -- view on health care. Because we're one family away from a really big bill, one bad medical --\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Yes, sir. And look, our message to Pennsylvania families is that President Trump is going to have your back, and he did have Pennsylvania families back during the first Trump administration because a lot of folks don't remember this. But President Trump inherited, and I'm talking about the private health care exchanges. Those exchanges were in a bit of a death spiral, because the health care that was offered was so bad that people wouldn't sign up until they got very sick, and that's a terrible way to actually run a health insurance program, and that's what we were doing in this country.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  implemented a number of reforms to make health care more stable in this country. And I'm not going to say that we fixed everything, because there's still a lot to do, but the guiding principle of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's health care plan is, number one, we want people to be able to choose a health care plan that makes sense for their family. Number two, we want them to be able to choose a doctor and keep their doctor, not have their health insurance plan, strip their doctor from them.\par \par \uc2 And number three is we want transparency and the prices that people are paying. There's no service in the world where you go in and you have no idea what you're actually buying, or how much you're actually buying it for. And we think that's one of the main reasons the health care is broken.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, this looks or presents very different problems, depending on the issue that we're talking about, OK. So let me give you an example. You go into a hospital, you have a young family. I've heard this story so many times. Young family has a baby, and, you know, they're -- they're right at the, you know, right at the moment where the woman needs pain medication.\par \par \uc2 And then, they choose an out of network provider. And they get home, they got a $20,000 surprise medical bill. We got to cut that crap out. We got to stop that. Make sure that people know what they're buying, know what they're paying for. That's really important.\par \par \uc2 But then let me give you another illustration of this problem. You all would probably be shocked to know, or maybe you wouldn't, that Europe pays lower prices for drugs that were invented in this country than Americans do. Well that's unfair and that's ridiculous, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's going to change it.\par \par \uc2 In fact, he fought to change it his first four years, and then Biden and Harris undid all of his important work. People don't give Trump enough credit for this, but towards the end of his administration, he really took on big pharma so that Americans would pay lower costs for prescription drugs. He wants Europeans, in the same way that Europeans have to pay more for their own defense.\par \par \uc2 They've also got to pay a little bit more for their own health care and stop freeloading off the United States of America. That'll lower costs for Pennsylvanians and that's an important thing we got to do, too. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 DAVE KENNEDY, WILLIAMSPORT SUN-GAZETTE: Yes. Dave Kennedy from the Williamsport Sun-Gazette. What do you do? What do you -- what would you like to do specifically to get people, young people, the opportunity to go to trade schools more or not necessarily --\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 D. KENNEDY: -- for your academic. But, you know, we need welders, we need all that kind of folks as well.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Yes, we do.\par \par \uc2 D. KENNEDY: -- specifically --\par \par \uc2 (END LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Watching the campaign trail here, and we want to bring in our panel in just a second. Vice President Harris is also campaigning in Pennsylvania today. She is in Bucks County where she is doing an event with Republicans, holding a voter registration event in that county for the first time in 2007, a crucial factor that could swing the battleground states 19 Electoral College votes. That's why Pennsylvania is so important.\par \par \uc2 So from there, she will sit down with Special Report anchor Bret Baier for her first interview on Fox News of this cycle. With that, we bring in Matt Gorman, Targeted Victory Executive Vice President and former Senior Adviser to Senator Tim Scott's campaign, and Juan Williams, Fox News Senior Political Analyst. Great to see you both. Thank you so much for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 Juan, let me start with you. What do you think about her decision to sit down with Bret Baier this evening? The campaign has been very reluctant to spend any time with the folks at Fox, and we're all looking forward to this.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: I love it. I mean, I just think this is great and it's something that I've counseled for a long time. I just think, you know, Martha, it's a new audience. I agree there are a lot of people, especially in the opinion leaders on Fox who are pro-Trump, but it doesn't mean that she doesn't have an opportunity to reach a new audience in some ways.\par \par \uc2 And I might add, I think there are a lot of people who are moderates and even liberals who watch Fox. But anyway, to get to your point, I think that she is very much interested in shaping away some of the non-Trump voters who are Republicans, people who voted for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  during the primaries. And that's a substantial number of Republicans, if you recall.\par \par \uc2 And so in the way that Trump is reaching out to minority voters, especially minority men, blacks, Latinos, to shave away at the number of minority voters who are voting for Harris, I think Harris is now looking at a lot of those non-MAGA Republicans, especially women with the hope that she can reduce his advantage in some red counties, in places like Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So this is the interesting question, Matt. And, you know, if she goes from 88 percent of black male vote to something like 82, can she still win if she can shave off some of these white women, suburban voters that she is appealing to, and also moderate, some moderate Republicans. It seems like that's the goal. Do you think that's a possible for her?\par \par \uc2 MATT GORMAN, TARGETED VICTORY EXECUTIVE VICE PRESIDENT: Yes. For her, it's necessary, right? I think settling back, obviously, I think the Fox Interview is a smart idea. I think Bret's going to do a great job tonight. But, you know, you see \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  going after men. She talks with Joe Rogan.\par \par \uc2 There's a big gender gap problem on the Democrat side when it comes to men. I mean, I think what they are seeing here is the gender gap on their side with men is worse than ours, necessarily with women.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I don't think it is, Matt. Why do you think that is?\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: They don't know --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: How are male voters moving away?\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: Culture, it's the biggest thing. And this has been under their nose for years now, right? We've talked a lot about Republicans and women in our issues. The male vote has been slipping away for them for years, and they haven't noticed it now.\par \par \uc2 Look, the people living in Northern Virginia, where in the futures -- these female shirts, they got those unlocked. Like -- but it's the people who can talk about college football, who are, you know, can talk about regular issues that, you know, men can do. They have a hard time reaching those folks, and it's now with drinks to go, a big problem for him.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Juan, you know, she's going on, Charlamagne Tha God and a number of other of other places. She promised to legalize marijuana. I think Trump has also supported that, and she has this program that is targeting black men for black men's opportunity. Is that -- does that resonate? I mean, is that a good way to go here?\par \par \uc2 And it may not be a legal program, actually, it may run into some discriminatory issues if you try to allocate federal funds based on who they're going to.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Right. But I think she has to do the outreach. I think there was criticism that, you know, some black men, especially those who aren't really engaged, felt like she wasn't paying attention. I think what she said on Charlamagne Tha God was very clear that, you know, you pick a lane. Is she too tough as a prosecutor or not tough enough? And she's saying, I was tough but I really was empathetic for people who just had some marijuana for private use.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I think there's a lot of questions being raised over whether or not that's the way that people want to be reached, but we will see 20 days to go. Gentlemen, thank you very much. Juan Williams and Matt Gorman, great to see you both. Quick break. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So right now, a memorial service for Ethel Kennedy, an American icon, political activist, mother of 11 children, one of the most beloved prominent members of America's Camelot era passed away last week at the age of 96 following a stroke. Her son's Douglas is -- and Bobby and Ethel's 10th child and, of course, a longtime colleague and friend and correspondent here at Fox News. So we send our condolences to Douglas and his family and the entire family.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House Correspondent Peter Doocy is reporting live from the North Lawn on today's memorial service. Hi, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: And, Martha, there have been tributes from many members of the Kennedy family, but some of the most poignant remarks so far have been from the three former presidents who are sitting -- two former presidents, one current president, sitting in the front row.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER US PRESIDENT: At some function, and suddenly this small woman with this huge smile and these twinkling eyes marches right up to me and grabs me by both hands. And looks up at me, and I realize it's Ethel Kennedy, which, of course, is even more disorienting.\par \par \uc2 And she looks up and she says, oh, I like you. You're going places.\par \par \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , Former Us President: I thought your mother was a cat's meow. She would flirt with me in the most innocent ways. The good Lord knows, if anybody ever deserve a quality escort to the pearly gates, it's you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: This afternoon's tribute to the late Ethel Kennedy is at St. Matthews in Dupont Circle. That is the same cathedral that was the site of President Kennedy's funeral. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Peter, thank you very much. Peter Doocy reporting from the White House.\par \par \uc2 And right now, Vice President Harris is speaking in Bucks County, Pennsylvania. Biden took Bucks County by four points in 2020. Let's check it for a minute here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), US VICE PRESIDENT AND PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: -- offended them and now the baton is in our hands. So I am joined today by over 100 Republican leaders from across Pennsylvania and across our country, who are supporting my candidacy for President of the United States. And I am deeply honored to have their support.\par \par \uc2 Some served in state houses, some in the \uc2 United States congress\uc2 , some worked for other Republican presidents and presidential nominees, including \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2 , \uc2 John McCain\uc2 , \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , George H. W. Bush and \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 . And some today served in \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's own administration. We also have with us Republican voters from here in Pennsylvania and beyond, who have been active in their Republican parties for years, who have supported Republican candidates up and down the ticket.\par \par \uc2 Now, I say all that to make an obvious point. In a typical election year, you all being here with me might be a bit surprising, dare I say, unusual, but not in this election. Not in this election, because at stake in this race are the Democratic ideals that our founders and generations of Americans before us have fought for. At stake in this election is the Constitution of the United States, it very self.\par \par \uc2 We are here today because we share a core belief that we must put country before party.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED SPEAKERS (in unison): USA! USA! USA! USA! USA!\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Indeed, because we -- and we chant that with such great pride, because we all know we all have so much more in common than what separates us. And at some point in the career of the folks who joined me on stage, one of the other things that we have in common is in our careers, we have each sworn an oath to uphold the Constitution of the United States,\par \par \uc2 And so, we know that sacred oath must always be honored and never violated, and that we should expect anyone who seeks the highest office in our land would meet that standard. We here know the Constitution is not a relic from our past, but determines whether we are a country where the people can speak freely and even criticize the president without fear of being thrown in jail or targeted by the military.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020241017ekag00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Trump Goes After Harris on Economy With Three Weeks to Go; Pro- Harris Group Releases Man Enough Ad; V.P. Harris Makes Push to Win Over Black Men; Harris Scrambles For Support With Black Male Voters; US Intel Tells Trump He's On Iran Hit List; Power Of the Catholic Vote In Tight 2024 Race; North Carolina Residents Recover From Helene's Devastation\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum; Aishah Hasnie; David Asman; Harris Faulkner;  Jessica Tarlov; Jack Keane, Bill McGurn, Danamarie McNicholl \par \uc2 8,134 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [15:00:00]\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Great. Thank you very much. You guys. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum, and this is the story.\par \par \uc2 So, you saw it here live, fascinating as this played out this afternoon, an unscripted former President Trump not holding back, going after the vice president on the issue of how she would handle the economy versus how he would handle it as we are three weeks from Election Day. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT, 2024 PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: They're going to wipe out our country. If she wins, and she won't even -- she doesn't know what the hell she's doing. If she wins, she's -- and she's not going to be thinking about -- I couldn't, every speech I made having to do with cars, but every speech I made having to do with tariffs, I'd mention that our country is being threatened by Mexico.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Fox Business anchor David Asman joins me with his analysis, but first to Congressional Correspondent Aishah Hasnie reporting live from Atlanta, where she watching and reporting on the former president's report this afternoon, and then he's got a big rally there tonight. Hi, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. And the former president is trying to urge supporters here in Georgia to get out and vote early. That's because early voting, in person voting, began today in the Peach State. This is a tossup. He knows that. Everyone knows that. And so voter turnout, a strong turnout, is critically important for the former president. And so, again, the campaign is pushing early voting. I am told that's going to be a big part of today's messaging at this rally today.\par \par \uc2 Now, he is getting some help from Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , very popular here in Georgia. And sources close to Governor Kemp's office confirm to Fox News that he is ramping up his political network to turn out the vote. He's getting a big assist from GOP mega donor Miriam Adelson. She just donated a million dollars to his Hardworking Americans PAC. That's going to help Trump a lot.\par \par \uc2 He's also getting some help from former Senator Kelly Loeffler's group called Greater Georgia. That group is reaching out to black voters, Hispanics, Asians and young voters. And since 2021, Martha, Kelly Loeffler, just herself and this group, have registered more than 40,000 new voters in this state. That is big in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Now, Trump was in Chicago today for remarks about the economy. Here's what he said about how it might swing for voters here in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Because we're about growth, she's got no growth whatsoever. And we're going to bring the companies back. We're going to lower taxes still further for companies that are going to make their product in the USA. We're going to protect those companies with strong tariffs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: So, early voting is the name of the game here in the Peach State, and making sure that every single person gets out, gets to the ballot box. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Aishah, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business Anchor David Asman is here. David, great to have you with us. Just a couple of quick polls here. These are ABC on Sunday, who do you trust more to handle the economy, Trump 46, Harris 38. And then let's go to, is the nation's economy getting better, worse, or staying the same? This is also an ABC poll out just two days ago, 59 percent say it is getting worse. That's a steep hill for Harris to climb.\par \par \uc2 What did you think of what the former president had to say today at this economic forum?\par \par \uc2 DAVID ASMAN, FOX BUSINESS ANCHOR: I thought a lot about it. First of all, I defined his economic policy. I was always kind of searching for, is he just a populist? Is he a free market? He's kind of a combination of \uc2 Ross Perot\uc2 . If you remember \uc2 Ross Perot\uc2 , he was very anti-free trade agreement with Mexico, the NAFTA Act, it was called back then, and \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , who was a big free trader for every -- so he's got a little bit of something for everybody, but he also has a little bit. for everybody to hate, particularly big business, big corporations in the United States who hated what he had to say about trade today, because he was kind of upping the ante on reciprocity.\par \par \uc2 Reciprocity simply means tit-for-tat. If, if China has 100 percent tariff on cars, we give them 100 percent tariffs. So, if somebody gives us 20 percent tariff on something, we'll give them a 20 percent.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Why shouldn't we?\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: But this -- exactly. But this went -- suggested that he would go a little bit beyond that, that he would actually up the ante for an entire industry, for example, if he wanted to build that industry strongly. That has a lot of people in the Chamber of Commerce and the other big corporate interests concerned about what that would mean to trade, in general, around the world.\par \par \uc2 And trade is -- you know, if you cut, cut back trade, we saw it before the, the depression, the Great Depression in the 30s, many people say that was one of the things that led to it, the Smoot-Hawley Act, which was a big bunch of tariffs put together worldwide.\par \par \uc2 However, having said all that, you look what the alternative is.\par \par \uc2 [15:05:02]\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has an economic plan that would jack up the corporate tax rate extraordinarily high, jack up all kinds of regulations that would particularly hit the small businesses. Small businesses can't deal with regulations the way big corporations can. Big corporations have lawyers, accountants, a whole slew of brainiacs to deal with these things. A small business, particularly one that's getting started, even if they, they get this bonus $25,000 or whatever from \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , they're going to have a really tough time.\par \par \uc2 And that's the big difference between Reagan and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  -- between Reagan. That was a Freudian slip between Trump and Kamala. Kamala believes in handouts, and Trump really believes in incentives, incentivizing people to work harder.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I think that's absolutely true. Quickly, you know, the tariffs to me are sort of more of a threat than a practicality.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Like, oh, this is what would happen, they would pay 100 percent. It's hanging over the head of this exchange and it impacts the exchange. And he did the same thing with the border. Here's what's going to happen if you don't stop people from coming. So, I mean, it's not exactly like, oh, it would cost this many dollars because it's the threat.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: I think you hit on a great point, which is that he has already begun negotiating.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: He knows that he has a very good chance of being the next president again, and he has already begun the process of negotiating -- of kind of threatening those people who would threaten us economically by coming out with a shot across the bow before he becomes president. So, I think that's right. He is already making a deal.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: David, thank you, always great to have you.\par \par \uc2 So, Harris supporters using some interesting tactics to go after the male vote as Trump's advantage among that group gets stronger in the polls.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm man enough to be emotional in front of my wife.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: In front of my kids.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: In front of my horse.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm man enough to tell you that I cry at Love Actually.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Ian Sams, senior spokesperson for the Harris campaign, and Faulkner Focus Anchor Harris Faulkner talk about her hosting of the town hall with former President Trump, coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [15:10:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN), U.S. VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: And I'm talking here on this one, especially to the guys. You keep hearing about this gap on there. I refuse to admit that that's real because I know that we care deeply. I know these issues matter to you. I know they matter to all of us. We need to get, especially young men, out there to vote. This is not damn WWE-type stuff.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. So, Vice President Harris' running mate, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , making his pitch to the guys at the \uc2 University of Wisconsin\uc2  in Eau Claire yesterday. Our Fox News power rankings show that former President Trump is doing better among men now than he did in the 2020 election. He is up over 2020 by another six points.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the group that is -- a group that is, a group that is backing Harris put out these videos that got a ton of pickup on social media. They are not from the campaign, but they are from a group that supports her. And they feature actors claiming that they are, quote, man enough to support Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Let's watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You think I'm afraid to rebuild a carburetor? I eat carburetors for breakfast.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I ain't afraid of bears. That's what bear hugs are for.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'll tell you another thing I sure as (BLEEP) am not afraid of, Women.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Harris Faulkner standing by in battleground Georgia, because tomorrow she is going to host a town hall there with former President Trump. We'll speak with her in just a moment. First, let's bring in Ian Sams, senior adviser for the Harris-Walz campaign. Ian, good to have you with us. Thank you very much for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 Do you embrace or disown those ads about being man enough?\par \par \uc2 IAN SAMS, SENIOR ADVISER, HARRIS-WALZ CAMPAIGN: Well, you know, thanks for having me, first of all. And, you know, it's political silly season, so, of course, there's a lot of comedians and groups out there making videos that don't necessarily reflect the view of the campaign. I'd say with Vice President Harris, she's trying to earn men's support across this country in the same way she's trying to earn women's support across this country, which is through ideas that actually help their lives, whether that's bringing tax cuts to 100 million middle class people or making sure that small businesses who people -- men out there who want to start a small business --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, listen, you know, because \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  brought it up, you know, I want to ask you --\par \par \uc2 SAMS: So, these are the things that she's running to make sure that she feels based on ideas instead of things like that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You hear \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  reaching out to men and saying, you know, I don't believe that there is this gender gap and you see where she's going to talk to voters over the coming days and she is reaching out to that male voter. So, why do you think she has lost six points since 2020 with male voters? Why do you think that is? What's behind that?\par \par \uc2 SAMS: Well, I think we'll see in three weeks when the votes get counted what the actual gender gap split is. And I think right now you could ask the exact same question, which is why \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is losing women by exponentially more --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, we're going to talk about that with Harris in just a minute. But my question to you is why is the gap growing with men?\par \par \uc2 SAMS: Well, I don't think that the national polls necessarily reflect an accurate snapshot. I mean, I think that in each battleground state, we're seeing different numbers, we're seeing her grow her support. The longer she talks about her ideas, including her economic ideas for the middle class, I think that she's growing that support every single day. I'm looking forward to Harris' event with President Trump.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But you must acknowledge, Ian, that when you look at where she's going and, you know, where she's doing campaign interviews right now, she's talking to Charlamagne Tha God, she just sat down with Roland Martin, she's losing men, and there's an effort, we have heard, to get on Joe Rogan as well. So, I mean, when you look at where a candidate is going, it's clear where they think they need ground to make up, right?\par \par \uc2 [15:15:00]\par \par \uc2 SAMS: Yes, I totally hear that. And you see \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  spending all his time on bro podcasts. And so I think that the question is, is he losing male support that he doesn't necessarily have if he's spending all that time talking to male comedians on podcasts are coming into conservative media and only talking to male anchors. I'm looking forward to his event with Harris with women --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. You're saying it's not happening. We'll see. But she's clearly spending a lot of time trying to reach those male voters. And she has said, you know, that I know I have to earn those mail voters' votes, and, clearly, that seems to be one of the priorities for the campaign.\par \par \uc2 On this Fox Power Rankings with regard to immigration, Ian, it says, do you think that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has explained her policies and plans to address illegal immigration? It was asked for of both candidates. Harris, yes, 38, no, 60 percent say that she has not articulated her plans to address illegal immigration. So, is that some -- is that one of the things that she hopes to clear up with Bret tomorrow night? And does she want to stop the millions of people from coming across the border?\par \par \uc2 SAMS: Well, I hope that tomorrow President Trump, who I know