d explained that in her administration, she would actually be even tougher than President Biden has been on raising the number before you can allow more people through the through the border at between the ports of entry.\par \par \uc2 So, she's actually proposing a stricter border crackdown --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Why do we allow anyone through the ports of entry? Can you answer that question to me? Why do we allow anyone to cross through the ports of entry?\par \par \uc2 SAMS: Well, I think that you've seen from this action that's been taken over the last six months dramatic reductions in crossings, undocumented crossings between the ports of entry. And I think that that's the kind of thing that we want to continue doing, which is bringing down illegal border crossings. And, in fact, that is exactly what her plan would do. And, unfortunately, President Trump blew it up because he wanted to run on the problem.\par \par \uc2 And can I make one point about men, because I think you raised something? I think that if you're a man, you're not afraid to put out your medical records. If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was a real man, he'd put out his medical records. And so I think he shouldn't be afraid to show the country his health and mental and physical fitness before this election. It's something we've been talking about for the last few days.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, if he's watching, I hope he understands the importance of telling the American people whether you're up for the job by showing your health information and showing whether you can be fit because his own former.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, maybe Harris will ask him about it. We haven't heard that being a major issue from voters when we ask people what they care about. It doesn't register very high. But we'll see. Maybe Harris will ask about that as well. And, Ian, I want to thank you very much for joining us, as always.\par \par \uc2 SAMS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good to see you. So, we are three weeks away, and there is so much for the country to decide over those three weeks.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Harris Faulkner in battleground Georgia, where early voting begins today. The Faulkner Focus anchor and Outnumbered co-host moderating an all women town hall with former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 So now the flip side of this question with former President Trump and women voters, hi, Harris, tell us what we can expect and look forward to tomorrow. Hi there.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS FAULKNER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Well, first of all, women know their power in every election, because we make up the largest active and registered group of voters in America. But they also know that every issue is their issue, and that they're not just specific things that you can tap women on the head and say, okay, we gave you that in terms of goodies.\par \par \uc2 So, the candidates are really in the last stretch for making their, Martha, you know this, they're closing arguments, particularly to that huge voting bloc.\par \par \uc2 [15:20:02]\par \par \uc2 And right now, from our power rankings at Fox News, we found that there's been a shift now late in the game to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , but only by three points.\par \par \uc2 So, with all of the good press and all the things, and talking about abortion in ways that she invokes freedom, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , and all of that, it hasn't gained her much. A cohesive down the stretch argument by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on the issue of abortion is something that voters want to see. That's what they're telling me here on the ground and you and I both talk to voters all the time with our interviews and so on and so forth. So, it's going to be interesting. We will dig into that topic as well as the economy, right? And I see the left playing race card, race card, race card with black men, black voters, whatever. The color is green. We're going to get into that.\par \par \uc2 So, I can't wait. A room full of women who are smart. I've been meeting some of them. I'm so excited and he's in the hot seat. It's going to be good. You might say he's outnumbered.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You can say that, and I bet you will say that tomorrow, and we're going to look forward to that. Speaking of what you just --\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: I hadn't thought of it until now.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I like it. It's a work in progress to all of us. So, I like that. I think you should keep it in.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Right?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: This is \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2 , you know, to the point that you just made, which is a great one, Harris. Here's what she had to say about what she's seeing in the polls right now, and this issue of black voters, Hispanic voters, women voters. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Have you noticed how sharply the polls have shifted in Trump's favor over the last couple weeks, with these claims that Trump is surging with black and Latino voters and leading in every single swing state, according to secret internal polls, despite all the Hitler talk? Yes, this race will be far too close, because, unfortunately, we do have a fascist groundswell in parts of this country, mainly among white men.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I guess that's not a big surprise, Harris.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: You know what? It is --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: No, not at all. And when she talks about fascism, are they discounting the fact that they took a man who actually, at this point, running against Trump, had better numbers than \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , took him off the ticket, and then put her on the ticket, with no way for voters to even make a say in the primary season? They just popped her on, anointed her, and kept going through a coronation at the \uc2 RNC\uc2  -- at the DNC convention, forgive me.\par \par \uc2 So, it is interesting that fascism is how they want to go,