s she had said, of black men, as polls show former President Trump is doing better with black voters than he did in 2016 or in 2020. So, you see that continued climb, 6 percent, 8 percent, 15 percent. And that is the biggest number ever for a Republican candidate, if that holds.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Harris said, as I said, she wants to earn those votes. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARTER: You're seeing, you know, in the polls and you're seeing black men aren't as, quote, excited or fired up for you. How do you respond to that?\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , U.S. VICE PRESIDENT, DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: Well, that's not my experience. Let's be clear that your underlying premise is not an assumption that I'm supposed to have black men in my back pocket in terms of their vote and that I should be taking that for granted because I don't. Black men are no different from anybody else. They expect that you have to earn their vote.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let's bring in Rob Smith, host of Can't Cancel Rob Smith podcast, and founder of Stop Woke, and an Iraq war veteran, and Jessica Tarlov, co-host of The Five, and a Fox News contributor, great to have both of you with us today.\par \par \uc2 Jessica, I want to get your reaction to that first. And I think that we are seeing just a broadening out of what makes people vote for the people they want to vote for that is going beyond the issue of color.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS HOST: Absolutely, I I don't think it was ever purely based on that, but I think that there has been an upheaval in terms of people speaking out about how they're being targeted and how they have felt about governments and their ability to deliver for them. And I think that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has learned the lesson, frankly, of past nominees in terms of saying the right thing, like I need to earn your vote, something that people had taken for granted before.\par \par \uc2 And Saturday Night Live actually in the weekend update had this great line that if you need 90 percent of a demographic to vote for you, maybe you have a flawed business model, and that we need we need to broaden it and we need to make sure that we're working as hard as possible.\par \par \uc2 [15:30:02]\par \par \uc2 And I think that's what her agenda for black men was about.\par \par \uc2 And I heard pushback like, isn't this racist? Isn't this pandering? But every group deserves to know exactly what it is that they're going to be getting from that administration. We don't balk at President Trump going out tomorrow. He's going to be talking to women who he has a huge problem with.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right. So let's play this from President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , because it continues to get a lot of attention, and I want to get your thoughts on this, Rob.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER US PRESIDENT: we have not yet seen the same kinds of energy and turnout in all quarters of our neighborhoods and communities as we saw when I was running. Now, I also want to say that that seems to be more pronounced with the brothers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: More pronounced with the brothers. Rob?\par \par \uc2 ROB SMITH, CAN'T CANCEL "ROB SMITH" HOST: Well, I don't think the Democrats, in any way, saw or foresaw the blowback that they were going to get from sending \uc2 Obama\uc2  out to basically shame black men for not supporting Harris enough, OK? And there's no universe in which if these poll numbers were holding up, you looked earlier, it said that 90 percent of black voters were for Democrats in 2020. Now we're looking at about 78 percent to 15 percent.\par \par \uc2 If these poll numbers are holding up for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  the same way that they would hold up for Democrats in the past, there's absolutely no way she would be going on a media blitz to target black men three weeks before an election. So there's obviously some desperation there. There's obviously some sort of turmoil there.\par \par \uc2 And what they're figuring out is that, you know, you have to talk to black men like you talk to every other demographic. Now, none other than \uc2 Nina Turner\uc2 , who was a far left progressive, went on one of the other shows and she basically said, the Democrats will not shame, bully or disrespect any other group of men, but they will do this to black men.\par \par \uc2 So I think that this is blowing up in their faces. I think it's a huge problem. And I think that this three weeks before the election, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and the Democrats certainly suddenly remembering that black men exist is going to be a little too -- too little too late.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I think --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: This been going on for a lot longer than three weeks. But this isn't something that --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But is it about the economy? I mean, these are people who everybody, you know, white people, black people, men, women --\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: A hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- want -- are upset about things they want to change. They shouldn't have to vote for someone just because --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Look, the agenda is rooted in economic issues. It's talking about black entrepreneurship. It's talking about mentorship and training programs, and apprenticeships. It is talking about the economy. Basically what's happened is the Harris campaign is being responsive to what they're hearing in these focus groups and in these town halls. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I have to push back on this because there's no way that we've been hearing this three days -- excuse me, three weeks before the election if that was true. And I think that with the Trump campaign has been doing is they're not running an identity based campaign. To your point, they've been talking about the economy, they've been talking about illegal immigrations like black men, black women, white men, white women. Like these are issues that affect all people --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I think that people are tired of hearing about themselves as voters in boxes --\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- and I think that is why, you know, people just are sort of repelled by. And when you ask, and I have asked former President Trump about women voters, he says, I think they like a good economy just as much as everybody else.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes, but they don't like him. He has a historic gender gap. I mean, Republicans typically don't do as well with women. This is beyond anything we've seen before. And you say they don't he doesn't talk to people like they're in boxes. Roll the tape of him talking to Jews, and how he says is going to be your fault if I lose, creating a dual identity, a dual loyalty issue for us. If you support Israel, you can't possibly vote for the Democrats, calling Doug Emhoff a crappy Jew.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, isn't that what Biden did when he said, if you don't support me, you ain't black.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: First of all, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is not on this ticket and that's the thing. This is a completely different race.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And seeing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has not only a Jewish daughter but a Jewish son-in-law --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: So what?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: -- and Jewish granddaughter --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It's like I have one --\par \par \uc2 SMITH: -- I don't think that that's pretty -- I don't think it's appropriate and I don't think it's kind of relevant to the conversation.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Really? You just said identity politics doesn't work, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  stands up there and --\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I didn't said anything about identity politics --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes really? People do not want to be siloed --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And you can also ask (inaudible) policy issue, you know, it's a question why not you support it.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Just saying as a Jew, we heard him loud and clear about this, and he has lost support from a lot of Jewish donors over the way that he talks about Jewish people and how we have a dual --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: (Crosstalk) Jewish support over --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I (inaudible) there's a big donors --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes. Some, I mean, we'll see how it pans out. She's actually running neck and neck where Biden was with it but --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We'll see. Thank you guys. Three weeks away, great to have you both here. Rob, thank you, Jessica, thank you.\par \par \uc2 So a chilling warning reportedly delivered directly to former President Trump, detailing Iran's vow to assassinate him within weeks. General Jack Keane, after this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Will you approve Mr. Trump's request to use military aircraft in the final stages of the campaign?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D), PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: As long as he didn't ask for 15. (Inaudible) I've told the department to give him every -- every single thing he needs for, any other -- (inaudible) president give all that he needs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Bombshell report in \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  today detailing a meeting between \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and intelligence officials last month, informing the former president of Iran's goal to assassinate him before Election Day. The Biden administration since delivered its own message to Tehran, warning that any attempt on Trump's life would amount to an act of war against the United States.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in retired four Star General Jack Keane, former Army Vice Chief of Staff, now Chairman of the Institute for the Study of War and Fox News Senior Strategic Analyst. General, great to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 This report goes on to say that US intelligence agencies assess that the biggest challenge for Iran was getting an operative into the United States and close to the former president, and that they were leaning on an overseas criminal syndicate or a group that could hire a potential hitman to do the job for them. What's your reaction to all of this?\par \par \uc2 JACK KEANE, FOX NEWS SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: Well, first of all, we've got to take it seriously. Obviously, there's been two attempts on the former president's life and a heightened threat from Iran. You have to understand where Iran is coming on this. The supreme leader had a personal and professional relationship with \uc2 Qasem Soleimani\uc2 . I mean, his foreign policy strategically was to dominate and control the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 He turned that over for implementation, \uc2 Qasem Soleimani\uc2 , who organized the proxies to do just that and implement the Supreme Leader's strategy. He trusted him more than he trusted any other government official. So when he was killed, that had a dramatic impact on him, personally and obviously professionally.\par \par \uc2 So he's involved in this and has directed it. There's just no doubt in my mind about that. And given the force that's behind that, there's some in government who say, well, the Iranians can't really pull this off. They don't have the network in the United States to do it.\par \par \uc2 I say you don't you don't -- don't even go down that line. Don't even talk about something like that and be dismissive of it. Take the threat seriously and work to protect the former president and all the other officials who are under this threat of assassination. And that means good intelligence work to get them.\par \par \uc2 And you're right, Martha, you're accurately pointing out that what they do is bring an Iranian operative into the country, and that operative attempts to hire somebody else to do it because they want deflection. You know, typical of them, they use the proxies as deflection. They're not directly involved. They want to have some other assassin do it, but that makes it much more difficult for them in trying to find somebody who's trustworthy, smart enough, skilled enough to be able to do something like that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I think of the guy who had an AK-47 on Masih Alinejad's porch in Brooklyn. She has been a very outspoken freedom fighter for the women of Iran. Quick question for you on this one, I just have about half a minute, sir. Your reaction to this report of the drones flying over Langley on a regular basis that are unidentifiable by the US military reportedly, and they also went over Nevada and, more recently, Edwards Air Force Base. What's going on here do you think?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Well, obviously some kind of surveillance and we've got rules, you know, against doing something like that, and why we permit that to happen is beyond me. I don't think we should just let people surveil our military bases, particularly something as significant as the ones that you're mentioning, without taking some action against them. We've got a right to protect ourselves.\par \par \uc2 If somebody was trying to come through the gate to surveillance, we'd stop them. Why are we not stopping them in the air? Just common sense, let's apply it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, you would shoot them down.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: If necessary, yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. General Keane, thank you very much. Always good to see you, sir.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: OK. Great talking to you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you very much. Swift blowback from the Catholic community to this video, which some believed to be mocking Holy Communion.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: In battleground states separated by razor thin margins, the power of the Catholic vote can have huge implications. We were talking about different voting groups earlier, and this is another one to take a look at. It could explain why Harris surrogate and Michigan Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  is now apologizing for this Dorito moment that some Catholics were outraged by and said that it was mocking the sacrament of Holy Communion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Mm, so that led some to stage a rosary rally outside the governor's mansion in protest. Let's bring in Bill McGurn, former Chief Speechwriter for President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  and a Fox News Contributor. His new Wall Street Journal column, which is a must read, writes Whitmer's video was a "in kind contribution to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's campaign". Bill, always good to have you with us. Why do you feel that way?\par \par \uc2 BILL MCGURN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, it's pretty obvious the white working class Catholic vote is a declining demographic, but people like \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  built their careers on it. It happens to be very important in swing states when they're all separated by a point, a shift in the Catholic vote could really make difference.\par \par \uc2 For example, Trump won the Catholic vote in 2016, lost it by one point to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in 2020 and lost the election. And Harris' numbers look more like \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So you also point out Pennsylvania is about 30 percent --\par \par \uc2 MCGURN: Thirty percent from the exit polls --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCGURN: -- last time.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So how does this play out in Pennsylvania?\par \par \uc2 MCGURN: Well, we don't know but we know that it, you know, it's a one point game. So anyone like you had people on talking about the black vote, black men and so forth. When it's so close, it's like a vote in the Senate that's by one vote. Everyone is deciding vote. And this constituent, Catholic constituency, they seem to make a difference in the past, very heavy, the white working class Catholic in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 So it could really come back to haunt the Harris campaign. Not so much \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  but it kind of illustrates how out of touch the Democrats are. Elite Democratic opinion is with ordinary Democratic voters in their coalition.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, the fact that she claimed that she had no idea that that could be interpreted that way?\par \par \uc2 MCGURN: I can believe her because I think that there -- they can be clueless about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  would not have made that mistake.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Correct.\par \par \uc2 MCGURN: And I can believe that they're so clueless to say, so out of touch. You know, so many times a gaffe is most damaging when the people don't realize what they're doing, what the effect, how other people are going to read that. I mean, it was pretty obvious what that ad was, objectively. The Catholic bishops opposed it, so it was pretty -- it's hard to look at that and not think communion.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, absolutely. This week is the \uc2 Al Smith\uc2  Dinner, which is a very big tradition. It's a huge fundraiser for \uc2 Catholic Charities\uc2  which do a tremendous amount of good around the world. But every four years they host, Cardinal Dolan, or whoever is the cardinal or archbishop at the time, host the Democrat and Republican candidate on either side of him. And it says that right on the invitation, guests of honor, Democratic candidate, Republican candidate.\par \par \uc2 MCGURN: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: In 40 years that -- it has never happened that someone declined. Why do you think \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  decline --\par \par \uc2 MCGURN: Yes. Again, I think they don't know. One could be her calculation. This is not constituency. I have to please anymore. But it was a tradition, a nice tradition. Two candidates come together in a civil context, help charity. And I think this is one of those things that's worse than a crime, a blunder by passing it up.\par \par \uc2 I don't know why they did. \uc2 Al Smith\uc2  was, of course, the Democratic nominee in '28, the first Catholic on the major party ticket to run for president.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. It also gives the opportunity to give a little bit of a roast to the other --\par \par \uc2 MCGURN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: She is --\par \par \uc2 MCGURN: The good nature -0-\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- happen to get the opportunity --\par \par \uc2 MCGURN: Good nature speeches on both sides.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Bill McGurn, always good to have you.\par \par \uc2 MCGURN: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you so much. So, two wounded survivors of the first attempt on President Trump's life in Butler, Pennsylvania are speaking out for the first time about Secret Service failures as they see it in Butler, Pennsylvania and how that day changed their lives forever.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID DUTCH, PENNSYLVANIA ASSASSINATION ATTEMPT SURVIVOR: I was more mad about it than anything. I mean, I was in the service, went through a war, didn't even get a scratch. And you go to a rally and you get shot in the crowd.\par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: When did you realize something was wrong?\par \par \uc2 JAMES COPENHAVER, PENNSYLVANIA ASSASSINATION ATTEMPT SURVIVOR: When I saw part of my sleeve go away and I heard pshew (sp?).\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The two men shot and wounded in the first assassination attempt on former President Trump, say that the Secret Service "failed them" and the -- them and the former president, I should say, at that rally in Butler, Pennsylvania. Seventy-four-year-old James Copenhaver and 57-year- old David Dutch say that they are still recovering from their injuries three months later.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Did the Secret Service fail former President Trump and fail you that day?\par \par \uc2 DUTCH: Oh, yes, big -- big time.\par \par \uc2 COPENHAVER: It was a rush job, you know, that was put together too quickly and they skipped steps. (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, 50 year old Corey Comperatore was killed at that rally protecting his wife and daughter. The acting Secret Service director says the agency takes full responsibility for the security failures, and he is calling for a "paradigm shift" in their operations. And we've just heard about the ongoing threat to the former president from Iranian operatives.\par \par \uc2 So \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  co-chair \uc2 Lara Trump\uc2  helping with the recovery efforts in her home state of North Carolina, where there is still so much devastation, look at that, more than two weeks after Hurricane Helene. Correspondent Danamarie McNicholl reporting live from Asheville, North Carolina. Hi, Danamarie.\par \par \uc2 DANAMARIE MCNICHOLL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. I'm here to show you what's left of the River Arts District here in Asheville. This is such an important time for tourism, and what stands right behind me is an art studio completely destroyed. We see baby clothing, we see furniture. We see antiques completely covered in mud. This was a really special community here that we know artists from all over came to make it their home.\par \par \uc2 The president of the River Arts District estimates 80 percent of this district destroyed, affecting over 300 working artists who have lost studio equipment and their cherished artwork and print. One artist told us that everything has been picked up by flood water dropped somewhere else in the area, so recovering their special art has been hard. They say the paintings have still been stuck in the ceilings. Artists are saying it's also really extremely difficult to clean up the mud here, because there's still no running water in this area.\par \par \uc2 Now, Jeffrey Burroughs, he's the president who got emotional and started to cry, saying that, you know what, the shock is wearing off. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY BURROUGHS, RIVER ARTS DISTRICT PRESIDENT: I was just standing on the bridge and looked down, and it was just art and beer, and wine, and shipping containers. And it felt so hopeless in that moment, because it was like the dreams and everything we had worked for were literally just being washed away.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCNICHOLL: And that's the same sentiment of a lot of artists that have gathered here today to clean up some of the stuff. Now, we heard from Governor Cooper and FEMA at a press conference this morning, they gave us some updates. They said about 170,000 people have applied for individual assistance with FEMA. They also said about $99 million has already been handed out to survivors here, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Danamarie, thank you very much. Very tough time still in Western North Carolina to be sure.\par \par \uc2 That is "The Story" for this Tuesday, October 15, and it is Tuesday. We are three weeks to go until Election Day. We'll see you back here tomorrow at 3:00. Big day tomorrow, Harris' town hall in Georgia and Bret Baier's interview with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  tomorrow evening. Your World starts right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020241016ekaf00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Biden Visits Areas Hit By Hurricane Milton In Florida; Trump, Harris Both Visited Arizona Border In 2024; Interview With Republican Vice Presidential Candidate J.D. Vance Walz (R-OH); Vance on Whether Would Support a National Abortion Ban, Campaign's Plan to Defund Planned Parenthood; Interview with Gov. \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  (D-MN), Outlines Stance on Electoral College; Debate Over Gun Rights; Harris-Walz Courting Black Voters; Trump Aims To Win Over Young, Male Voters; Some Top Democrats About Harris Losing Momentum; Florida Begins Recovery from Milton; Florida Gas Supply Remains Tight After Hurricane Milton; Election Impact from Hurricanes Helene & Milton; Late Sen. Lieberman's Book Uses Biblical Examples to Promote Healthy Political Debates\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Richard Fowler, Jonathan Serrie, Bill Melugin,  Alexis McAdams \par \uc2 10,431 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 Today one on one with both vice presidential candidates, only on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE (R-OH), VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: If you want a government that puts the interest of American citizens first, the only person to vote for is \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN), VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: This country and this world can't take four more years of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): With former President Trump and Vice President Harris neck and neck across every battleground, the running mates hoping to stand beside them in the White House join us to outline each of their ticket's vision to address America's biggest challenges -- from uncertainty surrounding the economy and the future of trade, to the escalating crisis in the Middle East, a contentious debate over abortion here at home, and how they each plan to unite an increasing divided nation.\par \par \uc2 Republican VP nominee J.D. Vance and Democrat VP nominee Tim Waltz, back for round two --\par \par \uc2 WALZ: I think folks know who I am, I'm proud to be on the ticket.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- both this hour only on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 Then, a second deadly storm batters the American South, leaving tens of thousands in Florida scrambling to pick up the pieces in the wake of yet another disaster.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You work all your life, you retire, and this is what you get.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm still full of emotion.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Our Sunday panel on the fallout for the economy and the 2024 election.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): Hello from FOX News here in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Here are some of your top headlines:\par \par \uc2 President Biden approving a major disaster declaration for Florida, ahead of his visit today to communities hit hard by Hurricane Milton, adding now into eight already proved for Hurricane Helene.\par \par \uc2 Israel is ordering the evacuation of more towns in southern Lebanon as it strikes Iran-backed \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  militant targets in that area the \uc2 IDF\uc2  says more than 300 projectiles were fired into Israel from Lebanon this weekend.\par \par \uc2 Both presidential candidates are on the campaign trail today. President Trump in battleground Arizona and Vice President Harris in North Carolina. Both of their running mates join us here on "FOX News Sunday" today.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, our interview with Republican vice presidential candidate J.D. Vance and then Democratic VP candidate \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  will join us.\par \par \uc2 But, first, we've got team coverage with FOX News correspondent Alexis McAdams covering Vice President Harris, and Bill Melugin reporting on President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Let's start with you, Bill, there in Prescott Valley, Arizona.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is continuing his blitz of battleground states with just weeks left to go before Election Day. Today, he's going to be here in Arizona where early voting is already underway and where it is expected to be a very, very tight race.\par \par \uc2 Take a look, the most recent FOX News polling out of Arizona shows \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  with a very narrow lead over VP \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  when it comes to registered voters here in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 However, a "Wall Street Journal" poll out last week shows the opposite. Take a look. It shows Harris with the slim lead over \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  amongst registered voters here in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Now, both Trump and Harris already made visits to the Arizona border back in September. Specifically, they both went to Border Patrol's Tucson, Arizona sector. That is the sector that saw the most illegal crossing of any sector along the southern border in fiscal year 2024, as illegal immigration is obviously expected to play a massive role with Arizona voters in this election.\par \par \uc2 And back out here live, former President Trump is scheduled to speak here in Prescott Valley, around 5:00 p.m. Eastern Time. He'll be coming here from California after he held a rally in Coachella last night -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Bill Melugin in Arizona, thank you, Bill.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to Alexis McAdams, who's been traveling with the Harris team in Greenville, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Alexis.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Shannon. Good morning from Greenville, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 This is going to be \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's 19th stop to the Tar Heel State, if you can believe it, just since she announced she was running for president. It shows you just how critical and crucial this swing state is again this election.\par \par \uc2 Now, recently, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , Shannon, released her medical history, saying she's in perfect health, now pressuring former President Trump to do the same.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES & 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We know that he does not have the ability to do the job. He is unfit for office. He talks at his rallies about fictional characters. He constantly is in a state of grievance about himself. He has no plans for the American people and then he just makes things up on a full-time basis.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Now, this weekend, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  stopping in Raleigh, North Carolina, to meet with leaders of the Black community here, talking about her plans for the future of this country, with just days until the election.\par \par \uc2 Now, Harris is holding a rally today in Greenville, North Carolina, hitting this state hard, Shannon, because the latest polling, especially from "The Wall Street Journal's" poll that just came out shows former President Trump with a slight edge in North Carolina, ahead by just one point.\par \par \uc2 And Harris says she knows the race is going to be close.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: So, this is a tight race, but the choice is clear. The voters have a choice between what we are offering, which is to look to the future of America and invest in the American people, or a \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who is constantly trying to take us backwards.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Now, from North Carolina, Harris will then head to Pennsylvania, Michigan, and Wisconsin, Shannon, which shows you just how important those states are, too. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes they are.\par \par \uc2 Alexis, thank you very much, reporting from North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Republican vice presidential nominee, Ohio Senator J.D. Vance.\par \par \uc2 Senator, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE (R-OH), VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Thanks for having me, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 All right. There's a lot of new polling out there. But let's start with the gender gap because there is a double digit lead for the vice president among women in nearly all of the battleground states.\par \par \uc2 "Politico" makes this observation: Rather than pick a running mate who appealed to suburban women in swing states, he chose J.D. Vance who was engineered to appeal to that self-styled alpha male crowd.\par \par \uc2 They suggest that you guys are banking on the bro vote to carry you across the finish line.\par \par \uc2 "Politico" has this line says, they've given up on the idea that they can get women voters.\par \par \uc2 Are you giving up on us?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: No, not at all, Shannon. Of course, that's -- that's a completely ridiculous argument for "Politico" to make.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, I'm -- I'm aware of the gender gap. I see the polling that everybody else does.\par \par \uc2 I also think there's some evidence that we've made some progress in the last few weeks, and, of course, we're still a few weeks from the actual uh game day here. And so, we're going to keep on making our argument to women voters, to male voters, to everybody that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the candidate of peace and prosperity.\par \par \uc2 I think two issues in particular where I just make my pitch directly to women voters, Shannon, is number one on public safety, we have a lot of young Americans right now who are dying of fentanyl overdoses. Fentanyl was brought into our communities, of course, through \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's open border.\par \par \uc2 Don't we want to raise our children as moms and dads in communities that are safe enough for kids to make mistakes without that mistake being laced with fentanyl and taking their life? Of course, don't we want our communities to not have these criminal gangs like we see in Aurora, Colorado, running rampage in that city?\par \par \uc2 I think that a lot of women voters, but frankly a lot of men voters, too, care a lot about public safety and I -- clearly, President Trump is the candidate of public safety.\par \par \uc2 The final point I make, Shannon, is that inflation often hits young women and women harder than other groups. Of course, we know that a lot of households, women do the grocery shopping. That's not true in my household, as a matter of fact.\par \par \uc2 But we know that women sometimes are the ones who see the effects of these higher rents, higher grocery prices, higher electric and utility bills because they're the ones who are often, you know, administering things. And I think that we can do such a better job for women to make it possible for them to afford the basic necessities of life.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Especially single moms because we know, Shannon, for single moms, they're the only ones often doing these things. They don't have a whole lot of help.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: They need their government to not be screwing things up so badly.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, listen, the -- this administration is going to point to good numbers that they got on the economy this week.\par \par \uc2 This is the headline the DNC sent out: Inflation drops to lowest level since pandemic under Biden Harris leadership. Trump's plans would cause, quote, inflation bomb.\par \par \uc2 They're touting the vice president's plans to cut taxes for the middle class, give a leg up to small businesses, help with child tax credits, which I know is one of the few things that you guys actually agree on, bring down the cost of home ownership -- they have different ideas about how to do that, they say, versus your plan which is just to give tax cuts to your wealthy friends.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, Shannon, I think that's ridiculous in a -- in a few ways. So, first of all, they're trying to accuse \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  of doing something that he didn't do during the four years that he was president. We have to remember that he delivered the fastest take-home pay increases in a generation. We had inflation at 1.5 percent and yes, he did selectively use tariffs to bring American manufacturing back to the United States to increase the wages of workers and to push back against communist China.\par \par \uc2 But he did that while delivering low inflation. And so, in some ways, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's entire argument Shannon is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to somehow do something radically different than what he did during his first four years in office.\par \par \uc2 What President Trump is in fact saying is we need to build upon the success of his four years in office, and frankly, get away from the record of failure of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , three and a years in office, trillions and trillions of dollars of new spending.\par \par \uc2 We have more American manufacturing workers that are struggling. We have a wide-open southern border and most importantly, whether it's single moms or single dads, we've got people who can't afford groceries and rent. That is not a record to hang your hat on, but that's the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  record.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, let's talk about tariffs because you mentioned that, and there are a lot of critics who say what you and President Trump are now talking about is something much broader than a targeted tariff. They say it's immediately going to add thousands of dollars of new costs for Americans every year.\par \par \uc2 A couple of former members of Congress, a Republican and a Democrat, wrote a piece in "The Hill" this week warning about this. They say a major element of his economic plan is the imposition of high tariffs on imports which would be much more likely to lead to a depression than anything that Harris is proposing.\par \par \uc2 They walk through President Hoover's decision to improve broad tariffs after the October '29 crash in the markets and they say it led to the same things they worry would happen now -- higher cost of living for all Americans, a tougher time higher tariffs on U.S. exports, and actually killing a lot of manufacturing jobs here in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 So why not be more precision about it than what it sounds like President Trump is suggesting on the campaign trail?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, first of all, Shannon, I think those -- those representatives fundamentally misunderstand history.\par \par \uc2 As President Trump says all the time, you know, William McKinley and Teddy Roosevelt built the American industrial powerhouse that we have today using tariffs to ensure that American workers got a fair deal and foreign competitors weren't able to undercut the wages of our workers.\par \par \uc2 We have to sort of back up and ask ourselves -- well, what is a tariff because people accuse it of being something that it's not. Think about if you're a foreign competitor working in China, using literal Chinese slave labor at $3 a day, an American middle class worker is never going to be able to compete on cost alone. We can compete on quality with those foreign slave laborers in China.\par \par \uc2 So if you're going to use slave labor in China what a tariff says is that you're going to pay a fat penalty before bringing those products back into the United States. That's really the only way to ensure that America has a viable industrial base. And, Shannon, we have to remember --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, why not do that in more precise way, a more surgical way than what we've been hearing from the campaign trail? Does President Trump plan something that is more targeted or is it as broad as we've been led to believe by his own statements?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, Shannon, I think that President Trump can do these things in a broad-based way, while also being targeted at the nations and at the industries that America most needs.\par \par \uc2 We have to remember, Shannon, that we followed the advice of those congressmen for 30 years in this country. China now has 32 percent of world manufacturing GDP. America has 18 percent of world manufacturing GDP.\par \par \uc2 Unless we use the power that we have economically to bring back good manufacturing, we're going to wake up in a country 20, 30 years from now where everything that we need, the pharmaceuticals that we put into the bodies of our children, the weapons of war that our troops use are made by foreign countries that don't like us very much.\par \par \uc2 The only way to prevent that and the only way to rebuild the prosperity we had 30 years ago is to do what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is proposing.\par \par \uc2 One final point on this tariff issue, Shannon, is for 200 years, the American economic miracle was built by using tariffs when appropriate to ensure that American workers could thrive.\par \par \uc2 We've gone the other direction for 30 years at the time that our middle class has been shrinking, inequality has been going up. We've tried the experiment of shipping our jobs to China and building prosperity off that. It's a failed experiment.\par \par \uc2 We need to get back to the Trump approach of rebuilding the American middle class and the manufacturing sector that really is the foundation of that middle class.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay, I want to get to the Trump and Vance approach when it comes to abortion. Clearly, that's one of the issues that appears to be leading to this gender gap that you guys have a disadvantage with.\par \par \uc2 Governor Walz when he joined us last week said here that you have supported a national abortion ban in the past. He may have been referring to this position you took in 2022. This is from your Senate debate then.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DEBATE MODERATOR: You would vote for \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 's 15-week ban?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: As I just said, I think it's totally reasonable to say you cannot abort a baby, especially for elective reasons, after 15 weeks of gestation. No civilized country allows it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. So not a federal ban per se on the procedure everywhere, but if you're talking about 15 or 20-week ban nationally, that is going to cut off access to a lot of people who have greater access in a number of different states.\par \par \uc2 Now, you've talked about how this is back to the states. Has your position on that changed? Would you vote for something that was a 15-, 20-week ban at the national level?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, Shannon, first of all, I do think it's reasonable to say, at a certain level, late-term abortions are barbaric if they're done for elective reasons. Of course, you have to have the exceptions but late-term abortions for elective reasons is something that no civilized country allows.\par \par \uc2 And certainly, there is a contrast here between Governor Walz and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . They want late term abortions funded by taxpayers forced on Christian hospitals and that's not something that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and I like. But even within that moral framework --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, but when you talk about a 15-week or 20-week ban, people will say that sounds like a national ban. I mean, we're just playing with semantics here.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: No, no, it's -- it's not, Shannon, because look, President Trump who, of course, is the leader of the Republican Party, has explicitly said that he would not support a national abortion ban. He has said that he thinks this issue should be left to the states. That is the official policy of the Republican Party in this election, and it's something that clearly President Trump believes in.\par \par \uc2 And I think it's important to say why, Shannon, because it illustrates how different our approaches are here. President Trump recognizes that California is a different state from Alabama, which is a different state from Pennsylvania. We want the voters in these states to make these decisions. We're sick of the nationalized culture war over this topic. And I think the best way is to return it to the voters in the states. President Trump has certainly made that the official policy of our party.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And quickly, on \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , you guys have talked about defunding them, taking away government funding. They say that no U.S. taxpayer funding can fund to abortion, can flow to abortion, at any stage. But they say they do hundreds of thousands of cancer screenings, birth control, which they say actually prevents another 500,000 abortions a year, and all kinds of other testing and medication contraception. Where would those patients go if \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  lost the funding?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, Shannon, what we've said is that we don't want taxpayer funding for abortions. And that, of course, has been the bipartisan approach over 40 years in this country, until, frankly, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  came along.\par \par \uc2 I know that \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  does a lot of things that a lot of young women, a lot of young families need. We're talking about we don't believe in taxpayer funding for abortions. That's been the policy, not just of the Republican Party, by the way, but even a lot of Democrats who are pro- choice, who want wide access to abortion, will say, we don't think taxpayers should be funding this stuff. And that, again, that bipartisan consensus is something President Trump very much supports.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so maybe not a full defunding of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , per se, but anything that you would be able to potentially track, which they say doesn't happen, but to abortion.\par \par \uc2 We have so many more questions. We hope you will come back soon, as Governor Walz is doing this week as well.\par \par \uc2 Senator, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Thanks for having me, Shannon. Take care\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, Democratic vice presidential candidate \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  is back with us, right here, only on Fox News Sunday. He's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, moments ago you heard from Republican Vice Presidential candidate J.D. Vance. And now to our exclusive interview with the Democratic VP candidate, Minnesota Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 . Welcome back for a second week in a row.\par \par \uc2 Good to see you, Governor.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN), VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Yeah, thanks, Shannon. Thanks for having me back.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so listen, I still have a lot of questions. I'm glad you're back for more of a conversation.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But when I heard that you wanted to come back this week, your team reached out. I was a little bit surprised. What's that about?\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Well, look, folks deserve to hear where we stand on this. Vice President Harris and I have an agenda for, you know, a new way forward, a manufacturing agenda. I was just in Michigan this week. And I think folks are still undecided out there. And I appreciate you. You ask good, hard questions and your viewers get a chance to hear.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, we appreciate you coming because we do want our viewers to hear from everybody. They've got decisions to make. Although you and I both, I don't know, it's hard to find an undecided voter at this point. But you've come across some. So maybe they're watching today.\par \par \uc2 OK, let's ask about the Electoral College. Because this week you made some news when you said it, quote, "needs to go."\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Campaign said not their position. You say you line up with the campaign now. One author who's written a book about why America has the Electoral College says this in a "Politico" piece.\par \par \uc2 "Cities already are the homes of America's major media, donor, academic and government centers. A simple direct democracy will centralize all power, government, business, money, media and votes in urban areas to the detriment of the rest of the nation."\par \par \uc2 Now, you did once refer to red areas on a map as, quote, "mostly rocks and cows." But the founders clearly wanted to protect voices in those areas. Do you not?\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Yeah. Well, the rest of that quote that they cut off was I said that includes my mom and my family. Look, my point on this was is people feel and they have to feel every vote counts in every place of the country.\par \par \uc2 And I think what the point was on this is, is they see us in battleground states and not across the country. So the campaign's position is clear on this. That's not to get rid of the Electoral College, but it's to focus on every vote.\par \par \uc2 And I think your viewers out there listening want to see these campaigns in every corner talking to every person. And look, as a rural guy, that's, again, you know how politics goes, cut off the back part of this. My point being on that was is those are areas that need to be heard. So every voter out there, every vote needs to count.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so let's get back to some things we didn't get to discuss last week. Let's talk about the issue of censorship and free speech. You've said there's no guarantee to free speech for misinformation or hate speech.\par \par \uc2 But first of all, Supreme Court very clear in a unanimous 2017 decision that hate speech is protected. That's the kind of stuff that needs to be protected, stuff none of us like to hear. But more importantly, who gets to decide on the issue of what misinformation is?\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Look, the First Amendment is foundational. It's something I spent my whole life, the vice president, and I think most Americans are clear on this. The point being on this, we're seeing censorship coming in the form of book bannings in different places. We're seeing it attempts in schools.\par \par \uc2 The issue on this was is the hate speech and the protected hate speech. Speech that's aimed at creating violence, speech that's aimed at threats to individuals. And that's what we're talking about in this. And the decision on that society decides on a lot of this. The idea of someone going on and threatening someone's life or a child's life online. I don't think any of your viewers think that that is something that should be acceptable.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: No, no, no, but that's not missing information. I mean, there's a distinction, you would say, though, between something like a threat and misinformation.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Certainly. Certainly. And that's what we're talking about. And I think the idea of making sure that folks understand are good consumers of that. Those are the decisions they make. But I've always defended your First Amendment rights, defend your right to disagree on every case. That robust discussion has to happen. And that's why I'm so opposed to book banning. That's why in Minnesota we ban the practice of book banning.\par \par \uc2 But you make the decision of what you're going to read, whether you find it objectionable or not. The difference is threats, that type of speech.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. A distinction there. OK, let's talk about guns, too. Because back in 2010, you had an A rating from the \uc2 NRA\uc2 . You were happy about it then. You said this.\par \par \uc2 I'm proud to stand with the \uc2 NRA\uc2  to protect our Second Amendment rights. And I'm truly grateful for their endorsement. Now, by 2018, you'd gone all the way to an F. But you were also then happy about that rating. Your running mate has said more than once, the vice president, that she is for mandatory gun buybacks. She also said at one point in the past, even if you legally possess a gun in the sanctity of your locked home, that doesn't mean that we're not going to walk in and check on things.\par \par \uc2 Now, you both say you're gun owners. So are you OK with that? Someone at some point saying you have to give one of your guns back, all of your guns back. And or somebody doing a warrantless search in your home of guns that you legally own.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Yeah, no one's talking about that. And I was with my guns yesterday out pheasant hunting with my friends --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We saw you.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: -- many of them conservatives. Many of them conservative on this. And I just want to be clear on this. The \uc2 NRA\uc2  is an organization that I grew up with was about gun safety. There has been a dramatic shift, and I don't think anybody can argue that, where the \uc2 NRA\uc2  went, especially on things that we can't even do research on, gun violence.\par \par \uc2 And I've made it clear that sitting with the Sandy Hook parents, sitting with some of these kids who were in schools where there were school shootings, and having friends that were around these things had a profound impact on me.\par \par \uc2 But look, I will defend the Second Amendment. And there's nothing that we're proposing, whether it's extreme risk protection orders or background checks, that will stop you from owning that. And I think we're talking about things like trigger locks and things that responsible gun owners do anyway.\par \par \uc2 So, look, this is a red herring issue that we can own guns, protect the Second Amendment. And then our first responsibility is protect our children and society. And I know we can do that. I think this idea of just brushing it off, saying, well, this is just the way things are, and why would we not research what's causing this? Why would the gun manufacturers oppose understanding exactly why America is so different than other gun-owning countries?\par \par \uc2 So, look, no one's coming for your guns. I continue to buy them. The Vice President is a gun owner. So many of your viewers are. But they also are concerned that we don't need to see our children shot in schools.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. I think everybody can agree on that. Now, the \uc2 NRA\uc2  would say that they are the foremost when it comes to programs for safety for hunters, for kids, for gun owners, that that is still part of their mission.\par \par \uc2 But this issue of whether Americans should be forced to give their guns back at some point or whether a warrantless search is OK, I'm assuming you're opposed to both of those.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Yeah, nobody's proposing that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Nobody's proposing your guns. That's exactly right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. All right.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: The thing is, Shannon, that I'm most concerned with, though, is, like guns in homes, the number one thing is children getting the gun and shooting themselves. We do education campaigns here. We give out free gun locks. 40,000 of these things at the state fair a couple years ago, those are legal gun owners saying, you're right, we can do some of these things.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, and I think that if you have children in your home, absolutely you've got an obligation to make sure --\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- that those things are under lock and key. OK. Now --\par \par \uc2 WALZ: That's right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- it appears that your evolution politically, your years in Congress, there in Minnesota, you've moved to the left. I think that is fair to say. Here's what the "Intelligencer" says, "Walz had a fairly conservative voting record in Congress where he represented a red district. He used that record to win the governorship and then moved sharply left."\par \par \uc2 Here's something that you said weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Don't ever shy away from our progressive values. One person's socialism is another person's neighborliness.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So Merriam-Webster Dictionary on Socialism says it's a system of society or group living in which there's no private property or this, condition of society in which the means of production are owned and controlled by the state. Did you mean to advocate for that here in the U.S.?\par \par \uc2 WALZ: No, no. Shannon, look, I'm a capitalist. I was making the point of is Republicans oftentimes talk about socialism. And what I would make the case of is we build our roads collectively together. I don't think anybody's arguing that you should have to build your own road from your house to your business place or whatever. So I said this definition that the right uses about this, things that we collectively do together, that look, I believe in moral capitalism. I believe capitalism works and it lifts everyone up.\par \par \uc2 But I also believe you have to make sure that there's things that we collectively do together. We educate our children by using our public schools. We build roads. We do some of the things like public health where we work together.\par \par \uc2 So the point being on this is of trying to divide that. But you certainly can't have a capitalism that says billionaires get everything and the middle class gets nothing. And I think \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' point on this is lifting up the middle class, making an opportunity economy, making housing affordable, making drug prices more affordable. That's what we're talking about. And so I said don't listen to them tell you that these things that we can do together, but also we want people to succeed.\par \par \uc2 And when you succeed, all we're asking is that you pay your fair share. I'm excited when people become, if they make it, their business grows, they become wealthy, that's fine. Just pay your fair share and then all of us do better.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so the word, did you not mean to use the word socialism? Was it a knucklehead thing or did you mean to use it?\par \par \uc2 WALZ: No, it was what the right uses that their -- would you describe Shannon and your viewers that building roads is socialism?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: No, but that's also not the government controlling all property and means of funding those roads. And taking from you and saying you don't have your private funds. Although people would argue about taxes, some people argue that they do feel like it feels confiscatory.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: No, we made no -- we made no -- look, the point being is, is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says that this is a socialist or Marxist government. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is actually doing just the opposite of that, creating a free economy that works and people can grow into the middle class. So I think the argument here is, is that just trying to blanket over.\par \par \uc2 Most people recognize that the Infrastructure Act is creating massive private wealth because of building that out together. And that's what \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has done. Understanding that the things that government should do, government can do. The things the private sector can do, they should.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: That's a big difference from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  skewing things towards the wealthy.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and listen, when you want to talk about the tax cuts, that they're going to be sunsetted unless there is -- depending on who wins and what happens in Congress as well, \uc2 IRS\uc2  status shows that that did benefit every class of taxpayer. So, you know, of course, the Trump Vance team is going to want to argue about that.\par \par \uc2 But let me ask you this. Your Minnesota record, this is a piece written in the Wall Street Journal by a longtime Minneapolis resident and attorney there. He says, under Mr. Walz, Minnesota has become a high crime state. Student achievement has tumbled as spending on schools has skyrocketed. Per capita gross domestic product has fallen below the national average.\par \par \uc2 And he says Minnesotans have joined the residents of New York, California and Illinois, fleeing their home states. And notes that you were elected by, you know, a much tighter margin the second time around than you were the first time. Because he says people are leaving with their feet and their votes and that they don't like the way that you govern Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Yeah, well, I won by nearly eight points on that. What I would say is it's a Republican writing that. I wouldn't be surprised. But the fact of the matter is Minnesota is recognized as a top five business state, top three state to raise children. We are number one in healthcare. Crime in America is at a 50-year low.\par \par \uc2 Contrast that to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's time in office. So look, Minnesota is a state --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But they're talking about crime in Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Yes. And we -- you can look at the statistics, you can see where we're at. We certainly know that during the Trump years, we saw murders go up and things, but they're all coming down.\par \par \uc2 The country, 50-year low, same thing here. So I think contrasting to that.\par \par \uc2 Look, this is about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  going to Detroit and saying Detroit stinks. This is about Republicans saying Minnesota's not good. This is where they go to states.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  actually going so far as to saying, maybe I won't give disaster aid to states that vote blue.\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  wants to be a president for all Americans. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is not going to divide states based on where they're at. This is just -- it's simply not the case. We simply have a high quality of life that we're proud of. I would invite folks to come to Minneapolis.\par \par \uc2 Come visit the 5-0 \uc2 Minnesota Vikings\uc2  and you'll see a state that's driving, a state that's building homes, a state that's focusing --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: -- on the middle class and jobs.\par \par \uc2 All right. Well, we will leave that there with your critics and their questions. They say the data proves out the claims they're making about crime and education and all those other things. So folks can check it out. We'll share some of that too.\par \par \uc2 Governor, I still have more questions. Maybe we'll talk again. It's great to have you back this week.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Well, you're great. I hope I can come back. Thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Thank you, Governor.\par \par \uc2 All right. With the election just three weeks away, both campaigns are urgently looking to shore up a critical group of voters, specifically black men.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Obama\uc2  doling out some tough love out there on the campaign trail, and President Trump is amping up his outreach. Our reaction from the Sunday panel, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA (D), FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We have not yet seen the same kinds of energy and turnout in all quarters of our neighborhoods and communities as we saw when I was running.\par \par \uc2 I also want to say that that seems to be more pronounced with the brothers.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: And my numbers with the black and especially black men, I love black men, I love them. I love them.\par \par \uc2 I've -- I'm -- I have gone through the roof with black men. Black men.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Former Presidents \uc2 Obama\uc2  and Trump on efforts to win over black male voters.\par \par \uc2 Let's talk about it with our Sunday group. "The Hill" editor-in-chief Bob Cusack. Mary Katharine Ham, columnist with "OutKick." \uc2 Forbes\uc2  contributing writer, Richard Fowler. And Cal Thomas, a syndicated columnist.\par \par \uc2 OK. That was a lot to take in there. There's been some tough love. There's been some excited talk out there.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " though did have this headline yesterday. They said, "Black voters drift from Democrats, imperiling Harris's bid," according to polling.\par \par \uc2 They say about 15 percent of black, likely voters said, they plan to vote for the former president according to the new poll, a six-point increase from four years ago.\par \par \uc2 So, Richard, fair to say we're talking about 15 percent, but it is a noticeable increase for the Trump-Vance ticket.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD FOWLER, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: There's no question that this race is contentious, right? There's no question that every vote is up for grabs, but let's get a couple of things out the way first. Black men support Democrats more than any other demographic in this country outside of black women, and for that, the Democrats should owe them a lot of gratitude.\par \par \uc2 And I think number two here is what we're seeing from just a couple of months ago when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was at the top of the ticket. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is outperforming \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  with black men. And based on my reporting and based on my sourcing and also based on the reports from Reuters, what we're going to see her do this week is actually go after black men in a very, very tactical way.\par \par \uc2 She's probably going to roll out a new policy agenda that includes some of the things you've already heard from her, the, you know, the homeowner credit, the tax credit for people with children.\par \par \uc2 But beyond that, I urge her and her campaign to actually focus on an issue that's more