is is the continuation of a trend among men, working class men, that is a little pronounced among Hispanic and black men. Voter ID for Hispanics has been shifting Republican. There's reporting from the Philadelphia paper this week saying, working class voters in Philadelphia are shifting toward Republicans.\par \par \uc2 And I just want to note part of that, the issue with men, is that I'm going to be charitable and say that the Democratic Party and its leading lights have not been welcoming to the idea of traditional masculinity for the last several years, right? You might not find yourself at home there.\par \par \uc2 This is a problem because if you look at an AEI survey from 2022, people who self-identify as very masculine at 50 percent or more are more likely to be Republican. And guess who self-identifies at the same amount as Republicans, black men and Hispanic men.\par \par \uc2 So I think this is a mapping over that is a problem for democratic messaging that's hard to overcome.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and you mentioned this issue about men and how they're received by the Democrat Party. This is something that James Carville talked about a long time strategist who said, you know, men are, in his words, he felt like they were shamed or told not to be so masculine or kind of preached to.\par \par \uc2 He's among the Democrats who are out there who are publicly expressing some concern. Here's a collection of some quotes. "I'm scared to death," that's from James Carville. "The sugar high is gone," that's Anthony Coley, who served in the Biden and \uc2 Obama\uc2  administrations. "If you're not nervous, you're not paying attention," that's Jamal Simmons from our Harris communications director.\par \par \uc2 So, Cal, that's one side of it. But do you think that the Trump campaign is getting overly confident?\par \par \uc2 CAL THOMAS, SYNDICATED COLUMNIST: Well, there's always that danger. But I think, you know, we've been talking about gender gaps and other kinds of gaps with respect to black men.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I was born in Washington, D.C. at a time when segregation was still in vogue. I still remember separate restrooms and water fountains. I was at \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 's "I Have a Dream" speech.\par \par \uc2 So what you have now though is a lot of younger African-Americans who don't have that experience. They don't have the civil rights agenda experience of their parents and grandparents. And they're starting to think for themselves. And they're looking at inflation and they're looking at the failed public schools in Chicago, for example, which is run by the progressive Mayor Johnson.\par \par \uc2 Twenty-five percent of high school graduates in the Chicago public school system, made up mostly of minorities, are -- do not -- are not at reading proficiency. That is an absolute scandal.\par \par \uc2 Poll after poll has shown that poor families want education choice for their children. And I think Trump has been hitting on that along with some other things that appeal to younger African-Americans. And that's why I think there is this kind of gap between black conservatives. Twenty-two percent now, according to one study I've seen, are now growing more conservative where older African-Americans are still in that Democrat Party room and voting for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, I mean, Bob, is there enough of a shift here to actually make an electoral difference in this? I mean, it's a very tight race nearly everything's within the margin of error.\par \par \uc2 BOB CUSACK, EDITOR-IN-CHIEF, THE HILL: Yes. No, absolutely. I mean, neither side should be overconfident at all. I mean, when \uc2 Obama\uc2  went out and stumped for \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  in 2016, there was way too much overconfident. He has dropped the mike saying Trump would never become president.\par \par \uc2 So those quotes from Democrats are very smart. At the same time, Harris has some soft spots that she needs to shore up. Two major unions didn't back her. We talked about the black male vote, Latino vote.\par \par \uc2 These are areas where Democrats are usually doing better. And I think that the Trump team is smart to capitalize on, but the same time, we got a long way to go, and in this thing is basically tight.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It is. And three weeks feels like an eternity.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Panels, don't go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 Up -- yes. Up next, we've got yet another major storm battering the American South. Millions of Floridians scrambling to rebuild. We're going to take you live to Florida ahead of President Biden's visit there to that devastated region.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Recovery efforts continuing in Florida after Hurricane Milton wreaked havoc on parts of the state. President Biden is on his way there to survey the damage on the ground.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Serrie is in Sarasota. Good to see you this morning, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Good to be with you, Shannon. I spoke with the manager of the New Pass Grill & Bait Shop here. He says it'll take him about three weeks to get everything cleaned up before he can fully reopen.\par \par \uc2 And what's taking place at this business behind me is just one example of what's going on at homes and businesses up and down these barrier islands off of Florida's Gulf Coast.\par \par \uc2 President Biden's visit today to neighboring Tampa Bay area comes just one day after he declared a major disaster for the state. That action makes federal grants and loans available to the affected individuals in 35 counties and tribal areas.\par \par \uc2 The President says early damage estimates for the state are approximately $50 billion for Hurricane Milton alone. And Milton arrived, remember, just days after Hurricane Helene struck some of these very same areas.\par \par \uc2 Milton was a powerful Category 3 hurricane when it made landfall Wednesday near Siesta Key, another barrier island community here in Sarasota County. Although it was spared, the 15-foot storm surge forecasters had feared Siesta Key suffered widespread wind damage.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA LEMMER, GENERAL MANAGER, SIESTA KEY OYSTER BAR: We honestly have been crying. We have been smiling. We have been so thankful. Honestly, very blessed.\par \par \uc2 KRISTIN HALE, OPERATIONS DIRECTOR, SIESTA KEY OYSTER BAR: And I think we all have that mentality out here. It's always the village. Yeah, we definitely care about our neighbors. Some of them have gotten a lot worse.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: Hurricane Milton temporarily disrupted fuel supplies along Florida's central Gulf Coast as gas stations slowly come back online. Police have been directing long lines of cars and more often than they would like intervening in arguments at the pump.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS NOCCO (R), PASCO COUNTY SHERIFF: The supply chains eventually will catch up. They will be there. Life will get back to normal. But in the meantime, we need your assistance to just make sure that everybody calms down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: According to the tracking service, poweroutage.us, just under 1 million Florida homes and businesses are still without electricity, but they are making progress. That figure is down from more than 3 million at the peak of the storm.\par \par \uc2 Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Jonathan Serrie live in Florida. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We're back with the panel now. And of course, a storm this close to the election, there are all kinds of questions about the political implications. "The Hill" has a piece saying, "One is the potential for a backlash against Trump's propensity for misstatements and his penchant for chaos -- even at such moments of crisis, the others blow back against Harris if she's perceived to be exaggerating her own role in the response."\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: Yeah, well, it's interesting, you know, DeSantis in Florida and Harris kind of exchange barbs about whether who was calling who back. And DeSantis, I think, had a pretty good point, like I've never dealt with Harris in prior storms. So, therefore, I think the White House is playing some games there.\par \par \uc2 But this is something where Democrats have to be very concerned. That's why the White House has pushed back against what Trump has said, saying, hey, that's not true. They've set up all these truth meters and that kind of thing.\par \par \uc2 But listen, as James Carville said, storms, storms can -- can -- can be our problem for the incumbent president. And that's basically Harris right now.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I mean, and Cal, there can be opportunity to step up and show leadership and command of the situation.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS: Well, there used to be, Shannon, I mean, this kind of thing, natural disasters used to be bipartisan. I mean, storms don't discriminate between Republicans and Democrats and minorities and minorities. I think this started going south with Katrina, where Democrats blamed \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  for not doing enough to help those folks in Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 But the fact was that the governor of Louisiana at the time, who was a Democrat, wouldn't allow the National Guard to come in, which Bush volunteered to do it. So when he flew over the state, he was mocked for not talking to the people on the ground and all the rest of it.\par \par \uc2 This should not be a political issue. And I think the fact that it has, shows how corrupting our politics have become. And it's really shameful. People need help. They should get help, whether they're Republicans or Democrats or any other demographic.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, I mean, Catherine, should the campaigns avoid talking about this storm in the context of the response? Because, you know, it does open you up for criticism, whether you're saying positive or negative things about FEMA, the soldiers, everybody else who's trying to show up and get something done.\par \par \uc2 HAM: I'm going to modify an old Mr. Rogers saying look for the helpers and say, look for the grownups. People are really hurting. And we so often have a vacuum of leadership and therefore a trust issue with leadership.\par \par \uc2 And in this case, I think honestly an antidote to that is these DeSantis press conferences during a Trump -- during a storm when he shows command of all these facts. Shelters for the disabled, shelters for the V.A., shelters for people with pets, corporate sponsorships for all sorts of ways to help. And when he's giving out this information in a consistent and trustworthy way, you get a -- you don't have this vacuum and you don't have room for as much issues with disinformation and people getting upset. Harris and Trump, I think, didn't take the opportunity to be those grownups. DeSantis and Biden teamed up to do so.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. And it is interesting to see that the two of them, Richard, are being very complimentary about each other. Not something we see all the time, but it gets back to Cal's point of when this used to be a bipartisan effort to save people's lives, to help them, to get them back on their feet.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: I think Mary Katharine is right. There's something that governors know how to do, because no matter whose party -- no matter which party you belong to, the goal is to help the people who live in your state. And I think that's what we've seen from all the governors that have been hit by these hurricanes.\par \par \uc2 But I think it's worth pointing out, as you think about this election and the impact of this natural disaster to sort of key in on what Governor Walz said a couple of segments ago. This is when you see the collective power of the American people, right?\par \par \uc2 The idea of rebuilding roads, rebuilding schools, opening up shelters, sending linemen from California to Florida to help them get the power restored. And the question will be for voters as they think about how this will impact them, which campaign is going to give you that collective power of the American people and which campaign is going to divide.\par \par \uc2 And I think what we've seen over the past couple of years, how long Trump has been running, is there's been so much misinformation put out around this hurricane specifically, which says, hmm, is he the one or is he not the one that's going to help the American people and bring the collective power together?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: MK, I see a little, I don't want to say twitching, but a little wanting to get in on this conversation.\par \par \uc2 HAM: No, just that spirit exists outside of government, right? That is something that we have seen thrive in North Carolina often in the absence of that. And it's a beautiful thing, but it doesn't have to be associated with a party.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: But government shouldn't be absent, though. That's the whole idea.\par \par \uc2 HAM: They shouldn't, but they often are.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, but bravo to, you know, female workers, to National Guardsmen, to churches, to volunteers, to a place like \uc2 Samaritan's Purse\uc2  and Red Cross and so many others who are fighting their way into these places and getting to people who desperately need their help.\par \par \uc2 Panel, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, how faith can shape our most contentious political debates. The late Senator Joe Lieberman left behind some advice and his family joins us to talk about his final message in a brand-new book.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FORMER SEN. JOE LIEBERMAN (D-CT): In American history, this sublime and serious combination of religion and democracy has overall been a force for great good.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The late Senator Joe Lieberman spent his life as a champion of religious freedom and diplomatic compromise. His new book, "Faith's Answers to America's Political Crisis," highlights how faith can help Americans through times of political strife and division. He passed away in March, but his message lives on in this new book.\par \par \uc2 I spoke with his widow and son, Hadassah and Matthew Lieberman. It's today's Sunday special.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to start with the introduction to the book that the senator wrote because he talks about how so many Americans, while they may be divided politically, the vast majority of them have some belief in God or a higher power, whether they formally practice religion or not, and how that's a good starting place for us.\par \par \uc2 He writes in the introduction, "Each of us is created in the image of God with the spark of our divine creator in us, and all of us, therefore, deserve to be treated equally with respect and kindness."\par \par \uc2 And Hadassah, that's a place I love to start when there are going to be heated debates. Talk more about that, just being able to see each other as valuable and respected.\par \par \uc2 HADASSAH LIEBERMAN, WIDOW OF JOE LIEBERMAN: Well, that was Joe. During campaigns, I used to say, what you see is what you get, and that was him. He believed those words sincerely for a long time and taught us that faith is a wonderful thing. Not everyone has it, but our beliefs in some larger entity than ourselves are important to teach us to keep us strong and to keep us together.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and people saw him model that in his life day to day.\par \par \uc2 J. LIEBERMAN: We believe in what both the Bible and the Declaration of Independence tells us that, as the Bible, of course, makes clear, we are not here by accident, but because of a divine, godly act of creation. And as the Declaration written by men of faith tells us, every one of us is a child of God.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: He also talks about in the book that there is a way to debate and to do it constructively. And Matthew, he talks about Abraham and God having their contentious debates and says, you know, we do have models for ways that we can have disagreements.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW LIEBERMAN, SON OF JOE LIEBERMAN: To use the Yiddish term, you know, those are great human acts of chutzpah to challenge God. But, you know, that's OK. That's part of our shared faith tradition. It's not adversarial in any negative way. And, you know, I feel like this book, by reminding people of all faiths, that this is part of what we're supposed to be living, can be sort of like a source of affirmation for people who kind of know they should be doing this politics stuff in a better way than they are.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Exactly. I want to get to sort of the conclusion, too, because he talks about all of these problems that we have, the obstacles that we have, and how he believes that faith will help us get past them. Do you think there is an open ear, an open audience among the American people to hear what he spells out in this book?\par \par \uc2 H. LIEBERMAN: Joey felt that if we work together and teach our children, our politicians, our leaders have to speak in ways that they want children to copy.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And, Matthew, what does it mean for you all to be able to bring this final work of his, this final sort of, you know, argument to the American people about how to get things right to this book and to the place where people can have it in their hands and hear this final beseeching from him?\par \par \uc2 M. LIEBERMAN: I think what he wanted to do with this last testament of his was to remind people of that and to kind of rouse them and say, hey, this is in us. We can do better. The golden rule should not stop at the threshold of politics. It should inform how we interact when we try to govern ourselves in this community.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, the late Senator's book, "Faith's Answers to America's Political Crisis" is now available where books are sold. Our thanks to Hadassah and Matthew.\par \par \uc2 Just a quick note, my podcast "Livin' The Bream" drops new today. This week I sat down with best-selling author Jack Carr. We talked about his new book, "Targeted: Beirut." It is a detailed account of the birth of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and modern-day terrorism and exactly what led to the deadly bombing of the Marine barracks.\par \par \uc2 Back in 1983, it is eerily timely, as this is a moment in which we are talking about all those things again.\par \par \uc2 That's it for today. Thanks for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a wonderful week. We'll see you next, Fox News Sunday.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020241013ekad00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Makes Pitch To Latino Voters At Town Hall; Barack Obama Shakes Black Men For Not Supporting \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ; Weird Democrats: Michigan Governor Roasted For Bizarre Doritos Video; \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  To LockedIn: Former \uc2 FTX\uc2  CEO Announces His New Role As Inmate; Gen Z Wearing Pajamas To Work\b0\par\par\uc2 Jesse Watters, Kayleigh McEnany, Jessica Tarlov, Shannon Bream,  Greg Gutfeld \par \uc2 8,458 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: -- all of the others that very smart leaders have viciously thrown out of their countries. These are very smart leaders. I would do it. I would have done it faster than them, I tell you. Soon as I thought of it, I said, you know that, remember I said it three years ago? I said, that's going to happen if they have open borders. And I --\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  holding a massive rally in Colorado. We'll keep an eye on that.\par \par \uc2 Hello everybody, I'm Jesse Watters along with \uc2 Kayleigh McEnany\uc2 , Jessica Tarlov, Shannon Bream and Greg Gutfeld. It's 5:00 in New York City, and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 Kamala capping off a week of train wreck interviews with one more flop, a town hall with Univision. Harris pandering hard to Latinos who are moving into Trump's column. So, she threw on the accent and busted out her tired, canned lines.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I hope your family is OK and your home is OK. Listen, I know prices are too high still. You know prices are too high still, and we have to deal with it.\par \par \uc2 Here's how I feel about it. Again, you've heard my story. I come from the working class. I'm never going to forget where I come from, and part of what we have to do is build what I call an opportunity economy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And get this, on the same day that Kamala was trying to portray herself as a hurricane hero picking a fight with \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  for allegedly ignoring her phone calls, she was getting glammed up for Vogue's cover, the digital version, not the actual cover, which has Billie Eilish on it.\par \par \uc2 And while Kamala fumbles in the home stretch, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's firing up another blue state. The former president out in Aurora, Colorado, the epicenter of the criminal migrant gang like Tren de Aragua. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Kamala has imported an army of illegal alien, gang members and migrant criminals, and she has had them resettled beautifully into your community to pray upon innocent American citizens. That's what they're doing, and no place is it more evident than right here. I will rescue \uc2 Aurora\uc2  and every town that has been invaded and conquered.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Shannon is the Harris campaign OK? Is it OK?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Si, that's as far as I go. (INAUDIBLE) Shannon.\par \par \uc2 Listen, I think the thing is, she is doing worse with Hispanic voters, potentially, than Hillary Clinton did, than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did. So, I clearly think that they see there's a need to make up some ground here.\par \par \uc2 But I think at the end of the day, Hispanic voters care about what most voters care about, which the polls tell us is the economy. They do care about immigration, and we all know people who immigrated here legally, they are the ones who are most concerned about the border. It takes years, it takes tons of court hearings. It takes thousands, tens of thousands of dollars to get that done.\par \par \uc2 So, when it comes to immigration, they may not be exactly where Democrats think that they are, and they're the ones who are going to want to see things and force people to not jump them in line, that kind of thing.\par \par \uc2 But if you're going to be talking to any group, I think right now, you have to be talking about food and groceries and gas and how you're going to fix that. And we've yet to hear that articulated.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: We haven't really heard a lot, have we, but we've heard a lot of accents, Jessica, can you explain the accent situation?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: No, I can't. It's a very common thing. I didn't think that was that pronounced, but it does happen when you were talking to other people who have a specific accent that you can dip into it. There's a formal term for it that escapes me at this moment, but --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Then dip.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It's not the dip, there's a formal term.\par \par \uc2 But this was incredibly important for her to do this. Democrats have been getting softer and softer with Latino voters over a number of cycles, and it's something that we obviously want to pull back from, not just electorally, but to make sure that these people feel that they're part of our coalition and that the Democrats are delivering for them.\par \par \uc2 And what she did offer in the town hall was a stark distinction in the way that she talks about immigration from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, she not only touted the bipartisan border deal, which \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  personally torpedoed, it was most conservative bipartisan bill that we've ever had, but she was talking about a pathway to citizenship, which he never does, and something that's overwhelmingly popular. About 70 percent of Americans are in favor of that. She was talking about protecting dreamers, which is also something that the broad majority of Americans are supportive of to things that are off the menu for Trump, who is in Aurora, Colorado. I'm talking about a situation with gang violence that the Republican mayor actually disputes was even happening.\par \par \uc2 But everything with him is doom and gloom. They're, you know, eating the cats, eating the dogs, higher rates of crime, etcetera. And she had a much more balanced vision of it.\par \par \uc2 And she also got to, like you said, talk about grocery prices, inflation issues. She was also asked by an independent voter about the, "primary process", and that this voter felt like it wasn't necessarily fair that she ended up the nominee. And this is something that has been circulating around. It doesn't -- Democrats aren't dissatisfied with it, but it's certainly a question, and I thought that it was really good that that got out there.\par \par \uc2 And she said that this is, you know, really about a party that believes in democracy and protecting the Constitution and certifying elections, and a rogue party on the other side, led by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I thought it was a good night for her, frankly, and she got to touch on a bunch of issues that matter.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  talking about amnesty. She calls it a pathway to citizenship. It's amnesty. You break into the country, you're given citizenship. If that's so overwhelmingly popular, as Jessica says, why didn't the Democrats do anything about that when they had the house, the Senate and the White House?\par \par \uc2 KAYLEIGH MCENANY\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Exactly. Look, number one, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  leads on immigration, and every poll that I have seen, so voters are with him on immigration.\par \par \uc2 Number two, you're exactly right. Democrats had unilateral control of government, they had the House, they had the Senate. If this bill was the panacea for our border issues, well, you had two years to create your panacea. You chose not to do it because you don't care about the border until it's politically caustic to you.\par \par \uc2 And also, you had the executive power that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had to stop what's happening at the border that Biden failed to use until three years into his presidency.\par \par \uc2 But the challenge, Jesse, is this election is going to be a battle between the power of the people and the power of the media. The power of the media is enormous. You've seen that vote cover where they tout Kamala as this candidate who was like mana that dropped from heaven. The ABC debate, it was three-on-one. The "60 Minutes" debate or the "60 Minutes" interview, they're editing her to make her look good.\par \par \uc2 But the people, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is ahead in six of seven swing states. Mark Halperin said there is a realistic scenario where he sweeps six of those states. Quinnipiac, which leans left, has \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  leading in two of the rest of states.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: What about the Wall Street Journal today?\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: It's the people versus the media. And imagine if -- yes, you want to talk about the Wall Street Journal?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I do, yes.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Where it's neck and neck, where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is still leading in about half of those states.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: But is that sweeping all of the swing states and Mark Halperin?\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Real Clear Politics average of polls, he is sweeping six of seven. And we know there's a polling bias that goes against Trump. Biden was 10 points up over Trump nationally this time around. You have Kamala, she's up two. I'll take Trump's cards any day.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right, and we're not calling it a red wave. It's just he happens to be winning every single battleground.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Correct.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: That's just not true.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Greg Gutfeld in a dashing shirt, we must add.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Well, thank you.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Cozy.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: We've all been touching it.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It's very cozy, and it's so nice, I didn't even really prepare for the show.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Good.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: So, I'm just going to rattle ridiculous things. Kamala on the cover of Vogue. I mean, it's an improvement over Joe who was on the cover of morgue.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: My goodness.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: So, about Hispanic voters. OK, you have to understand, this is -- I'm taking this from a male perspective. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is a woman who said more woke is better. There are no woke male Latinos, with the exception of Anna Navarro.\par \par \uc2 But men want leaders. It's not important if you call them dangerous, we will take dangerous over dip shittery, and that's what we're getting with Kamala. She doesn't come off as smart or bright or organized or capable, or even present when she's being interviewed, and you could say, oh, he's a danger to democracy. Right now, we can't touch that. It's a -- you know, it's a dystopian fantasy that you throw out to scare people.\par \par \uc2 What we can't touch are the real dangers, which is the border, crime on our streets, the danger to our wallets. These are all real dangers.\par \par \uc2 Fawning Harris -- fawning Harris -- a fawning reporter asked Harris if she could name three good things about Trump. Did you see that? And she stammered, and everybody laughed, ha, ha, ha.\par \par \uc2 The funny part about that is no one can give them three reasons, or even one reason to like Kamala, right? It's a pure -- and we know this, it's a pure anti-Trump vote. She wasn't the first, second, third, fourth, fifth choice for Democrats, for some reason, she cut in line. Talk about unfair.\par \par \uc2 So, this is a campaign that is based on emotional trauma. Harris is just the doll you use to say where Trump hurt you.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: She does say that he loves his family, which I thought was nice.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: She could have been nicer.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Come on, what do you think he would have said? I don't think he would have said she loves her family.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: He said she's very attractive but not my type.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I find her hot. I wouldn't touch her, but --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Compliment in his world.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Speaking of it, I want to talk directly to t