demark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I'm sure your constituents are grateful for your calm and standing by them. Derrick Henry, the Daytona Beach, Florida mayor.\par \par \uc2 Just want to update you that \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  is now planning to resume operations again in sages as the state tries to get back to normal or something close to it. That will do it for us. Here comes THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hi, I'm Greg Gutfeld, along with Judge Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Jesse Watters and she could take Strawberry Shortcake in a fistfight, Dana Perino, THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 Hurricane Milton carving a path of destruction in Florida, multiple deaths, major flooding in some areas, and over three million Floridians are still without power. Strong winds ripping the roof off of Tropicana Field, which is home to the \uc2 Tampa Bay Rays\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Let's go right to Steve Harrigan in Tampa, Florida. Hi, Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Greg, parts of this road were two or three feet under water this morning, that water is now gone and traffic is flowing. It's been really the start of cleanup here along the coast of Florida, the death toll has risen to at least 10 so far. Half of those people killed by tornadoes on the other coast, on the eastern coast.\par \par \uc2 The governor of Florida says there's been more than 350 rescues, some of those water rescues carried out by first responders with boats or high water vehicles. Others carried out by National Guard soldiers and several, two carried out just by ordinary civilians trying to help or save their neighbors.\par \par \uc2 The biggest movement has been with electricity. There were four million people without electricity. Now that's down to 3.2 million. In part, that's because the Governor DeSantis pre-positioned more than 50,000 line workers in the state to be