g to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  with women 15 to 20 points which is bigger than the margins of any Republicans have before.\par \par \uc2 And I don't hear the Republican, you know, tore about women. I just hear him even on with Andrew Schulz doubling down on, you know, it was good to get rid of Roe. That's no problem. And Andrew Schulz set him up perfectly with a hilarious joke about how Barron is such a stud and bet you're feeling bad about getting rid of Roe v. Wade and Trump can't even nail it because it's so ludicrous that he's a "protector of women."\par \par \uc2 That's the outreach to these women who are saying the Republican Party has abandoned us. They do not care about our needs. They do not understand our health -- they do not understand our healthcare issues. They don't understand family planning. They don't even know how it works. You don't even have a heartbeat in six weeks.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I think we -- it's hard for men to hear abortion described as family planning. I will --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Really? Would it be hard? We are talking about women --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Because -- let me finish. Because whenever you have to rely on a euphemism to describe the reality, the reality itself is awful. That's all I'm saying.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: But did you know --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Call it what it is. You're sucking a fetus out of the womb.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No, but --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: The reality is often when you're talking about, you know, some of these third-term abortions. It's about --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: That's not the reality. That's what you want to tell people the reality.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Well, no. That is a reality in some states.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: The reality is majority of abortions are in the first trimester.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: But the bottom line is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is protecting women in terms of law and order.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No, he's not. Women --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Are you kidding me?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Law and order --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: As the criminals run through this country and they're bailed out because people have \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  made a decision that we should bail everybody out.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Crime is down and abortions --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Wait. Jessica, you just said -- OK, women are overwhelmingly Democrat. There are more women voters, right? You guys have -- you have -- you can vote for abortion. Go for it. No one is stopping you. You have to convince -- you have to convince other women --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: We are.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Not men. You have to convince other men to your side.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No, but you have to --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: OK, guys. Up next, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  coming out with a plan to help the working class.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: As Democrats fear Kamala is bombing with the working class, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is scooping them up. The former President was out in the Motor City of Detroit and rolling out some game-changing ideas. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Today, I am also announcing that as part of our tax cuts, we will make interest on car loans fully deductible. We're going to make it fully deductible the interest payments. That's going to revolutionize your industry. This will stimulate massive domestic auto production and make car ownership dramatically more affordable for millions and millions of working American families. This is a phenomenal thing if I do say so myself.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right, Jesse, no tips on Social Security, no -- I mean, no tax on tips, no tips on -- tax on Social Security, car loans you can now deduct them. Is it any wonder why there's a realignment of working class with the Republicans?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: He's been very surgical with the way he's going after these voters with tax cuts. And men obviously big into cars. You got that. People that are working as waitresses or waiters obviously that appeals to them. The Culinary Union today just came out and told Politico, Dana, I'm sure you saw it in your reading, that Trump is going to win Nevada. And that we've already seen the teamsters snub \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . We've seen the firefighters snub her. That's because he is focused on the working men.\par \par \uc2 I don't know what she's focused on. She's focused on she says opportunity, ambition, no one really makes sense when she talks like that. But if you combine the tariffs which protect working men. If you -- if you protect the border which protects the working men's jobs, and then you do these surgical tax cuts, you have really invested in a workingmen recovery that we haven't seen since, I don't know, probably the 60s or 70s.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know, Jessica, yesterday you talked about how Kamla should have answered a question that was asked of her and how she's different from Joe. But if she can't even articulate why people should vote for her and we're seeing this union support like disintegrating, why should she be president?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I mean, I have 30 seconds. I can't do that. I can't say that she needs to in all of these rallies be very clear about her message. And she wouldn't be in a tied -- it wouldn't be a jump ball if she wasn't resonating with people. And she does have strong union support. Go talk to the Auto Workers. Like, they very clearly stand with them. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , the first president in history to stand on the picket line with them.\par \par \uc2 And I believe the Culinary Union is doing two things. One, I think they are genuinely concerned, and two they want Democrats to be running scared because we're going to perform better if that's the case. But this Michigan thing -- and obviously we desperately need to win Michigan. But on the ground, I don't see this.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, J.D. Vance had a rally there and there were guys wearing Auto Workers for Trump shirts that weren't actually Auto Workers, so just paid to wear the T-shirt there. When there was the Auto Worker strike, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  on the picket line, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had a rally that he said was in support of the unions but was at a non-union plant because the union workers didn't want him there with him. So, this is not wrapped up for Trump that the blue wall is going to disintegrate and go in his favor.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know, Greg, the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  gave $16 million to the Democrats. They gave $340,000 to the Republicans. Doesn't it show you that the Republicans are at least willing to work for your vote that you don't have to pay them?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. I think though it's less -- it's less about the Republicans and more about the Democrats. You know, we talk about people who live in their own bubbles. I prefer to think as one sheltered and one unsheltered. If you are sheltered from real-world problems, then you're your empathy is easily gamed and you believe the sob stories like, you know, BLM which turns out to be fraudulent, trans rapist you know, turn out to be going into women's prisons to rape them, every illegal is somehow fleeing being harm's way.\par \par \uc2 When you live in a bubble, you are easily gamed. Working-class people don't live in a bubble. They're not burdened by, you know, the gullibility that that the other classes do. And what do blue-collar people hate the most? Line-cutting, people that cut in line. And that's what happened with the Democrats when they introduced equity as a form of compassion to say this group can get in front of you. And even though you've been waiting in line, and even though you're worse off than they are, when it comes to natural disasters, we're going to put them there. When it comes to jobs, we're going to put them there.\par \par \uc2 You know, so you might have like, I don't know, a 23-year-old blue-collar dad with two sick parents and he's wondering why is this white-collar art history grad getting her loans cancelled while I'm paying for my truck?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Wrap it up, Dana.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I just think that -- don't sleep on the fact that the electric vehicle mandates have been very hard for the assembly workers because they require a lot less -- a lot -- fewer people to be able to assemble. And I think that this realignment that we're going through, I don't --- I hope I live long enough to see how it all turns out. But that might be I think also one of the reasons the electric vehicle mandate has been a disaster for them.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Is there something you should tell us?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes, I mean --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Wow.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I think you got another month.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Up next, the craziest raccoon story you'll ever hear.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Some of them are married.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It's a raccoon reckoning. A Washington woman calling 911 after a 100 of these mass bandits crashed her crib. It turns out she's been feeding them. We have very little time. Who wants to get on the raccoon fun.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: First of all, who thinks throwing food at animals is going to make them go away?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Fair. Dana?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I think of this as people that like ignore the rules and like ski in the back country when you're not supposed to. And then when you come to get rescued, you have to pay for it. She should have to pay for that.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Greg?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Raccoons are an oppressed species. They have an unfair reputation for mischief because of their bandit facial coloration. Imagine if you went around life looking like a burglar.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Jesse.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Fair.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You made a point in the last segment about people wearing t-shirts that didn't work in the auto industry. I looked into it. It's another Jessica hoax. It just took about a commercial break to debunk.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: OK.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, we have to figure it out.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: We had to -- we had to get to the bottom of this.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And we did.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: And we did. Thank God. "ONE MORE THING" is up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: All right, Jesse, go for it.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Please join Fox's support for Hurricane recovery efforts after Helene and Milton. Your donation enables the Red Cross to respond to and help people recover from the disaster. So, visit redcross.org/foxforward to support these efforts or just scan the QR code on your screen with your phone. I know a lot of people don't know about the scanning of the QR code thing. I just figured it out last month.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME," Johnny went to Brooklyn. Watch that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BELISARIO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL PRODUCER: Joe is out and Kamala is in. What do you think of her?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: God bless her because I don't know what she trying to play, but she ain't from the streets.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: She ain't from the street is right. She's from Berkeley, tonight at 8:00.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: All right, tonight, we got a great show. We're back. It's got Kat Timpf, Andrew Gruel, Michele Tafoya, Adam Hunter. And we got a special debate between \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Nice.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, tune for that.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Oh, they went with you?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: What?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: They picked you?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: They picked him as the moderator.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I'm moderating. Judge?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK, watch my new special, "SANCTUARY AMERICA." It's out on Fox Nation. Look at that picture. Why do we even -- why do we even have sanctuary cities? Is there any benefit at all to Americans? Why do we protect illegal criminals in a way that we don't even protect our own American criminals?\par \par \uc2 I speak with parents whose worlds have been shattered. America needs to hear their stories as they -- I'm going to go the whole way -- as they share harrowing details of their loss and the impotence of the American Criminal Justice System. You have to watch it because I worked really hard all.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: All right, eight seconds, Jessica.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh, there's a sound there.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: The show's over. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Thanks. No, they had sound, but they went over it.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I think you look beautiful.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Just look into my blue eyes.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020241011ekaa00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Angle Takes You Behind The Scenes Of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's Return To Butler, Pennsylvania. Trump to women voters, I will keep you safe; \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  does mostly friendly media blitz; \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  does raunchy podcast; \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  jumps in for Trump; The left ups their violent rhetoric against Trump; Media claims Trump is milking getting shot at; kamala chooses podcast over press conferences\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Laura Ingraham, Mollie Hemingway, Raymond Arroyo \par \uc2 8,390 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: Hurricane Helene recovery efforts in the state of the U.S. economy and getting ready for Milton. Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 06:00 p.m. in the East, 03:00 p.m. in the West. Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this Special Report. Fair, balanced, and still unafraid. Here's Laura.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening, everyone. I'm Laura Ingraham. This is The Ingraham Angle from Washington tonight. As always, thank you very much for joining us. In moments, our exclusive behind the scenes look at the return to Butler with former President Trump. But first, less than a month now before the election is about time, there are fresh indicators that Trump is picking up big steam.\par \par \uc2 Betting markets now show \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  surging and the Harris campaign, they are in such a panic and their internal numbers are not good, by the way, they're actually allowing her to speak to a Gen Z podcaster who specializes in sex and dating advice.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEX COOPER, CALL HER DADDY PODCASTER: I have seen girls on the street walk up to men and be like, do you know where a tampon goes? Do you know how many tampons we used?\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE U.S., (D) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I was the first Vice President or President to ever in office, go to a reproductive health care clinic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: After getting slammed for doing few real interviews, Kamala did a tape. Sit down with 60 minutes as well.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How are you going to pay for it?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Well, one of the things I'm going to make sure that the richest among us who can afford it, pay their fair share in taxes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We're dealing with the real world here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now you don't send her out, right, now you know why. And now why did \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  go back to Butler?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: You have to come back, and you have to do what you have to do. You can't let the bad guys hold you back.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: On this Indian summer October afternoon, the atmosphere at the fairgrounds in Butler was electric. Tens of thousands waiting to hear Trump, and all around us was love of country, love of God, love and gratitude for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the sacrifices he's made.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's really brave of him to come back here, and you can see the bravery of all the supporters here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Fight, fight, fight, USA. USA.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What happened at the first rally was, was an attack, and we can't stand for that.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, this is extremely emotional. President Trump's about to come out. The crowd is on the edge of the seat, extremely patriotic and extremely grateful, and he's actually here today to Butler round two, to finish it up. That's wild.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, we spent the afternoon behind the scenes with access to all the folks who keep the America first campaign charging forward, including Trump's bold VP pick, J.D. Vance.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE (R-OH), 2024 VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: It's just a reminder of how precious life is. In particular, what I believe was a genuine miracle that he is still with us, because everybody has seen that turn of the head at the very last second that I think kept the President alive. And I really do believe the President has good work to do for the country. That's why he's alive today, and that's why it's such a celebratory mood here in Butler, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It was also fascinating to watch Trump and his powerful new ally \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  in action. Now they were collaborating as if they had been working together for years.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Walking over to interview President Trump, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  will be speaking. That's pretty incredible to see the two of them together. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  restoring free speech for many people in the United States and the President refusing to back down. Both of them are fighters in their own way, so it's kind of fitting that they're together.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I even had the chance to chat with the \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  Founder and the X CEO himself, and his message is simple, and it's a dire warning that your constitutional rights are on the line, so vote for Trump like your life depends on it, because it does. Now, afterward, he also stopped by to say hi to our slightly smaller audience. But the day started very early with thousands lined up and going through security that had been locked down and for hours the perimeter was totally closed, and security, you could see them everywhere and in between, this time, sharp shooters on every building, Secret Service, local police everywhere.\par \par \uc2 So, the Angle had access to the precise spot on the stage where on July 13th, remember, President Trump was grazed by that bullet on the side that it was fired. Now you can see that this time, large temporary structures screened out that AGR building where Thomas Crooks got off eight shots before he himself was killed by that Secret Service sniper.\par \par \uc2 Now, those images of a defiant, fist pumping President Trump were everywhere on Saturday, on T-shirts, on posters and echoes and chants from the crowd.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Fight, fight, fight, blood coming down the face.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But perhaps the most poignant moment of the day happened at 06:11 p.m., the exact time shots rang out and when sadly, Corey Comparatore had been fatally hit.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I would like to ask everyone to join me in a moment of silence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I saw grown men tearing out over the unimaginable heartbreak of Corey's wife and children who were also there in the crowd.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now the people had come to see their political warrior and to be part of history.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Look at who we found here in Butler just wandering around the corral. \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2 , how are you, man?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MCCORMICK, U.S. SENATOR CANDIDATE: I'm glad to be here with you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This is an amazing day after the almost horrific loss of life for President Trump last time around. Obviously, everyone is remembering Corey Comparatore today and his family. But what does this mean for you to be here?\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: You know, it's closing the circle. I mean, it was such an unbelievable moment, and it was so iconic to see the President get off the mat and put his fist up in the air. And I think, if anything, in three months, the need for strength in the White House has become more clearer. And so I think him coming back is just a symbol of strength and courage and resilience at a time when our country needs it the most.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2  and Bob Casey are about tied now there in Pennsylvania. So, after a 91 minute rally and then a 15 minute call to supporters, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and I chatted about the moment the hurricane relief efforts, women voters, Kamala's scarcity strategy, and how he wants to be remembered.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: When you walked out, and Lee Greenwood was singing. I've known you for 20 plus years, a long time, and I looked at you, and there was an emotional moment where, like, I think people around me were starting to tear up. Were you close to tearing up at that moment?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, if I admitted that, I think I'd lose a lot of votes. I'm not supposed to -- I'm not supposed to. Do you want a president that cries? I don't think so. So, I'll say absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The ride to the hospital from here take us in the car.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, there was a lot of blood, and when I was down and bullets were flying over my head, I noticed tremendous amounts of blood, but I felt it's very, you know, they talk about surreal. It wasn't surreal. I had total understanding of where I was, and the Secret Service people were very brave because they were -- I got down. I think people were shouting, maybe, or whatever, but I got down pretty fast. I was always impressed with that. I got down fast. If I didn't get down that fast, that would have been a second miracle, but they were flying right over my head, and you could hear them. I didn't know you could hear bullets. You could hear they were whizzing by at 4000 miles an hour or something.\par \par \uc2 But I think that more than anything else, they wanted me to go up by stretcher, and I didn't like that. I had a lot of people watching. I don't want that, but also, and I thought they did a great job.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Because the President has to look --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, I don't want to go out. I felt that I was hit in the ear, and it felt like the top of the ear, it grazed my ear, but it was really bloody, and the Secret Service people and rightfully thought I was hit on three or four places, because there's so much blood, because the ear bleeds whatever -- they say, cartilage, a doctor at Butler said cartilage, and they say, because of cartilage, the ear bleeds more than any other part of the body. There was blood all over the place.\par \par \uc2 And I really thought that only was hit in the air. So they said, sure, you were hit in other locations, and they're going all over my body. They probably realized what a body this guy had, but they're going all over, and they didn't find -- I said, look, I'm not going on a stretcher and I'm -- get me up. I wanted my shoes because no, they hit me --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What was it with the shoes? What was it?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : They hit me. No, no, when they went down, they hit me so hard that, for some reason, I think they were going at a different angle. Both shoes were off.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Are you wearing those shoes today? Same shoes?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : It's different, but it's sort of similar, but it's sort of a little different. I have it. Those shoes are loaded up with lots of blood. I'm not -- I'm not looking to wear them again. But, you know, they got me to the car. I thought they did a very good job.\par \par \uc2 Some people say slow. I don't think slow. They got me to the car, and I think I might have been holding them back. You know, my first instinct was to finish my speech. It's crazy. Isn't that a crazy instinct? I'm pouring but my -- but they didn't -- that's, that's where they put their foot down. They got me to the car. I got in, I did a little talking, not much. Two very, very good people. Sean is fantastic. But two very, very good people. And they got me to the hospital. It was so professional. Everything was -- look, they had a bad moment with the building, a bad moment.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: How long did it take in the car approximately?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Seven, eight minutes. Very quick. And the hospital was great. And I didn't realize this. I never knew they did. When a President goes to an area, they close a hospital in its entirety. It's ready for exactly this. I didn't know they did that, but they did. So the hospital, I got there, the doctors were outside, the nurses and no people, they literally closed it, and almost like they expect this, it's a genius thing, but you would never do, who would ever think that?\par \par \uc2 And I was so impressed. They got me in, and they took care of it. Butler hospital did a fantastic job. The doctors were amazing, and also amazing on the other two gentlemen that were hurt very badly, they thought they weren't going to live, and they ended up living, and I think they'll be very healthy again. They were very, very good people, good Trump supporters, and we took good care of them.\par \par \uc2 I mean, they took two hard shots. They were not supposed to make it. And not only did they make it, it really came out. I think they're going to be 100 percent, but Corey didn't make it, and I felt we had an obligation to Corey to come back here and help him out. We raised a lot of money through different things. We raised a lot of money for the family, and they deserve it for what they've gone through. They really -- they really had a great father and great husband.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: When you saw them for the first time today, again, since that horrific day. What was that like?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, it was amazing. The daughters beautiful, two young, young girls and a beautiful mother, and they're at the bottom of the plane. I get off, and they're the first people I greeted and met. And I knew him from before, of course, from the tragic event. But, you know, they're having a hard time actually, they're having a really hard time. It was so, so crazy. The whole thing was just a crazy time. And we had to sort of come back and complete the circle. And that's what we did. Even the way I started, I sort of said, like, here we are. Let's go.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What was the first thing Melania said to you?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, she was, she was watching it on television. She was watching it, I think she paused it for a couple of minutes, and somebody called, and she was -- ran to the television. She's great. Look, she had a bigger problem. She had a problem with, how does Barron take it? You know, Barron was playing tennis with his friends, and somebody walked over and ran over and said, your father has been shot. He's been shot. Barron, and Barron really likes his father a lot, and so does Eric, Don, Tiffany, Ivanka, the whole group. But I've been a good father to the kids.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: How is he doing in college?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : He's doing great. He's always a great student. Barron is a very good student. He's a tall student, also, by the way, pretty tall student, but he's a great boy, and he came in, mom, mom, mom, what happened. So, and then they saw me get up, and then they saw the fist bump. You know, it's very interesting. When I got up, people thought maybe he's dead because, you know, they lifted. And they're very strong guys. They can get you up fast. But I said, I'm not going to go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 And I convinced him, I thought I was only hit in the ear, it's very interesting. But they got me up and I watched, and it was dead silent, even though I was standing up and envisioned because people didn't know I was alive or not. And then when I did the fist pump, everybody realized, and they're screaming, USA, USA, it was the craziest day. It was a crazy time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And what a lot of people don't know is that a lot of the people who were behind President Trump on July 13th waited in line to be behind him again. They thought that was very important for this 12 week anniversary of what was almost a more tragic day. More of my interview with President Trump when we return.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We all know that communities across the southeast have been flattened by Hurricane Helene, and now another Hurricane Milton is barreling toward Florida, and it intensified today. It's called explosively intensifying to a category five storm. That means the winds are up to 180 miles an hour.\par \par \uc2 But with all of this American suffering, why is \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  bragging about the gobs of money were given to another country? She tweeted; the U.S. will provide nearly 157 million in additional assistance to the people of Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: A lot of people here tonight, people who are sitting near me in the crowd tonight were wondering why our priorities seem to be elsewhere, not that other people don't need help, but very concerned about that.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : So, we're into almost $300 billion for Ukraine, and yet they're offering people $750 --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: For immediate needs.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: For the worst, yes, but for the worst hurricane that anybody has seen. But more importantly than that is they don't have the people. They're not doing -- it's a bad -- it's a very bad thing. This is --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: How would you do it differently?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I have a tremendous team of people here. They don't have any people here. The people, I was in North Carolina yesterday. I was in Georgia, and Georgia is different. You have a good governor. He's doing a very good job, but North Carolina is a disaster, and it was also hit very hard, but they don't have the people, and they're complaining there's no people around to help. That was a horrific storm, much worse. And you know, it's late in the season, you wouldn't think a thing like that could happen.\par \par \uc2 But you have to get the people out, and they have to be out much more. I was there for the entire day, and I saw virtually nobody, other than the people that were badly affected. And one man, he's got a house that's gone and he's sitting on a rock. He's sitting on a rock. What's gone on there? You have to help him, and you have to help him monetarily also. And it's only, you know, if you look, it's Virginia, it's Alabama, it's -- South Carolina was hit very hard. I spoke to their great governor there, and they were hit very hard. Florida was hit very hard.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Biden said the response has been robust and well-coordinated, Mr. President.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Yes, well, nobody, nobody says that. He doesn't know what robust is. He doesn't. Look, he should be there, and she should be there. She shouldn't be at fundraisers. She's at fundraisers, and her teleprompter went off, and she didn't do well with the teleprompter off.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That's happened to me a few times, by the way.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : But you will get through it. It happens to me a lot when the teleprompter goes off, you have to be able to do it. She didn't get through it. But she shouldn't be there anyway. She should be, I would say North Carolina is so bad and she should be --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: She was there today for three hours, I believe, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . But the Democrats accused you of politicizing the storm, even though you got to the storm ravaged area first, but that you were politicizing the storm and that that was unfair.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : You know, anything I do, they'll say, oh, it's political. If I do anything good, no matter what I do, they'll say, oh, he did it for politics. I mean, they could have gotten there way before me.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The 40,000 people in North Carolina who have requested absentee ballots. Only a thousand of those have been returned. This isn't the hurricane ravaged area of North Carolina. I think Politico just did a piece about this yesterday. How will you and the Republican Party ensure that all voters in hurricane hit areas have their votes counted, because this could be a close election. Those votes could matter. And all these mainstream publications are saying this now.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Republican areas got hit very hard. You know that, it got hit very, very hard. I believe and many people are dead. Many people are dead. They don't even know what it's going to be in terms of the number. You know, they have hundreds are missing. And I can't really speak to it. I can only say that I believe they're going to go out and vote if they have to crawl to a voting booth and that's what's happening.\par \par \uc2 Lara is working on it. Other people are working on it, and we're trying to make it convenient for them to, but they just lost their house. We're trying to make it convenient for them to go out and vote, but we are doing very well in certain areas that we weren't expected to do. Virginia, we're doing well. We're doing well all over. I think we're going to do great in North Carolina, because the response has been so bad to the hurricane, they have -- this response has been horrific --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But then Elon praised \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  saying he cleared the way for some of the helicopters to come in. He praised Buttigieg.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, that was two days later --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yesterday.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : -- because what happened is he sent his great gadgets and he has the one most -- the Starlinks, it's incredible for communication, but they were asking me, the people of North Carolina and representatives, they said, could you possibly speak to \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and get it. And Elon came through, and then they sent it in, and they wouldn't allow it to go in. And took two days and then Buttigieg, as you say, Buttigieg called and I guess after two days later --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Were they shamed, are you saying into --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Of course, there was huge publicity that they wouldn't allow. He had helicopters bringing stuff in that's not even gettable. You can't even get it. I mean, honestly, Elon did an amazing job, because I called him and I told him the situation, they were trying to get it, but you can't, you know, it's very hard to get. I called him and he had, he had things being delivered literally, by the time I got off the phone, I'm even wondering. It was so quick.\par \par \uc2 But then they got there, and they said they're going to confiscate it, they're going to keep it. \uc2 FEMA\uc2  said they're going to keep it. They wouldn't determine it. Then the publicity got crazy, and then all of a sudden, they released it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , I believe, on Friday said that he believes the election will be fair and transparent, but he's not sure it's going to be peaceful. And he walked into the White House briefing room to say that and other things. What do you think he was getting at; he was referencing some of your rhetoric?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I don't think he knows. I really don't. I think he has no idea what he said. I really don't. I think he's maybe referencing -- it could be him, but I don't think he really knows. Look, they took -- they had a coup. They took it away from him. He's an angry person right now because they took it away. They shouldn't have done that. I'm no fan of his, but they should not have taken it away. They took his 14 million votes, and they gave it to somebody who they didn't want. They didn't want her, because she was last on the list --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Who would have been -- I mean, do you think?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, I don't want to say --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Mistake on their part --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : -- because I'm not looking to build up somebody, but they had about 12 people, and she was the 13th, and 12 were better. All 12.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But do you think they made a mistake -- I mean, do you think they made a political mistake?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, I'll tell you on November 6th. I can't tell you. I mean, she's not doing very well right now, we're going up and you see that, we're leading in most of the polls and all, but we were up by 21 points. We were really beating we had the debate with Biden, and after that, we had this, I think, insurmountable lead. Then I had to do it all over again. Think of this. You're like in a fighter, you're a fighter, and they come out and the fighter is getting beaten badly, and they say, we'll take that fighter out, we'll give you a new fighter now, that's what happened to me. This can only happen to me, but we're doing well now --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , the lawfare that continues to be waged against you. It's almost every time you know you're up in the polls, something else comes down. On a personal level, how do you deal with that? Just processing it, superseding indictment, another memo comes out, another former aide says this or that. So, how do you, as a personal -- on a personal level deal with it?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, what's happened is it's been totally discredited. The biggest case was in Florida. We won the case in its entirety. We're leading in most of the other cases, and a couple of them are just really bad judges that are, you know, Democrat judges, Democrat areas. But I have many cases. It's all what they did is they said, let's go after this guy, and let's cost him a lot of money, but more importantly, a lot of time and a lot of thought.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: How will you restore faith in our justice system? A lot of people will say, well, he's just going to do to them what they did to him and get back at them. And --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : A lot of people say, that's what should happen, you want to know the truth.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But, but, right, well, but I think -- but I think punitively using government institutions is what got us in this mess in the first place. And my -- our town hall that we did back in February, one of the lines that really resonated I think with people, is when you said, my revenge is going to be my success.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, I do believe that, but I will say this, they have started a terrible precedent. We've never had this. We do have that in third world countries, banana republics, a lot in South America, where they go after somebody politically that's an opponent. We never had it to any great extent, really almost at all.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But you're not going to do that. When you get in office, you're going to look at all your political enemies --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: No, I want to make this the most successful country in the world. That's what I want to do.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And so for all the women out there who maybe don't like this tweet or this joke or this nickname, tonight, what do you say to them about the safety of our homeland?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I say to the women, and I think I do very well with the women, actually. I think it's a lot of fake polling. I think I do very well because I say I will keep you safe. I'm not going to allow prisoners to come out from Venezuela and many other countries all over the world, the Congo, the Middle East. They come out from the Congo in tremendous numbers. They're coming out of the Middle East, Yemen and lots of countries that aren't particularly friendly to us. I'm going to keep you safe. I'm not going to let people hurt you. I'm going to have a very strong --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: They were bugged by that, the protector line, like some of the people went crazy about that. He said he's going to be my protector. That's looking down on us or that's patronizing.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I'm looking up at them. I am going to protect. I'm going to protect men too. It's my job to protect as President, they don't protect right now, when they let 13,099 criminals, I mean murderers. These are people in prison, some getting ready for the death penalty, and they allowed them to come into our country. When I get them out, and when I say it's not happening, and when I close the border, so that bad people -- you know, we want people in our country, but they have to be people that are capable of loving our country and that are good people. They have to go pass tests.\par \par \uc2 So, I say to people, and I'm very -- I'm not ashamed of that. I saw a couple of wise guys on the Left saying, oh, who is he? I want to protect women. I'm going to protect women, and I'm going to protect men and I'm going to protect children, and that's my obligation to do it with the military and with law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: A 100 years from now, we'll probably all not be here. How do you want to be remembered?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I think somebody that really worked hard and turned a country around that's going bad. This country is going bad. We're not going to have a country, or certainly we're not going to have a country like this. So more than anything else, I think I want to have somebody say for a long time from now and in a short time from now, he was able to make America great again.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Scale of one to 10 this rally. What was it?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : This was something really special. Well, this was love. This was pure love. I think it was, maybe on a scale of 1 to 10, it was a 12.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I knew it.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Ahead. Kamala tries to win over voters by going on a raunchy podcast. And that's just the start of her new media strategy. We have a lot more coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I want to take a moment and can we try to think of any law that gives the government the power to make a decision --\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: I know what you're going to ask.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: -- about a man's body.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: No. No.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Is there any law?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: No. No.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I honestly thought this entire thing, I was watching this, I'm like, am I being punked watching this? This cannot be who the Democrats chose as their nominee. That? Really? That's their strategy for winning. OK.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Mollie Hemingway, editor-in-chief of The Federalist, Fox News contributor. Mollie, that's your Call Your Daddy podcast, OK? This is popular among the Gen Z-ers, so they're trying to get these, you know, kind of a niche audience. She has a pretty big audience, that young lady. She's done stuff about oral sex in the past and sex tips, but she's kind of made the show a little bit more mainstream-y.\par \par \uc2 But is this going to move the needle for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  where it needs to go?\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yeah, it's a popular podcast with a certain type of female and female voter that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  already has secured. You know, that misinformation right there about abortion laws, which of course are about the fact that there is a second life in play and that abortion is violence in the womb, and so that's why it's of interest to people.\par \par \uc2 But she needs to reach out to people who aren't just these low-information female voters. She needs to expand her electorate because she's struggling in the polls. And so, she realizes that she has not been doing a good job of doing any interviews, and so she said, oh, we're going to do all these interviews, the Call Her Daddy podcast, the View, Stephen Colbert. These are all people who are open, supportive advocates for her or donors to her. That's not what she needs.\par \par \uc2 She needs to actually, not just get asked some tough questions, but tackle them a little bit better than she has been. Well, a lot better than she has been.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, again, she does this. She's doing Howard Stern and The View, the usual round of kind of woke, popular, you know, media outlets. But her problem is among male voters. Male voters don't take her all that seriously. They don't think she has the confidence, the substance, etc.\par \par \uc2 And even though -- even liberals are complaining about this now. You know, you heard Andrea Mitchell over the weekend say, you know, she's got to up the game here. So, again, as a matter of political strategy, Stern, those votes -- I mean, I don't get it. I really, as a strategy matter, I think it's stupid.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Right. So, she -- this is the best she can do is this type of interview, the Call Her Daddy podcast, where she's talking about abortion and sex. She does all right there, but not even really anywhere near well enough to be deserving of the presidency when it comes to actual tough questions.\par \par \uc2 I mean, even in the Call Her Daddy podcast, they said, she said, judge me by my record. Well, she's been trying to campaign saying, don't judge her by her record. Don't judge her by her legislation, by her role in the Biden-Harris administration, by her campaign pledges. And she can't even make it out of an interview like this.\par \par \uc2 But even with a somewhat friendly interview, like the 60 Minutes interviewer, she was really struggling to answer questions there. She did not show command of the economy. She did not understand how to talk about the border. Those are the two big issues that people are concerned about.\par \par \uc2 And yes, they want more information from her, but every time she does an interview, she flubs it up in an embarrassing fashion where people just have a hard time envisioning her as president. And I think that's why she's struggling as much as she is in these polls.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Mollie, this is the problem. It was a problem with picking Biden because they knew he was on the down slope. Then they replaced Biden with someone they also knew was really not capable of this type of freewheeling conversation about policy and substance. They already knew that, but they went with her anyway.\par \par \uc2 And now they're seeing how it's playing out because they don't have a debate to talk about. So, they have to do these interviews. Every time she does an interview, it's another disaster. This is one month out.\par \par \uc2 How dangerous a territory, do you think they're in at this point, even with their money advantage?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: I think there are a lot of people who are actually wondering whether the coup against Biden was a fatal error. I mean, say what you want about Biden. Obviously, he has serious mental acuity problems that mean he shouldn't even be president. But he was able to at least pretend to be a moderate.\par \par \uc2 With \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , she is so extreme. Her positions are so extreme. You saw one of those Trump ads that really takes her on for how she supports transgender issues. And that's very much at odds with what the American public want.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Mollie, always great to see you. Thank you so much. Up next, Elon jumps in for Trump. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, the crowd went absolutely wild when \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  jumped onto the stage for Trump. He was like a kid. It was fun. And people were enjoying it. So, I mentioned earlier, it was just fascinating to see Elon and former President Trump collaborating together.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What is your friendship with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  like? I mean, what kind of guy is he?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT, 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Well, he's incredible.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What can you tell us about him?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : He's a different kind of a guy. He loves rocket ships and things, you know? I mean.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: When you say different kind of a guy, what exactly do you mean?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I'll tell you what he is. He's obviously smart as you get, but he's got a tremendous heart.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Joining me now, \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , former 2024 GOP presidential candidate. Vivek, I was there on Saturday. It was amazing. It's interesting because politics, obviously, isn't Musk's thing, you know that. But he's driving the left crazy because they think they should have the monopoly on wealthy people supporting politicians.\par \par \uc2 But he's doing this thing now where he's paying $47 for people in swing states to sign a petition to support the constitution. It's totally legal. His goal is to get a million registered voters in swing states to sign this petition in support of the Constitution, right to bear arms.\par \par \uc2 And if recruiters manage to find a million people, the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  writes, of course Musk is going to be on the hook for a staggering $47 million. Vivek, they're putting their money where their mouth is finally, and this is infuriating the left.\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2 , FORMER 2024 GOP PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Yeah, look, I mean, Elon's a friend and he looked at the future the same way I do for my kids, which is that the real inheritance we care about giving our kids isn't just a bunch of green pieces of paper. It is a country greater than the one we grew up in.\par \par \uc2 And I do think, Laura, the thing about this election, and I think Elon sees this more clearly than most, this is not just an ordinary election about higher or low tax rates. This is about the basic ideals that the country was founded on, free speech and protecting it at the top of the list. And so, I know that's important to Elon as it is to me.\par \par \uc2 And in 1776, when you think about it, it was George Washington leading from the front, but we also had a whole cadre of Founding Fathers from Benjamin Franklin, a great inventor, somebody who invented the lightning rod and the Franklin stove, to \uc2 Thomas Jefferson\uc2 , to \uc2 John Adams\uc2 . I put Elon in that Benjamin Franklin category.\par \par \uc2 And we are seeing a kind of re-founding, a type of alliance between different types of political and non-political figures that I think is actually pretty inspiring heading into this final stage of this election. And I think it's a good thing for the country.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Vivek, you and I have talked about this, I mean, a couple years ago about -- and I know you've been so involved in this. You're doing stuff with Charlie Kirk, I know, and others. But in the nuts and bolts of getting people registered and to the polls.\par \par \uc2 And canvassing, that has to be done. It's not just giving speeches. It's getting people to the polls. And the fact that Musk is investing so much money in this, you're investing so much time in this, this is awesome and it's spreading like wildfire on X. It's 200 million followers.\par \par \uc2 So, there's no limit to the number of people you can actually refer in this petition. So, everybody's going to be looking at it now that I mention it, but it's pretty cool. And I love the fact that the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  is so annoyed by it because it's totally legal. He hacked the system.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: That means you're doing it right, is if the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  has the reaction they do. But the beauty of this is you need to lay the rails. And I think conservatives for a long time, we've had the right ideas, had our heart in the right place. You got to execute. Speaking of another inventor, made the famous quote that a vision without execution is a hallucination.\par \par \uc2 Well, winning this election cannot be a hallucination. We got to turn it into a reality. That's going to require a lot of that on the ground execution.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You bet.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a businessman. The reason I'm compelled to support him as well is I think we need more businessmen. I'm partial myself as one. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is ultimately the most successful businessman on the planet.\par \par \uc2 And the reason you see all of these people supporting the same person for U.S. president is that we understand we got to not only have the vision but also execute. Elon's doing a great job. I think it's going to be one of the highest ROI investments for positive effects on American politics that we've seen in a long time.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I love it.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: And I'm feeling cautiously optimistic, Laura. So, we'll see.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah, Vivek, I am too. No one wants to jinx it, but we're watching you out there. Keep up the great work. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 All right. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  gets down and dirty for votes. Raymond Arroyo has it. Seen and Unseen is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's time for Seen and Unseen, where we reveal the stories behind the headlines. For that, we turn to Fox News contributor Raymond Arroyo. All right, Raymond, I know you were surprised by the lead up and the reaction to the president's return to Butler.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, Laura, you would think Trump's opponents would be tamping down the rhetoric, or at least acknowledging that the former president's been a target of at least two assassination attempts, but you'd be wrong.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I think that the people who want the fistfight are the base of the Democratic Party. Democrats want to see someone get up there and give a knuckle sandwich to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: He needs to bust him in his mouth. I'm saying it like that because he's dangerous. We've got to keep this troglodyte out of office.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Leveling up these two ideas, the laughable \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  took a, you know, bullet for democracy versus a president who wants to put the country first.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Attempted murder of a presidential candidate, Laura, is never laughable, no matter who the candidate is. I'm sorry. This is really unforgivable.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: No. And I asked J.D. Vance on Saturday about this emerging narrative from the left, Raymond, and it came from Maureen Dow to the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: She's saying you're milking the martyrdom. Martyrdom milking. What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE (R-OH), U.S. VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Well, I think that's a really disgusting comment. I mean, milking what martyrdom? He was nearly killed. He nearly lost his life and part of his service for the country. And I think it's totally reasonable to remind the American people that because of the Democrats, the leadership of the Democratic Party's obsessive hatred with this one person, he has become a target of now two assassination attempts. It's really disgraceful.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I mean -- so I guess he doesn't have a right to go back to Butler and finish the speech. By the way, Raymond, the best line was, as I was saying, that was the best way to start the speech.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: But Laura, this isn't milking. This is persisting. The man has been targeted not once, twice, and maybe more than that. The fact that he's gotten up and had the consciousness, the wherewithal to put that fist up, to press on, and that he does it every day, puts himself in danger, that is a remarkable thing. And I think no matter what your political persuasion, you have to say that.\par \par \uc2 Look, I saw you were on Trump Force One the other day after the rally. What was the in-flight meal like? Give me the scoop here. Lobster, filet mignon?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Not exactly.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This is the health food. Is this what we can expect from an RFK Jr. running the \uc2 HHS\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: All organic.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All organic here.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Not processed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Is that the inside scoop, \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I was hungry. It all tasted good to me. I didn't care. I didn't eat much throughout the day. It was Ronnie Jackson and Mike Waltz. We had a great conversation.\par \par \uc2 Look, Raymond, I think the amazing thing to have been there is contrary to what the media says, there was so much love in Butler. I mean, love of country, love of this president and former president and they hope to be president again. And just love for each other.\par \par \uc2 And we don't see that enough in politics. I'm not saying no one on the left has love. I'm not saying that. But there was just a lot of love there and that was nice. It was really nice --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: At the same time, Laura, while you were in Butler, as you mentioned earlier, V.P. Harris is doing her media blitz, kind of like the blitz of Berlin. But starting it with Alex Cooper and the Call Her Daddy Podcast is probably not the best way to build your credibility. Here's more of that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I have seen girls on the street walk up to men and be like, do you know where a tampon goes? Do you know how many tampons we use? Do you even know how, like, do you know what a X or Y or Z is of a part of our -- and they don't know the answer.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: I was the first vice president or president to ever, in office, go to a reproductive health care clinic, ever.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Really?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, she can't even answer the sanitary product question directly. I mean, just for context, I don't know why she would go on this show. The Call Her Daddy podcast, as you mentioned earlier, it's graphic sex talk and advice for women. I mean, you know, the images of this show, what happens, what Alex Cooper does. And I realize the Harris team thinks this is going to gain them votes.\par \par \uc2 But if you're going on a show like this, at least let's have a question about Willie Brown or your husband, allegedly, hitting a girlfriend across the mouth, you know, call me first hubby. But none of that came up.\par \par \uc2 But a lot of other filthy asides did, and Kamala just sat there and talked abortion.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah, well, I think it -- look, I get it, podcasts are popular. A lot of things are popular that you probably don't need to lower yourself to. But a lot of the kids watch us, a lot of young people watch us, and I just say, that's sad.\par \par \uc2 But Raymond, Raymond, I wish you were there in Butler. Thank you so much for your insights tonight. As always, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 All right, that's it for us tonight. Thank you for watching. I'll have more on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , so make sure to go there. Jesse Watters takes it all from here.\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020241008eka700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 VP Harris Talks Abortion On Popular Podcast; 10/7 Terror Attack Remebered As Mideast War Rages; DeSantis Speaks As Cat 5 Milton Approaches Florida; Trump-Inspired Song Tops Billboard Digital Chart\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Gerry Baker, Robert Wolf, Bryan Llenas \par \uc2 7,789 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thank you very much, John and Sandra. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum and this is THE STORY. Breaking right now, 365 days and the hostage families are still on watch, still waiting, and survivors are still mourning the loss of their loved ones a year after the barbaric October 7th attacks on Israel.\par \par \uc2 Anti-Israel protesters are out in the streets. As you can see, this is a live shot here in New York City today, some vowing to "honor the martyrs of Palestine", others chanting Israel is a terrorist state, the world remembering the day that 1,200 people were slaughtered at a music festival and in their kibbutz homes. Fears of a full-out war in the Middle East are very, very widespread.\par \par \uc2 250 people were kidnapped on October the 7th, 101 hostages are still being held. They have been there now for 365 days in the tunnels of Gaza. Jonathan Diller survived that attack. He was at the Nova Music Festival. He was supposed to be a drone operator that morning and he was with many of his friends, some of whom did not make it back. He's joining me in just a few moments and we're grateful to him for his time today.\par \par \uc2 First let's go to correspondent Bryan Llenas who is outside with the protesters here in New York. Hi, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Well, for the last few hours now, hundreds, if not thousands of anti-Israel protesters have begun marching here in New York City. You can see them starting in Wall Street, making their way north, uptown to Washington Square Park, as well as Union Square, which is home to \uc2 NYU\uc2 , another university.\par \par \uc2 There are also dueling protests that were at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 , pro- and anti-Israel protesters. But I need to show you this video. This happened in the last hour, about an hour and a half ago, where we were marching with these protesters and pro-anti-Israel protesters mobbed two pro-Israel protesters who were waving their Israeli flags.\par \par \uc2 And they ripped the flags out of their hands and the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  had to step in and move them away. That was just one confrontation that we saw. Besides that, that's what we've seen here. The message here on October 7th is that they want Palestine to be free. They are honoring the martyrs. And again, the \uc2 NYPD\uc2 , Martha, is on high alert today because there are also pro-Israeli demonstrations that are planned today in Central Park and Times Square where these protesters are also scheduled to meet.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in Clara. She is a pro-Palestinian protester as well as a rabbi here who is here for the cause. I want to do, Clara, if you can, and Oleg, if you can show their sign here. I wanted to give you a sense, Clara, can you explain why you're here on October the 7th when much of the world is mourning the death of those 1,200 Israelis, why you're decided to join the anti-Israel protest today?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We are here marching with our Jewish brothers to demand peace. Peace now. Our tax money should not go to murder innocent children in Gaza, and in Lebanon, and the West Bank, but to help American people who are also suffering. It's not about Israel, it's about peace, humanity, and this is why we're here together, to stop instrumentalizing October 7th for the purpose of profit, the purpose of genocide.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: I understand that. There has not been one mention since we've been in this protest mourning or honoring the hostages or those who died on that day, 365 days ago. And I'm wondering why that is, if this is about being together.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No, this is the wrong way of seeing things. We are all mourning the victims who are here for humanity. We mourn the victims of October 7th, but we're also mourning the victims that are dying right now as we speak. Over 42,000 people in Gaza died, over 2,000 people in Lebanon, children, the majority is children, doctors, teachers, these people are those who are suffering now so we should stop thinking and look at the future.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Got it and you have a sign here that shows President Biden and Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , genocide jail and cold-blooded Kamala. Martha, that's what we've got here for you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, Bryan, thank you very much. That is the scene in the streets right now. Let's bring in Jonathan Diller. He survived the October 7th \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terror attack on the Nova Music Festival. He was there from early that morning and four of his friends were murdered, including Amad Sarrousi, who was taken hostage by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and was just confirmed dead not that long ago early September. Jonathan thank you for being with me today. What goes through your mind as you see this protest in New York and I know you just spoke today to a group at \uc2 NYU\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN DILLER, SURVIVED OCTOBER 7TH ATTACK: Correct. I just came back from \uc2 NYU\uc2  to speak about what happened October 7th from my point of view. Honestly, to see the news, see all this propaganda in the streets, for me it's disgusting. It's a disgrace to see someone in the street telling me he's a pro-peace, he's a pro-activist in peace, peacemaking, doing all this propaganda. I don't know how that really makes sense. Doing graffitis on monuments and everything, how does that promote peace?\par \par \uc2 Well, Israel is mourning on their people, it's like mourning on another 9- 11. This is our 9-11. People on the streets, you ask them, a lot of them don't even know who did 9-11. So, of course they wouldn't know what happened on October 7th. There's so much misinformation out there and it's sad to see that this is the reality. You see people on the street chanting for death and Intifada. And it's really a disgrace, especially coming from someone who is Jewish himself.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let's put a picture of your dear friend, Amad Sarrousi, who was one of the four friends that you lost on that day. Here he is playing his guitar. Tell me a little bit, because I think it's important for people to know your story and to remember you were there that morning to be a drone operator. What was going through your mind and your heart and your body as this started to unfold? How did you make any sense of it?\par \par \uc2 DILLER: It was kind of just following my intuition of that day. You know, all the signs came up. We saw an injured girl. She was dying in front of our eyes and hundreds of missiles were coming from Gaza. And suddenly, we heard gunfire. Eventually, we start walking for hours in the desert to seek shelter. So, four of my friends didn't survive. But I'm lucky here to really speak on behalf of them. I feel like, really, I'm their voice.\par \par \uc2 It's my responsibility to really share this. Regarding Amad, he went to high school with me. He was a year younger than me. Great guy, beautiful smile, a pure soul. And he managed to somehow survive, and he was one of the hostages and not too long ago, there were six hostages in Gaza, they were in the tunnels and they were shot to death by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists because the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists didn't want to risk it that they will be alive and they will be alive and the Israelis will get them in a hostage jail, so there was this hope that Amad and the other hostages will come back including Hirsch, an American citizen as well.\par \par \uc2 They shot all six of them in the head, really before the \uc2 IDF\uc2  almost found them. So, it's a really tragic story and the best thing we can do is keep their memory alive by sharing these stories. And I won't stop doing it and I've been doing it for the last year quite a lot.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you think, you see that picture of \uc2 Bibi Netanyahu\uc2 , the Prime Minister, what do you think, you know, what goes through your mind when you look at that and what would you say about his leadership? Do you support him? Are you happy with what has happened in the recent weeks?\par \par \uc2 DILLER: Look, I'm not a big politics fan. I know he's trying to do his best job to fight all the fronts. Israel is really fighting against the \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  in the north, in Lebanon and in Gaza. The Lebanese don't want the \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  to be there. You can see the Lebanese are trying to flee away from all this terrorism that is forced upon them, as well as Gaza. You've got to free Gaza from \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . That's how you free them.\par \par \uc2 And we're doing everything we can to really protect ourselves from Iran's proxies and of course getting all the 101 hostages back because they didn't deserve this. And I didn't deserve this being in a music festival together with ravers. Imagine people in a music festival like Coachella, Burning Man, and just hundreds of terrorists shooting everyone like it's in a duck range. This is monstrous. \uc2 ISIS\uc2  does that. And that's what we're dealing with. That's the reality.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Jonathan, thank you. I am so glad that you're speaking to universities, and I hope that you can continue to do that. I know it's not easy in the face of what you're seeing here in the street, but it's so important for you to tell your side of the story and we're grateful for you coming by today. Thank you, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 DILLER: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Vice President Harris shied away a bit from calling the United States a real close "ally" of Netanyahu as Israel's war rages on following the murder of 1,200 people, the abduction of hundreds of others by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and the largest ever missile attack from Iran into Israel. Former U.S. Ambassador to Israel, Tom Nides, joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: The work that we have done has resulted in a number of movements in that region by Israel were very much prompted by or a result of many things including our advocacy for what needs to happen in the region.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Protests here in New York City. They are moving their way up towards Washington Square at the moment from lower Manhattan as the world marks one year since the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attacks on Israel. And fears that a wider war is brewing, perhaps already here, are growing more intense. Israel's defense minister warning, "everything is on the table" as far as Israel is concerned right now in terms of their response to Iran's massive missile strike last week on Israel. Thankfully, it was very much largely unsuccessful. Former U.S. ambassador to Israel, Thom Nides is here with me in New York.\par \par \uc2 Ambassador, welcome. It's good to have you back with us today.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 THOMAS NIDES, FORMER UNITED STATES AMBASSADOR TO ISRAEL: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you for being here. What do you -- what goes through your mind as you look at these massive protests that are heading up through midtown right now?\par \par \uc2 NIDES: You know, I'm not spending time worried about them. I'm worried about the families that are waking up today a year. I sat at this table four days after October 7th, OK? And these families still have their loved ones sitting in tunnels in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Every single day I talk to one of the families of one of his hostages, either those who are -- or sadly have been -- have been dead, have been killed, or still captive. That's what I'm worried about.\par \par \uc2 I'm worried about the what's going on in Israel today. I'm worried about what's going on, what \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  is doing to the Israelis in the north. So, I'm not worried about protesters walking up and down streets. I'm worried about these families. That's who my heart breaks for, and that's what I'm thinking about on October 7th.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Let's talk a little bit about the White House response to this throughout the course of these last 365 days. This is a bit of a look back at President Biden's comments throughout. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: My administration's support for Israel's security is rock solid and unwavering.\par \par \uc2 And conduct of the response in Gaza -- in the Gaza Strip has been over the top.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you aware of that? Are you comfortable with their plans, Mr. --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm more aware than you might know, and I'm comfortable with them stopping. We should have a cease fire now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Is that good advice for Israel? Cease fire now?\par \par \uc2 Martha, I don't think anyone, if you step back, can argue with how focused \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has been on the security of the State of Israel. There is a reason why 180 rockets that were shot off the State of Israel just a week ago were helped by shooting by our military, our ships that are seeing the Mediterranean.\par \par \uc2 The Iranians know we have Israel's back. The Iranians know, as \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  knows, that we've got Israel's back. And this president at some material political cost to him and to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , have stood by the security of the State of Israel, which is the right thing to do at the right time, at the right moment.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: This is Vice President Harris. She was asked whether Israel's Netanyahu is a close ally of the United States, and she hedged a bit on this and sort of broadened out the answer. Let's watch and get your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do we have a real close ally in Prime Minister Netanyahu?\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I think, with all due respect, the better question is, do we have an important alliance between the American people and the Israeli people. And the answer to that question is yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Does Netanyahu deserve a more full-throated supportive response in that moment?\par \par \uc2 NIDES: I've spent hundreds of hours of beating Netanyahu. OK?\par \par \uc2 Our support is not about one prime minister, one defense minister, it is about the state of Israel, and I don't think anyone can question \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's support for the security of the state. And that's what this means. That, to me, is what --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But they have time and time again, pushed them back. There is been discussion, you know, Macron is now calling for no more weapons to be sent to Israel, and there's been a lot of questioning of the response by Israel, and not a lot of outward damning of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and putting the blame on them and on Iran.\par \par \uc2 We hear more about them wanting to be -- Israel to be careful about their response than we do about blaming Iran outwardly.\par \par \uc2 NIDES: Well, this is what pushed back a little bit on the -- OK?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NIDES: I spent a lot of time on this topic, and I spent a lot of time listening to the words of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  about the need and support for the security of State of Israel.\par \par \uc2 It was \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  who made the decision to send those operations inside the Mediterranean. It is their decision to send the head of CENTCOM, General Kurilla, into the region to make sure Israel is secure. You can talk about words actions matter.\par \par \uc2 And this administration, I believe, firmly, supports the idea of security of the State of Israel.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I got to go. But is it time to take out those nuclear sites to the greatest of Israel's capability?\par \par \uc2 NIDES: We'll let the president who will be elected in maybe 30 days, have that decision about what ultimately, they are going to have to do about security of the state.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's Israel's decision, though.\par \par \uc2 NIDES: It's Israel's decision. They have security of the State of Israel, but they also need to understand clearly that we have their back.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But do you support Israel striking the nuclear sites inside Iran?\par \par \uc2 NIDES: I have the very strong view that we have to secure the State of Israel from all of its enemies, including principally Iran.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Thom Nides, thank you very much. Former ambassador to Israel. Always good to see you, Thom, and thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 NIDES: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. So, coming up, a husband and wife in North Carolina, clinging to a piece of wall as flood waters rush in. There is video of their experience when their house basically was dragged into the water. The husband survives and the wife floated away. They are still looking for her. We're going to talk to their daughter moments away.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we are just moments away from getting an update from Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Look at the size and enormity of Hurricane Milton, now barreling through the Gulf headed towards Florida.\par \par \uc2 Category 5 storm at this point, 175 miles an hour as it closes in on the coast, as the southeast is still very much recovering and in rescue mission mode from Hurricane Helene.\par \par \uc2 More than a week later, 58-year-old Kim Ashby is among those missing in North Carolina. She was with her husband Rod when the flood waters swept them into a river.\par \par \uc2 Rod tried to hang on to Kim, and at one point lost his grip. He survived. She is still missing. Her daughter. Jessica Meidinger joins me now. Jessica, I can't imagine what you and your family have been going through over the course of the past week.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, just know that everybody is thinking about you and your mom and these missing people. Tell me about your situation and what you understand about what happened.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA MEIDINGER, DAUGHTER OF MISSING NORTH CAROLINA WOMAN: Thank you. Yes. So, from the perspective of my mom, and when she is been last seen, her, and my stepdad went out there Thursday night to kind of lift the garage doors to allow flood waters to flow underneath so that their house didn't become a makeshift dam.\par \par \uc2 And Friday morning, there was this huge surge of water that we now understand came from a bridge downstream breaking that had dammed and just this huge flush of water coming through that really knocked everything off its base, and they had basically no time to react, and it slid into the water. And they floated in their house down until the river kind of turned, and they hit an embankment, and that embankment is really what made the house collapse.\par \par \uc2 But even after that, Rod was able to pull himself out of the debris and go reach my mom, who's about 10, 20 feet away, get her out of the debris, and they were floating on a wall of wood, and we now know that they hit in a large maple tree that was in the middle of the river as they were looking where they were going to get off, and they each just flew in different directions.\par \par \uc2 And that's the last that my mom has been seen. Rod was able to get himself out of the water, but she is -- has not been located since.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Oh, my God, I'm so sorry. This is -- you just never in a million years imagine that in this mountainous region, you know, something like this is going to happen.\par \par \uc2 I know -- can you -- can you tell us about your mom. She is a seventh-grade teacher and a really beloved member of the community. I know we have pictures to share with her. So, tell us about her.\par \par \uc2 MEIDINGER: Yes, I mean, my mom is a brilliantly beautiful person. She is a very vibrant person. As an educator, she is everything that you would want. She is compassionate, she is creative, she is compelling. She is more than a teacher for her kids, she is a mentor and she's an advocate. And she went above and beyond the call of duty to be there for these kids. And we've heard multiple stories from students in the present and 20 years past about just ways that she showed up for them that teachers typically don't.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEIDINGER: And as having her as a mother and having that type of support and that type of love that's there is -- there is nothing like it, and we were able to experience that firsthand. And we're incredibly blessed to be -- have been mentored by her.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know what? She is obviously a great lady, because she has a great daughter. Jessica, we -- you were presenting your mom and speaking on her behalf so strongly, and we hope for a miracle in this -- in this situation. No one could ever imagine that something like this would happen to your family member. Your mom was young and strong.\par \par \uc2 And what is your sense, before I let you go, of how many people are like your mom are missing in the area?\par \par \uc2 MEIDINGER: Goodness. I mean, it's hard to say anyone compares to my mom from a bias point of view, but I know that there are tons of people missing, and their families are hurting, just like our family.\par \par \uc2 And we have been blessed to have coverage in her story, shared to where we've had a lot of teams been able to come on site to help, but I actually like to take a second to thank some of those teens by name.\par \par \uc2 I got a list from my uncle. So, the Avery County Sheriff's Department, the Rowan County Sheriff's Department, Elk Park Volunteer Fire Department, Winston-Salem fire departments with for our rescue. Carry Fire Departments with water rescue and see wildlife select troops from Fort Liberty, Connecticut Search and Rescue, and Memphis, Tennessee Search and Rescue.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEIDINGER: So, we've had been blessed with a lot of people who have come on scene and are helping, and I -- but I know that there are so many families that have not received the wealth of resources that we have there hurting just as bad as we are.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, Kim, hang in there. And are -- you know, we are -- we are thinking about your family and all the others who also have missing family members. And I thank you for being with us, and we pray for a miracle for your mom. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 MEIDINGER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. What is -- unbelievable situation in our country. We all have to pull together for these people in Western North Carolina and other areas and the storm that's on its way.\par \par \uc2 So, obviously, we're about 30 days away from an election, and there's a bit of a switch in the media strategy for Vice President Harris, and it included a stop at the sometimes seen as controversial, "Call Her Daddy" podcast.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I don't hear, no. And I urge all the daddy gang, don't hear no. Just don't hear it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Jerry Baker and Robert Wolf, join me on the new Harris media strategy coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So we have some protesters out in New York City today, which was obviously expected as the world marks one year since the heinous \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attacks and hostage taking on Israel. A hundred and one people are still held in Israel by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . Vice President Harris saying that she will always make sure that Israel has what it needs to defend itself.\par \par \uc2 She's also talking about another issue in the election, which is a very hot issue, of course, of abortion. And here she is on "Call Her Daddy," the popular podcast that focuses on women's issues.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: A bunch of these guys up in these state capitols, are writing these decisions because they somehow have decided that they're in a better position to tell you what's in your best interest than you are to know what's in your own best interest. It's outrageous.\par \par \uc2 ALEX COOPER, HOST, 'CALL HER DADDY' PODCAST: It's outrageous. I mean, Daddy Gang, to put it in our TikTok terms. I have seen girls on the street walk up to men and be like, do you know where a tampon goes? Do you know how many tampons we use? Do you even know how, like, do you know what X or Y or Z are a part of our -- and they don't know the answer.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I was the first vice president or president to ever in office go to a reproductive healthcare clinic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Wow. I don't know. I get -- that's what you get if you go on "Call Her Daddy." I think you're gonna expect that you're going to get something like that. With me now, Gerry Baker, Wall Street Journal Editor at Large and Author of "American Breakdown," and Robert Wolf, former Economic Advisor to President Obama and Founder and CEO of 32 Advisors, both are Fox News contributors.\par \par \uc2 Robert, you were shaking your head a bit there. What's going through your mind?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT WOLF, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, let me just say I'm glad she's doing 60 minutes tonight. I'm glad she's doing Howard Stern. I don't know much about "Call Her Daddy," although I looked it up before. It seems like they get 10 million women listening to it on a weekly basis. It's the number one -- number two podcast on \uc2 Spotify\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So my guess is, with respect to get out the vote, between that and Howard Stern, you're getting kind of both bookends. I'd rather talk about October 7th. So my view is, listen, I don't have an issue with protest. It kind of hurts me that they're protesting this type of day.\par \par \uc2 We know one thing about Iran and their proxies, the Houthis, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , is that, it is clear that there's three things they have in common, their hate for America, their hate for Jews, and their hate for Israel. So my view, this is a day, you know, this is my week of the most holiest time of my year, between Rosh Hashanah and Yom Kippur. The idea that I'm watching this just hurts.\par \par \uc2 It feels like to me, we should be talking about the hostages. We should figure out how to do a ceasefire, and we should figure out how to take out Iran.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I read a piece this morning, Gerry, that talked about western civilization and how western civilization is standing in the way of the perpetuation and preservation of western civilization. Whether you look at Israel or you look at Ukraine, holding back the need to secure those elements of what's so important in the world.\par \par \uc2 GERRY BAKER, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes. Look, the world owes an extraordi - - the Western world, Western civilization, like owes a massive debt to Israel for what it's done in the last year, in what it's done over a very long period of time, but particularly in the last year in terms of inflicting the kind of damage on these enemies. And let's make no mistakes, make a stake about it.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, the, you know, \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2 , who the Israelis, thank God, executed last week, the Iranian leaders, they talk about it in these terms. Of course, they see Israel as something they want to eliminate, but they talk about it as their wider objective of essentially controlling the West, bringing the west back as it was, some of it was 600 years ago, back under the control of the kind of the extreme Islamic ideology that they have.\par \par \uc2 So Israel is on the front line of this war saving the rest of us from this extraordinary onslaught and their own objectives. And so many people in the west are saying, no, no, we can't support Israel. That's terrible. It's, you know, it's a really poor state of mind.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, it really feels like Netanyahu has just decided, OK, look, we've got a lame duck president, I've got to fit this thing in while I can. I've got to achieve as many of my goals as I possibly can. Robert, quick thought on that.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Absolutely. He's going to do what's single-handedly best for Israel, irrespective of all the different rhetoric for the good or, and/or the bad. And it's clear that unfortunately, getting back the hostages is taking a second step to making sure that they go after these Iran proxies in Iran itself.\par \par \uc2 And maybe that's -- I would rather see the other, but I think time right now is something he's got to capitalize on.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, I guess "Call Her Daddy" didn't ask any questions about the hostages.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: "Call Her Daddy" but don't ask her any difficult questions.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Exactly, all right. Thanks, you guys, very much. Good to see you both, Gerry Baker and Robert Wolf.\par \par \uc2 So later this hour, the singer songwriter behind this Trump inspired anthem, "Fighter," which you heard at the Butler, Pennsylvania rally over the weekend. We're going to talk to him when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 RON DESANTIS (R), FLORIDA: But as of right now, this is a ferocious hurricane. It is currently a little less than 700 miles southwest of Tampa. Storm surge watch has been issued for the Florida Gulf coast from Mainland Monroe County to the Dixie Levy County line, possibility of eight to twelve feet of storm surge is forecast from the Anclote River to Englewood, including Tampa Bay. Five to ten foot peak storm surges forecast from Yankee Town Southward to the Anclote River and from Englewood Southward to Bonita Beach, including Charlotte Harbor.\par \par \uc2 And those numbers can change and they can go up in certain areas depending on how the storm ultimately approaches the Florida Peninsula. Hurricane watches have been issued for portions of West Central, Florida and the Nature Coast. Tropical storm watches extend further south and north through Southwest Florida and the Keys and along Northern Florida.\par \par \uc2 The Florida Division of Emergency Management is actively addressing storm related resource requests. They're currently in the process of fulfilling 850 missions. They've deployed everything from sandbags to shelf stable meals, to tens of thousands of water bottles, to thousands of tarps. We've already sent truckloads of food and water to Central Florida in preparation for points of distribution site subsequent to the storm hitting.\par \par \uc2 We're also coordinating the deployment of more than 2000 feet of flood protection systems and prioritizing critical infrastructure like hospitals, wastewater treatment facilities and electrical infrastructure. We have generators deployed to support special needs sheltering. We also have Starlinks deployed in areas that have asked for that.\par \par \uc2 More than 200 ambulances and more than 30 para-transits are in Central Florida ready to support first responder operations as appropriate. \uc2 State of Florida\uc2  is amassing fuel reserves ahead of Milton, and staging it to be utilized as needed. Those quantities include 415,000 gallons of diesel, 389,000 gallons of gasoline, and there is, as I mentioned this morning, an additional 1.5 million gallons of both diesel and gasoline that are en route to the state.\par \par \uc2 Now, there is currently no fuel shortage in the state of Florida. Fuel continues to arrive at Florida ports. However, there are long lines at some gas stations. And because you've had a run on gas, there have been gas stations that have run out of fuel, but they are being resupplied with the fuel. So we're going to continue to ensure that we have fuel coming but they're still coming with the ports\par \par \uc2 We have some coming on the ground so people can go. You may be in a line, but if your gas station runs out, there's going to be more fuel on the way.\par \par \uc2 As I've mentioned, we have ramped up support for county debris removal operations. We're working this mission to supplement their mission 24/7, around the clock and we're going to continue to do that all the way until it is no longer safe to do so.\par \par \uc2 Please, local officials, these vendors. There's been vendors that they don't necessarily do. And then when there's some sunlight and people see they're not doing, then they start to work really hard. We got to get this, as much of this taken care of as possible. I know with Kevin and with Jared, putting the state assets to supplement, it has made a really significant difference. We want to continue to do that.\par \par \uc2 Those barrier islands like Pinellas and Manatee that are under evacuation orders, people obviously should heed those. But once that deadline passes, we're still going to have the debris removal operations ongoing all the way until we start to hit tropical storm force winds. So that's all the rest of today, through the night, all day tomorrow, most likely into the beginning of Wednesday morning.\par \par \uc2 So I appreciate all the effort that's gone into that on the state. We have deployed just on the state side to assist with the federal or, excuse me, with the local debris mission. Over 300 dump trucks to help with debris. And in the last 36 hours, state assets have been able to do 12,000 cubic yards of debris removed from communities and brought to landfill. That's 663 total loads.\par \par \uc2 So that's been a good mobilization. It will make a difference, but we need to keep doing that to mitigate the harm that could happen once the storm goes. Florida National Guard, State Guard, additional \uc2 FDOT\uc2  personnel, and Florida Highway Patrol are all engaged in that debris mission.\par \par \uc2 For the storm, we have 5,000 National Guardsmen that have been mobilized, and we will have, before the storm hits, another 3,000 National Guardsmen that will be on duty. We're also deploying major heavy equipment to assist with debris removal. And, of course, National Guard will be a big part of any search and rescue that needs to happen, as well as helping set up our points of distribution.\par \par \uc2 These debris and landfill sites are open not just from state assets or from the local debris contractors, but for private individuals and citizens. And so, if you've got a pickup truck, you get load debris up. We want you to go and do that.\par \par \uc2 I know we've had issues with those sites being left -- being closed when they should be open 24 -- we've opened them when we've seen that, but let's keep this going until it's no longer safe to do so. We're also coordinating the evacuation of pets from Pinellas, Manatee and DeSoto DeSoto Counties to Walton County's temporary emergency animal shelter. Obviously, we expect Floridians to take care of their own pets, but you've got some of these pets that are in shelters, and we don't want to see them abandoned in areas that are very, very dangerous.\par \par \uc2 We're also delivering a 2,500 gallon water tanker to Suwannee County animal control and coordinating veterinary assistance post-landfall for Hillsboro County. So the linemen that are being pre-staged, as is our practice in Florida, we will have over 30,000 linemen. Many are still en route. Some of them are coming from as far away as California.\par \par \uc2 You still have ongoing major power restoration missions in states like Georgia and North Carolina. That has taken a lot of resources, but all of our companies are committed to utilizing mutual aid. And so, you're going to end up having over 30,000, which is a massive number of folks to be staged. And, of course, we're anticipating significant power outages, and so it's important that we do that.\par \par \uc2 We have hundreds of state search and rescue personnel embedded in potential impact sites along the West Coast to begin immediate rescue operations as soon as the storm passes. We've established a temporary base camp at Tropicana Field in St. Petersburg to support ongoing operations, not just with the current debris mission but also post-landfall first responder missions.\par \par \uc2 So you should have a plan and you should be executing your plan, particularly if you're on the West Coast of the Florida peninsula. These evacuation orders have gone out in many of those places. I know more will be coming --\par \par \uc2 (END LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- all right. Another monster storm headed toward Florida. And you heard the precautionary actions being taken by Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in Florida. We'll keep an eye on that. We're got a breaking news on that throughout the course of the next few days, and a lot this hour as they are bracing for that.\par \par \uc2 And also, we've got the anti-Israel protests happening live right now in New York City. Meanwhile, look at the heartbreak on the faces of these people at a memorial ceremony at the site of the October 7th attacks. The ceremony's moment of silence was interrupted by the sounds of uncontrollable grief.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: These poor family members. What a year this has been that started on this day, October 7th, one year ago. With that, we bring in \uc2 Robert O'Brien\uc2 , who served as National Security Advisor in the Trump administration. Robert, it's good to have you back.\par \par \uc2 It's obviously such a difficult day for these families. It does remind me of 9/11 one year later when you listen to these cries, of this pain that is still so raw. Your thoughts on what we're seeing today, and I actually want to start by playing a bit of an interview that will play on "60 Minutes" tonight when Vice President Harris was asked if we are strong allies with \uc2 Bibi Netanyahu\uc2 . Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER, CBS NEWS HOST: Do we have a real close ally in Prime Minister Netanyahu?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I think, with all due respect, the better question is, do we have an important alliance between the American people and the Israeli people? And the answer to that question is yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: \uc2 Robert O'Brien\uc2 , your reaction and your thoughts on all of this?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT O'BRIEN\uc2 , FORMER TRUMP NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISOR: Well, \uc2 Bibi Netanyahu\uc2  is someone I got to know very well as we negotiated the Abraham Accords, Martha. And he's been the Israeli prime minister for twelve years, the longest serving Israeli prime minister since Ben-Gurion. He's democratically-elected.\par \par \uc2 And Israel is our strongest ally in the Middle East. We talk about democracy all the time, and the Democrats and Vice President Harris loves to talk about democracy and how important it is. Well, the people of Israel have elected \uc2 Bibi Netanyahu\uc2  to be their democratically-elected prime minister.\par \par \uc2 We need to support him. He's involved in a war for the very survival of the state of Israel. But as Gerry Baker said in your last segment, it's for the survival of the West. We owe Netanyahu a great debt and we owe the Israeli people a great debt for the sacrifices that they're undertaking to keep the West safe.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I want to get your response to this. This is President Biden and former President Trump on the issue of Israel potentially striking the nuclear weapon sites in Iran. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Would you support an attack on Iran's nuclear sites by Israel?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D), PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: The answer is no.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER US PRESIDENT AND PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: When they asked him that question, the answer should have been hit the nuclear first and worry about the arrest later.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Obviously, this is a big moment in the history of Israel as they think about what they can do with an absent leadership in \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . Nasrallah is gone, and the pager operation was quite successful. What do you think about this issue of the nuclear sites?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Anything that Israel or America could do to degrade the Iranian nuclear program is important. We saw Iran send 180 ballistic missiles at Israel just last week. Can you imagine if those are nuclear tips, Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: We got -- in those sites, and it's time for Iran to stop having a nuclear program immediately. They have no business having nuclear weapons.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, it's the one thing everyone seems to agree on, that Iran cannot have a nuclear weapons program. And there is a window here that perhaps should be taken advantage of, and we'll see what happens. \uc2 Robert O'Brien\uc2 , thank you. Please come back son.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We always enjoy having you. \uc2 Robert O'Brien\uc2 , former national security advisor in the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So that's part of the song that you may have heard at the Butler rally over the weekend. And it was inspired by former President Trump. It is now number one on Billboard's digital sales chart.\par \par \uc2 The former president obviously likes this song, so we're going to talk about that. We're going to talk about the return to Butler, Pennsylvania over the weekend for another rally at the spot where he survived his first assassination attempt. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's a monster. They never (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : That's a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH), VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: We heard the shots, you saw the blood., We all feared the worst, but you knew everything would be OK when President Trump raised his fist high in the air and shouted, fight, fight.\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK, CEO, \uc2 TESLA MOTORS\uc2 : The true test of someone's character is how they behave under fire.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And no one will ever stop us, no matter what dangers come our way.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Singer and songwriter Jon Kahn, co-wrote "Fighter," and he joins me now. Jon, welcome, and congratulations on this very successful song for you. Give us a sense of what your inspiration was and why you decided to write this.\par \par \uc2 JON KAHN, SINGER AND SONGWRITER: Well, Martha, thanks for having me. I think a lot of people think that the song was written in response to the first assassination attempt, when it actually was written before. And I, along with so many others, had seen so many forces thrown at President Trump since he was elected in 2016, from the impeachment to being de- platformed from social media to the lawfare.\par \par \uc2 And I think, you know, it all concluded for me when I saw, I was actually watching Fox News. And I saw after that bogus trial in New York and the conviction, the way he walked up to the microphones. And he just had that demeanor, that unflappable demeanor that said, it doesn't matter what you throw at me, I'm not going to stop.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And I just shook my head and I thought, he doesn't have to do this, and he could live a very comfortable life. And I just, you know, I went over to the piano and I thought, wow, what a fighter this man is. And played a few chords and took that idea to Nashville, where I worked with an amazing songwriter named Chris Wallin. And we finished the song in about an hour and a half.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, that's what happens when inspiration hits, right?\par \par \uc2 KAHN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, congratulations to you. Clearly, it is anthem for all the Trump supporters and this video was beautifully shot. And thank you very much for joining us today, it's good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 KAHN,: Thanks for having me, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And we need to look at this. We've seen a lot of very significant moments over the course of this campaign, and now 30 days to go. Jon, thank you so much for being with us so.\par \par \uc2 KAHN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Obviously, we're going to stay on top of this election over the next 30 days. There is so much at stake in this country as people start to head to the polls. And this week, California and Texas, and other states are starting to vote by mail. So stay with us as we continue our coverage of democracy 2024. Thank you for joining me today.\par \par \uc2 That's "The Story" on this Monday, October 7th as we remember 101 hostages still held by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . Remember them today in your prayers. Let's get them home. We'll see you back here tomorrow at 3:00.\par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020241008eka700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Gov. Walz on Abortion Stance, Campaign Economic Plan, Border Crisis; Israel Strikes \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  Positions in Beirut; Israel Says it Captured a Tunnel Where \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  was Planning a Cross Border Attack; Netanyahu Promises Retaliation for Iranian Ballistic Missile Attack; Speaker Johnson on Potential for Additional Aid to Israel and Disaster Funding; Biden Warns Congress of Future Need For More Disaster Funding; White House Urges Israel To Avoid Striking Iran Nuclear Facilities; Devastation Across Southeast After Hurricane Helene; Trump Returns To Site Of Assassination Attempt; Jack Smith Reveals New Evidence in Trump Election Case\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Trey Yingst, Griff Jenkins, Alexis McAdams, Juan  Williams \par \uc2 10,187 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 One month to Election Day, we sit down with Democratic vice presidential candidate \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  for his first Sunday show interview.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN), VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Character matters, character matters! And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): Fresh off his debate with Senator J.D. Vance, we tackle the issues facing Americans as we inch closer to November 5th -- from the response to Hurricane Helene, to rising prices at the checkout, a crisis averted at the ports for now, and the Middle East on the brink. We'll ask Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  what voters need to know about the Harris campaign's policies. It's a "FOX News Sunday" exclusive.\par \par \uc2 Plus --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It was just devastating.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We haven't received any response from FEMA.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Speaker Mike Johnson joins us with the latest on calls for Congress to provide emergency funding for the devastated Southeast.\par \par \uc2 And is the Keystone State the key to winning the White House?\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania Senator \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  joins us live.\par \par \uc2 Plus, former President Trump returns to the site of his first assassination attempt.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT & 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: On this very ground, a coldblooded assassin aim to silence me.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 We begin with some of your headlines today.\par \par \uc2 Israel launching fresh air strikes against Iran-backed \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  militants in Beirut, Lebanon, and striking what it calls a \uc2 Hamas\uc2  command and control center embedded at a mosque housing displaced Palestinians in the Gaza Strip.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine says Russia launched attack 87 drones and four missiles overnight, the majority which air defense forces were able to shoot down.\par \par \uc2 And forecasters are tracking tropical Storm Milton in the Gulf of Mexico, predicting it could make landfall on Florida's west coast Wednesday as a category hurricane, adding to the misery for millions of Americans struggling to recover from Hurricane Helene.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, our exclusive interview with Democratic vice presidential nominee \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , but first, team coverage of Helene's aftermath. Alexis McAdams has reaction from the campaign trail and Griff Jenkins is on the ground in hard-hit western North Carolina. That's where we begin Griff with you in Swannanoa, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yeah. Good morning, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 The devastation is simply unimaginable. There were roads out, bridges gone and here in this area a business ripped from its foundation. The river crested behind me at more than 25 feet, entire communities like the one here in Swannanoa was essentially cut off. You can see from our FOX flight drone team the magnitude of the destruction which will take weeks if not months if not years to rebuild.\par \par \uc2 As of this morning, the death -- death toll stands in more than 232 across seven states. That number expected to rise. Nearly a half a million remain without power as millions more try and pick up the pieces after this once in a generation storm.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Just devastating. I mean, this is just unreal, the way this has all happened.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Damage is, you know, catastrophic. I mean, it's 100 percent loss.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It saddens me seeing them come down here sorting through their stuff every day. I mean, look at that. They -- it saddens me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Soldiers, equipment and other resources from the \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2 's 82nd airborne have now been mobilized and are assisting the devastated region as the federal response continues to come under scrutiny. FEMA's Director \uc2 Deanne Criswell\uc2  and Governor Roy Cooper continue to be a regular presence on the ground in the region.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. ROY COOPER (D), NORTH CAROLINA: This unprecedented catastrophic storm requires an unprecedented response.\par \par \uc2 DEANNE CRISWELL\uc2 , FEMA ADMINISTRATOR: We have everything you need, Governor. Everybody here in North Carolina, and we are going to be able to continue to provide that assistance as long as you need it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: And as these communities, Shannon, try and clean this up, you can see how bad this is, they're reeling from one hurricane and now, we know that another tropical storm this one named Milton is churning in the Gulf of Mexico. The track showing that it may strengthen into a category hurricane and it's taken aim at Florida's panhandle once again -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, Griff. Those images are absolutely stunning. Thank you for being there on the ground for us in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 And joining us now, Alexis McAdams live in Butler, Pennsylvania, fresh off President Trump's return to the scene of that assassination attempt.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Alexis.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 A very emotional weekend here in the battleground state of Pennsylvania as the former president took to that stage behind me in Butler, in the same spot where he was nearly assassinated just 12 weeks ago, now with less than a month, if you can believe it until the presidential election. Both of these candidates are crisscrossing all of those states.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES & 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: You put me right to work --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS (voice-over): Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  back on the ground in North Carolina, her second visit to this state since it was ravaged by Hurricane Helene.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I think that these moments of crisis bring out some of the best of who we can be and who we are.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: With the cleanup far from over, Vice President Harris has pledged ongoing federal support. But former President Trump has said the current administration has done a lousy job.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : They are doing the worst job on a hurricane than any administration has ever done.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Both candidates focusing in on battleground states like Georgia.\par \par \uc2 This week, Trump and the state's governor, Brian Kemp, putting their differences aside, making their first public appearance together since their 2020 rift.\par \par \uc2 And in a triumphant return to Pennsylvania, the former president held another rally in Butler, standing in that same spot where he was nearly assassinated back in July.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: On this very ground, a coldblooded assassin aimed to silence me.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Honoring the life of Corey Comperatore, who was shot and killed at that rally.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Corey is not with us tonight and he should be.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Also joined on stage by \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 . The \uc2 Tesla\uc2  CEO says he was astounded by Trump's bravery.\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK, TESLA CEO: We had one president who couldn't climb a flight of stairs and another who was fist pumping after getting shot.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: On Friday, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  making a stop in the White House briefing room, pointing out a positive jobs report and addressing the averted dockworker strike, though recent polls show Harris is still struggling with the union vote.\par \par \uc2 And Democrats also bringing out their star power, former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , kicking off a swing state blitz.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS (on camera): And as tensions in the Middle East, Shannon, continue to rise foreign policy is sitting in the national spotlight this election. We know Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has been back in battleground Michigan, talking with leaders there in the community, Arab leaders specifically about her plan she says to end the war in Gaza, as former President Trump who's been a strong supporter of Israel is going to honor the lives of those who were killed on October 7th, tomorrow on that anniversary -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Alexis McAdams reporting from Pennsylvania -- Alexis, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Democratic vice presidential nominee, Minnesota Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Governor, great to have you with us on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN), U.S. VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Great to be with you, Shannon. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. So I want to pick up where Alexis left off there in the Middle East because you were asked in the debate the other night, a question about whether or not you would support a preemptive strike by Israel on Iran. You didn't really get to an answer on that.\par \par \uc2 So this administration continues to call for Israel to moderate its response after being attacked by Iran. Do you now think that Israel has a right to either strike oil facilities, nuclear facilities? What's your position on that?\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Well, look, let's remember how this started, and we're approaching tomorrow a tragic anniversary, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists murdered over 1,200 innocent Israelis, 46 Americans, took hostages.\par \par \uc2 We have been clear -- Israel has the right to defend itself. We've always stood in that position. We need the hostages returned and we need to bring an end to the humanitarian crisis in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 But again, the point is it, it's Iran, is at the heart of this, and their proxies that bring disruption throughout the region. That's why being in the Situation Room as vice president was working with Israelis to repel the attack we saw this week. They tried it back in April, the same thing, and that's the point of being and boxing Iran in.\par \par \uc2 President Trump tore up the nuclear deal that moved Iran closer to a nuclear weapon. He also allowed Iran to shoot down an American aircraft in international airspace with a tweet. And when Iranian weapons -- missiles fell on U.S. troops, he called it a headache when it was traumatic brain injury.\par \par \uc2 Just to be clear, we have always said, we reserve the right to protect American troops, our allies in the region and that there is consequences for Iran.\par \par \uc2 So what you're seeing right now is the ability to work with our allies, the ability to make sure Iran does not further and every administration has dealt with them for 45 years. Now, it's to make sure and there has never been a question, Israel has the right to defend itself and we stand with them to do that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, does that include --\par \par \uc2 WALZ: But we need to bring this to a conclusion.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Does that include a strike on Iran's nuclear or oil facilities?\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Specific ops -- specific operations will be dealt with at the time, but being very clear on this, Iran -- there -- where's consequences for what they do. That's what's happening right now. Israel's right to defend itself is not in question.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. You said in a radio interview that anti-Israel protesters were speaking out, quote, for all the right reasons. You know that some of them are carrying \uc2 Hamas\uc2  flags, some of them are calling for the extermination of the Jewish state.\par \par \uc2 Do you want to clarify your praise for them?\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Yeah, I'm certainly not talking about them. I'm talking about the people who understand that we have to end the humanitarian crisis but they understand we need to return the hostage bring peace to the region.\par \par \uc2 Look, I will always defend people's rights to First Amendment, but that's not the folks I'm talking about. Those folks are not speaking for the entire group. So I think it's very clear on this. Israel's right as we've stood with them. Antisemitism has no place in this country and Vice President Harris has been clear about that, I've been clear about it.\par \par \uc2 But the ability to bring a peaceful solution and understand Israel's right to defend itself, the return of the hostages and Gazans' right to live in - - in peace, too, is something that that we're trying to get to.\par \par \uc2 And that's going to take the leadership that we've seen out of Vice President Harris, bringing able to hold this coalition together.\par \par \uc2 And again, when Iran launched their missiles this week, she's in the Situation Room. That's different.\par \par \uc2 The people who are in the Situation Room with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  like \uc2 John Kelly\uc2 , his chief of staff, said he shouldn't be there. He's a damaged human being.\par \par \uc2 His -- his own defense secretary said he does not understand the situation --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: -- and is incapable of dealing with it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: So it's about character.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let's -- yeah, let's focus on what your position is and the vice president's position as well.\par \par \uc2 I want to talk about abortion, too, because this came up at the debate. It's been a winning argument for Democrats on many ballots, but I want to clarify what the law is there in Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 Abortion Finder, a website that helps women find access, says abortion is legal throughout pregnancy in Minnesota. There is no ban or limit on abortion in Minnesota based on how far along in a pregnancy you are.\par \par \uc2 You signed the bill that makes it legal through all nine months.\par \par \uc2 Is that a position you think Democrats should advocate for nationally?\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Look, the vice president and I have been clear the restoration of Roe versus Wade is what we're asking for. This is a woman's right --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But that law goes far beyond Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: -- to make her own choice.\par \par \uc2 The law does -- the law is very clear. It does not change that. That has been debunked on every occasion. But, look, this is a -- you talk about this being a win --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But, wait, wait, let's agree, what you signed is there's not a single limit through nine months of pregnancy. Roe had a trimester framework that did have limits through the pregnancy. The Minnesota law does not have that.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: This puts -- this puts the decision with the woman and her healthcare providers. The situation we have is when you don't have the ability of healthcare providers to provide that, that's where you end up with a situation like Amanda Zurawski in Texas, where they are afraid to do what's necessary.\par \par \uc2 This doesn't change any anything. It puts the decision back on to the woman, to the physicians.\par \par \uc2 And we know that this is simply something to be brought up -- to be very clear \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's asking for a nationwide abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Wait, he has said that he --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: And, again, we don't see this as a winning campaign --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- he has said repeatedly that he will not sign a national abortion ban. Are you calling that just it's a flat out lie?\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Yes, it -- of course, and Senator Vance has in the past said so too.\par \par \uc2 Now, look they may see this as an election issue. We see it as a right of women to make their own bodily decisions, and that's what the states like my state have the ability to put that in. States like Georgia force women to cross the border and then we have a death of Amber Thurman.\par \par \uc2 So let's be very clear trying to cut hairs on an issue on this is not where the American public is at. They want the restoration of Roe versus Wade. Vice President Harris said she would sign it. That's what -- we'll do when we're elected. We restore that right to women.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. To be clear, the Minnesota law is far beyond Roe v. Wade, and about the Amber Thurman case in Georgia, her family has -- and it's tragic. She is a young mother, who left behind a young son, but what her family has said is it was a complication from an abortion pill that she received and she didn't get proper care when she went to a Georgia hospital which had multiple opportunities to intervene there.\par \par \uc2 Her own attorney, the family's attorney says it wasn't the Georgia law, it was the hospitals -- what he claims is malpractice, not treating her when she clearly showed up in distress and still had the byproducts of her pregnancy because of that rare complication from the abortion pill. So just to be clear on the Georgia law and how her family and her attorney sees it.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: I think they also indicated that had she not go to North Carolina after the debate the other night that that she would have been in a better position.\par \par \uc2 So look these are the situations you're going to get into when you take this decision and put it at the hands of politicians like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , rather than women and their doctors. We trust women, we trust doctors and we know the outcomes of that are better.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. Let's clarify more changes that you signed in Minnesota as well. Several things were repealed with respect to reporting infants who may have survived an abortion attempt. Language that used to be part of the statute was taken out. We'll put this on the screen so people can see.\par \par \uc2 It used to require medical personnel to, quote, preserve the life and health of the born alive infant. That language has been repealed. It's no longer part of the law.\par \par \uc2 Also struck from the law was language about reporting infants that are born alive, what treatment they receive, and whether they live or die.\par \par \uc2 Why was it important to you to get that protection out of the law?\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Minnesota law aligns with every other case of what physicians are required by their ethical responsibilities, and so it changed nothing other than aligning with all care that physicians provide in any circumstance for any medical case. And so its alignment, as it's always been, it's the cases we've seen in Minnesota, the outcomes we get in our health care results are the best in the nation. And all it did was align with existing law.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But you do acknowledge it takes out the language about preserving the life of an infant who was born alive.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Every doctor has an ethical responsibility to provide what they can in each situation. That is the way the law is written now. It's the same for any person who shows up.\par \par \uc2 And again, this is a distraction from the real issue here is, is women being forced into miscarriages, women being forced to go back home, get sepsis, potentially die like we saw in cases in Texas. And maternal mortality rates in Texas have skyrocketed off the charts because of this. This is bad policy.\par \par \uc2 Vice President and I have been very clear on this. We will restore the rights of Roe vs. Wade. We will make sure women have their health care decisions. We will not put people's lives at risk because of the geography of where they live. And it will go back to the way it was before \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  gleefully said, we've got the best of both worlds here. Things are wonderful. The states are handling it. It's a beautiful thing. It's not a beautiful thing to see women dying. It's not a beautiful thing to put doctors at risk of being imprisoned.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It is not. It is not to see any single woman suffer. And by the way, there's not a single state, no matter how pro-life or restrictive their laws are, that do not allow a doctor to intercede. That's true in Georgia and Texas as well.\par \par \uc2 I want to turn to the economy. Our latest polling shows that 66% of Americans we talked to said that buying groceries is a, quote, "major problem" for them.\par \par \uc2 And when we ask people about how they feel about the policies of this administration versus the previous, double the amount of people surveyed say that Biden-Harris policies have hurt them rather than help them and their families economically. That's in stark contrast to how they feel when asked about the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 So what would a Harris-Walz administration do different than a Biden-Harris administration when it comes to convincing people that the economy is going to get better for them because their perception is that it's not?\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Well, we saw a blockbuster jobs report this week. We saw interest rates come down. And we've also seen that Vice President Harris laying out a middle-class agenda.\par \par \uc2 Look, I was in Ohio yesterday in Cleveland, in Cincinnati, and talking about this. Folks in Ohio know that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's policies led to 180,000 manufacturing jobs leaving. They understand when he left office we had more people unemployed percentage-wise than the Great Depression. What Vice President Harris is talking about is making it more affordable to buy housing. And we've seen it in Minneapolis. When you have more housing stock, we've seen rates go down. We've seen more houses available.\par \par \uc2 Also the idea of price gouging. With 37 states, Republican and Democratic states, have this. Corn is about $4.10 or so a bushel. That's where it's been for many years, up and down. But prices at the grocery stores have skyrocketed. Corporate profits have skyrocketed. And people are paying more. So we talk about that.\par \par \uc2 And then I think the fundamental difference here is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  kept his promise. He cut taxes for the wealthiest. She's talking about making sure things like a child tax credit, $6,000 child tax credit, put more money in people's pocket, making sure that we're able to buy those things, whether it's a crib or it's baby formula, get started. And she's simply asking that we can pay for it by asking those at the top to pay their fair share.\par \par \uc2 This is a fundamental difference in approaches to it. And we're seeing it working. We're seeing economists talk about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's proposal to add a 20% sales tax will cost $4,000. That's everybody. That's not me saying it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And just to note, that is a tariff, which by the way, this administration not only kept most of the Trump tariffs, but they added their own as well. So that's got to be part of the conversation.\par \par \uc2 I want to talk a little bit about your record as governor because it elucidates for people how you may serve as whether you end up being the president. You'll be one heartbeat away should your ticket be successful.\par \par \uc2 "Wall Street Journal" says this after you were chosen to join the ticket. As the Democratic governor of Minnesota, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  signed into law initiatives allowing immigrants in the country illegally to apply for driver's licenses, qualify for free tuition at state universities, and enroll in the state's free health care program for low-income residents."\par \par \uc2 Are those not magnets to draw people here many times on dangerous journeys for themselves? And why should your taxpayers in Minnesota or across the states pay for those programs?\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Well, that's not the vice president's position. But I will say this, Minnesota ranks as a top five business state. We rank as a top three state for children living and with a top state for health care access.\par \par \uc2 I would also add this. We also have some of the safest roads. Look, the Vice President has made it clear that she has policies that make a difference. Her border policies are the most strongest, the fairest we've seen. It's the bill that we saw --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Now, Governor, you know a lot of people, including your own party, would not join that statement. There are millions of people who have come here over the last few years that, you know, they -- they see this as an open border.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: The policies -- well, simply we have a policy. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sees it as a political. Look, \uc2 James Lankford\uc2  in Oklahoma, the Border Patrol agents, the "Wall Street Journal," the Chamber of Commerce all said pass this legislation.\par \par \uc2 You have to have Congress to authorize 1,500 new border agents. You have to have Congress to authorize DOJ to speed adjudications on these asylum claims. Those are things that would actually work. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  told us for four years he would deal with this. He didn't -- he didn't build his wall 2%. Mexico didn't pay for it.\par \par \uc2 This is a real bill that has bipartisan support. It has the experts on board, and it starts to tackle these issues. And we don't have to resort to demonizing people. We don't have to resort to making up or crafting stories, as Senator Vance said he did. Those things were not happening in Springfield, but it doesn't mean that we can't pass a piece of legislation to strengthen our border. That's what \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was talking about. She's talking about solving the problem.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Governor, that piece of legislation does include the wall that you guys have been so -- you've disparaged that. I mean, the Vice President has as well, so I don't know if she really intends to move forward with that. But it was negotiated by three or four senators, and many Republicans came out against it long before President Trump indicated he didn't like it either.\par \par \uc2 Listen, before we go, because I wish that we had a full hour, I want to give you a chance, because you called yourself a knucklehead this week, to talk about some of your misstatements.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You've modified your story or explained that you misspoke about things involving your military rank, about carrying a weapon in war, your 1995 DUI arrest, using IVF to have your beautiful children Gus and Hope, being in Hong Kong and China in the summer of 1989 during the Tiananmen events, a lot of people would say they couldn't get away with saying, I'm just too passionate, my grammar is not right, I'm a knucklehead. What do you say to the American people who think, I don't know that I can trust this guy with all those modifications to be the potential commander-in- chief of this country?\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Yeah, well I think they heard me. They heard me the other night speaking passionately about gun violence and misspeaking, and -- and I got to be honest with you, Shannon, I don't think people care whether I used IUI or IVF when we talk about this. What they understand is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would resist those things.\par \par \uc2 Look, I speak passionately. I had an entire career, decades before I was in public office. They know, and I'm very proud of my 24 years in service and my record. I have never disparaged someone else in this, but I know that's not what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does. They disparage everyone. The personal attacks.\par \par \uc2 I will own up when I misspeak. I will own up when I make a mistake. Let's be very clear. On that debate stage the other night, I asked one very simple question and Senator Vance would not acknowledge that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost the 2020 election. I think they're probably far more concerned with that than my wife and I used IUI to have our child and that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would restrict that.\par \par \uc2 So I think folks know who I am. My constituents here in Minnesota have elected me eight times. They know where I'm at and -- and I'm proud to be on the ticket and we'll deliver just like we have here in Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, and if we're going to deal 