orward and said, enough with Biden and Harris. We're going to protect ourselves because we can't count on them to help us do that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Senator, do you agree with the ambassador that Israel should move forward and disregard the advice of the Biden-Harris administration or no?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I really don't have any regard for what she's saying. And I'm not going to politicize, you know, what's about in Israel right now. And for me, it's about following Israel. And I've been very clear that my voice in that vote is going to follow Israel in that.\par \par \uc2 And whatever they decide to do and respond to Iran, I'm going to support that. Because Israel will have a better idea of the intelligence and the circumstances on the ground. And that's why I'm going to support and follow that.\par \par \uc2 And I also want to celebrate what Israel has been able to do. They've demolished \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , and now they have humiliated \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , and they are now cowering.\par \par \uc2 And Iran shot, you know, 200 missiles, and they vaporized those. So Iran now is left exposed and humiliated. And Israel has put them back on the ropes. And I am going to support what they continue to do.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. And we wait to see what their response will be. And we know that there are often very long time frames in the Middle East about responses and answering.\par \par \uc2 We know there's tension between President Biden and Prime Minister Netanyahu. There's been no secret of that. When asked whether Iran should - - Israel should strike Iran's nuclear facilities this week, President Biden said, quote, the answer is no.\par \par \uc2 Well, an opinion piece in "Wall Street Journal" puts it this way. Even before he talks with Mr. Netanyahu, the president engages in public lobbying aimed at blocking a sovereign state and American ally from deciding on its own what the best response to a direct military attack on its t