 prevail, just like \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did in 2020.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, well, we all appreciate the opportunity to spend time in Pennsylvania. So we'll be doing that a lot more for the next 30 days.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: All right, have me back.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We will.\par \par \uc2 OK, former President Trump, Vice President Harris navigating major issues and headlines as they make that final push for your vote. The Sunday panel's up next to discuss how they're responding to the conflict in the Middle East and the aftermath of a devastating hurricane here at home.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN), VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: The specific operations will be dealt with at the time. But being very clear on this, Iran - there was consequences for what they do. That's what's happening right now. Israel's right to defend itself is not in question.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That was Vice Presidential Candidate \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , speaking with us earlier in the show about the consequences for Iran after its missile attacks on Israel.\par \par \uc2 Time now for our Sunday group.\par \par \uc2 Susan Page, "USA Today" Washington bureau chief. Former Bush White House adviser Karl Rove. Juan Williams, Fox News senior political analyst. And Michael Allen, former Bush national security official.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to all of you on this Sunday.\par \par \uc2 So, "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " says this about these campaigns now having to deal with what's going on in the Middle East. They say, all Ms. Harris can do is pray that the widening war in the Middle East does not overwhelm her candidacy and confirm in the minds of the last few undecided voters." We're all still looking for them, by the way. "The idea that her opponent \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is promoting that the world is out of control thanks to the weak leadership of the Biden-Harris administration."\par \par \uc2 Michael.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ALLEN, FORMER BUSH NATIONAL SECURITY OFFICIAL: Well, three things are going to happen this week that will make sure the Middle East remains at the top of the political agenda. Israel is going to continue going into Lebanon. And we're going to have a big strike against Iran. I think they're not only -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You think this week?\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: I do. They're going to hit conventional weapons sites. They're going to hit energy infrastructure. And there's increasing political pressure on Netanyahu from his political opponents to hit nuclear infrastructure. And then Israel will have to assume a position of, here comes Iran back at them, or here comes Iran at the UAE or Saudi Arabia.\par \par \uc2 So, we're going to go through another escalation cycle before things will get back to normal.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and this has been a really difficult issue for Democrats. They've got different wings of their parties that have different feelings about some of these issues.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " also writes this, that "Harris' bind is getting worse. The anger that Palestinian Americans were feeling towards the administration - especially in the key swig state of Michigan - has now been joined by anger from Lebanese Americans also concentrated in Michigan, who are decrying the indiscriminate bombing of their homeland."\par \par \uc2 Juan.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, you know, this has become politicized in the way. You know by the way, congratulations on getting the vice presidential nominee. That was excellent. It was a very good interview.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: But I think he hailed (ph) it right when he said, the United States, the Biden administration, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and the vice presidential nominee, Mr. Walz, have all said that they support Israel. Not only does the United States support Israel, it's funding Israel, it's arming Israel and has forces on the ground. I think 40,000 American soldiers and - and Navy personnel on the ground in the region in case of the escalation that Michael is talking about.\par \par \uc2 But somehow now it's become sort of like, oh, Republicans support Israel and Democrats don't. This is a falsehood.\par \par \uc2 But again, it's in a political moment where people are looking for any edge. The polls are frozen. And as a result, people are trying to politicize hurricanes. They want to politicize what happens with migrants in Ohio, with crime, with inflation. People saying the good jobs report is a - is a fake. This is outrageous. But it just goes by these things (ph).\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and, Karl, to the point about Israel, the Democrats have been - and this - and the White House has been full-throated in their support of Israel. Obviously, there are critics who will say, well, then stop advising them publicly, or critiquing them publicly. But isn't that what allies do? They have those conversation.\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, yes, but you don't notice that Governor Walz did not answer your first question, which is, are - is the administration supportive of attacks on nuclear facilities and oil facilities? And, look, they're supportive of Israel, as long as Israel doesn't do summing they don't want them to do. So, they do have a problem on this, and it's a political problem.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned Michigan. Twenty thousand Arab American voters last time around, mostly around Dearborn, they voted two to one for - for Biden last time around. The latest poll shows 50/50. What does that mean? That means 200,000, 70 percent turnout, which is what they had last time around. That means that rather than winning 98-42, it's a 50-50 out of that 140,000 that are going to turn out, which means you wipe out one-third of the winning margin that Biden had last time around. Tossing in some college students upset about Israel, toss in some union households concerned about the EV mandates, toss in some young black men who don't - who - who think the economy was better under Trump then it is under Biden, and you've got a real problem in a blue state.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and Susan, these are problems that are real world problems for the administration now. Bringing in the hurricane response and those kinds of things. But they do turn into campaign issues too.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE, WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF, "\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 ": Yes, they do. But if you want to look for the issue that - of all these issues we've talked about and things that could happen in October, the hurricane - and perhaps the next hurricane are the ones with the biggest history of affecting presidential elections. It affected in 1992, the first President George Bush, helped your president in 2004 in his re-election campaign when his response to a hurricane was seen as - as being pretty robust.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, we talk about the Middle East, crucially important. We talk about mandates for hurricanes. Hurricanes, and whether a president and his administration is seen as responding effectively to them, that's one that really cuts politically.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and I want to touch on one other issue that's getting buried under these very important stories too. But Jack Smith's filing this week in the January 6th case against President Trump. And Judge Chutkan's decision to unseal a lot of that. It's 160 some pages of allegations against President Trump. Writing in "The Intelligencer," Elie Honig, who is not a conservative, he's a former federal prosecutor, says this, "Smith has essentially abandoned any pretense, he'll bend any rule, switch up on any practice, so long as he gets to chip away at Trump's electoral prospects. At this point there's simply no defending Smith's conduct on any sort of principled or institutional basis."\par \par \uc2 Susan, it's - it's a story.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: Well, and, of course, we've had - we've seen this before. We saw this in 2016, a lot of concern on the part of the Hillary Clinton campaign about the \uc2 FBI\uc2  director, \uc2 James Comey\uc2 , coming out, talking about a legal case.\par \par \uc2 You know, his - Jack Smith's defense is it's the judge who set the deadline. It's the judge who released the brief. But it does - but it was - I thought that was an interesting article by an unexpected source.\par \par \uc2 I do go back to, is there anyone in America who will be - whose vote would be affected by Trump's behavior after the 2020 election by the charges in this case? Will that affect their vote? Isn't every person who - who would be affected by that vote already to have chosen sides?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I hear you itching over here. We've got 10 seconds left, Juan.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I just - I - you know, it's one of these moments in life when you think, the guy tried to overthrow the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  and 47 percent of the American people think it's OK to put him back in the Oval Office? It's just like, what? How can that be?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I'm guessing you don't see it that way, Karl. Five seconds.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: No, look, I didn't like it when Comey intruded in the 2016 election, and I don't like it this time around that Smith did. This would better be dealt with after the election.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, Michael, I'm guessing you don't like it either.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Too close to the election.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, thank you, panel.\par \par \uc2 All right, the Supreme Court is kicking off its new term tomorrow. I've got a preview of my conversation with two justices, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Members of the Supreme Court arriving at the 72nd annual Red Masses Service at the Cathedral of St. Matthew here in Washington. Something they do at the start of every term. I talked one-on-one with former Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  and Current Justice \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 . My in-depth conversations with them are available on my "Livin' The Bream" podcast. That drops today. Check them out. You'll hear stories on and off the bench.\par \par \uc2 That's it for today. Thanks for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020241006eka600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 Union Seeks Higher Wages And Automation Restrictions; Strike Threatening Critical Supply Chains; Major Hurricane In Atlantic Amid Helene Rescue Efforts; Biden Tours Areas In Florida And Georgia; Trump Calls Biden Disaster Response A Disgrace; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  On Kamala Harris White House Chances Amid Trio Of Crises; U.S. Support For Embattled Ally; Israel And Iran Signal Potential For Escalation; Cheney Campaigning With Harris In Wisconsin; Israel Vows Major Response To Iran Missiles Attack, Trump Faces Criticism For Comments On 2020 Iran Attack; AP: Dockworkers Agree To Delay Walkabout Until January 15; Trump And Harris Campaigns Rally Support On The Trail; Melania Signals Support For Abortion in New York; Three Former Officers Convicted And Two Acquitted; Democrat Effort To Flip New York House Seat; Alleged Photos Of Lawyer In Halloween Blackface\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Brooke Taylor, Steve Harrigan, Bill Melugin, Grady  Trimble, Jacqui Heinrich, Mike Tobin \par \uc2 7,407 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I'm sorry, but we're out of time.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: That's it.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: We have to talk to Bret now.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Greg, you never want to ring affair -- fire.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: No, you don't.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right. Good evening, I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Israel is expanding its ground operation into Lebanon, warning some communities to evacuate, and for the second night in a row, massive explosions just within the past few minutes inside Beirut.\par \par \uc2 Here at home, as some residents return home in North Carolina, another huge storm threatens to strike the East Coast. We'll bring you the radar and where it's headed.\par \par \uc2 And a report from the \uc2 Homeland Security Department\uc2  watchdog claims current vetting practices for migrants could be creating risks for U.S. citizens.\par \par \uc2 But "BREAKING TONIGHT", officials are warning the strike at ports along the east and Gulf coasts of the U.S. could impact hurricane relief. President Biden saying today, progress is being made in negotiations, but some governors, they are not having it. With states with major ports, they are saying it's crucial for the union to reach an agreement and soon.\par \par \uc2 The comments come as the number of vessels stuck near 50 tonight, with the backlog estimated to double by the end of the week. Correspondent Brooke Taylor is at the \uc2 Port of Houston\uc2  again tonight with the latest. Good evening, Brooke.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE TAYLOR, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 You have the governors in New York and New Jersey who are siding with the dock workers.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in Florida, you have DeSantis telling the White House to step in and do it fast. But there is one thing that all of these governors can agree on, Bret, that's that the strike needs to end soon, before families at home feel the impact.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR (voice over): As tens of thousands of dock workers are on strike, shutting down major ports on the east and Gulf coasts, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has a plan to reopen his state's ports, saying it is unacceptable to leave Hurricane Helene victims without essential supplies.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: The Florida National Guard and Florida State Guard will be deployed to critical ports affected to maintain order and if possible, resume operations.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR (voice over): Meanwhile, on the west coast, the president of the local Longshoreman Union in San Francisco is standing strong with his fellow union workers.\par \par \uc2 DAVID GONZALES, \uc2 INTERNATIONAL LONGSHORE AND WAREHOUSE UNION\uc2 : We are not going to move any of your cargo.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes!\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR (voice over): The national union refuses to comment if all West Coast ports are on board. The striking workers along the east coast and Gulf of Mexico have two major demands: higher wages and a total ban on automation.\par \par \uc2 DENNIS DAGGETT, EXECUTIVE VICE PRESIDENT, INTERNATIONAL LONGSHOREMEN'S ASSOCIATION: Stay strong, guys. Stay strong. We got it.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR (voice over): ILA executive vice president Dennis Daggett, talking to picketers in New Jersey today.\par \par \uc2 DAGGETT: We fully understand your value and your worth and what you did for this country during covid.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: But consumers and business owners worry their demands are going to run the economy to the ground, with experts saying it's costing up to $4.5 billion a day.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: They want more pay, but it trickles down and it affects everybody.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I feel bad for the customers. The prices are tremendously high.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR (voice over): With both sides at a standstill and the world watching. President Biden, who has said he is not intervening, had this to say, today.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think we are making progress on that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How much progress, sir?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We'll find out soon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR (on camera): And if you at home wondering what products are going to be affected by this, I am here to tell you stop panic buying that toilet paper. Experts say the majority of toilet paper is made here in the United States, but come next week, you might start to see shortages of things like fresh fruit, bananas, auto parts, and alcohol. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Brooke, we'll follow it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Another hurricane could be headed for the East Coast. Hurricane Kirk is now a category forms -- 4 storm, could cause life threatening surf and rip current conditions this weekend, as you see the radar there.\par \par \uc2 It comes amid the long road to recovery for Hurricane Helene. Victims, residents in North Carolina have started to return to assess the damage, but many roads are still impassable.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Steve Harrigan reports just outside Asheville tonight with the latest. Good evening, Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Brett. The death toll now stands at 215.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Our town is gone, virtually. That's our town is just gone.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: As the roads slowly open up, the true scope of Helene's destruction is revealed in western North Carolina, mountain towns like Swannanoa.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is like what you see on television from Cat 5 hurricanes.\par \par \uc2 The more we looked, the more we realized, when are we coming back from this.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: Before and after images of the devastation are evoking comparisons to the great flood of 1916.\par \par \uc2 The Swannanoa River here rising 26 feet, more than five feet above its previous high set more than 100 years ago. It will be hard to recover from 10-foot-high flood waters that covered what's left of the town in mud. Fresh water and power are in limited supply, and cell service almost nonexistent.\par \par \uc2 For some areas, so too is any sign of help.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY WELLS, RESIDENT OF CANTON: We haven't seen FEMA, we haven't seen Red Cross, we haven't seen Hearts with Hands, we've seen no agencies come through here.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Some residents taking desperate measures to rescue others, including one who stole a kayak.\par \par \uc2 I yelled at him, and he said, sorry, man, people are drowning. So, I grabbed my kayak, and my life jacket, followed him. Some people were in their attics, and we could see their fingers sticking out through the attic vents. They were screaming.\par \par \uc2 So, we started going around, rescuing people.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): President Biden was back on the ground in hard hit communities in Florida and Georgia today, again, promising to foot the recovery bill for several months, following the deadliest hurricane to hit the U.S. since Katrina.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I promise you we have your back. We are going to stay until you're restored.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (on camera): They are waiting. A woman here whose house was completely destroyed, signed up for a FEMA emergency payment of $750. It will be direct deposit after 10 days. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Steve Harrigan, on the ground in North Carolina. Steve, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , says he will visit North Carolina next week to assess the hurricane damage. The former president held a rally in Saginaw, Michigan earlier today, and also spoke with correspondent Bill Melugin about those recovery efforts and some of the other top stories this evening.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you. We spoke with former President Trump backstage before his rally here in Saginaw today.\par \par \uc2 There is a whole lot going on in the world right now. We covered a variety of topics with him, including the federal response to Hurricane Helene. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: We're up to more than 200 people dead from Hurricane Helene. I'm sure you saw yesterday. DHS says FEMA doesn't have enough money to last hurricane season. What's your reaction to hearing that?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Well, they are doing a very bad job. They have given over a billion dollars to illegal migrants that came in, and now, they have no money for North Carolina, for Georgia, for South Carolina, and Alabama, Tennessee, and Florida. I think that's a disgrace.\par \par \uc2 This is a country that's being run by fools.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Biden says that Israel should not target Iranian nuke facilities. What are your thoughts on that? What should Israel respond?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I mean, that's the craziest thing I've ever heard. That's the biggest risk we have.\par \par \uc2 The biggest risk we have is nuclear. I mean, to make the statement, please leave their nuclear alone. I would tell you that that's not the right answer. That was the craziest answer, because, you know what, soon, they are going to have nuclear weapons, and then, you are going to have problems.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: What's your reaction to Melania's memoir about championing abortion rights and reproductive freedom? When you read it, what were your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We spoke about it, and I said, you have to write what you believe. I'm not going to tell you what to do. You have to write what you believe. She is very beloved. People love our former first lady. I can tell you that.\par \par \uc2 But I said, you have to stick with your heart. I've said that to everybody, you have to go with your heart. There are some people that are very, very far right on the issue, meaning without exceptions. And there are other people that view it a little bit differently than that.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is out on the campaign trail today with \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 . Your thoughts on that?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  lost for Congress. She was terrible. \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  is a stupid war hawk. All she wants to do is shoot missiles at people. I really think it hurts. I think, frankly, if Kamala -- I think they hurt each other. I think they are so bad, both of them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Former President Trump also told us he believes the dock workers strike would not have happened if he were sitting in the Oval Office right now. He told us he would have gotten all the stakeholders on the phone and done everything possible to try and avoid this strike.\par \par \uc2 Bret, we'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill Melugin, live in Saginaw, Michigan. Bill, thanks.\par \par \uc2 "BREAKING TONIGHT", former President Trump's lawyers have requested a delay to file a response to Special Counsel Jack Smith's immunity filing. We brought you that last night, 165 pages.\par \par \uc2 The judge, Judge Chutkan agreed to allow Trump's team to respond on November 7th, two days after election day.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  hopes to appeal to Republican voters in her Wisconsin event tonight. Her event is being held in a traditionally conservative location, and joining her, as you heard just there, is a member of a traditionally conservative family.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business correspondent Grady Trimble reports.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT (voice over): In Ripon, Wisconsin, known as the birthplace of the Republican Party, turned supporter, former congresswoman, Republican \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , a vocal Harris detractor in a 2020 interview on Fox News.\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): She is the epitome of a San Francisco liberal. She is even farther to the left than Barack Obama was. She seems to be farther to the left than \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  is.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE (voice over): After the January 6th Capitol riot, Cheney made it her mission to block former President Trump from getting back into the White House. On paper, the Republicans views appear to be diametrically opposed to the vice presidents, including on abortion.\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: It cannot be for the people, if you will not protect our babies.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: When Congress passes a bill to restore reproductive freedom, I will proudly sign it into law.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE (voice over): On immigration, Cheney in 2021, attacked Harris for not securing the southern border.\par \par \uc2 Here she is discussing national security, a hundred days after President Biden and Vice President Harris took office.\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: The president and the administration have demonstrated an inability to really understand and recognize how important it is to demonstrate strength.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE (voice over): Cheney sided with Trump on nearly 93 percent of her votes in Congress, but now she is backing Harris.\par \par \uc2 The Trump campaign, blasting Cheney as, "A stone cold loser who's so desperate for relevance and attention, she has debased herself by campaigning with a weak, failed and dangerously liberal \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: The latest Marquette poll shows Harris with a four-point lead over Trump here in Wisconsin. That's within the margin of error, so, this is expected to be an extremely tight race in a state that's seen as a must win for the Democrats if they want to secure the blue wall and the White House. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Grady Trimble, live in Ripon, Wisconsin. Grady, thanks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 Moments ago, Israel targeting a suburb of southern Beirut in airstrikes, massive explosions, as you see here, could be seen as planes landed at the nearby airport.\par \par \uc2 Western nations are preparing contingency plans for large scale evacuations of their citizens from Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. State Department\uc2  organizing additional flights for Americans to flee that country. It comes as Israel's military issues evacuation orders in southern Lebanon, signaling an expansion of its ground operation there.\par \par \uc2 Senior correspondent Mike Tobin reports on the situation from Haifa.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT (voice over): More airstrikes pushing closer to the heart of Lebanon's capital.\par \par \uc2 Israel says 15 targets were weapons production, weapons storage, and intelligence headquarters for \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (through translator): We heard the explosion at night. We came now and saw everything flattened to the ground. There is nothing left.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): The \uc2 Lebanese health ministry\uc2  says an additional 9,000 people were killed, bringing the total killed by airstrikes to nearly 2,000 over the past year, the \uc2 U.S. State Department\uc2  helped 250 Americans living in Lebanon escape the bombing.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW MILLER\uc2 , SPOKESMAN, \uc2 UNITED STATES STATE DEPARTMENT\uc2 : We are organizing additional flights to allow more us, citizens and their eligible family members, to depart Lebanon now.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: The \uc2 IDF\uc2  continues to operate on the ground in South Lebanon, with infantry and reservists joining the fight.\par \par \uc2 Civilians in 70 villages were told to evacuate north as Israel destroys weapons and tunnels.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. HERZI HALEVI, CHIEF OF THE GENERAL STAFF, \uc2 ISRAEL DEFENSE FORCES\uc2  (through translator): We will not allow \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  to establish itself in these places in the future.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  fired back for the first time on Wednesday, nine Israeli soldiers have been killed. Israel claims to have killed 15 \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  fighters.\par \par \uc2 A segment of a missile fired at Israel and intercepted now lays in the desert west of the Dead Sea and has become an attraction.\par \par \uc2 With fears that Israel's response to the missile attack could spark a direct war with Iran, President Biden is talking sanctions.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What kind of sanctions are you considering?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'll tell them before I tell you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Would you support Israel striking Iran's oil facilities, sir?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We are discussing that. I think -- I think that would be a little -- anyway.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (on camera): And live pictures now in the aftermath of those airstrikes. Why reports say the target was Hashem Safieddine. And anticipated to be the successor to \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2 . The \uc2 Lebanese Health Ministry\uc2  says 37 people were killed in airstrikes today. Israel also struck in the West Bank.\par \par \uc2 The target is said to be the leader of a \uc2 Hamas\uc2  cell in the town of Tulkarm in the West Bank. 14 people are reportedly killed. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mike Tobin in Haifa. Mike, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  juggles her official duties with her presidential campaign commitments -- we take you to the trail.\par \par \uc2 And later, are issues forum looks at the conflict in the Middle East. We hear from Democratic Delaware Senator Chris Coons and Republican Arkansas Senator Tom Cotton.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Stocks finished lower ahead of Friday's jobs report, analysts say the port strike and hurricane Helene recovery could distort the labor market picture in the year in the near term. The Dow losing 185 today, the S&P 500 fell 10, the NASDAQ lost seven.\par \par \uc2 Several major stories have already moved markets and could have further economic repercussions. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is working to maintain her campaign strategy while continuing her vice presidential responsibilities.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich reports on that balancing act.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It always gets this close. She's going to do fine.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): President Biden projecting confidence as a deadly storm, a massive strike and a war in the Middle East put his V.P. in the incumbent spotlight, shattering her pitch as the agent of change.\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: If you're one point apart, these kind of things could have a huge consequence on the election.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The trio of crises pulling Harris' attention off the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: She's not