ar \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich live in the North Lawn. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, our issues forum with Democratic Senator Chris Coons and Republican Senator Tom Cotton, focusing on their candidate's plan for the Middle East and what's happening now.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 5 in New York, where the man accused of choking a homeless person on the New York subway appears in court for a preliminary hearing. Former Marine Daniel Penny has pleaded not guilty to manslaughter and homicide charges.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Chestnut Hill, Massachusetts, one of the big stories there tonight from Fox 25, a court will decide whether a beaver raised by rescuers will remain in captivity or release -- be released into the wild. Massachusetts wildlife officials have ordered the release of Navy. The workers at Newhouse wildlife rescue filed a case to stop the beaver's release, arguing she would rather be around people and has no interest in bonding with her own species. So, there you go.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCALISE: They reversed all the sanctions that President Trump out in place that were crippling Iran's economy to go and support terrorism around the world. Iran's made about $100 billion, Lawrence, selling their oil on world markets. They're funding terrorism. \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , October 7th, came out of that.\par \par \uc2 You know, the Houthis that are attacking our troops, and Biden just sits back and does nothing. Kamala does nothing. Let them defend themselves. Israel has a right to self-defense, whatever it is, including nuclear sites.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As the conflict in the Middle East escalates, foreign policy has become a central argument on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 In tonight's Issues Forum, we will hear in a moment from Republican Arkansas Senator Tom Cotton on the Trump campaign's platform in just a bit.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Delaware Senator Chris Coons. He's the co-chair of the Harris presidential campaign.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS COON (D-DE), SENATOR FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE: Great to be with you again, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You heard the House majority leader there talking there, Congressman Scalise, about what he believes and many Republicans say has empowered Iran to be in the position to funnel money to its proxies and caused what we saw not only in April but just a few days ago.\par \par \uc2 COON: Well, let's be clear. President Biden and Vice President Harris have stood strongly by Israel's side following the hateful, heinous attacks of October 7 by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . President Biden has imposed more than 600 sanctions individually and against groups to constrain Iran and its regional group of proxies. And on April 14, pulled together a coalition of the United States, United Kingdom, France, UAE, Jordan, Saudi Arabia to partner with Israel to defend them against a barrage of 300 drones and missiles from Iran.\par \par \uc2 President Biden and Vice President Harris stand strongly with Israel and will continue to do so.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, Senator, President Biden has expressed a reluctance, if you will, to have Israel go forward, continue attacks. It's been a lot of calls for, obviously, a ceasefire, but also to not escalate, in not so many words. It seems like the Israeli Prime Minister has some wind behind his back and is changing the dynamic with the threats he sees to Israel. Do you think the U.S. should be limiting what they're doing, considering the amount of success they've had in recent days?\par \par \uc2 COON: Well, let's be clear. The recent killing of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leader \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2  was met with a strong, forceful statement of support, a recognition that Nasrallah had American blood on his hands for his role in the killing of hundreds of U.S. Marines now decades ago. But he's also been involved in killing thousands of innocent people throughout the region, Lebanese, Syrians, and Israelis. And tonight, as the Jewish people have celebrated Rosh Hashanah. It's important that we reemphasize that both President Biden and Vice President Harris are clear eyed, that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  are terrorist organizations, that this coming weekend, which will be the one year anniversary of the conflict in Gaza and of the horrific and ongoing practice by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  of holding hostage far too many individuals.\par \par \uc2 They are not restraining Israel. They're not preventing Israel from its actions against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and against \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , but they are urging caution about the possibility of a regional war should there be an all-out exchange between Iran and Israel.\par \par \uc2 As you know, just this past weekend, 180 missiles were launched by Iran at Israel. All of them were intercepted in no small part because of our ongoing close defense and security cooperation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Finally, Senator, what do you say to the Republican critics and maybe some inside the military who question whether Vice President Harris would make a good commander-in-chief? They point back to the debate and answer here and what she said just the other day. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOCLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: As of today, there is not one member of the United States military who is in active duty in a combat zone in any war zone around the world the first time this century. Iran is not only a threat to Israel, Iran is also a threat to American personnel in the region, American interests and innocent civilians.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOCLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Obviously talking about American personnel in the region. Changing that answer from the debate.\par \par \uc2 COON: We have 40,000 us troops in the region, and that's in no small part because President Biden and Vice President Harris stepped forward and decisively reallocated U.S. military resources to the Middle East following the October 7 attack. They have provided the support both regionally and on the ground that has been needed to make sure that there are the military resources, the intelligence resources to defend Israel against the ongoing attacks of Iran and its proxies. And you asked the question, am I confident that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  would be a strong commander-in-chief?\par \par \uc2 I am. Her service on the intelligence committee while she was in the Senate and her service as vice president makes her, in my view, the second most qualified leader in terms of foreign policy and national security, second only to President Biden given his 50 years of service in the senate.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: She just got that answer.\par \par \uc2 COON: And as vice president.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Answer wrong at the debate, right?\par \par \uc2 COON: Well, we are not currently in a declared war. The United States is not in a declared war against a major foe like the Vietnam War, the Korean War, the Second World War. So to that extent, we don't have troops in combat in a declared war. But, of course, any day of the week, we have troops deployed all around the world. And one of the things about our being in a dangerous world and having an important role in supporting our partners and allies around the world is that at any time, one of those members of the American armed forces could be targeted.\par \par \uc2 President Trump, when he was in charge, when he was president, destabilized and weakened our critical global alliances by being unpredictable, disrespectful, in some cases, actually picking fights with our closest partners and allies, whether in the Indo Pacific or in \uc2 NATO\uc2  and in Europe.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right.\par \par \uc2 COON: I think President Biden has strengthened \uc2 NATO\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Obviously, Republican --\par \par \uc2 COON: -- strengthened our alliances. And Vice President Harris will continue.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Republicans said that the enemies were kept on their toes because they didn't know what Trump was going to do. And I'm going to ask Senator Cotton about that. Senator Coons, we appreciate your time as always.\par \par \uc2 COON: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOCLIP)\par \par \uc2 LIZ CHENEY\uc2 , FORMER UNITED STATES REPRESENTATIVE: They are the ones who defended our capital, our democracy, and our lives. Many of them fought a bloody, hours-long battle on the west front of the capital. Go watch the video of that battle. It is sickening.\par \par \uc2 They are the ones who held the line and prevented far worse from happening that day. They are the true profiles encouraged. Do not let anyone lie.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOCLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Former Congressman woman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  in Wisconsin endorsing Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , talking there about January 6. For the Republican side, let's bring in Arkansas Republican Senator Tom Cotton.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  often held the same kind of beliefs on foreign policy that you do. The fact that she's endorsing Vice President Harris, what do you think that means? Big picture?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR), TRUMP CAMPAIGN SURROGATE: Well, Brett, just four years ago, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  publicly said that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was unfit to be the commander in chief would be dangerous for America and dangerous for the world. And I don't think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has changed in the last four years. I think \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  has changed.\par \par \uc2 I know \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  hasn't changed. You just mentioned that she served on the Senate Intelligence Committee, Senator Coons. I served with her there for four years. I can tell you she was just as naive and reckless in private on that committee as she is as the vice president and as a presidential candidate. At every turn, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  have made the world more dangerous.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You listened to Senator Coons there defending the Harris record, how they're handling the Middle East. What's your reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Well, what Senator Coons described is not a record I'm familiar with, Bret. From the very beginning of Israel's fight against bloodthirsty Iranian-backed terrorists a year ago, President Biden and Vice President Harris has put more pressure on Israel than they put on \uc2 Hamas\uc2  or \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  or Iran. You know, Senator Coon said that they can. Or they celebrated the killing of \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2 , \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  terrorist mastermind. That was the very same day they both called for a ceasefire against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Once again, they're putting pressure on Israel not to mount a vigorous retaliatory strike against Iran. After Iran has launched almost 200 ballistic missiles at innocent civilian populations in Israel. They imposed a de facto arms embargo on many categories of weapons earlier this year. They've insisted that they're not helping Israel with intelligence sharing against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Even today, the Biden administration took the side of the anti-Semitic United nations over Israel. That's the opposite of what we should be doing, which is what President Trump did four years backing Israel to the hilt and letting Israel win.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here's a question that the former president was asked about Iran and his dealings with Iran in Wisconsin on Tuesday. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOCLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you believe that you should have been tougher on Iran after they had launched ballistic missiles in 2020 on U.S. forces in Iraq, leaving more than 100 us soldiers injured?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: So first of all, injured. What does injured mean? Injured means. You mean because they had a headache because the bombs never hit the fort. So just so you understand, there was nobody ever tougher in Iraq.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOCLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Obviously, many organizations, Reuters had this headline February 10. More than 100 U.S. troops diagnosed with brain injuries from Iran attack. And there's criticism about that answer and about the policy. Your response to that?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Sure. Well, Bret, President Trump is right. There hasn't been anyone since \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  who's been tougher on Iran. President Trump ordered the killing of Iran's terrorist mastermind. By the end of the Trump administration, Iran was down to just a few billion dollars less than foreign currency. President Trump is contrasting the Americans who have died on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's watch, who have been killed by Iranian-backed terrorists throughout the Middle East, or the Americans who were killed in \uc2 Hamas\uc2 's attack on Israel, or who are still held hostage to this day. Nothing like that happened on President Trump's watch, in part because Iran was scared of America when President Trump was in the White House. Right now, they think they can push around and walk all over \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator, last thing, very quickly, just crossing the wires. This is AP. A source, the union for 45,000 us dockworkers agree to suspend the strike until January 15 to provide some time to negotiate a new contract. That's a long time. This brings an end, at least for the temporary time, to these port strikes that could have been crippling. Your reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Well, I don't know the details of the agreement, but it's welcome news. We don't want to have even more inflation and shortages of goods piled on top of what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  brought us to. So I hope they can reach a long-term agreement that is both satisfactory to the workers and to the port owners and operators.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Okay, Senator Cotton, thanks. Thanks for dealing with the breaking news as well. We appreciate it, in our issues forum.\par \par \uc2 We'll have the panel. Coming up next on the campaign trail, the latest breaking news and the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Breaking tonight, we're getting reports union workers have reached an agreement to suspend their strike until January, telling our Alexis Adams on the dock there that the strike is over. One of the delegates saying that the ILA union notified all workers that an agreement has been reached and that port workers must report back to work tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 We're being told that a final deal will have to be struck by January 15th, giving a lot of time here. Otherwise, the proposal for a 62% wage increase goes off the table. But it seems like for the interim, that this standoff with ships off the coasts of these ports will come to an end, which is a big deal for the economy. Let's bring in an abbreviated panel here with Byron York, Chief Political Correspondent, the Washington Examiner, and Julia Manchester, National Politics Reporter for The Hill.\par \par \uc2 Byron, a big deal. We don't have all the details yet, but seems like a win for the union at 62% wage increase if it all locks in at that. But more importantly, it doesn't affect the economy directly. It doesn't seem before November 5th.\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, WASHINGTON EXAMINER: And it's a big deal for the campaign, too, as well, because I think this was shaping up this longshoremen strike, which could easily have lasted until election day, as a really major disruptive force and a sense, something that gives the voters a sense of chaos under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who, by the way, were on the side of the strikers. So in addition to this terrible sort of slow-motion tragedy unfolding after Hurricane Helene and the administration's sluggish response to that, a devastating strike would just be about the worst thing politically that could happen. And now it looks like it's just gone away.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yeah. Julia, thoughts on this? Politically? Really?\par \par \uc2 JULIA MANCHESTER, THE HILL: Yeah. Look, I mean, I'm sure that Democrats are somewhat breathing a sigh of relief. You had Republicans including former President Trump, really tying Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to this strike. And, of course, this was happening as we saw the devastation across much of the southeast from Hurricane Helene. So there was a lot of anxiety, obviously, as to how that could impact recovery efforts or if the strike and the devastation caused in the southeast could cause more of an economic impact, but certainly a sigh of relief for Democrats. You know, a lot of Republicans I've chatted to really in the run-up tonight's breaking news, Bret, have essentially said they were confused. Just why did President Biden sort of not get involved in this or get involved at least publicly for political purposes?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, let's head to the campaign trail. Some sights and sound from that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOCLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : If lion Kamala gets four more years instead of a golden age, America will instead be plunged into a dark age.\par \par \uc2 TIM WALZ: Character matters. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has it. Her vision is about bringing us together.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: People are being reminded about the danger that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  poses to our country.\par \par \uc2 STEVE SCALISE (R-LA), HOUSE MAJORITY LEADER: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's first day was 1400 days ago. And look at the results. The world's on fire. Americans can't drive to go to the grocery store, let alone put food on the table.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOCLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, it seems like there's a lot happening day to day, but the polls are exactly where they've been. If you look at the real clear politics, average, head to head, it's basically where it's been, you know, within the margin of error here, two points. And as you look at the battleground states, it's, you know, swings day to day one way or another. But for the most part, it is really tight, especially in the seven states.\par \par \uc2 Byron, tonight we took live in Ripon, Wisconsin, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , former congresswoman, conservative, endorsing \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  largely. She said in her speech about January 6 and the former president's behavior during that day. Does that move the needle on Republicans or disaffected Republicans at all?\par \par \uc2 YORK: No, I think the one thing we can say is that given all the things that have happened in this campaign, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 's endorsement of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is probably not going to move the needle at all. Because, as you were saying, what has been remarkable about this is that all of these things that have happened, both conventions, an assassination attempt, all of these things that have happened have not really changed this race too much. You have these two really big dug-in sides on either the Democratic and the Republican side. And you have a small layer of voters in the middle who still apparently haven't decided which way to go. And that's what the whole next 33 days fight is about.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to play a sound byte from the former First Lady, Julia, about the issue of abortion. It's a full screen. I'm sorry. I'm sorry about that. I thought it was a sound byte.\par \par \uc2 And this is former First Lady Melania Trump. Why should anyone other than the woman herself have the power to determine what she does with her own body? "A woman's fundamental right of individual liberty to her own life grants her the authority to terminate her pregnancy as she wishes."\par \par \uc2 The former President was asked about that by Bill Belugian. He said she has her own thoughts. I told her to say what she believes. How do you think that affects the issue of abortion when it comes to former President Trump, Republicans, does it?\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: I think it does because these comments are coming on the heels of comments from Senator JD Vance, President Trump's running mate at the camp, at the -- excuse me, the Vice Presidential debate, where he essentially said that Republicans have to regain the trust of voters on this issue. And during that debate, we also saw former President Trump himself post a truth social that he would absolutely veto a national abortion ban, which Democrats say, you know, he would, you know, pass the national abortion ban so him pushing back on those claims.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think what you're seeing here, and although the former First Lady certainly went farther than Trump advanced it. But you're seeing the Trump campaign and Republicans trying to run to the center on this issue or trying to soften their stance on this issue.\par \par \uc2 Look, this has been an issue that has, you know, negatively impacted Republicans in past elections. In 2022, obviously, in the aftermath of overturning Roe versus Wade, we saw that this, you know, the red wave were supposed to see was in a way blunted because Democrats were focusing so much on abortion. We saw it also last year in Virginia's off-year 2023 legislative election so Republicans very cognizant of the fact that this is a tough issue for them. Polling also reflects that.\par \par \uc2 There was a \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2  poll released last month that showed 54% or 53% of Americans said they \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  more on that issue, 34% say the same thing about Trump. So a tough issue. This is them trying to regain their footing on it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Byron, quickly, I heard a lot of pundits say this hurts the former president. But to Julia's point, if anything, it seems like it softens and maybe shores up that he wouldn't pass or sign a national abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 YORK: Well, you know, those are both kind of persuasive arguments. But listen, I think the bottom line is what Mrs. Trump said right there sounds a lot more like \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  than it does the Republican ticket. And this is something that -- that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has had just a lot of trouble dealing with. And it's not really going to get any better because he did provide the judges and the energy and the presidential power that led to the repeal of Roe versus Wade. And when he says, well, we wanted to give it to the states, that's what everybody wanted. Actually, it's not what everybody wanted. It was what conservative constitutionalists wanted. But that is not -- that does nothing satisfy the majority of Americans who feel that abortion should be legal in some cases at least. So what Mrs. Trump said really sounds a lot more like a Democrat than a Republican.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Byron, Julia, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 YORK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: More breaking news tonight. Three former Memphis police officers have been convicted of charges of witness tampering and the 2023 killing of Tyree Nichols. Two were acquitted of federal civil rights violations in the case. His death sparked national protests and calls for changes in policing. Up next, we'll take you on the 50 races in 50 days and we promise to get one in every time. We will.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: 50 races in 50 days with Vice President Harris widely favored to win New York in the presidential election. Democrats are hoping to ride that expected momentum there to flip a pair of House races.\par \par \uc2 In the 17th district, incumbent Republican Congressman Mike Lawler faces former democratic representative Mondara Jones, who was called a progressive, a rising star until he got beat. Jones represented the district from 2021 to 2023 due to redistricting. He ran in New York's 10th district in 2022, losing in the primary to representative Dan Goldman. Now he's back to challenge the first-term Lawler, who finds himself today embroiled in a controversy after the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  released photos allegedly showing Lawler wearing blackface in college as part of a Michael Jackson Halloween costume.\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020241004eka300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Trump Campaigns In Battleground Michigan; Dockworkers Strike Over Pay, Automation; Melania Trump Defends Right To Choose; Suspects In Texas Girl Murder May Have Gang Ties\b0\par\par\uc2 Trace Gallagher, William La Jeunesse, Jonathan Hunt, Chad Pergram;  David Marcus; Marc Thiessen; Juan Williams; Lucas Tomlinson \par \uc2 8,762 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Sandra, John, thank you. Good to see you. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Trace Gallagher in for Martha MacCallum, and this is The Story.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump about to take the stage in Battleground, Michigan, where he will likely have a lot to say about Special Counsel Jack Smith's newly unsealed court filing surrounding the 2020 election case. The former president slamming the, quote, Department of Injustice and the timing of all this just 33 days before the election.\par \par \uc2 We are also monitoring President Biden's visit to hurricane-ravaged Georgia this hour and day three of the dock worker strike, threatening higher prices and shortages across the U.S. Marc Thiessen, Juan Williams and columnist David Marcus, who says the Biden administration appears, quoting, rudderless in the face of all that's going on.\par \par \uc2 But, first, as we await former President Trump, a closer look at the Hurricane Helene recovery effort and warning that federal money is drying up.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, HOMELAND SECURITY SECRETARY: We are meeting the immediate needs with the money that we have. We are expecting another hurricane hitting. We do not have the funds. FEMA does not have the funds to make it through the season.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Congress approving more than $600 million for a FEMA program providing resources to illegal migrants last year, and some Republicans say Washington does not have its priorities straight.\par \par \uc2 Senior Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram with the back story on Capitol Hill. Chad, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS SENIOR CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Trace, good afternoon. Here's the question. Why is there money to help communities deal with illegal immigrants, a man-made disaster, but when it comes to a natural disaster, FEMA's coffers could bleed dry? Some Republicans are mad at DHS Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Everyone should be waking up this morning outraged by that comment.\par \par \uc2 This is not something that has just happened recently. And now, right now, in Florida, North Carolina, Tennessee, Georgia, all of these states are hurting because of this truly natural disaster, not a Biden-Harris-made disaster.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: GOP Florida Representative Cory Mills helped distribute generators and other aid to people in the disaster zone. He asked why Congress spends money to help those here illegally compared to a natural disaster, or even sending money abroad.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. CORY MILLS (R-FL): It's our responsibility to put Americans first, whether you're in Florida's seventh district in my district or you're anywhere in the union. I think that it's important for us to prioritize Americans to restore hope and let them know they're not forgotten.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: But DHS has different accounts to help communities cope with the migrant crisis and handle the hurricane. Congress is now on track to approve additional money for FEMA later this fall.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL HAGERTY (R-TN): Nobody has seen anything like this in recorded history. The amount of damage, the flooding is just it's just hard to imagine. We've got to have resources quickly. We've got whole communities that are cut off bridges that are out, roadways that are out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The hurricane season runs until the end of November. House Speaker Mike Johnson concedes that Congress must address Helene, but he's worried about the national debt. Extra spending for war, terrorism, and natural disasters help drive the debt. Trace?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Chad Pergram live for us on Capitol Hill, Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Fox News Contributor Marc Thiessen, Fox News Senior Political Analyst Juan Williams, and columnist David Marcus, who writes for Fox today, quoting, the southeast is underwater, the Middle East is on fire, ports are closed, where is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 It's kind of amazing, David, let's begin with you, because it really is a compelling article, and the theme throughout this whole thing, whether it's Israel or whether it's the port strike, you name it, is that there has been a lack of a timely response by the federal government.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARCUS, FOX NEWS COLUMNIST: Yes. After \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  left the race, I was all over the country talking to various voters and I would ask them, do you think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is in charge? I don't even ask anymore because almost to a person they said, no, many of them would just start laughing.\par \par \uc2 I would say to Democrats, though, isn't this a problem that we don't have a functioning president and say, well, it's okay. He's got a great team, he's got experts. And the Democratic voters I talked to are comfortable with this notion of a president by committee. It doesn't matter who's in the Oval Office. The big wheel just keeps on turning.\par \par \uc2 Republican voters are horrified by this. They want one single accountable person actually making the decisions and they blame a lot of the recent failures on the fact that right now we don't seem to have that.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. And it's interesting, Marc Thiessen, because he makes a good point. You know, a lot of the Democrats we talked to are also fine with this power by committee. But it's interesting when you move on to subjects like Israel, The Wall Street Journal points out this morning quoting here the thinking best -- talking about Biden protecting Iran's nuclear program, the thinking is that has led to the collapse of U.S. deterrence on Mr. Biden's watch. Enemies rely on him to come to their defense by restraining allies from responding to aggression. Mr. Biden didn't even wait to keep Iran guessing. I mean, Iran, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , \uc2 Hamas\uc2  all know that Biden will keep telling Israel to stop instead of telling Israel to win.\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, I mean, the one area where I disagree with David is that one competent person, if it wasn't \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , would be \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . And Republicans ought to be careful what they wish for, because I don't want \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to spend a single minute on the Oval Office behind the Resolute Desk, not even a couple of months, because she's just as bad as Biden is, if not worse.\par \par \uc2 And, look, it's absolutely true. He's hanging out on the beach while we have the worst hurricane since Katrina. We have Iran firing missiles at Israel, which it has never done before until \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was president. It had always attacked Israel through proxies.\par \par \uc2 Now, they've twice attacked Israel directly with weapons and Biden is terrified of escalation. I interviewed President Trump at Mar-a-Lago the other day, and one of the things that became clear in discussing this whole issue with him is that the difference between Biden and Trump is that Biden fears escalation. Trump makes our enemies fear escalation. After he gave a red line to the Iranians and said, if you kill a single American in that region, I'm going to -- you're going to pay a price, not your proxies, you. And when they cross the red line, he killed \uc2 Qasem Soleimani\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And then he told them, I've picked out 52 targets inside Iran in honor of the 52 American hostages. And if you cross the line again, I'm going to strike Iran proper. And they backed down because they feared escalation more than he did. Our president right now fears escalation more than the Iranians, which is why we have escalation.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And it's a fair point, Juan, because you talk to -- as we do every day, you talk to American Jews, you talk to some of these victims of Helene, and they're not exactly giving the Biden-Harris administration high marks.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I don't understand much of this conversation because in the Middle East, I don't know what you would want the American administration to do beyond signal clear support for Israel, which is what the Biden administration has done. And in terms of escalation, we have 40,000 American soldiers and troops on the ground or in the seas in the region.\par \par \uc2 So, escalation could involve our people getting killed. We don't want it. We want peace and we want some kind of solution to the Middle East tensions. That's why you would encourage us -- I think encourage people to look for some resolution.\par \par \uc2 And with regard to the hurricane, Trace, you know, I don't think this is a -- it's a fair choice. It's a false choice. In fact, to say, oh, money's that were going to immigrants, and it's about $600 million, not even a dollop of the billions that we give just recent, just last week, we gave billions more, $20 billion to FEMA to help with hurricane recovery. You know, Trump likes to blame immigrants for everything, from crime to infectious disease.\par \par \uc2 And now for hurricanes, I mean, this is so transparent. You can see right through it. It's a political argument, not realistic in terms of what the president can do.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: David, I say I've got 30 seconds for you if you want to respond.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS: Yes. Look, you can't have a situation where \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  goes down to Appalachia and says that people who had their houses destroyed get $750 while New York City is handing out ATM cards where illegal immigrants are getting $350 a week. It's patently absurd. It's not a political question. It's a moral question and it's wrong.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. With that, we've got to go. Marc Thiessen, Juan Williams, David Marcus, thank you all.\par \par \uc2 Well, we will bring you to Michigan as soon as former President Trump takes the stage. Meantime, a grim milestone one week after Hurricane Helene tore through parts of the southeast, the death toll now surpassing 200 deaths. And while President Biden pledges millions of dollars, thousands of victims are still without access to water, electricity, and in many cases, a place to live.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think it's very disappointing, you know, that there's been such a delay in getting the help that this community needs.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We're lucky to be alive. And we know a lot of reporters keep asking about, you know, body counts and death. And the real truth is they're still looking for everyone. You know, they're in the river. They're in the trees. We don't know the scale yet.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, President Biden in route to Georgia right now for another survey of the destruction from Hurricane Helene. After visits to Florida and North Carolina, former President Trump says he plans to visit North Carolina next week. No word on an exact date yet. At least 200 people now dead in six states.\par \par \uc2 One of the most heartbreaking stories, the mother who lost her parents and her seven-year-old son, Megan Drye, took this photo on their roof in Asheville, North Carolina. Just before it collapsed. Only Megan survived. She paid tribute to her son, Micah, in an emotional interview with Fox weather anchors Stephen Morgan and Marissa Torres.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEGAN DRYE, LOST SON AND PARENTS IN FLOOD: I'm so proud of my son because in his last moments, he wasn't screaming for me. He was screaming, Jesus, save me, Jesus, I hear you, Jesus, I'm calling upon you. And his wildest dreams and everything that he wanted to be was a superhero. And that was his goal in life. And instead he's my hero. He was the smartest, bravest, hopeful, great friend, great son. I couldn't have asked for a better son and he was so happy up until the very end when he was screaming for Jesus. And in that moment, I think he found joy.\par \par \uc2 MARISSA TORRES, FOX NEWS METEOROLOGIST: You know, the innocence of Children is actually quite spectacular. Oh my gosh, I'm so sorry.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MORGAN, FOX NEWS METEOROLOGIST: Yes, it is, Marissa being a mother too, Megan.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, it really is a heart-rending account. Let's bring in Brian Trascher, vice president of the United Cajun Navy. He's standing by. Some of the volunteers involved in the group's rescue efforts say the damage done is of biblical correspondence. Very quickly, let's check in to with Lucas Tomlinson, who's live in Valdosta, Georgia. Lucas?\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Trace, good afternoon. We just saw Marine One flying overhead with President Biden aboard. He'll be on the ground at Moody Air Force Base here in Valdosta, Georgia, shortly. The death toll from Hurricane Helene across the southeast now over 200, making this the second deadliest hurricane to hit the U.S. mainland in the past 55 years.\par \par \uc2 Now, President Biden has just ordered a thousand active duty troops to join in the recovery and search and rescue efforts, as well as help clean up the destruction. Some of those troops, of course, from Moody Air Force Base, home of the pararescue jumpers, the most elite search and rescue forces in the world.\par \par \uc2 Now, when President Biden arrived in Tallahassee this morning in Florida, he was greeted by the mayor and state officials. Governor DeSantis was not among them. And here in Georgia, Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  does not plan to meet the president when he arrives here.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden just toured Keaton Beach about an hour and a half southeast of Tallahassee along the Gulf Coast. At least 19 people in Florida have been killed due to Hurricane Helene, and at least 33 have been killed here in Georgia. Biden also toured Perry, Florida, toured the damage by air aboard Marine One.\par \par \uc2 Now, here in Georgia, Trace, 1,400 members of the National Guard are helping their communities clear the roads and evacuate the stranded. Three counties in Georgia, Atkinson, Jefferson, Montgomery, are still 90 percent without power.\par \par \uc2 As I mentioned, President Biden should be on the ground at Moody Air Force Base here in Valdosta, Georgia shortly. He'll be touring the damage and then is expected to give remarks. Trace?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Lucas Thomason live for us, Lucas, thank you.\par \par \uc2 United Cajun Navy Vice President Brian Trascher joins us now on the phone from North Carolina with an update on the group's relief efforts. Brian, thank you for coming on. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 You know, the people we talked to who are victims of Helene say, in a lot of cases in a lot of these towns, they are the first responders because there's so much debris, so much destruction that the actual first responders and outside help are having a difficult time getting in. What are you finding, Brian?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN TRASCHER, VICE PRESIDENT, UNITED CAJUN NAVY: So, thanks for having me on, Trace. And, you know, like always, we go in, we try to be the first shrimp boots on the ground because we know that, you know, the federal government's not a first responder. They're a second responder and it takes a while to get that cavalry going.\par \par \uc2 But we started off, we based air operations in Hickory, North Carolina, and we had over 30 aircraft participate in missions, flying supplies and we've been evac'ing people out of the damaged areas. And just earlier today, I flew a helicopter into Burnsville, which is a town hit very hard. And there were people there from neighboring towns, like Bakersville and Rome Mountain, who found their way to Burnsville because it was the closest sort of like civilization they could find.\par \par \uc2 And they need medical attention. They said nobody's been there, nobody's touched them. And so we're just trying to send our teams further and further towards the Tennessee line. It seems like it gets worse the further you go that way.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: It's interesting. You say that, you know, the government is always second responders. I want to play this sound bite from Georgia Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 . He had this to say, Brian. I'll get back to you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. BRIAN KEMP (R-GA): I know there's a lot of people that are frustrated out there and I can certainly appreciate that and Marty and I felt that when we were on the road yesterday, but also told them this is unprecedented that we're dealing with, previously the worst storm Georgia power had ever dealt with. They had 1,700 poles that needed to be replaced.\par \par \uc2 Currently, there are over 8,000 that have to be replaced.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: The people that we talked to, Brian, are frustrated because the response, the federal response has not been as robust as they were hoping.\par \par \uc2 TRASCHER: Well, you know, I went through Katrina. I'm from New Orleans. You'll know that. And it was the same song and dance. You know, the feds, you know, they can do a lot. Once they get there, it's a question of when will they get there. And that's why organizations like the United Cajun Navy, you know, we feel are important to get there first. Because we don't have -- look, we don't have to ask our lawyers for permission to do anything. We just need them to show up at our bond hearing. That's our motto.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. What do you think the priority is right now, Brian? I've got to go but is this a water, a clean water thing? Is that the primary objective right now for these people?\par \par \uc2 TRASCHER: Yes, clean water, medical supplies, the fire stations are overwhelmed and we need -- they need oxygen, they need fuel. And we deployed several hundred Starlink units. They need communications and they need them now. So, keep the Starlinks coming. That's been the glimmer of hope. This whole thing is Starlink.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Brian Trascher, best of luck to you and your fellow members of the Cajun Navy. Thank you for the help. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, former President Trump taking the stage at a campaign rally in battleground Michigan, currently leaning Democrat. That's according to our latest power rankings. Let's listen.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT, 2024 PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: In this beautiful state with thousands of proud, hardworking American patriots, which is exactly what you are. Early mail-in voting is underway now in Michigan, so if you want, go out and get that ballot, return it as soon as possible, and if not, get a ballot, just go and vote. You got to vote. We got to win this election. Our country is being destroyed by these lunatics. And while you're at it, get as many other Trump supporters as you can, and they'll do the same.\par \par \uc2 33 days from now, we are going to win the state of Michigan. We are going to defeat Lying \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , one of the worst candidates ever. You never had a candidate like that. And how about the vice president? That's going to be president someday? I don't think so. I don't think so. Did you see the debate? I don't think that's going to be president. He called himself knucklehead. We don't want a knucklehead as a president. And we're going to make America great again.\par \par \uc2 And I'm asking every citizen all across our land to join me in launching a new golden age for America. As catastrophic as \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  -- you know, I never used the name Harris. Nobody knows what the hell I'm talking about. I say, you know, Harris is doing a lousy job. Who's Harris? So, we usually just say Lying Kamala. It's much better, more accurate.\par \par \uc2 But as bad as Kamala's failures have been, we have a plan to fix it and we're going to fix it very fast. It's going to go very quick. Can you imagine what she's doing to our country on the borders? Can you imagine? With everything else is bad. Can you imagine what they're doing? Why? Why are they doing it? They're killing our country.\par \par \uc2 We can soon have soaring income, skyrocketing wealth, millions and millions of new jobs and a booming middle class. You know, we had the best four-year period of any president in history and that's despite the China virus coming in, despite the China virus. Instead of a manufacturing recession, we can have a manufacturing renaissance, which is what we want, especially here, especially in this time.\par \par \uc2 We can live in cities and towns that are safe and clean and borders that are sealed and secure, wouldn't that be nice? And America can once again be strong and confident and free. I mean, how would you like to live like they're living where thousands and thousands of criminals are put into your town or your city? And all of a sudden your whole life changes, your whole life changes. You see what's happened with Springfield and \uc2 Aurora\uc2 . You see what happened, Springfield, Ohio. You see what's happening.\par \par \uc2 Would you like to have that happen to you? Good. I was just curious who the hell can do that? Who can do it? They inject 32,000 people from a foreign land that has lots of problems, but they eject 30 -- and your 50,000 people and now you have in a month you have you've almost doubled the size.\par \par \uc2 And it's a disaster what's going on in Aurora, where you have Venezuela and street gangs. They went into the same business as me, but they take their real estate with guns. I had to go borrow money from banks. They're in the real estate business.\par \par \uc2 They go in and the governor there in Colorado, Aurora, Colorado, the governor is petrified. He doesn't know what to do. He's never seen anything like it. Actually, nobody has seen anything like it when you get right down to it.\par \par \uc2 But we could be a nation that dreams big again. We can be a nation that builds things again. We can reclaim our pride, our dignity, our glorious American destiny, but only if we make a clean break from the failures of the past four years. They've been the worst four years. When you add everything up, the inflation, the lack of respect, the Afghanistan disaster, which was the most embarrassing period of time in our history, where we were just humiliated, and you add it all up and all we're going to do is a very simple thing. We're going to make our country so incredible and so fast. You only have one thing to do on November 5th. We're going to vote for a person named \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 . Have you ever heard of him? Have you ever heard him?\par \par \uc2 Nice hat.\par \par \uc2 But if Lying Kamala gets in, if she gets -- can you imagine another four years like we've lived the last four? Think of it. People are dying in North Carolina. They're dying all over those five, six states. They're dying and they're getting no help from our federal government because they have no money because their money's been spent on people that should not be in our country.\par \par \uc2 If Lying Kamala gets four more years instead of a golden age, America will instead be plunged into a dark age. Your family finances will be permanently destroyed. Your borders will be gone forever. They already are when you think of it, right? We don't have any borders. You're a border state now, you know that? Welcome. Congratulations. Tens of millions more illegal aliens will invade our cities and towns. Medicare and Social Security will buckle and collapse from the weight of all of these people becoming proud members of our Social Security System. Isn't that nice?\par \par \uc2 Remember, for four years I took care of it. I didn't raise the age five years. You know what that means? That means, darling I thought I was going to have Social Security. I thought I was going to retire a little early. But they've raised the age by five or six or seven years. That's what they're going to do to you. I won't, because I would have done it already if I was going to do it. But they're going to do it. They're going to do it.\par \par \uc2 And they're really destroying the whole system in the world. We'll be thrown into chaos and conflict like never before, although we're pretty close to that right now. And we're also very close to a third world war. And we have incompetent people running our country. We don't want to be -- we don't want to have them -- did you see where Biden said today when they asked about what about Israel, what are they doing? And he said, well, I don't know. He didn't even know where the hell he was.\par \par \uc2 You know, it's all these politicians now. I think I was the only one that ever went to the planes and the helicopters and talked, right? Now, they all sort of stop and they talk, but he has to do a good job of it. He talks -- but he said, well, they'll have to do. I don't want them to do anything, Israel, but as long as they don't destroy the nuclear weapons on the other side.\par \par \uc2 I said, did he say that? Now, isn't it supposed to be the opposite, Judge? We got some of the biggest judges in the country right here. But isn't it supposed to be the opposite? No, as long as you do destroy the nuclear weapons, right, he said. He's the worst foreign policy president in history, and everybody knew that from the beginning. You know, he said, as long as they don't destroy the nuclear, and I'm still trying to figure that one out. Nobody can figure it out. He can't figure it out either, come to think of it.\par \par \uc2 If you want to avoid this miserable fate for America, Michigan and the entire Midwest will have to go out and you have to get -- we have to be too big to rig, you know that, too big to rig. Turn out and vote in record numbers, too big to rig.\par \par \uc2 You know, last time, last election, we did great in 2016. A lot of people don't know we did much better in 2020. We won. We won. We did win. It was a rigged election.\par \par \uc2 It was a rigged election. You have to tell \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  -- that's why I'm doing it again. If I thought I lost, I wouldn't be doing this again.\par \par \uc2 You know where I'd be right now, on the beaches of Monte Carlo, maybe, or someplace, be having a nice life. But if I had my choice of being here with you today or being on some magnificent beach with the waves hitting me in the face, I would take you every single time because we're gonna make our country great. Every single time.\par \par \uc2 It's a very energetic crowd, this crow. And who else can fill up? Only Maga (inaudible). But who ever fills big places like this at 3:00 in the afternoon? We're going to be meeting. We're going to think of it.\par \par \uc2 I just left Wisconsin four days ago. Five days ago, we had a -- sort of a rally. It wasn't supposed to be 60,000 people showed up in Wisconsin. But the White House wouldn't give us proper security coverage so we end up in a room of about 900 people.\par \par \uc2 You have 60,000 people, 50,000 to 60,000 people outside waiting. But they don't want that because they don't want crowds. What they are doing is such a disgrace to our country. And so to those people in Wisconsin that didn't make it, I think they love us and I think they're going to vote.\par \par \uc2 By the way, we're up in all of the polls. We're up in every swing state, doing great. They had a honeymoon period. You know, they had like a honeymoon, I didn't know what a honeymoon period. They kept talking about the honeymoon. Because I beat Biden, we were up 21 points by their numbers, and they went to him.\par \par \uc2 Crazy \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , she's crazy as a bedbug. Crazy Nancy, but others, too, they went to him and they said, Joe, you're out. He said, I'm not. I'm not getting out. And they said, no, you're out. We're going to use the 25th amendment because you're fricking crazy, man.\par \par \uc2 And he eventually agreed to get out, which took about, you know, 24 hours. Remember, he said he would only get out if he was forced to by God. And that didn't work out. That didn't work out. So they said, you're getting out because you're going to lose. You're going to lose by a lot.\par \par \uc2 Right after the debate, they went to him, right? I don't know, did I do a good job or a bad job? They said, you should have taken it a little bit easy. But all of a sudden, we start hearing rumors, right, that he's getting out.\par \par \uc2 And then we heard they, you know, have the standard list of people, Democrats and Gavin New-scum and lots of people. He destroyed California. She destroyed California, by the way. She destroyed the greatest city in our country 20 years ago, San Francisco. And then she became AG.\par \par \uc2 She destroyed the state of California along with New-scum. But she had every bit -- did you ever hear of him, Gavin New-scum? He's a terrible governor, done a lousy job.\par \par \uc2 And you have such advantages, you know, you do have great weather. You have the beautiful ocean. You have everything. But you still need somebody that knows what they're doing. And she destroyed, think of it, she destroyed San Francisco when she was the DA, the crime started to be rampant. It was the best city, the best.\par \par \uc2 And then she destroyed California. And now she wants to destroy the United States of America. We're not going to let her go.\par \par \uc2 So you have to tell Lying Kamala that we've had enough. Kamala, we can't take you anymore. We're not going to stand for it anymore. Kamala, you're fired. Get out. You're fired. You're fired. Get the hell out of here, right?\par \par \uc2 Starting on day one of my new administration, we will end inflation and we will make America affordable again. It's not affordable. They kill you. They kill you with their spending, a combination of spending and their stupid energy policies.\par \par \uc2 They said we're going to get away from the Trump energy policies. We were energy independent. Your numbers were the lowest they were in 30 years. We're going to get lower numbers. By the way, we are going to from January 20, that's the day you take office. One year from January 20, your energy bills will be one-half what they are right now. Fifty percent, 5-0, they'll be one-half.\par \par \uc2 Because we're sitting on more liquid gold than any other country in the world, including Saudi Arabia and including Russia. We have more liquid gold right under our feet and we don't use it. We go to Venezuela to get their tar. And then we clean it up, you know where? We go to a very good -- I just left there, actually. We go to Houston, Texas where they're the only plant that will clean their tar.\par \par \uc2 It's tar. Instead of using our stuff, which is pure, as pure as there is. And it's so crazy what we're doing in this country. It's just crazy. But we have more liquid gold. You know, I did a thing called ANWR that's in Alaska. You know about it a little bit.\par \par \uc2 Reagan tried to get it. Everybody tried to get it. They couldn't do it. I got it.\par \par \uc2 In their first week, they terminated it. It's larger, perhaps as big as, but maybe larger than Saudi Arabia. Think of that. We would have supplied the entire Asian continent. We would have supplied Asia. We would have supplied everybody. But we'll have it redone very quickly because they didn't kill it like they wanted to, because they didn't kill it in Congress.\par \par \uc2 I actually got it approved in Congress as part of the best -- as part of the biggest tax cuts in history for this country, I got that approved in Congress. We got ANWR. So they didn't kill it in Congress, and I don't think they ever could. So we'll get it back very quickly. It's going to be back very fast.\par \par \uc2 But think of that, the biggest sight probably anywhere in the world, you never know exactly, but pretty much anywhere in the world, in Alaska. And it would have been great for Alaska, but it would have also been great for, you know, so big would have been great for our country. But we'll have it approved very quickly.\par \par \uc2 But they ended it in the first week. But they approved the Russian pipeline. I had that disapproved. I said, you're not going to build, you know, when they say, I was nice to Russia, I terminated their pipeline. I mean, it's the biggest thing they've ever done.\par \par \uc2 They had a pipeline going to Germany and all over Europe. I said, nope, you're not going to build it. You're not going to build it, Vladimir. I'm sorry, Vladimir. You're not building it, Vladimir. No, no, no, because it didn't make sense.\par \par \uc2 We're defending them with \uc2 NATO\uc2 , right? So we're defending them, all the countries over in Europe. They're not paying their bills. They're delinquent. You know, they're delinquent. They're not paying their bills. But we're defending them with \uc2 NATO\uc2 , and yet they're paying billions of dollars to the people that we're defending, the country that we're defending them against Russia.\par \par \uc2 So we defend them. And I said, you know, Angela, I met with \uc2 Angela Merkel\uc2 . I said, Angela, this doesn't make sense. We're defending you from Russia, but you're paying Russia billions of dollars a month. Somehow that's not working for me, Angela. And I terminated their pipeline right in the middle of that construction.\par \par \uc2 You think Putin was happy? I don't think so. You think Biden would have done that?\par \par \uc2 When Biden came in -- when Biden came in, one of his first acts was to unterminate. It was to let him build their pipeline. That wasn't good. And by the way, if he didn't, you wouldn't have had the war in Ukraine. You wouldn't have had it anyway if I was president.\par \par \uc2 If I was president, you wouldn't have had that. You wouldn't have had October 7th with Israel. Iran, as you know, was broke.\par \par \uc2 They had no money for \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . They had no money for \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . They had no money for anything. They would have made a deal. And they would have been happy. They would have made a deal.\par \par \uc2 And now look at what's happening over there. The whole world is blowing up. And the biggest threat we have is World War II, and we have incompetent people. And she's more incompetent than Biden.\par \par \uc2 And remember, that was a coup. That wasn't like -- that was a coup of an American --\par \par \uc2 (END LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Well, there you go. That's former President Trump in Saginaw, Michigan, holding a rally there with a big crowd turnout. Meantime, Vice President Harris railing against companies making record profits in the dockworker battle that could hit hard for millions of consumers. Senior National Correspondent William La Jeunesse is live at the port of Los Angeles with a preview. William?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, Tracy, you know, East Coast longshoremen are worried that robots are going to take their port union jobs. Well, what happened when longshoremen lost that fight here on the West Coast? That's story coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, vice president and -- or President Biden and Vice President Harris calling for a fair deal for dockworkers as the port strike hits day three, threatening to cause weeks of supply chain backlogs.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D), PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: There's a consortium of basically six owners that control all the ports from all the way to the East Coast down around the gulf. And they've made incredible profits, over 800 percent profit since the pandemic. And the owners are making tens of millions of dollars in this. The last thing they need is to profit off of this. It's time to then sit at the table and get this strike done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And Vice President Harris saying, quoting here, "This strike is about fairness. Foreign-owned shipping companies have made record profits and executive compensation has grown. The longshoremen who play a vital role transporting essential goods across America deserve a fair share of these record profits."\par \par \uc2 Senior national correspondent William La Jeunesse is live at the port of Los Angeles. William?\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Well, Tracy, this strike is not just about pay, a major sticking point is automation. The union worried that humans are going to be replaced by robots up in those cranes and down on the wharf. So that's why you need to take a look at this.\par \par \uc2 This is the difference between the East and the West Coast longshoremen contracts. On the left, the East Coast bans "any equipment devoid of human interaction," and they want it to stay that way. The West Coast Union fought this battle years ago and they lost, by contrast. This contract says fully mechanized and robotic operated marine terminals are allowed. And that's where the world is going.\par \par \uc2 China's largest port, you got four guys at computers handling about two dozen automated cranes delivering containers to automatic vehicles, and then to another automatic crane, putting those on trucks and trains. Now, upfront, it is expensive but it's more efficient and productive.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GERALDINE KNATZ, USC PROFESSOR AND MARITIME EXPERT: So the terminal operators started, you know, basically started to see a return on their investment after five to six years, some of it longer, because of the cost of implementing the automated equipment.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Study show that the semi-autonomous terminals here in Long Beach and LA processed containers about two times faster than a conventional terminal, but they also took about 600 jobs. Now, as for diverting traffic here to LA from the East Coast, two things.\par \par \uc2 Number one, once a ship is under sail, by and large it's committed to the itinerary. That cargo is going to go off Baltimore, New York. That's where it's going to sit.\par \par \uc2 However, we do have room here, right? There's about 20 percent excess capacity. That's room for about 40 more container ships per month here in LA Long Beach.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GENE SEROKA, PORT OF LOS ANGELES EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR: If a shipper decided tomorrow they wanted to move to cargo to Los Angeles, we'd see that box arrive in about a month.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: So if we're going to see that volume shift, Trace, we're probably going to see those numbers come out next week. As for the robots, they are on their way. Some will argue that the increase in productivity leads to more jobs. But the East Coast longshoremen don't believe it. They don't want to see robots coming to the port. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: William La Jeunesse live for us in the \uc2 Port of Los Angeles\uc2 . William, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Neil Bradley\uc2  is Chief Policy Officer for the \uc2 US Chamber of Commerce\uc2  and warns the strike could cause weeks of supply chain backlogs. Neil, great to have you on the show. We're in day three of this strike. If it goes a week, if it goes two weeks, what does it start to look like, Neil?\par \par \uc2 NEIL BRADLEY\uc2 , CHIEF POLICY OFFICER, US CHAMBER OF COMMERCE: Well, it starts to look like empty shelves in grocery stores. That's some of the first impact we'll see. We don't grow bananas here in the United States, we bring them in mainly through the East and Gulf Coast ports.\par \par \uc2 And those are perishable items. You can't really store those for a long time. So we're going to start to see shortages in grocery stores and restaurants. That's going to compound into retail stores.\par \par \uc2 The ones that are going to be the first hit are actually small businesses. Small businesses really can't afford to stockpile inventory months and months ahead of time. So small businesses that rely on this holiday sales season may find that they actually don't have any inventory to put up for sale.\par \par \uc2 And so, if this keeps going, we're talking about really profound negative consequences for businesses and consumers all across the country.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: William just talking about the fact that a lot of this has been the focus of, you know, wages, but there's also a lot of technology involved in these debates. \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  said this, and I want to get your take afterwards. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LARRY KUDLOW\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: The union has a point here, an important point. We got clobbered on inflation. There's been an affordability crisis for years. We haven't had a raise. The company's making good money.\par \par \uc2 The rank and file have a right to come at this. They probably deserve a pretty good raise.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Do they, Neil?\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: Well, of course they deserve a raise. And let's be clear, the container ship operators in the ports offered a 50 percent pay raise just to keep the union at the negotiating table. Not as a final deal, just to keep them at the table.\par \par \uc2 And the union bosses said, nope, not good enough. We would rather strike. And the union president made clear his strategy. He said he wanted to cripple, "cripple" the US economy, and he's doing that to gain leverage. I don't think any of us find it acceptable that one union boss can try to cripple the United States economy as a means of trying to achieve their ends.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. Meantime, the President, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , says he does not believe in Taft-Hartley. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Would you intervene in the dockworkers strike if they go on strike on Tuesday?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Why not?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Because it's collective arguing. I don't believe in Taft-Hartley.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And even if it was implemented, the union bosses say they would just slow things down. I mean, instead of unloading 30, they'd unload eight. And so that's kind of the thing here. Should the President be more stringent with the union bosses themselves, Neil?\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: He absolutely should. Listen, President Biden's predecessors have used Taft-Hartley on 33 separate occasions, including 14 times where we had port or maritime operations suspended just like we do now.\par \par \uc2 And here's the other thing. It's bipartisan. President Biden's six Democratic predecessors who had this law available to them, four of them used it in situations just like this.\par \par \uc2 And so, there's no foolproof plan here but the president should absolutely be invoking Taft-Hartley. He should force these parties back to the table. He should get the workers back unloading this produce and the essential goods that we need for hurricane recovery, for our store shelves and to support our economy.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I got 30 seconds. You know, you said first when I asked you that the thing you're going to see first is empty store shelves. And then you say produce. Is it the produce on the store shelves that we are going to see disappear first?\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: That's what you're going to see first because you really can't store that up ahead of time. It relies on real time delivery. And these are things that -- we can't supply bananas. We don't have the climate to grow bananas in the United States.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: And so, those are the types of things that will disappear first.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: \uc2 Neil Bradley\uc2 , thanks for coming on the show. We appreciate it, sir.\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, President Biden also weighing in on Israel's possible response to this week's missile attack from Iran. Israel launching more strikes on \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  targets in Lebanon today, and revealing it killed the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  government chief in Gaza three months ago. President Biden taking questions from reporters as he left the White House today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The hostages in Gaza, do you have any updates on this?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Not right now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Would you support Israel striking Iran's oil facilities, sir?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We're in discussion with that. I think that would be a little -- anyway.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And still ahead, former first lady makes sure there's no confusion about where she stands on abortion. And stunning new details possibly linking the suspects accused of brutally murdering twelve year old Jocelyn Nungaray and the violent Venezuelan gang Tren de Aragua.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS NUNGARAY, JOCELYN NUNGARAY'S MOTHER: I come to you all as a grieving mother to please help make this country make a change for the greater good. This country needs to make a change. We need to properly secure our borders. We need to be able to have that security knowing we are safe in our own country. And most importantly, we need to protect our children.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Days after Senator JD Vance said the Republican Party needed to regain trust on abortion, former first lady Melania Trump dropping a new video to carve out a clear stand on the hot button issue.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELANIA TRUMP, FORMER FIRST LADY: Individual freedom is a fundamental principle that I safeguard. Without a doubt, there is no room for compromise when it comes to this essential right that all women possess from birth, individual freedom. What does my body, my choice really mean?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: An excerpt from Melania Trump's upcoming memoir reportedly says restricting a woman's right to choose is the same as denying her control over her body. Former President Trump says he'd veto any federal abortion ban if reelected and is leaving it up to the states to decide on abortion access.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, disturbing reports today that the suspects in the murder of twelve year old Texas girl Jocelyn Nungaray, who are here in the US illegally, may have ties to the notorious Venezuelan gang Tren de Aragua. This is Jocelyn's mother, Alexis.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NUNGARAY: I have to go through the rest of my life with my son always asking for his sister. And I just really, really, really want everybody to please take into consideration and how important border control is, because we're losing very innocent people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: The Chief Correspondent \uc2 Jonathan Hunt\uc2  following the story from here in Los Angeles. Jonathan?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHIEF CORRESPONDENT: Trace, good afternoon. Jocelyn Nungaray was sexually assaulted, her hands and ankles tied, and she was then strangled to death, according to prosecutors. These two men, Franklin Pena and Johan Martinez-Rangel, have been charged with her murder and both are being held on a $10 million bond. And investigators now believe both are connected to the notorious Venezuelan gang Tren de Aragua.\par \par \uc2 Evidence of those ties was allegedly found on their TikTok accounts. According to a newly released search warrant, the evidence includes emojis commonly used by the gang, such as a ninja cross swords and an icon of a classical building, as well as tattoos and hand signals. Nungaray's mother says she's not surprised by the alleged gang links.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NUNGARAY: Individuals like that do not have a heart. They are nothing but monsters who are predators. And those are the kind of individuals that we so openly let in this country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Now, just last month, Texas Governor Greg Abbott designated TDA, as the gang is known, as a foreign terrorist organization, and Texas law enforcement vowed to do everything in its power to crack down on gang members.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEVE MCCRAW\uc2 , \uc2 TEXAS DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC SAFETY\uc2  DIRECTOR: Tren de Aragua, TDA, gangsters are like cockroaches. They multiply quickly. Small intrusions into communities become infestations if not aggressively pursued.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Now, both suspects in the murder, as you mentioned, Trace, entered the US illegally across our southern border.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And a lot of people are wondering, Jonathan, because you look at this in the totality and how many of these people are now in Aurora, Colorado, etcetera, is how they got in, how they got in unchecked because, because now they're starting to do some checks. As you said, some of those symbols are being checked.\par \par \uc2 So the big question is, over the past several years, how they kept getting in unchecked?\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Right. And more and more of them we're finding out about, and more we are finding out that they crossed illegally between ports of entry. There are -- Border Patrol agents are doing the best they can at those ports of entry. But it's in between as we know, it's open season.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Very quickly. Is there evidence that these members of Tren de Aragua in this country were actually in prisons in Venezuela before they were released?\par \par \uc2 HUNT: We do not have that information. We simply don't know the background of these particular two. But I'm sure investigators are looking into it very closely.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: \uc2 Jonathan Hunt\uc2 , Jonathan, thank you. Well, that is "The Story" of Thursday, October 3, 2024. But as always, "The Story" goes on. And I will see you back here for Fox News at Night, that's 8:00 in the West Coast, 11:00 in the East.\par \par \uc2 And tonight, we are going to be talking to a man who actually voted to impeach former President Trump. But now he says he will vote former President Trump because he thinks what Democrats call the threat to democracy is actually worse now than it was then. I'm Trace Gallagher. I'll see you back here tonight. "Your World with Neil Cavuto" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020241004eka300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Polls Show Dead Heat Between Trump, Harris\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Newt Gingrich \par \uc2 7,904 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: Welcome to "Hannity".\par \par \uc2 And tonight, we have a jam-packed show. Melania Trump, the former first lady, will join us with an in-depth look into what the Trump family has faced over the past several years and her views on this upcoming election a mere 34 days away.\par \par \uc2 Also, big new poll numbers for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who appears to be picking up momentum with just a short time until election day, although early voting of course is rolling out around the country.\par \par \uc2 Later, we will also check in with Sarah Carter. She is in Asheville, North Carolina. North Carolina has been devastated and in many ways abandoned where the death toll continues to rise following the devastation from Hurricane Helene. Now, after a weekend of star studded fundraisers and lavish parties with her friends on the West Coast and Hollywood, well, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  finally found the time to tore some damage in the Southeast.\par \par \uc2 And meanwhile, Kamala likely regrets picking fellow radical leftist Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  as a running mate. The joy seems most definitely gone after last night's debate. Now we know why the Harris campaign, you know, keeps Walz very far away from the media. He doesn't do interviews. We know why, because he can't.\par \par \uc2 The governor is a complete and total, well, frankly, buffoon. He is in way over his head, kind of like she is. He is way out of his league and Walz looked like a deer in the headlights, so visibly nervous, he did not have command of the facts.\par \par \uc2 He seemed ill-prepared. He said some pretty weird, bizarre things. Aren't many people, you know, that call themselves a knucklehead in the middle of a debate or brag about being friends with school shooters. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DEBATE MODERATOR: Governor Walz, you said you were in Hong Kong during the deadly Tiananmen Square protest in the spring of 1989, but Minnesota Public Radio and other media outlets are reporting that you actually didn't travel to Asia until August of that year. Can you explain that discrepancy?\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN), VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Yeah. Well, and