an agreement.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Gene Scalia, as always, great to see you, my friend. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 SCALIA: Thank you, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right. Coming up, Democrats and their media allies, they had to do some cleanup after \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 's poor showing last night. Plus, what's the story behind these expressive emotions during the debate? Raymond Arroyo has it all. Seen and Unseen is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It is time for Seen and Unseen, where we reveal the stories behind the headlines. With that, we turn to Fox News contributor Raymond Arroyo. All right, Raymond. Clearly, J.D. Vance won, but the media, they're telling a slightly different tale. Or it just doesn't matter anymore.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Laura, I haven't seen a cleanup operation like this since the feds raided Diddy's house. I mean, before it started, the debate was hyped as a turning point for the election. Then after Walz's performance, not so much.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: The vice president doesn't actually talk --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I think he had too much preparation, maybe.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: He had so many lines that he was clearly trying to say that he didn't listen. He didn't respond because he clearly had things in his mind.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I was definitely Minnesota -- nice last night. But, you know, listen, now on any debate stage like that, when you get on for the first time, you can have the butterflies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Butterflies. Laura, he looked like a butterfly whose wings had just gotten clipped. I mean, he's got that look on his face.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's like Don Rickles.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: And no one thought that this shocked look that he adopted through most of the debate, like H.R. Puffin stuff facing a tidal wave, that this was problematic. On the contrary, political rights today, I was laughing when I read this, when Walz felt especially passionate about something, he'd open his eyes wide at saucers. Eye popping can sometimes be a sign of surprise.\par \par \uc2 But for Walz, Laura Ingraham, it simply revealed an emotional intensity. It gave extra weight and his feelings and held our gaze. Was your gaze held by this, Laura?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I think we need a hyphen after wild. I'm sorry. Raymond, we should both try to do -- can you do that with your eyes? Like I can't make my eyes that big. I don't know. I can't do that. I tried to do that earlier today.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: My gosh, he looks like a squirrel. He's like a squirrel whose tail has just been ignited, you know. But you see the spin here. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' laugh, nervous laughter, suddenly is a siren of joy. And now this bug-eyed look of fear, invention, desperation, and worry is suddenly emotional intensity, in the words of Politico. When you got the media protecting you, Laura, you can go a long way.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, panic. The View girls also joined in, in the Walz cleanup crew.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: While they were, of course, throwing their usual barbs at Trump and Vance for winning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: The juvenile delinquent. I'm sorry. J.D. Vance made a choice.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: They are sociopathic liars. The two of them. You cannot have this guy in that position either. Both of them are horrible. And \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  is a real person and he's a smart guy. So, he made one faux pas.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now a lie is a faux pas?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: One faux pas. Laura, he lied about his abortion record in Minnesota, he did remove those protections for children who survive an abortion. He lied about his repeated travels to China and where he was. And then there was this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): I've become friends with school shooters. I've seen it. Look, the NRA, I was NRA guy for a long time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Friends with school shooters.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Wait, wait, wait. I know -- I thought I heard that last night. I said, this must be dreaming. This can't actually be happening. But he was asked about this last night, but this is what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Governor, you said you'd become friends with school shooters during the debate. Can you clarify what you meant on that?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Can you clarify what you meant when you said you'd be friends with school shooters?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: If I keep staring at the pepperonis long enough, they'll stop asking the question. I don't know why, Laura. If you just misspeak, say that. But why he had to wait a whole day to clarify this? He meant he was friends with victims of school shooters.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, so just say that.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Not the school shooters. But just say that, you know. But I don't know how long he can keep repeating this to get himself out of troubles and lies.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Perfect. And I'm a knucklehead at times, but it's always been about that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: He said that. I said, that's the headline. Knucklehead. Vice President Knucklehead at your service. How is that reassuring to America?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: They all keep saying, though, the vice president doesn't matter. In a day and age when we have people literally attempting to kill former presidents, and we don't know what they're attempting of the current ones, the vice president matters a lot. This is an important pick and an important decision. And the American people should consider it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, they're saying on the one hand, vice presidents don't matter that much. These debates don't matter. But then on the other hand, they are having a practically orgasmic reaction to this filing in federal court late today, where it's all about \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  said this and \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  did this. So that vice president matters.\par \par \uc2 But I guess when it's \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  and he can't get his facts straight and lies, like habitually lies, then it's just Tim. It's just part of the charm. All right, Raymond, if you listen closely though to the Democrats, you can hear, think glimmers of recognition that this ticket is going down, someone suggested Florida may be in play.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Internal campaign people, the senior levels of this campaign, people that I literally used to work with and was in the trenches of, I feel like I could be the expert on this. They are not saying they're going to win Florida.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Symone, the country's campaign doesn't waste any resources like that.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: All I'm saying is people should be concerned. You know what? I don't want to be wrong on election day. I don't want to be right. I want to be wrong. But I'm listening to some of the people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: She was angry last night. She was really mad at Walz.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: The fractures. The fractures are all over the place though, Laura. I mean, look, they're looking at the polls we're looking at, but the internal polls, I am told, are even more dire for the Harris campaign. And you hear that from Symone Sanders. She's the only truth teller on that panel. And boy, did they jump her for speaking the truth.\par \par \uc2 They should be concerned because of this candidacy and the issues they aren't speaking to at all.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: How about their record stinks? How about that as an issue? Their record is terrible and Americans are getting poorer. And I see Michael Steele on that set. Well, considering what he did to wreck the Republican Party, I hope he stays over the Democrats for a very long time. So, God bless. God bless him. But that was, that's a tragedy to see that on that panel. Raymond, great to see you as always. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right, up next, Republicans seizing the momentum in a key race that could determine, yes, the control of the Senate. Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Of course, we've all been focused on the big V.P. debate this week, but there was another extremely important debate on Monday that we should all pay attention to. It's the battle between incumbent Democrat John Tester and Republican Tim Sheehy for the Montana Senate seat.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Right now, in Montana, we're failing.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Gentlemen, -- this conversation. We're going to have to stop it there, I'm afraid.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Because I'm telling you, what he just said about community care was total horse.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Noted.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: It's remarkable. According to Senator Tester, it's my fault the senate bill didn't pass and I wasn't even a senator. Apparently, I've also sold our public lands in the last week and closed our hospitals. It's amazing how much influence I have for not being a senator.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: You ran for the office, Tim. Your statement matter.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Yeah, they do matter, and the statement is, secure the --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This race could be the deciding factor on which party controls the Senate. Latest polls have Sheehy up by almost six points. But Monday's debate showed us that this race will be heated until election day. Joining me now, Montana Senate candidate Tim Sheehy.\par \par \uc2 Tim, you're not taking anything for granted, but Senator Tester is trying to distance himself from the Biden-Harris administration. You see others like \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  trying to do that in Ohio as well. But Tester's voted with the administration, what is it, 91 percent of the time? So, what does America need to know about the fake moderate Democrats out there?\par \par \uc2 TIM SHEEHY (R), MONTANA U.S. SENATE CANDIDATE: Well, the numbers actually 100 percent of the time, because every single time the Biden-Harris agenda was on a chopping block, and as the 51st vote, he could have stopped all of it, he never did. Every single time from the Reflation Reduction Act to funding secret migrant flights into this country, big government socialist John Tester voted for it. That's a fact.\par \par \uc2 And he tried to define himself in the debate on Monday as a libertarian, which was my favorite. I love when these guys who are running in red states as big government socialists run to the libertarian lame goal to try to say that they are small government, freedom loving politicians, when what they really want to do is expand big government socialist policies to all of us.\par \par \uc2 So, we held them account on that. We held them to account on the wide open- border, on the reckless spending, and the failure of the V.A. of which she's the chairman of the committee. And I think we came out ahead.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Tell us what Montana is facing with the migrant issue. We don't think of Montana as a state that even has to deal with this. It's a, you know, it could use a lot more population a lot of people believe. But how do you find the migrant crisis actually affecting you there?\par \par \uc2 SHEEHY: Well, as everybody knows now, every state is a border state. And although we have a northern border, which is also a problem, the southern border still greatly affects us here. The -- even NBC News, not exactly a bastion of conservative border security think tank policy, even they did an article earlier this year, in 2024, highlighting how the cartels are utilizing our Indian reservations, which suffer from a lack of law enforcement and public safety infrastructure as conduits for their drug trade.\par \par \uc2 Our interstates have been conduits for human trafficking, which has doubled and tripled here in recent years. So, the border crisis is absolutely impacting Montana. Fentanyl overdoses, violent crime, human trafficking. We got a fantastic governor, a great attorney general, but they can only do so much.\par \par \uc2 On the fourth largest state with a million people, there's a lot of wide- open space. And unfortunately, our cartels have been able to exploit that. And we've been feeling that in every community across the state, from small towns to our travel communities, to our biggest cities. The fentanyl and crime crisis are affecting us.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah. But Tester said that he led on the bipartisan Fend-off Fentanyl Act, I guess. He said he led on that. So, are you saying he's lying?\par \par \uc2 SHEEHY: Well, too fast Tester's great. He'd go into D.C. and voting like a big government socialist, literally party line vote from wanting to end the filibuster to supporting the Inflation Reduction Act to funding secret migrant flights. And then he flies to Montana, puts on his MAGA hat and walks around like a border security, gun-toting, Bible-thumping conservatives.\par \par \uc2 So, he's supportive of the Biden-Harris agenda every step of the way, no exceptions. And we're holding him account on that because Montana does not want four more years of these disastrous policies.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Tim, who would be, do you think, your closest friend in this \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 ? I know you've gotten to know some of the senators because I've heard from them, but who would be your buddy? Like the kind of person you are, who would it be?\par \par \uc2 SHEEHY: Well, listen, you know, I've only gotten to know a few of them. And obviously, as a 38-year-old father of four young kids, is hard to make a lot of connection with some of these guys who are in their 70's. But Tom Cotton, I've come close. You know, he's another young veteran of the war on terror, also has young family. His wife, like mine, also served up overseas during the war.\par \par \uc2 So, we struck up a good relationship and he's been another kind of kindred younger guys who can give me some advice and it's been great. He is.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, you've just offended all the other people who you don't want to be friends with. I'm just kidding. But, Tim, it's good to see you tonight. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 All right, that's it for us tonight. Make sure to follow me on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . Remember, it's America now and forever. Jesse is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020241003eka200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Prosecutors Lay Out New Evidence In \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Election Case; Judge Releases Special Counsel January 6 Report; \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  Fighters Kill Several Israeli Soldiers; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Visit States Impacted By Hurricane Helene; Both Vance And Walz Praise Own Performance; Walz Tries To Tie Vance To Project 2025 Policies; Vance Calls Out Change In Abortion Bill Wording; Union Fight Over Wages And Automation; Hurricane Helene Disrupts Early Voting Efforts; Trump Accuses White House Of Political Response; Decisive And Painful Response Vowed Against Tehran; Trump Says He Acted Lawfully And Slams Special Counsel; Vance And Walz Spar In Vice Presidential Debate\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Steve Harrigan, Mike Tobin, Kevin Corke, Jonathan  Turley, Alexandria Hoff, Brooke Taylor, Peter Doocy, Jack Keane, Jessica  Tarlov, Harris Faulkner, Lawrence Jones \par \uc2 7,751 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: New Year. That's it for us. Have a great night, everybody.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Happy New Year. Five acorns and two leaves. All right, Dana, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I'm Bret Baier. Coming to you from Fox News World Headquarters in New York tonight, the vice presidential nominees return to the campaign trail after last night's debate, as President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  head to states impacted by Hurricane Helene. It comes as another crisis could soon take hold fully, with dozens of container ships backed up at ports across the country just two days into a workers strike.\par \par \uc2 But first, breaking tonight, a lengthy court filing from Special Counsel Jack Smith detailing arguments that former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  illegally tried to overturn the 2020 election that has been made public tonight, just 34 days before voters head to the polls in this election. Prosecutors laid out a sweeping account of President Trump's conduct after the 2020 results were announced, making the case that he is not immune from federal prosecution despite a \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  ruling on official acts. The former president is reacting to that document, calling it an unconstitutional witch hunt and labeling the justice system as broken.\par \par \uc2 Senior national Correspondent Kevin Corke has details on this lengthy filing from Washington. Good evening, Kevin.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN CORKE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. The release of the Jack Smith dossier really came as a surprise, and it's obviously going to prompt further claims of election interference, most likely by the former president. That's because, keep in mind, this is just the federal prosecutor's version of the events that happened post 2020 election in that period leading up to the Capitol riot on January the sixth.\par \par \uc2 Now, it's released with the election, as you pointed out, about a month away comes by way of federal trial judge \uc2 Tanya Chutkan\uc2 . Her decision coming months after the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  ruled that former presidents enjoy a great deal of immunity from criminal prosecution for official acts committed while in office. The court had ordered the judge to go over all the charges against Mr. Trump to determine whether he had immunity.\par \par \uc2 Now, Smith argues that the former president's efforts to block the certification of the 2020 election were private acts, not official, and therefore not deserving of presidential immunity.\par \par \uc2 "Working with a team of private co-conspirators, the defendant Mr. Trump acted as a candidate when he pursued multiple criminal means to disrupt through fraud and deceit, the government function by which votes are collected and counted, a function in which the defendant as president had no official role."\par \par \uc2 Now, Smith further suggested the former president knowing that his scheme to coerce then Vice President Pence to do his bidding was over, instead encouraged a mob to react. Again, quoting Smith, "The defendant also knew that he had only one last hope to prevent Biden certification as president, the large and angry crowd standing in front of him. So for more than an hour, the defendant delivered a speech designed to inflame his supporters and motivate them to march to the Capitol."\par \par \uc2 Now, on Truth Social the former president contended tonight that not only did he authorize the use of what 10,000 -- he said, 10,000 National Guard troops, Bret, to secure the Capitol and the City on January 6th, he also reminded the crowds, he said, to protest peacefully and patriotically. And again, he's calling for an end to the, "Witch hunt."\par \par \uc2 By the way, I should point this out, Bret, there is still no trial date set. It may never happen, depending on what happens in about 34 days. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Kevin Corke in Washington. Kevin, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in \uc2 George Washington University Law School\uc2  professor Jonathan Turley. Jonathan, thank you. I'm struck by this release. We knew it may come and that the judge in this case, Judge Chutkan, would allow this.\par \par \uc2 But in this filing, United States versus Trump, is this quote, "At its core, the defendant's scheme was a private one. He extensively used private actors and his campaign infrastructure to attempt to overturn the election results and operated in a private capacity as a candidate for office."\par \par \uc2 This massive filing, in summary, is an effort to put out all the details without going to trial before the election, right?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: It is, the timing is concerning. Jack Smith knows that this election will be the largest jury verdict in history, and this comes across as his closing election argument.\par \par \uc2 You know, this is the argument against Trump. It's one sided, it's quite elaborate, but it is an argument that he wants out there before the election.\par \par \uc2 And that will remain troubling for a lot of people. It's not going to change a lot of minds. But you know, he goes through every detail here.\par \par \uc2 What's striking about the filing is it all turns on what Trump honestly believed. You know, he says that the through line of these charges, this is a quote, was deceit.\par \par \uc2 Well, the president had attorneys saying that he did have a basis to challenge the election and that there could be a certification challenge. Democrats in the past had used this law to challenge certification. So it comes down to a question of, was it deceit?\par \par \uc2 And you know, for many people, this is a sort of a repackaging without substantive changes. The Supreme Court handed down a major ruling on presidential immunity. Smith kept the same four basic claims, and he just stripped out some references to official conduct, and now he has this mantra saying it's all private, to try to thread that needle to preserve the case.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, a lot of it deals with January 6, obviously, the former president now says, remember peacefully and patriotically marching to the capitol as he gave that speech to that crowd. And there's some stuff in here that's unflattering.\par \par \uc2 As you look at pictures from January 6 and this quote upon receiving a phone call alerting him that Pence, the vice president, had been taken to a secure location. Redacted, I'm guessing this might be \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  rushed to the dining room to inform the defendant in hopes that the defendant would take action to ensure Pence's safety. Instead, after redacted, delivered the news, the defendant looked at him and said only, so what?\par \par \uc2 What does this change, Jonathan, in the big picture? We know that this is not going to go to trial before 34 days. We don't know what happens after that. But what does this tonight change looking at the 160 plus pages?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Well, that's the real question here of why Judge Chutkan and who has said that she will not consider the election in her decisions, but why she still saw this as the right time to release this. This was a -- this is a new indictment. He went back to Smith, went back to the grand jury to secure a new indictment.\par \par \uc2 So, this trial is not going to occur anytime soon. There's no -- there's no date for it. So, for many, this is sort of a gratuitous act. You don't have to release this before the election.\par \par \uc2 But at the end of the day, it's much of the same evidence, and you're absolutely right, it does not put Trump in a -- in a good light, and all prosecutorial filings tend to do that. But there's damaging stuff here.\par \par \uc2 But the question is, is it criminal? And when you really drill down here, you're sort of left with just an assumption that everything Trump did or said he knew was a lie. And at one point he says, whether we win or lose, we're going to fight it like hell.\par \par \uc2 Now people read that line differently. You know, some will say, well, he just acknowledged that he didn't win. Others will say, it's like saying fight, fight, fight after the assassination attempt is how you view his character, and that's a little bit on unraveling when you go into a trial, it should be based on something more concrete.\par \par \uc2 Now, that's true that many trials come down to credibility and motivation, but this is a historic case, and I think that Smith was open and saying it's all about deceit, and we think that everything he said he knew was untrue.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, and the difference between official and unofficial acts is what the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  dealt with.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, thanks for hopping on tonight, digesting this quickly. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Also breaking tonight, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  says that Iran has gone way off board, but that Israel's response to yesterday's missile attack should be "In proportion." It comes amid speculation that Israel could target Iranian infrastructure like nuclear sites or possibly oil fields.\par \par \uc2 Israeli officials say the response will be painful, whatever it is with the anniversary of October 7th attacks less than one week away.\par \par \uc2 Senior correspondent Mike Tobin is again in Haifa, Israel tonight with the latest. Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. The air campaign into Lebanon is still very active, with Israel claiming another air strike within the last hour.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): For the first time in this conflict, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  fighters directly engaged Israeli forces. An ambush involving anti-tank missiles in South Lebanon killed eight Israeli soldiers.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER (through translator): I would like to send my deepest condolences to the families of our heroes who fell today in Lebanon. Make God avenge their death.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): This just one day after the unprecedented missile barrage Iran fired into Israel. Israeli air defenses intercepted many, guided missile destroyers, USS Cole and USS Bulkley fired 12 interceptors at the incoming barrage. Some Iranian missiles got through, hit civilian structures as well as air bases. Israel says its Air Force's capability is unaffected.\par \par \uc2 At today's emergency \uc2 U.N. Security Council\uc2  meeting with the Israeli ambassador watching, Iran's ambassador defended the attack.\par \par \uc2 AMIR SAEID IRAVANI, IRANIAN AMBASSADOR TO \uc2 U.N.\uc2 : Iran's response was a necessity to restore balance and deterrence. Israel must understand that each act of aggression it makes, it's not unpunished, and will be met with consequences.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): Weighing retaliation to the second direct attack from Iran. The Chiefs of Israel's defense establishment met in the Kirya, Israel's version of the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. HERZI HALEVI, \uc2 ISRAEL DEFENSE FORCES\uc2  (through translator): We have the capabilities to reach and strike any point in the Middle East, and those of our enemies who have not yet understood this will soon understand.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): Still cautious about escalation, U.S. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said he would not support an Israeli strike on Iranian nuclear facilities. Instead, he promised sanctions and called for a meeting of G7 leaders.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: All seven of us agree that they have a right to respond but they should respond in proportion.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (on camera): And with a fight still on multiple fronts, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has now claimed responsibility for those two Palestinian attackers who opened fire on civilians in Jaffa near Tel Aviv last night, they killed seven before the attackers were also killed, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mike Tobin live in Haifa. Mike, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in just a bit, we will hear from Fox News senior strategic analyst, retired General Jack Keane. He'll join us at the bottom of the hour.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, back in this country, President Biden viewed damage from Hurricane Helene in North Carolina today, as Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  met with emergency officials in Georgia. They will continue to assess the damage separately in other hard hit states later this week.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Steve Harrigan is in Maggie Valley, North Carolina again tonight with the latest. Good evening, Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. In cut off communities like this one, people go door to door to try to determine who's dead and who's alive.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This was a brick building, and it just pancakes that way.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Residents of western North Carolina survey what's left of their homes and businesses after one of the deadliest and costliest hurricanes to hit the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Search teams and dogs trudged through the mud looking for hundreds still unaccounted for as the death toll steadily rises from Helene.\par \par \uc2 In the close knit mountain communities, each loss is felt deeply, including the death of a longtime Food Lion cashier who died trying to save her mother.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN GLINES, NORTH CAROLINA RESIDENT: They unfortunately found her a few miles away from her house, swept away, still clutching her mother, and they were not recovered alive.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's all gone.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Many are counting their blessings, including one couple who narrowly avoided being swept away by a mudslide while driving through the Blue Ridge Mountains.\par \par \uc2 President Biden saw the damage today, flying over some of the hardest hit areas and meeting with first responders.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm here to say the United States, the nation has your back.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): The Pentagon says it will send in 1,000 active duty soldiers to deliver critical aid with so many still without running water, power and cell service.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We need so much help. We don't want to be forgotten.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Vice President Harris promised more help is on the way while visiting Georgia.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: The coordination that we have dedicated ourselves to will be long lasting, to get families, to get residents, to get neighborhoods back up and running.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (on camera): The death toll now stands at 182. People who live here say that number will go up, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Steve Harrigan live in North Carolina. Steve, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the vice presidential nominees return to the campaign trail. We'll take you there.\par \par \uc2 And then a live report from the \uc2 Port of Houston\uc2  amid the ongoing workers strike.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Both vice presidential nominees have received praise for demonstrating civility during last night's first and likely only debate. They largely focused attacks on the presidential nominees, rather than each other.\par \par \uc2 Today, both were being applauded for their performances by their party as they returned to the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Alexandria Hoff takes a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE (R-OH), VICE-PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: -- had debate last night. The vice-presidential debate. I thought it worked pretty well.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ, (D-MN), VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Not bad for a football coach. Not bad.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Senator J.D. Vance making stops in Michigan as Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  kicked off a bus tour in central Pennsylvania, both with the weight of a debate that was heavy on policy and light on personal attacks behind them.\par \par \uc2 On stage, it took Walz 45 minutes to accuse Trump and Vance of being tied to Project 2025. During last month's presidential debate, Harris mentioned it in the first eight minutes.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: It's good to see you.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Their Project 2025 is going to have a registry of pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice over): Trump's team has denied involvement with Project 2025, and \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 's plan does not call for a registry is going to have a registry of pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 States including Walz's Minnesota already provide anonymous abortion data to