er than the restriction on nuclear -- hitting nuclear targets, not to go robust, to go minimal. And why is that? It's the same thing they do with Zelenskyy, and it's the same thing they do with Netanyahu. They fear escalation.\par \par \uc2 For the American people, just think about, how do you win a war if you're going to fight a war and fear escalation? You can't.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: But that's where the administration is. They've got to be very frustrated. He'll hit back, I think, comprehensively and decisively. He's got, obviously, conventional targets to go after, the rocket missile force, the IRGC bases, maybe finally take out the IRGC Navy that is helping the Houthis, shut down the \uc2 Suez Canal\uc2 . That's certainly their economic targets, oil, et cetera, nuclear targets. Those, I think are less likely, but he's going to send a message to the Iranians.\par \par \uc2 They're already on their heels. And that's a fact. \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  are frustrated with them because they're not getting their support from them. And I think there's opportunity here to create civil unrest in Iran as long as Israel stays right on the course and stays tough dealing with them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I mean, General, you think about the last couple of weeks here, and we just saw that chart of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  command leadership being taken out, first by all of those pagers that exploded, then the -- the radios, and then the precision strikes, taking out specific leaders, including Nasrallah, before that, the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leaders -- leader killed in -- in Tehran, another long planned attack. The -- the question is, will the big target be, you know, these nuclear sites, or could it be oil fields, you know, or do you think it will be more targeted to people?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: I think it would be more targeted towards conventional military what I suggested the rock and missile forces, IRGC bases. They've been very successful usi