NER, "THE FAULKNER FOCUS" ANCHOR: You know, this is an issue that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  now gets to tee off on again and do it in a new way. You were reading from \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and Truth Social. One of the things that caught my eye is when he says, the release of this falsehood ridden unconstitutional J6 brief immediately following \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 's disastrous debate performance, and 33 days before the most important election in the history of our country.\par \par \uc2 Look, no one is lost on the fact that last night was not a win for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' team. I don't know how much a vice presidential debate can hurt her, but all eyes on that campaign. Then she goes and -- and looks down at least 11, 12 times at her cards in Georgia today, while \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is in North Carolina. The storm, the hurricane, happened Thursday of last week. They're just getting there.\par \par \uc2 They're not winning on issues that matter to the American people right now. That port strike, they're not winning on that. They could have used the Taft-Hartley Act and stopped it for an 80-day cooling. They didn't do that as a team, Biden-Harris. So this changes the game. Now, look, Jack Smith, his timing was handed to him. He got to go first. You said that. So he's going to lay this out. I don't know, Bret, I tend to think that people look at this as an opportunity for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to say they're not winning. They want your focus on something that isn't going to pay your bills.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, for all, Jessica, of the uproar about \uc2 James Comey\uc2  coming out before the election with \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and putting out there his assessment, even though they were not going to trial before the election, and all the hand wringing about that, for this 34 days when they know there's not going to be a trial before the election in this case, to come out in this format is kind of interesting.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Well, there are vastly different cases.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: So the Comey letter, which was even closer, was 11 days before the election was him --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Twenty three days difference. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I'm sick.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It is different when every day and all of this counts. And if this is an October surprise, it is very early in October that this happened. But the Comey letter was really for his own edification. I mean, there was no - - they weren't going to continue with the investigation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But isn't this Jack Smith edification, really?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No. It's no. And frankly, I mean, this proves that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was wrong to want the Supreme Court to take up the immunity aspect of this, because that's what pushed this timeline back. Jack Smith was very clear that he was going to proceed. He has exactly the same charges in this. He slimmed it down, but he was able to convince a grand jury that he should move ahead with all of these counts.\par \par \uc2 And they have -- they have some smart lawyers that are working for them who might have been able to mention to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that this just means that this case is going to come up closer to the election and --\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE JONES, FOX NEWS ANALYST: But not the trial.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: But that doesn't matter the American people --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It does. It really does, though.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No, it really doesn't. And people aren't buying, en masse. They are not buying this lawfare argument --\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: -- that he is a big victim and that he didn't do anything wrong. There is evidence in this of clear cut crimes and new evidence --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But what's not in this, Jessica, is a defense. There's not a defense. There's not anything that says this was an official act. It wasn't. I understand what you're saying about painting the picture, but the timing, Lawrence, is the question.\par \par \uc2 JONES: There's -- there's -- there's no coincidences when it comes to the timing. They wanted this to happen during this period of time. And it just goes back to, I mean, it -- it's actually wrong to say that it doesn't benefit the -- the president. Every time they weaponize the Department of Justice or the legal system against him, he shoots up in the polls.\par \par \uc2 So there is a rejection from the American people at large. But I think this, anybody that knows Jack Smith and his record, he doesn't really win legal cases. He tries to win them in the public eye, and then he gets overturned. And so I think there used to be a standard between the Department of General Department of Justice to shun the appearance, at least appear, to be fair, that seems to have changed lately.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Harris, let's go back to the debate last night, quickly. The headlines, Vance Confidence, Walz uneven and VP debate, we saw a lot of these articles. How did you take it?\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Well, I wasn't sure who the bios that were read early on in some of the reporting days before, who had the 18 years of -- of debate and leadership experience. I mean, I know they said it was \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , but it looked like it might have been J.D. Vance and his coolness. There were some things that J.D. Vance had that were advantages. For one, he is cool as a cucumber, cooler than the other side of the pillow, as Stuart Smith used to say, Stuart Scott rather.\par \par \uc2 But with -- with Walz, there was just this sort of rehearsed talking point. He wasn't nimble. He was not nimble. And I think he thought that maybe some of the work would be done for him by those moderators. He was wrong about that. They were fact checking his opponent more than they were him. But this really was important. I think it matters more than usually VP debates do.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you agree, Jessica?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I don't agree with the assessment.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: No, no, but that it matters.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Potentially, I mean the -- the Harris-Walz ticket is the one that has room to grow in terms of people actually wanting to know more about them. Undecided voters have been consistent. They know \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and they're sick of it, and that's that. But \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  had a very good night, if you look at the focus groups afterwards in Michigan and in Pennsylvania, his favorability went up from 14 to 37. And J.D. Vance has now gotten himself to a negative three.\par \par \uc2 JONES: The -- the reporting was --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: He won Indies by six.\par \par \uc2 JONES: The reporting was that he was nervous. And I thought that they were just trying to set expectations low, low. He was clearly nervous yesterday. You had a J.D. Vince that really prosecuted the case of what a MAGA movement is. Maybe \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  always goes into the pictures and all that. Well, you had J.D. Vince that colored within the lines.\par \par \uc2 And I think you see a worthy successor for the movement last night. He was likable, relatable, complimentary of the governor's will and this whole notion that J.D. Vance is more experienced. He's in politics for two years. OK. You got a sitting governor two terms that was in the Congress for over a decade.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: But just why he could talk about a record.\par \par \uc2 JONES: And he was not prepared.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: There were two items that I -- I thought that the left is really kind of owned the right on, abortion. And I -- I thought for sure that that would be an area where he would slide. I thought that J.D. Vance did a good job of being transparent that there needs to be growth within the GOP party, among women and family and so on and so forth in that report.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's great to have you guys.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Thanks for having us.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Thanks Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Fair, balanced and unafraid. Laura is up next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020241003eka200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Now, J.D. Vance In Battleground Michigan; Thousands Of Dockworkers Strike For Second Day; Israel Says, Retaliation Against Iran Will Be Painful; Walz And Vance Spar Over Abortion At VP Debate; 170 Dead, Hundreds Still Missing After Helene; Devastated North Carolina Communities In Need Of Food And Water; Breast Cancer Cases Rise As Deaths Decrease\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Jeff Flock, Larry Kudlow, Trey Yingst, Tomi  Lahren, Steve Harrigan, Gerri Willis \par \uc2 9,204 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE (R-OH), VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: It's because \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and her allies are trying to silence the American people rather than engage with them.\par \par \uc2 When you try to censor your fellow citizens, when you try to shut them up, you breed division and hatred. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and I are going to bring this country back together by encouraging the free exchange of ideas and this idea that our leaders ought to persuade their fellow citizens, not try to shut them up, which is what \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  wants to do. Open this country up, open up the dialogue and open up the debate. And that is how you bring American citizens back together in these divided times.\par \par \uc2 But on the second question about what do you do when you disagree with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? The answer is, first of all, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is at the top of the Republican ticket. He will be the next president of the United States. I've got two responsibilities. I've got two responsibilities. The first is to support the president in enacting an agenda that I believe is going to lead to rising take home pay and lower inflation for American citizens. That's number one. But, of course, if I disagree with the president, it is my responsibility to talk to him about it and to offer honest counsel in private.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things that I love about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is that he takes advice and feedback from everybody. As much as -- you know, it's funny when the president -- the only conversation that he and I ever had about whether I might become his vice presidential nominee was the Saturday before the convention. If you remember, that was the Saturday where he was shot in Bucks County, Pennsylvania. I saw him that morning in Florida. We ended our conversation. He hopped in a plane and flew to Pennsylvania where, of course, an assassin nearly took his life.\par \par \uc2 And you know what the president told me? He said that he talked to everybody about who his vice presidential nominee should be. And I think that's one of the great things is he's willing to take feedback. Of course, he makes the final decision, but he's willing to take inputs from everybody. And he even said that he was talking to the Mar-a-Lago gardener about who the vice presidential nominee should be. And I said, well, sir, what the hell did the Mar-a-Lago gardener say, because this affects my life? I'm very curious what these people you're talking to are saying about this very important decision.\par \par \uc2 But I think that it's one of the great things about his leadership style is he is in charge, he is in command, but he makes decisions after getting as much information as possible and that's how the country should be led.\par \par \uc2 You know, it actually raises an important question, you know, Robert, and I know, of course, the journalist who's asking this question, look, one of the things that people in D.C. say about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , and it's Democrats that say this, it's people she's served with in the Senate, and it's also Republicans, and, of course, they say this in private, but they'll say \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is very insecure. She doesn't like to be challenged. If you don't like to be challenged and you don't like to engage in open debate, you should be nowhere near the Oval Office of the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 Next question.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Thank you, Charlie Langton from Fox News, WBJ here.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Hi, Charlie.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: I talked to a lot of people here. They also said you were very cordial last night in the debate. And the message got through. But compare your style to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's style, where he was a little aggressive. Do you think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's message sometimes is lost because of his aggressive style?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: No, Charlie. Look, I think, first of all, there is a media caricature out there of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that is so different from the man that I've come to know. And I think that, you know, when he called me last night, you know what he said? He asked me how my wife was doing. And he said, you know, you just had a hell of a debate, I'm sure that you were nervous. Yes, you had every right to be nervous, but go and have a nice meal with your wife and go and enjoy this moment. Like that's the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that I know.\par \par \uc2 People say -- now, look, the other thing I love about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and I think it's true of all of us, we can be so devoted to and so grateful to this country and its incredible opportunities, but then also be kind of pissed off that the leadership is failing this great nation. There's no contract -- there's no tension in saying on the one hand, we've got the greatest country in the world, and on the other hand, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is doing everything she can to screw it up.\par \par \uc2 And I love that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can do both of those things at the same time. He can go out there and talk to the American people and be warm and engaged. He can talk to a normal American citizen about their struggles in paying for groceries and housing under \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' policies.\par \par \uc2 And then he can go on a debate stage and tell \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  that she ought to stop screwing up and she ought to stop talking about solving the country's problems, she ought to go and actually do it. That's the kind of president we need. That's the kind of leader that we need. And I think his message gets through. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Next question.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Hey, Senator, Craig Mauger from the Detroit News newspaper here. You were talking about your opposition to E.V. mandates. My question is, do you think the government should have any role in incentivizing, providing financial assistance to battery plants, to electric vehicle plants? Michigan is poised to benefit from some of these investments. Will you allow those to go forward? Will you try to interfere with those? Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, look I think there's certainly a role, and I believe this, I know \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  believes this, in encouraging innovation, but there's a difference between promoting innovation and sending hundreds of billions of dollars to favored industries that make their products in China. These are two very, very different things.\par \par \uc2 And here's -- the problem with a lot of these E.V. mandates is that this money was supposed to go to American workers, but \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' \uc2 IRS\uc2  found every way possible to allow that E.V. subsidy to go to manufacturers in China and not in the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 And here's the other problem with it. It's not just auto jobs that we're worried about here. Think about all the people who make carburetors. Think about all the people who make transmissions. There's an entire supply chain in the auto industry that China dominates when it comes to E.V.s and America dominates when it comes to gas powered cars.\par \par \uc2 So, before we go and subsidize the E.V. industry, let's open up the American supply chain for that industry so that American workers and American consumers benefit. All of the minerals, all of the rare earth materials, a lot of the battery technology, a lot of the supply chain for these electric vehicles is made in China.\par \par \uc2 And with the one hand, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  wants to make it harder to build that supply chain in the United States of America. And with the other hand, she wants to send money to an industry that's built in China. It's disgraceful and we got to stop doing it. Invest in American workers. Not in Chinese workers and Chinese firms.\par \par \uc2 And I know we unfortunately have to go to our next stop in the great state of Michigan. So, I'll take one more question and then I'll offer a final observation and then we'll hit the road.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Hi, Senator, Will Jones from Local 4.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Hey, Will.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: I spoke with some elected officials or election officials last week about their concern about people who are trying to undermine our elections. Why didn't you answer the question last night during the debate about who won the 2020 presidential election?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, look, here's the simple reason. The media is obsessed with talking about the election of four years ago. I'm focused on the election of 33 days from now because I want to throw \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  out of office and get back to common sense economic policies.\par \par \uc2 But I also think you can believe that America needs to have secure and free elections, but also talk about the fact that just a couple of weeks ago, Democrats in the \uc2 U.S. Congress\uc2  blocked a piece of legislation that would have ensured illegal aliens don't vote in our elections. If you believe in American democracy, if you believe in our constitutional republic, you should be trying to strengthen American election integrity and not weaken it.\par \par \uc2 So, we're going to talk about election integrity because I believe that every vote ought to count, but only the legally cast votes. And that's why we fight for election integrity. And that's why we care so much about it.\par \par \uc2 Now --\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Very important place for the next 33 days, that's for sure, and that is Auburn Hills, Michigan. And you're watching the vice presidential GOP candidate speaking. J.D. Vance had a very good night last night on the debate stage and folks around the Trump team are feeling good about their pick in J.D. Vance and how he performed last night, certainly.\par \par \uc2 So, there is a lot going on in the country right now that whoever is president for the next four years is going to have to deal with.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, President Biden has to deal with this situation. Thousands of dock workers are now on strike. It is day two. They're demanding very large pay hikes, and they want to ban on automation, as a battle between union workers and companies threatens to drive up prices for consumers who are going to end up being stuck in the middle.\par \par \uc2 This is the head of the union. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAROLD DAGGETT, PRESIDENT, ILA: People never gave a (BLEEP) about us until now, when they finally realized that the chain is being broke now. Cars won't come in, food won't come in, clothing won't come in. You know how many people depend on our jobs? Half the world. And it's time for them, and time for Washington to put so much pressure on them to take care of us, because we took care of them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Okay, that is the message. \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  is standing by. First, let's go quickly to Fox Business correspondent Jeff Flock. He's at the Port of Newark in New Jersey, where it's a quite an active scene. Hi, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF FLOCK, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Martha, hello to you. If you thought that was strident comments from the president of the International Longshoremen's Union. Well, it's backed up by what you see here behind me. I'll tell you, I've been through a lot of strikes in my time. These guys don't do it very often. The last one was 1977 when \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  was in a junior high, I think.\par \par \uc2 But I'll tell you when they do it, they seem to do it right and they do it loudly. And they are getting support in a lot of places, high places, like from the president and his administration, as well as, you know, surprisingly, to me, the truckers. Take a look at some pictures we have from earlier today. The truckers, you know, stand to be out of a job once the last of this freight gets delivered. If there's nothing coming in, they don't take anything anywhere. They don't make any money, but they had a big caravan out here today at the port of Newark.\par \par \uc2 And speaking of stop on the way, I leave you with a map that shows you how many ships are now making their way toward the ports that are now shut down by these folks, 120,000 -- 129 ships with thousands of containers aboard. I don't know what they're going to do. Some of them are already anchored offshore.\par \par \uc2 I leave the last word perhaps to these folks, as you mentioned, no automation. That's one of the key factors in this strike as well. In addition to the money, it sounds like a lot of money, but these guys say, you know, they work through the pandemic and the companies made hundreds of billions with a B dollars in profits, and they'd like to see some of that money,\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Jeff. Thank you. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So, joining me now, as promised, \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2 , anchor of Kudlow on \uc2 Fox Business Network\uc2 , which is coming up shortly. His upcoming interview with former President Trump is going to air this Friday on Kudlow. So, you don't want to miss that. Larry, it is great to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 LARRY KUDLOW\uc2 , FOX BUSINESS ANCHOR: Thank you, Martha. Make great pleasure. Just one little addition, 1977, I was actually the chief economist of a very large brokerage firm in New York City.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Indeed you are. And you're watching all this play out.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: I was not in junior high. You were not in junior high.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He was just trying to make you very youthful at that time. But so, so I want to play this. This is another sound bite from Harold Daggett, who is the \uc2 International Longshoremen's Association\uc2  president. And here's what he's talking about in terms of what their demands are. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAGGETT: First week, be all over the news every night, boom, boom. Second week, guys who sell cars can't sell cars because the cars ain't coming in off the ships. They get laid off. Third week, malls start closing down. They can't get the goods from China.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What are we headed for here, Larry?\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Well, look, I want to say, I mean, Wall Street and I see editorials and newspapers, everyone's beating up on the labor union here. I happen to think that this 77 percent demand is too high. Over six years, that would be close to 13 percent increase in wages every year over six years. I happen to think that's too high. But they've been clobbered by the inflation of the past three and a half-plus years. As you know, cost of living up 20 percent, and when you get through individual groceries and insurance and other things could be 30, 40 percent. They've been clobbered by that.\par \par \uc2 And the company who runs all this, USMX, they have had $400 billion in profits in the last three years. Now, I love profits. Don't get me wrong. I'm a free market capitalist. I'm just saying that the union has a point here, an important point. We got clobbered on inflation. There's been an affordability crisis for years. We haven't had a raise. The company's making good money, okay. Good money is good money. Again, I love profits, but they have a point.\par \par \uc2 I think 77 percent is too high.\par \par \uc2 The offer from the company was 50 percent. That would be 8 percent a year. The thing is no one knows who's going to win this election right now. No one knows what the future will be for inflation and the cost of living and groceries and mortgage rates and car loan rates and insurance rates. No one knows. And you're seeing a bunch of unions around the country that strikes a lot of places.\par \par \uc2 Now, to some extent, I think Mr. Biden is to blame here. He's encouraging that. But I don't think that's as important as the fact that inflation is a huge tax and it's robbed them of kitchen table necessities, and you're seeing them fight back. The rhetoric coming out of the leadership, I know, but the rank and file have a right to come at this. They probably deserve a pretty good raise.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I was disappointed that there was no question on this last night because I think it's a question that's very reflective of how a candidate feels because they are stuck between the consumer and the union. So, I think it would have been really interesting to hear them speak on this.\par \par \uc2 And I know you have to go, but just very quickly, this issue of automation, Larry, they're fighting back against automation. Other international ports are highly automated.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Yes, they are.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, you know, how do you -- this isn't the last time we're going to hear about this kind of question.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Now, there's a whole bunch of issues here. I'm not sure on how far -- there's going to be additional automation. You are right. Europe is much more automated. I believe Asia is too. The question is how fast and by what means and by what compensation, right? Because if you put people out of work and they've been there for 20, 30, 40 years, you got to pay them.\par \par \uc2 I'm not so -- look, the last time we had a dock strike -- I know I'm supposed to rap, but I'm going to say this. The last time we had a dock strike was not 1977. It was 2002. \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  was president, okay? It lasted 11 days, because more than that, you know, if you get into a week, two weeks, three weeks, four weeks, you're going to chop off GDP, you're going to really wreck the economy. The economy is not that sturdy in the first place and they'll take some actions.\par \par \uc2 But, nonetheless, vegetables are rotting. Equipment can't get in. We could lose a half a point of GDP depending on how far it goes. But I just want to say again, they have a point. Inflation has robbed them of a lot in the last three and a half years. They have a point.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'm glad you're here, Larry.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: I'm supposed to take the Wall Street position and beat them up? No, I'm not.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No. That's why I like talking to you, because I never know exactly where it's going to come down and I appreciate your opinion a lot. So, thank you for being here. And Larry's shows coming up on Fox Business at 4:00 four.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: I have to run up and get there,\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Run up and get there. Larry, thank you very much for stopping by. Good to see you, really good to see you.\par \par \uc2 So, President Biden chose the line after vowing an iron clad backing of Israel and issuing a veiled threat of don't to Iran.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Would you support an attack on Iran's nuclear sites by Israel?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , U.S. PRESIDENT: The answer is no. And I think there's things -- we'll be discussing with the Israelis what they're going to do, but then all seven of us agree that they have a right to respond, that they should respond in proportion.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We'll talk about that with retired Four \uc2 Star Charge\uc2  General \uc2 Frank McKenzie\uc2 , former commander of U.S. forces in the Middle East, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Back to the war in the Middle East, brand new video has been released of U.S. missile destroyers thwarting Iran's massive attack on Israel.\par \par \uc2 Immense power there. That's the USS Bulkeley. The world is waiting for what comes next in this high pressure conflict. Israel says that they are planning a painful response to the barrage of missiles that we saw, over 200 ballistic missiles, into Israel yesterday, President Biden is urging once again, a proportional response.\par \par \uc2 Retired Four Star General \uc2 Frank McKenzie\uc2 , former commander of U.S. Forces in the Middle East, joins me in just a moment. First to Chief Foreign Correspondent Trey Yingst, live in Haifa, Israel, with an exclusive first- time look inside Israel's military operations in Lebanon. Hi, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Martha, good afternoon. As Israeli forces started their ground operation into southern Lebanon, we were with a commando unit that approached a \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  position. Here's what we saw during that embed.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Now, Israeli forces are conducting a ground invasion into Southern Lebanon. We're embedded with an elite commando unit that just arrived at this \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  position carved into the side of a mountain. It's in tunnels and rooms like this the Israelis say they are finding Iranian weapons that were smuggled first to Syria and then into Southern Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 To get to this \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  position, we had to cross minefields and hike across an uneven terrain, pocked with craters, a result of the Israeli bombardment of Southern Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 All of this began on October 8th, when \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  followed \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and started attacking Northern Israel in the hours after the October 7th massacre. Now, Israel says they are conducting these limited raids across the border to destroy these \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  positions and allow Israeli civilians to return to their homes in the northern part of the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Just to give you an idea of how sensitive this military embed was, we are waiting to air our full report from Southern Lebanon until next hour when those forces are out of this area. We know the Israeli ground and air campaign against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  continues. Hundreds of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  fighters have been killed in the process along with hundreds of civilians. And tonight, we can report that eight Israeli soldiers were killed in battles against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  earlier today. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's a very hot war situation. Thank you very much, Trey Yingst, for joining us and for that extraordinary reporting from inside the tunnels in Southern Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in retired Four Star General \uc2 Frank McKenzie\uc2 , the former commander of U.S. Forces in the Middle East, now executive director of the \uc2 University of South Florida\uc2 's Global and National Security Institute. And he is the author of The Melting Point. General McKenzie, great to have you with us. Thank you so much for joining me today.\par \par \uc2 I wonder what goes through your mind as you listen Trey's report and you watch the events of the past couple of days where \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  is under tremendous pressure, Iran is also under tremendous pressure, having lost major leadership, and now we are at one of those perhaps tipping points in the Middle East. What is your take?\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 FRANK MCKENZIE\uc2  (RET.), FORMER CENTCOM COMMANDER: Well, Martha, I think actually the people that are under the most stress are the Iranians. They've watched their principal ally, \uc2 Lebanese Hezbollah\uc2 , be decapitated. They've watched significant attrition of their military capabilities. And now the Israelis are methodically clearing north of the border so that their Israeli citizens can return to the northern tier of kibbutzes cities and villages in Northern Israel.\par \par \uc2 All of this is bad news for Iran. Last night, they launched a major -- a second major ballistic missile attack on Israel, by any objective measure, that attack too failed. So now Iran has really delivered a maximum effort against Israel. They can't count on \uc2 Lebanese Hezbollah\uc2  to assist them in further attacks because of the decapitation and the ongoing Israeli military operations up there.\par \par \uc2 So, the people whose backs are really against the wall right now waiting for the Israeli response is Iranian senior leadership. They're not in a good place right now.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Two questions on that. The first one is about \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and what has happened to them. And were you surprised that they were able to launch over 200 missiles? And I think we're still learning about potential casualties in Israel but an extraordinary situation where - - I mean, isn't it humiliating to them that none of these missiles or the majority of them were not able to penetrate or to do any damage, or significant damage?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: I think it's very humiliating to them. The crown jewels of Iran actually are its ballistic missiles, its drones and its land attack cruise missiles. Now, in the attack of last night, they chose not to use drones or land attack cruise missiles. My theory would be in order to gain surprise against Israel because of the 12 to 14-minute time of flight. However, we're able to discern when the Iranians begin to deploy their missiles. We know ahead of time it's very difficult for Iran to gain surprise when they bring their missile force into the field.\par \par \uc2 So we were able to respond. The Israelis did a very good job. We certainly assisted them. And I agree with you. I think that attack was largely ineffective, and it's got to be profoundly embarrassing to the Iranians.\par \par \uc2 But one last point I'd make, Martha. The Iranians have a boundless ability to lie to themselves within their own chain of command about how successful or unsuccessful their attacks are. So, you don't know -- what we would want to know is how is that attack actually being reported to the supreme leader in Tehran? We might be surprised at what he's learning about it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, well, he's got to be a little nervous about his own future at this point because he watched the leader of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  be taken out in Tehran in a building there when he was at a ceremony and then Nasrallah, and he has to be wondering if his days are numbered as well.\par \par \uc2 With regard to what President Biden said about discouraging an Israeli attack on nuclear sites within Iran, I mean, that is the ultimate threat that faces Israel. And do they see this as a window that cannot be missed to mitigate that threat and to try to take out those sites, General?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: Well, Martha, I probably know as much about the Iranian nuclear program and the Iranian nuclear target as anybody in the world. That's a very large order to take out that program. If the Israelis wanted to do it, it would require vast expenditure of resources. It wouldn't be able to be done very quickly. It would be a long, hard slog. Can they do it? Possibly.\par \par \uc2 I would think actually, just speaking as a military observer, there are other more significant targets that you might want to undertake 1st, as you calibrate your response to an attack against Israel that caused very few casualties and failed, and actually had the effect, as I've noted earlier, of pushing Iran further and further against the wall and into a very lonely corner.\par \par \uc2 So, the nuclear program is certainly a target. There are other targets they could undertake. They might even choose to use restraint, although I would note that the restraint they used after the 13 April attack on Israel by Iran, Israelis fought back a couple of nights later with a very carefully sculpted, technologically impressive small attack that didn't prevent the Iranians from coming back with the big attack of yesterday.\par \par \uc2 So, the Israelis have probably learned that lesson. So, I suspect the Israeli response will be larger, probably much larger than what we saw in their response in April as to who is going to go against all of the initiative, and I mean, this absolutely all of the initiative is with Israel right now.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: General McKenzie, you're a wealth of information and have spent so much time in the region, and we're going to have you on speed dial, I think, over the next several weeks. And we so appreciate you spending time with us, sir. Thank you very much, general.\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So Harris and Walz ahead on the issue of abortion, but they seem to be peddling some fear in some cases when it comes to where the Trump- Vance ticket actually stands. There's a lot of information that gets thrown out there and we're going to do some fact checking on it, digging in with Tomi Lahren and Patrick Murphy next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID AXELROD, CNN SENIOR POLITICAL COMMENTATOR: You talk about his moderate position, his moderate position has been adopted over the course of this campaign, for purposes of this campaign, after he put the justices on the Supreme Court who overturned Roe, who have created a crisis for women who live in 20 states in this country.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT JENNINGS, CNN CONTRIBUTOR: His position is moderate, and it is in line with what I think most America.\par \par \uc2 AXELROD: Do you believe him when he said -- when he said that women should be --\par \par \uc2 JENNINGS: And I think Harris and Walz have proven they cannot discuss even a single restriction that they want.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Now, of course, they have said time and time and time again they want a national ban on abortion. So as this comes down to the final days, let's be very clear where we're headed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. We're going to break this down because they have not said that they want a national ban. Former President Trump debunked that claim during last night's vice presidential debate, saying that not only does he not support a national ban on abortion, he has not ever supported that. But said that he would veto a federal abortion ban if it came to his desk. He said, under any circumstances.\par \par \uc2 So here is how the hot button issue played out last night on both sides. Let's watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TIM WALZ (D), MINNESOTA GOVERNOR AND VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: Their Project 2025 is going to have a registry of pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O'DONNELL, \uc2 CBS\uc2  NEWS ANCHOR: Will you create a federal pregnancy monitoring agency?\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH), VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: No, Norah. Certainly we won't. And I'll statute that you signed into law. It says that a doctor, who presides over an abortion where the baby survives, the doctor is under no obligation to provide life saving care to a baby who survives a botched late term abortion. that is, I think, whether it's not pro-choice or pro- abortion.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. That was an interesting moment there, because there's not a lot of discussion about restriction or where a restriction should be in some of these states, including Minnesota. So here are the facts. Minnesota's abortion law does not limit the abortion procedure at any stage, all the way up to nine months. It does not limit it in terms of accessibility. And as for Project 2025, there is no mention of a pregnancy registry.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump has disavowed \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 's 900 plus page document. He says he has not read it. And there is a Republican platform that is quite clear on the issue of abortion. Democrats have their platform, which is also quite clear on this issue.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Tomi Lahren, host of Tomi Lahren is fearless on OutKick and Fox News Contributor and Patrick Murphy, former Pennsylvania congressman and the first Iraq veteran in Congress. Great to have both of you with us.\par \par \uc2 Patrick, why does \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  keep saying that they want a national ban? We heard the same thing from \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , and President Trump has not supported a national ban. He supported the Supreme Court to make a decision, and they made that decision, and he was supportive of that.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MURPHY (D), FORMER PENNSYLVANIA CONGRESSMAN: I think, Martha, because I think people are frustrated because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , in this case, says one thing and does another. I mean, he's the one that said publicly on air that women should be punished if they have an abortion. He's the one that said, hey, I'm going to nominate the Supreme Court justices who testified under oath that the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 , their confirmation hearing saying, hey, Roe v Wade has set a law and then, within years, overturned Roe v Wade in a Dobbs decision.\par \par \uc2 And these are tough decisions. You know, I'm Catholic, Martha, but, you know, I think that's what people are frustrated about, when people say one thing and do another. And as far as disavowing that, prior to 2025, I mean, JD Vance wrote the forward to it as, you know, and, you know, you just can act like, you know, you're an ostrich and buried your head in the sand and act like you don't know what's going on. That's the blueprint.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, you know, what's quite clear, Tomi, is that the Supreme Court, of course, he picked conservative judges because he's a conservative. Democrat president picked judges that they think will uphold their view of the Supreme Court, and we've seen how it plays out. All the states make their own decisions.\par \par \uc2 And in fact, JD Vance pointed out last night, he's also Catholic and he said, you know, I live in Ohio but I -- and -- but Ohio did not go the way I wanted it to go, he said, in a referendum vote. It was a more broad interpretation of the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 But, Tomi, Democrats can't -- they can't let this issue slip away, right? I mean, they have to make sure that people fear this kind of national ban is a huge part of what they are running on.\par \par \uc2 TOMI LAHREN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: And some would argue, including myself, that it's one of the only things that they are able to run on because of the list of things that are important to voters, this is the only one where they have a substantial lead. But I think that Senator JD Vance did an excellent job last night of calming fears, because there were many swing voters that were watching that, particularly women who would like to vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for economic purposes, for immigration purposes, but they were a little weary of the abortion question and issue.\par \par \uc2 But JD Vance effectively disarmed all of those arguments by making it explicitly clear that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would not sign a national abortion ban. He made that very clear. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  followed that up by making it clear on social media. So now the left, of course, is going to say that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would sign it, because that's all that they have in the toolbox.\par \par \uc2 But I think it was very effective for JD Vance to acknowledge that the Republican Party has had an issue messaging on this particular topic, and he understands that frustration. But he called fears last night and he did it in a compassionate way. A lot of Republicans have been unable to do that. He did it wonderfully.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. He said, you know, that Republicans have to be better at talking about this issue. Patrick, so you, I mean, we're clear on the fact that this decision, put it back to the states to decide, and they have been doing that. And they will continue to do that, the people closest to the issue in their own states.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: which is a frustrating thing, Martha, because he says, I'm not for a national band. But yet, more than one in three American women right now don't have reproductive choice anymore. That is a ban.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Governor Walz was so eloquent last night when he talked about Amanda (ph) and Nicole (ph) and Amber (ph). Amanda (ph) and Nicole (ph) almost died. Amber literally died because of this. I mean, this is why people are fired up.\par \par \uc2 And JD Vance -- and God bless him, he talks about we have to earn their trust. Well, they stop taking away their rights. As you know, under our system of government, we have federalism, meaning you have minimal rights under the Constitution and the Bill of Rights, minimal rights. States can give you more rights if they want. He is chipping away and chipping away at that, and that's why people --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But every time we've seen referendums, Patrick.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: -- are frustrated with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We've seen referendums in states, and they have -- majority of them have gone towards preserving abortion rights in states. So, Tomi, when people have the right to decide in their states, they are because of the issues that you raised and the way a lot of women feel in this country, they are preserving those rights.\par \par \uc2 LAHREN: Right. Empower the voters to make those decisions. And you might not agree with it, some of these decisions are going to come down and conservatives are going to be angry, and some liberals are going to be angry.\par \par \uc2 But I'd also like to point out that a lot of states have restrictions on things, legislation, regulations that some folks might not like. I had to leave the state of California because I didn't like the way voters in that state decided on a number of issues, so I left and I came to Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 So the voters ultimately have to decide. Not everybody is going to be happy, but that is up for the voters to decide. And if national opinion really is trending towards a more pro-choice attitude, then the states will reflect that. Let it play out and, again, stop fear mongering on a national abortion ban, because that's simply not the case and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and JD Vance could not have been clearer last night on that issue.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, one of the things, Patrick, that we never get when we're asked -- when the candidates are asked this is, what restriction would you approve of as a Democrat? You know is, is it -- is nine months your, you know, the line that you think should be upheld, or is there any restriction at all that Democrats --\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Yes, and that's why I should codify it under Roe v Wade, under federal law, which means that women have a right to choose their reproductive freedoms when -- until the baby is viable, right? Until the fetus is viable, right? So you have that right now.\par \par \uc2 They took that away. They gave it to the states. And you have states like Alabama, as you know, banned IVF, right? So that's why I think people are confused. And they're confused because they're like, wait time out. I thought the Republican Party used to be a limited government, less government type of organization. You can't pick and choose what part of the Constitution --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No, it's a state's issue, that's why. It's a state's issue. I mean, that's the court decided.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Women have a right to choose in America.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right. And they can vote in their state the way that they feel and over -- upholding the vote, upholding those rules in their states.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: And that's why they don't trust \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Martha. That's why they don't trust --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: (Inaudible) an opportunity to vote. I just want everybody on both sides to understand the parameters of what we're discussing in a clearly presented way. So Patrick Murphy, Tomi Lahren, thank you both. Good to have you both here today.\par \par \uc2 So President Biden and Vice President Harris deploying to the southeast after being pressed on by some on a lack of leadership as they saw it, in response to the devastating Hurricane Helene, which is another issue that we really heard almost nothing about last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And the hurricane, Mr. President, why weren't you and Vice President Harris here in Washington commanding this, this weekend?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: I was commanding. I was on the phone for at least two hours yesterday and the day before as well. I command on a telephone, called my security --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Is it not important for the country to see?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Republican Congressman Cory Mills, who is on a rescue mission in North Carolina, there are still hundreds of people, by most estimates, who are missing in this situation. It's a terrifying situation unfolding on our soil, and he says that FEMA, so far, is nowhere to be found. We'll talk to him next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: President Biden en route to North Carolina right now after getting a bird's eye view of the destruction that has been left by Hurricane Helene. Soon, Vice President Harris will also be there to survey the damage in Georgia. The death toll is now topping 170 people as the long journey to recover those who are still missing, which is said to be potentially in the hundreds, and then to rebuild. Just beginning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We have people on oxygen that are completely out now. We have babies up there with no formula, nothing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is like a Katrina on steroids.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We've lost friends. There's been landslides. The day that happened, it was so bad. Our children thought we were dead. And we had to hang on to boulders, trees and go through Roy Creek to get out but we're alive.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We tried to find our friends, with (inaudible). We don't know --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: -- if they're dead or alive.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I just absolutely heartbreaking. I mean, these people are going through the most horrific moments in their lives, and thank God those two people survived, hanging onto boulders in a mudslide. In moments, I'll speak with Florida Congressman Cory Mills. He is once again on the front lines of this disaster. He does this all the time when needed. First to senior correspondent Steve Harrigan in Maggie Valley, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 What a heartbreaking story we just heard, Steve. And I know there are many, many more of them out there.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: All across Western North Carolina, Martha, and because many communities like this one are still cut off one weekend, that death toll stands at 179. But people here say it's going to keep going up. That's because the only way to find out if someone is dead or alive here, someone has to go by foot door to door.\par \par \uc2 They use walkie-talkies here to communicate, hand radios to get news, and everything is paid in cash. And when you talk to people and say, what help have you gotten in the past week from any outside agency, most often, the answer is nothing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We're not getting the amount of support that we need right now. There are way more people impacted, way more people missing, definitely -- unfortunately, way more people that are going to be found dead in the coming days as far as I believe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: And drinking water is the big challenge. Many of the wells here have been contaminated, and they can't reopen the schools until there's running water that could be weeks away. Martha, back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Incredible. Steve, thank you very much for your coverage. Joining me now from Asheville, North Carolina is Florida Congressman Cory Mills. He's been on the ground there for days, helping to deliver much needed supplies to those hard hit areas and evacuate some who are stranded. As you remember, he helped rescue Americans from warzones in Afghanistan, also Israel and in Haiti.\par \par \uc2 Cory Mills, congressman, welcome. Thank you for what you do for people in these situations. Tell us what you're observing there and what we all need to understand.\par \par \uc2 REP. CORY MILLS (R-FL): (Inaudible) getting the necessary needs in here. That was that, you know, the people were wanting. And so, we've gone into - - we delivered now about five tons worth of supplies, about roughly 10,000. We've actually transported about 12,000 pounds between the two helicopters that we brought in (inaudible) which is only places you can get in is by rotary-wing helicopter.\par \par \uc2 So we've gone into Broad River, South Toe. We've gone into Banner Elk, Asheville, Burnsville. We've gone into Holler (ph). We've continued to try and go to at least five or six different locations, either conducting search and rescue operations to drop star, to do health and welfare checks on family members who's gone missing, or even just doing distribution for Starlink systems so that we can get our missing persons report down.\par \par \uc2 Because a lot of this people just haven't heard from their family members for two and three and four days. We were able to successfully evacuate someone from Bailey, North Carolina to Johnsonville yesterday, 83-year-old man yesterday was his birthday, showing signs of heart attack or hypertension. So we're able to medevac him and get him out, but also delivering insulin oxygen bottles.\par \par \uc2 A lot of people, it's not just food and water and non perishables, but it's medications that they're short on. So trying to fulfill those needs until the federal government can get up and do what they need to be doing.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, congressman, we missed -- you got cut off a little bit at the top. With, you know, are -- you're working with your private organization that you're part of, and what do you see in terms of federal help happening? Tell us the difference.\par \par \uc2 MILLS: So that's really where the missing pieces of the federal piece. And again, let's go ahead and point out the obvious, which is that FEMA had utilized a large majority of the personnel who should be here taking care of this, to be set and set -- replaced or repurposed, if you will, for immigration resettlement, when we need to be focusing on Americans and what's going on, whether it be in Maui Palestine, Ohio, Florida, North Carolina or Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 But I'm actually working with great organizations like Mercury One, charity for Glenn Beck, Universal Air, as well as for Sentinel Foundation and so many others. This is a group project where Americans are coming together to answer the call for Americans when the federal government fails to act.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So we're hearing one of the most horrifying details is that, it could be hundreds of people who are still missing. And we don't know if they are dead or alive, or if they're hanging onto a boulder like that woman just described, which was so heartbreaking. What is your sense? Do you have any sense of that and that number?\par \par \uc2 MILLS: Well, again, we've got about an estimate of around a non-confirmed report of roughly 1,000 to 1,200 people still being listed as a missing person. A lot of that, I will tell you, as we get Starlink in place, and there's so many units here that's allowing individuals to start reaching out to their family members in other states, I think that missing person report will come down, and then we'll be able to really conceive (inaudible) find (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, as you can tell, it's tough to maintain communication, and obviously it's easy to understand why. I just wanted to give him an extra second there to see if maybe we could get Congressman Mills back. I give him so much credit, because he leaves his job and he goes into these places, in shoppers.\par \par \uc2 He's fearless. He went into Haiti, in the worst of circumstances, into Ukraine. And he is part of these teams of, in many cases, former veterans who want to continue to help people, because that's what they like to do, and they want to be where they are needed.\par \par \uc2 And it's an extraordinary story of American heroism. Of course, you know, he doesn't want to be -- take credit for that but I do think that it's -- I just want people to understand the selflessness. And I have covered these hurricanes and difficult situations in the past.\par \par \uc2 I'm always amazed by the people who show up with, you know, huge campers and just make barbecue for everybody on this -- on who's there, you know, the first responders. Just people who literally leave their lives and go to where they are needed and help out. And it's very moving and very extraordinary.\par \par \uc2 So I ask all of you to, you know, look up these organizations, whether you want to do Red Cross, which Fox is doing, of course. And Congressman Mills just named a bunch of other organizations, Sentinel was one of them that, that you, you know, feel the best about, that you believe was is going to get your money where it needs to go. And these people also need time and volunteers, so what an effort.\par \par \uc2 We're going to continue to cover this story very closely. And our thanks to Congressman Mills, he's back at work, no doubt, doing what he's there for.\par \par \uc2 So we want to bring you this as well, an alarming new health trend that is emerging as we mark Breast Cancer Awareness Month. The \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  says that deaths from breast cancer have declined, but warns that new diagnoses are steadily rising, especially among women under 50, leading to both a physical and financial toll. Fox Business Correspondent and breast cancer survivor Gerri Willis is with me now.\par \par \uc2 Gerry, you have documented, you know, very graciously and publicly, your own battle with cancer, which I think has enlightened so many people. What did you learn in this work that you're doing now?\par \par \uc2 GERRI WILLIS, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Well, let me just say that study you just referenced shows that 1.4 percent more women were diagnosed with breast cancer who are under 50 this past year than the previous year. That sounds like nothing, but let me tell you, it's the biggest increase they've ever seen. So the numbers are really surprising.\par \par \uc2 The thing that we're learning about right now is that there's a financial burden that goes with cancer, and guess what? It doesn't only create economic hardship, it also results in worse outcomes for those in treatment. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAREN KNUDSEN\uc2 , CEO, \uc2 AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY\uc2  Those individuals will have effects of their cancer therapy that they carry with them throughout their entire life, not only a different risk of cancer but also financial implications. Someone who has had a cancer diagnosis will, over their lifetime, have a much higher cost of cancer of -- of health care than someone who has not had a cancer diagnosis.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIS: Three in five people who are diagnosed with cancer are faced with financial hardships like taking unpaid leave, losing health insurance or even losing a job. Simple things like traveling for treatment can make care unaffordable.\par \par \uc2 Sharon, pardon me, Shannon Barrett was recently declared free of cancer. She was treated in New York City, right here. She stayed at Hope Lodge, that's a facility that the \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  offers free to patients. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BARRETT, BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR: Women need to focus on their treatment and their recovery and the medicine. We need a community to help every breast cancer survivor get the treatment they need, and they are entitled to world class medicine. And that should not be held back because of any kind of financial burden.\par \par \uc2 WILLIS: Look, bottom line, doing what you can to make sure your wallet isn't devastated by cancer means getting screened. Treatment costs increase with the severity of the diagnosis. One year treatment costs for a woman diagnosed with stage one cancer is $5,700. But if the diagnosis is stage two, that cost increases 17 times.\par \par \uc2 Look, the best way to protect yourself, get tested. It is October Breast Cancer Awareness Month, get your mammogram.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I will be doing that this month. As you know, we have a history of breast cancer in my family, and there are so many great organizations out there. I would just name the Connie Dwyer Breast Cancer Foundation, which helps people who need help with financing these treatments. There's a lot of help out there for people who need it and thank you so much for this reporting --\par \par \uc2 WILLIS: Thank you, Martha, for always --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: (Crosstalk) this week, all right. So that is "The Story" for this Wednesday. Thank you very much, Gerri. "The Story" goes on as you know, so I look forward to seeing you back here tomorrow. Thanks for sharing your afternoon with me. And "Your World" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020241003eka200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Coverage of the Vice Presidential Debate\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Bret Baier, Sean Hannity, Brit Hume, Mike Tobin,  Dana Perino, Harold Ford Jr., Laura Ingraham, Jesse Watters, Aishah Hasnie,  Jacqui Heinrich, Kellyanne Conway, Jessica Tarlov, Pete Hegseth \par \uc2 18,474 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 October 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Live Event\par \uc2 FXLV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: It is now or never, just five weeks until Election Day. Ohio Senator \uc2 JD Vance\uc2  and Minnesota Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  about to come face-to-face for the first and only time. Stakes could not be higher.\par \par \uc2 Major crisis have broken out here at home and abroad.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: And I'm Bret Baier coming to you live tonight from Fox News World Headquarters in New York, the CBS News vice presidential debate just moments away. You can see it all right here, live on Fox.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the two candidates will duke it out on the national stage in the middle of a turbulent and consequential news cycle.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Here in the United States, Americans reeling from the impacts of Hurricane Helene and dockworkers kicking off a large scale strike along the Eastern Seaboard, and oversees the Middle East on the verge of an all-out war.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're tracking any and all developments out of that region tonight after Iran unleashed a barrage of missiles in all parts of Israel and all of that hanging over the debate stage tonight.\par \par \uc2 We have an all-star lineup of guests here with us tonight for analysis, but first we go to senior correspondent, Mike Tobin, live in Haifa, Israel with the latest on the ground.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL INTERNATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 With this fierce escalation of this unprecedented missile attack, Israeli's leaders are promising a punishing retaliation. Defense Minister Yoav Gallant said, Iran has not learned whoever attacks Israel pays a heavy price.\par \par \uc2 It was 180-plus missiles that were fired and did the 12-minute trip from Iran to here. When they arrived, they lit up the night sky with air defenses, not just the Iron Dome, but the Arrow Missile Systems and David's Sling as well.\par \par \uc2 Most of the missiles were intercepted. Some came crashing down in South and Central Israel. However, most of the damage that was caused was from shrapnel from previously intercepted rockets crashing down. In fact, we heard in the West Bank, a Palestinian man was killed as the shrapnel came crashing down.\par \par \uc2 Overall, the second direct attack on Israel was effective at scaring people and effective at galvanizing the Israeli leadership with a cause to respond.\par \par \uc2 Also in the old city of Jaffa, near Tel Aviv, there was a mass shooting, two attackers, at least one M-16 style rifle and a knife. Seven people are dead. Several are injured. The attackers themselves were killed, and tonight there is smoke rising over Beirut, fresh airstrikes -- Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mike Tobin, live in Haifa. Mike, thanks.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Former President Trump and Vice President Harris reacting to Iran's attack on Israel earlier today. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I condemn this attack unequivocally. I'm clear-eyed. Iran is a destabilizing, dangerous force in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: A short time ago, Iran launched 181 ballistic missiles at Israel. They are very close to global catastrophe. We have a nonexistent president and a nonexistent vice president who should be in charge, but nobody knows what's going on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: With that, we bring in Brit Hume, Fox News senior political analyst.\par \par \uc2 Brit, great to have you with us tonight as always.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, a very important day today that these candidates are going to have to confront and talk about head on.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes, I think that's right, Martha, and I think you know, each candidate will try to make the other's running mate's focus. No doubt, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  will be focused as much as he can be on Trump and JD Vance will be focused as much as he can be on Harris.\par \par \uc2 It will be interesting to hear how these two candidates speak about these foreign policy issues. You might expect in a normal situation for there to be a full-throated defense of Israel.\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  seemed to be making such a defense today. It will be interesting to see if Walz echoes that. It will also be interesting to see whether, whether JD Vance is as strong in defending Israel because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is, apart from saying continually that it would never have these conflicts in the Middle East and elsewhere would never have happened if he had been president, has been less than entirely full throated about what he would do and whether he fully supports Israel.\par \par \uc2 So all of that is going to be interesting tonight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, Brit, the background to this, the set up, and it's all about setting the table and expectations. There are all these articles how Democrats were worried and panicked about the Minnesota governor and how he was going to do whether Minnesota Nice would translate.\par \par \uc2 This is a guy who has run for governor. He was a congressman before that. He's been around the block with debates.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Oh yes. And look, he's a seasoned politician. You know, he'll be able to say what he wants to say tonight. And JD Vance, who's been, I think, to some extent, underestimated, you may recall, it wasn't so long ago, Bret, that people were talking about whether Trump might dump Vance off the ticket.\par \par \uc2 Well, Vance started going out and being on the Sunday shows and being interviewed widely, and he seemed to hold his own very well. So I expect both these men will be well prepared and will handle themselves effectively. They will see that Vance is someone that people believe.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Brit, stand by, if you would.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, let's bring in the rest of our panel, Dana, Perino, Harold Ford Jr, Laura Ingraham, and Jesse Watters.\par \par \uc2 Great to have all of you with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 You know, Brit brought up something very interesting, Dana at the top of this, and it was whether or not each of these candidates will be forceful on defending Israel, because there's been so much negative pushback against Netanyahu and against Israel on the campaign trail, we have all of those uncommitted voters.\par \par \uc2 So this is going to be tricky territory, in some ways for Walz.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I think definitely for Walz and an opportunity for Vance, because we've been talking a lot about Pennsylvania, if it all comes down to Pennsylvania and that race, and just in every poll, it's so tied. But in 2004, \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  won a larger percentage of the American Jewish vote than he had in the past, and that made a difference for him.\par \par \uc2 And in this particular situation, when we have talked to a voter last week in Pennsylvania, he said, I've always voted for Democrats, but this year, I'm a single issue voter, and that issue for me is Israel, and so I do think that Walz has a more difficult time on that tonight, but JD Vance has an opportunity to press that advantage on the Trump side.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Harold, it would be interesting to see how much they focus on the main nominees of the party, or how much they focus on each other as VP nominees. They both have vulnerabilities that have been exposed on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD\uc2 , JR. FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good to be with you. Now, I would agree.\par \par \uc2 To Dana's point, which builds on yours, I'm going to be looking to see as they go after the other, meaning he's going to go after Trump, the other is going to go after Harris. How cheerful are they as they do it and not so much contentious? How optimistic are they about the future in outlining with some specificity.\par \par \uc2 Now, neither the candidates really have at the top of their ticket candidates who are outlining specifics about the economy or specifics about Israel, specifics about really anything. I think there's some openings on abortion, and maybe Ukraine will go at each other on, but I think the country is really going to take the temperature tonight to see these are two men who could very well be president of the United States, if, God forbid, something happens. We've obviously seen an unbelievable three months with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  not being on the ticket.\par \par \uc2 So I think the measure of the man will be taken tonight. So how they perform, I think, will be important, and the substance, in some ways, will be secondary, because it's not their campaigns. They are serving at the pleasure of the person at the top of the ticket.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Laura, Americans have a lot on their minds tonight. They look at the Middle East, they look at this port situation, and they're still looking for missing people, hundreds of missing people, potentially after Helene.\par \par \uc2 So I think they're looking for some strength and some vision here.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST, "THE INGRAHAM ANGLE": I think confidence, maybe being a little bit more proactive, not quite so reactive as we've seen.\par \par \uc2 Even NBC did a big report earlier today about people still desperately seeking water and basic supplies that they were not really positioned as they should have been, and anticipating where the storm might track. So you can't predict everything about a natural disaster, but some questions remain there.\par \par \uc2 But I do think it's interesting that Biden and Trump are both saying we must avoid an all-out war. I mean, I think Trump is incredibly supportive of Israel. I might take issue with something that Brit said there. I mean, the Abraham Accords, obviously moving the embassy to Jerusalem.\par \par \uc2 But I think he and Biden are actually speaking to the desire for peace at a time where the world is very chaotic, but where Israel has the absolute right to defend itself and decapitate that \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leadership, as it did so brilliantly, frankly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jesse, you think about all the things that are going on all at once, it comes at a really consequential time.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST, "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME": So if I were JD Vance, I'd just say the ports are closed, the borders open. North Carolina is underwater. Missiles are flying. Biden and Harris are in hiding -- if they talk about cats, no, this is much more important than any of that.\par \par \uc2 If the moderators try that, stop, that's trivial. Stay focused on the issue. These people are hurting you. Biden and Harris are hurting you, the American people. Do it like that. Permission to make an analogy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Please.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: This election is so easy. We've both eaten at two restaurants. We've eaten at Trump's restaurant. Food was good. The prices were fair. We eat at Biden's restaurant. Food was terrible. The prices were high.\par \par \uc2 Now they put Kamala's name on Joe's restaurant, and they say, oh, it's totally different. The menu is the same. The prices are still high. The American people have a simple decision. You've eaten at both places. Go with your gut.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, Dana the polls, you mentioned it. They are so tight. Each one of these swing states, it fluctuates day to day, two, three points. You know, we don't have a full sense if the former president is under counted, he has been in the past, 2020 and 2016, but you're talking within two points in each one of the seven states.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Which is why I think -- I don't think that this debate is going to make a huge amount of difference. But when you've looked at any fluctuations, it has been on the Democratic side. You look at any situation with Democrats, they are like 90 percent, we're so glad that Biden is no longer the nominee.\par \par \uc2 But then, we are on -- what is it -- Day 73 -- when they've done very little talking. I believe it was one of Laura's producers that told me today that \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  in the CNN interview said 700 words total. That's it.\par \par \uc2 So then they wonder why people are in like in this "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " piece talking to Independent voters, saying we don't know anything about them. We don't know what they would -- we don't want they believe, and I think that Walz's position is even harder tonight, because he has to not only defend his own record, and Vance will press him on that. He has to defend Biden's record, which is very hard to do when 65 percent of country think we're on the wrong track.\par \par \uc2 And he has to try to remember what is \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' position today on issues that she might have had a different position on before, and she's never articulated herself why she's changed those positions. So now he's going to be on the hook to try to do that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. He's got a tall order. He has to be himself and two other people and defend the team essentially.\par \par \uc2 Harold, what about for Vance? What about for JD Vance? He has to be on his team as well and seek, in some ways, to reach across to voters who there aren't that many of them, but voters who don't feel sure yet, and maybe they're looking to learn a little bit more about him, his personality, and if they like him.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Look, voters have heard a lot about JD Vance. One of the things that the polling shows is that whether he is aligned with Project 2025 or not, he seems to be the person most identified on this ticket as being that person, too.\par \par \uc2 We've heard things about cats and dogs and things he said about women. It will be interesting to hear how he projects, and, frankly, how he talks about these issues.\par \par \uc2 I wasn't at all suggesting that the substance won't be critical, and that foreign policy and all of those issues won't be critical. I think Americans are going to want to just measure these guys. We don't -- we don't know them. We've only heard a lot of things about them.\par \par \uc2 And Jesse, I think your point about the restaurant names and so forth, I think people are going to look at these guys tonight and say, who can be president? Because that's what a VP at the end of the day, that's the job if God forbid, something happens to the president. So that's what I'm going to be most interested in this evening.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's bring in \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2 , host of the show called "Hannity." He is live in the spin room.\par \par \uc2 Sean, what do you think tonight?\par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST, "HANNITY": Yes, by the way, everyone is making brilliant comments tonight, and I think that everybody has brought up good points that we all should be looking for tonight.\par \par \uc2 I may be wrong here. Historically, vice presidential debates are not that impactful in terms of an election, but I think there's a huge opportunity for this to be a very consequential debate tonight, inasmuch as I think that JD Vance has an opportunity to prosecute the case, as Jesse really simplified it perfectly, that this is a choice election. This is an inflection point for the country.\par \par \uc2 And if JD Vance can prosecute that case and stick to the basics of the economy and immigration and law and order and energy in America's role in the world, especially in light of 181 ballistic missiles fired by Iran in to Israel today, this could be more impactful than people think.\par \par \uc2 If you go back to where Kamala started and how they've been hiding from the media, we still don't have an answer. Why did she co-sponsor the Green New Deal with Bernie Sanders? Why did \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , you know, say that she wants to eliminate private health insurance and have government health care for all? Why does she want to decriminalize illegal immigration and offer free housing, food and health care and education, sex change surgery and a path toward citizenship, which is amnesty, and of course, 11-and-a-half million unvetted Harris-Biden illegals, dozens of Americans murdered.\par \par \uc2 We shouldn't be talking about cats and dogs. We should be talking about murdered Americans, people that have been raped by Harris-Biden illegals, other victims of violent crime, the cost of the American people, if it becomes about her and fracking, I think that's impactful towards the important state of Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 I think the storm that Dana mentioned is another big issue. I think they have been very slow in their response and not as compassionate as Democrats like to portray themselves. I think tonight could be a very, very impactful night if JD Vance comes out and prosecutes more the case against Kamala than \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , although he has his own issues with feminine hygiene products in bathrooms and sex affirming care without parental consent, and building 30-foot ladder factories to get over 25 foot walls, this could be a big night tonight, guys, I think this could be bigger than people think. It's going to be a huge audience.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Sean, you know, our latest Fox News Power Rankings obviously have the top issues as immigration, the economy. The economy is leading by far, but immigration, then abortion is third.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Harris leads by 17 points on the issue of abortion. Expect that to be part of this. Do you think that that is a vulnerability for how JD Vance answers those questions?\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Look, I can't tell JD how to answer. I know how I would answer that question, and actually his, you know, very red state of Ohio is a great case and point for him to make. I mean, here's a red state, this vote went to the people of Ohio, and they overwhelmingly supported abortion rights for women, and that's what moving Roe v. Wade back to the states actually represents.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been very clear. He believes in the Supreme Court decision in terms of pretty much codifying the abortion pill and its widespread availability in the country in that decision, and certainly has been very clear that the states ought to decide. And certainly has been clear that, you know, abortion kind of like Bill and \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  should be legal, rare, and probably he might add the word early. And I think if JD Vance answers the question that way, I think that will solve that problem pretty quickly.\par \par \uc2 One of the things he will have to also watch out for is there's been a lot of demagoguery on abortion. They've actually tried to blame the border crisis on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . They tried to blame inflation on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . They have tried to tie \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to Project 2025, which he has said he knows nothing about. I don't even know anything about it. Maybe somebody on the panel can tell me what it's about, because I've never read it and I don't know the people involved.\par \par \uc2 So I think if he's got to -- he's got to -- if those opportunities come up, he's got to make sure he corrects the record. But more importantly, I would stay focused on those bread and butter issues.\par \par \uc2 Jesse laid it out perfectly. Laura laid it out perfectly. Dana and Brit and Harold laid it out perfectly. This is a choice election of all choice elections, and I don't think we've ever had a more radical, extreme ticket to run for president and vice president in history of the country.\par \par \uc2 If JD can expose that tonight, that will be a huge plus for the Trump campaign, in my view, and make it consequential.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Sean, we will check in in just a bit. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So anticipation is building as the countdown to this debate continues. We're going to have more on these VP candidates. They maybe have a little butterflies right now. They're about 20-some minutes away from the big night. We will take you there, and, of course, back and forth to the spin room, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We are minutes away from the vice presidential debate. You will see it right here on Fox. Senator JD Vance has been anticipating this moment to try to challenge Minnesota Governor Walz about his liberal record. Aishah Hasnie is standing by in the spin room with that side of the story. Hi, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hi there, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Well, we're getting a little bit more information about how JD Vance has been prepping here. I just spoke with senior adviser to the campaign, \uc2 Jason Miller\uc2 , and he told me that it's kind of like football. They've been watching a lot of tape, a lot of debate tape of Governor Walz's previous debates that he's done.\par \par \uc2 They're also raising the bar for the governor, saying that he's a very good debater. Don't listen to all of this stuff about how he's nervous or he's worried about what he's going to look in front of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . They think that this is just the expectation game they're playing.\par \par \uc2 They do expect Iran to be the first question tonight. Wouldn't preview an answer that Senator Vance is preparing for, but they did say that he will be ready, and they also say that the debate tonight is consequential.\par \par \uc2 They won't say how he's feeling tonight the senator, but they say that he is ready. And I'm also told that the president, former President Trump, will be watching the debate tonight. He didn't give JD Vance any advice, gave him some encouragement, but apparently he will be not live tweeting, but rather live truthing the debate as he watches tonight. Something interesting to look out for on the inter-webs out there as we watch this debate.\par \par \uc2 Back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Aishah. Thank you. We'll be watching that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let's go to Jacqui Heinrich, also in the spin room, with more on Minnesota Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 's preparations for tonight.\par \par \uc2 Hi Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha.\par \par \uc2 You know, I can't help but notice a bit of an effort to try to downplay the importance of this debate. The top Democratic surrogates, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , Josh Shapiro, are not here. The campaign didn't brief the press ahead of time. There are no new ads or messaging rollouts.\par \par \uc2 And broadly, there's sort of a sense that they want to get it over with the DNC Chair \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2  acknowledged that these reports that Walz is nervous, saying who wouldn't be might be a classic play to lower expectations, but a Minnesota state lawmaker kind of backed that up, saying that in private, Walz has a tendency to be defensive, shut down conversation when he's stressed. So his demeanor tonight could be a challenge as he tries to come off as this dad in plaid.\par \par \uc2 Broadly though, the campaign has kept him out of the spotlight since the DNC. He's done only one joint interview with the vice president, where he squirmed under questioning about his misrepresenting his military service, and today, the campaign completely ignored our questions, newly unearthed falsehood