ses me on this -- talking about and saying, I will create stories to bring attention to this. That vilified a large number of people who were here legally in the community of Springfield. The Republican governor said it's not true, don't do it.\par \par \uc2 There's consequences for this. There's consequence -- we could come together. Senator Lankford did it. We could come together and solve this if we didn't let \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  continue to make it an issue.\par \par \uc2 And the consequences in Springfield were, the governor had to send state law enforcement to escort kindergarteners to school.\par \par \uc2 I believe Senator Vance wants to solve this, but by standing with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and not working together to find a solution, it becomes a talking point. And when it becomes a talking point like this, we dehumanize and villainize other human beings.\par \par \uc2 [21:25:06]\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Tim --\par \par \uc2 BRENNAN: Governor -- Governor, your time is up. Senator, I'll give you one minute, but let me just ask you the question first. The governor has made the point, and I think as a sitting lawmaker, you know that Congress controls the purse strings and any funding.\par \par \uc2 So you have said repeatedly that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would, through executive action, solve this. Do you disagree that Congress controls the purse strings and would need to support many of the changes that you would actually want to implement?\par \par \uc2 You have one minute.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Look, Margaret, first of all, the gross majority of what we need to do with the southern border is just empowering law enforcement to do their job. I've been to the southern border more than our borders are. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has been, and it's actually heartbreaking because the border patrol agents, they just want to be empowered to do their job.\par \par \uc2 Of course, additional resources would help, but most of this is about the president and the vice president empowering our law enforcement to say, if you try to come across the border illegally, you've got to stay in Mexico. You've got to go back through proper channels.\par \par \uc2 Now, Governor Walz brought up the community of Springfield, and he's very worried about the things that I've said in Springfield.\par \par \uc2 Look, in Springfield, Ohio, and in communities all across this country, you've got schools that are overwhelmed. You've got hospitals that are overwhelmed. You have got housing that is totally unaffordable because we brought in millions of illegal immigrants to compete with Americans for scarce homes.\par \par \uc2 The people that I'm most worried about in Springfield, Ohio, are the American citizens who have had their lives destroyed by \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' open border. It is a disgrace, Tim. And I actually think, I agree with you, I think you want to solve this problem, but I don't think that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  does.\par \par \uc2 BRENNAN: Senator, your time is up. Governor, you have one minute to respond.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Yeah, well, it is law enforcement that asked for the bill. They helped craft it. They're the ones that supported it. It was -- that's because they know we need to do this.\par \par \uc2 Look, this issue of continuing to bring this up, of not dealing with it, of blaming migrants for everything. On housing, we could talk a little bit about Wall Street speculators buying up housing and making them less affordable, but it becomes a blame.\par \par \uc2 Look, this bill also gives the money necessary to adjudicate. I agree it should not take seven years for an asylum claim to be done. This bill gets it done in 90 days. Then you start to make a difference in this, and you start to adhere to what we know, American principles.\par \par \uc2 I don't talk about my faith a lot, but Matthew 25:40 talks about, to the least amongst us, you do unto me. I think that's true of most Americans. They simply want order to it. This bill does it, it's funded, it's supported by the people who do it, and it lets us keep our dignity about how we treat other people.\par \par \uc2 BRENNAN: Thank you, Governor. And just to clarify for our viewers, Springfield, Ohio does have a number of Haitian migrants who have legal status, temporary protected status.\par \par \uc2 Norah?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Margaret, but --\par \par \uc2 BRENNAN: Senator, we have so much to get to. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Margaret, I think it's important because --\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: We're going to turn out of the economy. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Margaret, the rules were that you guys weren't going to fact check. And since you're fact checking me, I think it's important to say what's actually going on. So there's an application called the CBP One app, where you can go on as an illegal migrant, apply for asylum or apply for parole and be granted legal status at the wave of a \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  open border wand.\par \par \uc2 That is not a person coming in, applying for a green card and waiting for 10 years.\par \par \uc2 BRENNAN: Thank you, Senator.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: That is the facilitation of a legal immigration, Margaret, by our own leadership.\par \par \uc2 BRENNAN: Thank you, Senator, for describing the legal process. We have so much to get to, Senator.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Those laws have been on the books since 1990.\par \par \uc2 BRENNAN: Thank you, gentlemen. We want to have --\par \par \uc2 VANCE: The CBP One app has not been on the books since 1990. It's something that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  created, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 BRENNAN: Gentlemen, the audience can't hear you because your mics are cut. We have so much we want to get to. Thank you for explaining the legal process.\par \par \uc2 Norah.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Thank you, Margaret. The economy is a top concern for voters. Each of your campaigns has released an economic plan. So let's talk about the specifics.\par \par \uc2 Governor Walz, Vice President Harris unveiled a plan that includes billions in tax credits for manufacturing, housing and a renewed child tax credit. \uc2 The Wharton School\uc2  says your proposals will increase the nation's deficit by $1.2 trillion. How would you pay for that without ballooning the deficit?\par \par \uc2 Governor, I'll give you two minutes.\par \par \uc2 WALZ: Yeah, thank you. And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and I do believe in the middle class because that's where we come from. We both grew up in that. We understand.\par \par \uc2 So those of you out there listening tonight, you're hearing a lot of stuff back and forth. And it's good. It's healthy. That's what this is supposed to happen.\par \par \uc2 You should be listening. How's this going to impact me? The bold forward plan that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  put out there is one is talking about this housing issue. The one thing is there's three million new houses proposed under this plan with down payment assistance on the front end to get you in a house.\par \par \uc2 A house is much more than just an asset to be traded somewhere. It's foundational to where you're at.\par \par \uc2 And then making sure that the things you buy every day, whether they be prescription drugs or other things, that there's fairness in that.\par \par \uc2 [21:30:04]\par \par \uc2 Look, the $35 insulin is a good thing, but it costs $5 to make insulin. They were charging $800 before this law went into effect.\par \par \uc2 As far as the housing goes, I've seen it in Minnesota, 12 percent more houses in Minneapolis. Prices went down on rent 4 percent. It's working.\par \par \uc2 And then making sure tax cuts go to the middle class, $6,000 child tax credit. We have one in Minnesota, reduces childhood poverty by a third. We save money in the long run, and we do the right thing for families.\par \par \uc2 And then getting businesses off the ground. The law, as it stands right now, is $5,000 tax credit for small business, increasing that to 