l immigration and also their economic policies in creating that contrast.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  believe bigger government. Government is a solution to everything. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , J.D. Vance, no tax on tips, no tax on overtime, no tax on Social Security. You get to decide what to do with your economic success.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And Laura, there will be a lot of surrogates here in the spin room tonight for Senator Vance, I want to show you around really quickly here, including the man, you could say, who is credited for getting him on the ticket in the first place, and that is Don Jr. He will be here tonight, as well as -- as what some of his senate colleagues including Senator Tom Cotton will also be here tonight. Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, we'll be watching. Aishah, thanks so much. All right. Trump's last debate was basically three on one, with the moderators taking him on almost as many times as Kamala did. But tonight, \uc2 CBS\uc2  says that its moderators will not do any live fact checking. They say that it's up to the candidates to fact check each other. But wait a second, not so fast. Oliver Darcy is reporting that CBS News is leaving open the possibility that the moderators may step in and offer fact checks of their own.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, \uc2 Jason Miller\uc2 , Trump Campaign Senior Advisor. Jason, Politico is reporting today that you were part of a small team that helped prep for tonight's debate, also Congressman Emmer and J.D.'s wife, who is brilliant in her own right. What can you tell us? Give us a little, little color here before the debate.\par \par \uc2 JASON MILLER\uc2 , TRUMP CAMPAIGN SENIOR ADVISOR: Yes. So, one thing is, keep in mind is \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  is actually pretty good. I don't know who gave that quote saying that that he was going to be a pushover. \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  will not be the person who's up there doing jazz hands and flapping around like he does at the rallies. He'll be sharp. He'll be disciplined. And here's the thing on \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , he will lie through his teeth.\par \par \uc2 I was watching one of his old debate clips earlier. He literally said, in a debate, you're not going to get a tax hike. I'm just going to roll back your tax cuts. So that's the kind of nonsense that he will say. It's like, no, you're going to raise taxes. So, he is really good at looking the American public in the eye and lying.\par \par \uc2 J.D. Vance is going to be ready. J.D. Vance is very sharp. He knows how to articulate President Trump's vision when it comes to the economy, securing the border. But look, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , professional politician. He's been doing this two decades. J.D. Vance for two years.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, the problem with debates, and I'm going to talk about this in my Angle, coming up. They're really designed in many ways, not to really get at the issues. Who is a better debater? It's really centering in on likability. Can you relate to the mom of three, single mom of three watching in Ohio tonight? Or could you, you know, can you relate to the Hispanic family in Arizona, what their issues and needs are?\par \par \uc2 So, it's cutting through that likability question. I think that's as big of a deal tonight as the substance, because the debate has been won on the substance, I think, on the border, on the economy, but the likability question, I think that's a bigger hurdle.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Yes, and when J.D. Vance is talking about his humble upbringing, when he's talking about his military service in the Marines, he is very good. It's a background the people can relate to. He's someone who was raised largely by his grandmother, who had tough evenings where they had to make decision --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: They used to love him, right, during hillbilly days. They love J.D. Vance.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Suddenly they don't like him anymore.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: They got the book, they got the movie, and then also they decided they didn't. So, when J.D. Vance is talking about his background, it connects with what President Trump is trying to do on the policy side, because people are suffering when it comes to the economy. We look around today with what's happening with Iran and Israel. The whole world has gone to hell because \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has made bad decisions. That's why this VP debate is consequential.\par \par \uc2 We see what Kamala did at the border. We see what she did with inflation. She was the last one in the room with Afghanistan, gave \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  the go ahead. That was disastrous, of course, fueled Iran and everything else. So, the VP debate is very important, but J.D. has an ability to connect with people because he knows exactly what it's like to struggle.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We're learning tonight that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  did not do the annual hurricane preparedness briefings that her predecessor \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  did in the Trump administration, and most administrations do on an annual basis. You can see that you don't want to just jump into a hurricane or prep for a hurricane if you don't know anything about it. She, according to the reports tonight, had not attended a single hurricane preparedness briefing during her time in office. Does that matter?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Well, it doesn't surprise me. I mean look at the administration's terrible response with regard to the Hurricane Helene this week. Look how terrible Biden and Harris were responding to East Palestine. This is really more of, their MO. The other thing to me, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  probably had a fundraiser in San Francisco or some other equally terrible excuse, but that's the type of inept leadership they have right now. And tonight, you're going to hear \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  say, we have to go and turn the page. Well, guess