e here.\par \par \uc2 I watched J.D. Vance debate during his run for the Senate and he is as cool and confident and concise as anybody you're going to meet. He's going to do quite well tomorrow night despite the bias from \uc2 CBS\uc2  and if they're not fact-checking, then he has a clear lane here to expose \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  for who he is.\par \par \uc2 HANNTY: I would pro -- if I was him -- well, by the way, you know, abortion back to the states, look at a red state like Ohio. And we know how they voted on abortion. So that is -- you know, the proof is in the pudding in his home state, but he -- if he prosecutes the case against Kamala and Walz, it'll be a knockout in my view. That -- that would be his mission in my view tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 All right. Appreciate both of you. Thank you, Tammy. And thank you, Joe Concha.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, wow, Israel, unbelievable military might, intelligence. We may not see such sophistication in a short period of time again in our lifetime, but it they have now had to begin a ground invasion in the southern part of Lebanon against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  targets after they got their 32- year leader over the weekend. We'll tell you about that. \uc2 Aaron Cohen\uc2  reacts, straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And this is a FOX News alert. The Israeli military says it has begun a, quote, limited ground invasion in southern Lebanon following Israel strikes that killed \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leader \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now also tonight, "Reuters" is reporting that Iran's supreme leader, the \uc2 Ayatollah Khamenei\uc2  is now hiding in a secure location, deep underground, obviously fearful of heading to hell where he belongs.\par \par \uc2 And meanwhile, you got "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ", the "AP", under fire, rightly so for praising the \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leader after his death. "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " publishing this tribute to the terrorist leader, writing that he wanted equality for all religions and was beloved by many. No, he was dedicating to wiping Israel off the map for those dumb asses of "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ".\par \par \uc2 And in an updated headline, the "AP" referred to Nasrallah as, quote, charismatic and shrewd. This is how repulsive and corrupt the media is in this country.\par \par \uc2 Here with reaction, Israeli special ops veteran, \uc2 Aaron Cohen\uc2  is with us.\par \par \uc2 You know, after Iran fired directly on Israel, I was really impressed how deep into Iranian territory Israel was able to penetrate. Then we had the number 5, 4, 3, 2, and then the number one leader of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  all taken out by Israel, huge military successes, each one in -- in and on their own merit. And then we have Nasrallah here, 32-year leader of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , we had the pager incident, the walkie-talkie incident and Bibi says regime change for Iran might come a lot sooner than people think.\par \par \uc2 I would say Israel is probably the mightiest military and smartest military force on the face of this Earth and I say that sadly because I want America to be that, and I feel our military has been weakened under Harris and Biden.\par \par \uc2 AARON COHEN\uc2 , ISRAELI SPECIAL OPS VETERAN: Sean, I agree with you 100 percent. I think that America definitely needs to step up how they view the global war -- war on terror. Israel's had to get after it for the last 11 days. We've been systematically taking out terrorists left and right, starting with the pager operation, Sean, that -- that's a 5-year operation right there just to establish and target those resources, flip and get them working for us.\par \par \uc2 But here's where I'm at with this, Sean. I really -- Israel doesn't have a choice. Israel's got to be good right now, and the reason why Israel's got to move quick, I believe that all of that psychological warfare that's being funded by Qatar and by Iran with all those bots that are being engine and fired up which is taken over the brains of the 14 to 24-year-olds and radicalized the entire Internet through TikTok, has really -- that psychological pressure, Sean, I believe made its way into the White House, forcing uh the handcuffs on Israel at Rafah, which I believe we would have been able to save Hersh, and those five additional hostages, just like we did, Sean, with Noa Argamani, our hostage rescue teams are the finest in the world.\par \par \uc2 And I believe that this same psychological pressure is going to work its way up into the Kamala-Biden White House, and they're going to start putting the breaks on Bibi again. Now, we're going to see it in Lebanon. And so, Israel's got to move quick. Bibi's moving quickly. Our shell dog unit, our JTAC unit just lays that that giant little hole that we put in the ground, taken out Nasrallah, and Israel's got to get those commandos in there to mop up the rest of those elements in southern Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 But my fear again is that the White House is weak-minded and immune to that psychological warfare of lies that's being pumped into the social media, handheld devices and there's nothing we can do about that, but work quickly, Sean.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And -- and we have a presidential candidate that wants every American to be courageous and never say "radical Islamic terrorism". No, you got to defeat the terrorism, win the war, Israel is doing their job. These people are dedicated to destroying the state of Israel.\par \par \uc2 Aaron, our prayers are with you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, we have more "Hannity" coming up, straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Before we go, a big programming announcement. Be sure to tune in right here tomorrow night. Don't miss the vice presidential debate. We will be doing post debate coverage live from the spin room starting at 11:00 Eastern, right out of New York. I don't want to be in New York.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, hope you'll join us. That's all the time we have left. Please set your DVR so you never miss an episode.\par \par \uc2 Let not your heart be troubled. Greg Gutfeld, he'll make you smile, next. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 Document SHAN000020241001ek9u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Death Toll Mounts Amid Widespread Devastation In North Carolina; Officials Say Hundreds Could Be Dead From Hurricane Helene; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Visits Hurricane Hit Georgia; Raids Into Lebanon As Ground Operation Looms; White House Laments Portrayal Of Migrant Criminal Data; Jean-Pierre: Harris Will Build Upon Biden Legacy; Union Threatens Walkout Over Wage And Labor Issues; Vice Presidential Candidates Gearing Up For \uc2 CBS\uc2  Debate; VA Staff Got Into Vance, Walz Medical Files, Sparking Investigation; RO Boston Arrests Fugitive Wanted By Salvadoran Authorities For Crimes Against Human Trafficking; North Carolina Reels From Devastating Impact Of Hurricane Helene\b0\par\par\uc2 John Roberts, Steve Harrigan, Mike Emanuel, Alexandria Hoff, Mike  Tobin, Peter Doocy, Grady Trimble, Alexis McAdams, Bret Baier, Josh  Kraushaar \par \uc2 8,075 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 September 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right. Well, guys, we'll catch you tomorrow, and that is it for us.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening, and welcome to Washington. I'm John Roberts in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the next phase of Israel's promised military operation is underway.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we look at the number of migrants released into the United States who were convicted of violent crimes.\par \par \uc2 And with a possible strike at seaports on the horizon, retailers are preparing for supply shortages.\par \par \uc2 But first, breaking tonight, officials say hundreds could be dead in the aftermath of Hurricane Helene, but confirming those numbers is proving difficult. Millions of people are without power and cell service.\par \par \uc2 In North Carolina, the lack of communication services have prompted hundreds of missing persons reports, with many others still stranded in their homes due to high flood waters.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Steve Harrigan is in Asheville, North Carolina tonight, where he's been surveying the devastation. Good evening, Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, John, the mayor of Asheville put it simply. She said, we need everything.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Everything is just --\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Wiped out and washed away.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Our whole house, bro.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Roads and bridges, homes and businesses all erased by the extended power of hurricane Helene.\par \par \uc2 GREGORY HARRISON, ASHEVILLE, NORTH CAROLINA RESIDENT: It's been an incredibly apocalyptic weekend for all of us here.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): More than two feet of rain socking parts of western North Carolina, causing the worst flooding here in a century.\par \par \uc2 The village of Chimney Rock gone, city of Asheville, underwater, historic Biltmore Village caked in mud, once picturesque Lake Lure filled with debris, residents say the rural town of Swannanoa is unrecognizable.\par \par \uc2 JARED QUINN, SWANNANOA, NORTH CAROLINA RESIDENT: It's a lot to process. You can barely recognize some areas, and you're trying to figure out where am I?\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Not only are thousands left homeless, but struggling for supplies, waiting for hours for clean water, food and gas.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't know what they're going to do to get us out of here.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): People are cut off from the outside world, with roads closed, no power and cell service, leaving many unable to get in touch with loved ones.\par \par \uc2 ESTHER MANHEIMER, MAYOR OF ASHEVILLE, NORTH CAROLINA: It is a dire situation. We are still in an active crisis situation.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): North Carolina's Governor activated more than 500 soldiers and airmen from the National Guard, and is asking people to stay off the roads as search teams look for those still missing.\par \par \uc2 GOV. ROY COOPER (D-NC): Everyone is pulling together to meet the needs of people in Western North Carolina, but we know there's a long way to go.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Helene left behind a 500 mile path of destruction spanning six states. It could take weeks to years to recover, while Florida's governor says his state has everything it needs, residents of places like Treasure Island are still inundated with sand and water.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My heart breaks for this entire community.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (on camera): The death toll continues to rise. It now stands at more than 120, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And it's raining again. Steve Harrigan, for us. Steve, thank you.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  says he will visit North Carolina on Wednesday to survey the damage in Asheville. He and Vice President Harris say they are prepared to help communities impacted by Hurricane Helene. It comes amid criticism that they were both absent from Washington as many people began to assess the damage.\par \par \uc2 Our chief Washington Correspondent Mike Emanuel looks at the federal response.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I want them to know we're not leaving until the job is done.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Holy smokes.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): In the wake of Hurricane Helene, federal teams are now coordinating with state and local authorities following what experts call, "historic, catastrophic flooding."\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH SHERWOOD-RANDALL, HOMELAND SECURITY ADVISER: The current data we have is that it looks like there could be as many as 600 lost lives.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice over): Vice President Harris was briefed aboard Air Force Two yesterday, reminiscent to some of the photo of President Bush during Hurricane Katrina. Former President Trump accused her of, "Leaving Americans to drown in staging a fake photo op."\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Let me get this straight, he's lying. Implies that we're not doing everything possible. We are.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice over): Harris is back in Washington. Early this morning, the vice president decided to cut short her campaign swing in Nevada several hours after former President Trump's team announced he'd be traveling to hard hit Georgia. She received an update a short time ago on federal action supporting emergency response and recovery efforts.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I plan to be on the ground as soon as possible, but as soon as possible, without disrupting any emergency response operations, because that must be the highest priority in the first order of business.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice over): The president pushing back when asked why he and Harris were not in Washington this weekend, leading the response.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Come on. Stop with the game will you. It's 90 miles from here. I was on the phone the whole time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (on camera): A senior congressional source tells Fox it would not be surprising if Congress were recalled to address damage from Helene, with some suggestion western North Carolina looks like New Orleans during Hurricane Katrina.\par \par \uc2 Back in 2005, lawmakers rushed through a ten billion aid package to help the Gulf Coast in the aftermath of Katrina, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: They certainly going to need something along those lines to recover from this. Mike, thank you.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: You bet.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: If you would like to help those impacted by Hurricane Helene, you can donate now at redcross.org/foxforward, or simply scan the QR code on your screen.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is criticizing the Biden-Harris's administration response to Hurricane Helene, the Republican nominee was in Georgia today to distribute supplies and receive a briefing on the storm's impact.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Alexandria Hoff is in Valdosta tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We pull together, we pitch in, we persevere.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): In the rubble of a storm wrecked antique mall. Former President Trump said he came to Valdosta with much needed supplies in tow.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : From oil to water to all sorts of equipment that's going to help them.\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice over): In partnership with Franklin Graham's relief organization \uc2 Samaritan's Purse\uc2 , Trump said he stands in solidarity with the people of Georgia and plans to travel to North Carolina later this week.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: As you know, our country is in the final weeks of a hard fought national election, but in a time like this when a crisis hits, when our fellow citizens cry out in need, none of that matters. We're not talking about politics.\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice over): But when asked if he has reached out to President Biden about federal relief efforts.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: No, I haven't reached out to him. No, I think he's sleeping right now.\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice over): Trump also accused the current administration of being unresponsive to Georgia Governor Brian Kemp. This morning, Kemp said that he did speak with President Biden after missing his call yesterday.\par \par \uc2 GOV. BRIAN KEMP (R-GA): I spoke to President Biden last night, about 5:00, just about the storm, about the devastation we've seen in our state. Vice President Harris and I have been playing phone tag.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOFF (on camera): And Trump said that earlier today, he spoke with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  about making Starlink available to those who are still struggling to communicate with their loved ones, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right, we'll see if that comes together. Alexandria Hoff for us in Valdosta, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The man accused of attempting to assassinate former President Trump has pleaded not guilty to federal charges. Ryan Routh appeared briefly in a West Palm Beach courtroom. A grand jury handed down a five count indictment last week. Routh's attorneys also requested that he have a jury trial.\par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  says the central bank is not in a hurry to lower interest rates. Stocks declined following those comments, but rallied late this afternoon, the Dow finished up 17, the S&P 500 gained 24, the NASDAQ rose 70.\par \par \uc2 Israel is countering threats from nearly every direction tonight, Yemen's Houthi rebels say they will escalate military operations in response to attacks. Israeli air strikes targeted Gaza today as troops in the north entered Lebanon ahead of what officials say will become a larger ground operation. Israel continues to target Beirut days after killing \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 's leader.\par \par \uc2 U.S. officials say his death is a good thing for the region. Our Senior Correspondent Mike Tobin is in Haifa, Israel tonight joins us. Hi, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: And John, for about four hours now, Israeli artillery has been thundering across the border into Lebanon. That started shortly after the army declared some small towns on the northern border closed military zones. Without \uc2 IDF\uc2  confirmation that Israeli boots are on the ground in Lebanon, explosions can be seen and heard there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): With at least two brigades of reservists, an elite paratrooper brigade with a compliment of tanks and armor, Israel's defense minister told troops in the north, stopping the rocket fire and returning Israelis safely to their homes would require their service and sacrifice.\par \par \uc2 YOAV GALLANT, ISRAELI MINISTER OF DEFENSE (through translator): We will implore all the capabilities at our disposal. And if someone on the other side did not understand what those capabilities entail, we mean all capabilities, and you are part of this effort.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): The \uc2 IDF\uc2  has been able to strike targets at will in the South of Lebanon, and has expanded the strikes into Beirut, killing operatives with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and the \uc2 Popular Front for the Liberation of Palestine\uc2 . All of that following the assassination of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leader \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2 , and the top of the \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  command structure.\par \par \uc2 Still the acting successor to \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2  said, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  fighters are ready for \uc2 IDF\uc2  ground forces.\par \par \uc2 NAIM QASSEM, HEZBOLLAH DEPUTY SECRETARY GENERAL (through translator): If the Israelis decide to enter by land, the resistance forces are ready for ground engagement.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): Israel has had a series of military successes, including Sunday's air strikes into parts of Houthi controlled Yemen, hinting the \uc2 IDF\uc2  could do the same thing to Iran. As Israel takes the war into a new phase, Israel does it in defiance of the U.S. President.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm comfortable with them stopping. We should have a cease fire now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (on camera): Also tonight, new air strikes into Dahiyeh, the southern Beirut neighborhood, the same neighborhood where \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2  was killed. Warnings went in ahead of the strikes.\par \par \uc2 Now, U.S. sources say this is not intended to be as large of an incursion as the 2006 in Lebanon. It is tended to be unlimited incursion aimed at specific \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  infrastructure, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: They're going to keep you busy there. Mike, no question about that. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And this just in from Baghdad, several rockets were fired at a base known as Camp Victory, used by Iraqi counterterrorism units near Baghdad International Airport.\par \par \uc2 A senior U.S. official says there were no U.S. casualties. The details are still unclear. Reuters cited Iraqi military officials who said two of the Katyusha rockets were successfully intercepted. The story is developing. We'll keep you apprised of it.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a closer look at immigration and customs enforcement data showing the number of migrants released into the U.S. under the Biden-Harris administration who have criminal records.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight, Russia fires missiles and drones across Ukraine in a 33rd consecutive night of aerial attacks beyond the front line. The attacks come as Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  marks the second anniversary of the annexation of four Ukrainian territories.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 200 people are dead in Nepal after heavy rainfall caused flooding and landslides in that Himalayan country, dozens were killed in a landslide that buried three busses and other vehicles as travelers waited on a blocked highway, rescue workers have been able to step in as the weather there has improved.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Rome, one of the big stories there tonight, Italian police arrest 19 people with links to a mafia group and Milan Soccer fan clubs. The leaders of the AC Milan and Inter Milan Ultra fan groups are among the accused. Prosecutors allege the suspects attempted to take control of business activities around the city's soccer stadium.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders t tonight, we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: "BREAKING TONIGHT", big news out of Georgia, where a judge has struck down that state's abortion law, which took effect in 2022, and effectively prohibited abortions beyond about six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 If it stands, the ruling could open up new avenues to access abortion in Georgia and for people in nearby states. Georgia was one of four states where the bans kick in after six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Well, last week, we reported on data from \uc2 Immigration and Customs Enforcement\uc2  that revealed thousands of migrants convicted of violent crimes have been released to roam around the United States.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump is blaming Vice President Harris for allowing the offenders to walk free. The Harris campaign and the Biden White House claim the numbers are distorted and span well beyond their administration.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House correspondent Peter Doocy takes a closer look at the more recent data that many Republicans say Harris is responsible for.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: False representation. I got to call that out. We got to call that out.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voice over): The White House is disputing figures released by the Deputy Director of ICE, who told Congress as many as 13,000 people convicted of homicide have crossed the U.S. border and are on ICE's non-detained docket.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: This is --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Can you clarify what the misrepresentation is?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: The numbers that is being put out about how many people are out is been falsely represented here.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): The issue isn't with the numbers themselves, but an official at the \uc2 Homeland Security Department\uc2  explains, "The data in this letter is being misrepresented. The data goes back decades; it includes individuals who entered the country over the past 40 years or more." That's no comfort for Republican critics.\par \par \uc2 REP. TOM EMMER (R-MN): Now, these are the only ones we know. Right? There are others that we probably don't know because they have left that border wide open.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The White House is extending restrictions on asylum since June, nobody has been granted asylum if border crossings reach 2,500 a day, until crossings fall to 1,500 a day for a week.\par \par \uc2 Now, restrictions won't be lifted until crossings fall to 1500 a day for a month.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Harris claims she'll go even further than President Biden.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: While we understand that many people are desperate to migrate to the United States, our system must be orderly and secure.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): White House officials don't see that as a slight against their handling of the border.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: The vice president was going to build upon what we have been able to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): And that's how Democrats are starting to explain why Vice President Harris hasn't done more on immigration in 3-1/2 years as vice president. It's not that now she is proposing something entirely new, she is just proposing something that will build on what Biden did. John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right. Peter Doocy, for us, parsing the words at the White House. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, port workers from across the country preparing to strike as businesses brace for supply chain shortages. First, here is what some of our fox affiliates around the corner -- of the country are covering tonight.\par \par \uc2 FOX32 in Chicago, as \uc2 Verizon\uc2  says it is making progress on a network issue, and service has started to be restored for some users. More than 100,000 users reported issues earlier today, saying they were unable to send messages or place calls.\par \par \uc2 FOX4 in Billings, Montana, where a man is sentenced for cloning giant sheep. Yes, you heard that right -- for trophy hunting.\par \par \uc2 81-year-old Arthur Jack Shubarth, pleaded guilty in March to charges that he conspired to clone a Marco Polo sheep that was brought illegally into the United States, and then breed the animals to create larger hybrid species.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look for Los Angeles from FOX 11, one of the big stories there tonight, \uc2 DirecTV\uc2  says it will acquire Dish, as it seeks to compete against streaming services. The debt exchange deal includes payment of $1 and the assumption of around $9.8 billion in debt.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Communications Commission\uc2  blocked an effort to merge the two companies more than two decades ago. The increase in streaming services makes it more likely that regulators will approve the pairing this time around.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight's live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Retailers and businesses are on edge tonight, ahead of possible supply chain disruptions. Union workers are prepared to go on strike at ports across the country tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Fox, business correspondent Grady Trimble is at the Port of Baltimore and