his is going to come down to the economy and immigration.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: What's the difference between your plan for the economy and for Americans versus \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  plan for the economy?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: Well, there are a lot of them. One is that we are bringing companies back, but a big one is that we are lowering taxes very simply and she is raising taxes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: So, John, this debate is going to be different here in New York City. CBS News says the candidates will be responsible for fact checking each other. But former President Trump saying today that debate will be rigged. So we're going to have to see exactly what happens then that debate stage, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right. Going to be a smackdown in New York. Alexis McAdams for us. Alexis, thank you.\par \par \uc2 At least a dozen veterans affairs staffers have been accused of accessing the medical records of both of the vice presidential nominees. The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reports investigators notified the Trump-Vance and Harris- Walz campaigns. The employees are under criminal investigation for potentially violating federal health privacy laws.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a preview of a new film shining a light on human trafficking and child labor. But as we head to break, basketball hall of Famer Dikembe Mutombo has died. The \uc2 NBA\uc2  announced his death from brain cancer. He is remembered as one of the best defensive players in \uc2 NBA\uc2  history and a longtime global ambassador for the game. Dikembe Mutombo was 58, and singer, songwriter and actor Kris Kristofferson has died.\par \par \uc2 The Texas native was known for his country and rock and roll songs, Sunday morning coming down and for the good times. He also starred alongside Barbra Streisand in the 1976 film a Star Is Born. A family spokeswoman says Kristofferson died peacefully at his home in Maui. He was 88 years old.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Immigration officials say they have arrested a Salvadoran national on Martha's Vineyard who was wanted by authorities in his home country for human trafficking charges. The 50-year-old is a got-a-way who entered the U.S. at an unknown date. He was deported at least one other time in June of 2011.\par \par \uc2 A new film now available on streaming services is shining a light on human trafficking and child labor. Bret Baier speaks with the director and the executive producer. Brett.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thanks, John. A new movie being described as a gripping tale of resilience and hope debuts on \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Prime Video and \uc2 Apple\uc2 . It's called "City of Dreams" and it's about child trafficking. The film follows one boy's harrowing journey as he is sent from Mexico to a sweatshop in Los Angeles. Author and speaker Tony Robbins. His executive producer, Mohit Ramchandani, is the writer and director. Thank you both for being here.\par \par \uc2 Tony, just give us the elevator pitch on this movie because I think it's really compelling. Just even looking at the trailer.\par \par \uc2 TONY ROBBINS, AUTHOR AND SPEAKER: It's really -- it's a movie you want to see just because it's a thriller will keep you on the edge of your seat. It's based on a series of true stories that have been combined together. And it's really -- it's about a boy that gets trafficked. He thinks he's coming to go to a soccer camp and maybe work play for the galaxy in LA, and he gets put into a sweatshop.\par \par \uc2 And so it's about slave labor, but it's hard to describe. It's beyond your imagination what these kids go through. And on the other side of it, though, it's a hero's journey, because this young man finds a way, against all odds, to free himself and free others. And then it's really called action, because right now, there are 12 million children in the world that are enslaved. It's the fastest growing criminal enterprise in the world, $150 billion.\par \par \uc2 But people have no idea what's happening when they hear 10, 11 million people coming across the border and we hear that government has told us we've lost 325,000 children. We know where they are. Well, this is where some of them are ending up.\par \par \uc2 Film has the power to move people emotionally. If you think about it, we have slavery in this country forever. What changed? It was Uncle Tom's Cabin, and it made people say, no more. And even \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2  read the book. I think about the impact that book had to change slavery. I think this film is a move in that direction.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let me play a clip from City of Dreams.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's good, it's good. It's the right decision for you whole family. You think you're going to run out onto the pitch tomorrow? No, no, no, no, no.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What kind of business you in?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The busy kind.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is real life. You know, it is gripping, but it is -- it transitions over to what is really happening today.\par \par \uc2 MO RAMCHANDANI, "CITY OF DREAMS" WRITER AND DIRECTOR: Yes, I mean, it was very shocking for me. I'm originally from India, and, you know, this stuff is happening India and China and third world countries. But I had read a case 10 years ago, about 72 immigrants trapped in a residential location in Almonte, California, and I couldn't believe it.\par \par \uc2 What's been even more shocking is, as I was making the movie, between 2019, 2020, 2021, you know, the cases just got worse. I connected with a guy at the Labor Department who runs the Wage and Hour Division for the entire west coast, and he started sending me cases.\par \par \uc2 And you know, eventually the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  last year did an expose about how 300,000 children have gone missing in our country, and many of them have ended up in the supply chains of some of the largest corporations in America.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to play some of the movie testimonials. Some people, you know, Tony, take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER STALLONE, ACTOR AND FILMMAKER: Every now and then, you come across a film that is actually truly important.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN SHEEN, EMMY WINNING ACTOR: Exposes in no uncertain terms the harsh, brutal, and devastating reality for numerous innocent victims of human trafficking right here in the United States.\par \par \uc2 PAT RILEY, PRESIDENT, \uc2 MIAMI HEAT\uc2 : It's a masterpiece about one of the most insidious problems that we have in the world and in our country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So it's not a partisan thing, Tony. I mean, it gets characterized as somehow left and right, but it really isn't.\par \par \uc2 ROBBINS: They think this is focusing on the issues around the border today, and it's not about that. It's about our children. Our children is not a left or right thing, you know, and we need to know what's going on. And the first step is awareness and getting enough emotion. This film will impact you. I mean, it's an incredible film on its own, but it's a film with a mission.\par \par \uc2 RAMCHANDANI: I'm showing you from a boy's point of view, what happens when you're trafficked across the border and you're. You're sold into slavery. And when I was making the film, it wasn't a big political issue.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I started this journey in 2019. Somebody said to me, you know, Mo, I know you tried to make this fairy tale like Slumdog Millionaire type story, but you happen to be pointing at the biggest vulnerability of one of the two political parties in America today in a year that they're trying to get elected.\par \par \uc2 And I was like, well, that wasn't my intention. My intention was to tell a heroic story of a kid who didn't have a voice. And by the end of the movie, he gets his voice back and he shows us what we need to do in order to clean up our country.\par \par \uc2 ROBBINS: So people will love it at Six Ethan film and hopefully will be inspired to participate in some way to put an end to this. Our children really need our help. This is really insane.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It is pretty crazy. It does cross party lines, and it shouldn't be an issue one way or another. The movie itself talks for itself. I mean, it's really, really powerful.\par \par \uc2 ROBBINS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But it tracks what we're dealing with real time. Here's the \uc2 House Judiciary Committee\uc2  hearing on human trafficking.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ANY BIGGS (R-AZ) HOUSE JUDICIARY COMMITTEE: Children are not for sale. Many children, especially those crossing the border as unaccompanied minors, are not safe.\par \par \uc2 REP. MADELEINE DEAN (D-PA) HOUSE JUDICIARY COMMITTEE: Child labor and sex trafficking to the production and distribution of child sexual abuse materials online harassment and sex torture. Perpetrators of these repulsive crimes hide within our society.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's Democrats and Republicans, Tony. And it's real time, and it's happening now. So the movie is current?\par \par \uc2 ROBBINS: Yes, I think so, too. I just hope people will get a chance to see it, let themselves be moved, show it to their family, show it to their friends. You can see it now. It's available on \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 Apple\uc2 , as you said, so you don't have to go to the movie theater to see it. But it's an important film to see.\par \par \uc2 And my wife and I personally have been involved in this trafficking issue for almost eight years. And with myself and some of our friends, we've saved over 71,000 children. And many of these kids, by the way, become social workers to go help other kids get out of this situation.\par \par \uc2 Because you can imagine the trauma they've been through, turning this around is such an important issue. And I really appreciate, Bret, you having us on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, Tony, Mo, we really appreciate the time. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RAMCHANDANI: Yes. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ROBBINS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: John, we'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right, Bret, a very important movie indeed. Up next, our panel on the political storm over the response to Hurricane Helene. And is there another political storm brewing over a looming port workers strike? And then it's a long way to take a dip and stand at the top of the podium.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ESTHER MANHEIMER (D) ASHVILE, NORTH CAROLINA MAYOR: This is a catastrophic situation that we have in Asheville, an unprecedented storm event.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: The federal government is not being responsive. The vice president, she's out someplace campaigning. The governor's doing a good job, but he's having a hard time getting the president on the phone.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , U.S. PRESIDENT: My first responsibility is to get all the help needed, those impacted areas.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: And I plan to be on the ground as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 GREG MURPHY (R) NORTH CAROLINA REPRESENTATIVE: I mean, this could flip an election. If those people are not able to get out and vote, heck, they're looking for shelter and food and medicine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: A tragic situation that the White House homeland security advisor says could result in the deaths of some 600 people. But because it's in two very important swing states, politics inevitably creeps into this disaster.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Guy Benson, political editor at Townhall.com. And host of the Guy Benson Show on Fox News Radio. \uc2 Josh Kraushaar\uc2  is the Fox News radio political analyst. And USA Today White House correspondent Francesca Chambers is here with us.\par \par \uc2 Francesca, start us off here, because it's Georgia and North Carolina and a disaster, and everybody wants to get the upper hand on this politics again inevitably creeps in.\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, \uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 : And the White House is trying to keep politics out of it today, they said, and now President Biden does say that he will go to North Carolina later this week. But as you were noting in the intro, he has said that he didn't want to go and be on the ground already. The White House said that because they didn't want him to be in the way. You covered the White House as well.\par \par \uc2 This is always the bait for presidents is how quickly do you go to a disaster area. You don't want to be accused of not going quickly enough, but you also don't want to put a strain on local resources. And you basically heard the same thing from the vice president today.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Yes, well, Trump said he wanted to go to North Carolina, but logistically, now was not the right time. So that's why he went to Valdosta, Georgia. But in terms of being where you're supposed to be, Biden and Harris have both come under criticism for he was at Rehoboth, she was out west doing fundraisers when all of this went down. Peter Doocy asked \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  about it at the White House briefing. Listen here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: People in western North Carolina were drowning in their houses this weekend. Others were losing everything. President Biden was at his beach house and Vice President Harris was hosting political fundraisers on the west coast. Is there a reason that they could not be here?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The president did exactly what a president in this moment needs to do, which is directing his team to take action.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Now, he pushed back against that, saying, look, I was on the phone the entire time, which he likely wasn't, but do you have to be at the White House?\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, "THE GUY BENSON SHOW": Well, first he said he was on the phone for 2 hours, and then the entire time he was at the beach. And I think if it were a republican president, we would be getting a lot of attention, a lot of scrutiny and conversation in the media about the president being yet again at the beach for yet another weekend with some very horrible things happening to Americans. Whether you care about the politics and think of them as voters, these are people in need of help.\par \par \uc2 And I think there would be some criticism of this more, I'd say direct and high decimal criticism if the parties were reversed. And look, the vice president, who's now the party frontrunner here, she's the torch carrier for the Democrats. She was at this glitzy fundraiser. She was with Alanis Morissette and Demi Lovato. They had beef Wellington in lobster rolls, I'm told, which sounds great. But as Peter asked, people were drowning. Optics actually do matter in politics.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Well, she had a military lunch on the way back aboard Air Force Two, the White House put out a picture of her talking to Criswell, the FEMA director, in a photo that looked slightly reminiscent of the one on the right, which was George Bush taking a look at Hurricane Katrina from Air Force One, Josh, back in 2005.\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR\uc2 , FOX NEWS RADIO POLITICAL ANALYST: Optics matter. Perception matters when you have natural disasters. And look, the scope and scale of the destruction of Hurricane Helene. I think was much worse than the White House realized. It took a little, and frankly, I think a lot of folks were slow on the uptick to understand how damaging this hurricane was.\par \par \uc2 But the images matter. And that showing that you have empathy and you care more about their well-being than the politics of the moment is really significant. And look, if it turns out that the recovery is slow, these are two of the most important swing states on the map. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  running neck and neck in North Carolina and Georgia. Every constituency matters. This could make a difference in a close election.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: But there's something else that could potentially hinder commerce in all of the swing states. If we go into a port strike tonight. Biden was asked if he would invoke Taft Hartley to stop a port strike from happening. Watch this exchange.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mister president, will you intervene in the dock workers strike if they go on strike on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Why not?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Because it's collective bargaining. I don't believe in Taft Hartley.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Collective bargaining. I don't believe in Taft Hartley. Obviously, he's concerned about the unions, but there are millions of other folks out there who may not be able to get what they need to get, particularly in time for Christmas. And he's going to leave his vice president with that.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: And the White House actually backed him up on that again today, \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  saying from the podium that he would not intervene and then raise collective bargaining again, raised the issue that he's been, as they say, the most pro-union president in history and from the White House podium and said that they again wanted to take politics out of this as well for the election.\par \par \uc2 But with the economy being the number one issue in poll after poll, it is certainly something that were this to happen and that's still quite speculative at this point, could definitely be something that could have an effect on the election.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: So how many Long Sherman are there compared to how many consumers are there at large?\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Right. I mean, that's a fair question. And I think, I'm sure the Harris campaign, just purely politics, they want no part of a strike here. They want no part of a protracted strike. This could be really problematic for the economy and messy. They want this resolved.\par \par \uc2 They're trying to sort of bring back some of the labor vote because we've seen that hemorrhaging and the Trump campaign gaining support there. So they're in a bit of a bind politically, but they would love for this to be resolved already yesterday.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: You got to wonder, didn't the Biden administration learn the first time around when the ports were having real problems with supply chain?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Yes, I mean, there's a lot of political risk. Biden has trumpeted his relationship with unions as a major theme of his presidency, but he didn't get the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  or Harris didn't get the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  endorsement. And it sounds like he's not willing to kind of work the phones and actually try to cut a last minute deal given his close relationships with a lot of these blue collar unions.\par \par \uc2 So this has a lot of political ramifications for Harris. If the economy takes a hit right before the election, would not be a good signal.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: It is important to note that there are other members of his administration, the White House said today, other senior White House officials who are on the phones while Biden himself has not gotten involved. They're on the phones with both sides at this point.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right. Want to go over this real quick because we understand that Israel has begun a limited ground incursion into southern Lebanon. This is what the Wall Street Journal Board said about the takeout of \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2  last week. Israel strike Friday against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leader \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2  justified defensive act against an enemy backed by Iran and bent on the Jewish state's destruction.\par \par \uc2 It's also a lesson for the Biden administration in how to deter an enemy using military force instead of hopeless pleading for restraint and deescalation. Guy is Israel really kind of showing the way here?\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Yes, through strength. It's what people in that neighborhood understand. And they have brought a world of hurt down on a lot of their enemies. And frankly, our enemies watching this wishy washy sort of tsking of the Israelis coming out of Washington and the Biden Harris administration is frankly embarrassing.\par \par \uc2 What America should be saying to Israel is, thank you, well done. Keep going and we will help as we can because the people that they're taking out not only want to destroy the Jewish state, they are stained with blood of Americans. So this is a great service Israel is performing for the world.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: So Josh, Biden was asked, what do you think about what Israel is doing? It says, I'm looking forward to a ceasefire. Michael Oren, who is the former Israeli ambassador to the U.S., was on my show earlier today and said this is an opportunity to completely changed the whole face of the Middle east. Why stop now?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: It's something of a muddled message from both Biden and the Harris campaign. Look, this is a moment of celebration. And if you look at the statements by both the president and the vice president, they celebrated the death of \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2 , the \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leader. But at the same time, they're like Israel. We don't want you to go too far. We don't want you to finish the job against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Look, we've interviewed a lot of Republicans and Democrats who have cheered Israel's operation. A little bit unusual for the White House not to get behind it fully. It's a real political opportunity to show that they're fully behind the Israelis.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right, well, we'll see where this goes as this operation begins. Gang, thanks very much. Francesca, great to see you again. It's been a long time. All right.\par \par \uc2 And finally tonight, a special day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, the crowd was definitely a bit louder for me at the end. I could feel them behind me as much as they were Kivo (ph) at the start, but it was fun.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Cliff divers from around the world gathered in Turkey to showcase their best stunts. Women jump from 69 feet, men from 89. Look at this. Into the Mediterranean Sea for the top title at the Cliff Diving World Series. Winners included a man from Great Britain and a woman from Australia. Somehow I don't know how they do it, but they all made it look very, very easy.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on Special Report, a preview of the vice presidential debate between democratic nominee \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  and Republican J.D. Vance. You can watch the debate simulcast in Fox News. We'll also have special coverage before and after the debate --\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020241001ek9u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Interview Senate Foreign Relations Committee Member, Sen. Chris Coons (D-DE); Interview With Former Secretary Of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 ; \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  Confirms Death of Leader \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2  Following IDF Strike; Iran Condemns Israeli Attacks in Lebanon; Harris Supports Getting Rid of Senate Filibuster to Pass Abortion Rights Bill; Harris Blames Trump for Tanking Border Bill; Final Weeks of Campaign 2024; Battle for Control of the Senate; ICE: More Than 425K Migrants Convicted Of Crimes That Are Not Being Detained; Fox Poll: Trump Leads Harris On Immigration, Border y At Least 15 Points In AZ, GA, NC, PA; U.S. Dockworkers On Verge Of Massive Strike; One Man Show Shares the Life and Legacy of Lincoln\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Mike Pompeo, Mark Meredith, Rich Edson, Mollie  Hemingway, Trey Yingst \par \uc2 9,641 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 September 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 Israel takes out the head of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and a top Iranian leader in Lebanon. Iran now vowing revenge.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DANIEL HAGARI, IDF SPOKESPERSON: \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  is dragging the state of Lebanon and the entire region into a wider escalation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The Islamic Republic of Iran will be on the side of Lebanon and resistance by all means.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): Fury from Iran after the Israeli airstrikes on the main \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leadership compound in Beirut.\par \par \uc2 Senate Foreign Relations Committee member Chris Coons joins us on what may happen ext in the volatile region.\par \par \uc2 Then, J.D. Vance and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  about to go head-to-head in their only scheduled faceoff before Election Day. Both VP candidates set to debate Tuesday just weeks before Election Day and with voting already underway in more than 20 states.\par \par \uc2 We'll talk with Trump-Vance surrogate, Senator Katie Britt.\par \par \uc2 Plus --\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES & 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: There are consequential issues at stake in this election and one is the security of our border.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Three years after her last visit, Vice President Harris travels to the border trying to close the gap with Trump on immigration.\par \par \uc2 Our Sunday panel weighs in on that and the shocking new number showing there are more than 13,000 non-citizens convicted of homicide apparently roaming free in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 And our Sunday special.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT BAKULA AS ABE LINCOLN: You can't be serious. You think I could make a formidable candidate for the presidency?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll take you behind the scenes with Scott Bakula playing Lincoln at the theater where an assassin took the life of our 16th president. It's a message for our divided times.\par \par \uc2 Do you see parallels now?\par \par \uc2 BAKULA: Oh, the parallels are too many to count.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Let's take a quick look at your headlines:\par \par \uc2 The Southeast is cleaning up behind the widespread path of destruction from Hurricane Helene. More than 60 people are dead from Florida to Virginia, and that death toll is expected to climb. Early estimates put the damage between $15 billion and $26 billion. Several dams across the region are threatening to break from all the record flooding.\par \par \uc2 If you can help, please visit redcross.org/foxforward to find out how you can donate money to the recovery effort. Or we've got a QR code there on your screen, you can hold up your phone and scan that.\par \par \uc2 More than two and a half million people are still without power. Georgia and the Carolinas particularly hard hit, millions of Americans still displaced and out of their homes. Some of the remnants of Helene are making their way up the East Coast today with more chances of local flooding in many areas.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the Middle East, the Israeli military announcing it has killed yet another top ranking \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  official in an airstrike, capping off a deadly week in Lebanon for the Iran-backed terror group which lost its leader, \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2 , in an Israeli strike in Beirut Friday.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we'll get reaction from Senator Chris Coons who sits on the Foreign Relations Committee.\par \par \uc2 But, first, we turn to Trey Yingst in Haifa, Israel.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Over the past 24 hours, hundreds of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  positions have been targeted across Lebanon, strong explosions rocked the skyline of Beirut this weekend, targeting specifically the stronghold of Dahieh, that was hit with Israeli airstrikes, taking out another \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  commander Nabil Kaouk, this after Israel confirmed Saturday it killed \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leader \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This has now given the region a new phase and unpredictable chapter in that same strike a deputy commander in Iran's IRGC was also killed, prompting more fears that Iran could decide to directly retaliate for the killings. Israeli forces remain on high alert for a possible response but a senior defense official told FOX News over the weekend that despite the continued rocket fire from Lebanon, the belief is that \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  is too disorganized at the moment to launch a serious counterattack of their own.\par \par \uc2 This comes as reports today indicate that Israel is conducting limited minor ground operations into Lebanon in preparation for an expected ground invasion. Thousands of Israeli troops are currently staging on Israel's northern border as President Biden calls for a ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Mr. President, is a ground incursion in Lebanon inevitable?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Time for a ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Will you respond to the missile attacks on the U.S. warships on the Red Sea?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We're responding.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: While the war unravels further, Lebanese civilians continue to evacuate southern Lebanon and southern Beirut, resulting in the displacement of families in the heart of the Lebanese capital. Some of Lebanon's most vulnerable residents with nowhere to go.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DALAL DAHER, LEBANESE RESIDENT FROM SOUTHERN BEIRUT (through translator): We are here because in our area, there are strikes and bombings and yesterday's strikes were unbelievable. We had fled before and then went back to our homes.\par \par \uc2 But then the bombing got more and more intense, so we came here waiting for Netanyahu to stop the bombing, so we can go back home.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: The United States is clearly looking for a diplomatic off-ramp here to avoid a broader regional war across the Middle East and prevent Israel from entering Lebanon on the ground -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Trey Yingst reporting from Israel -- thank you very much, Trey.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Delaware Democratic Senator Chris Coons, member of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back, Senator. Good to have you.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHRIS COONS (D-DE): Thanks, Shannon. It's always good to be on with you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to start here with what Prime Minister Netanyahu said at the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  just days ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER: As long as \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  chooses the path of war, Israel has no choice and Israel has every right to remove this threat and return our citizens to their homes safely, and that's exactly what we're doing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: He went on to imagine -- ask Americans to imagine what would happen if towns like El Paso and San Diego border towns were taking incoming fire, turned into complete ghost towns, he says. American officials would not stand for that for a single day.\par \par \uc2 And yet this administration continues to call for a ceasefire. You know, incoming has been happening since October 8th.\par \par \uc2 Why should we ask them to stand down? Do you think that's the right thing to do?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Well, let's be clear, Shannon, Vice President Harris put out a forceful statement as did President Biden that I joined in, saying that \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2 , the leader of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , is a murderous terrorist who had American blood on his hands from the attack on the American Marine barracks back in the 1980s and he's killed thousands of innocent civilians, Lebanese, Syrian, as well as Israeli.\par \par \uc2 And as you said in your question, they have been raining rockets and missiles onto northern Israel since October 7th.\par \par \uc2 So I think Israel is justified in their strikes against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The call for a ceasefire is really because of a concern that a ground invasion of southern Lebanon by the \uc2 IDF\uc2  might well turn into a long and grinding war of attrition and concerns that Iran may well now retaliate. But President Biden and Vice President Harris are clear that we will stand by our ally Israel and defend them against attacks by Iranian proxies or direct attacks by Iran.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Now, one of your GOP colleagues, Senator Ron Johnson, sees it differently. He says that Israel doesn't have the ability to fully trust us at this time.\par \par \uc2 Here's what he said yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON JOHNSON (R-WI): They always seem to want to throttle back Israel's ability and desire to defend itself. I think it's the very sad fact that Israel doesn't trust the United States under the leadership of President Biden and Vice President Harris. They just can't trust us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They don't have to ask our permission but there are things they're doing we're finding out when they're actually in play or after the fact.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of your colleague's assessment there that they can't trust us?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Ron's statement is just nonsense.\par \par \uc2 No one has been stronger in coming to the defense of Israel, especially after the hateful attacks by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  on October 7th than President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . In fact, in the first ever direct attack on Israel by Iran, the Biden-Harris administration coordinated a response by several of our close allies that ensure that none of the 300 missiles and drones that Iran launched at Israel hit any significant target or caused any major damage.\par \par \uc2 We have stood by Israel faithfully and loyally throughout the entire period that there are repeated attacks by \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  on the territory of Israel and by the Houthis on commercial shipping in the Red Sea.\par \par \uc2 So they should trust us. There have been some moments of tension because they don't inform us of what they're doing before they do. There is some question of course about the number of civilian deaths in Gaza as that war has ground on.\par \par \uc2 But the idea that there should be any question about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  standing strongly by Israel and their right to defend themselves from attack -- just nonsense.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to turn to another foreign hotpot which obviously is Ukraine and Russia. President Zelenskyy here in town asking for things this week meeting with the candidates, meeting with the current administration. What he really wants is the ability to use Western weapons deeper into Russia.\par \par \uc2 Putin out today, spokesman for the Kremlin saying they're considering changing their policy and their guidelines when it comes to the use of nuclear power.\par \par \uc2 Is that a threat? Is it bluffing? What do you think that the administration should do with respect to allowing those weapons for further use into Russia?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Well, look, we need to be careful because \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has shown that he's willing to take risks and gambles. Most of the world did not expect him to engage in the full broad spectrum invasion of Ukraine back in 2022, and President Biden and Vice President Harris have masterfully assembled a global coalition of 50 countries to defend Ukraine, to support Ukraine in their fight for freedom.\par \par \uc2 One of the more alarming things that President Trump and his running mate J.D. Vance have said is that they will somehow bring this war to an end in a day, and J.D. Vance in the Senate has questioned whether we should continue to support Ukraine's fight for freedom.\par \par \uc2 I think it's important that we give them the weapons, the tools, and the freedom to use those weapons in order to win this war against Russia.\par \par \uc2 But I respect that President Biden gets more detailed briefings than I do on the potential risks of escalation and when President Putin makes threats to potentially use tactical nuclear weapons, he has a very tough balancing act to strike.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, did -- were you encouraged by the meeting between President Trump and President Zelenskyy because they came out of it and seemed that they had met -- you know, had some kind of meeting of the minds, that they heard each other, I mean, President Trump said, you know, it hasn't changed where he is overall on trying to get this thing ended.\par \par \uc2 But even President Zelenskyy, he talked with our Griff Jenkins, too, sounded like he felt like it was a good meeting.\par \par \uc2 COONS: I'm glad they met. It's important that there be conversations between President Zelenskyy and both Republican and Democratic leaders of our nation, and candidates to be the next president of our nation.\par \par \uc2 But in the clip that you're showing right now, President Trump said, I'm a very good friend of Putin's and you could see Zelenskyy sort of stiffen up and go, well, I hope you're a better friend of Ukraine than you are a friend of Russia under Putin. So it was a strikingly awkward moment.\par \par \uc2 Putin is a war criminal. Putin has carried out horrific attacks in Ukraine. We should not have a president of the United States saying that he is seeking to be a better friend of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. Well, I want to ask you before you go quickly about our own border because we got numbers from ICE late in the week that showed a picture that was pretty shocking for most Americans, that there are hundreds of thousands of non-citizens here who have already been convicted of a crime and are not in ICE custody. That includes, by their numbers, more than 13,000 people convicted of homicide.\par \par \uc2 Were those numbers a shock to you? And what can and should we be doing about -- people, you know, get upset talking about mass deportations -- but targeting people that have been convicted criminals, murderers, rapists, should we not have an effort to get that done?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Shannon, thanks for a chance to talk to that, because that's a misleading number. Those 13,000 convicted of homicide, they may not be in ICE custody but most of them are in prison, state, federal or local prisons, and will be deported as soon as they finish serving their prison terms.\par \par \uc2 Under the Biden-Harris administration, they have prioritized the deportation of those who've committed violent crimes and have in fact deported them at a 50 percent higher rate than under former President Trump who didn't make that a key priority.\par \par \uc2 If we had been able to pass the tough bipartisan border security deal that my friends Senator Lankford and Murphy and Sinema negotiated, we would have $20 billion more being invested in deportations and be prioritizing the deportation of violent criminals, something all of us should want.\par \par \uc2 That bill didn't pass only because former President Trump called his allies in the Senate and said don't pass it. He wanted an issue, not a solution.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, well, as you know --\par \par \uc2 COONS: Vice President Harris has made it clear she would sign that bill into law.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: As you know, many of your Republican colleagues said they would not have voted for it anyway because they felt it contained many magnets to bring people here, work permits, and other things, and codified a flow that they already think is out of control. And they ask questions about if we're not enforcing current law, why would we enforce that bill if it was passed.\par \par \uc2 We'll see. If she becomes president, if you guys hold the Senate, maybe it comes up for another vote and we'll be watching that.\par \par \uc2 Senator Coons --\par \par \uc2 COONS: It will and it'll make us stronger. Thank you very much, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right. Joining us now, former Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Secretary, good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of what the senator had to say about the fact that the Biden-Harris administration has been full-throated and unequivocal in supporting Israel?\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: Well, good morning, Shannon