s collapsed. At that point, Iran's credibility with the rest of its partners in the access of regional militant groups will collapse, will risk collapsing if Tehran does not react." Do you worry about where this has pushed Iran to and what happens next?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Shannon, Trita Parsi has been an Iranian sympathizer for a long time. He works closely alongside some of the leaders inside of the country. The way to convince Iran to stay out of this conflict is to demonstrate that the Americans and the Israelis together can hold Iranian leadership to account, impose real costs on them.\par \par \uc2 When they see the leadership of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  destroyed, when they see \uc2 Hamas\uc2  dismantled, when they see the United States prepared to actually support that, and they see our Gulf partners recognize that they too can trust the United States of America, that's how you keep Iran at bay. It's how you push back against what, frankly, the Biden-Harris administration has allowed, the continuation of the Iranian nuclear program to build. There's only one way. You have to put pressure on them. You have to deny them resources. You can't hand them billions of dollars and then tell the Israelis to stand down and have any prayer.\par \par \uc2 Then not only will Israel do well, but we shouldn't forget this, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and Nasrallah killed more Muslims than just about anybody else over the last decades. This is a regional challenge. It's one for the world. And the United States demanding that Israel stop when the task ahead of them is to finish the job is a mistake and only creates further risk of escalation.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, it seemed wise for Prime Minister Netanyahu to speak at the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  about the people of Iran and people of Lebanon, the people who live in Gaza, and wanting to rescue them from their oppressors too, to your point.\par \par \uc2 Secretary, thank you.\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Coming up, we hit the campaign trail with live reports from the Trump and Harris camps in battleground states Wisconsin and Nevada. And we'll talk with Trump fan surrogate Senator Katie Britt of Alabama. She's next. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: As the VP candidates prepare for their debate Tuesday in New York, their running mates have been hard at work trying to win over voters in key battleground states. We've got team coverage.\par \par \uc2 Rich Edson in Wisconsin, where President Trump held a rally Saturday and Mark Meredith in Las Vegas, where the Vice President is set to rally this evening. And that's where we began.\par \par \uc2 Mark, with you. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, good morning to you. Vice President Harris is spending more time and energy trying to win over voters on three key issues, the economy, immigration and abortion. On Friday, Harris was in Arizona touring the U.S. southern border. Republicans blame Harris, who they've dubbed the border czar for the massive influx of migrants into the U.S. over recent years. But now Harris is trying to shift the blame to former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: The American people deserve a president who cares more about border security than playing political games.\par \par \uc2 While we understand that many people are desperate to migrate to the United States, our system must be orderly and secure.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: On the economy, Harris told a crowd in Pittsburgh she supports capitalism, a direct response to Trump's claims that she's a socialist. And on abortion, Harris is doubling down on a past pledge to end the Senate's filibuster rule to pass legislation she says would restore Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Abortion is one of many topics likely to come up on Tuesday night at the first and only vice presidential debate. Minnesota Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  will face off against Ohio Senator J.D. Vance. Walz has spent the last week in the Midwest on debate prep, even enlisting Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  to portray Vance.\par \par \uc2 Now, the debate will last for 90 minutes. There's not going to be any studio audience. But Shannon, a little bit different with this debate than the past ones. The microphones of both candidates will be left on throughout the entire event.\par \par \uc2 Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That could make it very interesting. All right. Mark Meredith, live in Nevada. Thank you, Mark.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to Rich Edson in Wisconsin. Hey, Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS STATE DEPARTMENT CORRESPONDENT: Hey, good morning, Shannon. Well, you've got Wisconsin, Pennsylvania, then back here to Wisconsin. That's former President Trump's schedule. He's hosting a rally this afternoon in Erie, Pennsylvania. That's after yesterday, an immigration-focused event in Wisconsin where he ridiculed Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  on her visit to the border.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT, CURRENT PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: She is a disaster. And she's not going to ever do anything for the border. And she didn't even want to get tough now, except her poll numbers were tanking.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: The Harris campaign is blaming Trump for tanking that bipartisan border bill and says that the former president wants to run on a problem instead of fixing it. Trump's running mate, Senator J.D. Vance, held a rally yesterday in Pennsylvania. He's also prepping for his debate now Tuesday with Minnesota Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Wisconsin is a key swing state. So is Pennsylvania. We have new "Fox News" polling there in Pennsylvania. Harris leads Trump by two percentage points among registered voters. Among likely voters, they are tied at 49% each.\par \par \uc2 Shannon?\p