ronger supporter of IVF than President Trump.\par \par \uc2 If you think about the situation that occurred in Alabama, President Trump came out immediately and said that he strongly supported IVF and he encouraged the Alabama legislature to act. They acted within six days of that, Shannon. And our Republican legislature and our Republican governor signed IVF protection into law.\par \par \uc2 We've continued to talk about this. Senator Ted Cruz and I led a letter with all 49 Republican senators signing on saying they strongly support nationwide access to IVF. And the truth is Democrats continue to use this issue as a scare tactic because IVF is legal and accessible in all 50 states. And President Trump has made it perfectly clear that he'll continue to make sure that that's the case.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So you voted against a measure, though, that was offered up by Democrats on IVF in the Senate there. And they continue to point to that to say Republicans aren't supportive of protecting IVF. Why did you vote no?\par \par \uc2 BRITT: So, Shannon, I'm so glad you broke that and brought this up. This is politics at its worst. If you think about what \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is doing, instead of putting funding bills on the floor that are so critically important to actually doing our job for the American people, of which he put none on the floor, instead of putting the National Defense Authorization Act on the floor so that our servicemen and women can be the best trained, equipped and ready across the planet, instead of putting the farm bill on the floor, which we do every five years, and our farmers are hurting. You can talk to any farmer in any corner of this country, and they say it is challenging to make ends meet. And we know food security as national security.\par \par \uc2 Instead of putting any of those bills on the floor, Shannon, he chose to put an IVF bill on the floor for a show vote. That bill extended into human cloning. That bill did not protect religious freedom. Something that we have agreed is a bipartisan issue, something we do, you know, consistently. This time they chose to rip that out.\par \par \uc2 I have been a -- there has been probably no vocal, more vocal supporter outside of President Trump than myself when it comes to making sure we have nationwide access to IVF. You have to get to 60 votes in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . Not one member of the Democratic Party came over to try to garner enough votes for 60. It was simply a show vote. It was never intended to pass. He would not allow us to have time for amendments or anything else.\par \par \uc2 So it's just summer, it's Schumer's summer of scare tactics that he has moved into the fall of fear-mongering. I think it's despicable because he is stoking fear that should not exist, and unfortunately, he is constantly playing politics and the American people lose when \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 's in charge.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, DNC Chair \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2  feels good about where they are. Obviously, the Senate is in play as well. Republicans are hoping to flip it, but he says he's feeling bullish on places like Montana, Ohio. He says even Florida and Texas and Missouri.\par \par \uc2 Is it possible that Republicans are overconfident? Is this possibly another midterm 2022 when that red wave did not materialize the way that you all had hoped? Because Democrats say when it comes to this issue of abortion, which is on a lot of state ballots as well, they feel like it's going to put them over the top and they're going to maintain control of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 BRITT: Look, I was actually with Steve Daines last night, who is the Chair of the NRSC, senator there from Montana. He is working diligently to ensure that we make sure that we take back the Senate. And of course, we hope to do that in a big fashion, but he is being diligent in every single sector.\par \par \uc2 We're taking nothing for granted. Even in places where we're ahead, we are working like we are five points behind. Because Shannon, we believe that the future of our country depends on it.\par \par \uc2 When you see \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  saying and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  saying they will break the filibuster to do radical things, and you know, whether it's taking gas cap powered cars off of the road, whether it is banning fracking when we know that we not only need to be energy independent, we need to return to the energy dominance of President Trump, where we look at our border. We know what's at stake.\par \par \uc2 Look, President Trump had the most secure border of all time. And now we look at what we have under Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's borders are. We have over 425,000 convicted criminals on the non-detained docket for ICE.\par \par \uc2 Look, I ran these numbers last year. They're up over 1.5 million from the numbers we had last year on the non-detained docket. Of that, we know to your earlier point when you opened the show, 13,000 convicted murderers. Do you understand that? Convicted murderers roaming our streets. We have 16,000 convicted, you know, people that are convicted of sexual assault.\par \par \uc2 Our country is not stronger. It is not safer. We've heard already on the show, literally, the world is on fire. Under President Trump, we actually got to see what peace through strength meant.\par \par \uc2 Putin never invaded Ukraine under President Trump. We see the Middle East. President Trump was able to institute the Abraham Accords, moving the embassy to Jerusalem. There has been no more pro-Israel president than President Trump. The world was a more stable place. Our border was more secure. Our dollar went further.\par \par \uc2 And ultimately, as women look and look at the next generation and they want to leave this country better for their children.\par \par \uc2 And when you watch this debate on Tuesday night and you see \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  be ask the question, are we better off than we are four years ago? He, just like \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , will not be able --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 BRITT: -- to answer that question.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let me --\par \par \uc2 BRITT: Because the answer to that is no.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Let me just say a couple things. You heard your colleague, Senator Coons, he said of those numbers of the people who are free here in the country, he says many of them are incarcerated. We've asked DHS for clarification on those numbers as well.\par \par \uc2 But I very quickly want to give you a chance to respond to what he said about Ukraine, that President Trump doesn't have the best interests of those folks at heart.\par \par \uc2 I know there was a meeting this week with President Zelenskyy, but there are many who worry about the Trump position, the Vance position, that it will include ceding some territory to Putin as a way to end this and that being, you know, something that the Ukrainian people do not want to accept. Quick comment from you on Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 BRITT: Look, there is a reason that President Putin only invaded Ukraine under \uc2 Obama\uc2 -Biden, and then again, under Biden-Harris.\par \par \uc2 The world respects President Trump. And I believe that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  does as well. And so I know that President Trump met with Zelenskyy. He understands the importance of standing firmly with our allies.\par \par \uc2 And you look at what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has done and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , slow-walking resources to Ukraine, and their disastrous withdrawal from Afghanistan, disastrous withdrawal, has created a ripple effect across this globe where people do not respect us.\par \par \uc2 The moment that we -- President Trump gets back in office, people will respect the United States and know what we mean, what we say, and we say what we mean. He will protect and stand firmly with our servicemen and women, and he will back the blue.\par \par \uc2 We will have law and order once again, safer streets. And we will have a more peaceful globe, guaranteed.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Well, about five weeks to go, we'll see what the American people decide.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 BRITT: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Thousands of U.S. port workers could walk off the job as early as Tuesday, throwing a wrench into our economy just weeks before the election. Our Sunday panel is here to debate the political fallout if dockworkers make good on their threats to strike.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And as president, I won't only bring back the border security bill that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tanked, I will do more to secure our border.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We're like a dumping ground. Over 647,570 migrant criminals are in our country.\par \par \uc2 You can't have her as your president. She doesn't know what the hell she's doing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Vice President Harris and President Trump each making the case that they are the one best equipped to handle the border crisis. It's time now for our Sunday group. See what they think.\par \par \uc2 "USA Today" White House correspondent Francesca Chambers. Mollie Hemingway, editor-in-chief of "The Federalist." Former 2020 Biden-Harris surrogate, \uc2 Kevin Walling\uc2 . And Mario Parker, \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  managing editor for \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. So we've heard these numbers. DHS is taking issue with them. But if we're talking about people who are released into this country, or at least from ICE custody, on the issue of homicide, the ICE letter that had all these numbers in it said this, most non-citizens who are convicted of homicide are typically not eligible for release from ICE custody, but in limited circumstances, ICE officers may use their discretion in making custody determinations and release non-citizens with conditions.\par \par \uc2 Mollie, that sounds like an acknowledgment that some of these people convicted of homicide are free in the country.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR-IN-CHIEF, THE FEDERALIST: Right. And even Senator Coons was saying that only a majority of people might be in state prisons or other facilities.\par \par \uc2 What we've seen in the last four years is an absolute explosion on the non- detained list. From 3.2 million illegal crossers in 2020 to 7.4 million, I think it is now. This is a result of just millions upon millions of people crossing into the border -- crossing the border. And yes, we have. And this could be from prior to the Harris administration.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: But we have 13,000 convicted murderers, nearly 16,000 convicted rapists, 56,000 people convicted of dangerous drug crimes. Whether that happened in a different country or this country, this is clearly not a cohesive immigration policy.\par \par \uc2 And it's clearly a result of the Biden administration going out of its way on day one. Biden and Harris worked to remove all of the protections that the Trump administration had painstakingly provided.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  says that she opposes migrant detention facilities. She doesn't support the wall. She compared ICE to the KKK and thinks they should be abolished. She wants free healthcare for illegal border crossers. That includes, in her mind, sex change surgeries paid for by taxpayers.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is a radically different position than the Trump administration, which sought to control the border.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and border patrol reaction to her going down there to visit in Douglas, Arizona on Friday was, Francesca, they called it a slap in the face, not only to border patrol, but to Americans as well. They said she not only ignored the problem, in their estimation, she helped create it.\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, \uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 : So Vice President Harris is trying to reframe the debate away from the issues that Mollie is talking about in areas that are more in her background, that are more in her court. She wants to talk about fentanyl. And she also wants to talk about transnational crime. Why is that?\par \par \uc2 Because the Biden administration cracked down on China, they say, when it comes to fentanyl precursors in the country. When she talks about her record as attorney general on transnational crime, it allows her to talk about going after drugs, guns, as well as the trafficking of people.\par \par \uc2 Why did she go to Arizona, though? When you look at the polling and Fox's own polling, she's down two points to Trump in the state of Arizona. In the USA Today/\uc2 Suffolk University\uc2  poll, that was six points. This is all in the margin of error.\par \par \uc2 But importantly, look at the issues. And that Fox poll, 15 points down in Arizona when it comes to the issue of immigration.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. And let me put some of this up because we've got several key battleground states that we did this polling and asked this question about who's better to control and help with the border.\par \par \uc2 Arizona, Trump's up 15. Georgia, he's up 15. North Carolina, up 15. Mario, in Pennsylvania, up 17 on that issue.\par \par \uc2 MARIO PARKER, BLOOMBERG MANAGING EDITOR FOR U.S. GOVERNMENT: Yes. And what you're saying again is Harris trying to shore up these vulnerabilities. This was a week where you saw her do that, both on the economic part where she released a report, but also with a visit to the border.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have been hitting her and not visiting the border for some time, right? We remember her first major misstep as vice president in 2021 when she gave the interview and she said, well, I haven't been to Europe either.\par \par \uc2 She's not going to over -- there's no world where she's going to overtake \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  among voters on the border, which you're seeing her do though is embrace a different tactic to say that we do need enforcement on the border, a sharp departure from what she ran on in 2019, and a sharp departure from how Democrats have talked about just rejiggering the entire system.\par \par \uc2 If she can cut into some of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's lead, again, she won't overtake him. The hope is that with this game of inches in the polls, that maybe she at least gets another two points or three points that could carry her electorate.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. That can make a huge difference.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, she may have another headache on her hands if there's this huge dockworker strike, which the White House is saying they're staying out of it. It could come as early as Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "POLITICO" says this, intervening to stop the strike could sour labor voters on Harris, who's trying to shore up support from blue collar workers who could decide the election in critical swing states.\par \par \uc2 Kevin, we've all heard the dire predictions about no vegetables, no bananas, no car parts. If this dockworker strike happens, it could be a massive impact and a hit on the economy.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN WALLING\uc2 , DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: It certainly can. And that's what most economists are saying, that this would be one of the most dangerous economic situations to play out that we've seen since COVID.\par \par \uc2 This is affecting 70,000 workers in contract negotiations right now. That contract expires, of course, on Tuesday, which is, you know, five weeks out from the election, another two months out from the Christmas season, which could be really dangerous for this economy.\par \par \uc2 But certainly, I think you're seeing an administration want to support both sides in this negotiation. The vast majority of organized labor is behind the vice president for her commitment to standing with workers. She's one of the first vice presidents and only