 to talk about a ceasefire. What they are trying to do in the U.S. is lay the groundwork for when that conversation can happen.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, "The New York Post," its editorial board, has been very critical of the way this administration has handled this, saying, "If Biden-Harris had any true compassion, any desire for real peace and not just a three-week joke of a ceasefire for the benefit of anti-Israel voters, they'd encourage Israel to finish off the terrorists and maybe even help. Instead, the Pentagon says the U.S. military won't provide intelligence or other support for Israeli operations in Lebanon."\par \par \uc2 Kevin.\par \par \uc2 WALLING: Well, certainly I don't believe that's the case. You know, Secretary Austin has had no less than three phone conversations just over the weekend with his counterpart, with the \uc2 IDF\uc2 . So that support for Israeli forces remains steadfast and lockstep. You saw that double down at \uc2 UNGA\uc2  last week with the president's remarks in support of Israel and on the sidelines, obviously, in support of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And you compare that to the language coming out of the Trump campaign in terms of our support for allies in remaining steadfast. So there's a contrast, I think, in terms of the world stage and in terms of that messaging, but there is no daylight between this administration and it support of the state of Israel.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, "THE FEDERALIST" EDITOR-IN-CHIEF AND FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I could not agree more that there is a major contrast between the Trump administration and the Biden administration, the Biden-Harris administration, on these issues. You'll remember during the Trump administration you had peace breaking out all over the world. You had peace breaking out in the Middle East with - with economic agreements between Israel and all of its neighbors, and isolating Iran, and you did not have Putin invading Ukraine. With this - elections have consequences. And with this Biden-Harris administration, you've got tremendous war and you've got a risk of World War III breaking out.\par \par \uc2 The - the situation in Israel is one thing. The situation in Ukraine has been a disaster for the people of Ukraine. You have a million casualties. You've lost so much of the population. You have the economic catastrophe. And there are no statesmen in D.C. talking about how to end this conflict with the least damage possible to Ukraine, except for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and - and, listen, Zelenskyy was here this week. He met with both Harris and Trump. He met with the current administration. I would have loved to have been a fly on the wall, Mario. That meeting between Presidents Trump and Zelenskyy.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: Indeed.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But they came out seeming like they'd had some kind of meaningful conversation.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: It looked OK, right? The body language looked OK at best. But just speaking with folks that are close to the former president, I mean they were offended, right, of how this all unfolded. For Zelenskyy to visit Pennsylvania, a battleground state that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had just been campaigning in, signing artillery on a government plane, no Republicans in the delegation, alongside Josh Shapiro, I mean that was just a tough one. So, tensions are - remain, but at least there is somewhat of a detente.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and it does seem that for now bipartisan support, there's enough of it on The Hill, more weapons, more money flowing that way.\par \par \uc2 WALLING: Thank God.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And we'll see whether the White House decides to allow further use of the weapons they have sent.\par \par \uc2 OK, panel, thank you very much. We'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a behind the scenes look with a new play here in the nation's capital actor Scott Bakula becomes \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2 , bringing the legacy of our 16th president to light at the very sight of his death.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You may know something about the history of what happened here at Ford's Theatre, but there's plenty you don't know about the life of President \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2 . Coming up we'll take you inside a new production here that's got some surprises.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's been nearly 160 years since an assassin's bullet cut \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2 's life short. Now, as America finds itself in yet another era of stark, political divisions, actor Scott Bakula is bringing the 16th president's legacy and message back into the spotlight as he portrays Lincoln in a one-man show at Ford's Theatre, the site where he was mortally wounded.\par \par \uc2 It's our "Sunday Special."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT BAKULA, ACTOR, "MISTER LINCOLN": Well, I have to be honest, Jesse, I must admit that the taste is in my mouth a little to become president. But there are other men far more capable than I am.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): Actor Scott Bakula is known for taking audiences back in time, with his hit science-fiction series "Quantum Leap." But this fall the longtime veteran as stage and screen shares a very different glimpse into the past with "Mister Lincoln," and intimate one-man play that pulls back the curtain on the triumphs, tragedies, and decisions that shaped one of history's most reviewed leaders, in our nation's most divided moment.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You are a seasoned, award-winning actor, but how do you tackle something like this?\par \par \uc2 BAKULA: It's daunting. Not just the fact of doing a one-man show, by doing Lincoln. The play goes from all different aspects and - and approaches in his life and times in his life. If you were memorizing the Gettysburg Address, that's one kind of work because that's something that I kind of - in your head you kind of know. But if I'm talking to his - to Mary Lincoln, that's a different kind of memorization.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): Adding to the emotional weight of Bakula's onstage performance is the play's venue, Ford's Theatre, the very sight of Lincoln's assassination.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The gravity of being in this place is partially because of the history that was made here.\par \par \uc2 BAKULA: Yes, that's the Lincoln Box. That is a sacred space up there.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But you mentioned, it is going to be referenced in the show?\par \par \uc2 BAKULA: Yes, very much so. It's -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: There's acknowledgment of it.\par \par \uc2 BAKULA: Yes. Right - almost right from the get-go. And I think it was important to do that because otherwise it just looms over the - over the top - over the whole show. And the show isn't about that.\par \par \uc2 You don't know what got us there, and you don't what happened to that man before.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And it sounds like people will, obviously, be entertained, but they're going to be educated at the same time.\par \par \uc2 BAKULA: Yes. Yes, they are.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): Educating audiences on what led to such a pivotal moment in our nation's history is a critical part of the theatre's mission. The venue features a one-of-a-kind museum where dozens of artifacts, including the small derringer pistol used by John Wilkes Booth offer a unique view into a country torn apart by war. And Lincoln's ultimately fatal struggle to put it back together.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: This is so much more than a theatre here.\par \par \uc2 PAUL TETREAULT, DIRECTOR, FORD'S THEATRE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's really special.\par \par \uc2 TETREAULT: It's a working theatre, but it's also a history place. It's a museum. And to be able to see, you know, this is the knife that John Wilkes Booth held up and yelled, "sic semper tyrannis" 160 years ago, we have that here!\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): But despite its tragic past, theatre director Paul Tetreault says political violence isn't the main takeaway from this historic space.\par \par \uc2 TETREAULT: We use as assassination as an entry point, but what we really want to talk about is Lincoln's legacy. And you look back at Lincoln and you think, you know, he was just a man.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): That humanity, along with Lincoln's unshakable resolve in the face of war, political division, and personal heartache, is what the play's leading man hopes the audience discovers about the 16th president.\par \par \uc2 BAKULA: I get surprised every day. I didn't know that he had loaded his cabinet with people that he ran against, that were - so he had voices in there all the time constantly that were - had the other side of an opinion, or - or - or not necessarily had his back. I didn't know that when he lost his second son in - that he was heard at night howling in pain. You could hear him outside the White House. He dealt with so much death in his life, and figured out how to keep going because he believed in something and he believed he had a mission. And - and he stayed with it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): Of course, to many, the mission of uniting a divided America seems just as daunting in 2024 as 1864.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And, of course, we think of President Lincoln, and - and just how deep those divisions, how split this country was. Do you see parallels now?\par \par \uc2 BAKULA: Oh, the parallels are - are - are too many to count. We are in a time now where it seems a lot of people feel like there's no going back or whatever. All the rhetoric. And we can't reach across the aisle. We can't - we can't solve this problem. And the civil war was pretty serious. You can't get worse than that.\par \par \uc2 But I think, at the end of the day, we have to say that - and this play is about that, but that there is hope, because we're here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: There is hope.\par \par \uc2 "Mister Lincoln" plays at Ford's Theatre until October 13th. If you happen to find yourself here in the nation's capital, you can get more information and tickets to the show at - the museum as well, at fords.org.\par \par \uc2 And just a quick note, my podcast, "Livin' the Bream" drops today. This week I sat down with Fox News chief foreign correspondent Trey Yingst to discuss his brand-new book "Black Saturday." Trey and his team have been covering the October 7th massacre and all the follow for nearly a year now. "Black Saturday" takes you inside the conflict with the stories of real people across the region. It is available starting Tuesday. You can preorder now. Get the book anywhere you like to get your books, and the podcast where you like to get your podcast.\par \par \uc2 And a quick programming note. Tun into Fox News Democracy `24 special coverage of the CBS News vice presidential debate simulcast. Fox News coverage on the channel begins Tuesday, October 1st, at 8:00 p.m. Eastern. And I'll be here on all of your Fox local stations 9:00 p.m. for coverage.\par \par \uc2 That's it for today. Thanks for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a wonderful week. We'll see you back here for the next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020240929ek9t00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Wants You To Forget She Created This Mess; Border Has Been Overrun Under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ; Trump's Plan To Save The Auto Industry; Biden Calls Kamala Harris Boss; Kamala Goes To The Border For Photo Op; Kamala Chameleon Suddenly Cares About The Border; The Best And Worst Political Ads Of The Week.\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Laura Ingraham, Bill Melugin, Raymond Arroyo, Sean  Duffy \par \uc2 7,802 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 September 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: Griff Jenkins has an exclusive interview with Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 . There's a lot to watch. Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 06:00 p.m. in the East, 03:00 p.m. on the West Coast. Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight and all this week. That's it for the Special Report. Fair, balanced, and still unafraid. The Ingraham Angle starts --\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening, everyone. I'm Laura Ingraham. This is The Ingraham Angle from Washington tonight. A lot happening here in the United States, of course, in the Middle East, rockets flying, looks like Israel may have taken out the leader of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . We'll stay on that. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is taking the stage any moment for a town hall in Warren, Michigan, all important Michigan focused on the economy and saving the auto industry.\par \par \uc2 And I hope you're sitting down for this. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has made a trip to the border. Yes. Hell has frozen over. She's going to speak live later this hour. But first, Kamala's high crimes at the border. That's the focus of tonight's Angle.\par \par \uc2 So, as I said, she's hitting the border, hoping to turn the page on her reputation as an open border zealot, but instead, the book was open to reveal the stomach churning truth about the criminal element she welcomed in. In response to a congressional request for information, ICE revealed that more than 13,000 illegal aliens convicted of murder are now in the United States. Oh, and they're not in custody.\par \par \uc2 More than 1800 have been accused of murder as well. Nearly 16,000 have been convicted of sexual assault. But the Harris campaign is out with a new ad celebrating her border cred.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has never backed down from a challenge. She put cartel members and drug traffickers behind bars, and she will secure our border.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, someone got paid huge money to produce that ad. They really think Americans are that stupid? Yes, that's what they think. Now her role as California's A.G. mostly involves state level crimes, not big cartel cases. And News Nation notes that her claims about jailing gang members for cross-border smuggling also seems inflated, because her cases mostly involve local drug offenses, not international smuggling operations.\par \par \uc2 And in the Tucson sector, where she's speaking today, under Harris and Biden Customs Border Protection logged 1.2 million, as they call them, encounters, since they took office. Now, during Trump's four years in office, almost 1 million fewer came in, or 246,000. Bill Melugin will give us key details and the total numbers coming up, they're truly shocking.\par \par \uc2 Now, this has already devastated cities and towns across America, and there's no other conclusion to draw except that Biden and Harris and Mayorkas conspired to intentionally subvert America's sovereignty and the rule of law.\par \par \uc2 Look, they believe borders are immoral, unless they're Ukraine's. They want an unending torrent of cheap labor flooding into the United States. And this is key, they want to change the electorate. Yes, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is right.\par \par \uc2 Now, if this isn't impeachable conduct, I don't know what is and I said this years ago, by the way, how is this not a high crime? High crimes and misdemeanors. Americans are dying because of these policies. And remember back in 2019, Democrats impeached \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  based on what he said on a phone call with Zelenskyy.\par \par \uc2 Now, Democrats spent countless manhours, untold amounts of taxpayer money on a sham proceeding meant to hobble his presidency and affect the campaign. And the catalyst, let's not forget, where Ukrainian apologists working in the White House.\par \par \uc2 Yet Joe and Kamala fling open our borders to millions of foreigners, they fast track them into our communities. They destroy local cultures. They strain already strained budgets, and all along we find out they've tried to hide the truth about the criminals that they allowed to walk right in and brutalize us. Again, I ask, how is that not a high crime in and of itself?\par \par \uc2 The families of the victims who Biden and Harris have left behind probably have another word for this. I would imagine the word treason is on their minds. Now look at these faces. They have been arrested for everything from rape, murder, carjacking, robbery, domestic, assault, DWI. None of them should have been here, and Harris's empty border rhetoric tonight does nothing to change the suffering endured by these families.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The horrors that my daughter suffered. and it's because of these open borders,\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: They are nothing but monsters who are predators, and those are the kind of individuals that we so openly let in this country. I believe the Biden-Harris administration open border policies are responsible for the death of my daughter.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and every Democrat who supports these open border policies are complicit in the deaths of 300,000 innocent Americans, including my son, Weston.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now it took ICE, by the way, about six months to get back to Congressman Gonzalez when he requested this information. But Harris has been so intent on keeping this out of the news that she won't even speak to her own administration's border chief, and she didn't speak to the last border patrol chief either.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RAUL ORTIZ, FORMER BORDER PATROL CHIEF: I've never had one conversation with the President or the Vice President for that matter, and so, I was the Chief of the Border Patrol. I commanded 21,000 people. That's a problem.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But pretending there's not a crisis of your own making is not a strategy, Madam Vice President. It's a tacit admission of failure. The border troll, by the way, it doesn't really need more resources. What it needs is a president who believes that it's the fundamental duty, really the first duty of a president to secure our borders, and her actions over the past three and a half years, that's all we need to know about her belief in our system and our sovereignty. If stopping fentanyl at the border was a priority, other nations would have been put on notice by \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Mexico would have been warned of dire trade consequences as they were under Trump.\par \par \uc2 If it didn't, you know, like stopping human trafficking is that priority for her, if they really cared about children that they now use as pawns, they would have kept Remain in Mexico in place, But instead, on day one, Joe and Kamala dismantled every effective Trump border policy that was in place and that kept America safe.\par \par \uc2 And by the 