rt them, and load them onto airplanes and fly them right into the American heartland.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, I cannot imagine anything harder, more difficult, or more demoralizing than signing up to defend America and being forced by \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to participate in its destruction. You talked about those numbers that you've led with today. Think about that.\par \par \uc2 Think about being an ICE officer and having to release, 425,000, 425,000 convicted criminals, 13,000 murderers, 15,000 rapists, and those are only the ones that we know about. The suffering, the misery, the bloodshed that she has unleashed through her policies. As President Trump said, today it's unforgivable. Some things in life cannot be parted. This is unforgivable, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Sean, when we think about what they did to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  with that obscene impeachment in 2019, because Vindman and company decided this was their moment, what is this what they've done? They fundamentally changed our country, harmed Americans. Their policies have ended up killing Americans. How is this really not rising to the level of impeachable conduct?\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: We know -- I think there's a two sets of rules, Laura. Republicans play by one set, which are the old rules, and Democrats play by the new set of rules, and they bludgeon the crap out of Republicans. And I think to your point, we all have to play by the same set of rules, and I think Republicans have to start playing the same game. This is an impeachable offense, no doubt.\par \par \uc2 But I come back to what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says on the immigration issue all the time, which is they open up their prisons, they let the worst people out of their prisons and ship them to America. Well now these numbers that we're talking about, of course, that's true.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, who's at the southern border vetting the people that come to our border? How do you let in all of these murderers and sexual predators into our country? They're not being vetted. So, anyone who comes to the border, in essence, they all get in. That's the policy and the plan of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 My concern, though, is the border's working. The border issue works for Trump in Arizona. It doesn't have the same potency in places like Georgia for the Fox polling. I think he has to hit this issue harder, how it plays in every single state, as well as the economy and double team it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I agree. Women are being affected by this open border. They're victims of the crimes more than men are. Women across the country, this is coming to a town and a city near you. Sean and Stephen, both of you, thank you tonight.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Thanks, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, we're still waiting for this photo op speech of Kamala's at the border. We're going to bring it to you in part live when she does. But coming up, Trump hits back at copy-cut cat Kamala. I said copy-cut Kamala. The Angle's 2024 ad war, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This is a Fox News alert, Kamala in Douglas, Arizona, for her first trip to the border in three years. She's coming out of the stage any moment. We'll bring it to you live when she does. But first, the 2024 ad war, that's well underway. We've seen some fabulous stunners and some downright stinky ones.\par \par \uc2 Each week of the campaign, we're going to dissect them, Republican, Democrat, issue our own report card. Joining me now, Terry Schilling, president of the American Principles Project. All right, Terry, we played some of this before. But since Kamala finally made it to the border, I want to get your thoughts on an immigration ad of hers. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Here's her plan. Hire thousands more border agents, enforce the law and step up technology, and stop fentanyl smuggling and human trafficking.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Terry, how do you grade it?\par \par \uc2 TERRY SCHILLING, PRESIDENT, AMERICAN PRINCIPLES PROJECT: Laura, I give that a D. It's a message that's not going to resonate with voters because it brings them to a very obvious question. Why haven't you done this in the past three and a half years? It's not credible. I give it a D.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah. Like, let's stop fentanyl smuggling. I'll do it. OK. You're right. So, it's a tough spot she's in with that ad. And it's why it's tough for her at this late stage to go to the border.\par \par \uc2 But let's move on to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Now he's seizing on Kamala being essentially a carbon copy of Biden. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT, 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: She doesn't have a plan. She copied Biden's plan.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: I build back better framework.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: I build back better framework.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: To a steady and stable growth.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Stable and steady growth.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Terry, Biden almost seems talented now compared to Kamala. I mean, that's the only problem I would say with that, but what's your grade?\par \par \uc2 SCHILLING: Laura, I give that an A. It's a very powerful ad. It's very important. If you watch the legacy media, if you watch Kamala's ads, you're going to think she wasn't part of this administration. It takes \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and ties her right back to her.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, a PAC supporting Cruz in Texas, Ted Cruz rolled out a $3 million ad buy against that Democrat Congressman \uc2 Colin Allred\uc2 . And its latest ad, I think it's getting a lot of attention online. Check this out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Texas girls are tough.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: You got this, let's do it again.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: But they're facing a new opponent they can't beat by digging deeper. \uc2 Colin Allred\uc2  could have stopped men from competing in women's sports, but instead, he voted against our daughters.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Terry, men in women's sports.\par \par \uc2 SCHILLING: Laura, I give that an A plus. I have been singing this from the mountaintops. Republicans need to seize on this huge opportunity to protect girls' sports. This is beautiful. It uses Allred's fame as a football player and uses it right against him.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Girls should play against girls. What a novel concept. All right, New York Democrat Congressman Pat Ryan. He's put abortion front and center in his campaign in this new ad.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I had an abortion then, so that I could have the family I have today.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Alison Esposito wants to ban abortion in New York and is funded by MAGA extremists who make abortion illegal nationwide.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That seems weird to me. Is she looking at a daughter? Is she -- I don't know what that is. Terry, your grade.\par \par \uc2 SCHILLING: I give it an F, Laura. It's an F. It's bad visuals, and it's a lie. No one wants to ban all abortions without exception.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, has Trump ever said that? I mean, he's never said that. I mean, and I think what he's consistently said, which I think is starting to break through, is that this is an issue best left to the people of each state to decide for themselves.\par \par \uc2 We saw what happened in Kansas. Kansas went far more liberal on abortion than a lot of people ever thought it would. Terry, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us tonight. Make sure to follow me on social media all weekend long. Remember, it's America now and forever. And you got to watch the game. Georgia versus Alabama. Trump apparently is going to be there. Wait, am I wearing an Alabama pin? Jesse Watters takes it from here.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020240928ek9r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 Hurricane Helene Approaching Florida Coast; Residents Prepare For Dangerous Storm; Former President Leads On Immigration In Key States; Multiple Sirens After Missile Fired From Yemen; Netanyahu Orders Airstrike On \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  Commander; Biden Announces $8 Billion Military Aid Package; Calls For Mayor Eric Adams Resignation After Federal Indictment; Rep. Mike Kelly (R-PA) and Rep. \uc2 Jason Crow\uc2  (D-CO) Are Interviewed On Bipartisan Efforts To Investigate First Assassination Attempt On Former President Trump; Hurricane Helene Upgraded To Category Four Storm Threatening Florida; Recent Polling Shows Close Presidential Race In Battleground States Of Arizona and Georgia; Ukrainian President Zelenskyy Meets With President Biden, Vice President Harris, And Former President Trump\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Steve Harrigan, Adam Klotz, Rich Edson, Mike Tobin,  Jaqui Heinrich, CB Cotton, Brandy Campbell \par \uc2 7,879 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 September 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS HOST: And that's it for us. Have a great night. See you tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Hey, Judge, I like the winner dogs. Really did.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS HOST: You know, you're welcome.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You're welcome.\par \par \uc2 All right. Good evening. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 We have new polling information tonight about the presidential election in two crucial swing states. That's coming up.\par \par \uc2 House lawmakers, meantime, tear into the Secret Service over the first attempted assassination against former President Trump. That is the subject of tonight's "Common Ground" segment.\par \par \uc2 And New York's mayor, resist calls to step down after being charged in a federal indictment.\par \par \uc2 But first, we're awaiting landfall for the powerful Hurricane Helene along the Florida panhandle.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DEANNE CRISWELL\uc2 , ADMINISTRATOR, \uc2 FEDERAL EMERGENCY MANAGEMENT AGENCY\uc2 : This is going to be a multi-state event with the potential for significant impacts --\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): Every minute that goes by brings us closer to having conditions that are going to be simply too dangerous to navigate.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There's going to come a point where we can't rescue you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I came to convince myself that it's safer to leave.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm a little bit nervous. But me against Mother Nature right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Helene is bringing heavy rain, high winds, what is predicted to be a massive storm surge. Tens of thousands of customers are already without power in the region. Experts say the worst is yet to come.\par \par \uc2 We have "FOX TEAM COVERAGE". Adam Klotz is at the fox Weather Center in New York to tell us where the system is and where it's headed.\par \par \uc2 We begin though with correspondent Steve Harrigan in Crystal River, Florida tonight, with the latest. Good evening, Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Just getting started here. The rain has picked up, the wind gusting as well into what is likely to be a Category 4 storm. That means, wind speeds of more than 130 miles per hour. That is strong enough to knock down a well- built house. To tear off the roof and to tear off exterior walls as well.\par \par \uc2 Along with those winds, a surge that in some places could be 15 to 20-feet high. Words being used by officials in Florida, unsurvivable. An unsurvivable storm surge.\par \par \uc2 We've talked to people here who are stressed out, many of them after two big storms in two years simply sound a little bit beaten down.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FRANKIE JOHNSON, RESIDENT, HORSESHOE BEACH, FLORIDA: We've had three --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It falls it that way --\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Three in the last 13 months.\par \par \uc2 That's the price you pay for wanting to live on the coast. But, you know, after a while, it kind of --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Now you know.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: It kind -- it kind of gets like, OK, you know, like you said, you know, it's enough. It's enough.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): The state taking multiple measures to try to keep people safe. Mandatory evacuations have been ordered along the coast. Schools have been shut down; airports shut down. More than 30,000 power workers are ready to go when needed. Also, the Coast Guard has been deployed along with high water rescue vehicles.\par \par \uc2 One of the particularly challenging aspects of this storm, the worst of it could come well after dark. Officials have warned Floridians what they can expect to hear overnight.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN GUTHRIE, DIRECTOR, FLORIDA DIVISION OF EMERGENCY MANAGEMENT: You will hear tree snapping, pine tree snapping, sound an awful lot like fireworks or potentially gunshots. So, please make sure, if you hear that, act immediately. Get to the interior portion of your home, away from glass, most -- again, most likely a bathroom, and stay there until you hear that sound stop.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (on camera): Landfall expected in the Big Bend area just north of me, about five hours from now. Bret, back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Steve, we'll head back for breaking details. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 Let's get an update of where Hurricane Helene is right now.\par \par \uc2 Fox News Meteorologist Adam Klotz is at the weather center in New York. Good evening, Adam.\par \par \uc2 ADAM KLOTZ, FOX NEWS CHANNEL METEOROLOGIST: Hey, good evening there, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Still, a powerful storm that has a little bit of room to grow a little bit stronger. You are looking at that Category 3 strength. It is on the high end of the Category 3 storm. It still could reach Category 4 levels. But in general, Category 3s are devastating storms. Catastrophic once you get to Cat 4. And again, we are right there on that borderline. This is going to be a powerful storm impacting a whole lot of people.\par \par \uc2 There is our center of circulation. This is the closest it's been to the Florida coast so far. Ultimately, though, it's going to continue to track north and make landfall there along the Florida panhandle, but it is a massive storm as well.\par \par \uc2 So, even if you're further away from that center of circulation, you're going to be feeling it.\par \par \uc2 We have seen plenty of these. That little red box, that is a tornado that is spun up currently in South Carolina, obviously, very far away from the center of this storm. That's something that's going to continue here for the next several hours.\par \par \uc2 Anyways, as you're looking at a tornado watch in place across portions of Florida, Georgia, getting into South Carolina, where I was just showing you, they currently have one on the ground.\par \par \uc2 There is your center of circulation now. That orange is talking about hurricane gusts now getting close to the Tampa area. But as far as tropical storm force winds, the storm is so large that it's pretty much covering the entire state of Florida. Hard to avoid at least some sort of impacts from this system.\par \par \uc2 The hurricane warnings are on place, ultimately, where the storm will make landfall and then it will drag to the north. So, from Tallahassee, running all the way up to Macon. They don't usually stay that strong that long.\par \par \uc2 This one is going to partially because how big and strong it is, partially because how fast it is moving. Everything in the red is a tropical storm warning. And as you can see, a lot of the southeast is going to be dealing with at least a tropical storm.\par \par \uc2 Again, it's such a quick mover. It's such a powerful one. By the time you get into Atlanta on Friday morning, we are still talking about a very strong storm here, Bret.\par \par \uc2 They could be dealing with a tropical storm. Atlanta, a tropical storm. So, a lot of folks are going to be dealing with this in their own way.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Adam, thank you. We'll have a live report from Tampa at the bottom of the hour.\par \par \uc2 OK, we are releasing new Fox polling right now on the presidential race. Two key battleground states.\par \par \uc2 First, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is up three points on Vice President Harris among likely voters in Arizona in our latest poll. The margin is two points among registered voters who had favored Harris by one-point last month.\par \par \uc2 Harris, however, leads the former president by three points among likely voters in Georgia and also by three points among registered voters there as well.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we take a look at the contrasting economic policies of the two major presidential nominees. Polls indicate the economy, inflation are the top issues for voters heading into the November election. Both candidates spoke about the economy today with highlights from senior national correspondent, Rich Edson.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Well, thank you very much --\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: At a press conference in New York, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ridiculed his opponent's trip to the southern border tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : She should save her airfare, and she should go back to the White House and tell the president to close the border.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Harris's campaign was more focused on the economy this afternoon, posting an 82-page economic plan on its web site.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Are you familiar with the neighborhood?\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Including promises to provide up to $25,000 in down payment help for first time home buyers. A $2,000 cap on out-of-pocket medication costs, and to implement a federal ban on price gouging -- the critics have called a version of socialist price controls.\par \par \uc2 To pay for this, Harris' plan proposes a minimum income tax on billionaires and other tax increases. Her campaign says no one earning less than $400,000 a year would pay more in federal taxes.\par \par \uc2 Even the Biden Harris administration has maintained and even broadened the Trump-era tariffs on Chinese imports. Harris criticized her opponent over his proposal to increase some tariffs to shore up domestic manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: You don't just throw around the idea of just tariffs across the board, and that's part of the problem with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He's just not very serious about how he thinks about some of these issues.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): Just released Fox News Sun Belt polls show among Georgia voters, Trump leads on the border by 15 percentage points and on the economy by four. The vice president leads by 18 on abortion.\par \par \uc2 In Arizona on the issues, Arizona voters give Trump a 15-percentage point advantage on immigration, eight on the economy, and Harris a 15-percentage point lead on abortion.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): Tomorrow, Harris is scheduled to visit the southern border at Douglas, Arizona. Trump travels back to the Midwestern swing states this weekend for rallies in Michigan and Wisconsin. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And Rich, another court date today for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's attorneys?\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Yes, another former presidential court date here. He is asking New York State appeals court to throw out or reduce the nearly half-billion- dollar judgment against him for inflating the value of his assets to get more favorable loan and insurance rates.\par \par \uc2 The five judges on this appeals panel appeared skeptical of that judgment and seemed concerned about possible overreach from state Attorney General Letitia James, from the penalty to the case itself.\par \par \uc2 The judges did not rule today, nor did Trump attend, even though he is in New York. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Rich, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Israeli forces have killed another top \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  terrorist. The ongoing bombardment into southern Lebanon comes as U.S. and Western allies push a 21-day ceasefire. Israeli leadership, meantime, is resisting, and just moments ago, air raid sirens rang out across Israel's populous central area, including Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 Senior correspondent Mike Tobin is in Haifa, Israel tonight with the latest on this. Mike, good evening.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: In the last half hour, Bret, the sirens sounded all over central Israel, including Tel Aviv, with reports of an inbound ballistic missile from Yemen.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IDF\uc2  says it was taken out with the aero missile defense system, which is capable of making interceptions at very high altitude, as the tensions and warfare here only increase.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): The man who commanded \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 's deadly drones and cruise missiles, Mohammad Surur, taken out with an Israeli air strike in the crowded suburb of Beirut, controlled by \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The assassination ordered by Israel's prime minister while flying to the U.S. Ordered with disregard for a 21-day cease fire, which the U.S. and France had just proposed.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , PRIME MINISTER, ISRAEL (through translator): My policy, our policy is clear. We are continuing to strike \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  with full force, and we will not stop.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): On the verge of full-scale war, Netanyahu told his forces to increase their attacks. Israel's air campaign hit some 75 targets in Lebanon, including several on the border with Syria.\par \par \uc2 MAJ. GEN. TOMER BAR, COMMANDER, ISRAEL AIR FORCE: We are going to prevent any possibility of weapons transfer from Iran.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): Rattling the saber, Israel's seventh brigade drilled in the north, as the army called up reservists, while the U.S. pushed for diplomatic space for talks.\par \par \uc2 GEN. LLOYD AUSTIN (RET), UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF DEFENSE: Let me be clear, Israel and Lebanon can choose a different path. Despite the sharp escalation in recent days, a diplomatic solution is still viable.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): In Beirut, some residents shaken by the air strikes vow to support the fight and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leader, \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ELIE SEBBAG, RESIDENT, NAHARIYA, ISRAEL (through translator): May God grant him a long life. We won't stop this war until war stops on Palestine.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  continues to fire rockets and drones. People who remain in Israel's north have endured the random fire for almost a year and have no patience for a ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 ELIE SEBBAG, RESIDENT, NAHARIYA, ISRAEL (through translator) (PH): We must finish with this. To kill them, to stop them, to do something. But not just not stop the war.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (on camera): Despite the defiance, Israel just secured $5 -- $8.7 billion in military aid from the U.S. $5 billion of that will go to air defenses like the Arrow and Iron Dome systems.\par \par \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  is in New York, because he is speaking at the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  tomorrow. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mike Tobin in Haifa. Mike, thanks.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is sending billions more tax dollars to Ukraine for its war effort against Russia. He is still resisting one major ask, though, from Ukrainian leader \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 , who was with him today.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We have to strengthen Ukraine's position on the battlefield. This will strengthen Ukraine's position in future negotiations.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: President Biden announcing an $8 billion package for Ukraine, including a weapons upgrade, new precision guided glide bombs. Zelenskyy, expressing thanks --\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, PRESIDENT OF UKRAINE: This will be great help.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): But walking away without a yes on his most urgent priority, permission to use U.S. missiles to strike military targets deep inside Russia.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): He believes if he could shut down those Russian air bases and missile bases that are attacking his country, that would give him leverage to get Putin to the table. I believe that too.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The White House chilling expectations.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Look, our policy has not changed.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): As one \uc2 NATO\uc2  ally says, Biden's resistance forces Ukraine to fight with one hand behind its back.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER STUBB, PRESIDENT OF FINLAND: I mean, I put myself in the situation. What if we were provided weapons in a war situation and we would not be allowed to use them for defense purposes within the framework of international law, which of course, Putin and Russia is breaking every day.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): After this --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And we continue to give billions of dollars to a man who refuses to make a deal, Zelenskyy.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Back then in a room --\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The V.P., using a separate meeting with Zelenskyy to look presidential and get political.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: There are some in my country who would instead force Ukraine to give up large parts of its sovereign territory. These proposals are the same of those of Putin.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Trump now says he will meet with Zelenskyy tomorrow, after some resistance. Republicans are furious Zelenskyy toward a munitions factory in battleground Pennsylvania this week with only state Democrats present, equating it to a campaign event.\par \par \uc2 Today, the White House pushed back, saying Zelenskyy went to a similar event in Utah with only Republican officials present just two months ago. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich, live in the North Lawn. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, New York City Mayor Eric Adams, takes a defiant stance as he faces multiple federal charges.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Fox Weather alert now, looking live in Tallahassee as the ocean picks up there, as Hurricane Helene gets closer, and we will take a look at exactly where landfall is going to hit.\par \par \uc2 If you have any doubts about where you are in comparison to this storm or where the storm is going, you need to go to Fox Weather, either get the app or go to the channel. You can stream it as well, and you see all the places you can get. Fox Weather, they are 24/7, on this storm.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, New York City Mayor Eric Adams is scheduled to be arraigned on federal corruption charges tomorrow. Adams says he's done nothing wrong. Has no plans to resign, but several Democratic colleagues are calling him on him to step aside following a federal indictment. Others want due process to play out. Correspondent CB Cotton has details from New York tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CB COTTON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Growing calls for New York City Democratic Mayor Eric Adams to step down. Prosecutors say Adams abused his power in a nearly decade long criminal scheme. Adams says he didn't do it.\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS, MAYOR OF NEW YORK CITY: I follow the rules, I follow the federal law.\par \par \uc2 COTTON (voice over): But many laws were broken. According to the indictment, more than 50 pages long, with prosecutors laying out a history of corruption that they say started when Adams was serving as Brooklyn Borough President and continued through Adams 2021 mayoral campaign.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors say Adams took bribes, sought out illegal campaign contributions and accepted free flights, dinners at high end restaurants and upgrades at luxury hotels from wealthy foreign nationals, including at least one Turkish government official.\par \par \uc2 DAMIAN WILLIAMS\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY: This was a multiyear scheme to buy favor with a single New York City politician on the rise, Eric Adams.\par \par \uc2 COTTON (voice over): But that Turkish official wanted the bribes repaid, according to the indictment, telling an Adams staffer that it was Adams', "Turn to support Turkey." The staffer responded, "I know."\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors say Adams complied and pressured the Fire Department to approve the opening of a new high rise consulate for Turkey in midtown Manhattan. The indictment comes amid at least three other ongoing and separate federal investigations linked to people in Adams administration, leading to a slew of resignations in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 While we expect Adams to surrender to law enforcement sometime next week, Adams says he's staying on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COTTON (on camera): Prosecutors also allege Adams defrauded New York City through illegal campaign donations, which they say helped him qualify for more than $10 million and matching public campaign funds, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: CB, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tech stocks led the way as markets hit new highs today, the Dow surged 260, the S&P 500 finished up 23, the NASDAQ jumped 108.\par \par \uc2 Up next, our Common Ground segment on the first Trump Assassination Task Force hearing.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 9 in Meridian, Idaho, as the man accused of murdering four University of Idaho students makes his first appearance in a Boise courthouse. It's the first hearing for Bryan Kohberger after a judge agreed to a defense motion for a change of venue.\par \par \uc2 Fox 8 in Cleveland as the judge approves a $600 million settlement for residents near the site of the East Palestine train derailment. \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  will pay households and businesses that submitted claims within a 20 mile radius of the accident. Around 55,000 claims were filed.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Detroit from our affiliate Fox 2, one of the big stories there tonight, two of the biggest names in music will join forces for a major concert at Ford Field. Billy Joel and Stevie Nicks will headline the event, scheduled for March 29th.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PATRICK SULLIVAN, FORMER SECRET SERVICE AGENT: The failure of most Secret Service personnel in Butler to be informed about the specific threat facing former President Trump stands out.\par \par \uc2 SGT. EDWARD LENZ, ADAMS TOWNSHIP, PENNSYLVANIA POLICE: In the local command post, there was no way for us to hear any of the Secret Service radio traffic.\par \par \uc2 LT. JOHN HEROLD, PENNSYLVANIA STATE POLICE: The \uc2 Pennsylvania State Police\uc2  provided all resources requested by the Secret Service. The Secret Service did or did not ask me, as the officer in charge of PSP assets to post a car, a trooper at the AGR international property.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bipartisan House lawmakers rip into the Secret Service for its handling of the first assassination attempt against former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  yesterday, it was the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Today, representatives in the other chamber, the House had very similar things to say about what they consider major mistakes leading up to the shots from the now deceased gunman.\par \par \uc2 Joining us tonight, House Trump Assassination Task Force Chairman Mike Kelly and Ranking Member \uc2 Jason Crow\uc2 . Congressmen, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 Chairman Kelly, what did you get from today? What do you -- what do you take away from it, there are a lot of people hunger -- hungry for answers.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE KELLY (R-PA): Yes. So, when Jason I first start on this task, one of the things we looked at early on was the blaming of local law enforcement for not doing what they were supposed to do. I think today was a validation, not only did that they do what they were supposed to, they did everything they were supposed to do. But they weren't informed of everything that was expected of them. And I think that was -- it was a great stain that was put on local law enforcement. They were deeply wounded that day because, and it still exists to this day, there are people live in my time that said you didn't do the right job and you should have been there. That's not the case.\par \par \uc2 I think one thing Jason and I looked at when we first had this ability to work on this task force, let's make sure we get the truth to the American people. You don't have to get it out quickly, but you have to realize at the end of the time the work that you did is believable, truthful, and there's no question about it. That's what we're trying to do.\par \par \uc2 I don't care where -- at the end of the day, where the blame falls. I do know this -- local law enforcement today had their chance nationally to explain what they did, and they can validate everything they did. They were a tremendous asset that day.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Yes. To your point, your colleague, Congresswoman Houlahan, had this to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. CHRISSY HOULAHAN, (D-PA) TRUMP ASSASSINATION TASK FORCE: I felt like that the witnesses were enormously forthcoming and cooperative, and it was clear that there was a lot that was going wrong there with communications. We want to make sure that we're being, very grave and serious about this and that we're talking to people who actually know that day, and these guys clearly were those people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congressman Crow, is there going to be some ta-da moment here, where people say, oh, this is really what the deal is?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JASON CROW\uc2 , (D-CO) TRUMP ASSASSINATION TASK FORCE: Well, Bret, I think what we see here, the story that's come together is that there was a series of cascading failures. I mean, any one of those failures alone could have been catastrophic. But there were a series of them in this case. You know, I'm a former Army ranger. I served in Iraq and Afghanistan. And what we learned in the military is you have to have unified command and control. You need to know who is in charge, who is making decisions, who people are reporting to. And you have to have communications. Units and elements need to be able to communicate with each other.\par \par \uc2 In Butler, none of those things appear to be true, which, you know, is just catastrophic to any type of operation like this. So, the very basics weren't handled, but then other things, like the lack of drone coverage, the lack of a good perimeter, a perimeter that was too small, a lack of guidance given to local law enforcement. So, a lot of questions remain, but certainly a lot of things were pretty astonishing to me today.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The \uc2 U.S. Secret Service\uc2 , Chairman Kelly, said about the passage of the continuing resolution that "This short-term funding will better equip the \uc2 U.S. Secret Service\uc2  to enhance security measures in the months ahead. We look forward to work with Congress on full year funding to deliver the additional personnel, technology, and equipment our workforce needs to do their jobs."\par \par \uc2 But we're being told that former President Trump was having a rally, an outdoor rally in Wisconsin this Saturday, and it's been moved indoors because of a shortage of Secret Service personnel to be able to protect it. It seems like they have issues because they had their hands full at the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  this week.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: I don't think there's any question about they have their hands full, and they don't have enough members of the Secret Service to do some of the things they are tasked with doing. And you find out that, depending on who is arriving where, how did we divide up the assets that we have. Now, I know when President Trump came to Butler, the first lady was also in Pittsburgh. So all of a sudden the Secret Service is trying to cover two different personalities at two different times in different places.\par \par \uc2 Look, we try to address that with providing more funding. But I don't know, I think Jason probably feels the same way on this. The answer to everything isn't always more money. Sometimes the answer is we need the personnel in the right place at the right time and make sure whatever you deploy that you are deploying it to the right place for the right reasons.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Speaking of the first lady, she was pretty vocal, Congressman Crow, about how she feels things are talked about. She was on an interview with FOX & FRIENDS this morning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELANIA TRUMP, FORMER FIRST LADY: We hear the leaders from the opposition party and mainstream media branding him as a threat to democracy, calling him vile names. They're only fueling a toxic atmosphere and giving power to all these people that they want to do harm to him. This needs to stop.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is a "Common Ground" segment, but what do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 CROW: What's very clear to me is that I condemn political violence from any direction of any sort. And so have all of my Democratic friends and my colleagues from President Biden to Vice President Harris to Leader Jeffries. We have all unequivocally condemned political violence. We are in a very sensitive time in our country, and words matter a lot. I mean, we see this happening in Springfield, Ohio, right now, where that community has come under assault because of a lot of rhetoric and narrative in their direction.\par \par \uc2 So everybody needs to take a step back here and just be very sensitive. And Americans must understand and elected officials have to be very clear when we're campaigning versus what is inappropriate language. We are in a campaign season. Everybody is going to campaign, and everybody is going to have rigorous, sometimes really tough, difficult debate. Nobody should shy away from that, because there is a lot at stake in this campaign cycle for both sides. So both sides are going to make their case, have tough debate. At the same time, we have to be able to condemn political violence wherever it comes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mr. Chairman, I only have a few seconds here, but last word. What do you hope comes out of all of this?\par \par \uc2 KELLY: I think that the nation understands that we solve all these political problems with ballots, not bullets, we are America. We are not a third world country. We understand going forward that everything we say could be taken one way or the other. Be careful of what you say and whom you say it to, because sometimes what we think may be casual conversation is not taken as casual by people who are hearing us.\par \par \uc2 So I think with Jason, he and I from the very beginning of this task force decided we are going to do what is right for America. It does not have a political bent for me nor him. We're a Republican and a Democrat, but at the end of the day, we are U.S. legislators, and we are going to provide the right answers for the American people. I do believe the American people can handle the truth and we are going to give it to them in an unbiased way.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We look forward to hearing it, and thank you both for coming on.\par \par \uc2 CROW: Thank you for having us.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You can see all of the "Common Ground" segments on my podcast "Common Ground." Find that and the "All-Star Panel" podcast under the Bret Baier podcasts at FOXNewspodcast.com, \uc2 Spotify\uc2 , or wherever you download podcasts. You can also see the discussion on the FOX News \uc2 YouTube\uc2  page.\par \par \uc2 Up next, tonight's 50 races in 50 days segment focuses on an Arizona congressional battle. Plus, a live report, we'll head down to Florida on the progress of hurricane Helene.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: FOX Weather alert now. Breaking tonight, hurricane Helene has been upgraded to a category four storm. That is a massive hurricane. Florida residents are feeling the evenings already of the system. It's approaching the Florida panhandle, has yet to make landfall, obviously. Airlines have canceled or delayed thousands of flights as the storm moves northward.\par \par \uc2 Brandy Campbell of FOX Weather is in Tampa right now feeling the early effects. Good evening, Brandy.\par \par \uc2 BRANDY CAMPBELL, FOX WEATHER: Yes, good evening, Bret. So, right now we're not going to be getting the immediate or direct impact from this hurricane as it is just about 130 miles offshore in the Gulf from us. But as you can see around me, we are still feeling the brunt of it. I have Bayshore Boulevard here in Tampa just off in the distance, you can see already extensive flooding occurring here, surrounding some of the homes closer to Bayshore Boulevard.\par \par \uc2 Now, this area, again, expected to see the worst impacts, of course, right now. But going into the later hours of tonight, we could feel hurricane force wind gusts between 60 and 70 miles per hour. But what is most notable about this area, Bret, is the storm surge. Again, you can see the flooding already happening. We could get storm surge up to eight feet. And just talking to locals here, they know this area. Bayshore Boulevard normally floods when they have these high tides and such. But people are telling me this is much higher than they are used to seeing.\par \par \uc2 And just for reference, again, we are about one street into the neighborhood. I'm standing on the grass right now. And so I have about a foot of water that I'm standing. In the curb is right here. That's maybe a foot-and-a-half. So I'm not sure how deep it is in the road, and then that just gets deeper once you go further to the bay, which is really choppy right now. And again, we've got the wind just kicking up, and it's only going to get worse as we get later into this evening. Bret, back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Brandy, stay safe. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 In tonight's 50 races in 50 days, a toss-up battle in Arizona's sixth district shaped by two hot button issues. First term Representative Juan Ciscomani squares off with former state legislator Kirsten Engel in a rematch from the 2022 midterms. Engel is targeting what she calls Ciscomani's record of extremism on abortion, while the Trump endorsed Ciscomani has honed on in on what he says is Engel's denial of the immigration crisis.\par \par \uc2 The district shares a border with Mexico along the southeast part of the state, an area neighboring the city of Douglas, home to Arizona's second largest port of entry. Also, it is where Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will make a campaign stop tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 In our news FOX polls, Harris tops former President Trump on abortion with registered voters in Arizona while the former president polls well ahead on immigration and the border.\par \par \uc2 Ciscomani bested Engel two years ago by a narrow margin, under two points, and the results this time around will likely hang on the knife's edge and maybe the presidential result.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we take a look at my latest FOX Nation documentary series "The Unauthorized History of Political Parties." It tells the story of how both major parties have adapted over the decades in response to a changing America, including how \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  reshaped the Democratic Party. Here is an excerpt.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: At the 2004 Democratic convention, a relatively unknown senator from Illinois took the podium. As Obama stepped onto the national stage, he set in motion a new chapter for the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  comes on. He is this amazing figure. But what's his coalition? It's not the Roosevelt coalition. It's voters who are liberal on a certain set of issues -- environmentalism, foreign policy, sexual mores. This is not the party of the working class anymore.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He really said the future of the Democratic Party is going to be those who were interested in high levels of education and international engagement, and he wanted to move toward a more progressive party that was appealing on the world stage as opposed to just within the United States.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: If you want to learn more about why and how the Democratic Party has transformed itself and what it means for this year's election, tune in to my brand-new series, "The Unauthorized History of Political Parties" streaming now on FOX Nation.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the latest from the campaign trail and our new FOX polls, right after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: If you have the dreams and the ambitions and the aspirations of what I believe you do, you are in my plan.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Families are suffering now, so if she has a plan, she should stop grandstanding and do it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He is saying whatever he thinks he needs to say to try to get elected.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't know how they plan on getting her across the finish line. President Trump is going to win this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, there you hear some of the sound from the campaign trail, this as we have two battle ground state polls, Arizona and Georgia, and they are basically exactly opposite, 51-48, 51-48. There you see the split. These are likely voters.\par \par \uc2 If you look inside this poll, in Arizona, the difference in Harris support over time, groups that favored Harris, you can see Hispanic voters from August to now, Harris plus 11. Women, Harris plus eight from 14. But look at the voters under 30. Harris plus 13 to Trump plus 12. That's an interesting note. In Georgia, trust to do a better job, you can see immigration, border, economy, and abortion, and the flip, it matches kind of the Real Clear Politics average of poll numbers in these various states. And really it all comes down to the battleground states.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, syndicated radio host \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2 , Democratic strategist Meghan Hays, and Matthew Continetti, fellow at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 OK, Hugh, you look at polls every day, some people glaze over as we are getting closer and closer to Election Day. By the way, everybody should look at likely voters now at this point in the election because these seven states, that's really where the game is.\par \par \uc2 HUGH HEWITT, SYNDICATED RADIO HOST: You are right, and those seven states will tell you, to borrow the cliche. I watched last night the vice president give her fifth consecutive dull, uninspiring, and substance-free interview. And I think the ice is freezing over, Bret. It began with Dana Bash, Brian Taft, the \uc2 National Association of Black Journalists\uc2 , Oprah, and then last night only 1.8 million watched her live at 7:00 p.m. And I'm thinking to myself she is losing the opportunity to make the sale. And I just don't think, boy, that clip of \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  reminded me, when you have got sparkle, you have got it. And she hasn't got it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Meghan, to that point, here is another clip. This is a clip from that interview on how to increase availability of affordable housing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Some of the work is going to be through what we do in terms of giving benefits and assistance to state and local governments around transit dollars. And looking holistically at the connection between that and housing, and looking holistically at the incentives we in the federal government can create for local and state governments to actually plan in a holistic manner.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So there is criticism of some of these interviews, and you wonder whether this is the reason maybe she is not doing more of them.\par \par \uc2 MEGHAN HAYS, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Look, I mean, I'm not sure what the campaign strategy is. I think that they should be doing more local media, if I'm being really honest here. But I do think that she is laying out her policies. People, the polls are starting to show that. She is closing the go gap a little on who people trust to take care of the economy, and she is closing the gap more than President Biden was.\par \par \uc2 It's also interesting to see in that same poll where people view her more as a change candidate. There's also an NBC poll that had her up 10 points as more of a change candidate above Trump. So the polling as where it is, a snapshot in time, it is showing some movement in her favor there and she is closing the gap.\par \par \uc2 I do think it's interesting with Georgia and Arizona being actually flipped, it just goes back to show how important Pennsylvania is going to be in this election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: No, that's exactly right, Meghan, 100 percent. Matthew, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI, AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE: Well, Bret, I will give you a holistic answer and a plan that's holistic. I just want to emphasize it's holistic just like the vice president did in her interview.\par \par \uc2 I'm looking at two groups, independents and Hispanic voters, both of them moving toward Trump in the Arizona poll. If that's national as well, that means a very good election night for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . On the economy, Harris has closed that gap a little bit. And so I think in these final weeks of the campaign, Trump needs to stress the economy in addition to the border, and he needs to remind voters that they were better off under Trump instead of the four years of Biden and Harris.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hugh, here is the former president and the vice president on Ukraine today, one meeting with Zelenskyy, one scheduled to.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : President Zelenskyy has asked to meet with me, and I will be meeting with him. It's a shame what is happening in Ukraine. One of the things that are very bothersome to me is the fact that Europe is paying only a small fraction of the money that the United States of America is paying.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: There are some in my country who would, instead, force Ukraine to give up large parts of its sovereign territory. They are proposals for surrender, which is dangerous and unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We had that unique thing where the Ukrainian president met with not only the president but the vice president. There was a separate press conference for the vice president. Obviously, she is the Democratic nominee. Could this issue be a big issue in the campaign, Hugh?\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: It should be because it matters in Pennsylvania and Ohio, and maybe in Michigan there's a Ukrainian-American population, and generally among people who like strength. Zelenskyy made a bad mistake yesterday, Bret, by appearing at what was a nakedly political event. Ingratitude scalds. When Putin first invaded Ukraine in 2014, Obama sent blankets. Trump, when Trump came into office, he sent antitank missiles. They were able to fend off the second invasion under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I think he has got a lot of explaining to do to the former president and hopefully gets it done, because there are a lot of hawks in the Republican Party who would like to support Zelenskyy, but that stunt in Pennsylvania yesterday was a very bad move on his part.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Meghan, there are -- you look at the headlines, "Politico", "Facing GOP attacks, Zelenskyy looks to sell White House on victory." "The Hill," "Zelenskyy visits Washington as election year divide grows over Ukraine war." CNN, "Republicans follow Trump's lead on icing out Zelenskyy." Is there some political calculus here by Democrats to try to get a \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  Republican voter by really, you know, talking and forcing the issue of Ukraine and supporting Ukraine against Russia?\par \par \uc2 HAYS: Yes, absolutely. But I also think if you go back to the debate, the former president couldn't say that he would support Ukraine and support our \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies in defending that democracy. So I think that they should be playing that messaging over and over again. But I also don't think that this is going to be an issue that people vote on. They are going to be voting on the economy and immigration and abortion. And I think that's where the campaigns need to be focused. But Zelenskyy is here, so I understand it's important that Zelenskyy is going to meet with Trump tomorrow. So it will be interesting to see what comes out of that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. And Matthew, to Meghan's point, immigration obviously still driving it, but the economy really is. Battleground states, we'll put up the RCP average. It's essentially, again, where it has been. We haven't seen much movement. It's been essentially tied. She heads to Arizona tomorrow and is going to head to a border stop. How will that go? Will hanging the lantern there on that issue, which is perceived as a vulnerability, somehow solve that issue?\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: I don't think it's a smart move, Bret. I think it will only remind people that she was put in charge of resolving the border crisis at the outset of this administration and it only grew worse over time. Immigration is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's signature issue. That's not where kamala Harris should be drawing a contrast if she hopes to win. And so I think this will be a good day for the president tomorrow, only contrasting himself with Harris on the border and also meeting with Zelenskyy, and I hope coming away convinced that America needs to continue to help Ukraine resist Russian aggression.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, panel, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 And finally tonight, today's throwback, way back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN F. KENNEDY\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: I think in the final analysis, it depends upon what we do here. I think it's time America started moving again.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD NIXON\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: But our disagreement is not about the goals for America but only about the means to reach those goals.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Sixty-four years ago today, then Senator \uc2 John F. Kennedy\uc2  and then Vice President \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  took part in the first nationally televised presidential debate, 70 million Americans watched the two men tackle the country's biggest issues and the modern political landscape was changed forever. If you heard it on the radio you said Nixon won. If you saw it on TV, many people said Kennedy won hands down.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, battleground state polling from North Carolina and Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight and every night. It's been a busy week. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. Remember, check out FOX Weather for the latest on hurricane Helene. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may 