r \uc2 RONALD ROWE, ACTING DIRECTOR OF THE UNITED STATES SPECIAL SERVICE: We are not capitalizing on a crisis. We are showing the math. We have finite resources.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Showing the math. Plus, a CNN reporter schooled by a Trump supporter.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELLE REEVE, CNN CORRESPONDENT: If you can afford a boat, you're not hurting so bad, right? Because a boat cost a lot of money and it's a lot of upkeep.\par \par \uc2 JOE HARNER, RETIRED MILITARY TRUMP SUPPORTER: Listen, nobody gave me (BEEP). I earned everything that I've got.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That Trump voter joins us in moments. And who is really running the government?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT, UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: I'd like to turn it over to Jill and -- for any comments she has. And it's all yours, kid.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Where is the visiting angel? But first, the ultimate insult, that's the focus of tonight's Angle.\par \par \uc2 Given the failures of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' economic and border record, her press team is left with recycled lines from 2016 and 2020.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE GANGEL, CNN SPECIAL CORRESPONDENT: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is very good at insulting people.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sticks to the same old tired playbook of insults.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But, the real insults come from Kamala herself, don't they, because she insults our intelligence every time she sits down for even a friendly interview.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE U.S., (D) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Hi. I'm \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , and this is the wired auto complete interview. How to pronounce \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ? \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . What does \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  stand for? I am a daughter of parents who are active in civil rights movement. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  laughing, I love to laugh. Kamala Harris-Taylor Swift, I am very proud to have the support of Taylor Swift.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Was that for a kindergarten? What was that? I thought it was a joke. My staff says it's real. It could not have been a softer interview and presentation. The only missing line or missing question was, do you like long walks on the beach? She could reveal that as well. And as for the one-on-one with liberal billionaire \uc2 Oprah Winfrey\uc2 , if there was such a thing as a verbal foot rub, this would be it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 , MEDIA MOGUL: It seems to us that something happened to you the moment \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , President Biden, stepped aside and withdrew his candidacy, that a veil or something dropped and you just stepped into your power.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What was that? But then came the surprise questions, and they were really kind of tough, like, how are you going to reduce the high cost of living?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We really would love to know what your plan is to help lower the cost of living.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Yours is a story I hear around the country as I travel and in terms of both rightly having the right to have aspirations and dreams and ambitions for your family and working hard and finding that the American dream is, for this generation and so many recently, far more elusive than it's been. And we need to deal with that, and there are a number of ways.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Far more elusive, the American, what? I love how she acts like some external force caused inflation, by the way, not her and Joe's idiotic spending bills. Now, that's insulting, and it's also arrogant to believe that Americans should vote for you, even as you are unwilling or maybe unable to explain your policies. Her answers kind of remind me of that college kid who, when called on by the professor, hasn't done the class reading. So, he or she just merely restates the question posed, as Kamala did on the question of how she is going to fix the border.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I have prosecuted transnational criminal organizations for the trafficking of guns, drugs and human beings. My work and my career has always been about saying, let's fix problems. Let's address the needs, because we know it's within our capacity to do that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Even Oprah seemed frustrated.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WINFREY: So, to answer Justin's question, now that that bill has gone and hasn't passed, will you reintroduce that?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I just wonder Oprah has got to be thinking to herself, man, I just should have run for President myself. It would have been easier than live coaching this woman. Now, every exchange was an easy Kamala layup. And by the end of the 68, interminable 68 minutes, Oprah had allowed Harris to kind of rebrand herself from an anti-Second Amendment fanatic to a modern day Annie Oakley.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I'm a gun owner. \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  is a gun owner.\par \par \uc2 WINFREY: I do not know that.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And if somebody breaks into my house --\par \par \uc2 WINFREY: And I thought that was powerful.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: -- they're getting shot. I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 WINFREY: Yes. Yes. I hear that. I hear that.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Probably should not have said that.\par \par \uc2 WINFREY: But I --\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: My staff will deal with that later.\par \par \uc2 WINFREY: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I'm sure they would have thought it was just perfect for Trump to laugh if he said someone would get shot if came into his home. They would have -- oh yeah. They would have said, oh, it's heartless. You have no compassion. What if you shoot the wrong person? This is just more insults to our intelligence. And of course, it was all calculated, but it was poorly carried out rebranding. Kamala is just like you. But then, you think to yourself, I don't speak like the words of one of those lame motivational posters hang in your company break room.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: We believe in what is possible. We believe in what can be, and we believe in fighting for that. That's how that's we came into being, freedom to just be, and that's who we are. We believe in all that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The freedom to just be. I love how Oprah goes.\par \par \uc2 Note her use of the phrase how we came into being there, and again, the freedom to be, freedom to be unless you're one of the infants in the womb who was aborted in Kamala's dream land. And speaking of that today, she uttered more insulting drivel about abortion in Atlanta. Her language was crafted specifically to mislead the public.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I made the decision early in my life that I wanted to do the work that was about protecting the most vulnerable among us, and doing the work that was about giving them dignity in the process.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Most vulnerable among us. How about those who can't yet speak for themselves? How condescending, though? Condescending to women who, by the way, are poorer than they were four years ago, are less safe than they were on the streets four years ago, and less confident in the future than they were four years ago. But, Kamala's crack group of high-paid pollsters and consultants believe that women's first, second and third, most important concerns are abortion, abortion, oh, and more abortion. Now, that's not an insult to single cat ladies, as they call them. It's an insult to women across the board, just as is her entire duck and cover campaign. That's an insult to every American who goes to work, who goes to school every day and is judged on the results of their work.\par \par \uc2 Now, we have to remember, Harris has always benefited in her career from special treatment, including in this campaign. Now, she didn't have to go to win the Iowa caucuses or tramp through the snows of New Hampshire to convince voters there, and she doesn't have to do press conferences with hostile reporters, as Trump does. She hasn't had to explain her policy toward China or Russia. Her role is simply to repeat platitudes and memorized phrases.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: We love our country. I love our country. I know we all do. That's why everybody is here right now. We love our country. We take pride in the privilege of being American.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I thought she said we were systemically racist. How do you take pride in that kind of country, and hope that the hundreds of millions of dollars that she has raised from millionaires and billionaires are going to be enough to drown out Trump's message of peace and prosperity? That's what they're hoping for. And if she wins, the establishment is going to make use of this template for all future campaigns where the voters are kept in the dark, receive no assurances about anything, and they just simply dine out on celebrity endorsements and personality plays. This is a complete rejection of the democratic process from the phony defenders of democracy, and it is one big insult to all of us. We're treated like subjects, not citizens, and that's the Angle.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Allison Huynh, former Obama Fundraiser and Silicon Valley Tech Pioneer, and Evan Barker, a former Democrat Campaign Fundraiser.\par \par \uc2 Evan, good to see you. You wrote an OpEd this week saying that you can't go back to the Democrats because of what you saw --\par \par \uc2 EVAN BARKER, FORMER DEMOCRAT FUNDRAISER: No. I cannot --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- at the DNC. And so, what's your reaction to this Oprah interview, which seems to into your concerns?\par \par \uc2 BARKER: I mean, it just felt like Oprah was lecturing working-class people who are struggling. She has no idea what it's like to not be able to afford groceries. She has no idea what it's like to go to the gas station and struggle to fill up her car. She is not affected by the economy right now. And so, it just felt extremely out of touch. And it goes along with everything that I've been saying the last couple of weeks, and what I wrote in my OpEd.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Allison, one of Kamala's great concerns that she reiterated during this interview is about the people not being able to really understand the information that's presented to them. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: -- very important that we all speak to our friends and our neighbors about misinformation and help them now see how it occurs, where it is occurring. We ask people to be alert about mis and disinformation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I love Meryl Streep is an expert on disinformation. What about that? It sounds like censorship?\par \par \uc2 ALLISON HUYNH, FORMER OBAMA FUNDRAISER, & SILICON VALLEY TECH PIONEER: It's censorship big time. The Harris-Biden administration have admitted that they've been censoring free speech with \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , with \uc2 Google\uc2 . They are big time in cahoots. I've been saying this all along, and now we have information to come out and prove this. But, back to the Oprah interview, it was all gibberish. I could not understand one word she was saying.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah. I mean, to some extent, people thought it was really funny, and it was funny because it was so bad, and then Oprah is nodding her head like it is some profound statement from one of the great political orders of our time.\par \par \uc2 And I thought to myself, Evan, Oprah really did make it on her own, right? She is really smart, really hard-working. She rose in a very difficult industry, was a total pioneer. And yet, she is sitting down with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and treating her like, oh yeah, she just -- she made it on her own, and she is there. She wanted on her own when she was handed this nomination, and it just -- to me, that dichotomy was something that could not be ignored.\par \par \uc2 BARKER: And did anyone catch --\par \par \uc2 HUYNH: Then creating the cult of a personality.\par \par \uc2 BARKER: -- how she basically --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Sorry. Evan first.\par \par \uc2 BARKER: Did anyone catch -- No. That's OK. Did anyone catch how Oprah basically made fun of people who work at \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's? Did anyone catch that part of her intro? She said that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  worked at \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's and told everybody else that currently works at \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's, all the working- class people, that there was hope for them yet. So, I just found the entire programming to be incredibly patronizing. It was a cringe fest. It just -- it's just everything that is emblematic of the Democratic Party and how out of touch they are right now with just regular people that are struggling.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I thought it was insulting. I mean, again, they say that Trump is insulting --\par \par \uc2 BARKER: It was.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- can be insulting. This was insulting to our intelligence, no policies, no answers, a lot of personal anecdotes, a lot of meaningless blather, cliches and platitudes, and yet, the audience are treated like train steels (ph), like they're supposed to applaud to this, Allison. Meanwhile, they're out there touting their \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  endorsement, Allison.\par \par \uc2 HUYNH: Yes. So, this book has been written before, two books, the "Cult of a Personality" and "The Communist Manifesto." And so, they want to just hide that Marxist policy behind really very slick, aspirational talk, right, just like what Obama did. So, they're doing that playbook with Kamala, although she is not as slick as Obama.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: No, for sure. I mean, she doesn't even approach Obama's talent.\par \par \uc2 Allison, Evan, great to see you both. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right. The cost of everything is up for everybody. But, if you're working for the Harris campaign, either officially or unofficially, your job is to convince voters to suck it all up, because it's really not that bad. They reserve that for Trump supporters going about their day. And one CNN reporter was especially snarky in her little interrogation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REEVE: What's your most important issue?\par \par \uc2 HARNER: The economy, getting interest rates down, getting it where we can afford to live in America.\par \par \uc2 REEVE: But, if you can afford a boat, you are hurting so bad, right?\par \par \uc2 HARNER: Listen, nobody gave (BEEP). I earned everything that I've got. I'm retired military, retired power plant.\par \par \uc2 REEVE: I think it's interesting that people who are a little bit more comfortable are still so concerned about the economy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Joining me now, the man you just saw in that video, Joe Harner, Joe, first off, she seemed to kind of imply that you had a boat. Do you have a boat?\par \par \uc2 HARNER: No, ma'am. I do not.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What did you take of her question to you? I mean, to me, and maybe I misread it, but to me, she was like, why are you really complaining? I mean, you're in this -- the boat parade. They hate the boat parades. They despise the boat parades because Trump flags, the American flag, patriotism. Everyone is having a good time. So, what do you think she was really trying to get at? She was treating you like a exhibit at the Smithsonian. Like, what an odd thing this person who likes \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HARNER: I believe that she was just trying to get under people's skins there, just by the questions that she was asking the different people that I saw at the boat parade and everything. But, what's curious is she wrote on the same party boat that I wrote on that I paid $50 to come to the boat parade on.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, did they air the full exchange that you had with them?\par \par \uc2 HARNER: No. They did not.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, what did we miss? I loved you. I loved it like no one gave me this. I earned every bit I got. I'm going to get you a better microphone --\par \par \uc2 HARNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- next time you do an interview with me. I don't know how you're miced up there, but it's -- we're going to -- I think you sound like you're on a boat in the middle of the ocean. But, nevertheless, how has the Biden- Harris economy impacted you, Joe?\par \par \uc2 HARNER: Well, it's impacted me because I'm retired. I am on fixed income now, and everything costs more. So, my money doesn't go as far gas, everything, gas, food, repairs on the house, anything we do costs more. Everybody is raising prices to try and cover what the Biden-Harris administration has done.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So, what do you say to older Americans out there, because there is some reports that seniors may be trending in some parts of the country toward \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ? So, what's your message for them tonight?\par \par \uc2 HARNER: I will tell them that they need to look at their budgets and look at what -- how they're spending the money, because the money doesn't go anywhere near what it did four years ago.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Joe, we appreciate it, and thank you for speaking out very emphatically and emotionally to the CNN reporter, but just stop complaining about the economy. OK? Because, after all, you want a boat, or you're near a boat. So, you shouldn't complain. All right. Joe, thank you.\par \par \uc2 After two assassination attempts in two months, can the Secret Service actually protect Trump? A lot of you are worried about this tonight, and we're on it next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROWE: This was a failure on the part of the \uc2 United States Secret Service\uc2 . It's important that we hold ourselves accountable for the failures of July 13th, and that we use the lessons learned to make sure that we do not have another failure like this again. We have to be very direct and clear with our state and local partners as to what we're asking of them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, so the Acting Secret Service director did admit some failures about July 13th in the assassination attempt at Butler field of President Trump, and there was more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROWE: While some members of the advance team were very diligent, there was complacency on the part of others that led to a breach of security protocols.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It seems like weasel words. But, when he was asked about what happened this past Sunday here in West Palm Beach, he defended the actions of the Secret Service.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROWE: No shot was fired at the former President. The former President was not exposed to where he was on the golf course, and what I've said, and I said this Monday, that the procedures work, the redundancies work, so that high level of protection is working.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And while he was at it, he had a little request.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROWE: We are not capitalizing on a crisis. We are showing the math. We have finite resources, and we are stretching those resources to their maximum right now, which is going to require us to replace these assets. It's going to require us to also adjust.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, they plead that they don't have enough money. What do they have? Well, the Secret Service budget has swelled to over $3 billion. But, where is it all going? About 87 percent of that budget, or $2.7 billion, is directed toward operations and support, which includes $1.2 billion in funding for protective operations, the division that oversees protection for the President, the Vice President and their families. But, where is the money going, the other money?\par \par \uc2 Well, of course, the Secret Service has its own DEI and Accessibility Program, complete with an Inclusion and Engagement Council. And Steve Scalise pointed this out. He said, if you look at their budget since 2017, we've increased it over their own request every single year. So, clearly, they've gotten more money. It's about allocation. Well, their primary role is to protect the President and the presidential candidates, and obviously, in this case, we see they failed.\par \par \uc2 Last night, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  gave us an update on his own investigation in Florida, which will, we hope, yield more answers than we've been getting from the feds.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Are the feds being cooperative with your investigation? Are you concerned that you're not getting the information you need?\par \par \uc2 RON DESANTIS, GOVERNOR, FLORIDA: No, they're not being cooperative. Yes, I am concerned. I mean, for example, we were rebuffed. Our investigators were rebuffed just going to the fence line outside of Trump International Golf Club. Hopefully, that tune will change. But, I can tell you right now, Laura, we have not gotten a lot of receptive response.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Joining us now, Charles Marino, former Secret Service Special Agent and author of "Terrorists on the Border and in Our Country". Charles, do you think the Secret Service should be asking for more money right now? There is a lot of questions about how they're using the funds that they have.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES MARINO, FORMER SECRET SERVICE SPECIAL AGENT, & FORMER DHS SENIOR ADVISOR: Yeah, fair point. I think what they need to demonstrate first is that they're using the resources they have effectively and that they're being put towards the greatest threat in terms of their protectees. Former President Trump faces threats that are off the chart. We know that our threat level here in the country is through the roof. They have to put this towards those protectees that need it most.\par \par \uc2 We have a Republican nominee and we have a Democratic nominee. Those should be the two priorities right now, and I think you see that reflected today in what the House passed earlier today, enhancing the protection for these two candidates.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So, they did. They unanimously passed a bill to enhance the Secret Service protection for President Trump. And the acting director was asked about the shift that he keeps referring to, and watch what he said\par \par \uc2 MARINO: OK.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROWE: With respect to the paradigm shift, this is about looking at the organization holistically, for example, the communications issue. Communications were problematic, and I think what -- where I'm looking at is we need to have communications that are more closely aligned to the operations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK. Charles, this just seems rudimentary to me, especially after 9/11 when we had such communication breakdown. So, the local law enforcement and the Secret Service, ideally, how do they interact, because it broke down in Butler?\par \par \uc2 MARINO: Well, this is a well-proven method up until Butler, Pennsylvania. The Secret Service relies on the counterpart system, and that is, you get everybody together that's participating in the overall security plan, and you make sure they're all on the same place to monitor communications and pass essential traffic to everybody on the ground to make sure they know what they need to know. And for some reason, I can't figure it out, and I don't think the Secret Service can, how this went so awry in Butler, Pennsylvania, where they had state and local law enforcement in one place and the Secret Service in another.\par \par \uc2 So, it's really not necessarily if you would come to me previously and asked me if this needed to be fixed. I would have told you no, because it's been done so many times over and over again. I think they just need to step their game up. They need to provide the resources necessary to the threat. They need to make sure communications are in step with one another. State and locals understand what's being requested of them at all of these locations and motorcade routes. And the Secret Service will go on. But, I think they realize some easy stuff is getting missed here, and it's endangering a lot of people now.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Charles, I'd return it to the Treasury Department, where it seemed to be a lot better. The \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  is a huge mess of a bureaucracy.\par \par \uc2 Charles, we'll have you back. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, \uc2 Sean "Diddy" Combs\uc2  reportedly on suicide watch. We have the latest next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Rapper and music mogul \uc2 Sean Diddy Combs\uc2  is reportedly on suicide watch in a Brooklyn jail. Fox News correspondent Matt Finn has the latest on the case. Matt, what can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 MATT FINN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Laura, tonight, Combs is on that suicide watch in Brooklyn's Metropolitan Detention Center. That is not the jail the rapper wants to be in. His attorney asked for a transfer to a prison in Essex County, New Jersey, but that did not happen.\par \par \uc2 People magazine reports the Metropolitan Detention Center is known for being dangerous and understaffed with a high number of deaths and suicides. Several courts have recognized it as not fit for pre-trial detention, according to the Daily Beast.\par \par \uc2 Combs is accused of organized what he calls freak-offs, destructive, and despicable sex sessions in which he allegedly hired men and women, typically in hotel rooms, providing drugs, and he sometimes filmed it all.\par \par \uc2 Court documents reading victims believe they could not refuse Combs' demands without risking their financial or job security or without repercussions in the form of physical or emotional abuse.\par \par \uc2 Now, Diddy's attorneys say, in part, that those sex parties were consensual and he's innocent. Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Matt, thank you. Joining me now, Brian Claypool, a criminal defense attorney. So, Brian, what Matt was just talking about these so- called freak-offs. These were no secret.\par \par \uc2 So, he'd been openly bragging about these wild parties, you know, there's like sex parties, and it looks like, you know, there were probably other -- lots of other things going on there, too. But he was bragging about this for, what, a couple of decades. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: This is what you need to do. Women, beautiful women. We need alcohols.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Right.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Alcohols.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Right.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Not just one alcohol.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Alcohols.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Right. Just blend. You need the ladies. You need the booze.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Right. Got to keep them there.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Right.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: You need locks on the doors.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: This is sounding kind of dangerous now.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: It's a little kinky.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Brian, that was creepy. But what took the Fed so long to do something?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CLAYPOOL, CRIMINAL DEFENSE ATTORNEY: Hey, Laura, always great to be with you. Yeah, I think it's the -- I'm in LA, it's the Hollywood celebrity path to justice where we simply look at a clip like that and we laugh and giggle about, oh, let's get alcohol. Let's get drugs. Let's get all these ladies at a party.\par \par \uc2 You know, for years, P. Diddy's gotten a hall pass. He's actually created his own kingdom where he makes his own rules and you got to follow his rules or you get penalized. And guess what, Laura, he's now in a federal penitentiary. And for the first time in his life, he's facing the, his own fate at the hands of somebody else.\par \par \uc2 And I will tell you, Laura, I've done federal cases that that's not the place you want to be in federal court. Ninety percent of that whole criminal indictments brought down are pled out, right? A plea deal is brought. And of the only 10 percent left, 90 percent of those defendants that goes to trial, they lose.\par \par \uc2 So, he is looking at a daunting uphill battle to fight these horrible charges.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Brian, one of the DHS agents involved in this raid on his Miami mansion described what he saw inside the house saying that, in my opinion, he's as bad as Epstein. These women are young, either barely legal or barely illegal.\par \par \uc2 Brian, this sounds like he was a monster.\par \par \uc2 CLAYPOOL: Yeah, Laura, I will tell you that this indictment was very detailed by the way, too. Your viewers should know there were over 300 subpoenas served, over 50 witnesses interviewed. Why is that important? Because the federal prosecutors aren't going to be leveling an indictment like this unless they have ammunition and they have cause to bring it.\par \par \uc2 And the core issue at this case, which the feds have to prove, which it's looking like they can, is that P. Diddy essentially controlled these women, that he threatened them with stopping the money that he was paying them, that he spiked their drinks with drugs that's been alleged in the indictment, and that he basically told them, I'm going to ruin your life unless you engage in these freak offs.\par \par \uc2 And that's really what the prosecutor's got to prove. And if you've got some victims coming forward to talk about that, you've got financial records that prove that, travel itinerary that proves that, P. Diddy has very little defense.\par \par \uc2 And I got to tell you, Laura, if this guy wants to fight these charges, he's going to have to step up and walk into a courtroom and talk about it. If he doesn't testify in this case, he is dead on arrival.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, who else is on these tapes? I mean, will the tapes ever see the light of day? I mean, that's what everybody's talking about. Who else is there? It's like with the Epstein, you know, his pleasure island. Who went? Who was there? What's the manifest?\par \par \uc2 But Brian, chances, one being least likely, 10 being most likely, will we learn of other celebrities that were at these freak offs?\par \par \uc2 CLAYPOOL: Yeah, I think you've got an eight or a nine for likelihood you're going to hear about it. Why? Because there are going to be more indictments coming down, Laura. That's an important point here because he's being charged with racketeering conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 You're going to have, let's start on the inner circle. You're going to have former bodyguards. You can possibly have former musicians that he was with. You've got personal assistants that are going to be -- they're already talking about this. They're good.\par \par \uc2 Some of those are likely to be charged. And then guess what? If you've got some music moguls that are sending women over these alleged freak offs, then you've got some top people that are going to start crumbling too, because that's -- they're all part of what's called this criminal enterprise.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Brian, always great to see you. Thank you so much. Now Team Trump showing just how dangerous the Democrats rhetoric has gotten. The Angle's 2024 ad war, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The 2024 ad war. So much fun is well underway. We've seen some really great ads. Seen some stunners, some stinkers too. Each week of the campaign, we're going to dissect them, Republican and Democrat, and issue our own Ingraham Angle report cards.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Terry Schilling, president of the American Principles Project. Terry, let's start with this powerful ad from former President Trump highlighting some of the rhetoric of the Democrats. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: If you had to be stuck in an elevator with either President Trump, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , or Jeff Sessions, who would it be?\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: Does one of us have to come out alive?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: We're in high school. I'd take you behind the gym and beat the hell out of them.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I just don't even know why there aren't uprisings all over the country. Maybe there will be.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: You cannot be civil with a political party that wants to destroy what you stand for.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I have thought an awful lot about blowing up the White House.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Terry, how do you grade that?\par \par \uc2 TERRY SCHILLING, PRESIDENT, AMERICAN PRINCIPLES PROJECT: Laura, I give that ad in A, I think it's very powerful. It uses the most well-known and recognizable Democrats, and it uses their own words against them to show just how vicious and evil they are. A.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah, well, I think they -- they're the ones saying Trump's dangerous. Trump's causing all this chaos and they seem to be urging it. So, again, using their own words, I think it's very powerful.\par \par \uc2 But Harris released an ad yesterday, Terry, touting her record on the, wait for it, the border. Check it out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: The border state prosecutor. She took on drug cartels. She backed the toughest border control bill in decades. Fixing the border is tough, so is \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Terry, that were --\par \par \uc2 SCHILLING: F. No way. I give it an F. This is not a believable ad. Anyone that's concerned about the border is going to be asking themselves a very simple question when they see this ad. Why didn't she do this in the first four years? F.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah, I don't think you can go back to your time as a prosecutor in California, but what happened? It's like in a resume, you know, when there's a gap in a resume, Terry, like, well, what happened in those years?\par \par \uc2 All right. There's another Trump ad focusing on one of Kamala's more pronounced progressive policy proposals. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Kamala supports taxpayer funded sex changes for prisoners.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Every transgender inmate in the prison system would have access.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Kamala's for they, them. President Trump is for you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, that just came out. That just came out today. Late today, Terry. What do you think about that one?\par \par \uc2 SCHILLING: I give that an A plus, Laura. This ad is so important to run. We saw in 2022 that the Democrats were able to counter every single attack on the economy and crime and inflation, but Democrats cannot counter this. They're too extreme and radical and they're going to lose their base if they moderate, I give this ad an A plus.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Wow. OK. And then there's a race between Senator Ted Cruz, incumbent, and his Democrat challenger, \uc2 Colin Allred\uc2 . That's kind of tightening up. Here's the latest ad from the Allred campaign. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Football hurts. No pain, no gain. You got to fight for it. What's Ted Cruz fought for? Himself. He wants to raise the retirement age for hardworking Texans. Now that hurts.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Terry, verdict.\par \par \uc2 SCHILLING: I give that a C minus. I don't see that, actually, changing any votes in Texas. It's mostly substanceless. I give it a C minus, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah. I mean, fought for himself. And the football, the football stuff gets old after a while. I'm sorry. Everyone likes to use their sport, sporting days as a analogy, but I find that to be, itself, kind of tired. Plus, Ted Cruz is a great Senator. Terry, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 All right. Kamala's latest word of the day. And why are Biden, Harris's significant others taking the lead? Raymond Arroyo, next. Friday Follies.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's Friday and that means it's time for Raymond Arroyo's birthday on Friday Follies. Happy birthday, Raymond.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I don't want to talk about that.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I'm so upset. We're not together. Wow. Rebecca --\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I didn't leave you. You left us.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- surprises for you. Well, she has some surprises for you, but I was going to surprise you by doing to you what you did to me a few years back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Final cake. Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Sorry, Laura. Oh my God.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Shame cake. You are the worst. You are the worst.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: You almost killed me with that Styrofoam platter, Ingraham. I remember that. I still have the scars here, but I won't show that.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah. Well, we covered a bit of the Oprah-Kamala show earlier, and her A-list cheer squad, Raymond, surrounded her, including Meryl Streep, your fave.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yeah. But Laura, she couldn't even properly address Streep. This is an Academy Award winner, been a public fixture for decades. Harris starts talking to Meryl Streep, and I think she needed a cue card. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: Thank you, Marilyn, and for your, just the gift that you give your doers, do your talent.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Who is Marilyn? Thank you, Marilyn. She doesn't know Streep's name? But you know, I guess with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , Laura, every hour is happy hour and ladies drink free. '\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I think the word of the day has been preventable. Preventable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Actually, Laura, I disagree with Marilyn, I mean, Meryl. The Kamala word of the day is clearly, story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: It is your story and the story of our friends before you is really -- that's the American story, right? In telling your story and being so strong in the way you do it and both of these stories, for you to tell these stories. This story is a story that is sadly not the only story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: It's unbelievable. It's simply unbelievable. It's like a child who learns one word, and then they struggle for the next thought and it never comes. But maybe this daytime talk candidacy will work. It's all emotion, but not a very good one. Certainly not an articulate one, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And then what about the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , as well?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, they described this event as, with abortion in focus. Harris's event with Oprah prompts cheers and tears. The tears are from the general public trying to figure out what the hell is she saying? That's what I think that is.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah, I mean, cheers and tears. Yeah, tears like, oh my gosh, Kim Jong-un and Xi and Putin are watching this going, this can't be real. Give us Oprah. All right, Raymond, there was a sighting today.\par \par \uc2 Joe, in Maryland Street. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is back. He held his first cabinet meeting in 11 months.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yeah, there's only one problem. He wasn't sitting at the head of the table. Guess who was?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: So, we're grateful that Jill is here today. Heard that clapping. It wasn't for me. When I came in, I'd like to turn it over to Jill and for any comments she has. And it's all yours, kid.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: It's all yours, kid. It's been hers all along. Jill Biden led the cabinet meeting. But I guess, look, when you're not shepherding Joe around or, you know, making sure he's not stumbling, I guess she's got a lot of free time. She even had a Rose Garden event without Joe.\par \par \uc2 So, I guess she's now the president in, you know, in Peccatore, in the heart.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And waiting.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: This is amazing.,\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I like her suit. I like that color.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I guess --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That's a pretty suit.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I guess the V.P. is just following Biden's lead. She also is relying on her spouse to do the heavy lifting. While Harris refuses to do one on one interviews with the media, her husband, Doug Emhoff, was subjected on NBC -- ABC, rather, to all the tough questions. He's talking about the economy and the infrastructure and filling in the gaps. She needs to be there. Joyous herself should be sitting for these interviews. Not behind --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Wait a second, Raymond. Is that what -- is that what women in the modern era are doing now? They're going back to relying on their husbands for everything. I thought they were their own people. I thought that was like, we are women here. Hear me roar. Now they're just -- we are women heroes bore, everybody, I guess.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Only in the Biden household, the women are leading the way. Finally, there are 40 million Christians who do not vote in the U.S., Laura. And there's a new film trying to change that. God is not dead and God we trust, it's in theaters this weekend. It was in the top 10 last week.\par \par \uc2 And there's a familiar moderator during the big debate in the movie. David White, Scott Baio, Dean Cain, and somebody you might know is in it. God's not dead --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The birthday boy is in it. What are you doing for your birthday? You're doing something fun?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I am going out for dinner and I'm not going to tell you where, because I don't want anybody dropping a cake in my life.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Raymond, happy birthday. Raymond is never ages. He always looks the same, because he doesn't go into the sun. Like when we go into the sun, if it's cloudy a little bit, Raymond's like, it's too sunny. Let's go inside. But that's why you look so good.\par \par \uc2 All right, Raymond, happy, happy, happy birthday. Everybody have a great weekend. Thank you for watching all week long. We'll see you back here, Monday night. And Jesse's next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020240921ek9k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Multiple Security Failures In Pennsylvania Shooting. Harris Highlights Abortion Issue. Biden Calls On Congress To Avoid Shutdown. Indo- Pacific Leaders To Gather In Wilmington, Senior \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  Military Commander Killed In Beirut. State-By-State Rules For Election Ballots; Most States in U.S. Offering Some Form Of Early Voting Or Mail-In Ballots. \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  Meets With Springfield, Ohio, Residents Upset By Influx Of Haitian Migrants. Democratic Lawmakers Attempting To Increase Regulations On Sports Betting; Democratic Presidential Candidate \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Criticized For Not Providing Policy Specifics. Secret Service Issues Report On Shortcoming That Led to Recent Assassination Attempts On Republican Presidential Candidate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, David Spunt, Aishah Hasnie, Jacqui Heinrich, Bret  Baier, Nate Foy, Bret Baier, Bill Melugin; Chad Pergram; Juan Williams \par \uc2 7,890 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 September 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: That's it for us. Good night.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Shannon Bream, in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Harris, gets back on the campaign trail after having a tough time answering questions at an event with \uc2 Oprah Winfrey\uc2 . What changes in the electoral process in Georgia could mean for the presidential election? And how Congress is trying to shape the future of sports gambling?\par \par \uc2 But first, "BREAKING TONIGHT", the acting head of the Secret Service says his agency failed in its mission to protect former President Trump. He blames, in part, complacency, poor communication, and a lack of clarity in dealing with local law enforcement, particularly, regarding coverage of that roof from which an assailant shot and nearly killed the Republican nominee.\par \par \uc2 The assessment of the first assassination effort comes just five days after the second attempt on the former president's life.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt, starts us off tonight from the Justice Department. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hello, Shannon. Good evening.\par \par \uc2 Acting Secret Service Director Ronald Rowe says it was poor communication that led to the assassination attempt of former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on July 13th.\par \par \uc2 Today, he announced some new changes, and he says he is going to rewrite the playbook of the storied agency.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RONALD ROWE, ACTING SECRET SERVICE DIRECTOR: The Secret Service did not give clear guidance or direction to our local law enforcement partners.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Acting Secret Service Director Ronald Rowe out with a blunt assessment of his agency.\par \par \uc2 ROWE: While some members of the advance team were very diligent, there was complacency on the part of others that led to a breach of security protocols.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): A complete internal report days even weeks away, but the summary released today follows a similar theme. "Some local police entities supporting the Butler venue had no knowledge that there were two separate communication centers on site."\par \par \uc2 Of particular concern, coordination around the building where shooter Thomas Crooks fired as many as eight rounds from an A.R.-15-style automatic rifle. "There was a lack of detailed knowledge by Secret Service personnel regarding the state or local law enforcement presence that would be present in and around the AGR complex."\par \par \uc2 Rowe said the agency is in the accountability phase, and people will likely face consequences.\par \par \uc2 ROWE: This agency has among the most robust table of penalties in the entirety of the federal government.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): House Speaker Mike Johnson is optimistic.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): We certainly hope that, that is true. We're going to do everything we can to make sure it is.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The yeas are 405. The nays are zeroed.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Today, members in the House unanimously passed a measure requiring presidential candidates to receive the same protection as sitting presidents.\par \par \uc2 Rowe insisted the levels are the same now, but there are skeptics.\par \par \uc2 REP. JARED MOSKOWITZ (D-FL): If this is the highest level of security based on the threats against President Trump, we need to create several higher levels of security, because it just seems to be inadequate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): Acting Director Rowe was also pressed on what happened last weekend in West Palm Beach, while that's a separate investigation and separate situation, according to the Secret Service. He does admit there is a common theme that they need to rewrite the book at the Secret Service, and he says changes are coming along the way. Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. David Spunt, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, there big changes coming for the electoral process in Georgia. This comes as early in person voting is now underway in some states. We'll take a closer look at that in just a few minutes.\par \par \uc2 But first, congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie has the latest on the Trump campaign. Hello, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Shannon. Good to see you. Tonight, the Trump campaign urging supporters to register to vote early in Virginia, where early voting began today, while getting news another very important state is making changes to how it counts its ballots.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Tonight, allies of former President Trump in Georgia, celebrating a victory in the critical swing state.\par \par \uc2 The state's election board now requiring ballots to be hand counted, despite critics on both sides of the aisle and the Secretary of State warning that will slow down results, Trump now heads to North Carolina Saturday, another key state on his path to victory, but where scandal now surrounds his endorsed candidate for governor, Lieutenant Governor Mark Robinson.\par \par \uc2 Fox confirms Robinson will not attend this weekend's Trump rally, as Democrats remind voters, Trump said this --\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: This is \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  on steroids.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): -- about the man, CNN reports wrote disturbing comments like, I'm a black Nazi on a porn site a decade ago.\par \par \uc2 Trump's running mate, J.D. Vance, sidestepping the topic on X, attempting to move the conversation back to Vice President Harris.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the former president addressed Iran's hacking of his campaign for the first time as he appealed to Jewish voters.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : What's that all about? That alone should get me elected.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): But then, raised eyebrows for saying Jewish Americans would be partly to blame if he lost in November.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And honestly, you ought to have your heads examined.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And just in, Georgia's Republican attorney general says that new ballot counting rule is "illegal" and will likely head to Court. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And we will cover it if it does.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: We will be watching this very closely.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Aishah, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: You bet.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, Vice President Harris was in Georgia earlier this afternoon. Her focus was on what she thinks is a winning issue there. Senior White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich is with the Harris campaign ahead of another rally tonight in Madison, Wisconsin. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Harris will try to cut into new Quinnipiac polling showing that Trump leads her on the economy here outside the margin of error. To that end, she'll be introduced by the local \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  president after the national organization declined to endorse a candidate, despite historically backing Democrats with more than half their membership supporting Trump.\par \par \uc2 The news out of Georgia this week bumped Wisconsin to second on her schedule.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: He brags about overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 He is proud, proud that women are dying, proud that doctors and nurses could be thrown in prison for administering care? How dare he?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Vice President Harris adding a campaign stop in Georgia after a report linking two deaths to the state's abortion restrictions. Harris highlighting 28-year-old Amber Nicole Thurman, who died of complications from abortion pills she got out of state.\par \par \uc2 Thurman's mother says a simple procedure would have saved her life, but doctors delayed treatment because of the state's laws.\par \par \uc2 SHANETTE THURMAN, MOTHER OF AMBER NICOLE THURMAN: I want you also to know, Amber was not a statistic. You are looking at a mother that is broken. The worst pain ever.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Georgia's governor calling the narrative misinformation, pointing to a pro-life advocate who says Georgia's laws, "Explicitly allows physicians to intervene in cases of medical emergencies or if the fetus has no detectable heartbeat, both of which applied to Thurman's case, and any assertion that she experienced a delay in care as a secondary effect of the law is mere speculation. One thing is clear, Thurman died from a legal chemical abortion, which abortion advocates have long argued is safer than Tylenol."\par \par \uc2 But Harris believes the issue is a winning one in Georgia, after Trump dodged this question at the debate.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS, ANCHOR, ABC NEWS: Would you veto a national abortion ban if it came to your desk?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, I won't have to.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Harris's messaging on other issues still muddled.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We really would love to know what your plan is to help lower the cost of living.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: A story I hear around the country as I travel. And in terms of both rightly having the right to have aspirations and dreams and ambitions for your family.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Harris is on track for the fewest interviews in decades, according to an Axios report. In the 59 days since she joined the ticket, Trump and Vance have done 70 interviews with print and T.V. reporters. Harris and Walz have done seven.\par \par \uc2 Her campaign said today that people shouldn't read too much into that, but confirmed that her focus will stay largely outside traditional (INAUDIBLE). Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, the door remains open here at Fox News across all of our flat forums. And Jacqui, you too, if you can get one there on the road. Thank you so much. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. President Biden is making one last big push to promote his agenda and shape his legacy. Today, he held his first full Cabinet meeting in a very long time, and he had some direct marching orders.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House correspondent Peter Doocy has details. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Good evening. Shannon. President Biden is basically trying to Trump proof his legacy and make it as hard as possible for Republicans to undo key achievements if they win back the White House.\par \par \uc2 That was the focus of the first Cabinet meeting in almost a year.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We only have four months left in the administration, and we're going to keep running through the tape.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): And that's why the president got the whole Cabinet together to tell them, according to a White House official, "To get as much work done as possible, whether that is moving funding out the door, announcing new programs or policies, or delivering on programs and policies we have already announced."\par \par \uc2 A headache lies 10 days ahead when the government will shut down if Congress cannot pass a continuing resolution to fund the government. But the president appears to be taking a hands-off approach to these negotiations.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Only path forward, and it's by working across the aisle. We got to have faith that our leaders will pull this together.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): This weekend, President Biden is hosting leaders of Australia, Japan and India at home in Wilmington, Delaware.\par \par \uc2 REAR ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET.), WHITE HOUSE NATIONAL SECURITY COMMUNICATIONS ADVISOR: Showing them a place and a community that shaped so much of the public servant and the leader that he became.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): The focus on Asia and Australia comes as recent Middle East diplomatic efforts crumble. According to a Wall Street Journal report about an Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  ceasefire, "There is no chance now of it happening, a Middle Eastern official added, shortly after the operation against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . Everyone is in a wait-and-see mode until after the election. The outcome will determine what can happen in the next administration."\par \par \uc2 Nearing retirement, President Biden remains optimistic.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: A lot of things don't look realistic until we get them done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 As part of this weekend's Quad summit, President Biden, right now, is hosting a personal meeting with the prime minister of Australia at his house in Wilmington, Delaware.\par \par \uc2 But for reasons that remain unknown, there are no coverage opportunities of tonight's meeting or any of the meetings over the weekend with leaders at the House, even though that is typically the kind of thing that White House leaders would want lots of photos and videos of out there. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It is. All right. Peter Doocy at the White House. Thank you, Peter.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed this Friday. The Dow gained 38 for a new record close. The S&P 500 dropped 11. NASDAQ fell 66.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow was up one and two-thirds percentage points. The S&P 500 gained 1-1/3. NASDAQ rose 1-1/2.\par \par \uc2 The federal government is suing some big pharmacy benefit managers over a system of drug rebates that regulators say has made the price of insulin soar. Three companies process about 80 percent of prescriptions here in the U.S., the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  alleges Caremark, \uc2 Express Scripts\uc2 , and Optum Rx have engaged in anticompetitive practices that spur price increases. All three companies deny wrongdoing.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Israel continues its offensive against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  terrorists in Lebanon, taking out a major target. We're going to give you a live report on that. But first, here is what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight.\par \par \uc2 Fox 56 in Lexington, Kentucky, as the sheriff is accused of killing a judge in the Letcher County courthouse.\par \par \uc2 Sheriff Shawn Stines allegedly shot District Judge Kevin Mullins after an argument Thursday afternoon inside the courthouse. Stines is accused in a federal lawsuit of ignoring abuse by one of his deputies, who later pleaded guilty to forcing a woman to have sex in Judge Mullins chambers.\par \par \uc2 FOX5 in New York, as authorities place Sean 'Diddy' Combs on suicide watch inside a special housing unit at a Brooklyn jail. There are suspicions the entertainment mogul has become a target.\par \par \uc2 Combs was denied bail after being charged with racketeering, conspiracy, and sex trafficking.\par \par \uc2 And this is live look at Lancaster, Pennsylvania, from FOX 43. one of the big stories there tonight, an effort to restart a unit of the infamous nuclear power plant, Three Mile Island. A separate unit of the plant suffered a partial meltdown in 1979, in one of the biggest industrial accidents in the country's history.\par \par \uc2 The restart requires federal, state, and local approvals. That is tonight's live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Families and supporters of hostages held by the terror group \uc2 Hamas\uc2  since the October 7th massacre in Israel rallied outside the United Nations today. They want the organization to address the crisis and secure a deal for their release. The Israeli military says about 101 hostages remain in captivity. It's believed about 35 of those have been killed.\par \par \uc2 The theme of next week's \uc2 U.N. General Assembly\uc2  is leaving no one behind.\par \par \uc2 Israel continues to pound terrorist targets in Lebanon tonight, an airstrike took out at least 14 people today, including one of the most wanted terrorists in the world. Correspondent Nate Foy is in Tel Aviv tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REAR ADM. DANIEL HAGARI, ISRAEL DEFENSE FORCES SPOKESPERSON: I can now confirm that Ibrahim Aqil was eliminated together with other senior terrorists in \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 's Radwan forces.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): An Israeli air strike killed the top \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  commander in Beirut. Ibrahim Aqil headed the group's Special Operations Unit under \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leader \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Prior to the strike, the U.S. offered $7 million for information on Aqil. He's connected to the 1983 bombings at the U.S. Embassy and Marine barracks that killed more than 300 people in Beirut.\par \par \uc2 According to the \uc2 U.S. State Department\uc2 , "In the 1980s, Aqil directed the taking of American and German hostages in Lebanon." The U.S. maintains it knew nothing about the strike beforehand.\par \par \uc2 Lebanon's health ministry\uc2  says it killed at least 14 people and injured 66 others. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  responded saying, "We consider this a new crime in the ongoing series of Zionist crimes, a violation of Lebanese sovereignty and an escalation of Zionist aggression."\par \par \uc2 Lebanon's Health Minister accused Israel of war crimes for exploding \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 's communication devices.\par \par \uc2 FIRASS ABIAD, LEBANESE HEALTH MINISTER: That is why detonating these devices in civilian areas and among civilians is clearly a war crime.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  fired more than 200 rockets into northern Israel today, the Iron Dome remains strong.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  shared a statement, "Our goals are clear, and our actions speak for themselves."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FOY (on camera): Tonight, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  is officially acknowledging the killing of Ibrahim Aqil in a new statement that was just released, there's no specific reference to planned retaliation, but Prime Minister Netanyahu did call for an emergency security meeting tonight to plan for whatever comes next, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Nate Foy in Tel Aviv, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, as this election cycles first in person votes are cast today, Bret Baier reports on all the different ways to vote ahead of Election Day across the U.S.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Has voter turnout increased with the early voting options?\par \par \uc2 It really is the same types of voters who are voting in different ways.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But first Beyond our Borders tonight, Dutch police arrest a 22-year- old man after a fatal stabbing in Rotterdam. They say the suspect may have been ideologically driven. He reportedly shouted repeatedly Allahu Akbar a number of times during the attack.\par \par \uc2 Two Russians set a record for the longest continuous stay on the International Space Station. \uc2 Russia's space agency\uc2  says Oleg Kononenko and Nikolai Chub broke the old record of 370 days, 21 hours and 22 minutes. That mark was set in September of last year by two Russians and an American.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Katsura, Japan, one of the big stories there tonight. Japan and China agreed to resolve their disputes over the discharge of treated radioactive wastewater from the tsunami hit Fukushima nuclear power plant into the Pacific Ocean. China subsequently banned Japanese seafood. Japan says China will start working toward easing the import ban and will join the expanded monitoring of wastewater discharges from that plant.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories beyond our borders tonight, we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: For tonight's featured race. We turn to the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  and a decision that may have a bearing on who wins in battleground Nevada. Presidential hopeful Jill Stein and other green party candidates were dealt a loss when the court rejected an emergency appeal to allow them on the ballot. The ruling could help the Democrats Senate nominee, but let's take a closer look at what's happening in the presidential race there.\par \par \uc2 Good luck finding any daylight between \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . They are in a virtual draw with Harris up just two tenths of a point. Both campaigns are banking on Nevada's six electoral votes as a must win in the race to 270 come November.\par \par \uc2 Back in 2020, incumbent Trump fell to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  by roughly 33,000 votes. And while he won the White House in 2016 Mr. Trump still fell short in the Silver State, losing to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  by more than 27,000 votes. This go round, both campaigns are appealing to Nevada's hospitality sector. Trump first calling for no taxes on tips, an idea \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  later matched.\par \par \uc2 The election process is officially underway in several states, but how those votes are being cast differs by location.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, my colleague Bret Baier takes a look at the options for voting all across the country.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thanks, Shannon, the first in person votes of the 2024 election were cast today in three states, Minnesota and South Dakota absentee voters are able to head to the polls. In Virginia, Any registered voter can now cast a ballot in the Commonwealth.\par \par \uc2 In at least nine states for those who requested them, absentee ballots are now heading to voters living overseas or serving in the U.S. military. Absentee ballots for other voters are also en route in four other states.\par \par \uc2 The first mail in ballots for any voter wishing to vote early by mail go out tomorrow in Maryland and New Jersey. So, what is the difference between all five of these options and what is available in your state.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we take a look at the upcoming election calendar and how these methods came to be.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): With more options than ever available to voters.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Vote early, vote absentee, vote on Election Day. Do whatever you want, but you have to vote.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): Presidential campaigns are treating every day like Election Day.\par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE (R), U.S. VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Get your neighbors to the polls, get your family members to the polls, knock on doors, make phone calls.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D), U.S. VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Every vote is going to matter, every precinct.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): In 2020, 69 percent of voters cast ballots before Election Day.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Is that something we can expect do you think to continue on that trend?\par \par \uc2 JOHN FORTIER, AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE: The 2020 was an extraordinary year where we saw this huge jump up. Even saw very different opinions of Democrats and Republicans about whether you should vote by mail.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Anytime you have mail in ballots, you're going to have problems.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): In 2020, Mississippi was the only state to require an excuse to vote early. Four states offered early in person voting. Everywhere else allowed mail in ballots without an excuse. Nine states in Washington D.C. held all mail elections in which all registered voters were sent a ballot without requesting one.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: They sent out millions and millions of ballots. They go all over the place. If I ran with an honest vote counter in California, I would win California.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Eight states and D.C. left the practice in place. New Jersey still permits all mail elections for small jurisdictions.\par \par \uc2 Some people are going to get out and go to the polls to be casting ballots for this election right now.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FORTIER, \uc2 AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE\uc2 : Some states will start with early in-person voting, a little bit at the clerk's office, but then in an intense way at major sites for several weeks before the election. There really is no one size fits all.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You say it's not one-size-fits-all, but it really is different dramatically in some states.\par \par \uc2 FORTIER: Yes, we have states, again, like Oregon and Washington who are basically 100 percent voting by mail. There are almost no polling place. We have some states in the northeast that some in the deep south who really look much more traditional.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alabama and New Hampshire have adopted the same strict requirements as Mississippi. Only absentee voters vote by mail with a valid excuse, such as illness, living out of state, or working a shift that conflicts with polling hours. Democrats question efforts to try to restrict access to voting.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Across our nation we are witnessing a full-on assault on other hard fought, hard won fundamental freedoms and rights, like the freedom to vote.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Seven other states have eliminated mail ballots and now offer in- person early voting instead, 36 continue to allow voters to cast ballots by mail.\par \par \uc2 For the people who say there should just be paper ballots, maybe there should just be one week to try to avoid this influx of coming through the mail, there's all kinds of talk out there how to make it better or safer. Is there one sense of what's better?\par \par \uc2 FORTIER: I think there are differences. I do think people have a good instinct that they want there to be a ballot there, a tangible, physical ballot. But we also have very complicated ballots with many people on the ballot, sometimes 10, 20 races that you are voting for in a particular election. And that really does mean we need to use technology to count them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: While 12 states, including swing state Arizona, will begin processing and counting ballots before Election Day, laws prevent results from being reported until November 5th. Fourteen states, including competitive Minnesota, will not begin tallying mail ballots until after the polls close.\par \par \uc2 I talk to businessmen around the country. They say we can track a package all around the world, know exactly where it's going to go, and, yet, we have this ballot process that we haven't fully made fast. We haven't made it quick. It's had some problems over the last at least two or three election cycles.\par \par \uc2 FORTIER: I think part of it is because states want to put in a lot of convenience parts to voting and they don't necessarily think of how they all fit together.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Toss up state Nevada also allows mostly mail elections and will still count ballots that arrive up to four days following Election Day so long as they are postmarked November 5th.\par \par \uc2 The FOX News Decision Desk can now project former Vice President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will win Pennsylvania and Nevada.\par \par \uc2 And in Pennsylvania, 2024 could look a lot similar to 2020 when it took four days after the election to call the race. Officials are reporting delays in sending out mail ballots. The hold up could cause issues on Election Day since Pennsylvania does not start processing mail ballots until 7:00 a.m. on November 5th.\par \par \uc2 We saw in 2020 and how long that took. How long do you think it could take in 2024 to figure out who won the presidential election?\par \par \uc2 FORTIER: I guess I'm an optimist and I think we'll probably see things be somewhat quicker than in 2020. I think there's a good chance that we will be able to know the outcome unless it's very close.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It is very, very close if you look at the polls. We have a number of weeks to go before Election Day. Have those individual states changed operations since 2020 to speed things up, to be more effective, to make sure there's checks and balances in place?\par \par \uc2 FORTIER: I think there has been some improvement, and they vary from state to state. The voting by mail is not going to be as high or as unexpected. And so I think many states will be ready for that and be able to count more quickly.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): Over the weekend, all states will send out absentee ballots to military and overseas voters as required by federal law. North Carolina sent those to voters today. All other absentee ballots from the state are expected to go out next week.\par \par \uc2 We've got a lot to cover. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We sure do. Bret, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we're going to take you to Springfield, Ohio, the center of the most recent illegal immigration controversy, to hear from overwhelmed residents.\par \par \uc2 And later, the future of sports betting, what some Democratic lawmakers want to do.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Two illegal immigrants from El Salvador are facing charges tonight on separate child rape cases on Nantucket island. The immigration and customs enforcement in Boston says the two got-aways were arrested last week.\par \par \uc2 Springfield, Ohio, has become ground zero in the fight over illegal immigration in recent days. Last night former Republican presidential candidate \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  heard from dozens of locals upset about the influx of at least 15,000 Haitian migrants. Correspondent Bill Melugin is in Springfield tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This country care more about Ukraine's borders, Israel's borders, everybody else's borders.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Frustration boiling over in Springfield, Ohio. A small city gripped by national headlines and overwhelmed by the arrival of 15,000 Haitian migrants in recent years.\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2 , (R) FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I blame the federal policies that are responsible for what's going on in this city.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Former GOP presidential candidate \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  held a town hall with hundreds of Springfield residents last night, allowing them to vent about the issues they say the mass influx of Haitians has brought.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We could have handled 3,000, 4,000 people. We are not unwelcoming to that effect.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Make the Haitians feel welcome, but they also have to learn our culture.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Springfield residents say they have been smeared as bigots and called unwelcoming for speaking out. We asked them to respond.\par \par \uc2 And there are people outside of Springfield who call you guys racist for being worried and upset about this. How do you respond to that?\par \par \uc2 DIANA DANIELS, SPRINGFIELD, OHIO, RESIDENT: Well, you know what? You don't live here so mind your own business. How about coming up and I change your population by 50 percent to see how you like it.\par \par \uc2 SANDY BLACK, SPRINGFIELD, OHIO, RESIDENT: It isn't racism. If they were 20,000 people from Sweden it would be the same thing.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Former President Trump has said he plans to visit Springfield in the coming weeks, but the city's Republican mayor said a Trump visit is not what's best for his city right now.\par \par \uc2 ROB RUE, (R) SPRINGFIELD, OHIO, MAYOR: Should he choose to change his plans, it would convey a significant message of peace to the city of Springfield.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): Meanwhile in Washington today, House Democrats in the Haiti caucus criticized Republicans for their focus for Haitians here in Springfield, calling it, quote, "dangerous and disgraceful." Those Democrats also said they plan to make their own visit here to Springfield in the future. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Bill Melugin, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Well, we are about to begin another big college and pro-football weekend, and that means gambling, both legal and illegal. Well, now two Democratic lawmakers are trying to shape the future of sports betting in the U.S. Here's senior congressional correspondent Chad Pergram.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: There are parlays.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Gambling can be done in secret on your phone without anybody knowing what you're doing.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The prop bets.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Betting online made it seem very easy to turn $100 into $200 or even more.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Desperate people trying to beat the odds.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thankfully, our story did not end in tragedy.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Gordon Douglas's son Andrew fell into the grips of gambling once he moved to a state with legal online sports gaming. Now lawmakers want to cut the action.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICHARD BLUMENTHAL, (D-CT): We're not banning gambling. We are banning practices that exploit and abuse people who legitimately want to gamble.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Blumenthal wants to prohibit betting ads during live games and curb bets on college sports. Student athletes sometimes receive threats if they meet a buzzer beater costing betters money. Lawmakers frame gambling as a public health cris.\par \par \uc2 REP. PAUL TONKO, (D-NY): Gambling disorders as an addiction are akin to heroin and opioid and tobacco and alcohol and cocaine use.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: There is also concern about superstars marketing gambling. LeBron James with \uc2 NFL\uc2  picks on Draft Kings, Wayne Gretzky pitching Bet MGM.\par \par \uc2 HARRY LEVANT, GAMBLING COUNSELOR: The sports leagues, teams, and players have entered into previously unthinkable partnerships with the gambling industry.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The \uc2 American Gaming Association\uc2  opposes the bill. It believes more regulation returns gaming to street corner bookies, and betting problems can be monitored on phones.\par \par \uc2 JOE MALONEY, \uc2 AMERICAN GAMING ASSOCIATION\uc2 : Our legal operators are here. We are not operating in the shadows. We are not operating in another domicile in the Caribbean.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): There are consequences for limits on gaming. Sports gambling generated $1.3 billion in local tax revenue already this year. People could wager a staggering $35 billion on the \uc2 NFL\uc2  this season. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That's real money. All right, Chad, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, the panel on the presidential race, the Oprah Winfrey livestream, plus Winners and Losers, and then, of course, "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We really would love to know what your plan is to help lower the cost of living.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Yours is a story I hear around the country as I travel. And in terms of both rightly having the right to have aspirations and dreams and ambitions.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: She is grossly incompetent. Look, she can't do an interview.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL WHATLEY, REPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: He is the only candidate who is out there talking to American families about the issues that they care about, jobs and the economy.\par \par \uc2 JARED MOSKOWITZ, (D-FL): You don't have to like everything about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . You can have disagreements. But look at the other choice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, let's talk about it with our panel, FOX News senior political analyst Juan Williams, Byron York, chief political correspondent of "The Washington Examiner," and Mollie Hemingway, editor in chief of "The Federalist." All right, happy Friday, everybody.\par \par \uc2 You heard a little bit from both of the candidates there. I want to read something from Peggy Noonan writing in "The Wall Street Journal" an op-ed piece. She calls the vice president an "Artless Dodger." She says "The race is deadlocked with six weeks to go, and if you are an undecided, unsure, or wavering voter, it looks like awful versus empty. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  stepped aside. Ms. Harris was elevated two months ago. That is enough time at least to start making clear what she believes, wants, and means to do. She hasn't. Only when speaking of her personal biography does she seem authoritative. Otherwise, she's airy, evasive, and nonresponsive." Juan, something tells me you might disagree.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: No. Look, I --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You don't?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No. I'll tell you what, I think all of us on this panel, but especially you because you are Shannon Bream, we would love to have \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  sit down and do an interview.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: She is invited. Open invite.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: There you go, from FOX News. But, in fact, we are not her audience. And if you look at her audience, which is American voters, and the people that Peggy Noonan is talking about, especially right now in a tight race, undecided or swing voters, you would have to say, wait a minute, her strategy seems to be working, because the polls that have come out this week show, you know, it's a very tight race but the momentum is with her.\par \par \uc2 And so you stop and think about it, and you say well, especially I was struck this week to see that some of the top pundits now say that she has a slight lead in terms of Electoral College for the first time. I have never seen that before. It could shift backwards. But I think that what we may see with the interviews is, especially if there is not a second debate, that her strategy may shift and that she will want the kind of attention that comes from having interviews with big stars like Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, listen, we would love to do a debate, too. That door is also open.\par \par \uc2 Let me put this up, though, because of a lot of FOX polling numbers. This week we asked what is the single most important factor motivating you to vote this election? Remaining at the top of the list is high prices followed by immigration. And Mollie, those are also two topics, two issues that voters give the edge to President Trump. So, why is that not showing up more in the head-to-head polls?\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR IN CHIEF, "THE FEDERALIST": I actually think it is showing up in the head-to-head polls. So if you think back to what 2016 and 2020 polls showed us, they said those are races where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had no shot. He was so far out, there was no way he could win. Well, he won in 2016. And 2020 was not the 10-point race that pollsters told us it was. It turned out to be down to 43,000 votes across three states.\par \par \uc2 And so now you have these tied polls for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , or maybe she is up a point or two. And that is not good news for her because she needs to be up at least as much as -- she needs to be five, seven, eight points ahead of where she is right now. And so, yes, the strategy has been let's keep her out of interviews because people might see that she is in completely over her head. She cannot articulate a policy thought to save her life. But, as she keeps struggling in the polls, I think you might see her speaking more.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, you mentioned that it came down to three states last time around. "Politico" agrees. They say this, "The electoral map is three states. There are really only three states that will decide -- Pennsylvania, North Carolina, and Georgia. If Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  can't carry Pennsylvania, her only hope is on a southern strategy. She must win either Georgia or North Carolina. She has no other path to the White House."\par \par \uc2 Byron, there are a lot of nervous Republicans because of some of the situation in North Carolina and Georgia. They are both a lot closer than the GOP would like them to be.\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, "THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER": Yes. That article is because North Carolina is now in play. If North Carolina were not in play, then Trump would have a lot more options. The whole path for Trump is to win everything he won in 2020, which he can do, and then he needs to win Georgia, and he needs to win Pennsylvania. That alone would get him to 270 electoral votes. Or he could win Georgia and Arizona and Nevada, maybe.\par \par \uc2 But, if he loses North Carolina, then, wow, things open up and he has got win something pretty big somewhere else. And I think a lot of Republicans, Trump supporters, thought that Georgia and North Carolina were kind of in the bag when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was the candidate. And now they are actually in play. And so, yes, they are worried.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let's talk about Georgia, because today there was a decision by the election board, the state election board there, that they are going to hand count ballots. There has been a lot of pushback to that, saying it's going to create errors and it's going to waste time. But just so folks know, they're also going to run the electronic ballots. They're not counting the ballots for names of candidates. They're just counting the numbers of ballots because they want to try to get them matched as closely to the electronic ballots. But Mollie, there a lot of people who say Republicans these are shenanigans. This is Republicans upsetting the apple cart and it's going to make a lot of confusion roll out in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: It's actually just one of the basic things can you do to restore election integrity is to make sure that the number of voters matches, or the number of ballots matches the number of votes tallied for different people. And we haven't had that in many states. And so a lot of states say, well, maybe we can't do a full audit of the entire election process, but at the very least we can make sure that the number of voters match the number of ballots that we receive.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, I want to make sure we hit on the other big story we heard today from the Secret Service. They've issued a report that -- the summary of it is just a few pages long. But we heard from the acting director who said this today in part.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RONALD ROWE, ACTING SECRET SERVICE DIRECTOR: The mission assurance review has identified deficiencies in the advanced planning and its implementation by Secret Service personnel. These employees will be held accountable.\par \par \uc2 I have directed that the Secret Service embark on a significant paradigm shift that will redefine how we conduct protective operations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Byron, what did you take from the presser today?\par \par \uc2 YORK: This is a really staggering set of errors by the Secret Service. And we have seen that -- once you got the video of one of the men who was actually shot, who survived, and you could see the shooter just not too far away on the roof, you thought, how in the world could this have happened? And the failures that were outlined today are still almost unbelievable in a so-called zero fail environment at the Secret Service. So, I think they need do a lot more than just fire a few people or discipline a few people.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Juan, I want to start with you for Winners and Losers. Do you want to say anything on the Secret Service report and then get us into that.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Can I speak about my paradigm shift?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Sure.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You have one? Make it quick.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I think, look, Congress, I will say on a positive note, because everybody thinks this was a failure, that there was a bipartisan vote, unanimous, to say we've got to do a better job. And we've got to now allow for a presidential level of protection for people who are running for president.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, can you quickly give us winners and losers?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: My winner, the U.S. economy. The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's decision this week to cut interest rates by a half point, a winner for consumers who have credit card debt and for people trying to get a home mortgage.\par \par \uc2 The loser, I would think GOP North Carolina candidate Mark Robinson. His tawdry history on pornographic websites and claiming to be a black nazi, not good.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: He denies it. But we will see as that plays out. Mollie, winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: My winner for the week \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who survived his second assassination attempt in two months. My loser is Dr. Jill Biden who took over a cabinet meeting that her husband was running, a rare cabinet meeting today, and has just done poorly with managing her husband.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Oh, OK. Byron?\par \par \uc2 YORK: All-sports edition. The winner is Shohei Ohtani. Dodgers star went six for six, three homeruns, two doubles, to steals, four runs scored, and 10 RBIs, one game.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Crazy, crazy.\par \par \uc2 YORK: My loser is professional golf. Both the PGA and LIV had tournaments this weekend and nobody watched. Nobody. They need to get their act together.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, it is football season. It's hard to pull ourselves away from that. OK, panel, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 OK, it's Friday. Time for -- you guessed it -- "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. P., how is your golf game?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I always said golf is a very dangerous game.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Keep walking.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The former president is far too predictable.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He is not the sitting president.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Success, we have to have it every day.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has had as many assassination attempts as \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has had interviews.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: If somebody breaks into my house, they're getting shot.\par \par \uc2 I probably should not have said that. But my staff will deal with that later.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : They looked at her. They said, we're not going there. I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This damn TV show of his has been on too long.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON, (R-LA) HOUSE SPEAKER: Here's the voice in my head. We don't want to talk about energy independence. Energy dominance. Dominance.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How do you think \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  operatives feel right now about using their cell phone or even using the toaster?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A year ago, Sean Combs was handed a key to New York City. Today he has been indicted.\par \par \uc2 BILL NELSON\uc2 , NASA ADMINISTRATOR: The women we honor today made it possible for earthlings to lift beyond the bounds of earth.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: My family, we have been pulled apart by political contempt. I never thought I would be able to say that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Read that First Amendment. You will then look up and see the Capitol signifying the relationship between journalism and our important democratic institutions.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They join together in driving the first nail for the 60th presidential inauguration platform.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): It will be here before you know it.\par \par \uc2 OK, Monday on SPECIAL REPORT, President Biden makes one of his final appearances on the world stage as he heads to New York for the United Nations general assembly.\par \par \uc2 Please join me for FOX NEWS SUNDAY. My guests include GOP congressman Mike Waltz and Democratic Congresswoman Madeleine Dean, members of the joint task force investigating the two assassination attempts on President Trump.\par \par \uc2 And remember, if you can't catch SPECIAL REPORT live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. eastern, 3:00 p.m. over on the west coast.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for watching SPECIAL REPORT. I'm Shannon Bream in for Bret Baier. I will see you Sunday for FOX News Sunday. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is up next.\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240921ek9k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Acting \uc2 US Secret Service\uc2  Director Says, People Will be Held Accountable; Oprah Hosts Star-Studded Sit-Down With V.P. Harris; Now, V.P. Harris Campaigns In Battleground Georgia; Harris Puts Focus On Abortion Rights In Georgia; New Strikes, Rocket Fire Raise Mideast Tensions; Trump Gives Eight-Year-Old Supporter Birthday Gift At Rally\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, David Spunt, Nicole Parker, Nate Foy \par \uc2 8,295 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 September 2024\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Okay. Thank you very much, you guys. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha McCallum. And this is THE STORY. How is it possible that two shooters came within yards of President Trump in just the past couple of months?\par \par \uc2 Well, moments ago, the acting Secret Service director, Ronald Rowe, tried to explain that. And he also tried to explain what he's doing to make sure that it cannot happen again. He did reveal some new details about what went wrong on July 13th, including how the gunman managed to get up on that roof and then people in the crowd spotted him and yet he was still able to fire shots. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He's on the roof, right here, right on the roof.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's still so chilling to listen to. Here's the acting Secret Service director just moments ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RONALD ROWE, ACTING SECRET SERVICE DIRECTOR: There should have been clear direction about what we needed done at the AGR building, That was discussed during the advance, but I think there was a lack of follow through based on the information I have now and what I'm seeing. There should have been better follow through on, in some aspects of that, about access control to that property, about access control to the collateral property of the AGR building. And so it was about not giving the state and locals clear direction on what we needed done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Okay. Former \uc2 FBI\uc2  Special Agent Nicole Parker and former Secret Service Special Agent Bill Gage are standing by. But first to Justice Correspondent David Spunt reporting on this live from the DOJ. Hi, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. We heard a lot of acronyms and words, but there's one theme that rings true. We got a summary of an internal Secret Service report that's due out in the next few days, maybe a week or two at most. And the theme is the lack of communication, full stop.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROWE: The Secret Service did not give clear guidance or direction to our local law enforcement partners. There were communication deficiencies between law enforcement personnel at the site.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Acting Director \uc2 Ron Rowe\uc2  took questions from the press this afternoon after he presented that summary of that internal report. It's kind of a look in the mirror report, problems leading up to July 13th when former President Trump was shot and almost killed. Rowe says the investigation is now in the penalty phase, the punishment of employees.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROWE: He has among the most robust table of penalties in the entirety of the federal government. And these penalties will be administered according to our disciplinary process.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: And he wouldn't say anything about names or how many people may potentially lose their jobs. We know that a summary was released today, and here are the highlights. Back to that communication where people were screaming, this report says the different radio frequencies used in the Butler Farm Show venue were not conducive for quickly sharing real time information. Rowe also said, Martha, that people were using cell phone text chains to communicate information instead of talking on the radio in real time. He says that's a problem.\par \par \uc2 And I'll end on this, the House of Representatives passed something unanimously today to give the same protection to candidates, presidential candidates, as actual sitting presidents. Rowe said that's been in place since July 13th. But members of Congress want this to be something permanent as law. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I heard that exchange. Those were your questions in there. David, thank you very much, David Spunt at DOJ.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Nicole Parker, former \uc2 FBI\uc2  special agent and Fox News contributor, and Bill Gage, former Secret Service special agent.\par \par \uc2 Bill, let me go to you first on this, you know, and I think about what happened on that rooftop. And, basically, what my takeaway from what Rowe had to say, Director Rowe had to say, was that they basically were told by the local folks, oh, we've got the AGR building, but there wasn't specific understanding of what that meant. Would that have ever happened when you were in the Secret Service?\par \par \uc2 BILL GAGE, FORMER SECRET SERVICE SPECIAL AGENT: Yes, and it could happen at any site. I was in vantage at many, many times. And, Martha, just being frank here, I've said this on this outlet and others, you know, within the first 24 hours of the Butler event happening, I said that there was some clear miscommunication between what the local police responsibilities were and the Secret Service's responsibilities, and I think acting Director Rowe said that today.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, it's very possible to happen.\par \par \uc2 And that's why the acting director said, you know, from here on out, we're going to make sure that there are clear lines of communication and clear areas of responsibility, essentially, I hate to dumb it down, but who is responsible for what and being very specific about that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But doesn't it strike you as strange that that wasn't already happening in a Secret Service operation?\par \par \uc2 GAGE: It does, in some respects. And I think Acting Director Rowe was very forthcoming today and say, listen, this was the direct result of some miscommunication between the local police and the Secret Service, specifically the site agent. And there also was a human error here, that there were some human errors involving members of the Secret Service and they're going to be held accountable.\par \par \uc2 Listen, at the end of the day, the Secret Service is staffed by humans and we know that humans make errors and, you know, the Secret Service tries to have redundant layers of security and redundant layers of supervision to sort of correct any deficiencies that might have happened and it didn't happen in Butler.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, Corey Comparatore's family, I'm sure that's cold comfort to them. You go to an event like this and your understanding is that Secret Service is everywhere and there's no reason to not feel safe in this environment.\par \par \uc2 Nicole, Director Rowe said that the line of sight area in the AGR building was acknowledged, but not mitigated and that the concern about somebody being up on the roof was not escalated to the level of supervisors. How does all this add up in your head as a former \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE PARKER, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: You know, again, it comes back to what we've been discussing over and over. And I think in these types of operations, when you're in law enforcement, whether it's \uc2 FBI\uc2 , Secret Service, whatever it might be, but particularly Secret Service who are sworn to protect, you know, the current president in this instance, potentially the future president and former president, but complacency and sloppiness.\par \par \uc2 If you are, you know, not attending to every single detail in your job, people potentially get killed, and that is what we saw on July 13th. And, sadly, you know, Corey is no longer with us, but President Trump could have also been killed. And I think it's so important for people in law enforcement to understand every single operation has to be treated like this is the one, this is the one, an adversary is present, and there could be another assassination attempt.\par \par \uc2 I think when things just go well for so long, and you know, there's no real issues, you know, we tend to get complacent. That's human nature. But you have to treat every single operation like this is the one.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, absolutely. And I hope that they're saying that to these agents and the people on the scene at every one of these events now. I mean, you may be the person who is on the scene when something happens. So, make sure that you're dotting your I's eyes and crossing your T's, and you know where all these lines of sight are.\par \par \uc2 Let's play this sound bite that has to do with the second attempt in West Palm Beach just this past Sunday. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROWE: Based on the information I have right now, I say that that situation was managed according to our training. You know, those -- as soon as it was -- as soon as our agent discovered the individual, took swift action, and, took steps to neutralize that threat.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Bill Gage, let me ask you this. In terms of the, the perimeter of the golf course, so what we understand is, well, they didn't know that he was going there, so they didn't, I guess, get there in time to sweep the perimeter area and to figure out if there was anybody along that fence line. Does that make sense to you?\par \par \uc2 GAGE: It absolutely does. I've seen those reports too, Martha. And, you know, one of the things I just want to caution people is, you know, in some respects it, listen, this is -- we're not a surveillance state like China or Russia. you know, the Secret Service has to operate within the confines of the Constitution and the Bill of Rights, and it's very difficult for the Secret Service. They just cannot come in and say, okay, we need a mile here perimeter, and this is a no go zone and shut businesses down and shut roadways down. So --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But they could walk along the fence. That is the only area that is open to the public. They could take a walk along there on the outside.\par \par \uc2 GAGE: They could. And, you know, it sounds like the protective model worked in this case. And, again, like I said, and during the first question, you know, there's redundancies built into these protective models. And yes, hey, they dropped the ball there. The wood line should have been swept, or at least, with a drone at a minimum. It would have picked somebody up.\par \par \uc2 And so I think that definitely is a gap and golf courses are very, very difficult to secure, Martha. And that's something the Secret Service is going to look at and do a better job next time.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Okay. Thank you. Bill Gage, Nicole Parker, good to have both of you with us today.\par \par \uc2 So, Vice President Harris gets some celebrity love from Oprah, but struggles with substantive answers to the questions that she was asked at times.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We really would love to know what your plan is to help lower the cost of living.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , U.S. VICE PRESIDENT, DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: In terms of both rightly having the right to have aspirations and dreams and ambitions for your family and working hard and finding that the American dream is, for this generation and so many recently far more elusive than it's been, and we need to deal with that. One is bringing down the cost of everyday necessities, including groceries.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2  and Clay Travis, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, President Biden today, mid-morning, held his first cabinet meeting in 11 months. You would imagine that a cabinet would have to meet fairly frequently, but this is the first time we've seen this in 11 months. First Lady Jill Biden was also there to speak about a project that she's working on. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , U.S. PRESIDENT: Today, at the top of our meeting, Jill's going to, give an update on the House initiative -- White House initiative to fundamentally change the approach and fund how we approach and fund women's health services. So, I'd like to turn it over to Jill and, for any comments she has. It's all yours, kid.\par \par \uc2 JILL BIDEN, U.S. FIRST LADY: You know, sometimes the White House surprises. When Joe became president, I knew I wanted to keep shining a light on the issues that I'd worked on for so many years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Okay. So, that's a bit of that from earlier today. The president says that this is the first time that his wife has joined for a cabinet meeting.\par \par \uc2 And voters hungry for specifics from Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . We know this from polling because 30-plus percent say they want to understand more about her policies. So, the vice president may need to look elsewhere, perhaps after last night's star studded campaign event to fulfill that promise. Last night, it was with \uc2 Oprah Winfrey\uc2  and here's just a sampling of the Democratic nominee's closing pitch last night. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I love our country. I know we all do. That's why everybody's here right now. We love our country.\par \par \uc2 This is a moment where we can and must come together as Americans, understanding we have so much more in common than what separates us.\par \par \uc2 We are an optimistic people. Americans, by character, are people who have dreams and ambitions and aspirations. We believe in what is possible. We believe in what can be.\par \par \uc2 This is a moment where we stand knowing what we are fighting for, we're not fighting against. It's what we're fighting for.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So that, answer was actually about three minutes long, and it was an answer to how would you -- what would you say, what's on your heart to reach out to independent voters, or undecided voters, I should say, was the lead into that.\par \par \uc2 So, with that, we bring in nationally syndicated radio show host \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2 , OutKick Founder Clay Travis and former Special Assistant to President Biden Meghan Hays. Welcome to all of you, Meghan, I think it's our first time having you on this particular show, so thank you for being here today.\par \par \uc2 Let's go down the line, Hugh, your reaction to this Oprah event.\par \par \uc2 HUGH HEWITT, NATIONALLY SYNDICATED RADIO HOST: I played a lot of it on my program this morning, Martha, because it's so bad, it had to be heard to be believed. The vice president's like an old jukebox with only six bad songs in it. And whether you hit MacArthur's Park or Imagine, it's always bad, it gets worse with repetition. Even when she has, as her wingman, the country's greatest communicator, Oprah, along with \uc2 Rush Limbaugh\uc2 , the only person to build and maintain an audience of 20 million people daily for years. I can't believe how bad the vice president was last night, and I hope a lot of people saw it, because I want \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to win.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Megan, what do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 MEGHAN HAYS, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: I mean, look, the vice president is meeting people where they are. She is out there talking to people. I think the movement in the polls is showing that, so I understand where people have different policy ideas or may not. Like her performance from last night, but I do think that she is meeting people where they are and people are hearing from her and are hearing what they want to hear because she's actually closing the gap in a lot of the polls with issues and different things of dealing with immigration and dealing with the economy and dealing with abortion. She's closing that gap on President Trump or former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 So, I mean, people might not like that she did something with Oprah or may not think that it was great, but, clearly, the voters in these battleground states disagree.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, she's about to do an event in Atlanta, I should mention, which we're going to take some of live. And she's going to be talking about reproductive freedom, which is the way that she addresses the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 But I want to play this on the gun issue from last night and then we'll go to you, Clay. Let's watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY, T.V. HOST: When you said you have guns at the debate, at the debate --\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I'm a gun owner too.\par \par \uc2 WINFREY: I did not know that.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: If somebody breaks in my house, they're getting shot.\par \par \uc2 WINFREY: Yes, yes, I hear that. I hear that.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I probably should not have said that.\par \par \uc2 My staff will deal with that later.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Clay, she said her staff will deal with that one later. Your thoughts on that exchange there. It got a lot of attention.\par \par \uc2 CLAY TRAVIS, FOUNDER, OUTKICK: Yes. So a couple of things stand out. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is on record saying she wants to have mandatory gun buyback programs. She's bragged about how she aspires to have the same success that Australia did in taking people's guns. And I think this is just part and parcel of what is the most profoundly inauthentic presidential campaign in history.\par \par \uc2 And what they're trying to do here with the bait and switch. Remember, Vice President Harris was the least popular vice president in the history of polling.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Not anymore, Clay. Her numbers have gone up dramatically since then.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS: That's right. And she hasn't done anything. She's barely talked to the media. She hasn't done a single open press conference. She hasn't explained, for instance, how she went from saying we need reparations to not even answering the question to whether she wants to ban straws, to banning fracking in Pennsylvania, to now wanting to build a border wall after saying that it was racist to build a border wall. She hasn't just changed her opinion, Martha. She's completely flipped them, and there's almost no criticism or analysis or requirement for explanation.\par \par \uc2 And I thought Hugh hit it well. One bit of this, and I watched it with Oprah last night, she got asked what she was going to do at the border. She gave a 2.5 minute word salad. And then Oprah, who was one of the great communicators of our era had to come back to her and say, so you would just pass a new bill, because she's unable to explain what she would do because she doesn't even know herself because she's taken diametrically opposed positions on virtually every issue that Americans care about.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Hugh, Brian Fallon, who's obviously very senior with the campaign, thinks that they may get another debate. And there's a, you know, suggestion that there should be one on Fox because, obviously, she would reach a lot of independent voters. What are your thoughts on that? Do you see that happening now? And do you think that President Trump would do that at this point?\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: The only debate I think can happen would be one that you and Bret moderated. I believe you would both be fair and both do follow-ups, and I have no idea what your politics are. I have no idea what Bret Baier's politics are. I would not agree to any of the other networks, and I've worked with them all. I've done moderated debates with Dana Bash. I've moderated debates with Lester and Kristen this past November. It requires that you be very even handed and they are very scripted. As you know, Martha, it's worked out beforehand. The ABC debate was the worst debate in history and we can't have that happen again. It was a disservice to both of the candidates, especially to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and to the American people.\par \par \uc2 So, if it happens again, I think that Brian Fallon ought to come hat in hand to you and Bret and beg you to beg \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to do another debate.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, we'll see how that plays out. We certainly would like to have that opportunity. Meghan, is that something you would advise to her at this point, because she is rising in the polls and she's going to need those people in the middle, many of whom watch this channel?\par \par \uc2 HAYS: Yes, I mean, I do think so. I think it's good for the American people. I think it's good for the American people to see them on stage together and have a conversation back and forth and see the contrast in real time. I think we saw an extremely amplified the debate in Philadelphia a couple of weeks ago. And I think that when we're talking about not having an analysis or not questioning her, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had the opportunity to do that at that debate and he didn't do that. He, again, decided to go into his crowd sizes and just a personal attacks on her.\par \par \uc2 So, some of this is the way they interact and leadership skills. But I do think the American people do really benefit from seeing the candidates debate on stage.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. We are watching this live event right now, and we're going to go there. So, Hugh, thank you very much, Clay Travis, Meghan Hays, thank you very much to all of you for being here today.\par \par \uc2 The vice president is speaking right now in Atlanta, as I mentioned. We understand that the focus here is going to be the issue of abortion. Let's watch a little bit of this here.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Abortion bans, extremists that have passed laws that criminalize health care providers, doctors and nurses, and punish women. In two states of those states, they provide for prison for life, prison for life for healthcare providers, for simply providing reproductive care, the care they so earnestly and rightly believe must be delivered. All Trump abortion bans, and think about this, many of these bans make no exception even for rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 Now, many of you know I started my career as a prosecutor specializing in crimes of violence against women and children. What many of you may not know is why. So, when I was in high school, my best friend, I learned, was being molested by her stepfather.\par \par \uc2 And I said to her, look, you got to come and stay with us. I called my mother. She said, of course, she does. And she came and she stayed with us.\par \par \uc2 And so I made the decision early in my life that I wanted to do the work that was about protecting the most vulnerable among us and doing the work that was about giving them dignity in the process.\par \par \uc2 And so -- well, thank you everybody here for being here and standing in solidarity around the importance of that.\par \par \uc2 And so I say to you then from that experience and from the work that I've done, the idea that someone who survives a crime of violence to their body, a violation of their body, would not have the right to make a decision about what happens to their body next? That's immoral. That's immoral.\par \par \uc2 And let us agree, and I know we do, one does not have to abandon their faith or deeply held beliefs to agree the government should not be telling her what to do. If she chooses she will talk with her pastor, her priest, her rabbi, her imam, but it should not be the government or \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  telling her what to do with her body.\par \par \uc2 And think about it, the stories that Dr. Reddick (ph) shares with us, the stories we heard last night, the stories we've been hearing for two years, one in three women in America lives in a state with a Trump abortion ban. This includes Georgia and every state in the south except Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Think about that when you also combine that with what we know has been longstanding neglect around an issue like maternal mortality. Think about that when you compound that with what has been longstanding neglect of women in communities with a lack of the adequate resources they need for health care, prenatal, during their pregnancy, postpartum. Think about that. And these hypocrites want to start talking about this is in the best interest of women and children. Well, where have you been? Where have you been when it comes to taking care of the women and children of America? Where have you been? How dare they? How dare they? Come on.\par \par \uc2 And we understand the impact of these bans and the horrific reality that women and families, their husbands, their partners, their parents, their children are facing as a consequence every single day. Since Roe was overturned, I have met women who were refused care during a miscarriage, wanted to have a child suffering a miscarriage. I met a woman, I've actually met several of them, who were turned away from the emergency room. One of early stages after the Dobbs decision came down, told me with tears, she was with her husband about how only when she developed sepsis did she receive emergency care. Only when she developed sepsis, did she receive emergency care.\par \par \uc2 And now we know that at least two women, and those are only the stories we know here in the state of Georgia, died because of a Trump abortion ban. One, and we heard about her story last night, a vibrant 28-year-old young woman. She was ambitious. You know, I talked with her mother and her sisters about her. And they described such an extraordinary life of a person.\par \par \uc2 She was excited. She was working hard. She was a medical assistant. She was going to nursing school, raising her six-year-old son. She was really proud that she had finally worked so hard that she gained the independence. Her family was telling me that she was able to get an apartment in a gated community with a pool for her son to play in. She was so proud and she was headed to nursing school.\par \par \uc2 And her name and we will speak her name.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED SPEAKERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Amber Nicole Thurman. Amber Nicole Thurman.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED SPEAKERS: Amber Nicole Thurman.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: That's right. And she had her future all planned out, and it was her plan. You know, let's understand just take pause on that for a moment. She had her plan, what she wanted to do for her son, for herself, for their future.\par \par \uc2 And so when she discovered that she was pregnant, she decided she wanted to have an abortion. But because of the Trump abortion ban here in Georgia, she was forced to travel out of state to receive the health care that she needed. But when she returned to Georgia, she needed additional care, so she went to a hospital.\par \par \uc2 But you see under the Trump Abortion Ban, her doctors could have faced up to a decade in prison for providing Amber the care she needed. Understand what a law like this means. Doctors have to wait until the patient is at death's door before they take action.\par \par \uc2 You know, on the other side of my, you know, the other folks that Trump and his running mate, and they'll talk about, oh, yes, but I, you know, I do believe in the exception to save mother's life. OK, all right. Let's break that down, shall we?\par \par \uc2 (END LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: OK, yes. So you hear Vice President Harris speaking there, and I actually want to play what former President Trump said at the debate with regard to that. There's no Trump abortion ban. It was an overturn of Roe v. Wade at the Supreme Court. And then each state decides what they want the laws to be in their state.\par \par \uc2 So here's a clip from Philadelphia on September 10. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), US FORMER PRESIDENT AND PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I'm not signing a ban, and there's no reason to sign a ban, because we've gotten what everybody wanted. Democrats, Republicans and everybody else, and every legal scholar wanted it to be brought back into the states. This issue has torn our country apart, and they've wanted it back in the States. And I did something that nobody thought was possible.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So now these decisions are made. Lots of them are on the ballot in November in terms of what each state and the people of that state want the laws to be in their own state. Let's bring in Jack Brewer, former \uc2 NFL\uc2  player and Chairman of the Jack Brewer Foundation. Mr. Brewer, always good to see you. Thank you very much for joining me today.\par \par \uc2 As you sit there and you listen to what the vice president is saying on this topic, what's your response?\par \par \uc2 JACK BREWER, CHAIRMAN, JACK BREWER FOUNDATION: It's just disappointing. It's sad to hear that she is manipulating so many people, saying that President Trump is representing something that he's clearly stated that he hasn't represented.\par \par \uc2 I'll tell you another thing that's really sad. It's sad that the majority of folks that she's talking to are black people, and you look at the abortion rates across America, over 40 percent of all abortions happen by from little black girls. Little black girls have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 But you don't hear \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who could actually speak into the issues that are causing these little girls to have abortion. Most of them aren't married. Most of them are having sex before marriage, and shouldn't be having sex. She'll never say that. She'll never talk about accountability and choice in being women with dignity. You'll never hear that come out of her mouth.\par \par \uc2 Another thing you won't hear come out of her mouth is talking about life. All \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  talks about his death, about killing babies, about abortion. She'll never talk about adoption. She'll never encourage people to go out and adopt these children, and talk about the -- really, the Christian way to speak and to react to these type of issues.\par \par \uc2 But she will tell you that you don't have to leave your faith to believe that you can abort babies. She says that. She tweets that. She puts that out to the American people, and so she's manipulating the minds of people.\par \par \uc2 And so, she's truly carrying the Jezebel spirit, I pray for her. I hope \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has an awakening, because it's just not right to have so much power and have so many African American women, minority women, underserved women looking up to you, and you go out there and you lie to the American people.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's interesting that you brought that up, because \uc2 Tony Dungy\uc2  tweeted today, I hear you make this statement all the time to Vice President Harris, exactly what faith are you talking about when you say that you don't have to abandon it to support abortion? Are you talking about the Christian faith that says all babies are made in the image of God?\par \par \uc2 And he goes on further from there. Just a quick thought on this before we leave.\par \par \uc2 BREWER: Yes. As he quotes there Jeremiah 1:5, he knew us when we were in the womb. We need voices like \uc2 Tony Dungy\uc2 . We are a fatherless nation right now. We have over a half a million foster care kids in America. We have 18.4 million fatherless kids in America. We need strong men like \uc2 Tony Dungy\uc2  who represent family in faith.\par \par \uc2 And I hope \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , the entire Democratic Party, wakes up and realize that this nation needs to move back to morality. We need to 10 Commandments back in our schools, and we need to talk about faith in our country again, not death.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, you walk the walk on this and you have a foundation that does extraordinary work with young people. And we thank you very much for that. And I know they do too, because they know that somebody is looking out for them, in Jack Brewer every day.\par \par \uc2 So thank you very much. It's good to have you here, sir.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: God bless you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Important points. So a cloud of smoke right now over Lebanon's capital as Israel targets a \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  terrorist linked to the 1983 bombings at the US embassy and the marine barracks in Beirut. Lot of American families connected to those lost that day are very glad to see that some justice has been done.\par \par \uc2 Former Prime Minister Naftali Bennett on the recent escalation of the war and where is this headed next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Israel says that it has taken out a \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  terrorist who was linked to the 1983 bombings at the United States Embassy and marine barracks in Beirut. That took the lives of more than 300 people, 241 of them were US servicemembers. Many of them were marines.\par \par \uc2 His name was Ibrahim Aqil, killed in this airstrike today in Beirut, according to the Israeli military. He had a $7 million price on his head from those attacks. Think about all the families that were impacted in 1983. American families whose young marines who were serving and were in those barracks, and more than 200 total of those servicemembers were killed. That's video from back then in 1983. But those memory is very fresh for those families here.\par \par \uc2 So former Israeli Prime Minister Naftali Bennett is here, we'll speak with him in just a moment. First, a live report from Nate Foy, our correspondent in Tel Aviv. Hi, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha. This is a massive blow to \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 's leadership structure. Not only Ibrahim Akil dying in this airstrike over Beirut, but by the \uc2 IDF\uc2  estimation, 10 other commanders who had leadership positions just across that border in Northern Israel.\par \par \uc2 So Aqil specifically, though, he's the top dog in this. He was one of the top two people in \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  under leader \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2 . And just earlier, \uc2 IDF\uc2  spokesman Daniel Hagari explained why they carried this strike out. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DANIEL HAGARI, IDF SPOKESMAN: These terrorists plan to do in Northern Israel what \uc2 Hamas\uc2  did in Southern Israel on October 7, invade Israeli homes and kill innocent people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: So \uc2 Hamas\uc2  put out a statement Martha saying, quote, "we consider this a new crime in the ongoing series of Zionist crimes, a violation of Lebanese sovereignty and an escalation of Zionist aggression."\par \par \uc2 Lebanon's health ministry\uc2  says, in total, 14 people were killed and 66 others injured in today's strike. Israel also hit several \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  terrorist sites closer to the border. They hit over 100 launching positions overnight. The two exchanged a lot of fire last night.\par \par \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  responded today with more than 200 rockets fired into Northern Israel. You see, the Iron Dome remains strong. There are no reports of injuries. Israel instructed its residents in the north to stay near shelters. Now tonight, Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  has called for an emergency security meeting as they await \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 's response.\par \par \uc2 Remember, Israel was very prepared for \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 's response after the killing of Fuad Shukr. That response happened in late August, after Israel carried out that strike in late July. So we'll see what \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  is going to do as a response to today's strike, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Nate, thank you very much, from Tel Aviv. Former Israeli Prime Minister Naftali Bennett joins me now. He was the 13th prime minister of Israel, unseating current Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , who then went back into power after the time that Bennett served from June of 2021 to June of 2022. He represented a party at that time, called the New Right Political Party.\par \par \uc2 At the end of his term, he served as the third alternate prime minister for five months before announcing that he would not, at that time, run again. Prime Minister Bennett, great to have you here today. Thank you --\par \par \uc2 NAFTALI BENNETT, FORMER ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- very much. So explain to Americans the meaning of these strikes against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , and how are they a signal that the war is entering a new phase and that we will see a lot more of this?\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Yes, they are. I want to explain to the American public, though, that \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  are two different organizations in two different geographies. \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , in an unprovoked fashion, began shooting rockets at Israel following \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ' assault on October 7th. And since then, they've been shooting 8,000 rockets in Israel. They killed dozens of Israelis, including 12 children in a soccer -- playing soccer. And the young children.\par \par \uc2 And Israel, generally, has been showing too much restraint, in my opinion, for the past 11 months, but enough is enough. And what we're seeing right now is the weeding out of these arc terrorists who should have died decades ago. Ibrahim Aqil should have not existed for the past 30 years. He killed over 200 Americans, by the way,\par \par \uc2 There was a $7 million bounty on his head from the United States. We're not going to ask -- we're not going to send a bill, because America is helping us greatly. But the point is, that these are big terrorists, global terrorists, and we're killing them, and that's just wonderful news for the world.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So with regard to the reaction to this extraordinary pager operation, it was carried out by Israel, can you confirm that?\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: I don't know.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: AOC, \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2 , spoke out about it. She said that it violated the tenants of international warfare. She was scolding Israel for carrying this out. I know you cannot confirm that.\par \par \uc2 What's your reaction when you hear what she says?\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: I would tell the terrorists to find a different job. I mean, if you're in the business of murdering citizens as your life career, you should expect lots of bad things will happen to you, and incidentally, to people around you. There is one way to safeguard your family, and it's to quit being a terrorist.\par \par \uc2 So I would recommend, highly recommend, that any \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  terrorist right now who seeks to live and to have a better future for his family, to call up Nasrallah and say, I quit, man. I don't want to die.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: There's a \uc2 UN\uc2  meeting this week in a rally today outside -- the brother of one of the hostages who was killed, of the six who were killed, along with Hersh Goldberg-Polin and four others. This is what he had to say. He's frustrated, of course, about what happened. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 OR GAT, SISTER WAS HOSTAGE KILLED BY \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 : Your life could have been saved so many times, you were meant to be returned to your life and to your loved ones. We can turn back the clock for you, Carmel, but we can control this moment. The time to act is now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: This piece in The Wall Street Journal today that indicated that the White House is really at the end of the road in terms of thinking that they will be able to help negotiate any kind of agreement before the end of Biden's term.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Anything to say to these families?\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: We need to bring the hostages home. They were kidnapped from their beds, from their homes early in the morning. They did nothing wrong to anyone. And it's our responsibility as the Jewish state to take care of our citizens.\par \par \uc2 I will say that, \uc2 Yahya Sinwar\uc2  is not under enough pressure, because if he were, he'd come to the negotiation table. He's not making -- he's not there, and I think we need to --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you believe he wants a deal?\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: I think not enough. And the only way to get him to want a deal is for him to see an imminent threat on his life, tanks rolling towards him. I think right now Israel is prosecuting this war way too slow, way too weak. We need to strengthen.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you think way when you hear American politicians say that they and the President, President Biden, basically saying you need to tone it down. This is too much what's happening in Gaza. What do you say to him right now?\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Well, if thousands of Americans were massacred from, let's say, some town in Mexico from al-Qaeda, hosted in Mexico, America would demolish that town. I mean, what would you do? What would you do if people invaded your homes here in New York or wherever, and butchered your families, and shot thousands and thousands of rockets for years. If anything, Israel's mistake is not doing the war, it's why we waited so long.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: This is former President Trump at the Israeli American Council last evening. And he's encouraging Jewish voters in America, saying that voting for him is a vote for Israel, for the future of Israel. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : If we continue down our current path with four more years of Kamala, Israel will be faced not just with an attack but with total annihilation. Anybody who is Jewish and loves being Jewish, and loves Israel is a fool if they vote for a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you agree with him?\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Well, obviously I never intervene in the domestic American politics. We've got enough of our mess in Israel. We don't need to push our noses into your mess. I will say this, that the Jews in America need Israel to be really strong, because ultimately, Israel is the only nation state of Jews in the world. It's a tiny little country, the size of New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 You know, people might think it's Texas or something, a tiny little tract of land surrounded by horrible people. And Jews need a strong Israel, and Israel needs a strong Jewish community across the world. And for all of that to happen, we just need to do one thing. We need to win.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You were quoted recently as saying that you would do whatever it takes if you were assigned, sweeping the floors to help your country. That's what you would do. Are you -- would you return to politics? Would you like to be prime minister again? And do you think you would do a better job than Netanyahu?\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: I'll do anything for my country. The only two things I care about in the world are my family and my nation, and I'll be at any place I need to be to help save and strengthen the State of Israel.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I hope you'll join us again. These are obviously very consequential times for Israel, for the Middle East. And next time, I hope we can dig into the issue of Iran, which obviously underlies all of this --\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- from \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . We may be headed into some very scary times, and I think it's important to keep this story in the forefront. So thank you, prime minister.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Very good to see you today. Thank you for being here. So coming up on a new episode of the Untold Story, author and senior writer at the National Review, Noah Rothman, and I sat down and we discussed how Israel infiltrated these pagers, according to Noah Rothman reporting on this, used by \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  operatives and where this conflict in the Middle East is going next. That's on Fox News podcast.com.\par \par \uc2 And next, the eight-year-old boy that is a very strong Trump supporter, and he received a special surprise of a lifetime earlier this week. Hi, there. We're going to talk to you right after the break. Stick around everybody.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I got you the best present you'll ever have. It's the best present you'll ever have.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So this little boy is such a big supporter of former President Trump. He even dresses up like him. Liam Licastro is bravely battling a rare brain disorder, and he just turned eight years old. And he got a surprise birthday gift from the former president backstage at the rally on Long Island outside of New York City.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I'm supposed to be in that stage but that doesn't matter, because I got you the best present you'll ever have. It's the best present you'll ever have. (Inaudible), OK?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Liam Licastro and his mom, Siobhan, join me now. Liam, what was that moment like? Were you surprised?\par \par \uc2 LIAM LICASTRO, 8-YEAR-OLD TRUMP SUPPORTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And what did you think of it all when he walked in with your present?\par \par \uc2 LICASTRO: I felt so happy.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So tell me, Liam, why you're such a big supporter of President Trump.\par \par \uc2 LICASTRO: Because he's better than \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And why have you -- ever since you were little, you always liked him. What was it that you first liked about him so much?\par \par \uc2 LICASTRO: That when I was -- saw him on TV, I would jump out off of the couch and start acting crazy.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, I saw how happy you were when you got a letter from him on your birthday, and you started to cry. And it was -- it made -- it such a happy moment. We can see it on the screen.\par \par \uc2 Now, Siobhan, tell me a little bit about how all of this came to be. And what was in the box? Lots of Trump stuff, I bet.\par \par \uc2 SIOBHAN LICASTRO, LIAM LICASTRO'S MOTHER: everybody was -- that's the famous question. Everyone wants to know what was in the box. So it was some Trump gear for Liam. It was some hats, some shirts, pens, straws, pins and some stickers for Trump-Vance.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And what was that moment with your son like? And did you ever think that your original request for a birthday card or birthday note would turn into this?\par \par \uc2 S. LICASTRO: Never in a million years. We received that letter on August 12, and it took a long time for me to post it. And it did. I was I wanted Liam's permission first to post it, because it's emotional.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It is. And was it emotional for you that day, Siobhan?\par \par \uc2 S. LICASTRO: Yes, very much.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Tell me what that felt like.\par \par \uc2 S. LICASTRO: So getting the letter or doing you --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: When you met him in that room, when you met him and you went to the rally.\par \par \uc2 S. LICASTRO: So the rally was just unbelievable. I've never seen anything like that before. And meeting him, I started to cry, because it's my son -- he loves him. It's one of his big heroes and role models. And just seeing the expression on my son's face was priceless.\par \par \uc2 This is a kid that they said may never walk, may never talk, may never eat, and here he is meeting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Just amazing.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, Liam, you are a special young man and little boy, I should say, at eight years old, and I'm so happy you got this dream come true, and it's so good to meet both of you. Thank you so much. I wish you lots of health and happiness in your future, Liam and your whole family, Siobhan. Thanks for being with us today.\par \par \uc2 S. LICASTRO: Thank you so much. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What a nice family. Thank you so much for coming on to THE STORY today.\par \par \uc2 That is THE STORY for today. Have a great weekend, everybody. THE STORY goes on on Monday. We'll see what happens over the weekend, and we will see you here Monday, for sure. Have a great evening.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020240921ek9k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview With John Driskell Hopkins; \uc2 Apple\uc2  iPhone 16 Hits Shelves; Secret Service Updates Trump Assassination Attempt Investigation; Looming Port Strike?; Three Mile Island Nuclear Plant to Restart; Harris Campaign Focuses on Abortion Rights; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Targets Jewish Vote\b0\par\par\uc2 David Spunt, Jeff Flock, Susan Li, Lydia Hu, Jonathan Serrie,  Aishah Hasnie, Edward Lawrence \par \uc2 7,456 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 September 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 EDWARD LAWRENCE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Forty-six days to go. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  making abortion the issue in Atlanta today before heading to Wisconsin. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , meanwhile, makes a play for the Jewish vote, but the election could hinge on an entirely different issue. More on that in a moment.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Edward Lawrence, in for Neil Cavuto. This is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 We have got FOX News team coverage with Jonathan Serrie in Atlanta with the Harris campaign and Aishah Hasnie in Washington the latest with the Trump campaign.\par \par \uc2 We begin with you, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Vice President Harris wrapped up her remarks just moments ago here in the Atlanta suburb of Cobb County.\par \par \uc2 It's a once-conservative stronghold that voted blue in the last two presidential elections,obviously, the vice president hoping to repeat that at an event focused on abortion rights, highlighting the deaths of two Georgia women who, according to a \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  investigation, were denied or delayed access to potentially lifesaving medical procedures as a result of state abortion restrictions that Harris blames on her opponent.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (D) AND U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: When \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was president, he hand-selected three members of the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 , the court of Thurgood and RBG, with the intention that they would overturn the protections of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 And, as he intended, they did.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: This event in Georgia follows a forum last night in Michigan hosted by \uc2 Oprah Winfrey\uc2 , in which the vice president heard directly from the family of Amber Nicole Thurman, a young Georgia woman who reportedly died after waiting 20 hours for doctors to perform a D&C to treat complications from abortion pills she had been prescribed out of state.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I want you all to know, Amber was not a statistic. She was loved by a family, a strong family.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: And abortion opponents, though, insists that you should not blame state laws, but the abortion procedures and medications themselves.\par \par \uc2 Harris after wrapping up this event has returned to her plane and she is heading to Wisconsin for an evening rally in Madison -- Edward.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Jonathan Serrie in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 So now to Aishah Hasnie in D.C., where the former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been courting the Jewish vote and taking aim at Iran -- Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: That's right, sort of going off the battleground path for a moment here to court the Jewish vote in America last night, and a lot of emotional moments in front of these voters, including this one right here where he vowed that he would bring the October 7 hostages home. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (R) AND CURRENT U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: That's a great group of people, and that's a lot of people too. We're going to get them out. We're going to get them out.\par \par \uc2 AUDIENCE: Bring them home! Bring them home!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: The former president also commenting for the first time about Iran stealing sensitive information from his campaign and offering it to the Biden campaign.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Iran has spied on my campaign -- think of this -- and given the information to Kamala. What's that all about? That alone should get me elected. If Kamala is reelected, Iran will quickly obtain nuclear weapons. They're already about ready to do it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Also, last night, Trump promised to ban refugee resettlements in the Gaza Strip and cut any federal funding to universities that allow antisemitism to reign on their campuses.\par \par \uc2 At the end of this, though, Edward, Trump complained about the lack of Jewish support he's been getting and told the crowd that if they're voting for Harris, they should have their heads examined, adding that they would be to blame if he lost.\par \par \uc2 And that got quite the reaction from second gentleman Douglas Emhoff, who wrote on X: "Last night, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  once again fanned the flames of antisemitism by trafficking in tropes, blaming and scapegoating Jews."\par \par \uc2 The former president doesn't have any public events on his schedule for today. He's expected to get back on the trail, though, in North Carolina, a critical state for him. It's a big Saturday rally, as his surrogates, though, fan the country tonight holding a lot of town halls -- Edward.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, those candidates all over the place.\par \par \uc2 Those are important issues, Aishah, but what if I told you these are the issues most voters are concerned with? Sixty-six percent say grocery prices are a major problem for their family in a new FOX News poll, up from 62 percent in May; 60 percent say housing costs are a major problem, up from 47 percent.\par \par \uc2 How this affects people currently in power, we need to help unraveling this.\par \par \uc2 So, with me now, GOP pollster \uc2 Lee Carter\uc2 , The Hill national reporter Julia Manchester, and Democratic strategist Doug Schoen.\par \par \uc2 So, Doug, I'm going to start with you on this, age before beauty, if you will.\par \par \uc2 What effect will this feeling reflected in the polls have on the election?\par \par \uc2 DOUG SCHOEN, FORMER BILL CLINTON POLLSTER: It works to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's advantage. The Biden/Harris team has a low rating on the economy. The poll numbers you cite, Edward, suggest that people are very upset with high grocery and housing, and I can add gasoline prices.\par \par \uc2 But Trump has to make the case that he has a better record from his presidency, but most importantly has an agenda of change to give people hope about the future. He's yet to do that, so for that to be fully realized as an electoral issue, the president, the former president, has to make the case that his economic agenda is superior to that of the vice president.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: That's an interesting point.\par \par \uc2 So, Lee, trying to break through on that, the two major issues favor former President Trump. I mean, 39 percent in the new polling we have from FOX News -- 39 percent of registered voters see higher prices as a motivating factor in voting; 32 percent say it's immigration, and the former president has a 10-point advantage in handling immigration and a five-point advantage in handling the economy.\par \par \uc2 So why is this race so closely, Lee?\par \par \uc2 LEE CARTER\uc2 , REPUBLICAN POLLSTER: Well, I think, frankly, it should be more than a five-point spread.\par \par \uc2 The interesting thing about what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is doing right now is he's running really hard on immigration and less so on the economy, especially since \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has taken over. And I think he needs to make that reverse, because he should be ahead by 10, 15, even 20 points on the economy. He used to be.\par \par \uc2 He's actually lost ground on this. And I think it's not because she's running such a smart economic plan. I think it's because he's not talking enough about it. When he was back at the \uc2 RNC\uc2 , he was talking about making America great again, making America wealthy again, making America safe again. And he was very, very focused on issues that matter most to the American people.\par \par \uc2 He's been a little bit on his heels. I think he needs to get more forward- looking again.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And it's interesting.\par \par \uc2 With the economy, with immigration, Julia, how does the Harris campaign then message all of this?\par \par \uc2 JULIA MANCHESTER, NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER, THE HILL: Well, look, the Harris campaign has been pointing on the economy. For example, they point back to the Trump's handling of the coronavirus pandemic and the economy that was taking place during that time.\par \par \uc2 Now, obviously, that was abnormal. There's pushback on that. But that's what the Harris campaign has been saying. And they have been talking about the Biden administration's recovery efforts coming out of COVID and talking about, for example, unemployment continuing to drop.\par \par \uc2 On immigration, though, I think that's a bit of an easier issue for them right now. And it's a bit ironic because we know that, a few weeks ago, the Harris campaign was more on the defensive about immigration. We know that, under the Biden administration, we have seen that has been a tough issue for Harris, the situation at the southern border.\par \par \uc2 But -- and I think this ties into what Lee was just saying earlier -- with former President Trump's comments at the debate talking about the unfounded claims about Haitian immigrants in Springfield, Ohio, a lot of Republicans have told me that served as a distraction.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: Instead of talking about those claims, why don't you talk about, for example, what would you do about the immigration system and how it's impacted communities like Springfield, Ohio?\par \par \uc2 So I think there's a frustration with the messaging there. And it's been easy for the Harris campaign to sort of go on the offensive on that issue.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, interesting.\par \par \uc2 So, overall prices -- bringing it back around to the economy, overall prices under Biden are up about 20 percent. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  asked about that at an event that was hosted by \uc2 Oprah Winfrey\uc2 . Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We really would love to know what your plan is to help lower the cost of living.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I am taking on, for example, price gouging, which is when most companies and corporations are good, but for those bad ones, they take advantage of people, especially during a pandemic or extreme weather, and they end up jacking up prices.\par \par \uc2 And there needs to be accountability and consequence for that. So I'm going to be taking on price gouging.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So none of the price gouging investigations, though, have amounted really to anything.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you look at the \uc2 FTC\uc2  were looking at oil companies about two years ago when gas prices were close to $5 a gallon and nothing came of that. So, Lee, what about help right now? What are you hearing about that?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Well, what's really interesting is when I have been talking to voters, they have not been satisfied with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' answers on the economy.\par \par \uc2 They say they do not understand what it is that she's really going to do that are going to bring the cost of living down. They understand a lot of her plans to spend a lot of money, but they're still saying they don't think that she's going to address the root causes or even gets it. And I think her first response here to go after price gouging is just a real miss.\par \par \uc2 I think a lot of people are still waiting to hear what it is, because the economy right now -- they will say the economy right now is \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' economy. They want to know what is she going to do different, and she has not outlined it. I don't think that this price gouging thing, as much as it sounds good, really addresses that root concern that people have.\par \par \uc2 And many people are really afraid of price gouging being really about price controls and other things as well.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Sure.\par \par \uc2 So the other big story this week, the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  and its lack of endorsement for either candidate, \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  president Sean O'Brien telling Neil that both parties should not take the union vote for granted. And yesterday, on the show, Sandra Smith spoke with union members, one backing Trump, the other backing Harris.\par \par \uc2 Here's what they both had to say. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: That poll making waves. Did it surprise you at all or is that what you hear as a member, overwhelming support for former President Trump?\par \par \uc2 J.W. PAINE, TEAMSTERS MEMBER: Actually, my -- I was surprised it was that low. I think it was really around 68 percent. But it really surprises me that we're not supporting the one that actually overwhelmingly won this race. And that's sad.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CURBEAM, CHAIR, TEAMSTERS NATIONAL BLACK CAUCUS: And we got to look at what the vice president has done for the labor movement. She has stood on picket lines with union members. She walked the picket line with union members. She has supported our pension plan, when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did not.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, Doug, how do you think this union vote is going to play out, especially in some of those key battleground states?\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: I think there's a real question, as Lee was suggesting, whether the populism of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will have any resonance. The polling with \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  certainly suggests that it isn't reaching a majority there.\par \par \uc2 And the question is whether Trump can take his advocacy of no sales -- no tax on tips and a limitation on interest on credit cards and turn it into an economic plan for working people. They have supported him in the past. The non-college-educated male vote has been the mainstay of his support.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: The real question is can he maximize that vote with an economic plan?\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And I wanted to get one quick one in here for Julia. In 20 seconds that we have left here, President Biden has changed it up to have his first Cabinet meeting in about 11 months today. And the vice president was not there in person. She was on the phone.\par \par \uc2 She's part of this administration, and the look in that Cabinet meeting was that maybe he wasn't quite in charge of the administration. Does that rub off on the vice president when you have a look like that, Julia?\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: You know, I think you're going to certainly see Republicans try to point that out, but Democrats and Harris' supporters would say, look, she is running for president. She is running to extend the Biden/Harris agenda into another administration.\par \par \uc2 So I think we might see those attacks, but, ultimately, I don't think it's anything new necessarily from what we have seen before in terms of those attacks on Biden and Harris and that dynamic.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Sure.\par \par \uc2 Julia, Lee, Doug, appreciate it. Thanks for the thoughtful insights.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, the Fed cuts and investors buy, the Dow closing the week at record highs.\par \par \uc2 We will go down to the floor of the \uc2 New York Stock Exchange\uc2  and see if this week's rally has some legs.\par \par \uc2 Plus, does this sound familiar?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There was an accident today at a nuclear power plant near Harrisburg, Pennsylvania. It's a bad one, one of the worst.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Forty-five years ago, a partial meltdown of the Three Mile Island nuclear plant gripping the nation. That was then, the plant reopening now, but not for the reasons you might think.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Record high for the Dow and a big week for your money, the boost sparked by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  cutting interest rates for the first time in four years.\par \par \uc2 Now, let's talk this out with New York Stock Exchange trader and Wall Street legend Peter Tuchman.\par \par \uc2 Peter, great to talk to you today.\par \par \uc2 So the markets wanted it and they got a half-a-percent cut. We saw a huge rally yesterday, mixed things today. Where does the market open on Monday? The sugar high still going?\par \par \uc2 PETER TUCHMAN, NYSE TRADER: Well, you know what, look, I mean, it's hard for me to really predict that.\par \par \uc2 I think everybody was caught by surprise by the 50-basis-point cut, but the market tells us what it thinks of something that's so radical and sort of off the beaten path. And I think the market is really thrilled with it.\par \par \uc2 As you know, everybody benefits from cutting this interest rate at this point. And I think possibly the 50-basis-point was part of the July story and part of the August story.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TUCHMAN: In my opinion, if you sort of overanalyze it a little bit, it really felt that maybe the Fed missed an opportunity in July. And that's why we had sort of those two weeks in the beginning of July.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 TUCHMAN: The August 5 massive sell-off may have been a missed opportunity on their part. This was well played.\par \par \uc2 I think the market responded really nice to it. You have to realize that today was actually a very constructive day. We had the S&P 500 rebalance. We had the largest option expiration date in history; $5.2 trillion went in and out of the market. For me, I trade the market on closing balances.\par \par \uc2 We had $8 billion for sale at one point and we flipped $8 billion to buy. So we had an absolute, an incredible amount of buy interest in this market.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 TUCHMAN: So I think that continues in a big way going forward surely into the next -- go ahead.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes. No, no. So I do want to get this in. I mean, so the Fed made the deeper cut.\par \par \uc2 Will the markets, do you think, try and push for another half-percent at the next meeting?\par \par \uc2 TUCHMAN: I think that we got the impression from \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2  when he spoke the other day on Wednesday that we're going to probably get two 25-basis- point cuts, one in November and maybe one in December, to close out the year with 1 total basis point move.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's what the market needs. We have been anticipating -- you have to realize the market, the market can handle virtually anything except expectations that are not fulfilled. They have been dangling this in front of us since January. January, we were looking at seven basis points -- seven cuts during the year and then maybe none.\par \par \uc2 And so this one was -- that's why the reception by the market on Thursday was so positive. And I think with two, maybe two more 25-basis-point cuts through the end of the year are going to be super positively received.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: You have so much institutional knowledge there, such a legend. Can you explain what the cheaper money does for the markets, for those who may not know?\par \par \uc2 TUCHMAN: You know what, I don't know if that's the answer to this question.\par \par \uc2 I think that there's so many new players in the market right now who are actually trading the market. You have got 50 million-plus new retail traders that have come in post-COVID, right? You have got huge institutional money, huge hedge fund money. A lot of people are playing ETFs and are playing in the option market.\par \par \uc2 So there's this enthusiasm around the market. There are still people sitting around with billions and billions of dollars in cash who have not been convinced that this market was not ready for a massive sell-off or a recession or a depression, all the things that the media has sort of been throwing at us over the last number of months.\par \par \uc2 So I think that finally when they get some clearance and the confidence is solid, I think it's going to unleash what we saw on way on Thursday, which was just a solid bull market. And I think there's a lot of money still to be spent in the marketplace.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: That's good, and good information. Thank you, Peter, right there in the trenches of all of it. Thank you for all of the insight on this. Just amazing. Great to talk to you again. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 TUCHMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So a nuclear -- yes.\par \par \uc2 A nuclear accident made Three Mile Island infamous. Now the tech industry might need the power plant more than ever. Find out why.\par \par \uc2 And could a possible port strike have holiday shoppers striking out?\par \par \uc2 Lydia Hu is in Bayonne, New Jersey with the bumpy details -- Lydia.\par \par \uc2 LYDIA HU, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Hey there, Edward.\par \par \uc2 Billions of dollars worth of goods are traded inside those containers on board those vessels every single day. What exactly is inside those containers? And what does a strike mean for those goods? We're going to get into all of that coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Secret Service facing continued scrutiny after those assassination attempts on former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , its acting director taking questions from the media today. Did we get any answers?\par \par \uc2 We're going to be back in about 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: A strike at major ports along the Gulf and East Coast could begin in less than two weeks, possibly costing the economy billions each day and putting a major freeze on the holiday shopping season.\par \par \uc2 To Lydia Hu live in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 So, Lydia, are we really concerned already about the holiday shopping season with this?\par \par \uc2 HU: Yes, we sure are.\par \par \uc2 Retailers are certainly raising concerns about the holidays shopping season, but we're also keeping an eye out for concerns about other types of products and industries. Experts say, if there were to be a strike, the public at large would first notice shortages and delays on things like autos and agricultural products like food.\par \par \uc2 And we can take a look at what imports are most brought into the ports that will be impacted by a strike if it happens on October 1 to see what other product categories could be impacted. In New York and New Jersey, we're seeing cars and semiconductors and furniture as top imports here.\par \par \uc2 Down in Savannah, the top imports include bulldozers, batteries and car parts. And, in Houston, TV equipment, air conditioners and commodities like iron and steel, all of these products could get snarled in a strike if that were to come on October 1.\par \par \uc2 Some industries, as I mentioned, are sounding the alarm. The Auto Car Association warns a strike will create a shortage of car parts. Think delays on your auto repairs. The \uc2 American Chemistry Council\uc2  concerned about chemicals needed for health care and household products and also semiconductor chips.\par \par \uc2 And the \uc2 National Restaurant Association\uc2  is warning that a strike will push prices up for restaurant operators on top of what we know are already high food prices and labor costs. And you asked about the holiday shopping season.\par \par \uc2 I had a chance to speak with the CEO of Balsam Hill. That's an artificial tree company. He says he's worried about having all the inventory he needs for this holiday season and he's redirecting some of his container ships and his orders from the East Coast over to the West Coast. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAC HARMAN, CEO, BALSAM HILL: It's something that keeps our supply chain team and me up at night. And I'm worried in particular that the government's not necessarily going to intervene because it's in the middle of an election year.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HU: As of right now, there are no further negotiations scheduled between the employers and the port workers. The deadline for this contract negotiation, it expires September 30. So we have got our eye on that October 1 for a possible strike -- Edward, back to you.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Hey, Lydia. Tick, tick, tick.\par \par \uc2 So joining me now is Steve Moore, economist.\par \par \uc2 Yes, Steve, so what effect, Steve, could the strike have on prices overall?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MOORE\uc2 , FORMER DONALD TRUMP CAMPAIGN ADVISER: Well, God forbid that we don't have Christmas trees for Christmas. I mean, that would be like the Grinch.\par \par \uc2 Look, this would be very negative for the economy, no question about it. So much of the merchandise that we buy goes through those ports, Ed, as you know. And so this could mean not just shortages, but also, I hate to say it, higher prices of these services and goods if they're not available.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So this is a tough spot for President Biden and Vice President Harris. They're the union administration, as they said.\par \par \uc2 So they have to be in support of the union and support of the strike should they go on strike. Could this be the October surprise because of the effect on the economy?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Well, look, I don't think the Biden administration wants a strike right now. You're quite right. It would just be a black eye for the administration.\par \par \uc2 But I also don't think it's appropriate, frankly, for an administration, whether it's Republican or a Democratic presidency, to weigh in and take a side one way or the other in these disputes. I mean, let them work it out, let the management and the labor unions work it out. And, usually, that's the best outcome.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, I want to shift on prices. We heard the Federal Reserve chairman come the closest I have ever heard him say mission accomplished when it comes to inflation.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: But one of the Federal Reserve members, Board of Governor \uc2 Michelle Bowman\uc2 ...\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: ... said that she preferred the quarter-of-a-percent cut to the half-percent cut, mainly because of core inflation, without food and energy prices, that sticky inflation. And she's worried about reigniting it.\par \par \uc2 Are we flirting with reigniting it?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Maybe, maybe. And I think that was a mistake. I think that Fed got some egg in their face yesterday, 45 or so days before the most important election in our lifetime to press its thumb on the scale.\par \par \uc2 I could have lived with a 25-basis-point, but as The Wall Street Journal called this, a jumbo rate cut, and it certainly was. I think it caught a lot of people by surprise. And I think the question a lot of Americans would ask is, look, it's an important election. You know, their next meeting, by the way, is the day after the election.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: So, some people are just saying, why couldn't they have waited?\par \par \uc2 And the Fed, as you know, is always saying, oh, we have to maintain our politically independent -- political independence. I don't think they look so politically independent here.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, the image issue.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: I want to switch gears on this. The \uc2 Tax Foundation\uc2  found that Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' plan could cost the economy 800,000 jobs, mainly because of the pullback that companies would have to pay the taxes, the increased taxes that she wants to give. What's your take on that?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes, it's a catastrophic tax increase.\par \par \uc2 And the Trump campaign -- and I work with the Trump people, just for full disclosure. We have got to really expose to the American people how dangerous this tax increase would be. I mean, you're talking about doubling the capital gains tax. You're talking about giving the United States the highest business tax rate in the world, taxing unrealized capital gains.\par \par \uc2 By the way, I think that estimate -- I know those folks at \uc2 Tax Foundation\uc2  - - I think it's a conservative estimate. You have -- some people on Wall Street are saying, if you put that tax increase in place, it could cause a 30 to 35 percent decline in the stock market.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So I would just quickly, in the last 20 seconds that we have getting into this...\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: I want to say, the former -- if former President Trump wins, and you see the 20 percent increase, we have seen 20 percent increases in prices under the Harris/Biden administration, can former President Trump actually lower prices?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Well, we had no inflation when Trump was president. His average inflation rate was 1.9 percent, 6.5 percent under Biden and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . So just look at their records, folks.\par \par \uc2 You have a guy who's been in the White House. We had a booming economy. Let's get that back, and, by the way, no inflation, 1.5 percent inflation when he left office. You remember that.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes. No, he's got a record that he's running on.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Steve Moore, economist. I appreciate it. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So now to Pennsylvania, and Three Mile Island, the site of the country's worst nuclear accident, set to reopen in a deal to power tech giant, Microsoft A.I. and their needs there.\par \par \uc2 Digging -- Jeff Flock digging deeper into this developing story -- Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF FLOCK, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Before there was Chernobyl, before there was Fukushima, there was this, Three Mile Island.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps on the left side of your screen, you see the cooling towers from reactor two. That's the one that melted down in March of 1979, spewing radioactivity into the environment around us, causing the evacuations of thousands of people.\par \par \uc2 On the other side of the screen would be reactor one. That wasn't affected, was restarted, but then shut down again in 2019 because it wasn't making any money. But now with money from \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , which has a voracious appetite for power because of artificial intelligence, they have agreed to buy the power from that reactor if it's restarted for 20 years.\par \par \uc2 And so it will be. By 2028, it should be up and running again,nuclear power again coming from Three Mile Island, nuclear power advocates saying nuclear is a lot safer now than it was in 1979.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JULIANN EDWARDS, THE NUCLEAR COMPANY: I mean, just like our phones, right, there's an evolution, a generational upgrade with every technology, and so the restart of Three Mile Island is a huge market signal, because we have reactors that are currently shut down that can be restarted.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: Among them the Palisades power plant in Covert, Michigan, which just got a $1.5 billion federal loan to restart, becoming the first U.S. nuclear power plant to be restarted after being shut down.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , they say they are seeking "to enable a decarbonized grid for our company, our customers and the world. This will bring a significant supply of net new reliable carbon free electricity to the power grid, recognizing the importance of nuclear energy."\par \par \uc2 It also answers the question from some critics about artificial intelligence and that voracious need for power, taking power away from the grid and perhaps driving demand up and costs for local consumers. This is a new addition to the grid. And \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and \uc2 Constellation Energy\uc2  that operates the plant say that's the future.\par \par \uc2 At Three Mile Island, I'm Jeff Flock with the \uc2 FOX Business Network\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And, Jeff, feed the A.I. beast.\par \par \uc2 So, \uc2 Apple\uc2  releasing its highly anticipated iPhone 16 today, but with an artificial intelligence holdup, how that affects sales coming up.\par \par \uc2 And the Secret Service still facing lots of questions after two assassination attempts on former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the answers we got today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RONALD ROWE, ACTING U.S. SECRET SERVICE DIRECTOR: And as a result of these failures, what has become clear to me is we need a shift in paradigm in how we conduct our protective operations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, acting Secret Service Director Ronald Rowe Jr. taking questions today on the security failures that led to the July 13 assassination attempt on former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 FOX News' David Spunt is live at the Justice Department with this investigation -- David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Edward.\par \par \uc2 More than two months after that tragedy in Butler, Pennsylvania, on July 13, and acting Secret Service Director \uc2 Ron Rowe\uc2  today said at a news conference that the Secret Service is now in what they call the penalty phase, but basically says that Secret Service employees will face consequences. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROWE: These employees will be held accountable and this agency has among the most robust table of penalties in the entirety of the federal government. And these penalties will be administered according to our disciplinary process. Due to federal regulations, I cannot get into personnel matters as they are proceeding.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Edward, last month five Secret Service employees were placed on administrative desk duty following that shooting in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 The Service has been working on an internal report for two months. It's almost complete, finishing touches, but, today, a summary was released. Some of the highlights include -- quote -- "A constant theme gathered from state and local law enforcement personnel who helped secure the Butler rally was the presence of communications deficiencies."\par \par \uc2 The building where Thomas Crooks, the 20-year-old shooter, took his shots, not in the secure perimeter. It was also not on the minds, apparently, of certain Secret Service personnel that day.\par \par \uc2 The report's summary continues: "There was a lack of detailed knowledge by Service personnel regarding the state or local law enforcement presence that would be present in and around the AGR complex." That's the building where Crooks was. "The different radio frequencies used at the Butler farm show venue were not conducive for quickly sharing real-time information."\par \par \uc2 Roe's said before important information was shared that day on text chains and text messages, instead of old-fashioned radio frequencies that they have used in the past. Meanwhile, on Capitol Hill today, the House passed unanimously 400 people -- 405 people in the chamber voted to zero to make sure that candidates, presidential candidates, get the exact same protection as sitting presidents.\par \par \uc2 The Secret Service director told me that they have been getting the same protection as President Biden since July 13. But Congress wants to make sure that this is enshrined as a law so it continues in the years and decades to come, Edward.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, David Spunt, thank you live at the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 So, let's turn to the expert on this, the retired Secret Service Agent in Charge Frank Loveridge.\par \par \uc2 Now, Frank, first of all, thank you for your Service in supporting this country.\par \par \uc2 What did you think about the answers we got today?\par \par \uc2 FRANK LOVERIDGE, FORMER SECRET SERVICE AGENT: Well, Edward, thank you for having me on.\par \par \uc2 One of my concerns was, we never addressed the whistle-blower allegations that were made to Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 's office raising concerns. You want to dispute those, you want to refute them if you can, because, if not, they become reality.\par \par \uc2 The one question that was asked of him was, did you deny any assets to the former President Trump's advance detail? And the way that question was phrased, according to whistle-blowers, it should have been phrased, were you aware of the war room or the manpower room telling any former President Trump advance ages not to ask for additional assets?\par \par \uc2 That should have been the question that was asked, and we needed an answer to that.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LOVERIDGE: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Well, no, I want to -- and I want to get this in there. That is a good point. What about the talk of complacency within the Secret Service that could have contributed to the failures in this assassination attempt? What do you think about that?\par \par \uc2 LOVERIDGE: Well, obviously, the protective plan was in writing on the Special Operations Division tactical survey, on the post-assignment log. We know where everyone was supposed to be.\par \par \uc2 The question is, why didn't someone go around on the perimeter, as a side agent should, and make sure that all of our assets were located where they were supposed to be? I think Acting Director \uc2 Ron Rowe\uc2  acknowledged that failure, and that was a huge failure.\par \par \uc2 That was complacency.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Frank Loveridge, I appreciate it. Again, thank you for your Service on this and insight related to this. This is something we're going to keep on, because now we have had a situation, two assassination attempts. It's just mind-boggling that we are talking about multiple attempts. Appreciate it. Thank you for your service there.\par \par \uc2 So, \uc2 Apple\uc2 's iPhone 16 hitting shelves today, but it's not as originally billed.\par \par \uc2 FOX's Susan Li, what are you seeing there?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN LI, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: If you build it, they will come. \uc2 Apple\uc2 's hoping that its next-generation A.I.-powered iPhone 16 will be a hit. But will it draw in the crowds for the world's biggest company?\par \par \uc2 I'm Susan Li outside \uc2 Apple\uc2 's flagship store in New York. I will get you more right after this short break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: The new \uc2 Apple\uc2  iPhone 16 hitting store shelves today. A slow A.I. rollout could be impacting shopper turnout.\par \par \uc2 FOX Business' Susan Li is live from the \uc2 Apple\uc2 's flagship store on New York's Fifth Avenue.\par \par \uc2 Now, Susan, is this a big miss for \uc2 Apple\uc2 , I mean, billing A.I. throughout this iPhone and then not delivering that today?\par \par \uc2 LI: Well, we still have long lineups, because those die-hard fans lined up bright and early.\par \par \uc2 And, yes, those next-generation A.I.-powered iPhone 16s did go on sale today, except, of course, without the actual A.I. software. And that's delayed until next month. But we had \uc2 Apple\uc2  CEO \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  on hand to open the doors and greet the customers at the iconic cube here on Fifth Avenue, eager fans that didn't seem to mind the wait until next month for those A.I. features.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So, every year, I'm changing the phone. Before, it was like I was waiting one month, two months. But, today, I don't know. I just came here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm interested on the new \uc2 Apple\uc2  Watch and also the new AirPods.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Apple Intelligence. I'm just waiting on it to come out and I'm going to use it a bunch. I love the use of A.I., so this is going to be fun.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LI: Yes, so I should show you that the lineups here are dwindling this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 And last year, we had lineups all the way down the block, but two reasons for this could be possibly that A.I. software delay and also in 2024 most people do get their iPhones now delivered straight to their door.\par \par \uc2 Now, Wall Street analysts, some say that the A.I. software delay means that the iPhone 16 isn't selling as well as the 15. But then some other Wall Street predictors say that this actually lengthens out the sales cycle for the iPhone 16 this year.\par \par \uc2 Now, one selling point remains, and that's those generous telco subsidies, trade-ins for free iPhone, also up to $1,000 back for the Pro models if you sign those longer dated contracts. Now, \uc2 Apple\uc2  kept the prices the same this year and that's to entice the upgraders. So the base model still starts at $799.\par \par \uc2 Remember, back in 2007, the first ever iPhone cost you $489. So if you inflation-adjust that, that might seem pricey in today's cost. But I will tell you this, that \uc2 Apple\uc2  has sold 2.5 billion devices, created $3.5 trillion dollars in value. So, yes, I think they have sold pretty well over those years -- back to you.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, people might also be going to dinner.\par \par \uc2 All right, Susan Li, thank you right there in New York.\par \par \uc2 So, you might know him from the Zac Brown Band, but John Driskell Hopkins wants to raise awareness for his work on a different project. The Grammy Award-winning musician is here and he's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Zac Brown Band founding member John Driskell Hopkins was diagnosed with \uc2 ALS\uc2  in 2021 after dealing with symptoms on stage.\par \par \uc2 Through his battle with the neurological disease, the Grammy Award-winning musician continues to perform. He's taking part in a benefit concert tonight to raise awareness and funding for ALS research.\par \par \uc2 John Driskell Hopkins joins me right now.\par \par \uc2 So what do you want to know -- what do you want people to know about this fight, John?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DRISKELL HOPKINS, MUSICIAN: I want people to know that \uc2 ALS\uc2  is not incurable. \uc2 ALS\uc2  is massively underfunded.\par \par \uc2 And we are working so hard to get the proper research and the proper funding into the hands that can do the most with it. And we have done amazing things so far, but we have so far to go.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So I have to ask -- I have to ask, how hard is it to perform at the level you do when you're dealing with issues from this disease?\par \par \uc2 HOPKINS: I'm very fortunate in that my progression is slow.\par \par \uc2 ALS\uc2  is typically a three-to-five-year life expectancy from diagnosis. And in late December this year, I will be at three years. So, some of us last longer than others. And my hands still work, but they're slower. And you can hear my speech is labored.\par \par \uc2 But I'm still singing it, so I'm still bringing it.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And do the -- what's the reaction for the other band members? Talk about the support that you're getting a little bit.\par \par \uc2 HOPKINS: Incredible support from the Zac Brown Band. Every day, these guys have my back, and gal. And I couldn't ask for a more supportive group of friends that are absolutely extended family to me.\par \par \uc2 And they're there to pick me up when I fall, and they're there to hold my arm when I stumble. And as long as I'm able to perform with excellence, I will be out on the road with Zac Brown Band.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: How can people help the cause that you're raising money for today?\par \par \uc2 HOPKINS: Well, you can quickly go to hoponacure.org. That's the 501(c)(3) that my wife and I funded in May of '22.\par \par \uc2 And at the end of this year, we will have supported over $3 million dollars in research and funded from Hop On A Cure. There are a million places to go if your heart is more into the caregiving. There are certainly great organizations to support there.\par \par \uc2 But we believe that we can knock this out and that we are going to do that through more research.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And, John, in the last 30 seconds that we have, tell me, where are scientists today? Are they making progress on it?\par \par \uc2 HOPKINS: We have made a lot of progress. And we're looking at things like head trauma, mitochondria, microbiome. This is all motor neuron disease.\par \par \uc2 And we have to figure out what happens to the motor neurons, and then we will have a lot more vision to defeat ALS, Alzheimer's, Parkinson's. It's all one big motor neuron dilemma.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Exactly. And, John, they need money for that. And the benefit concert that you're doing tonight is going to help. And folks who are watching this can also help.\par \par \uc2 John Driskell Hopkins, thank you for your time today and the battle that you're going through. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 So, be sure to tune in tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 HOPKINS: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Be sure to tune in tomorrow at 10:00 a.m. Eastern for "Cavuto Live" right here on FOX News.\par \par \uc2 For now, you know what's coming up? We all do. It's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020240920ek9k0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Declares A Win Despite No Teamster Endorsement; Karine Jean-Pierre Evades Query On \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  Non-Endorsement; Federal Reserve Cuts Rates For First Time On Four Years; New Round Of Explosions Rocks Lebanon; Democrats Look To Flip New York Congressional District Lost To Republican In 2022; Heritage Foundation President Confirms \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Did Not Endorse Project 2025 Policy Proposals; \uc2 Teamsters Union\uc2  Withholds Endorsement For Either Presidential Candidates; New Polls Shows Presidential Race Continues to be Close; Women Behind Early U.S. Space Program Honored with Congressional Gold Medals.\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Bryan Llenas, Peter Doocy, David Spunt, Kelly O'Grady,  Nate Foy, Juan Williams, Jennifer Griffin \par \uc2 7,681 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 September 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I think that she has an amazing voice and she has done amazing things for women's sports. And there are women who would love to be able to have a level playing field in sports.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: So, is she for the train -- what is she for?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: She just doesn't say anything, she doesn't talk about it, and she has a voice that would matter.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: In your face, Harold, I hope you learned a lesson.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Stop promoting her.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Despite, congratulations Billie Jean King. I'm proud of her.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's it for us.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right. I like the sexy geese better. OK, thanks, Greg.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 We're following two major stories tonight. We're getting our first look at the latest Fox News polling on the presidential race. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  up by two points over former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . We'll give you the inside of that poll.\par \par \uc2 Plus, no news may be good news for the former president. Bad news for the vice president. The \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  is refusing to endorse either one of the candidates tonight. Normally, that's a given for the Democratic nominee.\par \par \uc2 But first up those polls we mentioned, Harris has a 50 to 48 advantage on the former president among registered and likely voters. That is the same two point lead \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  enjoyed at this point in the 2016 campaign. The top issue for voters, the U.S. economy, by more than a 2-1 margin. Immigration comes in second, abortion third.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump has a 10 point trust advantage on immigration. He is up five now on the economy. Vice president leads by 16 on the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 We have Fox team coverage tonight, Peter Doocy is at the White House and has the administration reaction to that \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  decision.\par \par \uc2 But we begin with Correspondent Bryan Llenas on Long Island in Uniondale, New York, where the former president will address supporters in about an hour. Good evening, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening. The \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  revealing nearly 60 percent of Teamster union members voted in support of former President Trump in two separate polls, independently done electronically and by phone. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  receiving just 34 and 31 percent support respectively in those polls.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, while campaigning at a Bitcoin bar in Manhattan, New York City, the former president called the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  polling results a big thing and, "A great honor."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  carry a lot of weight. The Democrats cannot believe it. Look, it was always automatic that Democrats get the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 , and they said, we won't endorse the Democrats this year, so that was an honor for me.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice over): In a major win for former President Trump. For the first time since 1996, the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  leadership is opting out of endorsing the Democrats presidential candidate, even though \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  members voted overwhelmingly in support of Trump, the union's leadership wasn't willing to go there.\par \par \uc2 COREY LEWANDOWSKI\uc2 , \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  2024 CAMPAIGN SENIOR ADVISER: We don't care what the leadership says. OK, it's about the rank and file.\par \par \uc2 All of these guys, all of these women who have to get up and work every single day are with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  because they want to be able to keep more of their own money.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice over): The union's President Sean O'Brien citing strong political divides and few commitments from the candidates as reason for its non-endorsement.\par \par \uc2 SEAN O'BRIEN, PRESIDENT, TEAMSTERS: When we asked both candidates in the presidential forum roundtables about supporting railway workers and workers that are under the RLA to strike a non-interfere, we couldn't get strong commitments from either candidates.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice over): The non-endorsement is a major blow for Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , especially because \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  members polled earlier this year said they supported President Biden over Trump.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the Harris campaign is touting local union endorsements while downplaying the results of the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  polls, writing in a statement, "The vice president's strong union record is why \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  locals across the country have already endorsed her, alongside the overwhelming majority of organized labor."\par \par \uc2 The Union vote coming as the former president campaigns in deep blue New York, the rally is seen as an effort to bolster Republicans in three New York congressional races that could decide control of the House.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (on camera): Some 16,000 people are expected here at the Nassau Coliseum, we expect to hear former President Trump talk about the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  vote inflation and immigration.\par \par \uc2 Remember, according to New York City, they have spent nearly $5 billion caring for more than 200,000 migrants over the last two years, and those impacts are felt far and wide along Long Island, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bryan Llenas in Uniondale. We'll head there when the former president comes out. Bryan, thanks.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  non-decision, non-endorsement can fairly be interpreted as a loss for Vice President Harris, as you heard Bryan just report, it happened despite her openly campaigning for that endorsement just two days ago.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House Correspondent Peter Doocy has details live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, we have heard President Biden refer to himself hundreds of times over the years as the most pro- union president in history, and now his White House is explaining why his vice president left that meeting with the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  on Monday at the empty handed.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: As you know, this is a president and a vice president that has fought hard for unions throughout their administration.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Half of all Americans want to see the next president make major changes according to a Fox News poll, another 34 percent say they like drastic changes.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I love you back.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Among Trump supporters, 72 percent say they like him because they like his policies. Among Harris supporters, the number shrinks to 42 percent who like her because they like her policies. As she campaigns more and more and the country gets to know her.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Recently, I was in Nevada. I'm in the streets. Let me tell you, I'm everywhere.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Less than a third, 29 percent of registered voters in the Fox poll believe the economy is excellent or good. 70 percent believe it is only fair or poor.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 GARY PETERS\uc2  (D-MI): I think she continues to lean in on the issues that she's talking about, to help everyday families.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Democrats are now hitting Trump for not having a detailed health care plan.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS (I-VT): He has been in three political campaigns. He has been president for four years, and after all of that time, he is working on a concept for a plan.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): But on the trail, the Harris health care mentions are pretty general as well.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: We need to lower the cost of health care and continue to take on big pharma and cast the cap the cost of prescription medications.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): According to Politico, her media strategy is to make as little news as possible for the next 48 days.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I grew up understanding the children of the community are the children of the community.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): Vice President Harris tomorrow is going to head to Michigan to participate in a live stream with one of her campaign's highest profile supporters, Oprah. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy live on the North Lawn. Peter, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, we have new details about former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's security detail, a Secret Service source confirming to Fox that the former president's security has been increased after that first assassination attempt in Butler, Pennsylvania. It did help, they say, thwart a second such attempt in Florida Sunday.\par \par \uc2 That second incident has also delayed release of the Secret Service's report on the Butler security lapse. That's what we're being told. It also is increasing pressure on federal agencies such as the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt is at the Justice Department with all the latest. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hello, Bret, good evening. Members of the bipartisan congressional panel in the House investigating all of these attempts on former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's life, just received a briefing from the Secret Service and learned something new that they had not heard and we had not heard for the first time about the former president's protection while he was golfing on Sunday, watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JASON CROW\uc2  (D-CO): It appeared to be the same security package that was provided to former President Trump at that site, doing that activity as he actually received when he was the president. It was commensurate with presidential level security.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: The difference is that former President Trump does not receive the communications, the satellites, the nuclear launch codes as the current president or the vice president in any matter.\par \par \uc2 But still, they're all calls to increase the protection package for former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as the criminal investigation into those who wanted to hurt him continues.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , DIRECTOR, \uc2 FBI\uc2 : I want the American people to know that the men and women of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  are working tirelessly to get to the bottom of what happened.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): In his first public comments since the second assassination attempt on former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , \uc2 FBI\uc2  director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  is pledging a robust investigation into the suspect, Ryan Ruth, who is currently behind bars in Florida and facing federal gun charges.\par \par \uc2 This afternoon, Secret Service officials briefed members of Congress on the latest security breach.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE WALTZ (R-FL): I think the Secret Service has been much more forthcoming the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , in contrast, they haven't been as transparent.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): But a source close to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  probe tells Fox News the investigation remains in the early stages, and every detail won't be shared, since the suspect is alive and the Justice Department is building its case with more charges expected.\par \par \uc2 As for the shooting in Pennsylvania, Fox News is told, the Secret Service has delayed releasing an internal report examining the security failures by a few days, as agents are busy working on the Florida case.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICHARD BLUMENTHAL (D-CT): I am reaching the point of total outrage. The \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  has to be more forthcoming, not only to me, but to the American people.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): GOP Iowa Senator \uc2 Chuck Grassley\uc2  informed the Secret Service he has more evidence in his ongoing independent investigation that the agency assured local law enforcement it would handle the AGR building where suspected shooter Thomas Crooks was positioned.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): Bret, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  still does not have the motive of the shooter Crooks from July 13th. It's possible agents never figure out that motive. Look at the shooter from the hotel shooting in Las Vegas back in 2017, still no motive in that case, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, bipartisan outrage so far. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  says it is learning additional details about campaign hacking, beginning with Iran infiltrating former President Trump's campaign. We told you last month about the warnings from the Bureau over Iranian influence in both the Trump and Harris campaigns when it came to cyber-attacks.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  says employees in what was the Biden campaign received e-mails with materials hacked from the Trump campaign. The Justice Department has yet to file any charges, but we are told they are likely coming.\par \par \uc2 Markets, stocks finished down today following the announcement of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  interest rate cut of half a percentage point. A big move. The Dow lost 103, the S&P 500 fell 16. The NASDAQ lost 55.\par \par \uc2 The Fed is cutting its key interest rates by half a point. It was a long awaited decision. It is the first such reduction since March of 2020, it reflects the Fed's new focus on bolstering the job market. It comes just 48 days before the election.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Fox Business correspondent Kelly O'Grady tells us what that may mean for you and your family. Good evening, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. Yes, today's rate decision indicates the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  feels confident about its two percent inflation target, but wants to ensure the labor market doesn't continue to cool.\par \par \uc2 Now, 50 basis points is a big deal. Many investors rejoiced at the aggressive beginning to the rate cut cycle, but in recent years, we've only seen a half point cut in dire times, 9/11, COVID.\par \par \uc2 So, some analysts are raising concerns a larger cut could indicate we are in more economic trouble than advertised. Now, Fed Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  maintained he doesn't see signs of a recession or slowdown, and indicated we could expect a couple more cuts before the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 But Powell also emphasized the Fed will take it meeting by meeting.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JEROME POWELL\uc2 , CHAIRMAN, FEDERAL RESERVE: We are not on any preset course. If the economy remains solid and inflation persists, we can dial back policy restraint more slowly. If the labor market were to weaken unexpectedly, or inflation were to fall more quickly than anticipated, we are prepared to respond.\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY (voice over): What this means for the average consumer is interest rates will come down, and that likely includes mortgage rates. The 30-year fixed stands at 6.2 percent, it's tied to a longer term rate outlook, but this bigger than expected cut could move the needle.\par \par \uc2 Now, President Biden, though saw the cut as a clear victory, saying on X, "We just reached an important moment. Inflation and interest rates are falling while the economy remains strong. The critics said it couldn't happen, but our policies are lowering costs and creating jobs."\par \par \uc2 But some economists are cautioning, though, if you cut rates too quickly, inflation could rise back up to levels we saw earlier under the Biden- Harris administration.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY (on camera): So, this will continue to be a delicate balance between inflation and the labor market, all with an election in less than two months, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, Kelly, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  CEO says the company will begin furloughing a large number of employees to conserve cash during the strike by union machinists that began last week. Chief Executive \uc2 Kelly Ortberg\uc2  says the layoffs will be temporary and affect executives, managers and other employees.\par \par \uc2 Up next, additional exploding communications devices today in Lebanon and Fox News learns who is behind the attacks. We'll take you there.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around this country are covering tonight. Fox 2 in San Francisco, as police said, University of California campuses want the U.C. Board of Regents to sign off on plans to buy new weapons and ammunition. Officials want more drones, projectile launchers, sponge bullets and other crowd control measures.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Providence, Rhode Island from our affiliate WPRI, one of the big stories there tonight, one of the world's most famous multilevel marketing companies files for bankruptcy. \uc2 Tupperware\uc2  and some of its subsidiaries started chapter 11 proceedings yesterday. The Wall Street Journal reporting the company will still operate and will try to sell the brand while it is in bankruptcy. That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Cloudy Day in Washington, updating a story now we've been following here on SPECIAL REPORT, the two Jordanian nationals accused of attempting to illegally enter Marine Corps Base Quantico back in May were in court yesterday in Virginia to face misdemeanor trespassing charges.\par \par \uc2 A judge denied a motion to dismiss the charges against Hasan Hamdan and Mohammad Dabous. Both men were in the country illegally when they were arrested. They're currently free on bail, but in ICE removal proceedings.\par \par \uc2 The state department's internal watchdog says officials did not follow proper protocol when handling the security clearance suspension of the U.S. Special Envoy for Iran following allegations he may have mishandled classified information.\par \par \uc2 The report says \uc2 Robert Malley\uc2  was allowed to access classified meetings and information that could have otherwise been restricted.\par \par \uc2 For the past year, congressional Republicans have been demanding the State Department produce more information on the suspension and related allegations. Republican lawmakers allege Malley sent classified documents to his personal e-mail account, downloaded them to his personal mobile phone. The inspector general's report does not address the underlying allegations that led to the revocation of Malley's clearance, which are the subject of a separate investigation.\par \par \uc2 There's another round of explosions today of communications devices in Lebanon. This follows yesterday's pager blasts that left thousands injured and in total, dozens dead. A senior U.S. official tells Fox, Israel is behind the attacks. Correspondent Nate Foy has the latest tonight from Tel Aviv. A warning here, some of the images in this piece are graphic.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): A second wave of explosions in Lebanon, this time coming from handheld radios used by \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  terrorists. Here you see several detonate in Beirut.\par \par \uc2 This blast interrupted a funeral for three terrorists killed yesterday.\par \par \uc2 A senior U.S. official tells Fox, Israel is behind the explosions that took their lives. Israel embedded explosive devices in pagers delivered to \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  terrorists in recent months