y briefings to every candidate across the political spectrum. This is what we do in think tank world is create and cultivate these ideas.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: For a conservative think tank to have the Republican candidate distance himself from something like Project 2025, which is 922 pages of possible policy, isn't that a bad thing?\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: It's a bad thing if we forget what the reality is, which is that Project 2025 became the target of the radical left. Why did it become a target of the radical left? Because what the radical left can't have an intellectually honest conversation about they attempt to destroy and to mischaracterize. Literally everything you ran in the intro senators in that interview of the senators and the representatives is untrue about Project 2025. It's a menu of conservative boilerplate options. I don't think there is a conservative I have met who would say in those 922 pages I'll agree with everything. But the left can't run on ideas and policies because they are destroying the American dream. And that, by the way, is the motivation for --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: They spend a lot of time talking about it, not a lot of time explaining the details of it. It's very detailed. There is one -- this is \uc2 Jared Huffman\uc2 , Democrat from California. He leads a task force to stop Project 2025 -- apparently they are holding a news conference tomorrow -- that says "Trump's Project 2025," which we have talked about, is not the connection, "plans to resurrect a Victorian era law, the Comstock Act, to criminalize reproductive care nationwide. We must rectify past injustices and ensure our dark history cannot repeat itself under any future administration. Stop project 2025."\par \par \uc2 All right, deal specifically with that comment.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Totally untrue. We talk about the history of the Comstock law in the project, and so what the Congressman is doing is glomming on to a search function where those words are in there. And