lity of their support. The vast majority, nearly 60 percent of actual teamsters, are with Trump, you know, and in the same year they were with Biden. They were willing to vote with Biden over Trump. But the Kamala change, they are now going with Trump. That's got to be just very disconcerting to the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  campaign that they have lost so much ground among that type of middle class worker.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: He spoke at the Republican convention.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He did, the teamsters did. And that is quite something.\par \par \uc2 Panel, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, honoring some of the unsung heroes from the early days of the U.S. space program. You may remember this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Finally tonight, a SPECIAL REPORT salute to the women behind the men in the early days of the U.S. space program. Four of them were awarded the Congressional Gold Medal today for often overlooked but crucial contributions. Here is chief national security correspondent Jennifer Griffin.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We're programming and we're all OK.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: When John Glenn became the first American to orbit the earth in 1962, before liftoff he turned to a woman who served as one of \uc2 NASA\uc2 's human computers, Katherine Johnson, who began her career in segregated Virginia at Langley Aeronautical Laboratory. She was the woman he turned to.\par \par \uc2 KATHERINE JOHNSON, FORMER \uc2 NASA\uc2  MATHEMATICIAN: I computed his trajectory. From then on, any time they were going to compute trajectories, they were given mostly all of them to my branch. And I did most of the work on those by hand. But when he got ready to go, he said call her. And if she says the computer is right, I'll take it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The families of --\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Today on Capitol Hill, Johnson and three other 