n lack of resources. That's a cop out. Agencies had to deal with lack of resources or not enough resources all the time, you got to prioritize your resources and put them where they're needed the most, especially if your core mission is protection, that's where your resources need to go.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Frank, when you look at the -- and I know this area well, I've been to this club a number of times, it seems to me that it's fairly obvious that if it's not a wall, it's going to have a porous perimeter. And it would seem, again, if you cannot secure the perimeter of where the former President is, it wasn't effective. It's not the landscaper's fault or Trump's fault or his -- this is a breakdown once again, and the basic mission of this -- of the Secret Service.\par \par \uc2 And this -- in his press conference today, I'm just outraged by it, like patting each other on the back, going, oh, you did this, and we did this, and he saw this. Well, thank God, we did see the muzzle. But that guy should never gotten in those bushes.\par \par \uc2 LOVERIDGE: Laura, his 300 yards to 500 yards away from the President, and as the President progressed forward, he would have been even closer. The whole thing comes down to this. Did they treat this as an off the record movement? That's one of the things we do. We have off the record movements, impromptu movements, and we also have on the record movements.\par \par \uc2 Off the record movements, where we don't tell anyone, we're going there, but through predictive analysis, this individual could have easily thought Sunday afternoon, President Trump is going to be on the golf course. This should have been treated with all of the resources. They should have been out there, and we didn't have the right resources there. There was nobody on that perimeter walking around that would have spotted the assailant, and this never would have happened. We would have gotten before he even got into the street line.\par \par \uc2 INGRA