ic has gotten too heated, we should tone that down too.\par \par \uc2 And further, of course, say that we're going to be totally transparent. We're going to expedite all investigations with factual conclusions, not political conclusions, and recommendations that are clear and accountability that is just unquestionable.\par \par \uc2 Now, and we're not going to do this in months. We're going to do this in weeks. And why not say this is America, the voters must be the ones who determine the outcome in November, not an assassin's bullet. Period. Now, given that they'd also have to dial back their own Trump as a dictator nonsense, this probably won't happen, but it should.\par \par \uc2 Both sides can make it about the issues, but it's not just the election that's at stake here. How this moment is handled affects the credibility of the entire \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 . And if Harris were smart, and I know that might be a big ask, her number one priority right now would be to make it known nationally and throughout the world that everything in the federal government's power will be done to keep \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  safe. And that's the Angle.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now. Missouri Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 . Senator, today, the \uc2 U.S. Secret Service\uc2  and other officials came out and said that the methodologies that were put in place on Sunday were effective. The shooter did not get the President in his line of sight. He was chased off, and he was apprehended. And your response?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOSH HAWLEY\uc2  (R-MO): Well, I don't know how you can say it's effective when the shooter was able to be there on that golf course for 12 hours, Laura. I mean, this is a course that the President plays on a regular basis, by the way. This isn't like it's some new place that he parachuted into. He lives right next door, and the shooter's there for 12 hours and nobody notices. I mean, what really is going on here? It is of a pattern that we saw dating back to the Butler rally, when that shooter was allowed to get on that rooftop, and there's supposed to be law enforcement there, and they weren't. And there's supposed to be law enforcement on that perimeter, and they weren't.\par \par \uc2 So, we have a problem here to say the least, and I think it's time for Secret Service to tell us what is going on? Why all of these failings, and what are they going to do about it?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We hear they have the resources. President Biden had a cordial conversation with President Trump. I actually think it's really good that he reached out. I think he should bring President Trump in, and they really should do a joint appearance to reassure the country, because people are -- this happens again, and I don't -- again, I said it in the Angle. I don't think Humpty Dumpty gets put back together again. I think they're going to just shatter whatever is left at the trust that the people have in the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and in the Secret Service.\par \par \uc2 But Senator, I think it's important for us to remember that the Butler rally questions that this whistleblower and this whistleblower report that just came out raised. Those are still outstanding, and some of what you've discovered and brought to light in this whistleblower report is just devastating about why that roof was not secured before the President went out to speak.\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: The facts are devastating, Laura, and there are no answers to them. This is the problem. The Secret Service, at this remove of two months has still not answered the most basic questions, why wasn't the roof secured? We know that the site plan called for there to be a security perimeter around that building. There wasn't one. It called for there to be law enforcement on top of the roof. They weren't there, just like this guy at the perimeter of the golf course for 12 hours.\par \par \uc2 Why didn't Secret Service sweep it beforehand? Did they not have surveillance around the perimeter? It's the same series of questions now repeating itself over again. We still haven't gotten answers from the first time around. And for them now, Secret Service to be declaring victory, excuse me, but you haven't told us anything about what happened two months ago? They are slow walking at best, Laura, at best, slow walking, if not stonewalling Congress's request for facts, for documents. They owe the American people an accounting of what happened in July, just like they owe them an accounting of what happened over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We don't know whose job it was, right? What -- there must have been one or two individuals whose job it was to secure that roof. And in this whistleblower report, we discover that the issue of the oppressive heat, the temperature in the summer, was a factor in not getting the roof actually monitored and secured. Is that right?\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: It's absolutely ridiculous. That is, indeed, what whistleblowers tell me, Laura, what they say is, is that the plan, the security plan, called for there to be law enforcement on the roof and law enforcement around the building at Butler, there were neither. There was nobody on the roof except for the shooter, and there was nobody around the perimeter of the building.\par \par \uc2 And yes, the excuse was, well, it was awfully hot that day. Well, I mean, so because of that, the former President shot in the face and a good man is dead. I mean, that's the answer? Come on.\par \par \uc2 Where are the answers here? Why were there not law enforcement there? Why weren't they putting their best people on this detail? We also know from whistleblowers that the lead side agent is someone who was not trusted by other Secret Service agents, who was not highly regarded in the service and who didn't know what she was doing.\par \par \uc2 Why are these people running security for a former president and a current major party nominee? It's all inexplicable. And the longer we go without real answers, Laura, the worse it gets.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Well, and if DEI is part of what is factoring in here, or is it diversity, you know, check boxes, then that's a real problem. And I think the less we know, the more we can assume the facts are bad for the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the Secret Service. Senator, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 All right, the left's vile reaction to what happened yesterday, a second attempt on President Trump's life. So, who are these people? What does it reveal about who they really are deep down? That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Of all the attempts to try to divert attention away from the pattern of violence committed by these crazed left-wing lunatics, this was among the most pathetic.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Today's apparent assassination attempt comes amid increasingly fierce rhetoric on the campaign trail itself. Mr. Trump, his running mate J.D. Vance, continue to make baseless claims about Haitian immigrants in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Do you expect to hear anything from the Trump campaign about toning down the rhetoric, toning down the violence, or would that be typical of the former president?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I would love for us to have a unity-type moment, but I think it's probably going to be pretty fleeting as we've seen in the past.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So, it's Trump's fault that people are trying to take shots at him. Well, these people are total phonies. They know that you can't have unity. You certainly can't have credibility when their buddies are calling the nominee of one of our two major parties, Hitler, or a dictator-in- waiting. Or our unity and joy found in what House Minority Leader \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  tweeted out just hours after the shooting, saying, Extreme MAGA Republicans are the party of a national abortion ban and Trump's Project 2025. We must stop them. Must stop them.\par \par \uc2 Well, Trump has disavowed Project 2025, and even liberal media outlets have had to fact-check Democrats on this issue, but they don't care. They have nothing else. Baseless fear-mongering. Trump must be stopped. Hmm, maybe Dana Bash could ask \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , what does he mean by that? How far should people go to stop Hitler?\par \par \uc2 And the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  columnist Phil Bump on his head decided to focus on Trump supporters who are obviously concerned about his safety. They're the problem. Apparently, they should just shut up and let the experts handle it, writing, Trump world is eager to have Americans blame his opponent's rhetoric for assassination attempts, both to nullify their arguments and to cast Trump as a victim.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Florida Congressman Byron Donalds of the House Oversight Committee. Congressman, it's a shock that the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  is cratering in its readership. But we have heard a lot about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from the leftists leading up to this second assassination attempt, including, Congressman, from a well-known commentator, Keith Boykin, who says that if Trump is popular, it's obviously just because of racism. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: He's not dead because of white people. I mean, the majority of white people support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and they have supported him in 2016, supported him in 2020, and they'll probably support him in 2024. If it were up to black people or Latinos and Asian-Americans and other people of color, he would lose in a landslide. He's in the lead because he protects and projects the idea of protecting white supremacy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Congressman, that's the kind of stuff that these crazed lunatics are marinating in.\par \par \uc2 REP. BYRON DONALDS (R-FL): Laura, listening to Keith Boykin, frankly, is listening to the hysteria from the Democrat Party and the radical left. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is losing black voters because they destroyed the economy. She's losing Hispanic voters because she destroyed the border. And quite frankly, she's losing Jewish voters and a lot of other ethnicities because of the terrible foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 They want to throw out race as the reason, but the true reason is their policies are a disaster. Their rhetoric is dangerous and their leadership is nonexistent. This is why \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is still popular today. In my opinion, that's why he's going to win this presidential election. But obviously the story today is, this is the reason why you have crazies from the left who are now attempting to assassinate him.\par \par \uc2 That rhetoric is out of control. It is dangerous. And we're not going to be lectured to by Keith Boykin or anybody else on the Democrat side of the aisle. Clean up your own house before you come talk to us.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, one of the things that \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  keeps saying is she will do, quote, "whatever it takes," whatever it takes to stop \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And that comes on the heels of he's Stalin, Hitler, Pol Pot, like every dictator that ever lived, like he's at the end of democracy, there'll never be another election.\par \par \uc2 Well, if they actually mean that, then why aren't they pressed into having to answer, well, why wouldn't an assassination attempt against Hitler be applauded? If you really believe that about him, then where did you think this was going to go?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: You're absolutely right. And the truth is they will never answer. Because the radical media, by the way, demonstrated by how one-sided the debate last week was, coupled with the fact that they are in the tank for the radical Democrats in D.C., who want a Washington agenda, they want an elite agenda. But they don't want an agenda for the American people.\par \par \uc2 So, they'll never be made to answer for how disastrous their rhetoric has been.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Congressman, do you think Kamala's issued statement where she said there's no place for violence in America or she's glad \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's safe, was that sufficient?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: No, what she should do is go through all the ad campaign that they're running in the battleground states. Anything that references being destructive of democracy and all the other tropes and the lies, Project 2025, all that stuff, it should come down. Stick to the facts, stick to the policy, but she can't do that. And so, that's why in my view, they're not going to change.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Congressman, thank you very much. And coming up, the battle of the word salads. Kamala and Tim say they care about the middle class. We'll let you decide. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Talk about bringing down prices and making life more affordable for people. What are one or two specific things you have in mind for that?\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: Well, I'll start with this. I grew up a middle-class kid. I grew up in a community of hard-working people, you know, construction workers and nurses and teachers, and I try to explain to some people who may not have had the same experience, you know. but a lot of people will relate to this.\par \par \uc2 You know, I grew up in a neighborhood of folks who were very proud of their lawn, you know?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: What is your administration's plan to be more specific, a little bit on lowering the cost of living?\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): Yeah, well, look, we're producing more oil than we ever have in this country. We are producing more than any other country. We're expanding those options for it, whether it's wind and solar and things, getting at this heart of climate change.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Who needs wind turbines? You have all the hot air blowing from \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 . Joining me now, Victor Davis Hanson, senior fellow at Hoover. Victor, it's the same question that she was asked at the debate. She didn't answer at the debate. What's your policy to make life better for the middle class, and she says, I was raised middle-class. What is going on here?\par \par \uc2 VICTOR DAVIS HANSON, SENIOR FELLOW, HOOVER INSTITUTION: Yeah. Even that's not true. She grew up in one of the wealthiest neighborhoods in Montreal and a child with two PhDs. She seems to think it's important to be of the middle class, to have some experience with hardship. She did -- she had very little. She thinks it's important to have worked in the private sector, but I don't think she ever did. And when she suggests she worked at \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 , there's no evidence that she ever did.\par \par \uc2 So, what she really does is, every once in a while, she throws out something. Wage and price controls. She doesn't return to it. Subjecting millions and more Americans to the inheritance tax, a tax on unearned or unrealized profits, capital gains, increasing the federal income tax rate. And then she throws out things like, I'm going to cancel student loans or I'm going to make illegal aliens eligible for complete health care control.\par \par \uc2 But they're just a mishmash. And it's typical. It's throw-out something against the rich, give something to the poor, but don't ever coordinate it. Don't try to explain how you're going to pay for it. It's not serious.\par \par \uc2 So then when it's not serious, she just relies back on, I'm from the middle class, and I worked at \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's, and just banalities like that. And I think she thinks there's only 50 days left, Laura, so she can get by with it, next 50 days.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So, Victor, it's running out the clock, right? Just run out the clock, talk about joy, talk about love, do high fives with the Sorority Sisters, and have a lot of joy, spread a lot of happiness, and hope that you can just make it to election night.\par \par \uc2 HANSON: Yeah, there's one other thing. This message that she has is aimed at -- it's not aimed at the middle class, it's aimed at the subsidized poor to give them more subsidies, and it's going after the supposed evil rich. But the people who are backing her are the rich, and they approve of it because they have so much money, they don't think they'll be hurt or they'll have an exemption if she's in power.\par \par \uc2 But the targets are the middle class, lower middle class, upper middle class. Those are the people who will be really hurt by wage and price control and things like that.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Hammered, hammered, hammered. Lower wages from mass importation of migrants. Victor, as always, great to see you. All right, who's really out of their minds during this election season? And the Pope offers some very confusing voting advice. Seen and Unseen with Raymond Arroyo is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's time for Seen and Unseen, where we reveal the stories behind the headlines. For that, we turn to Fox News contributor Raymond Arroyo. All right, Raymond, language used during a campaign, it matters.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: It does. I mean, you talked about this earlier in the show. Language shapes reality, Laura, because thoughts become reality. And look, for all the talk today from Harris and Walz that violence has no place in politics, the rhetoric from the Democratic side has been pretty dire of late.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Don't give them the power. Look, are they a threat to democracy? Yes. Are they going to take our rights away? Yes. The fascists depend on fear.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: This election ain't no joke. We got to take this (BLEEP) by the throat.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: Let me tell you what our Project