gainst life. Not welcoming migrants is a sin. It is grave. Performing an abortion is killing a human being. It is an assassination.\par \par \uc2 You must vote and you must choose the lesser evil. Which is the lesser evil, that lady or that gentleman? I don't know.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, Laura, the Pope should know, killing a child is a graver moral sin than turning a migrant away at the border for not following protocol. One ends a life, the other protects the integrity of the nation and its people, which the church teaches is the nation's guide.\par \par \uc2 The moral equivalency here is very concerning, and I wish the Pope would be more-clear and simply not wade into these waters.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Raymond, I was at the Vatican this past summer, and I didn't see a lot of migrant tents that were allowed on the Vatican grounds, nor did I see anyone getting -- a migrant being ushered through past the long line of people waiting to get in, and if they didn't have tickets to get into the \uc2 Vatican Museum\uc2 , no entrada, por favor. No entrada.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: You and I can't even walk into the Vatican gardens without an invitation and permission --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: -- Vatican passport. So, come on. No, this is -- I wish the Pope were clearer, but look, there's no doubt the church has always taught abortion is intrinsically evil. And the Pope seems to be smudging that when he says, well, vote for the lesser of the two, and neither are pro-life. That's simply not true. It's just not true.\par \par \uc2 The Pope, I hope, would read more deeply into the agenda of both of these parties before speaking out. I always call this plain confusion when he does, you know, these aboard plain press conferences. They never end well.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, this is why you and I have a lot of fans in the Vatican, OK. Maybe it's time to turn the page. Turn the page at the Vatican. God bless.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I'm not saying that.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Raymond, great to see you as always. Thank you so much. Thank you for watching. Remember, it's America now and forever. We're going to turn the page to Jesse Watters.\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020240917ek9g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Trump Rips Harris Home State Policies; Harris Campaigns In Battleground Pennsylvania; Trump: Harris Is More Liberal Than Sanders Or Warren; Biden And Starmer Discuss Ukraine Defense Provisions; Biden Faces Criticism Over Speed Of Ukraine Decisions; Oregon Decriminalizes Hard Drug Possession In 2020; Polls Show Close Presidential Race in Swing States like Pennsylvania; \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' Changing Position On Fracking Examined; National Institutes Of Health Shuts Down Investigation Into So-Called Havana Syndrome Due To Subjects Being Coerced To Participate Without Informed Consent; \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Gives Interview In Pennsylvania; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Says He Will Not Debate \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Again\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Aishah Hasnie, Bryan Llenas, Trace Gallagher, Jacqui  Heinrich, Alexandria Hoff, Jennifer Griffin \par \uc2 7,565 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 September 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right. Have a great weekend, everybody.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Bye.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Jesse, good job saving time for the jackasses. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says he might agree to another debate with Vice President Harris, if he gets quote in the right mood. That comes just one day after saying there would be no third debate.\par \par \uc2 The Republican nominee took questions from reporters this afternoon in California. Congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie was out there. She starts us off tonight from Rancho Palos Verdes, California, with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 It is not a commitment, but it is the first time the former president has softened up to the idea of doing another debate. That off-the-cuff comment came right after he wrapped up a big press conference here, basically telling reporters that he didn't have to do another debate.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: What would it take to do another debate? What would you need or want?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: It would be -- I wouldn't need anything. I could do it tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Maybe, if I got in the right mood. I don't know. Right now, I'm leading.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Former President Trump leaving the door cracked open to another debate against Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , one day after he said that there would be no rematch, but defiant he won the first.\par \par \uc2 The president held a news conference at his Rancho Palos Verdes golf club, in between two high-dollar fundraisers in California. One, hosted by billionaire Tom Siebel, a relative of Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 's wife, Jennifer Siebel Newsom.\par \par \uc2 The GOP nominee, also ripping apart his opponent's home state.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: California has the highest inflation, the highest taxes, the highest gas prices, the most illegal aliens, the most regulations, the most expensive utilities.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): And calling Harris the "godmother of sanctuary cities".\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : She boasted, quote, we are a sanctuary city, and we always will be. Well, the sanctuary cities of crime.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Trump also took incoming about right-wing social media personality Laura Loomer, who's been traveling with the president this week. His own allies questioning her influence on him as she is getting blowback from conservatives over a tweet, saying, among other things, that the White House would smell like curry if vice president Harris won.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I don't know what she said, but I will go take a look and put out a statement later on. She brings a spirit to us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): Now, the former president is headed to Nevada to host a big rally there, Arizona, and Nevada, Bret, are two of the biggest concerns for Senate Republicans right now, because their candidates face a huge cash disparity with Senate candidates for the Democrats' side.\par \par \uc2 It is unclear at this hour if the former president will try to help them at all with fundraising after he wraps up these events here in California, because he, too is facing a major shortage, or at least a funding gap with his opponent. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah Hasnie, traveling with the former president, Aishah, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , meantime, will address supporters in Wellsboro, Pennsylvania. As you look there live in just a few minutes, you're seeing Senator Casey, there from Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 It's another visit to a state that could decide who sits in the Oval Office for the next four years.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Bryan Llenas is in Pennsylvania tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I am feeling very good about Pennsylvania, because there are a lot of people in Pennsylvania who deserve to be seen and heard.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Tonight, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  campaigning in rural Pennsylvania counties, Trump won by double digits in 2020.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Ultimately, I feel very strongly that got to earn every vote.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice over): In a new document, the Harris campaign is detailing its plan for rural America for the first time, promising to invest in rural infrastructure, lower energy costs for farmers, and lower grocery prices.\par \par \uc2 But the latest congressional Republican Joint Economic Committee report says Pennsylvanians are spending on average, 19 percent, $984 more a month on energy, food, housing, and transportation since the start of the Biden Harris administration. The race in Pennsylvania is tied, and it hinges on the economy, where polls show Trump is trusted more than Harris.\par \par \uc2 Inflation may not be the only problem for Harris. So, too is the support of Pennsylvania's steel workers.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reports the Biden-Harris administration will likely delay its decision on whether to block the acquisition of \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  by Japan's \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2 , until after the election. Presidents Biden and Trump are against the deal, but local Democrats and unions say they are for it.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS KELLY, MAYOR OF WEST MIFFLIN, PENNSYLVANIA: Nippon has promised new equipment, new modernization, new techniques. The jobs will be created, they will be secured, their pensions will be secured.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: The White House insists this is not a political calculation. It's simply waiting on a national security assessment.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: There is a process. \uc2 CFIUS\uc2  has to make a recommendation, has to be transmitted to the president, and the president then makes a decision. That has not occurred. That has not happened.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (on camera): And tonight, in a major ruling, Pennsylvania Supreme Court says that mail-in-ballots that are undated or incorrectly dated will not be counted in this election. This is a win for Republicans that appealed a lower court's decision that ruled that misdated ballots should count. This can make all the difference in a close election.\par \par \uc2 And take this in consideration, 16,000 mail-in-ballots were not counted in the primary simply because voters forgot to put in the proper date. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's a big deal. A big deal. We're going to dig deep into that in coming days and weeks. Bryan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight at 11:00 p.m. Eastern, a special interview with former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . FOX NEWS @ NIGHT anchor, Trace Gallagher spoke with the Republican presidential nominee earlier today in California. Here is an excerpt.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Bernie Sanders said the quiet part out loud when he said that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is going to the right to get into office. And then, he believes that she was still -- that she is still a progressive at heart.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : But she has actually left Bernie Sanders. She is more liberal. They don't like that term, liberal. They like progressive. But to me, it's liberal. But she is far more liberal than Bernie Sanders, or than Pocahontas. I mean, Elizabeth Warren -- Pocahontas. Commonly referred to as Pocahontas, is more conservative than this woman that's running for president, who got no votes, who was the first one to lose in the primaries, same primary to Biden.\par \par \uc2 And I guess, you had 22 people running. She was the first one to drop out. She never got to Iowa, and she is now running. I don't know. Is she a threat to democracy? You know, they call me a threat.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Of course.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : They call me a threat to democracy. I'm the least threat to democracy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Former president there. Trace Gallagher now joins us from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Trace, good job. What's your take away from the interview?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: You know, I took away that the bottom line here in this entire strategy is the former president, Bret, believes that his biggest asset against \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is to continue defining her as this far left liberal who is trying to move to the center to win the election, and then, would move back to the far left.\par \par \uc2 And the reason he thinks he will be successful is because his campaign does not believe that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has truly defined herself. She has changed policies, we know that. But hasn't explained why she changed. And if she continues to kind of leave voters in an issue-type limbo, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  believes that will benefit him. Of course, strategy is one thing, and outcome is the main thing. The outcome remains very unclear here. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I think we have another clip. This one about President Biden and the hat incident. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : So, I've just had a great relationship with Pennsylvania, with those people, and they asked me if I could come. I didn't know that Biden was going to be there. He was there earlier. Actually, more prime time, in a sense, right? Because I got there later. I went to the World Trade Center, and then, I went to Shanksville.\par \par \uc2 And we stopped at the firehouse, but he was there a couple of hours earlier, and I saw it on your show, actually.\par \par \uc2 Nobody said anything except they demanded he put on a Trump cap.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : You have to put on the Make America Again -- Great Again hat, right? And he did. I mean, he did. He didn't the first, it was very interesting. They said, put on the hat. And he said, I can't do that. They said, put on the hat. He put it on. I said, that's not very nice when you think about it.\par \par \uc2 But then, I came in a place, it went crazy. Yes, very nice feeling.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, he feels confident. I'm guessing, from those two clips. Anything else you take away from the interview?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, one thing I'm glad he watches the show. You know, the funny thing is, he walked in --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: It was the 9/11 right? It was 9/11, the 23 mark -- 23-year mark. And when he walked in after President Biden and Vice President Harris, they got no response, he got a huge response.\par \par \uc2 And when Trump walked into that same firehouse, you can hear the place just kind of erupted. But we spoke to him, Bret, for about 23 minutes. He was very open, gave us insight into how he plans to win this race.\par \par \uc2 And as for more debates, I was listening to Aishah Hasnie earlier. He's a very competitive man, as you know, Bret. He does not like to walk away from a fight, so, I would never count him out of doing another debate, maybe on Fox.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. We will tune in tonight. Look forward to seeing the rest of that interview. Trace, thanks.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The State Department is pursuing new sanctions on Russian state media tonight. The U.S. accuses a Kremlin news outlet of working hand in hand with the Russian military.\par \par \uc2 It says, R.T. has run fundraising campaigns to pay for sniper rifles, body armor, and other equipment for soldiers fighting in Ukraine. It had previously been sanctioned for its work to spread Kremlin propaganda and disinformation.\par \par \uc2 These new sanctions suggest R.T.'s role goes far beyond influence operations. Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  calls it a key part of Russia's war machine.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, President Biden and British Prime Minister \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  met today at the White House, on the agenda there, Ukraine. And whether it will be allowed to use long range missiles against Russia. \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has said that would cause war with \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Senior White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has details tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Mr. Prime Minister, welcome. Welcome.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Western diplomatic sources tell Fox President Biden and U.K. Prime Minister \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  would talk about green lighting. Ukraine's ask to use long range missiles on targets deep inside Russia.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine now faces a new wave of potential destruction after the state department says Iran gave Russia short range ballistic missiles, Biden would need to sign off for Ukraine to use British-Storm Shadow missiles, because components are U.S. made.\par \par \uc2 Kyiv has also asked to use U.S. ATACMS. Officials have signaled openness.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF STATE: -- as the battlefield has changed, we have adapted.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): But insist there is no change now.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: I would not expect there to be any announcements.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): U.S. has resisted lifting restrictions, as Putin warns that decision would put \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries at war with Russia.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I don't think much about \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): But the escalation fears are nothing new.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The equipment that we have provided is defensive, as you know, not offensive, and we see that as being a difference.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): On each capability the U.S. has ultimately provided, the initial answer was no. From HIMARS and ATACMS, to patriots, and fighter jets.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: This week in the debate, the vice president was promoting what this administration has done in terms of giving Ukraine what it needs. But the -- there is plenty of criticism outside of that to say that it's also effectively blocking a victory by slow walking these decisions.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: If you were to asked \uc2 NATO\uc2  leaders, if you were to asked President Zelenskyy itself -- himself, their thoughts on the president's leadership, I don't think they would have the words that you just said to me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: (on camera): Fox has told, Biden's meeting today isn't just about this decision, even though Canada today announced its support. Broadly, the U.S. and U.K. have been discussing how to best position Ukraine ahead of the \uc2 U.N. General Assembly\uc2 , where they want to arrive with credible proposals in hand to get other allies on board. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich on the North Lawn. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were up to cap the best week of the year. The Dow gaining 297. The S&P 500 finished ahead 30. The NASDAQ rose 114.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow jumped 2.6 percentage points. The S&P 500 surged four. The NASDAQ jumped six.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we look at Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's changing policies on decriminalization. And later, we'll talk about the key battleground state where energy could be the most pivotal issue.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Boar's Head says it is indefinitely closing a Virginia manufacturing plant linked to a deadly listeria outbreak. The company's Jarrett (ph) facility has been tied to the meat sliced at deli counters that caused the biggest listeria outbreak since 2011. The company also says it will permanently discontinue its Liverwurst product, which was linked to the illness.\par \par \uc2 As of August 8th, 57 people from 18 states had been hospitalized. Nine have died.\par \par \uc2 Workers at \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's West Coast factory are striking tonight. They overwhelmingly rejected a contract deal halting production of the plane maker's strongest selling model, and sending shares down more than two percent. The first strike since 2008 comes as \uc2 Boeing\uc2  is already under heavy scrutiny from regulators and customers after a door panel blew off a 737 Max jet in flight last January.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has taken significant criticism over changes to many of her past policy positions, especially back to 2019 when she ran for president.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we take a look at the Democratic presidential nominee's evolving stance on drug decriminalization, as evidenced in the same questionnaire that has exposed other inconsistencies.\par \par \uc2 Here's correspondent Alexandria Hoff.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: You have heard me say I just don't think people should have to go to jail for smoking weed.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): We know where Vice President Harris stands on marijuana reform, but not on hard drugs, at least not right now.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, then Senator Harris appeared to support full decriminalization of illicit substances and a recently resurfaced \uc2 ACLU\uc2  presidential candidate questionnaire, Harris wrote, the opioid crisis has reaffirmed the failure of criminalization. Full decriminalization with appropriate treatment responses could address this stark racial injustice and reduce incarceration.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, Oregon became the first state to try this by removing traditional penalties for drug possession. It was an experiment that ultimately failed.\par \par \uc2 KAMELAH ADAMS, PORTLAND, OREGON BUSINESS OWNER: I had a customer who had to walk over a lot of people who were on the stairwell in the parking garage doing drugs at least like five people. It impacts our business.\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice over): Between 2021 and 2023, homicides in Portland rose 11 percent, burglaries up almost 10 percent, car thefts increased by 33 percent and robberies by nearly 23 percent that is on top of a spike in homelessness and overdoses.\par \par \uc2 This year, Oregon passed legislation to recriminalize drug possession. When Fox reached out, the Harris campaign avoided a direct response, instead repeating the statement they used when asked about Harris's apparent policy reversals.\par \par \uc2 The vice president's positions have been shaped by three years of effective governance as part of the Biden-Harris administration.\par \par \uc2 On the 2019 \uc2 ACLU\uc2  survey as a whole.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL TYLER, HARRIS CAMPAIGN COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR: These old questionnaires are a complete distraction from the task at hand right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOFF (on camera): On the issues page of Harris's website, the campaign touts the Biden administration's efforts to help stop the flow of illicit substances. There's no mention of decriminalizing drugs or not. In fact, marijuana reform isn't mentioned either, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alex, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Pope Francis is strongly criticizing both U.S. presidential candidates over what he calls anti-life policies on abortion and migration. He's advising American Catholics to choose what he calls the lesser evil in the upcoming election.\par \par \uc2 He says when deciding between a candidate who supports abortion rights and one planning to deport migrants that both are against life, he did not mention either by name.\par \par \uc2 Up next, political experts Douglas Schoen and Karl Rove on how energy issues could be the difference in the presidential election, especially in one key state. Plus, we'll do a little what if scenario at the board.\par \par \uc2 First, what our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 5 in New York as entertainer Justin Timberlake pleads guilty to impaired driving, resolving the criminal case stemming from his June arrest in New York's Hamptons. The boy band singer turned solo star and actor appeared in Sag Harbor Village Court to enter a new plea to the lesser charge. The judge sentenced Timberlake to a $500 fine with a $260 surcharge and 25 hours of community service.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Boston College. One of the stories there tonight, the prizes for comical scientific achievement called the IG Nobles go to a study that explores the feasibility of using pigeons to guide missiles, and one that looks at the swimming abilities of dead fish. Winners received a transparent box containing historic items related to Murphy's Law the theme of the night and a nearly worthless Zimbabwean $10 trillion bill. There you go, Boston College.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to SPECIAL REPORT. As we get closer and closer to the election, these what if scenarios are getting tighter and tighter as we look at our path to 270 electoral votes, that's what you need to win the White House. These gray states are all the toss up states that we have currently. And there you see 225 to 219.\par \par \uc2 Now, if we take the Real Clear Politics of average -- average of recent polls in all these states, and we just decide that that's how these states are going to go. As of tonight, here's what we're looking at.\par \par \uc2 In Nevada, you're looking at \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  with a win there. However, next door in Arizona, a lead and will give that state to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In Michigan, it would go to Harris. In Wisconsin, that would also go to the vice president. However, down here in Georgia, it would go to former President Trump. And in North Carolina, also to former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Again, with this scenario, you come to the state of Pennsylvania that is tied, and whoever wins Pennsylvania gets to the magic number.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the what if scenario, above 270. You see that it could come down to this state. But the thing that we're factoring in here is that these states are going one way or another, even though the spread in some of these states is only one point or less than one point in the average of recent polls.\par \par \uc2 So, anything can happen. But clearly, the more that we look at this, the tighter it is for the state of Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Let's talk about fracking, because we're here in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Fracking? She's been against it for 12 years.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I will not ban fracking. I have not banned fracking.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : If she won the election the day after that election, they'll go back to destroying our country. And oil will be dead. Fossil fuel will be dead.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Vice President Harris is changing positions specifically on fracking, other issues as well. But fracking could be a difference in energy battleground of Pennsylvania. We just talked about it. Let's talk about it with Doug Schoen, former Clinton adviser, Democratic pollster, and Karl Rove, former deputy chief of staff to President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Karl, we talk about this issue and the flip-flops and trying to get how she has evolved on these policy issues, and the statements that the campaign has put out. But when it comes to energy, when it comes specifically to fracking, I don't think people fully understand what a big deal that is in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FORMER \uc2 GEORGE W. BUSH\uc2  SENIOR ADVISER: They don't. I mean, this has revolutionized, if you will, the oil industry and the natural gas industry in Pennsylvania and in eastern Oregon -- excuse me, eastern Ohio. And it means a lot of jobs. It not only means jobs in the oil patch but it means prosperity for the communities. It means demand for steel, for pipes, a demand for skilled technicians to run these systems.\par \par \uc2 And you know, look, this is -- the issue of fracking has revolutionized the energy industry in the northeast and made for more plentiful lower cost utility bills. So this would be a problem if she were in favor of abolishing fracking.\par \par \uc2 What we do know is, while she claims to have changed on this, she hasn't said anything about the fact that the Biden-Harris administration has clamped down dramatically in leasing on federal lands. We have oil be pumped in the United States today primarily because, in places like Texas, where there is private land, or where there were existing federal leases, fracking has helped us generate more energy from those areas. But you go to across the Texas, New Mexico border, and you will find that in New Mexico there is not as much federal leasing being done and very little leasing being done throughout the entire Rocky Mountain west.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Doug, if you look at the overall race, Real Clear Politics in the nation, she holds a slight lead, again, as we have been talking about. If you look at the swing states and look at the key states, and Pennsylvania one of them, it's 47.6 to 47.6. If you look at Pennsylvania now that Senate race with Casey and McCormick is tight, tighter. Some polls have it within the margin of error.\par \par \uc2 There are a lot of stops by the Democrats in Pennsylvania. \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 , the Pennsylvania governor, is going to be joining those stops this next week. But the topic is abortion on all these stops. They see that as their way to battle in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS SCHOEN, FORMER CLINTON ADVISER: They do. And while I believe the abortion issue has real resonance with suburban swing voters, particularly women in the Philadelphia suburbs, I think what Karl was suggesting is really a quite important. My party should have an all of the above set of policies and recognize explicitly that we need more drilling, we need fracking, we need domestic energy independence. And the only way to do that is to encourage domestic drilling in states like Pennsylvania, and to support not only the fracking industry, but the liquid natural gas.\par \par \uc2 I think Pennsylvania, if I remember correctly, is the second largest energy producer in our country. And what I would recommend is not only to say well, I'm not against it, but I'm for it. And here's my energy policy and here's what I'm committed to doing in terms of drilling. That, to me, is going to do more than just underscoring abortion, an issue that does work for Democrats, but I think has largely provided its benefit in the midterms two years ago. And certainly, most people are aware of the difference between the two parties.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. It's really interesting. The other thing is after this debate, Karl, we saw pundits on both sides and how they felt about it on points and style and everything else. Reuters had this write-up, "Some undecided voters not convinced by Harris after debate with Trump. Reuters interviewed 10 people who were still unsure before they watched the debate. Six afterwards said they would either vote for Trump or were leaning toward backing him. Three now said they would back Harris. One said still unsure how he would vote." Obviously small sample there, but Reuters doing their own interviews post-debate. The question is, you know, how much that debate moves things or if this race is kind of stuck where it is for the moment.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well, I think the race is pretty well stuck. But there will be some movement out of the debate. There have been bigger polls that have talked to hundreds if not over 1,000 people, and by a margin of basically two to one people think that she won it and he lost it. But as I said in my "Wall Street Journal" column Thursday, this is not going to help her as much as she hopes, and it's not going to hurt him as much as some people might fear.\par \par \uc2 But in a race like this, think about this, this number. If you take a look at the Real Clear Politics average and you look at the FiveThirtyEight average of the seven battleground states, in each poll, five of the seven states, the two candidates are separated in the average by less than one point. And so this is going to be -- and none of them, 2.9 percent is the biggest gap in the FiveThirtyEight, and 1.8 percent is the biggest gap in Real Clear Politics. So this is a very close race, so even a small change in public attitudes could have huge consequences by Election Day.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Doug, do you think there's another debate?\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: I don't think there is another debate. I think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  felt pretty clearly he had not done as well as he should have. And what Karl is saying I think is part of the calculus. Since he is not going to lose much, maybe a point or two, but not more. And that could be temporary, transitory. I think his attitude is let me run my campaign. And I think for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , while she is