can are now scheduled to return to earth on a \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  flight next year.\par \par \uc2 A long-term study of the medical problems experienced by Americans stationed in certain overseas locations is now being shut down. The \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2  say it is through investigating so-called Havana syndrome. Chief national security correspondent Jennifer Griffin tells us why from the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: This former CIA officer, who we'll call Adam, is known as Havana syndrome's patient zero, the name given to symptoms experienced by hundreds of CIA officers who have complained of severe sensory phenomena, pressure to the brain that led to vertigo, tinnitus, and cognitive impairment.\par \par \uc2 The intelligence community stated in March of 2023 it could find no evidence linking the alleged attacks to a foreign adversary. Now, in a new twist, the \uc2 NIH\uc2  has abruptly shut down its long-term study of Havana syndrome patients, citing an internal review that found the subjects had been coerced to participate without their informed consent, though the report says not on the part of \uc2 NIH\uc2  researchers. \uc2 NIH\uc2  has stopped the study out of an abundance of cautioned, adding, "In \uc2 NIH\uc2 's assessment, these investigative findings do not impact the conclusions of the study."\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Something was amiss in how it was being handled, how patients were being filtered into there, What was being done on the backend cooperation between \uc2 NIH\uc2  and the CIA.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Adam, who was working undercover in Cuba, first experienced symptoms in December of 2016. He and others believe they were victims of a pulsed energy attack, using weapons on which U.S. and Russian scientists once collaborated. The initial patient showing Havana syndrome symptoms who participated in the \uc2 NIH\uc2  study raised concerns that the CIA was including people who did not fit the profile, resulting in watered down data.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The CIA dictated who would go. \uc2 NIH\uc2  often complained to us behind the scenes that the CIA was not providing adequate matched control groups, and they flooded in a whole litany of people that likely weren't connected or had other medical issues that really muddied the water.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The CIA said its inspector general has been made aware of the finding, quote, "We take any claim of coercion or perceived coercion extremely seriously, and fully cooperated with \uc2 NIH\uc2 's review of this matter and have offered access to any information requested.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): The victims now want the Journal of American Medical Association to retract two articles it published last spring using early data from the \uc2 NIH\uc2  study that concluded there was no significant MRI detectable evidence of brain injury among the group of participants compared with a group of control participants, suggesting at the time it was all in their heads. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon. Jennifer, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel with the latest on the presidential race. First, Beyond our Borders tonight. North Korea provides a rare view into a secretive facility built to enrich uranium for nuclear bombs. This comes as leader Kim Jong-un calls for a rapid expansion of his nuclear weapons program.\par \par \uc2 And the Saudi surgeon performs the world's first robotic heart transplant. The procedure on the teenage patient lasted two-and-a-half hours. It was performed without opening the chest. A small incision was made on the side between the ribs. The boy's father says he is recovering and doing well.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. More news from Washington and around the world after a short break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: When you look at the kind of destruction that's taken place, it's all man-made or woman-made destruction.\par \par \uc2 Our country can be brought back because we are a next in decline.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I get so tired of the other guy talking we are a failing nation. We are the greatest nation in the history of the world.\par \par \uc2 REP. BYRON DONALDS, (R-FL): It's not a campaign of substance. It's a campaign of, I'm just trying to make things better. If she wanted to make things better, what has she been doing?\par \par \uc2 ADRIENNE ELROD, HARRIS-WALZ CAMPAIGN SPOKESWOMAN: She is taking her message directly to the American people. She is traveling the battleground states. She is drawing big crowds.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Traveling battleground states, one of the biggest, and we have been talking about it in this show, is Pennsylvania. She just did a local, this is Vice President Harris, just did a local news station interview in Pennsylvania. We have a piece that just surfaced from that. We'll play that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: When we talk about bringing down prices and making life more affordable for people, what are one or two specific things you have in mind for that?\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'll start with this. I grew up as a middle-class kid. My mother raised my sister and me. She worked very hard. She was able to finally save up enough money to buy our first house when I was a teenager.\par \par \uc2 You know, I grew up in a neighborhood of folks who were very proud of their lawn.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: She goes on to say a couple of specifics about small business and child tax credit, eventually, in that answer, but wanted to play that part.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2 , former Congressman from South Carolina, Leslie Marshall, Democratic strategist, and syndicated radio host \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2 . OK, Hugh, where are we tonight?\par \par \uc2 HUGH HEWITT, SYNDICATED RADIO HOST: That interview is stunning. I was looking forward to Trace Gallagher because the former president looks comfortable. I want specific answers, the Pennsylvania reporter asks, and she gives the same answer she gave at the debate.\par \par \uc2 I honestly don't think she has a thought in her head about anything that's specific. And I must say I think the opinions of the debate are changing as the week goes on. She was wrapped up in bubble wrap by Muir and Linsey Davis and ABC and \uc2 Disney\uc2 . She got no follow-up. She had no specifics. She was not fact checked. It's beginning to dawn on people there is no there there. She had a very bad few days, and that interview, oh my goodness gracious, she does not have a thought in her head.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That interview in its entirety on that station at 11:00 p.m. tonight. Hope to get more from that.\par \par \uc2 Leslie, Kim Strassel in "The Wall Street Journal", to Hugh's point, writes "Kamala's problem the debate didn't fix. Perhaps these interviewees are outliers and the debate was a Harris gamechanger, but wouldn't it be wiser to assume that Americans aren't stupid, that they are unhappy with inflation, crime, and chaos at the border, that they do care about an agenda? Until or unless Ms. Harris campaigns in a way that acknowledges as much, she will struggle against a beatable opponent. The press won't help her by spiking the football."\par \par \uc2 In other words, does she need to do more, even though a lot of pundits said she had a good night?\par \par \uc2 LESLIE MARSHALL, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: She had a great night. I'm sorry that it appeared to you -- I love you, Hugh, but didn't have a thought in her head? I was like, that was a master debate class, and I have been on a debate team. And in addition to that, she was very, very strong.\par \par \uc2 And I do feel she needs more specifics. I have always said that debates don't really move the needle. When you are talking about the needle, this race, when we say, oh, he or she is up in Michigan, or name a state which day or hour that we have a new poll, it's by less than one percent in a state, we have two percent undecided voter. There is not going to be a land landslide for either one of these people. You are talking about, perhaps, a one percent win.\par \par \uc2 But can you win over some of those people, those undecided voters, with more specifics, specifically on the economy? Yes. However, Bret, when you look at the economy, and we have the heist consumer sentiment in months. Would he have the lowest one-year inflation exception since December of 2020. We have the lowest retail gas prices in this year. We have S&P 500 at record numbers and continues to increase, 30-year mortgage rates are low. The U.S. household income is actually higher. So there is a perception of how we are feeling about the economy, and then there is the reality. And I don't think that she should ignore the reality, but then again, she can't ignore their feelings if the reality isn't sinking in to them at home yet.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Trey, you know, listening to these stats about the economy, that is what President Biden tried to do, is that you don't understand, you are actually winning on the economy despite what you feel at home. And that didn't seem to work, which is why "Bidenomics" lost as a term. Where do you think we are?\par \par \uc2 TREY GOWDY\uc2 , FORMER SOUTH CAROLINA REPRESENTATIVE: Telling the jury that they're wrong is never a smart idea. Telling the voters that you really don't feel what you feel, you really don't pay what you are paying, the numbers you see at the gas station really aren't the real numbers, I just wish at some point, Bret, someone would ask her about the next thing that comes out of your mouth. You had three years to do it. Why have you waited? I wish Trump had asked that.\par \par \uc2 I thought he had very uneven performance, and my position hasn't changed. He has good facts, he just didn't press them on Tuesday night. Whatever the next thing that comes out of her mouth is, why hasn't she done it yet?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He did do that in a close. That was the thing that got a lot of attention at the end.\par \par \uc2 Quick, Winners and Losers. Hugh, winner first.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: My winner is Gary Oldman and the "Slow Horses" show. It's the best drama on T.V. Season four opened up. It's wonderful. Loser, ABC, \uc2 Disney\uc2 , David Muir and Linsey Davis. They were off the scale bad, the worst presidential debate in history.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Leslie, winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: Winner, Taylor Swift for bringing over 400,000 people to register to vote with her suggestion. And the loser, anybody who says a woman is too emotional to be president. Kamala was calm and steady Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Trey, winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: Thomas Hammock, the coach of NIU, for proving why we love college football after he beat Notre Dame. The loser, all the voters would like to see an actually well-prepared \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on the debate stage with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  again.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, thank you, panel. Make it a great weekend. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It is Friday. That sound means it is time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: She is Biden. The worst --\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Clearly, I'm not \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And I am certainly not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Wait a minute, I'm talking now. If you don't mind, please. Does that sound familiar?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He clearly is provoked when he's called out for his own record.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: What you will also notice is that people start leaving his rallies early out of exhaustion and boredom.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think he had an opportunity to say how dare you talk about people coming to my rallies who are patriots, and something horrible just happened not long ago.\par \par \uc2 MELANIA TRUMP: The attempt to end my husband's life was a horrible distressing experience. There is definitely more to this story.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think the American people are going to be, shocked, astonished, and appalled by what we will report to them about the failures by the Secret Service.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are the United States of America. We do not bend to terror.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We can truly show the younger generation this is an America worth fighting for.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I believe the Biden-Harris administration's open border policies are responsible for the death of my daughter.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I could have been better. You know, I could have let down my window.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Back at home, we all have a lot of work to do. But from here, earth sure looks like a perfect world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: One week.\par \par \uc2 You are looking live now at the National Mall where a ceremony is about to begin for the new national World War I Memorial. Monday on SPECIAL REPORT, we're going to take a look at another new monument that will be added to the National Mall that honors fallen journalists. We will bring you that exclusively.\par \par \uc2 Please join Shannon Bream for FOX NEWS SUNDAY. Her guests, Pennsylvania Governor Hosh Shapiro, South Carolina Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , and actor Dennis Quaid.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight all this week from New York, from all over. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. Make it a great weekend. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE starts now.\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240914ek9d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Awaiting lawmakers after Secret Service briefing; Secret Service briefs lawmakers on Trump shooting; Melania Trump demands answers on shooting; Soon, V.P. Harris speaks in battleground North Carolina; New shifts in Harris, Trump race with 54 days to go; Trump says he won't take part in another debate; Voters not sold on V.P. Harris after debate vs. Trump; Voters Not Sold On VP Harris After Debate Vs Trump; Ohio City Becomes Center Of Migrant Crisis Debate; Ohio City Struggles W/ Influx Of Haitian Migrants\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, David Spunt, Peter Doocy, Christina Coleman \par \uc2 8,642 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 September 2024\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [15:00:00]\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right. Thank you very much, John and Sandra. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum, and this is The Story.\par \par \uc2 Ohio Governor \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  joins me exclusively this afternoon. He is doing what he can sending state troopers now to the city of Springfield, Ohio, where the small suburb of 50,000 is now dealing with an influx of 15,000 mostly Haitian migrants all entering during the Biden-Harris years. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We've got a lot of friends that have been born and raised here, and we feel like our city's being taken over.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's just a little bit of what you're going to hear from the citizens of Springfield and what they are dealing with.\par \par \uc2 But first on The Story, brand new information on Secret Service failures in the assassination attempt on President Trump. A hearing today with the acting Secret Service chief who says he cannot defend why the roof the gunman fired from was not secured.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He's on the roof, right here. He's right on the roof.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You can hear those people yelling, there's somebody on the roof. Florida Republican Congressman Michael Waltz on the task force, he is standing by.\par \par \uc2 But, first, to Justice Correspondent David Spunt reporting live from Washington. Hi, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Good afternoon. The Secret Service acting director, Ron Rowe, faced some pointed questions today from both the House and Senate. I caught up with the director before briefings with Senators on the internal Secret Service probe into the failed security posture of that day. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RON ROWE, SECRET SERVICE ACTING DIRECTOR: Looking forward to working with Congress to make sure that we never have a tragedy like July 13th again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Now, this was Rowe's first direct meeting with large groups of lawmakers since he publicly testified just a couple of weeks after his former boss, or a couple of days after his former boss, Kimberly Cheadle, resigned at the end of July amid pressure. Rowe promised a full review by the Secret Service of the failures leading up to and on July 13th. Fox News is told the mission assurance investigation, Martha, the internal probe run by the Secret Service looking at failures is near complete and will be presented to Congress and then the public soon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REN. RICHARD BLUMENTHAL (D-CT): We will have a report very soon that I think will absolutely shock the American people, or it should, about the lapses and lags in the protection that was afforded that day and the breakdown in communication, failure and responsibility.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Now, separately, we're learning several high level officials who the Secret Service -- worked at the Secret Service have connections to either direct or indirectly to the Butler security posture, have been encouraged to retire.\par \par \uc2 And there are several reasons behind this, Martha. The first, they have the time in, they're eligible to retire, but also they were encouraged to retire to avoid lengthy congressional interviews and investigations. Both of these are true, we're told. We've been told some of the retirements were planned for later this year, but have been accelerated.\par \par \uc2 Now, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is conducting a separate criminal probe into the shooter. That investigation is still underway. But over the next few weeks, we're going to see a lot more on Capitol Hill about this shooting and this internal investigation, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, this is something we need to understand really well. A former president of the United States was almost killed while the Secret Service was on detail. And we are going to continue to press this. David Spunt, thank you for your work at the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 With that, we bring in a member of the House task force who was at the Secret Service meeting, Florida Republican Michael Waltz, a former Green Beret commander.\par \par \uc2 We heard Senator Blumenthal there. Welcome, Congressman, always good to have you with us. And he said that the American people will be shocked. Can you give us any indication what you're learning about what did and didn't happen here?\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE WALTZ (R-FL): Yes, sure, just a few points, Martha. And, again, the service is finalizing what they call their mission assurance review, and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has a completely different investigation, which, frankly, I think we need to learn a lot more about much more quickly.\par \par \uc2 But on the Secret Service side, a couple of failings that have just jumped out. One, not all local law enforcement were there for the initial coordination meetings. One of the county departments, Beaver County, was not there where Butler County was. The counter-sniper team was not there. They had a representative there, but it's not the same as having the actual counter snipers, and the reason, they were waiting on approvals.\par \par \uc2 And so we still have more to unpack on whether those were normal approval times delayed.\par \par \uc2 [15:05:03]\par \par \uc2 And I still have questions with the ongoing Iranian threat that is not new. This has been ongoing for years. Why he didn't have counter-sniper teams earlier than the rally at Butler, because whenever Secret Service assets are denied, then they have to rely on local law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 We love our local law enforcement. Of course, they're great, but they're not trained to the same level as the \uc2 U.S. Secret Service\uc2  and protective operations. And then the biggest failing I think so far that's come out was the complete breakdown in communication. As different entities, we're seeing the shooter, we're seeing them with the range finder, we're seeing someone on the roof that people were calling into local law enforcement, that wasn't relayed over the common radio net. So, a lot of people are asking, why was President Trump allowed to go on with this suspicious activity? Well, the shift lead, as core protective detail, didn't know. It wasn't relayed to him.\par \par \uc2 What was being relayed was kind of point to point over text message, over phone, and they didn't follow the protocols of get it out there on the net. We have someone suspicious in this area. Everyone be aware. Those are just a few of the things that were coming out in the recent briefing, and I think it's important to let the American people know as soon as we know.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely. This is Melania Trump, the former first lady, who has questions, understandably, about what happened. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELANIA TRUMP, FORMER U.S. FIRST LADY: The attempt to end my husband's life was a horrible, distressing experience. Now, the silence around it feels heavy. I can't help but wonder why didn't law enforcement officials arrest the shooter before the speech? There is definitely more to this story, and we need to uncover the truth.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Don Lemon just posted that video, which we've posted here several times about Melania Trump and her questions. He thinks it's funny. I don't think it's funny. And I think especially with regard to the Iranian threat, which they knew about, they arrested a Pakistani man the day before who was threatening to take out U.S. leaders. And then you have the other Pakistani threat as well around the same timeframe. And this is something that, you know, within -- you and I talked about this -- within hours of the shooting. We were told immediately by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , oh, no connection. No connection at all. They hadn't been, gotten into this guy's social media. They hadn't gotten into anything yet. And yet the one thing they were willing to say right off the bat was that there was no connection.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Yes. Look, these threats are ongoing from Iran. And my question of the Biden administration, what have been the consequences? They are trying to assassinate not only President Trump but members of his cabinet that took the bold action of taking out the Iranian field general, Soleimani. It's unacceptable. The hacking, the assassination attempts, the attacks on Israel, the attacks on our ships, there have been no consequences to Iran and the ayatollahs.\par \par \uc2 I do want to state that President Trump's detail, everyone's detail has been dramatically elevated. That's important to put out there. But also, we have a lot of questions still from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  side of this on the show on the shooter's motives that are still absolutely outstanding.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And we certainly hope that communication has been stepped up. There ought to be one very clear channel between all of these people that says, we have somebody around here that we're worried about and that should shut things down immediately and put the president and the former president in a safe place until they figure out what's going on.\par \par \uc2 Well, thanks for your work on this. I know you've been digging into it and you care about it a lot. Good to see you as always. Chairman Waltz, thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: All right. Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, later this hour, a story exclusive with Republican Governor \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2 , as his state is now spending millions of dollars and using state troopers to try to help one small town with an influx of Haitian migrants that has exploded their population by a third in Springfield.\par \par \uc2 Also joining me, \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chair Michael Whatley on some shifts that we're seeing in some of the battleground states, we'll talk about where the election stands right now from his perspective, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [15:10:00]\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we are watching all the live events. We're going to see President Trump in Arizona a little bit later this afternoon. This is a live shot of Vice President Harris. She is about to speak in North Carolina, which is one of two crucial swing states. There are seven really.\par \par \uc2 And we're taking a look at some of the latest numbers that we're seeing. According to our new Fox power rankings, there has been a bit of a shift in Georgia and North Carolina. According to these rankings, those states were leaning R, but are now in the toss-up category. But I think in many regards, we've seen these states in the toss-up battleground category for some time that tilts the lead in the overall election forecast, according to these numbers to Harris in terms of Electoral College, 241 to 219 in this latest reading.\par \par \uc2 There's going to be many more readings in all of this as we move forward. And you've got about 78 in the toss-up category in terms of electoral votes. We are not seeing yet the impact from the debate. And obviously both sides are out there campaigning a lot.\par \par \uc2 Now, those 78 votes are in six states that are power ranking calls toss-up states. Arizona, where I said the former president is going to be in just an hour and a half, 5:00 Eastern Time, from now. You've got Georgia in that group, Nevada, North Carolina, Pennsylvania, and Wisconsin. So, you are going to see both of these candidates in these states pretty much nonstop over the course of the next, what are we, eight weeks, inside of eight weeks, I guess.\par \par \uc2 [15:15:08]\par \par \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  Chairman Michael Whatley joins me. But, first, let's pop over to White House Correspondent Peter Doocy, who is reporting live from Charlotte, as we await the vice president. Hi, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Martha, good afternoon. We are hearing today that it doesn't sound like Harris officials are in that big of a hurry to get into specifics about her policy proposals. We know we didn't get very many at all at the debate, and that is because they think, at least for now, it might be enough that she's just not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MITCH LANDRIEU, HARRIS-WALZ CAMPAIGN NATIONAL CO-CHAIR: I think that that really is going to be determinative, because at the end of the day, they want to know more about her, but they do understand that it's a choice between \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who's going to --want to go backwards, and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who's going to go forward.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  is reporting this afternoon that Harris advisers see the Trump Harris horse race as unchanged after Tuesday's debate, but the campaign still wants to keep the good vibes they are feeling after the debate going with a new ad that has clipped part of the face-off.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We're a nation that's in serious decline.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , U.S. VICE PRESIDENT, DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: What I do offer is a new generation of leadership for our country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: There are thousands of Harris supporters from the critical battleground state of North Carolina who are pouring in here. They've all been given a wristband like you would get at a concert that light up. There's music, there's a D.J., and something very uncommon in presidential politics, especially as we get into the general. It seems like the vice president is actually running on time. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We know. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  Chairman Michael Whatley is here with me in New York. Michael, good to see you again, thank you for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 So, what do you make of those power rankings to start our conversation sliding Georgia and North Carolina to toss-up from lean R?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL WHATLEY, REPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: We've always known we're going to have seven, eight, ten battleground states that are going to decide this election. It is not surprising to see that those seven, eight, ten states are going to be very close, that they're going to go up and down in the elections on it. We feel very comfortable about where we are in Georgia. We feel very comfortable about where we are in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 What we're seeing in every single one of these battleground states is that on this same day in 2024, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is doing better than he was on the same day in 2020 or 2016. So, we feel very comfortable about the trajectory of this race and the direction of this race.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I think that's so important to point out, because I covered both of those elections, and in 2016 Hillary Clinton was ahead, I think, maybe four or five points. I mean, it was something in that neighborhood, and Biden was ahead in 2020 around this point. So, we have never seen an election that looks this tight at this stage of the game.\par \par \uc2 So, in those states, you know, what is it going to look like? And your effort, one of your biggest efforts is to get out the vote. And that is really so crucial when you think about 44,000 votes dividing these two candidates in the last round.\par \par \uc2 WHATLEY: Yes. What we're trying to do is make sure that we're talking to every single voter that we can get them up and we can get them out, but it's not enough just to have our base voters vote. What we want to do is activate them to go out into their communities, go out and talk to friends and family members and colleagues and co-workers and tell them about the importance of the race and talk to them about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And that way we can motivate even more people to kind of come up and get out.\par \par \uc2 We also are focusing very hard on making sure that we get our low propensity voters, people who will vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  but they necessarily haven't necessarily voted for the last two years or four years or six years. We need to dynamite them off the couch. So, we have a full tilt effort right now in the field.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Give us a sense of what that looks like. I mean, you know, how is it different? How is that effort to get out the vote, because it was such a crucial issue in 2020? Obviously that was COVID. What does it actually look like? How many people do you have doing this? Are they going to people's houses? How are you activating, as you say?\par \par \uc2 WHATLEY: Yes, we are. We have people that are going and knocking on doors. We have people who are making phone calls. We're sending text messages. We're sending digital messages.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Is it a bigger effort than it was last time, in terms of people?\par \par \uc2 WHATLEY: It is a more focused effort than we have ever had, making sure that we're going out and we're getting those low propensity voters.\par \par \uc2 And then the other thing that we're seeing is a huge shift in black voters, Hispanic voters, Asian-American voters are supporting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  versus where they did in 2020. Because you know, right now the economy is hurting them and their families in a big way. And so we're seeing as prices go up, as grocery prices and housing prices, gasoline prices go up, those families are being hurt and they're leaving the Democratic Party because they know \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is the one who cast the tie breaking votes on $2.5 trillion worth of inflationary spending that have sent all of those prices higher and she's not put any solutions on the table at all to be able to help them going forward.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I think Michigan did not take RFK Jr. off the ballot, right? How much of a concern is that? I know he's putting out videos saying, even if I'm on the ballot, please don't vote for me.\par \par \uc2 [15:20:04]\par \par \uc2 That's tricky.\par \par \uc2 WHATLEY: It is. Look, RFK Jr. has been fantastic. He's a great partner and we're really excited to have him on board. \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2  and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and others, you know, we are seeing a lot of people leaving the Democratic Party and supporting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We are seeing in every single one of these polls, the number of people voting for other is continuing to go down as people are picking sides. And we are seeing the vast majority of those who were with RFK Jr. are coming over to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Michael Whatley, \uc2 RNC\uc2  chair, co-chair with \uc2 Lara Trump\uc2 , great to see you. You got to get busy a few weeks ahead of you and we're going to be watching every step of the way. Thank you very much. Good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 So, a blistering critique of ABC from \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2 , who says the debate was, quote, so in the tank for Harris, it was repulsive, according to Hewitt. He joins our political panel with Juan Williams, next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TAMARA, WASHINGTON, D.C. RESIDENT: Kamala was really baiting Trump, and she spent the majority of the time doing that, not covering some of the issues. She kept stating that she was here for the people, but I didn't necessarily feel that last night.\par \par \uc2 ALEX, CALIFORNIA RESIDENT: What stood out, maybe not moments, but clearly the moderators were pretty biased towards against Trump. So, that stood out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, some news breaking right now. Former President Trump says he will not take part in another debate.\par \par \uc2 [15:25:04]\par \par \uc2 Posting just moments ago on Truth Social, quote, polls clearly show that I won the debate against Comrade \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , adding, she was a no show at the Fox debate. He's referring to the September 4th debate that was proposed and accepted on the Trump side and Harris turned that down.\par \par \uc2 He goes on to say that she refused to do NBC and \uc2 CBS\uc2 . Kamala should focus on what she should have done during the last four-year period. There will be no third debate, says former President Trump this afternoon, just moments ago. And as I mentioned, he is on his way to Arizona for a campaign event at 5:00.\par \par \uc2 So, with that, let's bring in \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2 , nationally syndicated radio show host who has moderated presidential debates very well and calls this, quote, one of the worst moderated and most biased in U.S. history, and Juan Williams, Fox News senior political analyst. Good to have both of you with us. Thank you very much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 So, let's start there. Hugh, your reaction to what the president has had to say just now.\par \par \uc2 HUGH HEWITT, NATIONALLY SYNDICATED RADIO HOST: I am not surprised, Martha, and thank you for having me on. I think he knows that he is in the process of winning the debate on Tuesday night, and I want to explain that. \uc2 NFL\uc2  fans are familiar with the phrase, upon further review, the officials have reversed the call on the field due to overwhelming evidence. Well, upon further review, the American public has decided that debate was rigged. It was absolutely in the tank by ABC and \uc2 Disney\uc2  to hurt the former president and to help \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And the way I explain this to people, and it doesn't need much for one and we're of this age, but if you're under 62, it won't necessarily make sense. In 1972, the Soviets won the gold medal against the American basketball team in the Olympics. But that's only because the officials and the referees insisted that they win. \uc2 Doug Collins\uc2 , Jim Brewer, they can tell you the whole story, but it was rigged. And when you go back and look at the deep, deep bias that manifested itself, it was as though lawfare had become mediafare.\par \par \uc2 And that \uc2 Disney\uc2 , one example, not one question on China, is \uc2 Disney\uc2  worried about their theme parks? So, only in the extent that the Soviets won in 1972 did Kamala win on Tuesday night. I think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is reading internals, reading their polls and realizing the American people are disgusted with ABC. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  did not answer one question directly and they're moving towards it. He's winning.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Juan, it was so frustrating to watch her be asked the question that is on so many people's minds right at the top. Are you better off now than you were four years ago? And you know, it was an opportunity to say, look, you know, inflation is coming down, whatever her answer would have been, but she launched into a biographical answer and then she just segued right off of it.\par \par \uc2 And how do you sit there as, you know, two journalists who are in the moderator role and not say, wait, hold on, you didn't answer the question. Can you please go back, Vice President, and answer this question? I mean, you would have done that.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes, I think I would have, and I think Hugh or you would have done it. But let me just say, when you're coming into this kind of situation, into a debate, and you say, I think ABC's going to be biased, and here I'm really speaking for former President Trump. He said before the debate, I think ABC's going to be biased. He doesn't think much of David Muir. And then you don't prepare and you don't raise questions yourself. I mean, he had the microphone in front of him. He could have said, what about China, to Hugh's point, or he could have said, you know, I think that there are other important issues to be discussed here, so let's go back to are you better off than you were four years. He didn't do it. He looked unprepared, and I think that's why you're seeing even conservative pundits say, you know what, he lost that debate.\par \par \uc2 Now, people may want to blame the moderators and look for ulterior motives on the part of \uc2 Disney\uc2 , but the fact is he didn't perform well, and he didn't come across well to, you know, 60-plus million viewers who watched.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let's take a look at Lee Carter. You know, it's interesting when you look at her dials and the independent voters that she was talking to. I found this interesting that night, because on a lot of Trump's answers, Hugh, and that's why you're talking about the days to come in terms of the evaluation of the debate, it looked like independent voters were approving his answers along with Republicans, and they were separating from the blue line on the bottom. Watch what she said, or watch what they said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I feel like I just need to hear more. I feel like Kamala did better than I expected because I feel like she more so answered the questions directly as opposed to Trump. Don't get me wrong. She did deflect, but I feel like she did less often.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I like the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's closing statement too basically saying like, she's saying everything we want to hear, but if that's the case, why you didn't do all that stuff in the last four years?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [15:30:03]\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, that was a strong closing at the end, for sure, and it's something that I know is getting shared a lot on social media, why didn't you do all that stuff in the last four years? Hugh, your final thought here before we go.\par \par \uc2 HUGH HEWITT, NATIONALLY SYNDICATED RADIO HOST: Enormous tidal wave of reaction continues to come in. A debate isn't over in a day. There was an enormous audience, but there's an enormous reaction to the face plant by ABC that's being factored in. It's all working in the favor of the former President because people don't like cheating, and they view that debate as cheating and yes, he did prepare for media bias. But this was on a scale we have never seen before. Every question, every answer, five, six fact checks of Trump, none of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Weird, very weird.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: She did -- she did bring attention to Springfield. Very weird.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah, very -- it was uncomfortable in that regard, for sure. Thank you both, Juan Williams for being here. \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2 , thank you for being here. Great to see you both.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You bet we'll see you soon. So coming up, a story exclusive with Ohio Governor \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  on the new action that he is taking to help Springfield, Ohio deal with a very large migrant influx at the center of a national controversy, one town story he's trying to help next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What you have been hearing a lot in the past few days about the City of Springfield, Ohio. It's a small city about 50, 60,000 people. But this town has now become a hotbed of debate over the migrant crisis because, as I said, they have 50 to 60,000 people that live there. But imagine this, now they have 15,000 Haitian migrants over the course of the Biden-Harris administration have moved to their town. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIELD MALE: Is there a cut-off point that I mean for the population here, or, I mean, I feel like there should be a no vacancy sign, Things are going to get ugly.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIELD MALE: If the Haitians, as everybody wants to talk about, wants to come here and better their self, it's all well to do.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm scared. I'm scared for the women here. I'm scared for my granddaughters.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Ohio Republican Governor \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  is sending state law enforcement now to help out in Springfield, along with a two-and-a-half million dollar package that but he wants the Biden-Harris Administration to do more. He joins us exclusively in moments, but first, let's get the back story from Christina Coleman, who is on the ground in Springfield, Ohio, right now. Christina,\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA COLEMAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha, yes, you know, over the last four years, at least 15,000 Haitians have come to this small city here in Springfield. And this area just doesn't have the infrastructure to absorb such a quick, rapid, and large population boost. They're really struggling. They need more resources and I'm talking about medical care. You know, they need more school resources. They need more translation services. It's just added to the needs that this city already has. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You're asking for more money now for immigrants and our people, our citizens are living on the streets. They're not being taken care of. If you were -- if you're -- if you're going to -- when -- while you're asking for more money include our homeless people. Include -- there are veterans that are on the streets.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Yeah, that's a big issue, and it's going to get colder here. Winter is coming. Now, all of these issues were addressed in a heated commission meeting full of folks frustrated over this situation. Again, as I mentioned, Springfield needs a lot more resources, even driver education classes. \uc2 Ohio State Highway Patrol\uc2  has been dispatched here to help local police with traffic issues since some Haitians here are apparently still learning U.S. traffic laws. Just last year, an unlicensed Haitian hit a school bus in this community injuring at least two dozen students and killing an 11-year-old boy, drawing further attention to the reckless driving problem in this area amid this population boost.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIELD MALE: This issue is not a Republican or Democratic issue. This is an American issue. This is an Ohio issue. We, the people, have the ability to ensure our streets are safe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: And I should note, yes, it's an Ohio issue. This was also a concern in Denver. You know, you have that migrant surge there, the hospital system there needing more resources. You're seeing this with border towns down south there in Texas, in Eagle Pass, in many areas. So this, this city, Springfield highlights this growing problem when you have this boost in immigrants in all of these cities. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Christina, thank you very much. With that, we bring in Ohio Republican Governor \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2 . Governor, you know, I'm listening to these individuals, and the man who says, you know, we have to put up a no vacancy sign. We can't absorb any more people. And I think the underlying thought there is, it's not, you know, that people don't want to help people. It's just that you're -- all of your services are so overwhelmed. How did this happen? How did this happen to Springfield, Ohio? What's your explanation?\par \par \uc2 MIKE DEWINE\uc2 , (R) OHIO GOVERNOR: Well, Martha, you're absolutely right. I think people are used to and expect some immigrants to come in, but you gave the numbers. This is a community of 58,000, the last census, 15,000 people came in on top of that, so that's a dramatic change, whether they came from, Haiti, or wherever they came from. And look, why did they come? They came for jobs. When you talk to the employers who are employing them, they'll tell you, look, they're very hard workers. They're very family oriented. One employer was quoted to saying, I wish I could hire 100 other ones to fill the jobs that we have. So that's why,-- why they came. But I don't think anybody expected that.\par \par \uc2 You know, that big of a surge. I mean basically what happens is, you know, they are here under a classification. There are 16 countries under this classification and when they come in, you know, they can then apply for that. And what -- the reason Haiti is in there is because it's an extremely violent country and like a country that since the President was killed two years ago, the gangs are basically running the country. So, you know, that's why they fled Haiti, and that's, that's why they are here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you support that program and you support TPS, right?\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: Well -- yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The protective look --\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: Sure. So we have -- we have -- Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: Yes. Martha, we've always had some program -- we've always had some program like that, and remember? Well, you don't remember, but I remember way, way back. You know, in the 50s and 60s, we did that with Cuba when Castro was there. So there's nothing wrong with us being welcoming. But I think the Federal Government has an obligation.\par \par \uc2 You know, if you look at the people of Springfield, they had nothing to do with this decision. No one in the -- Ohio had anything to do with it. Basically, these people will come in and they are given papers, and then they can go wherever they want to. And what has happened is has happened many times with immigrants that go where there are other people from that country but this has been dramatic.\par \par \uc2 My point is the Federal Government needs to give Springfield some help. They need to give Ohio some help. You know, your reporter pointed out very well the problems. We have problems with primary care. They come -- you have 15,000 people who came from a country that does not have -- has virtually no medical care. In the last two years, it's gotten even worse as the doctors have fled Haiti. So we have to make sure that these people are immunized, that the children are immunized. We have to make sure that it's -- you know everyone in first that they have that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But how does this -- I guess the question is -- well, I understand the burden that this puts on you, and I know you have a -- you and your wife have a charity in Haiti in the name of your daughter who was killed in a car accident and that's good work. There's no doubt about that. But you have supported this -- this program of temporary protect, protection for different countries. But whose fault is it? Do you blame the Biden-Harris Administration for the fact that you are now dealing with something that is taking away the livelihood and the safety of people who live in Springfield, Ohio for generations?\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: Yes. Yes, Martha, I don't necessarily support the specifics of all the program. What I said is the concept and I also want to be very honest, the concept of us helping people in countries where the people risking their lives just even staying there and we've always been able to do that. But what I object to is there was no plan to go with it.\par \par \uc2 You know, we have, for example, people who are called refugees, and the government, if they place them somewhere, they put the support in there. They'd have a plan. The problem here is there is absolutely no plan at all when these people came in. And yes, I certainly blame the administration. They -- they need to have a plan. They need to develop that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So what are they doing for you? Have you been on the phone with -- I'm sorry to interrupt.\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: Yes. Sure.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Have you called President Biden and talked to him about what's going on in Springfield?\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: Look, I've talked -- look, look, I've talked to the Whitehouse. I've made it very clear the situation we are in. I told them we are looking for some Federal help. I told them that we will continue to look at the different streams of Federal dollars that are coming in to see if we can qualify, but we would like some help. They have said, we will try to help you. And so we're in a conversation back and forth.\par \par \uc2 My point, though, is a Governor shouldn't have to do this, a mayor shouldn't have to do this. There should be a program if you know -- this is a decision but was made -- that's made at the Whitehouse level about how many countries are on this list, who is let in how all this happens. And so we really need an overall plan and this won't be the last place.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No.\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: You know, Springfield, Ohio --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, we see what's happening in Aurora, Colorado as well. And I watched, you know, stories about this on other channels. I have, you know, all the different networks on, and I'm watching what everyone's saying in my workspace here. And you know, they're saying that it's all a big lie, that there's nothing wrong in Springfield, that nothing you know, that everything is okay. Do you agree with that?\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: Oh, absolutely not. No. Look, you know, this is a massive increase in population, and whether they're from Haiti or wherever they're from, there would be huge challenges. There's more challenges, frankly, because it comes from a country that does not have medical care. It also comes from a country where the driving habits, the driving laws are fundamentally different. So one of the things that we are doing is to try we brought in, as you said, the Highway Patrol. They've been in now for the third day, to run traffic so the local police can go about their normal business, and we can handle, you know, a lot of a lot of the traffic. So look, we're going to do what we need to do, but the Federal government --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Are they -- are these Haitian migrants allowed to drive vehicles in this town? Are they allowed to get driver's licenses?\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: The law in Ohio says that if you are 18 years of age, and you are here -- here legally which they are.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Under this program, yes.\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: That you -- yes, under this program, that you can get a driver's license. The problem is that once you're 18 in Ohio, there's no requirement that you have to have any drivers training.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, what do you want the National Guard members -- you're sending the National Guard, what do you want them to do? What's their mandate?\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: I'm not sending the guard in. I'm sending the patrol in. The patrols mandate is to run traffic and that's what they do.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right.\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: We don't have a state police in Ohio but we have people -- the Highway Patrol are very, very good and they run traffic again. Again, we want to give people in Springfield some assurance that we're watching the roads because there's been reports of erratic driving, and that is just not, not acceptable Springfield, or if I live there, you'd want somebody to come in and take care of that problem.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, absolutely Governor. And I really hope you get some response from the Whitehouse. You know, they say that this can only be handled in Congress. And we know how much power the Whitehouse has when it comes to issues of migration, their powers are clear. Thank you very much. Governor DeWine, good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: Thank you. Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So this was very exciting this morning, a new frontier for space exploration after this history making private spacewalk. The first time ever a private citizen in space on \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , 435 miles above Earth. At one point they were almost twice that high. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Commander Jared Isaacmen now emerging.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, really cool to see one of our out there. That's awesome.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And it's incredible. Veteran \uc2 NASA\uc2  Astronaut \uc2 Tom Jones\uc2 , who served on the Advisory Committee for this joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. As we continue to cover both sides of this race. Right now, you have the Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in Charlotte, North Carolina. Obviously one of the battleground states. It went to Trump in 2020 and 2016. It is now a toss-up on the Fox Power Rankings. Let's listen to a little bit of this then we'll go to Arizona in a little while to take President Trump's speech.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: We got a lot of work to do. We got a lot work to do. Okay. So North Carolina, you probably know, and please have a seat, if you have a chair. Two nights ago, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and I had our first debate. And I believe we owe it to the voters to have another debate because this election and what is at stake could not be more important.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday night, I talked about issues that I know matter to families across America, like bringing down the cost of living, investing in America's small businesses, protecting reproductive freedom and keeping -- and keeping our nation safe and secure. But that's not what we heard from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Instead, it was the same old show, that same tired playbook that we've heard for years, with no plans for how he would address the needs of the American people, because, you know, it's all about him. It's not about you.\par \par \uc2 Well, folks, I said it then