e're not going back to Trump, because things are so good right now.\par \par \uc2 But if things are so good right now, then why does the new banner at the Harris rally say "A New Way Forward"? It would stand to reason that if the status quo was something that could win an election, you don't need a new way forward. It would just be forward -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's a very good point.\par \par \uc2 Peter, were you getting from the crowd -- I mean, they're obviously seeing this renewed energy in the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  camp since the debate. And she seems to have found her footing here. But, again, some of these polls post the debate, they still show this very much a tight race.\par \par \uc2 And I understand really conclusive ones could still be a day or so off. But the fact of the matter is, it is still a close race. It might not have moved the needle. It's certainly emboldened her and her supporters. What's your take from just that room?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: From just this room, everybody that was here, several thousand -- we don't have a count from the fire marshal yet, but several thousand people were all very engaged and they were very excited to get a chance to express themselves and their excitement about how she did at the debate.\par \par \uc2 But in terms of the polls -- so there was a Quinnipiac North Carolina poll. This is a state that Trump won the last two general elections. But before the debate, a Quinnipiac polls show that Harris is up three, 50-47.\par \par \uc2 The thing is, does the debate, a good performance for her in the debate increase the margin because she was seen as having a successful night, or are there all these persuadable battleground state voters like we saw in some of the focus groups who do not think that they heard enough specifics from her?\par \par \uc2 And Mitch Landrieu, who's part of the campaign board in Wilmington, he was saying in an interview today that it essentially it doesn't sound like they are in a big 