ally, that he's going to talk about that decision that there will be no third debate.\par \par \uc2 However, obviously, there will still be events. Again, the economy, the high cost of housing he's expected to talk about. And one thing that we have heard him talk about a little bit more and going to expand on it today is illegal immigration and how it ties into all of it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (R) AND CURRENT U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: The first thing I do is close up the border. People have to come into our country illegally. They can't come in the way they're doing now. We have millions and millions of people coming in, even a month -- on a monthly basis, and they're destroying our country and it affects our economy.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: So, Tucson, Neil, is about an hour's drive from the Border Patrol's busiest sector, where about a third of all encounters on the southern border happen.\par \par \uc2 Looking at the economy here, according to the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , Arizona's unemployment rate in July was 3.4 percent. That's better than the nationwide rate of 4.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 Both candidates need independents to win this battleground state. They make up about a third of the electorate here. This is Trump's third visit to Arizona since \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  stepped aside in July. J.D. Vance has made three stops. Harris has been here once in that time frame, and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  campaigned in the Phoenix area on debate day.\par \par \uc2 FOX Digital reports the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  had planned to troll the former president here in Tucson with a newly released campaign video to be played on a mobile billboard just outside this event center. The topic, her debate comments on his rallies.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: What you will also notice is that people start leaving his rallies early out of exhaustion and boredom, and I will tell you, the one thing you will not hear him talk about is you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: So, Neil, this is a music hall in Tucson, Arizona. It seats about 2,200 people here, as always, long lines this morning, and they're still getting people seated for him to come to the mic here in just a little bit -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Alicia, I don't want to put you on the spot there. I know it's very loud, very noisy, but do we know what motivated the former president to say, all right, no additional debates, I'm done, that's it?\par \par \uc2 Are we aware of how that all went down?\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: Of how he made the decision -- I'm sorry, Neil, I can barely hear you, but I think you're asking me...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I understand.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: ... how he decided that he was not going to go onto the third -- onto another debate?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: It's -- OK, yes.\par \par \uc2 No, we don't have the details on that. He really laid it out in that TRUTH Social post that you read at the top of the show after the vice president was done speaking. He laid out he feels like he won. He's citing polls, as you mentioned, that are maybe from online sources that we haven't actually backed up as well.\par \par \uc2 But we will likely hear more. And we in the past, I can tell you, in talking to the campaign and asking folks on the ground here, what is the former vice president going to do in this situation, just name one, A, B, or C, and the answer is often, well, whatever he decides.\par \par \uc2 So if he decides to talk in detail when he steps to the mic here, none of us will really be surprised.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Alicia, thank you very much. And sorry to put you on the spot there with all that noise. I should have thought better of that.\par \par \uc2 Alicia Acuna there in Tucson, Arizona.\par \par \uc2 When the former president speaks, we will, of course, take you to Arizona live.\par \par \uc2 By the way, The Wall Street Journal is reporting when asked about whether \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was in fact ruling out a debate and they weren't playing games here, a spokeswoman simply said in an e-mail: "You heard the man. He's not debating."\par \par \uc2 All right, Doug Schoen, the former Clinton adviser, a Democratic strategist and pollster, Noelle Nikpour, a GOP fund-raiser.\par \par \uc2 Noelle, if you don't mind, I'd like to begin with you first and the fallout. I mean, you talk to big donors in the Republican Party. You're well-connected to those in the Republican Party. So, from a political standpoint, is it better in their mind that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  not debate again, that that performance he had with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was such that it might not be such a good idea?\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 NOELLE NIKPOUR, REPUBLICAN FUND-RAISER: A hundred percent on that. I think a lot of the donors, or at least the ones that I was texting with the night of the debate, were not exactly thrilled about how that debate went down.\par \par \uc2 What I will tell you, I do think it's smart for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  not to do the debate. I think that that was a good decision with the campaign. And I will tell you why, because depending on when that debate will be scheduled, and if he has another repeat performance like he did, I don't know if the campaign can recover that quickly.\par \par \uc2 I don't think they have enough time to recover from that. So you have got to keep in mind we have got 54 days before the election. And if there is another -- by the time the debate is set, by the time it happens, you have got to have ads ready. You have got to be able to respond to anything that would be negative.\par \par \uc2 So I think due to the timing, the debate, not doing it, I think, was a wise decision.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Doug, you have reminded me, because you're such a good historian as well on these debates and having coached and worked with candidates in the past on them, that we sometimes give them sort of undue importance and vitality.\par \par \uc2 And maybe with the exception of John F. Kennedy against \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  in that opening debate -- they had four -- it doesn't really change the needle much. I'm thinking maybe with \uc2 Gerald Ford\uc2  and \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  and, of course, President Ford at the time saying that Eastern Europe wasn't under communist rule, it can come back to smack you too.\par \par \uc2 But, by and large they don't make a difference, by and large. How do you see it?\par \par \uc2 DOUG SCHOEN, FORMER BILL CLINTON POLLSTER: And that's right, Neil.\par \par \uc2 I think, tops, this debate will temporarily get \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  an extra point or two, maybe three, but it'll be temporary. And Trump doesn't need - - excuse me -- another debate. He needs to campaign. And since he didn't do well -- Noelle was right -- there's no reason to debate.\par \par \uc2 And I think he wants to go run his campaign and demonstrate, as he did in 2016 and 2020, that he's a great closer, and he closed not with debate performance, but he closed with his rallies and his on-the-ground performance, where he exceeded the final polls.\par \par \uc2 So I think his campaign feels their way ahead of the game, that they have had so far only a narrow slippage. And, bottom line, you're not going to see him on the debate stage. It's the right decision for him. And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will goad him. I don't think it's going to work.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm reading -- of course, when you hear from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , guys and what he says about this, all the polls show that I won that debate, it's simply fair to say that consensus shows that she got the better of him.\par \par \uc2 Again, people can have mixed views on this. I understand that.\par \par \uc2 But, Noelle, well, one of the things I have discovered, that even some of the former president's closest allies have been saying, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , he could have done better. I'm paraphrasing here. But the most, I think, startling to me came from Robert F. Kennedy, Jr., who was a guest on this show.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, he is supporting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and might have a role in the Trump administration. I want you to react to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR\uc2 ., FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Vice President Harris clearly won the debate in terms of her delivery, her polish, her organization, and her preparation.\par \par \uc2 I think, on the substance, President Trump wins in terms of his governance. I would suspect that the polling over the next week is going to show probably a slight drop in his support, particularly among independents.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, and he's a key supporter, as you know, Noelle.\par \par \uc2 And that we're told that a number of Trump confidants were sort of being blunt and honest to say, all right, you brought your A-game when you when you debated \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . This wasn't your A-game. And that might be among the more polite critiques that he's had.\par \par \uc2 Do you think he bounced this decision or some of those same confidants or that he just went into this on his own?\par \par \uc2 NIKPOUR: Well, if you're talking about RFK and what he said, I don't think he will have a role anymore going forward if this is what he came out and said that, that he thought that he lost the debate, it wasn't his best debate and that he may have lost independent votes.\par \par \uc2 That is not the Trump campaign spin. The Trump campaign spin is that basically it was three against one, that the moderators and Ms. Harris were totally against him, they had it out for him, and that if he asked everybody, looking at everything that he has, that he's totally came out ahead and he won the debate, and why that she wants another debate, because she felt like she lost and he won.\par \par \uc2 That is the Trump spin. Trump never admits anything negative. Trump is always going to say that he came out the winner, no matter what the situation came out, and he is the winner. And this is how Trump and the Trump campaign spins it. So the sheer fact that somebody is a surrogate and somebody is kind of part of the Trump campaign camp, meaning RFK Jr., saying that he did not win, I would not look for RFK Jr. to have any role if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wins.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And whether he's listening to those confidants as well and others, even on this decision, Doug Schoen, not to debate -- now, he did bring up something that others have, that the moderators might not have treated him fairly, that he was constantly fact-checked, and that certainly \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was not on a couple of key things, including \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and whether he had support for this Project 2025.\par \par \uc2 He's continuously disavowed that. And also his views on in vitro fertilization. Far from separating himself from it, he's proposed funding it for couples. Even the fact that there are no American troops in combat zones overseas. Not a one was second-guessed by the moderators.\par \par \uc2 So does he have a point, or does that even matter at this point?\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Yes, he does have a point.\par \par \uc2 And that would enter, Neil, into his decision not to debate anymore, because he knows \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is not going to come on FOX News. She wants to go mainstream media. And he can't take that risk that three on one. And he does have a point. It's a fair point.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, guys, I want to thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Again, we will be hearing from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He will be speaking in Arizona shortly, in the next half-hour or so. And he might elaborate a little what was behind that decision to say no to another debate with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, keeping you up on this storm that's really now a tropical depression, but packing still very heavy winds, very unpredictable, very erratic. I'm talking about Francine and where she's headed next -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Whoa.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the first private space walk we have ever seen in the history of space travel. Everyone on board that capsule a private citizen, a couple of them very rich.\par \par \uc2 The impact of what you're seeing -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, sometimes people get relieved when they hear that a hurricane has been downgraded to a tropical depression. In the case of Francine, you might want to hold the phone on that.\par \par \uc2 Jane Minar, FOX Weather correspondent, has more from Louisiana -- Jane.\par \par \uc2 JANE MINAR, FOX WEATHER METEOROLOGIST: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, here we are in Mandeville, north of Lake Pontchartrain, and flooding pretty significant. Overnight, we had flash flood warnings that stretched not only on the north side of the lake, but flash flood emergencies in New Orleans.\par \par \uc2 We had about a good nine inches of rain last night. And this is the result. Lakeshore Drive here completely flooded out. Some of that water beginning to recede, as you can see off into the distance, deep enough still that not only do we have kayakers. We have a group of kids on their day off from school doing some swimming in that nearly waist-deep water.\par \par \uc2 If it were me, I probably wouldn't be getting in these waters. I have already seen a couple critters too close for comfort. Now, as far as this storm, Francine, you're right, downgraded tropical depression, but, boy, did it come into the fury, Category 2 hurricane making landfall down there in Terrebonne Parish.\par \par \uc2 We had 100-mile-power wind gusts, 70-mile-power wind gusts clocked here in New Orleans. We are still in cleanup mode. We just saw power crews that were able to get through some of these streets. You can see, now that water is beginning to recede, they're able to get down these roads and restore power to about the 15,000 customers here in St. Tammany Parish that are within the dark.\par \par \uc2 But we still have hundreds of thousands of customers without power across parts of Southern Louisiana. So we have a long way to go. Neil, it's hot. It's Southern Louisiana. The sun is shining. It is bright blue skies. We have got some debris scattered about across the park here.\par \par \uc2 For Lake Pontchartrain, we do have coastal flood advisories through this evening for some minor flooding to continue. But, overall, this is just our recovery mode as we go through the end of this week.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, it could have been a lot worse, to your point though, Jane. Thank you very much. Great coverage.\par \par \uc2 Jane Minar on all of that.\par \par \uc2 Steve Bender, FOX Weather meteorologist, on what we're looking at here and how far this goes.\par \par \uc2 What do you think, Steve?\par \par \uc2 STEVE BENDER, FOX WEATHER METEOROLOGIST: Neil, it's going to last through today and tomorrow and then congeal with something over the weekend. We will get to that.\par \par \uc2 This just coming in, by the way, 4:00 p.m. advisory, now post-tropical Francine. The impacts don't change, though. The rain doesn't instantly evaporate. It's still there. To Jane's point, yes, we have over 392,000 people without power.\par \par \uc2 Louisiana took the punch last night and they're seeing the worst with the power outages. Today, it's that tornadic risk through Central Alabama, Montgomery, Birmingham. And it's all because the winds out of the surface, they're coming out of the south. Then you look at winds aloft 10,000 feet. They're coming out of the west. All that means is wind shear creates that vorticity.\par \par \uc2 Notice with that some dynamic showers lasting all the way through the overnight hours. Now, this is going to be something that hopefully will clear out for areas along the coast. That's a good sign for areas like Mandeville, where Jane's at, or even out towards New Orleans, down even further south.\par \par \uc2 Rain still left in this system. This is going to be locked in over Memphis towards Jonesboro. Notice this. This is showing you that fragmented nature where the severe storms, not only tornadic conditions, but also heavy rain for the hill country of Northern Alabama.\par \par \uc2 This is what I was talking about, Neil, though, another area to watch in the Southeast over the weekend, a lot of that energy and instability impacting areas from the Carolinas down to North Florida, like Daytona beach.\par \par \uc2 So Atlantic hurricane season, it's coming to life.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So Steve, let me ask you. Everyone going into this said it was going to be a rough storm season.\par \par \uc2 BENDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I know you're explaining about the sand and the air and all this stuff that goes way over my head. You're brilliant. I barely get it.\par \par \uc2 But what's the situation conditions-wide now in the Atlantic and elsewhere as the season really picks up, presumably, steam?\par \par \uc2 BENDER: Yes, it's a great question.\par \par \uc2 Not only was it the Sahara dust, but it was all that we call it the ITCZ. It's that wave of monsoonal trough. It was so far up into Africa, the Sahara Desert, the driest place on our planet, was getting rain. Well, that's come back down south, so now it's feeding these storms again.\par \par \uc2 And then also late in the season, Neil, we don't look deep out in the Atlantic for these storms to turn up. We will see them out in the Western Atlantic, so near our East Coast, the Gulf and the Caribbean. That happens in late September and October and those conditions are favorable and so we could see these storms pick up.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, man, you're good. I appreciate that, Steve. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 You notice how nicely he answers an idiotic question? He just says, no, knucklehead.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, by the way, he's part of a team that's phenomenal. And, believe me, I have sampled a lot of these weather apps and all because I love to follow weather, but the FOX Weather app is really important, not only the hurricane headquarters and you get all the updates on the latest storms, but you can stream it for free at FOXWeather.TV.\par \par \uc2 I highly recommend it, because they get into stuff and have graphics that will just blow you away. No pun intended. But it really, really is incredible stuff.\par \par \uc2 All right, talk about incredible right now. You might have been noticing we have a bit of inflation problem in this country. Now, the administration, of course, has been saying it's not as much of a problem. If that's the case, then why is \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's continuing with a $5 value meal? It seems to acknowledge the obvious.\par \par \uc2 Yes, there is a problem. People won't come in to pay a lot unless you force the issue -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, if you price it right, they will come.\par \par \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's finding out, started out the hard way, now the good way with these $5 value meals. They were thought to be a money loser for them, but they're proving to be extremely popular. So that brings people into the stores and the restaurants, continuing this $5 value meal at most of the locations, right through the end of the year, because times are tight and prices are high, and they're trying to do something about it.\par \par \uc2 Gary Kaltbaum not at all surprised, a great read of the markets and \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's at the same time, here to give us sort of like the beef.\par \par \uc2 So what do you make of it, Gary? I mean, obviously it's working for McDonald's, and I think even they were surprised how much it's working.\par \par \uc2 GARY KALTBAUM, FOX BUSINESS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, look, they have to attract consumers that are price-conscious now.\par \par \uc2 But, Neil, it's also because \uc2 Burger King\uc2  and Wendy's and Steak 'n Shake $4 meals, so the competition is on. And we're just not seeing it in these areas, but you hear it from \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 Walmart\uc2 . Between the two, they only have about $1.3 trillion in sales that say that the lower income, middle income have some issues.\par \par \uc2 And now something you haven't seen in a very long time is zero percent financing going out five, six, seven years for autos. So most definitely businesses are leaning the other way. They know about price. They know where the consumer's at, and they are reacting to it in ways that we will try and attract them.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, you think about it too, we heard that mortgage rates fell again in the latest week, a 30-year fix at around 6.20 percent. A year ago at this time, it was a full percentage point higher. It's not really moving the needle when it comes to real estate for the time being.\par \par \uc2 But we are seeing more signs that I guess the market likes, Gary, because the Dow advanced today after the biggest intraday rally we have seen in, what, a couple of years, the major averages putting it together again, technology.\par \par \uc2 What is driving all of this, even with some of these concerns?\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: Well, the last two months, the swings have just been wild, but just, Neil, fundamentally, lower interest rates, as the cost of capital goes down for the consumer all the way up to the biggest of businesses, and we had another little one-two punch.\par \par \uc2 The second part is oil prices have really sank. So that is a huge help down the road for the consumer that's a little bit stretched right now. And I think the markets may be looking past what I see as unemployment issues right now, the little bit of job weakness.\par \par \uc2 So I think we're OK. I think the market the last couple of days, we were down 700 points yesterday. I think there was a lot of Xanax being taken out in yesterday's action, but, today, good follow-through. And let's hope it continues through the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 But, of course, we have that little thing called the election that I think will definitely set the stage for December.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, that's an understatement, my friend. Always good catching up with you, Gary. Thank you very much, Gary Kaltbaum.\par \par \uc2 I don't know if any of you saw this today. Sometimes, we have many problems on Earth and all this arguing, is there going to be debate and other debate, name-calling, all the other craziness, and then this magnificent image from deep space, well, not super deep space, but significantly higher than we have been on a mission going back to the Apollo program here.\par \par \uc2 But private astronauts, all two of them, one of them a billionaire, who journeyed outside this capsule to do something private citizens have never done before. Forget about flying into space, walking in space. It gets even bigger and better than that.\par \par \uc2 The read from Mike Massimino, the former \uc2 NASA\uc2  astronaut. We like him because he's a great author, fun guy, but also a successful Italian- American astronaut. What gets better than that?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JARED ISAACMAN, CIVILIAN ASTRONAUT: \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , back at home, we all have a lot of work to do, but, from here, Earth sure looks like a perfect world.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, I love our Jonathan Serrie. He loves any report, he gets knee-deep into it, that he does. But I kind of think he really gets jazzed when it's anything to do with a space mission, particularly what we saw happening today.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Serrie, tell us all about it, my friend.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, it brings out the inner child in me, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Jared Isaacman\uc2 , that billionaire entrepreneur, made history this morning. He became the first private citizen to set foot outside an orbiting spacecraft. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ISAACMAN: Back at home, we all have a lot of work to do, but, from here, Earth sure looks like a perfect world.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: Isaacman paid \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  an undisclosed amount of money to send him and three other private astronauts into space, but this is no tourist joyride.\par \par \uc2 The crew are conducting real science and testing \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 's new EVA suits for the first time in the vacuum of space. After Isaacman returned to the capsule, Sarah Gillis stepped outside. She is a \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  engineer who oversees astronaut training for the company on the ground.\par \par \uc2 So this is her first trip to space. Because the SpaceX Dragon spacecraft has no airlock, the cabin had to be completely depressurized during the space walk. So all four private astronauts were wearing these new EVA suits. The company hopes to one day use these spacesuits for extended human stays on the moon and Mars.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM GERSTENMAIER, VICE PRESIDENT, SPACEX: This pace of development that we get to do at \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  is very much like the pace of development that was required back in the early Apollo days. We're getting a chance to do that again, where we're really starting to push frontiers with the private sector and learning new things that we would not be able to learn by staying in the risk-free environment of here on Earth.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: The Polaris Dawn mission, which launched on Tuesday, is expected to last approximately five days, returning to Earth some time over the weekend, splashing down either in the Atlantic or Gulf of Mexico, depending on the weather -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: A personal question I want to ask you, my friend, and you don't mind it.\par \par \uc2 If they said, all right, Jonathan, we have a seat on a flight, we want to send you up there, would you go?\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: You know what? I don't think I have the right stuff. I get a little claustrophobic.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Really?\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: But if they said, we have a seat for you in mission control, that would be more my speed.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Ah. Well, you see, I hear from some of my viewers who overwhelmingly support my going into space, whether I wanted to or not.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Jonathan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: Well, honestly, Neil, would you want to do it? Do you want to do it? I would guess you would.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: In a minute, because they're looking for fitness buffs. Hello?\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: Good for you.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, all right.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, love having you on.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: I'm rooting for you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There you go, buddy.\par \par \uc2 One of my favorite guests, besides Jonathan, an astronaut of the real McCoy, is Mike Massimino, the former \uc2 NASA\uc2  astronaut, "Moonshot" author. He's just a great read and a passionate read of the whole space thing.\par \par \uc2 And he's just into it, and you can tell. He's like a kid in a candy store when he was up there so many times.\par \par \uc2 So, Mike, this is a different type of adventure. It's all private citizens, right, no government types on this one. And they -- so far, everything, God willing, going very well. But this is all \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 . What do you think of it?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MASSIMINO, FORMER NASA ASTRONAUT: I think it's quite amazing, Neil, and thanks for having me talk about this just, I think, very positive subject here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: And it's something that \uc2 NASA\uc2  has supported for decades. \uc2 NASA\uc2  has looked for opportunities to try to stimulate the economy, the -- growing the commercial space business.\par \par \uc2 And it really got off to a big start. It started years ago, but it really started about 12, 14 years ago as we were retiring the shuttle, encouraging companies like \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  and other companies to develop technology that \uc2 NASA\uc2  could use, but could also be used for private purposes.\par \par \uc2 So I think today was a huge -- a huge milestone in that. And Bill Gerstenmaier, former boss at \uc2 NASA\uc2 , who I know very well, is now at \uc2 SpaceX.\uc2  In that piece that you just showed, he referred to the 1960s. And this is kind of what's going on.\par \par \uc2 Back a few years ago, we had suborbital flights with \uc2 Blue Origin\uc2  and some other companies, and then we had an orbital flight that was private.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: And now they're doing space walks, just like back in the 1960s. It's like it's starting all over again.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm old enough to remember some of those space walks from the '60s. You didn't get the film from that until they returned to see what they did. Here, you're seeing it live and in action.\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But I am curious. This is -- for the two hours this whole thing was going on, two of the four astronauts in the capsule also had to be suited up because the vacuum of space after the hatch was open.\par \par \uc2 So that had to be kind of unusual. I mean, we're used to some of these larger ships, even with the ISS or some of these others that we have seen, back to Skylab, et cetera. But if they're going to go out, everyone's going to have that experience, right?\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: Yes, and that's the way they did it, Neil, back in the Gemini program. The very first space walks I was in...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's right.\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: ... both by the Soviets and by the U.S.\par \par \uc2 And in the Gemini program, they had two astronauts inside of that spacecraft, and they had to depress the entire spacecraft. So both of them were wearing space walking suits, and one would go out, similar to what was going on today, look around, do some maneuvering around to check out the suit.\par \par \uc2 It wasn't until we got the space shuttle going -- you mentioned Skylab, space shuttle, now on the ISS, where you have an airlock. And so you only depressurize that little section. But in this case, they had to depressurize the whole spaceship, and then they had to repressurize it again. You got to close the door and make sure you can get that atmosphere back in.\par \par \uc2 So that's all -- that could be a little tricky.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, if it were any of my kids, they'd leave the door open. I would say, what?\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But let me ask you, also, one of the things -- this is an unusual mission, right? It's kind of an elliptical orbit that sends it at its furthest point further than we have ever been since the Apollo program.\par \par \uc2 And I know, for this walk, it was a little closer to Earth. What do you make of what's going on here with this mission?\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: Well, I think the commander -- I know all the crew, and I know \uc2 Jared Isaacman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What he's trying to do is not just do a joyride, and I think the piece showed it really well. They're trying to accomplish some major objectives. We haven't sent people that high in a very long time, that altitude, where you're going into...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We lost Mike. I apologize for that. But he -- you can read the passion on this.\par \par \uc2 But to show you the significance of what you're watching, first of all, just the video, the crispness of this 4K video, but what it means about us and the fact that space is back, whether it's done privately, publicly, joint ventures.\par \par \uc2 This is one thing the United States does very well. And without getting overly wonky or Yankee Doodle Dandy about it, that's something to be proud of. We have a lot of divisions in this country. That ain't one of them.\par \par \uc2 Have an out-of-the-world rest of the day.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020240912ek9c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Americans Saw Through Kamala's Fluff; Kamala Takes A Page From The Cheney Playbook; The Angle Fact-Checks Kamala's Claims; Kamala Has No Plan To Help Struggling Americans; Kamala Ducks And Dodges Every Tough Questions; Officials Warn Mail Problems Could Disrupt Voting; Kamala's Open Border Victims Speak Out; Did Trump Appeal To Independent Voters Last Night?; MSNBC Needs To Calm Down, Liberal Panel Enchanted With Taylor Swift Endorsement\b0\par\par\uc2 Laura Ingraham, Ben Domenech, Ari Fleischer, Raymond Arroyo \par \uc2 8,100 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 September 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Tomorrow on Special Report, the latest Fox News Power Rankings in the presidential race. Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 06:00 p.m. in the East, 03:00 p.m. on the West Coast. Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this Special Report. Fair, balanced, and unafraid. Here's Laura.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening, everyone. I'm Laura Ingraham. This is The Ingraham Angle from Washington tonight. All right, the media predictably singing Kamala's praises. But what do actual voters think?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I didn't get the answers to fundamental questions that we need to know as voters.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, who needs answers. And we saw fireworks on the Hill when a Democrat insulted families of illegal alien crime.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 APRIL AGUIRRE, CRIME VICTIMS' ADVOCATE: Don't make an assumption that we're being used, that is insulting.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Plus, did a Left-wing celeb just overshadow Kamala's debate performance?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O'DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: This is, I think, the most important celebrity endorsement we've ever seen in a presidential campaign.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Tell us about it, Lawrence. But first, the devil's bargain. That's the focus of tonight's Angle. I want you to ignore most of the commentary you've heard from the pundit class on the debate last night, as usual, the talking heads misread the concerns of millions of Americans watching at home. Tonight, I'm going to give you the key takeaways from the debate.\par \par \uc2 First, ABC's moderators were essentially acting as \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's debate coaches. Time and again, they served up softballs with zero follow-ups.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS, ABC NEWS HOST: Vice President Harris, you call climate change an existential threat. What would you do to fight climate change?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR, ABC NEWS HOST: A really quick response here, Vice President Harris, on this notion of weaponization of the Justice Department. One of your campaign's top lawyers responded saying, "we won't let \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  intimidate us. We won't let him suppress the vote." Is that what you believe he's trying to do here?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You're great. Do you agree you're great? Now this is entirely predictable. Now, remember a top exec at \uc2 Disney\uc2  whose portfolio includes ABC News, is a longtime pal of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . But another key point, and I think very, very important, is that Vice President Harris showed us last night that she's really not a lot more than a puppet of the failed D.C. establishment.\par \par \uc2 Now, she took weeks to rehearse during which she said no, had no adversarial press interviews, and then with a friendly panel, showed that she can memorize some lines and speak in platitudes. From the start, Harris knew she would not be fact checked or asked real follow-up questions. But still, even with her absurd ABC running mates helping her, Harris revealed a lot about herself, and the picture was not altogether positive.\par \par \uc2 Regular Americans who don't sit in studios like this, who don't get paid to talk for a living, will not be moved.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: She didn't talk about her policy changes between 2020 and 2024. Her whole centrist, moderate stance is just a facade.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: She's saying she can fix the problems that her administration has caused, but I just don't know if I can afford to take that risk.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: All these policies that she's saying she's going to implement on day one on presidency. Why? Why does she have to wait until what, January 20th to do that?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Good point. Now, they didn't want to see Kamala smirks or his -- her head bobbing up and down. They wanted actual policy. They didn't get it. One Milwaukee woman who voted Democrat in the past elections told \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  that the debate nudged her unexpectedly toward Trump. And out of the 10 undecideds that Reuters talked to, six went for Trump after the debate.\par \par \uc2 Well, from her answer on Ukraine, we know this, she will follow the Dick and \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  playbook on foreign policy, meaning no end in sight to funding more endless wars that we seem to never be able to win. But gosh, did she memorize the names of those scary weapons?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE U.S., (D) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Because of our support, because of the air defense, the ammunition, the artillery, the javelins, the Abrams tanks that we have provided, Ukraine stands as an independent and free country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: By contrast, Trump wants to save lives on both sides of the war. Remember, that is something liberals used to support.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I want the war to stop. I want to save lives that are being uselessly, people being killed. I want to get the war settled. It's the U.S. best interest to get this war finished and just get it done.\par \par \uc2 MUIR: All right.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Negotiate a deal, because we have to stop all of these human lives from being destroyed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Peace. What a novel concept. And then came Harris on climate change, immigration, tariffs.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Former President had said the climate change is a hoax, and what we know is that it is very real. The \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  came up with a border security bill, which I supported. My opponent has a plan that I call the Trump sales tax. There was a woman who helped raise us. We call her our second mother. She was a small business owner. My plan is to give a $50,000 tax deduction to start up small businesses. I imagine and have actually a plan to build what I call an opportunity economy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, I love -- for some reason that just cracked me up all night, the opportunity -- what have we been having for the last three and a half years? Well, first of all, under Trump, the entire country was an opportunity economy. The Wall Street Journal amplified the devastating census data just released yesterday. We talked a little bit about this last night. Here's the upshot.\par \par \uc2 The upshot is that real median household income remains lower than in 2019 and has barely grown since 2020. The contrast between the first three years of Trump and Biden presidencies is striking. The Trump tax reform and deregulation boosted growth, which resulted in a tighter labor market and rising wages without fueling inflation. Boom. Facts.\par \par \uc2 He produced results, she produces decline. And if she's elected, it will be more of what we've already seen, more inflationary spending, more manufacturing jobs lost, more migrants flooding in, lower wages for American workers. In other words, on key domestic issues, Harris will be no different than the nightmare of \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . What we've been dealing with. Again, a continuous flow of humanity across the border, growth killing regulations, offshoring jobs and of course, higher taxes.\par \par \uc2 And finally, Americans who didn't know her radical views heard them last night for the very first time.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : She wants to do transgender operations on illegal aliens that are in prison. This is a radical Left liberal that would do this. She wants to confiscate your guns, and she will never allow fracking in Pennsylvania. Private medical insurance, that's another thing she doesn't want to -- she wants everybody to be on government insurance where you wait six months for an operation that you need to --\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: President.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: My values have not changed, and I'm going to discuss every one of the -- at least, every point that you've made.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: At least -- but I'm not getting back to that. She never did explain her flip flops, and, of course, the fake moderators never pressed her. However, from this, we can assume that she is still the same extremist she's been her entire life on social issues, beyond supporting unrestricted abortion in all 50 states, Harris is for paid sex surgeries for migrants, legalization of hard drugs, and of course, you bet, boys and girls sports, girls and boys sports, yes. It's all highly unpopular with working class Americans of all backgrounds.\par \par \uc2 But she does not care, and this is where the regime would let Vice President Harris have her fun. That's the bargain she made. If she agreed to be their figurehead on foreign policy, immigration, economic policy, they'd let her play undisturbed in the social issues sandbox. Again, what the voters actually prefer, that's totally irrelevant.\par \par \uc2 So, the Angle's take is that any post-debate celebration over in Kamala's camp, it's premature. Remember how over the moon they got when \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  was selected as her VP? Well, he became his own meme. And they, of course, were giddy, positively giddy, about the DNC, but the headlines last week, where's the bounce? There was no bounce.\par \par \uc2 And now, once again, they're doing the Snoopy dance after her debate performance. But still, Harris has a very heavy lift here, and unless she can convince enough voters that abortion up through the third trimester, Trump's gas prices, lower incomes, and the migrant explosion, all these issues people care about, just so every woman in Alabama can get an abortion up until the third trimester, she still faces major, major election headwinds. And that's the Angle.\par \par \uc2 All right, a point that to me seemed pretty obvious. If Harris supposedly ran away with the debate, why is her camp so eager for another one? Why not just drop the mic, hit the trail, and cruise to election night? Well, the truth is, despite the public display of enthusiasm. They know she did not stick the landing, and she needed to.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, you know, when you -- when you don't win, it's like a fighter. When a fighter has a bad fight, gets knocked out or loses the fight, the first thing he says is, we want a rematch. So, we won the debate according to every poll, every single poll, I think that, are we going to do a rematch? I just don't know. We'll think about it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Joining us now, RNC Co-Chair \uc2 Lara Trump\uc2 . Lara, great to see you. What about this idea of another debate? I know Fox would love to host a debate. Bret and Martha -- I think it would be -- everyone would watch, that's for sure. But it seems that he's mulling it. Not sure.\par \par \uc2 LARA TRUMP, CO-CHAIR, RNC: Yes, well, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , of course, had already agreed to a Fox News debate. It was last week on September 4th. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  didn't want to agree to that. I wonder why, Laura. Look, I think he's very open. I think the American people deserve to see these two people and hear their ideas and unfortunately, as you just laid out, last night, we really didn't get that. What a disservice that ABC News did to the country. They had an opportunity here to really get to know \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , really hear about all of her radical ideas.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the only one, by the way, who talked about that, but on the other side to give him an opportunity, to say things and of course, it was three on one. He was on defense the whole time, having to fight them all off. But I think what last night showed us, Laura, if it wasn't already crystal clear to everybody out there, is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not just running against a candidate. He's running against an entire system. This was a continuation last night of the Hollywood production that the Democrat Party has truly become, and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is one of their best actors. You said it, you give her a line, man, she will memorize it for four years.\par \par \uc2 They gave her the line to say, if anybody asked about how is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  doing? Is he up to the task? She would deliver that line and lie directly to the American people. They have cranked this machine up to high speed, and last night, you saw her in quite a performance alongside her fellow Thespians of David Muir and Linsey Davis.\par \par \uc2 But the problem they're having, you just said it is that the American people were not convinced by anything \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  said that she is the right fit. They're having a hard time convincing people that she is going to change anything for them based on the bad economy we have right now, the open border that she's been in charge of, the wars that have broken out under her watch. She didn't say a single thing that convinced anybody in this country that they deserve to -- that she deserves their vote --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Lara.\par \par \uc2 L. TRUMP: -- and they're having a big problem with that right now.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: To that point that you just raised, I find it fascinating to actually listen to real people, so I'll shut up, and let's listen to what an undecided voter said after watching the debate last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It's important to remember that we are voting for the leader of our country, and not w