ts tracking so much with Republicans last night and they did. They wanted to hear the meat on the economy and I was really, really surprised because the intensity of the independent support there for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was very, very strong, and I didn't expect it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Here with reaction, pollster Matt Towery of Insider Advantage and Harris Poll chairman \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2  is with us.\par \par \uc2 Matt, start with you.\par \par \uc2 I found those headlines, especially "Reuters," "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 ", not exactly conservative outlets, those dials were interesting as well and I spoke to two other pollsters today, they said they got the same reaction. So there seems to be widespread consensus on that issue. Why?\par \par \uc2 MATT TOWERY, POLLSTER: Well, I think it's pretty clear. I mean, it was not Trump's best debate, we know that. But it didn't hurt him at all, apparently. We had a poll that Robert Cahaly and I did last night during the debate and afterwards, it showed undecideds didn't move as well.\par \par \uc2 And I want to mention this, I'm in the field in Michigan and in 2020, Insider Advantage had the best, the most accurate numbers in Michigan of the final five in the Real Clear Politics average. I don't have the final numbers but I can look at them enough to tell you right now Trump is either even or ahead in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 And if that's the case, then whatever boost she gets from this debate is going to be short-lived if she gets one at all.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me ask you, \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 . I have worried that some of Kamala's radical positions like no restrictions even late term abortions, mandatory gun buyback, no border wall, decriminalize illegal immigration, free housing, healthcare, education and now taxpayer funded sex change surgery for illegals and for convicted felons, her sponsorship of the $93 trillion green new deal, she would literally eliminate the filibuster. I would argue that's the end of capitalism.\par \par \uc2 Eliminating private insurance, again her bill. She supported government-run health care, you can't say radical Islamic terrorism, you can't say illegal alien, all the taxes.\par \par \uc2 I would been worried that that's not going to -- that's not permeating into the consciousness of the American people fast enough especially with early voting. Now, when I -- when I hear in dependents and watch independents on, you know, on dials like that, I begin to think people really are starting to get it in spite of a media blackout. Am I wrong in my analysis?\par \par \uc2 MARK PENN\uc2 , FORMER SENIOR ADVISOR TO CLINTONS: Well, you're not wrong in your analysis because we asked in the polls pretty much what do you think \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's views are, and they guessed more correctly than you would think. They think she is more for open borders and policies, and that she is more to the left of Biden.\par \par \uc2 So I think some of this stuff is really sinking in and it's raising questions, and that's why you have a bunch of undivided voters who are not moving that quickly until they hear more about what she stands for and particularly more about the economy because most undecided voters, they're not -- they're not voting on immigration or abortion. They're really deciding on the economy and who they think will be a pres -- a better president who will make things better for their own lives.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah. I mean, the -- how do you sponsor the green new deal, co- sponsor it with Bernie? How do you co-sponsor government health care for all, universal health care, eliminate private insurance? How do you -- how do you want to decriminalize illegal immigration, free housing, healthcare, education and sex change operations?\par \par \uc2 And is there any way voters won't know those views before the election? How do you separate yourself as she seems to be trying to do by not talking very much about them? Mark?\par \par \uc2 PENN: Well, so far, she's been pretty -- so far, she's been pretty successful at keeping those views behind the scenes and also I think the Trump campaign hasn't really created an emotional reason why those views matter to the -- to the voters.\par \par \uc2 When I did the 3:00 a.m. ad, I made commander-in-chief relevant to the voters and everybody then voted on that. And so, they're kind of getting the sense. Maybe she's too far to the left but maybe not. They're not really sure.\par \par \uc2 But Trump hasn't really been able to drive that difference and last night they didn't give him a chance to do that and let me just -- let me just add, by the way, when the refs are not fair, the score of a game generally doesn't count.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: What do you think, Matt?\par \par \uc2 TOWERY: Well, I think this. I think the Trump campaign and their messaging, I watch their ads. I think they're pretty good actually.\par \par \uc2 I think the problem that the Republicans have is all these other PACs that are raising all this money in the name of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , they're running mediocre ads. They don't hit on any of the points you're talking about, Sean, and Trump cannot do this on his own.\par \par \uc2 I mean, there are super PACs all over this country helping the Democrats big money, somebody's got to get the messaging down for Trump other than just Trump because he can't do it all on his own. I think the campaign's doing a pretty good job to be honest with you.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. I appreciate both of you. \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , thank you. Matt Towery, thank you.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, new economic numbers not good for the Harris campaign and last night's debate proved that she does not know how to fix the mess that she helped create. We'll check in with Charles Payne, coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. More bad economic news today. The federal government r