ed this inflation, 40-year high inflation. We never thought we'd have to deal with anything like this.\par \par \uc2 So, to counteract that, the Fed has to take money out of the system. They have to raise interest rates and it becomes tough, right, because now your credit card, your basic credit card, 23 percent interest, store cards, 39 percent interest. People couldn't afford to buy a house with mortgages over 7 percent.\par \par \uc2 He had to do that because on the other end, he has no control over ironically is the White House's spending.\par \par \uc2 You know, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  talked about writing a whole lot of checks last night willy-nilly. No one on that, none of the moderators said how do you pay for this because you know how we're paying for all the money that they wrote all the checks that they wrote while they were in office so far is by crushing inflation.\par \par \uc2 And what ironically what happens is when the fed has to slow the economy down, they have to crush the jobs market. So, all eyes are in the jobs market which by the way it's absolutely imploding. We've had revisions to this jobs number.\par \par \uc2 And let me tell you this, August number which was weak in my opinion anyway probably ratchet it lower. So the Fed, whether they do 25 basis points or 50 basis points is not going to move the needle a lot between now and November. But there is a desperation amongst 80 percent of the population that are saying you've done enough.\par \par \uc2 Rich people aren't hurting. In fact, rich people made $500 billion off these higher rates. It's mostly the 75, 80 percent of Americans though who are saying no mas, we need relief, we need help now.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You mean, the people that can't buy a home for the first time, but her plan is to give $25,000 to first time home buyers and student loan forgiveness, and you know, basically, it's sort of like -- well, incremental new green deal-ism when you look at her economic plan on top of 