et. The Harris campaign believes the vice president did so well they want to do it all over again in a second debate. They even posted the full hour and 40 minutes from last night on X calling it their newest ad.\par \par \uc2 But while Harris landed a lot of punches, she also left a lot on the table.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Let's have a good debate.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Nice to see you.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The afterglow of Harris's good night may be shorter than her allies hope, \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reads, pundits said Harris won the debate. Undecided voters weren't so sure. "She did not see much different from Mr. Biden, and they wanted change, and most of all, what they wanted to hear and didn't was the fine print."\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MIKE BRAUN\uc2  (R-IN): It gets fleshed out in terms of what she's for. My prediction is she gets weaker.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Asked about this.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: She is Biden.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Clearly, I am not \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Top Harris surrogates were left reaching on the spin room floor.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: I didn't hear a single thing that was different than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's plans.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D-CA): Well, that's news to me. Look at just the difference the way she addressed the issue of abortion and how she was able to humanize it.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Again and again.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JOSH SHAPIRO (D-PA): She's laying out her vision. She's laying out her plans, her demeanor, her approach to leadership.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: His vision though.\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: Now, every leader is different.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Democrats still dancing around Harris's past support for progressive policies like gender transition surgery for detained migrants, funded by taxpayers.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Her values have not changed.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Harris did achieve her goal, get Trump angry, so he would spend the night defending himself, not articulating his plans, hoping the contrast would be enough.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was fired by 81 million people.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): But in the process, Harris failed to offer specifics, like how she would achieve a cease fire in Gaza and made false statements of her own.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: There is not one member of the United States military who is in active duty in a combat zone.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Forgetting the 3,400 U.S. troops working to defeat \uc2 ISIS\uc2  in Iraq and Syria, Marines under attack from Houthis since last fall, or the three soldiers killed by Iranian proxies in Jordan this January.\par \par \uc2 Today, no push for momentum from Harris taking the day off the trail to mark 9/11 going in for another handshake with Trump, whose team is seizing on this moment.\par \par \uc2 Biden trading hats with a Trump supporter at the Shanksville fire station. The White House says, after calling for unity.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): What Harris left unsaid in the debate renews pressure for interviews after a \uc2 New York Times\uc2  poll this week showed 31 percent of registered voters and 28 percent of likely voters say they need to learn more about her, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thanks, Jacqui. Last night provided Americans the opportunity to hear former President Trump and Vice President Harris's competing visions for the nation. Historically, following debates in the past, former President Trump has been behind in the polls, as it currently stands in the Real Clear Politics average of polls, Harris maintains a slight edge in that average. After the first debate in 2020 President Biden's lead over President Trump was much higher. He had a nine point advantage, and that first debate was characterized by many as chaotic with personal jabs and taunts amid clashes over policy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Make sure you, in fact, let people know, you're a Senator, I'm not going to answer the question because --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Why wouldn't you answer that question?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Because the question is -- the question is -- the question is -- the question -- will you shut up, man?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, so, four years earlier, a record average of 84 million viewers watched Hillary Clinton and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's first face off.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Donald supported the invasion of Iraq.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Wrong.\par \par \uc2 CLINTON: That is absolutely proved over and over again.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Wrong.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, following that debate, if you look at the polls, Trump trailed Clinton nationally again by nine percentage points. The location of that last night showdown between Trump and Harris is significant. Past debates have shown and appeared to benefit former President Trump in Pennsylvania polling.\par \par \uc2 Current polls show the former president and the vice president really in a dead heat in the critical swing state.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, President Biden led Trump in Pennsylvania, but following their first debate, Trump saw a slight boost there with voters.\par \par \uc2 Now, if we go in past presidential debates and past presidential races, President Biden obviously ended up winning the 2020 election. As you can see here, he won Pennsylvania by just -- let's see, one point, just a little bit over one percentage point.\par \par \uc2 Major cities in Philadelphia and Pittsburgh really boosted him. They're home to heavily Democratic urban residents, wealthy, college educated, suburban voters, both groups helping propel Biden to victory in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 But let's take a look back to 2016, we can see Trump won that state by less than one point, thanks in part to counties where Republican leaning voters, rural voters live.\par \par \uc2 And now let's zoom out in 2016, you can see if pass is prolog, Pennsylvania could play a pivotal role either way again in this election.\par \par \uc2 Senior National Correspondent Rich Edson takes a closer look at the debate and the impact of moderators.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I would do NBC. I'd do Fox too. I'd do Fox too. But right now we have to determine whether or not we even want to do it.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Former President Trump and Vice President Harris's campaign are already talking about a second debate. Harris' team says the V.P. is entertaining all offers. Trump says he did so well in the first, he may not need a second.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We had a great night last night.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): The first one featured Trump's criticism of Vice President Harris's policy flip flops.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : She's going to my philosophy now, in fact, I was going to send her a MAGA hat. She copied Biden's plan, and it's like four sentences, like, run, spot, run.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): And complains about the two moderators. They fact checked Trump about abortion and crime data, and let Harris slide when she said there are no American troops in combat zones.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : It was three to one. It was a rigged a deal.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): They also checked unsubstantiated claims over whether migrants in Springfield, Ohio eat people's pets.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : The people that came in, they're eating the cats, they're eating -- they're eating the pets.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): Though, Ohio Governor \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  announced today he is sending state aid to the area because a surge of Haitian migrants is causing, "Significant stress on its health and public safety systems." The Harris campaign says pinning Trump's debate performance on the refs is telling.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL TYLER, KAMALA HARRIS CAMPAIGN COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR: Complaining about the moderators, complaining about the rules is something that a losing campaign does.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): The Wall Street Journal editorial board adds, "She won the debate because she came in with a strategy to taunt and goad Mr. Trump into diving down rabbit holes of personal grievance and vanity that left her policies and history largely untouched. He always takes the bait." Like when she answered a question on immigration by knocking Trump's rallies.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: People start leaving his rallies early out of exhaustion and boredom.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : She said, people start leaving, people don't go to her rallies. There's no reason to go.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): There is at least one more debate on the calendar, the vice presidential debate between Minnesota Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  and Senator J.D. Vance that's scheduled for October 1st, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Rich, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A Pakistani man accused of plotting to kill prominent politicians here in the U.S. is facing terrorism charges tonight. Court documents reveal Asif Merchant is accused of attempting to commit an act of terrorism that transcends national boundaries, as well as murder for hire. His target in the foiled plot was alleged to be an American politician and official, possibly former President Trump. Merchant's alleged plot included two hit men, 25 actors to stage a fake protest and create additional chaos around the time of the murder and a woman who would take care of reconnaissance.\par \par \uc2 Our other big story tonight here in New York, it's the anniversary of the 9/11 terror attacks, both major party presidential nominees plus the current commander in chief present for a service at one of the sites targeted by terrorists. It was one of many memorials held today.\par \par \uc2 Senior Correspondent Eric Shawn reports tonight from here in New York.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ERIC SHAWN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): It has been 23 years. It seems as if not one day has gone by.\par \par \uc2 In New York City, at the Pentagon and in Shanksville, Pennsylvania, a time to remember.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: 23 years ago, my 23-year-old brother went to work and never came home.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN (voice over): President Biden, Vice President Harris, former President Trump and Senator J.D. Vance started at the outside of the World Trade Center in lower Manhattan. Biden and Trump separated by former Mayor \uc2 Michael Bloomberg\uc2 , for some family members, years of anguish has turned to anger.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The families deserve justice and accountability.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN (voice over): They lashed out at the Biden administration over the plea deal with accused 9/11 mastermind, Khalid Sheikh Mohammed, giving him life in prison, a decision Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  revoked.\par \par \uc2 The families also demanded that the government release evidence they say would expose Saudi Arabia's involvement in the attacks, a charge the Kingdom denies.\par \par \uc2 In Shanksville, a United Flight 93 crash after passengers heroically fought back against the hijackers. Biden and Harris participated in a wreath laying ceremony as did Trump.\par \par \uc2 At the Pentagon, the president and vice president also laid a wreath and defense officials honored the 184 people killed when hijacked Flight 77 crashed into the building. It is a day to honor the lives and sacrifice of those who are gone.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: 9/11 is about honor, remembering all those people who are here and all those who are so selfless to run into those buildings. And I think that there is something to remember about unity and community that is -- that stays with me, and it's the best way that I want to remember 9/11.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN (on camera): The threat of radical Islamic terrorism does remain with us. Just last week in Canada, a 20-year-old Pakistani man was arrested, accused of plotting a mass shooting event at a Jewish Center here in New York City, investigators say that that suspect proudly boasted that it would be the largest terrorist attack since 9/11. He is due in court on Friday, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Eric Shawn at Ground Zero. Eric, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, shortly in our Common Ground segment, two members of Congress, one Democrat, one Republican talk about their 9/11 memories, their shared experience at West Point and serving in Iraq, and what they're working on together.\par \par \uc2 Year over year, consumer price increases have reached a three year low. Today's Labor Department report says prices rose 2-1/2 percent in August from a year earlier. That's down from 2.9 percent in July. The Dow, like that seemed to gain 125, the S&P 500 was up 59, the NASDAQ jumped 370 today.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, we have live team coverage of Hurricane Francine's landfall in South Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 But first, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 2 in Detroit as Michigan's Attorney General announces what she calls a disappointing close to the investigation into former sports Doctor Larry Nassar.\par \par \uc2 Dana Nessel says her office reviewed more than 6,000 documents about how \uc2 Michigan State University\uc2  handled the sex abuse reports against Nassar. She says no new relevant information was found. Nassar is currently in prison after more than 100 women accused him of assault.\par \par \uc2 Fox 13 in Salt Lake City as a federal judge blocks one of Utah's new laws concerning social media access for young people from taking effect. That legislation required age verification and put restrictions on targeted advertising and personalized content.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Dallas from Fox 4, our affiliate there, one of the big stories there tonight, a player who bought a ticket in Texas wins an estimated Mega Millions jackpot of $800 million, congratulations.\par \par \uc2 Four players in other states matched five of the six balls to win a million dollars each, big winners.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: "FOX WEATHER ALERT" now, Hurricane Francine has just made landfall in South Louisiana. It is expected to bring life-threatening, storm surge, destructive winds. Some areas are under mandatory evacuation orders, power outages, flooding, property damaging -- damage, all possibilities. Seeing some of that.\par \par \uc2 We have "FOX TEAM COVERAGE". Adam Klotz is here at the Fox Weather Center in New York. But we begin with Robert Ray of Fox Weather in Louisiana, with the latest. Good evening, Robert.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT RAY, FOX WEATHER CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret, and everyone watching tonight from Houma, Louisiana, not too far from the coast of where Hurricane Francine just made landfall as a Category 2, whopping 100-mile an hour winds.\par \par \uc2 We just experienced the northeastern eyewall, which was the strongest. Heavy rains and wind pounding this region, knocking out power to thousands already.\par \par \uc2 The problem is storm surge is a major problem, seven to 10 feet in some areas on the coastline, like Grand Isle, that was really just demolished three years ago by Hurricane Ida.\par \par \uc2 Now, this storm is making its way up into the major metropolitan areas like New Orleans and Baton Rouge, and there could be extensive power outages there tonight, as well as a foot of rain in a region that has already taken ample amounts of precipitation this summer, they are well above the amounts.\par \par \uc2 We have a little bit of a lull right now, but this system is making its way up as the winds and the rain will continue here throughout the night. State of emergency in play. Nearly 2,500 National Guard members are activated to try and help anyone in peril, as well as swift boats and other entities that can help these folks.\par \par \uc2 It's going to be a long night here in the state of Louisiana, as the system will then track over to Mississippi, Alabama and into northern states. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Robert, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's find out where Francine is, where she is headed. Adam Klotz is here at the fox Weather Center with that. Good evening, Adam.\par \par \uc2 ADAM KLOTZ, FOX NEWS CHANNEL METEOROLOGIST: Hey there, Bret. Yes, still a long ways to go with the system. Even though we talk about a landfall, still a Category 2 storm. And ultimately, the legacy of this storm probably going to be the rain. So, it doesn't matter that the landfall is right now.\par \par \uc2 It still going to drag all this moisture with it, but you're still looking at winds at 100 miles an hour as it makes that landfall. Getting closer and closer to New Orleans, that is an area that's under a hurricane watch, because it's going to be a very close pass.\par \par \uc2 The hurricane warning, that's all of the parishes right along the coast, and then a couple of those inland. New Orleans, though, still going to feel the brunt of this, even though that eye wall isn't going to actually cross right over it.\par \par \uc2 It's currently actually seeing some of these tropical storm force winds. That's everything in the yellow. But as you get into the orange, that's more of a hurricane gust, and then, you see your hurricane winds there, closer to the core of the storm.\par \par \uc2 Again, this is just now making landfall, so, all of that's going to push inland. But as it runs inland, it's going to quickly start to weaken. That's just the nature of these systems, right? They need that warm water to ultimately fuel themselves.\par \par \uc2 But what is the -- what are the winds that currently -- well, we've got a coastal wind there, right along the coast, close to 70 miles an hour.\par \par \uc2 You run a little bit further inland, you look at a place like New Orleans, a gust up to 41 miles an hour. We expect that's going to continue to climb. But I told you that the legacy of this storm ultimately is going to be all of the moisture that this has picked up over the Gulf. It is bringing a ton of rain with it. That's that very front leading edge, and it is really coming down there.\par \par \uc2 These green boxes are currently flash flood warnings, so the rain coming down incredibly quickly. Nowhere for all that moisture to go. It's been raining, actually a lot along the northern Gulf Coast.\par \par \uc2 So, the grounds already saturated. Flooding is the really big concern. You see a three on a scale of four as far as our flash flood risk goes, on Wednesday. That's going to linger into Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, Bret, that's going to get up near Nashville on Friday. So, the system is just going to stall out in the Midwest, the South, and we're going to be talking about a whole lot of rain the next couple of days.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. We'll track it. Adam, thank you.\par \par \uc2 KLOTZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's head out west tonight. Southern California firefighters battling massive blaze burning in Los Angeles and San Bernardino counties. It broke out in the San Gabriel Canyon over the weekend, the fire torched about 48,000 acres, zero containment as of this morning, evacuation orders have been issued in several areas.\par \par \uc2 Up next, our "COMMON GROUND" segment on this. The 9/11 anniversary, featuring former West Point classmates, who served in Iraq together and now are in Congress.\par \par \uc2 First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. Mexico's senate votes to overhaul the country's judiciary, clearing the biggest hurdle for a controversial constitutional revision. It will make all judges stand for election in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 It's a change that critics fear will politicize the judicial branch and threaten the country's democracy.\par \par \uc2 Anti-war protesters clashed with police outside of military arms convention in the Australian city of Melbourne. Police say protesters hurled bottles and rocks and horse manure. They also sprayed officers with liquid irritants, some of them were identified as acid. Officers retaliated with pepper spray, flash distraction devices, and rubber bullets.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Jerusalem, one of the big stories there tonight. A rare bronze-era jar accidentally smashed by a 4-year-old visiting a museum in Israel is back on display tonight.\par \par \uc2 Restoration experts were actually able to carefully piece the jar back together. It's one of the only containers of its size, and from that period still complete when it was discovered.\par \par \uc2 That jar has been on display at the museum for 35 years. Be careful.\par \par \uc2 To some other stories "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight, we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEN. LLOYD AUSTIN (RET), UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF DEFENSE: We are the United States of America. We do not bend to terror.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Charles F Burlingame III, United States, Navy Reserve, retired.\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN: Democracy, freedom, and liberty under law. And those beliefs have inspired generations of patriots to step forward and to wear the cloth of our nation, and they still do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Defense Secretary Austin today at the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 Captain Burlingame, by the way, was the pilot of American Airlines Flight 77. Today is the 23rd anniversary of the 9/11 attacks. Tonight, we talk about that with two West Point graduates who now serve in Congress.\par \par \uc2 We welcome in, Michigan Republican Congressman John James and New York Democrat Pat Ryan. They were cadets at the time of the attacks. Each served in Iraq a few years later.\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Ryan, first to you. This day, obviously, brings a lot of memories, but take you back to West Point and kind of getting word of these towers coming down.\par \par \uc2 REP PAT RYAN (D-NY): Well, John and I actually, literally roomed across the hall from each other in the barracks, and I remember watching live that horrific second strike on the second tower.\par \par \uc2 And at that night, we actually, all 4,000 cadets went out on the grassy green plain and stood silently in the dark for taps. We didn't even know how many were lost, but we certainly knew our future, our country's future was going to change. And I think we -- it's so important that we keep that spirit in mind.\par \par \uc2 And the unity and patriotism that we felt, the impulse to run towards the danger and the damage.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congressman James?\par \par \uc2 REP. JOHN JAMES (R-MI): The thing that I remember the most about that is remembering it all hitting us. Before that time, we were there to get a good education. We were there to play sports. We were there to be a part of something bigger than ourselves. But that was the first moment we realized, we're going to war.\par \par \uc2 That was the moment that we realized that we would have to stand up in defense of this nation and make sure that things like this never happened again.\par \par \uc2 Our class, class 2004, has suffered the most casualties of any class since the Vietnam War, because we were the first class to take the oath of affirmation. That's essentially when you're rising to your junior year, that's when you start and your five-year obligation begins.\par \par \uc2 We're the first class to do that, and we paid a heavy toll for it. But we all love our country. We put our country first. We put our country first then. We're putting our country first now.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You all are working on a resolution for -- to designate this Patriot Week. Why do that, Congressman Ryan?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Well, I was just going to actually say, I'm wearing the names of our classmates on this memorial bracelet that so many fellow vets, where I know from many generations of conflict. We got to remind this country, we're in a really tough time. We talk about this a lot.\par \par \uc2 We got to remind us of our better angels. We got to remind everyone how we felt, how united, how our resolve, our strength, our shared pain and grief that we turned into action and pride.\par \par \uc2 And so I think about my two boys who are 2 and 5. I want them to feel that. I want every American to remember, starting with September 11th and going actually until Constitution Day, which would end Patriot Week, what this country is all about.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congressman James, I mean, we often see, and we're seeing your post, Congressman Ryan there. We often see a lot of fighting, obviously, on Capitol Hill about everything.\par \par \uc2 You guys have a long relationship. You both served in Iraq. And you obviously were at West Point together.\par \par \uc2 But there are things getting done on the big picture, Republicans and Democrats, up on Capitol Hill. One of the reasons we do this common ground.\par \par \uc2 JAMES: We are actually getting a lot of good things done, and a lot of them -- most of them that we've done is in a bipartisan manner. Everything from Patriot Week to our anti-human trafficking bill that just passed on what's called suspension. Essentially, it has a lot of bipartisan support, just passed by to not just look after American citizens, but understand that America has a moral obligation to look after the downtrodden, those who are enslaved across the world.\par \par \uc2 They're everything from reignite hope or veteran entry to apprenticeship. There's a number of things that we've done working together to make America and the world a better place.\par \par \uc2 We have undergone a lifetime in service when we swore an oath to the Constitution back when we were 18 years old. And we're continuing that service from combat to Congress, and we're looking forward to finishing strong in this 118th.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, after a debate last night, after -- as you're getting ready to head towards November 5th, Congressman Ryan, it's only going to get more divisive, I would assume, before election day. What do you tell people about that as you close in on November 5th?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Yes. I'm worried about our country. I'm worried about our politics. I'm worried about people in both parties calling to the worst of us. And I know it almost sounds cliche, but I think we really learned in our service.\par \par \uc2 It's about -- you know, when we were going on in patrol, I didn't care your party, I didn't care your race, gender, sexual orientation, what team you rooted for, although I'm a \uc2 Yankees\uc2  fan, but it was about the mission we had to accomplish together.\par \par \uc2 We, if I think, you know, by force of personality, in some ways, operate it that way here in Congress, and that's refreshing to me, that's how I'm going to -- that's a lot of what I'm going to talk about, and have talked about with my constituents in my community.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A serious point here, Congressman James, military recruitment. There's a lot of concern about the military. The goals back in 2021 were all met, but if you look at the latest, the current fiscal year, they're on track for recruitment goals, but it's, in part, because perhaps they've lowered those expectations. They've lowered the expectations from recent years.\par \par \uc2 Are you concerned about the military and people stepping up?\par \par \uc2 JAMES: I am. Leadership is important. And people need to remember that a United America is undefeated. They need to see that leadership emulated from the top, which is why I think there should be more veterans in Congress, not less.\par \par \uc2 People who are used to working in the most austere circumstances, recognizing that no one has a monopoly on the right answer, but in working together representing the people of the United States, we get to a more perfect union.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's the type of inspirational type of leadership that our younger generations are looking forward to.\par \par \uc2 It's up to us as leaders to be able to paint that vision and to be able to lead by example to actually craft in America that they're excited to fight for just like we were.\par \par \uc2 We were young, 17, 18, we got in the military. And we truly believe that by continuing to call on our higher angels and by leading by example, like we're doing right here today, working in a bipartisan manner to get things done for the American people.\par \par \uc2 We can truly show the younger generation, this is America worth fighting for.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You both are examples.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Ryan, Congressman James, we appreciate you coming on, especially on this day on the 9/11 anniversary. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You can see all the common ground segments on my podcast, Common Ground. Find that in the All Star Panel podcast under the Bret Baier podcast at foxnewspodcast.com. \uc2 Spotify\uc2  or if you download podcasts. You can also see this discussion on the Fox News \uc2 YouTube\uc2  page.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Howard Kurtz with the media buzz about last night's debate moderators in the fallout from it.\par \par \uc2 And later we hear from our special report New York panel about their thoughts on the debate and the election.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Speaker Mike Johnson has pulled a vote on a temporary spending bill that would keep federal agencies and programs funded for six months when the new budget year begins October 1st.\par \par \uc2 That move comes as it was becoming clear the measure lacked the votes to pass. The legislation included a provision that would require people registering to vote to provide proof of citizenship.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump and many of his supporters are characterizing last night's debate, with Vice President Harris, as three against one.\par \par \uc2 Let's take a look and get some perspective tonight from Fox News media analyst and host of Fox's "MEDIA BUZZ," Howard Kurtz.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOWARD KURTZ, FOX NEWS MEDIA ANALYST (voice-over): It's not just Republicans. The ABC moderators anchor, David Muir, and Linsey Davis, a weekend anchor and correspondent for such shows as \uc2 GMA\uc2  and Nightline were far more aggressive against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  correcting his version of events five times compared to zero for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It's not that the vice president didn't offer them opportunities.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES, DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: I made that very clear in 2020. I will not ban fracking.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: That's not true. She said \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  would oppose fracking.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And what has been happening under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's abortion bans? Couples who pray and dream of having a family are being denied IVF treatments.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Trump has actually been a leading advocate of in vitro fertilization.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the candidate, has said in this election, there will be a bloodbath.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Nothing from ABC, even though it was long ago established that Trump was talking about a bloodbath for the auto industry.\par \par \uc2 It was the same story when Harris accused Trump of warning to sign a national abortion ban and pursuing \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 's Project 2025, both of which he has disavowed. There were crickets from ABC.\par \par \uc2 But when the former president said crime was rising --\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR, ABC NEWS ANCHOR: President Trump, as you know, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  says overall violent crime is actually coming down in this country.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Harris was asked to explain her abandonment of left-wing stances from four years ago, but wasn't really pressed.\par \par \uc2 A sharp difference from when Trump was asked about January 6th.\par \par \uc2 MUIR: Is there anything you regret about what you did on that day?\par \par \uc2 Is there anything you regret about what you did on that day? Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I had nothing to do with that.\par \par \uc2 I thought it was terrible from the standpoint of ABC. They're the most dishonest, in my opinion.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: But the mainstream media heed prays on the ABC moderators. Today's "New York Times" headline. "ABC's Matter-of-Fact Moderators Built Factual Guardrails Around Trump."\par \par \uc2 But is that the role of a news organization?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: The hostile relationship between \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the media has now lasted a decade.\par \par \uc2 And last night, without question, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was the clear beneficiary.\par \par \uc2 Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Howie, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Overseas now. Ukraine is pleading for permission from the U.S. to strike inside Russia with long-range missiles.\par \par \uc2 The top diplomats from the U.S. and Great Britain were in Kyiv today for discussions.\par \par \uc2 Senior foreign affairs correspondent Greg Palkot shows us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): The war between Ukraine and Russia is intensifying.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian air defenses in action against Russian missiles and drones, after the Ukrainian drones struck Russia, including the Moscow area.\par \par \uc2 Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  arriving in Kyiv with new U.K. Foreign Minister \uc2 David Lammy\uc2 . Ukrainian officials had long-range missiles on their mind and the freedom to use them against Russia.\par \par \uc2 ANDRII SYBIHA, UKRAINIAN FOREIGN MINISTER (through translator): To defend our people, brave steps are necessary. It's important to lift all restrictions on American and British weapons against lawful targets on Russian territory.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: Meeting with the Ukrainian President Zelenskyy, Blinken said the topic was discussed and the details will be brought back to Washington.\par \par \uc2 While President Biden has said, there is movement on the issue, along with a promise of $717 million in new U.S. aid, there was strong support.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF STATE: We want Ukraine to win. And we're fully committed to keep marshalling the support that it needs for its brave defenders and citizens to do just that.\par \par \uc2 DAVID LAMMY\uc2 , BRITISH FOREIGN MINISTER: We are determined to see Ukraine win in their efforts over the coming months and over this period.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: And then there's a race for the White House and the different stances on Ukraine of Vice President Harris and former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 At another meeting, Zelenskyy said, victory over Russia depends mostly on the support of the United States, another variable for Ukraine to deal with.\par \par \uc2 OLEKSIY GONCHARENKO, UKRAINIAN PARLIAMENT MEMBER: I think that nothing will happen before American people will make their choice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: President Biden meets with the new U.K. Prime Minister, \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2 , at the White House Friday. Maybe more hope for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Greg, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, so who do you think won last night's debate? We'll ask our special panel here in New York and the way forward, after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Clearly, I am not \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And I am certainly not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And what I do offer is a new generation of leadership for our country.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : She's going to do all these wonderful things. Why hasn't she done it? She's been there for three and a half years.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's a good thing for the American people to see those two candidates on stage together.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): Even though the contest was three to one last night, he clearly prevailed.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT JENNINGS, CNN SENIOR POLITICAL COMMENTATOR (PH): It is a little hard to complain about the refs when you're not making your own jump shots.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, a lot of fallout from the debate last night. The headlines all over the place. You can see a few of them here. "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 ," "Harris dominates as Trump gets defensive."\par \par \uc2 "National Review," "Republicans blame debate moderators as Democrats take a victory lap."\par \par \uc2 "POLITICO," "It was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's night in the worst possible way."\par \par \uc2 But take a look at this "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " piece. "Pundits said Harris won the debate. Undecided voters weren't so sure. Undecided voters acknowledged that Ms. Harris seemed more presidential than Mr. Trump. And they said she laid out a sweeping vision to fix some of the country's most stubborn problems. But they also said she did not seem much different from Mr. Biden. And they wanted change. And most of all, what they wanted to hear, and didn't, was the fine print."\par \par \uc2 With that, let's bring in our special panel here. Dana Perino, co-anchor of "AMERICA'S NEWSROOM" and co-hosts of "THE FIVE."\par \par \uc2 "FIVE" co-hosts, Jessica Tarlov and Charles Payne, host of "MAKING MONEY WITH CHARLES PAYNE" on Fox Business.\par \par \uc2 OK, Jessica, I'm sure in democratic circles there was a lot of happiness.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS HOST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But that "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " piece does hit on something about undecided voters. And whether this does really move the needle.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Well, the debate was not for me. I know how I'm voting.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It's for the tens of thousands of people across six or seven states, 48 percent of whom said, they want to know more about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . And I think that she was successful in that.\par \par \uc2 And "The Times'" piece, it goes on to talk about this a bit. And then the FiveThirtyEight podcast, they were talking about it in The Daily Podcast as well, that there were a number of focus groups.\par \par \uc2 Like "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " had one, 24 people in it, 22 said that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  won. The CNN undecided voter focus group, a similar result.\par \par \uc2 She did everything that she needed to do or that it was possible to do in that one evening in 90 minutes.\par \par \uc2 First and foremost, looking presidential, which was noted in a lot of the commentary about it, making it clear that this is someone who you have known as a president, but he is no longer presidential at all.\par \par \uc2 And I just wanted to point out because it was in the intro, sought there. So blueprint, which is \uc2 Reid Hoffman\uc2 's polling outfit, has tested all of the messaging from last night.\par \par \uc2 The things that Kamala said, the number one best testing message was, I am not \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: So she did manage to get that one out there.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. So you mentioned the CNN undecided voter group. And you're right, they did vote to say that she did better.\par \par \uc2 But also had some interesting things to say in that group. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think it's important to remember that we are voting for the leader of our country and not who we like the most or who we want in our wedding party, but who is actually going to make our country better.\par \par \uc2 And we're in an incredibly unique situation where we've had both of the candidates in office before and we've gotten to see what they do.\par \par \uc2 And when facts come to facts, my life was better when Trump was in office.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And then, Charles?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX BUSINESS HOST: Yes. I think that's what a lot of people are going to think about last as they go, you know, to vote because things are getting worse rapidly, particularly in the economic front.\par \par \uc2 You know, we just had another disappointing job support and revisions to real job numbers. You know, they keep coming out revised significantly lower.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Can you explain that? I mean, does anybody explain that?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It is so frustrating. You know, they do these surveys. It's two surveys. One they call the household survey and the other the establishment, where they contact businesses.\par \par \uc2 And then one of the big problems, I think, with all government data, but particularly the jobs numbers recently, is fewer and fewer establishments fill out the survey.\par \par \uc2 So imagine that you only have a small handful of folks in the first place and then only half of them bother to fill it out.\par \par \uc2 And then they make these adjustments where they guess about birth and death and all these kind of things.\par \par \uc2 And consequently, you'll get a number that could be revised significantly lower, which we saw for June and July.\par \par \uc2 And the thing is, Wall Street reacts to the number that comes out on that particular Friday. Everyone watching this show knows the actual numbers because they're living those numbers. And I think that's important for the election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. You've digested a full day on two shows, Dana.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS HOST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What's your thought?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I think that we've had these election altering events all through the summer over the last 11 weeks, and the race remained tied. I think that last night, because \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  did not collapse. She overperformed expectations. And the Democrats are very happy.\par \par \uc2 And I think that another reason you'll see that is in the money that comes in. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been very consistent with his base and with the people who say they're going to vote for him.\par \par \uc2 If you've seen up and downs throughout this election, it has been with the Democrats who have to make a decision. Are they going to be committed and enthusiastic to vote for her?\par \par \uc2 And where she goes in the next few days and where the other candidates go is very interesting. They're trying to expand the map to North Carolina. The \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  people think that that is a real possibility and pay attention to the Senate Republicans who really want to make sure that they not just get back the majority but they have enough cushion.\par \par \uc2 They're looking for some more donations, right? They are concerned about a cash crunch because her cash advantage is increasing. Whoever wins the White House is likely to win the House. And so it is high stakes everywhere. It probably didn't change a lot last night, but because she didn't collapse, that's considered a win.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And Taylor Swift came out right after the debate and put out this post saying, she endorses \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Is that like the celebrity endorsement of endorsements, Jessica?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I mean, it's pretty good. Anyone who's pretending like this doesn't matter or that it doesn't potentially move the needle, even just a sheer voter registration, which is something that she's done before. And we saw a meaningful jump there.\par \par \uc2 Of course it matters. I thought that her post was incredibly thoughtful. She talked about exactly why it was that she was doing this and referenced back to the fake AI images of her that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was posting off his Truth Social accounts saying that she endorsed him.\par \par \uc2 And it felt like a very personal choice for her. And the contrast between the Taylor Swift of 2018, when she's going back and forth with her parents, like should I get involved? What should I say about a \uc2 Marsha Blackburn\uc2  race, and the Taylor Swift of today who just says straight up, it's important to me that women have control over their own bodies and that -- this is what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's team did and it's not OK with me.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I'm not sure Taylor Swift moved Charles. I don't think so. I'm not sure.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No? She's a capitalist.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let me -- let me ask you this, Charles.\par \par \uc2 First question of the debate, do you believe -- to Vice President Harris. Do you believe Americans are better off than they were four years ago?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: She really didn't have an answer to that. And that was a big --\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Not only did she not have an answer, but that's one -- it sets the stage for the rest of the night while she wasn't pressed on it. That and the inflation issue.\par \par \uc2 And I think the reason for it, they've done -- you're right, \uc2 Reid Hoffman\uc2  and these really billionaires who have backed in this campaign have done some serious great research.\par \par \uc2 And they know one thing, President Biden insulted the American public almost every time he spoke, saying, you're doing amazing. And he even would be offended when Paul said, no, he says, you know, essentially saying, people, Americans are dumb. They don't know how great it is. So they understood that fine line.\par \par \uc2 Can you admit that we sparked the greatest inflation scare in this country in four decades? And, you know, of course you can't admit that, but it exists.\par \par \uc2 And so she tried to want that thin line. And I think she was able to because the moderators let it get away with it. That's the most critical question in this election. And her feet were not held to the fire.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Trump could have asked too.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He could have --\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That first --\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: That's true.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: -- answer he could have.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And I thought that -- I can't remember who but smart --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Did you put your pen down?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: -- person was on "THE FIVE." I did not put pen down. Pen down was 917 at the first debate, Biden and Trump. I kept -- I kept taking notes this whole time, but I can't remember who -- which brilliant co-host it was on "THE FIVE" who said this that, on every single issue, Trump could have said, on immigration, you better off than four years ago, crime, economy. So anything that they brought up, he could have turned that back on her.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Second debate?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Maybe they'll have a chance.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Second debate?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Right here on Fox is the hope. I think people are warming to it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You think -- you think they will?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I sincerely hope so because if you want to know where the voters, you need to pick up par. They're watching here. And they're paying attention to this. And they will also see it as a brave act on her part and know that she can stand up for herself.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We shall see.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Panel, thanks.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. Thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Finally tonight, a Special Report Salute.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: First response.\par \par \uc2 PAUL VENETO, RETIRED FLIGHT ATTENDANT: Absolutely. They were the first ones to go up against terrorism that morning on those airplanes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Take a look at this. Retired flight attendant, Paul Veneto, is honoring the airplane crews who died in the 9/11 attacks by pushing a beverage cart from Boston's public garden to ground zero here in New York City.\par \par \uc2 A journey that mirrors the path of the two flights that hit the World Trade Center. Veneto had worked the day before September 11th on United Flight 175, which later crashed into the South Tower.\par \par \uc2 Paulie's Push is what he's calling it, honors his colleagues' heroism that day. That's pretty cool.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, the latest on Fox News power rankings in the presidential race.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch a slide, set your DVR 6:00 in the East, 3:00 P.M. on the West Coast.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for the SPECIAL REPORT, fair balanced and unafraid.\par \par \uc2 Here's Laura.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240912ek9b00001\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\p